arget_name": "sandy soil", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 14, "stable_key": "clu_6f1814f0ff6c14fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sand", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q34679", "wikidata_description": "granular material composed of finely divided rock and mineral particles, sand particles range in diameter from 0.063 to 2 mm (per ISO 14688)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand", "wikipedia_extract": "Sand is a granular material composed of finely divided mineral particles. Sand has various compositions but is usually defined by its grain size. Sand grains are smaller than gravel and coarser than silt. Sand can also refer to a textural class of soil or soil type; i.e., a soil containing more than 85 percent sand-sized particles by mass. The composition of sand varies, depending on the local rock sources and conditions, but the most common constituent of sand in inland continental settings and non-tropical coastal settings is silica (silicon dioxide, or SiO2), usually in the form of quartz. Calcium carbonate is the second most common type of sand."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Gravel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4407, "target_cluster_name": "shingle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sand is defined by its grain size, being smaller than gravel.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sand is defined by its grain size, being smaller than gravel."}, {"found_name": "Clay", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 344, "target_cluster_name": "Barro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sand is a textural class of soil, alongside clay and silt.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sand is a textural class of soil, alongside clay and silt."}, {"found_name": "Powder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "tar