_physician_1652", "source_name": "Humor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humor", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Humor", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humor adusto", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Humor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "humor", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humor", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humor adusto", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "humor", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humores", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "humor adusto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "humor", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "humor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humores", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 28, "stable_key": "clu_b43e42b88da137de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Bodily fluids central to humoral theory, believed to influence health and temperament.", "note": "Includes specific types like 'humor adusto' and 'humor phleugmatico'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540–500 BC)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bucho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3698, "target_cluster_name": "bucho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In premodern medicine, the stomach was considered a site for the generation and interaction of bodily humors, particularly related to digestion and temperament.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In premodern medicine, the stomach was considered a site for the generation and interaction of bodily humors, particularly related to digestion and temperament.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "sangue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1488, "target_cluster_name": "sangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In historical medical theory, blood was considered one of the four humors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In historical medical theory, blood was considered one of the four humors.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "baço", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1527, "target_cluster_name": "baço", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In humoral theory, the spleen was considered a source of melancholic humor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In humoral theory, the spleen was considered a source of melancholic humor.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fangue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1566, "target_cluster_name": "fangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In premodern medicine, blood was considered one of the four cardinal humors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In premodern medicine, blood was considered one of the four cardinal humors.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Viscuous Humors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3931, "target_cluster_name": "Viscuous Humors", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Viscous humors are a specific type of humor within the broader category of humors in historical medical theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Viscous humors are a specific type of humor within the broader category of humors in historical medical theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "phlegm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2513, "target_cluster_name": "phlegm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phlegm is one of the four humors (phlegm, blood, yellow bile, black bile) central to ancient and medieval medical theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phlegm is one of the four humors (phlegm, blood, yellow bile, black bile) central to ancient and medieval medical theory.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 8, "total_mentions": 702, "members": [{"entity_id": "galen", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 18, "variants": ["Galen"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek physician and philosopher", "ancient Roman physician"]}, {"entity_id": "galen", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 31, "variants": ["Dr. Galen", "Galen", "Galon"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek physician", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "galen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Galen"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "galen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galen"], "contexts": ["attests Pythagoras named Cosmos"]}, {"entity_id": "galen", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Galen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 478, "variants": ["Galen", "Gale", "Galem", "Galen.", "Galen. de cur. Ieteri", "Galen.inMeth. ibi", "Galeni", "Galenicos", "Galeniſtas", "Galeno", "Galenum", "Galenus", "Galenuslib", "Galia", "Galieno", "Galon"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "ancient medical authority cited multiple times"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 33, "variants": ["Galeno", "Gale", "Galen", "Galenon"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "ancient author on poisons"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 77, "variants": ["Galeno", "Gale.", "Galen", "Galenas", "Galenus"], "contexts": ["Ancient Greek physician", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 62, "variants": ["Galeno", "Galen", "Galero", "galena"], "contexts": ["Ancient medical authority", "ancient authority on medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Galen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Galen", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 29, "stable_key": "clu_a7804b80944c2d45", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galen", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek physician and highly influential medical authority.", "note": "Refers to the ancient physician, not the planet or musician.", "wikipedia_extract": "Aelius Galenus or Claudius Galenus (Greek: Κλαύδιος Γαληνός; September 129 – c. 216 CE), often anglicized as Galen () or Galen of Pergamon, was a Roman and Greek physician, surgeon, and philosopher. Considered to be one of the most accomplished of all medical researchers of antiquity, Galen influenced the development of various scientific disciplines, including anatomy, physiology, pathology, pharmacology, and neurology, as well as philosophy and logic. The son of Aelius Nicon, a wealthy Greek architect with scholarly interests, Galen received a comprehensive education that prepared him for a successful career as a physician and philosopher. Born in the ancient city of Pergamon (present-day Bergama, Turkey), Galen traveled extensively, exposing himself to a wide variety of medical theories and discoveries before settling in Rome, where he served prominent members of Roman society and eventually was given the position of personal physician to several emperors. Galen's understanding of anatomy and medicine was principally influenced by the contemporary theory of the four humors: black bile, yellow bile, blood, and phlegm, as first advanced by the author of On the Nature of Man in the Hippocratic corpus. Galen's views dominated and influenced Western medical science for more than 1,300 years."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Averroes", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 952, "target_cluster_name": "Averroes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Averroes, like many medieval scholars, engaged with and commented on the medical works of Galen.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Averroes, like many medieval scholars, engaged with and commented on the medical works of Galen.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was heavily influenced by and built upon the medical theories of Hippocrates, forming the foundation of Greco-Roman medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was heavily influenced by and built upon the medical theories of Hippocrates, forming the foundation of Greco-Roman medicine."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Galen and Dioscorides were prominent physicians of antiquity who wrote extensively on pharmacology and materia medica.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Galen and Dioscorides were prominent physicians of antiquity who wrote extensively on pharmacology and materia medica."}, {"found_name": "Alexander of Tralles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1707, "target_cluster_name": "Tralianus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alexander of Tralles was a later Byzantine physician who drew heavily from Galen's works and is considered a successor in the Galenic tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alexander of Tralles was a later Byzantine physician who drew heavily from Galen's works and is considered a successor in the Galenic tradition."}, {"found_name": "Aetius of Amida", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1573, "target_cluster_name": "Ætius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aetius of Amida was a Byzantine physician whose medical compilation extensively referenced and preserved Galen's teachings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aetius of Amida was a Byzantine physician whose medical compilation extensively referenced and preserved Galen's teachings."}, {"found_name": "Aretaeus of Cappadocia", "link_type": "conceptual