rote in Greek", "ancient physician cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Hippocrates", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hippocrates", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 31, "stable_key": "clu_caed569c40af7884", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hippocrates", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek physician, father of medicine", "wikidata_id": "Q5264", "wikidata_description": "Greek physician (c. 460 – c. 370 BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippocrates", "wikipedia_extract": "Hippocrates of Kos (; Ancient Greek: Ἱπποκράτης ὁ Κῷος, romanized: Hippokrátēs ho Kôios; c. 460 – c. 370 BC), also known as Hippocrates II, was a Greek physician and philosopher of the classical period who is considered one of the most outstanding figures in the history of medicine. He is traditionally referred to as the \"Father of Medicine\" in recognition of his lasting contributions to the field, such as the use of prognosis and clinical observation, the systematic categorization of diseases, and the (later discredited) formulation of humoral theory. His studies set out the basic ideas of modern-day specialties, including surgery, urology, neurology, acute medicine and orthopedics, and advanced the systematic study of clinical medicine. The Hippocratic school of medicine revolutionized ancient Greek medicine, establishing it as a discipline distinct from other fields with which it had traditionally been associated (theurgy and philosophy), thus establishing medicine as a profession."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle was a contemporary philosopher and scientist whose work, while distinct from Hippocrates' medical focus, engaged with similar questions about the natural world and human physiology, and they represent two towering intellectual figures of classical Greece.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aristotle was a contemporary philosopher and scientist whose work, while distinct from Hippocrates' medical focus, engaged with similar questions about the natural world and human physiology, and they represent two towering intellectual figures of classical Greece."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work on medicinal plants, De Materia Medica, is a foundational text in pharmacology and complements Hippocrates' broader medical corpus by detailing specific remedies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work on medicinal plants, De Materia Medica, is a foundational text in pharmacology and complements Hippocrates' broader medical corpus by detailing specific remedies."}, {"found_name": "Averroes", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 952, "target_cluster_name": "Averroes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Averroes, as a physician and scholar, would have been aware of and potentially engaged with the tradition of Hippocrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Averroes, as a physician and scholar, would have been aware of and potentially engaged with the tradition of Hippocrates.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was heavily influenced by and built upon the medical theories of Hippocrates, forming the foundation of Greco-Roman medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Galen was heavily influenced by and built upon the medical theories of Hippocrates, forming the foundation of Greco-Roman medicine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mesue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 244, "target_cluster_name": "Mesue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with those of Galen, formed the bedrock of medical knowledge that physicians like Mesue the Younger inherited and built upon.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with those of Galen, formed the bedrock of medical knowledge that physicians like Mesue the Younger inherited and built upon.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mathiolus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 252, "target_cluster_name": "Mathiolus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mattioli, as a significant figure in Renaissance medicine, would have been deeply familiar with and influenced by the foundational medical theories and writings attributed to Hippocrates, the 'father of medicine'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mattioli, as a significant figure in Renaissance medicine, would have been deeply familiar with and influenced by the foundational medical theories and writings attributed to Hippocrates, the 'father of medicine'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Julius Scaliger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3889, "target_cluster_name": "Julius Scaliger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Scaliger was a physician and scholar who engaged with the medical tradition of Hippocrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Scaliger was a physician and scholar who engaged with the medical tradition of Hippocrates.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rondeletius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 575, "target_cluster_name": "Rondeletius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the father of Western medicine. Ron