a broader sense of kind or type.", "wikidata_id": "Q13679", "wikidata_description": "free multilingual online species directory", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Species", "wikipedia_extract": "A species (pl. species) is the basic unit of classification and a taxonomic rank of an organism, as well as a unit of biodiversity. It can be defined as the largest group of organisms in which any two individuals of the appropriate sexes or mating types can produce fertile offspring, typically by sexual reproduction. Other ways of defining species include their karyotype, DNA sequence, morphology, behaviour, or ecological niche. In addition, palaeontologists use the concept of the chronospecies since fossil reproduction cannot be examined. The most recent rigorous estimate for the total number of species of eukaryotes is between 8 and 8.7 million."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Breed", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2181, "target_cluster_name": "breed", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In the context of biological classification, 'Breed' can be considered a more specific or applied term for a group within a species, often artificially selected, but historically and conceptually closely linked to the idea of a distinct group of organisms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "In the context of biological classification, 'Breed' can be considered a more specific or applied term for a group within a species, often artificially selected, but historically and conceptually closely linked to the idea of a distinct group of organisms."}, {"found_name": "Extinct species", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3360, "target_cluster_name": "extinct species", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct sub-category or state of the primary entity, 'Species', referring to species that no longer exist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is a direct sub-category or state of the primary entity, 'Species', referring to species that no longer exist."}, {"found_name": "Animal Kingdom", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2373, "target_cluster_name": "animal kingdom", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Animal Kingdom is a broad classification that encompasses many species. The concept of 'species' is fundamental to organizing and understanding the diversity within the Animal Kingdom.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Animal Kingdom is a broad classification that encompasses many species. The concept of 'species' is fundamental to organizing and understanding the diversity within the Animal Kingdom."}, {"found_name": "Botany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1098, "target_cluster_name": "botany", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Botany is the scientific study of plants, and 'species' is the fundamental unit of classification within botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Botany is the scientific study of plants, and 'species' is the fundamental unit of classification within botany."}, {"found_name": "Classification", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1977, "target_cluster_name": "classification", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Species is the basic unit of biological classification. The concept of classification is intrinsically linked to defining and organizing species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Species is the basic unit of biological classification. The concept of classification is intrinsically linked to defining and organizing species."}, {"found_name": "Evolution", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 540, "target_cluster_name": "Evolution", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evolutionary processes act upon populations of species, leading to the formation of new species and the modification of existing ones. The concept of species is central to understanding evolutionary history and mechanisms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evolutionary processes act upon populations of species, leading to the formation of new species and the modification of existing ones. The concept of species is central to understanding evolutionary history and mechanisms."}, {"found_name": "Fauna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 809, "target_cluster_name": "fauna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fauna refers to the animal life of a particular region, habitat, or geological period. The study and cataloging of fauna rely heavily on the identification and classification of species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fauna refers to the animal life of a particular region, habitat, or geological period. The study and cataloging of fauna rely heavily on the identification and classification of species."}, {"found_name": "Varieties", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1524, "target_cluster_name": "Varieties", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In biological classification, 'Varieties' are typically subdivisions of a 'Species', denoting distinct forms within that species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In biological classification, 'Varieties' are typically subdivisions of a 'Species', denoting distinct forms within that species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sphaeroptera barbata", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "Paranema cyathoides, Don, formerly known as Sphaeroptera barbata, Wall. ('Plantae Asiat.', lib. i., p. 42, 48), is indeed, nearly related to Cyathea, a species of which I have seen in the South Americ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "formerly known as"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 203, "members": [{"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 14, "variants": ["Dioscorides"], "contexts": ["source for Plinie's borrowings", "ancient medical writer"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 37, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diascoride", "Diascorides"], "contexts": ["author of a book on herbs", "ancient Greek physician and botanist"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dioscorides"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 14, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diofcorides", "Dioscoridos", "Dioscortes"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on herbs and medicines", "ancient authority on medicinal plants"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 135, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diascorídes", "Diosc", "Diosc.", "DioscondQs", "Dioscor", "Dioscorid.", "Dioscorídes", "Dioseorides", "Díoscorides"], "contexts": ["ancient author on materia medica", "ancient authority"]}, {"entity_id": "dio", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Dio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dio"], "contexts": ["ancient source on glass malleability"]}, {"entity_id": "diosc_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Diosc.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Diosc."], "contexts": ["ancient authority on plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 33, "stable_key": "clu_46f71b8b386d2f2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dioscorides", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek authority on materia medica and medicinal plants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pedanius Dioscorides (Ancient Greek: Πεδάνιος Διοσκουρίδης, Pedánios Dioskourídēs; c. 40–90 AD),  \"the father of pharmacognosy\", was a Greek physician, pharmacologist, botanist, and author of De materia medica (in the original Ancient Greek: Περὶ ὕλης ἰατρικῆς, Peri hulēs iatrikēs, both meaning \"On Medical Material\"), a 5-volume Greek encyclopedic pharmacopeia on herbal medicine and related medicinal substances, that was widely read for more than 1,500 years. For almost two millennia Dioscorides was regarded as the most prominent writer on plants and plant drugs. A native of Anazarbus, Cilicia, Asia Minor, Dioscorides likely studied medicine nearby at the school in Tarsus, which had a pharmacological emphasis, and he dedicated his medical books to Laecanius Arius, a medical practitioner there. Though he writes he lived a \"soldier's life\" or \"soldier-like life\", his pharmacopeia refers almost solely to plants found in the Greek-speaking eastern Mediterranean, making it likely that he served in campaigns, or travelled in a civilian capacity, less widely than supposed. The name Pedanius is Roman, suggesting that an aristocrat of that name sponsored him to become a Roman citizen. Between 50 and 70 AD Dioscorides wrote a five-volume book in his native Greek, Περὶ ὕλης ἰατρικῆς (Perì hylēs íatrikēs), known in Western Europe more often by its Latin title De materia medica (\"On Medical Material\"), which became the precursor to all modern pharmacopeias.", "birth_year": 40, "death_year": 90}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work on medicinal plants, De Materia Medica, is a foundational text in pharmacology and complements Hippocrates' broader medical corpus by detailing specific remedies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work on medicinal plants, De Materia Medica, is a foundational text in pharmacology and complements Hippocrates' broader medical corpus by detailing specific remedies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Moly", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2675, "target_cluster_name": "Moly", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "vero ftr uggendole, & colandole, come de* grafsi se detto . DELL^H ARMEL, L'H A R M E L e vna fpezie di ruta faluatics-, Jerta da a!cu- ni (fecondo Diofcoride) Moly, Befaf;ì,& Harmala,)a qua- le da vn", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Harmel"}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Galen and Dioscorides were prominent physicians of antiquity who wrote extensively on pharmacology and materia medica.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Galen and Dioscorides were prominent physicians of antiquity who wrote extensively on pharmacology and materia medica.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "so