, "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 52, "stable_key": "clu_070fb1b6d6123832", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q161419", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek god of the earth-encircling river, Oceanos", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oceanus", "wikipedia_extract": "In Greek mythology, Oceanus or Okeanos was one of the Titans, the children of Uranus (Sky) and Gaia (Earth), the brother and husband of the Titaness Tethys, and the father of the river gods and the Oceanids, as well as being the great river which encircled the entire world. According to M. L. West, the etymology of Oceanus is \"obscure\" and \"cannot be explained from Greek\". The use by Pherecydes of Syros of the form Ōgenós (Ὠγενός) for the name lends support for the name being a loanword. However, according to West, no \"very convincing\" foreign models have been found."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Venus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 97, "members": [{"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Venus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Venus"], "contexts": ["goddess associated with attraction"]}, {"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Venus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 67, "variants": ["Venus"], "contexts": ["planet associated with generation instruments", "planet that governs the French Pox"]}, {"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Venus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 16, "variants": ["venus", "Venus"], "contexts": ["sexual intercourse", "sexual activity"]}, {"entity_id": "vénus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Vénus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vénus"], "contexts": ["celestial body observed"]}, {"entity_id": "vénus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Vénus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Vénus"], "contexts": ["planet associated with inclination to scent", "title of the section"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Venus", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Venus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Venus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Venus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Venus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Venus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Vénus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Vénus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 53, "stable_key": "clu_aa9b855202dddaab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Relates to sexual desire, intercourse, and the planet Venus.", "note": "Can refer to the act of sex ('Venery') or the planet 'Venus'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun. It is often called Earth's \"twin\" or \"sister\" among the planets of the Solar System for its orbit being the closest to Earth's, both being terrestrial planets, and having the most similar and nearly equal size, mass, and surface gravity. Venus, though, is significantly different, especially as it has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K (464 °C; 867 °F) and a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, and as an inferior planet it is always relatively close to the Sun, either as the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cancer", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1709, "target_cluster_name": "Cancer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a planet that has astrological associations, often discussed in relation to zodiac signs like Cancer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Venus is a planet that has astrological associations, often discussed in relation to zodiac signs like Cancer.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 96, "members": [{"entity_id": "salt", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Salt", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["Salt", "salt"], "contexts": ["crystalline mineral compound", "substance to make waters wholesome"]}, {"entity_id": "salt", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Salt", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 24, "variants": ["Salt", "Salts"], "contexts": ["ingredient used with herb", "used with Balm"]}, {"entity_id": "sal", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["sal", "Sal"], "contexts": ["Salt", "used to preserve green anacardo"]}, {"entity_id": "sal", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 44, "variants": ["sal", "Sal"], "contexts": ["salt, a mineral", "added to water for preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "salt", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "salt", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Salt", "salt"], "contexts": ["collector of Egyptian antiquities", "absorbs moisture via capillary action"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Salt", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sal", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sal", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "salt", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sal", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sal", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Salt", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "salt", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "salt", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "sal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "salt", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 54, "stable_key": "clu_b7cebce5bcfaf712", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salt", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Refers to sodium chloride (NaCl), a fundamental chemical compound.", "wikidata_id": "Q2314", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium%20chloride", "wikipedia_extract": "Sodium chloride , commonly known as table salt, is an ionic compound with the chemical formula NaCl, representing a 1:1 ratio of sodium and chloride ions. It is transparent or translucent, brittle, hygroscopic, and occurs as the mineral halite. In its edible form, it is commonly used as a condiment and food preservative. Large quantities of sodium chloride are used in many industrial processes, and it is a major source of sodium and chlorine compounds used as feedstocks for further chemical syntheses. Another major application of sodium chloride is de-icing of roadways in sub-freezing weather. In addition to the many familiar domestic uses of salt, more dominant applications of the approximately 250 million tonnes per year production (2008 data) include chemicals and de-icing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mantimento", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2477, "target_cluster_name": "mantimento", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Salt is a mineral substance essential for life and used as a food additive.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Salt is a mineral substance essential for life and used as a food additive.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Potash", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2311, "target_cluster_name": "Potash", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Potassium carbonate is a salt, and 'salt' is a broad chemical category to which potassium carbonate belongs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Potassium carbonate is a salt, and 'salt' is a broad chemical category to which potassium carbonate belongs.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Swellings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 96, "members": [{"entity_id": "swellings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Swellings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 69, "variants": ["Swellings", "Swelings", "Swelling", "Swellings of the Spleen", "Swel∣lings", "Swounings", "Swou∣nings", "sweatings", "swelling", "swellings", "swellings of the Spleen", "swel∣lings", "swounings"], "contexts": ["condition treated by Alder-Tree leaves", "condition treated by Angelica"]}, {"entity_id": "swelling", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "swelling", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["swelling"], "contexts": ["enlargement of body part"]}, {"entity_id": "swelling", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "swelling", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["swelling"], "contexts": ["abnormal enlargement of body part"]}, {"entity_id": "swelling", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Swelling", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["Swelling"], "contexts": ["enlargement of body part"]}, {"entity_id": "swelling", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "swelling", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 24, "variants": ["swelling", "Swelling"], "contexts": ["a common symptom", "enlargement of a body part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Swellings", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Swellings", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Swellings", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Swelling", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Swellings", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "swelling", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "swelling", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Swelling", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "swelling", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "swelling", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Swelling", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "swelling", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Swelling", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "swelling", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 55, "stable_key": "clu_8bdd4412517edf49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Swelling", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Abnormal enlargement or puffiness of a body part.", "note": "A common symptom of various conditions.", "wikipedia_extract": "Edema (American English), also spelled oedema (Commonwealth English), and also known as fluid retention, swelling, dropsy and hydropsy, is the build-up of fluid in the body's tissue. Most commonly, the legs or arms are affected. Symptoms may include skin that feels tight, the area feeling heavy, and joint stiffness. Other symptoms depend on the underlying cause. Causes may include venous insufficiency, heart failure, kidney problems, low protein levels, liver problems, deep vein thrombosis, infections, kwashiorkor, angioedema, certain medications, and lymphedema. It may also occur in immobile patients (stroke, spinal cord injury, aging), or with temporary immobility such as prolonged sitting or standing, and during menstruation or pregnancy."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "quebraduras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 800, "target_cluster_name": "quebraduras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Swelling is a common and direct consequence of a fracture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Swelling is a common and direct consequence of a fracture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "carbunculos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1918, "target_cluster_name": "carbunculos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lumps (carbunculos) are a form of swelling, and 'swelling' is a general term that could encompass them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lumps (carbunculos) are a form of swelling, and 'swelling' is a general term that could encompass them.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Noli me Tangere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3305, "target_cluster_name": "Noli me Tangere", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "and applied to the Forehead; It also helpeth the Spots or Blemishes that happen in or about the Eyes, or in any other parts: The said wa∣ter fomented on Swellings and hot Inflamati∣ons of Womens sore ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of spreading ulcer"}, {"found_name": "Kings E∣vil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "applied description PAGE 7 to the nape of the Neck: The Herb also bru∣sed and with some Salt and Vinegar, and Hogs Greas laid upon any hard tumor or swelling; or that which is vulgarly called the King", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of hard tumor or swelling"}, {"found_name": "Kings evil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "without causing pain, being used with a little Verdigreese and Honey, and the great Cen∣taury Root: The same boyled in Vinegar and laid upon any Tumor or Swelling, doth very effectually dissolve and c", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of throat swelling"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 95, "members": [{"entity_id": "motion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Motion", "motion"], "contexts": ["act of moving", "prerequisite for birth"]}, {"entity_id": "motion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["motion"], "contexts": ["physical movement or action", "physical movement"]}, {"entity_id": "motion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["motion"], "contexts": ["movement of heavenly bodies", "fundamental principle in nature"]}, {"entity_id": "motion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 20, "variants": ["motion", "Motion"], "contexts": ["change in position", "change in position over time"]}, {"entity_id": "motion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "s