odily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 52, "variants": ["humoral theory", "humor"], "contexts": ["underlying medical theory", "implied medical theory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 64, "stable_key": "clu_cb377b0c332a9945", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "humoral theory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A medical theory explaining health based on bodily fluids (humors).", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540–500 BC)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "humido", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 537, "target_cluster_name": "humido", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Moisture is one of the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) in humoral theory, which was a dominant medical paradigm.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Moisture is one of the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) in humoral theory, which was a dominant medical paradigm.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "astrological medicine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 569, "target_cluster_name": "astrological medicine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrological medicine often integrated with humoral theory, using astrological configurations to diagnose imbalances in the four humors (blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile) and guide treatments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Astrological medicine often integrated with humoral theory, using astrological configurations to diagnose imbalances in the four humors (blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile) and guide treatments.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "natureza", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1530, "target_cluster_name": "natureza", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical understandings of vital force were often intertwined with humoral theories of the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historical understandings of vital force were often intertwined with humoral theories of the body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mars", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1551, "target_cluster_name": "Mars", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mars is a fundamental component of humoral theory, influencing the balance of humors, particularly choler.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mars is a fundamental component of humoral theory, influencing the balance of humors, particularly choler.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melancolicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1826, "target_cluster_name": "Melancolicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melancholy is a concept deeply embedded within the framework of humoral theory, which posited that bodily and mental states were determined by the balance of four humors, including black bile.", "confidence": 0.