h", "type": "concept", "note": "While the term 'pole' can refer to the Earth's axis, the context of 'magnetic pole' and 'extremities of Earth's axis' strongly points to the magnetic poles.", "wikidata_id": "Q63245279", "wikidata_description": "wandering point on the Earth where the geomagnetic field lines are directed vertically, either upwards or downwards"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 43, "members": [{"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 29, "variants": ["wind", "Wind"], "contexts": ["expelled by herb", "quality causing pains and torments"]}, {"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["wind"], "contexts": ["movement of air", "natural force for pollen dispersal"]}, {"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["wind"], "contexts": ["force causing earth shocks", "movement of air"]}, {"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wind"], "contexts": ["environmental factor"]}, {"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["wind"], "contexts": ["movement of air", "uniform force causing segregation"]}, {"entity_id": "southerly_winds", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "southerly winds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["southerly winds"], "contexts": ["winds from the south"]}, {"entity_id": "spring_east_winds", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "spring east winds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["spring east winds"], "contexts": ["winds in spring", "atmospheric disturbances in temperate zones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wind", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wind", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wind", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wind", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "wind", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wind", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 80, "stable_key": "clu_7e1d599efc5c0d62", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wind", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind", "note": "The terms 'southerly winds' and 'spring east winds' describe specific types of winds. The most general modern referent is 'winds'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is called anemology. The two main causes of large-scale atmospheric circulation are the differential heating between the equator and the poles, and the rotation of the planet, which is called the Coriolis effect. Within the tropics and subtropics, thermal low circulations over terrain and high plateaus can drive monsoon circulations. In coastal areas the sea breeze/land breeze cycle can define local winds; in areas that have variable terrain, mountain and valley breezes can prevail."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fumes", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2713, "target_cluster_name": "fumes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wind can carry and disperse fumes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wind can carry and disperse fumes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ventoſas ſarjadas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1853, "target_cluster_name": "ventoſas ſarjadas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Flatulence is a type of wind or gas expelled from the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Flatulence is a type of wind or gas expelled from the body.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "eye", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 64, "memb