milarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 87, "stable_key": "clu_21cafb1d4babceab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q11426", "wikidata_description": "element, compound, or alloy that is a good conductor of both electricity and heat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal", "note": "The Latin 'rerum metallicarum' directly translates to 'of metallic things' or 'metallic substances'. The context of electrical conductivity reinforces this.", "wikipedia_extract": "A metal (from Ancient Greek  μέταλλον (métallon) 'mine, quarry, metal') is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile (can be drawn into a wire) and malleable (can be shaped via hammering or pressing). A metal may be a chemical element, such as iron, an alloy, such as stainless steel, or a molecular compound, such as polymeric sulfur nitride. The general science of metals is called metallurgy, a subtopic of materials science; aspects of their electronic and thermal properties are also within the scope of condensed matter physics and solid-state chemistry, as it is a multidisciplinary topic. In colloquial use, materials such as steel alloys are referred to as metals, while others, such as polymers, wood, or ceramics are nonmetallic materials."}, "member_count": 19, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 440, "target_cluster_name": "Brass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc, both of which are 