, "source_name": "Latinos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "latinos", "similarity": 0.772}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Latinos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Latinos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "latinos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Latini", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "latinos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Latinos", "similarity": 0.772}], "id": 90, "stable_key": "clu_677f9e392a3ce23a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Latins", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "People associated with Latin language, culture, or ancient Rome.", "note": "Can refer to people, authors, or languages.", "wikipedia_extract": "The term Latin has been used to describe several groups of people throughout history, first referring to the inhabitants of the ancient Latium region, then to Catholic Christians of the Latin rite, and most recently to Romance-speaking peoples. The Latins were an ancient Italic people of the Latium region in central Italy (Latium Vetus, \"Old Latium\"), in the 1st millennium BC. Although they lived in independent city-states, they spoke Latin as a common language, held common religious beliefs, and extended common rights of residence and trade to one another. Collectively, these Latin states were known as the Latin League. A rupture between Rome, one of the Latin states, and the rest of the Latin League emerged as a result of the former's territorial ambitions. The Latin League fought against Rome in the Latin War (340–338 BC), which ended in a Roman victory."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ancient Greeks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1663, "target_cluster_name": "Greci", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Latin' has historically been used to refer to various groups, including those associated with ancient Rome and its cultural sphere, which was heavily influenced by and interacted with Ancient Greek culture and peoples.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'Latin' has historically been used to refer to various groups, including those associated with ancient Rome and its cultural sphere, which was heavily influenced by and interacted with Ancient Greek culture and peoples."}, {"found_name": "Italian people", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 462, "target_cluster_name": "Italians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The original inhabitants of Latium, the core region of the Latins, were ancestors of the Italian people. The term 'Latin' also later became associated with the Romance languages, which evolved from Latin and are spoken by many Italian people.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The original inhabitants of Latium, the core region of the Latins, were ancestors of the Italian people. The term 'Latin' also later became associated with the Romance languages, which evolved from Latin and are spoken by many Italian people."}, {"found_name": "cinamomo", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "que todos os médicos, se ha de dar mais fé que a esses padres da me- decina, que per falsa enformaçam escreverão. De modo que a que chamão os Gregos e Latinos cinamomo, chamam os Arábios quirfé ou qui", "confidence": 0.8, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "what the Greeks and Latins call cinnamon"}, {"found_name": "ambarum", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "muito delia, que saber como se gera. RUANO Dizey a verdade de tudo e deixayvos de falar essas cer- tezas. ORTA Âmbar dizem os Arábios, e ambarum os Latinos, por o custume da variação latina e uso, e a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "what the Latins call ambergris"}, {"found_name": "darchini", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "que todos os médicos, se ha de dar mais fé que a esses padres da me- decina, que per falsa enformaçam escreverão. De modo que a que chamão os Gregos e Latinos cinamomo, chamam os Arábios quirfé ou qui", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "what the Persians call cinnamon"}, {"found_name": "Cardiaca", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Melancholly Vapors from the Heart, to strengthen it, and make a merry cheerful blith soul, than this Herb, it may be kept in a Syrup or Conserve, therfore the Latins called it Cardiaca: Besides, it ma", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "what the Latins called it"}, {"found_name": "trochifci", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "acqua, ó fugo, òde- cozzione tanto, che faccino palla fimile aìle pillole. Forman- fi in girelìette , onde da'Grcci fono chiamati trochifci , Se da' Latini pafttlli, forfè dalla qualità della figura l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "what the Greeks call them"}, {"found_name": "pafttlli", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "s