nexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "emerald", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Esmeralda", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "emerald", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 107, "stable_key": "clu_b1e90d7bbe634bcb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Emerald", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q43513", "wikidata_description": "city in and county seat of King County, State of Washington, United States", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerald", "wikipedia_extract": "Emerald is a gemstone and a variety of the mineral beryl (Be3Al2(SiO3)6) colored green by trace amounts of chromium or sometimes vanadium. Beryl has a hardness of 7.5–8 on the Mohs scale. Most emeralds have many inclusions, so their toughness (resistance to breakage) is classified as generally poor. Emerald is a cyclosilicate. It occurs mainly in association with quartz, muscovite, albite, schorl, microcline, fluorite, smoky quartz and elbaite. The word \"emerald\" is derived (via Old French: esmeraude and Middle English: emeraude), from Vulgar Latin: esmaralda/esmaraldus, a variant of Latin smaragdus, which was via Ancient Greek: σμάραγδος (smáragdos; \"green gem\")."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "turquesa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2506, "target_cluster_name": "turquesa", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Pharmacopêas se tomava também pela esme- ralda : feru^egi, id est smaragdi; mas que Orta quer que seja a tur- queja, Orta tem rasio; o nome arábico da turquesa é j^\\^j:ijfi^í^4ft derivado do persiano ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for esmeralda", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Amethyst", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2673, "target_cluster_name": "A