rta_1563", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 111, "stable_key": "clu_2884614e5d3dcc2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ganges River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major river in India, important geographical and historical feature.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ganges ( GAN-jeez; in India: Ganga,  GUNG-ah; in Bangladesh: Padma,  PUD-mə) is a trans-boundary river of Asia which flows through India and Bangladesh. The 2,525-kilometre-long (1,569 mi) river rises in the western Himalayas in the Indian state of Uttarakhand. It flows south and east through the Gangetic plain of North India, receiving the right-bank tributary, the Yamuna, which also rises in the western Indian Himalayas, and several left-bank tributaries from Nepal that account for the bulk of its flow. In West Bengal, India, a feeder canal taking off from its right bank diverts 50% of its flow southwards, artificially connecting it to the Hooghly River. The Ganges continues into Bangladesh, its name changing to the Padma. It is then joined by the Jamuna, the lower stream of the Brahmaputra, and eventually the Meghna, forming the major estuary of the Ganges Delta, and emptying into the Bay of Bengal."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Turquia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 272, "target_cluster_name": "Turquia", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "índia* nos chamão Ganga), e vem o benjujr, que chamam ameií- doado, de Siam; e de todo este benjuy que vem á índia, a mór parte se gasta pêra a Arábia e Turquia e Pérsia. E porque nam cuideis que ha a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for India", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "India", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 17, "target_cluster_name": "India", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Ganges River flows through India.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Ganges River flows through India."}, {"found_name": "Indian", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2500, "target_cluster_name": "Indian", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Ganges River is a major river system central to the geography and culture of India.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Ganges River is a major river system central to the geography and culture of India.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "opus cireniacum", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "sey, por mais que lhe chamem opus cireniacum (que quer dizer çumo de Cirenia); porque eu sey que o principal não o ha senão na índia, que está alem do Ganges (a que os índia* nos chamão Ganga), e vem ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for juice of Cyrene"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 30, "members": [{"entity_id": "philosophy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_