in Westminster Abbey. Darwin's early interest in nature led him to neglect his medical education at the University of Edinburgh; instead, he helped Robert Edmond Grant to investigate marine invertebrates. His studies at the University of Cambridge's Christ's College from 1828 to 1831 encouraged his passion for natural science."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Huxley", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 871, "target_cluster_name": "Huxley", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Huxley was a prominent supporter and defender of Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection, often referred to as 'Darwin's Bulldog'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Huxley was a prominent supporter and defender of Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection, often referred to as 'Darwin's Bulldog'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alfred Russel Wallace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2104, "target_cluster_name": "Wallace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Co-discoverer of the theory of evolution by natural selection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Co-discoverer of the theory of evolution by natural selection."}, {"found_name": "Charles Lyell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1710, "target_cluster_name": "Lyell", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Influential geologist whose work on uniformitarianism provided a framework for Darwin's theory of evolution.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Influential geologist whose work on uniformitarianism provided a framework for Darwin's theory of evolution."}, {"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_na