l.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Linnæus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2721, "target_cluster_name": "Linnæus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Darwin's theory of evolution fundamentally changed biological classification and understanding, building upon and eventually challenging the Linnaean system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Darwin's theory of evolution fundamentally changed biological classification and understanding, building upon and eventually challenging the Linnaean system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "cuvier", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Cuvier"], "contexts": ["illustrious, insisted on conditions of existence", "urged no monkey in tertiary stratum"]}, {"entity_id": "cuvier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Cuvier", "M. Cuvier", "MM. Cuvier"], "contexts": ["author of Ossem. foss.", "scholar cited for fossil bones"]}, {"entity_id": "cuvier", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cuvier"], "contexts": ["naturalist"]}, {"entity_id": "cuvier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Cuvier"], "contexts": ["anatomist and paleontologist", "found animals in tertiary formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cuvier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cuvier", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 119, "stable_key": "clu_d43817dded10abfa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georges Cuvier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1769, "death_year": 1832, "description": "French naturalist and zoologist, founder of paleontology", "note": "The mention of 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' is an anomaly; the person is clearly Georges Cuvier based on other texts from the 19th century.", "wikidata_id": "Q171969", "wikidata_description": "French naturalist, zoologist and paleontologist (1769–1832)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges%20Cuvier", "wikipedia_extract": "Jean Léopold Nicolas Frédéric, baron Cuvier (23 August 1769 – 13 May 1832), known as Georges Cuvier (;  French: [ʒɔʁʒ(ə) kyvje]), was a French naturalist and zoologist, sometimes referred to as the \"founding father of paleontology\". Cuvier was a major figure in natural sciences research in the early 19th century and was instrumental in establishing the fields of comparative anatomy and paleontology through his work in comparing living animals with fossils. Cuvier's work is considered the foundation of vertebrate paleontology, and he expanded Linnaean taxonomy by grouping classes into phyla and incorporating both fossils and living species into the classification. Cuvier is also known for establishing extinction as a scientific fact—at the time, extinction was considered by many of Cuvier's contemporaries to be merely controversial speculation. In his Essay on the Theory of the Earth (1813), Cuvier proposed that now-extinct species had been wiped out by periodic catastrophic flooding events. In this way, Cuvier became the most influential proponent of catastrophism in geology in the early 19th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Darwin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 118, "target_cluster_name": "Darwin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Prominent naturalist whose work on extinction and comparative anatomy was a point of reference for Darwin's theories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Prominent naturalist whose work on extinction and comparative anatomy was a point of reference for Darwin's theories.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3990, "target_cluster_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was Georges Cuvier's younger brother and a fellow naturalist who collaborated with him on scientific work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Frederick Cuvier was Georges Cuvier's younger brother and a fellow naturalist who collaborated with him on scientific work."}, {"found_name": "Pallas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 446, "target_cluster_name": "Pallas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were leading figures in zoology and natural history during the late 18th and early 19th centuries. Cuvier's work on paleontology and comparative anatomy built upon the descriptive zoological work of naturalists like Pallas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were leading figures in zoology and natural history during the late 18th and early 19th centuries. Cuvier's work on paleontology and comparative anatomy built upon the descriptive zoological work of naturalists like Pallas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Buffon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 473, "target_cluster_name": "Buffon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Georges Cuvier was Buffon's successor as director of the Jardin du Roi and a highly influential figure in natural history, building upon Buffon's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Georges Cuvier was Buffon's successor as director of the Jardin du Roi and a highly influential figure in natural history, building upon Buffon's work.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "De Candolle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 721, "target_cluster_name": "De Candolle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naturalists in France during the same period, though their primary fields differed (paleontology vs. botany).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent naturalists in France during the same period, though their primary fields differed (paleontology vs. botany).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Abel Rémusat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1096, "target_cluster_name": "Abel Rémusat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century natural history and science in Paris. Cuvier was a leading zoologist and paleontologist, and Rémusat was a sinologist. They would have been contemporaries and likely acquainted 