les_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reptiles", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "reptiles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Reptiles", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "reptiles", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reptiles", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Reptiles", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reptiles", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 122, "stable_key": "clu_bed5199ede1edfc4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reptiles", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10811", "wikidata_description": "class of vertebrate animals (Vertebrata)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reptile", "note": "The Latin 'reptilibus' directly translates to 'reptiles'. The other member 'reptile' further confirms this. 'Invertebrate ones' seems like a misinterpretation or OCR error in its context if it's meant to be linked to 'reptiles'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Reptiles, as commonly defined, are tetrapod vertebrate animals with an ectothermic metabolism and amniotic development. Reptiles traditionally comprise four orders: Testudines (turtles), Crocodilia (crocodiles, alligators and gharials), Squamata (lizards and snakes) and Rhynchocephalia (tuatara), with about 12,000 extant species listed in the Reptile Database. The study of the traditional reptile orders, customarily in combination with the study of modern amphibians, is called herpetology. Reptiles have been subject to several conflicting taxonomic definitions. In classical evolutionary taxonomy, reptiles are gathered together under the class Reptilia ( rep-TIL-ee-ə), which corresponds to common usage. Modern cladistic taxonomy regards that group as paraphyletic, since genetic and paleontological evidence has determined that birds (class Aves) are the only surviving group of Dinosauria, a major clade of diapsids that are more closely related to crocodilians than to other living reptiles, and thus birds are nested among reptiles (under the clade Archosauria) from a phylogenetic perspective."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Reptiles are a class within the kingdom Animalia, sharing fundamental characteristics with other animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Reptiles are a class within the kingdom Animalia, sharing fundamental characteristics with other animals.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Saurians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2218, "target_cluster_name": "Saurians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical texts often used 'saurian' as a broader term that encompassed or was closely associated with reptiles, particularly lizards and crocodiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historical texts often used 'saurian' as a broader term that encompassed or was closely associated with reptiles, particularly lizards and crocodiles."}, {"found_name": "alligators", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2988, "target_cluster_name": "Alligators", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alligators are a specific type of crocodilian, which is an order within the traditional classification of reptiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alligators are a specific type of crocodilian, which is an order within the traditional classification of reptiles."}, {"found_name": "lizards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 136