able_key": "clu_08743b486b561b5e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hair", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Filamentous growth from follicles in the skin, used in remedies.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hair is a protein filament that grows from follicles found in the dermis. Hair is one of the defining characteristics of mammals. The human body, apart from areas of glabrous skin, is covered in follicles which produce thick terminal and fine vellus hair. Most common interest in hair is focused on hair growth, hair types, and hair care, but hair is also an important biomaterial primarily composed of protein, notably alpha-keratin. Attitudes towards different forms of hair, such as hairstyles and hair removal, vary widely across different cultures and historical periods, but it is often used to indicate a person's personal beliefs or social position, such as their age, gender, or religion. The word \"hair\" usually refers to two distinct structures: the part beneath the skin, called the hair follicle, or, when pulled from the skin, the bulb or root."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Spitzfindigkeit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "together. Now were these cases fair samples of all association, the distinction I drew might well be termed a _Spitzfindigkeit_ or piece of pedantic hair-splitting, and be dropped. But as a matter of ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of hair-splitting"}]}, {"canonical_name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "relationship", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "relationship", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["relationship"], "contexts": ["connection between organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["relation", "Relation"], "contexts": ["connection to topic or interest", "connection or association between things"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["relation"], "contexts": ["connection or link"]}, {"entity_id": "relação", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "relação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["relação"], "contexts": ["empirically admitted relation for comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["relation"], "contexts": ["philosophical concept of connection between entities", "connection or correspondence between entities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "relationship", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "relationship", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "relationship", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "relação", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "relationship", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "relação", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "relação", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "relação", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 143, "stable_key": "clu_d8fadd8c091a5b2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Relationship", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Covers both literal relationships and abstract connections.", "wikidata_id": "Q930933", "wikidata_description": "general relation between different objects or individuals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relation%20%28philosophy%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Relations are ways in which several entities stand to each other. They usually connect distinct entities but some associate an entity with itself. The adicity of a relation is the number of entities it connects. The direction of a relation is the order in which the elements are related to each other. The converse of a relation carries the same information and has the opposite direction, like the contrast between \"two is less than five\" and \"five is greater than two\". Both relations and properties express features in reality with a key difference being that relations apply to several entities while properties belong to a single entity."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 8, "variants": ["moisture", "moist humors", "moisture thereof"], "contexts": ["quality causing joint issues", "malignant quality of ulcers dried by fern"]}, {"entity_id": "humidades", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humidades", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidades"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "humidades_nutritivas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humidades nutritivas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidades nutritivas"], "contexts": ["nutritive fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["moisture"], "contexts": ["environmental condition affecting variation", "water in the atmosphere or soil"]}, {"entity_id": "humidad_quajada", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humidad quajada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidad quajada"], "contexts": ["proposed material for metal genesis"]}, {"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["moisture", "Moisture"], "contexts": ["water in the air", "water vapor in the air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humidades", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humidades nutritivas", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humidades", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humidades nutritivas", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humidad quajada", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Moisture", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humidad quajada", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Moisture", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 144, "stable_key": "clu_23d8facfd6757e9a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bodily fluid", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Liquid substances within the body, essential for its functions.", "note": "Includes concepts like humors or other bodily liquids.", "wikipedia_extract": "Body fluids, bodily fluids, or biofluids, sometimes body liquids, are liquids within the body of an organism. In lean healthy adult men, the total body water is about 60% (60–67%) of the total body weight; it is usually slightly lower in women (52–55%). The exact percentage of fluid relative to body weight is inversely proportional to the percentage of body fat. A lean 70 kg (150 lb) man, for example, has about 42 (42–47) liters of water in his body. The total body of water is divided into fluid compartments, between the intracellular fluid compartment (also called space, or volume) and the extracellular fluid (ECF) compartment (space, volume) in a two-to-one ratio: 28 (28–32) liters are inside cells and 14 (14–15) liters are outside cells. The ECF compartment is divided into the interstitial fluid volume – the fluid outside both the cells and the blood vessels – and the intravascular volume (also called the vascular volume and blood plasma volume) – the fluid inside the blood vessels – in a three-to-one ratio: the interstitial fluid volume is about 12 liters; the vascular volume is about 4 liters."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bucho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3698, "target_cluster_name": "bucho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The stomach produces and contains various bodily fluids, such as gastric juices, essential for digestion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The stomach produces and contains various bodily fluids, such as gastric juices, essential for digestion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fluids", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 393, "target_cluster_name": "fluids", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bodily fluid is a specific type of fluid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bodily fluid is a specific type of fluid."}, {"found_name": "parts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 467, "target_cluster_name": "parts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bodily fluids are contained within or secreted by body parts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bodily fluids are contained within or secreted by body parts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Skins", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3308, "target_cluster_name": "Skins", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin can secrete bodily fluids like sweat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Skin can secrete bodily fluids like sweat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "sangue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1488, "target_cluster_name": "sangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Blood 