smos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "fear", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fear", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Fear", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fear", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fear", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fear", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fear", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "fear", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fear", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 147, "stable_key": "clu_9b305e6e53dd4854", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fear", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fear", "wikipedia_extract": "Fear is an unpleasant subjective emotional state arising in response to perceived dangers or threats and which, when experienced, is typically associated with physiological and psychological changes. These changes frequently lead to behavioral reactions such as fight-or-flight responses or, in extreme cases, immobilization (freeze response). Unlike most (perhaps all) other animals, humans can experience fear in response to a present or anticipated stimulus and to imagined events. Most internalizing psychopathologies (depression, anxiety, suicidal thoughts and behaviors, and so forth) are associated with and maintained by dysfunctional, anxious, or fearful feelings and thoughts. Phobias, or exaggerated and often disabling fears, are usually similarly dysfunctional. Fear is often framed as a distinct product of evolution."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Affection", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3559, "target_cluster_name": "affection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are fundamental emotional states, often contrasted or considered in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are fundamental emotional states, often contrasted or considered in relation to each other."}, {"found_name": "Anger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1206, "target_cluster_name": "anger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are strong emotions that can influence behavior and perception.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are strong emotions that can influence behavior and perception."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Desire and fear are often seen as opposing forces or motivations in human behavior and decision-making.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Desire and fear are often seen as opposing forces or motivations in human behavior and decision-making."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fear is a primary emotion, and its relationship with the will (how one acts in response to fear) is 