opher, originator of atomism.", "wikidata_id": "Q41980", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher (c. 460–c. 370 BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democritus", "wikipedia_extract": "Democritus (, dim-OCK-rit-əs; Greek: Δημόκριτος, Dēmókritos, meaning \"chosen of the people\"; c. 460 – c. 370 BC) was an Ancient Greek pre-Socratic philosopher from Abdera, primarily remembered today for his formulation of an atomic theory of the universe. Democritus wrote extensively on a wide variety of topics. None of Democritus' original work has survived, except through second-hand references. Many of these references come from Aristotle, who viewed him as an important rival in the field of natural philosophy."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Anaxagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1057, "target_cluster_name": "Anaxagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers associated with atomism and cosmology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers associated with atomism and cosmology."}, {"found_name": "Empedocles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2664, "target_cluster_name": "Empedocles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are pre-Socratic philosophers who proposed theories about the fundamental nature of the universe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are pre-Socratic philosophers who proposed theories about the fundamental nature of the universe."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle critiqued and engaged with atomistic theories, including those of Democritus, making him a significant figure in the philosophical lineage.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aristotle critiqued and engaged with atomistic theories, including those of Democritus, making him a significant figure in the philosophical lineage."}, {"found_name": "Epicurus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3242, "target_cluster_name": "Epicurus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Epicurus adopted and developed Democritus's atomic theory in his own philosophical system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Epicurus adopted and developed Democritus's atomic theory in his own philosophical system."}, {"found_name": "Bacon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 185, "target_cluster_name": "Bacon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bacon's emphasis on empirical observation and the inductive method aligns with the atomistic philosophy of Democritus, which posited that reality is composed of indivisible particles and void. Bacon's philosophy can be seen as a precursor to later developments in atomic theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bacon's emphasis on empirical observation and the inductive method aligns with the atomistic philosophy of Democritus, which posited that reality is composed of indivisible particles and void. Bacon's philosophy can be seen as a precursor to later developments in atomic theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pythagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 354, "target_cluster_name": "Pythagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Socrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", 