eas use shilajit in folk and non-traditional (alternative) medicine (Ayurveda, Chinese, Tibetan). Shilajit is sold both in dry extract form and in dietary supplements, but there is limited evidence that shilajit has any beneficial effects on human health. Some sources have been found in studies to contain hazardous heavy metals, including lead.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitumen"}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "lignites", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1760, "target_cluster_name": "lignites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lignite is a precursor to bitumen and shares some of its properties as a fossil fuel.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lignite is a precursor to bitumen and shares some of its properties as a fossil fuel.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pitch", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2904, "target_cluster_name": "Pitch", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "which set together do signifie nothing, not only in the dictionary of man, but the subtiler vocabulary of Satan. That there is any power in _Bitumen_, Pitch, or Brimstone, to purifie the air from his ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Asphalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1214, "target_cluster_name": "Afphalto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen and asphalt are often used interchangeably in historical texts to refer to similar viscous, black, tar-like substances derived from petroleum or coal. While subtle distinctions might exist in modern chemistry, historical usage frequently conflates them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bitumen and asphalt are often used interchangeably in historical texts to refer to similar viscous, black, tar-like substances derived from petroleum or coal. While subtle distinctions might exist in modern chemistry, historical usage frequently conflates them."}, {"found_name": "wax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 716, "target_cluster_name": "wax", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen is a sticky, black, highly viscous liquid or semi-solid form of petroleum or natural asphalt, sharing hydrocarbon-based properties with waxes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bitumen is a sticky, black, highly viscous liquid or semi-solid form of petroleum or natural asphalt, sharing hydrocarbon-based properties with waxes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Charcoal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 857, "target_cluster_name": "Charcoal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen is a related fossil fuel, often found alongside coal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bitumen is a related fossil fuel, often found alongside coal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "resina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2624, "target_cluster_name": "resina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen is a naturally occurring viscous organic substance, often considered a type of mineral resin.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bitumen is a naturally occurring viscous organic substance, often considered a type of mineral resin.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pétrifications", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2932, "target_cluster_name": "pétrifications", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen is a natu