74", "target_name": "Venecia", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Venet.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Venetijs", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Venecia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Venezia", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Venecia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Venetijs", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Venezia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Venetijs", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 170, "stable_key": "clu_b3ce368969aca756", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venice", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major city-state in Italy, a historical center of trade and culture.", "wikipedia_extract": "Venice is a city in northeastern Italy and the capital of the region of Veneto. It is built on a group of 126 islands that are separated by expanses of open water and by canals; portions of the city are linked by 472 bridges. The islands are in the shallow Venetian Lagoon, an enclosed bay lying between the mouths of the Po and the Piave rivers (more exactly between the Brenta and the Sile). As of 2025, the city proper (comune of Venice) has 249,466 inhabitants, nearly 50,000 of whom live in the historical island city of Venice (centro storico), while most of the population resides on the mainland (terraferma), and about 25,000 live on other islands in the lagoon (estuario). Together with the cities of Padua and Treviso, Venice is included in the Padua-Treviso-Venice Metropolitan Area (PATREVE), which is considered a statistical metropolitan area, with a total population of 2.6 million. The name is derived from the ancient Veneti people who inhabited the region by the 10th century BC."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "ciudad", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2007, "target_cluster_name": "ciudad", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A historically significant city.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A historically significant city.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Candia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2010, "target_cluster_name": "Candia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venice controlled Crete, including Candia (Heraklion), for several centuries (13th-17th centuries). This period of Venetian rule significantly shaped the city's architecture, administration, and economy, making Venice a crucial related entity in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Venice controlled Crete, including Candia (Heraklion), for several centuries (13th-17th centuries). This period of Venetian rule significantly shaped the city's architecture, administration, and economy, making Venice a crucial related entity in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Padua", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2318, "target_cluster_name": "Padua", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Padua is geographically located near Venice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Padua is geographically located near Venice.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Tremecina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "Reyno, y dela provincia de | medeta Carrhagena:voaT remécina,muy clara; y muy ola | tbegera (a mucho mejor Gla que llaman deveta, que la mejor q traen de Venecia, Tiene todaslas virtudes queríe la nue", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_lab