nd atomic number 15. All elemental forms of phosphorus are highly reactive and are therefore never found in nature. Elemental phosphorus can be prepared artificially, the two most common allotropes being white phosphorus and red phosphorus. With 31P as its only stable isotope, phosphorus has an occurrence in Earth's crust of about 0.1%, generally as phosphate rock. A member of the pnictogen family, phosphorus readily forms a wide variety of organic and inorganic compounds, with as its main oxidation states +5, +3 and −3. The isolation of white phosphorus in 1669 by Hennig Brand marked the scientific community's first discovery of an element since antiquity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sulfur", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1820, "target_cluster_name": "Sulfur", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phosphorus is an element that shares some chemical properties with sulfur and was often discussed alongside it in early chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phosphorus is an element that shares some chemical properties with sulfur and was often discussed alongside it in early chemistry.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Carbon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 11, "target_cluster_name": "carbon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phosphorus is a chemical element, and Carbon is another chemical element. They are often discussed together in early chemistry as fundamental substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Phosphorus is a chemical element, and Carbon is another chemical element. They are often discussed together in early chemistry as fundamental substances."}, {"found_name": "Hydrogen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 731, "target_cluster_name": "Hydrogen", "source_book": "llm_generated