liberty' can appear in conjunction with other political or philosophical concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'liberty' can appear in conjunction with other political or philosophical concepts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "electrics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "electrum", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "electrum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["electrum"], "contexts": ["ancient alloy of gold and silver"]}, {"entity_id": "electrum", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electrum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["electrum"], "contexts": ["amber, attracts light objects when rubbed"]}, {"entity_id": "electrum", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Electrum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Electrum"], "contexts": ["alloy of gold and silver"]}, {"entity_id": "elettro", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "elettro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["elettro"], "contexts": ["Greek name for amber"]}, {"entity_id": "electrics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electrics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["electrics", "Electrics"], "contexts": ["materials that conduct electricity", "substances whose electric equilibrium is destroyed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "electrum", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "electrum", "similarity": 0.857}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "electrum", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Electrum", "similarity": 0.831}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electrum", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Electrum", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electrum", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "elettro", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Electrum", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "elettro", "similarity": 0.907}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "elettro", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electrics", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 187, "stable_key": "clu_7072b466013ae852", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrum", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Early modern texts refer to electrum both as an ancient alloy of gold and silver, and as amber, which exhibits electrostatic properties.", "wikidata_id": "Q203874", "wikidata_description": "shiny alloy of copper, nickel, and zinc", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nickel%20silver", "wikipedia_extract": "Nickel silver, maillechort, German silver, argentan, new silver, nickel brass, albata, or alpacca is a cupronickel (copper with nickel) alloy with the addition of zinc. The usual formulation is 60% copper, 20% nickel and 20% zinc. Nickel silver does not contain silver, but resembles it, which can make it attractive as a cheaper and more durable substitute. It is also well suited for plating with silver. A naturally occurring ore composition in China was smelted into the alloy known as paktong or báitóng (白銅) ('white copper' or cupronickel). The name German Silver refers to the artificial recreation of the natural ore composition by German metallurgists."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 440, "target_cluster_name": "Brass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrum is an alloy of copper, nickel, and zinc, while brass is an alloy of copper and zinc. They are closely related metallic alloys.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrum is an alloy of copper, nickel, and zinc, while brass is an alloy of copper and zinc. They are closely related metallic alloys."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pleurisy", "Pleuresy"], "contexts": ["acute illness", "inflammation of the pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pleurisy"], "contexts": ["acute inflammation of pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pleurisy"], "contexts": ["inflammation of pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleupe", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pleupe", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pleupe"], "contexts": ["acute illness, likely pleurisy"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pleurisy"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the pleura"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pleupe", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pleurisy", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pleupe", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 188, "stable_key": "clu_35ce3ed7307d574e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pleurisy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Inflammation of the pleura, causing sharp chest pain.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pleurisy, also known as pleuritis, is inflammation of the membranes that surround the lungs and line the chest cavity (pleurae). This can result in a sharp chest pain while breathing. Occasionally the pain may be a constant dull ache. Other symptoms may include shortness of breath, cough, fever, and weight loss, depending on the underlying cause. Pleurisy can be caused by a variety of conditions, including viral or bacterial infections, autoimmune disorders, and pulmonary embolism. The most common cause is a viral infection."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Inflamations", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 581, "target_cluster_name": "Inflamations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "consumption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 73, "target_cluster_name": "consumption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pleurisy is an inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. It is often associated with and can be a symptom of phthisis (tuberculosis).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pleurisy is an inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. It is often associated with and can be a symptom of phthisis (tuberculosis).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pneumonia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3755, "target_cluster_name": "pneumonia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, and can lead to pleurisy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, and can lead to pleurisy."}, {"found_name": "Cough", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1561, "target_cluster_name": "Cough", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cough is a common symptom of pleurisy, as the inflammation can irritate the lungs and chest cavity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cough is a common symptom of pleurisy, as the inflammation can irritate the lungs and chest cavity."}, {"found_name": "Fever", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 273, "target_cluster_name": "fiebres", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fever is a common symptom of pleurisy, often accompanying the inflammation and infection that can cause it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fever is a common symptom of pleurisy, often accompanying the inflammation and infection that can cause it."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ignorance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ignorance", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ignorance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ignorance"], "contexts": ["linked to devotion in naming", "brother of Madness"]}, {"entity_id": "ignorance", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ignorance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ignorance"], "contexts": ["lack of knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "ignorância", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ignorância", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ignorância"], "contexts": ["reason for applying European rules to the East"]}, {"entity_id": "ignorancia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ignorancia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ignorancia"], "contexts": ["lack of knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "ignorance", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ignorance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ignorance"], "contexts": ["lack of knowledge or information"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Ignorance", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ignorance", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Ignorance", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ignorância", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Ignorance", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ignorancia", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Ignorance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ignorance", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ignorance", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ignorância", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ignorance", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ignorancia", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ignorance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ignorance", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ignorância", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ignorancia", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ignorância", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ignorance", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "ignorancia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ignorance", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 189, "stable_key": "clu_a77993bb55584a2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ignorance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A lack of knowledge, understanding, or information.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ignorance is a lack of knowledge, information and understanding. Deliberate ignorance is a culturally-induced phenomenon, the study of which is called agnotology. The word \"ignorant\" is an adjective that describes a person in the state of being unaware, or even cognitive dissonance and other cognitive relation, and can describe individuals who are unaware of important information or facts. Ignorance can appear in three different types: factual ignorance (absence of knowledge of some fact), object ignorance (unacquaintance with some object), and technical ignorance (absence of knowledge of how to do something). Ignorance can have negative effects on individuals and societies, but can also benefit them by creating within them the desire to know more. For example, ignorance within science opens the opportunity to seek knowledge and make discoveries by asking new questions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Knowledge", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1743, "target_cluster_name": "Knowledge", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ignorance is the absence of knowledge, making it a direct conceptual opposite and thus strongly related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ignorance is the absence of knowledge, making it a direct conceptual opposite and thus strongly related.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ignorance can be a lack of belief or understanding.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ignorance can be a lack of belief or understanding."}, {"found_name": "Experience", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 105, "target_cluster_name": "experience", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ignorance is a lack of experience or knowledge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ignorance is a lack of experience or knowledge."}, {"found_name": "curiosity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2802, "target_cluster_name": "curiosity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity often arises from a state of ignorance, prompting a desire to overcome it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Curiosity often arises from a state of ignorance, prompting a desire to overcome it.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "geographical province", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "geographical_province", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "geographical province", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["geographical province"], "contexts": ["distinct area with unique species distribution", "distinct area for animal migration"]}, {"entity_id": "provincia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Provincia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Provincia"], "contexts": ["former administrative division"]}, {"entity_id": "province", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "province", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["province"], "contexts": ["administrative region"]}, {"entity_id": "província", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "província", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["província"], "contexts": ["province"]}, {"entity_id": "provincia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Provincia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Provincia"], "contexts": ["religious order's jurisdiction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "geographical province", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "province", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Provincia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "province", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Provincia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "província", "similarity": 0.808}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Provincia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Provincia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "province", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Provincia", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "província", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Provincia", "similarity": 0.808}], "id": 190, "stable_key": "clu_0bd18f6d6cefaf1c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Province", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The term 'province' is used historically for administrative regions and also in a biological context to denote a geographical area with distinct flora/fauna.", "wikidata_id": "Q1904", "wikidata_description": "province of Canada", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ontario", "wikipedia_extract": "Ontario is the southernmost province of Canada. Located in Central Canada, Ontario is the country's most populous province. As of the 2021 Canadian census, it is home to over 14 million people, which is 38.5% of the country's population. Ontario is the second-largest province by total area (after Quebec) and the fourth-largest jurisdiction of all the Canadian provinces and territories. It is home to the nation's capital, Ottawa, and its most populous city, Toronto, which is Ontario's provincial capital. Ontario is bordered by the province of Manitoba to the west, Hudson Bay and James Bay to the north, and Quebec to the east and northeast."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Canada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 736, "target_cluster_name": "Canada", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ontario is a province of Canada.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ontario is a province of Canada."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Guns", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "guns", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Guns", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guns"], "contexts": ["weaponry"]}, {"entity_id": "weapons", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Weapons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Weapons"], "contexts": ["source of invenomed wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "armas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Armas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Armas"], "contexts": ["weapons, defensive and offensive"]}, {"entity_id": "armas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "armas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["armas"], "contexts": ["weapons on the ship"]}, {"entity_id": "instrumentos_bellicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "instrumentos bellicos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instrumentos bellicos"], "contexts": ["war instruments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Weapons", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Armas", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Weapons", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Armas", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Weapons", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Weapons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "instrumentos bellicos", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Armas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Armas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "instrumentos bellicos", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 191, "stable_key": "clu_9ef97294cd67246d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "firearms", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While 'guns' and 'weapons' are broader, the context of 'invenomed wounds' and 'bellicos' points towards firearms as a specific and relevant early modern referent for causing injury, even if edged weapons also fit. Firearms became increasingly prominent during this period.", "wikidata_id": "Q12796", "wikidata_description": "personal weapon using combustion or an explosive charge to propel a projectile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firearm", "wikipedia_extract": "A firearm is any type of gun that shoots projectiles using high explosive pressure generated from combustion (deflagration) of chemical propellant, most often black powder in antique firearms and smokeless powder in modern firearms. Small arms is a subset of light firearms that is designed to be readily carried and operated by an individual. The term \"firearm\" is however variably defined in both technically and legally in different countries (see legal definitions), and can be used colloquially (sometimes incorrectly) to refer to any type of guns. The first firearms originated in 10th-century Song dynasty China (see gunpowder weapons in the Song dynasty), when bamboo tubes containing gunpowder and pellet projectiles were mounted on spears to make the portable fire lance, which was operable by a single person and was later used effectively as a shock weapon in the siege of De'an in 1132. In the 13th century, fire lance barrels were replaced with metal tubes and transformed into the metal-barreled hand cannon, and the technology gradually spread throughout Eurasia during the 14th century. Older firearms typically used black powder as a propellant, but modern firearms use smokeless powder or other explosive propellants."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cannon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 507, "target_cluster_name": "cannon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A cannon is a type of firearm, specifically a large-caliber gun designed to fire projectiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A cannon is a type of firearm, specifically a large-caliber gun designed to fire projectiles."}]}, {"canonical_name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["beginning or source"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["point or cause of beginning"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["beginning or source of something"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["starting point for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["hypothetical connector in language analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 192, "stable_key": "clu_578b2ca999b781a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "origin", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7376362", "wikidata_description": "start of a river or stream", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River%20source", "wikipedia_extract": "The headwater of a river or stream is the geographical point of its beginning, specifically where surface runoff water begins to accumulate into a flowing channel of water. A river or stream into which one or many tributary rivers or streams flow has many headwaters, these being all of the individual headwaters of its tributaries. Each headwater is one of the river or stream's sources, as it is the place where surface runoffs from rainwater, meltwater, or spring water begin accumulating into a more substantial and consistent flow that becomes a first-order tributary of that river or stream. The tributary with the longest channel of all the tributaries to a river or stream, such length measured from that tributary's headwater to its mouth where it discharges into the river or stream, is the main stem of the river or stream in question. The United States Geological Survey (USGS) states that a river's \"length may be considered to be the distance from the mouth to the most distant headwater source (irrespective of stream name), or from the mouth to the headwaters of the stream commonly known as the source stream\". As an example of the second definition above, the USGS at times considers the Missouri River as a tributary of the Mississippi River."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fluxos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 265, "members": [{"entity_id": "fluxes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "fluxes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 3, "variants": ["fluxes"], "contexts": ["disease treated with Crystal", "medical condition treated by Crocus Martis"]}, {"entity_id": "fluxes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652"