, noted for work on minerals and metallurgy", "note": "The primary referent seems to be the French chemist Laurent Rivière, known for his work in metallurgy and mineralogy, which aligns with the mention of establishing a school of mines. The mention of 'febrifugio' could refer to a work by a different Riverius, but the context of mines and scientific abstraction points strongly to Laurent Rivière."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a foundational figure in pharmacology and botany, whose work on materia medica was essential for physicians like Riverius who relied on herbal remedies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was a foundational figure in pharmacology and botany, whose work on materia medica was essential for physicians like Riverius who relied on herbal remedies."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was the most influential physician of antiquity, and his theories and practices formed the bedrock of Western medicine for over a millennium. Riverius, like most physicians of his time, was deeply indebted to and worked within the Galenic tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was the most influential physician of antiquity, and his theories and practices formed the bedrock of Western medicine for over a millennium. Riverius, like most physicians of his time, was deeply indebted to and worked within the Galenic tradition."}]}, {"canonical_name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 146, "members": [{"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 15, "variants": ["moist", "Moist", "moistens"], "contexts": ["quality associated with Angelica's virtues", "quality of humidity"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["moist"], "contexts": ["implied by discussion of 'grados de frialdad'"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["moist"], "contexts": ["a quality of substances", "quality of substances"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 113, "variants": ["Moist", "moist"], "contexts": ["a fundamental quality in early modern medicine", "medical quality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 200, "stable_key": "clu_4e6fcd5ff20dcbee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "moist", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A quality of dampness or humidity, often discussed in medical contexts.", "note": "This refers to the quality of being moist or humid, rather than a specific substance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540–500 BC)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humidity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 27, "target_cluster_name": "Humidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'moist' is directly related to the concept of 'humidity' as its defining characteristic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'moist' is directly related to the concept of 'humidity' as its defining characteristic."}, {"found_name": "Elements", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2703, "target_cluster_name": "Elements", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Moist is one of the fundamental qualities associated with the classical elements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Moist is one of the fundamental qualities associated with the classical elements.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "frio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 10, "total_mentions": 208, "members": [{"entity_id": "froid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "froid", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["froid"], "contexts": ["temperature experienced in November", "sensation of low temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "frio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "frio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 21, "variants": ["frio", "frios de complexión", "fría", "frío"], "contexts": ["cold", "condition Ambar can resolve"]}, {"entity_id": "frio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "frio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 12, "variants": ["frio"], "contexts": ["quality attributed to camphor", "quality of temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "frio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "frio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 102, "variants": ["frio", "Frio", "ar frio", "frios actu", "frios excessivos"], "contexts": ["cold", "a quality associated with illness"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 29, "variants": ["Cold", "cold", "cold caus"], "contexts": ["quality causing pains and torments", "quality associated with Angelica's virtues"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cold"], "contexts": ["quality of temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cold"], "contexts": ["quality causing freezing"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_