are directly involved in the process of mental change.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ideas are fundamental units of thought and are directly involved in the process of mental change.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Essay", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2575, "target_cluster_name": "Essay", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Essays are fundamentally vehicles for exploring and presenting ideas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Essays are fundamentally vehicles for exploring and presenting ideas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "forms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2727, "target_cluster_name": "forms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In Platonic and Neoplatonic philosophy, 'forms' (or Ideas) are the perfect, eternal archetypes of things in the material world. This is a direct philosophical connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In Platonic and Neoplatonic philosophy, 'forms' (or Ideas) are the perfect, eternal archetypes of things in the material world. This is a direct philosophical connection.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "sensations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 228, "target_cluster_name": "sensations", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "molecules are what Locke called 'simple ideas.' Some of Locke's successors made out that the only simple ideas were the sensations strictly so called. Which ideas the simple ones may be does not, howe", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of simple idea"}, {"found_name": "mental atoms or molecules", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "in the _combination_ of certain simple elements of consciousness that always remain the same. These mental atoms or molecules are what Locke called 'simple ideas.' Some of Locke's successors made out ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonymous with simple ideas"}, {"found_name": "simple ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "molecules are what Locke called 'simple ideas.' Some of Locke's successors made out that the only simple ideas were the sensations strictly so called. Which ideas the simple ones may be does not, howe", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of idea discussed by Locke"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 70, "members": [{"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Honey"], "contexts": ["used in various remedies", "sweet substance used as vehicle"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 65, "variants": ["Honey"], "contexts": ["ingredient added to decoction", "honey if it be to clens the womb"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["honey"], "contexts": ["substance with specific density"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["honey"], "contexts": ["doubled quantity mixed with remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Honey", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.849}], "id": 227, "stable_key": "clu_898a2e190a1d99c4", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants (primarily floral nectar) or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous. Honey bees stockpile honey in the hive. Within the hive is a structure made from wax called honeycomb."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sugar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 254, "target_cluster_name": "Sugar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honey is a natural sweetener produced by bees, often considered a precursor or alternative to refined sugar in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Honey is a natural sweetener produced by bees, often considered a precursor or alternative to refined sugar in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "wax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 716, "target_cluster_name": "wax", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honey is a viscous, sweet substance produced by bees, and shares a viscous, semi-solid consistency with some waxes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Honey is a viscous, sweet substance produced by bees, and shares a viscous, semi-solid consistency with some waxes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mantimento", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2477, "target_cluster_name": "mantimento", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honey is a sweet, viscous food substance produced by honey bees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Honey is a sweet, viscous food substance produced by honey bees.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Oximel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 671, "target_cluster_name": "Oximel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Oxymel is defined as a mixture of 'acid and honey', making 'Honey' a core component and etymological root.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Oxymel is defined as a mixture of 'acid and honey', making 'Honey' a core component and etymological root.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 137, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 57, "variants": ["sensations", "Sensations"], "contexts": ["raw input from senses", "raw sensory input"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["perceptions from the senses"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["awareness of stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["basic elements of thought", "basic units of experience"]}, {"entity_id": "sensation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 50, "variants": ["Sensation", "sensation"], "contexts": ["effect of attention", "awareness of stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "sensation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensation"], "contexts": ["initial response to nature"]}, {"entity_id": "sensation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensation"], "contexts": ["awareness of stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "sensation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["sensation"], "contexts": ["perceptual experience", "ultimately incomprehensible as the conscious something"]}, {"entity_id": "sense", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sense", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["sense", "Sense"], "contexts": ["fallacies of sense", "faculty of perception"]}, {"entity_id": "sense", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sense", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["sense"], "contexts": ["sensory faculty", "meaning or intelligibility"]}, {"entity_id": "senses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Senses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Senses", "senses"], "contexts": ["organs for perceiving the world", "necessarily inaccurate testimony"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 228, "stable_key": "clu_438e7f5110da8aa9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q173253", "wikidata_description": "physiological capacity of organisms that provides data for perception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense", "wikipedia_extract": "A sense is a biological system used by an organism for sensation, the process of  gathering information about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. Although, in some cultures, five human senses were traditionally identified as such (namely sight, smell, touch, taste, and hearing), many more are now recognized. Senses used by non-human organisms are even greater in variety and number. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli (such as a sound or smell) for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and perception are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's cognition, behavior and thought. In organisms, a sensory organ consists of a group of interrelated sensory cells that respond to a specific type of physical stimulus."}, "member_count": 11, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 226, "target_cluster_name": "ideas", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "molecules are what Locke called 'simple ideas.' Some of Locke's successors made out that the only simple ideas were the sensations strictly so called. Which ideas the simple ones may be does not, howe", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of simple idea", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "simple ideas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "the same. These mental atoms or molecules are what Locke called 'simple ideas.' Some of Locke's successors made out that the only simple ideas were the sensations strictly so called. Which ideas the s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for mental atoms/molecules"}, {"found_name": "sounds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "here? If the different sensations known as sounds are built out of a common unit, is it not to be rationally inferred that so likewise are the different sensations known as tastes, and the different s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of sensation"}, {"found_name": "tastes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "here? If the different sensations known as sounds are built out of a common unit, is it not to be rationally inferred that so likewise are the different sensations known as tastes, and the different s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of sensation"}, {"found_name": "odors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "here? If the different sensations known as sounds are built out of a common unit, is it not to be rationally inferred that so likewise are the different sensations known as tastes, and the different s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of sensation"}, {"found_name": "colors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "here? If the different sensations known as sounds are built out of a common unit, is it not to be rationally inferred that so likewise are the different sensations known as tastes, and the different s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of sensation"}, {"found_name": "muscular tension", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "other, are the first presented in consciousness. These, produced by the action of the muscles, necessitate reactions upon consciousness in the shape of sensations of muscular tension. Consequently, ea", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of sensation"}]}, {"canonical_name": "qualidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 67, "members": [{"entity_id": "calidad", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "calidad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Calidad", "calidad"], "contexts": ["quality of a lady", "quality of vapor"]}, {"entity_id": "qualidade", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "qualidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["qualidade"], "contexts": ["Quality of a substance, e.g., hot, cold"]}, {"entity_id": "qualità", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "qualità", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["qualità", "quah tà"], "contexts": ["qualities are removed or imparted by washing", "qualities of medicines that can change"]}, {"entity_id": "qualidade", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "qualidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 59, "variants": ["qualidade", "calidade", "ma qualidade", "qualidade gallica", "qualidade manifefta", "qualidade manifeſta", "qualidade peſtilencial", "qualidadeGallica", "qualidadegallica", "qualidadepeſtilente", "qualidadepungitiva", "qualidades", "qualidades gallica", "qua