mmation", "disease affecting joints"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gout", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Gout", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gout", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "gout", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gout", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Gout", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gout", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "gout", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gout", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Gout", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "gout", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Gout", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 234, "stable_key": "clu_342c16b447494be0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gout", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A metabolic disease causing joint inflammation.", "note": "Encompasses common and hip-related gout in historical texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Gout ( GOWT) is a form of inflammatory arthritis characterized by recurrent attacks of pain in a red, tender, hot, and swollen joint, caused by the deposition of needle-shaped crystals of the monosodium salt of uric acid. Pain typically comes on rapidly, reaching maximal intensity in less than 12 hours. In about half of cases, the joint at the base of the big toe is affected (Podagra). It may also result in tophi, kidney stones, or kidney damage. Gout is due to persistently elevated levels of uric acid (urate) in the blood (hyperuricemia). This occurs from a combination of diet, other health problems, and genetic factors."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sciatica", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1501, "target_cluster_name": "Ciatica", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "and soul Scabs, Sores, and Itch. The same also boyled in Wine, and applied to any Joynts full of pain and ach•or the Gout in the Hands or Feet, or the Hip-gout, called the Sciatica, and the Decoction ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Hip-gout"}, {"found_name": "reumas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2224, "target_cluster_name": "reumas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gout is a specific type of inflammatory arthritis that was historically often conflated with or considered a form of rheumatism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gout is a specific type of inflammatory arthritis that was historically often conflated with or considered a form of rheumatism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "lombrigas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1536, "target_cluster_name": "lombrigas", "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "eſte en rez mojola hina; por.veyricery quarro:horas, antes +1: Curan ſecos edtaagua¡nruchao enfermedades: Las enfer todo genero de mal de Buuas, todas llagas viejas, medades uFccrds,desháterofódone an", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of gout", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "gota coral", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1956, "target_cluster_name": "gota coral", "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "Las enfer todo genero de mal de Buuas, todas llagas viejas, medades uFccrds,desháterofódone anribuos, quicalos do Tf lores delas juncturas,que llaman gota artherica, y agua dela aró qualquier gengso d", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of gout", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "delirios", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 76, "members": [{"entity_id": "delirium", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "delirium", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 7, "variants": ["delirium"], "contexts": ["state due to foreign matters or brain changes", "confused mental state"]}, {"entity_id": "delirium", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "delirium", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["delirium"], "contexts": ["disturbed state of mind"]}, {"entity_id": "delirios", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "delirios", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 52, "variants": ["delirios", "Delirio", "Delirios", "delir.", "deliran-", "delirante", "delirantes", "delirar", "delirava", "deliria", "delirio", "deliriocom", "delirios implacaveis", "delirios teymosos", "delirium"], "contexts": ["Condition of delirium", "delirium"]}, {"entity_id": "delirium", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "delirium", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["delirium"], "contexts": ["state of severe confusion and disordered thought", "confused and disturbed state of mind"]}, {"entity_id": "phrensies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Phrensies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Phrensies"], "contexts": ["state of madness or delirium", "mental state treated with Opium"]}, {"entity_id": "phrensie", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Phrensie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phrensie", "Phrenetick"], "contexts": ["feverish delirium", "condition from eating Hemlock"]}, {"entity_id": "phrensies", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Phrensies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phrensies"], "contexts": ["mental derangement", "disturbed mental state"]}, {"entity_id": "phreneſis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Phreneſis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phreneſis", "Phrenes.", "Phrenitid", "phreneticorum"], "contexts": ["Condition of phrenitis", "illness of the frenetic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "delirium", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "delirium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "delirium", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "delirios", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "delirium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "delirium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "delirium", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "delirios", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "delirium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "delirium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "delirios", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "delirium", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 235, "stable_key": "clu_f12694a1865a3b02", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Delirium", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Delirium", "wikidata_id": "Q160796", "wikidata_description": "severe confusion that develops quickly, and often fluctuates in intensity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delirium", "note": "Historically, 'phrensy' could encompass a broader range of mental disturbances, but in these contexts, it strongly points to delirium or a state of acute mental derangement, often associated with fever.", "wikipedia_extract": "Delirium (formerly acute confusional state, an ambiguous term that is now discouraged) is a specific state of acute confusion attributable to the direct physiological consequence of a medical condition, effects of a psychoactive substance, or multiple causes, which usually develops over the course of hours to days. As a syndrome, delirium presents with disturbances in attention, awareness, and higher-order cognition. People with delirium may experience other neuropsychiatric disturbances including changes in psychomotor activity (e.g., hyperactive, hypoactive, or mixed level of activity), disrupted sleep-wake cycle, emotional disturbances, disturbances of consciousness, or altered state of consciousness, as well as perceptual disturbances (e.g., hallucinations and delusions), although these features are not required for diagnosis. Diagnostically, delirium encompasses both the syndrome of acute confusion and its underlying organic process known as an acute encephalopathy. The cause of delirium may be either a disease process inside the brain or a process outside the brain that nonetheless affects the brain. Delirium may be the result of an underlying medical condition (e.g., infection or hypoxia), side effect of a medication such as diphenhydramine, promethazine, and dicyclomine, substance intoxication (e.g., opioids or hallucinogenic deliriants), substance withdrawal (e.g., alcohol or sedatives), or from multiple factors affecting one's overall health (e.g., malnutrition, pain, etc.)."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "confusion", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2814, "target_cluster_name": "confusion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The provided description of Delirium explicitly states it is a 'specific state of acute confusion'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The provided description of Delirium explicitly states it is a 'specific state of acute confusion'."}, {"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coma represents a profound state of unconsciousness, which can be an endpoint or a severe manifestation of conditions that might also present with delirium, indicating a close relationship in the spectrum of altered consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coma represents a profound state of unconsciousness, which can be an endpoint or a severe manifestation of conditions that might also present with delirium, indicating a close relationship in the spectrum of altered consciousness."}, {"found_name": "Mania", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1323, "target_cluster_name": "Madness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania involves a state of elevated mood and energy, often accompanied by disorganized thinking and behavior, which can share features with or co-occur with delirium, particularly in the context of severe mental disturbances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania involves a state of elevated mood and energy, often accompanied by disorganized thinking and behavior, which can share features with or co-occur with delirium, particularly in the context of severe mental disturbances."}, {"found_name": "freneticos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 571, "target_cluster_name": "freneticos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frenzy can involve a state of disturbed consciousness and confusion, overlapping with the symptoms of delirium.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Frenzy can involve a state of d