nd can be magnetized to become permanent magnets, producing magnetic fields themselves. Demagnetizing a magnet is also possible. Only a few substances are ferromagnetic; the most common ones are iron, cobalt, nickel, and their alloys. All substances exhibit some type of magnetism."}, "member_count": 10, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Attraction (physics)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4186, "target_cluster_name": "attractions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetism is a fundamental force that causes attraction and repulsion between objects, often discussed alongside other physical forces of attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetism is a fundamental force that causes attraction and repulsion between objects, often discussed alongside other physical forces of attraction."}, {"found_name": "Attractive force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1111, "target_cluster_name": "attractive force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetism is a type of attractive force, and historical texts often discuss it in the context of other forces that draw objects together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetism is a type of attractive force, and historical texts often discuss it in the context of other forces that draw objects together."}, {"found_name": "Earth's magnetic field", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3603, "target_cluster_name": "Magnet. der Erde", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Earth's magnetic field is a prominent manifestation of magnetism, and its study is intrinsically linked to the concept of magnetism itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Earth's magnetic field is a prominent manifestation of magnetism, and its study is intrinsically linked to the concept of magnetism itself."}, {"found_name": "Diamagnetic Polarity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2841, "target_cluster_name": "diamagnetic polarity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diamagnetism is a specific type of magnetic response, directly related to the broader phenomenon of magnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diamagnetism is a specific type of magnetic response, directly related to the broader phenomenon of magnetism."}, {"found_name": "Electric force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2849, "target_cluster_name": "electric force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetism is one of the two aspects of electromagnetism, with electric force being the other. They are deeply intertwined.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetism is one of the two aspects of electromagnetism, with electric force being the other. They are deeply intertwined."}, {"found_name": "Electrical phenomena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3624, "target_cluster_name": "electrical phenomena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetism and electricity are closely related phenomena, often studied together under the umbrella of electromagnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetism and electricity are closely related phenomena, often studied together under the umbrella of electromagnetism."}, {"found_name": "Electrostatic attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1408, "target_cluster_name": "Electrick attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, electrostatic attraction and magnetism are both fundamental forces that can cause attraction between objects, and their study often overlaps in premodern texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While distinct, electrostatic attraction and magnetism are both fundamental forces that can cause attraction between objects, and their study often overlaps in premodern texts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Locke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 39, "members": [{"entity_id": "locke", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Locke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 34, "variants": ["Locke"], "contexts": ["philosopher on consciousness", "philosopher on discrimination"]}, {"entity_id": "hooke", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hooke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hooke"], "contexts": ["early figure in magnetism studies", "lofty mind, considered chronometry of earth"]}, {"entity_id": "locke", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Locke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Locke"], "contexts": ["philosopher influencing psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "hooke", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Hooke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hooke"], "contexts": ["experimenter on spinning tops and motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Locke", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hooke", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Locke", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Locke", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Locke", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Hooke", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hooke", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Locke", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hooke", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Hooke", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 242, "stable_key": "clu_6a121a9ad800789d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Locke", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1632, "death_year": 1704, "description": "Philosopher of consciousness and empiricism, influenced psychology.", "note": "The cluster mentions 'Hooke' and 'Hooker'. John Locke is the primary figure due to the strong association with 'principles_of_psychology_james_1890' and his philosophical significance.", "wikidata_id": "Q9353", "wikidata_description": "English philosopher and physician (1632–1704)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Locke", "wikipedia_extract": "John Locke (; 29 August 1632 (O.S.) – 28 October 1704 (O.S.)) was an English philosopher and physician, widely regarded as one of the most influential of the Enlightenment thinkers and commonly known as the \"father of liberalism\". His important works include A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689), Two Treatises of Government (1689/90), both published anonymously, and An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689/90). His writing on toleration contends that religion is a matter for the individual and that the churches are voluntary associations, ruling out religious coercion and uniformity; these lead to the idea of separation of church and state. His Two Treatises on Government argues for government based on the consent of the governed and the right to revolt against tyrannous government, which has lost consent. The Two Treatises had a direct influence on the language that Thomas Jefferson chose in his drafting the July 1776 Declaration of Independence during the American Revolution. Locke lived through the tumultuous political era of the English Civil War and Commonwealth of England after the execution of Charles I, Restoration of the Stuart monarchy, and the 1688 Glorious Revolution."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Francis Bacon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 185, "target_cluster_name": "Bacon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential English philosophers whose ideas significantly shaped subsequent intellectual and political thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential English philosophers whose ideas significantly shaped subsequent intellectual and political thought."}, {"found_name": "Isaac Newton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 71, "target_cluster_name": "Newton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent English thinkers of their respective eras, with Newton being a key figure in the scientific revolution and Locke a foundational figure in empiricism and political philosophy, often seen as intellectual successors in the broader context of English intellectual history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent English thinkers of their respective eras, with Newton being a key figure in the scientific revolution and Locke a foundational figure in empiricism and political philosophy, often seen as intellectual successors in the broader context of English intellectual history."}, {"found_name": "Immanuel Kant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 643, "target_cluster_name": "Kant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant was deeply influenced by Locke's epistemology and political philosophy, engaging with and building upon Locke's ideas in his own critical philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kant was deeply influenced by Locke's epistemology and political philosophy, engaging with and building upon Locke's ideas in his own critical philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Locke, as a major figure in empiricism, engaged with and reacted against Aristotelian philosophy, particularly in his theories of knowledge and metaphysics. Aristotle represents a foundational philosophical tradition that Locke's work implicitly or explicitly addressed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Locke, as a major figure in empiricism, engaged with and reacted against Aristotelian philosophy, particularly in his theories of knowledge and metaphysics. Aristotle represents a foundational philosophical tradition that Locke's work implicitly or explicitly addressed."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Locke was trained as a physician and had a significant interest in medicine, though his primary fame rests on his philosophy. Galen represents a historical pinnacle of medical thought that would have been part of the intellectual background for any physician-philosopher of Locke's time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Locke was trained as a physician and had a significant interest in medicine, though his primary fame rests on his philosophy. Galen represents a historical pinnacle of medical thought that would have been part of the intellectual background for any physician-philosopher of Locke's time."}, {"found_name": "Comte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2578, "target_cluster_name": "Comte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Auguste Comte's positivism, which emphasized empirical observation and scientific method, can be seen as a development and extension of empiricist traditions in philosophy, with John Locke being a foundational figure in that tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Auguste Comte's positivism, which emphasized empirical observation and scientific method, can be seen as a development and extension of empiricist traditions in philosophy, with John Locke being a foundational figure in that tradition.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Laplace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 51, "members": [{"entity_id": "laplace", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Laplace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laplace"], "contexts": ["author of 'Essai philosophique sur les probabilités'"]}, {"entity_id": "laplace", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Laplace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 26, "variants": ["Laplace"], "contexts": ["author of 'L'Exposition du Système du Monde'", "scientist known for work on mechanics"]}, {"entity_id": "la_place", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "La Place", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["La Place"], "contexts": ["mutual friend, scientist"]}, {"entity_id": "la_place", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "La Place", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 21, "variants": ["La Place"], "contexts": ["proved stability of solar system", "established principle for secular variations in orbits"]}, {"entity_id": "laplace", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Laplace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Laplace"], "contexts": ["astronomer and mathematician", "astronomer who proposed nebular hypothesis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Laplace", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "La Place", "similarity": 0.671}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "La Place", "similarity": 0.671}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Laplace", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "La Place", "similarity": 0.671}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Laplace", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Laplace", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "La Place", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "La Place", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "La Place", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Laplace", "similarity": 0.671}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "La Place", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Laplace", "similarity": 0.671}], "id": 243, "stable_key": "clu_6ed0019a024e8252", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pierre-Simon Laplace", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1749, "death_year": 1827, "description": "Mathematician and astronomer, developed probability theory.", "wikidata_id": "Q44481", "wikidata_description": "French mathematician and astronomer (1749–1827)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre-Simon%20Laplace", "wikipedia_extract": "Pierre-Simon, Marquis de Laplace (; French: [pjɛʁ simɔ̃ laplas]; 23 March 1749 – 5 March 1827) was a French polymath, a scholar whose work has been instrumental in the fields of physics, astronomy, mathematics,  engineering, statistics, and philosophy. He summarized and extended the work of his predecessors in his five-volume Mécanique céleste (Celestial Mechanics) (1799–1825). This work translated the geometric study of classical mechanics to one based on calculus, opening up a broader range of problems. Laplace also popularized and further confirmed Sir Isaac Newton's work. In statistics, the Bayesian interpretation of probability was developed mainly by Laplace. Laplace formulated Laplace's equation, and pioneered the Laplace transform which appears in many branches of mathematical physics, a field that he took a leading role in forming."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "André-Marie Ampère", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2134, "target_cluster_name": "Ampère", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent French scientists of the same era, with significant contributions to physics and mathematics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent French scientists of the same era, with significant contributions to physics and mathematics."}, {"found_name": "Carl Friedrich Gauss", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1176, "target_cluster_name": "Gauss", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were leading mathematicians and scientists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, known for their foundational work in their respective fields.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were leading mathematicians and scientists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, known for their foundational work in their respective fields."}, {"found_name": "Augustin-Jean Fresnel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2204, "target_cluster_name": "Fresnel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential French scientists working in physics and mathematics during the same period, making significant contributions to optics and mechanics respectively.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential French scientists working in physics and mathematics during the same period, making significant contributions to optics and mechanics respectively."}, {"found_name": "Siméon Denis Poisson", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1908, "target_cluster_name": "Poisson", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent French mathematicians and physicists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, contributing significantly to mechanics, probability, and mathematical physics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent French mathematicians and physicists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, contributing significantly to mechanics, probability, and mathematical physics."}, {"found_name": "Joseph Fourier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2132, "target_cluster_name": "Fourier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were leading French mathematicians and physicists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, with significant contributions to mathematical physics and analysis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were leading French mathematicians and physicists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, with significant contributions to mathematical physics and analysis."}, {"found_name": "mathematicians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1337, "target_cluster_name": "mathematicians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Laplace was a highly influential mathematician and astronomer, known for his work in probability and celestial mechanics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Laplace was a highly influential mathematician and astronomer, known for his work in probability and celestial mechanics.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mesue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 50, "members": [{"entity_id": "mesue", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Mesue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mesue"], "contexts": ["medieval medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "mesue", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mesue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 40, "variants": ["Mesue", "Mesuc", "Mesué"], "contexts": ["ancient author criticized", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "mesurunge", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mesurunge", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mesurunge"], "contexts": ["authority for Sarapiam"]}, {"entity_id": "mesue", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Mesue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mesue"], "contexts": ["medieval medical authority", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "meſues", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Meſues", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Meſues", "Mesues"], "contexts": ["cited for a book on fevers", "medical authority cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mesue", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mesurunge", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mesue", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Meſues", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mesue", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Meſues", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mesurunge", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mesue", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Mesue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Meſues", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 244, "stable_key": "clu_0b783f1aa98bc174", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mesue the Younger", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Influential medieval Arab physician and pharmacologist.", "note": "Multiple spellings suggest the same figure, a prominent medieval medical authority whose works were widely translated and used.", "wikidata_id": "Q3297191", "wikidata_description": "Syrian physician", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masawaih%20al-Mardini", "wikipedia_extract": "Masawaih al-Mardini (Yahyā ibn Masawaih al-Mardini; known as Mesue the Younger) was a physician. He was born in Iraq. After working in Baghdad, he entered the service of the Fatimid caliph Al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah. He died in 1015 in Cairo at the age of ninety. Masawaih al-Mardini was a Syriac Orthodox Christian. He is known due to his books on purgatives and emetics (De medicins laxativis) and on the complete pharmacopoeia in 12 parts called the Antidotarium sive Grabadin medicamentorum, which remained for centuries the standard textbook of pharmacy in the West."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mefue", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1957, "target_cluster_name": "Mefue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mefue is a direct transliteration of Mesue the Younger's name, often used interchangeably in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Mefue is a direct transliteration of Mesue the Younger's name, often used interchangeably in historical texts."}, {"found_name": "Melue", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4439, "target_cluster_name": "Melue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melue is a variant spelling or transliteration of Mesue the Younger's name, appearing in some historical sources.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Melue is a variant spelling or transliteration of Mesue the Younger's name, appearing in some historical sources."}, {"found_name": "cap.", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 594, "target_cluster_name": "cap.", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "nomes que de- vem ser corrompidos, scilicet, os nomes dos autores ; folga- ria muyto me dixestes disto o que sabeis. • Serapio, 3 19; Avicena, Lib. 2, cap. 3 18 (nota do auctor). Do Cravo 367 ORTA Não", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: also called", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Avicena", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "está cebar. RUANO Acerca dos indios he usado? ORTA Acerca dos fisicos da Pérsia, Arábia e Turquia se usa desta mezinha, porque sabem elles de cór Avicena, a que chamão elles Abolahi e a seus cinquo li", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: known to Persians, Arabs, and Turks", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Avicenna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Avicenna and Mesue the Younger were both highly influential physicians in the Islamic Golden Age, whose works were widely studied and translated in medieval and Renaissance Europe. They represent a shared intellectual tradition in medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Avicenna and Mesue the Younger were both highly influential physicians in the Islamic Golden Age, whose works were widely studied and translated in medieval and Renaissance Europe. They represent a shared intellectual tradition in medicine."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was a foundational figure in Greco-Roman medicine whose theories and practices heavily influenced physicians like Mesue the Younger and subsequent medical traditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was a foundational figure in Greco-Roman medicine whose theories and practices heavily influenced physicians like Mesue the Younger and subsequent medical traditions."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with those of Galen, formed the bedrock of medical knowledge that physicians like Mesue the Younger inherited and built upon.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with those of Galen, formed the bedrock of medical knowledge that physicians like Mesue the Younger inherited and built upon."}]}, {"canonical_name": "feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 50, "members": [{"entity_id": "feeling", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Feeling"], "contexts": ["sense of touch"]}, {"entity_id": "feeling", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 41, "variants": ["feeling", "FEELING", "Feeling"], "contexts": ["emotional state", "emotional experience"]}, {"entity_id": "feeling", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["feeling"], "contexts": ["emotional state"]}, {"entity_id": "feeling", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["feeling"], "contexts": ["emotional experience", "an emotional state or sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Feeling", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Feeling", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Feeling", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "feeling", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "feeling", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "feeling", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "feeling", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 245, "stable_key": "clu_37940425523d6f0f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Feeling", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the subjective experience of emotions or sensations, particularly touch.", "wikidata_id": "Q205555", "wikidata_description": "conscious subjective experience of emotion", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feeling", "wikipedia_extract": "According to the APA Dictionary of Psychology, a feeling is \"a self-contained phenomenal experience\"; feelings are \"subjective, evaluative, and independent of the sensations, thoughts, or images evoking them\". The term feeling is closely related to, but not the same as, emotion. Feeling may, for instance, refer to the conscious subjective experience of emotions. The study of subjective experiences is called phenomenology. Psychotherapy generally involves a therapist helping a client understand, articulate, and learn to effectively regulate the client's own feelings, and ultimately to take responsibility for the client's experience of the world. Feelings are sometimes held to be characteristic of embodied consciousness."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "feelings", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 618, "target_cluster_name": "feelings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity description explicitly states 'historical name: feelings', indicating 'feeling' is an older or alternative term for 'emotions'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity description explicitly states 'historical name: feelings', indicating 'feeling' is an older or alternative term for 'emotio