, "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Galbano", "galbano"], "contexts": ["galbanum, used in plaster", "incense for stupor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Calbanum", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Galbano", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Calbanum", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cancamo", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cancamo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cancamo", "similarity": 0.751}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Galbano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "galbano", "similarity": 0.587}], "id": 270, "stable_key": "clu_6aa02a1b5c0df5dc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galangal", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Alpinia galanga", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "description": "An aromatic root used medicinally and as a spice.", "note": "Distinct from galbanum.", "wikipedia_extract": "Alpinia galanga, a plant in the ginger family, bears a rhizome used largely as an herb in Unani medicine and as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery. It is one of four plants known as \"galangal\". Its common names include greater galangal, lengkuas, and blue ginger. The name \"galangal\" is probably derived from Persian qulanjan or Arabic khalanjan, which in turn may be an adaptation of Chinese gao liang jiang. Its names in North India are derived from the same root, including kulanja in Sanskrit, kulanjan in Hindi, and kholinjan in Urdu. The name \"lengkuas\", on the other hand, is derived from Malay lengkuas, which is derived from Proto-Western Malayo-Polynesian *laŋkuas, with cognates including Ilokano langkuás; Tagalog, Bikol, Kapampangan, Visayan, and Manobo langkáuas or langkáwas; Aklanon eangkawás; Kadazan Dusun hongkuas; Ida'an lengkuas; Ngaju Dayak langkuas; and Iban engkuas."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cheyroſo", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3200, "target_cluster_name": "cheyroſo", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "corrutamente escreve, nam lhe deis fé; porque todos os Arábios lhe chamão asi. E esta que chamamos galanga he de duas maneiras, scilicet, huma pequena, muyto cheirosa, trazida da China a estas terras,", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: known as", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cardamomo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 636, "target_cluster_name": "Cardamomo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is another spice from the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), often discussed alongside cardamom in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Galangal is another spice from the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), often discussed alongside cardamom in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "zedoaria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 767, "target_cluster_name": "zedoaria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Zedoary (Curcuma zedoaria) and Galangal (Alpinia galanga) are both members of the Zingiberaceae family (ginger family) and share similar rhizomatous growth habits and culinary/medicinal uses, often being confused or used interchangeably in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Zedoary (Curcuma zedoaria) and Galangal (Alpinia galanga) are both members of the Zingiberaceae family (ginger family) and share similar rhizomatous growth habits and culinary/medicinal uses, often being confused or used interchangeably in historical texts.", "is_reverse": t