that can resuscitate forgotten things"]}, {"entity_id": "fiebres", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "fiebres", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 30, "variants": ["fiebres", "fiebre", "fiebre mala", "fiebres agudas", "fiebres arfiuas", "fiebres calientes", "fiebres de mala qualidad", "fiebres erraticas", "fiebres largas importunas", "fiebres largas y importunas", "fiebres malignas", "fiebres peltilenciales", "fiebres peltilneciales", "fiebres pestilenciales", "fiebres pestilentes"], "contexts": ["acute illness treated with gargaparrilla water", "fevers, long and troublesome"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fevers", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "fevers", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fevers", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "fiebres", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fièvres épidémiques", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "fiebres", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fevers", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "fiebres", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 273, "stable_key": "clu_39013ff5aca96875", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fever", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An acute illness characterized by elevated body temperature and potential delirium.", "wikipedia_extract": "Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C (99.0 and 100.9 °F) in humans. The increase in set point triggers increased muscle contractions and causes a feeling of cold or chills. This results in greater heat production and efforts to conserve heat. When the set point temperature returns to normal, a person feels hot, becomes flushed, and may begin to sweat. Rarely a fever may trigger a febrile seizure, with this being more common in young children."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "consumption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 73, "target_cluster_name": "consumption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fever is a common symptom associated with the systemic infection characteristic of phthisis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fever is a common symptom associated with the systemic infection characteristic of phthisis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pleurisy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 188, "target_cluster_name": "Pleurisy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fever is a common symptom of pleurisy, often accompanying the inflammation and infection that can cause it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fever is a common symptom of pleurisy, often accompanying the inflammation and infection that can cause it.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "bexigas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 565, "target_cluster_name": "bexigas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fever (fevers) is a common symptom and a general category of illness that was frequently associated with and a primary indicator of many infectious diseases, including smallpox, in premodern medical discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fever (fevers) is a common symptom and a general category of illness that was frequently associated with and a primary indicator of many infectious diseases, including smallpox, in premodern medical discourse.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "copper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 57, "members": [{"entity_id": "copper", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "copper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["copper"], "contexts": ["chemical element and metal"]}, {"entity_id": "copper", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "copper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 30, "variants": ["Copper", "copper"], "contexts": ["metal in protoxides", "metal plated with silver for copying nature"]}, {"entity_id": "copper", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Copper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Copper"], "contexts": ["metal, can be formed from iron", "metal mixed with gold as an allay"]}, {"entity_id": "copper", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Copper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Copper"], "contexts": ["metal used for plates"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Cobre"], "contexts": ["metal brought from the Indies", "metal discussed for its temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["cobre"], "contexts": ["metal for coinage", "metal used for sieves"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["cobre"], "contexts": ["metal used for a pot", "material for a vessel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "copper", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "copper", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Copper", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Copper", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 274, "stable_key": "clu_8e4d465fcf6abfcf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Copper", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q753", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Cu and atomic number 29", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper", "wikipedia_extract": "Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu (from Latin cuprum) and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement. Copper is one of the few native metals, meaning metals that occur naturally in a directly usable, unalloyed metallic form. This led to very early human use in several regions, from c."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "metal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 87, "target_cluster_name": "metal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Copper is a fundamental metal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Copper is a fundamental metal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Brass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 440, "target_cluster_name": "Brass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brass is an alloy primarily composed