 extended, gravitationally unbound atmosphere or coma surrounding the nucleus, and sometimes a tail of gas and dust gas blown out from the coma. These phenomena are due to the effects of solar radiation and the outstreaming solar wind plasma acting upon the nucleus of the comet. Comet nuclei range from a few hundred meters to tens of kilometers across and are composed of loose collections of ice, dust, and small rocky particles. The coma may be up to 15 times Earth's diameter, while the tail may stretch beyond one astronomical unit. If sufficiently close and bright, a comet may be seen from Earth without the aid of a telescope and can subtend an arc of up to 30° (60 Moons) across the sky."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "planets", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2065, "target_cluster_name": "planets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Comets were celestial objects observed alongside planets and often discussed in the same astronomical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Comets were celestial objects observed alongside planets and often discussed in the same astronomical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "heavenly bodies", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1950, "target_cluster_name": "heavenly bodies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Comets are celestial bodies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Comets are celestial bodies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Comet of 1770", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2842, "target_cluster_name": "comet of 1770", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Comet of 1770 is a specific instance of a comet.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Comet of 1770 is a specific instance of a c