", "target_name": "M. Elie de Beaumont", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Beaumont, Elie de", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "M. Elie de Beaumont", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 302, "stable_key": "clu_779c6c4293b8a311", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste Élie de Beaumont", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1798, "death_year": 1874, "description": "French geologist known for his work on mountain ranges and geological time.", "wikidata_id": "Q1950452", "wikidata_description": "mountain in New Zealand", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount%20Elie%20de%20Beaumont", "wikipedia_extract": "Mount Elie de Beaumont is a 3,109 metres (10,200 ft) high mountain in the Southern Alps on the South Island of New Zealand and the northernmost Three-thousander of the country. It is surrounded by several glaciers like Johannes Glacier in the north, Burton Glacier to the east, Times Glacier to the west and Anna Glacier, a tributary to the Tasman Glacier, in the south. It was named by Julius von Haast after the French geologist Jean-Baptiste Élie de Beaumont."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "density", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "density", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "density", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["density"], "contexts": ["mass per unit volume", "measure of mass per unit volume"]}, {"entity_id": "densidade", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "densidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["densidade"], "contexts": ["property of diamonds"]}, {"entity_id": "density", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "density", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 15, "variants": ["density", "Density"], "contexts": ["mass per unit volume", "property of matter related to mass and volume"]}, {"entity_id": "densities", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Densities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Densities"], "contexts": ["mass per unit volume"]}, {"entity_id": "density", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "density", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["density"], "contexts": ["mass per unit volume"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "density", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "densidade", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "density", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "density", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Densities", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "density", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "densidade", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "densidade", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Densities", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "densidade", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "density", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Densities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "density", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 303, "stable_key": "clu_c77f92bc275b4995", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Density", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q29539", "wikidata_description": "mass of a sample, material or body divided by its volume", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Density", "wikipedia_extract": "Density (volumetric mass density or specific mass) is the ratio of a substance's mass to its volume. The symbol most often used for density is ρ (the lower case Greek letter rho), although the Latin letter D (or d) can also be used: {\\displaystyle \\rho ={\\frac {m}{V}},} where ρ is the density, m is the mass, and V is the volume. In some cases (for instance, in the United States oil and gas industry), density is loosely defined as its weight per unit volume, although this is scientifically inaccurate –  this quantity is more specifically called specific weight. For a pure substance, the density is equal to its mass concentration. Different materials usually have different densities, and density may be relevant to buoyancy, purity and packaging. Osmium is the densest known element at standard conditions for temperature and pressure."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Earth's Density", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3619, "target_cluster_name": "Density of the Earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a specific instance of the general concept of density, applied to a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This is a specific instance of the general concept of density, applied to a celestial body."}, {"found_name": "propriedades", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 508, "target_cluster_name": "propriedades", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Density is a fundamental physical property of water.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Density is a fundamental physical property of water.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "atmospheric pressure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 930, "target_cluster_name": "atmospheric pressure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Density is directly related to atmospheric pressure (Pressure = Density * Gravity * Height).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Density is directly related to atmospheric pressure (Pressure = Density * Gravity * Height).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "measurement", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1274, "target_cluster_name": "measurement", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Density is a physical property that is determined through measurement (mass per unit volume).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Density is a physical property that is determined through measurement (mass per unit volume).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "velocity of sound", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1469, "target_cluster_name": "velocity of sound", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The speed of sound in a medium is dependent on its density and elasticity. Density is a key physical property influencing sound propagation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The speed of sound in a medium is dependent on its density and elasticity. Density is a key physical property influencing sound propagation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "liquid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2020, "target_cluster_name": "liquid", "source_book": "llm_generated", 