 between magnetic and isothermal lines"]}, {"entity_id": "david_brewster", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "David Brewster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["David Brewster"], "contexts": ["counted dark bands in spectrum", "scientist who studied refraction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brewster, Sir David", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "SIR DAVID BREWSTER", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brewster, Sir David", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir David Brewster", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "SIR DAVID BREWSTER", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir David Brewster", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Brewster", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sir David Brewster", "similarity": 0.872}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Brewster", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "David Brewster", "similarity": 0.874}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir David Brewster", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "David Brewster", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 305, "stable_key": "clu_831e823efc1a92a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "David Brewster", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1781, "death_year": 1868, "description": "Scottish physicist, inventor, and writer on scientific subjects.", "wikidata_id": "Q15442692", "wikidata_description": "American journalist", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David%20Brewster%20%28journalist%29", "wikipedia_extract": "David Clark Brewster (born September 26, 1939) is an American journalist and the founder, editor and publisher of the Seattle Weekly and the Northwest news website Crosscut.com. He is also the founder, creator and former executive director of the nonprofit cultural center Town Hall Seattle. He was born on September 26, 1939, in Newark, New Jersey, the son of Gaylord Clark Brewster and Marjorie Jane Anderson. His father was a 1930 graduate of the University of Nebraska. He graduated in 1961, Phi Beta Kappa,  with a Bachelor of Arts from Yale University in New Haven, Connecticut, and he received  his master's degree from Yale University in 1963. In 1964, he married Joyce Skaggs, a 1961 graduate of Smith College."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chladni", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2131, "target_cluster_name": "Chladni", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicists of the 19th century, with Chladni's work on acoustics and wave phenomena being relevant to Brewster's investigations into light and optics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent physicists of the 19th century, with Chladni's work on acoustics and wave phenomena being relevant to Brewster's investigations into light and optics.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Brewster", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2453, "target_cluster_name": "Brewster", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is an alternative capitalization of the same name.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is an alternative capitalization of the same name.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "american_aloe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "American aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["American aloe"], "contexts": ["plant stem observed for rapid growth"]}, {"entity_id": "alõe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alõe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alõe"], "contexts": ["Greek and Latin name for aloe"]}, {"entity_id": "alôe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alôe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alôe"], "contexts": ["plantations on Socotra"]}, {"entity_id": "aloe", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["Aloe", "ALOE", "Aloe zoccoterino", "Aloè", "Aloé", "aloe"], "contexts": ["plant name", "juice of a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "aloe", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Aloe", "aloe", "aloé"], "contexts": ["herb used for purging", "ingredient in a purgative"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "American aloe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "American aloe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alõe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.748}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alõe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.748}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alôe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.749}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alôe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.749}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Aloe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 306, "stable_key": "clu_73b5a0df777bf274", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aloes", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLANT", "description": "A genus of succulent plants, known for their medicinal properties.", "note": "Refers to the plant genus 'Aloe' and its derivatives like 'aloés'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Agarwood, aloeswood, eaglewood, gaharuwood, commonly referred to as oud or oudh (from Arabic: عود, romanized: ʿūd, pronounced [ʕuːd]), is a fragrant, dark and resinous wood used in incense, perfume, and small hand carvings. It forms in the heartwood of Aquilaria trees after they become infected with a type of Phaeoacremonium mold, P. parasitica. The tree defensively secretes a resin to combat the fungal infestation. Prior to becoming infected, the heartwood mostly lacks scent, and is relatively light and pale in colouration. However, as the infection advances and the tree produces its fragrant resin as a final option of defense, the heartwood becomes very dense, dark, and saturated with resin. This product is harvested, and most famously referred to in cosmetics under the scent names of oud, oodh or aguru; however, it is also called aloes (not to be confused with the succulent plant genus Aloe), agar (this name, as well, is not to be confused with the edible, algae-derived thickening agent agar agar), as well as gaharu or jinko."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arábios", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 538, "target_cluster_name": "Arábios", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "do auctor). Do aloés ha poucas cousas que dizer que sejão notáveis, e porém fazervosây a vontade, e digo que o aloés ou aloa he latino e grego, e os Arábios o chamão cebar, e os Gu- zarates e Decanins", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for aloes", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "aloés çocoloríno", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4450, "target_cluster_name": "socotorino", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "muitas partes (i), mas a de Çocotora he muito mais louvada, e he mercadoria pêra a Turquia, a Pérsia e Arabía, e pêra toda a Europa; e por isso o chamam aloés çocoloríno; e dista esta ilha ou está apa", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of aloés"}, {"found_name": "aloa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "muito Plinio e Dioscorides**. • Arístot. Topic, libro 3 (nota do auctor). •• Plin., libr. 27, cap. 4; Diosc., libr. 3, cap. ai (nota do auctor). Do aloés ha poucas cousas que dizer que sejão notáveis,", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for aloés"}, {"found_name": "cebar", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "muito Plinio e Dioscorides**. • Arístot. Topic, libro 3 (nota do auctor). •• Plin., libr. 27, cap. 4; Diosc., libr. 3, cap. ai (nota do auctor). Do aloés ha poucas cousas que dizer que sejão notáveis,", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Arabic name for aloés"}, {"found_name": "bola", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "á qual elles chamam bola, e desta usam muito para curar cavalos, e para matar os bichos das chagas, e por tanto nam he muito chamarse acerca de nós o aloés ruym Do Aloés cahalino, como escreve um mode", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "name used for a type of aloés"}, {"found_name": "Aloés cahalino", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "á qual elles chamam bola, e desta usam muito para curar cavalos, e para matar os bichos das chagas, e por tanto nam he muito chamarse acerca de nós o aloés ruym Do Aloés cahalino, como escreve um mode", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of aloés"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "scaliger", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Scaliger"], "contexts": ["ancient authority, did not deny crystal is ice", "Renaissance scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "scaligero", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Scaligero"], "contexts": ["Defended Pliny's statement on aloes", "made notes on Orta's book"]}, {"entity_id": "scaligero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scaligero"], "contexts": ["Julius Caesar Scaliger"]}, {"entity_id": "scaliger", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scaliger"], "contexts": ["Renaissance scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Scaliger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.818}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Scaligero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 307, "stable_key": "clu_fe7b1dd04a97f390", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Julius Caesar Scaliger", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Influential Italian scholar and physician of the Renaissance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Julius Caesar Scaliger (; 23 April  or August 1484 – 21 October 1558), or Giulio Cesare della Scala, was an Italian scholar and physician, who spent a major part of his career in France. He employed the techniques and discoveries of Renaissance humanism to defend Aristotelianism against the New Learning. In spite of his contentious disposition, his contemporary reputation was high. Jacques Auguste de Thou claimed that none of the ancients could be placed above him and that he had no equal in his own time. Scaliger's father, Benedetto Bordone, was a miniaturist and illuminator. Scaliger himself was known in his youth by the family name Bordone, but later insisted that he was a scion of the house of La Scala, for a hundred and fifty years lords of Verona."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["Roman statesman and orator", "ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher on insight into nature"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["Roman philosopher and orator"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Cicero", "Cicer", "Cicer.", "Ciceron", "Cicerone"], "contexts": ["Ancient Roman orator and philosopher", "Ancient authority, author of 'de Officijs'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 308, "stable_key": "clu_ccb622fe0550f5ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cicero", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -106, "death_year": -43, "description": "Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, and orator.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicero", "wikipedia_extract": "Marcus Tullius Cicero ( SISS-ə-roh; Latin: [ˈmaːrkus ˈtulli.us ˈkikɛrɔː]; 3 January 106 BC – 7 December 43 BC) was a Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, philosopher, orator, writer and Academic skeptic, who tried to uphold optimate principles during the political crises that led to the establishment of the Roman Empire. His extensive writings include treatises on rhetoric, philosophy and politics. He is considered one of Rome's greatest orators and prose stylists and the innovator of what became known as \"Ciceronian rhetoric\". Cicero was educated in Rome and in Greece. He came from a wealthy municipal family of the Roman equestrian order, and served as consul in 63 BC. He greatly influenced both ancient and modern reception of the Latin language."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Augustus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2668, "target_cluster_name": "Augustus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cicero was a prominent figure during the late Roman Republic and his political career was significantly impacted by the rise of Augustus and the transition to the Roman Empire. Cicero's writings also offer insights into the political climate and figures of this era, including Augustus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cicero was a prominent figure during the late Roman Republic and his political career was significantly impacted by the rise of Augustus and the transition to the Roman Empire. Cicero's writings also offer insights into the political climate and figures of this era, including Augustus."}, {"found_name": "philosopher", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 431, "target_cluster_name": "philosopher", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cicero was a Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, orator, and philosopher.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cicero was a Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, orator, and philosopher.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lucie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2024, "target_cluster_name": "Lucie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Seneca was a prominent Stoic philosopher and statesman in ancient Rome, and Cicero was another highly influential Roman philosopher, orator, and statesman. They represent key figures in Roman intellectual and political history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Seneca was a prominent Stoic philosopher and statesman in ancient Rome, and Cicero was another highly influential Roman philosopher, orator, and statesman. They represent key figures in Roman intellectual and political history.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ennius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2686, "target_cluster_name": "Ennius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet and the national poet of Rome, whose work heavily influenced later Roman writers like Cicero.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet and the national poet of Rome, whose work heavily influenced later Roman writers like Cicero.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Quintiliopa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2823, "target_cluster_name": "Quintiliopa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quintilian was a student of Cicero's rhetorical tradition and frequently referenced his works.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Quintilian was a student of Cicero's rhetorical tradition and frequently referenced his works.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Juvenal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2902, "target_cluster_name": "Juvenal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Juvenal and Cicero were both influential Roman writers and orators, whose works are foundational to Latin literature and rhetoric.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Juvenal and Cicero were both influential Roman writers and orators, whose works are foundational to Latin literature and rhetoric.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theriaca"], "contexts": ["work by Nicander on venemous beasts and antidotes"]}, {"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["Theriaca", "Theriacas", "Therlaca", "Theríaca"], "contexts": ["medicine taken daily by Marco Antonio", "principal medicine against poison"]}, {"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 8, "variants": ["theriaca", "Thcriaca", "Theria", "Theriaca"], "contexts": ["compound medicine", "medicinal preparation for which calcite is burned"]}, {"entity_id": "theriac", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "theriac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["theriac"], "contexts": ["remedy mixed with oximel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Theriaca", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "theriaca", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "theriaca", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 309, "stable_key": "clu_c4bdd35a3f6a41ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Theriac", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The text mentions 'Theriaca' as a compound medicine, and references Nicander's work on antidotes. This likely refers to Theriaca Andromachi, a complex polypharmaceutical preparation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1546149", "wikidata_description": "medieval medical concoction", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theriac", "wikipedia_extract": "Theriac or theriaca is a medical concoction originally labelled by the Greeks in the 1st century AD and widely adopted in the ancient world as far away as Persia, China and India via the trading links of the Silk Route. It was an alexipharmic, or antidote for a variety of poisons and diseases. It was also considered a panacea, a term for which it could be used interchangeably: in the 16th century Adam Lonicer wrote that garlic was the rustic's theriac or Heal-All. The word theriac comes from the Greek term θηριακή (thēriakē), a feminine adjective signifying \"pertaining to animals\", from θηρίον (thērion), \"wild animal, beast\". The ancient bestiaries included information—often fanciful—about dangerous beasts and their bites. When cane sugar was an exotic Eastern commodity, the English recommended the sugar-based treacle as an antidote against poison, originally applied as a salve."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "triaga", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1961, "target_cluster_name": "triaga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Directly provided as an alternative name for Theriac.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Directly provided as an alternative name for Theriac.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bode", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2474, "target_cluster_name": "Bode", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "na mão, e lhe asopram pêra ver se lhe sai o Da pedra be^ar 233 vento; porque estas tem elles por contrafeitas. Chamase esta pedra pa^ar, do pa^am (bode así chamado), e asi quando ▼os cá pedem alguma m", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for pa^ar/locomium in some contexts", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "remedios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1584, "target_cluster_name": "remedios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Theriac is a complex historical compound medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Theriac is a complex historical compound medicine.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Longitude"], "contexts": ["angular distance east or west of prime meridian"]}, {"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["longitude"], "contexts": ["geographic divisions affecting temperature", "angular distance east or west of prime meridian"]}, {"entity_id": "longitudes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "longitudes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["longitudes", "latitudes", "longitude"], "contexts": ["measurement of east-west position", "measurement of north-south position"]}, {"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 16, "variants": ["Longitude", "longitude"], "contexts": ["celestial coordinate", "angular distance east or west of ecliptic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "longitude", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "longitude", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "longitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "longitudes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 310, "stable_key": "clu_31e5c2012249eeee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Longitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q36477", "wikidata_description": "geographic coordinate that specifies the east-west position of a point on the Earth's surface", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longitude", "wikipedia_extract": "Longitude (, AU and UK also ) is a geographic coordinate that specifies the east-west position of a point on the surface of the Earth, or another celestial body. It is an angular measurement, usually expressed in degrees and denoted by the Greek letter lambda (λ). Meridians are imaginary semicircular lines running from pole to pole that connect points with the same longitude. The prime meridian defines 0° longitude; by convention the International Reference Meridian for the Earth passes near the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, south-east London on the island of Great Britain. Positive longitudes are east of the prime meridian, and negative ones are west. Because of the Earth's rotation, there is a close connection between longitude and time measurement."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Celestial body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3227, "target_cluster_name": "celestial body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Longitude is a coordinate used to specify a position on the surface of a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Longitude is a coordinate used to specify a position on the surface of a celestial body."}, {"found_name": "Chronology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2825, "target_cluster_name": "Chronology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both longitude and chronology deal with measurement and position, one spatial and the other temporal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both longitude and chronology deal with measurement and position, one spatial and the other temporal."}, {"found_name": "Degree (angle)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1032, "target_cluster_name": "degré", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Longitude is an angular measurement, typically expressed in degrees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Longitude is an angular measurement, typically expressed in degrees."}, {"found_name": "Earth's rotation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3632, "target_cluster_name": "Earth's rotation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of longitude is intrinsically linked to the Earth's rotation and the establishment of prime meridians.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of longitude is intrinsically linked to the Earth's rotation and the establishment of prime meridians."}, {"found_name": "Figure of the Earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3146, "target_cluster_name": "Figure of Earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Earth's shape (e.g., flattening) affects the precise measurement and interpretation of longitude.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Earth's shape (e.g., flattening) affects the precise measurement and interpretation of longitude."}, {"found_name": "Geography", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1068, "target_cluster_name": "Geography", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Longitude is a fundamental component of geographical coordinates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Longitude is a fundamental component of geographical coordinates."}, {"found_name": "Geometry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2912, "target_cluster_name": "Geometry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The measurement and representation of longitude rely on geometric principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The measurement and representation of longitude rely on geometric principles."}, {"found_name": "Meridian", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 97, "target_cluster_name": "Meridian", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Meridians are the semicircles used to define and measure longitude.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Meridians are the semicircles used to define and measure longitude."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "chest", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Chest", "chest"], "contexts": ["part cleansed of phlegm by electuary", "cleansed of humors by decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "chest", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["chest"], "contexts": ["size increased by breathing in high regions"]}, {"entity_id": "barriga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "barriga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["barriga"], "contexts": ["belly"]}, {"entity_id": "barriga", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "barriga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["barriga", "barrigas das pernas"], "contexts": ["body part", "belly, body region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "chest", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "chest", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "chest", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "barriga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 311, "stable_key": "clu_c65bc54932f8735e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chest", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "description": "The part of the body between the neck and the abdomen; the thorax.", "wikipedia_extract": "The thorax (pl.: thoraces or thoraxes) or chest is a part of the anatomy of mammals and other tetrapod animals located between the neck and the abdomen. In insects, crustaceans, and the extinct trilobites, the thorax is one of the three main divisions of the body, each in turn composed of multiple segments. The human thorax includes the thoracic cavity and the thoracic wall. It contains organs including the heart, lungs, and thymus gland, as well as muscles and various other internal structures. The chest may be affected by many diseases, of which the most common symptom is chest pain. The word thorax comes from the Greek θώραξ thṓrax \"breastplate, cuirass, corslet\" via Latin: thorax."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Abdomen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 755, "target_cluster_name": "Abdomen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest and abdomen are the two primary divisions of the trunk in many animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The chest and abdomen are the two primary divisions of the trunk in many animals."}, {"found_name": "Body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 220, "target_cluster_name": "Body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest is a major part of the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The chest is a major part of the body."}, {"found_name": "Body Part", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 467, "target_cluster_name": "parts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest is a specific body part.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The chest is a specific body part."}, {"found_name": "Anatomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2892, "target_cluster_name": "Anatomia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest is a subject of anatomical study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The chest is a subject of anatomical study."}, {"found_name": "Human Anatomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3628, "target_cluster_name": "humani corporis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest is a subject of human anatomical study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The chest is a subject of human anatomical study."}, {"found_name": "pulmonum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 336, "target_cluster_name": "pulmonum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The lungs are located within the chest cavity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The lungs are located within the chest cavity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "breathing organ", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 521, "target_cluster_name": "breathing organ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The chest cavity houses and protects the primary respiratory organs (lungs).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The chest cavity houses and protects the primary respiratory organs (lungs).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "thorace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1153, "target_cluster_name": "thorace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The thorax is the anatomical region of the chest.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The thorax is the anatomical region of the chest.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ribs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1426, "target_cluster_name": "ribs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ribs form the primary structure of the chest.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ribs form the primary structure of the chest.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "New-York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "new-york", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "New-York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["New-York", "New York"], "contexts": ["state for comparison", "state with small relative population"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["NEW YORK", "New York"], "contexts": ["city of publication", "city where translation was published"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["New York"], "contexts": ["location for hemisphere comparison", "location with magnetic intensity"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New York"], "contexts": ["location of Professor Draper"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 312, "stable_key": "clu_495337c25e2318cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "New York", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city and state in the United States.", "note": "Includes references to both the city and the state, as well as a nearby city (Newark).", "wikipedia_extract": "The geography of New York City is characterized by its coastal position at the meeting of the Hudson River and the Atlantic Ocean in a naturally sheltered harbor. The city's geography, with its scarce availability of land, is a contributing factor in making New York the most densely populated major city in the United States. Environmental issues are chiefly concerned with managing this density, which also explains why New York is among the most energy-efficient and least automobile-dependent cities in the United States. The city's climate is temperate. New York City is located on the coast of the Northeastern United States at the mouth of the Hudson River in southeastern New York state. It is located in the New York–New Jersey Harbor Estuary, the centerpiece of which is the New York Harbor, whose deep waters and sheltered bays helped the city grow in significance as a trading city."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 10, "variants": ["sulphur", "Sulphur"], "contexts": ["flammable part in crystal", "used to corrode steel"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["trace component of aerolite analysis", "chemical element in meteoric masses"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["element, part of sulphuretted hydrogen gas", "interposed dielectric material"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["element in Fibrine composition"]}, {"entity_id": "brimstone", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Brimstone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Brimstone"], "contexts": ["sulfur, used for purification", "ingredient of gunpowder"]}, {"entity_id": "brimstone", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Brimstone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brimstone"], "contexts": ["mineral mixed with juice", "used with Darnel meal to dissolve knots"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 313, "stable_key": "clu_449ff9497b6ed72b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfur", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Brimstone is an archaic term for sulfur.", "wikidata_id": "Q1157552", "wikidata_description": "mineral (as opposed to the chemical element)", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soufre_%28min%C3%A9ral%29"}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "St. Johns Wort", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3754, "target_cluster_name": "ipericaó", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "together do signifie nothing, not only in the dictionary of man, but the subtiler vocabulary of Satan. That there is any power in _Bitumen_, Pitch, or Brimstone, to purifie the air from his uncleannes", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "an alternative name for Hipericon"}, {"found_name": "Acid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1608, "target_cluster_name": "acidos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfuric acid is a specific type of acid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sulfuric acid is a specific type of acid."}, {"found_name": "Alkali", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 863, "target_cluster_name": "alkalies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfuric acid reacts with alkalis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sulfuric acid reacts with alkalis."}, {"found_name": "Elements", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 794, "target_cluster_name": "elements", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfur is a chemical element, and sulfuric acid is a compound made from elements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sulfur is a chemical element, and sulfuric acid is a compound made from elements."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous substances", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2321, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfur can exist in gaseous forms, and sulfuric acid can produce gaseous emissions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sulfur can exist in gaseous forms, and sulfuric acid can produce gaseous emissions."}, {"found_name": "Hydrogen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 731, "target_cluster_name": "Hydrogen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfuric acid is composed of sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sulfuric acid is composed of sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen."}, {"found_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 279, "target_cluster_name": "hydrogène sulfuré", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hydrogen sulfide is a compound containing sulfur, and can be related to the production or decomposition of sulfuric acid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hydrogen sulfide is a compound containing sulfur, and can be related to the production or decomposition of sulfuric acid."}, {"found_name": "Vitriolo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 564, "target_cluster_name": "Vitriolo", "source_book