85,133,000 km2 (32,870,000 sq mi). It covers approximately 17% of Earth's surface and about 24% of its water surface area. During the Age of Discovery, it was known for separating the New World of the Americas (North America and South America) from the Old World of Afro-Eurasia (Africa, Asia, and Europe). Through its separation of Afro-Eurasia from the Americas, the Atlantic Ocean has played a central role in the development of human society, globalization, and the histories of many nations. While the Norse were the first known humans to cross the Atlantic, it was the expedition of Christopher Columbus in 1492 that proved to be the most consequential. Columbus's expedition ushered in an age of exploration and colonization of the Americas by European powers, most notably Portugal, Spain, France, and the United Kingdom."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Méditerranée", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 256, "target_cluster_name": "Méditerranée", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Mediterranean Sea is connected to the Atlantic Ocean via the Strait of Gibraltar.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Mediterranean Sea is connected to the Atlantic Ocean via the Strait of Gibraltar.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arctic Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 891, "target_cluster_name": "Polar Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Atlantic Ocean and the Arctic Ocean are adjacent and historically significant bodies of water, often discussed together in the context of exploration and geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Atlantic Ocean and the Arctic Ocean are adjacent and historically significant bodies of water, often discussed together in the context of exploration and geography."}, {"found_name": "Indian Oc