the fraction numerator) of the rate expresses the corresponding rate of change in the other (dependent) variable. In some cases, it may be regarded as a change to a value, which is caused by a change of a value in respect to another value. For example, acceleration is a change in velocity with respect to time. Temporal rate is a common type of rate, in which the denominator is a time duration (\"per unit of time\"), such as in speed, heart rate, and flux. In fact, often rate is a synonym of rhythm or frequency, a count per second (i.e., hertz); e.g., radio frequencies or sample rates."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "meteoric stones", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1783, "target_cluster_name": "meteoric stones", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "correctly termed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling stars). Their planetary velocity, magnitude, form, observed height. Per", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym of"}, {"found_name": "falling stars", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2314, "target_cluster_name": "falling stars", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "correctly termed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling stars). Their planetary velocity, magnitude, form, observed height. Per", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym of"}, {"found_name": "Acceleration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2252, "target_cluster_name": "acceleration", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity with respect to time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Acceleration is the rate of 