first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "call Space and Time; and so there is a conceivable kind of consciousness which does not contain the truths, commonly called _à priori_, involved in the organization of these forms of relations. But th", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of truth involved in organization"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Wollaston", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "wollaston", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Wollaston", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Mr. Wollaston", "Wollaston"], "contexts": ["observed fossil land-shells in Madeira", "convinced sea residence affects insect colours"]}, {"entity_id": "d''_wollaston", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "D'' Wollaston", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["D'' Wollaston", "M. Wollaston"], "contexts": ["scientist associated with a property", "physicist consulted on phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "wollaston", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Wollaston", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wollaston"], "contexts": ["scientist discussing atmosphere limit"]}, {"entity_id": "wollaston", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Wollaston", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Wollaston"], "contexts": ["discoverer of photographic rays", "discovered dark lines in spectrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Wollaston", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "D'' Wollaston", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Wollaston", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Wollaston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Wollaston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wollaston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "D'' Wollaston", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Wollaston", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "D'' Wollaston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wollaston", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Wollaston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wollaston", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 341, "stable_key": "clu_c4629c27868931e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Hyde Wollaston", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1766, "death_year": 1828, "description": "Chemist and mineralogist known for chemical discoveries.", "wikidata_id": "Q312975", "wikidata_description": "English chemist and physicist (1766–1828)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William%20Hyde%20Wollaston", "wikipedia_extract": "William Hyde Wollaston  (; 6 August 1766 – 22 December 1828) was an English chemist and physicist who is famous for discovering the chemical elements palladium and rhodium. He also developed a way to process platinum ore into malleable ingots, patented the camera lucida, and made contributions in electricity and spectroscopy. He was born in East Dereham in Norfolk, the son of the Francis Wollaston (1737–1815), a noted amateur astronomer, and his wife Althea Hyde. He was one of 17 children, but the family was financially well-off and he enjoyed an intellectually stimulating environment. He was educated privately (and remotely) at Charterhouse School from 1774 to 1778 then studied Sciences at Gonville and Caius College, Cambridge. In 1793 he obtained his doctorate (MD) in medicine from Cambridge University, and was a Fellow of his college from 1787 to 1828."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humphry Davy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1877, "target_cluster_name": "Sir Humphry Davy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent chemists and physicists in the early 19th century, known for their work with elements and chemical processes. Davy was a senior figure and Wollaston was a contemporary who also made significant contributions to chemistry and physics, including the discovery of elements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent chemists and physicists in the early 19th century, known for their work with elements and chemical processes. Davy was a senior figure and Wollaston was a contemporary who also made significant contributions to chemistry and physics, including the discovery of elements."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Norwège", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "norway", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Norway", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Norway"], "contexts": ["Norway in the north", "geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "norwège", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Norwège", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Norwège", "Nonvège"], "contexts": ["country where Tifiârd is located", "country"]}, {"entity_id": "norge", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Norge", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Norge"], "contexts": ["country mentioned in a travelogue"]}, {"entity_id": "norway", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Norway", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Norway"], "contexts": ["Northern hemisphere location", "country in Northern hemisphere"]}, {"entity_id": "norway", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Norway", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Norway"], "contexts": ["country with opposite coasts", "European country mentioned in relation to tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Norway", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Norwège", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Norway", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Norge", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Norway", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Norway", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Norwège", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Norwège", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Norge", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Norge", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Norway", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Norway", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 342, "stable_key": "clu_6c1b17363b16ece0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Norway", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Norway", "wikidata_id": "Q184224", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown%20rat", "wikipedia_extract": "The brown rat (Rattus norvegicus), also known as the common rat, street rat, sewer rat, wharf rat, Hanover rat, Norway rat and Norwegian rat, is a widespread, common species of rat. One of the largest muroids, it is a brown or grey rodent with a body length of up to 28 cm (11 in) long, and a tail slightly shorter than that. It weighs between 140 and 500 g (4.9 and 17.6 oz). Thought to have originated in northern China and neighbouring areas, it has now spread to all continents except Antarctica, and is the dominant rat in Europe and much of North America, having become naturalised across the world. With rare exceptions, the brown rat lives wherever humans live, particularly in urban areas. They are omnivorous, reproduce rapidly, and can be a vector for several human diseases."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "rat", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3344, "target_cluster_name": "rat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norwège is the French spelling of Norway.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Norwège is the French spelling of Norway.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Scandinave", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 810, "target_cluster_name": "Scandinave", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norway is a core constituent country of Scandinavia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Norway is a core constituent country of Scandinavia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Spitzbergen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2055, "target_cluster_name": "Spitzbergen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Svalbard is part of Norway.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Svalbard is part of Norway.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "franklin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Franklin", "Franzini", "Franzinl"], "contexts": ["captain who made discoveries", "captain who made observations"]}, {"entity_id": "franklin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franklin"], "contexts": ["scientist associated with lightning"]}, {"entity_id": "franklin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Franklin"], "contexts": ["scientist whose ideas were germinated by Halley's", "observed clouds near American north pole"]}, {"entity_id": "john_franklin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "John Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["John Franklin"], "contexts": ["author of 'Narrative of a Journey'"]}, {"entity_id": "franklin", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Franklin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Franklin"], "contexts": ["snatched thunderbolt from heaven", "honored with a medal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "John Franklin", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "John Franklin", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Franklin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "John Franklin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Franklin", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 343, "stable_key": "clu_2cf3c3e8d3cb3b35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Benjamin Franklin", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1706, "death_year": 1790, "description": "Polymath and Founding Father known for electricity experiments.", "note": "While 'John Franklin' is mentioned, the context of lightning points strongly to Benjamin Franklin. The term 'Franklin' alone often refers to him in scientific contexts of the period.", "wikidata_id": "Q34969", "wikidata_description": "American polymath and statesman (1706–1790)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin%20Franklin", "wikipedia_extract": "Benjamin Franklin (January 17, 1706 [O.S. January 6, 1705] – April 17, 1790) was an American polymath: a writer, scientist, inventor, statesman, diplomat, printer, publisher and political philosopher. Among the most influential intellectuals of his time, Franklin was one of the Founding Fathers of the United States; a drafter and signer of the Declaration of Independence; and the first postmaster general. Born in the Province of Massachusetts Bay, Franklin became a successful newspaper editor and printer in Philadelphia, the leading city in the colonies, publishing The Pennsylvania Gazette at age 23. He became wealthy publishing this and Poor Richard's Almanack, which he wrote under the pseudonym \"Richard Saunders\". After 1767, he was associated with the Pennsylvania Chronicle, a newspaper known for its revolutionary sentiments and criticisms of the policies of the British Parliament and the Crown."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lavoisier", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 475, "target_cluster_name": "Lavoisier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent scientists of the Enlightenment era, contributing to different but related fields (chemistry and electricity/physics) and engaging with similar scientific methodologies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent scientists of the Enlightenment era, contributing to different but related fields (chemistry and electricity/physics) and engaging with similar scientific methodologies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Faraday", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 248, "target_cluster_name": "Faraday", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Franklin's early experiments with electricity laid groundwork for later discoveries in the field, including those of Faraday.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Franklin's early experiments with electricity laid groundwork for later discoveries in the field, including those of Faraday.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fahrenheit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1629, "target_cluster_name": "Fahrenheit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Benjamin Franklin conducted significant experiments with electricity and is known for his contributions to meteorology, including work related to temperature scales. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit was the inventor of the first practical thermometer and the Fahrenheit scale.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Benjamin Franklin conducted significant experiments with electricity and is known for his contributions to meteorology, including work related to temperature scales. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit was the inventor of the first practical thermometer and the Fahrenheit scale."}, {"found_name": "physiciens", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 692, "target_cluster_name": "physiciens", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Franklin was a polymath who made significant contributions to physics, particularly in his studies of electricity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Franklin was a polymath who made significant contributions to physics, particularly in his studies of electricity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Barro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 10, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "barro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "barro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["barro"], "contexts": ["moistened clay"]}, {"entity_id": "barro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "barro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["barro"], "contexts": ["mud", "clay material