": "Cambridge", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cambridge", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cambridge", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cambridge", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cambridge", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cambridge", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cambridge", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 352, "stable_key": "clu_71e61914ce24c548", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Cambridge (  KAYM-brij) is a city and non-metropolitan district in the county of Cambridgeshire, England. It is the county town of Cambridgeshire and is located on the River Cam, 55 miles (89 km) north of London. As of the 2021 United Kingdom census, the population of the City of Cambridge was 145,700; the population of the wider built-up area (which extends outside the city council area) was 181,137. There is archaeological evidence of settlement in the area as early as the Bronze Age, and Cambridge became an important trading centre during the Roman and Viking eras. The first town charters were granted in the 12th century, although modern city status was not officially conferred until 1951. The city is well known as the home of the University of Cambridge, which was founded in 1209 and consistently ranks among the best universities in the world."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Oxford", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 410, "target_cluster_name": "Oxford", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cambridge is a city in England, like Oxford, and is also home to a major university, historically a rival to Oxford.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cambridge is a city in England, like Oxford, and is also home to a major university, historically a rival to Oxford.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "vision", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "vision", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "vision", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 13, "variants": ["vision"], "contexts": ["the faculty or power of seeing", "sense of sight"]}, {"entity_id": "visão", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "visão", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["visão"], "contexts": ["sight"]}, {"entity_id": "vision", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vision", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vision"], "contexts": ["the sense of sight", "the faculty or act of seeing"]}, {"entity_id": "vision", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vision", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vision"], "contexts": ["the faculty or power of seeing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "visão", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "visão", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "visão", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 353, "stable_key": "clu_f483f1c0235035bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vision", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q162668", "wikidata_description": "ability to interpret the surrounding environment using light in the visible spectrum", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual%20perception", "wikipedia_extract": "Visual perception is the ability to detect light and use it to form an image of the surrounding environment. Photodetection without image formation is classified as light sensing. In most vertebrates, visual perception can be enabled by photopic vision (daytime vision) or scotopic vision (night vision), with most vertebrates having both. Visual perception detects light (photons) in the visible spectrum reflected by objects in the environment or emitted by light sources. The visible range of light is defined by what is readily perceptible to humans, though the visual perception of non-humans often extends beyond the visual spectrum. The resulting perception is also known as vision, sight, or eyesight (adjectives visual, optical, and ocular, respectively)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Attention", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 198, "target_cluster_name": "attention", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vision is often directed by attention, and attention can be focused on visual stimuli.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vision is often directed by attention, and attention can be focused on visual stimuli."}, {"found_name": "Perception", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1513, "target_cluster_name": "perception", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vision is a primary mode of sensory perception, allowing for the detection and interpretation of light and images.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vision is a primary mode of sensory perception, allowing for the detection and interpretation of light and images."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Pythagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher with allegorical precepts", "philosopher whose doctrine was perverted"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pythagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher", "first used 'Cosmos' for universe order"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pythagoras", "Pi-tágoras"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher", "ancient philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "pitagora", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Pitagora", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pitagora"], "contexts": ["philosopher, transmitted principles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 354, "stable_key": "clu_59ac505d087e06b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pythagoras", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -570, "death_year": -495, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher and mathematician", "wikidata_id": "Q10261", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher and mathematician (c. 570 – c. 495 BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagoras", "wikipedia_extract": "Pythagoras of Samos (Ancient Greek: Πυθαγόρας; c. 570 – c. 495 BC) was an ancient Ionian Greek philosopher, polymath, and the eponymous founder of Pythagoreanism. His political and religious teachings were well known in Magna Graecia and influenced the philosophies of Plato, Aristotle, and, through them, Western philosophy. Modern scholars disagree regarding Pythagoras's education and influences, but most agree that he travelled to Croton in southern Italy around 530 BC, where he founded a school in which initiates were allegedly sworn to secrecy and lived a communal, ascetic lifestyle. In antiquity, Pythagoras was credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Plato", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 113, "target_cluster_name": "Plato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pythagorean philosophy influenced Plato's ideas, particularly concerning mathematics and the soul.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pythagorean philosophy influenced Plato's ideas, particularly concerning mathematics and the soul.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pythagoras influenced Aristotle's philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pythagoras influenced Aristotle's philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Anaxagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1057, "target_cluster_name": "Anaxagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers."}, {"found_name": "Anaximenes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4321, "target_cluster_name": "Anaximenes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers."}, {"found_name": "Archimedes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_c