ng coal-mining waste dumps. Aluminium sulfate is rarely, if ever, encountered as the anhydrous salt. It forms a number of different hydrates, of which the hexadecahydrate Al2(SO4)3·16H2O and octadecahydrate Al2(SO4)3·18H2O are the most common. The heptadecahydrate, whose formula can be written as [Al(H2O)6]2(SO4)3·5H2O, occurs naturally as the mineral alunogen."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bórax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1311, "target_cluster_name": "bórax", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are mineral salts used in historical medicine and industry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are mineral salts used in historical medicine and industry.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1362, "target_cluster_name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alum is a double sulfate salt, often containing aluminum and potassium. Both alum and magnesium sulfate are salts with sulfate anions and are used in similar applications like dyeing and medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alum is a double sulfate salt, often containing aluminum and potassium. Both alum and magnesium sulfate are salts with sulfate anions and are used in similar applications like dyeing and medicine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pedras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1494, "target_cluster_name": "pedras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alum is a mineral salt, and as such, it is a specific type of mineral substance that could be found within or associated with geological formations, thus related to 'stones'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alum is a mineral salt, and as such, it is a specific type of mineral substance that could be found within or associated with geological formations, thus related to 'stones'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Folio", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "flax", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Flax", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Flax"], "contexts": ["plant used for charcoal in gunpowder"]}, {"entity_id": "leaf", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Leaf", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Leaf"], "contexts": ["part of a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "folio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Folio", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Folio", "Folio indo", "Folio j", "Folio vero", "folio"], "contexts": ["leaf for syrup", "leaf ingredient in a preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "leaf", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "leaf", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["leaf"], "contexts": ["part of a plant", "part of a plant affected by weight"]}, {"entity_id": "foliage", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "foliage", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["foliage"], "contexts": ["mixed mass on branches"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Flax", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Folio", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Leaf", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "leaf", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Leaf", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Folio", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 358, "stable_key": "clu_14f3b928e73ed1c0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leaf", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "The part of a plant attached to a stem or branch.", "wikipedia_extract": "A leaf (pl.: leaves) is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata (pores that intake and output gases), the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "folhas", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 195, "target_cluster_name": "folhas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for 'leaves', a direct translation of the entity 'Leaf'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Portuguese for 'leaves', a direct translation of the entity 'Leaf'."}, {"found_name": "Bark", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1836, "target_cluster_name": "Bark", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bark is a part of a plant, distinct from leaves but a major component of woody plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bark is a part of a plant, distinct from leaves but a major component of woody plants."}, {"found_name": "Branches", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 806, "target_cluster_name": "Branches", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Branches are structural components of plants from which leaves grow.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Branches are structural components of plants from which leaves grow."}, {"found_name": "Buds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3309, "target_cluster_name": "Buds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buds are immature leaves, often developing into leaves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Buds are immature leaves, often developing into leaves."}, {"found_name": "Flower", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 150, "target_cluster_name": "flower", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Flowers are reproductive structures of plants, often associated with leaves and stems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Flowers are reproductive structures of plants, often associated with leaves and stems."}, {"found_name": "Root", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 536, "target_cluster_name": "Root", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Roots are the underground part of a plant, contrasting with the above-ground leaves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Roo