", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q370597", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indo-Pacific%20bottlenose%20dolphin", "wikipedia_extract": "The Indo-Pacific bottlenose dolphin (Tursiops aduncus) is a species of bottlenose dolphin. This dolphin grows to 2.6 m (8.5 ft) long, and weighs up to 230 kg (510 lb). It lives in the waters around India, northern Australia, South China, the Red Sea, and the eastern coast of Africa. Its back is dark grey and its belly is lighter grey or nearly white with grey spots. The Indo-Pacific bottlenose dolphin is generally smaller than the common bottlenose dolphin, has a proportionately longer rostrum, and has spots on its belly and lower sides. It also has more teeth than the common bottlenose dolphin — 23 to 29 teeth on each side of each jaw compared to 21 to 24 for the common bottlenose dolphin."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mer", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 921, "target_cluster_name": "mer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Indian Ocean is a specific sea, directly related to the general concept of 'sea'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Indian Ocean is a specific sea, directly related to the general concept of 'sea'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Atlantic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 318, "target_cluster_name": "Atlantic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Atlantic Ocean and the Indian Ocean are two of the major oceanic divisions of the world, and their exploration and trade routes were often interconnected.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Atlantic Ocean and the Indian Ocean are two of the major oceanic divisions of the world, and their exploration and trade routes were often interconnected.", "is_reverse"