ransition metal."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 440, "target_cluster_name": "Brass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brass is an alloy primarily composed of copper and zinc, and historically, lead was sometimes added to brass to improve its workability.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Brass is an alloy primarily composed of copper and zinc, and historically, lead was sometimes added to brass to improve its workability."}, {"found_name": "Copper", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 274, "target_cluster_name": "copper", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lead and copper are both heavy metals that were extensively used in antiquity and the pre-modern period for various applications, including metallurgy and plumbing. They were often discussed together in texts concerning metals and their properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lead and copper are both heavy metals that were extensively used in antiquity and the pre-modern period for various applications, including metallurgy and plumbing. They were often discussed together in texts concerning metals and their properties."}, {"found_name": "Gold", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1539, "target_cluster_name": "ouro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lead was considered a base metal in alchemy, and a primary goal was its transmutation into gold, a more noble metal. This alchemical relationship is frequently discussed in pre-modern texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lead was considered a base metal in alchemy, and a primary goal was its transmutation into gold, a more noble metal. This alchemical relationship is frequently discussed in pre-modern texts."}, {"found_name": "Litharge", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 775, "target_cluster_name": "Lithargyro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Litharge is a lead monoxide, a specific compound derived from lead, and thus intrinsically related to the element itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Litharge is a lead monoxide, a specific compound derived from lead, and thus intrinsically related to the element itself."}, {"found_name": "Silver", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 667, "target_cluster_name": "Silver", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Silver and lead are often found together in ores and were historically processed together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Silver and lead are often found together in ores and were historically processed together.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lithargyro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 775, "target_cluster_name": "Lithargyro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Litharge is a natural mineral form of lead(II) oxide, PbO. It is derived from the oxidation of lead ores.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Litharge is a natural mineral form of lead(II) oxide, PbO. It is derived from the oxidation of lead ores.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Tin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 939, "target_cluster_name": "Tin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lead is another common metal that, like tin, has been used for centuries in various applications, including plumbing and metallurgy, and can be alloyed with tin (e.g., pewter).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lead is another common metal that, like tin, has been used for centuries in various applications, including plumbing and metallurgy, and can be alloyed with tin (e.g., pewter).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Estanho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2242, "target_cluster_name": "Estanho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tin and lead are both common soft metals used in historical applications like pewter and solders.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tin and lead are both common soft metals used in historical applications like pewter and solders.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "quadrupedes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "quadrupedes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Quadrupedes", "quadrupedes"], "contexts": ["animals with four legs", "four-legged animals with speech advantage"]}, {"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed mammals", "animals with four feet"]}, {"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed mammals", "animals distributed geographically"]}, {"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed animals", "animals with four feet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "quadrupedes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quadrupeds", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quadrupeds", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.646}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Quadrupeds", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.646}], "id": 369, "stable_key": "clu_39b9fb6f8654f07e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quadruped", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The terms 'quadrupedes', 'quadrumana', and 'quadrupeds' all refer to four-limbed animals, with 'quadrumana' being a specific subgroup (primates with grasping feet).", "wikipedia_extract": "A tripod stance is a behaviour in which quadruped animals rear up on their hind legs and use their tail to support this position. Several animals use this behaviour to improve observation or surveillance, and during feeding, grooming, thermoregulation, or fighting. The common dwarf mongoose (Helogale parvula) adopts a tripod stance when being vigilant for predators. In a similar mammal, the thirteen-lined ground squirrel (Ictidomys tridecemlineatus), vigilance behaviour includes four postures: (1) quadrupedal alert (all four feet on the ground with head above the horizontal); (2) semiupright alert (on hind feet with a distinctive slouch); (3) upright alert (on hind feet with back straight and mostly perpendicular to the ground); (4) extended upright alert (similar to upright alert except that the squirrel extends its hind legs, (see image of meerkats). In meerkats (Suricata suricatta), the tripod stance may be adopted relatively briefly during foraging, in which case it is termed guarding behaviour, or for substantially longer periods when the animal is not foraging, in which case it is termed sentinel behaviour. Macropods can stand erect on their hind legs, supported by their tail as the third leg of the tripod.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripod_stance"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mammals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 266, "target_cluster_name": "mammals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Many historical texts would have considered quadrupeds to be a primary characteristic of mammals, though this is not exclusive.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Many historical texts would have considered quadrupeds to be a primary characteristic of mammals, though this is not exclusive.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quadrupeds are a type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quadrupeds are a type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Vertebrata", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 702, "target_cluster_name": "vertebrata", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quadrupeds are a major group within Vertebrata.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quadrupeds are a major group within Vertebrata."}, {"found_name": "Reptiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 122, "target_cluster_name": "reptiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Many reptiles are quadrupeds and exhibit quadrupedal locomotion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Many reptiles are quadrupeds and exhibit quadrupedal locomotion."}, {"found_name": "Wolf", "link_type