 "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mist is essentially a cloud at ground level, formed by the same process of water vapor condensation.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Law"], "contexts": ["system of rules enforced by society", "religious or legal statutes"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Law"], "contexts": ["contrasted with Gospel"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["law"], "contexts": ["abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy", "relationship between sensation and stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Law", "law"], "contexts": ["constant course of procedure in nature", "constant course of procedure"]}, {"entity_id": "lois_immuables", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lois immuables", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lois immuables"], "contexts": ["unchangeable natural laws"]}, {"entity_id": "unchangeable_laws", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "unchangeable laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["unchangeable laws"], "contexts": ["laws that do not change over time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Law", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 377, "stable_key": "clu_824d3a365d77f39b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Law of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'constant course of procedure in nature' points to natural law, rather than legal statutes.", "wikidata_id": "Q29524", "wikidata_description": "system of law that is purportedly determined by nature, and is thus universal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_law", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Empirical law", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4194, "target_cluster_name": "empirical law", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'law' in 'law of nature' can refer to an empirical generalization derived from observation, similar to how 'empirical law' is used. Both terms describe observed regularities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'law' in 'law of nature' can refer to an empirical generalization derived from observation, similar to how 'empirical law' is used. Both terms describe observed regularities."}, {"found_name": "General laws", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3235, "target_cluster_name": "Laws in general", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The phrase 'law of nature' is a specific instance of a broader concept of 'general laws' that govern phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The phrase 'law of nature' is a specific instance of a broader concept of 'general laws' that govern phenomena."}, {"found_name": "First Cause", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2659, "target_cluster_name": "First Cause", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of a 'law of nature' often implies an underlying order or principle, which can be linked to philosophical discussions of a 'First Cause' or ultimate origin of these laws.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of a 'law of nature' often implies an underlying order or principle, which can be linked to philosophical discussions of a 'First Cause' or ultimate origin of these laws."}, {"found_name": "Latina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1122, "target_cluster_name": "Latina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latin was the language of Roman law, and legal terminology and concepts derived from Latin persisted in many European legal systems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Latin was the language of Roman law, and legal terminology and concepts derived from Latin persisted in many European legal systems.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "testiculos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "testicles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "testicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["testicles", "Testicles"], "contexts": ["male reproductive organs"]}, {"entity_id": "testicles", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Testicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Testicles"], "contexts": ["comparison for seed shape", "male reproductive organs"]}, {"entity_id": "testículos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "testículos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["testículos"], "contexts": ["body part"]}, {"entity_id": "testiculos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "testiculos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["testiculos", "testiculo"], "contexts": ["testicles", "body part of goat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Testicles", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "testículos", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Testicles", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "testículos", "similarity": 0.97}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Testicles", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "testículos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 378, "stable_key": "clu_1864f7b1a25ffeab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "testicles", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Male reproductive organs.", "wikipedia_extract": "A testicle, also called testis (pl. testes) is the male gonad in all gonochoric animals, including humans, and is homologous to the ovary, which is the female gonad. Its primary functions are the production of sperm and the secretion of androgens, primarily testosterone. The release of testosterone is regulated by luteinizing hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary gland. Sperm production is controlled by follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary gland and by testosterone produced within the gonads. Male humans have two testicles of similar size contained within the scrotum, which is an extension of the abdominal wall. Scrotal asymmetry, in which one testicle extends farther down into the scrotum than the other, is common."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Castoreum", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "sou