nus of æsthetic feeling", "feeling of enjoyment"]}, {"entity_id": "enjoyment", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "enjoyment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["enjoyment"], "contexts": ["degree of pleasure from nature"]}, {"entity_id": "pleasures", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pleasures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pleasures"], "contexts": ["feelings of enjoyment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "pleasure", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "pleasure", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "pleasure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "enjoyment", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "pleasure", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleasures", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pleasure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "enjoyment", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pleasure", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleasures", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "enjoyment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleasures", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 384, "stable_key": "clu_996033f6f4d0407f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pleasure", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q208195", "wikidata_description": "broad class of mental states that humans and other animals experience as positive, enjoyable, or worth seeking", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleasure", "wikipedia_extract": "Pleasure is experience that feels good, that involves the enjoyment of something. It contrasts with pain or suffering, which are forms of feeling bad. It is closely related to value, desire and action: humans and other conscious animals find pleasure enjoyable, positive or worthy of seeking. A great variety of activities may be experienced as pleasurable, like eating, having sex, listening to music or playing games. Pleasure is part of various other mental states such as ecstasy, euphoria and flow. Happiness and well-being are closely related to pleasure but not identical with it."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Affection", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3559, "target_cluster_name": "affection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Affection is a feeling of liking or fondness, which is a form of pleasure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Affection is a feeling of liking or fondness, which is a form of pleasure."}, {"found_name": "Appetite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2030, "target_cluster_name": "Appetite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Appetite is a desire for something, often associated with the pleasure of satisfying that desire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Appetite is a desire for something, often associated with the pleasure of satisfying that desire."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Desire is a longing for something, and the fulfillment of desire is a source of pleasure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Desire is a longing for something, and the fulfillment of desire is a source of pleasure."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions, including pleasure and its opposite, pain, are closely linked to the will and decision-making processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emotions, including pleasure and its opposite, pain, are closely linked to the will and decision-making processes."}, {"found_name": "Evil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3793, "target_cluster_name": "evil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evil is often understood as the opposite of good, and by extension, the opposite of pleasure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evil is often understood as the opposite of good, and by extension, the opposite of pleasure."}, {"found_name": "Feeling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 245, "target_cluster_name": "feeling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pleasure is a type of feeling or sensation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pleasure is a type of feeling or sensation."}, {"found_name": "Ambition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4281, "target_cluster_name": "Ambition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambition is a strong desire for achievement, power, or success, which is often pursued for the pleasure it is expected to bring.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ambition is a strong desire for achievement, power, or success, which is often pursued for the pleasure it is expected to bring."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Angelica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "angelica", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Angelica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Angelica", "Ange∣lica"], "contexts": ["plant with admirable use", "herb with admirable virtues"]}, {"entity_id": "angélica_archangelica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Angélica archangelica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Angélica archangelica"], "contexts": ["plant name", "angelica herb"]}, {"entity_id": "angelica", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "angelica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 1, "variants": ["angelica"], "contexts": ["fragrant root for pounding"]}, {"entity_id": "angelicas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "angelicas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["angelicas"], "contexts": ["flower causing uterine accidents", "plant, angelica"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Angelica", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Angélica archangelica", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Angelica", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "angelica", "similarity": 0.834}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Angelica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "angelicas", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 385, "stable_key": "clu_b238eb518921748d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Angelica", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Angelica archangelica", "family": "Apiaceae", "description": "Aromatic herb with medicinal properties, often used for digestive issues.", "wikipedia_extract": "Angelica archangelica, commonly known as angelica, garden angelica, wild celery, and Norwegian angelica, is a biennial plant from the family Apiaceae, a subspecies of which is cultivated for its sweetly scented edible stems and roots. Like several other species in Apiaceae, its appearance is similar to several poisonous species (Conium, Heracleum, and others), and should not be consumed unless it has been identified with absolute certainty. Synonyms include Archangelica officinalis Hoffm. and Angelica officinalis Moench. During its first year, it grows only leaves, but during its second year, its fluted stem can reach a height of 2.5 meters (just over 8 feet), and the root is used in flavoring preparations. Its leaves consist of numerous small leaflets divided into three principal groups, each of which is again subdivided into three lesser groups. The edges of the leaflets are finely toothed or serrated."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "herbe", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1696, "target_cluster_name": "herbe", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "amongst the Ranters, I never read or heard of such Blasphemy: The Heathens and Papists were bad, and ours wors, the Papists giving Idola∣trous Names to Herbs for their Vertues sake, not for their fair", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Umbellifera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 882, "target_cluster_name": "Umbellifera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Angelica is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Angelica is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Parsnip", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2356, "target_cluster_name": "Parsnip", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are tall herbaceous plants in the Apiaceae family, often found in similar habitats and sometimes confused due to their appearance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are tall herbaceous plants in the Apiaceae family, often found in similar habitats and sometimes confused due to their appearance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Herb of the Holy Ghost", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Names to Herbs for their Vertues sake, not for their fair looks; and therfore some called this an Herb of the Holy Ghost, others more moderate called it Angelica, becaus of its Angelical Vertues, and ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for Angelica"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Outubro", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "october", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "October", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["October"], "contexts": ["month when Misselto berries ripen", "month when berries are ripe"]}, {"entity_id": "octobre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Octobre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Octobre"], "contexts": ["month of observation", "month"]}, {"entity_id": "ottobre", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Ottobre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ottobre"], "contexts": ["month of October", "month of the year"]}, {"entity_id": "outubro", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Outubro", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Outubro"], "contexts": ["month", "month of the year"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "October", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Octobre", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "October", "target_bo