"CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["affinity"], "contexts": ["likeness or similarity", "felt connection or attraction"]}, {"entity_id": "affinity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "affinity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["affinity"], "contexts": ["force of chemical attraction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 388, "stable_key": "clu_3d831919bdc2d04b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "affinity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q382497", "wikidata_description": "automorphism of an affine space", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affine%20transformation", "wikipedia_extract": "In Euclidean geometry, an affine transformation or affinity (from the Latin, affinis, \"connected with\") is a geometric transformation that preserves lines and parallelism, but not necessarily Euclidean distances and angles. More generally, an affine transformation is an automorphism of an affine space (Euclidean spaces are specific affine spaces), that is, a function which maps an affine space onto itself while preserving both the dimension of any affine subspaces (meaning that it sends points to points, lines to lines, planes to planes, and so on) and the ratios of the lengths of parallel line segments. Consequently, sets of parallel affine subspaces remain parallel after an affine transformation. An affine transformation does not necessarily preserve angles between lines or distances between points, though it does preserve ratios of distances between points lying on a straight line. If X is the point set of an affine space, then every affine transformation on X can be represented as the composition of a linear transformation on X and a translation of X. Unlike a purely linear transformation, an affine transformation need not preserve the origin of the affine space."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemical affinity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 727, "target_cluster_name": "chemical affinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While 'affinity' in geometry refers to a specific type of transformation, 'chemical affinity' refers to the tendency of substances to combine, a related but distinct concept within the broader historical understanding of 'affinity' as a force or relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While 'affinity' in geometry refers to a specific type of transformation, 'chemical affinity' refers to the tendency of substances to combine, a related but distinct concept within the broader historical understanding of 'affinity' as a force or relationship."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["location of observation", "city in Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["city in Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["city with mild winter temperature", "city with mean winter temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["location of dedication", "city on isothermal line of 59° Fahrenheit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 389, "stable_key": "clu_859bcad1a3aec5e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Florence", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city in Tuscany, Italy, a center of Renaissance art and commerce.", "note": "Refers to the city and its historical significance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Florence (Italian: Firenze) is the capital and most populous city of the Italian region of Tuscany, with 362,353 inhabitants as of 2025. It is also the capital of the eponymous metropolitan province, which counts 989,460 inhabitants. Florence was a centre of medieval European trade and finance and one of the wealthiest cities of that era. It is considered by many academics to have been the birthplace of the Renaissance, becoming a major artistic, cultural, commercial, political, economic and financial center. During this time, Florence rose to a position of enormous influence in Italy, Europe, and beyond. Its turbulent political history includes periods of rule by the powerful Medici family and numerous religious and republican revolutions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Toscana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2151, "target_cluster_name": "Toscana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Florence is the capital city of Tuscany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Florence is the capital city of Tuscany.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Siena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2833, "target_cluster_name": "Siena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Siena and Florence are historically significant cities in Tuscany, Italy, often rivals and closely linked in regional history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Siena and Florence are historically significant cities in Tuscany, Italy, often rivals and closely linked in regional history.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Tartaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "tartarie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tartarie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tartarie", "Tarentaise"], "contexts": ["region excluding Tibet and Korea", "valley in the French Alps"]}, {"entity_id": "tartary", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tartary", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tartary"], "contexts": ["region with grass plains"]}, {"entity_id": "tartaria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Tartaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Tartaria"], "contexts": ["region of origin for Avicena", "historical region"]}, {"entity_id": "tartary", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tartary", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tartary"], "contexts": ["region with distinct flora", "location where Rhodiola rosea was found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tartarie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tartary", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tartarie", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tartaria", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Tartary", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tartary", "similarity": 0.785}], "id": 390, "stable_key": "clu_eace91dc5dbf8139", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tartary", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region in Central and Northern Asia.", "note": "Refers to a vast historical region, often vaguely defined, in Central and Northern Asia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tartary (Latin: Tartaria; French: Tartarie; German: Tartarei; Russian: Тартария, romanized: Tartariya) or Tatary (Russian: Татария, romanized: Tatariya) was a blanket term used in Western European literature and cartography for a vast part of Asia bounded by the Caspian Sea, the Ural Mountains, the Pacific Ocean, and the northern borders of China, India, and Persia, at a time when this region was largely unknown to European geographers. The active use of the toponym (place name) can be traced from the 13th to the 19th centuries. In European sources, Tartary became the most common name for Central Asia that had no connection with the real polities or ethnic groups of the region; until the 19th century, European knowledge of the area remained extremely scarce and fragmentary. In modern English-speaking tradition, the region formerly known as Tartary is usually called Inner Asia or Central Eurasia. Much of this area consists of arid plains, the main nomadic population of which in the past was engaged in animal husbandry. Ignorance surrounding Tartary's use as a place name has spawned pseudohistoric conspiracy theories including ideas of a \"hidden past\" and \"mud floods\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Persia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 168, "target_cluster_name": "Persia", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "provincia dita XJzbeque, que he parte da Tartaria, que nós chamamos, ou <los Moguoras, como elles chamão qua; bem que Andreas £elunensis chame áquella parte Pérsia, mas isto he largo xnodo tomando Pér", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: larger region containing XJzbeque, and possibly synonymous with a broader Persia", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Chinese Tartary", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3463, "target_cluster_name": "Chinese Tartary", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chinese Tartary is a specific geographical region that falls within the broader, less defined area historically known as Tartary. It represents a sub-region or a more specific designation within the larger 'Tartary' concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chinese Tartary is a specific geographical region that falls within the broader, less defined area historically known as Tartary. It represents a sub-region or a more specific designation within the larger 'Tartary' concept."}, {"found_name": "Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 231, "target_cluster_name": "Asie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tartary was a blanket term used in Western European literature and cartography for a vast part of Asia. Therefore, Asia is the continent within which Tartary was situated.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tartary was a blanket term used in Western European literature and cartography for a vast part of Asia. Therefore, Asia is the continent within which Tartary was situated."}, {"found_name": "xá", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Rumes, porque os Turcos sio da Asia-menor e da província da Natolia, e os Rumes sam de Constantinopla e do seu império I 3a UzBBQus he a provinda de Tartaria, e confina com a China. . 11 9a XÁ quer di", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "meaning king in Persia"}, {"found_name": "xeque", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Rumes, porque os Turcos sio da Asia-menor e da província da Natolia, e os Rumes sam de Constantinopla e do seu império I 3a UzBBQus he a provinda de Tartaria, e confina com a China. . 11 9a XÁ quer di", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "different from 'xa'"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Boodt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bowne", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Bowne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bowne"], "contexts": ["philosopher of metaphysics", "Professor discussing inner construction of thought"]}, {"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bode"], "contexts": ["astronomical yearbook editor", "astronomer associated with Jahrbuch"]}, {"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bode"], "contexts": ["law assumed in computing Neptune’s position"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bowne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bowne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bode", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 391, "stable_key": "clu_f6da1bfc8b95a7f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arnold Geulincx", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1624, "death_year": 1669, "description": "Flemish philosopher associated with Cartesianism and Spinozism.", "note": "The references to 'Boodt' in 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' likely refer to the mineralogist Anselmus Boëtius de Boodt. However, the references to 'Bode' and 'Bowne' in other texts suggest a potential confusion with philosophers like Arnold Geulincx, who also discussed metaphysics. The specific spelling 'Boodt' in the context of a 1563 text points strongly to Boëtius de Boodt. However, given the other references, there might be multiple distinct individuals or significant OCR variability. Given the prompt's emphasis on early modern texts across multiple languages, the 'Boodt' of 'coloquios_da_orta' is most likely Anselmus Boëtius de Boodt. The others ('Bode', 'Bowne') are harder to place definitively without more context, but 'Bode' appearing with Humboldt and Somerville suggests a scientific or geographical association, while 'Bowne' with James might be philosopher Borden Bowne. The mention of 'author of Gemmarum et lapidarum historia' strongly points to Anselmus Boëtius de Boodt. Therefore, this cluster appears to conflate at least two, possibly three, individuals. Focusing on the most concrete identifier within the cluster as presented, and acknowledging the ambiguity.", "wikidata_id": "Q316559", "wikidata_description": "Flemish Cartesian philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold%20Geulincx", "wikipedia_extract": "Arnold Geulincx (Dutch pronunciation: [ˈɑrnɔlt ˈxøːlɪŋks]; 31 January 1624 – November 1669), also known by his pseudonym Philaretus, was a Flemish philosopher, metaphysician, and logician. He was one of the followers of René Descartes who tried to work out more detailed versions of a generally Cartesian philosophy. Samuel Beckett cited Geulincx as a key influence and interlocutor because of Geulincx's emphasis on the powerlessness and ignorance of the human condition. Geulincx was born in Antwerp. He studied philosophy and theology at the University of Leuven and was made professor of philosophy there in 1646. He lost his post in 1658, possibly for religious reasons, or (as has been suggested) a combination of unpopular views and his marriage in that year."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "René Descartes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 875, "target_cluster_name": "Descartes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arnold Geulincx was a prominent follower of René Descartes and developed his philosophical ideas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Arnold Geulincx was a prominent follower of René Descartes and developed his philosophical ideas."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Plater.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "plateau", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Plateau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Plateau"], "contexts": ["studied shades of gray", "determined shade of gray judgments"]}, {"entity_id": "plater_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Plater.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Plater."], "contexts": ["author of a text", "author of tomo 3."]}, {"entity_id": "plateau", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Plateau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plateau"], "contexts": ["performed experiments on accidental colours"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plateau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Plateau", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 392, "stable_key": "clu_6884e1e9abcfa413", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plater", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1581, "death_year": 1650, "description": "Swiss physician and anatomist, son of Felix Platter.", "note": "The reference to 'Plater.' in 'polyanthea_medicinal' and 'Plateario' in 'historia_medicinal_monardes_1574' strongly suggests variations of the name Plater or Platærus, referring to the renowned physician Felix Plater or his son, Plater. The contexts 'studied shades of gray' and 'Platerius in the chapter on Iron' also align with medical and scientific writings. 'Plateau' in James's 'principles_of_psychology' is likely a misattribution or refers to a different, unrelated Plateau.", "wikidata_id": "Q14927887", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plateros", "wikipedia_extract": "Plateros is a genus of net-winged beetles in the family Lycidae and the tribe Platerodini. Species are recorded from the Americas, Africa and Asia, including Sri Lanka, Indochina, Korea and Japan."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fluids"], "contexts": ["liquid substances"]}, {"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["fluids"], "contexts": ["earth's crust components", "state of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "liquid_fluids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "liquid fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquid fluids"], "contexts": ["substances in liquid state"]}, {"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["fluids"], "contexts": ["substances that flow", "transparent medium with uniform properties"]}, {"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "