in animals"]}, {"entity_id": "brown", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Brown", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Brown"], "contexts": ["African traveler"]}, {"entity_id": "brown", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Brown", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["brown", "Brown"], "contexts": ["color after heat application", "found peculiar vegetation in Australia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 0.654}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 0.654}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 0.654}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brown", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brown", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 406, "stable_key": "clu_16d2f0f4bd150be4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Brown", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "African traveler, researcher of brain function, author.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Brown appear in the period. The context of African travel and brain function suggests possible figures like Mungo Park or early neurologists, but without more context, it's difficult to pinpoint.", "wikidata_id": "Q637377", "wikidata_description": "Scottish personal servant and favourite of Queen Victoria (1826–1883)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Brown%20%28servant%29", "wikipedia_extract": "John Brown (8 December 1826 – 27 March 1883) was a Scottish personal attendant and favourite of Queen Victoria for many years after working as a gillie for Prince Albert. He was appreciated by many (including the Queen) for his competence and companionship, and resented by others (most notably her son and heir apparent, the future Edward VII, the rest of the Queen's children, ministers, and the palace staff) for his influence and informal manner. The exact nature of his relationship with Victoria was the subject of great speculation by contemporaries. John Brown was born on 8 December 1826 at Crathienaird, Crathie and Braemar Aberdeenshire, to Margaret Leys and John Brown, and went to work as an outdoor servant (in Scots gillie or ghillie) at Balmoral Castle, which Queen Victoria and Prince Albert leased in February 1848, and purchased outright in November 1851. Brown had several younger brothers and a sister, three of whom also entered the royal service. His brother Archibald Anderson \"Archie\" Brown, 15 years John's junior, eventually became personal valet to Victoria's youngest son, Prince Leopold, Duke of Albany."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "color", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 10, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "color", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "color", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["color"], "contexts": ["perceptual quality", "visual property of light"]}, {"entity_id": "color", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "color", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["color"], "contexts": ["property of the atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "color", "book