"source_name": "color", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "color", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "color", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "colore", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "color", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "colore", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 407, "stable_key": "clu_a7db73f2463d2f31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "color", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1261", "wikidata_description": "state of the United States of America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color", "note": "The context in 'principles_of_psychology_james_1890' and 'relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825' might lean towards pigments or optical phenomena, but 'color' as a general concept covers these.", "wikipedia_extract": "Color (or colour in Commonwealth English; see spelling differences) is the visual perception based on the electromagnetic spectrum. Though color is not an inherent property of matter, color perception is related to an object's light absorption, emission, reflection and transmission. For most humans, visible wavelengths of light are the ones perceived in the visible light spectrum, with three types of cone cells (trichromacy). Other animals may have a different number of cone cell types or have eyes sensitive to different wavelengths, such as bees that can distinguish ultraviolet, and thus have a different color sensitivity range. Animal perception of color originates from different light wavelength or spectral sensitivity in cone cell types, which is then processed by the brain. Colors have perceived properties such as hue, colorfulness, and lightness."}, "member_count": 16, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Absorption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2054, "target_cluster_name": "absorption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Color perception is related to an object's light absorption.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Color perception is related to an object's light absorption."}, {"found_name": "Electrical phenomena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3624, "target_cluster_name": "electrical phenomena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Color is a visual perception based on the electromagnetic spectrum, which is related to electrical phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Color is a visual perception based on the electromagnetic spectrum, which is related to electrical phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Chemical properties", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1251, "target_cluster_name": "chemical properties", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Color is a visual perception related to how objects interact with light, which is tied to their chemical properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Color is a visual perception related to how objects interact with light, which is tied to their chemical properties."}, {"found_name": "Dog-wood", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "blackish colour, with many white spots theron: but the inner Bark next unto the Wood is yellow, which being chew∣ed will turn the Spittle neer unto a Saffron colour. The Leaves are somwhat like those ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "called in Sussex"}]}, {"canonical_name": "action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "actions", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epi