 Ukraine to the east; Slovakia and the Czech Republic to the south; and Germany to the west. The territory has a varied landscape, diverse ecosystems, and a temperate climate. Poland is composed of sixteen voivodeships and is the fifth most populous member state of the European Union (EU), with over 38 million people, and the fifth largest EU country by land area, covering 312,696 km2 (120,733 sq mi). The capital and largest city is Warsaw; other major cities include Kraków, Wrocław, Łódź, Poznań, and Gdańsk."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Genève", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "genève", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Genève", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Genève"], "contexts": ["city in Switzerland", "location for barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "geneva", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Geneva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Geneva"], "contexts": ["location with low meteor count", "city near Pregny"]}, {"entity_id": "geneve", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Geneve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geneve"], "contexts": ["city where Michael de Normandie was consul"]}, {"entity_id": "geneva", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Geneva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geneva"], "contexts": ["location of water boring experiment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Genève", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Genève", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geneve", "similarity": 0.865}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Genève", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Geneva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geneve", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Geneva", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Geneve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 419, "stable_key": "clu_79a40e5c269f501a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genoa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major port city in Italy, historically significant for trade and exploration.", "note": "Refers to the city of Genoa, also known historically as Gênes or Geneve in some texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Genoa ( JEN-oh-ə; Italian: Genova [ˈdʒɛːnova] ; Ligurian: Zêna [ˈzeːna]) is the sixth-largest city in Italy and the capital of the Italian region of Liguria. As of 2025, 565,301 people live within the city's administrative limits. While its metropolitan city has 818,629 inhabitants, more than 1.5 million people live in the wider metropolitan area stretching along the Italian Riviera. On the Gulf of Genoa in the Ligurian Sea, Genoa has historically been one of the most important ports on the Mediterranean: it is the busiest port in Italy and in the Mediterranean Sea and twelfth-busiest in the European Union. Genoa was the capital of one of the most powerful maritime republics for over seven centuries, from the 11th century to 1797. Particularly from the 12th century to the 15th century, the city played a leading role in the history of commerce and trade in Europe, becoming one of the largest naval powers of the continent and considered among the wealthiest cities in the world."}, "cross_references": [{"f