get_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "pines", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 425, "stable_key": "clu_89c8a03499080453", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pine", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pinus", "family": "Pinaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q1258277", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinellia%20ternata", "note": "'pine-apple' in Darwin's *Origin of Species* is an archaic term for the fruit of the pineapple plant, but the presence of 'pines' and 'pineta' referring to coniferous trees in other contexts makes 'Pine' the most encompassing modern referent.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pinellia ternata (Chinese: 半夏, Japanese: カラスビシャク), crow-dipper, is a plant that is native to China, Japan, and Korea. However, it also grows as an invasive weed in parts of Europe (Austria, Germany) and in North America (California, Ontario, the northeastern United States). The leaves are trifoliate, and the flowers are of the spathe and spadix form that is typical of plants in the family Araceae. The plant spreads by rhizomes, and there are also small bulblets (also known as bulbils) at the base of each leaf. Flowers are borne in spring. This plant is toxic in raw form and must be processed."}, "member_count": 11, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3028, "target_cluster_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pinus occidentalis is a species within the genus Pinus, and the entity 'Pine' (historical name: pines) likely refers to the genus Pinus or common pine trees. This represents a specific taxonomic relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pinus occidentalis is a species within the genus Pinus, and the entity 'Pine' (historical name: pines) likely refers to the genus Pinus or common pine trees. This