ted", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tin is a component in the alloy brass, which is made with copper.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Porcellana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "porcellane", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Porcellane", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porcellane"], "contexts": ["type of dish"]}, {"entity_id": "porcelaines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "porcelaines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["porcelaines", "porcelaine"], "contexts": ["ceramic goods from China", "porcelain"]}, {"entity_id": "porcellana", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Porcellana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["porcellana", "Porcellana"], "contexts": ["herb with viscous juice", "herb that is viscous with humidity"]}, {"entity_id": "porcelain", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "porcelain", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["porcelain"], "contexts": ["ceramic material for manufacture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "porcelaines", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Porcellana", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porcelaines", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Porcellana", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porcelaines", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Porcellana", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 441, "stable_key": "clu_2e16bf65067c0939", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porcelain", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A hard, white, ceramic material, often decorated, originating from China.", "note": "Also refers to a plant with a viscous juice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Porcelain (), also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 °C (2,200 and 2,600 °F). The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware. The manufacturing process used for porcelain is similar to that used for earthenware and stoneware, the two other main types of pottery, although it can be more challenging to produce. It has usually been regarded as the most prestigious type of pottery due to its delicacy, strength, and high degree of whiteness. It is frequently both glazed and decorated."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "M. James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["cited observer of hypnotic trance"]}, {"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["measured for Great Survey, determined earth's density", "measurements in General Survey of Great Britain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.684}], "id": 442, "stable_key": "clu_d05b62dcdb8efae3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William James", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1842, "death_year": 1910, "description": "American philosopher and psychologist, influential in functionalism.", "note": "The mention of 'M. James' in Humboldt's work likely refers to William James, given the context of scientific discussions and his later prominence. 'Saint associated with a plant name' is a less likely context for William James himself, but could refer to a plant named after a saint that he discussed.", "wikidata_id": "Q125249", "wikidata_description": "American philosopher, psychologist, and pragmatist (1842–1910)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William%20James", "wikipedia_extract": "William James (January 11, 1842 – August 26, 1910) was an American philosopher and psychologist. The first educator to offer a psychology course in the United States, he is considered to be one of the leading thinkers of the late 19th century, one of the most influential philosophers and is often dubbed the \"father of American psychology.\" A Review of General Psychology analysis, published in 2002, ranked James as the 14th most eminent psychologist of the 20th century. A survey published in American Psychologist in 1991 ranked James's reputation in second place, after Wilhelm Wundt, who is widely regarded as the founder of experimental psychology. In an empirical study by Haggbloom et al. using six criteria such as citations and recognition, James was found to be the 14th most eminent psychologist of the 20th century. James wrote widely on many topics, including epistemology, education, metaphysics, psychology, religion, and mysticism."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Granville Stanley Hall", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 922, "target_cluster_name": "Hall", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Granville Stanley Hall was a prominent psychologist and educator in the United States, and a contemporary and colleague of William James. They were both key figures in the development of psychology as an academic discipline in America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Granville Stanley Hall was a prominent psychologist and educator in the United States, and a contemporary and colleague of William James. They were both key figures in the development of psychology as an academic discipline in America."}, {"found_name": "Herbert Spencer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3526, "target_cluster_name": "Herbert Spencer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Herbert Spencer was a highly influential English philosopher and sociologist whose theories of evolution and social Darwinism had a significant impact on late 19th-century thought, including that of William James.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Herbert Spencer was a highly influential English philosopher and sociologist whose theories of evolution and social Darwinism had a significant impact on late 19th-century thought, including that of William James."}, {"found_name": "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2302, "target_cluster_name": "Hegel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was a German philosopher whose work profoundly influenced subsequent philosophical thought, including the development of pragmatism and idealism, areas with which William James engaged deeply.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was a German philosopher whose work profoundly influenced subsequent philosophical thought, including the development of pragmatism and idealism, areas with which William James engaged deeply."}]}, {"canonical_name": "temperate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "temperate", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "temperate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["temperate", "temper"], "contexts": ["quality of the herb", "quality of pansies"]}, {"entity_id": "templada", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "templada", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["templada"], "contexts": ["temperate land in New Granada"]}, {"entity_id": "tempérée", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tempérée", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["tempérée"], "contexts": ["relating to moderate temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "templada", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "templada", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["templada", "templar"], "contexts": ["considered temperate", "effect of Azogue"]}, {"entity_id": "temperata", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "temperata", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["temperata"], "contexts": ["temperate quality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "temperate", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "tempérée", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "temperate", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "temperata", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "templada", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "templada", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "templada", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "temperata", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tempérée", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "temperata", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "templada", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "temperata", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 443, "stable_key": "clu_ce45c6d3c7187773", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Temperate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a moderate climate or quality, distinct from extreme heat or cold.", "wikidata_id": "Q167466", "wikidata_description": "main climate class", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temperate%20climate", "wikipedia_extract": "In geography, the temperate climates of Earth occur in the middle latitudes (approximately 23.5° to 66.5° N/S of the Equator), which span between the tropics and the polar regions of Earth. These zones generally have wider temperature ranges throughout the year and more distinct seasonal changes compared to tropical climates, where such variations are often small; they usually differ only in the amount of precipitation. In temperate climates, not only do latitudinal positions influence temperature changes, but various sea currents, prevailing wind direction, continentality (how large a landmass is) and altitude also shape temperate climates. The Köppen climate classification defines a climate as \"temperate\" C, when the mean temperature is above −3 °C (26.6 °F) but below 18 °C (64.4 °F) in the coldest month to account for the persistence of frost. However, some adaptations of Köppen set the minimum at 0 °C (32.0 °F). Continental climates are classified as D and considered to be varieties of temperate climates, having more extreme temperatures, with mean temperatures in the coldest month usually being below −3 °C (26.6 °F)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Crabs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "crabs", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Crabs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Crabs"], "contexts": ["animal used for medicine", "juice used for stomach heat and faintings"]}, {"entity_id": "crabs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "crabs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["crabs"], "contexts": ["cave-dwelling crustaceans", "crustacean with a hard shell"]}, {"entity_id": "crab", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "crab", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["crab"], "contexts": ["marine arthropod"]}, {"entity_id": "crab", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "crab", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["crab"], "contexts": ["crustacean with a distinct consciousness", "example of different consciousness"]}, {"entity_id": "crab", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crab", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["crab"], "contexts": ["extreme of body shortening in Annulosa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Crabs", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "crabs", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_nam