"found_name": "Priests", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 459, "target_cluster_name": "Priests", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Clergyman is a broader term that encompasses priests, referring to ordained ministers in various religions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Clergyman is a broader term that encompasses priests, referring to ordained ministers in various religions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Religiofo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1675, "target_cluster_name": "Religiofo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nuns are members of a religious order, similar to how clergymen are members of the clergy. Both are distinct roles within religious institutions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nuns are members of a religious order, similar to how clergymen are members of the clergy. Both are distinct roles within religious institutions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Praticos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2056, "target_cluster_name": "Praticos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In some historical contexts, clergymen also practiced medicine, blurring the lines between religious and medical roles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In some historical contexts, clergymen also practiced medicine, blurring the lines between religious and medical roles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Berkeley", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2107, "target_cluster_name": "Berkeley", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "George Berkeley was a clergyman.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: George Berkeley was a clergyman.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "precipitar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "précipitation", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "précipitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["précipitation"], "contexts": ["chemical process"]}, {"entity_id": "precipitation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "precipitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["precipitation"], "contexts": ["process of settling out", "water falling from the atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "precipitar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "precipitar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["precipitar", "Precipitar", "precipitando", "precipitaçoens"], "contexts": ["causing a substance to fall down", "chemical process of preparing medicines"]}, {"entity_id": "precipitation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "precipitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["precipitation"], "contexts": ["water falling from atmosphere as rain, snow, etc."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "précipitation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "precipitation", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "précipitation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "precipitation", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "precipitation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "precipitar", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "precipitation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "precipitation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "precipitar", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "precipitation", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 451, "stable_key": "clu_79588e92bf625e2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "precipitation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Chemical process causing a substance to fall out of solution.", "wikipedia_extract": "In meteorology, precipitation is any product of the condensation of atmospheric water vapor that falls from clouds due to gravitational pull. The main forms of precipitation include drizzle, rain, rain and snow mixed (\"sleet\" in Commonwealth usage), snow, ice pellets, graupel and hail. Precipitation occurs when a portion of the atmosphere becomes saturated with water vapor (reaching 100% relative humidity), so that the water condenses and \"precipitates\" or falls. Thus, fog and mist are not precipitation; their water vapor does not condense sufficiently to precipitate, so fog and mist do not fall. (Such a non-precipitating combination is a colloid.) Two processes, possibly acting together, can lead to air becoming saturated with water vapor: cooling the air or adding water vapor to the air. Precipitation forms as smaller droplets coalesce via collision with other rain drops or ice crystals within a cloud. Short, intense periods of rain in scattered locations are called showers."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Atmospheric", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4409, "target_cluster_name": "atmospheric", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Precipitation is a phenomenon that occurs within the atmosphere and is directly related to atmospheric conditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Precipitation is a phenomenon that occurs within the atmosphere and is directly related to atmospheric conditions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "odor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "odor", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "odor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["odor"], "contexts": ["olfactory property of substances", "sense of smell"]}, {"entity_id": "odors", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "odors", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["odors"], "contexts": ["sensory input"]}, {"entity_id": "odor", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "odor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["odor"], "contexts": ["sensory perception"]}, {"entity_id": "odore", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "odore", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["odore"], "contexts": ["quality of water", "smell of plants"]}, {"entity_id": "odour", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "odour", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["odour"], "contexts": ["quality of smell", "distinguished feelings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "odor", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "odor", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "odor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "odore", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "odor", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "odour", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "odors", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "odor", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "odors", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "odour", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "odor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "odore", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "odor", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "odour", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "odore", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "odour", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 452, "stable_key": "clu_2776feb488187061", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Odor", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q24774756", "wikidata_description": "InterPro Family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olfactory%20receptor", "wikipedia_extract": "Olfactory receptors (ORs), also known as odorant receptors, are chemoreceptors expressed in the cell membranes of olfactory receptor neurons and are responsible for the detection of odorants (for example, compounds that have an odor) which give rise to the sense of smell. Activated olfactory receptors trigger nerve impulses which transmit information about odor to the brain. In vertebrates, these receptors are members of the class A rhodopsin-like family of G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs). The olfactory receptors form the largest multigene family in vertebrates consisting of around 400 genes in humans and 1400 genes in mice. In insects, olfactory receptors are members of an unrelated group of ligand-gated ion channels. In vertebrates, the olfactory receptors are located in both the cilia and synapses of the olfactory sensory neurons and in the epithelium of the human airway."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Smelling", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2521, "target_cluster_name": "Smelling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Odor is a direct synonym for Smell, referring to the sensation perceived by the olfactory organ.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Odor is a direct synonym for Smell, referring to the sensation perceived by the olfactory organ.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Martin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "martin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Martin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["Martin", "Martinez"], "contexts": ["observer in January 1826", "observer and year"]}, {"entity_id": "martin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Martin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martin"], "contexts": ["scholar who studied Plato's Timaeus"]}, {"entity_id": "martin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Martin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martin"], "contexts": ["on striped mules"]}, {"entity_id": "martin_(andrés_de_s_)", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Martin (Andrés de S.)", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martin (Andrés de S.)"], "contexts": ["early modern scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Martin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Martin", "similarity": 0.703}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Martin", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Martin (Andrés de S.)", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 453, "stable_key": "clu_113616ff3cd47c00", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Martin", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Historical figure, likely a scholar or observer referenced in scientific texts.", "note": "The specific 'Martin' is not identifiable without more context, but 'Andrés de S. Martin' is a notable figure. The generic reference to 'Martin' makes it difficult to pinpoint a single individual with high confidence.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bilis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "bilis", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bilis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bilis"], "contexts": ["bile, one of the humors"]}, {"entity_id": "bilis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bilis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["bilis"], "contexts": ["humor causing illness", "bile, one of the humors"]}, {"entity_id": "bilis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "bilis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bilis"], "contexts": ["Latin for bile"]}, {"entity_id": "bile-cells", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bile-cells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bile-cells"], "contexts": ["cells forming rudimentary liver"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bilis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bilis", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "bilis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bile-cells", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 454, "stable_key": "clu_c8b4d8fac4d3ab63", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bile", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A humor of the body, associated with illness and bodily functions.", "note": "Refers to the bodily fluid within the context of humoral theory.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bile (from Latin bilis), also known as gall, is a yellow-green fluid produced by the liver of most vertebrates that aids the digestion of lipids in the small intestine. In humans, bile is primarily composed of water, is produced continuously by the liver, and is stored and concentrated in the gallbladder. After a human eats, this stored bile is discharged into the first section of the small intestine, known as the duodenum through the ampulla of vater in the duodenal wall . In the human liver, bile is composed of 97–98% water, 0.7% bile salts, 0.2% bilirubin, 0.51% fats (cholesterol, fatty acids, and lecithin), and 200 meq/L inorganic salts. The two main pigments of bile are bilirubin, which is orange-yellow, and its oxidised form biliverdin, which is green. About 400 to 800 milliliters (14 to 27 U.S. fluid ounces) of bile is produced per day in adult human beings."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "humers", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3925, "target_cluster_name": "humers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the Latin term for 'bile', one of the four humors in humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the Latin term for 'bile', one of the four humors in humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fegato", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3747, "target_cluster_name": "fegato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bile is produced by the liver.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bile is produced by the liver.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "baço", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1527, "target_cluster_name": "baço", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spleen is closely associated with the production and circulation of bile in humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The spleen is closely associated with the production and circulation of bile in humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Vine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "vine", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Vine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Vine", "vine"], "contexts": ["plant dedicated to Bacchus", "plant with antipathy to colewort"]}, {"entity_id": "vigne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vigne", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vigne"], "contexts": ["plant for cultivation"]}, {"entity_id": "vine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vine"], "contexts": ["plant producing grapes for wine", "plant cultivated for wine production"]}, {"entity_id": "vines", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vines", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vines"], "contexts": ["first vegetation zone on volcano"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vine", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "vigne", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "vines", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 455, "stable_key": "clu_36d64d3cb73d2331", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wine", "confidence": "low", "type": "object", "description": "A liquid used in medicine, derived from grapes.", "note": "While 'vinho' directly translates to wine, 'Vine' could refer to the plant itself. Given the context of usage in medicine, 'wine' as a substance is a more fitting identification.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wine is an alcoholic drink made from fermented grape juice. It is produced and consumed in many regions around the world, in a wide variety of styles which are influenced by different varieties of grapes, growing environments, viticulture methods, and production techniques. Wine has been produced for thousands of years, the earliest evidence dating from c. 6000 BCE in present-day Georgia. Its popularity spread around the Mediterranean during Classical antiquity, and was sustained in Western Europe by winemaking monks and a secular trade for general drinking. New World wine was established by settler colonies from the 16th century onwards, and the wine trade increased dramatically up to the latter half of the 19th century, when European vineyards were largely destroyed by the invasive pest phylloxera. After the Second World War, the wine market improved dramatically as winemakers focused on quality and marketing to cater for a more discerning audience, and wine remains a popular drink in much of the world."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Grape", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1220, "target_cluster_name": "Grape", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The historical name 'Vine' for Wine directly refers to the grape plant, from which wine is made.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The historical name 'Vine' for Wine directly refers to the grape plant, from which wine is made."}, {"found_name": "Figs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2166, "target_cluster_name": "Figs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fig trees are often cultivated similarly to grapevines, and both produce fruit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fig trees are often cultivated similarly to grapevines, and both produce fruit.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ivy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2693, "target_cluster_name": "Ivy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ivy is a type of vine, and 'Vine' is listed as a candidate. The description of Ivy explicitly states it is a 'clinging evergreen vine'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ivy is a type of vine, and 'Vine' is listed as a candidate. The description of Ivy explicitly states it is a 'clinging evergreen vine'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Instruments", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "instrument", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "instrument", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instrument"], "contexts": ["device for scientific measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "instruments", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Instruments", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["instruments", "Instruments"], "contexts": ["tools used for scientific observation", "tools used in scientific observation"]}, {"entity_id": "instruments", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "instruments", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["instruments"], "contexts": ["tools used for scientific observation and measurement", "tools used for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "instrumentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "instrumentos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instrumentos"], "contexts": ["tools for delivery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "instrument", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Instruments", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "instruments", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "instrumentos", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 456, "stable_key": "clu_6e18e1816b9cd014", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "scientific instruments", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q8037077", "wikidata_description": "Livery company of the City of London", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worshipful%20Company%20of%20Scientific%20Instrument%20Makers", "wikipedia_extract": "The Worshipful Company of Scientific Instrument Makers is one of the 111 livery companies of the City of London. It ranks 84th in the order of precedence for the livery companies. The Company was originally formed in 1956 when science and manufacturing were key to the UK economy  and the memory of war time defence requirements were still fresh and the UK was a large-scale manufacturer of specialist electronic and mechanical scientific instruments. It is one of the Modern Livery Companies formed since 1926 having been granted its livery status by the City in 1963. In 2021, it was granted a Royal Charter. As with the original Livery Companies it supports the profession and the craft of scientific instrument making, this is achieved through the exchange of ideas and information between members and guests, and also by its charitable donations supporting those in or retired from the profession."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "musical instruments", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2894, "target_cluster_name": "musical instruments", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'instrument' is a direct synonym for 'musical instruments' in a broader sense, as musical instruments are a type of instrument.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'instrument' is a direct synonym for 'musical instruments' in a broader sense, as musical instruments are a type of instrument.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Thermometer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id