1, "variants": ["priest"], "contexts": ["religious official"]}, {"entity_id": "priest", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "priest", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["priest"], "contexts": ["religious official"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priests", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "prêtre", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priests", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priests", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priest", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "prêtre", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priest", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Priest", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "prêtre", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "prêtre", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "priest", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "priest", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 459, "stable_key": "clu_27b6659e36f711e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Priest", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Clerical role in religious institutions, often involved in rituals.", "wikidata_id": "Q42603", "wikidata_description": "person who consecrates his life to some divinity and whose main functions are to direct religious rites and offer sacrifices to the divinity (for a minister use Q1423891)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priest", "wikipedia_extract": "A priest is a religious leader authorized to perform the sacred rituals of a religion, especially as a mediatory agent between humans and one or more deities. They also have the authority or power to administer religious rites; in particular, rites of sacrifice to, and propitiation of, a deity or deities. Their office or position is the \"priesthood\", a term which also may apply to such persons collectively. A priest may have the duty to hear confessions periodically, give marriage counseling, provide prenuptial counseling, give spiritual direction, teach catechism, or visit those confined indoors, such as the sick in hospitals and nursing homes. According to the trifunctional hypothesis of prehistoric Proto-Indo-European society, priests have existed since the earliest of times and in the simplest societies, most likely as a result of agricultural surplus and consequent social stratification. The necessity to read sacred texts and keep temple or church records helped foster literacy in many early societies."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Apostles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1296, "target_cluster_name": "Apostles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apostles were a group of twelve disciples chosen by Jesus, who are considered the first priests in Christianity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Apostles were a group of twelve disciples chosen by Jesus, who are considered the first priests in Christianity."}, {"found_name": "Clergyman", "lin