fidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Madagascar is a large island nation located off the southeastern coast of Africa."}, {"found_name": "East Indies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 497, "target_cluster_name": "East Indies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, Madagascar was sometimes grouped with or considered part of the East Indies due to its location and trade routes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, Madagascar was sometimes grouped with or considered part of the East Indies due to its location and trade routes."}, {"found_name": "Antarctic Islands", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4030, "target_cluster_name": "Antarctic islands", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Madagascar is geographically located in the Southern Hemisphere, and while not an Antarctic island, it shares a general latitudinal proximity and oceanic context with islands in that region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Madagascar is geographically located in the Southern Hemisphere, and while not an Antarctic island, it shares a general latitudinal proximity and oceanic context with islands in that region."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lavoisier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "lavoisier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lavoisier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lavoisier"], "contexts": ["evaluator of wheat price", "evaluated kilogram of wheat"]}, {"entity_id": "lavoisier", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Lavoisier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lavoisier"], "contexts": ["Author of early Chemistry text", "author of Chemistry text"]}, {"entity_id": "lavoisier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lavoisier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lavoisier"], "contexts": ["expressed idea about Prometheus myth", "chemist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lavoisier", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Lavoisier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lavoisier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lavoisier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Lavoisier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lavoisier", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 475, "stable_key": "clu_8f86aa826fd2c352", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antoine Lavoisier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1743, "death_year": 1794, "description": "French chemist, Father of modern chemistry", "note": "The presence of 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' is an anomaly, as Lavoisier was born centuries later. This suggests an error in the provided context or data. However, the other contexts strongly point to the famous chemist.", "wikidata_id": "Q39607", "wikidata_description": "French nobleman and chemist (1743–1794)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine%20Lavoisier", "wikipedia_extract": "Antoine-Laurent de Lavoisier ( lə-VWAH-zee-ay; French: [ɑ̃twan lɔʁɑ̃ də lavwazje]; 26 August 1743 – 8 May 1794), also Antoine Lavoisier after the French Revolution, was a French nobleman and chemist who was central to the 18th-century chemical revolution and who had a large influence on both the history of chemistry and the history of biology. It is generally accepted that Lavoisier's great accomplishments in chemistry stem largely from his changing the science from a qualitative to a quantitative one. Lavoisier is noted for his discovery of the role oxygen plays in combustion, opposing the prior phlogiston theory of combustion. He named oxygen (1778), recognizing it as an element, and also recognized hydrogen as an element (1783). By using more precise measurements than previous experimenters, he confirmed the  developing theory that, although matter in a closed system may change its form or shape, its mass always remains the same (now known as the law of conservation of mass), which led to the development of the balanced physical and chemical reaction equations that we still use today. Lavoisier helped construct the metric system, wrote the first extensive list of elements, in which he predicted the existence of silicon, and helped to reform chemical nomenclature."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemist", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 609, "target_cluster_name": "Chymicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'."}, {"found_name": "Chemists", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1124, "target_cluster_name": "chemists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'."}, {"found_name": "Georges-Louis Leclerc, Comte de Buffon", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 473, "target_cluster_name": "Buffon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buffon's work on natural history and geology, particularly his emphasis on observation and experimentation, provided a broader scientific context that influenced Lavoisier's approach to chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Buffon's work on natural history and geology, particularly his emphasis on observation and experimentation, provided a broader scientific context that influenced Lavoisier's approach to chemistry."}, {"found_name": "Benjamin Franklin", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 343, "target_cluster_name": "Franklin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent scientists of the Enlightenment era, contributing to different but related fields (chemistry and electricity/physics) and engaging with similar scientific methodologies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent scientists of the Enlightenment era, contributing to different but related fields (chemistry and electricity/physics) and engaging with similar scientific methodologies."}, {"found_name": "Galileo Galilei", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 931, "target_cluster_name": "Galileo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galileo's revolutionary approach to physics, emphasizing mathematical reasoning and empirical observation, laid groundwork for the scientific revolution that Lavoisier was a key part of in chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galileo's revolutionary approach to physics, emphasizing mathematical reasoning and empirical observation, laid groundwork for the scientific revolution that Lavoisier was a key part of in chemistry."}, {"found_name": "Alessandro Volta", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 966, "target_cluster_name": "Volta", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were pivotal figures in the development of chemistry and physics, with Volta's work on electricity being foundational for many later scientific advancements, including those in chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were pivotal figures in the development of chemistry and physics, with Volta's work on electricity being foundational for many later scientific advancements, including those in chemistry."}, {"found_name": "Carl Wilhelm Scheele", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1258, "target_cluster_name": "Scheele", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Scheele was a contemporary chemist whose discoveries, particularly in the area of gases, significantly influenced Lavoisier's own work and the broader chemical revolution.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Scheele was a contemporary chemist whose discoveries, particularly in the area of gases, significantly influenced Lavoisier's own work and the broader chemical revolution."}, {"found_name": "Humphry Davy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1877, "target_cluster_name": "Sir Humphry Davy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Davy was a prominent chemist who built upon Lavoisier's foundational work, particularly in the understanding of elements and combustion, and engaged in debates that furthered chemical theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Davy was a prominent chemist who built upon Lavoisier's foundational work, particularly in the understanding of elements and combustion, and engaged in debates that furthered chemical theory."}, {"found_name": "physiologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1103, "target_cluster_name": "physiologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antoine Lavoisier's work in chemistry, particularly his studies on respiration and combustion, laid crucial groundwork for understanding metabolic processes in physiology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antoine Lavoisier's work in chemistry, particularly his studies on respiration and combustion, laid crucial groundwork for understanding metabolic processes in physiology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Hartmanus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1581, "target_cluster_name": "Hartmanus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antoine Lavoisier is famously known for overturning Stahl's phlogiston theory with his theory of combustion based on oxygen. Their work represents a critical transition in the history of chemistry, from the phlogiston era to modern chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antoine Lavoisier is famously known for overturning Stahl's phlogiston theory with his theory of combustion based on oxygen. Their work represents a critical transition in the history of chemistry, from the phlogiston era to modern chemistry.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "spirit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "spirit", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spirit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["spirit"], "contexts": ["non-material essence or soul", "abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spiritu", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiritu"], "contexts": ["Latin for spirit or breath"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "spiritus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["spiritus", "SPIRITUS"], "contexts": ["spirit", "vapors or spirits"]}, {"entity_id": "spirit", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Spirit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["spirit", "Spirit"], "contexts": ["non-physical substance, potential cause of sensory impressions", "antithetical conception to Matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spirit", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spiritu", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spirit", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "spiritus", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spirit", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Spirit", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "spiritu", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "spiritus", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "spiritu", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Spirit", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "spiritus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Spirit", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 476, "stable_key": "clu_6d1f0035bef709d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spirit", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Covers philosophical, religious, and scientific (e.g., breath) connotations of 'spirit' across languages.", "wikidata_id": "Q9174", "wikidata_description": "social-cultural system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religion", "wikipedia_extract": "Religion is a range of social-cultural systems, including designated behaviors and practices, ethics, morals, beliefs, worldviews, texts, sanctified places, prophecies, or organizations, that generally relate humanity to supernatural, transcendental, and spiritual elements—although there is no scholarly consensus over what precisely constitutes a religion. It is an essentially contested concept. Different religions may or may not contain various elements ranging from the divine, sacredness, faith, and a supernatural being or beings. The origin of religious belief is an open question, with possible explanations including awareness of individual death, a sense of community, and dreams. Religions have sacred histories, narratives, and mythologies, preserved in oral traditions, sacred texts, symbols, and holy places, that may attempt to explain the origin of life, the universe, and other phenomena. Religious practice may include rituals, sermons, commemoration or veneration (of deities or saints), sacrifices, festivals, feasts, trances, initiations, matrimonial and funerary services, meditation, prayer, music, art, dance, or public service."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Divinity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2345, "target_cluster_name": "Divinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of 'Spirit' in a religious or philosophical context is often closely associated with or considered synonymous with 'Divinity'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of 'Spirit' in a religious or philosophical context is often closely associated with or considered synonymous with 'Divinity'."}, {"found_name": "Atheism", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 993, "target_cluster_name": "Atheism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Atheism' is the direct negation or absence of belief in 'Spirit' or 'Divinity', making it a conceptually related counterpoint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "'Atheism' is the direct negation or absence of belief in 'Spirit' or 'Divinity', making it a conceptually related counterpoint."}, {"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Belief is a fundamental component of religious and spiritual systems, often directed towards or involving the concept of 'Spirit'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Belief is a fundamental component of religious and spiritual systems, often directed towards or involving the concept of 'Spirit'."}, {"found_name": "Conscience", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2716, "target_cluster_name": "Conscientiousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Conscience' is frequently understood as an inner voice or moral compass, often attributed to or influenced by 'Spirit'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "'Conscience' is frequently understood as an inner voice or moral compass, often attributed to or influenced by 'Spirit'."}, {"found_name": "Evil", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3793, "target_cluster_name": "evil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In many theological and philosophical frameworks, 'Evil' is conceptualized as a force or principle that stands in opposition to 'Spirit' or the divine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In many theological and philosophical frameworks, 'Evil' is conceptualized as a force or principle that stands in opposition to 'Spirit' or the divine."}, {"found_name": "natureza", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1530, "target_cluster_name": "natureza", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vital force is often discussed in conjunction with, or as a component of, the spirit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Vital force is often discussed in conjunction with, or as a component of, the spirit.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "malicia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2462, "target_cluster_name": "malicia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Capoeira incorporates spiritual elements and is often described as having a spiritual dimension.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Capoeira incorporates spiritual elements and is often described as having a spiritual dimension.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "demonio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "devil", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "devil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["devil"], "contexts": ["religious explanation for woman's behavior"]}, {"entity_id": "demonio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "demonio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Demonio", "demonio"], "contexts": ["entity that advises and deceives", "demon"]}, {"entity_id": "demonio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "demonio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["demonio"], "contexts": ["demon", "cause of demonic diseases"]}, {"entity_id": "devil", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "devil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["devil"], "contexts": ["concept name in Pawnee language"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "devil", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "demonio", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "devil", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "demonio", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "devil", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "devil", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "demonio", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "demonio", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "demonio", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "devil", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "demonio", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "devil", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 477, "stable_key": "clu_7e188e7b204f8e62", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Demon", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Encompasses both religious and folkloric entities that cause harm or deception, in Spanish and English.", "wikidata_id": "Q177413", "wikidata_description": "collective action (e.g. march) by people who are supporting or protesting against a cause of concern", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demon", "wikipedia_extract": "A demon is an evil or malevolent supernatural entity. Historically, belief in demons, or stories about demons, occurs in folklore, mythology, religion, occultism, and literature; these beliefs are reflected in media including fiction, comics, film, television, and video games. Belief in demons probably goes back to the Paleolithic age, stemming from humanity's fear of the unknown, the strange and the horrific. In ancient Near Eastern religions and in the Abrahamic religions, including early Judaism and ancient-medieval Christian demonology, a demon is considered a harmful spiritual entity that may cause demonic possession, calling for an exorcism. Large portions of Jewish demonology, a key influence on Christianity and Islam, originated from a later form of Zoroastrianism, and was transferred to Judaism during the Persian era. Demons may or may not be considered to be devils: minions of the Devil."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Evil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3793, "target_cluster_name": "evil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A demon is a type of evil supernatural entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A demon is a type of evil supernatural entity."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "arts", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Arts"], "contexts": ["academic disciplines", "fields of study and skill"]}, {"entity_id": "artes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Artes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Artes"], "contexts": ["arts"]}, {"entity_id": "arts", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arts"], "contexts": ["human creative endeavors"]}, {"entity_id": "arts", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Arts", "arts"], "contexts": ["creative and imaginative activities", "literary delineation, narrative, dramatic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Arts", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Artes", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Arts", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "arts", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Arts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862"