, "variants": ["saint"], "contexts": ["honored by dancing in choir"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Saints", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "saint", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saints", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saint", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "saint", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saints", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Saints", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saint", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "saint", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saints", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "saint", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "saint", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 484, "stable_key": "clu_3040d676ee4330fc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Holy person, venerated figure in religious contexts", "wikidata_id": "Q43115", "wikidata_description": "person who has been recognized for having an exceptional degree of holiness, sanctity, and virtue; may or may not be a canonised saint", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint", "wikipedia_extract": "In Christian belief, a saint, also known as a hallow, is a person who is recognized as having an exceptional degree of holiness, likeness, or closeness to God. However, the use of the term saint depends on the context and denomination. Official ecclesiastical recognition, and veneration, is conferred on some denominational saints through the process of canonization in the Catholic Church or glorification in the Eastern Orthodox Church after their approval. The saints are honored in the liturgical calendars of Evangelical Lutheranism and Anglicanism. In other nonconformist denominations, such as the Plymouth Brethren, and following from Pauline usage, saint refers broadly to any holy Christian without special recognition or selection. While the English word saint (deriving from the Latin sanctus) originated in Christianity, historians of religion tend to use the appellation \"in a more general way to refer to the state of special holiness that many religions attribute to certain people\", referring to the Hindu rishi, Sikh bhagat or guru, the Shintoist kami, the Taoist immortal or zhenren, the Jewish tzadik, the Islamic walī/fakir, and the Buddhist arhat or bodhisattva also as saints."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Apostles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1296, "target_cluster_name": "Apostles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apostles are a specific group of early followers of Jesus Christ, who are considered saints in Christianity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Apostles are a specific group of early followers of Jesus Christ, who are considered saints in Christianity."}, {"found_name": "Jesus Christ", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 13, "target_cluster_name": "Christ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jesus Christ is the central figure of Christianity, and is considered the ultimate saint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jesus Christ is the central figure of Christianity, and is considered the ultimate saint."}, {"found_name": "John the Baptist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2497, "target_cluster_name": "St. John", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John the Baptist is a significant figure in Christianity, recognized as a saint and a precursor to Jesus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "John the Baptist is a significant figure in Christianity, recognized as a saint and a precursor to Jesus."}, {"found_name": "God", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 555, "target_cluster_name": "Deos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, the concept of God is intrinsically linked to the veneration of saints in Christian theology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While distinct, the concept of God is intrinsically linked to the veneration of saints in Christian theology."}, {"found_name": "St. John", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2497, "target_cluster_name": "St. John", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John the Baptist is venerated as a saint in Christianity and Islam.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John the Baptist is venerated as a saint in Christianity and Islam.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Æsculapius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2903, "target_cluster_name": "Æsculapius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Asclepius was deified and worshipped as a god, and in later periods, figures like him could be venerated in ways similar to saints.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Asclepius was deified and worshipped as a god, and in later periods, figures like him could be venerated in ways similar to saints.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "musc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "musk", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Musk", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Musk"], "contexts": ["used in sweet ointments"]}, {"entity_id": "musc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "musc", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 2, "variants": ["musc"], "contexts": ["odor produced by crocodiles", "strong odor from caymans"]}, {"entity_id": "musk", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "musk", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["musk"], "contexts": ["used for perfuming"]}, {"entity_id": "musk", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "musk", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["musk"], "contexts": ["perfume from animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Musk", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "musk", "similarity": 0.757}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Musk", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "musk", "similarity": 0.589}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "musc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "musk", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "musk", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "musk", "similarity": 0.827}], "id": 485, "stable_key": "clu_e2ba34245a490fe9", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Musk is a class of aromatic substances commonly used as base notes in perfumery. They include glandular secretions from animals such as the musk deer, numerous plants emitting similar fragrances, and artificial substances with similar odors. Musk was a name originally given to a substance with a strong odor obtained from a gland of the musk deer. The substance has been used as a popular perfume fixative since ancient times and is one of the most expensive animal products in the world. The name originates from the Late Greek μόσχος 'moskhos', from Persian mushk and Sanskrit मुष्क muṣka (lit. 'testicle') derived from Proto-Indo-European noun múh₂s meaning \"mouse\". The deer gland was thought to resemble a scrotum."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "spiders", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "spiders", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "spiders", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["spiders"], "contexts": ["animals with no metamorphosis", "example of animal development rule"]}, {"entity_id": "spiders", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spiders", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiders"], "contexts": ["arachnids found in amber"]}, {"entity_id": "spider", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "spider", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["spider"], "contexts": ["venomous creature"]}, {"entity_id": "spiders", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "spiders", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiders"], "contexts": ["higher division of Annulosa with reduced segments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "spiders", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spiders", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "spiders", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "spiders", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "spiders", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "spider", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "spiders", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "spiders", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "spider", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "spiders", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 486, "stable_key": "clu_5f00f67bc5e475b3", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Spiders (order Araneae) are air-breathing arthropods that have eight limbs, chelicerae with fangs generally able to inject venom, and spinnerets that extrude silk. They are the largest order of arachnids and rank seventh in total species diversity among all orders of organisms. Spiders are found worldwide on every continent except Antarctica, and have become established in nearly every land habitat. As of January 2026, 53,680 spider species in 139 families have been recorded by taxonomists. However, there has been debate among scientists about how families should be classified, with over 20 different classifications proposed since 1900. Anatomically, spiders (as with all arachnids) differ from other arthropods in that the usual body segments are fused into two tagmata, the cephalothorax or prosoma, and the opisthosoma, or abdomen, and joined by a small, cylindrical pedicel."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Scorpio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 999, "target_cluster_name": "Scorpio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Scorpions are arachnids, and spiders are also arachnids. Both are commonly discussed together in historical texts concerning arthropods and venomous creatures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Scorpions are arachnids, and spiders are also arachnids. Both are commonly discussed together in historical texts concerning arthropods and venomous creatures.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Venemous Beasts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1558, "target_cluster_name": "Venemous Beasts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spiders, many of which are venomous, are often discussed alongside other venomous creatures in historical zoological and medical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spiders, many of which are venomous, are often discussed alongside other venomous creatures in historical zoological and medical texts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "atolls", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "atolls", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "atolls", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["atolls"], "contexts": ["rings of coral marking sunken islands", "ring-shaped coral islands"]}, {"entity_id": "îles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["îles"], "contexts": ["location of emigration", "location of Caribe people"]}, {"entity_id": "islands", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["islands"], "contexts": ["land masses surrounded by water"]}, {"entity_id": "islands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Islands"], "contexts": ["character of their floras"]}, {"entity_id": "island", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1