e is a specific type of force that relates to the exertion of power to increase volume or spread.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Expansive Force is a specific type of force that relates to the exertion of power to increase volume or spread."}]}, {"canonical_name": "laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "laws", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laws"], "contexts": ["definite principles governing physical phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "laws", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["laws"], "contexts": ["principles governing phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "laws", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["laws"], "contexts": ["principles governing phenomena", "regulating forces of the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "laws", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laws"], "contexts": ["rules of conduct enforced by authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "laws", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laws", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 489, "stable_key": "clu_f139fc7bb94c2ebb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scientific Law", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q408891", "wikidata_description": "statement based on repeated experimental observations that describes some aspects of the universe", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific%20law", "wikipedia_extract": "Scientific laws or laws of science are statements, based on repeated experiments or observations, that describe or predict a range of natural phenomena. The term law has diverse usage in many cases (approximate, accurate, broad, or narrow) across all fields of natural science (physics, chemistry, astronomy, geoscience, biology). Laws are developed from data and can be further developed through mathematics; in all cases they are directly or indirectly based on empirical evidence. It is generally understood that they implicitly reflect, though they do not explicitly assert, causal relationships fundamental to reality, and are discovered rather than invented. Scientific laws summarize the results of experiments or observations, usually within a certain range of application. In general, the accuracy of a law does not change when a new theory of the relevant phenomenon is worked out, but rather the scope of the law's application, since the mathematics or statement representing the law does not change."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Empirical law", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4194, "target_cluster_name": "empirical law", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical laws are a specific type of scientific law derived from observation and experiment, fitting within the broader concept of scientific law.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Empirical laws are a specific type of scientific law derived from observation and experiment, fitting within the broader concept of scientific law."}, {"found_name": "psycho-physics", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "brain-physiology, and the recent minutely cultivated physiology of the sense-organs, together with what is technically known as 'psycho-physics,' or the laws of correlation between sensations and the ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for"}, {"found_name": "laws governing variation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "evidence_snippet": "indeed is, on the theory of natural selection, that more cases of the want of absolute perfection have not been observed. The complex and little known laws governing variation are the same, as far as ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "laws governing variation are mentioned in parallel to the laws governing the production of specific forms"}]}, {"canonical_name": "campo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "campos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Campos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Campos"], "contexts": ["geographic area"]}, {"entity_id": "campo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "campo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["campo"], "contexts": ["rural