nning of the 19th century. Settler Joseph Vial and his family were among the first non-native people to settle in the Countryside area in 1833. The area remained large expanses of rural farmland until the Great Chicago Fire of 1871, when the fire sent thousands of city dwellers into what is now west suburban Chicago. Land sold for only $2 an acre, which made areas such as Countryside a welcome respite from the congestion and industry in Chicago."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "caloric", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "calorique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "calorique", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["calorique"], "contexts": ["heat energy"]}, {"entity_id": "caloric", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "caloric", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["caloric"], "contexts": ["hypothetical substance of heat"]}, {"entity_id": "caloric", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "caloric", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["caloric"], "contexts": ["historical term for heat"]}, {"entity_id": "caloric", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "caloric", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["caloric"], "contexts": ["heat substance absorbed and radiated", "substance absorbed by elements, related to heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "calorique", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "calorique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "calorique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "caloric", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "caloric", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "caloric", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "caloric", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 491, "stable_key": "clu_36833d98fe33220e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caloric Theory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The caloric theory was an early modern scientific concept that heat was a fluid substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q845261", "wikidata_description": "obsolete scientific theory of heat flow", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caloric%20theory", "wikipedia_extract": "The caloric theory is a superseded scientific theory that heat consists of a self-repellent fluid called \"caloric\" that flows from hotter bodies to colder bodies. Caloric was also thought of as a weightless gas that could pass in and out of pores in solids and liquids. The caloric theory was superseded by the mid-19th century in favor of the mechanical theory of heat, but nevertheless persisted in some scientific literature—particularly in more popular treatments—until the end of the 19th century. Phlogiston theory is replaced by combustion in oxygen In the history of thermodynamics, the initial explanations of heat were thoroughly confused with explanations of combustion. After J. J."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "ice", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 275, "target_cluster_name": "ice", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caloric theory was a dominant scientific theory explaining heat, which is directly related to the phase transitions of substances like water (ice, liquid, gas).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caloric theory was a dominant scientific theory explaining heat, which is directly related to the phase transitions of substances like water (ice, liquid, gas).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "light", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3819, "target_cluster_name": "light", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical theories of heat often discussed its relationship to light and other forms of energy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historical theories of heat often discussed its relationship to light and other forms of energy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 530, "target_cluster_name": "heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caloric theory is a historical theory of heat, proposing heat as a fluid called caloric.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Caloric theory is a historical theory of heat, proposing heat as a fluid called caloric."}]}, {"canonical_name": "old age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "old_age", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "old age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["old age"], "contexts": ["natural decline with aging", "later stage of life"]}, {"entity_id": "velhice", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "velhice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["velhice"], "contexts": ["state of being old"]}, {"entity_id": "velhice", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "velhice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["velhice"], "contexts": ["stage of life"]}, {"entity_id": "old_age", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "old age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["old age"], "contexts": ["later stage of life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "old age", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "velhice", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "old age", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "velhice", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "old age", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "old age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "velhice", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "velhice", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "velhice", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "old age", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "velhice", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "old age", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 492, "stable_key": "clu_a552996f5bdf758f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Old age", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q191089", "wikidata_description": "consists of ages nearing or surpassing the life expectancy of human beings", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_age", "wikipedia_extract": "Old age is the range of ages for people nearing and surpassing life expectancy. People of old age are called old people, old-timers, the elderly, elders, senior citizens, seniors, or older adults. Old age is not a definite biological stage: the chronological age denoted as \"old age\" varies culturally and historically. Some disciplines and domains focus on the aging and the aged, such as the organic processes of aging (senescence), medical studies of the aging process (gerontology), diseases that afflict older adults (geriatrics), technology to support the aging society (gerontechnology), and leisure and sport activities adapted to older people (such as senior sport). Old people often have limited regenerative abilities and are more susceptible to illness and injury than younger adults. They face social problems related to retirement, loneliness, and ageism."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Age", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 174, "target_cluster_name": "age", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Old age is a specific stage within the broader concept of 'Age'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Old age is a specific stage within the broader concept of 'Age'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "electrical machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "electrical_machine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "electrical machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electrical machine"], "contexts": ["device for generating electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "electrical_instruments", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electrical instruments", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electrical instruments"], "contexts": ["devices for measuring electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "electrical_machine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electrical machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["electrical machine"], "contexts": ["device for generating static ele