"found_name": "division of labour", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2533, "target_cluster_name": "division of labour", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The division of labor is a key characteristic of industrial production, enabling mass production and specialization of tasks within factories and industries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The division of labor is a key characteristic of industrial production, enabling mass production and specialization of tasks within factories and industries.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cannon", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cannon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "cannon", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cannon"], "contexts": ["object producing a real sound"]}, {"entity_id": "cannon_ball", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cannon ball", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cannon ball"], "contexts": ["used for velocity comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "lança", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "lança", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lança"], "contexts": ["shape of the leaf"]}, {"entity_id": "cannon", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cannon", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cannon"], "contexts": ["weapon that produces loud sound"]}, {"entity_id": "bullet", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bullet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bullet"], "contexts": ["arrested, travels with undiminished speed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cannon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cannon ball", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cannon", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "lança", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cannon", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cannon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cannon", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bullet", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cannon ball", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cannon", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cannon ball", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bullet", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "lança", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cannon", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 507, "stable_key": "clu_519e33b818d10c76", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cannon", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "note": "The context 'shape of the leaf' is likely a misattribution or OCR error; 'lança' usually refers to a spear, but in some contexts could relate to projectile weapons. 'cannon ball' and 'bullet' suggest a military projectile context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannon", "wikipedia_extract": "A cannon (plural either cannons or cannon) is a large-caliber gun classified as a type of artillery, which usually launches a projectile using explosive chemical propellant. Gunpowder (\"black powder\") was the primary propellant before the invention of smokeless powder during the late 19th century. Cannons vary in gauge, effective range, mobility, rate of fire, angle of fire and firepower; different forms of cannon combine and balance these attributes in varying degrees, depending on their intended use on the battlefield. A cannon is a type of heavy artillery weapon. The word cannon is derived from several languages, in which the original definition can usually be translated as tube, cane, or reed. The earliest known depiction of cannons may have appeared in Song dynasty China as early as the 12th century; however, solid archaeological and documentary evidence of cannons do not appear until the 13th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Guns", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 191, "target_cluster_name": "Guns", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A cannon is a type of firearm, specifically a large-caliber gun designed to fire projectiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A cannon is a type of firearm, specifically a large-caliber gun designed to fire projectiles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "firearms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 191, "target_cluster_name": "Guns", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A cannon is a type of firearm.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A cannon is a type of firearm."}, {"found_name": "shot", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3544, "target_cluster_name": "shot", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Shot is a projectile fired from a cannon.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Shot is a projectile fired from a cannon."}]}, {"canonical_name": "propriedades", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "characteristics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "characteristics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["characteristics"], "contexts": ["essential qualities of a thing"]}, {"entity_id": "propriedades", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "propriedades", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["propriedades"], "contexts": ["qualities or characteristics"]}, {"entity_id": "propriedades", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "propriedades", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["propriedades"], "contexts": ["properties and virtues", "hidden virtues of substances"]}, {"entity_id": "properties", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "properties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["properties"], "contexts": ["characteristics of an object"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "characteristics", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "propriedades", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "characteristics", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "propriedades", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "characteristics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "properties", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "propriedades", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "propriedades", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "propriedades", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "properties", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "propriedades", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "properties", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 508, "stable_key": "clu_72093f6f54f9aa27", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "properties", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6478447", "wikidata_description": "overview about physical and chemical properties of pure water", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Properties%20of%20water", "wikipedia_extract": "Water (H2O) is a polar inorganic compound that is at room temperature a tasteless and odorless liquid, which is nearly colorless apart from an inherent hint of blue. It is by far the most studied chemical compound and is described as the \"universal solvent\" and the \"solvent of life\". It is the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth and the only common substance to exist as a solid, liquid, and gas on Earth's surface. It is also the third most abundant molecule in the universe (behind molecular hydrogen and carbon monoxide). Water molecules form hydrogen bonds with each other and are strongly polar. This polarity allows it to dissociate ions in salts and bond to other polar substances such as alcohols and acids, thus dissolving them."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Evaporation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2390, "target_cluster_name": "évaporation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evaporation is a process directly related to the physical properties of water.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evaporation is a process directly related to the physical properties of water."}, {"found_name": "Cooling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 720, "target_cluster_name": "cooling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process that affects water and is related to its thermal properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cooling is a process that affects water and is related to its thermal properties."}, {"found_name": "Density", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 303, "target_cluster_name": "density", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Density is a fundamental physical property of water.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Density is a fundamental physical property of water."}, {"found_name": "Chemical composition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 449, "target_cluster_name": "chemical composition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Water's chemical composition (H2O) is central to its properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Water's chemical composition (H2O) is central to its properties."}]}, {"canonical_name": "poets", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "poets", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "poets", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["poets"], "contexts": ["writers of poetry"]}, {"entity_id": "poet", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "poet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["poet"], "contexts": ["writer of verse"]}, {"entity_id": "poets_of_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "poets of nature", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["poets of nature"], "contexts": ["early users of expression"]}, {"entity_id": "poetas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "poetas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["poetas"], "contexts": ["writers of verse"]}, {"entity_id": "poet", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "poet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["poet"], "contexts": ["refers to an unnamed poet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "poets", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "poets of nature", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "poets", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "poetas", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "poets", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "poet", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "poet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "poetas", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "poet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "poet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "poets of nature", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "poetas", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "poetas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "poet", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 509, "stable_key": "clu_6226a424d9f5429a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "poet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Author of poetry or verse, often as early users of expression."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Loeb", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1006, "target_cluster_name": "Loeb", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Matthias Jakob Schleiden published poems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Matthias Jakob Schleiden published poems.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Creta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cretan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cretan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cretan"], "contexts": ["from the island of Crete"]}, {"entity_id": "creta", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Creta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Creta"], "contexts": ["island where Dictamo grows", "island, Crete"]}, {"entity_id": "creta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Creta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Creta"], "contexts": ["location of açaroro according to Pliny"]}, {"entity_id": "creta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Creta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Creta"], "contexts": ["location of a plague"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cretan", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Creta", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Creta", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Creta", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Creta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Creta", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Creta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Creta", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 510, "stable_key": "clu_41ed4ce9c1a1b25c", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Crete ( KREET; Greek: Κρήτη, Modern: Kríti [ˈkriti], Ancient: Krḗtē [krɛ̌ːtεː]) is the largest and most populous of the Greek islands, the 90th largest island in the world, and the fifth largest island in the Mediterranean Sea, after Sicily, Sardinia, Cyprus, and Corsica. Crete is located approximately 100 km (62 mi) south of the Peloponnese, and about 300 km (190 mi) southwest of Anatolia. Crete has an area of 8,450 km2 (3,260 mi2) and a coastline of 1,046 km (650 mi). It bounds the southern border of the Aegean Sea, with the Sea of Crete (or North Cretan Sea) to the north and the Libyan Sea (or South Cretan Sea) to the south. Crete covers 260 km from west to east but is narrow from north to south, spanning three degrees of longitude but only half a degree of latitude. Crete and its surrounding islands and islets form the Region of Crete (Greek: Περιφέρεια Κρήτης), which is the southernmost of the 13 top-level administrative units of Greece, and the fifth most populous of Greece's regions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Candia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2010, "target_cluster_name": "Candia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Creta is the Latin name for the island of Crete, where Heraklion (historically Candia) is located. In many premodern texts, the island and its major city were often conflated or used interchangeably.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Creta is the Latin name for the island of Crete, where Heraklion (historically Candia) is located. In many premodern texts, the island and its major city were often conflated or used interchangeably.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "grego", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 773, "target_cluster_name": "grego", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crete (Creta) is a large island south of mainland Greece, historically and culturally linked to Greece.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Crete (Creta) is a large island south of mainland Greece, historically and culturally linked to Greece.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Chypre", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1169, "target_cluster_name": "Chypre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crete (modern Greek: Kríti) is an island in the Mediterranean Sea, south of Cyprus. Both are significant islands in the Eastern Mediterranean with long histories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Crete (modern Greek: Kríti) is an island in the Mediterranean Sea, south of Cyprus. Both are significant islands in the Eastern Mediterranean with long histories.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ásia menor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2070, "target_cluster_name": "Ásia menor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crete is an island historically and geographically associated with Anatolia and the Aegean region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Crete is an island historically and geographically associated with Anatolia and the Aegean region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Levante", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2619, "target_cluster_name": "Levante", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crete is an island within the Eastern Mediterranean, often included in broader definitions of the Levant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Crete is an island within the Eastern Mediterranean, often included in broader definitions of the Levant.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hot,_cold,_moist,_dry", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hot, cold, moist, dry"], "contexts": ["qualities in early modern medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "hot,_cold,_moist,_dry", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hot, cold, moist, dry"], "contexts": ["fundamental qualities in humoral theory"]}, {"entity_id": "hot,_cold,_moist,_dry", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hot, cold, moist, dry"], "contexts": ["qualities of elements and body"]}, {"entity_id": "hot,_cold,_moist,_dry", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["hot, cold, moist, dry"], "contexts": ["qualities of substances", "implied medical qualities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 511, "stable_key": "clu_4d881c8ef941bce7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humoral qualities", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "These are the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) central to ancient Greek and early modern European medical theories, particularly humoralism, describing the nature of elements, substances, and the body."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humidity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 27, "target_cluster_name": "Humidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Humidity is one of the four primary humoral qualities and a fundamental concept in understanding bodily states and treatments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Humidity is one of the four primary humoral qualities and a fundamental concept in understanding bodily states and treatments."}, {"found_name": "Dry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 38, "target_cluster_name": "dry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dryness is one of the four primary humoral qualities and a fundamental concept in understanding bodily states and treatments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dryness is one of the four primary humoral qualities and a fundamental concept in understanding bodily states and treatments."}, {"found_name": "Astrological medicine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 569, "target_cluster_name": "astrological medicine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Humoral qualities were often interpreted and influenced by astrological considerations, with celestial bodies and their positions affecting the balance of humors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Humoral qualities were often interpreted and influenced by astrological considerations, with celestial bodies and their positions affecting the balance of humors."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Theology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "theology", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Theology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theology"], "contexts": ["divine subject matter"]}, {"entity_id": "théologie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "théologie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["théologie"], "contexts": ["study of religious doctrines"]}, {"entity_id": "theologia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Theologia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theologia"], "contexts": ["Religious doctrine"]}, {"entity_id": "theology", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "theology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["theology"], "contexts": ["study of the nature of God"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theology", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "théologie", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theology", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Theologia", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theology", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "theology", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "théologie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Theologia", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "théologie", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "theology", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Theologia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "theology", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 512, "stable_key": "clu_c84b5a51279bb7ac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Theology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34178", "wikidata_description": "study of the nature of deities and religious beliefs", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theology", "wikipedia_extract": "Theology is the study of religious belief from a religious perspective, with a focus on the nature of divinity and the history behind religion. It is taught as an academic discipline, typically in universities and seminaries. It occupies itself with the unique content of analyzing the supernatural, but also deals with religious epistemology, asks and seeks to answer the question of revelation. Revelation pertains to the acceptance of God, gods, or deities, as not only transcendent or above the natural world, but also willing and able to interact with the natural world and to reveal themselves to humankind. Theologians use various forms of analysis and argument (experiential, philosophical, ethnographic, historical, and others) to help understand, explain, test, critique, defend or promote any myriad of religious topics. As in philosophy of ethics and case law, arguments often assume the existence of previously resolved questions, and develop by making analogies from them to draw new inferences in new situations."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Divinity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2345, "target_cluster_name": "Divinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Theology is the study of divinity, and divinity itself refers to the state of being divine or a god.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Theology is the study of divinity, and divinity itself refers to the state of being divine or a god."}, {"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Theology is the study of religious belief.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Theology is the study of religious belief."}, {"found_name": "Scripture", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2670, "target_cluster_name": "Scripture", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Theology is the study of Divinity, often based on Scripture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Theology is the study of Divinity, often based on Scripture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Atheism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 993, "target_cluster_name": "Atheism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atheism is the direct opposite of theistic belief systems, which are the primary subject of theology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Atheism is the direct opposite of theistic belief systems, which are the primary subject of theology."}, {"found_name": "Divine wisdom", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3269, "target_cluster_name": "Divine Wisdom", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Theology is the study of divinity and often explores divine wisdom.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Theology is the study of divinity and often explores divine wisdom."}, {"found_name": "First Cause", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2659, "target_cluster_name": "First Cause", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of a First Cause is a fundamental topic within many theological discussions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of a First Cause is a fundamental topic within many theological discussions."}, {"found_name": "Faith", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_clust