: "Hot water is a manifestation of heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hot water is a manifestation of heat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "solar heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2660, "target_cluster_name": "solar heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Solar heat is a specific form of heat, often discussed in contrast to or as a source of general heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Solar heat is a specific form of heat, often discussed in contrast to or as a source of general heat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Variables", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "degrees of latitude, and produce a belt of calms of that breadth encircling the globe, known as the calms of the equator, or the Variables of seamen. The heat of the sun rarefies the air so much, that", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "known as the calms of the equator, or the Variables of seamen"}]}, {"canonical_name": "figado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 187, "members": [{"entity_id": "alhigado", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "alhigado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["alhigado"], "contexts": ["related to the liver"]}, {"entity_id": "fígado", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fígado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["fígado", "figado"], "contexts": ["organ of the body", "organ that can be obstructed"]}, {"entity_id": "figado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "figado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 181, "variants": ["figado", "Figado", "figado affado", "figado aflado", "figado eſquentado", "figado hep", "figadodemodo", "fígado"], "contexts": ["liver", "organ near the stomach"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alhigado", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fígado", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alhigado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "figado", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fígado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "figado", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 531, "stable_key": "clu_6c1c2dfad9fb7ad7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "liver", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A large organ in the abdomen responsible for many metabolic functions.", "wikipedia_extract": "The liver is a major metabolic organ exclusively found in vertebrates, which performs many essential biological functions such as detoxification of the organism, and the synthesis of various proteins and various other biochemicals necessary for digestion and growth. In humans, it is located in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, below the diaphragm and mostly shielded by the lower right rib cage. Its other metabolic roles include carbohydrate metabolism, the production of a number of hormones, conversion and storage of nutrients such as glucose and glycogen, and the decomposition of red blood cells. Anatomical and medical terminology often use the prefix hepat- from ἡπατο-, from the Greek word for liver, such as hepatology, and hepatitis. The liver is also an accessory digestive organ that produces bile, an alkaline fluid containing cholesterol and bile acids,  which emulsifies and aids the breakdown of dietary fat. The gallbladder, a small hollow pouch that sits just under the right lobe of liver, stores and concentrates the bile produced by the liver, which is later excreted to the duodenum to help with digestion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "hepaticas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ſobeja ha de occafionardoenças , cu camaras fanguinolentas , as quaes ſaó como criticas, & não devem cauſar cuydado. Outras vezesfuccedem por fraqueza do figado , que não podendoaperfeyçoar o ſangue ,", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of bloody flux related to liver weakness"}]}, {"canonical_name": "pedra", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 184, "members": [{"entity_id": "stone", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Stone", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 77, "variants": ["Stone", "Stone in the Bladder", "Stone in the Kidneys", "Stone in the Reins", "Stone in the Reins or Kidneys", "Stones in the Kidneys", "stone"], "contexts": ["material in the body", "broken and expelled by ash-tree leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "pedra_da_bixigua", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pedra da bixigua", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["pedra da bixigua", "pedra da bexiga"], "contexts": ["gallstone or kidney stone", "bladder stones"]}, {"entity_id": "pedra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pedra", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 105, "variants": ["pedra", "Pedra", "PedraNephritica", "achaquesde pedra", "pedra dabexiga", "pedra no bofe", "pedrada bexiga", "pedradabexiga", "pedradosrins", "pedras dos rins", "pedras na bexiga"], "contexts": ["calculus or stone in the body", "stone found in a man's head"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Stone", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pedra", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pedra da bixigua", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pedra", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 532, "stable_key": "clu_b48b80a7fe0fd8c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "calculus", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A hard deposit, such as a kidney stone or gallstone.", "wikipedia_extract": "Calculus is the mathematical study of continuous change, in the same way that geometry is the study of shape and algebra is the study of generalizations of arithmetic operations. Originally called infinitesimal calculus or the calculus of infinitesimals, it has two major branches, differential calculus and integral calculus. Differential calculus analyses instantaneous rates of change and the slopes of curves; integral  calculus analyses accumulation of quantities and areas under or between curves. These two branches are related to each other by the fundamental theorem of calculus. Calculus uses convergence of infinite sequences and infinite series to a well-defined mathematical limit. Calculus is the \"mathematical backbone\" for solving problems in which variable quantities change with time or another reference value."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 170, "members": [{"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["earth"], "contexts": ["planet on which we live", "celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 96, "variants": ["earth", "Earth"], "contexts": ["our planet, a subject of scientific study", "our planet"]}, {"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 58, "variants": ["Earth"], "contexts": ["celestial body, the planet we inhabit", "celestial body, subject of conception"]}, {"entity_id": "globe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "globe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["globe"], "contexts": ["the Earth", "the Earth as a spherical body"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_globe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial globe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["terrestrial globe"], "contexts": ["the Earth", "another term for Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "globe", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "globe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["globe"], "contexts": ["the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "our_planet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "our planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["our planet"], "contexts": ["Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "nossa_terra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "nossa terra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nossa terra"], "contexts": ["local origin of the herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "earth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 533, "stable_key": "clu_2a9ff2e0dd12be32", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global", "wikidata_id": "Q36133", "wikidata_description": "natural body consisting of layers that are primarily composed of minerals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil", "wikipedia_extract": "Soil, also commonly referred to as earth, is a mixture of organic matter, minerals, gases, water, and organisms that together support the life of plants and soil organisms. Some scientific definitions distinguish dirt from soil by restricting the former term specifically to displaced soil. Soil consists of a solid collection of minerals and organic matter (the soil matrix), as well as a porous phase that holds gases (the soil atmosphere) and a liquid phase that holds water and dissolved substances both organic and inorganic, in ionic or in molecular form (the soil solution). Accordingly, soil is a complex three-state system of solids, liquids, and gases. Soil is a product of several factors: the influence of climate, relief (elevation, orientation, and slope of terrain), organisms, and the soil's parent materials (original minerals) interacting over time. It continually undergoes development by way of numerous physical, chemical and biological processes, which include weathering with associated erosion."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Argillaceous slate", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2406, "target_cluster_name": "argillaceous slate", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Argillaceous slate is a type of rock composed primarily of clay minerals, which are a key component of soil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Argillaceous slate is a type of rock composed primarily of clay minerals, which are a key component of soil."}, {"found_name": "Basalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1665, "target_cluster_name": "basalt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Basalt is a common extrusive igneous (volcanic) rock formed from the rapid cooling of basaltic lava exposed at or very near the surface of a terrestrial planet or a moon. It is a major component of the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Basalt is a common extrusive igneous (volcanic) rock formed from the rapid cooling of basaltic lava exposed at or very near the surface of a terrestrial planet or a moon. It is a major component of the Earth's crust."}, {"found_name": "Diorite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1717, "target_cluster_name": "diorite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diorite is an intrusive igneous rock intermediate in composition between volcanic and plutonic rocks. It is found in the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diorite is an intrusive igneous rock intermediate in composition between volcanic and plutonic rocks. It is found in the Earth's crust."}, {"found_name": "Earth's crust", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 884, "target_cluster_name": "earth's crust", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Soil is formed on the Earth's crust, making it a directly related geological layer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Soil is formed on the Earth's crust, making it a directly related geological layer."}, {"found_name": "world", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 163, "target_cluster_name": "world", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'World' in the context of 'World Aquatics' refers to the global scope of the organization's activities, which is intrinsically linked to the planet Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'World' in the context of 'World Aquatics' refers to the global scope of the organization's activities, which is intrinsically linked to the planet Earth.", "is_reverse