74", "source_name": "vomito", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitorios", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 535, "stable_key": "clu_87ada1d52596e2e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Emetics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Substances or practices used to induce vomiting.", "note": "Refers to the concept of inducing vomiting, not the act itself.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vomiting (scientifically known as emesis, and otherwise known as puking, and throwing up) is the forceful expulsion of the contents of one's stomach through the mouth and sometimes the nose. The substance so produced (the erstwhile contents of the stomach, now expelled) is also known as vomit or vomitus. Vomiting can be the result of ailments like food poisoning, gastroenteritis, pregnancy, motion sickness, or hangover; or it can be an after effect of diseases such as brain tumors, elevated intracranial pressure, or overexposure to ionizing radiation. The feeling that one is about to vomit is called nausea; it often precedes, but does not always lead to vomiting. Impairment due to alcohol or anesthesia can cause inhalation of vomit. In severe cases, where dehydration develops, intravenous fluid may be required."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Purging", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1489, "target_cluster_name": "purga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics are a type of purgative, used to expel substances from the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emetics are a type of purgative, used to expel substances from the body."}, {"found_name": "Antidote", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1916, "target_cluster_name": "antidoto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics are sometimes used as a form of treatment or antidote in cases of poisoning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emetics are sometimes used as a form of treatment or antidote in cases of poisoning."}, {"found_name": "Medicine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 209, "target_cluster_name": "Medicina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics are substances used in medical practice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emetics are substances used in medical practice."}, {"found_name": "Evacuation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1559, "target_cluster_name": "evacuação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics cause an evacuation of the stomach's contents.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emetics cause an evacuation of the stomach's contents."}, {"found_name": "evacuação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1559, "target_cluster_name": "evacuação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics induce the evacuation of the stomach.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emetics induce the evacuation of the stomach.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Root", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 140, "members": [{"entity_id": "root", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Root", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Root"], "contexts": ["plant part"]}, {"entity_id": "root", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Root", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 138, "variants": ["Root", "Roots"], "contexts": ["part of Angelica used medicinally", "part of the Arsmart plant"]}, {"entity_id": "root", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "root", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["root"], "contexts": ["a part of a plant used medicinally"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Root", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Root", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Root", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "root", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Root", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "root", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 536, "stable_key": "clu_2260fb5866e5b564", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Root", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "The underground part of a plant, often used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "In vascular plants, the roots are the organs of a plant that are modified to provide anchorage for the plant and take in water and nutrients into the plant body, which allows plants to grow taller and faster. They are most often below the surface of the soil, but roots can also be aerial or aerating, that is, growing up above the ground or especially above water. Roots perform several essential and specialised roles that support plant growth, development and survival. Their primary functions are anchorage, uptake (absorption) of water and dissolved minerals, and conduction of these resources to the shoot. Beyond these, roots carry out a range of important secondary and adaptive functions — storage of reserves, synthesis of growth regulators, gas exchange in waterlogged environments, facilitation of symbiotic nutrient acquisition, and vegetative propagation. Anchorage and mechanical support."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "açafrão", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1887, "target_cluster_name": "Afaro", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "cur^ cuma. Ibn Baithar explica claramente esta deslocação de nome. Paliando do rhizoma da curcuma, diz assim : ... «os habitantes de Basra chamam a esta raiz al-kurkum, e al-kurkum é o açafrão; e cham", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for al-kurkum"}, {"found_name": "Bruscus", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3908, "target_cluster_name": "Bruscus", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Bones and Parts out of Joynt. It is called Bruscus in some places, and in Sussex Kneeholly, and Knecholm. The com∣mon way of using it is to boyl the Roots of it and Parsly, and Fennel, and Smallage in", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a Latin name for the plant whose roots are used"}, {"found_name": "St. Johns Wort", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3754, "target_cluster_name": "ipericaó", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "there is in _Hipericon_ [SN: St. Johns _Wort, so called by Magicians_.] to make good the name of _fuga Dæmonis_, any such Magick as is ascribed unto the Root _Baaras_ by _Josephus_, or _Cynospastus_ b", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for Hipericon"}, {"found_name": "Folio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 358, "target_cluster_name": "Folio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Roots are the underground part of a plant, contrasting with the above-ground leaves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Roots are the underground part of a plant, contrasting with the above-ground leaves.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bark", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1836, "target_cluster_name": "Bark", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bark is the outer layer of a tree trunk or root, and is a