arrots are a root vegetable.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Carrots are a root vegetable.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1688, "target_cluster_name": "gengibre", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "are but the several parts and fruits of the same tree, is the common belief of those which daily use them. Whereof to speak distinctly, Ginger is the root of neither Tree nor Shrub, but of an herbaceo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "is a type of"}, {"found_name": "al-kurkum", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "cur^ cuma. Ibn Baithar explica claramente esta deslocação de nome. Paliando do rhizoma da curcuma, diz assim : ... «os habitantes de Basra chamam a esta raiz al-kurkum, e al-kurkum é o açafrão; e cham", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for curcuma root"}, {"found_name": "ruscus", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "claro; porque o mirtus silpestris he o que chamão ruscus; e os que não falam tam bem latím lhe chamão bruscus; que he huma firutiçe conhe- cida, cuja raiz entra no xarope de raizes: e deste parecer he", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for mirtus silpestris"}, {"found_name": "quirpele", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "outros lhe chamam quirpele) e pelejam com estas serpentes muitas vezes; e se sabe que ha de pe- lejar com ella, ou se teme disso, morde hum pedaço desta raiz que está descuberto, e lambese coma mão, o", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for the plant"}, {"found_name": "imgi", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "e Decanins e Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai", "confidence": 0.8, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for a plant in Malabar"}, {"found_name": "TetterBerries", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "good against al fretting and running Cankers, Gan∣grenes, and Tetters, and therfore the Berries are by some Country People called Tetter∣Berries. The Root clenseth the Skin wonder∣fully from al black ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a vernacular name for berries used against cankers"}, {"found_name": "Kneeholly", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Bones and Parts out of Joynt. It is called Bruscus in some places, and in Sussex Kneeholly, and Knecholm. The com∣mon way of using it is to boyl the Roots of it and Parsly, and Fennel, and Smallage in", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a vernacular name for the plant"}, {"found_name": "Knecholm", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Bones and Parts out of Joynt. It is called Bruscus in some places, and in Sussex Kneeholly, and Knecholm. The com∣mon way of using it is to boyl the Roots of it and Parsly, and Fennel, and Smallage in", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a vernacular name for the plant"}, {"found_name": "dariard", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "mercadoria que se leva muyta pêra Arábia e Pérsia; e nesta cidade ha pouco delia, e no Malavar muyto, scilicet, em Cananor e Calecut. Chamão os Canarins a esta raiz alad; e os Malavares também lhe cha", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "the name for this root used by the Persians, meaning 'yellow wood'"}, {"found_name": "adrac", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "e Decanins e Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for a plant when green"}, {"found_name": "sucte", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "e Decanins e Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for a plant when dry"}]}, {"canonical_name": "humido", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 139, "members": [{"entity_id": "humido", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humido", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["humido", "humedad", "humida", "húmidad"], "contexts": ["moist quality", "quality, moist"]}, {"entity_id": "humida", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "humida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humida"], "contexts": ["quality of an ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "humido", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humido", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 111, "variants": ["humido", "dehumidade", "humidade", "humidadedo ar", "humidades", "humidado", "humidas", "humidi", "humididade", "humiditas", "humidonatural", "humidos", "humidum", "umido", "úmido"], "contexts": ["quality causing ailments", "moist"]}, {"entity_id": "humida", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 21, "variants": ["humida", "humidade da bexiga", "humidade preciſamente neceſſaria"], "contexts": ["moist", "quality of lunar rays"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humido", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "humida", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humido", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humido", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humido", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humida", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "humida", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humido", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "humida", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humida", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 537, "stable_key": "clu_48ac93564522025b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moisture", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the humoral quality of moistness in ancient and medieval medicine.", "wikidata_id": "Q6894868", "wikidata_description": "form of abiotic stress that occurs when the moisture of plant tissues is reduced to suboptimal levels", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moisture%20stress", "wikipedia_extract": "Moisture stress is a form of abiotic stress that occurs when the moisture of plant tissues is reduced to suboptimal levels. Water stress occurs in response to atmospheric and soil water availability when the transpiration rate exceeds the rate of water uptake by the roots and cells lose turgor pressure. Moisture stress is described by two main metrics, water potential and water content. Moisture stress has an effect on stomatal opening, mainly causing a closure in stomata as to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide assimilation. Closing of the stomata also slows the rate of transpiration, which limits water loss and helps to prevent the wilting effects of moisture stress. This closing can be triggered by the roots sensing dry soil and in response producing the hormone ABA which when transported up the xylem into the leaves will reduce stomatal conductance and wall extensibility of growing cells."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humidity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 27, "target_cluster_name": "Humidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'Moisture' and 'Humidity' refer to the presence of water or vapor in the air or other substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Both 'Moisture' and 'Humidity' refer to the presence of water or vapor in the air or other substance."}, {"found_name": "Humoral theory", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 64, "target_cluster_name": "humoral theory", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Moisture is one of the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) in humoral theory, which was a dominant medical paradigm.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Moisture is one of the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) in humoral theory, which was a dominant medical paradigm."}, {"found_name": "Dry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 38, "target_cluster_name": "dry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dryness is the direct opposite of moisture, and these two qualities are often discussed in conjunction, particularly in humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dryness is the direct opposite of moisture, and these two qualities are often discussed in conjunction, particularly in humoral theory."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arábios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 138, "members": [{"entity_id": "arábios", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Arábios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 118, "variants": ["arábios", "Arábios"], "contexts": ["people who discern aloés quality", "people from Arabia"]}, {"entity_id": "arabi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Arabi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Arabi"], "contexts": ["group who wrote about Cardamomo", "authorities on medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "arabes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arabes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Arabes", "Arabum"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authorities", "Arab physicians"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Arábios", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Arabi", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Arábios", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arabes", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Arabi", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arabes", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 538, "stable_key": "clu_6c85b7ad5b196f9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arab physicians", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Medical authorities and writers from the Arab world.", "note": "Refers to people from Arabia, specifically in the context of historical medical knowledge.", "wikipedia_extract": "In the history of medicine, \"Islamic medicine\", also known as \"Arabian medicine\" is the science of medicine developed in the Middle East, and usually written in Arabic, the lingua franca of Islamic civilization. Islamic medicine adopted, systematized and developed the medical knowledge of classical antiquity, including the major traditions of Hippocrates, Galen and Dioscorides. During the post-classical era, Middle Eastern medicine was the most advanced in the world, integrating concepts of Modern Greek, Roman, Mesopotamian and Persian medicine as well as the ancient Indian tradition of Ayurveda, while making numerous advances and innovations. Islamic medicine, along with knowledge of classical medicine, was later adopted in the medieval medicine of Western Europe, after European physicians became familiar with Islamic medical authors during the Renaissance of the 12th century. Medieval Islamic physicians largely retained their authority until the rise of medicine as a part of the natural sciences, beginning with the Age of Enlightenment, nearly six hundred years after their textbooks were opened by many people. Aspects of their writings remain of interest to physicians even today."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "aloa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 306, "target_cluster_name": "Aloe", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "do auctor). Do aloés ha poucas cousas que dizer que sejão notáveis, e porém fazervosây a vontade, e digo que o aloés ou aloa he latino e grego, e os Arábios o chamão cebar, e os Gu- zarates e Decanins", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for aloes"}, {"found_name": "cinamomo", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 44, "target_cluster_name": "Canela", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "de dar mais fé que a esses padres da me- decina, que per falsa enformaçam escreverão. De modo que a que chamão os Gregos e Latinos cinamomo, chamam os Arábios quirfé ou quirfd, e os Pérsios darchini, ", "confidence": 0.8, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Greek and Latin name for the same plant"}, {"found_name": "canela", "link_type": "contested_identity", "link_strength": 0.7, "target_cluster_id": 44, "target_cluster_name": "Canela", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "os causidicos. ORTA Chamam os Arábios á cássia lignea, salihacha; e os Pér- sios assi a chamão; e os índios e os que não sabem física por os livros arábios, lhe chamam o nome que chamam á ca- nela; po", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "confused with cassia lignea in local nomenclature"}, {"found_name": "carabe", "link_type": "contested_identity", "link_strength": 0.7, "target_cluster_id": 2149, "target_cluster_name": "carabe", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "lacre não o tem, posto que Aviçena diz que o tem. E chamárse luc por o Belunense, pode ser que asi o achou escrito nos oríginaes antigos ; porém aguora os Arábios todos o chamam locsumutri; e em repre", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "a substance mistaken for lac"}, {"found_name": "árabes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 253, "targe