zucar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Azucar"], "contexts": ["sugar"]}, {"entity_id": "sugar", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sugar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sugar"], "contexts": ["entry in glossary"]}, {"entity_id": "açucar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "açucar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["açucar", "allucar", "aſlucar", "aſſucar"], "contexts": ["sugar, used in remedies", "sweetener for infusions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sucre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sugar", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sucre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "açucar", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Azucar", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sugar", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Azucar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "açucar", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sugar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "açucar", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 546, "stable_key": "clu_8956eb73e3d0abe4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sugar", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Saccharum officinarum", "family": "Poaceae", "description": "Sweet crystalline substance obtained from various plants, especially sugarcane.", "note": "Refers to the plant source and the substance derived from it.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saccharum officinarum is a large, strong-growing species of grass in the sugarcane genus. Its stout stalks are rich in sucrose, a disaccharide sugar which accumulates in the stalk internodes. It originated in New Guinea, and is now cultivated in tropical and subtropical countries worldwide for the production of sugar, ethanol and other products. It is one of the most productive and most intensively cultivated kinds of sugarcane. Saccharum officinarum, a perennial plant, grows in clumps consisting of a number of strong unbranched stems. A network of rhizomes forms under the soil which sends up secondary shoots near the parent plant."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Saccharum ofBcinarum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3049, "target_cluster_name": "Saccharum ofBcinarum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "primary product derived from sugarcane", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: primary product derived from sugarcane", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "colica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 106, "members": [{"entity_id": "colica", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Colica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 3, "variants": ["colica", "Colica"], "contexts": ["colic, a type of abdominal pain", "colic, a type of illness"]}, {"entity_id": "cólica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cólica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["cólica"], "contexts": ["colic, treated with coconut"]}, {"entity_id": "colica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "colica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 100, "variants": ["colica", "Colic", "Colica", "Colica Nephritica", "Colica biliofa", "Colicas", "colica Nephritica", "colica Pictonica", "colica flatulenta", "colica fleymatica", "colica nephritica", "colicas", "colicas Ictericas", "colicas Nephriticas", "colicas Nepo", "colicas biliofas", "colicas flatuotas", "colicas nephriticas", "colicasNephriticas", "colicis"], "contexts": ["disease dependent on stomach", "colic"]}, {"entity_id": "cólica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cólica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["cólica"], "contexts": ["abdominal pain", "colic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Colica", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cólica", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Colica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "colica", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Colica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cólica", "similarity": 0.806}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cólica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "colica", "similarity": 0.878}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cólica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cólica", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 547, "stable_key": "clu_5c630d01b3e36ba4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Colic", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Severe abdominal pain, often associated with the digestive system.", "wikipedia_extract": "Colic or cholic () is a form of pain that starts and stops abruptly. Colic pain is usually caused by the obstruction of a smooth muscle passageway in the body (e.g. the small or large intestine, bile duct, or ureter). The obstruction triggers muscular contractions (peristalsis) to force the blockage out of the organ, resulting in episodic pain that fluctuates with the contractions. Although the term primarily refers to pain caused by this mechanism, any pain which occurs in episodes or fluctuates in intensity may be termed colic or colicky pain. Colic episodes can cause extreme pain, nausea, sweating, and vomiting. The blockage of important passageways in the body can lead to severe and potentially fatal complications if not cleared, such as cholecystitis in the case of biliary colic, or in kidney stone disease."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 70, "target_cluster_name": "pain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Colic is a type of pain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Colic is a type of pain."}, {"found_name": "Contractions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4201, "target_cluster_name": "contractions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Colic involves muscular contractions (peristalsis) to force a blockage.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Colic involves muscular contractions (peristalsis) to force a blockage."}, {"found_name": "Cramps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1602, "target_cluster_name": "Cramps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Colic pain is often described as cramping or spasmodic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Colic pain is often described as cramping or spasmodic."}]}, {"canonical_name": "association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 103, "members": [{"entity_id": "association", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 81, "variants": ["ASSOCIATION", "Association", "association"], "contexts": ["linking of thoughts or ideas", "linking of ideas or events"]}, {"entity_id": "associations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "associations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["associations"], "contexts": ["connections between ideas or stimuli", "linking of ideas or sensations"]}, {"entity_id": "association", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["association"], "contexts": ["linking of ideas or memories", "linking of ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "association", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["association"], "contexts": ["linking of ideas or thoughts", "linking of ideas or sensations"]}, {"entity_id": "associations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "associations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["associations"], "contexts": ["pre-established links between ideas", "linking of ideas in the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "doctrine_of_association", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "doctrine of association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctrine of association"], "contexts": ["historical development of associationism"]}, {"entity_id": "doctrine_of_correlation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "doctrine of correlation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctrine of correlation"], "contexts": ["implies pre-existence of forces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "association", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "association", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "association", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "association", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "association", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "associations", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "associations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "association", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "associations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "association", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "associations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "associations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "association", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "association", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "association", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "associations", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 548, "stable_key": "clu_74784160ccb88622", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Doctrine of association", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster links 'doctrine of association' with 'doctrine of correlation'. While both are philosophical/scientific doctrines, 'associationism' is a specific school of thought related to mental connections, whereas 'correlation' is a statistical or scientific relationship. The commonality is the concept of relatedness or connection.", "wikidata_id": "Q18355989", "wikidata_description": "ideology proposed by C. Fourier, asserting the future inevitability of communal associations of people co-working and co-living", "wikipedia_extract": "The motte-and-bailey fallacy (named after the motte-and-bailey castle) is a form of argument and an informal fallacy where an arguer conflates two positions that share similarities: one modest and easy to defend (the \"motte\") and one much more controversial and harder to defend (the \"bailey\"). The arguer advances the controversial position, but when challenged, insists that only the more modest position is being advanced. Upon retreating to the motte, the arguer may claim that the bailey has not been refuted (because the critic refused to attack the motte) or that the critic is unreasonable (by equating an attack on the bailey with an attack on the motte). Philosopher Nicholas Shackel, who coined the term, prefers to speak of a motte-and-bailey doctrine instead of a fallacy. In 2005, Shackel described the reference to medieval castle defense like this: A Motte and Bailey castle is a medieval system of defence in which a stone tower on a mound (the Motte) is surrounded by an area of land (the Bailey) which in turn is encompassed by some sort of a barrier such as a ditch. Being dark and dank, the Motte is not a habitation of choice.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourierism"}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct synonym for the Doctrine of Association, representing the same philosophical concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This is a direct synonym for the Doctrine of Association, representing the same philosophical concept."}, {"found_name": "study", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1266, "target_cluster_name": "study", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The doctrine of association, concerning how ideas are linked, is a concept that can be studied and analyzed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The doctrine of association, concerning how ideas are linked, is a concept that can be studied and analyzed.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "traditions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1300, "target_cluster_name": "traditions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Doctrine of association' (or 'association of ideas') is a philosophical and psychological concept that explains how ideas are linked in the mind. This process is closely related to how traditions are formed, transmitted, and maintained within a society or culture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The 'Doctrine of association' (or 'association of ideas') is a philosophical and psychological concept that explains how ideas are linked in the mind. This process is closely related to how traditions are formed, transmitted, and maintained within a society or culture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "continuity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2059, "target_cluster_name": "continuity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Continuity is a fundamental aspect of how ideas are linked and form chains of thought, which is the core of the 'Doctrine of association' concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Continuity is a fundamental aspect of how ideas are linked and form chains of thought, which is the core of the 'Doctrine of association' concept.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Espagne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 9, "total_mentions": 191, "members": [{"entity_id": "espagne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Espagne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 94, "variants": ["Espagne", "Espagn", "Espana", "Espaîia", "espagnol", "espagnoles", "l'Espagne"], "contexts": ["country where Iberians were established", "country with cistes and lentisques"]}, {"entity_id": "spagna", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Spagna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Spagna"], "contexts": ["country visited by some physicians", "place where people would know things"]}, {"entity_id": "spagna", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Spagna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Spagna"], "contexts": ["country with good river waters", "country where Cassia is imported"]}, {"entity_id": "espanha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Espanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 71, "variants": ["Espanha", "Hespanha", "Hespaña"], "contexts": ["country", "place of origin for a herb"]}, {"entity_id": "espanha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Espanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Espanha"], "contexts": ["country of King Carlos II"]}, {"entity_id": "spain", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Spain"], "contexts": ["shore location for compass variation", "country in Southwestern Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "spain", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Spain"], "contexts": ["native land of Turnsole", "country where the plant grows"]}, {"entity_id": "spain", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Spain"], "contexts": ["European country", "country of origin for pointer"]}, {"entity_id": "spain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Spain"], "contexts": ["country with population oscillations", "country with Cortes"]}, {"entity_id": "spain", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Spain"], "contexts": ["In the year 1799 he went to Spain", "southern European country"]}, {"entity_id": "spanish", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Spanish", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Spanish"], "contexts": ["relating to Spain", "origin of Mares"]}, {"entity_id": "spanish", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Spanish", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spanish"], "contexts": ["language and people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Espagne", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Spagna", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Espagne", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Spagna", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Spagna", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Spagna", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 549, "stable_key": "clu_083c37cff4080d59", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spain", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "Q29", "wikidata_description": "country in southwestern Europe with territories in Africa", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain", "wikipedia_extract": "Spain, officially the Kingdom of Spain, is a country in Southern and Western Europe with territories in North Africa. Featuring the southernmost point of continental Europe, it is the largest country in Southern Europe and the fourth-most populous European Union (EU) member state. Spanning the majority of the Iberian Peninsula, its territory also includes the Canary Islands, in the Eastern Atlantic Ocean; the Balearic Islands, in the Western Mediterranean Sea; and the autonomous cities of Ceuta and Melilla, in mainland Africa. Peninsular Spain is bordered to the north by France, Andorra, and the Bay of Biscay; to the east and south by the Mediterranean Sea and Gibraltar; and to the west by Portugal and the Atlantic Ocean. Spain's capital and largest city is Madrid; other major urban areas include Barcelona, Valencia, Seville, Zaragoza, Málaga, Murcia, and Palma de Mallorca. In early antiquity, the Iberian Peninsula was inhabited by Celts, Iberians, and other pre-Roman peoples."}, "member_count": 12, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Spaniards", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3322, "target_cluster_name": "Spaniards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the French name for Spain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the French name for Spain.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Asthma", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 97, "members": [{"entity_id": "astmatick", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Astmatick", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 6, "variants": ["Astmatick"], "contexts": ["suffering from asthma", "condition of shortness of breath"]}, {"entity_id": "asma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "asma", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["asma"], "contexts": ["respiratory illness treated with anacardo"]}, {"entity_id": "asthma", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Asthma", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 90, "variants": ["Asthma", "Afma", "Afthma", "Afthmas", "Afthmaticos", "Asma", "Asthimas", "Asthm.", "Asthmas", "Asthmati", "afma", "asma", "asmas", "asthma", "asthmas", "aſthmas"], "contexts": ["condition treated by bloodletting", "respiratory condition causing difficulty breathing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Astmatick", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "asma", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Astmatick", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Asthma", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "asma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Asthma", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 550, "stable_key": "clu_1a5775e66a84b8a8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Asthma", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A chronic respiratory condition causing difficulty in breathing.", "wikipedia_extract": "Asthma is a common long-term inflammatory disease of the airways. It is characterized by variable and recurring symptoms and reduced lung function. Symptoms include episodes of wheezing, coughing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath. A sudden worsening of asthma symptoms sometimes called an 'asthma attack' or an 'asthma exacerbation' can occur when allergens, pollen, dust, or other particles, are inhaled into the lungs, causing the bronchioles to constrict and produce mucus, which then restricts oxygen flow to the alveoli. These may occur a few times a day or a few times per week. Depending on the person, asthma symptoms may become worse at night or with exercise."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Wheesing", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2097, "target_cluster_name": "Wheesing", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wheezing is a primary symptom of asthma.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wheezing is a primary symptom of asthma."}, {"found_name": "Cough", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1561, "target_cluster_name": "Cough", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Asthma is a chronic respiratory disease characterized by inflammation and narrowing of the airways, often leading to coughing, wheezing, and shortness of breath.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Asthma is a chronic respiratory disease characterized by inflammation and narrowing of the airways, often leading to coughing, wheezing, and shortness of breath.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "latitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 102, "members": [{"entity_id": "latitude", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "latitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 59, "variants": ["latitude", "LATITUDE BORÉALE", "LATITUDES", "latitude australe", "latitude boréale", "latitude méridionale", "latitude nord", "latitudes", "latitudes géographiques", "longitude", "longitudes"], "contexts": ["geographic coordinate", "geographic coordinates"]}, {"entity_id": "latitude", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "latitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["latitude"], "contexts": ["geographical measurement", "angular distance north or south of equator"]}, {"entity_id": "latitude", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Latitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["latitude", "Latitude"], "contexts": ["intermediate between pole and equator", "angular distance north or south of ecliptic"]}, {"entity_id": "latitudes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "latitudes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["latitudes"], "contexts": ["geographic coordinate system", "geographic divisions affecting heat absorption"]}, {"entity_id": "geographical_latitude", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "geographical latitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["geographical latitude"], "contexts": ["latitude based on Earth's shape", "angular distance north or south of equator"]}, {"entity_id": "geographical_latitudes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "geographical latitudes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["geographical latitudes"], "contexts": ["position on Earth's surface"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "latitude", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "latitude", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "latitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Latitude", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "latitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Latitude", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 551, "stable_key": "clu_12df94246f819fae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Latitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34027", "wikidata_description": "angle between zenith and a plane parallel to the equator", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latitude", "wikipedia_extract": "In geography, latitude is a geographic coordinate that specifies the north-south position of a point on the surface of the Earth or another celestial body. Latitude is given as an angle that ranges from −90° at the south pole to 90° at the north pole, with 0° at the Equator. Lines of constant latitude, or parallels, run east-west as circles parallel to the equator. Latitude and longitude are used together as a coordinate pair to specify a location on the surface of the Earth. On its own, the term latitude normally refers to the geodetic latitude as defined below. Briefly, the geodetic latitude of a point is the angle formed between the vector perpendicular (or normal) to the ellipsoidal surface from the point, and the plane of the equator."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Geography", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1068, "target_cluster_name": "Geography", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude is a fundamental concept within the field of geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latitude is a fundamental concept within the field of geography."}, {"found_name": "Celestial body", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3227, "target_cluster_name": "celestial body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude is a coordinate used to specify position on a celestial body, including the Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latitude is a coordinate used to specify position on a celestial body, including the Earth."}, {"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude, in astronomical contexts, is often measured relative to the ecliptic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latitude, in astronomical contexts, is often measured relative to the ecliptic."}, {"found_name": "Aphelion", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 864, "target_cluster_name": "Aphelion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aphelion is a point in an orbit, and latitude can be used to describe positions relative to orbital planes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aphelion is a point in an orbit, and latitude can be used to describe positions relative to orbital planes."}, {"found_name": "Elliptical orbit", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude can be used to describe positions within an elliptical orbit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latitude can be used to describe positions within an elliptical orbit."}, {"found_name": "temperate regions", "link_type": "conceptual_ov