l", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herchel"], "contexts": ["name correction in transcription notes"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_j_", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Sir J.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sir J."], "contexts": ["individual mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_john", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sir John", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sir John"], "contexts": ["scientist discussing solar phenomena", "quoted on solar and earth's atmospheric effects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir John Herschel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sir John Herschel", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir John Herschel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sir John Herschel’s", "similarity": 0.97}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir John Herschel", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir John Herschel", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Sir John Herschel", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir John Herschel", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Sir John Herschel’s", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir John Herschel", "similarity": 0.97}], "id": 554, "stable_key": "clu_6dbd4639018bf39b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Herschel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1792, "death_year": 1871, "description": "Astronomer and mathematician, son of William Herschel.", "wikidata_id": "Q14278", "wikidata_description": "English polymath, mathematician, astronomer, chemist, inventor and photographer (*1792 – †1871)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Herschel", "note": "The context of 'connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858' and discussions of solar phenomena strongly point to Sir John Herschel.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir John Frederick William Herschel, 1st Baronet  (; 7 March 1792 – 11 May 1871) was an English polymath active as a mathematician, astronomer, chemist, inventor and experimental photographer who invented the blueprint and did botanical work. Herschel originated the use of the Julian day system in astronomy. He named seven moons of Saturn and four moons of Uranus – the seventh planet, discovered by his father Sir William Herschel. He made many contributions to the science of photography, and investigated colour blindness and the chemical power of ultraviolet rays. His Preliminary Discourse (1831), which advocated an inductive approach to scientific experiment and theory-building, was an important contribution to the philosophy of science. Herschel was born in Slough, Buckinghamshire, the son of Mary Baldwin and astronomer Sir William Herschel."}, "member_count": 9, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent scientists and explorers who made significant contributions to natural history and geography. Humboldt's extensive work in exploration and scientific observation likely influenced or was known to Herschel.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent scientists and explorers who made significant contributions to natural history and geography. Humboldt's extensive work in exploration and scientific observation likely influenced or was known to Herschel."}, {"found_name": "Charles Darwin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 118, "target_cluster_name": "Darwin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century science. Darwin's work on evolution and Herschel's contributions to astronomy and photography represent major advancements in different fields of science during the same era.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century science. Darwin's work on evolution and Herschel's contributions to astronomy and photography represent major advancements in different fields of science during the same era."}, {"found_name": "Isaac Newton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 71, "target_cluster_name": "Newton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newton was a foundational figure in physics and mathematics, and Herschel, as an astronomer and mathematician, worked within the scientific tradition established by Newton. Herschel's astronomical work, in particular, built upon Newtonian principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Newton was a foundational figure in physics and mathematics, and Herschel, as an astronomer and mathematician, worked within the scientific tradition established by Newton. Herschel's astronomical work, in particular, built upon Newtonian principles."}, {"found_name": "Sir William Herschel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1701, "target_cluster_name": "Sir William Herschel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Herschel was the son of William Herschel and also a prominent astronomer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Herschel was the son of William Herschel and also a prominent astronomer.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dalton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2442, "target_cluster_name": "Dalton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Herschel was a prominent astronomer and physicist who also made contributions to mathematics and optics. His work, like Dalton's, was foundational in several scientific disciplines and he was a respected figure in the scientific community of the 19th century.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Herschel was a prominent astronomer and physicist who also made contributions to mathematics and optics. His work, like Dalton's, was foundational in several scientific disciplines and he was a respected figure in the scientific community of the 19th century.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Deos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 10, "total_mentions": 139, "members": [{"entity_id": "deos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Deos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 22, "variants": ["Deos", "Deus", "deuses"], "contexts": ["God, invoked in speech", "God"]}, {"entity_id": "deos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Deos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 62, "variants": ["Deos", "Deos immortal", "Deæ", "Dio", "dio", "dios"], "contexts": ["God", "God, the greatest benefactor of the world"]}, {"entity_id": "deities", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "deities", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["deities"], "contexts": ["divine beings"]}, {"entity_id": "god", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "God", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 13, "variants": ["God", "GOD"], "contexts": ["ultimate origin of being and perfection", "divine commandement"]}, {"entity_id": "god", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "God", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 13, "varia