ld freeze on Mars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Azogue", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "azougue", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Azogue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azougue", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azougue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azougue", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 559, "stable_key": "clu_bae5a353b1b58807", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quicksilver", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q925", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Hg and atomic number 80", "wikipedia_extract": "Quicksilver is a 2024 romantasy novel by British author Callie Hart, the first in her Fae & Alchemy trilogy. Hart self-published the book in June 2024 after declining her literary agent's request for substantial revisions. Initial sales were modest, but the book found an audience after Hart sent copies to BookTok creators on TikTok, generating over three million views within two months. The viral attention sparked a 10-bidder auction for publishing rights. Forever, an imprint of Grand Central Publishing, won the auction, releasing a traditional edition on December 3, 2024 that debuted at number one on The New York Times Best Seller list. That same week, Netflix acquired film adaptation rights for seven figures.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quicksilver_(Hart_novel)", "note": "'Azougue' and 'Azogue' are archaic terms for mercury, commonly used in Iberian languages."}, "member_count": 11, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lithargyro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 775, "target_cluster_name": "Lithargyro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Litharge (lead oxide) and Quicksilver (mercury) are metallic oxides and 