he healing process or complication of wounds.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "reumas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2224, "target_cluster_name": "reumas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rheumatic disorders are often characterized by inflammation of the joints or connective tissues.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Rheumatic disorders are often characterized by inflammation of the joints or connective tissues.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Canafistula", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 56, "members": [{"entity_id": "cañafistola", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cañafistola", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Cañafistola", "Cañafiltola", "Cañafiltoía en cólerua", "Cañafito"], "contexts": ["plant used for purgative pulp", "plant discussed in the following section"]}, {"entity_id": "canafistola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "canafistola", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["canafistola", "Canafistola", "canajistola", "canefistola", "canáfistola"], "contexts": ["fruit of the tree", "plant with medicinal pulp"]}, {"entity_id": "canafistula", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Canafistula", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 38, "variants": ["Canafistula", "Cana fistula", "Canafiftula", "Canaristula", "canafi", "canafiftula", "canafifula", "canafil tula", "canafistula", "canafistula freſca", "canafiſtu", "canafiſtula", "canasiſtula", "caſcasde canafiſtula"], "contexts": ["fresh cassia pulp", "plant used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Cañafistola", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "canafistola", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "canafistola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Canafistula", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 582, "stable_key": "clu_6b605913393f3e39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cassia fistula", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cassia fistula", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "A flowering plant whose pulp is used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cassia fistula, also known as golden shower, purging cassia, Indian laburnum, kani konna, or pudding-pipe tree, is a flowering plant in the family Fabaceae. The species is native to the Indian subcontinent and adjacent regions of Southeast Asia. It is the official state flower of Kerala state and Delhi UT in India, as well as the national tree and flower of Thailand. It is also a popular ornamental plant and is also used in herbal medicine. Cassia fistula* is widely known by local names across the Indian subcontinent and other regions due to its prominent medicinal, cultural, and ornamental importance. The golden shower tree is a medium-sized tree, growing to 10–20 m (33–66 ft) tall with fast growth."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Leguminosa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2184, "target_cluster_name": "Leguminosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fabaceae is also known as Leguminosa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fabaceae is also known as Leguminosa."}, {"found_name": "Senna", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 668, "target_cluster_name": "Senne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula is closely related to plants in the genus Senna, and historically, the distinction between Cassia and Senna was sometimes blurred or debated in medicinal contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cassia fistula is closely related to plants in the genus Senna, and historically, the distinction between Cassia and Senna was sometimes blurred or debated in medicinal contexts."}, {"found_name": "Turbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 255, "target_cluster_name": "Turbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Turpeth and Cassia fistula are purgative plants used in traditional medicine, and historical texts sometimes conflated or closely associated them due to similar medicinal properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Turpeth and Cassia fistula are purgative plants used in traditional medicine, and historical texts sometimes conflated or closely associated them due to similar medicinal properties.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cássia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 317, "target_cluster_name": "cássia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula is a specific species within the genus Cassia, and thus directly related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cassia fistula is a specific species within the genus Cassia, and thus directly related.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cassia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 317, "target_cluster_name": "cássia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula is a species within the genus Cassia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cassia fistula is a species within the genus Cassia."}, {"found_name": "Caesalpinia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4040, "target_cluster_name": "Cœsalpinia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula was formerly classified under the genus Caesalpinia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cassia fistula was formerly classified under the genus Caesalpinia."}, {"found_name": "Fabaceae", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2184, "target_cluster_name": "Leguminosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula belongs to the family Fabaceae.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cassia fistula belongs to the family Fabaceae."}, {"found_name": "Senne", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 668, "target_cluster_name": "Senne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cassia fistula is a species within the genus Cassia, which is closely related to Senna. Both are known for their medicinal uses, particularly as laxatives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cassia fistula is a species within the genus Cassia, which is closely related to Senna. Both are known for their medicinal uses, particularly as laxatives.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mirabolani", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1647, "target_cluster_name": "Mirabolani", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are species within the broader category of myrobalans, often used in traditional medicine and dyeing. Cassia fistula is a specific type of cassia tree, and myrobalans are fruits from trees in the Terminalia genus, which are sometimes grouped with or confused with cassia due to similar medicinal properties and appearance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are species within the broader category of myrobalans, often used in traditional medicine and dyeing. Cassia fistula is a specific type of cassia tree, and myrobalans are fruits from trees in the Terminalia genus, which are sometimes grouped with or confused with cassia due to similar medicinal properties and appearance.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Myrrha", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 54, "members": [{"entity_id": "myrrhe", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Myrrhe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Myrrhe"], "contexts": ["aromatic resin"]}, {"entity_id": "myrrha", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Myrrha", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 31, "variants": ["Myrrha", "Myrra", "Myrrba", "Myrrhatroglody", "Myrrna", "myrrha"], "contexts": ["resin with strong odor", "resin from an Arabian tree"]}, {"entity_id": "myrrha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "myrrha", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 22, "variants": ["myrrha", "Myrrha"], "contexts": ["myrrh used in a liniment", "aromatic resin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Myrrhe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Myrrha", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Myrrha", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "myrrha", "similarity": 0.723}], "id": 583, "stable_key": "clu_45435d58660b7179", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Myrrh", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Commiphora myrrha", "family": "Burseraceae", "note": "Although Myrrh is a resin, it is derived from plants.", "wikidata_id": "Q1137175", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_extract": "A resin is a solid or highly viscous liquid that can be converted into a polymer. Resins may be biological or synthetic in origin, but are typically harvested from plants. Resins are mixtures of organic compounds insoluble in water, predominantly terpenes. Technically, resins should not be confused with gums, which consist predominantly of water-soluble polysaccharides, although these two terms are often interchangeable in the less formal context. Common resins include pine oleoresins, amber, hashish, frankincense, myrrh and the animal-derived resin, shellac. Resins are used in varnishes, adhesives, food additives, incenses and perfumes.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicely"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bdellio", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 947, "target_cluster_name": "Bdellio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bdellium and myrrh are both resins from the Burseraceae family, often used similarly in incense and traditional medicine, and historically confused or grouped together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bdellium and myrrh are both resins from the Burseraceae family, often used similarly in incense and traditional medicine, and historically confused or grouped together.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Frankincense", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 653, "target_cluster_name": "incenso", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Myrrh and Frankincense are both aromatic resins harvested from trees, often used together in ancient religious and medicinal practices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Myrrh and Frankincense are both aromatic resins harvested from trees, often used together in ancient religious and medicinal practices."}, {"found_name": "Bdellium", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 947, "target_cluster_name": "Bdellio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bdellium is another resinous substance, often mentioned alongside myrrh in historical texts, and sometimes confused with it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bdellium is another resinous substance, often mentioned alongside myrrh in historical texts, and sometimes confused with it."}, {"found_name": "Storax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2505, "target_cluster_name": "Storace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Storax is a balsamic resin, similar in its aromatic properties and historical uses to myrrh.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Storax is a balsamic resin, similar in its aromatic properties and historical uses to myrrh."}, {"found_name": "Gomma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2355, "target_cluster_name": "Gomma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Myrrh is a resinous substance obtained from trees of the genus Commiphora, making it a specific type of plant gum/resin.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Myrrh is a resinous substance obtained from trees of the genus Commiphora, making it a specific type of plant gum/resin.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ladano", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2640, "target_cluster_name": "Ladano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Labdanum and Myrrh are aromatic resins with historical medicinal and perfumery uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Labdanum and Myrrh are aromatic resins with historical medicinal and perfumery uses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "estoraque", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2873, "target_cluster_name": "estoraque", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Storax is a natural fragrant resin, and 'Myrrh' is another type of fragrant resin often discussed in similar contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Storax is a natural fragrant resin, and 'Myrrh' is another type of fragrant resin often discussed in similar co