ctor and former disc jockey. He is co-founder of the London-based production company Shona Productions with his wife Zoe Stewart. He directed the crime drama television series The Five which first aired in April 2016 on the Sky 1 channel. Tonderai began his career in the media working as a writer, producer and presenter for radio. From early 1993 until late 1998 Tonderai was heavily involved in radio, and was the writer and producer of The Mark Tonderai Show, hosted on BBC Radio 1, and the Jam. Tonderai was also involved in numerous BBC Radio 4 projects, such as the Worldly Wise, Rainbow Nation and Week Ending, as well as Kiss 100's Rude Awakening, hosted on Kiss 100 London."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Buuas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2225, "target_cluster_name": "Buuas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Plague' is a direct synonym for Bubonic plague, often used interchangeably in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'Plague' is a direct synonym for Bubonic plague, often used interchangeably in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Guts", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2087, "target_cluster_name": "Guts", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "not long since there was a raging Diseas called the Bloody Flux, the Colledg of Physitians not knowing what to make of it, called it the Plague in the Guts, for their wits were at ne plus ultra about ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: also called the Plague in the Guts", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "bexigas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 565, "target_cluster_name": "bexigas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plague (peste) was another devastating epidemic disease that frequently occurred alongside or in the same historical periods as smallpox, leading to comparisons and shared discussions in medical literature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Plague (peste) was another devastating epidemic disease that frequently occurred alongside or in the same historical periods as smallpox, leading to comparisons and shared discussions in medical literature.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bubões", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1751, "target_cluster_name": "Bubões", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buboes are a primary symptom of the plague, a specific and historically significant disease.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Buboes are a primary symptom of the plague, a specific and historically significant disease.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 54, "members": [{"entity_id": "madrid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 45, "variants": ["Madrid"], "contexts": ["city where Portulano was published", "city name"]}, {"entity_id": "madrid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Madrid"], "contexts": ["court of Madrid"]}, {"entity_id": "madrid", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Madrid"], "contexts": ["city where book was published", "city of publication"]}, {"entity_id": "new-madrid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "New-Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New-Madrid"], "contexts": ["location of mounds"]}, {"entity_id": "new_madrid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["New Madrid"], "contexts": ["location in Missouri, USA", "location of hot steam ejection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Madrid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Madrid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Madrid", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Madrid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Madrid", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Madrid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 589, "stable_key": "clu_873aed685508f355", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Madrid", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLACE", "description": "Capital city of Spain, a significant location in historical texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Madrid is the capital and most populous city of Spain. It had a population of over 3.4 million in the city proper in 2025, and a metropolitan area population of approximately 6.8 million. Madrid is the second-largest city in the European Union (EU), after Berlin, and its metropolitan area is the second-largest in the EU, after Paris. The municipality covers an area of 605.77 km2 (233.89 sq mi). Madrid lies on the River Manzanares in the central part of the Iberian Peninsula at about 660 m (2,170 ft) above mean sea level. The capital city of both Spain and the surrounding autonomous community of Madrid, it is the political, economic and cultural centre of the country."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Berlin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 267, "target_cluster_name": "Berlin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Madrid and Berlin are both major European capitals and are explicitly compared in the description as the second-largest city in the EU.