le, Marco Poio, n, 378; Lembranças da Imdea, 39, nos Subsídios; Fernão Mendes Pinto, Peregrin. cap. xum). Nota (4) Orta refere-se a uma de duas ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "a plant possibly referred to as"}, {"found_name": "kamctranga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "me- dico. (Cf. Duarte Barbosa, Livro, 283, 365 e 374; Yule, Marco Poio, n, 378; Lembranças da Imdea, 39, nos Subsídios; Fernão Mendes Pinto, Peregrin. cap. xum). Nota (4) Orta refere-se a uma de duas ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "a plant possibly referred to as"}]}, {"canonical_name": "distilled Water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 49, "members": [{"entity_id": "distilled_water", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "distilled Water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 47, "variants": ["distilled Water", "Distilled Water", "Distilled Waters", "Distilled water", "distilled water", "distil∣led Water", "dististilled Water"], "contexts": ["liquid prepared by distillation", "water from plantain used for washing sores"]}, {"entity_id": "agua_destilada", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "agua destilada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua destilada"], "contexts": ["distilled water of the herb"]}, {"entity_id": "distilled_water", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "distilled water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["distilled water"], "contexts": ["used for washing the plate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "distilled Water", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "agua destilada", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "distilled Water", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "distilled water", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 595, "stable_key": "clu_55349767f64b3924", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "distilled water", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q274959", "wikidata_description": "water that has had many of its impurities removed through distillation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distilled%20water", "wikipedia_extract": "Distilled water is water that has been purified by boiling it into vapor and condensing the vapor back into liquid in a separate container. Any impurities in the original water, such as non-volatile or mineral components, that do not boil below or near the boiling point of water remain in the original container. For example, water escaping as steam from a boiler of heating system or steam engine, leaves behind any dissolved materials, which leads to mineral deposits known as boiler scale. In general, non-purified water can leave behind mineral deposits, or could cause or interfere with chemical reactions. Distillation is a method for removing impurities from water and other fluids. Distilled water has not been proven to be healthier for drinking than hard water."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "freshwater", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2794, "target_cluster_name": "fresh water", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Distilled water is a purified form of freshwater.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Distilled water is a purified form of freshwater."}, {"found_name": "Diarhodon Abhatis", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": 2950, "target_cluster_name": "Diarhodon Abhatis", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "more usually called Su∣gar of Roses; Syrup of dryed Roses, and Ho∣ney of Roses; The Cordial Pouder called Diarhodon Abhatis', and Aromatica Rosa∣rum; The Distilled Water of Roses, Vine∣gar of Roses, O", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a cordial powder made from roses."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Marco Polo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 48, "members": [{"entity_id": "marco_polo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Marco Polo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marco Polo"], "contexts": ["narrator of riches of India and Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "marco_polo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Marco Polo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 43, "variants": ["Marco Polo"], "contexts": ["gave exact news on amber extraction", "author of 'Marco Polo'"]}, {"entity_id": "marco_polo", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Marco Polo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Marco Polo"], "contexts": ["felt difficulty on mountains of Central Asia", "Venetian traveler in Central Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Marco Polo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Marco Polo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Marco Polo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Marco Polo", "similarity": 0.797}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Marco Polo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Marco Polo", "similarity": 0.797}], "id": 596, "stable_key": "clu_25f0beb7df74636d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marco Polo", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Venetian merchant and explorer known for his travels to Asia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Marco Polo (c. 1254 –  8 January 1324) was a Venetian merchant, explorer, and writer who travelled through Asia along the Silk Road between 1271 and 1295. His travels are recorded in The Travels of Marco Polo (also known as Book of the Marvels of the World  and Il Milione, c. 1300), a book that described the then-mysterious culture and inner workings of the Eastern world, including the wealth and great size of the Mongol Empire and China under the Yuan dynasty, giving Europeans their first comprehensive look into China, Persia, India, Japan, and other Asian societies. Born in Venice, Marco learned the mercantile trade from his father and his uncle, Niccolò and Maffeo, who travelled through Asia and met Kublai Khan. In 1269, they returned to Venice to meet Marco for the first time. The three of them embarked on an epic journey to Asia, exploring many places along the Silk Road until they reached \"Cathay\". They were received by the royal court of Kublai Khan, who was impressed by Marco's intelligence and humility."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Christopher Columbus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 327, "target_cluster_name": "Columbus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world."}, {"found_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3017, "target_cluster_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world."}, {"found_name": "Giovanni Battista Ramusio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1656, "target_cluster_name": "Ramusio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ramusio was a geographer and editor who compiled and published accounts of voyages, including those of explorers like Marco Polo, making their discoveries accessible to a wider audience.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ramusio was a geographer and editor who compiled and published accounts of voyages, including those of explorers like Marco Polo, making their discoveries accessible to a wider audience."}, {"found_name": "Ramusio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1656, "target_cluster_name": "Ramusio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were renowned travelers and writers whose accounts of distant lands influenced European perceptions of the world and inspired further exploration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were renowned travelers and writers whose accounts of distant lands influenced European perceptions of the world and inspired further exploration.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 60, "members": [{"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Intelligence", "intelligence"], "contexts": ["capacity for reasoning and understanding", "ability to learn and reason"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intelligence"], "contexts": ["capacity for learning and reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 20, "variants": ["Intelligence", "intelligence"], "contexts": ["human capacity for learning and reasoning", "capacity for learning and reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intellect", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Intellect", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Intellect"], "contexts": ["divine mind holding original ideas", "faculty susceptible to weakness"]}, {"entity_id": "intellection", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intellection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intellection"], "contexts": ["process of understanding and reasoning", "process of understanding or reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intellect", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "intellect", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["intellect"], "contexts": ["faculty of reasoning and understanding", "faculty for comprehending nature"]}, {"entity_id": "intellect", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "intellect", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intellect"], "contexts": ["faculty of reasoning and understanding"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Intelligence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Intelligence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 597, "stable_key": "clu_d9c43d1a7a5ac499", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "intelligence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q183560", "wikidata_description": "generalized neurodevelopmental disorder"}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Throat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 77, "members": [{"entity_id": "throat", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Throat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 44, "variants": ["Throat"], "contexts": ["body part gargled with decoction", "body part"]}, {"entity_id": "throat", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "throat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["throat"], "contexts": ["part of the neck behind the mouth", "air passes through"]}, {"entity_id": "throat", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "throat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["throat"], "contexts": ["part of the neck"]}, {"entity_id": "garganta", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "garganta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["garganta"], "contexts": ["throat part"]}, {"entity_id": "garganta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "garganta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["garganta"], "contexts": ["body part"]}, {"entity_id": "garganta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "garganta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 28, "variants": ["garganta", "Garganta"], "contexts": ["body part", "part of the throat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Throat", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "throat", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Throat", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "throat", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "throat", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "throat", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 598, "stable_key": "clu_2ca6866e6da625f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Throat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Garganta' is the direct translation of 'throat' in Portuguese and Spanish, referring to the anatomical region.", "wikidata_id": "Q1783924", "wikidata_description": "organ system cancer in the head or neck region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head%20and%20neck%20cancer", "wikipedia_extract": "Head and neck cancer is a general term encompassing multiple cancers that can develop in the head and neck region. These include cancers of the mouth, tongue, gums and lips (oral cancer), voice box (laryngeal), throat (nasopharyngeal, oropharyngeal, hypopharyngeal), salivary glands, nose and sinuses. Head and neck cancer can present a wide range of symptoms depending on where the cancer developed. These can include an ulcer in the mouth that does not heal, changes in the voice, difficulty swallowing, red or white patches in the mouth, and a neck lump. The majority of head and neck cancer is caused by the use of alcohol or tobacco (including smokeless tobacco). An increasing number of cases are caused by the human papillomavirus (HPV)."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Larynx", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1261, "target_cluster_name": "laryngis mufculi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The larynx is part of the throat, also known as the voice box.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The larynx is part of the throat, also known as the voice box."}, {"found_name": "Glottis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2735, "target_cluster_name": "glottis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The glottis is a part of the throat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The glottis is a part of the throat."}, {"found_name": "Esophagus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 641, "target_cluster_name": "Ofofago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The esophagus is a part of the throat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The esophagus is a part of the throat."}, {"found_name": "boca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 649, "target_cluster_name": "boca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The throat is anatomically connected to the mouth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The throat is anatomically connected to the mouth.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Neck", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2031, "target_cluster_name": "Neck", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The throat is a part of the neck.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The throat is a part of the neck.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 47, "members": [{"entity_id": "cera", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["cera"], "contexts": ["wax used to collect balsam", "wax used in ointments"]}, {"entity_id": "cera", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "cera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 20, "variants": ["cera", "Cera", "cera tedesca"], "contexts": ["substance for adulteration", "substance that dissolves in water like milk"]}, {"entity_id": "cera", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 25, "variants": ["cera"], "contexts": ["ingredient in masticatories", "ingredient in an emplaster"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cera", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "cera", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cera", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cera", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "cera", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cera", "similarity": 0.839}], "id": 599, "stable_key": "clu_510876298ce3bba5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wax", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A malleable substance derived from bees or plants, used in preparations.", "note": "Includes mentions of 'Ceruss' (white lead), which is a distinct substance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Waxes are a diverse class of organic compounds that are lipophilic solids that are  malleable near ambient temperatures. They include higher alkanes and lipids, typically with melting points above about 40 °C (104 °F), melting to give low viscosity liquids. Waxes are insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar organic solvents such as hexane, benzene and chloroform. Natural waxes of various types are produced by plants and animals and occur in petroleum. Waxes are organic compounds that characteristically consist of long aliphatic alkyl chains, although aromatic compounds may also be present. Natural waxes may contain unsaturated bonds and include various functional groups such as fatty acids, primary and secondary alcohols, ketones, aldehydes and fatty acid esters."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cera bianca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "gns, & leggiermente fi {prc ma ; riftornando di nuouo à infonder ui altre rck come da prima; facendo cosi fino « , alla terza infufione,& cola- Cera bianca to l'vltima volta per ogni li- olio rofàto b", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of cera"}, {"found_name": "cire de Cuba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "evidence_snippet": "terre et la réfrac- tion, 1° 47' 10\". Haut. , 2^4 toises. ^ Voyez plus haut, p. 33o. J'ai déjà fait remarquer (p. 427) que la cire de Cuba, qui est un objet de commerce très-important, est due à des a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "specific type of cire"}]}, {"canonical_name": "skin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 46, "members": [{"entity_id": "skin", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "skin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["skin"], "contexts": ["can disturb or relieve the head"]}, {"entity_id": "skin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "skin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 23, "variants": ["skin"], "contexts": ["outer protective covering of the body", "outer protective layer of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "pelle", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pelle", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 22, "variants": ["pelle", "pelles"], "contexts": ["skin that can be constipated", "skin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "skin", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "skin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "skin", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pelle", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "skin", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pelle", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 600, "stable_key": "clu_11ab1305492f7c31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Skin", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'skin' is a physical organ, in these contexts it refers to the concept of the outer covering and its properties.", "wikidata_id": "Q272206", "wikidata_description": "family of reptiles (Reptilia)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin", "wikipedia_extract": "Skin is the layer of usually soft, flexible outer tissue covering the body of a vertebrate animal, with three main functions: protection, regulation, and sensation. Other animal coverings, such as the arthropod exoskeleton, have different developmental origin, structure and chemical composition. The adjective cutaneous means \"of the skin\" (from Latin cutis 'skin'). In mammals, the skin is an organ of the integumentary system made up of multiple layers of ectodermal tissue and guards the underlying muscles, bones, ligaments, and internal organs. Skin of a different nature exists in amphibians, reptiles, and birds. Skin (including cutaneous and subcutaneous tissues) plays crucial roles in formation, structure, and function of extraskeletal apparatus such as horns of bovids (e.g., cattle) and rhinos, cervids' antlers, giraffids' ossicones, armadillos' osteoderm, and os penis/os clitoris."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bodily parts", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3109, "target_cluster_name": "bodily parts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin is the outer covering of the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Skin is the outer covering of the body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Galls", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "OMICRON~}{~GREEK SMALL LETTER FINAL SIGMA~}] it carrieth the gall in the head, is farther to be enquired. And though the discoloured particles in the skin of an Heron be commonly termed Galls, yet is ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for discolored particles in the skin of an Heron"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Choller", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 46, "members": [{"entity_id": "choller", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Choller", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 41, "variants": ["Choller", "Chol"], "contexts": ["humor associated with anger and bile", "bodily fluid governed by Mars"]}, {"entity_id": "choleras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "choleras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["choleras", "cholerícos"], "contexts": ["type of humors evacuated by the root", "humors described as choleric"]}, {"entity_id": "cholericis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cholericis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["cholericis"], "contexts": ["body humors with ailments", "choleric humors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Choller", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "choleras", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Choller", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cholericis", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "choleras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cholericis", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 601, "stable_key": "clu_3be1b55806cdf973", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Choleric humor", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to one of the four humors of ancient and medieval medicine, associated with bile and temperament, not the modern disease cholera."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "baço", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1527, "target_cluster_name": "baço", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spleen was historically associated with the production or regulation of humors, particularly choleric humor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The spleen was historically associated with the production or regulation of humors, particularly choleric humor.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fangue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1566, "target_cluster_name": "fangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Blood was associated with the choleric humor in humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Blood was associated with the choleric humor in humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "humores melancolicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1814, "target_cluster_name": "humores melancolicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melancholic humors are one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside choleric, sanguine, and phlegmatic humors. They are distinct but part of the same theoretical framework.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Melancholic humors are one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside choleric, sanguine, and phlegmatic humors. They are distinct but part of the same theoretical framework.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "phlegm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2513, "target_cluster_name": "phlegm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phlegm is one of the four humors in ancient and medieval medicine, alongside choler (yellow bile), blood, and melancholy (black bile). Choleric humor is a specific type of bile, closely related to the humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phlegm is one of the four humors in ancient and medieval medicine, alongside choler (yellow bile), blood, and melancholy (black bile). Choleric humor is a specific type of bile, closely related to the humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "volcans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 46, "members": [{"entity_id": "volcans", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "volcans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 31, "variants": ["volcans", "Volcancitos", "Volcans", "Volcans d'air", "Volcans de Guatimala", "Volcans des Antilles", "volcans boueux", "volcans d'air", "volcans de tdrbaco"], "contexts": ["natural phenomena", "natural geological formations"]}, {"entity_id": "volcano", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Volcano", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 11, "variants": ["Volcano", "volcano"], "contexts": ["igneous mountain activity", "geological formation erupting lava"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_volcanoes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial volcanoes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["terrestrial volcanoes"], "contexts": ["volcanic force on Earth", "volcanoes on Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_mountains", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic mountains"], "contexts": ["mountains formed by volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanoes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "volcanoes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanoes"], "contexts": ["vents for molten rock and gases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "volcans", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Volcano", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "volcans", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "terrestrial volcanoes", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "volcans", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volcanic mountains", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "volcans", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volcanoes", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Volcano", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volcanoes", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "terrestrial volcanoes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volcanoes", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "volcanic mountains", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volcanoes", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 602, "stable_key": "clu_c1a28a08bcc5cf29", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcano", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q2428769", "wikidata_description": "census-designated place (CDP) in Hawaii County, Hawaii, United States", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano%2C%20Hawaii", "wikipedia_extract": "Volcano, or Volcano Village, is a census-designated place (CDP) in Hawaii County, Hawaii, United States located in the District of Puna with a small portion of the CDP in the District of Kaʻū. The population was 2,575 at the 2010 census, up from 2,231 at the 2000 census. Volcano is located on the east side of the island of Hawaiʻi, on the border of Hawaii Volcanoes National Park and near the northeast rim of Kīlauea's summit caldera. According to the United States Census Bureau, the CDP has a total area of 58.6 square miles (151.7 km2), all of it land. Hawaii Route 11 passes through the southern part of the community, leading northeast 27 miles (43 km) to Hilo and southwest 37 miles (60 km) to Nāʻālehu. Its altitude ranges from below 2,800 feet (850 m) in the southeast to over 6,200 feet (1,900 m) at its uninhabited northern end."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chimborazo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 708, "target_cluster_name": "Chimborazo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chimborazo is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chimborazo is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes."}, {"found_name": "Cotopaxi", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1777, "target_cluster_name": "Cotopaxi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cotopaxi is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cotopaxi is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes."}, {"found_name": "Antisana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1867, "target_cluster_name": "Antisana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antisana is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antisana is a volcano in Ecuador, and Volcano is a CDP in Hawaii known for its proximity to Kīlauea, an active volcano. Both entities are related to the geological phenomenon of volcanoes."}, {"found_name": "volcano, properly so called", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "raised from its depths, while, on the other hand, those near promontories appear (according to reason) to have been separated from the continent.\" A volcano, properly so called, exists only where a pe", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Volcano"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Chili", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 53, "members": [{"entity_id": "chili", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Chili", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chili"], "contexts": ["country in Western South America"]}, {"entity_id": "chili", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chili", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 34, "variants": ["Chili"], "contexts": ["country inhabited by people compared to Caribes", "geographic location with pronounced so