_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This entry directly links emotions with 'will', suggesting a historical exploration of their interplay and interdependence, a specific and notable relationship."}, {"found_name": "Discord", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1209, "target_cluster_name": "discord", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Discord represents a state of disharmony or conflict, often stemming from or leading to negative emotions, thus it is a related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Discord represents a state of disharmony or conflict, often stemming from or leading to negative emotions, thus it is a related concept."}, {"found_name": "emotion", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "forces; in overcoming which it is dispersed and equilibrated. When tracing out the correlation and equivalence of forces, we saw that each sensation and emotion, or rather such part of it as remains a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym/related concept to feelings"}]}, {"canonical_name": "fumo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "smoke", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "smoke", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["smoke"], "contexts": ["particulate matter from combustion", "gaseous emission"]}, {"entity_id": "humo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["humo", "humos"], "contexts": ["smoke from tobacco", "smoke from a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "fumo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fumo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 32, "variants": ["fumo", "Fumo", "fumo da chaminè", "fumos", "fumus"], "contexts": ["fumigation", "smoke, used in drying milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "smoke", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humo", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "smoke", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fumo", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fumo", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 619, "stable_key": "clu_b8cd9a6075a5a5ba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "smoke", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q662860", "wikidata_description": "breathing in smoke after burning a substance and inhaling, done in order to be tasted and absorbed into the bloodstream", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoking", "wikipedia_extract": "Smoking is a human behavior which involves the combustion of a substance, usually plant material, and the inhalation of resulting fumes. Today, smoking is mostly practiced by rolling the dried leaves of the tobacco plant into a cigarette. Other forms of tobacco smoking include the use of a smoking tobacco with a pipe or cigar, or using a bong. Cigarette smokers almost always inhale the smoke; most pipe and cigar smokers do not inhale. Smoking is primarily practiced as a route of administration for psychoactive chemicals because the active substances within the burnt, dried plant leaves (or other chemical) can vaporize into a gaseous state and be delivered into the respiratory tract, where they are rapidly absorbed into the bloodstream through the lungs and can reach the central nervous system. In the case of tobacco smoking, these active substances are a mixture of aerosol particles that include the pharmacologically active alkaloid nicotine, which stimulates the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors in the brain, and other non-psychoactive chemicals that result from combustion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Smoke is a mixture of gases and particulate matter, often considered a type of air pollution or a component of the atmosphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Smoke is a mixture of gases and particulate matter, often considered a type of air pollution or a component of the atmosphere."}, {"found_name": "Flame", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 423, "target_cluster_name": "Flame", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Smoke is a byproduct of combustion, which produces flame.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Smoke is a byproduct of combustion, which produces flame."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous substances", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2321, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Smoke is composed of various gases and particulate matter, fitting the description of gaseous substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Smoke is composed of various gases and particulate matter, fitting the description of gaseous substances."}, {"found_name": "Atmosphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 213, "target_cluster_name": "atmosphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Smoke, being composed of gases and particles, can be considered a component or pollutant within the atmosphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Smoke, being composed of gases and particles, can be considered a component or pollutant within the atmosphere."}, {"found_name": "joyna", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "212. eſtaõ ligados ,& incapazes de cohabitarcomfuas mulheres, havemos de rirnos Crol.in Præfat. admonit. fol.143. delles?Naoſerá razaó; pois comos taes fumos tenho curado a alguns quey. xoſos dotal ac", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for unidentified herb"}]}, {"canonical_name": "calamo aromático", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "calamo_aromático", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "calamo aromático", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 19, "variants": ["Calamo aromático", "calamo aromático", "CALAMO AROMÁTICO"], "contexts": ["plant with strong smell", "aromatic reed"]}, {"entity_id": "calamo_aromatico", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "calamo aromatico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 17, "variants": ["calamo aromatico", "CaUmo aromatico", "Calamo aromat", "Calamo aromati", "Calamo aromatico", "Calamo aroo", "Calamo odorato", "Calamoaromat"], "contexts": ["aromatic reed similar to Acoro", "aromatic reed of spices"]}, {"entity_id": "calamo_aromatico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "calamo aromatico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["calamo aromatico"], "contexts": ["spice used in a syrup", "used in confection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "calamo aromático", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "calamo aromatico", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "calamo aromático", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "calamo aromatico", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "calamo aromatico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "calamo aromatico", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 620, "stable_key": "clu_fbd25dc3e074c601", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sweet Flag", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "An aromatic reed plant (Acorus calamus) used as a spice and in medicine.", "note": "Known for its aromatic properties and medicinal applications.", "wikipedia_extract": "Acorus calamus (also called sweet flag, sway or muskrat root, among many other common names) is a species of flowering plant with psychoactive chemicals. It is a tall wetland monocot of the family Acoraceae, in the genus Acorus. Although used in traditional medicine over centuries to treat digestive disorders and pain, it has no clinical evidence of safety or efficacy and may be toxic if ingested, and so has been commercially banned for use in food in the United States. Sweet flag is a herbaceous perennial, 2 m (79 in) tall. Its leaves resemble those of the iris family. Sweet flag consists of tufts of basal leaves that rise from a spreading rhizome."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sangue di drago", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1168, "target_cluster_name": "Sangue di drago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of Dragon's Blood mentions 'Calamus spp.' and 'Calamus rotang' as sources. Sweet Flag is also known as 'calamo aromático' and 'Acoro', which are related to the genus Calamus. This suggests a potential connection or confusion in historical texts regarding these plants and their resins.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The description of Dragon's Blood mentions 'Calamus spp.' and 'Calamus rotang' as sources. Sweet Flag is also known as 'calamo aromático' and 'Acoro', which are related to the genus Calamus. This suggests a potential connection or confusion in historical texts regarding these plants and their resins.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ferula", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2476, "target_cluster_name": "ferula", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are aromatic plants with historical medicinal uses, though they belong to different families (Apiaceae for Fennel, Acoraceae for Sweet Flag).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are aromatic plants with historical medicinal uses, though they belong to different families (Apiaceae for Fennel, Acoraceae for Sweet Flag).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "bolo armenio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 335, "target_cluster_name": "bolo armenio", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Radia di paftinaca faluarica, bianch;^» roflp* &roffà, volgarmente detti pififi<. • ciani , Berberi. Crcfpino , cioè berberi volgare. U^alo armeno. Bolo armeno de irElbabiancOjgùlIo^ ó roffo, ò il roì", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for 'Calaxno odorato'", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cassab", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 317, "target_cluster_name": "cássia", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "auctor). 142 Colóquio undécimo ORTA Serapio está corruto, e Avicena está emmendado, e mais os Arábios físicos lhe chamam este nome*, e o mesmo soa cassab que calamo, e aldirira dos aromáticos ; porque", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for calamo"}, {"found_name": "junco odorato", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2466, "target_cluster_name": "junco odorato", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "esquinanto he vocábulo corruto greguo, que quer dizer JM, e per excelência se chama asi acerca dos Greguos, como vós melhor sabeis. RUANO Outros o chamam junco odorato, ou casi todos os Gre- guos, e C", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for calamo", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cardamomo", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 636, "target_cluster_name": "Cardamomo", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Crcfpino , cioè berberi volgare. U^alo armeno. Bolo armeno de irElbabiancOjgùlIo^ ó roffo, ò il roìlb che U porta d'Alcf- fandria . Calaxno odorato* Calamo aromatico delle Spczierie^d ibaccdictti mino", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name"}, {"found_name": "Sallafras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1912, "target_cluster_name": "Sallafras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Sassafras and Sweet Flag (Acorus calamus) were historically used in traditional medicine and perfumery, often for similar aromatic and medicinal properties. They were sometimes confused or grouped together due to their strong scents and perceived therapeutic benefits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Sassafras and Sweet Flag (Acorus calamus) were historically used in traditional medicine and perfumery, often for similar aromatic and medicinal properties. They were sometimes confused or grouped together due to their strong scents and perceived therapeutic benefits.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Moly", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2675, "target_cluster_name": "Moly", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Moly and Sweet Flag are described as aromatic plants, suggesting a potential use in perfumery or medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_labe