roìlb che U porta d'Alcf- fandria . Calaxno odorato* Calamo aromatico delle Spczierie^d ibaccdictti mino", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for calamo aromatico"}]}, {"canonical_name": "acrimonia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "acrimonia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "acrimonia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["acrimonia"], "contexts": ["quality of the Saissasfras bark"]}, {"entity_id": "acrimonia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "acrimonia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["acrimonia"], "contexts": ["quality of sharpness"]}, {"entity_id": "acrimonia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "acrimonia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 36, "variants": ["acrimonia", "acrimoniada", "acrimoniado", "acrimoniado sangue", "acrimoniaurine"], "contexts": ["sharpness or bitterness", "acridity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "acrimonia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "acrimonia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "acrimonia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "acrimonia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "acrimonia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "acrimonia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 621, "stable_key": "clu_5be36632f508b90d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "acridity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A sharp, biting, or acrid taste or smell; pungency.", "note": "Refers to a sensory quality of sharpness or bitterness.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540–500 BC)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "acidez", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2434, "target_cluster_name": "acidez", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acidity is a direct translation of acridity in Portuguese.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Acidity is a direct translation of acridity in Portuguese."}, {"found_name": "Azedos", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1593, "target_cluster_name": "Azedos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A related taste sensation, often associated with sourness or sharpness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A related taste sensation, often associated with sourness or sharpness.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mercurial", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 53, "members": [{"entity_id": "mercury", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mercury", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Mercury"], "contexts": ["plant name", "Roman god, messenger"]}, {"entity_id": "mercury", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Mercury", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 15, "variants": ["Mercury"], "contexts": ["god associated with herbs", "planet associated with Fennel"]}, {"entity_id": "mercurial", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mercurial", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 18, "variants": ["Mercurial", "Mercur", "Mercurial."], "contexts": ["mentioned with birth anomalies", "author cited for a remedy"]}, {"entity_id": "hieronymo_monruo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Hieronymo Monruo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hieronymo Monruo"], "contexts": ["learned man in Medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "hieronymo_montuo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hieronymo Montuo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 15, "variants": ["Hieronymo Montuo", "Hie-ronymus Mercurialis", "Hieronym. Mercorialis", "Hieronym. Mercur.", "Hieronym. Mercurialis", "Hieronym. Mon.", "Hieronym. Sorian.", "Hieronymo Mercurial", "Hieronymo Soriano", "Hieronymus Ca.", "Hieronymus Mercurialis", "Hieronymus Montaus", "Hieronymus Montuus", "Jeronymo Mercurial"], "contexts": ["referred to Valeriano's statement", "refers to a case of conception and birth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mercury", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Mercury", "similarity": 0.836}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mercury", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mercurial", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 622, "stable_key": "clu_28ea01df2955b2bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Girolamo Mercuriale", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1530, "death_year": 1606, "description": "Italian physician, philosopher, and botanist; author of De Arte Gymnastica.", "wikidata_id": "Q129011568", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Girolamo Mercuriale or Mercuriali (Italian: Geronimo Mercuriali; Latin: Hieronymus Mercurialis, Hyeronimus Mercurialis) (September 30, 1530 – November 8, 1606) was an Italian philologist and physician, most famous for his work De Arte Gymnastica. Born in the city of Forlì, the son of Giovanni Mercuriali, also a doctor, he was educated at Bologna, Padua and Venice, where he received his doctorate in 1555. Settling in Forli, he was sent on a political mission to Rome. The pope at the time was Paul IV. In Rome, he made favorable contacts and had free access to the great libraries where, with sweeping enthusiasm, he studied the classical and medical literature of the Greeks and Romans. His studies of the attitudes of the ancients toward diet, exercise, and hygiene and the use of natural methods for the cure of disease culminated in the publication of his De Arte Gymnastica(Venice, 1569).", "note": "The spelling 'Monruo' and 'Montuo' are likely transcriptions/misspellings of Girolamo Mercuriale, a prominent physician and scholar referenced in similar contexts."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Joannes Messis Mercurialis", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1771, "target_cluster_name": "Mercurialis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the same individual, as indicated by the shared surname and the context of historical figures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is the same individual, as indicated by the shared surname and the context of historical figures."}, {"found_name": "Andreas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1395, "target_cluster_name": "Andreas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Girolamo Mercuriale was a prominent physician and medical historian of the same era as Vesalius, contributing to the broader medical landscape.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Girolamo Mercuriale was a prominent physician and medical historian of the same era as Vesalius, contributing to the broader medical landscape.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mercurialis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1771, "target_cluster_name": "Mercurialis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a variant spelling of the same name, referring to the same individual.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a variant spelling of the same name, referring to the same individual.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Capivatius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1906, "target_cluster_name": "Capivatius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential physicians and scholars of medicine in the 16th century, with overlapping interests in anatomy and medical history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential physicians and scholars of medicine in the 16th century, with overlapping interests in anatomy and medical history.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Aldrovandus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2256, "target_cluster_name": "Aldrovandus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were Italian scholars of the Renaissance, with Aldrovandi being a key figure in natural history and Mercuriale in medicine and physical culture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were Italian scholars of the Renaissance, with Aldrovandi being a key figure in natural history and Mercuriale in medicine and physical culture.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name