alanga", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 11, "variants": ["GALANGA", "galanga", "Galanga"], "contexts": ["root for comparison of color", "a root with nodes, fragrant and sharp"]}, {"entity_id": "galanga", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Galanga", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galanga"], "contexts": ["aromatic root used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "a,_galanga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "A, Galanga", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["A, Galanga"], "contexts": ["plant description"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "galanga", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Galanga", "similarity": 0.797}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Galanga", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "A, Galanga", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 627, "stable_key": "clu_6aa02a1b5c0df5dc-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galangal", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Alpinia galanga", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "description": "An aromatic root used medicinally and as a spice.", "note": "Distinct from galbanum.", "wikipedia_extract": "Alpinia galanga, a plant in the ginger family, bears a rhizome used largely as an herb in Unani medicine and as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery. It is one of four plants known as \"galangal\". Its common names include greater galangal, lengkuas, and blue ginger. The name \"galangal\" is probably derived from Persian qulanjan or Arabic khalanjan, which in turn may be an adaptation of Chinese gao liang jiang. Its names in North India are derived from the same root, including kulanja in Sanskrit, kulanjan in Hindi, and kholinjan in Urdu. The name \"lengkuas\", on the other hand, is derived from Malay lengkuas, which is derived from Proto-Western Malayo-Polynesian *laŋkuas, with cognates including Ilokano langkuás; Tagalog, Bikol, Kapampangan, Visayan, and Manobo langkáuas or langkáwas; Aklanon eangkawás; Kadazan Dusun hongkuas; Ida'an lengkuas; Ngaju Dayak langkuas; and Iban engkuas."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amomum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2029, "target_cluster_name": "Amomum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is in the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), and Amomum is a related genus within the same family, often used similarly as spices and in traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galangal is in the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), and Amomum is a related genus within the same family, often used similarly as spices and in traditional medicine."}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1688, "target_cluster_name": "gengibre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is a close relative of ginger, belonging to the same family (Zingiberaceae), and is often used as a spice and in traditional medicine, sometimes interchangeably or in conjunction with ginger.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galangal is a close relative of ginger, belonging to the same family (Zingiberaceae), and is often used as a spice and in traditional medicine, sometimes interchangeably or in conjunction with ginger."}, {"found_name": "Turmeric", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1807, "target_cluster_name": "Curcuma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal, ginger, and turmeric are all rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, commonly used as spices and in traditional medicine, and are often grouped together in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galangal, ginger, and turmeric are all rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, commonly used as spices and in traditional medicine, and are often grouped together in historical texts."}, {"found_name": "Spices", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2144, "target_cluster_name": "especiarias", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is explicitly mentioned as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galangal is explicitly mentioned as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery."}, {"found_name": "chamligiam", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "escrito chamligiam ou galungem, como Serapio* lido corrutamente escreve, nam lhe deis fé; porque todos os Arábios lhe chamão asi. E esta que chamamos galanga he de duas maneiras, scilicet, huma pequen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for galanga"}, {"found_name": "galungem", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "escrito chamligiam ou galungem, como Serapio* lido corrutamente escreve, nam lhe deis fé; porque todos os Arábios lhe chamão asi. E esta que chamamos galanga he de duas maneiras, scilicet, huma pequen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for galanga"}, {"found_name": "bunga-pala", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "maça^ que Ainslie escreve também bunga-pala. Segundo CrawÃird, aqueUe nome da no^ nSo é propriamente originário de Banda, onde os naturaes lhe chamam galago; mas é corrente em todo o archipelago, e fo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_