2f79daf694b125e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turks", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'Turks' refers to a people, in these contexts, it's used more broadly as an ethnic or regional descriptor, and less as specific individuals.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ofofago", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "oesophagus", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "oesophagus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["oesophagus"], "contexts": ["upper part expanded by turbit", "tube connecting mouth to stomach"]}, {"entity_id": "ofofago", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ofofago", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 26, "variants": ["Ofofago", "Oefopbagus", "Ofophago", "Olofago", "Oſophagode", "ofofago", "oofago", "oſophago"], "contexts": ["esophagus", "tube for swallowing"]}, {"entity_id": "œsophagus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "œsophagus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["œsophagus"], "contexts": ["tube for swallowing food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "oesophagus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ofofago", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "oesophagus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "œsophagus", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Ofofago", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "œsophagus", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 641, "stable_key": "clu_ba12885b455e71bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Esophagus", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "note": "The cluster includes variations like 'oesophagus' and 'Ofofago', which are historical spellings of the modern anatomical term.", "wikidata_id": "Q173710", "wikidata_description": "vertebrate organ through which food passes to the stomach", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esophagus", "wikipedia_extract": "The esophagus (American English) or oesophagus (British English) ( ) is an organ in vertebrates through which food passes, aided by peristaltic contractions, from the pharynx to the stomach. The esophagus is a fibromuscular tube, about 25 cm (10 in) long in adult humans, that travels behind the trachea and heart, passes through the diaphragm, and empties into the uppermost region of the stomach. During swallowing, the epiglottis tilts backwards to prevent food from going down the larynx and lungs. The word esophagus is from Ancient Greek οἰσοφάγος (oisophágos), from οἴσω (oísō), future form of φέρω (phérō, \"I carry\") + ἔφαγον (éphagon, \"I ate\"). The wall of the esophagus from the lumen outwards consists of mucosa, submucosa (connective tissue), layers of muscle fibers between layers of fibrous tissue, and an outer layer of connective tissue. The mucosa is a stratified squamous epithelium of around three layers of squamous cells, which contrasts to the single layer of columnar cells of the stomach."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bucho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3698, "target_cluster_name": "bucho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The esophagus is the tube that connects the pharynx to the stomach, making it anatomically and functionally linked.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The esophagus is the tube that connects the pharynx to the stomach, making it anatomically and functionally linked.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Throat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 598, "target_cluster_name": "Throat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The esophagus is a part of the throat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The esophagus is a part of the throat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "boca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 649, "target_cluster_name": "boca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The esophagus is a passage from the mouth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The esophagus is a passage from the mouth.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "café", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "café", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "café", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 30, "variants": ["CAFÉ", "café", "Café"], "contexts": ["precious production from Caracas", "cultivated plant for export"]}, {"entity_id": "coffee", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "coffee", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["coffee"], "contexts": ["plant requiring hot climate"]}, {"entity_id": "coffee", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "coffee", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["coffee"], "contexts": ["beverage plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "café", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "coffee", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "café", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "coffee", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "coffee", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "coffee", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 642, "stable_key": "clu_389c828435d0cf65", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coffee", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Coffea L.", "family": "Rubiaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q156354", "wikidata_description": "genus of flowering plants in the family Rubiaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffea", "wikipedia_extract": "Coffea is a genus of flowering plants in the family Rubiaceae. Coffea species are shrubs or small trees native to tropical and southern Africa and tropical Asia. The seeds of some species, called coffee beans, are used to flavor various beverages and products. The fruits, like the seeds, contain a large amount of caffeine, and have a distinct sweet taste. The plant ranks as one of the world's most valuable and widely traded commodity crops and is an important export product of several countries, including those in Central and South America, the Caribbean and Africa. The coffee trade relies heavily on two of the over 120 species, Coffea arabica (commonly known simply as \"Arabica\"), which accounts for 60–80% of the world's coffee production, and Coffea canephora (known as \"Robusta\"), which accounts for about 20–40%."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Vegetation", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2157, "target_cluster_name": "Vegetation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for 'Coffee', a specific type of plant life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Portuguese for 'Coffee', a specific type of plant life.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cacao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 811, "target_cluster_name": "cacao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are tropical plants cultivated for their seeds, which are used to produce popular beverages.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are tropical plants cultivated for their seeds, which are used to produce popular beverages."}, {"found_name": "Tea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 855, "target_cluster_name": "chá", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are popular beverages derived from the leaves/seeds of specific plants, often discussed in similar contexts of trade and consumption.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are popular beverages derived from the leaves/seeds of specific plants, often discussed in similar contexts of trade and consumption."}, {"found_name": "Palm tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 782, "target_cluster_name": "palmeira", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coffee plants are often described as shrubs or small trees, and palm trees are a common type of tree found in similar tropical regions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coffee plants are often described as shrubs or small trees, and palm trees are a common type of tree found in similar tropical regions."}, {"found_name": "Shrubs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 472, "target_cluster_name": "arbustes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coffee plants are botanically classified as shrubs or small trees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coffee plants are botanically classified as shrubs or small trees."}, {"found_name": "Tropical plants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2853, "target_cluster_name": "tropical plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coffee is native to tropical regions and is a prime example of a tropical plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coffee is native to tropical regions and is a prime example of a tropical plant."}, {"found_name": "chá", 