tion of German Idealism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hegel was a highly influential German philosopher who developed his own system of thought in response to and critique of Kant's philosophy. He is a central figure in the post-Kantian tradition of German Idealism."}, {"found_name": "Baruch Spinoza", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4511, "target_cluster_name": "Spinola", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spinoza was a rationalist philosopher whose metaphysics and epistemology were significant points of reference and critique for Kant's own philosophical system, particularly in Kant's development of transcendental idealism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spinoza was a rationalist philosopher whose metaphysics and epistemology were significant points of reference and critique for Kant's own philosophical system, particularly in Kant's development of transcendental idealism."}, {"found_name": "Goethe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1020, "target_cluster_name": "Goethe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant was a foundational philosopher of the Enlightenment, a period and intellectual movement that significantly shaped Goethe's worldview and writings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kant was a foundational philosopher of the Enlightenment, a period and intellectual movement that significantly shaped Goethe's worldview and writings.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Freytag", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2840, "target_cluster_name": "Freytag", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant's philosophy laid the groundwork for German Idealism, and Hegel's work is a direct continuation and critique of Kantian thought, making Kant a foundational figure for Hegel's philosophical system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kant's philosophy laid the groundwork for German Idealism, and Hegel's work is a direct continuation and critique of Kantian thought, making Kant a foundational figure for Hegel's philosophical system.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Schelling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2600, "target_cluster_name": "Schelling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant's philosophy laid the groundwork for German idealism, a movement to which Schelling belonged. Schelling's work can be seen as a development and extension of Kantian concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kant's philosophy laid the groundwork for German idealism, a movement to which Schelling belonged. Schelling's work can be seen as a development and extension of Kantian concepts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Leonhard", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2766, "target_cluster_name": "Leonhard", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Karl Leonhard Reinhold was a philosopher who helped to popularise the work of Immanuel Kant and whose philosophy influenced German idealism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Karl Leonhard Reinhold was a philosopher who helped to popularise the work of Immanuel Kant and whose philosophy influenced German idealism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Schiller", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2815, "target_cluster_name": "Schiller", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant's philosophy was foundational to German Idealism, a movement with which Schiller was deeply engaged. Schiller's aesthetic and ethical theories often responded to or developed Kantian concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kant's philosophy was foundational to German Idealism, a movement with which Schiller was deeply engaged. Schiller's aesthetic and ethical theories often responded to or developed Kantian concepts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "orbit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "orbit", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "orbit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["orbit"], "contexts": ["path of a celestial body", "path of celestial body around another"]}, {"entity_id": "orbit", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "orbit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Orbit", "orbit"], "contexts": ["path of celestial body", "path of a celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "orbit", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "orbit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["orbit"], "contexts": ["path of celestial body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "orbit", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "orbit", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "orbit", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "orbit", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "orbit", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "orbit", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 644, "stable_key": "clu_4ae8b48fa1ff14a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orbital mechanics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'orbit' in early modern scientific texts refers to the path of celestial bodies, which is a fundamental concept in orbital mechanics.", "wikidata_id": "Q842433", "wikidata_description": "field of classical mechanics concerned with the motion of spacecraft", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbital%20mechanics", "wikipedia_extract": "Orbital mechanics or astrodynamics is the application of ballistics and celestial mechanics to rockets, satellites, and other spacecraft. The motion of these objects is usually calculated from laws of motion and of universal gravitation derived by Isaac Newton. Astrodynamics is a core discipline within space-mission design and control. Celestial mechanics treats more broadly the orbit dynamics of systems under the influence of gravity, including both spacecraft and natural astronomical bodies such as star systems, planets, moons, and comets. Orbital mechanics focuses on spacecraft trajectories, including orbital maneuvers, orbital plane changes, and interplanetary transfers, and is used by mission planners to predict the results of propulsive maneuvers. General relativity is a more exact theory than Newton's laws for calculating orbits, and it is sometimes necessary to use it for greater accuracy or in high-gravity situations (e.g."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aphelion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 864, "target_cluster_name": "Aphelion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aphelion is a specific point in an orbit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aphelion is a specific point in an orbit."}, {"found_name": "Celestial body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3227, "target_cluster_name": "celestial body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Orbital mechanics describes the motion of celestial bodies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Orbital mechanics describes the motion of celestial bodies."}, {"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is a fundamental plane in celestial mechanics and orbital calculations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The ecliptic is a fundamental plane in celestial mechanics and orbital calculations."}, {"found_name": "Elliptical orbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elliptical orbits are a key concept and outcome of orbital mechanics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elliptical orbits are a key concept and outcome of orbital mechanics."}, {"found_name": "Epicycles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4138, "target_cluster_name": "épicycles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Epicycles were a historical model used to explain planetary motion within orbits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Epicycles were a historical model used to explain planetary motion within orbits."}, {"found_name": "Focus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3571, "target_cluster_name": "focus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The focus is a critical point in the definition of an elliptical orbit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The focus is a critical point in the definition of an elliptical orbit."}, {"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Orbital mechanics is a subfield of astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Orbital mechanics is a subfield of astronomy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "quartas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "quartana", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "quartana", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 3, "variants": ["quartana", "Quartanas"], "contexts": ["fever with daily chills", "fever, treated with balm before onset"]}, {"entity_id": "quartans", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "quartans", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["quartans", "quartfias"], "contexts": ["quartan fevers, potentially treated by the stone", "fevers, likely quartan fevers"]}, {"entity_id": "quartas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "quartas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 27, "variants": ["quartas", "Quartaa", "Quartana", "Quartanam", "Quartas", "quartana", "quartanas", "quartans", "quartãs", "quartās"], "contexts": ["quartan fevers", "Quartan fever"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "quartana", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "quartans", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "quartana", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quartas", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "quartans", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quartas", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 645, "stable_key": "clu_b3bafc6ad0a2cb5f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quartan fever", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A type of malaria characterized by fevers occurring every fourth day.", "wikipedia_extract": "Quartan fever is one of the four types of malaria which can be contracted by humans. It is specifically caused by the Plasmodium malariae species, one of the six species of the protozoan genus Plasmodium. Quartan fever is a form of malaria where an onset of fever occurs in an interval of three to four days, hence the name \"quartan\". It is transmitted by bites of infected female mosquitoes of the genus Anopheles. Symptoms include fevers which range from approximately 40–41 °C (104–106 °F) and occur periodically in 72 hour intervals. Although cases of malaria have occurred throughout the world, quartan fever typically occurs in the subtropics."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "febre latica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4497, "target_cluster_name": "febre latica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A type of fever characterized by occurring every fourth day.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A type of fever characterized by occurring every fourth day.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Tides", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "tides", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tides", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["tides", "Tides"], "contexts": ["oceanic movements", "propagation causes sea movement"]}, {"entity_id": "tides", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tides", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 21, "variants": ["tides", "Tides"], "contexts": ["rise and fall of sea levels", "rise and fall of sea surface"]}, {"entity_id": "tide", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tide", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["tide"], "contexts": ["raised in the Atlantic", "rise and fall of sea levels"]}, {"entity_id": "tides", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tides", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["tides"], "contexts": ["oceanic phenomenon influenced by moon and sun", "aqueous agency originating from astronomical motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Tides", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "tide", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tides", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 646, "stable_key": "clu_44fc1650813f3fae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tides", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster explicitly mentions 'Tides' and 'tide' in contexts related to oceanic movements and sea levels, directly corresponding to the modern concept of tides.", "wikidata_id": "Q7800866", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tidestromia", "wikipedia_extract": "Tidestromia is a genus with about six or seven species of annual or subshrub perennial plants native to desert and semi-arid regions of the western United States, Mexico and tropical America in the family Amaranthaceae. A common name of some species is honeysweet. The stems are reddish and contrast conspicuously with the silvery leaves. This genus is named for the botanist Ivar Tidestrom. Tidestromia carnosa - fleshy honeysweet Tidestromia gemmata - TransPecos honeysweet Tidestromia lanuginosa - woolly tidestromia Tidestromia oblongifolia - Arizona honeysweet Tidestromia suffruticosa - shrubby honeysweet Everitt, J.H.; Lonard, R.L.; Little, C.R. (2007)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aetna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "etna", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Etna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Etna"], "contexts": ["volcanic mountain", "volcano in Sicily"]}, {"entity