, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling st", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for aerolites"}, {"found_name": "meteors", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "pass through the atmosphere, but leave no residuum. These, which are known as falling stars and meteors, are periodical; but that is by no means the case with aërolites, which are also ignited by the ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for aërolites"}, {"found_name": "shooting stars", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "shooting stars, which has frequently been observed to be opposite to that of the Earth, may be considered as conclusive arguments against the hypothesis that aërolites derive their origin from the so-", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for aerolites"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cardano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "cardan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cardan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Cardan"], "contexts": ["scholar who rejected crystal formation by cold", "scholar who delivered information about Guascus' loadstone"]}, {"entity_id": "cardan", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Cardan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cardan"], "contexts": ["philosopher and mathematician lost in thought"]}, {"entity_id": "cardano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cardano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 16, "variants": ["Cardano", "Cardanus"], "contexts": ["referred by Rolfincio, tells a story", "philosopher and physician"]}, {"entity_id": "cardan", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cardan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cardan"], "contexts": ["early modern medical author", "mentioned in relation to cures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cardan", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cardan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cardan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cardano", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cardan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cardan", "similarity": 0.689}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cardan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cardano", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cardan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cardan", "similarity": 0.689}], "id": 679, "stable_key": "clu_904d92c8ba24e6bc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gerolamo Cardano", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1501, "death_year": 1576, "description": "Italian polymath, physician, mathematician, astrologer, and gambler.", "wikidata_id": "Q184530", "wikidata_description": "Italian Renaissance mathematician, physician, astrologer (1501-1576)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerolamo%20Cardano", "wikipedia_extract": "Gerolamo Cardano (Italian: [dʒeˈrɔːlamo karˈdaːno]; also Girolamo or Geronimo; French: Jérôme Cardan; Latin: Hieronymus Cardanus; 24 September 1501– 21 September 1576) was an Italian polymath whose interests and proficiencies ranged through those of mathematician, physician, biologist, physicist, chemist, astrologer, astronomer, philosopher, music theorist, writer, and gambler. He became one of the most influential mathematicians of the Renaissance and one of the key figures in the foundation of probability; he introduced the binomial coefficients and the binomial theorem in the Western world. He wrote more than 200 works on science. Cardano partially invented and described several mechanical devices including the combination lock, the gimbal consisting of three concentric rings allowing a supported compass or gyroscope to rotate freely, and the Cardan shaft with universal joints, which allows the transmission of rotary motion at various angles and is used in vehicles to this day. He made significant contributions to hypocycloids - published in De proportionibus, in 1570. The generating circles of these hypocycloids, later named \"Cardano circles\" or \"cardanic circles\", were used for the construction of the first high-speed printing presses."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Archimedes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 480, "target_cluster_name": "Archimedes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were renowned mathematicians and inventors, with Archimedes being a foundational figure in ancient Greek mathematics and Cardano a significant figure in Renaissance mathematics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were renowned mathematicians and inventors, with Archimedes being a foundational figure in ancient Greek mathematics and Cardano a significant figure in Renaissance mathematics."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were polymaths who made extensive contributions to a wide range of fields, including natural philosophy and mathematics. Cardano was deeply influenced by Aristotelian thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were polymaths who made extensive contributions to a wide range of fields, including natural philosophy and mathematics. Cardano was deeply influenced by Aristotelian thought."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant figures in the history of medicine and pharmacology, with Dioscorides being a foundational figure in ancient herbal medicine and Cardano a physician who studied and wrote on medical topics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant figures in the history of medicine and pharmacology, with Dioscorides being a foundational figure in ancient herbal medicine and Cardano a physician who studied and wrote on medical topics."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pedro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "peter", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Peter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Peter"], "contexts": ["impatient disciple", "Biblical disciple who asked about John"]}, {"entity_id": "peter", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Peter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Peter"], "contexts": ["individual in thought experiment", "a hypothetical individual"]}, {"entity_id": "pedro", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pedro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 19, "variants": ["Pedro", "D. Pedro de Lencastre", "Pedro Af-", "Pedro Castello", "Pedro Fernandez", "Pedro Ferrás", "Pedro de Barbuda", "Pedro de Caftilho", "Pedro de Castilho", "Pedro de Castro", "Pedro de Caſtilho", "PedroCefar", "PedrodaCoſta", "Pedrode Barbuda"], "contexts": ["mentioned in comparison", "person with sympathetic stomach to milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peter", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Peter", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peter", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pedro", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Peter", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pedro", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 680, "stable_key": "clu_8689087ae1e81624", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peter", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "A common given name, often appearing in comparative contexts.", "note": "Too general to identify a specific historical figure without further context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "fictional character from the Family Guy franchise", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter%20Griffin", "wikipedia_extract": "Peter Löwenbräu Griffin Sr. (né Justin Peter Griffin) is a fictional character and the protagonist of the American animated sitcom Family Guy. He is voiced by series creator Seth MacFarlane, and first appeared on television, along with the rest of the Griffin family, in the episode \"Death Has a Shadow\" on January 31, 1999. Peter was created and designed by MacFarlane himself. MacFarlane was asked to pitch a pilot to the Fox Broadcasting Company based on Larry & Steve, a short made by MacFarlane which featured a middle-aged character named Larry and an intellectual dog, Steve. For the series, Larry was renamed Peter."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "antipathia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "antipathies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Antipathies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["antipathies", "Antipathies"], "contexts": ["mutual aversion or repulsion", "opposing forces between substances"]}, {"entity_id": "antipathy", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Antipathy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Antipathy", "Antypathy"], "contexts": ["used to cure disease", "principle of cure by opposition"]}, {"entity_id": "antipathia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "antipathia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 13, "variants": ["antipathia", "Antipathia", "antidote", "antipathias"], "contexts": ["aversion between substances", "aversion or opposition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Antipathies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Antipathy", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Antipathies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antipathia", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Antipathy", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antipathia", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 681, "stable_key": "clu_d8107a3cebbcd509", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antipathy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q581459", "wikidata_description": "dislike for something or somebody", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antipathy", "wikipedia_extract": "Antipathy is a dislike for something or somebody, the opposite of sympathy. While antipathy may be induced by experience, it sometimes exists without a rational cause-and-effect explanation being present to the individuals involved. Thus, the origin of antipathy has been subject to various philosophical and psychological explanations, which some people find convincing and others regard as highly speculative. The exploration of a philosophical aspect for antipathy has been found in an essay by John Locke, an early modern  17th century philosopher. Interpersonal antipathy is often irrationally ascribed to mannerisms or certain physical characteristics, which are perceived as signs for character traits (e.g., close, deep set eyes as a sign for dullness or cruelty). Further, the negative feeling sometimes takes place rapidly and without reasoning, functioning below the level of attention, thus resembling an automatic process."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sympathia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 104, "target_cluster_name": "sympathia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antipathy is the direct conceptual opposite of sympathy, and they are frequently contrasted in discussions of human relationships and natural philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antipathy is the direct conceptual opposite of sympathy, and they are frequently contrasted in discussions of human relationships and natural philosophy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Anger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1206, "target_cluster_name": "anger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antipathy is a form of strong dislike, which can manifest as anger or be closely associated with it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antipathy is a form of strong dislike, which can manifest as anger or be closely associated with it."}, {"found_name": "Malice", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2462, "target_cluster_name": "malicia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antipathy implies a negative disposition towards something or someone, which can be a component of malice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antipathy implies a negative disposition towards something or someone, which can be a component of malice."}, {"found_name": "Discord", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1209, "target_cluster_name": "discord", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antipathy between individuals or groups often leads to discord.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antipathy between individuals or groups often leads to discord."}, {"found_name": "Attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 82, "target_cluster_name": "attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antipathy is the opposite of attraction, representing a repulsion or aversion rather than a drawing towards.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antipathy is the opposite of attraction, representing a repulsion or aversion rather than a drawing towards."}]}, {"canonical_name": "gypse", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "gypsum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Gypsum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gypsum"], "contexts": ["ingredient for dishes"]}, {"entity_id": "gypse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gypse", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 15, "variants": ["gypse"], "contexts": ["gypsum mineral", "type of rock"]}, {"entity_id": "gypses", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gypses", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["gypses"], "contexts": ["gypsum mineral"]}, {"entity_id": "gypsum", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "gypsum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["gypsum"], "contexts": ["mineral beds formed from fluids", "product of anhydrite absorption of water"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphates_of_lime", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphates of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphates of lime"], "contexts": ["mineral salt"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphate_of_lime", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sulphate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["sulphate of lime", "Sulphate of Lime"], "contexts": ["crystal for polarized light phenomena", "mineral compound used in optics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gypsum", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "gypses", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gypsum", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "gypsum", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gypse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "gypsum", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gypses", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "gypsum", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 682, "stable_key": "clu_ad6b865b5496e109", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gypsum", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q82658", "wikidata_description": "mineral, calcium sulfate with bounded water", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gypsum", "wikipedia_extract": "Gypsum is a soft sulfate mineral composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate, with the chemical formula CaSO4·2H2O. It is widely mined and is used as a fertilizer and as the main constituent in many forms of plaster, drywall and blackboard or sidewalk chalk. Gypsum also crystallizes as translucent crystals of selenite. It forms as an evaporite mineral and as a hydration product of anhydrite. The Mohs scale of mineral hardness defines gypsum as hardness value 2 based on scratch hardness comparison. Fine-grained white or lightly tinted forms of gypsum known as alabaster have been used for sculpture by many cultures including Ancient Egypt, Mesopotamia, Ancient Rome, the Byzantine Empire, and the Nottingham alabasters of Medieval England."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chalk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 829, "target_cluster_name": "chalk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chalk is a soft, porous sedimentary rock, a form of limestone composed of the mineral calcite. Gypsum is also a soft mineral, and both are used for writing surfaces (blackboards/sidewalks) and in plasters.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chalk is a soft, porous sedimentary rock, a form of limestone composed of the mineral calcite. Gypsum is also a soft mineral, and both are used for writing surfaces (blackboards/sidewalks) and in plasters."}, {"found_name": "salgema", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 840, "target_cluster_name": "salgema", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both rock salt and gypsum are common mineral deposits found in geological strata, often associated with evaporite formations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both rock salt and gypsum are common mineral deposits found in geological strata, often associated with evaporite formations.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "marmo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1062, "target_cluster_name": "marmo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both marble and gypsum are minerals that can be found in geological formations and have historical uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both marble and gypsum are minerals that can be found in geological formations and have historical uses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "formations calcaires", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1162, "target_cluster_name": "formations calcaires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gypsum is a mineral often found in association with calcareous formations and can be a component of some sedimentary rocks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gypsum is a mineral often found in association with calcareous formations and can be a component of some sedimentary rocks.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "bórax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1311, "target_cluster_name": "bórax", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are mineral substances with historical uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are mineral substances with historical uses.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Conserve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "conserve", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Conserve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 17, "variants": ["Conserve", "Conserv"], "contexts": ["preserved plant preparation", "preparation of Betony"]}, {"entity_id": "conserva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "conserva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 6, "variants": ["conserva", "conservas"], "contexts": ["preserved violets", "Method of preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "conserva", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "conserva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["conserva"], "contexts": ["conserves, a type of compound medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Conserve", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "conserva", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Conserve", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "conserva", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "conserva", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "conserva", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 683, "stable_key": "clu_b600864a96cc4e7c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conserve", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A pharmaceutical preparation, typically of fruits or herbs preserved with sugar.", "note": "Refers to a preserved preparation, often medicinal.", "wikipedia_extract": "Shellac () is a resin secreted by the female lac bug on trees in the forests of India and Thailand. Chemically, it is mainly composed of aleuritic acid, jalaric acid, shellolic acid, and other natural waxes. It is processed and sold as dry flakes and dissolved in alcohol to make liquid shellac, which is used as a brush-on colorant, food glaze and wood finish. Shellac functions as a tough natural primer, sanding sealant, tannin-blocker, odor-blocker, stain, and high-gloss varnish. Shellac was once used in electrical applications as it is a good insulator and seals out moisture. Phonograph and 78 rpm gramophone records were made of shellac until it was replaced by vinyl.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shellac"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "shell lac", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4479, "target_cluster_name": "shell lac", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct alternative name for shellac.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is a direct alternative name for shellac."}, {"found_name": "lacca", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1891, "target_cluster_name": "lacca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the Italian word for shellac, often used in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is the Italian word for shellac, often used in historical texts."}, {"found_name": "Birdlime", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3843, "target_cluster_name": "Birdlime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both shellac and birdlime are sticky substances derived from natural sources, historically used for similar purposes (e.g., trapping birds).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both shellac and birdlime are sticky substances derived from natural sources, historically used for similar purposes (e.g., trapping birds)."}, {"found_name": "Electuary", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1794, "target_cluster_name": "Electuary", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are preparations made with fruit or herbs preserved with sugar or honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are preparations made with fruit or herbs preserved with sugar or honey.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "marmelada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1886, "target_cluster_name": "marmelada", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conserves are fruit preserves, often made with sugar, and share a similar preparation method and category of foodstuff with marmalade.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conserves are fruit preserves, often made with sugar, and share a similar preparation method and category of foodstuff with marmalade.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cardiaca", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Herb to drive Melancholly Vapors from the Heart, to strengthen it, and make a merry cheerful blith soul, than this Herb, it may be kept in a Syrup or Conserve, therfore the Latins called it Cardiaca: ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Latin name for the herb"}, {"found_name": "Electuary of Roses", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "dry up the Rhewms and watering of them. Of the Red Roses are usually made many Compositions all serving to sundry good uses; Viz. Electuary of Roses; Conserve both moist and dry, which is more usually", "confidence": 0.8, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of composition made from red roses"}, {"found_name": "Syrup of dryed Roses", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "dry up the Rhewms and watering of them. Of the Red Roses are usually made many Compositions all serving to sundry good uses; Viz. Electuary of Roses; Conserve both moist and dry, which is more usually", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a composition made from red roses"}, {"found_name": "Hȯney of Roses", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "dry up the Rhewms and watering of them. Of the Red Roses are usually made many Compositions all serving to sundry good uses; Viz. Electuary of Roses; Conserve both moist and dry, which is more usually", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a composition made from red roses"}]}, {"canonical_name": "poros", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "pores", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pores"], "contexts": ["part of the body to be opened"]}, {"entity_id": "pores", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["pores"], "contexts": ["small openings in meteorite structure"]}, {"entity_id": "poros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "poros", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 22, "variants": ["poros", "poros do corpo", "porosidades", "pòros"], "contexts": ["pores in the brain", "pores, small openings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pores", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pores", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pores", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "poros", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "pores", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "poros", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 684, "stable_key": "clu_cb8881759d3787b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pores", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Minute openings in the surface of the skin or other tissues.", "note": "The contexts relate to biological structures for absorption or excretion.", "wikipedia_extract": "Poros (Greek: Πόρος; Modern Greek pronunciation: [ˈporos]) is a small Greek island-pair in the southern part of the Saronic Gulf, about 58 km (36 miles; 31 nautical miles) south of the port of Piraeus and separated from the Peloponnese by a 200 m (656 ft) wide sea channel, with the town of Galatas on the mainland across the strait. Its surface area is about 31 square kilometres (12 sq mi) and it has 3,261 inhabitants (2021). The ancient name of Poros was Pogon. Like other ports in the Saronic, it is a popular weekend destination for Athenian travellers. Poros consists of two islands: Sphaeria (Greek: Σφαιρία, pronounced [sfeˈria]), the southern part, which is of volcanic origin, where today's city is located, and Kalaureia (Greek: Καλαυρία, [kalavˈria]), also Kalavria or Calauria (meaning 'gentle breeze'), the northern and largest part. A bridge connects the two islands over a narrow strait."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "New Zealand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "new_zealand", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "New Zealand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 17, "variants": ["New Zealand"], "contexts": ["country whose trees were tabulated", "country with endemic productions"]}, {"entity_id": "new_zealand", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Zealand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["New Zealand"], "contexts": ["location of bird bones", "island nation mentioned in passage"]}, {"entity_id": "new_zealand", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "New Zealand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["New Zealand"], "contexts": ["geographic location east of South Pole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "New Zealand", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New Zealand", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "New Zealand", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New Zealand", "similarity": 0.844}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New Zealand", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New Zealand", "similarity": 0.844}], "id": 685, "stable_key": "clu_9c012e6ff069253b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "New Zealand", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "New Zealand", "wikidata_id": "Q664", "wikidata_description": "island country in the southwest Pacific Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New%20Zealand", "wikipedia_extract": "New Zealand is an island country in the southwestern Pacific Ocean. It consists of two main landmasses—the North Island (Te Ika-a-Māui) and the South Island (Te Waipounamu)—and over 600 smaller islands. It is the sixth-largest island country by area and lies east of Australia across the Tasman Sea and south of the islands of New Caledonia, Fiji, and Tonga. The country's varied topography and sharp mountain peaks, including the Southern Alps (Kā Tiritiri o te Moana), owe much to tectonic uplift and volcanic eruptions. New Zealand's capital city is Wellington, and its most populous city is Auckland. The islands of New Zealand were the last large habitable land to be settled by humans."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Australia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 75, "target_cluster_name": "Australia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "New Zealand is geographically proximate to and often discussed in relation to Australia, being an island country in the southwestern Pacific Ocean east of Australia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "New Zealand is geographically proximate to and often discussed in relation to Australia, being an island country in the southwestern Pacific Ocean east of Australia."}, {"found_name": "Antarctic Islands", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4030, "target_cluster_name": "Antarctic islands", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "New Zealand has territorial claims and historical connections to several islands in the Antarctic region, such as the Ross Dependency.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "New Zealand has territorial claims and historical connections to several islands in the Antarctic region, such as the Ross Dependency."}, {"found_name": "Indian Ocean islands", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4405, "target_cluster_name": "South-Indian archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While New Zealand is in the Pacific, historical exploration and trade routes sometimes linked regions in the Indian and Pacific Oceans, leading to broader discussions of archipelagos and island groups.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While New Zealand is in the Pacific, historical exploration and trade routes sometimes linked regions in the Indian and Pacific Oceans, leading to broader discussions of archipelagos and island groups."}, {"found_name": "Island", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 487, "target_cluster_name": "atolls", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "New Zealand is explicitly defined as an island country, making 'Island' a fundamental related concept in geographical descriptions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "New Zealand is explicitly defined as an island country, making 'Island' a fundamental related concept in geographical descriptions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Teneriffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "ténériffe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ténériffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ténériffe"], "contexts": ["location with lava promontories", "location south of which sandstone is found"]}, {"entity_id": "teneriffe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Teneriffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Teneriffe"], "contexts": ["location of observations", "location of a volcano"]}, {"entity_id": "peak_of_teneriffe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Peak of Teneriffe"], "contexts": ["mountainous location with natural impressions", "volcanic mountain summit"]}, {"entity_id": "peak_of_teneriffe", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Peak of Teneriffe"], "contexts": ["location with prevailing west winds", "location of high-altitude heat measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "teneriffe", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Teneriffe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Teneriffe"], "contexts": ["location of observation on a peak", "volcano with successive zones of vegetation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ténériffe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Teneriffe", "similarity": 0.801}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ténériffe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Teneriffe", "similarity": 0.801}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Teneriffe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Teneriffe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Teneriffe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peak of Teneriffe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Teneriffe", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 686, "stable_key": "clu_4e4a9b5bbbf485b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tenerife", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "Q40846", "wikidata_description": "largest island of the Canary Islands", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenerife", "wikipedia_extract": "Tenerife ( TEN-ə-REEF-(ay); Spanish: [teneˈɾife] ; formerly spelled Teneriffe) is the largest and most-populous island of the Canary Islands, an autonomous community of Spain. With a land area of 2,034.38 km2 (785.48 mi2) and a population of 972,018 inhabitants as of January 2026, it is the most-populous island in Spain and the entire Macaronesia region. Tenerife is also home to 42.7% of the total population of the archipelago. More than seven million tourists (7,384,707 in 2024) visit Tenerife each year, making it by far the most visited island in the archipelago. It is one of the most important tourist destinations in Spain and the world, hosting one of the world's largest carnivals, the Carnival of Santa Cruz de Tenerife. The capital of the island, Santa Cruz de Tenerife, is also the seat of the island council (cabildo insular)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Mica", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "mica", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mica", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["mica"], "contexts": ["mineral decomposing in granites and porphyries", "simple mineral in composite rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "mica", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mica", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["Mica", "mica"], "contexts": ["crystal for polarized light phenomena", "crystal exhibiting colored images with polarized light"]}, {"entity_id": "mica", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mica", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["mica"], "contexts": ["silicate mineral", "mineral component of granite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mica", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mica", "similarity": 0.793}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mica", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mica", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Mica", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mica", "similarity": 0.793}], "id": 687, "stable_key": "clu_7b87f0eab1924bb2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mica", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q114675", "wikidata_description": "group of phyllosilicate minerals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mica", "wikipedia_extract": "Micas ( MY-kəz) are a group of silicate minerals whose outstanding physical characteristic is that individual mica crystals can easily be split into fragile elastic plates. This characteristic is described as perfect basal cleavage. Mica is common in igneous and metamorphic rock and is occasionally found as small flakes in sedimentary rock. It is particularly prominent in many granites, pegmatites, and schists, and \"books\" (large individual crystals) of mica several feet across have been found in some pegmatites. Micas are used in products such as drywalls, paints, and fillers, especially in parts for automobiles, roofing, and in electronics. The mineral is used in cosmetics and food to add \"shimmer\" or \"frost\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Crystal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 696, "target_cluster_name": "Crystal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mica is a mineral that forms crystals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mica is a mineral that forms crystals."}, {"found_name": "Minerals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 138, "target_cluster_name": "minerals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mica is a group of silicate minerals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mica is a group of silicate minerals."}, {"found_name": "Crystalline rocks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3030, "target_cluster_name": "roches cristallines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mica is a common component of crystalline rocks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mica is a common component of crystalline rocks."}, {"found_name": "Metamorphic rocks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2992, "target_cluster_name": "metamorphic schists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mica is a common component of metamorphic rocks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mica is a common component of metamorphic rocks."}, {"found_name": "mica schist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "which contain some remains of sea weeds from the silurian or cambrian sea. On what did these so-called 'most ancient' formations rest, if gneiss and mica schist must be regarded as changed sedimentary", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rock containing mica"}]}, {"canonical_name": "pestilentes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcate