d with herb and dish"]}, {"entity_id": "alexander_the_great", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Alexander the Great", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alexander the Great"], "contexts": ["historical figure with eventful life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alexander", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Alexander", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alexander", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alexandre", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Alexander", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alexandre", "similarity": 0.873}], "id": 697, "stable_key": "clu_20cb7cbeba335f2e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alexander the Great", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -356, "death_year": -323, "description": "Macedonian king, conqueror, and historical figure.", "wikidata_id": "Q8409", "wikidata_description": "king of Macedonia and conqueror of Achaemenid Persia (356–323 BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander%20the%20Great", "wikipedia_extract": "Alexander III of Macedon (Ancient Greek: Ἀλέξανδρος, romanized: Aléxandros; 20/21 July 356 BC – 10/11 June 323 BC), most commonly known as Alexander the Great, was king of the ancient Greek kingdom of Macedon. He succeeded his father Philip II to the throne in 336 BC at the age of 20, and spent most of his reign conducting a lengthy military campaign throughout Asia and Egypt. By the age of 30, he had created one of the largest empires in history, stretching from Greece to northwestern India. He was undefeated in battle and is widely considered to be one of history's greatest and most successful military commanders. Born in Pella, Macedon, Alexander was tutored by philosopher and polymath Aristotle until the age of 16. In 335 BC, shortly after assuming the throne of Macedon, he launched a campaign in the Balkans and reasserted control over Thrace and parts of Illyria before marching on the city of Thebes, which was subsequently destroyed."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle tutored Alexander the Great, a significant historical relationship that influenced both individuals and the spread of Hellenistic culture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aristotle tutored Alexander the Great, a significant historical relationship that influenced both individuals and the spread of Hellenistic culture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Achilles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3249, "target_cluster_name": "Achilles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alexander the Great admired Achilles and considered him a model.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alexander the Great admired Achilles and considered him a model."}, {"found_name": "Cyrus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3886, "target_cluster_name": "Cyrus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alexander the Great admired Cyrus the Great and sought to emulate him.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alexander the Great admired Cyrus the Great and sought to emulate him."}, {"found_name": "Ptolemy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 824, "target_cluster_name": "Ptolemy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in the Hellenistic world, with Ptolemy being one of Alexander's generals and a founder of a dynasty.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures in the Hellenistic world, with Ptolemy being one of Alexander's generals and a founder of a dynasty.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Amat.", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 902, "target_cluster_name": "Amat.", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle was the tutor of Alexander the Great, a historically significant relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aristotle was the tutor of Alexander the Great, a historically significant relationship.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Julian", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2663, "target_cluster_name": "Julian", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Julian the Apostate, as a Roman Emperor, was a significant historical figure who ruled during a period of transition and conflict, and Alexander the Great represents a pinnacle of military and imperial achievement that would have been a point of reference or comparison for rulers of Julian's era.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Julian the Apostate, as a Roman Emperor, was a significant historical figure who ruled during a period of transition and conflict, and Alexander the Great represents a pinnacle of military and imperial achievement that would have been a point of reference or comparison for rulers of Julian's era.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Carlos Magno", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2881, "target_cluster_name": "Carlos Magno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were legendary military leaders and conquerors who left a significant mark on history and culture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_rela