"creations; and yet the feelings and elements of feeling which it reveals must have been already there--in an unconscious state. We all know _practically_ the difference between the so-called sonant an", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of consonant"}, {"found_name": "surd", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "creations; and yet the feelings and elements of feeling which it reveals must have been already there--in an unconscious state. We all know _practically_ the difference between the so-called sonant an", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of consonant"}]}, {"canonical_name": "frog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "frog", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "frog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 19, "variants": ["Frog", "FROG", "frog"], "contexts": ["animal used for nerve-centre study", "living animal used in experiment"]}, {"entity_id": "frog", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "frog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["frog"], "contexts": ["animal whose blood contains animalcules"]}, {"entity_id": "frog", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "frog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["frog"], "contexts": ["limbs of a dead frog", "animal used in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Frogs"], "contexts": ["oviparous quadruped with analogous joints"]}, {"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Frogs", "frogs"], "contexts": ["amphibian species", "animals that cannot cross wide ocean spaces"]}, {"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 6, "variants": ["frogs"], "contexts": ["creatures for vivisection study", "animal studied for reflex movements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "frog", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "frog", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "frog", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "frog", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "frog", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "frog", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 699, "stable_key": "clu_662718af8ba9edd4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "frog", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Amphibious animal commonly used in scientific experiments.", "wikipedia_extract": "A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura (coming from the Ancient Greek ἀνούρα, literally 'without tail'). Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be called toads, but the distinction between frogs and toads is informal and purely cosmetic, and does not reflect taxonomy or evolutionary history. Frogs are widely distributed, ranging from the tropics to subarctic regions, but the greatest concentration of species diversity is in tropical rainforest and associated wetlands. They account for around 88% of extant amphibian species, and are one of the five most diverse vertebrate orders. The oldest fossil \"proto-frog\" Triadobatrachus is known from the Early Triassic of Madagascar (250 million years ago), but molecular clock dating suggests their divergence from other amphibians may extend further back to the Permian, 265 million years ago. Adult frogs have a stout body, protruding eyes, anteriorly-attached tongue, limbs folded underneath, and no tail (the \"tail\" of tailed frogs is an extension of the male cloaca)."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Salamander", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3808, "target_cluster_name": "Salamander", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both frogs and salamanders are amphibians, often grouped together in premodern texts due to their similar life cycles and habitats.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both frogs and salamanders are amphibians, often grouped together in premodern texts due to their similar life cycles and habitats."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Perihelion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "perihelion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "perihelion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["perihelion"], "contexts": ["point closest to the Sun in orbit"]}, {"entity_id": "perihelion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Perihelion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 19, "variants": ["Perihelion", "perihelion"], "contexts": ["point of closest approach to the Sun", "point nearest the sun in orbit"]}, {"entity_id": "perihelion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "perihelion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["perihelion"], "contexts": ["point closest to the sun"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "perihelion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Perihelion", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "perihelion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "perihelion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Perihelion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "perihelion", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 700, "stable_key": "clu_823ef5121e7832da", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Perihelion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q622176", "wikidata_description": "precession (rotation) of the orbit of a celestial body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apsidal%20precession", "wikipedia_extract": "In celestial mechanics, apsidal precession (or apsidal advance) is the precession (gradual rotation) of the line connecting the apsides (line of apsides) of an astronomical body's orbit. The apsides are the orbital points farthest (apoapsis) and closest (periapsis) from its primary body. The apsidal precession is the first time derivative of the argument of periapsis, one of the six main orbital elements of an orbit. Apsidal precession is considered positive when the orbit's axis rotates in the same direction as the orbital motion. An apsidal period is the time interval required for an orbit to precess through 360°, which takes the Earth about 112,000 years and the Moon about 8.85 years. The ancient Greek astronomer Hipparchus noted the apsidal precession of the Moon's orbit (as the revolution of the Moon's apogee with a period of approximately 8.85 years); it is corrected for in the Antikythera Mechanism (circa 80 BCE) (with the supposed value of 8.88 years per full cycle, correct to within 0.34% of current measurements)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "grego", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 773, "target_cluster_name": "grego", "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "the ellipse. NOTE 49, p. 9. _Apsides._ The points P and A, fig. 10, at the extremities of the major axis of an orbit. P is commonly called the perihelion, a Greek term signifying _round the sun_; and ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: a point on the major axis of an orbit, defined by a Greek term", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Aphelion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 864, "target_cluster_name": "Aphelion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aphelion is the point in an orbit farthest from the sun, while perihelion is the point closest to the sun. Both are specific points in an elliptical orbit defined by the apsides.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aphelion is the point in an orbit farthest from the sun, while perihelion is the point closest to the sun. Both are specific points in an elliptical orbit defined by the apsides."}, {"found_name": "orbits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1379, "target_cluster_name": "orbits", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Perihelion is the point in an orbit closest to the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Perihelion is the point in an orbit closest to the Sun.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "elliptical orbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Perihelion is the point in the orbit of a planet, asteroid, or comet at which it is closest to the sun. Elliptical orbits are characterized by having an aphelion and a perihelion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Perihelion is the point in the orbit of a planet, asteroid, or comet at which it is closest to the sun. Elliptical orbits are characterized by having an aphelion and a perihelion.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "conjunção", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "conjunction", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "conjunction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["conjunction"], "contexts": ["alignment of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "conjunção", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "conjunção", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 13, "variants": ["conjunção", "Conjunçaõmenfal", "aconjunçaó", "conjunçaó", "conjunçãomenfal", "conjunções"], "contexts": ["conjunction, astrological or medical", "conjunction, possibly astrological or medical"]}, {"entity_id": "conjunction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "conjunction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["conjunction"], "contexts": ["alignment of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "conjunctions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "conjunctions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["conjunctions"], "contexts": ["epochs computed by Lalande"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "conjunction", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "conjunção", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "conjunction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "conjunction", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "conjunction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "conjunctions", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "conjunção", "target_b