es associated with occultism, alchemy, and natural philosophy in the early modern period, often discussed in similar intellectual contexts."}, {"found_name": "Alchemists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3326, "target_cluster_name": "Alquimistas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paracelsus is a central and highly influential figure within the historical tradition of alchemy, often considered a key innovator and proponent of its principles and practices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paracelsus is a central and highly influential figure within the historical tradition of alchemy, often considered a key innovator and proponent of its principles and practices."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paracelsus was a critic of Aristotelian natural philosophy and medicine, and his work often represents a departure from or a challenge to Aristotelian doctrines, making their relationship one of intellectual opposition and engagement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paracelsus was a critic of Aristotelian natural philosophy and medicine, and his work often represents a departure from or a challenge to Aristotelian doctrines, making their relationship one of intellectual opposition and engagement."}, {"found_name": "Averroes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 952, "target_cluster_name": "Averroes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant commentators and interpreters of ancient philosophical and medical texts, particularly Aristotle. Averroes was a foundational figure in Islamic philosophy whose influence extended to medieval and early modern European thought, which Paracelsus engaged with, often critically.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant commentators and interpreters of ancient philosophical and medical texts, particularly Aristotle. Averroes was a foundational figure in Islamic philosophy whose influence extended to medieval and early modern European thought, which Paracelsus engaged with, often critically."}, {"found_name": "Avicenna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Avicenna was a towering figure in Islamic medicine and philosophy whose works were central to medical education in Europe during Paracelsus's time. Paracelsus was a vocal critic of the Galenic-Avicennian medical tradition, positioning himself in opposition to Avicenna's authority.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Avicenna was a towering figure in Islamic medicine and philosophy whose works were central to medical education in Europe during Paracelsus's time. Paracelsus was a vocal critic of the Galenic-Avicennian medical tradition, positioning himself in opposition to Avicenna's authority."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a foundational authority on materia medica and pharmacology. Paracelsus, while often critical of traditional herbalism, engaged with and sought to reform the understanding of medicinal substances, a field Dioscorides had pioneered.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was a foundational authority on materia medica and pharmacology. Paracelsus, while often critical of traditional herbalism, engaged with and sought to reform the understanding of medicinal substances, a field Dioscorides had pioneered."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was the preeminent authority in ancient Greco-Roman medicine. Paracelsus famously and vehemently rejected much of Galenic medicine, advocating for new theories and treatments, making their relationship one of direct, critical opposition and a defining aspect of Paracelsus's medical reformism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was the preeminent authority in ancient Greco-Roman medicine. Paracelsus famously and vehemently rejected much of Galenic medicine, advocating for new theories and treatments, making their relationship one of direct, critical opposition and a defining aspect of Paracelsus's medical reformism."}, {"found_name": "Helmonte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1706, "target_cluster_name": "Helmonte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Helmont was heavily influenced by Paracelsus's alchemical and iatrochemical ideas, and both were key figures in the transition from Galenic medicine to new chemical and physiological understandings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Helmont was heavily influenced by Paracelsus's alchemical and iatrochemical ideas, and both were key figures in the transition from Galenic medicine to new chemical and physiological understandings.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Crolius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2163, "target_cluster_name": "Crolius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paracelsus was a revolutionary figure in medicine and alchemy, whose iatrochemical theories and emphasis on practical application profoundly impacted subsequent alchemical and medical thought, including Croll's.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Paracelsus was a revolutionary figure in medicine and alchemy, whose iatrochemical theories and emphasis on practical application profoundly impacted subsequent alchemical and medical thought, including Croll's.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mizaldus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2710, "target_cluster_name": "Mizaldus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in Renaissance medicine and natural philosophy, though with different approaches. Mizaldus was influenced by Paracelsian ideas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures in Renaissance medicine and natural philosophy, though with different approaches. Mizaldus was influenced by Paracelsian ideas.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 14, "variants": ["Pigeons", "pigeons"], "contexts": ["domestic birds studied for breeds", "domesticated bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 6, "variants": ["pigeons"], "contexts": ["animals used in experiments", "birds studied for vision after hemisphere removal"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeons"], "contexts": ["animal"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pigeon"], "contexts": ["synonym for Dove", "common bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 13, "variants": ["Pigeon", "pigeon"], "contexts": ["domesticated bird breeds", "domesticated bird species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 704, "stable_key": "clu_6e3b7fbe969c8aeb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pigeon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10856", "wikidata_description": "family of birds (Aves)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbidae", "wikipedia_extract": "Columbidae is a bird family consisting of doves and pigeons. It is the only family in the order Columbiformes. These are stout-bodied birds with small heads, relatively short necks and slender bills that in some species feature fleshy ceres. They feed largely on plant matter, feeding on seeds (granivory), fruit (frugivory), and foliage (folivory). In colloquial English, the smaller species tend to be called \"doves\", and the larger ones \"pigeons\", although the distinction is not consistent, and there is no scientific separation between them. Historically, the common names for these birds involve a great deal of variation."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pombos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1919, "target_cluster_name": "pombos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'pigeon' is often used interchangeably with 'dove' in common usage, and the entity description explicitly states that the rock dove is the wild form of the bird that is commonly referred to as a 'pigeon'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'pigeon' is often used interchangeably with 'dove' in common usage, and the entity description explicitly states that the rock dove is the wild form of the bird that is commonly referred to as a 'pigeon'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "coton", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "coton", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "coton", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 18, "variants": ["coton", "Coton", "cotonnier"], "contexts": ["material used for bands", "plant used for fiber"]}, {"entity_id": "cotton", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cotton", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cotton"], "contexts": ["fibrous plant material"]}, {"entity_id": "cotton", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cotton", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["cotton"], "contexts": ["agricultural commodity traded", "importing cotton"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "coton", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cotton", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "coton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cotton", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cotton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cotton", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 705, "stable_key": "clu_f89bc72959cc74df", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cotton", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Gossypium", "family": "Malvaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q11457", "wikidata_description": "plant fiber from the genus Gossypium", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton", "wikipedia_extract": "Cotton (from Arabic  qutn) is a soft, fluffy staple fiber that grows in a boll, or protective case, around the seeds of the cotton plants of the genus Gossypium in the mallow family Malvaceae. The fiber is almost pure cellulose, and can contain minor percentages of waxes, fats, pectins, and water. Under natural conditions, the cotton bolls will increase the dispersal of the seeds. The plant is a shrub native to tropical and subtropical regions around the world, including the Americas, Africa, Egypt and India. The greatest diversity of wild cotton species is found in Mexico, followed by Australia and Africa. Cotton was independently domesticated in the Old and New Worlds."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Vegetation", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2157, "target_cluster_name": "Vegetation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "French for 'Cotton', a specific type of plant life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: French for 'Cotton', a specific type of plant life.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Plant fibers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2956, "target_cluster_name": "fibres", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cotton is a primary example of a plant fiber, and historical texts often discuss them together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cotton is a primary example of a plant fiber, and historical texts often discuss them together."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cordova", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "cordova", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cordova", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Cordova", "Cordoba"], "contexts": ["location south of Pampas", "province in Buenos Aires"]}, {"entity_id": "córdova", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Córdova", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Córdova"], "contexts": ["city in Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "cordova", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cordova", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Cordova", "cordova"], "contexts": ["cordovan water for moistening vapors", "city known for its waters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cordova", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Córdova", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cordova", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cordova", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Córdova", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cordova", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 706, "stable_key": "clu_dafb7b0d2fcd24a6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Córdoba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "City in Spain, also a province in Argentina.", "wikipedia_extract": "Córdoba ( KOR-də-bə; Spanish: [ˈkoɾðoβa] ), or sometimes Cordova ( KOR-də-və), is a city in Andalusia, Spain, and the capital of the province of Córdoba. With a population of 324,902 as of 2024, it is the 12th-largest city in Spain and the 3rd-largest in Andalusia. The city primarily lies on the right bank of the Guadalquivir in the south of the Iberian Peninsula. Once a Roman colony, it was taken over by the Visigothic Kingdom in the sixth century and then conquered by the Muslims in the eighth century. Córdoba became the capital of the Emirate and then Caliphate of Córdoba, from which the Umayyad dynasty ruled al-Andalus. Under Umayyad rule, Córdoba was transformed into a centre of education and learning, and by the 10th century it had grown to be the second-largest city in Europe."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Saussure", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "saussure", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saussure", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Saussure"], "contexts": ["scholar who experimented on soil fertility", "measurer of mountain heights"]}, {"entity_id": "saussure", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Saussure", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Saussure"], "contexts": ["measured Mount Vesuvius elevation", "compared measurements with author"]}, {"entity_id": "mm__saussure", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "MM. Saussure", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["MM. Saussure"], "contexts": ["observed mine temperatures"]}, {"entity_id": "saussure", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Saussure", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saussure"], "contexts": ["discovered red lichen in Alps"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saussure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Saussure", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saussure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "MM. Saussure", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saussure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Saussure", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Saussure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "MM. Saussure", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Saussure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Saussure", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 707, "stable_key": "clu_5e7a91569da7c5be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horace-Bénédict de Saussure", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1740, "death_year": 1799, "description": "Swiss geologist, meteorologist, and botanist, studied alpine environments.", "wikidata_id": "Q115525", "wikidata_description": "Genevan scientist (1740-1799)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horace%20B%C3%A9n%C3%A9dict%20de%20Saussure", "wikipedia_extract": "Horace Bénédict de Saussure (French: [ɔʁas benedikt də sosyʁ]; 17 February 1740 – 22 January 1799) was a Genevan geologist, meteorologist, physicist, mountaineer and Alpine explorer (specifically the Mont Blanc massif), often called the founder of alpinism and modern meteorology, and considered to be the first person to build a successful solar oven. Horace Bénédict de Saussure was born 17 February 1740, in Conches, near Geneva, in the Republic of Geneva. Saussure's family were Genevan patricians. His father, Nicolas de Saussure, was an agriculturist, agronomist and author. Because his mother was \"sickly\", Saussure was brought up by his mother's sister and her husband the Genevan naturalist Charles Bonnet, who sparked Horace-Bénédict's early interest in botany. After attending the \"Collège\" of his hometown, he completed his studies at the Geneva Academy in 1759 with a dissertation on heat (Dissertatio physica de igne)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent explorers and naturalists of their time, with Humboldt being a major figure in geography and natural sciences, a field Saussure also contributed to.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent explorers and naturalists of their time, with Humboldt being a major figure in geography and natural sciences, a field Saussure also contributed to."}, {"found_name": "Geologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 956, "target_cluster_name": "geologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saussure is described as a geologist and a founder of alpinism, which involves geological exploration of mountains.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Saussure is described as a geologist and a founder of alpinism, which involves geological exploration of mountains."}, {"found_name": "Aimé Bonpland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1671, "target_cluster_name": "M. Bonpland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked with Alexander von Humboldt, a contemporary and peer of Saussure in the exploration of natural sciences and geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked with Alexander von Humboldt, a contemporary and peer of Saussure in the exploration of natural sciences and geography."}, {"found_name": "Ramond", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1979, "target_cluster_name": "Ramond", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saussure was a pioneering naturalist and geologist, particularly known for his work in the Alps, a region that Ramond also extensively studied and wrote about. Ramond was deeply influenced by Saussure's work and methods.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Saussure was a pioneering naturalist and geologist, particularly known for his work in the Alps, a region that Ramond also extensively studied and wrote about. Ramond was deeply influenced by Saussure's work and methods.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Chimborazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "chimborazo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chimborazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 10, "variants": ["Chimborazo"], "contexts": ["location in South America", "mountain peak in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "cimborazo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cimborazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cimborazo"], "contexts": ["mountain name"]}, {"entity_id": "chimborazo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chimborazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Chimborazo"], "contexts": ["mountain elevation data", "great Colossus of the Andes"]}, {"entity_id": "chimborazo", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chimborazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chimborazo"], "contexts": ["mountain for gravitational comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chimborazo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chimborazo", "similarity": 0.76}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chimborazo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chimborazo", "similarity": 0.76}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cimborazo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chimborazo", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cimborazo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chimborazo", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Chimborazo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chimborazo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 708, "stable_key": "clu_8bc009f320e189a6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chimborazo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q238492", "wikidata_description": "province of Ecuador", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimborazo%20Province", "wikipedia_extract": "Chimborazo (Spanish: [tʃimboˈɾaso] ) is a stratovolcano in Ecuador and the Cordillera Occidental range of the Andes. Its last known eruption is believed to have occurred around AD 550. Although not the tallest mountain in the Andes or on Earth relative to sea level, its summit is the farthest point on Earth's surface from the Earth's center due to its location along the planet's equatorial bulge. Chimborazo's height from sea level is 6,263 m (20,548 ft), well below that of Mount Everest at 8,849 m (29,032 ft). Chimborazo is the highest mountain in Ecuador and the 39th-highest peak in the entire Andes. It is a popular destination for mountaineering due to its challenging climbing routes, which involve traversing snow, ice, and rocky terrain."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chimborazo is a prominent volcano in the Andes mountain range.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chimborazo is a prominent volcano in the Andes mountain range.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Quito", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 210, "target_cluster_name": "Quito", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chimborazo is a volcano and the 