ationship": "reverse: Bananas are botanically classified as berries.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Medicamentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 25, "members": [{"entity_id": "medicines", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Medicines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Medicines"], "contexts": ["Medicines of higher Natures"]}, {"entity_id": "medicines", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Medicines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Medicines"], "contexts": ["compounds for treating illness", "compounded of herbs, roots, flowers, seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "medicamentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Medicamentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 14, "variants": ["Medicamentos", "medicament.", "medicamenta", "medicamentapa", "medicamenti", "medicamento", "medicamentos", "medicamentoſas", "medicamentum"], "contexts": ["medicines, remedies", "general term for remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "medicine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "medicine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["medicine"], "contexts": ["can intoxicate the head"]}, {"entity_id": "medicinas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "medicinas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["medicinas", "medicinas compueſtas"], "contexts": ["general term for medicinal preparations", "compound medicines"]}, {"entity_id": "medicine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "medicine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["medicine"], "contexts": ["practice influenced by anatomy and physiology", "described by physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Medicines", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Medicines", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Medicines", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicamentos", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Medicines", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicamentos", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 719, "stable_key": "clu_420ad2ed0e2b03e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medicine", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the practice of healing and remedies.", "wikidata_id": "Q12140", "wikidata_description": "substance used to diagnose, cure, treat, or prevent disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substance_dependence", "wikipedia_extract": "Substance dependence, also known as drug dependence, is a biopsychological situation whereby an individual's functionality is dependent on the necessitated re-consumption of a psychoactive substance because of an adaptive state that has developed within the individual from psychoactive substance consumption that results in the experience of withdrawal and that necessitates the re-consumption of the drug. A drug addiction, a distinct concept from substance dependence, is defined as compulsive, out-of-control drug use, despite negative consequences. An addictive drug is a drug which is both rewarding and reinforcing. ΔFosB, a gene transcription factor, which is now known to be a critical component and common factor in the development of virtually all forms of behavioral and drug addictions, but not dependence. The International Classification of Diseases classifies substance dependence as a mental and behavioural disorder. In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) (released in 2013), substance abuse and substance dependence were eliminated and replaced with the single diagnosis of substance use disorders."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "remedios", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1584, "target_cluster_name": "remedios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'Medicines' in Spanish.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Direct translation of 'Medicines' in Spanish.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "medicamente", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2482, "target_cluster_name": "medicamente", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'medicamentos' to 'Medicine'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Direct translation of 'medicamentos' to 'Medicine'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alcohol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 131, "target_cluster_name": "alcohol", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alcohol is a psychoactive substance that can lead to substance dependence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alcohol is a psychoactive substance that can lead to substance dependence."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cooling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "cooling", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "cooling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 16, "variants": ["cooling", "Cools", "cool", "coolness", "cools"], "contexts": ["temperature quality", "quality of Blites"]}, {"entity_id": "cooling", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cooling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["cooling"], "contexts": ["factor in rock transformation", "process of losing heat"]}, {"entity_id": "cooling", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "cooling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cooling"], "contexts": ["process of losing heat"]}, {"entity_id": "enfriar", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "enfriar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["enfriar"], "contexts": ["quality attributed to iron and mercury"]}, {"entity_id": "enfriamos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "enfriamos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["enfriamos", "resfriamos"], "contexts": ["becoming cold due to fear"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "cooling", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cooling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "cooling", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cooling", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cooling", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cooling", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 720, "stable_key": "clu_9876bdebb9b0fe76", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cooling", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooling", "note": "Refers to the process or quality of becoming cold or decreasing in temperature.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cooling is removal of heat, usually resulting in a lower temperature and/or phase change. Temperature lowering achieved by any other means may also be called cooling. The transfer of thermal energy may occur via thermal radiation, heat conduction or convection. Examples can be as simple as reducing temperature of tea."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aromaticum Rosarum", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2702, "target_cluster_name": "Aromaticum Rosarum", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "the Mouth, Throat, or other parts, both to clens and heal them, and to stay the Fluxes of Humors falling upon them; it is also used in Clisters both to cool and clens. The Cordial Pouders called Diarh", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "causa fria", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 4473, "target_cluster_name": "causa fria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a process directly related to the concept of coldness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a process directly related to the concept of coldness.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "friction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 155, "target_cluster_name": "friction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While friction typically generates heat, the concept of cooling is its inverse and is relevant when considering thermal equilibrium or the absence of frictional heating.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While friction typically generates heat, the concept of cooling is its inverse and is relevant when considering thermal equilibrium or the absence of frictional heating.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "propriedades", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 508, "target_cluster_name": "propriedades", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process that affects water and is related to its thermal properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is a process that affects water and is related to its thermal properties.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Conduction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2567, "target_cluster_name": "conduction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conduction is a primary mechanism by which cooling (heat transfer) occurs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Conduction is a primary mechanism by which cooling (heat transfer) occurs."}, {"found_name": "molecular motion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 861, "target_cluster_name": "molecular motion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process that involves a decrease in the kinetic energy of molecules, thus reducing molecular motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is a process that involves a decrease in the kinetic energy of molecules, thus reducing molecular motion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "radiations of heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4412, "target_cluster_name": "radiations of heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is the process of losing heat, which is directly related to the emission and absorption of thermal radiation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is the process of losing heat, which is directly related to the emission and absorption of thermal radiation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "théorie de la chaleur", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1360, "target_cluster_name": "théorie de la chaleur", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is the opposite process of heating and is directly related to the transfer and loss of heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is the opposite process of heating and is directly related to the transfer and loss of heat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "congelation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1407, "target_cluster_name": "congelation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is the process that leads to freezing and hypothermia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is the process that leads to freezing and hypothermia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "reverberation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1409, "target_cluster_name": "reverberation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process of heat loss, and while sound energy can be converted to heat, it's not a direct synonym or primary relationship to reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is a process of heat loss, and while sound energy can be converted to heat, it's not a direct synonym or primary relationship to reverberation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Spring", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1680, "target_cluster_name": "Spring", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring follows winter, a period of cooling, and precedes summer's heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spring follows winter, a period of cooling, and precedes summer's heat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "liquid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2020, "target_cluster_name": "liquid", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process that affects liquids, potentially leading to changes in state (e.g., freezing).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling is a process that affects liquids, potentially leading to changes in state (e.g., freezing).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "rain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2196, "target_cluster_name": "rain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling of atmospheric moisture is a primary mechanism for condensation and precipitation (rain).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cooling of atmospheric moisture is a primary mechanism for condensation and precipitation (rain).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "De Candolle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "de_candolle", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "De Candolle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["De Candolle"], "contexts": ["philosophically shown organic beings compete", "founded divisions of Umbelliferæ on seed differences"]}, {"entity_id": "de_candolle", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "De Candolle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["De Candolle"], "contexts": ["proposed nomenclature"]}, {"entity_id": "de_candolle", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "De Candolle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 15, "variants": ["De Candolle", "A. De Candolle", "De CandoUe"], "contexts": ["author on origin of cultivated plants", "author of Orig. des plantes cultivées"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "De Candolle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "De Candolle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "De Candolle", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "De Candolle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "De Candolle", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "De Candolle", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 721, "stable_key": "clu_7ad15409f070cd19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Augustin Pyramus de Candolle", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Swiss botanist influential in the classification of plants.", "note": "Also refers to his son, Alphonse Pyramus de Candolle.", "wikipedia_extract": "Augustin Pyramus (or Pyrame) de Candolle (UK: , US: , French: [kɑ̃dɔl]; 4 February 1778 – 9 September 1841) was a Swiss botanist. René Louiche Desfontaines launched de Candolle's botanical career by recommending him at a herbarium. Within a couple of years de Candolle had established a new genus, and he went on to document hundreds of plant families and create a new natural plant classification system. Although de Candolle's main focus was botany, he also contributed to related fields such as phytogeography, agronomy, paleontology, medical botany, and economic botany. De Candolle originated the idea of \"Nature's war\", which influenced Charles Darwin and the principle of natural selection. De Candolle recognized that multiple species may develop similar characteristics that did not appear in a common evolutionary ancestor; a phenomenon now known as convergent evolution."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Boussingault", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 572, "target_cluster_name": "Boussingault", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "De Candolle was a renowned Swiss botanist. Boussingault, while known for agricultural chemistry, also conducted significant botanical studies and collections during his travels, particularly in South America, suggesting a professional connection within the natural sciences.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: De Candolle was a renowned Swiss botanist. Boussingault, while known for agricultural chemistry, also conducted significant botanical studies and collections during his travels, particularly in South America, suggesting a professional connection within the natural sciences.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 851, "target_cluster_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent botanists of the same era.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent botanists of the same era."}, {"found_name": "Carl Linnaeus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2721, "target_cluster_name": "Linnæus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Linnaeus was a foundational figure in botany, and de Candolle built upon his work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Linnaeus was a foundational figure in botany, and de Candolle built upon his work."}, {"found_name": "Antoine Laurent de Jussieu", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3366, "target_cluster_name": "Jussieu", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jussieu was a significant figure in plant taxonomy, a field de Candolle also contributed to.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jussieu was a significant figure in plant taxonomy, a field de Candolle also contributed to."}, {"found_name": "Georges Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 119, "target_cluster_name": "Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naturalists in France during the same period, though their primary fields differed (paleontology vs. botany).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent naturalists in France during the same period, though their primary fields differed (paleontology vs. botany)."}, {"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naturalists and explorers of the same era, with overlapping interests in botany and natural history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent naturalists and explorers of the same era, with overlapping interests in botany and natural history."}, {"found_name": "Abel Rémusat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1096, "target_cluster_name": "Abel Rémusat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century natural history and science, with Rémusat being a sinologist and de Candolle a botanist. They likely interacted within the scientific circles of Paris.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures in 19th-century natural history and science, with Rémusat being a sinologist and de Candolle a botanist. They likely interacted within the scientific circles of Paris.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ramond", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1979, "target_cluster_name": "Ramond", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential botanists of the same era, contributing to the classification and understanding of plant life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential botanists of the same era, contributing to the classification and understanding of plant life.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Linnæus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2721, "target_cluster_name": "Linnæus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "De Candolle was a major figure in botany after Linnaeus, working to refine and expand plant classification systems, often referencing and building upon Linnaean principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: De Candolle was a major figure in botany after Linnaeus, working to refine and expand plant classification systems, often referencing and building upon Linnaean principles.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "lune", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "lune", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lune", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 14, "variants": ["Lune", "lune", "LUNE"], "contexts": ["celestial body", "celestial body with circular basins"]}, {"entity_id": "moon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "moon", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["moon"], "contexts": ["natural satellite of the earth", "celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "luna", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Luna", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Luna"], "contexts": ["celestial body, the moon"]}, {"entity_id": "astre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "astre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["astre"], "contexts": ["celestial body, likely the moon"]}, {"entity_id": "astrea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Astrea", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Astrea"], "contexts": ["primary planet in the solar system", "asteroid planet discovered by Hencke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lune", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "moon", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lune", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Luna", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "moon", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Luna", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 722, "stable_key": "clu_3abf67743346cdb5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moon", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'lune' was used in some contexts to refer to the moon, particularly in older texts or when discussing its astronomical or astrological significance. 'Luna' is the Latin word for moon.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon", "wikipedia_extract": "The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits around Earth at an average distance of 384,399 kilometres (238,854 mi), a distance roughly 30 times the width of Earth. It completes an orbit (lunar month) in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period (lunar day) to its orbital period (lunar month)."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "heavenly bodies", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1950, "target_cluster_name": "heavenly bodies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Moon is a celestial body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "planets", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2065, "target_cluster_name": "planets", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "system, that is to say, the variously-formed matter circling round the Sun, consists, according to the present state of our knowledge of 'eleven primary planets',* eighteen satellites p 91 or secondar", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for secondary planets", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "celestial bodies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1950, "target_cluster_name": "heavenly bodies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon is a celestial body."}, {"found_name": "Sun", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 573, "target_cluster_name": "Sun", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon orbits the Earth in relation to the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon orbits the Earth in relation to the Sun."}, {"found_name": "planet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2065, "target_cluster_name": "planets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a satellite, often discussed alongside planets in astronomical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon is a satellite, often discussed alongside planets in astronomical contexts."}, {"found_name": "Star", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3914, "target_cluster_name": "Star", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a celestial object, often discussed in relation to stars.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon is a celestial object, often discussed in relation to stars."}, {"found_name": "comet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 291, "target_cluster_name": "comet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a celestial object, often discussed alongside comets in astronomical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon is a celestial object, often discussed alongside comets in astronomical contexts."}, {"found_name": "luminary", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3164, "target_cluster_name": "illum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Moon is a natural luminary.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Moon is a natural luminary."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "biot", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Biot"], "contexts": ["scholar who fixed magnetic equator position"]}, {"entity_id": "biot", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 13, "variants": ["Biot"], "contexts": ["investigated twilight phenomenon", "author of 'Traité d'Astronomie Physique'"]}, {"entity_id": "biot", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Biot"], "contexts": ["discovered circular polarization", "discovered properties in liquids"]}, {"entity_id": "mm__biot", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "MM. Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["MM. Biot"], "contexts": ["noticed magnetic force differences"]}, {"entity_id": "m__biot", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "M. Biot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["M. Biot"], "contexts": ["contributed mathematical portion of memoir", "contributed to scientific facts"]}, {"entity_id": "biot,_m_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Biot, M.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Biot, M."], "contexts": ["balloon ascent, sound transmission, polarization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Biot", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Biot", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Biot", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Biot", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Biot", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Biot", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 723, "stable_key": "clu_d7d86b2fa24ddf74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste Biot", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1774, "death_year": 1862, "description": "French physicist, astronomer, and mathematician. Investigated polarization and magnetic equator.", "wikidata_id": "Q58328", "wikidata_description": "French scientist (1774-1862)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste%20Biot", "wikipedia_extract": "Jean-Baptiste Biot (; French: [ʒɑ̃ batist bjo]; 21 April 1774 – 3 February 1862) was a French physicist, astronomer, and mathematician who co-discovered the Biot–Savart law of magnetostatics with Félix Savart, established the reality of meteorites, made an early balloon flight, and studied the polarization of light. The biot (a CGS unit of electrical current), the mineral biotite, and Cape Biot in eastern Greenland were named in his honour. Jean-Baptiste Biot was born in Paris on 21 April 1774 the son of Joseph Biot, a treasury official. He was educated at Lyceum Louis-le-Grand and École Polytechnique in 1794. Biot served in the artillery before he was appointed professor of mathematics at Beauvais in 1797. He later went on to become a professor of physics at the Collège de France around 1800, and three years later was elected as a member of the French Academy of Sciences."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Félix Savary", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4333, "target_cluster_name": "Savary", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Félix Savary is listed as a co-discoverer of the Biot-Savart law with Jean-Baptiste Biot.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Félix Savary is listed as a co-discoverer of the Biot-Savart law with Jean-Baptiste Biot."}, {"found_name": "Félix de Azara", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1076, "target_cluster_name": "Azara", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Félix de Azara was a naturalist and explorer whose work on South American fauna was studied by Biot.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Félix de Azara was a naturalist and explorer whose work on South American fauna was studied by Biot."}, {"found_name": "François Arago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 217, "target_cluster_name": "Arago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "François Arago was a contemporary French physicist and astronomer who collaborated with and was influenced by Biot.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "François Arago was a contemporary French physicist and astronomer who collaborated with and was influenced by Biot."}, {"found_name": "Dominique François Jean Arago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1818, "target_cluster_name": "M. Arago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dominique François Jean Arago was a contemporary French physicist and astronomer who collaborated with and was influenced by Biot.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dominique François Jean Arago was a contemporary French physicist and astronomer who collaborated with and was influenced by Biot."}, {"found_name": "Fourier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2132, "target_cluster_name": "Fourier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent French physicists and mathematicians who worked in the early 19th century. They collaborated and corresponded, particularly on topics related to heat and optics. Biot's experimental work often tested or confirmed theoretical predictions made by Fourier.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent French physicists and mathematicians who worked in the early 19th century. They collaborated and corresponded, particularly on topics related to heat and optics. Biot's experimental work often tested or confirmed theoretical predictions made by Fourier.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ampère", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2134, "target_cluster_name": "Ampère", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Biot was a contemporary physicist and mathematician who collaborated with and was influenced by Ampère's work on electromagnetism. They engaged in scientific correspondence and their research areas overlapped significantly.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Biot was a contemporary physicist and mathematician who collaborated with and was influenced by Ampère's work on electromagnetism. They engaged in scientific correspondence and their research areas overlapped significantly.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Analysis", "analysis"], "contexts": ["breaking down into parts", "title of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["analysis", "Analysis"], "contexts": ["skill required to follow scientific outlines", "breaking down into components"]}, {"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["analysis"], "contexts": ["breaking down into components"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Analysis", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "analysis", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "analysis", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 724, "stable_key": "clu_1b827e2a6fa3cd4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "analysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Both 'analyze' and 'analysis' in this context refer to the intellectual process of breaking down complex subjects into their constituent parts, a fundamental concept in scientific and philosophical inquiry.", "wikidata_id": "Q15751184", "wikidata_description": "mathematics journal", "wikipedia_extract": "A concept is an abstract idea that serves as a foundation for more concrete principles, thoughts, and beliefs. Concepts play an important role in all aspects of cognition. As such, concepts are studied within such disciplines as linguistics, psychology, and philosophy, and these disciplines are interested in the logical and psychological structure of concepts, and how they are put together to form thoughts and sentences. The study of concepts has served as an important flagship of an emerging interdisciplinary approach, cognitive science. In contemporary philosophy, three understandings of a concept prevail: mental representations, such that a concept is an entity that exists in the mind (a mental object) abilities peculiar to cognitive agents (mental states) Fregean senses, abstract objects rather than a mental object or a mental state Concepts are classified into a hierarchy, higher levels of which are termed \"superordinate\" and lower levels termed \"subordinate\". Additionally, there is the \"basic\" or \"middle\" level at which people will most readily categorize a concept.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemical analysis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2604, "target_cluster_name": "chemical analysis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis is a specific scientific application of the general concept of analysis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical analysis is a specific scientific application of the general concept of analysis."}, {"found_name": "Calculation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1380, "target_cluster_name": "calculation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calculation is a process that often involves analysis to arrive at a result.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Calculation is a process that often involves analysis to arrive at a result."}]}, {"canonical_name": "gases", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "gase