/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric%20current", "wikipedia_extract": "An electric current is a flow of charged particles, such as electrons or ions, moving through an electrical conductor or space. It is defined as the net rate of flow of electric charge through a surface. The moving particles are called charge carriers, which may be one of several types of particles, depending on the conductor. In electric circuits the charge carriers are often electrons moving through a wire. In semiconductors they can be electrons or holes. In an electrolyte the charge carriers are ions, while in plasma, an ionized gas, they are ions and electrons."}, "member_count": 10, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "electric explosions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1093, "target_cluster_name": "electric explosions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrical discharge is a transient flow of electric current.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electrical discharge is a transient flow of electric current.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "electro-magnetism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1802, "target_cluster_name": "electro-magnetism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric currents are a fundamental component of electromagnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electric currents are a fundamental component of electromagnetism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "electric tension", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2828, "target_cluster_name": "electric tension", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric current is the flow of electric charge, which is driven by electric potential difference. A potential difference is necessary for a current to flow.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electric current is the flow of electric charge, which is driven by electric potential difference. A potential difference is necessary for a current to flow.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "trachyte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1626, "target_cluster_name": "trachyte", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "h vocanoes are justy termed in many languages \"fire-emitting mountains,\" mountains of this kind are not formed by the gradual accumulation of ejected currents of lava, but their origin seems rather to", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of rock associated with volcanic formation"}, {"found_name": "labradoritic augite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "h vocanoes are justy termed in many languages \"fire-emitting mountains,\" mountains of this kind are not formed by the gradual accumulation of ejected currents of lava, but their origin seems rather to", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of mineral associated with volcanic formation"}]}, {"canonical_name": "chemical affinity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "chemical_affinity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemical affinity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["chemical affinity"], "contexts": ["force acting on molecules", "force driving chemical reactions"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_affinity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "chemical affinity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["chemical affinity", "Chemical Affinity"], "contexts": ["tendency of atoms to combine", "force of attraction between particles"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_affinities", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "chemical affinities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["chemical affinities"], "contexts": ["forces attracting chemical elements", "series of chemical affinities"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_affinity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemical affinity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["chemical affinity"], "contexts": ["force causing chemical combination", "general power studied by science"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_affinities", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemical affinities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemical affinities"], "contexts": ["attraction between different chemical units"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical affinity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical affinities", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinities", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "chemical affinity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinities", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "chemical affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinity", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "chemical affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical affinities", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 727, "stable_key": "clu_19774b1a781ab273", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemical affinity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q382783", "wikidata_description": "used to describe or characterise elements' or compounds' readiness to form bonds", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical%20affinity", "wikipedia_extract": "In chemical physics and physical chemistry, chemical affinity is the electronic property by which dissimilar chemical species are capable of forming chemical compounds. Chemical affinity can also refer to the tendency of an atom or compound to combine by chemical reaction with atoms or compounds of unlike composition. The idea of affinity is extremely old. Many attempts have been made at identifying its origins. The majority of such attempts, however, except in a general manner, end in futility since \"affinities\" lie at the basis of all magic, thereby pre-dating science. Physical chemistry, however, was one of the first branches of science to study and formulate a \"theory of affinity\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cohesive force", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2484, "target_cluster_name": "cohesive force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both cohesive force and chemical affinity describe forces that hold particles together, though chemical affinity is more specific to interactions between different substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both cohesive force and chemical affinity describe forces that hold particles together, though chemical affinity is more specific to interactions between different substances.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "attraction and repulsion", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2658, "target_cluster_name": "attraction and repulsion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical texts often used 'attraction' metaphorically or literally to describe the forces that bind chemical substances together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historical texts often used 'attraction' metaphorically or literally to describe the forces that bind chemical substances together.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 82, "target_cluster_name": "attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity is a specific type of attraction between chemical species, often discussed alongside general concepts of attraction in physics and philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity is a specific type of attraction between chemical species, often discussed alongside general concepts of attraction in physics and philosophy."}, {"found_name": "attractive", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1309, "target_cluster_name": "attractive", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity describes the tendency of certain chemical substances to combine, a concept closely related to attraction at the molecular level.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical affinity describes the tendency of certain chemical substances to combine, a concept closely related to attraction at the molecular level.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "physical forces", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 345, "target_cluster_name": "physical forces", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity is a specific type of force that governs chemical reactions, a subset of physical interactions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical affinity is a specific type of force that governs chemical reactions, a subset of physical interactions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "affinities", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 388, "target_cluster_name": "affinities", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While 'affinity' in geometry refers to a specific type of transformation, 'chemical affinity' refers to the tendency of substances to combine, a related but distinct concept within the broader historical understanding of 'affinity' as a force or relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While 'affinity' in geometry refers to a specific type of transformation, 'chemical affinity' refers to the tendency of substances to combine, a related but distinct concept within the broader historical understanding of 'affinity' as a force or relationship.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Chemical force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2487, "target_cluster_name": "chemical force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity is a fundamental concept related to the forces that drive chemical reactions and the formation of compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity is a fundamental concept related to the forces that drive chemical reactions and the formation of compounds."}, {"found_name": "Chemical reactions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1769, "target_cluster_name": "chemical action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity is the driving force behind chemical reactions, determining the likelihood and nature of compound formation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity is the driving force behind chemical reactions, determining the likelihood and nature of compound formation."}, {"found_name": "Forces of attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4344, "target_cluster_name": "forces of attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity is a specific manifestation of attractive forces at the molecular level, leading to the formation of chemical bonds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity is a specific manifestation of attractive forces at the molecular level, leading to the formation of chemical bonds."}, {"found_name": "cohesion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 749, "target_cluster_name": "cohesion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity describes the attraction between different types of molecules, which is a related but distinct concept from cohesion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical affinity describes the attraction between different types of molecules, which is a related but distinct concept from cohesion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical change", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 989, "target_cluster_name": "chemical change", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity describes the tendency of substances t