lor. Indigo is a natural dye obtained from the leaves of some plants of the Indigofera genus, in particular Indigofera tinctoria. Dye-bearing Indigofera plants were once common throughout the world. Blue colorants are relatively rare. Since indigo is insoluble, it is also referred to as a pigment (C.I. Pigment Blue 66, C.I.)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "blue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1762, "target_cluster_name": "blue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Indigo is a specific shade of blue, often considered a distinct color in historical contexts, particularly in discussions of pigments and dyes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Indigo is a specific shade of blue, often considered a distinct color in historical contexts, particularly in discussions of pigments and dyes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Violet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1852, "target_cluster_name": "Violet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Indigo is a deep blue-violet color, often considered a shade of violet or closely related to it in historical color terminology and dye production.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Indigo is a deep blue-violet color, often considered a shade of violet or closely related to it in historical color terminology and dye production.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "mace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "mace", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mace"], "contexts": ["flosculous coat of nutmeg fruit"]}, {"entity_id": "mace", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Mace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mace"], "contexts": ["spice from nutmeg", "spice added to decoctions"]}, {"entity_id": "mace", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "mace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["mace", "Macc", "Mace"], "contexts": ["fragrant spice for pounding", "aril of nutmeg, a spice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mace", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Mace", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mace", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "mace", "similarity": 0.856}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mace", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "mace", "similarity": 0.856}], "id": 734, "stable_key": "clu_6804b87751aca508", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mace", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myristica fragrans", "family": "Myristicaceae", "description": "Spice from the aril of the nutmeg fruit.", "note": "Contexts for 'Ma' and 'Mayz' suggest possible confusion with maize (corn).", "wikipedia_extract": "Myristica fragrans, commonly known as the nutmeg tree, is an evergreen species indigenous to the Maluku Islands of Indonesia. This aromatic tree is economically significant as the primary source of two distinct spices: nutmeg, derived from its seed, and mace, obtained from the seed's aril. Valued for centuries in global spice trade, M. fragrans is now widely cultivated throughout tropical regions, including parts of Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Malaysia), South Asia (Kerala in India, Sri Lanka), East Asia (Guangdong and Yunnan in China, Taiwan), the Caribbean (notably Grenada), and South America. Myristica fragrans is an evergreen tree, usually 5–15 m (16–49 ft) tall, but occasionally reaching 20 m (66 ft) or even 30 m (98 ft) on Tidore. The alternately arranged leaves are dark green, 5–15 cm (2.0–5.9 in) long by 2–7 cm (0.8–2.8 in) wide with petioles about 1 cm (0.4 in) long. The species is dioecious, i.e. \"male\" or staminate flowers and \"female\" or carpellate flowers are borne on different plants, although occasional individuals produce both kinds of flower."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nutmeg", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2692, "target_cluster_name": "Nutmeg", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mace is the aril of the nutmeg seed, making it a distinct spice derived from the same plant (Myristica fragrans).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mace is the aril of the nutmeg seed, making it a distinct spice derived from the same plant (Myristica fragrans)."}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2918, "target_cluster_name": "Ginger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mace is the aril of the nutmeg fruit, and ginger is a spice from a rhizome. Both are common spices often found together in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mace is the aril of the nutmeg fruit, and ginger is a spice from a rhizome. Both are common spices often found together in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "frutas", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": 72, "target_cluster_name": "frutas", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "ordinato ,& determinato, s^inten dein quello modo cioè j che i fiori ,i'herbe frciche , ^1^ le fec- che odorate , le radici piccole , & odorate , i frutti , & fcorze forcfttere,& odoratg, comeje noci ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: spice derived from nutmeg, requiring little cooking", "is