 Portugal and Spain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Count of Redondo is a noble title, and 'fidalgo' refers to a member of the nobility in Portugal and Spain."}, {"found_name": "Señores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2620, "target_cluster_name": "Señores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fidalgo is a Portuguese and Galician term for a nobleman or gentleman, closely related in social standing to the English 'Gentleman'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fidalgo is a Portuguese and Galician term for a nobleman or gentleman, closely related in social standing to the English 'Gentleman'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cohesion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "cohesion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "cohesion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["cohesion"], "contexts": ["force holding matter together", "concept of sticking together"]}, {"entity_id": "cohesion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "cohesion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["cohesion", "Cohesion"], "contexts": ["force holding particles together", "force of attraction between particles"]}, {"entity_id": "cohesion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cohesion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["cohesion"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter", "tendency of parts to stick together"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cohesion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cohesion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cohesion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cohesion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "cohesion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cohesion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 749, "stable_key": "clu_9035919fe3fbb00a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cohesion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q216828", "wikidata_description": "chemical property of like molecules sticking together", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohesion%20%28chemistry%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In chemistry and physics, cohesion (from Latin  cohaesiō 'cohesion, unity'), also called cohesive attraction or cohesive force, is the action or property of like molecules sticking together, being mutually attractive. It is an intrinsic property of a substance that is caused by the shape and structure of its molecules, which makes the distribution of surrounding electrons irregular when molecules get close to one another, creating an electrical attraction that can maintain a macroscopic structure such as a water drop. Cohesion allows for surface tension, creating a \"solid-like\" state upon which light-weight or low-density materials can be placed. Water, for example, is strongly cohesive as each molecule may make four hydrogen bonds to other water molecules in a tetrahedral configuration. This results in a relatively strong Coulomb force between molecules. In simple terms, the polarity (a state in which a molecule is oppositely charged on its poles) of water molecules allows them to be attracted to each other."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cohesive force", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2484, "target_cluster_name": "cohesive force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The definition explicitly states that cohesion is also called cohesive attraction or cohesive force.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The definition explicitly states that cohesion is also called cohesive attraction or cohesive force."}, {"found_name": "Attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 82, "target_cluster_name": "attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cohesion is a specific type of attraction between like molecules.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cohesion is a specific type of attraction between like molecules."}, {"found_name": "Chemical affinity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 727, "target_cluster_name": "chemical affinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity describes the attraction between different types of molecules, which is a related but distinct concept from cohesion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity describes the attraction between different types of molecules, which is a related but distinct concept from cohesion."}, {"found_name": "Forces of attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4344, "target_cluster_name": "forces of attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cohesion is a manifestation of attractive forces between molecules.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cohesion is a manifestation of attractive forces between molecules."}]}, {"canonical_name": "stars", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "groups_of_stars", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "groups of stars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["groups of stars"], "contexts": ["application of 'cosmos'"]}, {"entity_id": "stars