s to the pith or marrow of a plant, not the Medlar fruit itself.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mespilus germanica, known as the medlar or common medlar, is a large shrub or small tree in the rose family Rosaceae. When the genus Mespilus is included in the genus Crataegus, the correct name for this species is Crataegus germanica (L.) Kuntze. The fruit of this tree, also called medlar, has been cultivated since Roman times. It is usually available in winter and eaten when bletted. It may be consumed raw and in a range of cooked dishes. Under ideal circumstances, the deciduous plant grows up to 8 metres (26 feet) tall."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lithargyro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "litharge", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Litharge", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Litharge"], "contexts": ["danger from taking, treated by parsley"]}, {"entity_id": "lithargyro", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Lithargyro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 14, "variants": ["Lithargyro", "LITHARGYRO", "Litargyro doro", "Lithargyro d'argento", "Lytargirodoro", "lichargyro", "litharge", "lithargyro", "lithargyro d argento", "lytargiro", "lytharg.ro"], "contexts": ["a substance, likely lead monoxide", "spuma d'argento, generated from lead"]}, {"entity_id": "lithargyrij", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lithargyrij", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lithargyrij"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Litharge", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Lithargyro", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Litharge", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lithargyrij", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Lithargyro", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lithargyrij", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 775, "stable_key": "clu_33f3309632b021df", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Litharge", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A heavy, dull yellow oxide of lead; lead monoxide.", "wikipedia_extract": "Litharge (from Greek lithargyros, lithos 'stone' + argyros 'silver' λιθάργυρος) is one of the natural mineral forms of lead(II) oxide, PbO. Litharge is a secondary mineral which forms from the oxidation of galena ores. It forms as coatings and encrustations with internal tetragonal crystal structure. It is dimorphous with the yellow orthorhombic form massicot. It forms soft (Mohs hardness of 2), red, greasy-appearing crusts with a very high specific gravity of 9.14–9.35. PbO may be prepared by heating lead metal in air at approximately 600 °C (lead melts at only 300 °C)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Boticarios", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 587, "target_cluster_name": "Boticarios", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "primeyro lugar ,entre os remedios exteriores , àmanteyga de chumbo , cuja preparação quero enfinar em fer viçodobemcommum. Tomemde fezesdeouro, aqueos Boticarios chamão Litargirio , moidas fubtiliſſim", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lead", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 368, "target_cluster_name": "Lead", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Litharge is a lead monoxide, a specific compound derived from lead, and thus intrinsically related to the element itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Litharge is a lead monoxide, a specific compound derived from lead, and thus intrinsically related to the element itself.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Quicksilver", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 559, "target_cluster_name": "azougue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Litharge (lead oxide) and Quicksilver (mercury) are metallic oxides and were historically important in alchemy and early chemistry, often discussed together in texts concerning metals and their transformations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Litharge (lead oxide) and Quicksilver (mercury) are metallic oxides and were historically important in alchemy and early chemistry, often discussed together in texts concerning metals and their transformations."}, {"found_name": "chumbo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1692, "target_cluster_name": "chumbo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Litharge is a lead oxide, a compound derived from lead.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Litharge is a lead oxide, a compound derived from lead.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Great Britain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 42, "members": [{"entity_id": "great_britain", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Great Britain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Great Britain"], "contexts": ["island nation with peculiar mammals", "flora of Great Britain"]}, {"entity_id": "great_britain", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Great Britain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Great Britain"], "contexts": ["nation with extensive commerce and navy"]}, {"entity_id": "great_britain", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Great Britain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "coun