rcle is a two-dimensional shape, while a sphere is a three-dimensional object. Both are fundamental geometric forms defined by points equidistant from a center.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A circle is a two-dimensional shape, while a sphere is a three-dimensional object. Both are fundamental geometric forms defined by points equidistant from a center."}, {"found_name": "Globe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1825, "target_cluster_name": "globe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A globe is a spherical model of the Earth or other celestial body, directly representing a sphere in a physical form.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A globe is a spherical model of the Earth or other celestial body, directly representing a sphere in a physical form."}, {"found_name": "Spheroid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1966, "target_cluster_name": "spheroid", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A spheroid is a shape that is almost a sphere, often an oblate or prolate spheroid, differing slightly from a perfect sphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A spheroid is a shape that is almost a sphere, often an oblate or prolate spheroid, differing slightly from a perfect sphere."}]}, {"canonical_name": "brahmanes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "brahmins", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Brahmins", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Brahmins"], "contexts": ["followers of ancient Indian myth"]}, {"entity_id": "brahmanes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "brahmanes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 13, "variants": ["brahmanes", "Brahmane", "Brahmanes", "Bramanes", "Bramenes"], "contexts": ["group for whom hemp use was prohibited", "Indian priests or ascetics"]}, {"entity_id": "brahmins", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Brahmins", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brahmins"], "contexts": ["people in India using seven-day periods", "measured time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brahmins", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "brahmanes", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brahmins", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brahmins", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "brahmanes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brahmins", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 784, "stable_key": "clu_e71147a63563afba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Brahmins", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Priestly caste in Hinduism, associated with ancient Indian practices.", "wikidata_id": "Q194497", "wikidata_description": "1500–500 BC Indo-Aryan religious practices of northwest India", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical%20Vedic%20religion", "wikipedia_extract": "The historical Vedic religion, also called Vedism or Brahmanism, and sometimes ancient Hinduism or Vedic Hinduism, constituted the religious ideas and practices prevalent amongst some of the Indo-Aryan peoples of the northwest Indian subcontinent (Punjab and the western Ganges plain) during the Vedic period (c. 1500–500 BCE). These ideas and practices are found in the Vedic texts, and some Vedic rituals are still practised today. The Vedic religion is one of the major traditions which shaped modern Hinduism, though present-day Hinduism is significantly different from the historical Vedic religion. The Vedic religion has roots in the Indo-Iranian culture and religion of the Sintashta (c. 2200–1750 BCE) and Andronovo (c."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "humores frios", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "humores_frios", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humores frios", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 10, "variants": ["humores frios", "humores frios o mixtos"], "contexts": ["cold humors", "cold humors causing illness"]}, {"entity_id": "humor_frío", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "humor frío", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humor frío"], "contexts": ["type of humor causing pain"]}, {"entity_id": "humor_frio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humor frio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["humor frio"], "contexts": ["cold humor affecting nerves", "cold and moist humor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humores frios", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "humor frío", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humores frios", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humor frio", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "humor frío", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humor frio", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 785, "stable_key": "clu_1610816b0394cbe9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cold humors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the ancient medical concept of humors, specifically those with a cold quality, as per humoral theory.", "wikidata_id": "Q5782572", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek and Roman system of medicine involving four fluid types", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humorism", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 562, "target_cluster_name": "Humors", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct component of humoral theory, referring to one of the types of humors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a direct component of humoral theory, referring to one of the types of humors.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melancolicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1826, "target_cluster_name": "Melancolicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melancholy was often associated with cold and dry humors, particularly black bile, and its symptoms were understood through this lens.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Melancholy was often associated with cold and dry humors, particularly black bile, and its symptoms were understood through this lens.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cozimentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "cozimientos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cozimientos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["cozimientos"], "contexts": ["decoctions for enemas"]}, {"entity_id": "cozimento", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cozimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["cozimento"], "contexts": ["Boiled mixture", "decoction of herbs"]}, {"entity_id": "cozimentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cozimentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["cozimentos", "Cozimentos", "cozimento peytoral"], "contexts": ["decoctions", "digestive processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cozimientos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cozimento", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cozimientos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cozimentos", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cozimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cozimentos", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 786, "stable_key": "clu_c2e113f693883241", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Decoctions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Preparations made by boiling plant material in water.", "wikipedia_extract": "Decoction is a method of extraction by boiling herbal or plant material (which may include stems, roots, bark and rhizomes) to dissolve the chemicals of the material. It is the most common preparation method in various herbal medicine systems. Decoction involves first drying the plant material; then mashing, slicing, or cutting the material to allow for maximum dissolution; and finally boiling in water to extract oils, volatile organic compounds and other various chemical substances. Occasionally, aqueous ethanol or glycerol may be used instead of water. Decoction can be used to make tisanes, tinctures and similar solutions. Decoctions and infusions may produce liquids with differing chemical properties, as the temperature or preparation difference may result in more oil-soluble chemicals in decoctions versus infusions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Tincture", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 905, "target_cluster_name": "Tintura", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tinctures and decoctions are both common methods of preparing herbal medicines, differing primarily in the solvent used (alcohol vs. water) and the boiling process.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tinctures and decoctions are both common methods of preparing herbal medicines, differing primarily in the solvent used (alcohol vs. water) and the boiling process."}, {"found_name": "caldo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1505, "target_cluster_name": "caldo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Broth is a type of decoction, a liquid made by boiling or simmering ingredients.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Broth is a type of decoction, a liquid made by boiling or simmering ingredients.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "medicamente", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2482, "target_cluster_name": "medicamente", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Decoctions are a method of preparing medicaments by boiling.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Decoctions are a method of preparing medicaments by boiling.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Aries", "category": "C