the Sun is in Aries at the time of the vernal equinox.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The vernal equinox is traditionally located in the constellation Aries, and the Sun is in Aries at the time of the vernal equinox.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Março", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2332, "target_cluster_name": "Março", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The astrological sign Aries begins in late March.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The astrological sign Aries begins in late March.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lyra", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2452, "target_cluster_name": "Lyra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aries is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aries is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Leo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2837, "target_cluster_name": "Leo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Leo and Aries are both zodiacal constellations, part of the same celestial band and system of astrological and astronomical classification. They are often discussed in relation to each other within these contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Leo and Aries are both zodiacal constellations, part of the same celestial band and system of astrological and astronomical classification. They are often discussed in relation to each other within these contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "avenca", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "avens", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Avens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Avens"], "contexts": ["plant with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "avenca", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "avenca", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["avenca"], "contexts": ["herb used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "avenca", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "avenca", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 13, "variants": ["avenca", "Avenca"], "contexts": ["herb used in decoction", "ingredient in decoction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Avens", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "avenca", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Avens", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "avenca", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "avenca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "avenca", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 788, "stable_key": "clu_3bc6aca06bc0e821", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "maidenhair fern", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Adiantum spp.", "family": "Pteridaceae", "description": "A delicate fern genus, historically used in herbal remedies.", "note": "The English 'Avens' is a different plant (Geum spp.), but the contexts here strongly point to Avenca (maidenhair fern).", "wikipedia_extract": "Adiantum (), the maidenhair fern (not to be confused with the similar-looking maidenhair spleenwort fern), is a genus of about 250 species of ferns in the subfamily Vittarioideae of the family Pteridaceae, though some researchers place it in its own family, Adiantaceae. The genus name comes from Greek, meaning \"unwetted\", referring to the fronds' ability to shed water without becoming wet. They are distinctive in appearance, with dark, often black stipes and rachises, and bright green, often delicately cut leaf tissue. The sori are borne submarginally, and are covered by reflexed flaps of leaf tissue which resemble indusia. Dimorphism between sterile and fertile fronds is generally subtle. They generally prefer humus-rich, moist, well-drained sites, ranging from bottomland soils to vertical rock walls."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Polipodio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 666, "target_cluster_name": "Polipodio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Maidenhair Fern is a type of fern, and Polypody is a genus of ferns.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Maidenhair Fern is a type of fern, and Polypody is a genus of ferns.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fern", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 994, "target_cluster_name": "Fern", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Maidenhair Fern is a specific type of fern.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Maidenhair Fern is a specific type of fern.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "galapagos_archipelago", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Galapagos archipelago", "Galapagos Archipelago"], "contexts": ["islands with distinct bird populations", "archipelago with endemic birds"]}, {"entity_id": "galapagos", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Galapagos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Galapagos"], "contexts": ["islands related to America", "birds of"]}, {"entity_id": "galapagos_islands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Galapagos Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Galapagos Islands"], "contexts": ["islands with peculiar land-birds and marine birds", "islands with reptiles and unique plants"]}, {"entity_id": "îles_galapagos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles Galapagos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["îles Galapagos"], "contexts": ["Galapagos Islands"]}, {"entity_id": "galapagos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Galapagos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galapagos"], "contexts": ["volcanic island with augmented gravitation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "îles Galapagos", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galapagos", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "îles Galapagos", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galapagos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos Islands", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "îles Galapagos", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Galapagos Islands", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galapagos", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "îles Galapagos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Galapagos", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 789, "stable_key": "clu_16990224cacc931a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A volcanic archipelago in the Pacific Ocean.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Galápagos Islands (Spanish: Islas Galápagos) are an archipelago of volcanic islands in the Eastern Pacific, located around the equator, 485 nautical miles (898 km; 558 mi) west of the mainland of South America. They form the Galápagos Province of the Republic of Ecuador, with a population of slightly over 33,000 (2020). The province is divided into the cantons of San Cristóbal, Santa Cruz, and Isabela, the three most populated islands in the chain. The Galápagos are famous for their large number of endemic species, which were studied by Charles Darwin in the 1830s and inspired his theory of evolution by means of natural selection. All of these islands are protected as part of Ecuador's Galápagos National Park and Marine Reserve. There is no firm evidence that Polynesians or the Indigenous peoples of South America reached the islands before their accidental discovery by Bishop Tomás de Berlanga in 1535."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chili", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 603, "target_cluster_name": "Chili", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Galapagos Archipelago is geographically associated with the broader South American context and Pacific Ocean region relevant to Chile.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Galapagos Archipelago is geographically associated with the broader South American context and Pacific Ocean region relevant to Chile.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "so