oxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plutarchus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Plutarch", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 801, "stable_key": "clu_0d5875301dc766f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plutarch", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 46, "death_year": 120, "description": "Greek biographer, historian, and essayist, author of Parallel Lives.", "wikidata_id": "Q41523", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher and historian (c. AD 46 – after AD 119)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plutarch", "wikipedia_extract": "Plutarch (; Ancient Greek: Πλούταρχος, Ploútarchos, Koine Greek: [ˈplúːtarkʰos]; before AD 50 – after 120) was a Greek and later Roman Middle Platonist philosopher, historian, biographer, essayist, and priest at the Temple of Apollo in Delphi. He is known primarily for his Parallel Lives, a series of biographies of illustrious Greeks and Romans, and Moralia, a collection of essays and speeches. Upon becoming a Roman citizen, he was possibly named Lucius Mestrius Plutarchus (Λούκιος Μέστριος Πλούταρχος). Plutarch was born to a prominent family in the small town of Chaeronea, about 30 kilometres (19 mi) east of Delphi, in the Greek region of Boeotia. His family was long established in the town; his father was named Autobulus and his grandfather was named Lamprias. His brothers, Timon and Lamprias, are frequently mentioned in his essays and dialogues, which speak of Timon in particular in the most affectionate terms."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ancient Greeks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1663, "target_cluster_name": "Greci", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch was a prominent figure of Ancient Greek literature and philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch was a prominent figure of Ancient Greek literature and philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Ancient Authors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3885, "target_cluster_name": "Ancient Writers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch was a prolific ancient author.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch was a prolific ancient author."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch was a philosopher and historian, and Aristotle was a foundational figure in Greek philosophy and science.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch was a philosopher and historian, and Aristotle was a foundational figure in Greek philosophy and science."}, {"found_name": "Herodotus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 337, "target_cluster_name": "Heródoto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Plutarch and Herodotus were significant Greek historians.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Plutarch and Herodotus were significant Greek historians."}, {"found_name": "Homer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2160, "target_cluster_name": "Homer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch, as a Greek writer, would have been deeply familiar with and influenced by Homer, the foundational poet of Greek literature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch, as a Greek writer, would have been deeply familiar with and influenced by Homer, the foundational poet of Greek literature."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch, as a philosopher and historian, would have been aware of Hippocrates, the father of Western medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch, as a philosopher and historian, would have been aware of Hippocrates, the father of Western medicine."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plutarch, as a philosopher and historian, would have been aware of Galen, a prominent physician and medical writer of antiquity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plutarch, as a philosopher and historian, would have been aware of Galen, a prominent physician and medical writer of antiquity."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Deer"], "contexts": ["quadrupedes with different leg joints", "animal discussed in chapter"]}, {"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Deer"], "contexts": ["animal fed by Beech in forests", "animals fed in parks, forrests, and chases"]}, {"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["deer"], "contexts": ["herbivorous mammal", "ungulate prey"]}, {"entity_id": "rein-deer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "rein-deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["rein-deer"], "contexts": ["domesticated cervid species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Deer", "similarity": 0.812}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "deer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "rein-deer", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "deer", "similarity": 0.799}], "id": 802, "stable_key": "clu_0a2b19428d91b1fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Deer", "confidence": "high", "type": "ANIMAL", "family": "Cervidae", "description": "Hoofed mammals hunted for meat and other products.", "wikipedia_extract": "A deer (pl.: deer) or true deer is a hoofed ruminant ungulate of the family Cervidae (informally the deer family). Cervidae is divided into subfamilies Cervinae (which includes, among others, muntjac, elk (wapiti), red deer, and fallow deer) and Capreolinae (which includes, among others reindeer (caribou), white-tailed deer, roe deer, and moose). Male deer of almost all species (except the water deer), as well as female reindeer, grow and shed new antlers each year. These antlers are bony extensions of the skull and are often used for combat between males. The musk deer (Moschidae) of Asia and chevrotains (Tragulidae) of tropical African and Asian forests are separate families that are also in the ruminant clade Ruminantia; they are not especially closely related to Cervidae. Deer appear in art from Paleolithic cave paintings onwards, and they have played a role in mythology, religion, and literature throughout history, as well as in heraldry, such as red deer that appear in the coat of arms of Åland."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Red Deer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3976, "target_cluster_name": "stag", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Red Deer is a specific species within the Cervidae family, which is the family that defines 'deer'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Red Deer is a specific species within the Cervidae family, which is the family that defines 'deer'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Horses"], "contexts": ["equine animals", "quadrupedes with different leg joints"]}, {"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["Horses", "horses"], "contexts": ["domesticated animal", "animal subject to size restrictions"]}, {"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["horses"], "contexts": ["living objects in tapestries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Horses", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 803, "stable_key": "clu_8f504d1d22bde89b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "horse", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Equus caballus", "family": "Equidae", "description": "A domesticated mammal with hooves, widely used by humans.", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "South pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "north_pole", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "North Pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Pole"], "contexts": ["northernmost point of Earth's axis"]}, {"entity_id": "south_pole", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "South Pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["south pole", "South Pole"], "contexts": ["region of geographical discoveries", "location of magnetic observations"]}, {"entity_id": "south_pole", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "South pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 11, "variants": ["South pole", "south pole", "South Pole"], "contexts": ["southernmost point of Earth", "southernmost point of Earth's axis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "North Pole", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "South Pole", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "North Pole", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "South pole", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "South Pole", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "South pole", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 804, "stable_key": "clu_d5a47245113a97b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's poles", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Antarctica/Arctic", "note": "The cluster includes both 'North Pole' and 'South Pole', referring to the geographic poles of the Earth."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Southern Ocean", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1213, "target_cluster_name": "Southern Ocean", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The northernmost point on Earth, located within the Arctic Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The northernmost point on Earth, located within the Arctic Ocean.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic and Antarctic are the two polar regions of Earth, often discussed in contrast or parallel.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Arctic and Antarctic are the two polar regions of Earth, often discussed in contrast or parallel."}, {"found_name": "Equator", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 61, "target_cluster_name": "equator", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Equator is a fundamental geographical line of reference, often contrasted with the poles in discussions of global geography and climate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Equator is a fundamental geographical line of reference, often contrasted with the poles in discussions of global geography and climate."}, {"found_name": "southern hemisphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1025, "target_cluster_name": "southern hemisphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Southern Hemisphere encompasses the South Pole, a key geographical point.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Southern Hemisphere encompasses the South Pole, a key geographical point.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hair", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hair"], "contexts": ["extimulating part used to kill dragon"]}, {"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["Hair", "hair"], "contexts": ["body part, plant helps shedding", "body part that can be made black"]}, {"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["hair"], "contexts": ["filament from animal skin", "thin filament used for comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Hair", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "hair", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "hair", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 805, "stable_key": "clu_333ed6d580e1f655", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hair", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Identified as animal hair based on 'filament from animal skin' and 'body part, plant helps shedding'.", "wikidata_id": "Q866324", "wikidata_description": "organ found in the dermal layer of the mammalian skin that regulates hair growth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hair%20follicle", "wikipedia_extract": "Hair is a protein filament that grows from follicles found in the dermis. Hair is one of the defining characteristics of mammals. The human body, apart from areas of glabrous skin, is covered in follicles which produce thick terminal and fine vellus hair. Most common interest in hair is focused on hair growth, hair types, and hair care, but hair is also an important biomaterial primarily composed of protein, notably alpha-keratin. Attitudes towards different forms of hair, such as hairstyles and hair removal, vary widely across different cultures and historical periods, but it is often used to indicate a person's personal beliefs or social position, such as their age, gender, or religion. The word \"hair\" usually refers to two distinct structures: the part beneath the skin, called the hair follicle, or, when pulled from the skin, the bulb or root."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Feathers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1149, "target_cluster_name": "feathers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both hair and feathers are proteinaceous epidermal outgrowths found in mammals and birds, respectively, serving protective and insulating functions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both hair and feathers are proteinaceous epidermal outgrowths found in mammals and birds, respectively, serving protective and insulating functions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Branches", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"enti