odies", "astronomical events"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "eclipses of the Sun", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "eclipses", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "eclipses of the Sun", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "eclipses", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "eclipses", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "eclipses", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 817, "stable_key": "clu_a5a52b39dfd94a6e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "eclipses", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q937756", "wikidata_description": "organization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eclipse%20Foundation", "wikipedia_extract": "The Eclipse Foundation AISBL is an independent, not-for-profit organization chartered in the European Union that acts as a steward of the Eclipse open source software development community. It has over 350 members, and represents the world's largest sponsored collection of Open Source projects and developers. The Foundation focuses on key services such as intellectual property (IP) management, ecosystem development, and IT infrastructure. The Eclipse Project was originally created by IBM in November 2001 and was supported by a consortium of software vendors. In 2004, the Eclipse Foundation was founded to lead and develop the Eclipse community. It was created to allow a vendor-neutral, open, and transparent community to be established around Eclipse."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and eclipses occur when celestial bodies pass through or near this path.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and eclipses occur when celestial bodies pass through or near this path."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Weber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "webers", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Webers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Webers"], "contexts": ["researchers on sensory perception", "researchers on leg movement speed"]}, {"entity_id": "weber", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Weber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Weber"], "contexts": ["collaborator on magnetic results", "anatomical investigations on pelvis form"]}, {"entity_id": "weber", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Weber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Weber"], "contexts": ["German experimental psychologist", "scientist who formulated Weber's law"]}, {"entity_id": "wilhelm_weber", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Wilhelm Weber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wilhelm Weber"], "contexts": ["physicist interested in magnetic instruments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Webers", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Weber", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Weber", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Wilhelm Weber", "similarity": 0.861}], "id": 818, "stable_key": "clu_d7ff2e8a35c7ac3b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wilhelm Weber", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1804, "death_year": 1891, "description": "German physicist, known for work on electromagnetism and wave phenomena.", "note":