lower and upper chalk, as the last of the flotz strata, which begin with mountain limestone; terti", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of lower and upper stratum", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "hygromètre à baleine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "hygromètre_à_baleine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hygromètre à baleine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 9, "variants": ["hygromètre à baleine", "HYGROMÈTRE", "hygromètre", "hygromètre de Deluc", "hygromètre à cheveu"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring humidity", "whalebone hygrometer for humidity measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "hygrometer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hygrometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["hygrometer"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring humidity"]}, {"entity_id": "hygrometer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hygrometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["hygrometer", "Hygrometer"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring humidity", "instrument for measuring dew-point"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hygromètre à baleine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hygrometer", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hygromètre à baleine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hygrometer", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "hygrometer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hygrometer", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 830, "stable_key": "clu_0438fcb18de9a09d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hygrometer", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'hygromètre à baleine' is a specific type of hygrometer that uses whalebone. 'Hygrometer' is the general modern term.", "wikidata_id": "Q183173", "wikidata_description": "instrument used for measuring the moisture content in the atmosphere", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygrometer", "wikipedia_extract": "A hygrometer is an instrument that measures humidity: that is, how much water vapor is present. Humidity measurement instruments usually rely on measurements of some other quantities, such as temperature, pressure, mass, and mechanical or electrical changes in a substance as moisture is absorbed. By calibration and calculation, these measured quantities can be used to indicate the humidity. Modern electronic devices use the temperature of condensation (called the dew point), or they sense changes in electrical capacitance or resistance. The maximum amount of water vapor that can be present in a given volume (at saturation) varies greatly with temperature; at low temperatures a lower mass of water per unit volume can remain as vapor than at high temperatures. Thus a change in the temperature changes the relative humidity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Thermometer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 320, "target_cluster_name": "Thermometer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are meteorological instruments used to measure atmospheric conditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are meteorological instruments used to measure atmospheric conditions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "compass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 428, "target_cluster_name": "compass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are scientific instruments often found on ships for navigation and weather observation.", 