he Jurassic Age).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'age' is frequently used to denote specific divisions or spans within geological time (e.g., the Jurassic Age)."}, {"found_name": "tertiary formations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1928, "target_cluster_name": "tertiary formations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Tertiary Period is a specific geological period, and 'Geological Period' is its broader category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Tertiary Period is a specific geological period, and 'Geological Period' is its broader category.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "ammonites", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Ammonites", "ammonites"], "contexts": ["group that went extinct suddenly", "extinct marine invertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["ammonites", "Ammonites nodosus"], "contexts": ["fossil found in jurassic limestone", "quelques ammonites"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ammonites"], "contexts": ["fossil cephalopods", "extinct marine mollusks"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ammonites", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ammonites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammonites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 847, "stable_key": "clu_8d32ba7f7b8c3878", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ammonites", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Ammonitida", "description": "Extinct marine molluscs with coiled shells, found as fossils.", "note": "Ammonites are extinct cephalopods. While historically sometimes classified differently, they are now recognized as an extinct group of molluscs.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ammonoids are extinct, typically coiled-shelled cephalopods composing the subclass Ammonoidea. They are more closely related to living octopuses, squid, and cuttlefish (which compose the clade Coleoidea) than they are to nautiluses (family Nautilidae), which they resemble. The earliest ammonoids appeared during the Emsian stage of the Early Devonian, around 410-408 million years ago, with the last species vanishing during or soon after the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event approximately 66 million years ago. They are often called ammonites, which is most frequently used for members of the order Ammonitida, the only remaining group of ammonoids from the Jurassic up until their extinction. Ammonoids exhibited considerable diversity over their evolutionary history, with over 10,000 species having been described. Ammonoids are excellent index fossils, and they have been frequently used to link rock layers in which a particular species or genus is found to specific geologic time periods."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cephalopodes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1187, "target_cluster_name": "Cephalopodes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are a subclass within the Cephalopoda phylum.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonites are a subclass within the Cephalopoda phylum."}, {"found_name": "Cuttlefish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2994, "target_cluster_name": "cuttle-fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are more closely related to living cuttlefish than to nautiluses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonites are more closely related to living cuttlefish than to nautiluses."}, {"found_name": "Mollusks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2101, "target_cluster_name": "molluscs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are a type of extinct mollusk.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonites are a type of extinct mollusk."}, {"found_name": "Trilobites", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 959, "target_cluster_name": "Trilobites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are extinct marine mollusks that often co-occur in fossil records with trilobites, representing other common marine life forms from ancient seas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammonites are extinct marine mollusks that often co-occur in fossil records with trilobites, representing other common marine life forms from ancient seas.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "savage", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "savages", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "savages", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["savages"], "contexts": ["indigenous people", "barbarous people"]}, {"entity_id": "savage", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "savage", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 