arrangement of its molecules.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fluidity, the ability of a substance to flow, is a direct consequence of the motion and arrangement of its molecules."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "lime", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["lime"], "contexts": ["element in nepheline", "substance held in solution by water"]}, {"entity_id": "lime", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["lime", "Lime"], "contexts": ["calcium oxide or carbonate", "substance in water producing negative electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "lime", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["lime"], "contexts": ["mineral used in high temperature processes", "calcium oxide"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_of_lime", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "carbonate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carbonate of lime", "carbonate of lime"], "contexts": ["chemical compound studied", "mineral transformed into limestone and dolomite"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_of_lime", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "carbonate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["carbonate of lime", "Carbonate of lime"], "contexts": ["mineral with many crystallization varieties", "mineral compound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "lime", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lime", "similarity": 0.662}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "lime", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lime", "similarity": 0.838}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Lime", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lime", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 862, "stable_key": "clu_81fdc30667366b06", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Calcium carbonate", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Contexts suggest both calcium oxide (lime) and calcium carbonate (limestone).", "wikidata_id": "Q23767", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium%20carbonate", "wikipedia_extract": "Calcium carbonate is a chemical compound with the chemical formula CaCO3. It is a common substance found in rocks as the minerals calcite and aragonite, most notably in chalk and limestone, eggshells, gastropod shells, shellfish skeletons and pearls. Materials containing much calcium carbonate or resembling it are described as calcareous. Calcium carbonate is the active ingredient in agricultural lime. Limescale is calcium carbonate produced when calcium ions in hard water react with carbonate ions. Calcium carbonate has medical use as a calcium supplement or as an antacid, but excessive consumption can be hazardous and cause hypercalcemia and digestive issues."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fundament", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 753, "target_cluster_name": "Fundament", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calcium carbonate (Lime) is a common example of a base discussed in historical chemical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Calcium carbonate (Lime) is a common example of a base discussed in historical chemical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chalk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 829, "targ