mistry%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In chemistry, there are three definitions in common use of the word \"base\": Arrhenius bases, Brønsted bases, and Lewis bases. All definitions agree that bases are substances that react with acids, as originally proposed by G.-F. Rouelle in the mid-18th century. In 1884, Svante Arrhenius proposed that a base is a substance which dissociates in aqueous solution to form hydroxide ions OH−. These ions can react with hydrogen ions (H+ according to Arrhenius) from the dissociation of acids to form water in an acid–base reaction. A base was therefore a metal hydroxide such as NaOH or Ca(OH)2."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Potash", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2311, "target_cluster_name": "Potash", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Potassium carbonate is a type of alkali, and historical texts often use 'alkali' as a general term that would encompass potash.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Potassium carbonate is a type of alkali, and historical texts often use 'alkali' as a general term that would encompass potash.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sulphur", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 313, "target_cluster_name": "Sulphur", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sulfuric acid reacts with alkalis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sulfuric acid reacts with alkalis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Vitriolo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 564, "target_cluster_name": "Vitriolo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acids and alkalis are fundamental chemical opposites, often discussed in relation to each other in premodern chemistry and alchemy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": 