": "oxidation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, oxidation was often understood and studied in the context of combustion, as the process of burning involves rapid oxidation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historically, oxidation was often understood and studied in the context of combustion, as the process of burning involves rapid oxidation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "destruction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2527, "target_cluster_name": "destruction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Combustion is a process that often results in destruction by fire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Combustion is a process that often results in destruction by fire.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "igneous", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2649, "target_cluster_name": "igneous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Combustion is a process involving heat and chemical change, often associated with the high temperatures involved in igneous rock formation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Combustion is a process involving heat and chemical change, often associated with the high temperatures involved in igneous rock formation.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Meca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "mecca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mecca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mecca"], "contexts": ["site of pilgrim assemblage"]}, {"entity_id": "meca", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Meca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Meca", "Mecca"], "contexts": ["origin of goods to Diu", "strait from where a substance is brought"]}, {"entity_id": "mekka", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mekka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mekka"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "mecca", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Mecca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mecca"], "contexts": ["origin of a type of Sena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mecca", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Meca", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mecca", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mekka", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mecca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mecca", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Meca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mecca", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mekka", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mecca", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 867, "stable_key": "clu_1b6824642e457912", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mecca", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Holy city in Saudi Arabia, ancient center of trade.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mecca, officially Makkah al-Mukarramah, is the holiest city in Islam. It is located in the Hejazi region of western Saudi Arabia and is the capital of Mecca Province. Mecca is considered the birthplace of Islam and is the birthplace of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. It is 70 km (43 mi) inland from Jeddah on the Red Sea, in a narrow valley 277 m (909 ft) above sea level. Its metropolitan population in 2022 was 2.4 million, making it the third–most populated city in Saudi Arabia after Riyadh and Jeddah. The Cave of Hira atop the Jabal al-Nour, just outside the city, is where Muslims believe the Quran was first revealed to Muhammad."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "herbe", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1696, "target_cluster_name": "herbe", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "fe repete muytas vezes cadadia,não deyxandoféccar ospannos. Porèm ſe eſta aguanão baſtar, podem recorrerparafomentações de agua cozida comhuma máo-chea de herva anagalis de flor vermelha , chamada vul", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym of 'afombra'", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arabia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 169, "target_cluster_name": "Arabia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mecca is located in the Hejazi region of western Saudi Arabia, which is part of the Arabian Peninsula.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mecca is located in the Hejazi region of western Saudi Arabia, which is part of the Arabian Peninsula."}]}, {"canonical_name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "melancolia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 4, "variants": ["melancolia", "melancholias", "melancholicos", "melancholías"], "contexts": ["melancholy treated by the water", "people suffering from melancholy"]}, {"entity_id": "melancolia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 7, "variants": ["melancolia"], "contexts": ["treated by Damasquo", "humor associated with illness"]}, {"entity_id": "affectibus_melancholicis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "affectibus melancholicis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["affectibus melancholicis"], "contexts": ["melancholic affections"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "melancolia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "melancolia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "melancolia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "affectibus melancholicis", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "melancolia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "affectibus melancholicis", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 868, "stable_key": "clu_62d1a3c18415a8e5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Melancholy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A state of prolonged sadness, low spirits, or humors.", "note": "Historically linked to the 'melancholic humor'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Melancholia or melancholy (Ancient Greek: μελαγχολία, romanized: melancholía; from μέλαινα χολή, mélaina cholḗ, 'black bile') is a concept found throughout ancient, medieval, and premodern medicine in Europe that describes a condition characterized by markedly depressed mood, bodily complaints, and sometimes hallucinations and delusions. Besides a pathological condition, melancholy could also refer to a mood or temperament and at times it was even used as a description of the human condition in general. Melancholy (or more precisely the 'black bile', from which melancholy derives its name) was regarded as one of the four temperaments matching the four humours. Until the 18th century, doctors and other scholars classified melancholic conditions as such by their perceived common cause – an excess of a notional fluid known as \"black bile\", which was commonly linked to the spleen. Hippocrates and other ancient physicians described melancholia as a distinct disease with mental and physical symptoms, including persistent fears and despondencies, poor appetite, abulia, sleeplessness, irritability, and agitation. Later, fixed delusions were added by Galen and other physicians to the list of symptoms."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mania", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1612, "target_cluster_name": "Mania", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, mania and melancholy were often considered two poles of the same bipolar disorder or related states of mental imbalance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historically, mania and melancholy were often considered two poles of the same bipolar disorder or related states of mental imbalance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Doudos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2820, "target_cluster_name": "Doudos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Madness' (Doudos) is related to 'Melancholy' as historical classifications of mental distress often distinguished between states of agitation (mania/madness) and profound sadness or depression (melancholy), though the boundaries were fluid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: 'Madness' (Doudos) is related to 'Melancholy' as historical classifications of mental distress often distinguished between states of agitation (mania/madness) and profound sadness or depression (melancholy), though the boundaries were fluid.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Corte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "corte", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "corte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["corte"], "contexts": ["court, source of orders"]}, {"entity_id": "corte", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Corte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Corte"], "contexts": ["court or legal jurisdiction"]}, {"entity_id": "corte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Corte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Corte", "Corte de Portugal", "Cortede França"], "contexts": ["location of a case", "location of a woman"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "corte", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Corte", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "corte", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corte", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Corte", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corte", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 869, "stable_key": "clu_053a6929e6ca977a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "court", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Royal court, legal jurisdiction, or enclosed space.", "note": "Contexts suggest both the physical space of a court and its associated authority.", "wikipedia_extract": "A court is an institution, often a government entity, with the authority to adjudicate legal disputes between parties  and administer justice in civil, criminal, and administrative matters in accordance with the rule of law. Courts generally consist of judges or other judicial officers, and are usually established and dissolved through legislation enacted by a legislature. Courts may also be established by constitution or an equivalent constituting instrument. The practical authority given to the court is known as its jurisdiction, which describes the court's power to decide certain kinds of questions, or petitions put to it. There are various kinds of courts, including trial courts, appellate courts, administrative courts, international courts, and tribunals. A court is any person or institution, often as a government institution, with the authority to adjudicate legal disputes between parties and carry out the administration of justice in civil, criminal, and administrative matters in accordance with the rule of law."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Berberi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "berberis", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Berberis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Berberis", "berberis"], "contexts": ["plant used for juice", "genus of plants yielding rusoif"]}, {"entity_id": "berberi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Berberi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Berberi", "BERBERI", "Bcrberi", "berberi"], "contexts": ["plant name, context incomplete", "common barberry"]}, {"entity_id": "berberis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "berberis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["berberis"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a remedy for diabetes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Berberis", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Berberi", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Berberis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "berberis", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Berberi", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "berberis", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 870, "stable_key": "clu_0446aa6b8a21e523", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "barberry", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Berberis vulgaris", "family": "Berberidaceae", "description": "Shrub with tart red berries, used in traditional medicine and as an ingredient.", "wikipedia_extract": "Berberis vulgaris, also known as common barberry, European barberry or simply barberry, is a shrub in the genus Berberis native to the Old World. It produces edible but sharply acidic berries, which people in many countries eat as a tart and refreshing fruit. It is a deciduous shrub growing up to 4 metres (13 feet) high. The leaves are small, oval, 2–5 centimetres (3⁄4–2 inches) long and 1–2 cm (1⁄2–3⁄4 in) broad, with a serrated margin; they are borne in clusters of 2–5 together, subtended by a three-branched spine 3–8 millimetres (1⁄8–5⁄16 in) long. The flowers are yellow, 4–6 mm (1⁄8–1⁄4 in) across, produced on 3–6 cm (1+1⁄8–2+3⁄8 in) long panicles in late spring. The fruit is an oblong red berry 7–10 mm (1⁄4–3⁄8 in) long and 3–5 mm (1⁄8–3⁄16 in) broad, ripening in late summer or autumn."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bolo armenio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 335, "target_cluster_name": "bolo armenio", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Radia di paftinaca faluarica, bianch;^» roflp* &roffà, volgarmente detti pififi<. • ciani , Berberi. Crcfpino , cioè berberi volgare. U^alo armeno. Bolo armeno de irElbabiancOjgùlIo^ ó roffo, ò il roì", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for 'crefpino'", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bolo armeno", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 335, "target_cluster_name": "bolo armenio", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "mofcade , ò Gherofani. corpobaifiino Bfen bianco, & Bccn Radia di paftinaca faluarica, bianch;^» roflp* &roffà, volgarmente detti pififi<. • ciani , Berberi. Crcfpino , cioè berberi volgare. U^alo arm", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for 'Bolo armeno de irElbabiancOjgùlIo^ ó roffo'"}, {"found_name": "Berries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 718, "target_cluster_name": "Berries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Barberries are a type o