s_1890", "source_name": "Descartes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Descartes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 875, "stable_key": "clu_03c5a85678021c21", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "René Descartes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1596, "death_year": 1650, "description": "French philosopher, mathematician, and scientist, influential in mind-body dualism.", "wikidata_id": "Q9191", "wikidata_description": "French philosopher, mathematician, and scientist (1596–1650)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/René_Descartes", "wikipedia_extract": "René Descartes ( day-KART, also  DAY-kart; French: [ʁəne dekaʁt] ; 31 March 1596 – 11 February 1650) was a French philosopher, scientist, and mathematician, widely considered a seminal figure in the emergence of modern philosophy and science. Mathematics was paramount to his method of inquiry, and he connected the previously separate fields of geometry and algebra into analytic geometry. Refusing to accept the authority of previous philosophers, Descartes frequently set his views apart from the philosophers who preceded him. In the opening section of the Passions of the Soul, an early modern treatise on emotions, Descartes goes so far as to assert that he will write on this topic \"as if no one had written on these matters before.\" His best known philosophical statement is \"cogito, ergo sum\" (\"I think, therefore I am,\" French: \"Je pense, donc je suis\"). Descartes has often been called the father of modern philosophy, and he is largely seen as responsible for the increased attention given to epistemology in the 17th century. He was one of the key figures in the Scientific Revolution, and his Meditations on First Philosophy and other philosophical works continue to be studied."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Boodt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 391, "target_cluster_name": "Boodt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arnold Geulincx was a prominent follower of René Descartes and developed his philosophical ideas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Arnold Geulincx was a prominent follower of René Descartes and developed his philosophical ideas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was a highly influential ancient physician whose medical theories were dominant for centuries and were being challenged and re-evaluated during Descartes' era.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was a highly influential ancient physician whose medical theories were dominant for centuries and were being challenged and re-evaluated during Descartes' era."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle's philosophy and scientific views were the established framework that Descartes sought to overturn and replace with his own mechanistic and rationalist system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aristotle's philosophy and scientific views were the established framework that Descartes sought to overturn and replace with his own mechanistic and rationalist system."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates was a foundational figure in ancient medicine, whose humoral theory was a cornerstone of medical practice that Descartes' era was beginning to question.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates was a foundational figure in ancient medicine, whose humoral theory was a cornerstone of medical practice that Descartes' era was beginning to question."}, {"found_name": "mathematicians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1337, "target_cluster_name": "mathematicians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Descartes was a philosopher and mathematician who developed Cartesian geometry, bridging algebra and geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Descartes was a philosopher and mathematician who developed Cartesian geometry, bridging algebra and geometry.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Adder", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "adder", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Adder", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Adder"], "contexts": ["type of serpent"]}, {"entity_id": "adder", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Adder", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["Adder"], "contexts": ["venomous beast", "serpent with antipathy to ash tree"]}, {"entity_id": "adder", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "adder", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["adder"], "contexts": ["reptile with a venomous fang"]}, {"entity_id": "viper", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Viper", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Viper"], "contexts": ["reptile with a frequent oratorical tradition", "reptile found in amber"]}, {"entity_id": "viper", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "viper", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["viper"], "contexts": ["animal that bit Louis V."]}, {"entity_id": "vipera", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "vipera", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 6, "variants": ["vipera", "Vipera", "vipere"], "contexts": ["animal whose venom is discussed", "viper used in trochisci composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Adder", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Adder", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Adder", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "adder", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Adder", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "adder", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 876, "stable_key": "clu_7c5e4a17c4c40ddf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adder", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q192056", "wikidata_description": "species of snake belonging to the viper family (Viperidae)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adder", "wikipedia_extract": "Vipera berus, commonly known as the common European adder and the common European viper, is a species of venomous snake in the family Viperidae. The species is extremely widespread and can be found throughout much of Europe, and as far as East Asia. There are three recognised subspecies. Known by a host of common names including common adder and common viper, the adder has been the subject of much folklore in Britain and other European countries. It is not regarded as especially dangerous; the snake is not aggressive and usually bites only when really provoked, stepped on, or picked up. Bites can be very painful, but are rarely fatal.", "note": "The term 'vipera' is the Latin genus for vipers, while 'Viper' and 'viper' are its English equivalents."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Viper", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 821, "target_cluster_name": "Viper", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'adder' is often used as a common name for certain species of viper, particularly in British English.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'adder' is often used as a common name for certain species of viper, particularly in British English.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "French", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "french", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "French", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 6, "variants": ["French"], "contexts": ["nationality of Hollerius", "people who eat the herb"]}, {"entity_id": "franceſes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Franceſes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Franceſes", "Frances", "Franceses"], "contexts": ["called the tree Sassafras", "learned from the Indios"]}, {"entity_id": "francez", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Francez", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Francez"], "contexts": ["nationality of Samuel, a feather merchant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "French", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Franceſes", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "French", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Francez", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Franceſes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Francez", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 877, "stable_key": "clu_d7c508e0ceaa8340", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "French", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to nationality or origin, not a specific person.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_people", "wikipedia_extract": "French people (French: Les Français, lit. 'The French') are a nation primarily located in Western Europe that share a common French culture, history, and language, identified with the country of France. The French people, especially the native speakers of langues d'oïl from northern and central France, are primarily descended from Romans (or Gallo-Romans, western European Celtic and Italic peoples), Gauls (including the Belgae), as well as Germanic peoples such as the Franks, the Visigoths, the Suebi and the Burgundians who settled in Gaul from east of the Rhine after the fall of the Roman Empire, as well as various later waves of lower-level irregular migration that have continued to the present day. The Norsemen also settled in Normandy in the 10th century and contributed significantly to the ancestry of the Normans. Furthermore, regional ethnic minorities also exist within France that have distinct lineages, languages and cultures such as Bretons in Brittany, Occitans in Occitania, Basques in the French Basque Country, Catalans in northern Catalonia, Germans in Alsace, Corsicans in Corsica and Flemings in French Flanders. France has long been a patchwork of local customs and regional differences, and while most French people still speak the French language as their mother tongue, languages like Picard, Poitevin-Saintongeais, Franco-Provencal, Occitan, Catalan, Auvergnat, Corsican, Basque, French Flemish, Lorraine Franconian, Alsatian, Norman, and Breton remain spoken in their respective regions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Charles VIII of France", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2633, "target_cluster_name": "Carlos VIII", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Charles VIII was a King of France, and thus a prominent figure associated with the French nation and its history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Charles VIII was a King of France, and thus a prominent figure associated with the French nation and its history."}, {"found_name": "Louis XIV", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1091, "target_cluster_name": "Louis XIV", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Louis XIV was a King of France, and thus a prominent figure associated with the French nation and its history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Louis XIV was a King of France, and thus a prominent figure associated with the French nation and its history."}, {"found_name": "English people", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1727, "target_cluster_name": "Anglois", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'French' refers to a nation and culture. 'English people' refers to another distinct nation and culture, often in historical comparison or contrast with the French.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'French' refers to a nation and culture. 'English people' refers to another distinct nation and culture, often in historical comparison or contrast with the French."}, {"found_name": "Italian people", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 462, "target_cluster_name": "Italians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'French' refers to a nation and culture. 'Italian people' refers to another distinct nation and culture, often in historical comparison or contrast with the French.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'French' refers to a nation and culture. 'Italian people' refers to another distinct nation and culture, often in historical comparison or contrast with the French."}, {"found_name": "Germanicus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap"