"high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Euphorbia", "family": "Euphorbiaceae", "description": "A plant and its medicinal resin, used as a purge.", "note": "Refers to both the plant genus and the medicinal resin derived from it.", "wikipedia_extract": "Euphorbia is a large and diverse genus of flowering plants, commonly called spurge, in the family Euphorbiaceae. Euphorbias range from tiny annual plants to large and long-lived trees, with perhaps the tallest being Euphorbia ampliphylla at 30 m (98 ft) or more. The genus has roughly 2,000 members, making it one of the largest genera of flowering plants. It also has one of the largest ranges of chromosome counts, along with Rumex and Senecio. Euphorbia antiquorum is the type species for the genus Euphorbia. It was first described by Carl Linnaeus in 1753 in Species Plantarum."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "leite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "goats_milk", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Goats Milk", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Goats Milk"], "contexts": ["animal product used in medicine", "remedy for Henbane poisoning"]}, {"entity_id": "leite", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "leite", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["leite", "leite de cabras"], "contexts": ["Milk", "used to prepare anacardo for asthma"]}, {"entity_id": "milk", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "milk", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["milk"], "contexts": ["animal product"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Goats Milk", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "leite", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Goats Milk", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "milk", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "leite", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "milk", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 879, "stable_key": "clu_834b7f0ab9f15b76", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Milk", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Curdled Milk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3956, "target_cluster_name": "Curdled Milk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curdled Milk is a direct transformation of Milk, often discussed in relation to its properties and uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curdled Milk is a direct transformation of Milk, often discussed in relation to its properties and uses."}]}, {"canonical_name": "antidotos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "antidotes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Antidotes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Antidotes"], "contexts": ["medicines for venemous bites containing Spignel", "substances to counteract poison"]}, {"entity_id": "antidoti", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "antidoti", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["antidoti"], "contexts": ["compound medicines, uses black chalciti"]}, {"entity_id": "antidotos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "antidotos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 8, "variants": ["antidotos", "Antidotos", "antidota"], "contexts": ["substances to counteract poisons", "antidotes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Antidotes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "antidoti", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Antidotes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antidotos", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "antidoti", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antidotos", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 880, "stable_key": "clu_93792dae3f7a0507", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antidote", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A substance that counteracts a poison or disease.", "note": "Includes general antidotes and specific remedies, like those for syphilis.", "wikipedia_extract": "An antidote is a substance that can counteract a form of poisoning. The term ultimately derives from the Greek term φάρμακον ἀντίδοτον (pharmakon antidoton), \"(medicine) given as a remedy\". An older term in English which is now rare is atterlothe, derived from \"atter\" (\"poison, venom, morbid fluid\"). Antidotes for anticoagulants are sometimes referred to as reversal agents. The antidotes for some particular toxins are manufactured by injecting the toxin into an animal in small doses and extracting the resulting antibodies from the host animals' blood. This results in an antivenom that can be used to counteract venom produced by certain species of snakes, spiders, and other venomous animals."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "triaga", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1961, "target_cluster_name": "triaga", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "esta pedra pa^ar, do pa^am (bode así chamado), e asi quando ▼os cá pedem alguma mezinha contra a peçonha, lhe cha- mam pa:(am^ e asi chamam o locomio* e a triaga alguns. Este nome de pa:{ar lhe chamam", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for triaga", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "remedios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1584, "target_cluster_name": "remedios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antidotes are a specific type of medicine used to counteract poisons.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antidotes are a specific type of medicine used to counteract poisons.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "venenos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2363, "target_cluster_name": "venenos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antidotes are specifically designed to counteract the effects of poisons.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antidotes are specifically designed to counteract the effects of poisons.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "medicamente", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2482, "target_cluster_name": "medicamente", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antidotes are a specific type of medicament designed to neutralize poisons.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antidotes are a specific type of medicament designed to neutralize poisons.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ostrich", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "ostrich", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Ostrich", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 7, "variants": ["ostrich", "Ostrich"], "contexts": ["large flightless bird", "bird that lays a score of eggs"]}, {"entity_id": "american_ostrich", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "American ostrich", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["American ostrich"], "contexts": ["ostrich species with imperfect nesting instinct"]}, {"entity_id": "ostrich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ostrich", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["ostrich"], "contexts": ["bird resembling dinornis", "animal previously thought to be dodo"]}, {"entity_id": "ostrich", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ostrich", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["ostrich"], "contexts": ["large flightless bird"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ostrich", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ostrich", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ostrich", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ostrich", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "American ostrich", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ostrich", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "American ostrich", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ostrich", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ostrich", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ostrich", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 881, "stable_key": "clu_548fb5db4faf005a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ostrich", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Includes references to 'American ostrich', which belongs to the genus Rhea, but the primary referent is clearly the Ostrich (Struthio).", "wikidata_id": "Q2354075", "wikidata_description": "heraldic animal", "wikipedia_extract": "The common ostrich (Struthio camelus), or simply ostrich, is a species of flightless bird native to certain areas of Africa and is the largest living species of bird and thus the largest living dinosaur. The common ostrich is one of two extant species of ostriches, the only living members of the genus Struthio in the ratite group of birds. The other is the Somali ostrich (Struthio molybdophanes), which has been recognized as a distinct species by BirdLife International since 2014, having been previously considered a distinctive subspecies of ostrich. The common ostrich belongs to the order Struthioniformes. Struthioniformes previously contained all the ratites, such as the kiwis, emus, rheas, and cassowaries. However, a recent genetic analysis has found that the group is not monophyletic, as it is paraphyletic with respect to the tinamous, so the ostriches are now classified as the only members of the order.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_ostrich"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Apteryx", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2731, "target_cluster_name": "Apteryx", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apteryx, commonly known as the kiwi, is another large, flightless bird, though distinct from the ostrich.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Apteryx, commonly known as the kiwi, is another large, flightless bird, though distinct from the ostrich."}, {"found_name": "Birds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 5, "target_cluster_name": "birds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ostrich is a species of bird.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The ostrich is a species of bird."}, {"found_name": "Phoenix", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3240, "target_cluster_name": "Phoenix", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The phoenix is a mythical bird often associated with ostriches due to their size and exotic nature in some historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The phoenix is a mythical bird often associated with ostriches due to their size and exotic nature in some historical contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Umbellifera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "umbelliferæ", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Umbelliferæ", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Umbelliferæ", "umbelliferæ"], "contexts": ["plant order with seed differences", "plant family"]}, {"entity_id": "umbelliferous", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Umbelliferous", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Umbelliferous"], "contexts": ["family of flowering plants (Apiaceae)"]}, {"entity_id": "umbelliferous_plants", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "umbelliferous plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["umbelliferous plants"], "contexts": ["plants with umbrella-shaped flower clusters"]}, {"entity_id": "umbellifera", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Umbellifera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Umbellifera", "Umbelliferae", "Umbelliferce", "Umbelliferee"], "contexts": ["plant family with aromatic seeds", "plant family, subject of correction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Umbelliferæ", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "umbelliferous plants", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Umbelliferæ", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Umbellifera", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Umbelliferous", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "umbelliferous plants", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Umbelliferous", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Umbellifera", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "umbelliferous plants", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Umbellifera", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 882, "stable_key": "clu_be6c584e36042dae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apiaceae", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Apiaceae", "family": "Apiaceae", "note": "Early modern texts often referred to families by descriptive names like 'Umbelliferae' or 'Umbellifera'.", "wikidata_id": "Q145794", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apiaceae", "wikipedia_extract": "Apiaceae ( ), also called Umbelliferae, is a family of mostly aromatic flowering plants named after the type genus Apium, and commonly known as the celery, carrot, or parsley family, or simply as umbellifers. It is the 16th-largest family of flowering plants, with more than 3,800 species in about 446 genera, including such well-known, and economically important plants as ajwain, angelica, anise, asafoetida, caraway, carrot, celery, chervil, coriander, cumin, dill, fennel, lovage, cow parsley, parsley, parsnip and sea holly, as well as silphium, a plant whose exact identity is unclear and which may be extinct. The family Apiaceae includes a significant number of phototoxic species, such as giant hogweed, and a smaller number of highly poisonous species, such as poison hemlock, water hemlock, spotted cowbane, fool's parsley, and various species of water dropwort. Most Apiaceae are annual, biennial or perennial herbs (frequently with the leaves aggregated toward the base), though a minority are woody shrubs or small trees such as Bupleurum fruticosum. Their leaves are of variable size, and alternately arranged, or with the upper leaves becoming nearly opposite. The leaves may be petiolate or sessile."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Carrot", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2940, "target_cluster_name": "Carrots", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carrot (Daucus carota) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the carrot family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carrot (Daucus carota) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the carrot family."}, {"found_name": "Parsley", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2339, "target_cluster_name": "Petrofellino", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Parsley (Petroselinum crispum) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the parsley family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Parsley (Petroselinum crispum) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the parsley family."}, {"found_name": "Fennel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2476, "target_cluster_name": "ferula", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is a genus within the Apiaceae family."}, {"found_name": "Coriander", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1767, "target_cluster_name": "coentro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coriander (Coriandrum sativum) is a species within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coriander (Coriandrum sativum) is a species within the Apiaceae family."}, {"found_name": "Ammi visnaga", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2467, "target_cluster_name": "ameos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammi visnaga is a species within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammi visnaga is a species within the Apiaceae family."}, {"found_name": "ameos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2467, "target_cluster_name": "ameos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammi visnaga belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammi visnaga belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ferula", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2476, "target_cluster_name": "ferula", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fennel belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fennel belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Angelica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 385, "target_cluster_name": "Angelica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Angelica is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Angelica is a genus within the Apiaceae family."}, {"found_name": "Anise", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2499, "target_cluster_name": "anime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anise (Pimpinella anisum) is a species within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Anise (Pimpinella anisum) is a species within the Apiaceae family."}, {"found_name": "coentro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1767, "target_cluster_name": "coentro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coriander is a member of the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Coriander is a member of the Apiaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "disease", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "disease", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "disease", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["disease"], "contexts": ["affects purple plums and yellow peaches", "abnormal condition of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "disease", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "disease", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["disease"], "contexts": ["a disorder of structure or function", "impairs automatic agency functions"]}, {"entity_id": "disease", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "disease", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["disease"], "contexts": ["abnormal condition of the body", "a pathological condition affecting the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "disease", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "disease", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "disease", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disease", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "disease", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disease", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 883, "stable_key": "clu_954831f7ae2147f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "disease", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster represents the general concept of disease rather than a specific ailment.", "wikidata_id": "Q12136", "wikidata_description": "abnormal condition negatively affecting organisms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disease", "wikipedia_extract": "A disease is a particular abnormal condition that adversely affects the structure or function of all or part of an organism and is not immediately due to any external injury. Diseases are often known to be medical conditions that are associated with specific signs and symptoms. A disease may be caused by external factors such as pathogens or by internal dysfunctions. For example, internal dysfunctions of the immune system can produce a variety of different diseases, including various forms of immunodeficiency, hypersensitivity, allergies, and autoimmune disorders. In humans, disease is often used more broadly to refer to any condition that causes pain, dysfunction, distress, social problems, or death to the person affected, or similar problems for those in contact with the person. In this broader sense, it sometimes includes injuries, disabilities, disorders, syndromes, infections, isolated symptoms, deviant behaviors, and atypical variations of structure and function, while in other contexts and for other purposes these may be considered distinguishable categories."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "males", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3175, "target_cluster_name": "males", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'illness' is a general term that encompasses specific instances of 'disease'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'illness' is a general term that encompasses specific instances of 'disease'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "earth's crust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "earth's_crust", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "earth's crust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["earth's crust"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "earth's_crust", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "earth's crust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["earth's crust", "Earth's crust"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the planet", "outermost layer of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "earth’s_crust", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Earth’s crust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Earth’s crust"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the planet", "moulded and re-moulded by forces"]}, {"entity_id": "crust_of_the_earth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "crust of the earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["crust of the earth"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the planet"]}, {"entity_id": "crust_of_the_earth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "crust of the Earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["crust of the Earth"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the planet"]}, {"entity_id": "crust_of_the_earth", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crust of the earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["crust of the earth"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "earth's crust", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "earth's crust", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "earth's crust", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth’s crust", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earth's crust", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth’s crust", "similarity": 0.994}], "id": 884, "stable_key": "clu_c86a0d82d608ce50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's crust", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 533, "target_cluster_name": "earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Soil is formed on the Earth's crust, making it a directly related geological layer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Soil is formed on the Earth's crust, making it a directly related geological layer.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Basalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1665, "target_cluster_name": "basalt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Basalt is a common type of igneous rock found in the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Basalt is a common type of igneous rock found in the Earth's crust."}, {"found_name": "Granite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1634, "target_cluster_name": "granite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Granite is a common type of intrusive igneous rock found in the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Granite is a common type of intrusive igneous rock found in the Earth's crust."}]}, {"canonical_name": "hauteur", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "height", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "height", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["height"], "contexts": ["vertical dimension of landforms"]}, {"entity_id": "hauteur", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hauteur", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["hauteur", "hauteur barométrique", "hauteur barométrique moyenne", "hauteur solsticiales", "hauteurs moyennes"], "contexts": ["geographic coordinate (altitude)", "measurement for temperate sites"]}, {"entity_id": "heights", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "heights", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Heights", "heights"], "contexts": ["vertical distances", "mode of ascertaining by Fahrenheit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "height", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "hauteur", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "height", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "heights", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hauteur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "heights", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 885, "stable_key": "clu_4ed66a67dd727ccd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "altitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The texts refer to the vertical dimension of landforms and geographic coordinates, which directly relates to altitude.", "wikidata_id": "Q190200", "wikidata_description": "height in relation to a certain reference point", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altitude", "wikipedia_extract": "Altitude is a distance measurement, usually in the vertical or \"up\" direction, between a reference datum and a point or object. The exact definition and reference datum varies according to the context (e.g., aviation, geometry, geographical survey, sport, or atmospheric pressure). Although the term altitude is commonly used to mean the height above sea level of a location, in geography the term elevation is often preferred for this usage. In aviation, altitude is typically measured relative to mean sea level or above ground level to ensure safe navigation and flight operations. In geometry and geographical surveys, altitude helps create accurate topographic maps and understand the terrain's elevation. For high-altitude trekking and sports, knowing and adapting to altitude is vital for performance and safety."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Elevation", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 380, "target_cluster_name": "elevation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elevation is a synonym for altitude, referring to height above a reference level.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elevation is a synonym for altitude, referring to height above a reference level."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bourbon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "bourbon", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bourbon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["BOURBON", "Bourbon"], "contexts": ["location with sugar cultivation", "location of sugar cultivation"]}, {"entity_id": "bourbon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bourbon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bourbon"], "contexts": ["island now known as Réunion"]}, {"entity_id": "bourbon", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bourbon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bourbon"], "contexts": ["island in the Indian Ocean (Réunion)"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bourbon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bourbon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bourbon", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bourbon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bourbon", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bourbon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 886, "stable_key": "clu_de3fae7be6ed5cf5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Réunion", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "Historically referred to as Bourbon Island.", "wikidata_id": "Q17070", "wikidata_description": "overseas department of France in the Indian Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%A9union", "wikipedia_extract": "Réunion (; French: [la ʁe.ynjɔ̃] ; Reunionese Creole: La Rényon; known as Île Bourbon before 1848) is an island in the Indian Ocean that is an overseas department and region of France within the African region. Part of the Mascarene Islands, it is located approximately 679 kilometres (367 nautical miles) east of the island of Madagascar and 175 kilometres (94 nmi) southwest of the island of Mauritius. As of January 2026, it had a population of 910,985. Its capital and largest city is Saint-Denis. Before the arrival of French colonial subjects and immigrants in the 17th century, Réunion was an uninhabited island. Its tropical climate led to the development of a plantation economy focused primarily on sugar; slaves from East Africa were imported as fieldworkers, followed by Malays, Vietnamese, Chinese, and Indians as indentured laborers."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Indian Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 362, "target_cluster_name": "Indian Ocean", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Réunion is an island in the Indian Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Réunion is an island in the Indian Ocean."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Filipinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "filipinas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Filipinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Filipinas"], "contexts": ["fertile district with Rio de Galafre", "jurisdiction mentioned in census data"]}, {"entity_id": "îles_philippines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles Philippines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["îles Philippines", "Iles Philipi)ines"], "contexts": ["islands for new route", "towards the Philippine Islands"]}, {"entity_id": "philippinas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Philippinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philippinas"], "contexts": ["archipelago where Magalhães died"]}, {"entity_id": "philippine_islands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Philippine Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philippine Islands"], "contexts": ["islands in Southeast Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Filipinas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Philippinas", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Filipinas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Philippine Islands", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "îles Philippines", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Philippinas", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "îles Philippines", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Philippine Islands", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Philippinas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Philippine Islands", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 887, "stable_key": "clu_b79a593cae408536", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philippines", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago and fertile district, historically known as Filipinas.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Philippines, officially the Republic of the Philippines, is an archipelagic country in Southeast Asia. Located in the western Pacific Ocean, it consists of about 7,641 islands, with a total area of about 300,000 square kilometers, which are broadly categorized in three main geographical divisions from north to south: Luzon, Visayas, and Mindanao. With a population of over 112 million, it is the world's fourteenth-most-populous country. The Philippines is bounded by the South China Sea to the west, the Philippine Sea to the east, and the Celebes Sea to the south. It shares maritime borders with Taiwan to the north, Japan to the northeast, Palau to the east and southeast, Indonesia to the south, Malaysia to the southwest, Vietnam to the west, and China to the northwest. It has diverse ethnicities and a rich culture."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Philippines is a major archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Philippines is a major archipelago."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Marseille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "marseille", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Marseille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Marseille"], "contexts": ["market for Crimean flour", "city in France"]}, {"entity_id": "marseilles", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Marseilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Marseilles"], "contexts": ["coast mentioned", "city on the Mediterranean coast"]}, {"entity_id": "marseilles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Marseilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Marseilles"], "contexts": ["location of comet sighting", "location of comet observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Marseille", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Marseilles", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Marseille", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Marseilles", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Marseilles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Marseilles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 888, "stable_key": "clu_663fbe58727823c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marseille", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major port city in southern France.", "wikipedia_extract": "Marseille (Provençal Occitan: Marselha; see below) is a city in southern France, the prefecture of the department of Bouches-du-Rhône and of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region. Situated in the Provence region, it is located on the coast of the Mediterranean Sea, near the mouth of the Rhône river. Marseille is the second-most populous city proper in France, after Paris, with 877,215 inhabitants in 2022 (Jan. census) over a municipal territory of 241 km2 (93 sq mi). Together with its suburbs and exurbs, the Marseille metropolitan area, which extends over 3,972 km2 (1,534 sq mi), had a population of 1,900,957 at the Jan. 2022 census. The cities of Marseille, Aix-en-Provence, and 90 suburban municipalities have formed since 2016 the Aix-Marseille-Provence Metropolis, an indirectly elected metropolitan authority now in charge of wider metropolitan issues, with a population of 1,922,626 at the Jan. 2022 census. Founded c. 600 BC by Greek settlers from Phocaea, Marseille is the oldest city in France, as well as one of Europe's oldest continuously inhabited settlements."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Connecticut", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "connecticut", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Connecticut", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Connecticut"], "contexts": ["state in the United States", "state in New England"]}, {"entity_id": "connecticut", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Connecticut", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Connecticut"], "contexts": ["state in the United States"]}, {"entity_id": "connecticut", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Connecticut", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Connecticut"], "contexts": ["state where New Haven is located", "state in the United States"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Connecticut", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Connecticut", "similarity": 0.782}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Connecticut", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Connecticut", "similarity": 0.782}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Connecticut", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Connecticut", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 889, "stable_key": "clu_45400baa003bf2b1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Connecticut", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q379648", "wikidata_description": "river in the New England region of the United States", "wikipedia_extract": "Connecticut (  kə-NET-ih-kət) is a state in the New England region of the Northeastern United States. It borders Rhode Island to the east, Massachusetts to the north, New York to the west, and Long Island Sound to the south. Its capital is Hartford, and its most populous city is Bridgeport. Connecticut lies between the major hubs of New York City and Boston along the Northeast Corridor, where the New York-Newark Combined Statistical Area, which includes four of Connecticut's seven largest cities, extends into the southwestern part of the state. Connecticut is the third-smallest state by area after Rhode Island and Delaware, and the 29th most populous with more than 3.6 million residents as of 2024, ranking it fourth among the most densely populated U.S. states. The state is named after the Connecticut River, the longest in New England, which roughly bisects the state and drains into the Long Island Sound between the towns of Old Saybrook and Old Lyme."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Massachusetts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2408, "target_cluster_name": "Massachusetts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Massachusetts and Connecticut are neighboring states in the United States, often discussed together in historical and geographical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Massachusetts and Connecticut are neighboring states in the United States, often discussed together in historical and geographical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Auvergne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "auvergne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Auvergne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Auvergne"], "contexts": ["region in France"]}, {"entity_id": "auvergne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Auvergne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Auvergne"], "contexts": ["region in France", "region with come-shaped volcanic summits"]}, {"entity_id": "auvergne", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Auvergne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Auvergne"], "contexts": ["region containing Chaudes Aigues", "region with temperature measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Auvergne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Auvergne", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Auvergne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Auvergne", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Auvergne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Auvergne", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 890, "stable_key": "clu_1e6acf86567019ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Auvergne", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q1152", "wikidata_description": "cultural and historical region of France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auvergne", "wikipedia_extract": "Auvergne (; French: [ovɛʁɲ] ; Occitan: Auvèrnhe or Auvèrnha) is a cultural region in central France. It is now largely part of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes administrative region. Auvergne is generally regarded as conterminous with the land area of the historical Province of Auvergne, which was dissolved in 1790, and with the now-defunct administrative region of Auvergne, which existed from 1956 to 2015. The region is home to a chain of volcanoes known collectively as the \"chaîne des Puys\". The volcanoes began forming about 70,000 years ago, and most have eroded, leaving plugs of hardened magma that form rounded hilltops known as puys. The last confirmed eruption occurred around 4040 BCE."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2383, "target_cluster_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Clermont-Ferrand is located in the Auvergne region of France.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Clermont-Ferrand is located in the Auvergne region of France.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Polar Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "mer_polaire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mer Polaire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mer Polaire"], "contexts": ["sea forming a water divide", "body of water"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_sea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Polar Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Polar Sea"], "contexts": ["sea near Siberian coast", "location of Wrangel's observations"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_ocean", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Polar Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Polar Ocean"], "contexts": ["ocean with milder climates", "ocean with tides"]}, {"entity_id": "south_polar_seas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "south polar seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["south polar seas"], "contexts": ["seas around the South Pole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mer Polaire", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Polar Sea", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mer Polaire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Polar Ocean", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mer Polaire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "south polar seas", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Polar Sea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Polar Ocean", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Polar Sea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "south polar seas", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 891, "stable_key": "clu_f48d29fd6d55ea30", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arctic Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "International", "note": "While 'south polar seas' is mentioned, the majority of references to 'Polar Sea' and 'Polar Ocean' in early modern texts, especially in geographical contexts like Humboldt's work, typically refer to the Arctic Ocean. The description 'sea near Siberian coast' further supports this.", "wikidata_id": "Q25322", "wikidata_description": "polar region on the Earth's northern hemisphere", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic", "wikipedia_extract": "The Arctic (; from Ancient Greek  ἄρκτος (árktos) 'bear') is the polar region of Earth that surrounds the North Pole, lying north of the Arctic Circle. The Arctic region, from the IERS Reference Meridian travelling east, consists of parts of northern Norway (Nordland, Troms, Finnmark, Svalbard and Jan Mayen), northernmost Sweden (Västerbotten, Norrbotten and Lappland), northern Finland (North Ostrobothnia, Kainuu and Lappi), Russia (Murmansk, Siberia, Nenets Okrug, Novaya Zemlya), the United States (Alaska), Canada (Yukon, Northwest Territories, Nunavut), Danish Realm (Greenland), and northern Iceland (Grímsey and Kolbeinsey), along with the Arctic Ocean and adjacent seas. Land within the Arctic region has seasonally varying snow and ice cover, with predominantly treeless permafrost under the tundra. Arctic seas contain seasonal sea ice in many places. The Arctic region is a unique area among Earth's ecosystems. The cultures in the region and the Arctic indigenous peoples have adapted to its cold and extreme conditions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mer", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 921, "target_cluster_name": "mer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic Ocean is a specific sea, a part of the larger interconnected body of seawaters referred to as 'the sea'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Arctic Ocean is a specific sea, a part of the larger interconnected body of seawaters referred to as 'the sea'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Atlantic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 318, "target_cluster_name": "Atlantic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Atlantic Ocean and the Arctic Ocean are adjacent and historically significant bodies of water, often discussed together in the context of exploration and geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Atlantic Ocean and the Arctic Ocean are adjacent and historically significant bodies of water, often discussed together in the context of exploration and geography.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Canada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 736, "target_cluster_name": "Canada", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Canada's territory extends northward into the Arctic Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Canada's territory extends northward into the Arctic Ocean.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic Ocean is the body of water within the Arctic region.", "confidence": 0.85, "au