1574", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "tinta", "similarity": 0.825}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tinta", "similarity": 0.785}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tintura", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tinta", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 905, "stable_key": "clu_db8c6f501bab656b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tincture", "confidence": "high", "type": "SUBSTANCE", "description": "An alcoholic extract of plant material or other substances.", "wikipedia_extract": "A tincture is typically an extract of plant or animal material dissolved in ethanol (ethyl alcohol). Solvent concentrations of 25–60% are common, but may run as high as 90%. In chemistry, a tincture is a solution that has ethanol as its solvent. In herbal medicine, alcoholic tinctures are made with various ethanol concentrations, which should be at least 20% alcohol for preservation purposes. Other solvents for producing tinctures include vinegar, glycerol (also called glycerine), diethyl ether and propylene glycol, not all of which can be used for internal consumption. Ethanol has the advantage of being an excellent solvent for both acidic and basic (alkaline) constituents."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cozimentos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 786, "target_cluster_name": "cozimentos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tinctures and decoctions are both common methods of preparing herbal medicines, differing primarily in the solvent used (alcohol vs. water) and the boiling process.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tinctures and decoctions are both common methods of preparing herbal medicines, differing primarily in the solvent used (alcohol vs. water) and the boiling process.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alcohol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 131, "target_cluster_name": "alcohol", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alcohol is the primary solvent used in the preparation of tinctures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alcohol is the primary solvent used in the preparation of tinctures."}]}, {"canonical_name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["example of an extra-mental reality", "machine with moving power"]}, {"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["engine powered by steam", "example of a labouring force"]}, {"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["engine powered by steam", "machine powered by steam"]}, {"entity_id": "steam_engine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "steam engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["steam engine"], "contexts": ["machine powered by steam", "engine powered by steam"]}, {"entity_id": "steam_engine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "steam engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["steam engine"], "contexts": ["machine powered by steam"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "steam engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam engine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 906, "stable_key": "clu_72e9bc9d27c69f20", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "steam engine", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q12760", "wikidata_description": "heat engine that performs mechanical work using steam as its working fluid", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam%20engine", "wikipedia_extract": "A steam engine is a heat engine that performs mechanical work using steam as its working fluid. The steam engine uses the force produced by steam pressure to push a piston back and forth inside a cylinder. This pushing force can be transformed by a connecting rod and crank into rotational force for work. The term \"steam engine\" is normally applied to reciprocating engines, although some authorities have also referred to the steam turbine and devices such as Hero's aeolipile as \"steam engines\". The essential feature of steam engines is that they are external combustion engines, where the working fluid is separated from the combustion products. The ideal thermodynamic cycle used to analyze this process is called the Rankine cycle."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Machine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2796, "target_cluster_name": "machine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A steam engine is a specific type of machine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A steam engine is a specific type of machine."}, {"found_name": "Mechanical engines", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3233, "target_cluster_name": "Mechanical engines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A steam engine is a type of mechanical engine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A steam engine is a type of mechanical engine."}, {"found_name": "Piston", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3775, "target_cluster_name": "piston", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A piston is a key component of a steam engine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A piston is a key component of a steam engine."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "agricola", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Agricola"], "contexts": ["author of 'de natura fossilium'", "ancient Roman author"]}, {"entity_id": "agrícola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Agrícola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Agrícola"], "contexts": ["physician praising bezoars", "early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "agricola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Agricola", "Agricol"], "contexts": ["early modern author", "author of 'Comment. in Popp.tract.deAntimonio'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Agricola", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Agrícola", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Agricola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agricola", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Agrícola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agricola", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 907, "stable_key": "clu_98c950fe8f81c073", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georgius Agricola", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Early modern German scholar, physician, and author.", "note": "Refers to Georgius Agricola, a prominent figure in early modern natural knowledge.", "wikipedia_extract": "Georgius Agricola (; born Georg Bauer; 24 March 1494 – 21 November 1555) was a German Humanist scholar, mineralogist and metallurgist. Born in the small town of Glauchau, in the Electorate of Saxony of the Holy Roman Empire, he was broadly educated, but took a particular interest in the mining and refining of metals. He was the first to drop the Arabic definite article al-, exclusively writing chymia and chymista in describing activity that we today would characterize as chemical or alchemical, giving chemistry its modern name. For his groundbreaking work De Natura Fossilium published in 1546, he is generally referred to as the father of mineralogy and the founder of geology as a scientific discipline. He is well known for his pioneering work De re metallica libri XII, that was published in 1556, one year after his death. This 12-chapter work is a comprehensive and systematic study, classification and methodical guide on all available factual and practical aspects that are of concern for mining, the mining sciences and metallurgy, investigated and researched in its natural environment by means of direct observation."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Albertus Magnus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2461, "target_cluster_name": "Alberto Magno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Albertus Magnus was a prominent medieval scholar whose work on natural philosophy and science influenced later thinkers like Agricola.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Albertus Magnus was a prominent medieval scholar whose work on natural philosophy and science influenced later thinkers like Agricola."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle's comprehensive approach to natural philosophy and his systematic classification of knowledge served as a foundational influence for many early modern scholars, including Agricola.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aristotle's comprehensive approach to natural philosophy and his systematic classification of knowledge served as a foundational influence for many early modern scholars, including Agricola."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides' work on materia medica was a key text for understanding medicinal plants and minerals, subjects central to Agricola's interests in mineralogy and metallurgy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides' work on materia medica was a key text for understanding medicinal plants and minerals, subjects central to Agricola's interests in mineralogy and metallurgy."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen's extensive writings on medicine and anatomy were a cornerstone of medical knowledge in Agricola's time, and his influence extended to related fields like the study of minerals for medicinal purposes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen's extensive writings on medicine and anatomy were a cornerstone of medical knowledge in Agricola's time, and his influence extended to related fields like the study of minerals for medicinal purposes."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates, as the 'father of medicine,' established principles of observation and rational inquiry that influenced the broader scientific and medical traditions that Agricola engaged with.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates, as the 'father of medicine,' established principles of observation and rational inquiry that influenced the broader scientific and medical traditions that Agricola engaged with."}, {"found_name": "Pliny the Elder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 202, "target_cluster_name": "Plinio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pliny the Elder's Natural History was a vast compendium of knowledge about the natural world, including minerals and metals, making it a significant reference for Agricola's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pliny the Elder's Natural History was a vast compendium of knowledge about the natural world, including minerals and metals, making it a significant reference for Agricola's work."}, {"found_name": "Hartmanus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1581, "target_cluster_name": "Hartmanus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Georgius Agricola, a 16th-century German scholar, is considered the 'father of metallurgy and mineralogy.' While Stahl's primary focus was chemistry and medicine, Agricola's foundational work in related earth sciences and his systematic approach to observation and description in his field make him a relevant precursor in the broader context of German scientific inquiry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Georgius Agricola, a 16th-century German scholar, is considered the 'father of metallurgy and mineralogy.' While Stahl's primary focus was chemistry and medicine, Agricola's foundational work in related earth sciences and his systematic approach to observation and description in his field make him a relevant precursor in the broader context of German scientific inquiry.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "caterpillar", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "caterpillars", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Caterpillars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caterpillars"], "contexts": ["insects indicating common corruption"]}, {"entity_id": "caterpillar", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "caterpillar", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["caterpillar"], "contexts": ["larval stage of a moth or butterfly", "larval stage of butterflies and moths"]}, {"entity_id": "caterpillars", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "caterpillars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["caterpillars"], "contexts": ["larval stage of moths and butterflies", "larval stage of butterflies and moths"]}, {"entity_id": "caterpillar", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "caterpillar", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["caterpillar"], "contexts": ["larval stage of a b