ey%20Hall", "wikipedia_extract": "Granville Stanley Hall (February 1, 1844 – April 24, 1924) was an American psychologist and educator who earned the first doctorate in psychology awarded in the United States of America at Harvard University in the nineteenth century. His interests focused on human life span development and evolutionary theory. Hall was the first president of the American Psychological Association and the first president of Clark University. A 2002 survey by Review of General Psychology ranked Hall as the 72nd most cited psychologist of the 20th century, in a tie with Lewis Terman. Born in Ashfield, Massachusetts, Hall grew up on a farm with his parents, Granville Bascom Hall, who served on the Massachusetts legislature, and Abigail Beals, who attended school at Albany Female Seminary and went on to become a teacher herself. As a child, Hall spent much of his time reading and taking advantage of the educational opportunities he could gain from his parents and the local schools."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "M. James", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 442, "target_cluster_name": "M. James", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Granville Stanley Hall was a prominent psychologist and educator in the United States, and a contemporary and colleague of William James. They were both key figures in the development of psychology as an academic discipline in America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Granville Stanley Hall was a prominent psychologist and educator in the United States, and a contemporary and colleague of William James. They were both key figures in the development of psychology as an academic discipline in America.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Herbert Spencer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3526, "target_cluster_name": "Herbert Spencer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential figures in the development of psychology and education, with Spencer's evolutionary theories impacting Hall's work on child development and adolescence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential figures in the development of psychology and education, with Spencer's evolutionary theories impacting Hall's work on child development and adolescence."}]}, {"canonical_name": "astronomical observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "observations_astronomiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "observations astronomiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Observations astronomiques", "observations astronomiques"], "contexts": ["astronomical measurements and records", "determining position using celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical_observations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "astronomical observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomical observations"], "contexts": ["proofs for attraction determined by matter", "methods used in astronomy"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical_observation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "astronomical observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["astronomical observation"], "contexts": ["observing celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical_observations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "astronomical observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["astronomical observations"], "contexts": ["determining positions using celestial bodies", "observations of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical_observation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_some