"Desire is often a primary driver or 'purpose' behind actions and intentions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Desire is often a primary driver or 'purpose' behind actions and intentions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "curiosity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2802, "target_cluster_name": "curiosity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity is a form of desire, specifically a desire to know or learn.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Curiosity is a form of desire, specifically a desire to know or learn.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "nervous shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_shock", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["nervous shock"], "contexts": ["mental shock or trauma", "sudden physical or mental impact"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["condition causing flaccidity in frog"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["sudden impact"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["sensation from electric discharge", "disagreeable sensation from electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous shock", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 926, "stable_key": "clu_273da65741918860", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous shock", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Trauma response", "wikidata_id": "Q16998640", "wikidata_description": "concept in English law", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nervous%20shock", "wikipedia_extract": "In English law, a nervous shock is a psychiatric / mental illness or injury inflicted upon a person by intentional or negligent actions or omissions of another. Often it is a psychiatric disorder triggered by witnessing an accident, for example an injury caused to one's parents or spouse. Although the term \"nervous shock\" has been described as \"inaccurate\" and \"misleading\", it continues to be applied as a useful abbreviation for a complex concept. The possibility of recovering damages for nervous shock, particularly caused by negligence, is strongly limited in English law. To amount in law to \"nervous shock\", the psychiatric damage suffered by the claimant must extend beyond grief or emotional distress to a recognised mental illness, such as anxiety neurosis or reactive depression. Damages for bereavement suffered as a result of the wrongful death of a close one are available under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976, while courts can also award damages for \"pain and suffering\" as a result of physical injury."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Grief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1325, "target_cluster_name": "grief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Witnessing traumatic events, a common cause of nervous shock, often leads to profound grief.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Witnessing traumatic events, a common cause of nervous shock, often leads to profound grief."}, {"found_name": "Exhaustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3509, "target_cluster_name": "exhaustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The psychological and physiological impact of nervous shock can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The psychological and physiological impact of nervous shock can lead to extreme exhaustion."}, {"found_name": "Ciatica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1501, "target_cluster_name": "Ciatica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica can be caused by nerve compression or irritation, and 'nervous shock' is a related concept in the context of neurological distress.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sciatica can be caused by nerve compression or irritation, and 'nervous shock' is a related concept in the context of neurological distress.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Faintings", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2269, "target_cluster_name": "Faintings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sudden emotional or physical shock can cause fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sudden emotional or physical shock can cause fainting.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "centre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"ent