enus Bryonia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bryonia is a genus of flowering plants in the gourd family. Bryony  is its best-known common name. They are native to western Eurasia and adjacent regions, such as North Africa, the Canary Islands and South Asia. Bryonies are perennial, tendril-climbing, diclinous or dioecious herbs with palmately lobed leaves and flowers in axillary clusters. The fruit is a smooth, globular berry. Bryonia is used as a food plant by the larvae of some Lepidoptera (butterflies and moths), including the tortrix moth Phtheochroa rugosana (recorded on red bryony, B. dioica) and the cabbage moth (Mamestra brassicae)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Colocynthis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3818, "target_cluster_name": "Colocynthis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Colocynth and Bryony are types of gourds or cucurbits, often with medicinal or toxic properties, and are sometimes confused or grouped together in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Colocynth and Bryony are types of gourds or cucurbits, often with medicinal or toxic properties, and are sometimes confused or grouped together in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "heavens", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "heaven", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Heaven", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Heaven"], "contexts": ["religious afterlife destination", "afterlife reward"]}, {"entity_id": "heavens", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "heavens", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["heavens"], "contexts": ["celestial sphere, space", "celestial regions"]}, {"entity_id": "heavens", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "heavens", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["heavens"], "contexts": ["the sky and celestial bodies", "celestial sphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Heaven", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "heavens", "similarity": 0.833}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Heaven", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "heavens", "similarity": 0.833}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "heavens", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "heavens", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 941, "stable_key": "clu_7297c94f67f8f5e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cosmos", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'heavens' in early modern texts encompasses both the religious concept of an afterlife destination and the physical realm of celestial bodies and space. 'Cosmos' best captures the latter, scientific sense, while the religious sense is more theological. Given the context with Humboldt, 'Cosmos' is the most fitting modern referent for the scientific interpretation.", "wikidata_id": "Q476937", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmos%20%28plant%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Cosmos is a genus, with the same common name of cosmos, consisting of flowering plants in the daisy family. The generic name Cosmos derives either from the Greek κόσμος (cosmos) '(ordered) world' – in reference to the neat, orderly arrangement of the floral structures – or the Greek κόσμημα (kósmima) 'jewel' – in reference to the jewel-like colors of the capitula (composite flowers). Cosmos are herbaceous perennial plants or annual plants growing 0.3–2 metres (1–6+1⁄2 ft) tall. The leaves are simple, pinnate, or bipinnate, and arranged in opposite pairs. The flowers are produced in a capitulum with a ring of broad ray florets and a center of disc florets; flower color varies noticeably between the different species. The genus includes several ornamental plants popular in gardens."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "creation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 12, "target_cluster_name": "creation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Creationism posits that the cosmos originated from supernatural acts of divine creation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Creationism posits that the cosmos originated from supernatural acts of divine creation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Jupiter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 553, "target_cluster_name": "Jupiter", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jupiter is a component of the cosmos.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jupiter is a component of the cosmos.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Stars", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2672, "target_cluster_name": "Stars", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The cosmos encompasses all of space and time, including stars.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The cosmos encompasses all of space and time, including stars.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Cosmos' historically referred to the heavens or the universe, which is the primary subject of Astronomy. The description also notes the etymological link to the Greek word for 'ordered world', directly relating to the study of the universe's order.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'Cosmos' historically referred to the heavens or the universe, which is the primary subject of Astronomy. The description also notes the etymological link to the Greek word for 'ordered world', directly relating to the study of the universe's order."}, {"found_name": "Genus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 263, "target_cluster_name": "genus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Cosmos' is explicitly described as a genus of plants. Therefore, 'Genus' is a direct taxonomic relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Cosmos' is explicitly described as a genus of plants. Therefore, 'Genus' is a direct taxonomic relationship."}, {"found_name": "Botany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1098, "target_cluster_name": "botany", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "As 'Cosmos' is a genus of flowering plants, Botany is the scientific discipline that studies them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "As 'Cosmos' is a genus of flowering plants, Botany is the scientific discipline that studies them."}, {"found_name": "falling stars", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2314, "target_cluster_name": "falling stars", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Meteors are phenomena observed within the cosmos or heavens.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Meteors are phenomena observed within the cosmos or heavens.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pythagoreans", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2504, "target_cluster_name": "Pythagoreans", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pythagorean thought involved a conception of the cosmos as an ordered and harmonious whole.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pythagorean thought involved a conception of the cosmos as an ordered and harmonious whole.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cosmos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "following. In order adequately to estimate the dignity of the Cosmos, it is requisite that the sidereal portion, termed by Kant the 'natural history of the heavens', should not be made subordinate to ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["straits with compass variation", "strait at the southern tip of South America"]}, {"entity_id": "straits_of_magellan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "straits of Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["straits of Magellan"], "contexts": ["location with needle declination"]}, {"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["Straits"]}, {"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["strait in South America", "Explorer whose voyage influenced demarcation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "straits of Magellan", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "straits of Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 942, "stable_key": "clu_52320083452c3030", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strait of Magellan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A sea passage in South America named after an explorer.", "note": "Refers to both the geographical strait and the explorer Ferdinand Magellan.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Strait of Magellan (Spanish: Estrecho de Magallanes), also called the Straits of Magellan, is a navigable sea route in southern Chile separating mainland South America to the north and the Tierra del Fuego archipelago to the south. Considered the most important natural passage between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, the strait is approximately 570 km (310 nmi; 350 mi) long and 2 km (1.1 nmi; 1.2 mi) wide at its narrowest point. In 1520, the Spanish expedition of the Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan, after whom the strait is named, became the first Europeans to discover it. Magellan's original name for the strait was Estrecho de Todos los Santos (\"Strait of All Saints\"). The King of Spain, Emperor Charles V, who had sponsored the Magellan-Elcano expedition, changed the name to the Strait of Magellan in honor of Magellan. The route is difficult to navigate due to frequent narrows and unpredictable winds and currents."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cape Horn", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 791, "target_cluster_name": "cap de Horn", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Strait of Magellan and Cape Horn are both significant geographical features at the southern tip of South America, often considered in relation to each other as passages around the continent.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Strait of Magellan and Cape Horn are both significant geographical features at the southern tip of South America, often considered in relation to each other as passages around the continent."}]}, {"canonical_name": "faculties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "faculties", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Faculties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faculties"], "contexts": ["mental powers or abilities"]}, {"entity_id": "faculties", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "faculties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["faculties"], "contexts": ["scholastic principle of mind", "powers or capacities of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "faculties", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "faculties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["faculties"], "contexts": ["innate capacities of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_powers", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mental powers", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mental powers"], "contexts": ["primary cognitive abilities", "faculties of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_power", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name"