"confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Bdellium is a known aromatic resin from various Commiphora species, fitting the context of an aromatic resin from a tree.", "wikipedia_extract": "Commiphora wightii, with common names Indian bdellium-tree, gugal, guggal, guggul, gugul, or mukul myrrh tree, is a flowering plant in the family Burseraceae, which produces a fragrant resin called gugal, guggul or gugul, that is used in incense and vedic medicine (or ayurveda). The species is native to western India, from where it was introduced westward to southern Pakistan and the middle-east. It prefers arid and semi-arid climates and is tolerant of poor soil. Commiphora wightii grows as a shrub or small tree, reaching a maximum height of 4 m (13 ft), with thin papery bark. The branches are thorny. The leaves are simple or trifoliate, the leaflets ovate, 1–5 cm (0.39–1.97 in) long, 0.5–2.5 cm (0.20–0.98 in) broad, and irregularly toothed.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commiphora_wightii"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "myrrh", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 583, "target_cluster_name": "Myrrha", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bdellium and myrrh are both resins from the Burseraceae family, often used similarly in incense and traditional medicine, and historically confused or grouped together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bdellium and myrrh are both resins from the Burseraceae family, often used similarly in incense and traditional medicine, and historically confused or grouped together."}, {"found_name": "frankincense", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 653, "target_cluster_name": "incenso", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bdellium, myrrh, and frankincense are all aromatic resins historically used together in incense and perfumery, and sometimes grouped or confused in ancient