d", "evidence_snippet": "Fucus is a genus of brown algae, historically a significant and well-documented type of seaweed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fucus is a genus of brown algae, historically a significant and well-documented type of seaweed."}, {"found_name": "Seaweeds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2319, "target_cluster_name": "Seaweeds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Seaweeds are a common and direct descriptor for many types of algae, particularly those found in the ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Seaweeds are a common and direct descriptor for many types of algae, particularly those found in the ocean."}, {"found_name": "mosses", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2317, "target_cluster_name": "mosses", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Algae are simple aquatic plants, and historical classifications sometimes grouped them with or near bryophytes like mosses due to their non-vascular nature and aquatic/moist habitats.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Algae are simple aquatic plants, and historical classifications sometimes grouped them with or near bryophytes like mosses due to their non-vascular nature and aquatic/moist habitats.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Corallines", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2938, "target_cluster_name": "Corallines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Corallines are a type of red algae, specifically belonging to the order Corallinales. Historical texts often grouped them together or discussed them in close proximity due to their marine habitat and plant-like appearance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Corallines are a type of red algae, specifically belonging to the order Corallinales. Historical texts often grouped them together or discussed them in close proximity due to their marine habitat and plant-like appearance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lerius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3226, "target_cluster_name": "Lerius", "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "parent plant grew. The quantity of algæ in that accumulation known as the sargassa or grassy sea is so great, that the early navigators, Columbus and Lerius, compared it to extensively inundated meado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of plant found in the sargassa sea", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "sargassa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "different kinds affect different depths, their seeds being of such specific gravity as to remain and germinate where the parent plant grew. The quantity of algæ in that accumulation known as the sarga", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of accumulation of algae"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mr. Blyth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__blyth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. Blyth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Blyth", "Mr. Blyth"], "contexts": ["communicated facts on cattle", "judge of cross-bred geese fertility"]}, {"entity_id": "baily", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Baily"], "contexts": ["reported on pendulum experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "baily", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Baily"], "contexts": ["president of the Astronomical Society", "experimental philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__baily", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mr. Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mr. Baily"], "contexts": ["obtained density of earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Blyth", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Baily", "similarity": 0.845}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baily", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baily", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baily", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Baily", "similarity": 0.85}], "id": 958, "stable_key": "clu_b720cdc1d51e550d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edward Blyth", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1810, "death_year": 1873, "description": "Naturalist and zoologist, noted for work on animal behavior.", "wikidata_id": "Q358754", "wikidata_description": "English zoologist and pharmacist (1810–1873)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward%20Blyth", "wikipedia_extract": "Edward Blyth (23 December 1810 – 27 December 1873) was an English zoologist who worked for most of his life in India as a curator of zoology at the Royal Asiatic Society of Bengal in Calcutta (now Kolkata). He catalogued the specimens in the collection in his Catalogue of the Birds of the Asiatic Society in 1849. He did not collect specimens himself but received and described bird specimens from A.O. Hume, Samuel Tickell, Robert Swinhoe among others. His Natural History of the Cranes was published posthumously in 1881. On 23 December 1810, Blyth was born in London."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alfred Russel Wallace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2104, "target_cluster_name": "Wallace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent zoologists who worked in India and made significant contributions to the understanding of its fauna, particularly birds. They were contemporaries and likely aware of each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent zoologists who worked in India and made significant contributions to the understanding of its fauna, particularly birds. They were contemporaries and likely aware of each other's work."}, {"found_name": "Hugh Falconer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2103, "target_cluster_name": "Dr. Falconer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hugh Falconer was a contemporary Scottish geologist, paleontologist, and botanist who also worked extensively in India. He was a prominent figure in Indian natural history during Blyth's tenure, and they likely interacted or were aware of each other's research.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hugh Falconer was a contemporary Scottish geologist, paleontologist, and botanist who also worked extensively in India. He was a prominent figure in Indian natural history during Blyth's tenure, and they likely interacted or were aware of each other's research."}, {"found_name": "Dr. Falconer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2103, "target_cluster_name": "Dr. Falconer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were naturalists who worked in India and corresponded with each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were naturalists who worked in India and corresponded with each other.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["trilobites", "Trilobites"], "contexts": ["ancient marine arthropods found in fossils", "extinct group of arthropods"]}, {"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["trilobites"], "contexts": ["fossil organism, first traces of organization", "fossil arthropod group"]}, {"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Trilobites", "trilobites"], "contexts": ["fossil arthropod group", "fossil arthropods"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Trilobites", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "trilobites", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Trilobites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trilobites", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "trilobites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trilobites", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 959, "stable_key": "clu_83a5381cb8df5de2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trilobites", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Extinct marine arthropods with a three-lobed body.", "note": "These are well-known fossil organisms.", "wikipedia_extract": "Trilobites (; meaning \"three-lobed entities\") are extinct marine arthropods that form the class Trilobita. One of the earliest groups of arthropods to appear in the fossil record, trilobites were among the most successful of all early animals, existing in oceans for almost 270 million years, with over 22,000 species having been described. Because trilobites had wide diversity and an easily fossilized mineralised exoskeleton made of calcite, they left an extensive fossil record. The study of their fossils has facilitated important contributions to biostratigraphy, paleontology, evolutionary biology, and plate tectonics. Trilobites are placed within the clade Artiopoda, which includes many organisms that are morphologically similar to trilobites, but are largely unmineralised. The relationship of Artiopoda to other arthropods is uncertain."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trilobites are a class of extinct marine arthropods, thus a type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Trilobites are a class of extinct marine arthropods, thus a type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Crustaceans", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 348, "target_cluster_name": "crustaceans", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trilobites are arthropods, and crustaceans are a subphylum of arthropods. While not directly crustaceans, they share a common ancestral lineage and body plan characteristics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Trilobites are arthropods, and crustaceans are a subphylum of arthropods. While not directly crustaceans, they share a common ancestral lineage and body plan characteristics."}, {"found_name": "ammonites", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 847, "target_cluster_name": "Ammonites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are extinct marine mollusks that often co-occur in fossil records with tri