the eye, responsible for light refraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The cornea is a key component in the optical axis of the eye, responsible for light refraction.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amsterdam"], "contexts": ["location of publication"]}, {"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amsterdam"], "contexts": ["city Varenius sought refuge in"]}, {"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Amsterdam", "Amstelo-dami", "Amstelodami", "Amstelsedami"], "contexts": ["publication location", "publication location of Niebuhr's book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 980, "stable_key": "clu_fc53f079d20e0c64", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amsterdam", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major city in the Netherlands, a hub for trade and publication.", "wikipedia_extract": "Amsterdam (Dutch: [ˌɑmstərˈdɑm] ; lit. 'Dam in the Amstel') is the capital and largest city of the Netherlands. It has a population of 933,680 in June 2024 within the city proper, 1,457,018 in the urban area and 2,480,394 in the metropolitan area. Located in the Dutch province of North Holland, Amsterdam is colloquially referred to as the \"Venice of the North\", for its large number of canals, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Amsterdam was founded at the mouth of the Amstel River, which was dammed to control flooding. Originally a small fishing village in the 12th century, Amsterdam became a major world port during the Dutch Golden Age of the 17th century, when the Netherlands was an economic powerhouse. Amsterdam was the leading centre for finance and trade, as well as a hub of secular art production."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hollande", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 127, "target_cluster_name": "Hollande", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amsterdam is a major city and the capital of the Netherlands.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amsterdam is a major city and the capital of the Netherlands.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "method", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "methods", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "methods", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["methods"], "contexts": ["systematic ways of inquiry"]}, {"entity_id": "methods", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "methods", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["methods"], "contexts": ["procedures for scientific investigation"]}, {"entity_id": "method", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "method", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["method", "Method", "Methodica"], "contexts": ["medical treatise", "a medical treatise by Galen"]}, {"entity_id": "method_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Method.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Method.", "Method. Cur. morb."], "contexts": ["medical method", "part of book title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "methods", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "methods", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "methods", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "method", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "methods", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Method.", "similarity": 0.836}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "methods", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "method", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "methods", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Method.", "similarity": 0.836}], "id": 981, "stable_key": "clu_9f0aa83aced229ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Method", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Systematic approach or procedure for inquiry or practice.", "wikipedia_extract": "A proof of concept (POC or PoC), also known as proof of principle, is an inchoate realization of a certain idea or method in order to demonstrate its feasibility or viability. A proof of concept is usually small and may or may not be complete, but aims to demonstrate in principle that the concept has practical potential without needing to fully develop it. A proof of value (PoV) is sometimes used along proof of concept, and differs by focusing more on demonstrating the potential customer use case and value, and is usually less in-depth than a proof of concept. The term has been in use since 1967. In a 1969 hearing of the Committee on Science and Astronautics, Subcommittee on Advanced Research and Technology, proof of concept was defined as following: The Board defined proof of concept as a phase in development in which experimental hardware is constructed and tested to explore and demonstrate the feasibility of a new concept. One definition of the term \"proof of concept\" was by Bruce Carsten in the context of a \"proof-of-concept prototype\" in his magazine column \"Carsten's Corner\" (1989): Proof-of-Concept Prototype is a term that (I believe) I coined in 1984."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "practice", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1940, "target_cluster_name": "practice", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Practices are often specific methods or ways of doing things.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Practices are often specific methods or ways of doing things.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "appetitus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "lust", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lust", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["lust"], "contexts": ["intense sexual desire"]}, {"entity_id": "gana_de_comer", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "gana de comer", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["gana de comer"], "contexts": ["loss of appetite", "appetite, a symptom"]}, {"entity_id": "appetitus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "appetitus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["appetitus", "appetentia"], "contexts": ["appetite", "appetite or desire for food"]}, {"entity_id": "apetite", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "apetite", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["apetite"], "contexts": ["appetite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "gana de comer", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "appetitus", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "gana de comer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "appetitus", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "gana de comer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 982, "stable_key": "clu_6d285ea1128424d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Appetite", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'appetitus' in early modern medical texts encompasses both normal hunger and abnormal appetites, including intense cravings and loss of appetite. 'Gana de comer' specifically means appetite for food.", "wikidata_id": "Q254327", "wikidata_description": "medical symptom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anorexia%20%28symptom%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Anorexia is a medical term for a loss of appetite. While the term outside of the scientific literature is often used interchangeably with anorexia nervosa, many possible causes exist for a loss of appetite, some of which may be harmless, while others indicate a serious clinical condition or pose a significant risk. Anorexia in this usage is a symptom, not a diagnosis. The symptom also occurs in non-human animals, such as cats, dogs, cattle, goats, and sheep. In these species, anorexia may be referred to as inappetence. As in humans, loss of appetite can be due to a range of diseases and conditions, as well as environmental and psychological factors."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mines", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mines"], "contexts": ["excavations for minerals", "locations where minerals are extracted"]}, {"entity_id": "coal_mines", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Coal mines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coal mines"], "contexts": ["excavations for coal"]}, {"entity_id": "minas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "minas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["minas"], "contexts": ["Source of gold"]}, {"entity_id": "mines", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Mines", "mines"], "contexts": ["locations with high temperature", "locations for extracting minerals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mines", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "minas", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mines", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mines", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Coal mines", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "minas", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Coal mines", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mines", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "minas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mines", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 983, "stable_key": "clu_69a3e49d5f2dd557", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mines", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "Refers to excavations for minerals, including coal and gold. 'Minas' in Spanish also refers to mines.", "wikidata_id": "Q820477", "wikidata_description": "place for the extraction of minerals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mine%20%28mining%29"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["repulsion"], "contexts": ["force pushing objects apart"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Repulsion", "repulsion"], "contexts": ["force between electric charges", "force pushing objects apart"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "repulsions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["repulsions"], "contexts": ["cause motion of thunder clouds"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["repulsion"], "contexts": ["forces of attraction and repulsion being universally co-existent", "force pushing objects apart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Repulsion", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "repulsions", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Repulsion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "repulsions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 984, "stable_key": "clu_d545a961be32be1e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Repulsion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Encompasses physical forces pushing objects apart, including electrostatic and possibly atmospheric phenomena.", "wikidata_id": "Q113644972", "wikidata_description": "German record label"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Helvigius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "hevelius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hevelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hevelius"], "contexts": ["observed comet nucleus changes"]}, {"entity_id": "helvigius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Helvigius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Helvigius", "Helvvigius"], "contexts": ["observer of a strange phenomenon", "referent of Boneto de Dysenteria"]}, {"entity_id": "hevelius", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hevelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Hevelius"], "contexts": ["described similar appearance in 1682 comet", "observed comet head contraction phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hevelius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Helvigius", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hevelius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hevelius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Helvigius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hevelius", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 985, "stable_key": "clu_3079067083b41d33", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johannes Hevelius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1611, "death_year": 1687, "description": "Polish astronomer and mayor of Danzig, renowned for comet observations.", "note": "The name 'Helvigius' appears to be an occasional variant or misspelling of 'Hevelius' in the texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q57963", "wikidata_description": "astronomer setting up the cartography of the Moon", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes%20Hevelius", "wikipedia_extract": "Johannes Hevelius (in German also known as Hevel; Polish: Jan Heweliusz; (1611-01-28)28 January 1611 – 28 January 1687) was a  councillor and chairman of the city council of the Old Town, Gdańsk (in the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth). As an  astronomer, he gained a reputation as \"the founder of lunar topography\", and described ten new constellations, seven of which are still used by astronomers. According to the Polish Academy of Sciences (1975) the origin of the name goes back to the surname Hawke, a historical alternative spelling for the English word hawk, which changed into Hawelke or Hawelecke. In Poland he is known as Jan Heweliusz. Other versions of the name include Hewel, Hevel, Hevelke or Hoefel, Höwelcke, Höfelcke. According to Feliks Bentkowski (1814), during his early years he also signed as Hoefelius."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1081, "target_cluster_name": "Cassini", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent astronomers who made significant contributions to the study of the Moon and celestial mapping. Cassini was Hevelius's predecessor and successor as Royal Astronomer in Danzig, and they corresponded and influenced each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent astronomers who made significant contributions to the study of the Moon and celestial mapping. Cassini was Hevelius's predecessor and successor as Royal Astronomer in Danzig, and they corresponded and influenced each other's work."}]}, {"canonical_name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystallization"], "contexts": ["formation of crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Crystallization", "crystallization"], "contexts": ["process of forming crystals", "influence on transmissive power of bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["crystallization"], "contexts": ["process of forming crystals", "formation of crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_state", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "crystalline state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline state"], "contexts": ["state of matter with ordered atomic structure"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_form", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystalline form", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["crystalline form"], "contexts": ["external shape of crystals", "reason for difference in heat transmission"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_forms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystalline forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline forms"], "contexts": ["geometric shapes of crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_forms", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crystalline forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline forms"], "contexts": ["shape of salt crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 986, "stable_key": "clu_b9096518445ed4be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "crystallization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q284256", "wikidata_description": "process by which a solid with a highly organised atomic or molecular structure forms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystallization", "wikipedia_extract": "Crystallization is a process that leads to solids with a uniform pattern of atoms or molecules, i.e. a crystal. The uniform nature of a crystalline solid can be contrasted with amorphous solids in which atoms or molecules lack regular organization. Crystallization can occur by various routes including precipitation from solution, freezing of a liquid, or deposition from a gas. Attributes of the resulting crystal can depend largely on factors such as temperature, air pressure, cooling rate, or solute concentration. Crystallization occurs in two main phases. The first is nucleation, the appearance of a crystalline phase from either a supercooled liquid or a supersaturated solvent."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aggregation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2616, "target_cluster_name": "aggregation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crystallization is a specific form of aggregation where particles arrange into a regular structure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crystallization is a specific form of aggregation where particles arrange into a regular structure."}, {"found_name": "Condensation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1255, "target_cluster_name": "condensations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crystallization often follows or is a form of condensation, where a substance changes from a gaseous or liquid state to a solid crystalline state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crystallization often follows or is a form of condensation, where a substance changes from a gaseous or liquid state to a solid crystalline state."}, {"found_name": "Fluidity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1210, "target_cluster_name": "fluidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crystallization is the process by which a fluid (liquid or gas) transforms into a solid crystal, thus it is directly related to the loss of fluidity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crystallization is the process by which a fluid (liquid or gas) transforms into a solid crystal, thus it is directly related to the loss of fluidity."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous state", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1478, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crystallization can occur from the gaseous state (deposition) or liquid state, making the gaseous state a precursor in some crystallization processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crystallization can occur from the gaseous state (deposition) or liquid state, making the gaseous state a precursor in some crystallization processes."}]}, {"canonical_name": "olio rosato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "olio_rosado", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "olio rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["olio rosado"], "contexts": ["rose oil, an ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "olio_rosato", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "olio rosato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 5, "variants": ["olio rosato", "OLIO ROSATO", "OLIO ROSATO CO"], "contexts": ["rose oil preparation", "preparation made from roses"]}, {"entity_id": "azeyte_rosado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "azeyte rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["azeyte rosado", "azeyte rofado"], "contexts": ["rose oil", "rose oil, medicinal preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "olio rosado", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "olio rosato", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "olio rosado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte rosado", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "olio rosato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte rosado", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 987, "stable_key": "clu_a704a005f00d10b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rose oil", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q83054", "wikidata_description": "essential oil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose%20oil", "wikipedia_extract": "Rose oil (rose otto, attar of rose, attar of roses, or rose essence) is an essential oil that is extracted from the petals of various types of rose. Rose ottos are extracted through steam distillation, while rose absolutes are obtained through solvent extraction, the absolute being used more commonly in perfumery. The production technique originated in Iran. Even with their high price and the advent of organic synthesis, rose oils are still perhaps the most widely used essential oil in perfumery. Two major species of rose are cultivated for the production of rose oil: Rosa damascena production today is dominated by 3 producers account for over 70% of the Rose oil market share: It is also grown on a smaller scale in Afghanistan, Armenia, Azerbaijan, Bosnia, Croatia, Cyprus, Ethiopia, Georgia, Greece, Jordan, Lebanon, India, Iran, Iraq, Israel, Moldova, North Macedonia, Oman, Serbia, Syria, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, Pakistan, Romania, Russia, Ukraine, United Arab Emirates and Yemen. Rosa centifolia, the cabbage rose, which is more commonly grown in Morocco, Egypt and France."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aromaticum Rosarum", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2702, "target_cluster_name": "Aromaticum Rosarum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a Latin name for a rose-based aromatic preparation, closely related to rose oil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This is a Latin name for a rose-based aromatic preparation, closely related to rose oil."}, {"found_name": "Rose Honey", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1653, "target_cluster_name": "mel rosado", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a preparation made with honey and rose, i