_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Herbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1018, "stable_key": "clu_46761665ab038f55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Herbert", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1778, "death_year": 1847, "description": "Botanist and author on plant hybridization and sexual systems.", "note": "Referenced for his work on plant hybridization and competition, aligning with botanical studies of the era."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "insecto", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "rodent", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rodent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rodent", "rodent"], "contexts": ["wider range than other rodents", "animal order with specific habits"]}, {"entity_id": "rodent", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rodent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["rodent"], "contexts": ["mammal with complex nervous system", "animal with even fewer lower brain centers"]}, {"entity_id": "insecto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "insecto", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["insecto"], "contexts": ["produces lacca on tree branches", "true generator of lacca"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rodent", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rodent", "similarity": 0.641}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rodent", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "insecto", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 1019, "stable_key": "clu_192c7288f298eb4b", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Rodents (from Latin rōdēns, 'gnawing') are a group of mammals characterized by a single pair of continuously growing incisors in each of the upper and lower jaws. About 40% of all mammal species belong to the order Rodentia ( roh-DEN-shə or roh-DEN-chə). They are native to all major land masses except for Antarctica, and several oceanic islands, though they have subsequently been introduced to most of these land masses by human activity. Rodents are extremely diverse in their ecology and lifestyles and can be found in almost every terrestrial habitat, including human-made environments. Species can be arboreal, fossorial (burrowing), saltatorial/ricochetal (leaping on their hind legs), or semiaquatic. However, all rodents share several morphological features, including having only a single upper and lower pair of ever-growing incisors."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "insects", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'insect' from Latin.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Direct translation of 'insect' from Latin.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["proposed law of compensation of growth", "on compensation of growth"]}, {"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["author who advised on enterprise", "author, significant in books"]}, {"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["established truth of organismal development"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1020, "stable_key": "clu_377298077e368be9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1749, "death_year": 1832, "description": "Poet, playwright, and natural philosopher who studied morphology.", "note": "Cited for his contributions to morphology and the concept of 'Urpflanze' (primordial plant), relating to organismal development.", "wikidata_id": "Q5879", "wikidata_description": "German writer, artist, natural scientist and politician (1749–1832)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann%20Wolfgang%20von%20Goethe", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe (28 August 1749 – 22 March 1832) was a German polymath who is widely regarded as the most influential writer in the German language. His work has had a wide-ranging influence on literary, political, and philosophical thought in the Western world from the late 18th century to the present. A poet, playwright, novelist, scientist, statesman, theatre-director, and critic, Goethe wrote a wide range of works, including plays, poetry and aesthetic criticism, as well as treatises on botany, anatomy, and colour. Goethe took up residence in Weimar in 1775 following the success of his first novel, The Sorrows of Young Werther (1774), and joined a thriving intellectual and cultural environment under the patronage of Duchess Anna Amalia that formed the basis of Weimar Classicism. He was ennobled by Karl August, Duke of Saxe-Weimar, in 1782. Goethe was an early participant in the Sturm und Drang literary movement."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Friedrich Schiller", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2815, "target_cluster_name": "Schiller", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent German literary figures of the same era, with Schiller being a contemporary and peer of Goethe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent German literary figures of the same era, with Schiller being a contemporary and peer of Goethe."}, {"found_name": "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2302, "target_cluster_name": "Hegel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hegel was a major philosopher whose work was deeply influenced by and engaged with the intellectual currents of Goethe's time and thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hegel was a major philosopher whose work was deeply influenced by and engaged with the intellectual currents of Goethe's time and thought."}, {"found_name": "Immanuel Kant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 643, "target_cluster_name": "Kant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kant was a foundational philosopher of the Enlightenment, a period and intellectual movement that significantly shaped Goethe's worldview and writings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kant was a foundational philosopher of the Enlightenment, a period and intellectual movement that significantly shaped Goethe's worldview and writings."}, {"found_name": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3452, "target_cluster_name": "Rousseau", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rousseau's ideas on nature, education, and sentiment were highly influential on Goethe and the Sturm und Drang movement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rousseau's ideas on nature, education, and sentiment were highly influential on Goethe and the Sturm und Drang movement."}, {"found_name": "William Shakespeare", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2808, "target_cluster_name": "Shakespeare", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Goethe deeply admired Shakespeare and considered him a major influence on his own literary development, often referring to him as a model.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Goethe deeply admired Shakespeare and considered him a major influence on his own literary development, often referring to him as a model."}]}, {"canonical_name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "optic_nerve", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "optic nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic nerve"], "contexts": ["nerve for vision, pigment-coated"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerves", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["optic nerves"], "contexts": ["nerves transmitting visual information", "nerves for vision"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerve-fibres", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optic nerve-fibres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic nerve-fibres"], "contexts": ["fibers in the eye for light perception"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerves", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["optic nerves"], "contexts": ["nerves transmitting visual information", "receives light vibrations to produce sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "optic nerve-fibres", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optic nerves", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optic nerve-fibres", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1021, "stable_key": "clu_d86dc082cf00838a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Optic Nerve", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q7098797", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optic%20nerve%20hypoplasia", "wikipedia_extract": "Optic nerve hypoplasia (ONH) is a medical condition arising from the underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of the optic nerve(s). This condition is the most common congenital optic nerve anomaly. The optic disc appears abnormally small because not all the optic nerve axons have developed properly. It is often associated with endocrinopathies (hormone deficiencies), developmental delay, and brain malformations. The optic nerve, responsible for transmitting visual signals from the retina to the brain, has approximately 1.2 million nerve fibers in the average person. In those diagnosed with ONH, however, there are noticeably fewer nerves."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Auditory nerve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3092, "target_cluster_name": "auditory nerve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are specific types of nerves within the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are specific types of nerves within the nervous system."}, {"found_name": "Motor neuron", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2581, "target_cluster_name": "motor nerve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are specific types of nerves within the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are specific types of nerves within the nervous system."}, {"found_name": "Nervous system", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1588, "target_cluster_name": "nervous system", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The optic nerve is a component of the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The optic nerve is a component of the nervous system."}, {"found_name": "Nervous tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The optic nerve is composed of nervous tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The optic nerve is composed of nervous tissue."}, {"found_name": "Cornea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1083, "target_cluster_name": "Cornea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The cornea is part of the optical system of the eye, working in conjunction with the optic nerve.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The cornea is part of the optical system of the eye, working in conjunction with the optic nerve.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nerve-tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific type of nerve tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A specific type of nerve tissue.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "motor nerve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2581, "target_cluster_name": "motor nerve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are types of nerves within the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are types of nerves within the nervous system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__smith", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. Smith", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mr. Smith"], "contexts": ["described ant slave-making behavior"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__adams", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mr. Adams"], "contexts": ["predicted planet simultaneously", "discovered a planet"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__adams", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Mr. Adams"], "contexts": ["computed Neptune's perturbation on Uranus", "computed Neptune's perturbation of Uranus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Smith", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 0.859}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Smith", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 0.859}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mr. Adams", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1022, "stable_key": "clu_bec505913f87c114", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Couch Adams", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1819, "death_year": 1892, "description": "Mathematician who predicted Neptune's existence", "note": "The context mentions predicting a planet and computing Neptune's perturbation, strongly suggesting John Couch Adams, though 'Mr. Adams' is generic. The mention of 'Mr. Smith' in the same cluster is likely a mistake or alternative reference in the source text and is ignored for the primary identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q20015", "wikidata_description": "English mathematician and astronomer (1819–1892)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Couch%20Adams", "wikipedia_extract": "John Couch Adams ( KOOTCH; 5 June 1819 – 21 January 1892) was a British mathematician and astronomer. He was born in Laneast, near Launceston, Cornwall, and died in Cambridge. His most famous achievement was predicting the existence and position of Neptune, using only mathematics. The calculations were made to explain discrepancies with Uranus's orbit and the laws of Kepler and Newton. At the same time, but unknown to each other, the same calculations were made by Urbain Le Verrier. Le Verrier sent his co-ordinates to Berlin Observatory astronomer Johann Gottfried Galle, who confirmed the existence of the planet on 23 September 1846, finding it within 1° of Le Verrier's predicted location."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "James Challis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3651, "target_cluster_name": "Challis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Challis was a colleague of Adams at the Cambridge Observatory and assisted Adams in his astronomical work, including the search for Neptune.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Challis was a colleague of Adams at the Cambridge Observatory and assisted Adams in his astronomical work, including the search for Neptune."}, {"found_name": "Johann Galle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3143, "target_cluster_name": "Galle, Dr.", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galle was the astronomer who, based on Adams's calculations, discovered Neptune.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galle was the astronomer who, based on Adams's calculations, discovered Neptune."}]}, {"canonical_name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["social"], "contexts": ["living in communities"]}, {"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["SOCIAL", "social"], "contexts": ["social constituents of the 'Me'", "division of empirical life of self"]}, {"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["social"], "contexts": ["related to human society and its organization", "relating to society and its organization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1023, "stable_key": "clu_f8967dca297c8fb6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Social", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Government", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2489, "target_cluster_name": "Government", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Social' relates to the organization and interaction of people within a society, and 'government' is a primary mechanism for structuring and managing these social interactions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "'Social' relates to the organization and interaction of people within a society, and 'government' is a primary mechanism for structuring and managing these social interactions."}, {"found_name": "Human nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 513, "target_cluster_name": "Human Nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Social' describes the inherent tendency of humans to form societies and interact, which is a fundamental aspect of 'human nature'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "'Social' describes the inherent tendency of humans to form societies and interact, which is a fundamental aspect of 'human nature'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ocean-currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "marine_currents", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "marine currents", 