in the Earth's fluid envelopes, one in the atmosphere and the other in the oceans, often interacting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are types of currents within the Earth's fluid envelopes, one in the atmosphere and the other in the oceans, often interacting."}]}, {"canonical_name": "southern hemisphere", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "northern_and_southern_hemispheres", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "northern and southern hemispheres", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["northern and southern hemispheres"], "contexts": ["divisions of Earth by equator"]}, {"entity_id": "western_hemisphere", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Western hemisphere", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Western hemisphere"], "contexts": ["geographic division"]}, {"entity_id": "southern_hemisphere", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "southern hemisphere", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["southern hemisphere"], "contexts": ["half of celestial sphere south of ecliptic", "half of the Earth south of the equator"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "northern and southern hemispheres", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Western hemisphere", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "northern and southern hemispheres", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "southern hemisphere", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Western hemisphere", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "southern hemisphere", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 1025, "stable_key": "clu_0e1cf682ca26b371", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Southern Hemisphere", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q41228", "wikidata_description": "part of Earth that lies south of the equator", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern%20Hemisphere", "wikipedia_extract": "The Southern Hemisphere is the half (hemisphere) of Earth that is south of the equator. It contains all or part of five continents (the whole of Antarctica, the whole of Australia, about 90% of South America, about one-third of Africa, and some islands off the continental mainland of Asia) and four oceans (the whole Southern Ocean, the majority of the Indian Ocean, the South Atlantic Ocean, and the South Pacific Ocean), as well as New Zealand and most of the Pacific Islands in Oceania. Its surface is 80.9% water, compared with 60.7% water in the Northern Hemisphere, and it contains 32.7% of Earth's land. More than 850 million people live in the Southern Hemisphere, representing around 10–12% of the total global human population. Owing to the tilt of Earth's rotation relative to the Sun and the ecliptic plane, summer is from December to February (inclusive) and winter is from June to August (inclusive). September 22 or 23 is the vernal equinox and March 20 or 21 is the autumnal equinox."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Southern Hemisphere is the direct geographical opposite of the Arctic region, representing the two major divisions of the Earth's surface based on latitude.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Southern Hemisphere is the direct geographical opposite of the Arctic region, representing the two major divisions of the Earth's surface based on latitude."}, {"found_name": "Equator", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 61, "target_cluster_name": "equator", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Equator is the dividing line that defines the Southern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Equator is the dividing line that defines the Southern Hemisphere."}, {"found_name": "Earth's poles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 804, "target_cluster_name": "South pole", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Southern Hemisphere encompasses the South Pole, a key geographical point.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Southern Hemisphere encompasses the South Pole, a key geographical point."}, {"found_name": "Australia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 75, "target_cluster_name": "Australia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Australia is entirely located within the Southern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Australia is entirely located within the Southern Hemisphere."}, {"found_name": "Antarctic region", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3397, "target_cluster_name": "antarctic regions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Antarctic region is a significant part of the Southern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Antarctic region is a significant part of the Southern Hemisphere."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Richardson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__richard", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "M. Richard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Richard"], "contexts": ["sagaciously saw genus retention"]}, {"entity_id": "richard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Richard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Richard"], "contexts": ["author of manuscripts"]}, {"entity_id": "richardson", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Richardson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Richardson"], "contexts": ["observed clouds near American north pole", "observed Aurora near the north pole"]}, {"entity_id": "richard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Richard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Richard"], "contexts": ["botanist who published memoir"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "M. Richard", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Richard", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "M. Richard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Richard", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Richard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Richardson", "similarity": 0.834}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Richard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Richard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1026, "stable_key": "clu_c12e210cba61bc73", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sir John Richardson", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1787, "death_year": 1865, "description": "Arctic explorer and naturalist, observed clouds.", "note": "Multiple 'Richardson' figures could fit the context of arctic exploration and observation. Sir John Richardson is a strong candidate given the historical context of Darwin and Humboldt's writings. The reference to 'clouds near American north pole' strongly suggests him.", "wikidata_id": "Q545944", "wikidata_description": "Scottish naval surgeon, naturalist and arctic explorer (1787–1865)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Richardson%20%28naturalist%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir John Richardson FRS FRSE (5 November 1787 – 5 June 1865) was a Scottish naval surgeon, naturalist and Arctic explorer. Richardson was born at Nith Place in Dumfries the son of Gabriel Richardson, Provost of Dumfries, and his wife, Anne Mundell. He was educated at Dumfries Grammar School. He was then apprenticed to his maternal uncle, Dr James Mundell, a surgeon in Dumfries. Richardson studied medicine at Edinburgh University, and became a surgeon in the navy in 1807. He traveled with John Franklin in search of the Northwest Passage on the Coppermine Expedition of 1819–1822."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "James Clark Ross", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2614, "target_cluster_name": "Sir James Ross", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sir John Richardson was a surgeon and naturalist on James Clark Ross's Arctic expedition (1848-1849). Ross was also a prominent Arctic explorer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sir John Richardson was a surgeon and naturalist on James Clark Ross's Arctic expedition (1848-1849). Ross was also a prominent Arctic explorer."}, {"found_name": "Edward Sabine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1616, "target_cluster_name": "Sabine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Richardson and Sabine were involved in Arctic exploration and scientific endeavors, with Sabine being a key figure in the Royal Society during Richardson's career.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Richardson and Sabine were involved in Arctic exploration and scientific endeavors, with Sabine being a key figure in the Royal Society during Richardson's career."}, {"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Humboldt was a highly influential naturalist and explorer whose work inspired many scientists, including Richardson, who was also a naturalist and explorer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Humboldt was a highly influential naturalist and explorer whose work inspired many scientists, including Richardson, who was also a naturalist and explorer."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "saint-thomas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Saint-Thomas"], "contexts": ["Barbade, Martinique and Saint-Thomas", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "st__thomas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["St. Thomas"], "contexts": ["island near a magnetic equator node", "island on the western coast of Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "st__thomas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "St. Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Thomas"], "contexts": ["island experiencing hurricanes"]}, {"entity_id": "île_saint-thomas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "île Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["île Saint-Thomas"], "contexts": ["island in Gulf of Guinea"]}, {"entity_id": "island_of_st__thomas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Island of St. Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Island of St. Thomas"], "contexts": ["location of observed hurricane event"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saint-Thomas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Thomas", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saint-Thomas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "St. Thomas", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "St. Thomas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "St. Thomas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1027, "stable_key": "clu_eb4584dafa02ea9a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint Thomas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States Virgin Islands", "note": "Multiple references to 'Saint-Thomas' and 'St. Thomas' as an island, particularly in the context of Humboldt's works and its location near the magnetic equator, strongly indicate the Caribbean island.", "wikidata_id": "Q20851010", "wikidata_description": "census district of the United States Virgin Islands, includes the Island of Saint Thomas", "wikipedia_extract": "Thomas the Apostle also known as Didymus (Greek: Δίδυμος, romanized: Dídymos 'twin'), was one of the Twelve Apostles of Jesus according to the New Testament. Thomas is commonly known as \"doubting Thomas\" because he initially doubted the resurrection of Jesus when he was told of it (as is related in the Gospel of John); he later confessed his faith (\"The lord of me and the God of me\") on seeing the places where the wounds appeared still fresh on the holy body of Jesus after the Crucifixion of Jesus. According to traditional accounts of the Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala, Thomas travelled outside the Roman Empire to preach the Gospel, travelling through southern India in the modern states of Kerala and Tamil Nadu, and eventually reached Muziris (modern-day North Paravur and Kodungalloor) in 52 AD. He started the Church of the East in the region around the Van Province, Edessa, and Hakkari regions of Upper Mesopotamia (modern-day southeastern Turkey), presumably sometime between mid-late 30s and 45, and spent many years evangelizing both Jews and non-christians in the area before heading further east in the late 40s. In 1258, some of the relics were brought to Ortona, in Abruzzo, Italy, where they have been held in the Church of Saint Thomas the Apostle. He is regarded as the patron saint of India among its Christian adherents, and the Feast of Saint Thomas on July 3 is celebrated as Indian Christians' Day.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_the_Apostle"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Kamtschatka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "kamtschatka", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Kamtschatka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Kamtschatka", "Kamlschatka"], "contexts": ["region in Siberia", "peninsula"]}, {"entity_id": "kamtschatka", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kamtschatka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kamtschatka"], "contexts": ["peninsula with mountains", "peninsula in Russia"]}, {"entity_id": "kamt-schatka", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kamt-schatka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kamt-schatka"], "contexts": ["peninsula east of magnetic declination line"]}, {"entity_id": "kamtschatka", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kamtschatka", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kamtschatka"], "contexts": ["peninsula in Russia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kamtschatka", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kamtschatka", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kamtschatka", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kamt-schatka", "similarity": 0.581}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kamtschatka", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kamtschatka", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Kamtschatka", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kamtschatka", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Kamt-schatka", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kamtschatka", "similarity": 0.581}], "id": 1028, "stable_key": "clu_9406b2da5822b310", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kamchatka Peninsula", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q993", "wikidata_description": "peninsula in Eastern Russia between the Pacific Ocean and the Sea of Okhotsk", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamchatka%20Peninsula", "wikipedia_extract": "The Kamchatka Peninsula (Russian: полуо́стров Камча́тка, romanized: poluóstrov Kamchátka, pronounced [pəlʊˈostrəf kɐmˈt͡ɕætkə], Ainu: Cupka) is a 1,250-kilometre-long (777 mi) peninsula in the Russian Far East, with an area of about 270,000 km2 (100,000 sq mi). The Sea of Okhotsk bounds the peninsula's western coastline, immediately offshore of the peninsula and below the Bering Sea runs the 9,600-metre-deep (31,496 ft) Kuril–Kamchatka Trench. Its eastern coastline is the Bering Sea, part of the Pacific Ocean. The Kamchatka Peninsula, the Commander Islands, and Karaginsky Island constitute Kamchatka Krai of the Russian Federation. The majority of the 322,079 inhabitants are ethnic Russians, with about 13,000 being Koryaks (2014). More than half of the population lives in Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky (179,526 in 2010) and nearby Yelizovo (38,980)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Péninsule", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2116, "target_cluster_name": "Péninsule", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kamchatka Peninsula is a specific example of a peninsula, fitting the definition of the entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kamchatka Peninsula is a specific example of a peninsula, fitting the definition of the entity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "ammoniaque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "ammoniaque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ammoniaque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["ammoniaque", "ammonite"], "contexts": ["substance that does not precipitate plant sap", "ammonia"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_of_ammonia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "carbonate of ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["carbonate of ammonia"], "contexts": ["atmospheric compound"]}, {"entity_id": "ammoniacal_compounds", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ammoniacal compounds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ammoniacal compounds"], "contexts": ["compounds containing ammonia"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ammonia"], "contexts": ["chemical for photographic spectrum", "compound in atmosphere consumed by plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "carbonate of ammonia", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ammoniacal compounds", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ammonia", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "carbonate of ammonia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ammonia", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1029, "stable_key": "clu_e23eaef60284a52b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ammonia", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q4087", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound, NH₃", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonia", "wikipedia_extract": "Ammonia is an inorganic chemical compound of nitrogen and hydrogen with the formula NH3. A stable binary hydride and the simplest pnictogen hydride, ammonia is a colourless gas with a distinctive pungent smell. It is widely used in fertilizers, refrigerants, explosives, cleaning agents, and is a precursor for numerous chemicals. Biologically, it is a common nitrogenous waste, and it contributes significantly to the nutritional needs of terrestrial organisms by serving as a precursor to fertilisers. Around 70% of ammonia produced industrially is used to make fertilisers in various forms and composition, such as urea and diammonium phosphate. Ammonia in pure form is also applied directly into the soil."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fundament", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 753, "target_cluster_name": "Fundament", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonia is a common chemical base discussed in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammonia is a common chemical base discussed in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonia is a gas, and historically, gases were often discussed in relation to 'air' as a general concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonia is a gas, and historically, gases were often discussed in relation to 'air' as a general concept."}, {"found_name": "Alkali", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 863, "target_cluster_name": "alkalies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonia is a base, and historically, bases were often grouped under the term 'alkali'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonia is a base, and historically, bases were often grouped under the term 'alkali'."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous substances", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2321, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonia is a gas, and 'gaseous substances' is a broader category that includes ammonia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ammonia is a gas, and 'gaseous substances' is a broader category that includes ammonia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Danemark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "danemark", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Danemark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Danemark", "Danemarck"], "contexts": ["country with sugar consumption", "maritime power"]}, {"entity_id": "danish", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Danish", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Danish"], "contexts": ["language and origin of 'gaard'"]}, {"entity_id": "denmark", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Denmark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Denmark"], "contexts": ["country with opposite coasts", "European country mentioned in relation to tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Danemark", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Danish", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Danemark", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Denmark", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Danish", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Denmark", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 1030, "stable_key": "clu_580026bfe334a8f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Denmark", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A Scandinavian country, known for maritime activity and sugar consumption.", "wikipedia_extract": "Denmark is a Nordic country in Northern Europe. It is the metropole and most populous constituent of the Kingdom of Denmark, also known as the Danish Realm, a constitutionally unitary state that includes the autonomous territories of the Faroe Islands and Greenland in the north Atlantic Ocean. Metropolitan Denmark, also called \"continental Denmark\" or \"Denmark proper\", consists of the northern Jutland peninsula and an archipelago of 406 islands. It is the southernmost of the Scandinavian countries, lying southwest of Sweden, south of Norway, and north of Germany, with which it shares a short border. Denmark proper is situated between the North Sea to the west and the Baltic Sea to the east. The Kingdom of Denmark, including the Faroe Islands and Greenland, has roughly 1,400 islands greater than 100 square metres (1,100 sq ft) in area; 443 have been named and 78 are inhabited."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Greenland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 316, "target_cluster_name": "Greenland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Scandinave", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 810, "target_cluster_name": "Scandinave", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Denmark is a core constituent country of Scandinavia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Denmark is a core constituent country of Scandinavia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Faroe Islands", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3983, "target_cluster_name": "Faroe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Faroe Islands are a constituent country within the Kingdom of Denmark.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Faroe Islands are a constituent country within the Kingdom of Denmark."}, {"found_name": "malde Loanda", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "centuria 2.mihi fol. 38, \" cabeça ſe enleão ,& embaração de maneyra , que ,, 1.63.ibi: Rane mute in Seriphos:muta nãoſe podem deſembaraçar ; os de Dinamarca , Flandres , & Alemanha ,, etiamcicadesfunt", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for scurvy"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Prusse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "prusse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Prusse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Prusse"], "contexts": ["country with ports", "country mentioned with population data"]}, {"entity_id": "prussia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Prussia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Prussia"], "contexts": ["region of observation", "nation where Minden is located"]}, {"entity_id": "prussia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Prussia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Prussia"], "contexts": ["location mentioned for a phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prusse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prusse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Prussia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1031, "stable_key": "clu_b9e413f82b87c5eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Prussia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region and former German state, known for its coast.", "wikipedia_extract": "Prussia (; German: Preußen [ˈpʁɔʏsn̩] ; Old Prussian: Prūsija) was a German state centred on the North European Plain that originated from the 1525 secularization act of the Prussian part of the State of the Teutonic Order. For centuries, the House of Hohenzollern ruled Prussia, expanding its size with the Prussian Army. Prussia, with its capital at Königsberg and then, when it became the Kingdom of Prussia in 1701, Berlin, decisively shaped the history of Germany. Prussia formed the German Empire when it united the German states in 1871. It was de facto dissolved by an emergency decree transferring powers of the Prussian government to German Chancellor Franz von Papen in 1932 and de jure by an Allied decree in 1947. The name Prussia derives from the Old Prussians who were conquered by the Teutonic Knights – an organized Catholic medieval military order of German crusaders –  in the 13th century."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "degré", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "degré", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "degré", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["degré", "degré équatorial"], "contexts": ["unit of angular measurement", "measurement related to Earth's equator"]}, {"entity_id": "degrees", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "degrees", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["degrees"], "contexts": ["unit of angular measurement", "unit of angular measure"]}, {"entity_id": "degree", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "degree", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["degree"], "contexts": ["unit of angular measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "degree", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "degree", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["degree"], "contexts": ["level or extent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "degré", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "degrees", "similarity": 0.846}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "degrees", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "degree", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "degree", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "degree", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1032, "stable_key": "clu_c4bad17dc9f94a26", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Degree (angle)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q57571141", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 21 June 2007"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Longitude", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 310, "target_cluster_name": "Longitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Longitude is an angular measurement, typically expressed in degrees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Longitude is an angular measurement, typically expressed in degrees.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Circle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 334, "target_cluster_name": "circle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A circle is a fundamental geometric shape defined by points equidistant from a center, and its properties are measured using degrees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A circle is a fundamental geometric shape defined by points equidistant from a center, and its properties are measured using degrees."}, {"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and its position and motion are described using celestial coordinates that involve degrees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and its position and motion are described using celestial coordinates that involve degrees."}, {"found_name": "Geometry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2912, "target_cluster_name": "Geometry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geometry is the branch of mathematics concerned with the properties and relations of points, lines, surfaces, solids, and higher dimensional analogues. Degrees are a fundamental unit of angular measurement within geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geometry is the branch of mathematics concerned with the properties and relations of points, lines, surfaces, solids, and higher dimensional analogues. Degrees are a fundamental unit of angular measurement within geometry."}, {"found_name": "Variables of seamen", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "trade-wind, and that from the S. the S.E. trade-wind. Now these winds being in contrary directions cross at the equator, balance each other through about 6 degrees of latitude, and produce a belt of c", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for calms of the equator"}, {"found_name": "Canton’s phosphorus", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "it. Sulphuret of calcium, known as Canton’s phosphorus, and the sulphuret of barium, or Bologna stone, possess the phosphorescent property in an eminent degree. Multitudes of fish are endowed with the", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "known as"}]}, {"canonical_name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["phosphorescence", "phosphorescence de la mer"], "contexts": ["property of the insects", "emission of light"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Phosphorescence", "phosphorescence"], "contexts": ["sea phenomenon", "emission of light by sea"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Phosphorescence", "phosphorescence"], "contexts": ["light emission independent of external influence", "emission of light after excitation"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescent", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "phosphorescent", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["phosphorescent"], "contexts": ["emitting light"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescent", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "phosphorescent", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["phosphorescent"], "contexts": ["emitting light after exposure to sun", "phenomena of light emission"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1033, "stable_key": "clu_b710e5de3d14943a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "phosphorescence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q192275", "wikidata_description": "process in which energy absorbed by a substance is released relatively slowly in the form of light", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorescence", "wikipedia_extract": "Phosphorescence is a type of photoluminescence related to fluorescence. When exposed to light (radiation) of a shorter threshold wavelength, a phosphorescent substance will glow, absorbing the light and reemitting it at a longer wavelength. Unlike fluorescence, a phosphorescent material does not immediately reemit the radiation it absorbs. Instead, a phosphorescent material absorbs some of the radiation energy and reemits it for a much longer time after the radiation source is removed. In a general sense, there is no distinct boundary between the emission times of fluorescence and phosphorescence (i.e.: if a substance glows under a black light it is generally considered fluorescent, and if it glows in the dark it is often simply called phosphorescent). In a modern, scientific sense, the phenomena can usually be classified by the three different mechanisms that produce the light and the typical timescales at which they emit light: fluorescence, triplet phosphorescence, and persistent phosphorescence."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Incandescence", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1185, "target_cluster_name": "incandescence", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Incandescence is a related phenomenon where light is produced by heat, whereas phosphorescence is light produced by absorption and re-emission of radiation, not necessarily heat-driven.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Incandescence is a related phenomenon where light is produced by heat, whereas phosphorescence is light produced by absorption and re-emission of radiation, not necessarily heat-driven."}, {"found_name": "Canton’s phosphorus", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "other. Among the bodies which generally become phosphorescent when exposed to heat, there are some specimens which do not possess this property; wherefore phosphorescence cannot be regarded as an esse", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "known as"}]}, {"canonical_name": "rock crystal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "cristal_de_roche", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cristal de roche", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["cristal de roche"], "contexts": ["Rock crystal, a form of quartz", "substance disseminated in limestone"]}, {"entity_id": "rock_crystal", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "rock crystal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["rock crystal"], "contexts": ["mineral studied with polarized light", "transparent variety of quartz"]}, {"entity_id": "crystal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crystal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystal"], "contexts": ["inorganic solid with ordered structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cristal de roche", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "rock crystal", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "rock crystal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystal", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 1034, "stable_key": "clu_038ee25a0b9c6178", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rock crystal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q2050687", 