al astronomy and geodesy, fields related to Fraunhofer's work in optics and spectroscopy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Grana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "grain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "grain", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["grain", "grains"], "contexts": ["measure of agricultural yield", "small particles"]}, {"entity_id": "grana", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Grana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Grana", "grano"], "contexts": ["dye material brought from the Indies", "seed-like object found in fake stones"]}, {"entity_id": "grana", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Grana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["grana", "Grana"], "contexts": ["dyeing substance, also called Cocco", "cochineal, a red dye"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "grain", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Grana", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "grain", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Grana", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Grana", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Grana", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1036, "stable_key": "clu_f98da2b1345e8412", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "grain", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "A grain is a small, hard, dry fruit (caryopsis) – with or without an attached hull layer – harvested for human or animal consumption. A grain crop is a grain-producing plant. The two main types of commercial grain crops are cereals and legumes. After being harvested, dry grains are more durable than other staple foods, such as starchy fruits (plantains, breadfruit, etc.) and tubers (sweet potatoes, cassava, and more). This durability has made grains well suited to industrial agriculture, since they can be mechanically harvested, transported by rail or ship, stored for long periods in silos, and milled for flour or pressed for oil. Thus, the grain market is a major global agricultural market that includes crops such as maize, rice, soybeans, wheat and other grains.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grain"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "food", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 615, "target_cluster_name": "alimentos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grain is a primary type of food for humans and animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Grain is a primary type of food for humans and animals."}, {"found_name": "broth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1505, "target_cluster_name": "caldo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Broth can be made from grains or used to cook grains.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Broth can be made from grains or used to cook grains."}, {"found_name": "meat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 219, "target_cluster_name": "carne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grains are often consumed alongside meat as part of a meal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Grains are often consumed alongside meat as part of a meal."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sir W. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__hamilton", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["M. Hamilton"], "contexts": ["gave an evaluation of the peninsula", "considered independent states"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_w__hamilton", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Sir W. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sir W. Hamilton"], "contexts": ["philosopher, critic of Cartesianism", "philosopher cited for lectures on metaphysics"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_wm_hamilton", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sir Wm Hamilton"], "contexts": ["philosopher discussing consciousness and the Absolute"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Hamilton", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Sir W. Hamilton", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Hamilton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "similarity": 0.893}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Sir W. Hamilton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 1037, "stable_key": "clu_ee6f0134c7803bbf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sir William Hamilton", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1856, "description": "Scottish idealist philosopher and critic of knowledge"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["university city", "city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["city used for longitude determination", "city of simultaneous observations"]}, {"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["location of M. Kupffer's observations", "summer and winter mean temperature compared"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 1038, "stable_key": "clu_2e3636009c061d3f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kazan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "The mentions of 'Kasan' as a city used for longitude determination and a university city, situated in European Russia, strongly point to Kazan.", "wikidata_id": "Q900", "wikidata_description": "city in the Republic of Tatarstan, Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazan", "wikipedia_extract": "Kazan is the largest city and capital of Tatarstan, Russia. The city lies at the confluence of the Volga and the Kazanka rivers, covering an area of 425.3 square kilometres (164.2 square miles), with a population of over 1.3 million residents, and up to nearly 2 million residents in the greater metropolitan area. Kazan is the fifth-largest city in Russia, being the most populous city on the Volga and within the Volga Federal District. Historically, Kazan was the capital of the Khanate of Kazan, and was conquered by Ivan the Terrible in the 16th century, at which point the city became a part of the Tsardom of Russia. The city was seized (and largely destroyed) during Pugachev's Rebellion (1773–1775), but was later rebuilt during the reign of Catherine the Great. In the following centuries, Kazan grew to become a major industrial, cultural and religious centre of Russia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astrakhan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2753, "target_cluster_name": "Astrakhan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrakhan and Kazan were both important cities in the Golden Horde and later in the Russian Empire, often serving as administrative and trade centers in the Volga region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Astrakhan and Kazan were both important cities in the Golden Horde and later in the Russian Empire, often serving as administrative and trade centers in the Volga region."}, {"found_name": "Barnaul", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2798, "target_cluster_name": "Barnaul", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Barnaul and Kazan are both major cities located on rivers in Russia, serving as regional centers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Barnaul and Kazan are both major cities located on rivers in Russia, serving as regional centers."}, {"found_name": "China", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 41, "target_cluster_name": "China", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Kazan and China represent large geographical and political entities with historical significance in trade and cultural exchange, particularly along routes like the Silk Road.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Kazan and China represent large geographical and political entities with historical significance in trade and cultural exchange, particularly along routes like the Silk Road."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kazan is a city in Russia, which is a transcontinental country bridging Europe and Asia. Therefore, it is geographically and historically related to Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kazan is a city in Russia, which is a transcontinental country bridging Europe and Asia. Therefore, it is geographically and historically related to Europe."}, {"found_name": "Great Lakes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1350, "target_cluster_name": "lakes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kazan is situated at the confluence of major rivers, similar to how the Great Lakes are a significant hydrological feature. This relates to geographical descriptions of large water bodies and their importance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kazan is situated at the confluence of major rivers, similar to how the Great Lakes are a significant hydrological feature. This relates to geographical descriptions of large water bodies and their importance."}, {"found_name": "Russia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 146, "target_cluster_name": "Russia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kazan is the capital of Tatarstan, a federal subject of Russia. Therefore, it is directly related to Russia as a major city within the country.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kazan is the capital of Tatarstan, a federal subject of Russia. Therefore, it is directly related to Russia as a major city within the country."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Newfoundl