ic sparks as a byproduct of their operation."}, {"found_name": "Electrical conductor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3230, "target_cluster_name": "conducting wire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrical conductors are necessary for the controlled passage of electricity that can lead to an electric spark.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrical conductors are necessary for the controlled passage of electricity that can lead to an electric spark."}, {"found_name": "Electrode", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3485, "target_cluster_name": "Plates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrodes are often the points between which an electric spark occurs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrodes are often the points between which an electric spark occurs."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Orion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "orion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Orion", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orion"], "contexts": ["constellation perceived by consciousness"]}, {"entity_id": "orion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Orion", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Orion"], "contexts": ["nebula and constellation", "nebulous spot in the sky"]}, {"entity_id": "ε_orionis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ε Orionis", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["ε Orionis"], "contexts": ["star in Orion constellation"]}, {"entity_id": "θ_orionis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "θ Orionis", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["θ Orionis"], "contexts": ["multiple star system in our hemisphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Orion", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Orion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Orion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ε Orionis", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Orion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "θ Orionis", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Orion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ε Orionis", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Orion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "θ Orionis", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 1046, "stable_key": "clu_3b25a2bd1ffcaf16", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orion (constellation)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "References to 'Orion', 'ε Orionis', 'θ Orionis', and 'Epsilon Orionis' in the context of constellations and nebulae clearly identify the constellation Orion. While Orion is also a mythological figure, the astronomical references are dominant here.", "wikipedia_extract": "Orion is a prominent set of stars visible during winter in the northern celestial hemisphere. It is one of the 88 modern constellations; it was among the 48 constellations listed by the 2nd-century AD/CE astronomer Ptolemy. It is named after a hunter in Greek mythology. Orion is most prominent during winter evenings in the Northern Hemisphere, as are five other constellations that have stars in the Winter Hexagon asterism. Orion's two brightest stars, Rigel (β) and Betelgeuse (α), are both among the brightest stars in the night sky; both are supergiants and slightly variable. There are a further six stars brighter than magnitude 3.0, including three making the short straight line of the Orion's Belt asterism."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Orion is a prominent constellation, a subject of study within astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Orion is a prominent constellation, a subject of study within astronomy."}, {"found_name": "Celestial body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3227, "target_cluster_name": "celestial body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Orion is a constellation, composed of celestial bodies (stars).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Orion is a constellation, composed of celestial bodies (stars)."}, {"found_name": "Aldebaran", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3027, "target_cluster_name": "Aldebaran", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aldebaran is a star located in the constellation Orion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aldebaran is a star located in the constellation Orion."}, {"found_name": "Aries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 787, "target_cluster_name": "Aries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aries is another constellation, often discussed alongside other constellations like Orion in astronomical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aries is another constellation, often discussed alongside other constellations like Orion in astronomical contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Konigsberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "königsberg", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Königsberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Königsberg"], "contexts": ["academicism of Kant's existence"]}, {"entity_id": "konigsberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Konigsberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Konigsberg"], "contexts": ["city where Kant published his essay", "city where essay was published"]}, {"entity_id": "königsberg", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Königsberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Königsberg"], "contexts": ["location of Professor Moser's work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Königsberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Konigsberg", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Königsberg", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Königsberg", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Konigsberg", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Königsberg", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 1047, "stable_key": "clu_7995762c9b4e9b9f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Königsberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city known for its academic importance and barometric observations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Königsberg (, German: [ˈkøːnɪçsbɛʁk]  or [ˈkøːnɪksbɛʁk] ) was the historic German name of the city that is now modern-day Kaliningrad, Russia. The city was founded in 1255 on the site of the small Old Prussian settlement Twangste by the Teutonic Knights during the Baltic Crusades. It was named in honour of King Ottokar II of Bohemia, who led a campaign against the pagan Old Prussians, a Baltic tribe that was native to the area. A Baltic port city, it successively became the capital of the Królewiec Voivodeship, State of the Teutonic Order, the Duchy of Prussia and the provinces of East Prussia and Prussia. Königsberg remained the coronation city of the Prussian monarchy from 1701 onwards, though the capital was Berlin. From the thirteenth century, it was inhabited by Germans and Old Prussians, then also by Poles, Lithuanians and French from the 14th, 15th and 17th centuries, respectively."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "precession_of_the_equinoxes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "precession of the equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["precession of the equinoxes"], "contexts": ["astronomical phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "precession_of_the_equinoxes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "precession of the equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["precession of the equinoxes"], "contexts": ["gradual shift in Earth's axial orientation", "wobble of Earth's axis"]}, {"entity_id": "precession_of_the_equinoxes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "precession of the equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["precession of the equinoxes"], "contexts": ["slow shift in Earth's axial orientation", "slow shift of Earth's axis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "precession of the equinoxes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1048, "stable_key": "clu_c0adf5a2b8a79e9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Precession of the equinoxes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q83094", "wikidata_description": "gravity-induced, slow, and continuous change in the orientation of an astronomical body's rotational axis", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axial%20precession", "wikipedia_extract": "In astronomy, axial precession is a gravity-induced, slow, and continuous change in the orientation of an astronomical body's rotational axis. In the absence of precession, the astronomical body's orbit would show axial parallelism. In particular, Earth's axial precession is the gradual shift in the orientation of Earth's axis of rotation. It has a cycle of approximately 26,000 years, tracing out a double cone with a half-aperture of about 23.4°, an angle known as the obliquity of the ecliptic. This is similar to the precession of a spinning top. The term \"precession\" typically refers only to this largest part of the motion; other changes in the alignment of Earth's axis—nutation and polar motion—are much smaller in magnitude."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Precession of the equinoxes is a fundamental concept within the field of astronomy, describing a slow change in the orientation of a celestial body's rotational axis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Precession of the equinoxes is a fundamental concept within the field of astronomy, describing a slow change in the orientation of a celestial body's rotational axis."}, {"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The precession of the equinoxes describes the slow shift of the equinoxes along the ecliptic, the apparent path of the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The precession of the equinoxes describes the slow shift of the equinoxes along the ecliptic, the apparent path of the Sun."}, {"found_name": "Earth's rotation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3632, "target_cluster_name": "Earth's rotation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Axial precession is a change in the orientation of an astronomical body's rotational axis, directly related to Earth's rotation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Axial precession is a change in the orientation of an astronomical body's rotational axis, directly related to Earth's rotation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "virtudes magneticas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetic_property", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic property", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic property"], "contexts": ["property of magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_properties", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic properties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic properties"], "contexts": ["characteristics of magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "virtudes_magneticas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "virtudes magneticas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["virtudes magneticas"], "contexts": ["powers of magnets", "magn