 "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galvanism, the study of electricity produced by chemical reactions, is historically linked to the understanding of magnetism and electromagnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galvanism, the study of electricity produced by chemical reactions, is historically linked to the understanding of magnetism and electromagnetism."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ritter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ritter's", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ritter's", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ritter's"], "contexts": ["author of geography work"]}, {"entity_id": "ritter", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Ritter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Ritter"], "contexts": ["discoverer of photographic rays", "discoverer of chemical rays"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ritter's", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ritter", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 1050, "stable_key": "clu_9a10f7686f4cefa4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Wilhelm Ritter", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1776, "death_year": 1810, "description": "German physicist known for early work on ultraviolet light.", "note": "The context clues like \"discoverer of photographic rays\" strongly point to J.W. Ritter, a German chemist and physicist.", "wikidata_id": "Q58868", "wikidata_description": "German chemist, physicist and philosopher"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hans Christian Ørsted", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2447, "target_cluster_name": "Oersted", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicists in the early 19th century, with Ritter being a pioneer in photochemistry and Ørsted discovering electromagnetism. They were contemporaries and likely aware of each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent physicists in the early 19th century, with Ritter being a pioneer in photochemistry and Ørsted discovering electromagnetism. They were contemporaries and likely aware of each other's work."}, {"found_name": "Alessandro Volta", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 966, "target_cluster_name": "Volta", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Volta's invention of the voltaic pile was a foundational development in electricity, a field that Ritter also explored, particularly in relation to chemical effects and light.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Volta's invention of the voltaic pile was a foundational development in electricity, a field that Ritter also explored, particularly in relation to chemical effects and light."}, {"found_name": "André-Marie Ampère", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2134, "target_cluster_name": "Ampère", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ampère's work on the mathematical theory of electromagnetism built upon the discoveries of scientists like Ørsted and was part of the broader electrical science that Ritter was investigating.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ampère's work on the mathematical theory of electromagnetism built upon the discoveries of scientists like Ørsted and was part of the broader electrical science that Ritter was investigating."}, {"found_name": "August Wilhelm Hoffmann", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2416, "target_cluster_name": "Hoffmann", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were German chemists and scientists. Hoffmann was a leading figure in organic chemistry in the mid-to-late 19th century, a period following Ritter's foundational work in electrochemistry and photochemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were German chemists and scientists. Hoffmann was a leading figure in organic chemistry in the mid-to-late 19th century, a period following Ritter's foundational work in electrochemistry and photochemistry."}]}, {"canonical_name": "limoens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "limones", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Limones", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Limones"], "contexts": ["fragrance reminiscent of lemons"]}, {"entity_id": "limões", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "limões", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["limões"], "contexts": ["fruit from the region", "commodity sold with betel"]}, {"entity_id": "limas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "limas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["limas"], "contexts": ["fruit from the region"]}, {"entity_id": "limoens", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "limoens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["limoens", "limas doces", "limoens doces"], "contexts": ["lemons, used in preparation", "sweet lemons"]}, {"entity_id": "lemon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lemon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lemon"], "contexts": ["fruit with sour taste"]}, {"entity_id": "limão", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "limão", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["limão"], "contexts": ["fruit, lemon", "used with nimbo leaves for wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "lemon", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "lemon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lemon"], "contexts": ["fruit used for juice in preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "lemon", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lemon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lemon"], "contexts": ["essential oil with circular polarization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Limones", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "limões", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Limones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "limoens", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "limões", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "limoens", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "limas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "limoens", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 1051, "stable_key": "clu_5d64023fd8c9fced", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Citrus limon", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Citrus limon L.", "family": "Rutaceae", "note": "The terms 'limoens', 'limões', and 'limas' all refer to citrus fruits, primarily lemons and limes. 'Limas' can also specifically refer to limes, but in the context of early modern texts and often alongside 'limões', it is a reasonable inclusion under the broader umbrella of citrus commonly known as lemons and limes.", "wikidata_id": "Q500", "wikidata_description": "nothospecies of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon", "wikipedia_extract": "The lemon (Citrus × limon) is a species of small evergreen tree in the Citrus genus of the flowering plant family Rutaceae. A true lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange. Its origins are uncertain, but some evidence suggests lemons originated during the 1st millennium BC in what is now northeastern India. Some other citrus fruits are called lemon. The yellow fruit of the lemon tree is used throughout the world, primarily for its juice. The pulp and rind are used in cooking and baking."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lemon balm", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2018, "target_cluster_name": "cidra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is sometimes referred to as 'citron' in older texts, and while distinct from the lemon fruit, it shares a common etymological root and is a related herb.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is sometimes referred to as 'citron' in older texts, and while distinct from the lemon fruit, it shares a common etymological root and is a related herb."}, {"found_name": "Orange tree", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3926, "target_cluster_name": "Orange", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange, making the orange tree a close relative.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange, making the orange tree a close relative."}, {"found_name": "limon", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1314, "target_cluster_name": "limon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'lime' is also referred to as 'limon' in historical texts, which is a direct cognate and often used interchangeably with 'lemon' in historical contexts, especially when referring to the fruit's name in different Romance languages or dialects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'lime' is also referred to as 'limon' in historical texts, which is a direct cognate and often used interchangeably with 'lemon' in historical contexts, especially when referring to the fruit's name in different Romance languages or dialects.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Citrus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2465, "target_cluster_name": "citrinos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Citrus limon is a species within the Citrus genus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Citrus limon is a species within the Citrus genus."}, {"found_name": "laranja", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1733, "target_cluster_name": "laranja", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon is a closely related citrus fruit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lemon is a closely related citrus fruit.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "citrinos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2465, "target_cluster_name": "citrinos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Citrus limon is a specific species within the Citrus genus, representing a key example of a citrus fruit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Citrus limon is a specific species within the Citrus genus, representing a key example of a citrus fruit.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "music", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "muzica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Muzica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Muzica"], "contexts": ["art not necessary for healing"]}, {"entity_id": "music", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Music", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Music", "music"], "contexts": ["theory of musical sounds", "sympathetic notes, sound undulations"]}, {"entity_id": "music", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "music", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["music", "Music"], "contexts": ["art form originating from rhythm in sound", "art form"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Muzica", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Music", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Muzica", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "music", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Music", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "music", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 1052, "stable_key": "clu_1edb748ac1859f3b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Music", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music", "wikipedia_extract": "Music is the arrangement of sound to create some combination of form, harmony, melody, rhythm, or otherwise expressive content. Music is generally agreed to be a cultural universal that is present in all human societies. Definitions of music vary widely in substance and approach. While scholars agree that music is defined by a small number of specific elements, there is no consensus as to what these necessary elements are. Music is often characterized as a highly versatile medium for expressing human creativity. Diverse activities are involved in the creation of music, and are often divided into categories of composition, improvisation, and performance."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Art", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1556, "target_cluster_name": "Arte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Music is often considered a form of art, and historical texts frequently discuss them together as related creative expressions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Music is often considered a form of art, and historical texts frequently discuss them together as related creative expressions."}, {"found_name": "Crescendo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4278, "target_cluster_name": "crescendo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crescendo is a musical term indicating a gradual increase in loudness, directly related to the practice and theory of music.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crescendo is a musical term indicating a gradual increase in loudness, directly related to the practice and theory of music."}, {"found_name": "Discord", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1209, "target_cluster_name": "discord", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Discord is a musical term referring to a harsh or unpleasant combination of sounds, directly related to the practice and theory of music.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Discord is a musical term referring to a harsh or unpleasant combination of sounds, directly related to the practice and theory of music."}, {"found_name": "malicia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2462, "target_cluster_name": "malicia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Music is an integral component of Capoeira, accompanying its practice and performance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Music is an integral component of Capoeira, accompanying its practice and performance.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["death", "Death"], "contexts": ["consequence of transgression", "cessation of life"]}, {"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Death"], "contexts": ["final end for man", "state of being cold"]}, {"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["death"], "contexts": ["cessation of life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Death", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Death", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 1053, "stable_key": "clu_e6de5df08f94374e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "death", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The irreversible cessation of all biological functions that sustain a living organism.", "wikipedia_extract": "Death is the end of life; it is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Death is thought to eventually and inevitably occur in all organisms; though some organisms, such as the immortal jellyfish, are biologically immortal, they can however still die from means other than the effects of aging. Something that is not considered an organism may be said \"to die\" in a figurative sense when it reaches an inactive state, such as when a star runs out of fuel. As of the early 21st century, 56 million people die per year. As of 2022, an estimated total of almost 110 billion humans have died, or roughly 94% of all humans to have ever lived. The cause of death is usually considered important, and an autopsy can be done to determine it."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Life", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 89, "target_cluster_name": "Life", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Death is the direct opposite and end of life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Death is the direct opposite and end of life."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Antiquity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "antiquity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Antiquity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Antiquity"], "contexts": ["ancient times and traditions", "respect for ancient times"]}, {"entity_id": "antiquity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "antiquity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["antiquity"], "contexts": ["ancient times, especially classical antiquity", "ancient times"]}, {"entity_id": "antiguidades", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "antiguidades", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["antiguidades"], "contexts": ["antiquities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Antiquity", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "antiquity", "similarity": 0.84}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Antiquity", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antiguidades", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "antiquity", "targe