", "found_relationship": "reverse: Latin is the language of ancient Rome and its associated civilization.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "British Museum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1235, "target_cluster_name": "British Museum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The British Museum is a major repository of artifacts from antiquity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The British Museum is a major repository of artifacts from antiquity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "traditions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1300, "target_cluster_name": "traditions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Tradition' often refers to the transmission of customs, beliefs, and knowledge from past generations, making it inherently linked to the concept of 'Antiquity' as the period from which these traditions originate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'Tradition' often refers to the transmission of customs, beliefs, and knowledge from past generations, making it inherently linked to the concept of 'Antiquity' as the period from which these traditions originate.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Latines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "latines", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Latines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Latines"], "contexts": ["people speaking Latin", "people who transcribed Greek texts"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Latin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["ancient Roman language", "language"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Latin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["ancient language and people", "language with dwindling inflexions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Latines", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Latines", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1055, "stable_key": "clu_8abd9968b3ca508d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Latin language", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q397", "wikidata_description": "Indo-European language of the Italic branch", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin", "wikipedia_extract": "Latin (lingua Latina or Latinum) is a classical language belonging to the Italic branch of the Indo-European languages. Latin was originally spoken by the Latins in Latium (now known as Lazio), the lower Tiber area around Rome, Italy. Through the expansion of the Roman Republic, it became the dominant language in the Italian Peninsula and subsequently throughout the Roman Empire. It has greatly influenced many languages, including English, having contributed many words to the English lexicon, particularly after the Christianisation of the Anglo-Saxons and the Norman Conquest. Latin roots appear frequently in the technical vocabulary used by fields such as theology, the sciences, medicine, and law. By the late Roman Republic, Old Latin had evolved into standardised Classical Latin."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Latina", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1122, "target_cluster_name": "Latina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plural or variant form referring to the Latin language.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Plural or variant form referring to the Latin language.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Civil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3939, "target_cluster_name": "Civil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latin was the language of Roman civil administration and law.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latin was the language of Roman civil administration and law."}, {"found_name": "Antiquity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1054, "target_cluster_name": "Antiquity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latin is the language of ancient Rome and its associated civilization.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latin is the language of ancient Rome and its associated civilization."}, {"found_name": "Etymology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1201, "target_cluster_name": "Etymologies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The study of the origins of words often traces them back to Latin.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The study of the origins of words often traces them back to Latin."}, {"found_name": "Education", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1064, "target_cluster_name": "education", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latin was a cornerstone of classical education for centuries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Latin was a cornerstone of classical education for centuries."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hesiod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentio