 literature, often discussed together as the earliest major figures of the epic tradition."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Anaxagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "anaxagoras", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Anaxagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Anaxagoras"], "contexts": ["philosopher whose assertions were rejected by Aristotle", "ancient philosopher who affirmed snow is black"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagoras", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anaxagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anaxagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher who predicted aërolites", "ancient philosopher on aerolites and ether"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagoras_the_clazomenian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anaxagoras the Clazomenian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anaxagoras the Clazomenian"], "contexts": ["philosopher with doctrine on celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagoras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Anaxagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anaxagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher on metal duality"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagore_de_clazomènes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Anaxagore de Clazomènes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anaxagore de Clazomènes"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher", "ancient philosopher from Clazomenae"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagoras_of_clazomenae", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anaxagoras of Clazomenae", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anaxagoras of Clazomenae"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anaxagoras", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Anaxagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anaxagoras", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Anaxagoras the Clazomenian", "similarity": 0.856}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anaxagoras", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Anaxagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Anaxagoras", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Anaxagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Anaxagoras the Clazomenian", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Anaxagoras", "similarity": 0.856}], "id": 1057, "stable_key": "clu_ae96b4aa3bca371a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anaxagoras", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -500, "death_year": -428, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher and early cosmologist", "wikidata_id": "Q83041", "wikidata_description": "5th-century BC Greek philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaxagoras", "wikipedia_extract": "Anaxagoras (; Ancient Greek: Ἀναξαγόρας, Anaxagóras, 'lord of the assembly'; c. 500 BC – c. 428 BC) was a Pre-Socratic Greek philosopher. Born in Clazomenae at a time when Asia Minor was under the control of the Persian Empire, Anaxagoras came to Athens. In later life he was charged with impiety and went into exile in Lampsacus. Responding to the claims of Parmenides on the impossibility of change, Anaxagoras introduced the concept of Nous (Cosmic Mind) as an ordering force."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Plato", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 113, "target_cluster_name": "Plato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anaxagoras was an early influence on Plato's thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anaxagoras was an early influence on Plato's thought.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Democrito", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 156, "target_cluster_name": "Democrito", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers associated with atomism and cosmology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are ancient Greek philosophers associated with atomism and cosmology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pythagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 354, "target_cluster_name": "Pythagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are ancient Greek philosophers.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "philosopher", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 431, "target_cluster_name": "philosopher", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anaxagoras was a pre-Socratic Greek philosopher.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anaxagoras was a pre-Socratic Greek philosopher.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Socrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 460, "target_cluster_name": "Socrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anaxagoras was an early influence on Socrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anaxagoras was an early influence on Socrates.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Anaximenes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4321, "target_cluster_name": "Anaximenes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are Pre-Socratic philosophers from Miletus or nearby Ionia, representing a continuation of the Milesian school of thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are Pre-Socratic philosophers from Miletus or nearby Ionia, representing a continuation of the Milesian school of thought."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anaxagoras was a significant figure in the intellectual lineage that influenced Aristotle, particularly in cosmology and the concept of Nous (Mind).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Anaxagoras was a significant figure in the intellectual lineage that influenced Aristotle, particularly in cosmology and the concept of Nous (Mind)."}, {"found_name": "Empedocles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2664, "target_cluster_name": "Empedocles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were Pre-Socratic philosophers who proposed theories about the fundamental nature of the cosmos, with Empedocles building upon or reacting to Anaxagoras' ideas about 'seeds' and 'mind'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were Pre-Socratic philosophers who proposed theories about the fundamental nature of the cosmos, with Empedocles building upon or reacting to Anaxagoras' ideas about 'seeds' and 'mind'."}, {"found_name": "Heraclito", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1306, "target_cluster_name": "Heraclito", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are pre-Socratic philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are pre-Socratic philosophers.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Philolaus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2259, "target_cluster_name": "Philolaus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Philolaus was a Pythagorean philosopher, and Anaxagoras was a pre-Socratic philosopher who also engaged with cosmological and metaphysical ideas, representing a related but distinct philosophical tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Philolaus was a Pythagorean philosopher, and Anaxagoras was a pre-Socratic philosopher who also engaged with cosmological and metaphysical ideas, representing a related but distinct philosophical tradition.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gunpowder", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "gunpowder", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Gunpowder", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Gunpowder"], "contexts": ["explosive mixture", "discovered causes of meteors"]}, {"entity_id": "gunpowder", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "gunpowder", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["gunpowder"], "contexts": ["used by miners, source of heat"]}, {"entity_id": "gunpowder", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "gunpowder", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["gunpowder"], "contexts": ["substance that explodes", "explosion causes propulsion of a bullet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gunpowder", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "gunpowder", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gunpowder", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "gunpowder", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "gunpowder", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "gunpowder", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1058, "stable_key": "clu_b6990047befd8e58", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gunpowder", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q12861", "wikidata_description": "explosive most commonly used as propellant in firearms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunpowder", "wikipedia_extract": "Gunpowder, commonly referred to as black powder to distinguish it from modern smokeless powder, is the earliest known chemical explosive. It consists of a mixture of sulfur, charcoal (which is mostly carbon), and potassium nitrate (saltpeter). The sulfur and charcoal act as fuels, while the saltpeter is an oxidizer. Gunpowder has been widely used as a propellant in firearms, artillery, rocketry, and pyrotechnics, including use as a blasting agent for explosives in quarrying, mining, building pipelines, tunnels, and roads. Gunpowder is classified as a low explosive because of its relatively slow decomposition rate, low ignition temperature and consequently low brisance (breaking/shattering). Low explosives deflagrate—burning at subsonic speeds—whereas high explosives detonate, producing a supersonic shockwave."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Powder", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1120, "target_cluster_name": "Powder", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gunpowder is a specific type of powder, often discussed in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gunpowder is a specific type of powder, often discussed in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "phosphorus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 173, "target_cluster_name": "phosphorus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phosphorus is a key component in the historical production of gunpowder, contributing to its flammability and explosive properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phosphorus is a key component in the historical production of gunpowder, contributing to its flammability and explosive properties.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Nitro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 350, "target_cluster_name": "Nitro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saltpeter (nitro) is a primary component of gunpowder.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Saltpeter (nitro) is a primary component of gunpowder.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Charcoal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 857, "target_cluster_name": "Charcoal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gunpowder is a combustible mixture that historically utilized charcoal as a key component.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gunpowder is a combustible mixture that historically utilized charcoal as a key component.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "salitre", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1211, "target_cluster_name": "salitre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Potassium nitrate is a key component of gunpowder.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Potassium nitrate is a key component of gunpowder.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Willow", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "willow", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Willow", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Willow"], "contexts": ["wood used for small-coal", "tree used in gunpowder composition"]}, {"entity_id": "willow", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Willow", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Willow", "Willow-Hearb"], "contexts": ["tree where the herb grows", "or Willow Leavs"]}, {"entity_id": "willows", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "willows", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["willows"], "contexts": ["tree species in Himalaya"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Willow", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Willow", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Willow", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "willows", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 1059, "stable_key": "clu_087f818ec4bd4fc5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Willow", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Salix L.", "family": "Salicaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q36050", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow", "wikipedia_extract": "Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species (plus numerous hybrids) of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions. Most species are known as willow, but some narrow-leaved shrub species are called osier, and some broader-leaved species are referred to as sallow (from Old English sealh, related to the Latin word salix, willow). Some willows (particularly arctic and alpine species) are low-growing or creeping shrubs; for example, the dwarf willow (Salix herbacea) rarely exceeds 6 centimetres (2+1⁄2 in) in height, though it spreads widely across the ground. Willows have watery bark sap rich in salicin, soft, usually pliant, tough wood, slender branches, and large, fibrous roots that are often stoloniferous. The roots are remarkable for their toughness, size, and tenacity to live, and roots readily sprout from aerial parts of the plant."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cozimento", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1654, "target_cluster_name": "cozimento", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "nafcem as moſcas ;& tem differentes nomes , porque huns the chamão Ulmeiro , outros Choupo , outros Salgueiro. A entrecaſca do pao do Ulmeiro cura o feu cozimento todas aschagas interiores,& exteriore", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "arvore", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1632, "target_cluster_name": "arvore", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "uſou de chapejaras tacs chagas com a aguada infuſaódo ,,entrecaſco do Ulmeiro,ou Choupo,& foycomomilagrofaa brevidadecom ,, quefarou. Advirto que eſta arvore he aquela , que gera humas bolſas , em que", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for the tree", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cura", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1748, "target_cluster_name": "cura", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "em que nafcem as moſcas ;& tem differentes nomes , porque huns the chamão Ulmeiro , outros Choupo , outros Salgueiro. A entrecaſca do pao do Ulmeiro cura o feu cozimento todas aschagas interiores,& ex", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for the tree with medicinal bark", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Poplar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1215, "target_cluster_name": "Poplar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Poplars (genus Populus) are closely related to Willows (genus Salix), both belonging to the family Salicaceae. They share similar habitats and growth forms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Poplars (genus Populus) are closely related to Willows (genus Salix), both belonging to the family Salicaceae. They share similar habitats and growth forms."}, {"found_name": "Alder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2349, "target_cluster_name": "Alder-Tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alders (genus Alnus) are often found in similar moist habitats as willows and are also deciduous trees/shrubs, though they belong to a different family (Betulaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alders (genus Alnus) are often found in similar moist habitats as willows and are also deciduous trees/shrubs, though they belong to a different family (Betulaceae)."}, {"found_name": "Ash", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2502, "target_cluster_name": "Ash-tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ash trees (genus Fraxinus) are deciduous trees that can be found in similar temperate regions and often share ecological niches with willows, though they belong to the Oleaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ash trees (genus Fraxinus) are deciduous trees that can be found in similar temperate regions and often share ecological niches with willows, though they belong to the Oleaceae family."}, {"found_name": "Tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 24, "target_cluster_name": "Tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Willow is a type of tree, and 'Tree' is a broader category encompassing it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Willow is a type of tree, and 'Tree' is a broader category encompassing it."}, {"found_name": "Shrubs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 472, "target_cluster_name": "arbustes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Willow is a type of shrub, and 'Shrubs' is a broader category encompassing it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Willow is a type of shrub, and 'Shrubs' is a broader category encompassing it."}, {"found_name": "Alder-Tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2349, "target_cluster_name": "Alder-Tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alder and Willow are often found in wet or riparian environments and share ecological similarities. Both have historical uses in crafts and medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alder and Willow are often found in wet or riparian environments and share ecological similarities. Both have historical uses in crafts and medicine.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Oak", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "oaks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Oaks", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Oaks"], "contexts": ["large deciduous tree", "type of tree"]}, {"entity_id": "oak", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Oak", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["oak", "Oak"], "contexts": ["common tree studied", "tree from which seeds were found"]}, {"entity_id": "oaks", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Oaks", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["oaks", "Oaks"], "contexts": ["flourish at high altitudes near equator", "trees near the equator"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oaks", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Oak", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oaks", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oaks", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Oak", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oaks", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 1060, "stable_key": "clu_650dc89049f43ea5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "oak", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Quercus", "family": "Fagaceae", "description": "A large tree known for its wood and acorns, with medicinal uses.", "note": " 'Quercia' is the Italian word for oak, and 'Quercus' is the genus name.", "wikipedia_extract": "An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 500 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve. Ecologically, oaks are keystone species in habitats from Mediterranean semi-desert to subtropical rainforest."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Acorns", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2922, "target_cluster_name": "Acorns", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acorns are the nuts of oak trees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Acorns are the nuts of oak trees.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "circle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "circle", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Circle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["circle", "Circle"], "contexts": ["geometric shape with all points equidistant", "geometric shape with one center"]}, {"entity_id": "circle", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "circle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["circle"], "contexts": ["geometric shape with a real form", "geometric shape for imagination"]}, {"entity_id": "circles", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "circles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["circles"], "contexts": ["round shapes in a diagram"]}, {"entity_id": "great_circle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "great circle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["great circle"], "contexts": ["circle on sphere with same center"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Circle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "circle", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Circle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "circles", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "circle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "great circle", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 1061, "stable_key": "clu_215c6938db8dd9ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Circle", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 783, "target_cluster_name": "sphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A circle is a two-dimensional shape, while a sphere is a three-dimensional object. Both are fund