eres used in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "marble", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "marble", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["marble"], "contexts": ["granular or saccharoidal origin", "calcareous rock undergoing metamorphosis"]}, {"entity_id": "marmoris", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "marmoris", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marmoris"], "contexts": ["white marble found"]}, {"entity_id": "marmoreum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "marmoreum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marmoreum"], "contexts": ["material of the pavement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Marble", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "marble", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Marble", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "marmo", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "marble", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "marmo", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 1062, "stable_key": "clu_8cf50c2b98972f7a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marble", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Contexts suggest both the rock and potentially a salt derived from it.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble", "wikipedia_extract": "Marble is a metamorphic rock consisting of carbonate minerals (most commonly calcite  (CaCO3) or dolomite (CaMg(CO3)2) that have recrystallized under the influence of heat and pressure. It has a crystalline texture, and is typically not foliated (layered), although there are exceptions. In geology, the term marble refers to metamorphosed limestone, but its use in stonemasonry more broadly encompasses unmetamorphosed limestone. Pure white marble arises from very clean limestone or rock also called dolomite. However, its signature veins and swirls result from impurities like clay, sand, iron oxides, or chert originally present in the protolith—magnesium-rich varieties may show green tones due to serpentine. These impurities are redistributed and recrystallized during metamorphism."}, "member_count": 8, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "rock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 383, "target_cluster_name": "rock", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is a metamorphic rock composed of recrystallized carbonate minerals, most commonly calcite.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Marble is a metamorphic rock composed of recrystallized carbonate minerals, most commonly calcite.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Calcareous formations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1162, "target_cluster_name": "formations calcaires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is a type of calcareous formation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Marble is a type of calcareous formation."}, {"found_name": "Calcium carbonate", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 862, "target_cluster_name": "Lime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is primarily composed of calcium carbonate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Marble is primarily composed of calcium carbonate."}, {"found_name": "Chalk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 829, "target_cluster_name": "chalk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both marble and chalk are forms of calcium carbonate, with marble being a metamorphic rock and chalk a sedimentary rock.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both marble and chalk are forms of calcium carbonate, with marble being a metamorphic rock and chalk a sedimentary rock."}, {"found_name": "Crystalline rocks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3030, "target_cluster_name": "roches cristallines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is a crystalline rock.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Marble is a crystalline rock."}, {"found_name": "Gems", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1119, "target_cluster_name": "gemms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is often used as a decorative stone, similar to gems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Marble is often used as a decorative stone, similar to gems."}, {"found_name": "Gypsum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 682, "target_cluster_name": "gypse", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both marble and gypsum are minerals that can be found in geological formations and have historical uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both marble and gypsum are minerals that can be found in geological formations and have historical uses."}, {"found_name": "Iceland spar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1763, "target_cluster_name": "Iceland spar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, the primary mineral in marble.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, the primary mineral in marble."}, {"found_name": "formations calcaires", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1162, "target_cluster_name": "formations calcaires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marble is a metamorphic rock composed primarily of recrystallized carbonate minerals, most commonly calcite, formed from limestone.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Marble is a metamorphic rock composed primarily of recrystallized carbonate minerals, most commonly calcite, formed from limestone.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "beans", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beans"], "contexts": ["food enjoined abstinence from"]}, {"entity_id": "beans", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Beans", "Bean"], "contexts": ["vegetable with virtues", "vegetable with various medicinal uses"]}, {"entity_id": "beans", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["beans"], "contexts": ["plant with seeds providing nutriment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beans", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Beans", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beans", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "beans", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Beans", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "beans", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 1063, "stable_key": "clu_4e2f73833f0dabb7-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bananas", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "description": "Tropical fruit-bearing plants.", "note": "The mention of 'beans' in the Darwin context seems an outlier and likely a misattribution or a secondary example. The dominant context is 'bananes'.", "wikipedia_extract": "A banana is an elongated, edible fruit—botanically a berry—produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids. Musa species are native to tropical Indomalaya and Australia; they were probably domesticated in New Guinea."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fruit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 72, "target_cluster_name": "frutas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bananas are a type of fruit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bananas are a type of fruit."}, {"found_name": "berry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 718, "target_cluster_name": "Berries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bananas are botanically classified as berries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bananas are botanically classified as berries."}, {"found_name": "herbaceous plant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1217, "target_cluster_name": "herbaceous Plant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description states that bananas grow on large treelike herbaceous flowering plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The description states that bananas grow on large treelike herbaceous flowering plants."}]}, {"canonical_name": "education", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "education", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "education", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["education"], "contexts": ["process of teaching and learning"]}, {"entity_id": "education", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "education", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["education", "Education"], "contexts": ["process of learning and training", "journal on education"]}, {"entity_id": "education", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "education", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["education", "Education"], "contexts": ["book by Herbert Spencer", "process of learning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "education", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "education", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "education", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "education", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "education", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "education", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1064, "stable_key": "clu_18f6833f37ea72ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Education", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7922", "wikidata_description": "theory and practice of education"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Latines", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1055, "target_cluster_name": "Latines", "source_book": "llm_genera