tism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1802, "target_cluster_name": "electro-magnetism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electromagnetism describes forces of attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electromagnetism describes forces of attraction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "magnetic lines of force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2456, "target_cluster_name": "magnetic lines of force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetic field lines are used to describe and visualize magnetic forces of attraction and repulsion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnetic field lines are used to describe and visualize magnetic forces of attraction and repulsion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2487, "target_cluster_name": "chemical force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'Chemical force' and 'Attractive force' refer to fundamental forces that govern interactions between entities, with 'Chemical force' being a specific manifestation or type of attractive force in the context of chemical bonding and reactions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both 'Chemical force' and 'Attractive force' refer to fundamental forces that govern interactions between entities, with 'Chemical force' being a specific manifestation or type of attractive force in the context of chemical bonding and reactions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Molecular attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2607, "target_cluster_name": "Molecular attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This term describes a type of force that is inherently related to the concept of attraction, including molecular attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This term describes a type of force that is inherently related to the concept of attraction, including molecular attraction.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "universal gravitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "law_of_universal_gravitation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "law of universal gravitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["law of universal gravitation"], "contexts": ["fundamental physics principle"]}, {"entity_id": "universal_gravitation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "universal gravitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["universal gravitation", "Universal Gravitation"], "contexts": ["Newton's law of gravity", "force attracting bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "law_of_gravitation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "law of gravitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["law of gravitation"], "contexts": ["fundamental law of physics"]}, {"entity_id": "law_of_gravitation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "law of gravitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["law of gravitation"], "contexts": ["fundamental principle of physics", "fundamental force of attraction between masses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "law of universal gravitation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "universal gravitation", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "law of universal gravitation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "law of gravitation", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "law of universal gravitation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law of gravitation", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "law of gravitation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law of gravitation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1112, "stable_key": "clu_4ab1189160db9c60", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Universal Gravitation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "The gravity of Earth, denoted by g, is the net acceleration that is imparted to objects due to the combined effect of gravitation (from mass distribution within Earth) and the centrifugal force (from the Earth's rotation). It is a vector quantity, whose direction coincides with a plumb bob and strength or magnitude is given by the norm {\\displaystyle g=\\|{\\mathit {\\mathbf {g} }}\\|} In SI units, this acceleration is expressed in metres per second squared (in symbols, m/s2 or m·s−2) or equivalently in newtons per kilogram (N/kg or N·kg−1). Near Earth's surface, the acceleration due to gravity, accurate to 2 significant figures, is 9.8 m/s2 (32 ft/s2). This means that, ignoring the effects of air resistance, the vertical component of velocity of an object falling freely will increase in the downwards direction by about 9.8 metres per second (32 ft/s) every second. The precise strength of Earth's gravity varies with location. The conventional value for standard gravity is 9.80665 m⋅s−2‍ by definition, originally adopted by the CGPM in 1901.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_of_Earth"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Centrifugal force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 315, "target_cluster_name": "Centrifugal force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of Universal Gravitation explicitly mentions its relation to centrifugal force as part of the net acceleration experienced by objects on Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The description of Universal Gravitation explicitly mentions its relation to centrifugal force as part of the net acceleration experienced by objects on Earth."}, {"found_name": "Force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 59, "target_cluster_name": "Force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Universal Gravitation is a fundamental concept within the broader study of forces in physics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Universal Gravitation is a fundamental concept within the broader study of forces in physics."}, {"found_name": "Attraction (physics)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4186, "target_cluster_name": "attractions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Universal Gravitation is a specific type of attractive force between masses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Universal Gravitation is a specific type of attractive force between masses."}, {"found_name": "Earth's rotation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3632, "target_cluster_name": "Earth's rotation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of Universal Gravitation notes that Earth's rotation contributes to the net acceleration (centrifugal force), which is part of the definition of 'g'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The description of Universal Gravitation notes that Earth's rotation contributes to the net acceleration (centrifugal force), which is part of the definition of 'g'."}, {"found_name": "Figure of the Earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3146, "target_cluster_name": "Figure of Earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The shape of the Earth (its flattening) influences the local value of 'g', which is related to Universal Gravitation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The shape of the Earth (its flattening) influences the local value of 'g', which is related to Universal Gravitation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "magneto-electric machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetic_apparatus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic apparatus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic apparatus"], "contexts": ["device utilizing magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetic_apparatus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electro-magnetic apparatus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electro-magnetic apparatus"], "contexts": ["apparatus for measuring electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "magneto-electric_machine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magneto-electric machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 3, "variants": ["magneto-electric machine"], "contexts": ["machine generating electricity from magnetism", "device generating electricity from magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "magneto-electric_apparatus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Magneto-Electric Apparatus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magneto-Electric Apparatus"], "contexts": ["device for magneto-electric effects"]}, {"entity_id": "magneto-electric_machine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "magneto-electric machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magneto-electric machine