": "Chymists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chymists"], "contexts": ["Practitioners of alchemy and chemistry", "practitioners who used Crystal for the Stone"]}, {"entity_id": "chemists", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemists"], "contexts": ["scientists studying chemical composition"]}, {"entity_id": "chemists", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["chemists"], "contexts": ["scientists studying chemical combinations", "practitioners who study chemical substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Chymists", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Chymists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1124, "stable_key": "clu_ee4bf6d3daae08ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemists", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Practitioners of alchemy, chemistry, and chemical sciences.", "wikidata_id": "Q13107184", "wikidata_description": "health facility where medicines are sold and medical advice is given", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharmacy%20%28shop%29", "wikipedia_extract": "A pharmacy (also called drugstore in North American English or community pharmacy or chemist in Commonwealth English and both in Australian English) is a premises which provides pharmaceutical drugs, among other products. At the pharmacy, a pharmacist oversees the fulfillment of medical prescriptions and is available to counsel patients about prescription and over-the-counter drugs or about health problems and wellness issues. A typical pharmacy would be in the commercial area of a community. In most countries, a premises for prescription drugs is subject to legislation; with requirements for storage conditions, staff qualifications, equipment, record keeping (especially of controlled drugs) and other matters, all specified in legislation. It was once the case that pharmacists stayed within the premises compounding/dispensing medications, but there has been an increasing trend towards the use of trained pharmacy technicians, with the pharmacist spending more time communicating with patients. Pharmacy technicians are now more dependent upon automation to assist them in their new role dealing with patients' prescriptions and patient safety issues."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lavoisier", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 475, "target_cluster_name": "Lavoisier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lavoisier is a central figure in the history of chemistry, often referred to as the 'father of modern chemistry'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "atoms", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 84, "target_cluster_name": "atoms", "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "seems impossible on any other condition than the existence of ultimate atoms; and though the combining weights of the respective elements are termed by chemists their “equivalents,” for the purpose of", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "assumed ultimate constituents, related to combining weights termed equivalents"}, {"found_name": "elements", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 794, "target_cluster_name": "elements", "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "seems impossible on any other condition than the existence of ultimate atoms; and though the combining weights of the respective elements are termed by chemists their “equivalents,” for the purpose of", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "possess combining weights termed equivalents by chemists"}, {"found_name": "Chymicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 609, "target_cluster_name": "Chymicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plural form of the same profession.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Plural form of the same profession.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Authors ancient or modern", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3855, "target_cluster_name": "Authors ancient or modern", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemists authored texts detailing their experiments and theories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemists authored texts detailing their experiments and theories.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Liege", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "liege", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Liege", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Liege"], "contexts": ["city where Mandevil died"]}, {"entity_id": "liège", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Liège", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liège"], "contexts": ["city in Belgium"]}, {"entity_id": "liege", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Liege", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Liege"], "contexts": ["city in Belgium", "location of a coal basin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Liege", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Liège", "similarity": 0.756}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Liege", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Liege", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Liège", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Liege", "similarity": 0.756}], "id": 1125, "stable_key": "clu_45114c789bd9f4e5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Liège ( lee-EZH, lee-AYZH; French: [ljɛʒ] ; Walloon: Lîdje [liːtʃ]; Dutch: Luik [lœyk] ; German: Lüttich [ˈlʏtɪç] ) is a city and municipality in the Belgian region of Wallonia, and the capital of the eponymous province. The city is situated in the valley of the Meuse, in the east of Belgium, not far from borders with the Netherlands (Maastricht is about 33 km (20.5 mi) to the north) and with Germany (Aachen is about 53 km (32.9 mi) north-east). In Liège, the Meuse meets the river Ourthe. The city is part of the sillon industriel, the former industrial backbone of Wallonia. It is still the principal economic and cultural centre of the region. The municipality consists of the following sections (sub-municipalities): Liège proper, Angleur, Bressoux, Chênée, Glain, Grivegnée, Jupille-sur-Meuse, Rocourt, and Wandre."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Rye", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rye", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rye", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rye"], "contexts": ["plant whose straws are attracted", "grain with poisonous ears"]}, {"entity_id": "rye", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Rye", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rye"], "contexts": ["location near Appledore where Devils-bit is found", "city in Kent"]}, {"entity_id": "rye", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rye", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rye"], "contexts": ["agricultural staple crop"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rye", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Rye", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rye", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rye", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Rye", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rye", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 1126, "stable_key": "clu_6a2567d5cccbb28c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rye", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Secale cereale Linnaeus", "family": "Poaceae", "note": "The term 'seigle' is French for rye. The mention of 'Seseli' in the Florentine Ricettario suggests a potential ambiguity, as Seseli is a genus of herbs. However, the context of 'grain' strongly points to rye.", "wikidata_id": "Q12099", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye", "wikipedia_extract": "Rock and Rye is a term (both generically and brand names) for a bottled liqueur or mixed cocktail composed of rye whiskey and rock candy (crystallized sugar) or fruit. As early as 1914, United States government publications discuss disputes regarding beverages labeled \"rock and rye\", including a case of a beverage so marketed which was found by the Bureau of Chemistry to consist of \"water, sugar, glucose, and artificial coloring matters, sold in imitation of a rock and rye cordial\". Among non-alcoholic beverages, Rock and Rye continues to be a popular flavor of the Faygo brand of soda pop. Related products mentioned in the early 20th century include Rock and Rum and Rock and Gin. Slow & Low produced by Hochstadter's since 1884 produces a Rock & Rye drink that according to the label is \"served straight and as a proper old-fashioned since the 1800's Rock and Rye Union Made with Straight rye Whiskey Raw Honey, Navel Orange, Rock Candy and Bitters.\" It is 84 Proof Produced by Hochstadter's from Scobyville, NJ. In the film National Lampoon's Animal House, Boon (Peter Riegert) orders a \"double Rock and Rye and seven Carlings\" at a bar."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Corn", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 426, "target_cluster_name": "Corn", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rye and corn are both cereal grains, commonly cultivated and consumed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Rye and corn are both cereal grains, commonly cultivated and consumed.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Farelos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 287, "target_cluster_name": "Farelos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bran is a component of cereal grains, including rye.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bran is a component of cereal grains, including rye.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Meal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2698, "target_cluster_name": "Meal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rye is a grain that is milled into flour, and historical texts may refer to rye flour simply as 'flour' or 'meal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Rye is a grain that is milled into flour, and historical texts may refer to rye flour simply as 'flour' or 'meal'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bread", "