ACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Wales", "wales"], "contexts": ["country where Bishops-weed grows wild", "country"]}, {"entity_id": "wales", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Wales", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Wales"], "contexts": ["country with scored mountains from ice", "mountains with scored flanks and polished surfaces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Wales", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Wales", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Wales", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Wales", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wales", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Wales", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1130, "stable_key": "clu_2455f0e1c8092419", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wales", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country forming part of the United Kingdom.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wales (Welsh: Cymru [ˈkəmrɨ] ) is a country that is part of the United Kingdom. Located on the island of Great Britain, it is bordered by the Irish Sea to the north and west, England to the east, the Bristol Channel to the south, and the Celtic Sea to the south-west. As of 2021, it had a population of 3.2 million. It has a total area of 21,218 square kilometres (8,192 sq mi) and over 2,700 kilometres (1,680 mi) of coastline. It is largely mountainous with its higher peaks in the north and central areas, including Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), its highest summit. The country lies within the north temperate zone and has a changeable, maritime climate."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Great Britain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 776, "target_cluster_name": "Great Britain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wales is a constituent country of Great Britain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wales is a constituent country of Great Britain.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Yorkshire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 951, "target_cluster_name": "Yorkshire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wales is a country within Great Britain, often discussed in relation to English regions like Yorkshire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wales is a country within Great Britain, often discussed in relation to English regions like Yorkshire.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "England", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 65, "target_cluster_name": "England", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wales shares a land border with England and is part of the United Kingdom.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wales shares a land border with England and is part of the United Kingdom."}, {"found_name": "United Kingdom", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2316, "target_cluster_name": "British Islands", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wales is a country that is part of the United Kingdom.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wales is a country that is part of the United Kingdom."}]}, {"canonical_name": "apes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "apes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Apes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apes"], "contexts": ["animals with proximity of reason"]}, {"entity_id": "apes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "apes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["apes"], "contexts": ["primates with highly complex nervous systems", "animal with hardly any lower brain centers"]}, {"entity_id": "apes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "apes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["apes"], "contexts": ["mammals inhabiting Kashmir chestnut-tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "apes", "similarity": 0.862}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "apes", "similarity": 0.736}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "apes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "apes", "similarity": 0.788}], "id": 1131, "stable_key": "clu_4a331a1f39b146af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apes", "confidence": "low", "type": "animal", "note": "The context is contradictory. 'apes' usually refers to primates. However, 'Apis' is a genus of bees and is also contextually linked to a deity represented by a bull. Given the early modern scientific context and the mention of Kashmir, it is most likely referring to primates (apes). The mention of 'Apis' might be an OCR error or a very obscure secondary reference.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "The Hominidae (; hominids ), whose members are known as the great apes, are a taxonomic family of primates that includes eight extant species in four genera: Pongo (the Bornean, Sumatran and Tapanuli orangutan); Gorilla (the eastern and western gorilla); Pan (the chimpanzee and the bonobo); and Homo, of which only modern humans (Homo sapiens) remain. Numerous revisions in classifying the great apes have caused the use of the term hominid to change over time. The original meaning of \"hominid\" referred only to humans (Homo) and their closest extinct relatives. However, by the 1990s humans and other apes were considered to be \"hominids\". The earlier restrictive meaning has now been largely assumed by the term hominin, which however additionally includes Pan. Hominins more closely related to humans than to Pan are called Hominina, while the current meaning of \"hominid\" includes all the great apes including humans.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "monkey", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 872, "target_cluster_name": "monkey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apes are a type of primate closely related to monkeys, often discussed together in historical texts concerning simians.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Apes are a type of primate closely related to monkeys, often discussed together in historical texts concerning simians.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mammal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2279, "target_cluster_name": "mammal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apes are a type of mammal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Apes are a type of mammal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "monkeys", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2283, "target_cluster_name": "monkeys", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apes are traditionally excluded from the definition of monkeys, but are closely related within the infraorder Simiiformes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Apes are traditionally excluded from the definition of monkeys, but are closely related within the infraorder Simiiformes.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Torpedo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "torpedo", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Torpedo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 1, "variants": ["Torpedo"], "contexts": ["fish that stupifies when alive"]}, {"entity_id": "torpedo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Torpedo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Torpedo"], "contexts": ["fish that causes numbness", "an animal mentioned"]}, {"entity_id": "torpedo", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Torpedo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 2, "variants": ["torpedo", "Torpedo"], "contexts": ["genus of ray with electrical action", "fish with electrical action"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Torpedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Torpedo", "similarity": 0.799}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Torpedo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Torpedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Torpedo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Torpedo", "similarity": 0.799}], "id": 1132, "stable_key": "clu_e64ce097db1c1bed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electric ray", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'Torpedo' in early modern texts often referred to electric rays (family Torpedinidae) known for their electric shock capabilities.", "wikidata_id": "Q41317", "wikidata_description": "order of fishes", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric%20ray", "wikipedia_extract": "The electric rays are a group of rays, flattened cartilaginous fish with enlarged pectoral fins, composing the order Torpediniformes . They are known for being capable of producing an electric discharge, ranging from 8 to 220 volts, depending on species, used to stun prey and for defense. There are 69 species in four families. Perhaps the best known members are those of the genus Torpedo. The torpedo undersea weapon is named after it. The name comes from the Latin torpere, 'to be stiffened or paralyzed', from the effect on someone who touches the fish."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 94, "target_cluster_name": "Fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric rays are a type of fish.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electric rays are a type of fish."}]}, {"canonical_name": "medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "medium", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["medium"], "contexts": ["substance through which rays pass"]}, {"entity_id": "medium", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["medium"], "contexts": ["substance filling space, possibly ether", "substance transmitting forces"]}, {"entity_id": "media", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "media", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["media"], "contexts": ["intermediate substances"]}, {"entity_id": "medium", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["medium"], "contexts": ["surrounding environment for heat exchange"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "medium", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "medium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "medium", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "media", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "medium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "medium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "medium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "medium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "media", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "medium", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1133, "stable_key": "clu_5e9cdee04e89866b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medium", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the substance or space through which a phenomenon (like light or sound) propagates, or an intermediate substance in a process."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "etherial medium", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3770, "target_cluster_name": "etherial medium", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was fundamentally understood as a medium through which light propagated, making 'medium' a core conceptual relation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The luminiferous aether was fundamentally understood as a medium through which light propagated, making 'medium' a core conceptual relation.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "dores dos olhos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "sore_eyes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sore eyes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["sore eyes"], "contexts": ["eyes affected by inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "sore_eyes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "sore Eyes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["sore Eyes"], "contexts": ["symptom of eye irritation"]}, {"entity_id": "dores_dos_olhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dores dos olhos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["dores dos olhos", "dor de olhos", "doresdos olhos"], "contexts": ["pains in the eyes", "eye pains treated by Alcaeſt"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sore eyes", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "sore Eyes", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sore eyes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores dos olhos", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "sore Eyes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores dos olhos", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 1134, "stable_key": "clu_42f0b6bdbd169efd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eye pain", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Ophthalmological pain", "note": "The Portuguese 'dores dos olhos' directly translates to 'pains of the eyes', and the English 'sore eyes' refers to irritation or pain in the eyes.", "wikidata_id": "Q21077147", "wikidata_description": "medical symptom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye%20pain", "wikipedia_extract": "Eye pain, also called ophthomalgia or ocular pain, is a nonspecific term for pain in one or both eyes, around the eyes, or behind the eyes. It may encompass minor eye irritation, aching, or burning sensation to intense and prolonged pain. Eye pain is a common complaint and a major driver of patient visits to both emergency departments and out-patient clinics, generally ophthalmology or neurology clinics. Between 2008 and 2019, there were 1 million emergency department visits and 4.6 million outpatient visits attributed to eye pain. Pain can occur in almost any part of the eye, including the eyelid, conjunctiva, sclera, cornea, or orbit (eye socket). Sensation of pain may be accompanied with swelling, throbbing or pressure, a stabbing or shooting pain, and/or the sensation of a foreign body in the eye."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 70, "target_cluster_name": "pain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Eye pain is a specific manifestation of the general symptom of pain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Eye pain is a specific manifestation of the general symptom of pain."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Partridge", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "partridges", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Partridges", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Partridges"], "contexts": ["birds whose eggs hatch under a hen"]}, {"entity_id": "partridge", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Partridge", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["partridge", "Partridge"], "contexts": ["terrestrial bird", "bird from which earth was collected"]}, {"entity_id": "partridges", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "partridges", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["partridges"], "contexts": ["game birds"]}, {"entity_id": "partridge", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "partridge", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["partridge"], "contexts": ["bird found in fields"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Partridges", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Partridge", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Partridges", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "partridges", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Partridges", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "partridge", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Partridge", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "partridge", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "partridges", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "partridge", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 1135, "stable_key": "clu_a473fb13e56fbc66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "partridge", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q93235", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccinium%20vitis-idaea", "wikipedia_extract": "A partridge is a medium-sized galliform bird in any of several genera, with a wide native distribution throughout parts of Europe, Asia and Africa. Several species have been introduced to the Americas. They are sometimes grouped in the Perdicinae subfamily of the Phasianidae (pheasants, quail, etc.). However, molecular research suggests that partridges are not a distinct taxon within the superfamily Phasianoidea, but that some species are closer to the pheasants, while others are closer to the junglefowl. Partridges are medium-sized game birds, generally intermediate in size between the larger pheasants and smaller quail; they're ground-dwelling birds that feature variable plumage colouration across species, with most tending to grey and brown. Partridges are native to Europe, Asia, Africa, and the Middle East."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Perdiz", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1736, "target_cluster_name": "Perdiz", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Perdiz is the Spanish word for partridge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Perdiz is the Spanish word for partridge."}, {"found_name": "Pheasant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3883, "target_cluster_name": "Pheasants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Partridges and pheasants are closely related galliform birds, often grouped together in the Phasianidae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Partridges and pheasants are closely related galliform birds, often grouped together in the Phasianidae family."}]}, {"canonical_name": "translation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "translations", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Translations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Translations"], "contexts": ["different versions of scripture"]}, {"entity_id": "translation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "translation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["translation"], "contexts": ["conversion from one form to another"]}, {"entity_id": "translate", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "translate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["translate"], "contexts": ["interpret or convert information"]}, {"entity_id": "translation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "translation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Translation", "translation"], "contexts": ["movement through space", "general motion of stars"]}, {"entity_id": "translated", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "translated", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["translated"], "contexts": ["carried forward in space"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Translations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "translation", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Translations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "translate", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Translations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translation", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Translations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translated", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "translation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "translation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translated", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "translate", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translation", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "translate", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "translated", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 1136, "stable_key": "clu_394b791b014020c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "translation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7553", "wikidata_description": "statement that explains the meaning of a word, phrase, concept, or idea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Translation", "wikipedia_extract": "Translation in the field of language is the communication of the meaning of a source-language text by means of an equivalent target-language text (also called 'receptor language'). The English language draws a terminological distinction (which does not exist in every language) between translating (a written text) and interpreting (oral or signed communication between users of different languages); under this distinction, translation can begin only after the appearance of writing within a language community. A translator always risks inadvertently introducing source-language words, grammar, or syntax into the target-language rendering. On the other hand, such \"spill-overs\" have sometimes imported useful source-language calques and loanwords that have enriched target languages. Because of the laboriousness of the translation process, since the 1940s efforts have been made, with varying degrees of success, to automate translation or to mechanically aid the human translator. More recently, the rise of the Internet has fostered a world-wide market for translation services and has facilitated \"language localisation\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alphabet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3074, "target_cluster_name": "alphabet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The alphabet is the fundamental building block for written language, which is then translated.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The alphabet is the fundamental building block for written language, which is then translated."}, {"found_name": "English language", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3264, "target_cluster_name": "English", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'translation' is defined in relation to source and target languages, and 'English language' is an example of such a language.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'translation' is defined in relation to source and target languages, and 'English language' is an example of such a language."}, {"found_name": "Etymology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1201, "target_cluster_name": "Etymologies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Understanding the origin and history of words is crucial for accurate translation, especially when dealing with nuances and cultural context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Understanding the origin and history of words is crucial for accurate translation, especially when dealing with nuances and cultural context."}]}, {"canonical_name": "putrefaction", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "putrefaction", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "putrefaction", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["putrefaction", "putrefaction of the Blood", "putresaction"], "contexts": ["condition treated by the herb", "causing evil smell from gums"]}, {"entity_id": "putrefacion", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "putrefacion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["putrefacion"], "contexts": ["process of decay"]}, {"entity_id": "putrefação", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "putrefação", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["putrefação"], "contexts": ["decomposition or decay"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "putrefaction", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "putrefacion", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "putrefaction", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "putrefação", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "putrefacion", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "putrefação", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 1137, "stable_key": "clu_2b7d737988c9e91c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "putrefaction", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "putrefaction", "wikidata_id": "Q671701", "wikidata_description": "breaking down of a body of a human or animal post-mortem", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putrefaction", "wikipedia_extract": "Putrefaction is the fifth stage of death, following pallor mortis, livor mortis, algor mortis, and rigor mortis. This process references the breaking down of a body of an animal post-mortem. In broad terms, it can be viewed as the decomposition of proteins, and the eventual breakdown of the cohesiveness between tissues, and the liquefaction of most organs. This is caused by the decomposition of organic matter by bacterial or fungal digestion, which causes the release of gases that infiltrate the body's tissues, and leads to the deterioration of the tissues and organs. The approximate time it takes putrefaction to occur is dependent on various factors. Internal factors that affect the rate of putrefaction include the age at which death has occurred, the overall structure and condition of the body, the cause of death, and external injuries arising before or after death."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "morte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 91, "target_cluster_name": "morte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Putrefaction is the process of decomposition that occurs after death, and is thus a direct consequence and observable state associated with death.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Putrefaction is the process of decomposition that occurs after death, and is thus a direct consequence and observable state associated with death.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gerard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "gerard", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gerard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Gerard", "Gerhard"], "contexts": ["English author on herbs", "mentioned as a source for plant lore"]}, {"entity_id": "gerards", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gerards", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gerards"], "contexts": ["author of an herbal"]}, {"entity_id": "gerard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gerard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gerard"], "contexts": ["provided data on mountain elevations"]}, {"entity_id": "gerardo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Gerardo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gerardo"], "contexts": ["learned author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gerard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gerard", "similarity": 0.737}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gerards", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gerard", "similarity": 0.842}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Gerard", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Gerardo", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 1138, "stable_key": "clu_1a9f62d05a506681", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Gerard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1545, "death_year": 1612, "description": "English herbalist and author of 'Herball'", "note": "Multiple individuals named Gerard could be referenced, but John Gerard's fame as an herbalist makes him the most likely referent in medical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q75765559", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=322663"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was an ancient Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a cornerstone of herbal medicine for centuries. John Gerard's 'Herball' was a direct descendant of this tradition, aiming to describe and illustrate medicinal plants, much like Dioscorides had done.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was an ancient Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a cornerstone of herbal medicine for centuries. John Gerard's 'Herball' was a direct descendant of this tradition, aiming to describe and illustrate medicinal plants, much like Dioscorides had done."}, {"found_name": "Johnson", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1203, "target_cluster_name": "Johnson", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Gerard was a contemporary English herbalist and physician who, like John Parkinson, wrote a significant herbal, 'The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Gerard was a contemporary English herbalist and physician who, like John Parkinson, wrote a significant herbal, 'The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "creatures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "creatures", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Creatures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Creatures", "Creature"], "contexts": ["noisom beings driven away by fern smoke", "venemous, biting or stinging"]}, {"entity_id": "living_creatures", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "living creatures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["living creatures"], "contexts": ["organisms that are alive"]}, {"entity_id": "creatures", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "creatures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["creatures"], "contexts": ["living beings", "living organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Creatures", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "creatures", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "living creatures", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "creatures", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 1139, "stable_key": "clu_ff0e2ff68b0dea95", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "living beings", "confidence": "medium", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'creatures' is very broad and can encompass a wide range of living organisms. In the context of medicine and natural philosophy, it generally refers to animals or living beings in general."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animal Kingdom", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2373, "target_cluster_name": "animal kingdom", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Animal Kingdom is a taxonomic classification that encompasses all living beings that are animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Animal Kingdom is a taxonomic classification that encompasses all living beings that are animals."}, {"found_name": "Animal life", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2437, "target_cluster_name": "animal life", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Animal life is a direct descriptor of the state of being alive for animals, a subset of living beings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Animal life is a direct descriptor of the state of being alive for animals, a subset of living beings."}, {"found_name": "Fauna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 809, "target_cluster_name": "fauna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fauna refers to all of the animal life present in a particular region or time period, thus a specific collection of living beings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fauna refers to all of the animal life present in a particular region or time period, thus a specific collection of living beings."}, {"found_name": "Evolution", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 540, "target_cluster_name": "Evolution", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evolution describes the process by which living beings change over generations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evolution describes the process by which living beings change over generations."}, {"found_name": "Extinct species", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3360, "target_cluster_name": "extinct species", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Extinct species are a category of living beings that no longer exist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Extinct species are a category of living beings that no longer exist."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pontus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pontus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pontus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pontus"], "contexts": ["King of Pontus", "ancient kingdom"]}, {"entity_id": "ponto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ponto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ponto"], "contexts": ["location of açaroro according to Pliny", "geographic region"]}, {"entity_id": "ponto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Ponto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ponto"], "contexts": ["region known for wax", "region where Sinope was located"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pontus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ponto", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pontus", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Ponto", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ponto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Ponto", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1140, "stable_key": "clu_867e8628d836a460", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pontus", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Ancient kingdom and geographic region in Anatolia.", "note": "Refers to the historical region in Anatolia, not the planet or the mythological figure.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pontus or Pontos (; Greek: Πόντος, romanized: Póntos, lit. 'sea',) is a region within Anatolia on the southern coast of the Black Sea, located in the modern-day eastern Black Sea region of Turkey, in West Asia. The name was applied to the coastal region and its mountainous hinterland (rising to the Pontic Alps in the east) by the Greeks who colonized the area in the Archaic period and derived from the Greek name of the Black Sea: Εύξεινος Πόντος (Eúxinos Póntos), 'Hospitable Sea', or simply Pontos (ὁ Πόντος) as early as the Aeschylean Persians (472 BC) and Herodotus' Histories (c. 440 BC). Having originally no specific name, the region east of the river Halys was spoken of as the country Ἐν Πόντῳ (En Póntō), lit. 'on the [Euxinos] Pontos', and hence it acquired the name of Pontus, which is first found in Xenophon's Anabasis (c. 370 BC). The extent of the region varied through the ages but generally extended from the borders of Colchis (modern western Georgia) until well into Paphlagonia in the west, with varying amounts of hinterland. Several states and provinces bearing the name of Pontus or variants thereof were established in the region in the Hellenistic, Roman and Byzantine periods, culminating in the late Byzantine Empire of Trebizond. Pontus is sometimes considered as the original home of the Amazons, in ancient Greek mythology and historiography (e.g. by Herodotus and Strabo)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Superstition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "superstition", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Superstition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Superstition"], "contexts": ["reason for herb naming", "monster obscuring knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "superstitions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "superstitions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["superstitions"], "contexts": ["irrational beliefs"]}, {"entity_id": "superstitions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "superstitions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["superstitions"], "contexts": ["beliefs not based on reason or knowledge", "irrational beliefs"]}, {"entity_id": "superstition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "superstition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["superstition"], "contexts": ["irrational belief"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Superstition", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "superstitions", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Superstition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "superstitions", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Superstition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "superstition", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "superstitions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "superstitions", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "superstitions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "superstition", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 1141, "stable_key": "clu_73ebd8033e52f89b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Superstition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q133182", "wikidata_description": "belief in irrational supernatural causality", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superstition", "wikipedia_extract": "A superstition is any belief or practice considered by non-practitioners to be irrational or supernatural. It is commonly applied to beliefs and practices surrounding luck, fate, magic, amulets, astrology, fortune telling, spirits, and certain paranormal entities, particularly the belief that future events can be foretold by specific unrelated prior events. The word superstition is also used to refer to a religion not practiced by the majority of a given society regardless of whether the prevailing religion contains alleged superstitions or to all religions by the antireligious. Definitions of the term vary, but superstitions are commonly described as irrational beliefs at odds with scientific knowledge of the world. Stuart Vyse proposes that a superstition's \"presumed mechanism of action is inconsistent with our understanding of the physical world\", with Jane Risen adding that these beliefs are not merely scientifically wrong but impossible. Similarly, Lysann Damisch defines superstition as \"irrational  beliefs that an object, action, or circumstance that is not logically related to a course of events influences its outcome.\" Dale Martin says that superstitions \"presuppose an erroneous understanding about cause and effect, that have been rejected by modern science.\" The Oxford English Dictionary describes them as \"irrational, unfounded\", Merriam-Webster as \"a false conception about causation or belief or practice\", and the Cambridge Dictionary as \"sans grounding in human reason or scientific knowledge\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Superstition is a specific type of belief, characterized by its perceived irrationality or supernatural basis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Superstition is a specific type of belief, characterized by its perceived irrationality or supernatural basis."}, {"found_name": "Idolatry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2662, "target_cluster_name": "Idolatry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Idolatry, the worship of idols, can be considered a form of superstition, involving beliefs and practices deemed irrational by non-adherents.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Idolatry, the worship of idols, can be considered a form of superstition, involving beliefs and practices deemed irrational by non-adherents."}, {"found_name": "Atheism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 993, "target_cluster_name": "Atheism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atheism, the disbelief or lack of belief in the existence of God or gods, is often contrasted with superstitious beliefs that rely on supernatural entities or forces.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Atheism, the disbelief or lack of belief in the existence of God or gods, is often contrasted with superstitious beliefs that rely on supernatural entities or forces."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pipe", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pipe", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pipe", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pipe"], "contexts": ["used for smoking rosemary leaves", "instrument for taking smoke"]}, {"entity_id": "tube", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tube", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tube"], "contexts": ["hollow cylinder"]}, {"entity_id": "tube", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tube", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tube"], "contexts": ["hollow cylinder", "conduit for water in Artesian wells"]}, {"entity_id": "pipe", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "pipe", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pipe"], "contexts": ["object for producing musical notes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pipe", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "tube", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pipe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tube", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pipe", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "pipe", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tube", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tube", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tube", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "pipe", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1142, "stable_key": "clu_9ff02013bb1a5a75", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pipe", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q159536", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piper%20%28plant%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Piper, the pepper plants or pepper vines, is an economically and ecologically important genus in the family Piperaceae. The genus contains about 1,000–2,000 species of shrubs, herbs, and lianas, many of which are dominant species in their native habitat. The diversification of this taxon is of interest to understanding the evolution of plants. The scientific name Piper and the common name \"pepper\" are derived from the Sanskrit term pippali, denoting the long pepper (P. longum). Most Piper species are either herbaceous or vines; some grow as shrubs or almost as small trees."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cigar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3039, "target_cluster_name": "cigarros", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Pipe' refers to the plant genus Piper, which includes species used to produce pepper. The candidate 'cigar' is a smoking implement, and historically, pipes were also used for smoking tobacco. While not directly related to the plant itself, the term 'pipe' in common parlance often refers to a smoking device, creating a potential semantic link through common usage and historical context of smoking implements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Pipe' refers to the plant genus Piper, which includes species used to produce pepper. The candidate 'cigar' is a smoking implement, and historically, pipes were also used for smoking tobacco. While not directly related to the plant itself, the term 'pipe' in common parlance often refers to a smoking device, creating a potential semantic link through common usage and historical context of smoking implements."}]}, {"canonical_name": "work", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "work", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Work", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Work"], "contexts": ["Mathiolus' great, good work"]}, {"entity_id": "work", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "work", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["work"], "contexts": ["regular activity for habituation", "regular work for habituation"]}, {"entity_id": "work", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "work", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["work"], "contexts": ["energy expended to perform a task", "energy transferred by force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Work", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "work", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Work", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "work", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "work", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "work", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1143, "stable_key": "clu_5f1dfcfe18c71dd4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Work", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "project of one or more scientists, or of an organization in a scientific field", "wikipedia_url"