auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Incandescence is the emission of light by a body when it is heated, a phenomenon directly related to heat.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["soda"], "contexts": ["chemical element in meteoric masses", "chemical compound found in rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Soda"], "contexts": ["a substance used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["soda", "Soda"], "contexts": ["chemical used with gum guaiacum", "alkali decomposed by electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soda", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.802}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soda", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Soda", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 1186, "stable_key": "clu_cf450bdc30b8d55e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sodium", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'soda' can refer to sodium carbonate or hydroxide, in early modern scientific contexts discussing meteoric masses and chemical uses, elemental sodium or its compounds are strongly implied. Given the chemical context and 'chemical element' mention, elemental sodium is the most appropriate modern referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q658", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Na and atomic number 11", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium", "wikipedia_extract": "Sodium is a chemical element; it has symbol Na (from Neo-Latin natrium) and atomic number 11. It is a soft, silvery-white, highly reactive metal. Sodium is an alkali metal, being in group 1 of the periodic table. Its only stable isotope is 23Na. The free metal does not occur in nature and must be prepared from compounds. Sodium is the sixth most abundant element in the Earth's crust and exists in numerous minerals such as feldspars, sodalite, and halite (NaCl)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alkali", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 863, "target_cluster_name": "alkalies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sodium is a highly reactive alkali metal. Historical texts often discuss 'alkali' as a general category of substances, and sodium compounds (like soda ash) were key examples of alkalis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sodium is a highly reactive alkali metal. Historical texts often discuss 'alkali' as a general category of substances, and sodium compounds (like soda ash) were key examples of alkalis."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "cephalopodes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cephalopodes"], "contexts": ["animal substance preserved in Oxford clay", "marine mollusks"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopoda", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalopoda", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopoda"], "contexts": ["marine mollusc group"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalaspides", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalaspides", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalaspides"], "contexts": ["family of fossil fish"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopodes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopodes"], "contexts": ["animals with horny maxillae"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopods", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Cephalopods", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopods"], "contexts": ["shelled marine invertebrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopoda", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopoda", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalaspides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalaspides", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1187, "stable_key": "clu_43bac36bad346c8a", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A cephalopod  is any member of the molluscan class Cephalopoda  (Greek plural κεφαλόποδες, kephalópodes; \"head-feet\") such as a squid, octopus, cuttlefish, or nautilus. These exclusively marine animals are characterized by bilateral body symmetry, a prominent head, and a set of arms or tentacles (muscular hydrostats) modified from the primitive molluscan foot. Fisherman sometimes call cephalopods \"inkfish\", referring to their common ability to squirt ink. The study of cephalopods is a branch of malacology known as teuthology. Cephalopods became dominant during the Ordovician period, represented by primitive nautiloids. The class now contains two, only distantly related, extant subclasses: Coleoidea, which includes octopuses, squid, and cuttlefish; and Nautiloidea, represented by Nautilus and Allonautilus."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "crustaceans", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 348, "target_cluster_name": "crustaceans", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cephalopodes and Crustaceans are both distinct groups of invertebrates, often discussed together in zoological contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cephalopodes and Crustaceans are both distinct groups of invertebrates, often discussed together in zoological contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ammonites", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 847, "target_cluster_name": "Ammonites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are a subclass within the Cephalopoda phylum.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammonites are a subclass within the Cephalopoda phylum.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "shell-fish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1016, "target_cluster_name": "shell-fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cephalopodes are a class of mollusks, 