ated", "found_relationship": "Carbon is the fundamental element upon which organic compounds are based."}, {"found_name": "Carbonic acid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 260, "target_cluster_name": "carbonic acid", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carbonic acid is a simple compound often discussed in relation to organic chemistry, though technically inorganic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carbonic acid is a simple compound often discussed in relation to organic chemistry, though technically inorganic."}, {"found_name": "Hydrogen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 731, "target_cluster_name": "Hydrogen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hydrogen is a key element in most organic compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hydrogen is a key element in most organic compounds."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "espiritu_santo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Espiritu Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Espiritu Santo"], "contexts": ["coast of Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "corpo_santo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Corpo Santo"], "contexts": ["island named after", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "corpo_santo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Corpo Santo", "CorpoSanto"], "contexts": ["location of apothecary", "location where Samuel lived"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Espiritu Santo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Espiritu Santo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Corpo Santo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1190, "stable_key": "clu_c29863263cb982bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Corpo Santo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A place, island, or street, likely named after a saint.", "wikipedia_extract": "Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela created by José Louzeiro for Rede Manchete, aired on March 30 and October 2, 1987, with 162 chapters. Written by Louzeiro, with supervised by Wilson Aguiar Filho, with the collaboration of Cláudio MacDowell, Eliane Garcia, Leila Míccolis and Maria Cláudia Oliveira, under the direction of Ary Coslov and Walter Campos and general direction by José Wilker. It features Christiane Torloni, Reginaldo Faria, Sílvia Buarque, Nathalia Timberg, Lídia Brondi, Otávio Augusto, Jonas Bloch and Sérgio Viotti in the main roles. The beautiful widow Simone Reski moves with her family – her daughter Lucinha, her aunt Maria, and her maid Isaura – from the suburbs to downtown Rio de Janeiro. She immediately meets Téo, a friendly bachelor and bon vivant, unaware that he is involved with the video smuggling and pornographic film production mafia. Téo is interested in Simone because he sees Lucinha as a new nymphet star for his films."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alagoas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4472, "target_cluster_name": "alagoas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alagoas is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alagoas is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context."}, {"found_name": "Bahia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2546, "target_cluster_name": "Bahia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context."}, {"found_name": "Brazil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 96, "target_cluster_name": "Brasil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brazil is the country of origin for the telenovela Corpo Santo. This is a direct and fundamental relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Brazil is the country of origin for the telenovela Corpo Santo. This is a direct and fundamental relationship."}]}, {"canonical_name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["regimento", "regimiento"], "contexts": ["dietary regimen", "misspelling in errata"]}, {"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["regimento"], "contexts": ["royal decree or regulation"]}, {"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["regimento", "Regimento"], "contexts": ["diet or regimen", "guidelines for the sick"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "regimento", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Regimento", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Regimento", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 1191, "stable_key": "clu_aaaa72027ac75dac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "regimen", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'dietary regimen' strongly suggests the medical/health meaning.", "wikidata_id": "Q10358588", "wikidata_description": "military formation of regiment size built around elements of the infantry branch"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "alambicco", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "alambique", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "alambique", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["alambique", "alambiques"], "contexts": ["distillation apparatus", "used in distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "alambique", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alambique", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alambique"], "contexts": ["vessel used for distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "alambicco", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "alambicco", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 3, "variants": ["alambicco", "alembicco", "alimbicco"], "contexts": ["apparatus for distillation", "distillation instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "alembic", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Alembic", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alembic"], "contexts": ["distillation apparatus"]}, {"entity_id": "alembic", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Alembic", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Alembic", "alembic"], "contexts": ["instrument for distillation", "implied in distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "alembics", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "alembics", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alembics"], "contexts": ["distillation apparatus"]}, {"entity_id": "alembic", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alembic", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alembic"], "contexts": ["implied by distillation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "alambique", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "alambicco", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "alambicco", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 1192, "stable_key": "clu_9f98981b1cc286e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alambic", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A type of distillation apparatus used for separating liquids.", "wikipedia_extract": "An alembic (from Arabic: الإنبيق, romanized: al-inbīq, originating from Ancient Greek: ἄμβιξ, romanized: ambix, 'cup, beaker') is an alchemical still consisting of two vessels connected by a tube, used for distillation of liquids. The complete distilling apparatus consists of three parts: the \"cucurbit\" (Arabic: ḳarʿa; Greek: βῖκος, bîkos), the still pot containing the liquid to be distilled, which is heated by a flame the \"head\" or \"cap\" (إِنْبِيق‎, ʾinbīq; Greek ἄμβιξ, ambix) which fits over the mouth of the cucurbit to receive the vapors, with an attached downward-sloping \"tube\" (σωλήν, sōlēn) the \"receiver\" (قَابِلَة, qābila; ἄγγος, angos or φιάλη, phialē) container In the case of another distilling vessel, the retort, the \"cap\" and the \"cucurbit\" have been combined to form a single vessel. The anbik is also called the raʾs (the Arabic word raʾs means \"head\") of the cucurbit. The liquid in the cucurbit is heated or boiled; the vapour rises into the anbik, where it cools by contact with the walls and condenses, running down the spout into the receiver. A modern descendant of the alembic is the pot still, used to produce distilled beverages. Dioscorides's ambix, described in his De materia medica (c. 50 C.E.), is a helmet-shaped lid for gathering condensed mercury."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bain-marie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 953, "target_cluster_name": "bagno Maria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A double boiler used in distillation, often employed in conjunction with or as part of an alembic apparatus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A double boiler used in distillation, often employed in conjunction with or as part of an alembic apparatus."}, {"found_name": "Flask", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 125, "target_cluster_name": "flask", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A common vessel used in distillation apparatus, including alembics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A common vessel used in distillation apparatus, including alembics."}, {"found_name": "Glass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 22, "target_cluster_name": "Glass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The primary material from which alembics are historically constructed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The primary material from which alembics are historically constructed."}, {"found_name": "Pots", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3280, "target_cluster_name": "Pots", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "General term for vessels, which would include the components of an alembic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "General term for vessels, which would include the components of an alembic."}, {"found_name": "Vessel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1195, "target_cluster_name": "vaso", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A general term encompassing the component parts of an alembic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A general term encompassing the component parts of an alembic."}]}, {"canonical_name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dedo"], "contexts": ["finger, where rings are worn"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dedo"], "contexts": ["part of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["dedo", "dedo annular"], "contexts": ["used to stir water", "part of the hand"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1193, "stable_key": "clu_a51a1857989dc47f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "finger", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Part of the hand, used for manipulation and ornamentation.", "wikipedia_extract": "A finger is a prominent digit on the forelimbs of most tetrapod vertebrate animals, especially those with prehensile extremities (i.e. hands) such as humans and other primates. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), and short digits (i.e. significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals) are typically referred to as toes, while those that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are flexibly articulated and opposable, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects. Usually humans have five digits, the bones of which are termed phalanges, on each hand, although some people have more or fewer than five due to congenital disorders such as polydactyly or oligodactyly, or accidental or intentional amputations. The first digit is the thumb, followed by the index finger, middle finger, ring finger, and little finger or pinkie. According to different definitions, the thumb can be called a finger, or not."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hand", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 76, "target_cluster_name": "hand", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The finger is a part of the hand.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The finger is a part of the hand."}, {"found_name": "Yjada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "reftafiar la fangre. ........ Algunos que padecen limo. dolangre emosbyda) 11.0! + fe han remediado con haderanillos:defta pipdras y traer los pueſtos enel dedo ala conrihaa.. Y:aláá miſmo en fluxo me", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of stone"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rosado", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["rosado"], "contexts": ["rosy substance"]}, {"entity_id": "rosada", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rosada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["rosada"], "contexts": ["rose water, an ingredient", "rose water"]}, {"entity_id": "rosado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rosado", "rosado"], "contexts": ["rose-flavored ingredient in an electuary", "aromatic powder for stomach fomentation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "rosado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rosado", "similarity": 0.711}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rosada", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rosado", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1194, "stable_key": "clu_4db5e28563a879f2-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rosewater", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "description": "Fragrant liquid distilled from roses, used in medicine and cosmetics.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rose water or rosewater is a flavoured water created by steeping rose petals in water. It is typically made as a by-product during the distillation of rose petals to create rose oil for perfumes. Rose water is widely utilized to flavour culinary dishes and enhance cosmetic products, and it is significant in religious rituals throughout Eurasia. Iran is a major producer, supplying around 90% of the world's rose water demand. Central Iran is home to the annual Golabgiri festival each spring. Thousands of tourists visit the area to celebrate the rose harvest for the production of rosewater."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "vaso", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaso"], "contexts": ["vessel for drinking goat's milk"]}, {"entity_id": "vaso_piccolo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "vaso piccolo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaso piccolo"], "contexts": ["small vessel for burning sulfur"]}, {"entity_id": "vaso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 4, "variants": ["vaso"], "contexts": ["container for gargle", "container for water"]}, {"entity_id": "vessel", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Vessel", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vessel"], "contexts": ["to receive willow water"]}, {"entity_id": "vessel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vessel", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vessel"], "contexts": ["container for liquids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vaso", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "vaso piccolo", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vaso", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaso", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "vaso piccolo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaso", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1195, "stable_key": "clu_a60495649e91dc56", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vessel", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'vaso' can refer to various containers, but 'vessel' is the most general and accurate translation in this context.", "wikidata_id": "Q987767", "wikidata_description": "device creating a partially or fully enclosed space that can be used to contain, store, and transport objects or materials", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Container", "wikipedia_extract": "A container is any receptacle or enclosure for holding an item. Things kept inside of a container are protected on several sides by being inside of its structure. Humans have used containers for at least 100,000 years, and possibly for millions of years. Containers are used in storage, packaging, and transportation, including shipping. A container is any receptacle or enclosure for holding an item used in storage, packaging, and transportation, including shipping. A container can also be considered as a basic tool, consisting of any device creating a partially or fully enclosed space that can be used to contain, store, and transport objects or materials."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bagno Maria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 953, "target_cluster_name": "bagno Maria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A bain-marie is a type of vessel used for heating.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A bain-marie is a type of vessel used for heating.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "alambicco", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1192, "target_cluster_name": "alambicco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A general term encompassing the component parts of an alembic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A general term encompassing the component parts of an alembic.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "tapsia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "tapsia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tapsia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["tapsia"], "contexts": ["plant name", "plant with large roots"]}, {"entity_id": "tapfia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "tapfia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tapfia"], "contexts": ["plant from which juice is extracted"]}, {"entity_id": "tapfia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "tapfia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tapfia"], "contexts": ["plant substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tapsia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "tapfia", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tapsia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "tapfia", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "tapfia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "tapfia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1196, "stable_key": "clu_54c2601805395c1a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tapsia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "A plant, likely from the carrot family, known for its roots.", "note": "Possibly Ferula feruloides or a similar plant in the Apiaceae family.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tapsia (also spelled Tapsiya) is a village in the Gopiballavpur II CD block in the Jhargram subdivision of the Jhargram district in the state of West Bengal, India. Tapsia is located at 22°15′40.5″N 86°54′53.6″E. Jhargram subdivision, the only one in Jhargram district, shown in the map alongside, is composed of  hills, mounds and rolling lands. It is rather succinctly described in the District Human Development Report, 2011 (at that time it was part of Paschim Medinipur district), “The western boundary is more broken and picturesque, for the lower ranges of the Chhotanagpur Hills line the horizon, the jungle assumes the character of forest, and large trees begin to predominate. The soil, however, is lateritic, a considerable area is unproductive, almost uninhabited, especially in the extreme north-west where there are several hills over 1000 feet in height. The remainder of the country is an almost level plain broken only by the sand hills.” 3.48% of the population lives in urban areas and 96.52% lives in the rural areas. 20.11% of the total population belonged to scheduled castes and 29.37% belonged to scheduled tribes."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "meliloto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meliloto"], "contexts": ["plant used for scent comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "meliloto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["meliloto", "Meliloto"], "contexts": ["plant with yellow flowers", "melilot herb used in preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "meli_loto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Meli loto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Meli loto"], "contexts": ["Ingredient in a preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "meliloti", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "meliloti", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meliloti"], "contexts": ["melilot ingredient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "meliloto", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Meli loto", "similarity": 0.808}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meliloti", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meliloti", "similarity": 0.962}], "id": 1197, "stable_key": "clu_30c3452ef6f67533", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sweet clover", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Melilotus officinalis", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The description of yellow flowers points strongly to the genus Melilotus, commonly known as sweet clover."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Leguminosa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2184, "target_cluster_name": "Leguminosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sweet clover is a plant belonging to the Fabaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sweet clover is a plant belonging to the Fabaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "fluid mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fluids"], "contexts": ["bodily liquids"]}, {"entity_id": "fluid_mass", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fluid mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["fluid mass"], "contexts": ["matter in a liquid state", "rotating body of liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "mass", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mass"], "contexts": ["uniform body of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "juices", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "juices", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["juices"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_juices", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic juices", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic juices"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fluids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fluid mass", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fluid mass", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mass", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1198, "stable_key": "clu_978d3b5a10ebbc57", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "juices", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster includes both bodily fluids and plant extracts, a broader concept than a specific substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q1058795", "wikidata_description": "liquid originating from the inside of an organism, including fluids excreted or secreted, and body water that normally is not", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold-pressed_juice", "wikipedia_extract": "Cold-pressed juice is juice that uses a hydraulic press to extract juice from fruit and vegetables, as opposed to other methods such as centrifugal or single auger. Without pasteurization or high-pressure processing (HPP), cold-pressed juices can be stored in a refrigerator for up to three days when phytochemical and micronutrient degradation occurs. Some juicers use technology that helps delay oxidation which can allow for slightly longer storage in refrigerators. This type of juice has been commercially produced for decades, but became more common in some countries since 2013. In general, these juices are more expensive than other types of juices, as they are made from 100% fruit and vegetables without any added ingredients. Although cold-pressed juices were produced over several decades, the products gained more common use during the fad of juice \"cleansing\", expanding into a wider industry in the early 21st century."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fluids", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 393, "target_cluster_name": "fluids", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Juices are a specific type of fluid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Juices are a specific type of fluid."}]}, {"canonical_name": "sun's rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "sun's_rays", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sun's rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sun's rays"], "contexts": ["energy from the sun"]}, {"entity_id": "sun's_rays", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sun's rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sun's rays"], "contexts": ["heat retained by air"]}, {"entity_id": "sun’s_rays", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sun’s rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, 