]}, {"entity_id": "vine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vine"], "contexts": ["climbing plant, often bearing grapes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Grape", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "grapes", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Grape", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "grape", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Grape", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "grapes", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "grapes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "grapes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "grape", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "grapes", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 1220, "stable_key": "clu_8355b5eb29bf2782", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vine", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "While 'vine' can refer to many climbing plants, the context of 'yielding grapes' strongly suggests the grapevine.", "wikidata_id": "Q30046", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant in the grape vine family Vitaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitis%20vinifera", "linnaean": "Vitis vinifera", "family": "Vitaceae", "wikipedia_extract": "Vitis vinifera, the common grape vine, is a species of flowering plant, native to the Mediterranean region, Central Europe, and southwestern Asia, from Morocco and Portugal north to southern Germany and east to northern Iran. As of 2012, there were between 5,000 and 10,000 varieties of Vitis vinifera grapes though only a few are of commercial significance for wine and table grape production. The wild grape is sometimes classified as Vitis vinifera var. sylvestris (in some classifications considered Vitis sylvestris), with Vitis vinifera var vinifera restricted to cultivated forms. Domesticated vines have hermaphrodite flowers, but V. vinifera var."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Vine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 455, "target_cluster_name": "Vine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The historical name 'Vine' for Wine directly refers to the grape plant, from which wine is made.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The historical name 'Vine' for Wine directly refers to the grape plant, from which wine is made.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cup", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cup", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cup", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cup"], "contexts": ["vessel for drinking", "drinking vessels made of wood"]}, {"entity_id": "taça", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "taça", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["taça"], "contexts": ["use of shell", "vessel, originally meaning mollusk shell"]}, {"entity_id": "cup", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "cup", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cup"], "contexts": ["vessel for liquid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cup", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "taça", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cup", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cup", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "taça", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cup", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1221, "stable_key": "clu_fc380682835ea6df", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cup", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q146037", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cupressaceae", "wikipedia_extract": "Cupressaceae or the cypress family is a family of conifers. The family includes 27–30 genera (17 monotypic), which include the junipers and redwoods, with about 130–140 species in total. They are monoecious, subdioecious or (rarely) dioecious trees and shrubs up to 116 m (381 ft) tall. The bark of mature trees is commonly orange- to red-brown and of stringy texture, often flaking or peeling in vertical strips, but smooth, scaly or hard and square-cracked in some species. The family reached its peak of diversity during the Mesozoic era. The leaves are arranged either spirally, in decussate pairs (opposite pairs, each pair at 90° to the previous pair) or in decussate whorls of three or four, depending on the genus."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ventofas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1789, "target_cluster_name": "Ventofas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cupping glasses are a specific type of cup used in a medical procedure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cupping glasses are a specific type of cup used in a medical procedure.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "City", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "city", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "City", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["City"], "contexts": ["location of familiar dish", "location where Alex†ander Porredg is familiar"]}, {"entity_id": "ciudad", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ciudad", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ciudad"], "contexts": ["designation for Trinidad"]}, {"entity_id": "city", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "city", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["city"], "contexts": ["central business district", "location where units cluster together"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Ciudad", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 1222, "stable_key": "clu_1ff1b1c8e7d61e1d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "City", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "capital and most populous city in France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris", "wikipedia_extract": "Paris is the capital and largest city of France, with an estimated city population of 2,047,602 in an area of 105.4 km2 (40.7 sq mi), and a metropolitan population of 13,239,090 as of January 2026. Located on the river Seine in the centre of the Île-de-France region, it is the largest metropolitan area and fourth-most populous city in the European Union (EU). Nicknamed the City of Light, partly because of its role in the Age of Enlightenment, Paris has been one of the world's major centres of finance, diplomacy, commerce, culture, fashion, and gastronomy since the 17th century. Administratively, Paris is divided into twenty arrondissements (districts), each having their own cultural identity. Haussmann's renovation of Paris, which created new boulevards, parks, and public works, gave birth to a modern city known as the \"capital of the 19th century\". Paris is a major railway, motorway, and air-transport hub; in 2024 Charles de Gaulle Airport was the EU's busiest airport."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "villages", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1250, "target_cluster_name": "villages", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A city is a type of settlement, often a large and complex one.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: A city is a type of settlement, often a large and complex one.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ciudad", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2007, "target_cluster_name": "ciudad", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for 'city'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spanish for 'city'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "casa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1272, "target_cluster_name": "casa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A city is a large human settlement, and houses are the fundamental building blocks of cities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A city is a large human settlement, and houses are the fundamental building blocks of cities.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Oats", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "oats", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Oats", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Oats"], "contexts": ["cereal grain", "grain used in a poultice"]}, {"entity_id": "oat", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "oat", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1,