ound_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'Difference' is a specific type of 'Distinction', referring to the way in which things are not the same.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Resemblance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1929, "target_cluster_name": "Resemblance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ability to perceive resemblances often relies on the ability to distinguish between entities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ability to perceive resemblances often relies on the ability to distinguish between entities.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "likeness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2060, "target_cluster_name": "likeness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Making a distinction often involves recognizing differences, which implicitly highlights what is similar within categories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Making a distinction often involves recognizing differences, which implicitly highlights what is similar within categories.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "similarity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2122, "target_cluster_name": "similarity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Distinction is the act of recognizing differences, which is the inverse of recognizing similarities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Distinction is the act of recognizing differences, which is the inverse of recognizing similarities.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "distinguish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2800, "target_cluster_name": "distinguish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'distinguish' is the verb form of the noun 'distinction', referring to the act of making or perceiving a difference.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'distinguish' is the verb form of the noun 'distinction', referring to the act of making or perceiving a difference.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Spitzfindigkeit", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "things tending to be thought again together, or contiguous ideas tending to recur together. Now were these cases fair samples of all association, the distinction I drew might well be termed a _Spitzfi", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for pedantic hair-splitting"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bohemia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "bohemia", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Bohemia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bohemia"], "contexts": ["region with Silurian deposits"]}, {"entity_id": "bohemia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bohemia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bohemia"], "contexts": ["historical region in Central Europe", "region of Joachimsthal mine"]}, {"entity_id": "bohemia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bohemia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bohemia"], "contexts": ["Rey de Bohemia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bohemia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bohemia", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bohemia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bohemia", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bohemia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bohemia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1237, "stable_key": "clu_636e2770e6b81150", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bohemia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Czech Republic", "wikidata_id": "Q39193", "wikidata_description": "historical region in the Czech Republic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bohemia", "wikipedia_extract": "Bohemia ( boh-HEE-mee-ə; Czech: Čechy [ˈtʃɛxɪ] ; German: Böhmen [ˈbøːmən] ) is the westernmost and largest historical region of the Czech Republic. Bohemia can also refer to a wider area consisting of the historical Lands of the Bohemian Crown ruled by the Bohemian kings, including Moravia and Czech Silesia, in which case the smaller region is referred to as Bohemia proper as a means of distinction. Bohemians principality became a part of Great Moravia, and then an independent Duchy of Bohemia, which became a kingdom in the Holy Roman Empire. This subsequently became a part of the Habsburg monarchy and the Austrian Empire. After World War I and the establishment of an independent Czechoslovak state, the whole of Bohemia became a part of Czechoslovakia, defying claims of the German-speaking inhabitants that regions with German-speaking majority should be included in the Republic of German-Austria. Between 1938 and 1945, these border regions were annexed to Nazi Germany as the Sudetenland."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Prague", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2769, "target_cluster_name": "Prague", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Prague was the historical capital of Bohemia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Prague was the historical capital of Bohemia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Silesia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2831, "target_cluster_name": "Silesia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bohemia and Silesia were historically linked regions within Central Europe, often sharing political and cultural ties, particularly under Habsburg rule.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bohemia and Silesia were historically linked regions within Central Europe, often sharing political and cultural ties, particularly under Habsburg rule.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Palaeontology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "palæontology", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "palæontology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["palæontology"], "contexts": ["study of fossil organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "palaeontology", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Palaeontology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Palaeontology", "palaeontology"], "contexts": ["study of ancient life through fossils", "study of fossils"]}, {"entity_id": "palæontology", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Palæontology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Palæontology"], "contexts": ["study of fossil remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "palæontology", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Palaeontology", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "palæontology", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Palæontology", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Palaeontology", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Palæontology", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 1238, "stable_key": "clu_133c4f41e21517e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Palaeontology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q246729", "wikidata_description": "scientific journal of the Palaeontological Association", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleontology", "wikipedia_extract": "Paleontology or palaeontology is the scientific study of the life of the past, mainly but not exclusively through the study of fossils. Paleontologists use fossils as a means to classify organisms, measure geologic time, and assess the interactions between prehistoric organisms and their natural environment. While paleontological observations are known from at least the 6th century BC, the foundation of paleontology as a science dates back to the work of Georges Cuvier in 1796. Cuvier demonstrated evidence for the concept of extinction and how the life of the past was not necessarily the same as that of the present. The field developed rapidly over the course of the following decades, and the French word paléontologie was introduced for the study in 1822, which was derived from the Ancient Greek word for 'ancient' and words describing relatedness and a field of study. Further advances in the field accompanied the work of Charles Darwin who popularized the concept of evolution."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fossils", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1152, "target_cluster_name": "fossils", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fossils are the primary evidence studied in paleontology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fossils are the primary evidence studied in paleontology."}, {"found_name": "Extinct species", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3360, "target_cluster_name": "extinct species", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology is the study of extinct species through fossils.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paleontology is the study of extinct species through fossils."}, {"found_name": "Geology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 278, "target_cluster_name": "Geology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology is closely linked to geology, as fossils are found within geological strata and help date them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paleontology is closely linked to geology, as fossils are found within geological strata and help date them."}, {"found_name": "Fauna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 809, "target_cluster_name": "fauna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology studies the past fauna of the Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paleontology studies the past fauna of the Earth."}, {"found_name": "Botany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1098, "target_cluster_name": "botany", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology also studies fossilized plants (paleobotany).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paleontology also studies fossilized plants (paleobotany)."}]}, {"canonical_name": "mountain chains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "chains_of_mountains", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chains of mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["chains of mountains"], "contexts": ["ranges of high landforms"]}, {"entity_id": "mountain_chains", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mountain chains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mountain chains"], "contexts": ["series of mountains", "separates botanical districts"]}, {"entity_id": "mountain-chains", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mountain-chains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mountain-chains"], "contexts": ["series of mountains", "series of connected mountains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chains of mountains", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mountain chains", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chains of mountains", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mountain-chains", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "mountain chains", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mountain-chains", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 1239, "stable_key": "clu_6a6caccae4b60aaf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mountain ranges", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q46831", "wikidata_description": "geographic area containing several geologically related mountains", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain%20range", "wikipedia_extract": "A mountain range or hill range is a series of mountains or hills arranged in a line and connected by high ground. A mountain system or mountain belt is a group of mountain ranges with similarity in form, structure, and alignment that have arisen from the same cause, usually an orogeny. Mountain ranges are formed by a variety of geological processes, but most of the significant ones on Earth are the result of plate tectonics. Mountain ranges are also found on many planetary mass objects in the Solar System and are likely a feature of most terrestrial planets. Mountain ranges are usually segmented by highlands or mountain passes and valleys. Individual mountains within the same mountain range do not necessarily have the same geologic structure or petrology."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Andes are another major mountain range, and historical texts discussing geography would often compare or contrast them with other significant ranges like the Alps.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Andes are another major mountain range, and historical texts discussing geography would often compare or contrast them with other significant ranges like the Alps."}]}, {"canonical_name": "natural law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "law_of_nature", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "law of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["law of nature"], "contexts": ["fundamental principle of the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "natural_law", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natural law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["natural law"], "contexts": ["principle governing planetary motion", "fundamental principle governing nature"]}, {"entity_id": "law_of_nature", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "law of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Law of Nature", "law of nature"], "contexts": ["undulatory theory as", "fundamental principle of the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "laws_and_nature_of_things", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "laws and nature of things", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laws and nature of things"], "contexts": ["principles governing external reality"]}, {"entity_id": "laws_of_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "laws of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["laws of nature"], "contexts": ["governing forces of the physical world", "principles governing natural phenomena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "law of nature", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "natural law", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "law of nature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "law of nature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "natural law", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "law of nature", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 1240, "stable_key": "clu_bf6447e86aea3d66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laws of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21191188", "wikidata_description": "1st episode of the 3rd season of Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_law"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Economy of nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1147, "target_cluster_name": "economy of nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Economy of nature' is a historical concept that describes the interconnectedness and balance of natural systems, which is a precursor and closely related idea to the modern concept of 'Rights of nature' or 'Laws of nature' as inherent principles governing ecosystems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Economy of nature' is a historical concept that describes the interconnectedness and balance of natural systems, which is a precursor and closely related idea to the modern concept of 'Rights of nature' or 'Laws of nature' as inherent principles governing ecosystems."}]}, {"canonical_name": "africaine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "african_mainland", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "African mainland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["African mainland"], "contexts": ["source of plants and animals for African islands"]}, {"entity_id": "africaine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "africaine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["africaine"], "contexts": ["African race", "African colony"]}, {"entity_id": "afri", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Afri", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Afri"], "contexts": ["mentioned in a collection of voyages"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "African mainland", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "africaine", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "africaine", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Afri", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 1241, "stable_key": "clu_c4075b6473f6fd72", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Africa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Continent, its regions, islands, and people.", "note": "Refers to the continent of Africa and its associated regions and people.", "wikipedia_extract": "Africa is the world's second-largest and second-most populous continent after Asia. At about 30.3 million km2 (11.7 million square miles) including adjacent islands, it covers around 20% of Earth's land area and 6% of its total surface area. With nearly 1.4 billion people as of 2021, it accounts for about 18% of the world's human population. Africa's population is the youngest among all the continents; the median age in 2012 was 19.7, when the worldwide median age was 30.4. Based on 2024 projections, Africa's population will exceed 3.8 billion people by 2100. Africa is the least wealthy inhabited continent per capita and second-least wealthy by total wealth, ahead of Oceania."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Haut-Canada", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "haut-canada", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Haut-Canada", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Haut-Canada"], "contexts": ["region with population count", "region in Canada"]}, {"entity_id": "upper_canada", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Upper Canada", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Upper Canada"], "contexts": ["former British colony"]}, {"entity_id": "upper_canada", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Upper Canada", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Upper Canada"], "contexts": ["historical region of Canada"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Haut-Canada", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Upper Canada", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Haut-Canada", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Upper Canada", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Upper Canada", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Upper Canada", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1242, "stable_key": "clu_0887ac53401c2823", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Upper Canada", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "wikidata_id": "Q795427", "wikidata_description": "former British colony in North America"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Canada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 736, "target_cluster_name": "Canada", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Upper Canada was a British colony that existed from 1791 to 1841, located in the western part of the colony of Canada. It is a direct predecessor to the modern province of Ontario.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Upper Canada was a British colony that existed from 1791 to 1841, located in the western part of the colony of Canada. It is a direct predecessor to the modern province of Ontario."}]}, {"canonical_name": "étrangers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "étrangers", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "étrangers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["étrangers", "étrangers non domiciliés"], "contexts": ["foreigners in Havana", "foreigners"]}, {"entity_id": "stranger", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "stranger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["stranger"], "contexts": ["unfamiliar person"]}, {"entity_id": "emigrants", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Emigrants", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Emigrants"], "contexts": ["people leaving their country"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "étrangers", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "stranger", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "étrangers", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Emigrants", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 1243, "stable_key": "clu_22e7ecc8eb1055d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Foreigners", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "note": "The term 'étrangers' and 'stranger' can refer to anyone not belonging to a specific group, but in the context of historical texts, it often implies foreign residents or visitors. 'Emigrants' refers to people leaving a country, which is a related but distinct concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q473741", "wikidata_description": "person not having citizenship in a country"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Stewart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "stewart", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Stewart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Stewart", "M. Stewart"], "contexts": ["author of 'View of the present state of Jamaica'", "source for Jamaican import value"]}, {"entity_id": "stewart", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Stewart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Stewart"], "contexts": ["Classic writer on psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "stewart", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Stewart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Stewart"], "contexts": ["cited in Pharmacographia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Stewart", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Stewart", "similarity": 0.758}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Stewart", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Stewart", "similarity": 0.758}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Stewart", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Stewart", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1244, "stable_key": "clu_8018ceeb023c9227", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stewart", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Historical figure mentioned in scientific and psychological texts.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Stewart are mentioned; the context suggests different persons.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stewart%20%28name%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Stewart is a Scottish and English surname, also used as a given name. It is possibly derived from the old English word \"stigweard\", a compound of \"stig\" meaning household, and \"weard\", a guardian (ward), or from the Gaelic Stiùbhart meaning steward. Alternative spellings are Stuart, Steward and Steuart. The surname Stewart has large concentrations in the United States (mainly in the Deep South, and the other southern states), United Kingdom (mainly in Scotland, Northern Ireland, North East England, South West England, Cumbria, Lancashire, and Yorkshire), Canada, Jamaica, New Zealand, Australia and elsewhere that has large Scottish or Ulster Scots diaspora. The progenitor of the Stewart family was Alan fitz Flaad, a Breton knight who settled in England after the Norman Conquest. His son, Walter fitz Alan, relocated to Scotland during the Anarchy and became the High Steward of Scotland, hence the origin of the surname."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Africains", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "africains", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Africains", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Africains"], "contexts": ["people from Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "african_nations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "African nations", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["African nations"], "contexts": ["group with dark-colored skin"]}, {"entity_id": "african", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "African", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["African"], "contexts": ["person from Africa, described as black"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Africains", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "African nations", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Africains", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "African", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "African nations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "African", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 1245, "stable_key": "clu_ae50f57eb54b5d88", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Africans", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q125017449", "wikidata_description": "publication by Dr. James Lowell Gibbs, Jr. detailing peoples and cultural practices on the content of Africa"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "African explorer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4227, "target_cluster_name": "African explorer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An African explorer is a specific type of person from Africa, making it a closely related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An African explorer is a specific type of person from Africa, making it a closely related concept."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sierra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sierra", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sierra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Sierra"], "contexts": ["mountainous region", "mountainous part of Cuba"]}, {"entity_id": "sierra", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sierra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sierra"], "contexts": ["mountainous region in Mexico"]}, {"entity_id": "sierras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sierras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["sierras"], "contexts": ["devnas ſierras a otras por via."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sierra", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sierra", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sierra", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "sierras", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sierra", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "sierras", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 1246, "stable_key": "clu_89bfe44f4c856193", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sierra Nevada", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Mountain range, often associated with snow.", "note": "Refers to a mountain range, particularly the one in South America, or generally mountainous regions.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Sierra Nevada ( see-ERR-ə nih-VA(H)D-ə) is a mountain range in the Western United States, between the Central Valley of California and the Great Basin. The vast majority of the range lies in the state of California, although the Carson Range spur lies primarily in Nevada. The Sierra Nevada is part of the American Cordillera, an almost continuous chain of mountain ranges that forms the western \"backbone\" of the Americas. The Sierra runs 400 mi (640 km) north-south, and its width ranges from 50 mi (80 km) to 80 mi (130 km) across east–west. Notable features include the General Sherman Tree, the largest tree in the world by volume; Lake Tahoe, the largest alpine lake in North America; Mount Whitney at 14,505 ft (4,421 m), the highest point in the contiguous United States; and Yosemite Valley sculpted by glaciers from one-hundred-million-year-old granite, containing high waterfalls. The Sierra is home to three national parks, twenty-six wilderness areas, ten national forests, and two national monuments."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are major mountain ranges.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are major mountain ranges."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Richer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "richer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Richer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Richer"], "contexts": ["Richer", "charged by the academy to examine barometric height"]}, {"entity_id": "richer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Richer", "ca