: "Scheele was a contemporary chemist whose discoveries, particularly in the area of gases, significantly influenced Lavoisier's own work and the broader chemical revolution.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Scheele was a contemporary chemist whose discoveries, particularly in the area of gases, significantly influenced Lavoisier's own work and the broader chemical revolution.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fahrenheit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1629, "target_cluster_name": "Fahrenheit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent chemists and physicists active in the 18th century, known for their work with gases and thermometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent chemists and physicists active in the 18th century, known for their work with gases and thermometry.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "gaz_nitreux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gaz nitreux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["gaz nitreux"], "contexts": ["gas used in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrous_oxide_gas", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nitrous oxide gas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nitrous oxide gas"], "contexts": ["gas that can affect consciousness", "intoxicating gas"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrous_acid_gas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nitrous acid gas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrous acid gas"], "contexts": ["gas producing dark bands in spectrum"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrogen_gas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nitrogen gas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrogen gas"], "contexts": ["component of Earth's atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrous", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "nitrous", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrous"], "contexts": ["exhalations in clouds"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrous_oxide", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nitrous oxide", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nitrous oxide"], "contexts": ["gas used as an anesthetic", "anesthetic agent causing loss of discrimination"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrous_oxide", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nitrous oxide", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrous oxide"], "contexts": ["gas causing exhilaration"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gaz nitreux", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gaz nitreux", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nitrous acid gas", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gaz nitreux", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nitrogen gas", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nitrous acid gas", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nitrogen gas", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 1259, "stable_key": "clu_de72c1c0a596011a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nitrous oxide", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'nitrogen gas' is mentioned, the context of affecting consciousness and 'nitrous acid gas' points strongly to nitrous oxide (N2O).", "wikidata_id": "Q905750", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrous%20oxide", "wikipedia_extract": "Nitrous oxide (dinitrogen oxide or dinitrogen monoxide), commonly known as laughing gas, nitrous, or historically as factitious air, among others, is a chemical compound, an oxide of nitrogen with the formula N2O. At room temperature, it is a colourless non-flammable gas, and has a slightly sweet scent and taste. At elevated temperatures, nitrous oxide is a powerful oxidiser similar to molecular oxygen. Nitrous oxide has significant medical uses, especially in surgery and dentistry, for its anaesthetic and pain-reducing effects, and it is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines. Its colloquial name, \"laughing gas\", coined by Humphry Davy, describes the euphoric effects upon inhaling it, which cause it to be used as a recreational drug inducing a brief \"high\". When abused chronically, it may cause neurological damage through inactivation of vitamin B12."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hydrogen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 731, "target_cluster_name": "Hydrogen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide is a compound of nitrogen and oxygen, while hydrogen is a simple element. Both are gases and were studied in early chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nitrous oxide is a compound of nitrogen and oxygen, while hydrogen is a simple element. Both are gases and were studied in early chemistry.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nitrogen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 761, "target_cluster_name": "nitrogen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide is a compound of nitrogen and oxygen, and was historically studied alongside elemental nitrogen.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nitrous oxide is a compound of nitrogen and oxygen, and was historically studied alongside elemental nitrogen.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide is a gas, and 'Air' is a general term for the gaseous mixture surrounding the Earth, often used in historical texts to refer to various gases.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nitrous oxide is a gas, and 'Air' is a general term for the gaseous mixture surrounding the Earth, often used in historical texts to refer to various gases."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous substances", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2321, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide is a specific type of gaseous substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nitrous oxide is a specific type of gaseous substance."}, {"found_name": "Anesthetics", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3523, "target_cluster_name": "anæsthetics", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide was historically used as an anesthetic, and 'Anesthetics' is a category that includes it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nitrous oxide was historically used as an anesthetic, and 'Anesthetics' is a category that includes it."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lacaille", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "la_caille", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "La Caille", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["La Caille"], "contexts": ["astronomer who determined southern star declinations"]}, {"entity_id": "la_caille", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "La Caille", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["La Caille"], "contexts": ["astronomer who made pendulum measurements"]}, {"entity_id": "lacaille", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lacaille", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lacaille"], "contexts": ["astronomer who cataloged stars", "his globular nebulous cluster and nebula"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "La Caille", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "La Caille", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "La Caille", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lacaille", "similarity": 0.775}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "La Caille", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lacaille", "similarity": 0.775}], "id": 1260, "stable_key": "clu_027acd0eede3a7f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1713, "death_year": 1762, "description": "French astronomer known for southern hemisphere star cataloging.", "wikidata_id": "Q59003750", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published on March 1952"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent astronomers and explorers who made significant contributions to cartography and the cataloging of celestial objects. Humboldt's extensive travels and scientific work often intersected with astronomical observations and geographical surveys, areas where Lacaille also excelled.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent astronomers and explorers who made significant contributions to cartography and the cataloging of celestial objects. Humboldt's extensive travels and scientific work often intersected with astronomical observations and geographical surveys, areas where Lacaille also excelled."}, {"found_name": "Charles Messier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2002, "target_cluster_name": "Messier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were highly influential astronomers known for their cataloging of deep-sky objects. Lacaille's catalog was one of the earliest comprehensive lists, and Messier built upon and expanded this tradition with his own famous catalog, which is still in use today.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were highly influential astronomers known for their cataloging of deep-sky objects. Lacaille's catalog was one of the earliest comprehensive lists, and Messier built upon and expanded this tradition with his own famous catalog, which is still in use today."}, {"found_name": "Messier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2002, "target_cluster_name": "Messier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille was a French astronomer who created a catalog of southern hemisphere stars, complementing Messier's northern hemisphere focus and contributing to astronomical cataloging.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille was a French astronomer who created a catalog of southern hemisphere stars, complementing Messier's northern hemisphere focus and contributing to astronomical cataloging.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "laryngis mufculi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "larynx", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "larynx", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["larynx"], "contexts": ["lower larynx of birds"]}, {"entity_id": "larynx", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "larynx", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["larynx"], "contexts": ["voice box"]}, {"entity_id": "superior_laryngeal", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "superior laryngeal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["superior laryngeal"], "contexts": ["nerve inhibiting inspiration"]}, {"entity_id": "laryngis_mufculi", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "laryngis mufculi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["laryngis mufculi", "laryngis"], "contexts": ["muscles of the larynx", "part of the throat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "larynx", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "larynx", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "larynx", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "superior laryngeal", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "larynx", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "laryngis mufculi", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "larynx", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "laryngis mufculi", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "superior laryngeal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "laryngis mufculi", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1261, "stable_key": "clu_fa257edb0cd6ef08", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "muscles of the larynx", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6940335", "wikidata_description": "striated muscle groups which move the larynx", "wikipedia_extract": "The larynx (pl.: larynges or larynxes), commonly called the voice box, is an organ in the top of the neck involved in respiration, producing sound and protecting the trachea against food aspiration. The opening of the larynx into the pharynx, known as the laryngeal inlet, is about 4–5 centimeters in diameter. The larynx houses the vocal cords, and manipulates pitch and volume, which is essential for phonation. It is situated just below where the tract of the pharynx splits into the trachea and the esophagus. The triangle-shaped larynx consists largely of cartilages that are attached to surrounding structures by muscles or by fibrous and elastic tissue components. The larynx is lined by a ciliated columnar epithelium except for the vocal folds.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscle_du_larynx"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Throat", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 598, "target_cluster_name": "Throat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The larynx is part of the throat, also known as the voice box.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The larynx is part of the throat, also known as the voice box.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Glottis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2735, "target_cluster_name": "glottis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The glottis is the part of the larynx that produces sound, making it a directly related anatomical structure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The glottis is the part of the larynx that produces sound, making it a directly related anatomical structure."}, {"found_name": "Muscles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 265, "target_cluster_name": "muscles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity is specifically 'muscles of the larynx', so 'Muscles' is a broader category that is directly related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity is specifically 'muscles of the larynx', so 'Muscles' is a broader category that is directly related."}, {"found_name": "Human Anatomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3628, "target_cluster_name": "humani corporis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The muscles of the larynx are a component of the broader field of human anatomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The muscles of the larynx are a component of the broader field of human anatomy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "electric discharge", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_discharge", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "electric discharge", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["electric discharge"], "contexts": ["flow of electrical energy through body", "flow of electric current"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_discharges", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric discharges", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric discharges"], "contexts": ["electrical phenomena in clouds"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_discharge", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric discharge", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric discharge"], "contexts": ["transmission through steel bar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "electric discharge", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "electric discharges", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "electric discharge", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric discharge", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric discharges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric discharge", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 1262, "stable_key": "clu_bc5ad99d716712ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electric discharge", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q488071", "wikidata_description": "flow of electric charge through a gas, liquid or solid", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric%20discharge", "wikipedia_extract": "In electromagnetism, an  electric discharge is the release and transmission of electricity in an applied electric field through a medium such as a gas (i.e., an outgoing flow of electric current through a non-metal medium). The properties and effects of electric discharges are useful over a wide range of magnitudes. Tiny pulses of current are used to detect ionizing radiation in a Geiger–Müller tube. A low steady current can illustrate the gas spectrum in a gas-filled tube. A neon lamp is an example of a gas-discharge lamp, useful both for illumination and as a voltage regulator. A flashtube generates a short pulse of intense light useful for photography by sending a heavy current through a gas arc discharge."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric discharge occurs through a medium, often air, and historical texts frequently discuss the relationship between electricity and atmospheric phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electric discharge occurs through a medium, often air, and historical texts frequently discuss the relationship between electricity and atmospheric phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Elastic fluids", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 964, "target_cluster_name": "fluides élastiques", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, 'elastic fluids' was a term used to describe substances exhibiting properties similar to gases and sometimes associated with electrical phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, 'elastic fluids' was a term used to describe substances exhibiting properties similar to gases and sometimes associated with electrical phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Gas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 171, "target_cluster_name": "gas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric discharge is defined as occurring through a gaseous medium, making gas a fundamental related substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electric discharge is defined as occurring through a gaseous medium, making gas a fundamental related substance."}, {"found_name": "Luminiferous Aether", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1472, "target_cluster_name": "ethereal fluid", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In some historical theories, the luminiferous aether was proposed as a medium through which electrical phenomena, including discharges, propagated.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In some historical theories, the luminiferous aether was proposed as a medium through which electrical phenomena, including discharges, propagated."}, {"found_name": "lightning", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1878, "target_cluster_name": "lightning", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lightning is a natural form of electric discharge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lightning is a natural form of electric discharge.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Latin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Latin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["language of distinction", "language and country"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Latin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["language of translation"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Latin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["ancient Indo-European language"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1263, "stable_key": "clu_a04061d00b9f884f", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Latin (lingua Latina or Latinum) is a classical language belonging to the Italic branch of the Indo-European languages. Latin was originally spoken by the Latins in Latium (now known as Lazio), the lower Tiber area around Rome, Italy. Through the expansion of the Roman Republic, it became the dominant language in the Italian Peninsula and subsequently throughout the Roman Empire. It has greatly influenced many languages, including English, having contributed many words to the English lexicon, particularly after the Christianisation of the Anglo-Saxons and the Norman Conquest. Latin roots appear frequently in the technical vocabulary used by fields such as theology, the sciences, medicine, and law. By the late Roman Republic, Old Latin had evolved into standardised Classical Latin."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "silence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "silence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "silence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["silence"], "contexts": ["absence of sound"]}, {"entity_id": "silence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "silence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["silence"], "contexts": ["absence of sound"]}, {"entity_id": "silence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "silence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["silence"], "contexts": ["absence of sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "silence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "silence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "silence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "silence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "silence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "silence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1264, "stable_key": "clu_0bba1059bc396051", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "silence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q502261", "wikidata_description": "lack of audible sound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silence", "wikipedia_extract": "Silence is the absence of ambient audible sound, the emission of sounds of such low intensity that they do not draw attention to themselves, or the state of having ceased to produce sounds; this latter sense can be extended to apply to the cessation or absence of any form of communication, whether through speech or other medium. Remaining mute can be a symptom of mental illness. Sometimes speakers fall silent when they hesitate in searching for a word, or interrupt themselves before correcting themselves. Discourse analysis shows that people use brief silences to mark the boundaries of prosodic units, in turn-taking, or as reactive tokens, for example, as a sign of displeasure, disagreement, embarrassment, desire to think, confusion, and the like. Relatively prolonged intervals of silence can be used in rituals; in some religious disciplines, people maintain silence for protracted periods, or even for the rest of their lives, as an ascetic means of spiritual transformation. In the philosophy of perception and the science of perception, there has been a longstanding controversy as to how humans experience silence: \"the perceptual view (we literally hear silence), and the cognitive view (we only judge or infer silence)\", with prominent theories holding the latter view."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Discord", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1209, "target_cluster_name": "discord", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Discord is the opposite of silence, representing a state of disharmony and noise.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Discord is the opposite of silence, representing a state of disharmony and noise."}]}, {"canonical_name": "oscillation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "oscillation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "oscillation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["oscillation"], "contexts": ["swinging back and forth", "rapid shifting of attention"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "oscillation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["oscillation"], "contexts": ["back and forth movement", "rapid movement back and forth"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "oscillation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["oscillation"], "contexts": ["back and forth movement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "oscillation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "oscillation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "oscillation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "oscillation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1265, "stable_key": "clu_d9c913adc46705f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oscillation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_referenc