nippet": "Breeds are subdivisions within animal species, a concept relevant to zoology, particularly in the study of domestic animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Breeds are subdivisions within animal species, a concept relevant to zoology, particularly in the study of domestic animals."}]}, {"canonical_name": "paraliticos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "paraplegics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Paraplegics", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paraplegics"], "contexts": ["individuals with leg paralysis"]}, {"entity_id": "paraliticos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "paraliticos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["paraliticos"], "contexts": ["condition caused by coldness"]}, {"entity_id": "paraliticos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "paraliticos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["paraliticos", "paraHticos"], "contexts": ["people with paralysis", "affliction treated with cooked Raiz da china"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Paraplegics", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "paraliticos", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Paraplegics", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "paraliticos", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "paraliticos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "paraliticos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1269, "stable_key": "clu_e550e16a7776668d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paralysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Loss of muscle function and movement.", "note": "Covers general paralysis and related conditions like paralytic dementia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Paralysis (pl.: paralyses; also known as plegia) is a loss of motor function in one or more muscles. Paralysis can also be accompanied by a loss of feeling (sensory loss) in the affected area if there is sensory damage. In the United States, roughly 1 in 50 people have been diagnosed with some form of permanent or transient paralysis. The word \"paralysis\" derives from the Greek παράλυσις, meaning \"disabling of the nerves\" from παρά (para) meaning \"beside, by\" and λύσις (lysis) meaning \"making loose\". A paralysis accompanied by involuntary tremors is usually called \"palsy\". Paralysis is most often caused by damage in the nervous system, especially the spinal cord."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Royal Society", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "royal_society", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Royal Society", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Royal Society"], "contexts": ["British scientific institution"]}, {"entity_id": "royal_society", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Royal Society", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Royal Society"], "contexts": ["scientific institution in London", "scientific society in London"]}, {"entity_id": "royal_society", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Royal Society", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Royal Society"], "contexts": ["scientific society where paper was read", "scientific society in London"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Royal Society", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Royal Society", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Royal Society", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Royal Society", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Royal Society", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Royal Society", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1270, "stable_key": "clu_d9854b0f4e4e2430", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Royal Society", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1892802", "wikidata_description": "wildlife conservation organisation in the United Kingdom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal%20Society%20for%20the%20Protection%20of%20Birds", "wikipedia_extract": "The Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) is a charitable organisation registered in England and Wales and in Scotland. It was founded in 1889. It works to promote conservation and protection of birds and the wider environment through public awareness campaigns, petitions and through the operation of nature reserves throughout the United Kingdom. In 2023/24 the RSPB had revenue of £169 million, 2,517 employees, 13,500 volunteers and 1.1 million members (including 195,000 youth members), making it one of the world's largest wildlife conservation organisations. The RSPB has many local groups and maintains 222 nature reserves. The origins of the RSPB lie with two groups of women, both formed in 1889: The Plumage League was founded by Emily Williamson at her house in Didsbury, Manchester, as a protest group campaigning against the use of great crested grebe and kittiwake skins and feathers in fur clothing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cambridge Observatory", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4359, "target_cluster_name": "Cambridge Observatory", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Royal Society is a learned society that historically supported and engaged with scientific institutions like observatories, particularly in astronomy and related fields.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Royal Society is a learned society that historically supported and engaged with scientific institutions like observatories, particularly in astronomy and related fields."}, {"found_name": "Annales de Chimie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1778, "target_cluster_name": "Annales de Chimie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Royal Society, a leading scientific body, would have engaged with and disseminated research published in significant scientific journals like Annales de Chimie, especially concerning advancements in chemistry and related sciences.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Royal Society, a leading scientific body, would have engaged with and disseminated research published in significant scientific journals like Annales de Chimie, especially concerning advancements in chemistry and related sciences."}, {"found_name": "Comptes Rendus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1905, "target_cluster_name": "Comptes Rendus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Royal Society, as a major scientific institution, would have had interactions with and disseminated research from other significant scientific publications, including Comptes Rendus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Royal Society, as a major scientific institution, would have had interactions with and disseminated research from other significant scientific publications, including Comptes Rendus."}]}, {"canonical_name": "humana", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_person", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "human perso