"home"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "home", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "casa", "similarity": 0.907}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "home", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "casa", "similarity": 0.907}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "casa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "casa", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1272, "stable_key": "clu_064b24bb470efdb3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "house", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q83310", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House", "wikipedia_extract": "A house is a single-unit residential building. It may range in complexity from a rudimentary hut to a complex structure of wood, masonry, concrete or other material, outfitted with plumbing, electrical, and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems. Houses use a range of different roofing systems to keep precipitation such as rain from getting into the dwelling space. Houses generally have doors or locks to secure the dwelling space and protect its inhabitants and contents from burglars or other trespassers. Most conventional modern houses in Western cultures will contain one or more bedrooms and bathrooms, a kitchen or cooking area, and a living room. A house may have a separate dining room, or the eating area may be integrated into the kitchen or another room."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "villa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3016, "target_cluster_name": "villa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A villa is a type of house, often a large or luxurious one, and the terms are sometimes used interchangeably or with overlapping meanings in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A villa is a type of house, often a large or luxurious one, and the terms are sometimes used interchangeably or with overlapping meanings in historical contexts."}, {"found_name": "aldea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3050, "target_cluster_name": "aldeas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An aldea is a small rural settlement, often consisting of a collection of houses, and is thus closely related to the concept of a house as a building within a community.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An aldea is a small rural settlement, often consisting of a collection of houses, and is thus closely related to the concept of a house as a building within a community."}, {"found_name": "city", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1222, "target_cluster_name": "City", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A city is a large human settlement, and houses are the fundamental building blocks of cities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A city is a large human settlement, and houses are the fundamental building blocks of cities."}, {"found_name": "Settlement", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1250, "target_cluster_name": "villages", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A settlement is a place where people establish a community, and houses are the primary structures within settlements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A settlement is a place where people establish a community, and houses are the primary structures within settlements."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Imbecillitate", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "idiotism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "idiotism", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["idiotism"], "contexts": ["severe mental impairment"]}, {"entity_id": "imbecillitate", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Imbecillitate", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Imbecillitate", "imbecillitate"], "contexts": ["weakness"]}, {"entity_id": "idiotcy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "idiotcy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["idiotcy"], "contexts": ["severe intellectual disability"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "idiotism", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Imbecillitate", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "idiotism", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "idiotcy", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Imbecillitate", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "idiotcy", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 1273, "stable_key": "clu_cd36f3fac3125af4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "intellectual disability", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "intellectual disability", "note": "The terms 'idiots' and 'idiotcy' were historical medical classifications for individuals with severe intellectual disabilities, now understood as intellectual disability.", "wikidata_id": "Q183560", "wikidata_description": "generalized neurodevelopmental disorder", "wikipedia_extract": "SYNGAP1-related intellectual disability is a monogenetic developmental and epileptic encephalopathy that affects the central nervous system. Symptoms include intellectual disability, epilepsy, autism, sensory processing deficits, hypotonia and unstable gait. The first signs of SYNGAP1-related encephalopathy are typically gross motor delays in infancy followed by developmental delays, seizure onset and language impairment. Penetrance is 100%. Mild to severe intellectual or developmental disability is present in the majority of patients. Epilepsy is present in the majority of cases, with approximately 80-98% of patients affected by seizures.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SYNGAP1-related_intellectual_disability"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "measurement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "measurement", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "measurement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["measurement"], "contexts": ["process of quantifying magnitudes"]}, {"entity_id": "measurement", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "measurement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["measurement"], "contexts": ["act of determining quantity", "inaccuracy from local attraction"]}, {"entity_id": "measure", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "measure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["measure"], "contexts": ["determination of size or quantity"]}, {"entity_id": "measurement", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "measurement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["measurement"], "contexts": ["act of determining quantity"]}, {"entity_id": "measurements", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "measurements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["measurements"], "contexts": ["quantification of phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "measurements", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "measurements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["measurements"], "contexts": ["process of determining quantities"]}, {"entity_id": "measurements", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "measurements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["measurements"], "contexts": ["of Etna"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "measurement", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "measurement", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "measurement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "measure", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "measurement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "measurement", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "measurement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "measure", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "measurement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "measurement", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1274, "stable_key": "clu_fefae37f6b212f16", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Measurement", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q12453", "wikidata_description": "process of assigning quantities to objects or events", "wikipedia_extract": "Measurement is the quantification of attributes of an object or event, which can be used to compare with other objects or events. In other words, measurement is a process of determining how large or small a physical quantity is as compared to a basic reference quantity of the same kind. The scope and application of measurement are dependent on the context and discipline. In natural sciences and engineering, measurements do not apply to nominal properties of objects or events, which is consistent with the guidelines of the International Vocabulary of Metrology (VIM) published by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM). However, in other fields such as statistics as well as the social and behavioural sciences, measurements can have multiple levels, which would include nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio scales. Measurement is a cornerstone of trade, science, technology and quantitative research in many disciplines.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Measurement"}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Calculation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1380, "target_cluster_name": "calculation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calculation is a fundamental method used in measurement to determine quantities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Calculation is a fundamental method used in measurement to determine quantities."}, {"found_name": "Chronology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2825, "target_cluster_name": "Chronology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, which inherently involves measurement of time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, which inherently involves measurement of time."}, {"found_name": "Age", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 174, "target_cluster_name": "age", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Age is a measurement of the duration of existence, a specific attribute that is quantified.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Age is a measurement of the duration of existence, a specific attribute that is quantified."}, {"found_name": "Density", "link_type": 