llite, and thus is intrinsically linked to the Earth's surface."}, {"found_name": "Augitic lava", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1714, "target_cluster_name": "lava", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lava, such as augitic lava, is molten rock that erupts from volcanoes and solidifies to form igneous rocks, which are a significant component of the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lava, such as augitic lava, is molten rock that erupts from volcanoes and solidifies to form igneous rocks, which are a significant component of the Earth's crust."}, {"found_name": "Basalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1665, "target_cluster_name": "basalt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Basalt is a common type of extrusive igneous (volcanic) rock formed from the rapid cooling of basaltic lava exposed at or very near the surface of a terrestrial planet or a moon. It is a key rock type found within the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Basalt is a common type of extrusive igneous (volcanic) rock formed from the rapid cooling of basaltic lava exposed at or very near the surface of a terrestrial planet or a moon. It is a key rock type found within the Earth's crust."}, {"found_name": "Granite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1634, "target_cluster_name": "granite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Granite is a common type of felsic intrusive igneous rock that is granular and phaneritic in texture. It is a major component of continental crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Granite is a common type of felsic intrusive igneous rock that is granular and phaneritic in texture. It is a major component of continental crust."}, {"found_name": "Limestone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 259, "target_cluster_name": "Calcaire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Limestone is a sedimentary rock composed primarily of calcite (calcium carbonate, CaCO3). It is a common rock type found within the Earth's crust, often formed from the accumulation of shells, coral, algal, and fecal debris.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Limestone is a sedimentary rock composed primarily of calcite (calcium carbonate, CaCO3). It is a common rock type found within the Earth's crust, often formed from the accumulation of shells, coral, algal, and fecal debris."}, {"found_name": "Argillaceous schist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1986, "target_cluster_name": "schiste argileux", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Argillaceous schist is a type of metamorphic rock derived from shale or mudstone, which are sedimentary rocks. These rock types are found within the Earth's crust.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Argillaceous schist is a type of metamorphic rock derived from shale or mudstone, which are sedimentary rocks. These rock types are found within the Earth's crust."}]}, {"canonical_name": "polar regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "polar_regions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "polar regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["polar regions"], "contexts": ["cold regions at Earth's poles", "cold regions around the poles"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_seas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polar seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["polar seas"], "contexts": ["location of Captain Scoresby's observations"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_regions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polar regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["polar regions"], "contexts": ["regions around the Earth's poles"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_regions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "polar regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["polar regions"], "contexts": ["areas around the Earth's poles"]}, {"entity_id": "cold_regions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cold regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["cold regions"], "contexts": ["areas of low temperature on Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "regiões_frias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "regiões frias", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regiões frias"], "contexts": ["cold geographical areas"]}, {"entity_id": "frigid_zones", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "frigid zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["frigid zones"], "contexts": ["polar regions of the Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "polar regions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polar seas", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "polar regions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polar regions", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "polar regions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polar regions", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "polar seas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polar regions", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "polar regions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polar regions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1283, "stable_key": "clu_7f8065360d67ac5b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polar regions", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q60670", "wikidata_description": "regions around the Earth's geographical poles", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar%20regions%20of%20Earth", "country": "Antarctica, Arctic", "wikipedia_extract": "The polar regions, also called the frigid zones or polar zones, of Earth are Earth's polar ice caps, the regions of the planet that surround its geographical poles (the North Pole and the South Pole), lying within the polar circles. These high latitudes are dominated by floating sea ice covering much of the Arctic Ocean in the north, and by the Antarctic ice sheet on the continent of Antarctica and the Southern Ocean in the south. The Arctic has various definitions, including the region north of the Arctic Circle (currently Epoch 2010 at 66°33'44\" N), or just the region north of 60° north latitude, or the region from the North Pole south to the timberline. The Antarctic is usually defined simply as south of 60° south latitude, or the continent of Antarctica. The 1959 Antarctic Treaty uses the former definition. The two polar regions are distinguished from the other two climatic and biometric belts of Earth, a tropics belt near the equator, and two middle latitude regions located between the tropics and polar regions."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic is the other major polar region on Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Arctic is the other major polar region on Earth."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Macedonia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "macedonia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Macedonia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macedonia"], "contexts": ["region where Aristotle observed red snow", "region where snow was observed"]}, {"entity_id": "macedónia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Macedónia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macedónia"], "contexts": ["place where Macedonians are from", "location of a diamond"]}, {"entity_id": "macedonia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Macedonia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Macedonia"], "contexts": ["place where borrace is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Macedonia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Macedónia", "similarity": 0.827}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Macedonia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Macedonia", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Macedónia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Macedonia", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1284, "stable_key": "clu_f2bc0086a5f1bf29", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Ciacova (Hungarian: Csák; German: Tschakowa; Serbian: Чаково, romanized: Čakovo; Turkish: Çakova) is a town in Timiș County, Romania. It administers four villages: Cebza, Macedonia, Obad and Petroman. When it was declared a town in 2004, the villages of Gad and Ghilad, which it administered up to that point, were split off to form Ghilad commune. In both Romanian and Hungarian vocabularies, there are the names ceacău (in Romanian), csákó (in Hungarian) and csák (in Old Hungarian): ceacău, meaning \"tall and hard military cap made of leather or felt\"; it was used until the end of World War II by hunting and targeting troops, then only by police troops. It is therefore possible that Ciacova got its name from this word and meant a locality around a defense post. csák, meaning \"peak\". Accepting this translation, the Ciacova Fortress is explained as a \"peak of defense\" against any enemy intervention from the east or southeast."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodies", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodies"], "contexts": ["occupying regions of space"]}, {"entity_id": "bodies", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["bodies"], "contexts": ["celestial objects", "physical entities"]}, {"entity_id": "external_bodies", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["external bodies"], "contexts": ["objects outside the system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bodies", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external bodies", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external bodies", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 1285, "stable_key": "clu_3b49c8f11d41fb97", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bodies", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "natural objects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1318, "target_cluster_name": "natural objects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'bodies' can refer to physical entities, which are a type of natural object.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'bodies' can refer to physical entities, which are a type of natural object.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "polarized ray", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "colored_polar_light", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "colored polar light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["colored polar light"], "contexts": ["aurora borealis phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "polarized_ray", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polarized ray", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["polarized ray"], "contexts": ["light wave with specific orientation"]}, {"entity_id": "polarized_ray_of_light", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polarized ray of light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["polarized ray of light"], "contexts": ["light whose rotation is affected by glass compression"]}, {"entity_id": "polarized_ray_of_light", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "polarized ray of light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["polarized ray of light"], "contexts": ["light with vibrations in one plane"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "colored polar light", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polarized ray of light", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "colored polar light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polarized ray of light", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "polarized ray", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polarized ray of light", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "polarized ray of light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "polarized ray of light", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1286, "stable_key": "clu_2cbb11c0550fddff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "polarized light", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q12046176", "wikidata_description": "optical microscopy technique", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polarized%20light%20microscopy", "wikipedia_extract": "Polarized light microscopy can mean any of a number of optical microscopy techniques involving polarized light. Simple techniques include illumination of the sample with polarized light. Directly transmitted light can, optionally, be blocked with a polariser oriented at 90 degrees to the illumination. More complex microscopy techniques which take advantage of polarized light include differential interference contrast microscopy and interference reflection microscopy. Scientists will often use a device called a polarizing plate to convert natural light into polarized light. These illumination techniques are most commonly used on birefringent samples where the polarized light interacts strongly with the sample and so generating contrast with the background."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gobi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "gobi", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gobi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gobi"], "contexts": ["elevated desert region"]}, {"entity_id": "gobi", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Gobi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gobi", "Gobl"], "contexts": ["desert on caravan route", "geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "great_gobi", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Great Gobi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Great Gobi"], "contexts": ["arid Asiatic desert"]}, {"entity_id": "gobi", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Gobi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gobi"], "contexts": ["effect of the expansion of air over"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Gobi", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Gobi", "similarity": 0.786}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Gobi", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gobi", "similarity": 0.786}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Gobi", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Great Gobi", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Gobi", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gobi", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1287, "stable_key": "clu_99e4b8a8477b941b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gobi Desert", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mongolia, China", "wikidata_id": "Q42070", "wikidata_description": "desert in China and Mongolia"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Western China", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3383, "target_cluster_name": "Western China", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gobi Desert is a significant geographical feature located in and around China.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Gobi Desert is a significant geographical feature located in and around China.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "chemical decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "chemical_decompositions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemical decompositions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemical decompositions"], "contexts": ["breaking down chemical substances"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_decomposition", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "chemical decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["chemical decomposition"], "contexts": ["breaking down substances by electricity", "effect of voltaic agency"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_decomposition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemical decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["chemical decomposition"], "contexts": ["influenced by heat and electricity", "breaking down of chemical compounds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical decompositions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical decomposition", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical decompositions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical decomposition", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "chemical decomposition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical decomposition", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1288, "stable_key": "clu_d13f6bb6e5e7b821", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "chemical decomposition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q192036", "wikidata_description": "breakdown of a single entity (normal molecule, reaction intermediate, etc.) into two or more fragments; opposite or reverse process of a synthesis reaction"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Combustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 866, "target_cluster_name": "combustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Combustion is a chemical process that involves rapid reaction between a substance with an oxidant, usually oxygen, to produce heat and light. This is a specific type of chemical decomposition where a substance breaks down into simpler substances with the release of energy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Combustion is a chemical process that involves rapid reaction between a substance with an oxidant, usually oxygen, to produce heat and light. This is a specific type of chemical decomposition where a substance breaks down into simpler substances with the release of energy."}, {"found_name": "Decay", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4296, "target_cluster_name": "decay", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Decay, in a chemical context, refers to the slow breakdown of a substance, often due to oxidation or other chemical reactions. This is a form of chemical decomposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Decay, in a chemical context, refers to the slow breakdown of a substance, often due to oxidation or other chemical reactions. This is a form of chemical decomposition."}, {"found_name": "Chemical change", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 989, "target_cluster_name": "chemical change", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical decomposition is a specific type of chemical change where a compound is broken down into simpler substances. Chemical change is a broader category that encompasses decomposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical decomposition is a specific type of chemical change where a compound is broken down into simpler substances. Chemical change is a broader category that encompasses decomposition."}, {"found_name": "Chemical reactions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1769, "target_cluster_name": "chemical action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical decomposition is a specific type of chemical reaction where a single compound separates into two or more simpler substances. Chemical reactions are the broader category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical decomposition is a specific type of chemical reaction where a single compound separates into two or more simpler substances. Chemical reactions are the broader category."}, {"found_name": "Chemical analysis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2604, "target_cluster_name": "chemical analysis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis often involves inducing chemical decomposition to identify the constituent parts of a substance. Decomposition is a tool used in analysis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical analysis often involves inducing chemical decomposition to identify the constituent parts of a substance. Decomposition is a tool used in analysis."}]}, {"canonical_name": "agua azerada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "agua_azerada", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "agua azerada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["agua azerada", "agua Azera", "agua Azerada"], "contexts": ["acidulated water preparation", "steel-infused water"]}, {"entity_id": "agua_salgada", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "agua salgada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua salgada"], "contexts": ["water that makes reeds greener"]}, {"entity_id": "agua_salgada", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "agua salgada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua salgada"], "contexts": ["salt water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "agua azerada", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "agua salgada", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "agua salgada", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua salgada", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 1289, "stable_key": "clu_e346559b4a2d1303-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "water", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A transparent liquid essential for life, used medicinally and as a medium.", "note": "Includes Portuguese ('agua'), French ('eau'), and generic terms ('fluid').", "wikipedia_extract": "Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans and the fluids of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "freshwater", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2794, "target_cluster_name": "fresh water", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of a specific type of water.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Direct translation of a specific type of water."}, {"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Water and air are fundamental elements often discussed together in early natural philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Water and air are fundamental elements often discussed together in early natural philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Fluids", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 393, "target_cluster_name": "fluids", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Water is a primary example of a fluid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Water is a primary example of a fluid."}, {"found_name": "Hot water", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1484, "target_cluster_name": "agua caliente", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific state or preparation of water discussed in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A specific state or preparation of water discussed in historical texts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "juncturas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "juncturas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "juncturas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["juncturas", "junéturas"], "contexts": ["joints", "joints of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "junturas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "junturas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["junturas"], "contexts": ["joints"]}, {"entity_id": "junturas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "junturas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["junturas"], "contexts": ["ailment treated with cooked Raiz da china"]}, {"entity_id": "juncturas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "juncturas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["juncturas"], "contexts": ["joints that can be affected by dropsy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "juncturas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "junturas", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "juncturas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "juncturas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "junturas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "junturas", "similarity": 0.779}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "junturas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "juncturas", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 1290, "stable_key": "clu_7f4ade4e4f587696", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "joints", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The points where two bones meet; connections in the body.", "wikipedia_extract": "A joint or articulation (or articular surface) is the connection made between bones, ossicles, or other hard structures in the body which link an animal's skeletal system into a functional whole. They are constructed to allow for different degrees and types of movement. Some joints, such as the knee, elbow, and shoulder, are self-lubricating, almost frictionless, and are able to withstand compression and maintain heavy loads while still executing smooth and precise movements. Other joints such as sutures between the bones of the skull permit very little movement (only during birth) in order to protect the brain and the sense organs. The connection between a tooth and the jawbone is also called a joint, and is described as a fibrous joint known as a gomphosis. Joints are classified both structurally and functionally."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Feet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 134, "target_cluster_name": "Feet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Joints are the points where bones meet, and are crucial components of the foot's structure and function.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Joints are the points where bones meet, and are crucial components of the foot's structure and function.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "organs of motion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 429, "target_cluster_name": "organs of motion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Joints are a key component of the musculoskeletal system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Joints are a key component of the musculoskeletal system.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "spine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 457, "target_cluster_name": "spine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spine is composed of numerous joints (intervertebral discs and facet joints) that allow for flexibility and movement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The spine is composed of numerous joints (intervertebral discs and facet joints) that allow for flexibility and movement.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "shoulders", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1417, "target_cluster_name": "shoulders", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The shoulder joint is a specific type of joint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The shoulder joint is a specific type of joint.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "leprolos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "leprolos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "leprolos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["leprolos", "leproſos"], "contexts": ["people with red inflamed faces", "condition resembling leprosy"]}, {"entity_id": "leprosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "leprosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["leprosas"], "contexts": ["lepers"]}, {"entity_id": "leprofos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "leprofos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["leprofos"], "contexts": ["disease of leprosy", "leprosy-like conditions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "leprolos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "leprosas", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "leprolos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "leprofos", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1291, "stable_key": "clu_f5eb3d9eaa5a3353", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leprosy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Leprosy", "wikidata_id": "Q100400591", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Noli me Tangere", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3305, "target_cluster_name": "Noli me Tangere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Noli me Tangere is a historical term often used synonymously with or as a specific type of severe skin ulceration, which could include manifestations of leprosy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Noli me Tangere is a historical term often used synonymously with or as a specific type of severe skin ulceration, which could include manifestations of leprosy."}, {"found_name": "lepra", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 579, "target_cluster_name": "lepra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Leprosy is the English translation of the historical term 'lepra'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Leprosy is the English translation of the historical term 'lepra'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cibo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "comida", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "comida", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["comida"], "contexts": ["food"]}, {"entity_id": "cibo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cibo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cibo"], "contexts": ["food"]}, {"entity_id": "cibo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cibo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["cibo", "cibi", "ciborum"], "contexts": ["food", "foodstuffs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "comida", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cibo", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "comida", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cibo", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cibo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cibo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1292, "stable_key": "clu_267533c63e8dbd62-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "food", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Substances providing nourishment and sustenance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Food is any substance consumed by an organism for nutritional support. Food usually consists of plant, animal, or fungal origin and contains essential nutrients such as carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, or minerals. The substance is ingested by an organism and assimilated by the organism's cells to provide energy, maintain life, or support growth. Different species of animals have different feeding behaviours that satisfy the needs of their metabolisms and have evolved to fill specific ecological niches within specific geographical contexts. Omnivorous humans are highly adaptable and have adapted to obtaining food in many different ecosystems. Humans generally use cooking to prepare food for consumption."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Malta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "malta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Malta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Malta"], "contexts": ["island of origin for Rontecalli"]}, {"entity_id": "malta", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Malta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Malta"], "contexts": ["island with a white earth", "island and country, source of earth"]}, {"entity_id": "malta", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Malta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Malta"], "contexts": ["location of astronomical observations", "location of Saturn's rings observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Malta", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Malta", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Malta", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Malta", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Malta", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Malta", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1293, "stable_key": "clu_641bcf4ea4c0c38f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malta", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Malta", "wikidata_id": "Q233", "wikidata_description": "country in Southern Europe situated on an archipelago in the Mediterranean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malta", "wikipedia_extract": "Malta, officially the Republic of Malta, is an island country in Southern Europe located in the Mediterranean Sea, between Sicily and North Africa. It consists of an archipelago 80 km (50 mi) south of Italy, 284 km (176 mi) east of Tunisia, and 333 km (207 mi) north of Libya. The two official languages are Maltese and English but Maltese is recognised as the national language. The country's capital is Valletta, which is the smallest capital city in the European Union (EU) by both area and population. With a population of about 574,250 spread over an area of 316 km2 (122 sq mi), Malta is the world's tenth-smallest country by area and the ninth-most densely populated. Various sources consider the country to consist of a single urban region, for which reason it is often described as a city-state."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mediterranean Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 256, "target_cluster_name": "Méditerranée", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Malta is an island country located in the Mediterranean Sea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Malta is an island country located in the Mediterranean Sea."}]}, {"canonical_name": "adaptation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "coadaptation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "coadaptation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["coadaptation"], "contexts": ["mutual adaptation of species"]}, {"entity_id": "adaptation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "adaptation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["adaptation"], "contexts": ["process of adjusting to new conditions", "process of adjusting to surrounding conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "adaptation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "adaptation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["adaptation"], "contexts": ["suitability of parts for a function"]}, {"entity_id": "social_adaptation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "social adaptation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["social adaptation"], "contexts": ["adjustment to societal norms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "coadaptation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "adaptation", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "adaptation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "social adaptation", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 1294, "stable_key": "clu_f8282502a3a8da2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adaptation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q483921", "wikidata_description": "evolutionary biological process that fits organisms to their environment, adaptive trait, or state reached by an evolving population", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adaptation", "wikipedia_extract": "In biology, adaptation has three related meanings. Firstly, it is the dynamic evolutionary process of natural selection that fits organisms to their environment, enhancing their evolutionary fitness. Secondly, it is a state reached by the population during that process. Thirdly, it is a phenotypic trait or adaptive trait, with a functional role in each individual organism, that is maintained and has evolved through natural selection. Historically, adaptation has been described from the time of the ancient Greek philosophers such as Empedocles and Aristotle. In 18th and 19th-century natural theology, adaptation was taken as evidence for the existence of a deity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "natural selection", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 529, "target_cluster_name": "natural selection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Natural selection is a primary mechanism driving adaptation, where organisms with traits better suited to their environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, leading to adaptations over time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Natural selection is a primary mechanism driving adaptation, where organisms with traits better suited to their environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, leading to adaptations over time.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Evolution", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 540, "target_cluster_name": "Evolution", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Adaptation is a key mechanism and outcome of evolution, referring to traits that increase an organism's survival and reproduction in its environment.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Adaptation is a key mechanism and outcome of evolution, referring to traits that increase an organism's survival and reproduction in its environment.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Economy of nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1147, "target_cluster_name": "economy of nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This concept, often discussed in relation to adaptation, refers to the balance and interconnectedness of living organisms and their environment, shaped by adaptive processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This concept, often discussed in relation to adaptation, refers to the balance and interconnectedness of living organisms and their environment, shaped by adaptive processes."}, {"found_name": "sexual selection", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1841, "target_cluster_name": "sexual selection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sexual selection is a mechanism of adaptation, driving evolutionary change in traits related to reproduction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sexual selection is a mechanism of adaptation, driving evolutionary change in traits related to reproduction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "integration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1873, "target_cluster_name": "integration", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Adaptation is a key component of integration, as immigrants must adapt to the host society and the host society may adapt to immigrants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Adaptation is a key component of integration, as immigrants must adapt to the host society and the host society may adapt to immigrants.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Struggle for Life", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1975, "target_cluster_name": "Struggle for Life", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Struggle for Existence' is a core concept in evolutionary theory, and adaptation is the process by which organisms become better suited to their environment through this struggle.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The 'Struggle for Existence' is a core concept in evolutionary theory, and adaptation is the process by which organisms become better suited to their environment through this struggle.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Columella", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "columella", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Columella", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Columella"], "contexts": ["Roman writer on agriculture", "ancient writer on agriculture"]}, {"entity_id": "columela", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Columela", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Columela"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on plants and medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "columella", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Columella", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Columella"], "contexts": ["ancient author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Columella", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Columela", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Columella", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Columella", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Columela", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Columella", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 1295, "stable_key": "clu_7415d47257b2641d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Columella", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 4, "death_year": 70, "description": "Roman writer on agriculture, author of De Re Rustica"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conc