ie was told"]}, {"entity_id": "ferrara", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ferrara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ferrara"], "contexts": ["city associated with the Duke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ferrara", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ferrara", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ferrara", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ferrara", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ferrara", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ferrara", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1297, "stable_key": "clu_dac12304fb30d629", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Ferrara (; Italian: [ferˈraːra] ; Emilian: Fràra [ˈfraːra]) is a city and comune (municipality) in Emilia-Romagna, Northern Italy, capital of the province of Ferrara. As of 2016, it had 132,009 inhabitants. It is situated 44 kilometres (27 miles) northeast of Bologna, on the Po di Volano, a branch channel of the main stream of the Po River, located 5 km (3 miles) north. The town has broad streets and numerous palaces dating from the Renaissance, when it hosted the court of the House of Este. For its beauty and cultural importance, it has been designated by UNESCO as a World Heritage Site. The first documented settlements in the area of the present-day Province of Ferrara date from the 6th century BC."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fallar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fall", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Fall", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fall"], "contexts": ["event of human and angelic sin"]}, {"entity_id": "fallar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fallar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["fallar"], "contexts": ["speaking"]}, {"entity_id": "falling", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "falling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["falling"], "contexts": ["action causing loss of paramagnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fall", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "falling", "similarity": 0.853}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fallar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "falling", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 1298, "stable_key": "clu_58624fdf5ba87599", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fall", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context spans religious (sin), physical (falling), and abstract (loss of paramagnetism) meanings of 'fall'.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "falling stars", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2314, "target_cluster_name": "falling stars", "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "sphere of the earth’s attraction, are ignited by the velocity with which they pass through the atmosphere, but leave no residuum. These, which are known as falling stars and meteors, are periodical; b", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "meteors", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2314, "target_cluster_name": "falling stars", "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "sphere of the earth’s attraction, are ignited by the velocity with which they pass through the atmosphere, but leave no residuum. These, which are known as falling stars and meteors, are periodical; b", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "Autumn", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 911, "target_cluster_name": "Autumn", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fall is a historical name for Autumn.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fall is a historical name for Autumn."}]}, {"canonical_name": "faculty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "faculty", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "faculty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["faculty"], "contexts": ["inherent power or capacity"]}, {"entity_id": "faculty", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "faculty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["faculty"], "contexts": ["mental capacity or power", "source of perception"]}, {"entity_id": "faculty", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "faculty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["faculty"], "contexts": ["mental ability or capacity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "faculty", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "faculty", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "faculty", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "faculty", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "faculty", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "faculty", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1299, "stable_key": "clu_ad9555ea82e9f13f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Faculty", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In early modern philosophy and medicine, 'faculty' referred to an inherent power or capacity of an organ or the mind.", "wikipedia_extract": "A faculty or academic division is an academic and administrative unit within a university or college comprising one subject area or a group of related subject areas, possibly also delimited by level (e.g. undergraduate). In North America, academic divisions are sometimes titled colleges, schools, or departments, with universities occasionally using a mixture of terminology, e.g., Harvard University has a Faculty of Arts and Sciences and a Law School. The medieval University of Bologna, which served as a model for most of the later medieval universities in Europe, had four faculties: students began at the Faculty of Arts, graduates from which could then continue at the higher Faculties of Theology, Law, and Medicine. The privilege to establish these four faculties was usually part of medieval universities' charters, but not every university could do so in practice. The Faculty of Arts took its name from the seven liberal arts: the trivium (grammar, rhetoric, dialectics) and the quadrivium (arithmetic, music, geometry and astronomy).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faculty_(division)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "faculties", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 943, "target_cluster_name": "faculties", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Mental Faculties' is explicitly noted as having the historical name 'faculties', which is present in the candidate list as 'Faculty'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'Mental Faculties' is explicitly noted as having the historical name 'faculties', which is present in the candidate list as 'Faculty'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "human intellect", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1441, "target_clus