tal 'faculty' or innate capacity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In premodern discourse, 'genius' could be considered a specific mental 'faculty' or innate capacity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Coition", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "violent haling of the weak unto the stronger. And this is also the doctrine of _Gilbertus_, by whom this motion is termed Coition, and that not made by any faculty attractive of one, but a Syndrome an", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for motion"}]}, {"canonical_name": "traditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "tradition", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Tradition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tradition"], "contexts": ["passing down of beliefs and practices"]}, {"entity_id": "tradition", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Tradition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tradition"], "contexts": ["linked to superstition in naming"]}, {"entity_id": "traditions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "traditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["traditions"], "contexts": ["customs passed down"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Tradition", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Tradition", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Tradition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "traditions", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Tradition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "traditions", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1300, "stable_key": "clu_bf98cfb034384c2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tradition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antiquity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1054, "target_cluster_name": "Antiquity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Tradition' often refers to the transmission of customs, beliefs, and knowledge from past generations, making it inherently linked to the concept of 'Antiquity' as the period from which these traditions originate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'Tradition' often refers to the transmission of customs, beliefs, and knowledge from past generations, making it inherently linked to the concept of 'Antiquity' as the period from which these traditions originate."}, {"found_name": "History", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 177, "target_cluster_name": "history", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tradition is a fundamental component of historical study, representing the continuity of cultural practices and beliefs across time. Historical accounts often analyze and interpret traditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tradition is a fundamental component of historical study, representing the continuity of cultural practices and beliefs across time. Historical accounts often analyze and interpret traditions."}, {"found_name": "Doctrine of association", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 548, "target_cluster_name": "association", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Doctrine of association' (or 'association of ideas') is a philosophical and psychological concept that explains how ideas are linked in the mind. This process is closely related to how traditions are formed, transmitted, and maintained within a society or culture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Doctrine of association' (or 'association of ideas') is a philosophical and psychological concept that explains how ideas are linked in the mind. This process is closely related to how traditions are formed, transmitted, and maintained within a society or culture."}, {"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to 'Doctrine of association', 'Association of Ideas' describes the mental linkage of concepts, which is a core mechanism for the transmission and perpetuation of traditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Similar to 'Doctrine of association', 'Association of Ideas' describes the mental linkage of concepts, which is a core mechanism for the transmission and perpetuation of traditions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "proportion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "proportion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "proportion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["proportion"], "contexts": ["relationship between quantities"]}, {"entity_id": "proportion", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "proportion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["proportion", "proporcional"], "contexts": ["principle of interaction", "proportional, reasoning in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "proportion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "proportion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["proportion"], "contexts": ["relationship between quantities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "proportion", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "proportion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "proportion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "proportion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "proportion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "proportion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1301, "stable_key": "clu_5096f35b4a8d7560", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "proportion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q47694", "wikidata_description": "principle of architectural theory that describes the relationships between elements of a design", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proportion%20%28architecture%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Proportion is a central principle of architectural theory and an important connection between mathematics and art. It is the visual effect of the relationship of the various objects and spaces that make up a structure to one another and to the whole. These relationships are often governed by multiples of a standard unit of length known as a \"module\". Proportion in architecture was discussed by Vitruvius, Leon Battista Alberti, Andrea Palladio, and Le Corbusier among others. In classical architecture, the module was established as the radius of the lower shaft of a classical column, with proportions expressed as a fraction or multiple of that module. Architecture in Roman antiquity was rarely documented except in Vitruvius' treatise De architectura."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Art", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1556, "target_cluster_name": "Arte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Proportion is a fundamental principle in architectural theory and art.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Proportion is a fundamental principle in architectural theory and art."}, {"found_name": "Geometry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2912, "target_cluster_name": "Geometry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Proportion is a mathematical concept often explored and applied within geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Proportion is a mathematical concept often explored and applied within geometry."}, {"found_name": "Figure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3849, "target_cluster_name": "figure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Proportion relates to the visual representation and dimensions of figures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Proportion relates to the visual representation and dimensions of figures."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hebreus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "hebrews", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hebrews", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hebrews"], "contexts": ["people from whom Ægyptians may have learned"]}, {"entity_id": "hébreux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hébreux", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hébreux"], "contexts": ["ancient people"]}, {"entity_id": "hebreus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hebreus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hebreus", "Hebreos"], "contexts": ["people mentioned in sacred books", "Hebrew people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hebrews", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Hébreux", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hebrews", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hebreus", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hébreux", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hebreus", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 1302, "stable_key": "clu_d3d71083d7ae083b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hebrews", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Semitic people, historically associated with Judaism and early scholarship.", "wikidata_id": "Q570868", "wikidata_description": "ancient nation of the Middle East", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrews", "wikipedia_extract": "The Hebrews (Hebrew: עִבְרִיִּים / עִבְרִים, Modern: ʿĪvrīm / ʿĪvrīyyīm, Tiberian: ʿĪḇrīm / ʿĪḇrīyyīm; ISO 259-3: ʕibrim / ʕibriyim) were an ancient Semitic-speaking people. Historians mostly consider the Hebrews as synonymous with the Israelites, with the term \"Hebrew\" denoting an Israelite from the nomadic era, which preceded the establishment of the Kingdom of Israel and Judah in the 11th century BCE. However, in some instances, the designation \"Hebrew\" may also be used historically in a wider sense, referring to the Phoenicians or other ancient Semitic-speaking civilizations, such as the Shasu on the eve of the Late Bronze Age collapse. It appears 34 times within 32 verses of the Hebrew Bible. Some scholars regard \"Hebrews\" as an ethnonym, while others do not, and others still hold that the multiple modern connotations of ethnicity may not all map well onto the sociology of ancient Near Eastern groups. By the time of the Roman Empire, the term Hebraios (Greek: Ἑβραῖος) could refer to any member of the Jewish people in general (as Strong's Hebrew Dictionary puts it: \"any of the Jewish nation\") or, at other times, specifically to those Jews who lived in Judea, which was a Roman province from 6 CE to 135 CE."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Egyptians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 148, "target_cluster_name": "Egyptians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ancient Egyptians and Hebrews had significant historical interactions, most notably the biblical narrative of the Exodus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ancient Egyptians and Hebrews had significant historical interactions, most notably the biblical narrative of the Exodus.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ibrahim", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1067, "target_cluster_name": "Ibrahim", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Abraham is considered the progenitor of the Hebrews (Israelites) in religious and historical narratives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Abraham is considered the progenitor of the Hebrews (Israelites) in religious and historical narratives.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mouros", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1557, "target_cluster_name": "Mouros", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, both 'Moors' and 'Hebrews' were significant minority religious and cultural groups present in the Iberian Peninsula during the periods covered by the concordance. Historical texts might discuss them in parallel or in contrast, indicating a contextual relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While distinct, both 'Moors' and 'Hebrews' were significant minority religious and cultural groups present in the Iberian Peninsula during the periods covered by the concordance. Historical texts might discuss them in parallel or in contrast, indicating a contextual relationship.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hercra", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "heraiscus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Heraiscus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Heraiscus"], "contexts": ["famous scholar of former ages"]}, {"entity_id": "hercra", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hercra", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hercra", "Hcrcra"], "contexts": ["historian of the Indies", "author of historical work"]}, {"entity_id": "herneſto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Herneſto", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herneſto"], "contexts": ["author of tract on antimony"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Heraiscus", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Hercra", "similarity": 0.853}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hercra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Herneſto", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 1303, "stable_key": "clu_3bca53ec2d3e8d4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hercra", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient scholar, possibly an historian or alchemist.", "note": "The identification of 'Hercra' and 'Herneſto' with 'Heraiscus' is speculative based on phonetic similarity in historical texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q108624022", "wikidata_description": "Neoplatonist philosopher in Egypt"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hercules", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 495, "target_cluster_name": "Hercules", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Hercra' is likely a variant or misspelling of the classical hero 'Hercules'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Hercra' is likely a variant or misspelling of the classical hero 'Hercules'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Evangelho", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "gospel", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Gospel", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gospel"], "contexts": ["Christian teachings"]}, {"entity_id": "gospel", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gospel", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gospel"], "contexts": ["religious doctrine"]}, {"entity_id": "evangelho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Evangelho", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Evangelho"], "contexts": ["religious text", "Gospel, where the story is told"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gospel", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Gospel", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gospel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Evangelho", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gospel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Evangelho", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 1304, "stable_key": "clu_fde5f7b4a8a3ff74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gospel", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The Portuguese term 'Evangelho' directly translates to 'Gospel' and is used interchangeably with the English term in the provided contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q18055855", "wikidata_description": "protein-coding gene in the species Homo sapiens", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gospel of Thomas (also known as the Coptic Gospel of Thomas) is a non-canonical sayings gospel. It was discovered near Nag Hammadi, Egypt, in 1945 among a group of books known as the Nag Hammadi library. Scholars speculate the works were buried in response to a letter from Bishop Athanasius declaring a strict canon of Christian scripture. Most scholars place the composition during the second century, while some have proposed dates as late as 250 AD and others have traced its signs of origins back to 60 AD. Some scholars have seen it as evidence of the existence of a \"Q source\" that might have been similar in its form as a collection of sayings of Jesus, without any accounts of his deeds or his life and death, referred to as a sayings gospel, though most conclude that Thomas depends on or harmonizes the Synoptics. The Coptic-language text, the second of seven contained in what scholars have designated as Nag Hammadi Codex II, comprises 114 sayings attributed to Jesus.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Thomas"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Divinity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2345, "target_cluster_name": "Divinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gospel is a specific message or account related to Divinity, particularly in Christianity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Gospel is a specific message or account related to Divinity, particularly in Christianity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Apocalypse", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3251, "target_cluster_name": "Apocalyps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gospel of Thomas is a non-canonical text, often discussed in relation to canonical apocalyptic literature and its theological implications.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gospel of Thomas is a non-canonical text, often discussed in relation to canonical apocalyptic literature and its theological implications."}, {"found_name": "Genesis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2958, "target_cluster_name": "Genesis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gospel of Thomas presents a collection of sayings attributed to Jesus, which can be seen as a distinct, though non-canonical, narrative of early Christian thought, sometimes contrasted with the canonical Gospels which begin with the Nativity narrative (often linked to Genesis in a broader sense of origins).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gospel of Thomas presents a collection of sayings attributed to Jesus, which can be seen as a distinct, though non-canonical, narrative of early Christian thought, sometimes contrasted with the canonical Gospels which begin with the Nativity narrative (often linked to Genesis in a broader sense of origins)."}, {"found_name": "Religion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3306, "target_cluster_name": "Religion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gospel of Thomas is a religious text central to discussions of early Christianity and Gnosticism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gospel of Thomas is a religious text central to discussions of early Christianity and Gnosticism."}]}, {"canonical_name": "sex", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "feminine_sex", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "feminine sex", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["feminine sex"], "contexts": ["biological sex"]}, {"entity_id": "sex", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "sex", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["sex"], "contexts": ["biological distinction between male and female", "biological classification of individuals"]}, {"entity_id": "sex", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sex", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sex"], "contexts": ["biological distinction between male and female"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "feminine sex", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "sex", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sex", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sex", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1305, "stable_key": "clu_6038c51f34c9ca3a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sex", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Heraclito", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "heraclitus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Heraclitus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Heraclitus"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher who held the Sun was no bigger than it appeared"]}, {"entity_id": "heraclitus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Heraclitus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Heraclitus"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher, river analogy"]}, {"entity_id": "heraclito", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Heraclito", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Heraclito", "Heraclio"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher", "Emperor during a plague in Rome"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Heraclitus", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Heraclitus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Heraclitus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Heraclito", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Heraclitus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Heraclito", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 1306, "stable_key": "clu_7c37d109d40f72d2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heraclitus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -535, "death_year": -475, "description": "Pre-Socratic Greek philosopher, known for flux and fire.", "wikidata_id": "Q41155", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher (late 6th/early 5th-century BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heraclitus", "wikipedia_extract": "Heraclitus (; Ancient Greek: Ἡράκλειτος, romanized: Hērákleitos; fl. c. 500 BC) was an ancient Greek pre-Socratic philosopher from the city of Ephesus, which was then part of the Persian Empire. He exerts a wide influence on Western philosophy, both ancient and modern, through the works of such authors as Plato, Aristotle, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, Friedrich Nietzsche, and Martin Heidegger. Little is known of Heraclitus's life. He wrote a single work, of which only fragments survive."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Socrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 460, "target_cluster_name": "Socrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Heraclitus was a pre-Socratic philosopher whose ideas were discussed and critiqued by Socrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Heraclitus was a pre-Socratic philosopher whose ideas were discussed and critiqued by Socrates.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Anaxagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1057, "target_cluster_name": "Anaxagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are pre-Socratic philosophers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are pre-Socratic philosophers."}, {"found_name": "Democritus", "link_ty