ions of valuable minerals and gems."}, {"found_name": "amethyst", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2673, "target_cluster_name": "Amethyst", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both ruby and amethyst are historically considered precious gemstones, often grouped together in discussions of valuable minerals and gems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both ruby and amethyst are historically considered precious gemstones, often grouped together in discussions of valuable minerals and gems."}, {"found_name": "emerald", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 107, "target_cluster_name": "esmeralda", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both ruby and emerald are historically considered precious gemstones, often grouped together in discussions of valuable minerals and gems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both ruby and emerald are historically considered precious gemstones, often grouped together in discussions of valuable minerals and gems."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Dyarrhea", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "diarhea", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Diarhea", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Diarhea"], "contexts": ["medical condition with frequent occurrence"]}, {"entity_id": "dyarrhea", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dyarrhea", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dyarrhea", "as Diarrheas"], "contexts": ["diarrhea", "appropriate and effective for curing diarrheas"]}, {"entity_id": "diarrhœa", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "diarrhœa", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["diarrhœa"], "contexts": ["frequent loose bowel movements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Diarhea", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dyarrhea", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Diarhea", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "diarrhœa", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Dyarrhea", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "diarrhœa", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 1313, "stable_key": "clu_826da343cc3ead94", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "diarrhea", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "diarrhea", "wikidata_id": "Q4293116", "wikidata_description": "Human disease", "wikipedia_extract": "Diarrhea (American English), also spelled diarrhoea or diarrhœa (British English), is the condition of having at least three loose, liquid, or watery bowel movements in a day. It often lasts for a few days and can result in dehydration due to fluid loss. Signs of dehydration often begin with loss of the normal stretchiness of the skin and irritable behaviour. This can progress to decreased urination, loss of skin color, a fast heart rate, and a decrease in responsiveness as it becomes more severe. Loose but non-watery stools in babies who are exclusively breastfed, however, are normal. The most common cause is an infection of the intestines due to a virus, bacterium, or parasite—a condition also known as gastroenteritis.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diarrhea"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cholera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 835, "target_cluster_name": "cholera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cholera is a severe diarrheal disease characterized by extreme fluid loss and dehydration, often presenting with watery stools. It is a distinct but related condition due to its prominent diarrheal symptoms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cholera is a severe diarrheal disease characterized by extreme fluid loss and dehydration, often presenting with watery stools. It is a distinct but related condition due to its prominent diarrheal symptoms."}, {"found_name": "diſinterías", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4446, "target_cluster_name": "diſinterías", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diarrhea is a primary symptom of dysentery, and historical texts often describe them in close relation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Diarrhea is a primary symptom of dysentery, and historical texts often describe them in close relation.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "limon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lime-tree", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lime-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lime-tree"], "contexts": ["type of tree"]}, {"entity_id": "limon", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "limon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["limon"], "contexts": ["sediment deposited by floods", "lemon tree"]}, {"entity_id": "limon", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Limon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Limon"], "contexts": ["tree used for comparison of plant size"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lime-tree", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "limon", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lime-tree", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Limon", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vo