vorous mammals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "carnivora", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "carnivora", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "carnivora", "similarity": 0.74}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "carnivora", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "carnivora", "similarity": 0.74}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "carnivora", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "carnivora", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1335, "stable_key": "clu_10e5d138adb62473", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "carnivorans", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q81875", "wikidata_description": "organism that eats mostly or exclusively animal tissue", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnivore", "wikipedia_extract": "A carnivore , or meat-eater (Latin, caro, genitive carnis, meaning meat or flesh and vorare meaning \"to devour\"), is an animal or plant whose nutrition and energy requirements are met by consumption of animal tissues (mainly muscle, fat and other soft tissues) as food, whether through predation or scavenging. The technical term for mammals in the order Carnivora is carnivoran, and they are so-named because most member species in the group have a carnivorous diet, but the similarity of the name of the order and the name of the diet causes confusion. Many but not all carnivorans are meat eaters; a few, such as the large and small cats (Felidae) are obligate carnivores whose diet requires nutrients found only in animal flesh. Other classes of carnivore are highly variable. The ursids (bears), for example: while the Arctic polar bear eats meat almost exclusively (more than 90% of its diet is meat), almost all other bear species are omnivorous, and one species, the giant panda, is nearly exclusively herbivorous. Dietary carnivory is not a distinguishing trait of the order."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mammals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 266, "target_cluster_name": "mammals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carnivorans are a specific order of mammals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Carnivorans are a specific order of mammals.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "lion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1017, "target_cluster_name": "Lion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lions are a species within the family Felidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lions are a species within the family Felidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora."}, {"found_name": "tiger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1146, "target_cluster_name": "tiger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tigers are a species within the family Felidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tigers are a species within the family Felidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora."}, {"found_name": "wolf", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1969, "target_cluster_name": "Wolf", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wolves are a species within the family Canidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wolves are a species within the family Canidae, which belongs to the order Carnivora."}, {"found_name": "Bear", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 937, "target_cluster_name": "Bear", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bears are a 