and distance."}, {"found_name": "Diameter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2457, "target_cluster_name": "diameter", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diameter is a fundamental measure of size for circular or spherical objects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diameter is a fundamental measure of size for circular or spherical objects."}, {"found_name": "Depth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3639, "target_cluster_name": "depth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Depth is a dimension of size, particularly relevant for three-dimensional objects or spaces.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Depth is a dimension of size, particularly relevant for three-dimensional objects or spaces."}, {"found_name": "Extension (in physics/philosophy)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2210, "target_cluster_name": "extension", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Extension refers to the spatial extent or size of an object, a core concept in physics and philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Extension refers to the spatial extent or size of an object, a core concept in physics and philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Figure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3849, "target_cluster_name": "figure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Figure can refer to the shape and size of an object.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Figure can refer to the shape and size of an object."}, {"found_name": "Figure of the Earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3146, "target_cluster_name": "Figure of Earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Figure of the Earth' specifically refers to its overall size and shape.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Figure of the Earth' specifically refers to its overall size and shape."}, {"found_name": "Elevation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 380, "target_cluster_name": "elevation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elevation is a measure of vertical size or height.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elevation is a measure of vertical size or height."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ox", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ox", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ox", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["ox"], "contexts": ["animal with leg bone homologies"]}, {"entity_id": "ox", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ox", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["ox"], "contexts": ["extant animal found as fossil"]}, {"entity_id": "ox", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ox", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["ox"], "contexts": ["domesticated bovine animal", "animal with fused bone cylinders"]}, {"entity_id": "oxen", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "oxen", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["oxen"], "contexts": ["domesticated cattle for breeding"]}, {"entity_id": "oxen", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "oxen", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["oxen"], "contexts": ["domesticated bovine animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ox", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ox", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ox", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ox", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ox", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ox", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1343, "stable_key": "clu_984aaf62c47a10da", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "An ox (pl.: oxen), also known as a bullock (in British, Australian, and Indian English), is a large bovine, trained and used as a draft animal. Oxen are commonly castrated adult male cattle, because castration inhibits testosterone and aggression, which makes the males docile and safer to work with. Cows (intact females) or bulls (intact males) may also be used in some areas. Oxen are used for ploughing, for transport (pulling carts, hauling wagons and even riding), for threshing grain by trampling, and for powering machines that grind grain or supply irrigation among other purposes. Oxen may be also used to skid logs in forests, particularly in low-impact, select-cut logging. Oxen are usually yoked in pairs."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "vaches", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 793, "target_cluster_name": "vaches", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An ox is a castrated male bovine, often used for draft work, and is closely related to cows within the same species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An ox is a castrated male bovine, often used for draft work, and is closely related to cows within the same species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cattle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 101, "target_cluster_name": "cattle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ox is a castrated male bovine, used for draft, a type of cattle.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ox is a castrated male bovine, used for draft, a type of cattle.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cold climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "cold_climates", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cold climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cold climates"], "contexts": ["thicker fur in"]}, {"entity_id": "cold_regions_of_the_atmosphere", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "cold regions of the atmosphere", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cold regions of the atmosphere"], "contexts": ["where invisible vapour is condensed"]}, {"entity_id": "torrid_climates", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "torrid climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["torrid climates"], "contexts": ["climate where life decreases"]}, {"entity_id": "frigid_climates", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "frigid climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["frigid climates"], "contexts": ["climate where life decreases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold climates", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cold regions of the atmosphere", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "torrid climates", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "frigid climates", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "cold regions of the atmosphere", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "frigid climates", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1344, "stable_key": "clu_98f9d964f1bc21eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Climate zones", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "This cluster encompasses a range of climate descriptions, including cold, torrid, and frigid, as well as atmospheric regions where condensation occurs. 'Climate zones' is the most appropriate modern concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q58330355", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "zone torride", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2186, "target_cluster_name": "zone torride", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Torrid Zone is a specific type of climate zone.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Torrid Zone is a specific type of climate zone.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic is a primary example of a cold climate zone.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Arctic is a primary example of a cold climate zone."}, {"found_name": "temperate zone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2535, "target_cluster_name": "temperate zone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The temperate zone is a specific type of climate zone.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The temperate zone is a specific type of climate zone.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "mer intérieure", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "mer_intérieure", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mer intérieure", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mer intérieure", "mers intérieures"], "contexts": ["sea with multiple outlets", "inland bodies of water"]}, {"entity_id": "inland_seas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "inland seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inland seas"], "contexts": ["large bodies of water within land"]}, {"entity_id": "internal_seas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "internal seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["internal seas"], "contexts": ["separates botanical districts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mer intérieure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "inland seas", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mer intérieure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "internal seas", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "inland seas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "internal seas", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 1345, "stable_key": "clu_f2616d716ed568dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inland seas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q231312", "wikidata_description": "Interisland Sea in the Pacific Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seto%20Inland%20Sea", "wikipedia_extract": "The Seto Inland Sea (瀬戸内海, Seto Naikai),    sometimes shortened to the Inland Sea, is the body of water separating Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu, three of the four main islands of Japan. It serves as a waterway connecting the Pacific Ocean to the Sea of Japan. It connects to Osaka Bay and provides a sea transport link to industrial centers in the Kansai region, including Osaka and Kobe. Before the construction of the San'yō Main Line, it was the main transportation link between Kansai and Kyūshū. Yamaguchi, Hiroshima, Okayama, Hyōgo, Osaka, Wakayama, Kagawa, Ehime, Tokushima, Fukuoka, and Ōita prefectures have coastlines on the Seto Inland Sea; the cities of Hiroshima, Iwakuni, Takamatsu, and Matsuyama are also located on it. The Setouchi region encompasses the sea and surrounding coastal areas."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mer", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 921, "target_cluster_name": "mer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Inland seas are a type of sea, and thus related to the general concept of 'sea'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Inland seas are a type of sea, and thus related to the general concept of 'sea'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pacific Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 570, "target_cluster_name": "Mer du Sud", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Seto Inland Sea connects to the Pacific Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Seto Inland Sea connects to the Pacific Ocean."}, {"found_name": "seas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2596, "target_cluster_name": "seas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Inland seas are a type of sea, and 'seas' is a general term.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Inland seas are a type of sea, and 'seas' is a general term.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "intensités", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "intensités", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "intensités", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intensités", "I