son for notability, cause of death (if known), and reference. Stuart Altman, 88, American economist. Marat Amankulov, 55, Kyrgyz politician, MP (2015–2021). Yvan Aumont, 87, French engineer and journalist (Lys rouge)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Roxburgh", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1711, "target_cluster_name": "Roxburgh", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in the East India Company's botanical and geographical surveys of India and surrounding regions, often collaborating or working in overlapping spheres of interest.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures in the East India Company's botanical and geographical surveys of India and surrounding regions, often collaborating or working in overlapping spheres of interest.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Crawfiird", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1822, "target_cluster_name": "Crawfiird", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Crawfurd and William Moorcroft were contemporaries and both involved in exploration and natural history in Asia, particularly India and Southeast Asia. They likely interacted or were aware of each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Crawfurd and William Moorcroft were contemporaries and both involved in exploration and natural history in Asia, particularly India and Southeast Asia. They likely interacted or were aware of each other's work.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Nepaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "nepaul", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nepaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nepaul"], "contexts": ["plateau region for observations"]}, {"entity_id": "nepaul", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nepaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nepaul"], "contexts": ["region in the Himalaya"]}, {"entity_id": "nepaul", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Nepaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nepaul"], "contexts": ["region near Khasya mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "ne_paul", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ne paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ne paul"], "contexts": ["region between India and China"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nepaul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nepaul", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nepaul", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Nepaul", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nepaul", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ne paul", "similarity": 0.596}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Nepaul", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Nepaul", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Nepaul", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ne paul", "similarity": 0.596}], "id": 1353, "stable_key": "clu_16490be483d55331", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nepal", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Nepal", "wikipedia_extract": "Kathmandu (Nepali: [ˈkaʈʰmaɳɖu]) is the capital and largest city of Nepal, situated in the central part of the country within the Kathmandu Valley. As per the 2021 Nepal census, it has a population of 845,767 residing in 105,649 households, with approximately 4 million people in the surrounding metropolitan area. The city stands at an elevation of 1,324 metres (4,344 ft) above sea level. Recognized as one of the oldest continuously inhabited places in the world, Kathmandu's history dates back to the 2nd century AD. Historically known as the Nepal Mandala, the valley has been the cultural and political hub for the Newar people, a significant urban civilization in the Himalayan region. Kathmandu served as the royal capital of the Kingdom of Nepal and is home to numerous palaces, temples, and gardens reflecting its rich heritage.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kathmandu"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sphaeroptera barbata", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "Museum since 1831), does not grow in Nepaul, but is found on the mountains of Silhet, to the northwest of Calcutta, in lat. 24 degrees 50 minutes. The Nepaul fern, Paranema cyathoides, Don, formerly k", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Paranema cyathoides"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Daubuisson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "daubuisson", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Daubuisson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Daubuisson"], "contexts": ["author in 'Journ. de phys.'"]}, {"entity_id": "d'aubuisson", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "D'Aubuisson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["D'Aubuisson"], "contexts": ["scientist publishing in Journal de Physique"]}, {"entity_id": "daubuisson", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Daubuisson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Daubuisson"], "contexts": ["observed mine temperatures", "made observations in mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Daubuisson", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "D'Aubuisson", "similarity": 0.811}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Daubuisson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Daubuisson", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "D'Aubuisson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Daubuisson", "similarity": 0.811}], "id": 1354, "stable_key": "clu_5996ec03c2e6909a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-François d'Aubuisson", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1771, "death_year": 1841, "description": "French geologist and mining engineer, known for earth temperature studies.", "wikidata_id": "Q1303613", "wikidata_description": "French engineer", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois%20d%27Aubuisson%20de%20Voisins", "wikipedia_extract": "Jean-François d’Aubuisson de Voisins (19 April 1769 – 20 August 1841) was a French geologist and mining engineer who also contributed studies in hydrodynamics and hydrology. D'Aubuisson was born in Toulouse, the son of a namesake squire and his wife Jeanne-Francoise Dassié. His early education was at the Benedictine College of Soréze from 1779. He moved to the Artillery School, Metz in 1786 and joined military service in 1791, serving six years in Spain. After the war he retired to Freiberg where he taught mathematics and simultaneously studied at the Bergakademie under Abraham Gottlob Werner. He translated Werner's work into French with annotations."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in geology and natural sciences during the 19th century, with Humboldt being a foundational figure in many of the fields d'Aubuisson worked in.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent figures in geology and natural sciences during the 19th century, with Humboldt being a foundational figure in many of the fields d'Aubuisson worked in."}, {"found_name": "Charles Lyell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1710, "target_cluster_name": "Lyell", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential geologists in the 19th century, contributing to the understanding of geological processes and history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential geologists in the 19th century, contributing to the understanding of geological processes and history."}, {"found_name": "Georges Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 119, "target_cluster_name": "Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent French naturalists and scientists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, working in related fields of natural history and earth sciences.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent French naturalists and scientists of the late 18th and early 19th centuries, working in related fields of natural history and earth sciences."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pequet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 1, "members": [{"entity_id": "peuchet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Peuchet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peuchet"], "contexts": ["author of statistical work"]}], "edges": [], "id": 1355, "stable_key": "clu_12ce6f9920c27fcc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "André-Michel Guerry", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1802, "death_year": 1866, "description": "French statistician, studied crime and moral statistics.", "note": "The reference to 'Petiver' and 'Pequet' suggests a possible confusion or multiple figures being referenced. However, 'author of statistical work' strongly points to Guerry, whose foundational work in moral statistics fits the description.", "wikidata_id": "Q441271", "wikidata_description": "French lawyer and statistician (1802-1866)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9-Michel%20Guerry", "wikipedia_extract": "André-Michel Guerry (French: [ɡɛʁi]; December 24, 1802 – April 9, 1866) was a French lawyer and amateur statistician. Together with Adolphe Quetelet he may be regarded as the founder of moral statistics which led to the development of criminology, sociology and ultimately, modern social science. Guerry was born in Tours, Indre-et-Loire, the only child of Michel Guerry, a building contractor, whose family had a long history as innkeepers, merchants, farmers and trades-people. About 1817-1820 he studied at the Imperial College of Tours (now the Lyceum Descartes, founded in 1807) and subsequently studied law at the University of Poitiers. About 1824–1825 he moved to Paris and was admitted to the bar as a royal advocate. Shortly after, he was employed by the Ministry of Justice."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "tropic of Cancer", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "tropique_du_cancer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tropique du Cancer", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropique du Cancer"], "contexts": ["northern limit for sugar production mentioned"]}, {"entity_id": "tropic_of_cancer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tropic of Cancer", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropic of Cancer"], "contexts": ["latitude line in the Northern Hemisphere"]}, {"entity_id": "tropic_of_cancer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tropic of Cancer", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["tropic of Cancer"], "contexts": ["northernmost latitude where sun is directly overhead", "limit of trade-winds north of equator"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropique du Cancer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tropic of Cancer", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropique du Cancer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropic of Cancer", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "tropic of Cancer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropic of Cancer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1356, "stable_key": "clu_ccceef0aa71f3a85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tropic of Cancer", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global", "wikidata_id": "Q176635", "wikidata_description": "line of northernmost latitude at which the Sun can be directly overhead", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropic%20of%20Cancer", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tropic of Cancer, also known as the Northern Tropic, is the Earth's northernmost circle of latitude where the Sun can be seen directly overhead. This occurs on the June solstice, when the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun to its maximum extent. It also reaches 90 degrees below the horizon at solar midnight on the December solstice. Using a continuously updated formula, the circle is currently 23°26′09.2″ (or 23.4359°) north of the Equator. Its Southern Hemisphere counterpart, marking the most southerly position at which the Sun can be seen directly overhead, is the Tropic of Capricorn. These tropics are two of the seven major circles of latitude that mark maps of Earth, the others being the Arctic and Antarctic circles, the Equator, and the Poles (which are degenerate circles)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Equator", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 61, "target_cluster_name": "equator", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Tropic of Cancer is defined in relation to the Equator, being a specific latitude north of it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Tropic of Cancer is defined in relation to the Equator, being a specific latitude north of it."}, {"found_name": "Arctic Circle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 469, "target_cluster_name": "Polar Circle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Tropic of Cancer is one of the two major tropical circles of latitude, alongside the Arctic Circle (and Antarctic Circle), which define climate zones.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Tropic of Cancer is one of the two major tropical circles of latitude, alongside the Arctic Circle (and Antarctic Circle), which define climate zones."}, {"found_name": "Earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 533, "target_cluster_name": "earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Tropic of Cancer is a geographical line of latitude on the Earth's surface.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Tropic of Cancer is a geographical line of latitude on the Earth's surface."}]}, {"canonical_name": "fornalhas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "fourneaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fourneaux", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fourneaux"]