label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bellows are often used to supply air to furnaces for combustion."}]}, {"canonical_name": "chimie végétale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "chimie_végétale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "chimie végétale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["chimie végétale"], "contexts": ["field of chemistry concerning plants"]}, {"entity_id": "organische_chemie", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Organische Chemie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Organische Chemie"], "contexts": ["scientific publication"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_chemistry", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic chemistry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic chemistry"], "contexts": ["chemistry of carbon compounds"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_chemistry", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organic chemistry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic chemistry"], "contexts": ["facts supporting derivation of vital forces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "chimie végétale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Organische Chemie", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "chimie végétale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic chemistry", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "chimie végétale", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic chemistry", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Organische Chemie", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic chemistry", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "organic chemistry", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic chemistry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1358, "stable_key": "clu_8746a6cd9513dd10", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "organic chemistry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The early modern German term 'Organische Chemie' directly translates to 'organic chemistry', and the French 'chimie végétale' refers to the chemistry of plants, which is a historical precursor and part of organic chemistry.", "wikidata_id": "Q11351", "wikidata_description": "subdiscipline within chemistry involving the scientific study of carbon-based compounds, hydrocarbons, and their derivatives", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organic%20chemistry", "wikipedia_extract": "Organic chemistry is a subdiscipline within chemistry involving the scientific study of the structure, properties, and reactions of organic compounds and organic materials, i.e., matter in its various forms that contain carbon atoms. Study of structure determines their structural formula. Study of properties includes physical and chemical properties, and evaluation of chemical reactivity to understand their behavior. The study of organic reactions includes the chemical synthesis of natural products, drugs, and polymers, and study of individual organic molecules in the laboratory and via theoretical (in silico) study. The range of chemicals studied in organic chemistry includes hydrocarbons (compounds containing only carbon and hydrogen) as well as compounds based on carbon, but also containing other elements, especially oxygen, nitrogen, sulfur, phosphorus (included in many biochemicals) and the halogens. Organometallic chemistry is the study of compounds containing carbon–metal bonds."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemistry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 304, "target_cluster_name": "Chemistry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Organic chemistry is a subdiscipline of chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organic chemistry is a subdiscipline of chemistry."}, {"found_name": "Chemical reactions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1769, "target_cluster_name": "chemical action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Organic chemistry studies the reactions of organic compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organic chemistry studies the reactions of organic compounds."}, {"found_name": "Chemical properties", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1251, "target_cluster_name": "chemical properties", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Organic chemistry studies the properties of organic compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organic chemistry studies the properties of organic compounds."}, {"found_name": "Chemical composition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 449, "target_cluster_name": "chemical composition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Organic chemistry studies the composition of organic compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organic chemistry studies the composition of organic compounds."}, {"found_name": "Animal economy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4068, "target_cluster_name": "économie animale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, the study of living organisms and their processes (animal economy) was closely linked to what would become organic chemistry, particularly in understanding biological functions through chemical principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, the study of living organisms and their processes (animal economy) was closely linked to what would become organic chemistry, particularly in understanding biological functions through chemical principles."}, {"found_name": "Bodily functions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3004, "target_cluster_name": "organic actions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to 'animal economy', the study of bodily functions often involved chemical explanations and was a precursor to understanding biochemical processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Similar to 'animal economy', the study of bodily functions often involved chemical explanations and was a precursor to understanding biochemical processes."}, {"found_name": "Digestion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 338, "target_cluster_name": "digestion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Digestion is a key biological process that involves chemical transformations, a core area of study within organic chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Digestion is a key biological process that involves chemical transformations, a core area of study within organic chemistry."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arabian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "gulf-shvain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gulf-shvain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gulf-shvain"], "contexts": ["ocean current"]}, {"entity_id": "arabian_gulf", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Arabian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arabian Gulf"], "contexts": ["region with monsoon knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "arabian_gulf", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Arabian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Arabian Gulf"], "contexts": ["sea with prevailing monsoons", "location of monsoons"]}, {"entity_id": "persian_gulf", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Persian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Persian Gulf"], "contexts": ["gulf in ancient geography", "body of water connected to fissures"]}, {"entity_id": "persian_gulf", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Persian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Persian Gulf"], "contexts": ["sea region for pearl fishing"]}, {"entity_id": "persian_gulf", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Persian Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Persian Gulf"], "contexts": ["inlet of the Indian Ocean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gulf-shvain", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Arabian Gulf", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gulf-shvain", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Arabian Gulf", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arabian Gulf", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Arabian Gulf", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1359, "stable_key": "clu_26eafcf219710a5d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Persian Gulf", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Iran", "note": "The 'Arabian Gulf' is a modern political term, while 'Gulf-shvain' and the contexts point to the historical and geographical entity known as the Persian Gulf.", "wikidata_id": "Q37643", "wikidata_description": "1990–1991 conflict between Iraq and a 42-country coalition", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf%20War", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gulf War was an armed conflict between Iraq and a 42-country coalition led by the United States. The coalition's efforts were in two phases: Operation Desert Shield, which marked the military buildup from August 1990 to January 1991; and Operation Desert Storm, from the bombing campaign against Iraq on 17 January until the American-led liberation of Kuwait on 28 February. On 2 August 1990, Iraq, governed by Saddam Hussein, invaded neighboring Kuwait and occupied the country within two days. The invasion was primarily over Kuwait's alleged slant drilling in Iraq's Rumaila oil field, and Iraq's large debt to Kuwait from the recently ended Iran–Iraq War. After a brief rump puppet government known as the Republic of Kuwait, Iraq split Kuwait's territory, absorbing the north into Basra Governorate, and establishing Kuwait Governorate in the south. The invasion of Kuwait was met with immediate international condemnation."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arabia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 169, "target_cluster_name": "Arabia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Persian Gulf is geographically adjacent to and historically intertwined with the Arabian Peninsula.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Persian Gulf is geographically adjacent to and historically intertwined with the Arabian Peninsula."}, {"found_name": "Ormuz", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1542, "target_cluster_name": "Ormuz", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hormuz was located on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hormuz was located on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"