 "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lichens", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lichens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lichens"], "contexts": ["organisms collected on the shore"]}, {"entity_id": "lichens", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "lichens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lichens"], "contexts": ["inferior plants on mountain plateaux"]}, {"entity_id": "lichens", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lichens", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["lichens"], "contexts": ["composite organisms of fungi and algae", "low order of vegetable creation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lichens", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "lichens", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lichens", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lichens", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "lichens", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lichens", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1361, "stable_key": "clu_00a7bf01f2d39f02", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lichen", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Lecanoraceae", "note": "The term 'lichen' in early modern texts refers to the composite organism, not a single species. While many specific species and genera were described, the cluster refers to lichens in general. The family Lecanoraceae is a common example, but not exhaustive.", "wikidata_id": "Q567898", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichenaula", "wikipedia_extract": "Lichenaula is a genus of Australian moth of the family Xyloryctidae. The genus was first published by amateur entomologist Edward Meyrick in 1890. Lichenaula afflictella (Walker, 1864) Lichenaula appropinquans T.P. Lucas, 1901 Lichenaula calligrapha Meyrick, 1890 Lichenaula circumsignata T.P. Lucas, 1900 Lichenaula comparella (Walker, 1864) Lichenaula neboissi F.G. Neumann, 1970 Lichenaula petulans T.P."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Seaweeds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2319, "target_cluster_name": "Seaweeds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to algae, seaweeds were sometimes grouped with lichens in older classifications or descriptions due to shared characteristics like growth in damp environments and a perceived simplicity of form.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Similar to algae, seaweeds were sometimes grouped with lichens in older classifications or descriptions due to shared characteristics like growth in damp environments and a perceived simplicity of form."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulfate_de_magnésie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sulfate de magnésie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulfate de magnésie"], "contexts": ["chemical composition of purgative salt"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphate_of_magnesia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphate of magnesia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphate of magnesia"], "contexts": ["chemical compound studied"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphate_of_magnesia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sulphate of magnesia"], "contexts": ["crystals losing transparency when heated", "chemical salt used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sulfate de magnésie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphate of magnesia", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sulfate de magnésie", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphate of magnesia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sulphate of magnesia", "similarity": 0.997}], "id": 1362, "stable_key": "clu_00a236bcea0f4f1a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magnesium sulfate", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Also known as Epsom salt, historically used as a purgative.", "wikidata_id": "Q288266", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnesium%20sulfate", "wikipedia_extract": "Magnesium sulfate or magnesium sulphate is a chemical compound, a salt with the formula MgSO4, consisting of magnesium cations Mg2+ (20.19% by mass) and sulfate anions SO2−4. It is a white crystalline solid, soluble in water. Magnesium sulfate is usually encountered in the form of a hydrate MgSO4·nH2O, for various values of n between 1 and 11. The most common is the heptahydrate MgSO4·7H2O, known as Epsom salt, which is a household chemical with many traditional uses, including bath salts. The main use of magnesium sulfate is in agriculture, to correct soils deficient in magnesium (an essential plant nutrient because of the role of magnesium in chlorophyll and photosynthesis). The monohydrate is favored for this use; by the mid 1970s, its production was 2.3 million tons per year with production increasing to approximately 2.6 million tons in 2024."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sulfur", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1820, "target_cluster_name": "Sulfur", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnesium sulfate is a salt containing sulfur, known historically as Epsom salt.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnesium sulfate is a salt containing sulfur, known historically as Epsom salt.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "salts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1114, "target_cluster_name": "salts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnesium sulfate is a specific example of a salt, often referred to by its chemical name in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnesium sulfate is a specific example of a salt, often referred to by its chemical name in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 357, "target_cluster_name": "Alum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alum is a double sulfate salt, often containing aluminum and potassium. Both alum and magnesium sulfate are salts with sulfate anions and are used in similar applications like dyeing and medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alum is a double sulfate salt, often containing aluminum and potassium. Both alum and magnesium sulfate are salts with sulfate anions and are used in similar applications like dyeing and medicine."}, {"found_name": "magnésie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2092, "target_cluster_name": "magnésie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnesium sulfate is a specific salt derived from magnesium.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnesium sulfate is a specific salt derived from magnesium.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "lizards", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lizards", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lizards", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 2, "variants": ["lizards"], "contexts": ["animals where vision persists after hemisphere removal", "animals studied for vision after hemisphere removal"]}, {"entity_id": "lizards", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "lizards", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lizards"], "contexts": ["small scaled reptiles"]}, {"entity_id": "lizard", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lizard", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lizard"], "contexts": ["reptilian creature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lizards", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "lizards", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lizards", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lizard", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "lizards", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lizard", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 1363, "stable_key": "clu_38e92d2105bb46ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "lizards", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Reptiles, including references to the Cayman.", "note": "Spanish term for lizards.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lizard is the common name used for all squamate reptiles other than snakes (and to a lesser extent amphisbaenians), encompassing over 7,000 species, ranging across all continents except Antarctica, as well as most oceanic island chains. The grouping is paraphyletic as some lizards are more closely related to snakes than they are to other lizards. Lizards range in size from chameleons and geckos a few centimeters long to the 3-meter-long Komodo dragon. Most lizards are quadrupedal, running with a strong side-to-side motion. Some lineages (known as \"legless lizards\") have secondarily lost their legs, and have long snake-like bodies. Some lizards, such as the forest-dwelling Draco, are able to glide."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "reptiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 122, "target_cluster_name": "reptiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lizards are a specific order within the traditional classification of reptiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lizards are a specific order within the traditional classification of reptiles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Saurians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2218, "target_cluster_name": "Saurians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saurians is an older term that often included lizards and crocodiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Saurians is an older term that often included lizards and crocodiles."}]}, {"canonical_name": "sentence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "sentence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sentence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sentence"], "contexts": ["grammatical unit of expression"]}, {"entity_id": "sentences", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sentences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sentences"], "contexts": ["grammatical units of language"]}, {"entity_id": "sentence", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sentence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sentence"], "contexts": ["grammatical unit of speech"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sentence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sentences", "similarity": 0.823}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sentence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sentence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "sentences", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sentence", "similarity": 0.823}], "id": 1364, "stable_key": "clu_c8821e1f65a6f1f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sentence (linguistics)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the grammatical unit of language."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "thread", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "thread", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thread", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["thread"], "contexts": ["fine strand of material", "marker in telescope view"]}, {"entity_id": "thread", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "thread", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thread"], "contexts": ["material for suspension"]}, {"entity_id": "thread", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thread", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thread"], "contexts": ["material for twisting into a spiral"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thread", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "thread", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thread", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thread", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "thread", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thread", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1365, "stable_key": "clu_27c036b9d787624e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thread", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q244111", "wikidata_description": "species of worm", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thread-local_storage", "wikipedia_extract": "In computer programming, thread-local storage (TLS) is a memory management method that uses static or global memory local to a thread. The concept allows storage of data that appears to be global in a system with separate threads. Many systems impose restrictions on the size of the thread-local memory block, in fact often rather tight limits. On the other hand, if a system can provide at least a memory address (pointer) sized variable thread-local, then this allows the use of arbitrarily sized memory blocks in a thread-local manner, by allocating such a memory block dynamically and storing the memory address of that block in the thread-local variable. On RISC machines, the calling convention often reserves a thread pointer register for this use. While the use of global variables is generally discouraged in modern programming, some older operating systems such as UNIX were originally designed for uniprocessor hardware and often use global variables to store important values."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "vibrating string", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1465, "target_cluster_name": "vibrating string", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'thread' in the context of computer programming (thread-local storage) and 'vibrating string' can be conceptualized as entities that carry or transmit information or state, albeit in very different domains. A thread in computing is a sequence of execution that carries state, much like a vibrating string carries wave information. This is a conceptual analogy rather than a direct equivalence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both 'thread' in the context of computer programming (thread-local storage) and 'vibrating string' can be conceptualized as entities that carry or transmit information or state, albeit in very different domains. A thread in computing is a sequence of execution that carries state, much like a vibrating string carries wave information. This is a conceptual analogy rather than a direct equivalence."}]}, {"canonical_name": "magnitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnitude", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "magnitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnitude"], "contexts": ["size or extent"]}, {"entity_id": "magnitudine", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "magnitudine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnitudine"], "contexts": ["size"]}, {"entity_id": "magnitude", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["magnitude"], "contexts": ["measure of star brightness", "size or extent of an object"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "magnitude", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "magnitudine", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "magnitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnitude", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "magnitudine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnitude", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 1366, "stable_key": "clu_12c2ff6336dd7296", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magnitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The contexts provided (size, extent, measure of star brightness) clearly point to the general concept of magnitude.", "wikidata_id": "Q2091629", "wikidata_description": "mathematical concept related to comparison and ordering", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnitude%20%28mathematics%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In mathematics, the magnitude or size of a mathematical object is a property which determines whether the object is larger or smaller than other objects of the same kind. More formally, an object's magnitude is the displayed result of an ordering (or ranking) of the class of objects to which it belongs. Magnitude as a concept dates to Ancient Greece and has been applied as a measure of distance from one object to another. For numbers, the absolute value of a number is commonly applied as the measure of units between a number and zero. In vector spaces, the Euclidean norm is a measure of magnitude used to define a distance between two points in space. In physics, magnitude can be defined as quantity or distance."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "irritation", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "irritation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "irritation", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["irritation"], "contexts": ["stimulation causing a reaction"]}, {"entity_id": "irritations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "irritations", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["irritations"], "contexts": ["state of being stimulated or inflamed"]}, {"entity_id": "irritação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "irritação", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["irritação"], "contexts": ["cause of tears"]}, {"entity_id": "irritability", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "irritability", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["irritability"], "contexts": ["tendency to be easily annoyed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "irritation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "irritação", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "irritation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "irritability", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "irritations", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "irritação", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "irritations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "irritability", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "irritação", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "irritability", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 1367, "stable_key": "clu_4cf4194737f3c249", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Irritation, in biology and physiology, is a state of inflammation or painful reaction to allergy or cell-lining damage. A stimulus or agent which induces the state of irritation is an irritant. Irritants are typically thought of as chemical agents (for example phenol and capsaicin) but mechanical, thermal (heat), and radiative stimuli (for example ultraviolet light or ionising radiations) can also be irritants. Irritation also has non-clinical usages referring to bothersome physical or psychological pain or discomfort. Irritation can also be induced by some allergic response due to exposure of some allergens for example contact dermatitis, irritation of mucosal membranes and pruritus. Mucosal membrane is the most common site of irritation because it contains secretory glands that release mucus which attracts the allergens due to its sticky nature."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cortical Irritations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "discharged in the last resort. The existence of this so-called 'motor zone' is established by the lines of evidence successively given below: (1) _Cortical Irritations._ Electrical currents of small i", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of irritation"}]}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_laws", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mechanical laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical laws"], "contexts": ["laws governing physical systems"]}, {"entity_id": "physical_laws", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "physical laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["physical laws"], "contexts": ["fundamental principles governing the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_natural_law", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mechanical natural law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical natural law"], "contexts": ["law governing planetary motion"]}, {"entity_id": "physical_laws", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "physical laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["physical laws"], "contexts": ["fundamental principles governing the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "dynam