ons are inherently related to distances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elliptical orbits describe the paths of celestial bodies, and their dimensions are inherently related to distances."}, {"found_name": "Focus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3571, "target_cluster_name": "focus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In the context of ellipses and orbits, the focus is a key point around which distances are measured.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In the context of ellipses and orbits, the focus is a key point around which distances are measured."}]}, {"canonical_name": "aromoticos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "aromatic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "aromatic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["aromatic"], "contexts": ["objective fact of quality"]}, {"entity_id": "aromoticos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "aromoticos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["aromoticos", "aromaticos"], "contexts": ["misspelling in errata", "correction in errata"]}, {"entity_id": "aromatic", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "aromatic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["aromatic"], "contexts": ["fragrant quality of substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "aromatic", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "aromoticos", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "aromatic", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aromatic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "aromoticos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aromatic", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1374, "stable_key": "clu_a801eb7a7b8aa88e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aromatic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14864889", "wikidata_description": "The chemical reactions and pathways involving aromatic compounds, any organic compound characterized by one or more planar rings, each of which contains conjugated double bonds and delocalized pi electrons, as carried out by individual cells."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sapid quality", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "107.) I should prefer to say that we perceive that objective fact, known to us as the peppermint taste, to contain those other objective facts known as aromatic or sapid quality, and coldness, respect", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "is a kind of quality"}]}, {"canonical_name": "motions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "motions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "motions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["motions"], "contexts": ["internal physical processes"]}, {"entity_id": "motions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Motions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Motions"], "contexts": ["movement of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "motions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "motions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["motions"], "contexts": ["movements impressed on units", "movements from one place to another"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "motions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Motions", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "motions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "motions", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Motions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "motions", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 1375, "stable_key": "clu_d38d17f6e3a37db9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q79782", "wikidata_description": "change in position of an object over time; a body is said to be in motion if it changes its position or orientation with respect to its immediate surroundings", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motion", "wikipedia_extract": "In physics, motion is when an object changes its position with respect to a reference point in a given time. Motion is mathematically described in terms of displacement, distance, velocity, acceleration, speed, and frame of reference to an observer, measuring the change in position of the body relative to that frame with a change in time. The branch of physics describing the motion of objects without reference to their cause is called kinematics, while the branch studying forces and their effect on motion is called dynamics. If an object is not in motion relative to a given frame of reference, it is said to be at rest, motionless, immobile, stationary, or to have a constant or time-invariant position with reference to its surroundings. Modern physics holds that, as there is no absolute frame of reference, Isaac Newton's concept of absolute motion cannot be determined. Everything in the universe can be considered to be in motion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Acceleration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2252, "target_cluster_name": "acceleration", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which is a measure of motion. Therefore, acceleration is directly related to the concept of motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which is a measure of motion. Therefore, acceleration is directly related to the concept of motion."}, {"found_name": "Force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 59, "target_cluster_name": "Force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In physics, forces are the cause of changes in motion (acceleration). Newton's laws of motion explicitly link force and motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In physics, forces are the cause of changes in motion (acceleration). Newton's laws of motion explicitly link force and motion."}, {"found_name": "Elliptical orbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An elliptical orbit describes the path of celestial bodies under gravitational influence, which is a specific and important type of motion studied in astronomy and physics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An elliptical orbit describes the path of celestial bodies under gravitational influence, which is a specific and important type of motion studied in astronomy and physics."}, {"found_name": "Circular polarization", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1952, "target_cluster_name": "Circular polarization", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Circular polarization describes the path of the electric field vector of an electromagnetic wave, which is a form of wave motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Circular polarization describes the path of the electric field vector of an electromagnetic wave, which is a form of wave motion."}]}, {"canonical_name": "beats", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "beats", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "beats", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["beats"], "contexts": ["discrete units of time or sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "beats", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "beats", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["beats"], "contexts": ["intervals of silence and intensity in sound", "audible pulses in sound"]}, {"entity_id": "beat", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "beat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["beat"], "contexts": ["rhythmic pulse in music"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "beats", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "beats", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "beats", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "beat", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "beats", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "beat", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 1376, "stable_key": "clu_9593e5340f4dae37", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "beats", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to rhythmic or temporal units, can be in music, sensation, or time."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lemnos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lemnos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lemnos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lemnos"], "contexts": ["island with volcanic history", "island with a chart"]}, {"entity_id": "lemnos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Lemnos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemnos"], "contexts": ["island, source of Terra Lemnia"]}, {"entity_id": "lemno", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Lemno", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemno"], "contexts": ["island associated with medicinal earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lemnos", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Lemnos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lemnos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Lemno", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Lemnos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Lemno", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 1377, "stable_key": "clu_50babbe7ed807281", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Lemnos (Ancient Greek: Λῆμνος [ˈlɛ̂ːmnos]) or Limnos (Modern Greek: Λήμνος [ˈlimnos]) is a Greek island in the northern Aegean Sea. Administratively the island forms a separate municipality within the Lemnos regional unit, which is part of the North Aegean region. The principal town of the island and seat of the municipality is Myrina. At 477.583 square kilometres (184.396 sq mi), it is the 8th-largest island of Greece. Lemnos is primarily a flat island, but the western region, particularly the northwest, is rocky and mountainous. At 430 meters above sea level, Mount Skopia is the highest point."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Newtonian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "newtonian_law", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Newtonian law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Newtonian law"], "contexts": ["laws of motion and universal gravitation"]}, {"entity_id": "newtonian_theory", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Newtonian theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Newtonian theory"], "contexts": ["theory of light velocity"]}, {"entity_id": "newtonian", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Newtonian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Newtonian"], "contexts": ["supposition about matter and forces", "related to Isaac Newton's physics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Newtonian law", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Newtonian theory", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Newtonian law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Newtonian", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Newtonian theory", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Newtonian", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1378, "stable_key": "clu_58eb6da198a0fa21", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Newtonian mechanics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the foundational principles of physics established by Isaac Newton, encompassing his laws of motion and universal gravitation.", "wikidata_id": "Q11397", "wikidata_description": "sub-field of mechanics, which is concerned with the set of physical laws describing the motion of bodies under the action of a system of forces", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classical%20mechanics", "wikipedia_extract": "Newton's laws of motion are three physical laws that describe the relationship between the motion of an object and the forces acting on it. These laws, which provide the basis for Newtonian mechanics, can be paraphrased as follows: A body remains at rest, or in motion at a constant speed in a straight line, unless it is acted upon by a force. At any instant of time, the net force on a body is equal to the body's acceleration multiplied by its mass or, equivalently, the rate at which the body's momentum is changing with time. If two bodies exert forces on each other, these forces have the same magnitude but opposite directions. The three laws of motion were first stated by Isaac Newton in his Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy), originally published in 1687. Newton used them to investigate and explain the motion of many physical objects and systems."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Acceleration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2252, "target_cluster_name": "acceleration", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Acceleration is a direct consequence and component of Newtonian mechanics, describing the rate of change of velocity under the influence of forces.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Acceleration is a direct consequence and component of Newtonian mechanics, describing the rate of change of velocity under the influence of forces."}, {"found_name": "Action and reaction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 961, "target_cluster_name": "Action and reaction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newton's third law of motion, 'action and reaction', is a fundamental principle within Newtonian mechanics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Newton's third law of motion, 'action and reaction', is a fundamental principle within Newtonian mechanics."}, {"found_name": "Attraction (physics)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4186, "target_cluster_name": "attractions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newtonian mechanics extensively deals with forces of attraction, most notably universal gravitation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Newtonian mechanics extensively deals with forces of attraction, most notably universal gravitation."}, {"found_name": "Centrifugal force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 315, "target_cluster_name": "Centrifugal force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Centrifugal force is a concept directly analyzed and explained within the framework of Newtonian mechanics, particularly in relation to circular motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Centrifugal force is a concept directly analyzed and explained within the framework of Newtonian mechanics, particularly in relation to circular motion."}, {"found_name": "Force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 59, "target_cluster_name": "Force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Forces are the central concept in Newtonian mechanics, as described by Newton's laws of motion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Forces are the central concept in Newtonian mechanics, as described by Newton's laws of motion."}, {"found_name": "Forces of attraction and repulsion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2658, "target_cluster_name": "attraction and repulsion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newtonian mechanics provides the framework for understanding both attractive and repulsive forces, such as gravity and electrostatic forces.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship