45", "source_name": "Marcet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Marcet", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1382, "stable_key": "clu_bbc98d489281fcf6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jane Marcet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1769, "death_year": 1858, "description": "Chemist and scientific writer, author of 'Conversations on Chemistry'.", "note": "The 'Marquiz' in 'polyanthea_medicinal' is likely a misspelling or variant of Marcet, given the context of scientific citations.", "wikidata_id": "Q433093", "wikidata_description": "British writer of scientific books", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane%20Marcet", "wikipedia_extract": "Jane Marcet (; née Haldimand; 1 January 1769 – 28 June 1858) was an English salonnière of Republic of Geneva descent, and an innovative writer of popular, explanatory science books. She also broke ground with Conversations on Political Economy (1816), which explain the ideas of Adam Smith, Malthus and David Ricardo. Jane Marcet was born in London on 1 January 1769, one of twelve children of a wealthy Genevan merchant and banker, Anthony Francis Haldimand (1740/41–1817), and his wife Jane (died 1785). She was educated at home with her brothers. Her studies included Latin (essential for the sciences), chemistry, biology and history, as well as topics more usual for young ladies in England. She took over the running of the family at age 15, after her mother's death, managing the house and helping to bring up her younger siblings."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Volga", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "volga", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Volga", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Volga"], "contexts": ["river mouth in Eastern Russia"]}, {"entity_id": "volga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Volga", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Volga"], "contexts": ["ancient name for river", "river name"]}, {"entity_id": "volga", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Volga", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Volga"], "contexts": ["river, also called Rha"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Volga", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Volga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Volga", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Volga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Volga", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Volga", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1383, "stable_key": "clu_0ea6c3a07411aabd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volga River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major river in Russia, historically known as Rha.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Volga (Russian: Волга, pronounced [ˈvolɡə] ) is the longest river in Europe and the longest endorheic basin river in the world. Situated in Russia, it flows through Central Russia to Southern Russia and into the Caspian Sea. The Volga has a length of 3,531 km (2,194 mi), and a catchment area of 1,360,000 km2 (530,000 sq mi). It is also Europe's largest river in terms of average discharge at delta – between 8,000 m3/s (280,000 cu ft/s) and 8,500 m3/s (300,000 cu ft/s) – and of drainage basin. It is widely regarded as the national river of Russia. The hypothetical old Russian state, the Rus' Khaganate, arose along the Volga c. 830 AD."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astrakhan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2753, "target_cluster_name": "Astrakhan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrakhan is a major city located on the Volga River delta, near its mouth on the Caspian Sea. Historically, it has been a significant port and administrative center closely tied to the river's importance for trade and transportation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Astrakhan is a major city located on the Volga River delta, near its mouth on the Caspian Sea. Historically, it has been a significant port and administrative center closely tied to the river's importance for trade and transportation."}, {"found_name": "Caspian Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1983, "target_cluster_name": "Mer Caspienne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Volga River flows directly into the Caspian Sea, making it the primary riverine source of water for this large inland body of water. Their geographical relationship is fundamental.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Volga River flows directly into the Caspian Sea, making it the primary riverine source of water for this large inland body of water. Their geographical relationship is fundamental."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Angola", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "angola", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Angola", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Angola"], "contexts": ["location of magnetic zone"]}, {"entity_id": "angola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Angola", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Angola"], "contexts": ["place where a plant is common"]}, {"entity_id": "angola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Angola", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Angola"], "contexts": ["place of origin", "place mentioned for verification of creatures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Angola", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Angola", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Angola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Angola", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Angola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Angola", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1384, "stable_key": "clu_ce43600962a378d5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Angola, officially the Republic of Angola, is a country on the western coast of Southern Africa. It is the second-largest Portuguese-speaking (Lusophone) country after Brazil in both total area and population and is the seventh-largest country in Africa. It is bordered by Namibia to the south, the Democratic Republic of the Congo to the north, Zambia to the east, and the Atlantic Ocean to the west. Angola has an exclave province, the province of Cabinda, that borders the Republic of the Congo and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The capital and most populous city is Luanda. Angola has been inhabited since the Palaeolithic Age."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Guiné", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2507, "target_cluster_name": "Guiné", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Guinea and Angola are historical regions and modern countries in West and Southwest Africa, respectively, with significant colonial histories tied to European exploration and trade.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Guinea and Angola are historical regions and modern countries in West and Southwest Africa, respectively, with significant colonial histories tied to European exploration and trade.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "stability", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "stability", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": 