lombo, who was a student of Andreas Vesalius. Fabricius is considered a transitional figure between Vesalius and William Harvey, and his work on anatomy built upon the foundations laid by Vesalius.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Forestus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1805, "target_cluster_name": "Forestus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential physicians and anatomists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to medical knowledge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential physicians and anatomists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to medical knowledge.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pareus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2093, "target_cluster_name": "Pareus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were pivotal figures in the advancement of anatomical and surgical knowledge during the Renaissance, though Vesalius focused more on pure anatomy and Paré on surgical practice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were pivotal figures in the advancement of anatomical and surgical knowledge during the Renaissance, though Vesalius focused more on pure anatomy and Paré on surgical practice.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fontanus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2414, "target_cluster_name": "Fontanus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Felice Fontana was a significant figure in the history of anatomy, following in the footsteps of Andreas Vesalius, who revolutionized the field.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Felice Fontana was a significant figure in the history of anatomy, following in the footsteps of Andreas Vesalius, who revolutionized the field.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Apologue", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "apologue", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Apologue", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apologue"], "contexts": ["moral tale or fable"]}, {"entity_id": "apologia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Apologia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apologia"], "contexts": ["work by Pico Mirandolano"]}, {"entity_id": "apologia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Apologia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apologia"], "contexts": ["a defense or justification, likely of a scientific or medical argument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apologue", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Apologia", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apologue", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Apologia", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Apologia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Apologia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1396, "stable_key": "clu_ff56ead7221037b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Apologue' can refer to a fable or moral tale. 'Apologia' can mean a defense or justification. In a scientific or medical context, it likely refers to a defense of an argument.", "wikidata_id": "Q1416320", "wikidata_description": "statement justifying one's actions or religion (different from apologizing or saying sorry)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apologia", "wikipedia_extract": "An apologia (Latin for apology, from Ancient Greek: ἀπολογία, lit. 'speaking in defense') is a formal defense of an opinion, position or action. The term's current use, often in the context of religion, theology and philosophy, derives from Justin Martyr's First Apology (AD 155–157) and was later employed by John Henry Newman's Apologia Pro Vita Sua (English: A Defense of One's Own Life) of 1864, which presented a formal defense of the history of his Christian life, leading to his acceptance by the Catholic Church in 1845. In Ancient Greece, an apologia was primarily used as a legal defense:  The accuser would present a kategoria, or accusation, and the defendant would present his apologia, or defense. In modern usage, apologia describes a formal defense of an idea or belief, rather than against a legal charge. In both ancient and modern uses it differs from an apology, or expression of regret."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Apocalypse", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3251, "target_cluster_name": "Apocalyps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'apology' derives from the Greek 'apologia' meaning 'speaking in defense'. The 'Apocalypse' (also from Greek) refers to a revelation or unveiling, often associated with divine pronouncements and prophetic visions, which can be seen as a form of 'speaking forth' or 'revealing' a truth, thus sharing a conceptual root in the act of communication or declaration, albeit with different specific meanings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'apology' derives from the Greek 'apologia' meaning 'speaking in defense'. The 'Apocalypse' (also from Greek) refers to a revelation or unveiling, often associated with divine pronouncements and prophetic visions, which can be seen as a form of 'speaking forth' or 'revealing' a truth, thus sharing a conceptual root in the act of communication or declaration, albeit with different specific meanings."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Demonstrations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "demonstrations", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Demonstrations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Demonstrations"], "contexts": ["proofs or evidence"]}, {"entity_id": "demonstration", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "demonstration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["demonstration"], "contexts": ["proof or explanation"]}, {"entity_id": "demonstration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "demonstration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["demonstration"], "contexts": ["logical proof"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Demonstrations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "demonstration", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Demonstrations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "demonstration", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "demonstration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "demonstration", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1397, "stable_key": "clu_dd0eb8efdf04f350", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Demonstration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a proof, evidence, or logical explanation.", "wikidata_id": "Q175331", "wikidata_description": "collective action (e.g. march) by people who are supporting or protesting against a cause of concern", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political%20demonstration", "wikipedia_extract": "A political demonstration is an action by a mass group or collection of groups of people in favor of a political or other cause or people partaking in a protest against a cause of concern; it often consists of walking in a mass march formation and either beginning with or meeting at a designated endpoint, or rally, in order to hear speakers. It is different from mass meeting. Demonstrations may include actions such as blockades and sit-ins. They can be either nonviolent or violent, with participants often referring to violent demonstrations as \"militant.\" Depending on the circumstances, a demonstration may begin as nonviolent and escalate to violence. Law enforcement, such as riot police, may become involved in these situations. Police involvement at protests is ideally to protect the participants and their right to assemble."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "study", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1266, "target_cluster_name": "study", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstration is a method used within studies to prove or illustrate a concept or theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Demonstration is a method used within studies to prove or illustrate a concept or theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bodily action", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3565, "target_cluster_name": "bodily action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstrations are a form of collective bodily action.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Demonstrations are a form of collective bodily action."}, {"found_name": "Conflict", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3582, "target_cluster_name": "conflict", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstrations often arise from or lead to conflict.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Demonstrations often arise from or lead to conflict."}, {"found_name": "Government", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2489, "target_cluster_name": "Government", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstrations are a form of political action often directed towards or in response to government.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Demonstrations are a form of political action often directed towards or in response to government."}, {"found_name": "truth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1924, "target_cluster_name": "truth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstration is a method of establishing truth, especially in logic and mathematics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Demonstration is a method of establishing truth, especially in logic and mathematics.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Magitians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "magitians", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Magitians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magitians"], "contexts": ["those who practice magic"]}, {"entity_id": "mago", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mago", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["mago"], "contexts": ["wise men who adored Jesus"]}, {"entity_id": "magiſtri", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Magiſtri", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magiſtri"], "contexts": ["masters of medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magitians", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "mago", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magitians", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Magiſtri", "similarity": 0.859}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mago", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Magiſtri", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 1398, "stable_key": "clu_7ce5b60eee605d8c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magician", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Practitioner of magic, wise man, or master of a craft.", "note": "The cluster includes terms that can refer to practitioners of magic, wise men (like the Magi), and masters of a trade or discipline, suggesting a broad meaning that might not always align strictly with 'magician'."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Agrippa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3330, "target_cluster_name": "Agrippa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa was a highly influential figure in the Renaissance occult tradition, authoring 'De Occulta Philosophia libri tres' (Three Books of Occult Philosophy). His work directly engaged with and systematized magical theories, making him a key figure for anyone studying 'Magitians' or similar concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa was a highly influential figure in the Renaissance occult tradition, authoring 'De Occulta Philosophia libri tres' (Three Books of Occult Philosophy). His work directly engaged with and systematized magical theories, making him a key figure for anyone studying 'Magitians' or similar concepts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Servius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "servius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Servius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Servius"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on Centaur origins"]}, {"entity_id": "servius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Servius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Servius"], "contexts": ["ancient commentator on Virgil"]}, {"entity_id": "servius", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Servius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Servius"], "contexts": ["quoted regarding Ethiopia and India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Servius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Servius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Servius", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Servius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Servius", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Servius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1399, "stable_key": "clu_f9b323fff3d353b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Servius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman grammarian and commentator on Virgil", "note": "The contexts describe Servius as an ancient authority on classical literature and history, fitting the identification of Servius Grammaticus, the Roman commentator on Virgil.", "wikipedia_extract": "Galba ( GAL-bə; born Servius Sulpicius Galba; 24 December 3 BC – 15 January AD 69) was Roman emperor, ruling for 7 months from 8 June AD 68 to 15 January 69. He was the first emperor in the Year of the Four Emperors and assumed the throne following Emperor Nero's suicide. Born into a wealthy family, Galba held at various times the positions of praetor, consul, and governor of the provinces of Gallia Aquitania, Germania Superior, and Africa during the first half of the first century AD. He retired from his positions during the latter part of Claudius' reign (with the advent of Agrippina the Younger), but Nero later granted him the governorship of Hispania. Taking advantage of the defeat of Vindex's rebellion and Nero's suicide, he became emperor with the support of the Praetorian Guard. Galba's physical weakness and general apathy led to his rule being dominated by favorites.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galba"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Galba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3872, "target_cluster_name": "Galba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Servius Sulpicius Galba was the birth name of the Roman emperor Galba.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Servius Sulpicius Galba was the birth name of the Roman emperor Galba."}, {"found_name": "Macrobius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2671, "target_cluster_name": "Macrobius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Servius is described as a grammarian who authored commentaries on Virgil, a classical author whose works Macrobius also commented on. This suggests a shared intellectual tradition and area of study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Servius is described as a grammarian who authored commentaries on Virgil, a classical author whose works Macrobius also commented on. This suggests a shared intellectual tradition and area of study.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Metaphors", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "metaphors", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Metaphors", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Metaphors"], "contexts": ["figurative language"]}, {"entity_id": "metaphor", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "metaphor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["metaphor"], "contexts": ["figure of speech comparing dissimilar things"]}, {"entity_id": "metaphors", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "metaphors", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["metaphors"], "contexts": ["figurative expressions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metaphors", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "metaphor", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metaphors", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metaphors", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "metaphor", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metaphors", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 1400, "stable_key": "clu_b1a56223b097d2fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metaphor", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q13847217", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaphorocera", "wikipedia_extract": "Metaphorocera is a genus of flies in the family Tachinidae."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Resemblance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1929, "target_cluster_name": "Resemblance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphors function by drawing resemblances between two seemingly dissimilar things.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Metaphors function by drawing resemblances between two seemingly dissimilar things.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "image", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2026, "target_cluster_name": "image", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Images are often used metaphorically to convey meaning, creating a strong conceptual link between the two.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Images are often used metaphorically to convey meaning, creating a strong conceptual link between the two.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "forms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2727, "target_cluster_name": "forms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of 'form' can be related to how ideas or concepts are expressed or understood, similar to how metaphors shape understanding.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The concept of 'form' can be related to how ideas or concepts are expressed or understood, similar to how metaphors shape understanding.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Geber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "geber", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Geber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geber"], "contexts": ["learned Arab who accepted Prophet's doctrine"]}, {"entity_id": "geber", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Geber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geber"], "contexts": ["authority on metal generation"]}, {"entity_id": "geber", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Geber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geber"], "contexts": ["learned author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Geber", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Geber", "similarity": 0.665}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Geber", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geber", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Geber", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geber", "similarity": 0.665}], "id": 1401, "stable_key": "clu_8fe209a87a482b87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jabir ibn Hayyan", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 721, "death_year": 815, "description": "Influential alchemist and chemist, credited with early scientific methods.", "note": "Often referred to by the Latinized name 'Geber' in European texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q167852", "wikidata_description": "8th-century Islamic alchemist and author", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jabir%20ibn%20Hayyan", "wikipedia_extract": "Abū Mūsā Jābir ibn Ḥayyān (Arabic: أَبو موسى جابِر بِن حَيّان, variously called al-Ṣūfī, al-Azdī, al-Kūfī, or al-Ṭūsī), died c. 806−816, is the purported author of a large number of works in Arabic, often called the Jabirian corpus. The c. 215 treatises that survive today mainly deal with alchemy and chemistry, magic, and Shi'ite religious philosophy. However, the original scope of the corpus was vast, covering a wide range of topics ranging from cosmology, astronomy and astrology, over medicine, pharmacology, zoology and botany, to metaphysics, logic, and grammar. The works attributed to Jabir, which are tentatively dated to c."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 609, "target_cluster_name": "Chymicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jabir ibn Hayyan's work is foundational to the development of chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jabir ibn Hayyan's work is foundational to the development of chemistry."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Adrian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "adrian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Adrian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Adrian"], "contexts": ["Emperor, determined for Aristotle"]}, {"entity_id": "adrian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Adrian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Adrian"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "adrianus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Adrianus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Adrianus"], "contexts": ["Pontifex Sumnus, cited for plague observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Adrian", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Adrian", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Adrian", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Adrianus", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Adrian", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Adrianus", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 1402, "stable_key": "clu_6310617bc6af6c16", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hadrian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 76, "death_year": 138, "description": "Roman Emperor, cited for observations related to medicine and plague.", "note": "Also appears as 'Adrianus' in Latin texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q43387", "wikidata_description": "central district and city in Edirne Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edirne", "wikipedia_extract": "Edirne (US: ; Turkish: [e.ˈdiɾ.ne]), historically known as Orestias, Orestiada, Adrianople, or Adriana is a city in Turkey, in the northwestern part of the province of Edirne in Eastern Thrace. Situated 7 km (4.3 mi) from the Greek and 20 km (12 mi) from the Bulgarian borders, Edirne was the second capital city of the Ottoman Empire from the 1360s to 1453, before Constantinople became its capital. The city is a commercial centre for woven textiles, silks, carpets and agricultural products and has a growing tourism industry. It is the seat of Edirne Province and Edirne District. Its population is 180,002 (2022). The city was founded and named after the Roman emperor Hadrian as Hadrianopolis (Adrianople in English, ; Ἁδριανούπολις in Greek) on the site of the Greek city of Orestias, which was itself founded on an earlier Thracian settlement named Uskudama."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "prejudice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "prejudice", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "prejudice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["prejudice"], "contexts": ["preconceived opinion"]}, {"entity_id": "préjugé", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "préjugé", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["préjugé"], "contexts": ["unfounded belief or opinion"]}, {"entity_id": "prejudices", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "prejudices", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["prejudices"], "contexts": ["part of character, hard to escape"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "prejudice", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "préjugé", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "prejudice", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "prejudices", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "préjugé", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "prejudices", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 1403, "stable_key": "clu_4f6ed1fef56cbefe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "prejudice", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q742736", "wikidata_description": "partiality that prevents objective or alternative consideration of an issue or situation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bias", "wikipedia_extract": "Bias is a disproportionate weight in favor of or against an idea or thing, usually in a way that is inaccurate, closed-minded, prejudicial, or unfair. Biases can be innate or learned. People may develop biases for or against an individual, a group, or a belief. In science and engineering, a bias is a systematic error. Statistical bias results from an unfair sampling of a population, or from an estimation process that does not give accurate results on average. The word appears to derive from Old Provençal into Old French biais, \"sideways, askance, against the grain\"."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fortune", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "fortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fortune"], "contexts": ["chance or luck"]}, {"entity_id": "fortune", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Fortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fortune"], "contexts": ["astrological influence"]}, {"entity_id": "good_fortune", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "good fortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["good fortune"], "contexts": ["luck or favorable circumstances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "fortune", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Fortune", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "fortune", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "good fortune", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 1404, "stable_key": "clu_e606642a4d4c9890", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fortune", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3811, "target_cluster_name": "chance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fortune is often seen as a manifestation or synonym of chance, particularly in pre-modern thought where the two concepts were closely intertwined and often used interchangeably to describe events beyond human control.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fortune is often seen as a manifestation or synonym of chance, particularly in pre-modern thought where the two concepts were closely intertwined and often used interchangeably to describe events beyond human control."}, {"found_name": "Astrological Influence", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3953, "target_cluster_name": "Infortune", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In pre-modern contexts, fortune was frequently understood as being directly influenced or determined by astrological forces and celestial configurations. 'Infortune' is explicitly listed as a related term, highlighting this connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In pre-modern contexts, fortune was frequently understood as being directly influenced or determined by astrological forces and celestial configurations. 'Infortune' is explicitly listed as a related term, highlighting this connection."}, {"found_name": "Ambition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4281, "target_cluster_name": "Ambition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambition is often portrayed as a force that either seeks to control or is subject to fortune. The pursuit of ambition is frequently discussed in relation to the whims of fortune, either as a means to achieve it or as a potential downfall.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ambition is often portrayed as a force that either seeks to control or is subject to fortune. The pursuit of ambition is frequently discussed in relation to the whims of fortune, either as a means to achieve it or as a potential downfall."}, {"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Belief systems, particularly religious and philosophical ones, often incorporated notions of fortune, fate, or divine providence, shaping how individuals understood and reacted to their circumstances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Belief systems, particularly religious and philosophical ones, often incorporated notions of fortune, fate, or divine providence, shaping how individuals understood and reacted to their circumstances."}, {"found_name": "Free will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3567, "target_cluster_name": "free-will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The relationship between free will and fortune (or fate) was a perennial philosophical debate. Texts often explored the extent to which human agency could overcome or was subject to predetermined fortune.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The relationship between free will and fortune (or fate) was a perennial philosophical debate. Texts often explored the extent to which human agency could overcome or was subject to predetermined fortune."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Triangle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "triangle", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Triangle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Triangle"], "contexts": ["geometric shape with three angles"]}, {"entity_id": "triangles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "triangles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["triangles"], "contexts": ["used for linking coasts"]}, {"entity_id": "triangles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "triangles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["triangles"], "contexts": ["geometric shapes for surveying"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Triangle", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "triangles", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Triangle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "triangles", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "triangles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "triangles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1405, "stable_key": "clu_cea0ef9703be4f6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "triangle", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q201735", "wikidata_description": "idiophone type of musical instrument in the percussion family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triangle%20%28musical%20instrument%29", "wikipedia_extract": "The triangle, or  musical triangle, is a musical instrument in the percussion family, classified as an idiophone in the Hornbostel-Sachs classification system. Triangles are made from a variety of metals including aluminum, beryllium copper, brass, bronze, iron, and steel. The metal is bent into a triangular shape with one open end. The instrument is usually held by a loop of some form of thread or wire at the top curve to enable the triangle to vibrate, and it is struck with a metal rod called a \"beater\". The triangle theoretically has indefinite pitch, and produces a plurality of overtones when struck with an appropriate beater. Iconography is the primary source for knowledge of the history of the triangle, and provides insight into the musical and social context in which the instrument developed."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Musical instruments", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2894, "target_cluster_name": "musical instruments", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The triangle is a type of musical instrument.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The triangle is a type of musical instrument."}, {"found_name": "prism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1874, "target_cluster_name": "prism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The most common type of optical prism is a triangular prism, making the geometric shape closely related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The most common type of optical prism is a triangular prism, making the geometric shape closely related.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "darkness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "darkness", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "darkness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["darkness"], "contexts": ["negative quality, from evil principle"]}, {"entity_id": "dark", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "dark", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dark"], "contexts": ["absence of light"]}, {"entity_id": "darkness", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "darkness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["darkness"], "contexts": ["absence of light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "darkness", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "dark", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "darkness", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "darkness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "dark", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "darkness", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 1406, "stable_key": "clu_3a8a7dcd2371dc90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "darkness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "congelation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "congelation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "congelation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["congelation"], "contexts": ["process of freezing"]}, {"entity_id": "congélation", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "congélation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["congélation"], "contexts": ["process of freezing"]}, {"entity_id": "congelation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "congelation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["congelation"], "contexts": ["process of freezing into ice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "congelation", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "congélation", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "congelation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "congelation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "congélation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "congelation", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 1407, "stable_key": "clu_2338c1fe4efe3f45", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Freezing", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1036696", "wikidata_description": "condition caused when the human body gets too cold", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothermia", "wikipedia_extract": "Hypothermia is defined as a body core temperature below 35.0 °C (95.0 °F) in humans. Symptoms depend on the temperature. In mild hypothermia, there is shivering and mental confusion. In moderate hypothermia, shivering stops and confusion increases. In severe hypothermia, there may be hallucinations and paradoxical undressing, or the victim's removal of their clothing, as well as an increased risk of the heart's stopping. Hypothermia has two main types of causes."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fusion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 433, "target_cluster_name": "fusion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Freezing is the inverse process of melting, occurring at the same temperature for a given substance under constant pressure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Freezing is the inverse process of melting, occurring at the same temperature for a given substance under constant pressure.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "théorie de la chaleur", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1360, "target_cluster_name": "théorie de la chaleur", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Freezing is a phase transition that involves the release or absorption of heat, relevant to the theory of heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Freezing is a phase transition that involves the release or absorption of heat, relevant to the theory of heat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Animal heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3893, "target_cluster_name": "animal heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical texts often discussed the relationship between external cold (freezing) and the body's internal heat production or loss.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historical texts often discussed the relationship between external cold (freezing) and the body's internal heat production or loss."}, {"found_name": "Cooling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 720, "target_cluster_name": "cooling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is the process that leads to freezing and hypothermia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cooling is the process that leads to freezing and hypothermia."}, {"found_name": "Heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 197, "target_cluster_name": "calor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Heat is the inverse concept to freezing and is central to understanding temperature regulation and its loss.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Heat is the inverse concept to freezing and is central to understanding temperature regulation and its loss."}, {"found_name": "Isotherms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2324, "target_cluster_name": "isothermal lines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Isotherms represent lines of equal temperature, which are relevant when discussing temperature gradients and the effects of cold.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Isotherms represent lines of equal temperature, which are relevant when discussing temperature gradients and the effects of cold."}, {"found_name": "Conduction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2567, "target_cluster_name": "conduction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conduction is a primary mechanism by which heat is lost from the body, leading to freezing.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Conduction is a primary mechanism by which heat is lost from the body, leading to freezing."}, {"found_name": "decomposition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2085, "target_cluster_name": "decomposition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Freezing is a process of decomposition of liquid into solid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Freezing is a process of decomposition of liquid into solid.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Electrick attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "electrick_attraction", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Electrick attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Electrick attraction"], "contexts": ["phenomenon caused by sulphureous effluvium"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_attraction", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Magnetic attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magnetic attraction"], "contexts": ["force between magnetic objects"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_attraction", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Electric attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Electric attraction"], "contexts": ["force separating bodies by electrical properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Electrick attraction", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Magnetic attraction", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Electrick attraction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Electric attraction", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 1408, "stable_key": "clu_133b367e5df62776", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrostatic attraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Magnetic attraction' is also present in this cluster, but the dominant theme is electrical phenomena.", "wikidata_id": "Q40792669", "wikidata_description": "scientific article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "attractive", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1309, "target_cluster_name": "attractive", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a specific manifestation of attraction between charged particles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electrostatic attraction is a specific manifestation of attraction between charged particles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "magnetism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 241, "target_cluster_name": "magnetism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, electrostatic attraction and magnetism are both fundamental forces that can cause attraction between objects, and their study often overlaps in premodern texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While distinct, electrostatic attraction and magnetism are both fundamental forces that can cause attraction between objects, and their study often overlaps in premodern texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "attractive force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1111, "target_cluster_name": "attractive force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific manifestation of attractive force related to electric charges.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A specific manifestation of attractive force related to electric charges.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 82, "target_cluster_name": "attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a specific type of attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrostatic attraction is a specific type of attraction."}, {"found_name": "Forces of attraction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4344, "target_cluster_name": "forces of attraction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a specific type of force of attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrostatic attraction is a specific type of force of attraction."}, {"found_name": "Forces of attraction and repulsion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2658, "target_cluster_name": "attraction and repulsion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is one of the fundamental forces of attraction and repulsion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrostatic attraction is one of the fundamental forces of attraction and repulsion."}, {"found_name": "Electrical phenomena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3624, "target_cluster_name": "electrical phenomena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a key electrical phenomenon.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electrostatic attraction is a key electrical phenomenon."}, {"found_name": "electro-magnetism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1802, "target_cluster_name": "electro-magnetism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a manifestation of electric forces, which are part of electromagnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electrostatic attraction is a manifestation of electric forces, which are part of electromagnetism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "electric force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2849, "target_cluster_name": "electric force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electrostatic attraction is a direct consequence and manifestation of electric force, making them intrinsically related in historical scientific discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Electrostatic attraction is a direct consequence and manifestation of electric force, making them intrinsically related in historical scientific discourse.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "reverberation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "reverberation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "reverberation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reverberation"], "contexts": ["operation of heating substances"]}, {"entity_id": "reverberação_do_ar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "reverberação do ar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reverberação do ar"], "contexts": ["vibration of air"]}, {"entity_id": "reverberation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "reverberation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reverberation"], "contexts": ["repeated echoes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "reverberation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "reverberação do ar", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "reverberation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "reverberation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "reverberação do ar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "reverberation", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 1409, "stable_key": "clu_53c3f90bcdc75223", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reverberation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This concept has multiple meanings: heating in furnaces, sound reflection, and echoes. The context of air vibration points to sound.", "wikidata_id": "Q468809", "wikidata_description": "persistence of sound after the sound is produced", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reverberation", "wikipedia_extract": "In acoustics, reverberation (commonly shortened to reverb) is a persistence of sound after it is produced. It is often created when a sound is reflected on surfaces, causing multiple reflections that build up and then decay as the sound is absorbed by the surfaces of objects in the space – which could include furniture, people, and air. This is most noticeable when the sound source stops but the reflections continue, their amplitude decreasing, until zero is reached. Reverberation is frequency dependent: the length of the decay, or reverberation time, receives special consideration in the architectural design of spaces which need to have specific reverberation times to achieve optimum performance for their intended activity. In comparison to a distinct echo, that is detectable at a minimum of 50 to 100 ms after the previous sound, reverberation is the occurrence of reflections that arrive in a sequence of less than approximately 50 ms. As time passes, the amplitude of the reflections gradually reduces to non-noticeable levels."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Absorption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2054, "target_cluster_name": "absorption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Absorption is the inverse process of reverberation, where sound energy is diminished rather than persisting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Absorption is the inverse process of reverberation, where sound energy is diminished rather than persisting."}, {"found_name": "Crescendo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4278, "target_cluster_name": "crescendo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crescendo describes a gradual increase in loudness, which is a dynamic aspect of sound that interacts with reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crescendo describes a gradual increase in loudness, which is a dynamic aspect of sound that interacts with reverberation."}, {"found_name": "Conduction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2567, "target_cluster_name": "conduction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conduction is a mechanism of heat transfer, and while related to energy transfer, it is distinct from sound wave propagation and reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Conduction is a mechanism of heat transfer, and while related to energy transfer, it is distinct from sound wave propagation and reverberation."}, {"found_name": "Cooling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 720, "target_cluster_name": "cooling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cooling is a process of heat loss, and while sound energy can be converted to heat, it's not a direct synonym or primary relationship to reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cooling is a process of heat loss, and while sound energy can be converted to heat, it's not a direct synonym or primary relationship to reverberation."}, {"found_name": "Elasticity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 798, "target_cluster_name": "elasticity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elasticity is a property of materials that allows them to return to their original shape after deformation, which is fundamental to the propagation of sound waves that cause reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elasticity is a property of materials that allows them to return to their original shape after deformation, which is fundamental to the propagation of sound waves that cause reverberation."}, {"found_name": "Expansion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 933, "target_cluster_name": "Expansion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Expansion is a change in volume, and while sound waves expand, it's a broader concept than reverberation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Expansion is a change in volume, and while sound waves expand, it's a broader concept than reverberation."}, {"found_name": "Fluidity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1210, "target_cluster_name": "fluidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fluidity is a property of fluids, and sound travels through fluids, but it's not a direct relationship to reverberation itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fluidity is a property of fluids, and sound travels through fluids, but it's not a direct relationship to reverberation itself."}, {"found_name": "Force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 59, "target_cluster_name": "Force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Force is a fundamental concept in physics, and sound waves exert forces, but it's a very general relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Force is a fundamental concept in physics, and sound waves exert forces, but it's a very general relationship."}]}, {"canonical_name": "sublimation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sublimation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sublimation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sublimation"], "contexts": ["operation of heating substances"]}, {"entity_id": "sublimação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sublimação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sublimação"], "contexts": ["sublimation, a solid to gas transition"]}, {"entity_id": "sublimation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sublimation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sublimation"], "contexts": ["transition from solid to gas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sublimation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sublimação", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sublimation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sublimation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "sublimação", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sublimation", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 1410, "stable_key": "clu_56e7e744a1d90590", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sublimation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Evaporation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2390, "target_cluster_name": "évaporation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sublimation is a specific type of phase transition, closely related to evaporation, where a substance changes directly from a solid to a gas without passing through the liquid phase. Both involve changes of state driven by heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sublimation is a specific type of phase transition, closely related to evaporation, where a substance changes directly from a solid to a gas without passing through the liquid phase. Both involve changes of state driven by heat."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Atoms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "atoms", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Atoms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Atoms"], "contexts": ["fundamental particles"]}, {"entity_id": "atomos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "atomos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atomos"], "contexts": ["atoms of salt, acid, and alkali in the human body"]}, {"entity_id": "atom", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "atom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atom"], "contexts": ["fundamental particle of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Atoms", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "atomos", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Atoms", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "atom", "similarity": 0.869}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "atomos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "atom", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1411, "stable_key": "clu_c362e2c37fea0ea8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atom", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9121", "wikidata_description": "smallest unit of a chemical element", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atom", "wikipedia_extract": "Atoms are the basic particles of the chemical elements and the fundamental building blocks of matter. An atom consists of a nucleus of protons and generally neutrons, surrounded by an electromagnetically bound swarm of electrons. The chemical elements are distinguished from each other by the number of protons that are in their atoms. For example, any atom that contains 11 protons is sodium, and any atom that contains 29 protons is copper. Atoms with the same number of protons but a different number of neutrons are called isotopes of the same element. Atoms are extremely small, typically around 100 picometers across."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "physics", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 110, "target_cluster_name": "physics", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The study of atoms and their properties is a central theme in modern physics, particularly quantum mechanics and atomic physics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The study of atoms and their properties is a central theme in modern physics, particularly quantum mechanics and atomic physics.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical composition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 449, "target_cluster_name": "chemical composition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atoms are the fundamental building blocks that determine chemical composition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Atoms are the fundamental building blocks that determine chemical composition.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical combination", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 781, "target_cluster_name": "chemical combination", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical combination describes how atoms interact and bond to form molecules.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical combination describes how atoms interact and bond to form molecules.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Chemical affinity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 727, "target_cluster_name": "chemical affinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical affinity describes the tendency of atoms to combine with other atoms, a key aspect of atomic behavior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemical affinity describes the tendency of atoms to combine with other atoms, a key aspect of atomic behavior."}, {"found_name": "Force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 59, "target_cluster_name": "Force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Forces, such as attractive and repulsive forces, govern the interactions between atoms and their components.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Forces, such as attractive and repulsive forces, govern the interactions between atoms and their components."}, {"found_name": "chemical action", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1769, "target_cluster_name": "chemical action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atoms are the fundamental units that are rearranged during chemical reactions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Atoms are the fundamental units that are rearranged during chemical reactions.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "manganes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "manganes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "manganes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["manganes"], "contexts": ["mineral used in glass making"]}, {"entity_id": "manganese", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "manganese", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["manganese"], "contexts": ["chemical element in meteoric masses"]}, {"entity_id": "manganese", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "manganese", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["manganese"], "contexts": ["metal in protoxides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "manganes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "manganese", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "manganes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "manganese", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "manganese", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "manganese", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1412, "stable_key": "clu_89a511e3fbe0e4a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Manganese", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q731", "wikidata_description": "chemical element, symbol Mn and atomic number 25", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manganese", "wikipedia_extract": "Manganese is a chemical element; it has symbol Mn and atomic number 25. It is a hard, brittle, silvery metal, often found in minerals in combination with iron. First isolated in the 1770s, manganese is a transition metal with many industrial alloy uses, particularly in stainless steels. It improves strength, workability, and resistance to wear. Manganese oxide is used as an oxidising agent, as a rubber additive, and in glass making, fertilizers, and ceramics. Manganese sulfate can be used as a fungicide."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Metal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 87, "target_cluster_name": "metal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Manganese is a metal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Manganese is a metal."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Almanack", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "almanack", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Almanack", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Almanack"], "contexts": ["annual publication with astronomical data"]}, {"entity_id": "calendar", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "calendar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["calendar"], "contexts": ["system for organizing dates"]}, {"entity_id": "calendar", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "calendar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["calendar"], "contexts": ["system for organizing days"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Almanack", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "calendar", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Almanack", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "calendar", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "calendar", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", 