not a substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q1758798", "wikidata_description": "synovial ball and socket joint in the shoulder", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoulder%20joint", "wikipedia_extract": "The shoulder joint (or glenohumeral joint from Greek glene, eyeball, + -oid, 'form of', + Latin humerus, shoulder) is structurally classified as a synovial ball-and-socket joint and functionally as a diarthrosis and multiaxial joint. It involves an articulation between the glenoid fossa of the scapula (shoulder blade) and the head of the humerus (upper arm bone). Due to the very loose joint capsule, it gives a limited interface of the humerus and scapula and is the most mobile joint of the human body. The shoulder joint is a ball-and-socket joint between the scapula and the humerus. The socket of the glenoid fossa of the scapula is itself quite shallow, but it is made deeper by the addition of the glenoid labrum. The glenoid labrum is a ring of cartilaginous fibre attached to the circumference of the cavity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Joints", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1290, "target_cluster_name": "juncturas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The shoulder joint is a specific type of joint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The shoulder joint is a specific type of joint."}, {"found_name": "Ligaments", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 827, "target_cluster_name": "limbs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ligaments are connective tissues that stabilize the shoulder joint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ligaments are connective tissues that stabilize the shoulder joint."}, {"found_name": "Muscles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 265, "target_cluster_name": "muscles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Muscles are essential for the movement of the shoulder joint.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Muscles are essential for the movement of the shoulder joint."}, {"found_name": "Bone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4023, "target_cluster_name": "bone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The shoulder joint is formed by the articulation of bones (scapula and humerus).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The shoulder joint is formed by the articulation of bones (scapula and humerus)."}, {"found_name": "Arm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 140, "target_cluster_name": "arm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The shoulder joint is the primary articulation of the arm with the torso.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The shoulder joint is the primary articulation of the arm with the torso."}]}, {"canonical_name": "meatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "meatus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "meatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["meatus"], "contexts": ["passages or openings"]}, {"entity_id": "meatus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["meatus"], "contexts": ["openings or passages"]}, {"entity_id": "meatos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "meatos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["meatos"], "contexts": ["passageways"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "meatus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "meatus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "meatus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meatos", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meatus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meatos", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1418, "stable_key": "clu_8487882258499c31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meatus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to anatomical passages or openings.", "wikidata_id": "Q748953", "wikidata_description": "anatomical cavity that filters vocalizations", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vocal%20tract", "wikipedia_extract": "The vocal tract or speech apparatus is the cavity in mammals and in birds where the sound produced at the sound source (larynx in mammals; syrinx in birds) is filtered. In mammals, it consists of the laryngeal cavity, the pharynx, the oral cavity, and the nasal cavity. In birds, it consists of the trachea, the syrinx, the oral cavity, the upper part of the esophagus, and the beak. The estimated average length of the vocal tract in men is 16.9 cm and 14.1 cm in women."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sex", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sex", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sex", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sex"], "contexts": ["biological characteristic"]}, {"entity_id": "sexual", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Sexual", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sexual"], "contexts": ["relating to sex"]}, {"entity_id": "sexo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sexo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sexo"], "contexts": ["patient's sex"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sex", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Sexual", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sex", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sexo", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 1419, "stable_key": "clu_a262d6213287a1d3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sex", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'sex' and its variations clearly refer to the biological characteristic."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Defluxions of humors", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "defluxions_of_humors", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Defluxions of humors", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Defluxions of humors"], "contexts": ["eye condition treated with water"]}, {"entity_id": "humorales", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humorales", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["humorales"], "contexts": ["diseases related to humors"]}, {"entity_id": "moods", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "moods", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["moods"], "contexts": ["temporary states of mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Defluxions of humors", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humorales", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Defluxions of humors", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "moods", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "humorales", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "moods", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 1420, "stable_key": "clu_f055a8a1f965bf26", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humoralism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The phrase 'Defluxions of humors' refers to a concept within the humoral theory of medicine, where imbalances or excessive flow of bodily fluids (humors) were believed to cause disease or temporary states. 'Moods' in this context likely refers to the emotional or physiological states influenced by these humors.", "wikidata_id": "Q5782572", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek and Roman system of medicine involving four fluid types", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humorism", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "round", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "round", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "round", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["round"], "contexts": ["shape of heads"]}, {"entity_id": "round", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "round", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["round"], "contexts": ["shape attribute of an object"]}, {"entity_id": "round", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "round", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["round"], "contexts": ["spherical or circular shape"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "round", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "round", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "round", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "round", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "round", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "round", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1421, "stable_key": "clu_186616daf0a8c080", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "roundness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q128171531", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Body roundness index (BRI) is a calculated geometric index used to quantify an aspect of a person's individual body shape. Based on the principle of body eccentricity, it provides a rapid visual and anthropometric tool for health evaluation. Introduced in 2013, the BRI calculation can be used to estimate total and visceral body fat. Ranges of healthy body roundness have been established to accurately classify people with healthy fat mass (weight) compared to obese people who are at risk for morbidities. Compared to traditional metrics, such as the body mass index (BMI, which uses weight and height), BRI may improve predictions of the amount of body fat and the volume of visceral adipose tissue. Despite its common use, BMI can misclassify individuals as obese because it does not distinguish between a person's lean body mass and fat mass.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_roundness_index"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Circle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 334, "target_cluster_name": "circle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'roundness' of a body can be conceptually related to the geometric shape of a circle, especially in anthropometric measurements where deviations from circularity are assessed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'roundness' of a body can be conceptually related to the geometric shape of a circle, especially in anthropometric measurements where deviations from circularity are assessed."}, {"found_name": "Figure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3849, "target_cluster_name": "figure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Roundness is a specific geometric attribute that contributes to the overall 'figure' or shape of an object or body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Roundness is a specific geometric attribute that contributes to the overall 'figure' or shape of an object or body."}, {"found_name": "Curvature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1394, "target_cluster_name": "curvature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Roundness is a specific type of curvature, implying a smooth, unbroken, and generally convex shape.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Roundness is a specific type of curvature, implying a smooth, unbroken, and generally convex shape."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "teebbevfareper rAmomo* Nelfuccidaneo fi porrà illuo fcambio. DELL^ ARISTOLOCHIE. ' Va RIsTologia detta da Greci Ariflolochia é di tre fpezie ( fecondo Diofcoride ) cioè rotonda,lunga, e clema- tite: f", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of Aristolochia", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Medicina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 209, "target_cluster_name": "Medicina", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "1 naturali fono pili, cioè, l'allume fcif- file, il rotondo, lo ftrongyle , l aftragalote, il liquido, il pia- tite eilplinthite: ma neli'vfo della Medicina fono fo'a- mente tre , lo Icifsile , il rot", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of alum used in medicine", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dogberry-Tree", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Dogberry-Tree, called in Sussex Dog-wood, but blacker, and not so long. The Flowers are white, coming forth with the Leaves at the Joynts, which turn into smal round Berries, first green, afterwards r", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Dog-wood"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hills", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hills", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hills", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hills"], "contexts": ["location where moonwort grows"]}, {"entity_id": "hills", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hills", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["hills"], "contexts": ["naturally raised areas of land"]}, {"entity_id": "collinas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "collinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["collinas"], "contexts": ["hills that were buried"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hills", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hills", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hills", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "collinas", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hills", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "collinas", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 1422, "stable_key": "clu_e0eead2057219cb1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hills", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Various", "note": "The term 'hills' and its Spanish equivalent 'collinas' consistently refer to naturally raised areas of land.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hill", "wikipedia_extract": "A hill is a landform that extends above the surrounding terrain. It often has a distinct summit, and is usually applied to peaks which are above elevation compared to the relative landmass, though not as prominent as mountains. Hills fall under the category of slope landforms. The distinction between a hill and a mountain is unclear and largely subjective, but a hill is universally considered to be not as tall, or as steep as a mountain. Geographers historically regarded mountains as hills greater than 1,000 feet (304.8 meters) above sea level. In contrast, hillwalkers have tended to regard mountains as peaks 2,000 feet (610 m) above sea level."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Land", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 157, "target_cluster_name": "land", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hills are a specific type of landform, making 'Land' a broader category that encompasses hills.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hills are a specific type of landform, making 'Land' a broader category that encompasses hills."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Portsmouth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "portsmouth", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Portsmouth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Portsmouth"], "contexts": ["coastal town"]}, {"entity_id": "portsmouth", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Portsmouth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Portsmouth"], "contexts": ["city vis-à-vis Alexandria"]}, {"entity_id": "portsmouth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Portsmouth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Portsmouth"], "contexts": ["westernmost point of observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Portsmouth", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Portsmouth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Portsmouth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Portsmouth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Portsmouth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Portsmouth", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1423, "stable_key": "clu_6f30da145d2f5651", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Portsmouth", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "note": "Portsmouth is a well-established city and naval base in the UK.", "wikidata_id": "Q72259", "wikidata_description": "city in Hampshire, England, United Kingdom"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "consuming", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "consuming", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "consuming", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["consuming"], "contexts": ["property of honeysuckles"]}, {"entity_id": "consume", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "consume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["consume"], "contexts": ["effect of Sulfur"]}, {"entity_id": "consumption", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "consumption", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["consumption"], "contexts": ["use of goods and services"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "consuming", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "consume", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "consuming", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "consumption", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "consume", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "consumption", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 1424, "stable_key": "clu_6c2f035546f1ad2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Consumption", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In the context of early modern medicine, 'consuming' and 'consumption' often referred to wasting diseases, particularly tuberculosis, but could also describe the depletion or use of something."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animal heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3893, "target_cluster_name": "animal heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical medical theories often linked wasting diseases like consumption to imbalances or deficiencies in 'animal heat'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historical medical theories often linked wasting diseases like consumption to imbalances or deficiencies in 'animal heat'."}, {"found_name": "Digestion", "link_type": "concep