ata are layers of rock, often sedimentary, containing fossils."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Invertebrata", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "invertebrata", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Invertebrata", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Invertebrata"], "contexts": ["group of animals"]}, {"entity_id": "invertebrata", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "invertebrata", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["invertebrata"], "contexts": ["animals without a backbone"]}, {"entity_id": "invertebrate", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "invertebrate", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["invertebrate"], "contexts": ["classification of animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Invertebrata", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "invertebrata", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Invertebrata", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "invertebrate", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "invertebrata", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "invertebrate", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 1434, "stable_key": "clu_d4075e059dd99690", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Invertebrates", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q43806", "wikidata_description": "animal without a vertebral column", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invertebrate", "wikipedia_extract": "Invertebrates are animals that neither develop nor retain a vertebral column (commonly known as a spine or backbone), which evolved from the notochord. It is a paraphyletic grouping including all animals excluding the chordate subphylum Vertebrata, i.e. vertebrates. Well-known phyla of invertebrates include arthropods, molluscs, annelids, echinoderms, flatworms, cnidarians, and sponges. The majority of animal species are invertebrates; one estimate puts the figure at 97%. Many invertebrate taxa have a greater number and diversity of species than the entire subphylum of Vertebrata."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Invertebrates are a major sub-grouping of Animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Invertebrates are a major sub-grouping of Animals."}, {"found_name": "Vertebrata", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 702, "target_cluster_name": "vertebrata", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Invertebrates are defined in opposition to Vertebrata, as animals that lack a vertebral column.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Invertebrates are defined in opposition to Vertebrata, as animals that lack a vertebral column."}, {"found_name": "Insects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Insects are a major phylum within the Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Insects are a major phylum within the Invertebrates."}, {"found_name": "Crustaceans", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 348, "target_cluster_name": "crustaceans", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crustaceans are a major subphylum within the Arthropods, which are Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crustaceans are a major subphylum within the Arthropods, which are Invertebrates."}, {"found_name": "Mollusks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2101, "target_cluster_name": "molluscs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mollusks are a major phylum within the Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mollusks are a major phylum within the Invertebrates."}, {"found_name": "Cephalopodes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1187, "target_cluster_name": "Cephalopodes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cephalopodes are a class within the Mollusks, which are Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cephalopodes are a class within the Mollusks, which are Invertebrates."}, {"found_name": "Brachiopods", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4380, "target_cluster_name": "Brachiopoda", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brachiopods are a phylum of marine animals, considered Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Brachiopods are a phylum of marine animals, considered Invertebrates."}, {"found_name": "Crinoidea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4381, "target_cluster_name": "Crinoidea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crinoidea are a class of echinoderms, which are Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crinoidea are a class of echinoderms, which are Invertebrates."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Australian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "australian", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Australian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Australian"], "contexts": ["con