 "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fever"], "contexts": ["implied by burning of effigies"]}, {"entity_id": "fever", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fever", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 5, "variants": ["fever"], "contexts": ["elevated body temperature", "condition causing delirium"]}, {"entity_id": "fever", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Fever", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fever"], "contexts": ["elevated body temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "febre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "febre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1486, "stable_key": "clu_39013ff5aca96875-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fever", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An acute illness characterized by elevated body temperature and potential delirium.", "wikipedia_extract": "Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C (99.0 and 100.9 °F) in humans. The increase in set point triggers increased muscle contractions and causes a feeling of cold or chills. This results in greater heat production and efforts to conserve heat. When the set point temperature returns to normal, a person feels hot, becomes flushed, and may begin to sweat. Rarely a fever may trigger a febrile seizure, with this being more common in young children."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "febre latica", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 4497, "target_cluster_name": "febre latica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for fever.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spanish for fever.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 374, "members": [{"entity_id": "antilles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 352, "variants": ["Antilles", "ANTILLES", "Antill'is", "Antilles Angiuises", "Antilles Anglaises", "Antilles Angloises", "Antilles angl.", "Antilles angloiseSj", "Antilles angloises", "Antilles anoloises", "Antilles bollandoises", "Antilles espagnoles", "Antilles espognoles", "Antilles fiançoises", "Antilles françoises", "Antilles holl., dan. et suéd,", "Antilles hollandoise", "Antilles hollandoises", "Antilles non espagnoles", "Antilles uollandoises", "Grandes Antilles", "Grandes et Petites Antilles", "Grandes et les Pelites-Antilles", "Grandes-Antilles", "Islas Antillas", "Mer des Antilles", "Méditerranée des Antilles", "Petites Antilles", "Petites-Antilles", "Petites-Antilles Angloises", "archipel des grandes et des petites Antilles", "iles Antilles", "les autres Antilles", "mer des Antilles", "îles Antilles"], "contexts": ["islands in the Caribbean Sea", "geographical location, islands"]}, {"entity_id": "antilles", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Antilles"], "contexts": ["western part of the Antilles", "island group containing St. Vincent"]}, {"entity_id": "antilhas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Antilhas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antilhas"], "contexts": ["region where guaiacam grows"]}, {"entity_id": "antilhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Antilhas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 12, "variants": ["Antilhas"], "contexts": ["origin of 'pào santo'", "source of false-santo"]}, {"entity_id": "petites-antilles_hollandoises", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Petites-Antilles hollandoises", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Petites-Antilles hollandoises"], "contexts": ["Dutch Lesser Antilles"]}, {"entity_id": "lesser_antilles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lesser Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["lesser Antilles"], "contexts": ["islands east of hurricane origin", "islands east of Caribbean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Antilles", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Antilles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1487, "stable_key": "clu_d993c2b3996b0697", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antilles", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago in the Caribbean Sea, comprising numerous islands.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Antilles is an archipelago within the West Indies in the Caribbean region of the Americas. It is bordered by the Caribbean Sea to the south and west, the Gulf of Mexico to the northwest, and the North Atlantic Ocean to the north and east. The Antillean islands are divided into two smaller archipelagos: the Greater Antilles and the Lesser Antilles. The Greater Antilles include the Cayman Islands and larger islands of Cuba, Hispaniola (subdivided into the nations of the Dominican Republic and Haiti), Navassa Island, Jamaica, and Puerto Rico. The Lesser Antilles contains the northerly Leeward Islands and the southeasterly Windward Islands, as well as the Leeward Antilles immediately north of Venezuela. The Lucayan Archipelago, consisting of The Bahamas and the Turks and Caicos Islands, though a part of the West Indies, is generally not included among the Antillean islands."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"fou