ated", "evidence_snippet": "Blood is one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside melancholic, choleric, and phlegmatic humors. They are distinct but part of the same theoretical framework.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Blood is one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside melancholic, choleric, and phlegmatic humors. They are distinct but part of the same theoretical framework.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "spirits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2348, "target_cluster_name": "spirits", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Animal spirits were often described as being derived from or closely associated with blood, particularly its more refined or vital aspects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Animal spirits were often described as being derived from or closely associated with blood, particularly its more refined or vital aspects.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cerebral circulation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2583, "target_cluster_name": "cerebral circulation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cerebral circulation is the flow of blood to the brain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cerebral circulation is the flow of blood to the brain.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "quermes", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "podeo ser; mas o que vos dixe, sey que pasa asi. Huma cousa vos peço, por merçe, que levandovos Deos a Espanha, nam consentais que deitem, por lacre, sangue de drago na confeiçam da laca, nem creais q", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Arabic name for kermes"}]}, {"canonical_name": "purga", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 224, "members": [{"entity_id": "purga", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "purga", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["purga"], "contexts": ["act of purging or cleansing", "act of purging with medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "purgas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "purgas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["purgas", "purgar"], "contexts": ["medical practice of cleansing the body", "medical practice for illness"]}, {"entity_id": "purga", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "purga", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 215, "variants": ["purga", "Purga", "Purgas", "purga branda", "purgabis", "purgado", "purgamětorum", "purgan", "purganJo", "purgandi", "purgando", "purgans", "purgante", "purgantibus", "purgar", "purgar commedicamento", "purgar o corpo", "purgas", "purgas alviducas", "purgas vomitivas", "purgat.", "purgatio", "purgation", "purgatione", "purgationem", "purgationem viſcerum", "purgationes", "purgationibus", "purgationispurgatis", "purgations", "purgatios", "purgativa", "purgativas", "purgatives", "purgaçao", "purgaçaó", "purgaçaõ", "purgaçoens menſaes", "purgação", "purgações", "purgações menſaes", "purgaó", "purged", "purgetur", "purging", "purgou", "purgue", "purguem", "repurgar"], "contexts": ["medicines that induce purging", "medical practice of cleansing the body"]}, {"entity_id": "purges", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Purges", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Purges"], "contexts": ["medical procedures"]}, {"entity_id": "purging", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "purging", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["purging"], "contexts": ["medical practice discussed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "purga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "purga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "purgas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "purga", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 1489, "stable_key": "clu_dbe84f442f9bc7d0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purging", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Medical practice of cleansing the body, often with medicines.", "note": "Refers to inducing vomiting or bowel movements for medical purposes.", "wikipedia_extract": "In medicine, a cathartic is a substance that accelerates defecation. This is similar to a laxative, which is a substance that eases defecation, usually by softening feces. It is possible for a substance to be both a laxative and a cathartic. However, agents such as psyllium seed husks increase the bulk of the feces. Cathartics such as sorbitol, magnesium citrate, magnesium sulfate, or sodium sulfate were previously used as a form of gastrointestinal decontamination following poisoning via ingestion. They are no longer routinely recommended for poisonings."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Vomitorios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 535, "target_cluster_name": "Vomitorios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emetics are a type of purgative, used to expel substances from the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emetics are a type of purgative, used to expel substances from the body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "evacuação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1559, "target_cluster_name": "evacuação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Purging is a form of bodily evacuation, often through the bowels.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Purging is a form of bodily evacuation, often through the bowels.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Apoplexia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 193, "members": [{"entity_id": "apoplexia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Apoplexia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 193, "variants": ["Apoplexia", "Apople-xias", "ApopleVeiga", "Apopleticis", "Apopleticos", "Apoplex", "Apoplexi", "Apoplexia adelapſu", "Apoplexia cognofcenda", "Apoplexia exfanguine", "Apoplexia fimulata", "Apoplexia forti", "Apoplexia fortis", "Apoplexia lethalis", "Apoplexia mor", "Apoplexia pituitosa", "Apoplexias", "Apoplexie", "Apoplexy", "Apopleça", "apofteana", "apoftema", "apopleticos", "apoplexi", "apoplexia", "apoplexias", "apoplexies", "apoplexy", "apostema", "apostemada", "apostemata", "aposterma", "apoſtema", "poplexias"], "contexts": ["chapter title about apoplexy", "stroke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Aphasia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Apoplexia", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 1490, "stable_key": "clu_47c633cbe0bacb85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apoplexy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A medical condition, often referring to a stroke.", "wikipedia_extract": "Apoplexy (from Ancient Greek  ἀποπληξία (apoplexia) 'a striking away') refers to the rupture of an internal organ and the associated symptoms. Informally or metaphorically, the term apoplexy is associated with being furious, especially as \"apoplectic\". Historically, it described what is now known as a hemorrhagic stroke, typically involving a ruptured blood vessel in the brain; modern medicine typically specifies the anatomical location of the bleeding, such as cerebral apoplexy, ovarian apoplexy, or pituitary apoplexy. From the late 14th to the late 19th century, the diagnosis apoplexy referred to any sudden death that began with abrupt loss of consciousness, especially when the victim died within seconds after losing consciousness. The word apoplexy was sometimes used to refer to the symptom of sudden loss of consciousness immediately preceding death. Strokes, ruptured aortic aneurysms, and even heart attacks were referred to as apoplexy in the past, because before the advent of biomedical science, the ability to differentiate abnormal conditions and diseased states was limited."}, "member_count": 1, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cap.", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 594, "target_cluster_name": "cap.", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "XVII. Da Parleſia da boca,&do roſto, a quevulgarmente chamão Torturada boca. Tratado 2. fol. 124. CAP. XVIII. Da Parleſia do ſeſſo. Tratado 2. fol. 127. CAP. XIX. Da Convulſao, & Eſpaſmo. Tratado 2. f", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cumana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 186, "members": [{"entity_id": "cumana", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cumana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 174, "variants": ["Cumana", "CUMANA", "Cumanacoa", "Cumanagoto"], "contexts": ["coasts of Cumana", "either at Cumana"]}, {"entity_id": "cumana", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cumana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Cumana"], "contexts": ["location of observed meteor showers", "location of extraordinary star fall"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cumana", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cumana", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1491, "stable_key": "clu_067e0f2074c730d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cumaná", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q319476", "wikidata_description": "capital city of Sucre, Venezuela", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumaná", "wikipedia_extract": "Cumaná (Spanish pronunciation: [kumaˈna]) is the capital city of Venezuela's Sucre State. It is located 402 kilometres (250 mi) east of Caracas. Cumaná was one of the first cities founded by Spain in the mainland Americas and is the oldest continuously-inhabited Hispanic-established city in South America. Its early history includes several successful counters by the indigenous people of the area who were attempting to prevent Spanish incursion into their land, resulting in the city being refounded several times. The municipality of Sucre, which includes the capital city, Cumaná, had a population of 358,919 at the 2011 Census; the latest estimate (as of mid-2016) is 423,546. The city is located at the mouth of the Manzanares River on the Caribbean coast, in the northeast of Venezuela."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caripe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 623, "target_cluster_name": "Caripe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caripe is a municipality in Monagas, Venezuela. Cumaná is a major city and port in Sucre, Venezuela, located to the northeast of Caripe. Both are significant locations within eastern Venezuela and would likely appear in geographical or historical accounts of the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caripe is a municipality in Monagas, Venezuela. Cumaná is a major city and port in Sucre, Venezuela, located to the northeast of Caripe. Both are significant locations within eastern Venezuela and would likely appear in geographical or historical accounts of the region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.