_snippet": "nam era bom arábio, mas era an- daluz, e a lingoa própria em que Avicena escreveo he a que se usa na Siria e Mesopotâmia, e na Pérsia ou Tartaria (donde Avicena era) e a esta lingoa chamam elles araby", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "means 'ponente' (west) in Arabic"}, {"found_name": "assabel diriri", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "que he arábio e de auctoridade, o chama assabel diriri*. • SerapiO) cap. 2o5 (nota do auctor). 142 Colóquio undécimo ORTA Serapio está corruto, e Avicena está emmendado, e mais os Arábios físicos lhe ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "an authoritative Arabic term for a substance"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 191, "members": [{"entity_id": "vomito", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vomito", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["vomito"], "contexts": ["symptom of illness"]}, {"entity_id": "vomitos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 161, "variants": ["Vomitos", "Vomitos continuos", "Vomitos de colera negra", "Vomitos de humor negro", "Vomitos de leyte", "Vomitos de leyte nas crianças", "Vomitos quotidianos", "Vomitos quotidianos colericos", "Vomitos verdes nas crianças", "Vomitosdehumor verde", "Vomitus", "vomita", "vomitar", "vomitaſſe", "vomiting", "vomitione", "vomitos", "vomitos continuos", "vomitos de agulhas", "vomitos de alfinetes", "vomitos de cabellos", "vomitos de cabeças depregos", "vomitos de colera com o comer", "vomitos de colera pura", "vomitos de coleras", "vomitos de cor negra", "vomitos de fangue", "vomitos de galinha", "vomitos de humor negro", "vomitos rebeldes", "vomitosconti", "vomits", "vomitum", "vomituras", "vomitus"], "contexts": ["Therapeutic practice discussed", "act of expelling stomach contents"]}, {"entity_id": "vomitings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Vomitings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 22, "variants": ["Vomitings", "Vomi", "Vomiting", "Vomi∣ting", "Vomi∣tings"], "contexts": ["symptom caused by fresh bark", "condition stopped by Bistort"]}, {"entity_id": "vomitos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["vomitos"], "contexts": ["symptom of illness", "expulsion of stomach contents"]}, {"entity_id": "vomilo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vomilo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["vomilo"], "contexts": ["illness entering hospital", "disease affecting coastal inhabitants"]}, {"entity_id": "vomiting", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vomiting", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["vomiting"], "contexts": ["expulsion of stomach contents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vomito", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitos", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 1493, "stable_key": "clu_1050146bb34cd63b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vomiting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "The act of expelling stomach contents; a symptom or illness.", "note": "Can refer to the symptom or a specific illness characterized by vomiting.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vomiting (scientifically known as emesis, and otherwise known as puking, and throwing up) is the forceful expulsion of the contents of one's stomach through the mouth and sometimes the nose. The substance so produced (the erstwhile contents of the stomach, now expelled) is also known as vomit or vomitus. Vomiting can be the result of ailments like food poisoning, gastroenteritis, pregnancy, motion sickness, or hangover; or it can be an after effect of diseases such as brain tumors, elevated intracranial pressure, or overexposure to ionizing radiation. The feeling that one is about to vomit is called nausea; it often precedes, but does not always lead to vomiting. Impairment due to alcohol or anesthesia can cause inhalation of vomit. In severe cases, where dehydration develops, intravenous fluid may be required."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Stupor can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting."}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Unconsciousness can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting."}]}, {"canonical_name": "pedras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 157, "members": [{"entity_id": "piedras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "piedras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 34, "variants": ["piedras", "Piedra", "Piedra yma", "piedra", "piedra pagua"], "contexts": ["stones from the Indies with medicinal virtues", "stones expelled with pain"]}, {"entity_id": "pedras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pedras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 123, "variants": ["pedras", "Pedra Jafpe", "PedraZafira", "pedra Peritis", "pedra em pò", "pedra hume", "pedraPeritis", "pedrade", "pedrahume", "pedrahunme", "pedras brancas", "pedras de sal", "pedrasBazares", "pedrasCordeaes", "pedrinhas", "pòs das pedras"], "contexts": ["stones in the bladder", "stones, mineral substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "piedras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pedras", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 1494, "stable_key": "clu_9060541922a79cc4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stones", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This term encompasses various mineral concretions, including gallstones, kidney stones, and possibly precious or semi-precious stones valued for medicinal properties.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_%28geology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In geology, a rock (also called a stone) is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects. Rocks are usually grouped into three main groups: igneous rocks, sedimentary rocks and metamorphic rocks."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bezaar", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1118, "target_cluster_name": "Bezoar", "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "coſas másaprourchanesBezaar,fi fe pudia - .* rc hallar,que demueſtra con quáta difficultad (e auía. Y en el capitulo de losque tomaró Napelo: dize. La piedra llamada Bezaar cetrina coloras da,pura,y q", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for calculus"}, {"found_name": "Earths", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1183, "target_cluster_name": "earths", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of 'stones' (rocks) as aggregates of minerals and mineraloid matter directly relates to the broader category of 'Earths', which often encompasses various mineral and soil compositions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The description of 'stones' (rocks) as aggregates of minerals and mineraloid matter directly relates to the broader category of 'Earths', which often encompasses various mineral and soil compositions."}, {"found_name": "Minerals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 138, "target_cluster_name": "minerals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rocks, or stones, are defined as aggregates of minerals, establishing a fundamental compositional relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rocks, or stones, are defined as aggregates of minerals, establishing a fundamental compositional relationship."}, {"found_name": "Alum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 357, "target_cluster_name": "Alum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alum is a mineral salt, and as such, it is a specific type of mineral substance that could be found within or associated with geological formations, thus related to 'stones'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alum is a mineral salt, and as such, it is a specific type of mineral substance that could be found within or associated with geological formations, thus related to 'stones'."}, {"found_name": "porfido", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1903, "target_cluster_name": "porphyry", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "PiomboA .1 rame fi macinano in quefto modo.Liman fi fottiimcnte. Se la limatura fi macini con alcuno hunìóre fu ilmacmelloconcauo del porfido,òaltra pietra dura pa/!,fi per pezza fotnlc alciando dare ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of hard stone used for grinding"}, {"found_name": "Cardanilho", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": ", que he admiravel. Tomem de vinho branco fem geflo hum quartilho ,de mel duas onças &meya , em panela vidrada , ferva afogo lento,& então ajuntaráóde pedra hume crua tres oytavas ,deverdete, chamado ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for verdigris"}, {"found_name": "piedra de la Yjada", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "pipdras y traer los pueſtos enel dedo ala conrihaa.. Y:aláá miſmo en fluxo meltrual de mugeres.'. ::. € La otra picdra,que llaman de la Yjada, esvna Dela ple- piedra quela muy fina dellas parece plaſm", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of stone"}, {"found_name": "Bezaar cetrina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "coſas másaprourchanesBezaar,fi fe pudia - .* rc hallar,que demueſtra con quáta difficultad (e auía. Y en el capitulo de losque tomaró Napelo: dize. La piedra llamada Bezaar cetrina colo