"disease", "description": "A condition associated with humors, particularly bile.", "note": "The term 'camaras' is likely a misspelling or archaic form related to 'cholericas' (choleric) or conditions affecting the stomach/bowels.", "wikipedia_extract": "Camaraou noir is a red French wine grape variety that was historically grown in South West France but is now more widely planted in the Spanish wine region of Galicia where it is known as Espadeiro. However, despite its Spanish synonym Camaraou noir has no relationship to the Portuguese wine grape Espadeiro that is used to make red Vinho Verde. The grape may have some relation to the Jurançon and Béarn wine grape Camaralet de Lasseube which is also known as Camaraou blanc but DNA analysis has shown that the two varieties are distinct and not color mutations of one or the other. Ampelographers believe that the name Camaraou derives from the Béarnese word camarau which may have, in turn, be derived itself from the Gascon word cama that means \"leg, trunk\" and could be a reference to viticultural practice of growing grapevines up trees as trellising. Another possibility is that the name has some connection to the commune of Camalès located in the Hautes-Pyrénées department of southwestern France. A late 18th century document shows that a Camarau grape was growing in the Pyrénées-Atlantiques department, French Basque Country and in the País Vasco region of northern Spain, however the description of this grapes seems to indicate that it was more likely the white Camaraou blanc (Camaralet de Lasseube) grape."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cholera", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 835, "target_cluster_name": "cholera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Cholericas' is a historical name for a disease, and 'Cholera' is a direct modern medical term for a specific disease that shares a similar etymological root and historical understanding of symptoms, often involving severe gastrointestinal distress and dehydration, which could be broadly categorized under 'cholericas' in older medical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Cholericas' is a historical name for a disease, and 'Cholera' is a direct modern medical term for a specific disease that shares a similar etymological root and historical understanding of symptoms, often involving severe gastrointestinal distress and dehydration, which could be broadly categorized under 'cholericas' in older medical contexts."}, {"found_name": "hepaticas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "Outras vezesfuccedem por fraqueza do figado , que não podendoaperfeyçoar o ſangue , como he justo , o deyxa fahir da cor de lavadurade carne ,& eſtas camaras ſe chamão hepaticas, & ſe curão confortand", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of camaras related to the liver"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Venezuela", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 144, "members": [{"entity_id": "venezuela", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Venezuela", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 141, "variants": ["Venezuela", "VENEZUELA", "VENEZtELA", "Venczuela", "Vene- zuela", "Veneziuela", "Venezuala", "le Venezuela"], "contexts": ["eastern plains of Venezuela", "steppes of Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "venezuela", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Venezuela", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Venezuela"], "contexts": ["South American country", "country with littoral chain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Venezuela", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Venezuela", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1496, "stable_key": "clu_b7528bce5729afd9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venezuela", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q717", "wikidata_description": "country in South America", "wikipedia_extract": "On 3 January 2026, the United States launched a military strike in Venezuela and captured incumbent Venezuelan president Nicolás Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores. The US operation, codenamed Operation Absolute Resolve, began around 2 a.m. local time, when explosions were observed. The US Armed Forces bombed infrastructure across northern Venezuela to suppress air defenses as an apprehension force attacked Maduro's compound in Caracas. Maduro and Flores were transported to New York City by US forces to face their trial there. The US federal government announced that Maduro and Flores had been indicted on several charges related to narcoterrorism.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venezuela"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1497, "target_cluster_name": "Caracas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital and largest city of Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Caracas is the capital and largest city of Venezuela."}, {"found_name": "Equatorial America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venezuela is located in Equatorial America, a region defined by its proximity to the equator.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venezuela is located in Equatorial America, a region defined by its proximity to the equator."}, {"found_name": "Caribbean Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2786, "target_cluster_name": "Caribbean Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venezuela has a long Caribbean coastline and its history and economy are closely tied to the Caribbean Sea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venezuela has a long Caribbean coastline and its history and economy are closely tied to the Caribbean Sea."}, {"found_name": "Cumaná", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1491, "target_cluster_name": "Cumana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cumaná is a major city and port in Venezuela, historically significant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cumaná is a major city and port in Venezuela, historically significant."}, {"found_name": "Coro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1932, "target_cluster_name": "Coro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coro is a historic city in Venezuela, known for its colonial architecture and UNESCO World Heritage site status.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coro is a historic city in Venezuela, known for its colonial architecture and UNESCO World Heritage site status."}, {"found_name": "Valencia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1674, "target_cluster_name": "Valencia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Valencia is the third-largest city in Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Valencia is the third-largest city in Venezuela.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2110, "target_cluster_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "San Carlos de Río Negro is a city in Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: San Carlos de Río Negro is a city in Venezuela.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Caracas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 137, "members": [{"entity_id": "caracas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Caracas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 131, "variants": ["Caracas", "Carache", "Carachi"], "contexts": ["city with steppes", "city mentioned with primitive mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "ca_racas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ca racas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ca racas"], "contexts": ["city in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "caraccas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Caraccas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Caraccas"], "contexts": ["city destroyed by earthquake", "location with granite ledges"]}, {"entity_id": "caracas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Caracas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caracas"], "contexts": ["location where loud noise was heard"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Caracas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Caraccas", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Caracas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Caracas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ca racas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Caracas", "similarity": 0.542}], "id": 1497, "stable_key": "clu_5d62f66e6b9e38c4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caracas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q1533", "wikidata_description": "capital and largest city of Venezuela", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caracas", "wikipedia_extract": "Caracas, officially Santiago de León de Caracas (CCS), is the capital and largest city of Venezuela, and the center of the Metropolitan Region of Caracas (or Greater Caracas). Caracas is located along the Guaire River in the northern part of Venezuela, within the Caracas Valley of the Venezuelan coastal mountain range. The valley is close to the Caribbean Sea, separated from the shore by a steep 2,200-meter-high (7,200-foot) mountain range, Cerro El Ávila. To the south there are more hills and mountains that form the valley. The Metropolitan Region of Caracas has an estimated population of over 5 million inhabitants. The historic center of the city is the Caracas Cathedral, located on Bolívar Square, though some consider the center to be Plaza Venezuela, located in the Los Caobos area."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Orinoco", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 322, "target_cluster_name": "Orinoco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, the country where a significant portion of the Orinoco River's drainage basin is located. Historical texts often discuss rivers in relation to the major cities and political entities within their regions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, the country where a significant portion of the Orinoco River's drainage basin is located. Historical texts often discuss rivers in relation to the major cities and political entities within their regions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cumana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1491, "target_cluster_name": "Cumana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cumaná is located 402 kilometers east of Caracas, indicating a geographical proximity and historical relationship within Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cumaná is located 402 kilometers east of Caracas, indicating a geographical proximity and historical relationship within Venezuela.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Venezuela", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1496, "target_cluster_name": "Venezuela", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital and largest city of Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caracas is the capital and largest city of Venezuela.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Portocabello", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1568, "target_cluster_name": "Portocabello", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Puerto Cabello is a major port city within Venezuela, located relatively near Caracas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Puerto Cabello is a major port city within Venezuela, located relatively near Caracas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Truxillo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1622, "target_cluster_name": "Truxillo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are significant cities within the same nation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are significant cities within the same nation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Silla de Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1681, "target_cluster_name": "Silla de Caracas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "El Ávila National Park is located in the coastal region of central-northern Venezuela, and Caracas is the capital city of Venezuela, implying a close geographical proximity and likely historical connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: El Ávila National Park is located in the coastal region of central-northern Venezuela, and Caracas is the capital city of Venezuela, implying a close geographical proximity and likely historical connection.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "las Trincheras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2288, "target_cluster_name": "las Trincheras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical and administrative relationship within the same country.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical and administrative relationship within the same country.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 137, "members": [{"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 10, "variants": ["vinagre"], "contexts": ["Product made from fruit peel", "acidic liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 127, "variants": ["vinagre", "Vinacearum", "Vinagre", "vina", "vinacea", "vinagre Roſado", "vinagre aguado", "vinagre branco forte", "vinagre forte", "vinagre fortiffimo", "vinagre fortissimo", "vinagre rofado", "vinagre rolado", "vinagre rosado", "vinagre roſado", "vinagreRofado", "vinagreRoſado", "vinagrebem forte", "vinagreforte"], "contexts": ["vinegar, an acidic liquid", "added to remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vinagre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vinagre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1498, "stable_key": "clu_255994a3f1e92c80", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vinegar", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "An acidic liquid produced by fermentation of ethanol.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vinegar (from Old French  vyn egre 'sour wine') is an odorous aqueous solution of diluted acetic acid and trace compounds that may include flavorings or naturally occurring organic compounds. Vinegar typically contains from 4% to 18% acetic acid by volume. Usually, the acetic acid is produced by a double fermentation—converting simple sugars to ethanol using yeast, and then converting ethanol to acetic acid using acetic acid bacteria. Many types of vinegar are made, depending on source materials. The product is now mainly used in the culinary arts as a flavorful, acidic cooking ingredient, salad dressing, or pickling agent. Various types are used as condiments or garnishes, including balsamic vinegar and malt vinegar."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arnoglofia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ſobre a teſta pannos molhados empartes iguaes deagua rofada , & cumo de ervamoura , em que tenhão desfeyto quatro grãos de laudano opiado , & huma onçade vinagrerofado,he bom remedio Asfor lhas,& raiz", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "also called tanchagem macho or lingua arietis"}, {"found_name": "tanchagem macho", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ſobre a teſta pannos molhados empartes iguaes deagua rofada , & cumo de ervamoura , em que tenhão desfeyto quatro grãos de laudano opiado , & huma onçade vinagrerofado,he bom remedio Asfor lhas,& raiz", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Arnoglofia"}, {"found_name": "lingua arietis", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ſobre a teſta pannos molhados empartes iguaes deagua rofada , & cumo de ervamoura , em que tenhão desfeyto quatro grãos de laudano opiado , & huma onçade vinagrerofado,he bom remedio Asfor lhas,& raiz", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Arnoglofia"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Parleſia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 134, "members": [{"entity_id": "parlesia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "parlesia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["parlesia"], "contexts": ["paralysis affecting the body"]}, {"entity_id": "parleſia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Parleſia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 133, "variants": ["Parleſia", "Parle", "Parlefia", "Parlefiada", "Parlesia", "Parleſia daboca", "Parleſia do Sefſo", "Parleſia do feffo", "Parleſia do ſeſſo", "Parleſiada boca", "Parleſiadoroſto", "Parleſias", "Patlefias", "parlefia", "parlesia", "parleſia", "parleſia bastarda", "parleſia infieri", "parleſias", "parlidas"], "contexts": ["Condition of paralysis", "affects nerves, a type of paralysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "parlesia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Parleſia", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 1499, "stable_key": "clu_4cce6ae4d31676ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "paralysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Loss of muscle function in one or more parts of the body.", "wikipedia_extract": "Paralysis (pl.: paralyses; also known as plegia) is a loss of motor function in one or more muscles. Paralysis can also be accompanied by a loss of feeling (sensory loss) in the affected area if there is sensory damage. In the United States, roughly 1 in 50 people have been diagnosed with some form of permanent or transient paralysis. The word \"paralysis\" derives from the Greek παράλυσις, meaning \"disabling of the nerves\" from παρά (para) meaning \"beside, by\" and λύσις (lysis) meaning \"making loose\". A paralysis accompanied by involuntary tremors is usually called \"palsy\". Paralysis is most often caused by damage in the nervous system, especially the spinal cord."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Nevimaterni", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "corpo , a que osDoutores chamãoNevimaterni, ouMaculematerna. Tratado 2. fol . 100. CAP. XV. De todas asdoençasdo fomno.Tratado2.fol.103. CAP. XVI . Da Parleſia. Tratado 2. fol. 110. CAP. XVII. Da Parl", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for a condition of the body"}, {"found_name": "Maculematerna", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "corpo , a que osDoutores chamãoNevimaterni, ouMaculematerna. Tratado 2. fol . 100. CAP. XV. De todas asdoençasdo fomno.Tratado2.fol.103. CAP. XVI . Da Parleſia. Tratado 2. fol. 110. CAP. XVII. Da Parl", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for a condition of the body"}, {"found_name": "Torturada boca", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "corpo , a que osDoutores chamãoNevimaterni, ouMaculematerna. Tratado 2. fol . 100. CAP. XV. De todas asdoençasdo fomno.Tratado2.fol.103. CAP. XVI . Da Parleſia. Tratado 2. fol. 110. CAP. XVII. Da Parl", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vulgar name for paralysis of the mouth and face"}]}, {"canonical_name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 137, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_fluid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric fluid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric fluid"], "contexts": ["hypothetical electrical substance"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 128, "variants": ["Electricity", "electricity"], "contexts": ["fundamental force and phenomenon", "means of instantaneous time conveyance"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["electricity"], "contexts": ["form of energy", "phenomenon of electric charge"]}, {"entity_id": "electricities", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electricities", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["electricities"], "contexts": ["opposite electrical charges", "fundamental electrical charges"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric fluid", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electricity", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 1500, "stable_key": "clu_82dfcb230d5fc795", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "electricity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the scientific understanding of electrical phenomena and the hypothetical 'electric fluid'.", "wikidata_id": "Q383973", "wikidata_description": "process of generating electrical power", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity%20generation", "wikipedia_extract": "Electricity generation is the process of generating electric power from sources of primary energy. For utilities in the electric power industry, it is the stage prior to its delivery (transmission, distribution, etc.) to end users or its storage, using for example, the pumped-storage method. Consumable electricity is not freely available in nature, so it must be \"produced\", transforming other forms of energy to electricity. Production is carried out in power stations, also called \"power plants\". Electricity is most often generated at a power plant by electromechanical generators, primarily driven by heat engines fueled by combustion or nuclear fission, but also by other means such as the kinetic energy of flowing water and wind. Other energy sources include solar photovoltaics and geothermal power."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ciatica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 169, "members": [{"entity_id": "ciática", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ciática", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 3, "variants": ["ciática"], "contexts": ["a medical condition", "painful condition affecting the sciatic nerve"]}, {"entity_id": "ciatica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ciatica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 116, "variants": ["Ciatica", "Ciaticas", "Hecticas", "Sciatica", "ciatica", "ciaticas", "hectica", "hecticas", "hepaticas"], "contexts": ["pain in the hip and leg", "illness treated by Quintilio's powders"]}, {"entity_id": "sciatica", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Sciatica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 49, "variants": ["Sciatica", "Stiatica"], "contexts": ["ailment treated by the herb", "pain treated by Angelica water"]}, {"entity_id": "sciática", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sciática", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["sciática"], "contexts": ["sciatica treated by the water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ciática", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ciatica", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 1501, "stable_key": "clu_51950a4a56c48d49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sciatica", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Pain radiating along the path of the sciatic nerve.", "note": "Includes 'Ciatica', 'Sciatica', and 'ciática'. 'Catarros' (colds/catarrh) and 'ciragra' (gout of the hand) are less direct but contextually related to pain and illness.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sciatica is pain going down the leg from the lower back. This pain may extend down the back, outside, or front of the leg. Onset is often sudden following activities such as heavy lifting, though gradual onset may also occur. The pain is often described as shooting. Typically, symptoms occur on only one side of the body; certain causes, however, may result in pain on both sides. Lower back pain is sometimes present."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pains", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1512, "target_cluster_name": "pains", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "putrefaction; the shingles also, and all other sorts of running, and soul Scabs, Sores, and Itch. The same also boyled in Wine, and applied to any Joynts full of pain and ach•or the Gout in the Hands ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for Hip-gout", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "the Kings Evil", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3223, "target_cluster_name": "feffo", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins, the Juyce being used as a Bath un∣to them, or made into an Oyntment: It is no less effectual to give eas of pains to the hot Gout,, the Sciatica, and the Inflamations and Swelling", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for Kernels or Knots in the Neck or Throat"}, {"found_name": "Gout", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 234, "target_cluster_name": "Gout", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "and soul Scabs, Sores, and Itch. The same also boyled in Wine, and applied to any Joynts full of pain and ach•or the Gout in the Hands or Feet, or the Hip-gout, called the Sciatica, and the Decoction ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Hip-gout", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Back pain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3172, "target_cluster_name": "dolor del espaldas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica is a specific type of pain that originates in the lower back and radiates down the leg, making 'back pain' a broader but directly related category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sciatica is a specific type of pain that originates in the lower back and radiates down the leg, making 'back pain' a broader but directly related category."}, {"found_name": "Pain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 70, "target_cluster_name": "pain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica is fundamentally a type of pain, making 'Pain' a direct hypernym and thus a related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sciatica is fundamentally a type of pain, making 'Pain' a direct hypernym and thus a related concept."}, {"found_name": "Nervous shock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 926, "target_cluster_name": "nervous shock", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica can be caused by nerve compression or irritation, and 'nervous shock' is a related concept in the context of neurological distress.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sciatica can be caused by nerve compression or irritation, and 'nervous shock' is a related concept in the context of neurological distress."}, {"found_name": "Rheumatism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 796, "target_cluster_name": "Rheumatismos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, sciatica was often conflated with or considered a form of rheumatism due to the shared symptom of pain in the limbs and back.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, sciatica was often conflated with or considered a form of rheumatism due to the shared symptom of pain in the limbs and back."}, {"found_name": "reumas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2224, "target_cluster_name": "reumas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica involves pain along the sciatic nerve, often radiating down the leg, and can be a symptom or manifestation of rheumatic conditions affecting the lower back or pelvis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sciatica involves pain along the sciatic nerve, often radiating down the leg, and can be a symptom or manifestation of rheumatic conditions affecting the lower back or pelvis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "China", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 41, "target_