ed blood cells and holds a reserve of blood, which can be valuable in case of hemorrhagic shock, and also recycles iron. As a part of the mononuclear phagocyte system, it metabolizes hemoglobin removed from senescent red blood cells. The spleen is a center of activity of the mononuclear phagocyte system and is analogous to a large lymph node, as its absence causes a predisposition to certain infections."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Jamaïque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 78, "members": [{"entity_id": "jamaïque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jamaïque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 69, "variants": ["Jamaïque", "la Jamaïque"], "contexts": ["island with population similar to New England states", "island with an excellent work"]}, {"entity_id": "jamaica", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Jamaica"], "contexts": ["location where meteors were observed", "island with invariant magnetic declination"]}, {"entity_id": "jamaica", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jamaica"], "contexts": ["island whose longitudes are discussed", "island nation"]}, {"entity_id": "jamaica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jamaica"], "contexts": ["island where guaiacam grows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jamaïque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jamaica", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 1518, "stable_key": "clu_ec03453ff106d384", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jamaica", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Jamaica", "wikidata_id": "Q766", "wikidata_description": "island state in the Caribbean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica", "wikipedia_extract": "Jamaica is an island country in the Caribbean, covering 10,990 square kilometres (4,240 sq mi). It is a popular tourism and resort destination. It is the third-largest island in the Greater Antilles and the Caribbean, after Cuba and the island of Hispaniola. Jamaica lies about 145 km (78 nmi) south of Cuba, 191 km (103 nmi) west of Hispaniola (the island containing Haiti and the Dominican Republic), and 215 km (116 nmi) southeast of the Cayman Islands (a British Overseas Territory). With 2.8 million people, Jamaica is the third most populous Anglophone country in the Americas and the fourth most populous country in the Caribbean. Kingston is the country's capital and largest city."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antilles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1487, "target_cluster_name": "Antilles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jamaica is part of the Greater Antilles, a sub-region of the Antilles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jamaica is part of the Greater Antilles, a sub-region of the Antilles."}, {"found_name": "Caribbean Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2786, "target_cluster_name": "Caribbean Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jamaica is an island country located in the Caribbean Sea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jamaica is an island country located in the Caribbean Sea."}, {"found_name": "Cuba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 236, "target_cluster_name": "Ile de Cuba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jamaica is geographically close to Cuba and is the third-largest island in the Greater Antilles after Cuba and Hispaniola.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jamaica is geographically close to Cuba and is the third-largest island in the Greater Antilles after Cuba and Hispaniola."}, {"found_name": "Puerto rico", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2294, "target_cluster_name": "Puerto rico", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jamaica (aka Jamaïque) is another major island in the Greater Antilles, often grouped with Puerto Rico in discussions of the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jamaica (aka Jamaïque) is another major island in the Greater Antilles, often grouped with Puerto Rico in discussions of the region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Caribbean Islands", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2776, "target_cluster_name": "Caribbean Islands", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jamaica is an island country situated in the Greater Antilles, part of the Caribbean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jamaica is an island country situated in the Greater Antilles, part of the Caribbean.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "coraçao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 74, "members": [{"entity_id": "coração", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "coração", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["coração"], "contexts": ["body part that amber benefits"]}, {"entity_id": "coraçam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "coraçam", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["coraçam"], "contexts": ["body part to be cooled"]}, {"entity_id": "coraçao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "coraçao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 72, "variants": ["coraçao", "coracao", "coraçaó", "coraçaõ", "coraçaō", "coração"], "contexts": ["body part", "heart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "coração", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "coraçao", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "coraçam", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "coraçao", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 1519, "stable_key": "clu_0c20f31a5aae2b4e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "heart", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "description": "The central organ of the circulatory system.", "note": "The term 'coraçao' is Portuguese for heart.", "wikipedia_extract": "The heart is a muscular organ found in humans and other animals. This organ pumps blood through the blood vessels. The heart and blood vessels together make up the circulatory system. The pumped blood carries oxygen and nutrients to the tissue, while carrying metabolic waste such as carbon dioxide to the lungs. In humans, the heart is approximately the size of a closed fist and is located between the lungs, in the middle compartment of the chest, called the mediastinum. In humans, the heart is divided into four chambers: upper left and right atria and lower left and right ventricles."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aorta", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2696, "target_cluster_name": "Arteria magna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The aorta is the main artery carrying blood away from the heart.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The aorta is the main artery carrying blood away from the heart."}, {"found_name": "Artery", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1775, "target_cluster_name": "arterias", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart."}, {"found_name": "Blood", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1488, "target_cluster_name": "sangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The heart pumps blood throughout the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The heart pumps blood throughout the body."}, {"found_name": "Veins", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 525, "target_cluster_name": "veas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Veins carry blood back to the heart.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Veins carry blood back to the heart."}, {"found_name": "Thorax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1153, "target_cluster_name": "thorace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The heart is located within the thorax (chest).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The heart is located within the thorax (chest)."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Amazone", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 85, "members": [{"entity_id": "amazone", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Amazone", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 72, "variants": ["Amazone", "AMAZONES", "Amazones", "l'Amazone"], "contexts": ["river in South America", "river basin reached by Caribes"]}, {"entity_id": "river_amazons", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "river Amazons", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["river Amazons"], "contexts": ["river with woody regions"]}, {"entity_id": "rivière_des_amazones", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rivière des Amazones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Rivière des Amazones", "RIVIÈRE DES AMAZONES", "Riviere des Amazones", "rivière de Hudson", "rivière de l'Amazone", "rivière des Amazones", "rivière des Illinois"], "contexts": ["River explored by Pinson", "river in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "river_of_the_amazons", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "river of the Amazons", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["river of the Amazons"], "contexts": ["river where tides are evident far inland"]}, {"entity_id": "amazon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amazon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amazon"], "contexts": ["river in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "amazons", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Amazons", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amazons"], "contexts": ["river with perceptible tides and forests"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Amazone", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "river Amazons", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 1520, "stable_key": "clu_0d600c7b60e13ab2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amazon River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "The French 'Amazone' and 'Rivière des Amazones' directly translate to Amazon and River of the Amazons.", "wikidata_id": "Q205329", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon%20river%20dolphin", "wikipedia_extract": "The Amazon River (UK: , US: ; Portuguese: rio Amazonas, Spanish: río Amazonas) in South America is the largest river by discharge volume of water in the world, and the longest or second-longest river system in the world, a title which is disputed with the Nile. The headwaters of the Apurímac River on Nevado Mismi had been considered, for nearly a century, the Amazon basin's most distant source until a 2014 study found it to be the headwaters of the Mantaro River on the Cordillera Rumi Cruz in Peru. The Mantaro and Apurímac rivers join, and with other tributaries form the Ucayali River, which in turn meets the Marañón River upstream of Iquitos, Peru, forming what countries other than Brazil consider to be the main stem of the Amazon. Brazilians call this section the Solimões River above its confluence with the Rio Negro forming what Brazilians call the Amazon at the Meeting of Waters (Portuguese: Encontro das Águas) at Manaus, the largest city on the river. The Amazon River has an average discharge of about 215,000–230,000 m3/s (7,600,000–8,100,000 cu ft/s)—approximately 6,591–7,570 km3 (1,581–1,816 cu mi) per year, greater than the next seven largest independent rivers combined. Two of the top ten rivers by discharge are tributaries of the Amazon river."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "equatorial America", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Amazon River flows through and is a defining feature of Equatorial America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Amazon River flows through and is a defining feature of Equatorial America.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Amazon River basin is largely defined by the Andes mountains, which are its source.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Amazon River basin is largely defined by the Andes mountains, which are its source.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "South America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 63, "target_cluster_name": "South America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Amazon River is located in South America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Amazon River is located in South America."}, {"found_name": "Nile", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 128, "target_cluster_name": "Nil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Amazon River's length is disputed with the Nile.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Amazon River's length is disputed with the Nile."}, {"found_name": "Apure River", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2543, "target_cluster_name": "Rio Apure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apure River is a tributary of the Amazon River.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Apure River is a tributary of the Amazon River."}, {"found_name": "Orinoco River", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 322, "target_cluster_name": "Orinoco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Amazon and Orinoco river systems are connected by the Cassiquiare Canal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Amazon and Orinoco river systems are connected by the Cassiquiare Canal."}, {"found_name": "Cassiopeia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2438, "target_cluster_name": "Cassiopeia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Cassiquiare River is a distributary of the Orinoco that flows into the Rio Negro, which is part of the Amazon River system, thus forming a natural canal between the Orinoco and Amazon river systems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Cassiquiare River is a distributary of the Orinoco that flows into the Rio Negro, which is part of the Amazon River system, thus forming a natural canal between the Orinoco and Amazon river systems.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "La Plata", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1726, "target_cluster_name": "La Plata", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Río de la Plata is a major river estuary in South America, and the Amazon River is another major river in South America, often discussed in relation to South American geography and exploration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Río de la Plata is a major river estuary in South America, and the Amazon River is another major river in South America, often discussed in relation to South American geography and exploration.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rio de la Madeira", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2547, "target_cluster_name": "Rio de la Madeira", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Madeira River is a major tributary of the Amazon River.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Madeira River is a major tributary of the Amazon River.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "alambre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 73, "members": [{"entity_id": "alambres", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alambres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["alambres"], "contexts": ["wires, possibly referring to metal wires"]}, {"entity_id": "alambre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "alambre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 72, "variants": ["alambre", "Alambre", "Alambre branco", "alambre bianco", "alambre branco", "alambres"], "contexts": ["fumes used in remed