y searchable database of stars and planets that is associated with the K2 \"Second Light\" plan of the Kepler space telescope mission. Examples of related stars include:  EPIC 201563164, EPIC 204278916, EPIC 204376071 and EPIC 249706694. Examples of related planets include: EPIC 203771098 b, EPIC 203771098 c, EPIC 206011691 c and EPIC 211945201 b. In November 2013, a new mission plan named K2 \"Second Light\" was presented for consideration. K2 would involve using Kepler's remaining capability, photometric precision of about 300 parts per million, compared with about 20 parts per million earlier, to collect data for the study of \"supernova explosions, star formation and Solar-System bodies such as asteroids and comets, ... \" and for finding and studying more exoplanets."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "latitude", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 551, "target_cluster_name": "latitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude, in astronomical contexts, is often measured relative to the ecliptic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Latitude, in astronomical contexts, is often measured relative to the ecliptic.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Meridian", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 97, "target_cluster_name": "Meridian", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, which is a fundamental concept in understanding celestial meridians and their relation to astronomical observations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, which is a fundamental concept in understanding celestial meridians and their relation to astronomical observations.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "solar system", "link_type": "conceptual_o