n that framework."}, {"found_name": "Elliptical orbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While the ecliptic is a great circle, the orbits of celestial bodies are often elliptical, and these orbits are understood and described in relation to the ecliptic plane.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While the ecliptic is a great circle, the orbits of celestial bodies are often elliptical, and these orbits are understood and described in relation to the ecliptic plane."}, {"found_name": "Sirius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1700, "target_cluster_name": "Sirius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky, and the positions of stars like Sirius are often described in relation to it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky, and the positions of stars like Sirius are often described in relation to it.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "equinoxes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1803, "target_cluster_name": "equinoxes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The equinox is the point where the ecliptic intersects the celestial equator. The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky over the course of a year, and the equinoxes are two points on this path.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The equinox is the point where the ecliptic intersects the celestial equator. The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky over the course of a year, and the equinoxes are two points on this path.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nutation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1833, "target_cluster_name": "nutation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nutation is a perturbation of the Earth's axis that causes it to wobble around the pole of the ecliptic, the plane of Earth's orbit.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nutation is a perturbation of the Earth's axis that causes it to wobble around the pole of the ecliptic, the plane of Earth's orbit.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "periodic time", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2137, "target_cluster_name": "periodic time", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is a fundamental reference plane in astronomy, and orbital periods are often described in relation to it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is a fundamental reference plane in astronomy, and orbital periods are often described in relation to it.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "lunar eclipses", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2293, "target_cluster_name": "lunar eclipses", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and lunar eclipses occur when the Moon passes through the Earth's shadow, which is aligned with the ecliptic plane.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and lunar eclipses occur when the Moon passes through the Earth's shadow, which is aligned with the ecliptic plane.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Solstices", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2455, "target_cluster_name": "Solstices", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere over the course of a year. The solstices are two specific points on the ecliptic, marking the extreme northern and southern declinations of the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere over the course of a year. The solstices are two specific points on the ecliptic, marking the extreme northern and southern declinations of the Sun.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "planetary motions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2896, "target_cluster_name": "planetary motions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and planetary orbits are often described in relation to it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, and planetary orbits are often described in relation to it.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ormuz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 56, "members": [{"entity_id": "ormuz", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ormuz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ormuz"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "ormuz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ormuz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 54, "variants": ["Ormuz"], "contexts": ["city where Persians, Arabs, Turks sell aloés", "city where goods are sold"]}, {"entity_id": "or-muz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Or-muz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Or-muz"], "contexts": ["named strait"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ormuz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ormuz", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ormuz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Or-muz", "similarity": 0.704}], "id": 1542, "stable_key": "clu_88d8829a3e31e976", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hormuz", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A historic port city and trading hub on the Persian Gulf.", "note": "The spelling 'Ormuz' is an older variant.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Kingdom of Hormuz (also known as Hormoz or, archaically, Ormus; Persian: هرمز; Portuguese: Ormuz) was located in the eastern side of the Persian Gulf and extended as far as Bahrain in the west at its zenith. The Kingdom was established in the 11th century initially as a dependency of the Kerman Seljuk Sultanate, and later as an autonomous tributary of the Salghurid Turkmens and the Ilkhanates. In its last phase Hormuz became a client state of the Portuguese Empire in the East. The island was a major economic base of economic exchanges and marine trade as an important port between the Persian Gulf and the Indian Ocean. This port was originally located on the southern coast of Iran to the east of the Strait of Hormuz, near the modern city of Minab, and was later relocated to the island of Jarun which came to be known as Hormuz Island, which is located near the modern city of Bandar-e Abbas. Popular etymology derives \"Hormuz\" from the Middle Persian pronunciation of the name of the Zoroastrian god Ahuramazda."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "guoma", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2057, "target_cluster_name": "Gomme", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "vermelha. O vulgo tem que isto he fruto, mas eu soube que era guoma ou resina: elles tem esta por mais san que a que usamos, e desta usam mais na Pérsia e em Ormuz, porque a que aqui usamos não a dam ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for the substance"}, {"found_name": "çupari", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 4398, "target_cluster_name": "sugar-cane", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Guzarates e os Decanins a chamam çupari; e estes tem muyto pouca, somente na fralda do mar, e he muyto boa essa que ha em Chaul, porque he mercadoria pêra Ormuz ; e milhor he a de Mombaim, terra e ylh", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "local name"}, {"found_name": "Persian Gulf", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1359, "target_cluster_name": "Arabian Gulf", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hormuz was located on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hormuz was located on the eastern side of the Persian Gulf."}, {"found_name": "poa", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "terra, chamase pinam; e em Çeilam ha mayor cantidade delia, que farta a huma parte do Decam, scilicet, a terra do Co- tamaluco e a Bisnaga: e de Çeilam a levam a Ormuz e a Cambaia, e ás ylhas de Maldi", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "local name for areca nut"}, {"found_name": "aljôfar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "oríen- taes a estas pérolas boas, por ventura porque eram de cor dourada? QRTA Nam, senão porque vinham da banda do Oriente, e porque este estreito de Ormuz era oriental a respeito da nossa Europa, o ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of pearl"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Panama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 55, "members": [{"entity_id": "panama", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Panama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Panama"], "contexts": ["impassable isthmus separating faunas"]}, {"entity_id": "panama", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Panama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 54, "variants": ["Panama"], "contexts": ["province in South America", "city with population 50,000"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Panama", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Panama", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1543, "stable_key": "clu_3023bc3f0a2d1276", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Panama", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Isthmus and province in Central America.", "note": "Includes both the geographical isthmus and the historical region/city.", "wikipedia_extract": "Panama, officially the Republic of Panama, is a country located at the southern end of Central America, bordering South America. It is bordered by Costa Rica to the west, Colombia to the southeast, the Caribbean Sea to the north, and the Pacific Ocean to the south. Its capital and largest city is Panama City, whose metropolitan area is home to nearly half of the country's over 4 million inhabitants. Before the arrival of Spanish colonists in the 16th century, Panama was inhabited by a number of different indigenous tribes. It broke away from Spain in 1821 and joined the Republic of Gran Colombia, a union of Nueva Granada, Ecuador, and Venezuela. After Gran Colombia dissolved in 1831, Panama and Nueva Granada eventually became the Republic of Colombia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Central America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1339, "target_cluster_name": "Central America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Panama is geographically located at the southern end of Central America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Panama is geographically located at the southern end of Central America."}]}, {"canonical_name": "mel", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 55, "members": [{"entity_id": "mel", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mel", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["mel"], "contexts": ["sweet substance mixed with aloes", "honey, less solutive"]}, {"entity_id": "mel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mel", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 45, "variants": ["mel", "Mel"], "contexts": ["Honey, flowing from lips", "honey for pills"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1544, "stable_key": "clu_3ec41cc0f35475db", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Honey", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'mel' can refer to a substance produced by animals, in this context, it most directly refers to the substance honey itself, often used medicinally."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Queen bee", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3391, "target_cluster_name": "queen-bee", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The queen bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The queen bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species."}, {"found_name": "Drone bee", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3325, "target_cluster_name": "drones", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The drone bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The drone bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species."}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honey is produced by bees, making 'bees' a direct and essential related entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Honey is produced by bees, making 'bees' a direct and essential related entity."}, {"found_name": "abelha", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3212, "target_cluster_name": "abelha", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the Portuguese word for 'bee', the producer of honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is the Portuguese word for 'bee', the producer of honey."}, {"found_name": "Cardanilho", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3749, "target_cluster_name": "Cartamo", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "em quatro, ou cinco partes , fem tocar nas chagas , & logo fetoquemcom eftaagua , que he admiravel. Tomem de vinho branco fem geflo hum quartilho ,de mel duas onças &meya , em panela vidrada , ferva a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for verdete"}, {"found_name": "Ants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2034, "target_cluster_name": "Ants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While ants do not produce honey, they are often discussed in proximity to bees in premodern texts concerning insects and their societal structures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While ants do not produce honey, they are often discussed in proximity to bees in premodern texts concerning insects and their societal structures."}, {"found_name": "Wasps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2271, "target_cluster_name": "Wasps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wasps are hymenopterans, like bees, and are sometimes discussed in relation to bees in premodern texts, though they do not produce honey in the same way.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wasps are hymenopterans, like bees, and are sometimes discussed in relation to bees in premodern texts, though they do not produce honey in the same way."}, {"found_name": "Silkworm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3296, "target_cluster_name": "silkworm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Silkworms are insects that produce a valuable substance (silk), and are sometimes discussed alongside other insects that produce valuable substances like honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Silkworms are insects that produce a valuable substance (silk), and are sometimes discussed alongside other insects that produce valuable substances like honey."}, {"found_name": "Melipona", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2530, "target_cluster_name": "Melipona", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melipona bees are known for producing honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Melipona bees are known for producing honey.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Floride", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 56, "members": [{"entity_id": "floride", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Floride", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 42, "variants": ["Floride", "Floride occidentale", "Florides"], "contexts": ["geographical region", "northern extremity connected to South America"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["land in the Western Indies", "region where Saissasfras tree is found"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["region mentioned in relation to Gulf-stream"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["region affected by storm curve"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Floride", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 1545, "stable_key": "clu_e069c91ac1a0251b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Florida", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in the southeastern United States, historically explored.", "note": "The spelling 'Floride' is a historical variant.", "wikipedia_extract": "Florida (  FLORR-ih-də; Spanish: [floˈɾiða] ) is a state in the Southeastern and South Atlantic regions of the United States. It borders the Gulf of Mexico to the west, Alabama to the northwest, Georgia to the north, the Atlantic Ocean to the east, the Straits of Florida to the south, and The Bahamas to the southeast. About two-thirds of Florida occupies a peninsula between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. It has the longest coastline in the contiguous United States, spanning approximately 1,350 miles (2,170 km), not including its many barrier islands. It is the only state that borders both the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. With a population of over 23 million, it is the third-most populous state in the United States and ranks seventh in population density as of 2020."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Gulf of Mexico", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 577, "target_cluster_name": "Golfe du Mexique", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf of Mexico borders Florida to the west.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf of Mexico borders Florida to the west."}, {"found_name": "Atlantic Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 318, "target_cluster_name": "Atlantic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Atlantic Ocean borders Florida to the east.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Atlantic Ocean borders Florida to the east."}, {"found_name": "Bahamas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 918, "target_cluster_name": "Bahama", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Straits of Florida lie between Florida and the Bahamas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Straits of Florida lie between Florida and the Bahamas."}]}, {"canonical_name": "azeyte", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 62, "members": [{"entity_id": "azeyte", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Azeyte", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 16, "variants": ["Azeyte", "Aceyte", "Azeyte de Azahar", "Azeyte de Liquidambar", "Azeyte de a ote", "Azeyto", "aceyte", "azeyte", "azeyte Mir", "azeyte de Almen", "azeyte de Almendras dulces", "azeyte de Oliua", "azeytedeLiquidambar"], "contexts": ["oil", "substance, oil"]}, {"entity_id": "azeyte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "azeyte", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 38, "variants": ["azeyte", "Azeyte", "azeyte commum", "azeyte velho"], "contexts": ["emetic ingredient", "mixed with vinegar"]}, {"entity_id": "olio_di", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "olio di", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["olio di", "Olio dt Kolfo", "olio di camp", "olio di coto"], "contexts": ["oil preparation", "oil made from quince"]}, {"entity_id": "oleum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "oleum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["oleum"], "contexts": ["oil used in wound treatment", "oily substance in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Azeyte", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 1546, "stable_key": "clu_0d39b020acfac381", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oil", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The contexts 'oil' and 'emetic ingredient' strongly suggest a general term for oil, which could be vegetable or animal.", "wikidata_id": "Q170885", "wikidata_description": "hydrophobic liquid containing volatile aroma compounds from plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essential%20oil", "wikipedia_extract": "Essential oils are compodes of hydrophobic volatile organic compounds, usually in liquid form, obtained from plant material. Essential oils are also known as volatile oils, ethereal oils, aetheroleum, or simply as the oil of the plant from which they were extracted, such as oil of clove. An essential oil is essential in the sense that the oil contains the fragrance or essence of the plant. The term \"essential\" used here does not indicate that it is nutritionally required in the diet, as with essential amino acid or essential fatty acid. Essential oils, according to ISO, are produced either by steam distillation, by mechanical manipulation of the rind of the fruits of the genus Citrus (also called cold pressing), or by dry distillation. Other methods, such as solvent extraction, are not accepted and, in fact, result in different perfumed products (pommades, concretes, and absolutes)."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hyffopo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 54, "members": [{"entity_id": "hylopo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Hylopo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hylopo"], "contexts": ["ingredient in decoctions", "hyssop, an ingredient in a syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "hyffopo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hyffopo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 51, "variants": ["hyffopo", "Hyffopo", "Hyflopo", "Hyfopo", "Hyslopo", "Hysope", "Hyſiopo", "hyfiopo", "hyflop", "hyflopo", "hysoppo", "hyſtopo", "hyſſopo"], "contexts": ["hyssop, an herb", "herb used in preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Hylopo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hyffopo", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 1547, "stable_key": "clu_ca964e1c33d5dbce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hyssop", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Hyssopus officinalis", "family": "Lamiaceae", "description": "An aromatic herb used historically in medicine and religious practices.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hyssopus officinalis or hyssop is a shrub in the Lamiaceae or mint family native to Southern Europe, the Middle East, and the region surrounding the Caspian Sea. Due to its purported properties as an antiseptic, cough reliever, and expectorant, it has been used in traditional herbal medicine. Hyssop is a brightly coloured shrub or subshrub that ranges from 30 to 60 cm (12 to 24 in) in height. The stem is woody at the base, from which grow a number of upright branches. Its leaves are lanceolate, dark green, and from 2 to 2.5 cm (3⁄4 to 1 in) long. During the summer, hyssop produces pink, blue (ssp. aristadus), or, more rarely, white fragrant (ssp. f. albus) flowers."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hyfopo", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 997, "target_cluster_name": "Hysop", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "fcguenti ricette. LOCH DUnTìPATRO LPCH DI PRÀk-iSip feco7ido(j aleno. C9^^o^od$Pmlo* , Lin {emc abbrufta- Imedimarrob.} Viiài Ghiaggiuolo I Hyfopo DEL RICETTARIO- i6f Hyfopo Logon zia App»o cioè prczz", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Popayan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 53, "members": [{"entity_id": "popayan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Popayan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 47, "variants": ["Popayan", "Popajan"], "contexts": ["mountainous population center in Colombia", "city with population 171,000"]}, {"entity_id": "popayan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Popayan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Popayan"], "contexts": ["city in Colombia", "city in 1788 with specific latitude and elevation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Popayan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Popayan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1548, "stable_key": "clu_55aa952688a12997", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Popayán", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antioquia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1757, "target_cluster_name": "Antioquia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Popayán was the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, which included the province of Antioquia. Both are significant historical regions in Colombia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Popayán was the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, which included the province of Antioquia. Both are significant historical regions in Colombia."}, {"found_name": "Bogotá", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1562, "target_cluster_name": "Bogota", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bogotá was the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, of which Popayán was a major city and administrative center.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bogotá was the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, of which Popayán was a major city and administrative center."}, {"found_name": "Cali", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2189, "target_cluster_name": "Cali", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cali is a major city in the historical region of Popayán, located within the same modern-day department of Valle del Cauca in Colombia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cali is a major city in the historical region of Popayán, located within the same modern-day department of Valle del Cauca in Colombia."}, {"found_name": "Equatorial America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Popayán is located in the northern part of South America, which falls within the broader geographical and historical context of Equatorial America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Popayán