ogether as related creative expressions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "British Museum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1235, "target_cluster_name": "British Museum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The British Museum houses vast collections of art from various cultures and periods.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The British Museum houses vast collections of art from various cultures and periods.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "proportion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1301, "target_cluster_name": "proportion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Proportion is a fundamental principle in architectural theory and art.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Proportion is a fundamental principle in architectural theory and art.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "photography", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1922, "target_cluster_name": "photography", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Photography is a form of visual art.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Photography is a form of visual art.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "representations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2301, "target_cluster_name": "representations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Art is a form of representation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Art is a form of representation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "symbolic conceptions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2594, "target_cluster_name": "symbolic conceptions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Art often employs symbolic representation to convey meaning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Art often employs symbolic representation to convey meaning.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "literature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2785, "target_cluster_name": "literature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Art and literature are often considered closely related forms of creative expression, with historical texts frequently discussing them in conjunction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Art and literature are often considered closely related forms of creative expression, with historical texts frequently discussing them in conjunction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "dancing", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2899, "target_cluster_name": "dancing", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dance is a form of art.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Dance is a form of art.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "CONFEZIONE D'AS- fetida", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "panno, & alla colatura bene {premuta aggiugni. iVkkItiumato Ub ii. Et bolii à cpofumazione del l'aceto, & col mele detto fa lat- touaro fecondo l'arte. JLAT T OVARI PVRGANTl. HI ER A SEMP^-ICE di Gale", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of purgative confection"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mouros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 50, "members": [{"entity_id": "mouros", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mouros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 49, "variants": ["Mouros", "Moura", "Mouro", "mouro", "mouros", "mouros africanos"], "contexts": ["wash themselves every three days", "people of North Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "mouros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mouros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mouros"], "contexts": ["Moors, Muslim inhabitants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mouros", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mouros", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1557, "stable_key": "clu_268d2a8c34330061", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'Moors' historically referred to Muslim inhabitants of Northwest Africa and Iberia, in this context, it likely functions as a descriptor of a group of people and their practices, rather than a specific medical or scientific entity.", "wikidata_id": "Q30198", "wikidata_description": "wetland dominated by herbaceous plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh", "wikipedia_extract": "In ecology, a marsh is a wetland that is dominated by herbaceous plants rather than by woody plants. More in general, the word can be used for any low-lying and seasonally waterlogged terrain. In Europe and in agricultural literature low-lying meadows that require draining and embanked polderlands are also referred to as marshes or marshland. Marshes can often be found at the edges of lakes and streams, where they form a transition between the aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. They are often dominated by grasses, rushes or reeds. If woody plants are present they tend to be low-growing shrubs, and the marsh is sometimes called a carr."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arábios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 538, "target_cluster_name": "Arábios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical texts often use 'Moors' to refer to Arab and Berber peoples, including those who practiced medicine in regions like Al-Andalus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historical texts often use 'Moors' to refer to Arab and Berber peoples, including those who practiced medicine in regions like Al-Andalus.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arab", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 253, "target_cluster_name": "árabes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Moors' was often used in medieval and early modern European texts to refer to the Muslim inhabitants of the Iberian Peninsula, who were largely of Arab and Berber descent. Therefore, 'Arab' is a closely related ethno-linguistic group often conflated with or included under the umbrella term 'Moors'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'Moors' was often used in medieval and early modern European texts to refer to the Muslim inhabitants of the Iberian Peninsula, who were largely of Arab and Berber descent. Therefore, 'Arab' is a closely related ethno-linguistic group often conflated with or included under the umbrella term 'Moors'."}, {"found_name": "Arab physicians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 538, "target_cluster_name": "Arábios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to 'Arab', 'Arab physicians' refers to a group strongly associated with the historical Moorish presence and influence in Iberia and the wider Mediterranean world, particularly in the context of scientific and medical knowledge transmission.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Similar to 'Arab', 'Arab physicians' refers to a group strongly associated with the historical Moorish presence and influence in Iberia and the wider Mediterranean world, particularly in the context of scientific and medical knowledge transmission."}, {"found_name": "Hebrews", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1302, "target_cluster_name": "Hebreus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, both 'Moors' and 'Hebrews' were significant minority religious and cultural groups present in the Iberian Peninsula during the periods covered by the concordance. Historical texts might discuss them in parallel or in contrast, indicating a contextual relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While distinct, both 'Moors' and 'Hebrews' were significant minority religious and cultural groups present in the Iberian Peninsula during the periods covered by the concordance. Historical texts might discuss them in parallel or in contrast, indicating a contextual relationship."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Venemous Beasts", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 49, "members": [{"entity_id": "venemous_beasts", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Venemous Beasts", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 19, "variants": ["Venemous Beasts", "Venemous Creature", "Venemous Serpents", "Venemous serpents", "venemous Beasts", "venemous creature"], "contexts": ["creatures whose bites are under Mars", "dangerous animals"]}, {"entity_id": "venom", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Venom", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 13, "variants": ["Venom"], "contexts": ["substance expelled by the herb", "substance borrage helps resist and expel"]}, {"entity_id": "animal_venenoſo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "animal venenoſo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 16, "variants": ["animal venenoſo", "animaes muyto venenofos", "animaes venenofos", "animaes venenoſos", "animal venenofo", "animal venenoso", "animalium venenatorum", "bichos venenofos", "bichos venenoſos"], "contexts": ["venomous animal", "poisonous animal"]}, {"entity_id": "venom", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "venom", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["venom"], "contexts": ["poisonous substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Venemous Beasts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "animal venenoſo", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Venom", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "animal venenoſo", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Venom", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "venom", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1558, "stable_key": "clu_0a60c4ce26e66420", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venomous Animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Creatures capable of inflicting harm through venomous bites or stings.", "wikipedia_extract": "The following is a list of poisonous animals, which are animals that passively deliver toxins (called poison) to their victims upon contact such as through inhalation, absorption through the skin, or after being ingested. They are often distinguished from venomous animals, which actively inject their toxins (called venom) into their victims through a venom apparatus such as fangs or a stinger. The only difference between poisonous animals and venomous animals is how they deliver the toxins. This list deals exclusively with poisonous animals. This list is a partial list of animals that are poisonous to humans and other animals in that their flesh is toxic if consumed, or in some cases if they are touched: Some of these toads are \"milked\" for their bufotoxins."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Spiders", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 486, "target_cluster_name": "spiders", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spiders, many of which are venomous, are often discussed alongside other venomous creatures in historical zoological and medical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spiders, many of which are venomous, are often discussed alongside other venomous creatures in historical zoological and medical texts."}, {"found_name": "Viper", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 821, "target_cluster_name": "Viper", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vipers are a specific type of snake known for their venom, making them a direct example within the broader category of venomous animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vipers are a specific type of snake known for their venom, making them a direct example within the broader category of venomous animals."}, {"found_name": "Rattlesnake", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2999, "target_cluster_name": "rattlesnake", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rattlesnakes are a specific type of venomous snake, commonly cited as an example of dangerous fauna.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rattlesnakes are a specific type of venomous snake, commonly cited as an example of dangerous fauna."}]}, {"canonical_name": "evacuação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 49, "members": [{"entity_id": "evacuação", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "evacuação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["evacuação"], "contexts": ["expulsion of waste from the body"]}, {"entity_id": "evacuação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "evacuação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 48, "variants": ["evacuação", "Evacuações", "evacuando", "evacuar", "evacuartanto", "evacuated", "evacuatimor", "evacuation", "evacuativos", "evac