sychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "may, the difficulty is always the same: _the Many known by the One_. Or does one seriously think he understands better _how_ the knower 'connects' its objects, when one calls the former a transcendent", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for objects"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Imaginação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 82, "members": [{"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["imagination", "Imagination"], "contexts": ["faculty for forming new ideas", "faculty of forming mental images"]}, {"entity_id": "imaginação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Imaginação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 31, "variants": ["Imaginação", "Imagination", "Imaginativos", "imaginatio", "imagination", "imaginationes", "imaginativos", "imaginaçaõ", "imaginação", "imaginaçıō"], "contexts": ["Concept of imagination", "imagination"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 25, "variants": ["imagination", "Imagination"], "contexts": ["Soul's faculty", "mental faculty of forming images"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["imagination"], "contexts": ["faculty for forming new ideas", "faculty of forming mental images"]}, {"entity_id": "fancies", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fancies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fancies"], "contexts": ["human preferences and whims"]}, {"entity_id": "fancy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fancy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Fancy", "fancy"], "contexts": ["imagination or mind", "imagination where words contract relation"]}, {"entity_id": "fancies", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fancies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fancies"], "contexts": ["relics of sensory motions within us"]}, {"entity_id": "fancy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fancy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fancy"], "contexts": ["imagination and creativity"]}, {"entity_id": "fantasia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Fantasia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fantasia", "fantasia", "fantaſia"], "contexts": ["faculty of imagination", "resides in the head"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Imagination", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Imaginação", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 1586, "stable_key": "clu_fcbb3a8ed8d0ad7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "imagination", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q91048696", "wikidata_description": "vocal track by Bob Dylan; 2017 studio recording; cover version", "wikipedia_extract": "Sociological imagination is a term used in the field of sociology to describe a framework for understanding social reality that places personal experiences within a broader social and historical context. It was coined by American sociologist C. Wright Mills in his 1959 book The Sociological Imagination to describe the type of insight offered by the discipline of sociology. Today, the term is used in many sociology textbooks to explain the nature of sociology and its relevance in daily life. In The Sociological Imagination, Mills attempts to reconcile two different and abstract concepts of social reality: the \"individual\" and the \"society.\" Accordingly, Mills defined sociological imagination as \"the awareness of the relationship between personal experience and the wider society.\" In exercising one's sociological imagination, one seeks to understand situations in one's life by looking at situations in broader society. For example, a single student who fails to keep up with the academic demands of college and ends up dropping out may be perceived to have faced personal difficulties or faults; however, when one considers that around 50% of college students in the United States fail to graduate, we can understand this one student's trajectory as part of a larger social issue.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sociological_imagination"}, "member_count": 9, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The sociological imagination requires connecting individual experiences to broader social forces, which is a form of associating ideas and concepts. The candidate term directly addresses this cognitive process.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The sociological imagination requires connecting individual experiences to broader social forces, which is a form of associating ideas and concepts. The candidate term directly addresses this cognitive process."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ego", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 41, "members": [{"entity_id": "ego", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ego", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 39, "variants": ["ego", "Ego"], "contexts": ["last fruit of representations", "individual's sense of self"]}, {"entity_id": "ego", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ego", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["ego"], "contexts": ["the self or 'I'", "the self, the individual being"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ego", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ego", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1587, "stable_key": "clu_7b222fd05e7d33da", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Self", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'ego' in a philosophical or psychological context often refers to the self or the 'I'.", "wikidata_id": "Q910150", "wikidata_description": "Process of a disordered system forming organized structures without external direction.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-assembly", "wikipedia_extract": "Self-assembly is a process in which a disordered system of pre-existing components forms an organized structure or pattern as a consequence of specific, local interactions among the components themselves, without external direction. When the constitutive components are molecules, the process is termed molecular self-assembly. Self-assembly can be classified as either static or dynamic. In static self-assembly, the ordered state forms as a system approaches equilibrium, reducing its free energy. However, in dynamic self-assembly, patterns of pre-existing components organized by specific local interactions are not commonly described as \"self-assembled\" by scientists in the associated disciplines. These structures are better described as \"self-organized\", although these terms are often used interchangeably."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Thought", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 534, "target_cluster_name": "thought", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "Many known by the One_. Or does one seriously think he understands better _how_ the knower 'connects' its objects, when one calls the former a transcendental Ego and the latter a 'Manifold of Intuitio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for knower"}, {"found_name": "Things", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3069, "target_cluster_name": "THINGS", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "Many known by the One_. Or does one seriously think he understands better _how_ the knower 'connects' its objects, when one calls the former a transcendental Ego and the latter a 'Manifold of Intuitio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for objects"}, {"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of 'self-assembly' involves components interacting locally to form an organized structure, which can be seen as a form of 'association of ideas' where individual ideas or components come together to form a larger concept or structure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of 'self-assembly' involves components interacting locally to form an organized structure, which can be seen as a form of 'association of ideas' where individual ideas or components come together to form a larger concept or structure."}, {"found_name": "Cognition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 656, "target_cluster_name": "cognition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Self-assembly, as a process of forming organized structures from disordered components, shares conceptual parallels with cognitive processes, which involve the organization and integration of information to form understanding.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Self-assembly, as a process of forming organized structures from disordered components, shares conceptual parallels with cognitive processes, which involve the organization and integration of information to form understanding."}, {"found_name": "Conception", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 282, "target_cluster_name": "conception", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The formation of an organized structure through self-assembly can be metaphorically linked to the process of 'conception,' where disparate elements or ideas are brought together to form a coherent whole or a new idea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The formation of an organized structure through self-assembly can be metaphorically linked to the process of 'conception,' where disparate elements or ideas are brought together to form a coherent whole or a new idea."}, {"found_name": "transcendental Ego", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "Many known by the One_. Or does one seriously think he understands better _how_ the knower 'connects' its objects, when one calls the former a transcendental Ego and the latter a 'Manifold of Intuitio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for knower"}, {"found_name": "Manifold of Intuition", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "Many known by the One_. Or does one seriously think he understands better _how_ the knower 'connects' its objects, when one calls the former a transcendental Ego and the latter a 'Manifold of Intuitio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for objects"}]}, {"canonical_name": "nervous system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANAT