n Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 256, "target_cluster_name": "Méditerranée", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Mediterranean region is often included in the definition of the 'Eastern world'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Mediterranean region is often included in the definition of the 'Eastern world'."}, {"found_name": "must", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "melancólicos*. Quanto aos «ciúmes», que os fazem cair em «muy grande fíiría», é este um estado perfeitamente conhecido, em que o elephante se torna, o que no Oriente chamam must, O elephante musty o q", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for elephant in the East"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Falling-sickness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 45, "members": [{"entity_id": "falling_sickness", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Falling sickness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["Falling sickness"], "contexts": ["epilepsy"]}, {"entity_id": "falling-sickness", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Falling-sickness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 38, "variants": ["Falling-sickness", "Fall", "Falling Sicknes", "Falling sicknes", "Falling sickness", "Falling-Sickness", "Fal∣ling-sickness", "falling-sickness"], "contexts": ["epilepsy", "neurological condition treated by Briony"]}, {"entity_id": "epileptical", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Epileptical", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["Epileptical"], "contexts": ["condition mistletoe virtues were expected to help"]}, {"entity_id": "epilepsy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "epilepsy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["epilepsy"], "contexts": ["neurological disorder causing seizures"]}, {"entity_id": "epileptic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "epileptic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["epileptic"], "contexts": ["man with chronic condition", "potential element in the case"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Falling sickness", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Falling-sickness", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 1591, "stable_key": "clu_f567c13cf326623b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Epilepsy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Epilepsy", "wikidata_id": "Q41571", "wikidata_description": "human neurological disease causing seizures", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epilepsy", "wikipedia_extract": "Epilepsy is a group of neurological disorders characterized by a tendency for recurrent, unprovoked seizures. A seizure is a sudden burst of abnormal electrical activity in the brain that can cause a variety of symptoms, ranging from brief lapses of awareness or muscle jerks to prolonged convulsions. These episodes can result in physical injuries, either directly, such as broken bones, or through causing accidents. The diagnosis of epilepsy typically requires at least two unprovoked seizures occurring more than 24 hours apart. In some cases, however, it may be diagnosed after a single unprovoked seizure if clinical evidence suggests a high risk of recurrence. Isolated seizures that occur without recurrence risk or are provoked by identifiable causes are not considered indicative of epilepsy."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Guatimala", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 40, "members": [{"entity_id": "guatimala", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guatimala", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 39, "variants": ["Guatimala", "GuATiMALA", "Gualiiuala"], "contexts": ["Country in the Americas", "Spanish captaincy general"]}, {"entity_id": "guatimala", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guatimala", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guatimala"], "contexts": ["volcano location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guatimala", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guatimala", "similarity": 0.825}], "id": 1592, "stable_key": "clu_c323f95817731c0c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guatemala", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Guatemala", "wikidata_id": "Q1555", "wikidata_description": "capital and largest city of the Republic of Guatemala", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guatemala%20City", "wikipedia_extract": "Guatemala City (Spanish: Ciudad de Guatemala), also known colloquially by the nickname Guate, is the national capital and largest city of the Republic of Guatemala. It serves as the municipal capital of the surrounding Guatemala Department. Its metropolitan area is also the largest in Central America. The city is located in a mountain valley called Valle de la Ermita (English: Hermitage Valley) in the south-central part of the country. Guatemala City is the site of the native Mayan city of Kaminaljuyu in Mesoamerica, which was occupied primarily between 1500 BCE and 1200 CE. The present city was founded by the Spanish after their colonial capital, now called Antigua Guatemala, was destroyed by the devastating 1773 Santa Marta earthquake and its aftershocks."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antigua Guatemala", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2112, "target_cluster_name": "Antigua", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antigua Guatemala was the former capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and is a significant historical city within the modern nation of Guatemala.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antigua Guatemala was the former capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and is a significant historical city within the modern nation of Guatemala."}, {"found_name": "Antigua", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2112, "target_cluster_name": "Antigua", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antigua Guatemala was the capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and the name 'Guatemala' itself is often used to refer to the historical region and its capital.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antigua Guatemala was the capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and the name 'Guatemala' itself is often used to refer to the historical region and its capital.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Azedos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 40, "members": [{"entity_id": "azedo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "azedo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["azedo"], "contexts": ["sour flavor", "Taste quality"]}, {"entity_id": "azedas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "azedas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["azedas"], "contexts": ["taste of the fruit", "taste quality of the fruits"]}, {"entity_id": "azedos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Azedos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 24, "variants": ["Azedos", "azcdos", "azeda", "azedar", "azedo", "azedos", "azeedo"], "contexts": ["sour substances used for treatment", "sour quality of substances"]}, {"entity_id": "azedas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "azedas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Azedas", "azedas"], "contexts": ["sour", "sour grapes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Azedos", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azedas", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azedas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Azedos", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azedas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azedas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1593, "stable_key": "clu_413cbfe353639f4b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sour", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Having a sharp, acidic taste or quality.", "note": "Refers to a taste quality.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sourness, also known as sour, is a basic taste sensation perceived through hydrogen ions from acidic compounds interacting with taste bud receptors, and it can also refer to an unpleasant or acidic quality in taste, disposition, or tone. It is a warning sign for potentially spoiled food, though it is balanced in some food products by sweetness, and it is associated with a low pH. The most common foods with natural sourness are fruits, such as lemon, lime, grape, orange, tamarind, and bitter melon. Fermented foods, such as wine, sour cream, vinegar or yogurt, may have sour taste. Children show a greater enjoyment of sour flavors than adults, and sour candy containing citric acid or malic acid is common."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "acidez", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2434, "target_cluster_name": "acidez", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct Spanish translation of 'acidity', a core component of sourness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Direct Spanish translation of 'acidity', a core component of sourness."}, {"found_name": "acridity", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 621, "target_cluster_name": "acrimonia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A related taste sensation, often associated with sourness or sharpness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A related taste sensation, often associated with sourness or sharpness."}]}, {"canonical_name": "stimulus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 39, "members": [{"entity_id": "stimulus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "stimulus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 24, "variants": ["stimulus"], "contexts": ["agent causing a response", "trigger for reaction"]}, {"entity_id": "stimuli", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "stimuli", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 14, "variants": ["stimuli", "Stimuli"], "contexts": ["agents causing a physiological response", "factors eliciting a response"]}, {"entity_id": "stimuli", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "stimuli", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["stimuli"], "contexts": ["agents causing a response"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "stimulus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stimuli", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "stimuli", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stimuli", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1594, "stable_key": "clu_d4a2749b2f79c50b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stimulus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14859560", "wikidata_description": "process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell or an organism (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of a stimulus"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Action and reaction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 961, "target_cluster_name": "Action and reaction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stimulus and reaction are fundamental concepts in understanding how systems respond to external influences, often discussed together in scientific and philosophical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Stimulus and reaction are fundamental concepts in understanding how systems respond to external influences, often discussed together in scientific and philosophical contexts."}, {"found_name": "Appetite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2030, "target_cluster_name": "Appetite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Appetite is a form of internal stimulus that drives behavior and physiological responses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Appetite is a form of internal stimulus that drives behavior and physiological responses."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Desire can be considered a psychological stimulus that motivates action and behavior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Desire can be considered a psychological stimulus that motivates action and behavior."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and will are often responses to stimuli, and can also act as internal stimuli themselves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Emotions and will are often responses to stimuli, and can also act as internal stimuli themselves."}, {"found_name": "Impulse", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3080, "target_cluster_name": "impulse", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An impulse is a sudden stimulus that causes a reaction or action.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An impulse is a sudden stimulus that causes a reaction or action."}, {"found_name": "Instinct", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1633, "target_cluster_name": "instinct", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instincts are innate responses to specific stimuli, representing a fundamental biological mechanism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instincts are innate responses to specific stimuli, representing a fundamental biological mechanism."}, {"found_name": "Perception", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1513, "target_cluster_name": "perception", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Perception is the process by which organisms interpret and organize sensory information from stimuli in the environment.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Perception is the process by which organisms interpret and organize sensory information from stimuli in the environment."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Venus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 39, "members": [{"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Venus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 15, "variants": ["Venus"], "contexts": ["inner planetary group member", "a planet"]}, {"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Venus", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 24, "variants": ["Venus"], "contexts": ["planet observed during transit", "planet with retrograde apsides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Venus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Venus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1595, "stable_key": "clu_da049e0ab22c08a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venus", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikipedia_extract": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun. It is often called Earth's \"twin\" or \"sister\" among the planets of the Solar System for its orbit being the closest to Earth's, both being terrestrial planets, and having the most similar and nearly equal size, mass, and surface gravity. Venus, though, is significantly different, especially as it has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K (464 °C; 867 °F) and a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, and as an inferior planet it is always relatively close to the Sun, either as the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "heavenly bodies", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1950, "target_cluster_name": "heavenly bodies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Venus is a celestial body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "planets", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2065, "target_cluster_name": "planets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a specific planet, and historical texts discussing planets would likely mention individual planets.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Venus is a specific planet, and historical texts discussing planets would likely mention individual planets.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sun", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 573, "target_cluster_name": "Sun", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun."}, {"found_name": "planet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2065, "target_cluster_name": "planets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a planet.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is a planet."}, {"found_name": "celestial bodies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1950, "target_cluster_name": "heavenly bodies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is a celestial body."}, {"found_name": "Faint celestial objects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3170, "target_cluster_name": "telescopic planets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a celestial object that can be observed with telescopes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is a celestial object that can be observed with telescopes."}, {"found_name": "Neptune", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2411, "target_cluster_name": "Neptune", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Neptune and Venus are planets in our solar system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Neptune and Venus are planets in our solar system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Laguna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 39, "members": [{"entity_id": "laguna", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Laguna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 28, "variants": ["Laguna"], "contexts": ["mentioned regarding Çocotora island", "author whose work was corrected"]}, {"entity_id": "laguna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Laguna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Laguna", "Lagun"], "contexts": ["translator of Dioscorides", "cited as an authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Laguna", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Laguna", "similarity": 0.718}], "id": 1596, "stable_key": "clu_81a76c2d0e1dd83d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Andrés Laguna", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1511, "death_year": 1559, "description": "Spanish physician, botanist, and pharmacologist; translator of Dioscorides.", "wikidata_id": "Q524380", "wikidata_description": "Spanish botanist (1499-1559)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Laguna wrote a significant commentary on Dioscorides' De Materia Medica, a foundational text in pharmacology and botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Laguna wrote a significant commentary on Dioscorides' De Materia Medica, a foundational text in pharmacology and botany."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Laguna, like many physicians of his era, was deeply influenced by and commented upon the works of Galen, a central figure in ancient Greek and Roman medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Laguna, like many physicians of his era, was deeply influenced by and commented upon the works of Galen, a central figure in ancient Greek and Roman medicine."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Obstructions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "obstructions", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Obstructions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 26, "variants": ["Obstructions", "Obstructi", "Obstructions of the Gall", "Obstructions of the Liver", "Obstructions of the Liver and Spleen", "Obstru∣ctions", "obstructions"], "contexts": ["blockages in organs", "condition borrage syrup helps open"]}, {"entity_id": "obstrucções", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "obstrucções", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 7, "variants": ["obstrucções"], "contexts": ["blockages in the body", "obstructions"]}, {"entity_id": "obstrucçoens", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "obstrucçoens", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["obstrucçoens"], "contexts": ["blockages in the body", "blockages"]}, {"entity_id": "obstructione", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "obstructione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["obstructione"], "contexts": ["obstruction, medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "obstructioni", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "obstructioni", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["obstructioni"], "contexts": ["blockage in the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Obstructions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "obstrucções", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Obstructions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "obstrucçoens", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Obstructions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "obstructione", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Obstructions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "obstructioni", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 1597, "stable_key": "clu_e9ce01c10881a497", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obstruction", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Blockage or impediment within the body or organs.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bowel obstruction, also known as intestinal obstruction, is a mechanical or functional obstruction of the intestines that prevents the normal movement of the products of digestion. Either the small bowel or large bowel may be affected. Signs and symptoms include abdominal pain, vomiting, bloating and not passing gas. Mechanical obstruction is the cause of about 5 to 15% of cases of severe abdominal pain of sudden onset requiring admission to hospital. Causes of bowel obstruction include adhesions, hernias, volvulus, endometriosis, inflammatory bowel disease, appendicitis, tumors, diverticulitis, ischemic bowel, tuberculosis and intussusception. Small bowel obstructions are most often due to adhesions and hernias, while large bowel obstructions are most often due to tumors and volvulus."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ardores", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "ardor", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ardor", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["ardor"], "contexts": ["burning sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "ardores", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ardores", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 37, "variants": ["ardores", "ardor", "ardores da ourina", "ardores da pelle", "ardores da urina", "ardores de corpo", "ardores de estomago", "ardores de ourina", "ardores na ourina", "ardoresda ourina", "ardoresda pelle", "ardoresdaourina"], "contexts": ["burning sensations", "symptom of burning sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ardor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ardores", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1598, "stable_key": "clu_2034853ee889c7e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Burning sensation", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A symptom characterized by a feeling of heat or burning.", "wikipedia_extract": "Dysesthesia is an unpleasant, abnormal sense of touch. Its etymology comes from the Greek word \"dys,\" meaning \"bad,\" and \"aesthesis,\" which means \"sensation\" (abnormal sensation). It often presents as pain but may also present as an inappropriate, but not discomforting, sensation. It is caused by lesions of the nervous system, peripheral or central, and it involves sensations, whether spontaneous or evoked, such as burning, wetness, itching, electric shock, and pins and needles. Dysesthesia can include sensations in any bodily tissue, including most often the mouth, scalp, skin, or legs. It is sometimes described as feeling like acid under the skin."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 70, "target_cluster_name": "pain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Burning sensation is a type of pain, a specific modality of painful sensation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Burning sensation is a type of pain, a specific modality of painful sensation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "alcaçuz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 43, "members": [{"entity_id": "alcaçuz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alcaçuz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["alcaçuz", "alcacuz", "alcaçui", "alcaçusf", "alçacus"], "contexts": ["licorice plant", "licorice, used medicinally"]}, {"entity_id": "alcaçuz", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "alcaçuz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 30, "variants": ["alcaçuz", "alcaçus", "alcaçùz", "alcáçuz"], "contexts": ["licorice used in a syrup", "root used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "liquorish", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Liquorish", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liquorish"], "contexts": ["make stridulous noise upon divulsion"]}, {"entity_id": "liquoris", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Liquoris", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Liquoris"], "contexts": ["English plant with woody stalks and leaves", "plant root used for comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alcaçuz", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "alcaçuz", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1599, "stable_key": "clu_12583106928e6634", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Licorice", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Glycyrrhiza glabra", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The description 'make stridulous noise upon divulsion' is unusual for licorice, but the primary identification as 'Liquoris' strongly points to it. It's possible the text refers to a specific preparation or a less common characteristic.", "wikidata_id": "Q15447514", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Licorice_pipe", "wikipedia_extract": "A licorice pipe is a candy made of licorice shaped like a tobacco pipe, often with red sugar balls on the head of the pipe. A pipe may weigh around 16 grams, and the licorice is usually soft and sweet. There are also other varieties, for example with blue or yellow sugar balls, the latter with a taste of sea salt. The first known licorice pipe (Finnish: lakupiippu) was manufactured as early as the 1920s at the Heikki Huhtamäki factory in Kokkola, Finland. In January 2010, displaying licorice pipes for sale in stores was banned in Norway. In August 2013, licorice pipes again made headlines because of a forthcoming EU directive on tobacco prevention, with the potential of a ban which could affect the sale of sweets and toys imitating tobacco products."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "macella", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "macela", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "macela", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["macela"], "contexts": ["a plant compared to negundo"]}, {"entity_id": "macella", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "macella", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 37, "variants": ["macella", "Macella", "Macellagalega", "macela", "macelagalega"], "contexts": ["yarrow used in decoction", "plant used in emetic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "macela", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "macella", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1600, "stable_key": "clu_32f03fb1a5893ee5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yarrow", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Achillea millefolium", "family": "Asteraceae", "description": "A medicinal herb used in decoctions and emetics.", "wikipedia_extract": "Achillea millefolium, commonly known as yarrow () or common yarrow, is a flowering plant in the family Asteraceae. Growing to 1 metre (3+1⁄3 feet) tall, it is characterized by small whitish flowers, a tall stem of fernlike leaves, and a pungent odor. The plant is native to temperate regions of Eurasia and North America. It has been introduced as feed for livestock in New Zealand and Australia. It has some potential uses, including in traditional medicine. Achillea millefolium is an erect, herbaceous, perennial plant that produces one to several stems 0.2–1 metre (8–40 inches) in height, and has a spreading rhizomatous growth form."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Elicryſon", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "queheexcellente nas dores de Colica , que procedemde retençãoda camara ,&flatos. Tomemde trocifcos deAlandal fubtiliſſimamente moidos ,meya oytava , de cabeças de macellagalega , chamada Elicryſon ,& ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for macella"}, {"found_name": "cardo dos pizoeyros", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "couſadeſtas duas onças ,de maçans doces machucadas huma duzia ,de raizesdecardopenteador , aque chamão cardo dos pizoeyros , huma mão chea;ajuntemde macella, &de endro , de cada couſa deſtas hum punha", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for cardopenteador"}, {"found_name": "Joyna", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "queheexcellente nas dores de Colica , que procedemde retençãoda camara ,&flatos. Tomemde trocifcos deAlandal fubtiliſſimamente moidos ,meya oytava , de cabeças de macellagalega , chamada Elicryſon ,& ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for macella"}]}, {"canonical_name": "desmayos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "desmayos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "desmayos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["desmayos"], "contexts": ["fainting spells"]}, {"entity_id": "desmayos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "desmayos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 37, "variants": ["desmayos", "defmayo", "deimayos", "demaſias", "desmaios", "desmayados", "desmayo", "desmayos fyncopaes", "desmayos mortaes"], "contexts": ["fainting spells", "symptom attributed to the uterus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "desmayos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "desmayos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1601, "stable_key": "clu_056b4b13da7c8326", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fainting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "syncope", "wikidata_id": "Q7659241", "wikidata_description": "religious art concept", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swoon%20of%20the%20Virgin", "wikipedia_extract": "The Swoon of the Virgin, in Italian Lo Spasimo della Vergine, or Fainting Virgin Mary was an idea developed in the late Middle Ages, that the Virgin Mary had fainted during the Passion of Christ, most often placed while she watched the Crucifixion of Jesus. It was based on mentions in later texts of the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus (Acta Pilati), which describe Mary swooning. It was popular in later medieval art and theological literature, but as it was not mentioned in the Canonical Gospels, it became controversial - Protestants rejecting it outright, and from the 16th century discouraged also by many senior Catholic churchmen. The swoon might be placed during the episode of Christ Carrying the Cross, as on the Via Dolorosa in Jerusalem, but very commonly also during the Crucifixion of Jesus; Nicholas Penny estimates that \"about half of the surviving paintings of the Crucifixion made between 1300 and 1500 will be found to include the Virgin fainting\". It also appeared in works showing the Deposition from the Cross and Entombment of Christ, as well as the 15th-century novelty of Christ taking leave of his Mother. A fainting Mary is sometimes shown in art as early as the 12th century, and becomes common by the middle of the 13th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "syncope", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 300, "target_cluster_name": "Syncope", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Syncope is the medical term for fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Syncope is the medical term for fainting."}, {"found_name": "Faintings", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2269, "target_cluster_name": "Faintings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spanish for fainting.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting is a temporary state of unconsciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fainting is a temporary state of unconsciousness."}, {"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting (syncope) is a temporary loss of consciousness, which can be a precursor to or a less severe manifestation of conditions that might lead to coma.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fainting (syncope) is a temporary loss of consciousness, which can be a precursor to or a less severe manifestation of conditions that might lead to coma.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting (syncope) is a sudden, temporary loss of consciousness, which shares the characteristic of impaired consciousness with stupor, though typically less prolonged.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fainting (syncope) is a sudden, temporary loss of consciousness, which shares the characteristic of impaired consciousness with stupor, though typically less prolonged.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cramps", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "cramps", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cramps", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 36, "variants": ["Cramps", "Cramp"], "contexts": ["cause sinews to shrink", "condition helped by oil"]}, {"entity_id": "cramps", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cramps", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["cramps"], "contexts": ["result of eating fungi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cramps", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cramps", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 1602, "stable_key": "clu_dff298897e89d139", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cramps", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "cramps", "wikidata_id": "Q3696489", "wikidata_description": "Italian record label"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "colica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 547, "target_cluster_name": "colica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Colic pain is often described as cramping or spasmodic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Colic pain is often described as cramping or spasmodic.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "puxos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1603, "target_cluster_name": "puxos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cramps are involuntary muscle contractions, which are a component of convulsions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cramps are involuntary muscle contractions, which are a component of convulsions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Convulsions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 652, "target_cluster_name": "Convulsions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cramps are a type of involuntary muscle contraction, which can be closely related to or a symptom of convulsions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cramps are a type of involuntary muscle contraction, which can be closely related to or a symptom of convulsions."}, {"found_name": "Contractions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4201, "target_cluster_name": "contractions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cramps are a form of involuntary muscle contraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cramps are a form of involuntary muscle contraction."}]}, {"canonical_name": "puxos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "puxo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "puxo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["puxo"], "contexts": ["for the flux whatever it may be"]}, {"entity_id": "puxos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "puxos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 36, "variants": ["puxos", "Puxos", "puxos deourina"], "contexts": ["medical condition", "convulsions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "puxo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "puxos", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 1603, "stable_key": "clu_5ae70d2675a1469c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Convulsions", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Medical condition characterized by sudden, involuntary muscle contractions.", "note": "Also refers to 'flux whatever it may be'.", "wikipedia_extract": "A convulsion is a medical condition where the body muscles contract and relax rapidly and repeatedly, resulting in uncontrolled shaking. Because epileptic seizures typically include convulsions, the term convulsion is often used as a synonym for seizure. However, not all epileptic seizures result in convulsions, and not all convulsions are caused by epileptic seizures. Non-epileptic convulsions have no relation with epilepsy, and are caused by non-epileptic seizures. Convulsions can be caused by epilepsy, infections (including a severe form of listeriosis which is caused by eating food contaminated by Listeria monocytogenes), brain trauma, or other medical conditions. They can also occur from an electric shock or improperly enriched air for scuba diving."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cramps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1602, "target_cluster_name": "Cramps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cramps are involuntary muscle contractions, which are a component of convulsions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cramps are involuntary muscle contractions, which are a component of convulsions."}, {"found_name": "Tremor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1480, "target_cluster_name": "tremor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tremor is an involuntary quivering movement, which can be a symptom or a related phenomenon to convulsions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tremor is an involuntary quivering movement, which can be a symptom or a related phenomenon to convulsions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "elrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "elrey", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "elrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 35, "variants": ["elrey", "El Rey", "ElRey", "elRey nosso senhor", "elrey nosso senhor"], "contexts": ["royal title, the king", "the king"]}, {"entity_id": "elrey", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ElRey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["ElRey", "ElRey Chriſtianiffimo"], "contexts": ["the King", "King who granted permission for surgery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "elrey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ElRey", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 1604, "stable_key": "clu_9699c19388bc2ffd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "King", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This entity refers to the royal title 'King', rather than a specific individual, based on the contexts provided.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarch", "wikipedia_extract": "A monarch () is a head of state for life or until abdication, and therefore the head of state of a monarchy. A monarch usually exercises the highest authority and power in the state. Usually, a monarch either personally inherits the lawful right to exercise the state's sovereign rights (often referred to as the throne or the crown) or is selected by an established process from a family or cohort eligible to provide the nation's monarch. Alternatively, an individual may proclaim oneself monarch, or even usurp power, as many Ancient Greek tyrants did. If a young child is crowned the monarch, then a regent is often appointed to govern until the monarch reaches the requisite adult age to rule. Monarchs, as such, bear a variety of titles – king or queen, prince or princess (e.g., Sovereign Prince of Monaco), emperor or empress (e.g., Emperor of China, Emperor of Ethiopia, Emperor of Japan, Emperor of India), archduke, duke or grand duke (e.g., Grand Duke of Luxembourg), emir (e.g., Emir of Qatar), sultan (e.g., Sultan of Oman), or pharaoh."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Catholic Monarchs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4392, "target_cluster_name": "Catholic monarchs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific historical designation for monarchs of Spain, related to the general concept of 'King'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A specific historical designation for monarchs of Spain, related to the general concept of 'King'."}, {"found_name": "Rey de Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2078, "target_cluster_name": "Rey de Portugal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'King' is a generic title that encompasses 'King of Portugal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: 'King' is a generic title that encompasses 'King of Portugal'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Duque de Baviera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2100, "target_cluster_name": "Duque de Baviera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bavaria was ruled by Kings in addition to Dukes, and these titles represent sovereign rulers of the same territory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bavaria was ruled by Kings in addition to Dukes, and these titles represent sovereign rulers of the same territory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Major", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2522, "target_cluster_name": "Major", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Lord Mayor, particularly in historical contexts, could hold significant authority and prestige, sometimes rivaling or acting in place of royal representatives within a city or region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Lord Mayor, particularly in historical contexts, could hold significant authority and prestige, sometimes rivaling or acting in place of royal representatives within a city or region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Emperadores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2523, "target_cluster_name": "Emperadores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The King is a sovereign ruler, often subordinate to an Emperor in historical hierarchies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The King is a sovereign ruler, often subordinate to an Emperor in historical hierarchies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "governador", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2773, "target_cluster_name": "governador", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A Governor is typically a subordinate official to a King or monarch, often appointed to rule a territory on their behalf.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A Governor is typically a subordinate official to a King or monarch, often appointed to rule a territory on their behalf.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Uvas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "uvas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "uvas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["uvas"], "contexts": ["fruit", "fruit found on the island"]}, {"entity_id": "uvas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Uvas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 31, "variants": ["Uvas", "Uvas de caó", "Uvasde cao", "polpa de uvas pafladas", "uvas", "uvas brancas", "uvas de cao", "uvas de cão", "uvas de tad", "uvas decão", "uvas paffadas", "uvas pafladas", "uvas paladas", "uvas paſladas", "uvas paſſadas", "uvasde cao"], "contexts": ["fruit that is not poison when green", "fruit used to wrap medicines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "uvas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Uvas", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 1605, "stable_key": "clu_03219ba515d0888f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Grapes", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Fleshy fruit of the vine, used in medicine and food.", "note": "Includes 'uvas ferraes' (fermented grapes).", "wikipedia_extract": "A grape is a fruit, botanically a berry, of the deciduous woody vines of the flowering plant genus Vitis. Grapes are a non-climacteric type of fruit, generally occurring in clusters. The cultivation of grapes began approximately 8,000 years ago, and the fruit has been used as human food throughout its history. Eaten fresh or in dried form (as raisins, currants and sultanas), grapes also hold cultural significance in many parts of the world, particularly for their role in winemaking. Other grape-derived products include various types of jam, juice, vinegar and oil. The Middle East is generally described as the homeland of grapes and the cultivation of this plant began there 6,000–8,000 years ago."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ribes uva-crispa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3745, "target_cluster_name": "herva dos paſſarinhos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ribes uva-crispa is the scientific name for the gooseberry, a closely related fruit often discussed alongside grapes in historical texts concerning fruit cultivation and consumption.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ribes uva-crispa is the scientific name for the gooseberry, a closely related fruit often discussed alongside grapes in historical texts concerning fruit cultivation and consumption."}, {"found_name": "fruit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 72, "target_cluster_name": "frutas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grapes are a type of fruit, and 'fruit' is a broader category that would encompass grapes in historical classifications.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Grapes are a type of fruit, and 'fruit' is a broader category that would encompass grapes in historical classifications."}, {"found_name": "uvas decão", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "tresem tres horas 4 Quem lavar muytos dias as almorreymas cegas com agua de ciſter- na , em quetenhão fervido huma mao-chea de huma herva , a que chamam uvas decão ,que naſcepelos telhados , obfervara", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for plant"}, {"found_name": "uvas decao", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "pelaboca.As pirolas que fe fazem de hum eſcropulo de ruybarbo , outro de alcaçuz , feyto tudo em pò , & misturado com cumode enſayaó menor , aque chamão uvas decao, miſturando tudo com huma oytavade t", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for plant"}]}, {"canonical_name": "frangão", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "franguos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "franguos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["franguos", "frangão", "frangãos"], "contexts": ["chickens used in remedies", "young poultry used as food"]}, {"entity_id": "frangão", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "frangão", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 34, "variants": ["frangão", "Frangaó", "figadode frangão", "franga", "franganitos", "frangaó", "frangaō", "frangáo"], "contexts": ["animal for cooking", "chicken, an animal product"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "franguos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "frangão", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 1606, "stable_key": "clu_a116e227f907f7e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chicken", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Domestic fowl, used for food and in remedies.", "note": "Includes young poultry.", "wikipedia_extract": "The chicken (Gallus gallus domesticus) is a domesticated form of the red junglefowl (Gallus gallus), originally native to Southeast Asia. It was first domesticated around 8,000 years ago and is one of the most common and widespread domesticated animals in the world. Chickens are primarily kept for their meat and eggs, though they are also kept as pets. As of 2023, the global chicken population exceeds 26.5 billion, with more than 50 billion birds produced annually for consumption. Specialized breeds such as broilers and laying hens have been developed for meat and egg production, respectively. A hen bred for laying can produce over 300 eggs per year."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cocks", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2669, "target_cluster_name": "Cocks", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for a large chicken or rooster.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Portuguese for a large chicken or rooster.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Elicryſon", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "chamada Elicryſon ,& vulgarmente Joyna , húa duzia ,de centaurea menorhuma oytava , de herva docedoze grãos , tudo ſe cozacom hum quartilhode caldode frangaó ,& tomandoquatro onças deſte caldo , coado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "named entity, possibly a synonym for Joyna"}, {"found_name": "Joyna", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "chamada Elicryſon ,& vulgarmente Joyna , húa duzia ,de centaurea menorhuma oytava , de herva docedoze grãos , tudo ſe cozacom hum quartilhode caldode frangaó ,& tomandoquatro onças deſte caldo , coado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vulgar name for Elicryſon"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alface", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 37, "members": [{"entity_id": "alfaça", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alfaça", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alfaça"], "contexts": ["plant that is never hot"]}, {"entity_id": "alface", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alface", "c