lius.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hieronymus Fabricius was a student of Realdo Colombo, who was a student of Andreas Vesalius. Fabricius is considered a transitional figure between Vesalius and William Harvey, and his work on anatomy built upon the foundations laid by Vesalius."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hieronymus Fabricius, like many anatomists of his era, engaged with and often critiqued the anatomical theories of Galen, which had been dominant for centuries. His work represents a move away from strict adherence to Galenic principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hieronymus Fabricius, like many anatomists of his era, engaged with and often critiqued the anatomical theories of Galen, which had been dominant for centuries. His work represents a move away from strict adherence to Galenic principles."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hieronymus Fabricius, as a prominent physician and anatomist of the Renaissance, was part of an intellectual tradition that looked back to the ancient Greek physicians, including Hippocrates, for foundational medical knowledge and methodology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hieronymus Fabricius, as a prominent physician and anatomist of the Renaissance, was part of an intellectual tradition that looked back to the ancient Greek physicians, including Hippocrates, for foundational medical knowledge and methodology."}]}, {"canonical_name": "hemorrhoidaes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "hemor∣rhoids", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hemor∣rhoids", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hemor∣rhoids"], "contexts": ["piles, painful swelling", "swollen veins in the anus, subject to ulcers"]}, {"entity_id": "hemorrhoidaes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hemorrhoidaes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 33, "variants": ["hemorrhoidaes", "Hamorroidibus", "Hemophthifis", "Hemopsisis", "Hemorroidas", "Hemorroidibus", "Hæmortha", "Mamorrhoidum", "bemorrhagia", "hemicrania", "hemorrhagia narium", "hemorrhagianarium", "hemorrhagias", "hemorrhagijsfiftendis", "hemorrhoi", "hemorrhoids", "hemorroidaes", "hemorroidibus", "hæmorroidibus", "morrhoidaes"], "contexts": ["hemorrhoids, a condition treated", "condition treated by goat's milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hemor∣rhoids", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hemorrhoidaes", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 1610, "stable_key": "clu_b78402cb687b1bcf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hemorrhoids", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Swollen veins in the anus or rectum, often painful.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hemorrhoids (or haemorrhoids), also known as piles, are vascular structures in the anal canal. In their normal state, they are cushions that help with stool control. They become a disease when swollen or inflamed; the unqualified term hemorrhoid is often used to refer to the disease. The signs and symptoms of hemorrhoids depend on the type present. Internal hemorrhoids often result in painless, bright red rectal bleeding when defecating. External hemorrhoids often result in pain and swelling in the area of the anus."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "índias", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "índias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "índias", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 35, "variants": ["índias", "Western Índias", "Índias", "Índias Occidentaes", "Índias occidentaes", "índia major", "índia nugor", "índias Ocidentales", "índias chamadas occidentaes", "índias mais orientaes", "índias occidentaes", "índias ocddentaes", "índias ocidentaes", "índias orientaes"], "contexts": ["people who called the goma lac", "places referred to as yours"]}, {"entity_id": "indian", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Indian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indian"], "contexts": ["referring to Indian lunar tables"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "índias", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Indian", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 1611, "stable_key": "clu_cd60ee02b6be6a97", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indies", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Multiple", "note": "Refers to the East Indies and/or the West Indies, depending on context.", "wikidata_id": "Q213137", "wikidata_description": "region encompassing South (Em(Indian subcontinent) and Southeast Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East%20Indies", "wikipedia_extract": "The East Indies (or simply the Indies) is a term used in historical narratives of the Age of Discovery. The Indies broadly referred to various lands in the East or the Eastern Hemisphere, particularly the islands and mainlands found in and around the Indian Ocean by Portuguese explorers, soon after the Cape Route was discovered. In a narrow sense, the term was used to refer to the Malay Archipelago, which today comprises the Philippine Archipelago, Indonesian Archipelago, Borneo, and New Guinea. Historically, the term was used in the Age of Discovery to refer to the coasts of the landmasses comprising the Indian subcontinent and the Indochinese Peninsula along with the Malay Archipelago. During the era of European colonization, territories of the Spanish Empire in Asia were known as the Spanish East Indies for 333 years before the American conquest and later the independence of the Philippines. Dutch occupied colonies in the area were known for about 300 years as the Dutch East Indies until Indonesian independence."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Peru", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 67, "target_cluster_name": "Peru", "source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "evidence_snippet": "prouincia llamada Mechoacan. Y no ſolo ‘ea Mexico, y en toda Íu-tierra, fe purgan con ello | -eomo purga excelentifsima,dexadas todas las 0- ‘tras:pere enel Peru y en todas las partes delas Irr- 'días", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for 'Indias', also where the purgative is used", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "East Indies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 497, "target_cluster_name": "East Indies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Indies' is a historical name that broadly referred to various lands in the East, particularly those explored by Portuguese navigators. 'East Indies' is a more specific and commonly used term for this region, often used interchangeably or as a direct clarification of 'Indies'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Indies' is a historical name that broadly referred to various lands in the East, particularly those explored by Portuguese navigators. 'East Indies' is a more specific and commonly used term for this region, often used interchangeably or as a direct clarification of 'Indies'."}, {"found_name": "Sevilha", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "americanas hespa- nholas a Sevilha e outros mercados de Hespanha, d'onde, comovemos pelo nosso Orta, passava a Portugal, sendo exportada d*aqui para a índia oriental. (Cf. Pharmac, 93; PoU, Schmauss, ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "market for goods from the Spanish Americas"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mania", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "insanity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "insanity", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["insanity"], "contexts": ["condition of altered self due to brain changes", "mental disorder characterized by delusions"]}, {"entity_id": "mania", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mania", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mania"], "contexts": ["state of elevated mood and energy", "mental disorder characterized by excitement"]}, {"entity_id": "mania", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mania", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 30, "variants": ["Mania", "Maniacap", "Maniaco", "Manian", "Manicas", "Maníacos", "Manîas", "mania", "maniaco"], "contexts": ["mental illness", "a mental illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "insanity", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mania", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mania", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mania", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 1612, "stable_key": "clu_a9ee0f691914a87c-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mania", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A mental disorder characterized by elevated mood, energy, and excitement.", "note": "Includes broader historical concepts of madness and mental derangement.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mania ( MAIN-ee-yah; also known as manic syndrome) is a psychiatric behavioral syndrome defined as a state of abnormally elevated arousal, affect, and energy level. During a manic episode, an individual will experience rapidly changing emotions and moods, highly influenced by surrounding stimuli. Although mania is often conceived of as a \"mirror image\" to depression, the heightened mood can be dysphoric as well as euphoric. As the mania intensifies, irritability can be more pronounced and result in anxiety or anger. The symptoms of mania include elevated mood (either euphoric or irritable), flight of ideas, pressure of speech, increased energy, decreased \"need\" and desire for sleep, and hyperactivity. They are most plainly evident in fully developed hypomanic states, however, in full-blown mania, these symptoms become progressively exacerbated."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Madness", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1323, "target_cluster_name": "Madness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity description explicitly states 'Mania (MAIN-ee-yah; also known as manic syndrome)' and 'Mania (aka Madness)' is present in the candidate list.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The entity description explicitly states 'Mania (MAIN-ee-yah; also known as manic syndrome)' and 'Mania (aka Madness)' is present in the candidate list."}, {"found_name": "Melancholy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 868, "target_cluster_name": "melancolia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, mania and melancholy were often considered two poles of the same bipolar disorder or related states of mental imbalance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, mania and melancholy were often considered two poles of the same bipolar disorder or related states of mental imbalance."}, {"found_name": "Depression", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1324, "target_cluster_name": "tristezas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania is often discussed in conjunction with depression as opposing states within bipolar disorder.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania is often discussed in conjunction with depression as opposing states within bipolar disorder."}, {"found_name": "Delirium", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 235, "target_cluster_name": "delirios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both mania and delirium involve altered mental states, though the specific characteristics differ. Historically, they could be confused or seen as related phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both mania and delirium involve altered mental states, though the specific characteristics differ. Historically, they could be confused or seen as related phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Frenzy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 571, "target_cluster_name": "freneticos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frenzy describes a state of uncontrolled excitement or wild behavior, which shares characteristics with the elevated arousal and energy described in mania.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Frenzy describes a state of uncontrolled excitement or wild behavior, which shares characteristics with the elevated arousal and energy described in mania."}, {"found_name": "Insanity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1178, "target_cluster_name": "insane", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania is a specific type of mental disorder that falls under the broader category of insanity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania is a specific type of mental disorder that falls under the broader category of insanity."}, {"found_name": "Mental disorder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4238, "target_cluster_name": "mental derangement", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania is a specific type of mental disorder.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania is a specific type of mental disorder."}, {"found_name": "Emotional disturbance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2173, "target_cluster_name": "Passions of the Heart", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania is characterized by abnormally elevated affect and rapidly changing emotions, fitting the description of an emotional disturbance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania is characterized by abnormally elevated affect and rapidly changing emotions, fitting the description of an emotional disturbance."}]}, {"canonical_name": "modorras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "modorras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "modorras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["modorras"], "contexts": ["fevers of bad quality, commonly called modorras"]}, {"entity_id": "modorras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "modorras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 35, "variants": ["modorras", "modorras invenciveis"], "contexts": ["lethargy treated by stibium", "lethargy, a disease"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "modorras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "modorras", "similarity": 0.782}], "id": 1613, "stable_key": "clu_a868ee7fd7ecdedc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lethargy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Lethargy", "note": "The description points to profound lethargy in febrile patients, aligning with the modern understanding of lethargy as a symptom or condition of excessive tiredness and sluggishness, often associated with illness.", "wikidata_id": "Q15637420", "wikidata_description": "state of tiredness, weariness, fatigue, or lack of energy", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lethargy", "wikipedia_extract": "Lethargy is a state of tiredness, sleepiness, weariness, fatigue, sluggishness, or lack of energy. It can be accompanied by depression, decreased motivation, or apathy. Lethargy can be a normal response to inadequate sleep, overexertion, overworking, stress, lack of exercise, improper nutrition, drug abuse, boredom, or a symptom of an underlying illness or disorder. It may also be a side effect of medication or caused by an interaction between medications and alcohol. It may also be an altered level of consciousness. When part of a normal response, lethargy often resolves with rest, adequate sleep, decreased stress, physical exercise, and good nutrition."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a severe form of lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a severe form of lethargy."}, {"found_name": "Exhaustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3509, "target_cluster_name": "exhaustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Exhaustion is a state of extreme physical or mental tiredness, often a cause or symptom of lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Exhaustion is a state of extreme physical or mental tiredness, often a cause or symptom of lethargy."}, {"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coma is a state of prolonged unconsciousness that is a more severe manifestation of lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coma is a state of prolonged unconsciousness that is a more severe manifestation of lethargy."}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which can be a symptom or consequence of extreme lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which can be a symptom or consequence of extreme lethargy."}, {"found_name": "Depression", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1324, "target_cluster_name": "tristezas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lethargy is a common symptom of depression, characterized by a lack of energy and motivation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lethargy is a common symptom of depression, characterized by a lack of energy and motivation."}, {"found_name": "Sleep", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1316, "target_cluster_name": "sleep", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lethargy is characterized by excessive sleepiness or a state of being drowsy, closely related to the natural state of sleep.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lethargy is characterized by excessive sleepiness or a state of being drowsy, closely related to the natural state of sleep."}]}, {"canonical_name": "alcanfor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 36, "members": [{"entity_id": "aljôfar", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "aljôfar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["aljôfar"], "contexts": ["substance to be discussed", "type of pearl"]}, {"entity_id": "alcanfor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "alcanfor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 24, "variants": ["alcanfor", "Alcanfor", "alcanfort"], "contexts": ["camphor", "camphor added to a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "aljôfar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "alcanfor", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1614, "stable_key": "clu_7d4b98b718fb257a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Camphor", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While historically sometimes classified as a mineral, modern classification is chemical.", "wikidata_id": "Q27089415", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "China", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 41, "target_cluster_name": "China", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "que a aqua é colida in tertio, emquanto a camphora é frigida. Da Camjbra e das Carambolas 1 59 RUÂNO Pois Ruelio e Mateolo Senense dizem que a da China he milhor, e dizem que a milhor de todalas camfa", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym of camfora", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "alcaparras", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2150, "target_cluster_name": "alcaparras", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "desta terra, que em Goa, quando outra monção vem, já he gas- tada toda a seda (3). RUANO Dos nomes e compreisam delia me dizey. i6o Colóquio duodécimo ORTA Capur e cafur dizem os Arábios e toda a outr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Capur (camphor)", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Balsam", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2139, "target_cluster_name": "Balſamo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both camphor and balsam are aromatic resins used in historical medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both camphor and balsam are aromatic resins used in historical medicine."}, {"found_name": "Oil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 203, "target_cluster_name": "olio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Camphor is often processed into or used with oils in historical medicinal preparations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Camphor is often processed into or used with oils in historical medicinal preparations."}, {"found_name": "aromas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1044, "target_cluster_name": "aromi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Camphor is known for its strong aroma, and 'aromas' is a general term for fragrant substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Camphor is known for its strong aroma, and 'aromas' is a general term for fragrant substances."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Choco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "choco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Choco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 34, "variants": ["Choco", "Choco Barbacoas"], "contexts": ["city with population 22,000", "place where platinum is found"]}, {"entity_id": "choco", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Choco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Choco"], "contexts": ["location of ravines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Choco", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Choco", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1615, "stable_key": "clu_32b88e0bef1bc5d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chocó", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "wikidata_id": "Q230584", "wikidata_description": "department of Colombia", "wikipedia_extract": "Chocó Department (Spanish pronunciation: [tʃoˈko]) is a department of the Pacific region of Colombia known for hosting the largest Afro-Colombian population in the nation, and a large population of Amerindian and mixed African-Amerindian Colombians. It is in the west of the country, and is the only Colombian department to have coastlines on both the Pacific Ocean and the Atlantic Ocean. It contains all of Colombia's border with Panama. Its capital is Quibdó. Chocó has a diverse geography, unique ecosystems and unexploited natural resources; however, its population has one of the lowest standards of living of all departments in Colombia. A major factor cited by the government is the rugged, montane rainforest environment and the hot, hyperhumid climate.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chocó_Department"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antioquia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1757, "target_cluster_name": "Antioquia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antioquia is a neighboring department in Colombia, historically and geographically linked to Chocó.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antioquia is a neighboring department in Colombia, historically and geographically linked to Chocó."}, {"found_name": "Darién", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1642, "target_cluster_name": "Darien", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Darién is a region in Panama and Colombia, historically and geographically connected to Chocó, often discussed in relation to exploration and indigenous populations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Darién is a region in Panama and Colombia, historically and geographically connected to Chocó, often discussed in relation to exploration and indigenous populations."}, {"found_name": "Equatorial America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chocó is located in the equatorial region of South America, making this a relevant geographical descriptor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chocó is located in the equatorial region of South America, making this a relevant geographical descriptor."}, {"found_name": "Rio Magdalena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1621, "target_cluster_name": "Rio Magdalena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chocó is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River forms part of its eastern border.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chocó is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River forms part of its eastern border.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sabine", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "sabine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sabine", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 25, "variants": ["Sabine"], "contexts": ["captain who provided observations", "observer of observations"]}, {"entity_id": "sabine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sabine", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Sabine"], "contexts": ["led expedition for pendulum experiments", "author of work on magnetic disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "mrs__sabine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mrs. Sabine", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mrs. Sabine"], "contexts": ["translator of the work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sabine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sabine", "similarity": 0.765}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sabine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mrs. Sabine", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 1616, "stable_key": "clu_0ed2ce579720f8e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edward Sabine", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1883, "description": "Irish scientist known for pendulum experiments and magnetic observations.", "wikidata_id": "Q311223", "wikidata_description": "British Army general (1788-1883)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward%20Sabine", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir Edward Sabine (; 14 October 1788 – 26 June 1883) was an Irish physicist, geodesist,astronomer, geophysicist, ornithologist, polar explorer, soldier, and the 30th president of the Royal Society. He led the effort to establish a system of magnetic observatories in various parts of British territory all over the globe. Much of his life was devoted to their direction and to analysing their observations. Other research focused on the birds of Greenland, ocean temperatures, the Gulf Stream, barometric measurement of heights, arc of the meridian, glacial transport of rocks, the volcanoes of the Hawaiian Islands and various points of meteorology. Edward Sabine was born 14 October 1788 in Great Britain St. (Parnell St.), Dublin."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Richardson", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1026, "target_cluster_name": "Richardson", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Richardson and Sabine were involved in Arctic exploration and scientific endeavors, with Sabine being a key figure in the Royal Society during Richardson's career.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Richardson and Sabine were involved in Arctic exploration and scientific endeavors, with Sabine being a key figure in the Royal Society during Richardson's career.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century scientific exploration and geophysics, with Humboldt being a foundational figure in Earth sciences and Sabine building upon such work in his magnetic observations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent figures in 19th-century scientific exploration and geophysics, with Humboldt being a foundational figure in Earth sciences and Sabine building upon such work in his magnetic observations."}, {"found_name": "James Clark Ross", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2614, "target_cluster_name": "Sir James Ross", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sabine's work on magnetic observatories and polar exploration was closely linked to expeditions like those led by Sir James Clark Ross, who also conducted significant magnetic surveys.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sabine's work on magnetic observatories and polar exploration was closely linked to expeditions like those led by Sir James Clark Ross, who also conducted significant magnetic surveys."}, {"found_name": "Christoph Heinrich Friedrich Henrichsen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2200, "target_cluster_name": "Hansteen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hansteen was a contemporary geophysicist whose work on terrestrial magnetism and aurora borealis was highly relevant to Sabine's own research and collaborations in the field.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hansteen was a contemporary geophysicist whose work on terrestrial magnetism and aurora borealis was highly relevant to Sabine's own research and collaborations in the field."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sun", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "sol", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sol", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sol"], "contexts": ["our Sun"]}, {"entity_id": "sun", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Sun", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 34, "variants": ["Sun", "sun"], "contexts": ["star at the center of solar system", "celestial body exerting action"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sol", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sun", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 1617, "stable_key": "clu_5922fe5d0336a336", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sun", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "N/A", "note": "Refers to the star at the center of our solar system."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "olhos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "olhos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "olhos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["olhos"], "contexts": ["broken in illness"]}, {"entity_id": "olhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "olhos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 34, "variants": ["olhos", "Olhos", "olho"], "contexts": ["eyes", "body part affected by fluxion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "olhos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "olhos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1618, "stable_key": "clu_3885853a7791c596", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eyes", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "The organs of sight in humans and animals.", "wikipedia_extract": "An eye is a sensory organ that allows an organism to perceive visual information. It detects light and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system. In higher organisms, the eye is a complex optical system that collects light from the surrounding environment, regulates its intensity through a diaphragm, focuses it through an adjustable assembly of lenses to form an image, converts this image into a set of electrical signals, and transmits these signals to the brain through neural pathways that connect the eye via the optic nerve to the visual cortex and other areas of the brain. Eyes with resolving power have come in ten fundamentally different forms, classified into compound eyes and non-compound eyes. Compound eyes are made up of multiple small visual units, and are common on insects and crustaceans."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chelidonium", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3912, "target_cluster_name": "Chelidonium", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "restore it. It is called Chelidonium from the Greek word 〈 in non-Latin alphabet 〉, which signifies a Swallow, be∣cause they say, That if you prick out the Eyes of yong Swallows when they are in the N", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "name of an herb, derived from Greek word for swallow"}, {"found_name": "St. Antho-nies Fire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "200 in them. The Herb bruised and applied to the Forehead and Temples, allayeth exces∣sive heat therein, hindring rest and sleep; and applied to the Eyes taketh away the redness* 8.500 and Inflamation", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of break-out on the skin"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Plague", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "plague", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Plague", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plague"], "contexts": ["contagious epidemic disease"]}, {"entity_id": "plagues", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Plagues", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plagues"], "contexts": ["contagious epidemic disease"]}, {"entity_id": "plague", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Plague", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 32, "variants": ["Plague", "Plague-sores"], "contexts": ["disease expelled by the herb", "epidemic disease resisted by Angelica"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plague", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Plague", "similarity": 0.84}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plagues", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Plague", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 1619, "stable_key": "clu_a50d60775402059c-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plague", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A highly contagious and often fatal infectious disease.", "note": "Covers terms related to the bubonic plague and similar epidemic diseases.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mark Tonderai (born 1974) is a British-Zimbabwean entrepreneur, film director, writer, actor and former disc jockey. He is co-founder of the London-based production company Shona Productions with his wife Zoe Stewart. He directed the crime drama television series The Five which first aired in April 2016 on the Sky 1 channel. Tonderai began his career in the media working as a writer, producer and presenter for radio. From early 1993 until late 1998 Tonderai was heavily involved in radio, and was the writer and producer of The Mark Tonderai Show, hosted on BBC Radio 1, and the Jam. Tonderai was also involved in numerous BBC Radio 4 projects, such as the Worldly Wise, Rainbow Nation and Week Ending, as well as Kiss 100's Rude Awakening, hosted on Kiss 100 London."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Buboes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1751, "target_cluster_name": "Bubões", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buboes are a primary symptom of bubonic plague, a major form of plague. Their presence is intrinsically linked to the diagnosis and understanding of plague in historical medical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Buboes are a primary symptom of bubonic plague, a major form of plague. Their presence is intrinsically linked to the diagnosis and understanding of plague in historical medical texts."}, {"found_name": "Epidemics", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 520, "target_cluster_name": "epidemics", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Plague is a type of epidemic disease. The term 'epidemics' broadly categorizes the phenomenon of widespread disease outbreaks, of which plague is a prominent historical example.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Plague is a type of epidemic disease. The term 'epidemics' broadly categorizes the phenomenon of widespread disease outbreaks, of which plague is a prominent historical example."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "amatus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Amatus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amatus"], "contexts": ["author cited for experimental testimony"]}, {"entity_id": "amatus_lusitanus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 33, "variants": ["Amatus Lusitanus", "Amat. Lufitan.", "Amat. Lufitanus", "Amato Lufitano", "Amatus Lufitanus", "Amatus Lufuan", "Examat. Lufitan.", "Veiga Lufitanus"], "contexts": ["author of Centuriae", "author of medical observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Amatus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "similarity": 0.855}], "id": 1620, "stable_key": "clu_01c39e7977f9cd54", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1511, "death_year": 1588, "description": "Portuguese physician and botanist, known for his medical observations and writings.", "wikidata_id": "Q455777", "wikidata_description": "Portuguese physician (1511-1568)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amatus%20Lusitanus", "wikipedia_extract": "João Rodrigues de Castelo Branco, better known as Amato Lusitano and Amatus Lusitanus (1511–1568), was a notable Portuguese Jewish physician of the 16th century. Like Herophilus, Galen, Ibn al-Nafis, Michael Servetus, Realdo Colombo and William Harvey, he is credited as making a discovery in the circulation of the blood. He is said to have discovered the function of the valves in the circulation of the blood. Lusitano was born in 1511 in Castelo Branco, Portugal. He was a descendant of a Marrano family called Chabib (= Amatus, \"beloved\" in Latin), and was brought up in the Jewish faith. After having graduated with honors as M.D."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portuguezes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 218, "target_cluster_name": "Portuguezes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amatus Lusitanus was a prominent Portuguese physician and humanist of the Renaissance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amatus Lusitanus was a prominent Portuguese physician and humanist of the Renaissance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mathiolus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 252, "target_cluster_name": "Mathiolus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Amatus Lusitanus and Mattioli were prominent Italian physicians and botanists of the Renaissance period. They were contemporaries and their contributions to medicine and the study of plants were significant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Amatus Lusitanus and Mattioli were prominent Italian physicians and botanists of the Renaissance period. They were contemporaries and their contributions to medicine and the study of plants were significant.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Italians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 462, "target_cluster_name": "Italians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amatus Lusitanus was an Italian physician and botanist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amatus Lusitanus was an Italian physician and botanist.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Monardes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0