active.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Avicenna was a highly influential physician whose works were central to medical education and practice throughout the Middle Ages and into the Renaissance, a period during which Amatus Lusitanus was active."}, {"found_name": "Averroes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 952, "target_cluster_name": "Averroes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Averroes, like Avicenna, was a key figure in Islamic medicine whose commentaries and original works were foundational for medical scholarship in Europe during the Renaissance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Averroes, like Avicenna, was a key figure in Islamic medicine whose commentaries and original works were foundational for medical scholarship in Europe during the Renaissance."}, {"found_name": "Forestus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1805, "target_cluster_name": "Forestus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicians in the 16th century, known for their medical writings and practices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent physicians in the 16th century, known for their medical writings and practices.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Capivatius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1906, "target_cluster_name": "Capivatius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicians and anatomists of the 16th century, often mentioned in similar contexts regarding medical practice and learning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent physicians and anatomists of the 16th century, often mentioned in similar contexts regarding medical practice and learning.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alpin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2364, "target_cluster_name": "Alpin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicians and botanists of the 16th century.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent physicians and botanists of the 16th century.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Nicolaus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2688, "target_cluster_name": "Nicolaus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant physicians of the Renaissance period, known for their contributions to medical practice and literature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were significant physicians of the Renaissance period, known for their contributions to medical practice and literature.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rio Magdalena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "rio_magdalena", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rio Magdalena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 33, "variants": ["Rio Magdalena", "Maria Magdalena", "Rio Magdaleua", "RioMagdalena"], "contexts": ["river with coconut trees along its banks", "river where palm trees were seen"]}, {"entity_id": "magdalena_river", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Magdalena River", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magdalena River"], "contexts": ["river near Honda"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rio Magdalena", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Magdalena River", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 1621, "stable_key": "clu_0475370359423f52", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magdalena River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "wikidata_id": "Q6115369", "wikidata_description": "river in Mexico City", "wikipedia_extract": "The Magdalena River (Spanish: Río Magdalena, Spanish pronunciation: [ˈri.o maɣðaˈlena]; less commonly Rio Grande de la Magdalena) is the main river of Colombia, flowing northward about 1,528 kilometres (949 mi) through the western half of the country. It takes its name from the biblical figure Mary Magdalene. It is navigable through much of its lower reaches, in spite of the shifting sand bars at the mouth of its delta, as far as Honda, at the downstream base of its rapids. It flows through the Magdalena River Valley. Its drainage basin covers a surface of 273,000 square kilometres (105,000 sq mi), which is 24% of the country's area and where 66% of its population lives. The Magdalena River is the largest river system of the northern Andes, with a length of 1,612 km."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antioquia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1757, "target_cluster_name": "Antioquia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antioquia is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River flows through it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antioquia is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River flows through it."}, {"found_name": "Bogotá", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1562, "target_cluster_name": "Bogota", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bogotá is the capital of Colombia, and the Magdalena River is its principal river.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bogotá is the capital of Colombia, and the Magdalena River is its principal river."}, {"found_name": "Chocó", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1615, "target_cluster_name": "Choco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chocó is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River forms part of its eastern border.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chocó is a department in Colombia, and the Magdalena River forms part of its eastern border."}, {"found_name": "Equatorial America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Magdalena River is located in Equatorial America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Magdalena River is located in Equatorial America."}, {"found_name": "Honda", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1776, "target_cluster_name": "Honda", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honda is a town in Colombia located on the Magdalena River.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Honda is a town in Colombia located on the Magdalena River."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Truxillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "truxillo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Truxillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 32, "variants": ["Truxillo", "Trujillo", "Trusillo", "Truxilio"], "contexts": ["city in Venezuela", "mountains in Maracaybo province"]}, {"entity_id": "truxillo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Truxillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Truxillo"], "contexts": ["city in Peru", "city hearing subterranean thunder-clap"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Truxillo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Truxillo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1622, "stable_key": "clu_6d7dfb9304e427de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trujillo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "The text mentions 'Truxillo' in Venezuela and Peru. Trujillo in Venezuela is the primary referent based on Humboldt's travels.", "wikidata_id": "Q1020535", "wikidata_description": "state capital of Trujillo State, Venezuela", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trujillo,_Trujillo", "wikipedia_extract": "Trujillo is the capital city of Trujillo State in Venezuela. About 40,000 people live in this city, located in El Valle de Los Mukas. Founded by one of the \"Conquistadores de America\", Diego García de Paredes (1506–1563), son of Diego García de Paredes (the father), (1466–1534), Spanish soldier and duellist, native of Trujillo in Extremadura, Spain. In 1678, Trujillo was the farthest point in a daring raid on Spanish-held Venezuela, carried out by six pirate ships and 700 men led by the French buccaneer Michel de Grammont. Simon Bolívar promulgated the Decree of War to the Death in the city on 15 June 1813."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1497, "target_cluster_name": "Caracas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are significant cities within the same nation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are significant cities within the same nation."}, {"found_name": "Coro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1932, "target_cluster_name": "Coro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coro is a city in Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are located within Venezuela, indicating a potential regional or historical connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coro is a city in Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are located within Venezuela, indicating a potential regional or historical connection."}, {"found_name": "Cumaná", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1491, "target_cluster_name": "Cumana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cumaná is a city in Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are located within Venezuela, indicating a potential regional or historical connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cumaná is a city in Venezuela, and Trujillo is the capital of Trujillo State in Venezuela. Both are located within Venezuela, indicating a potential regional or historical connection."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cassiquiare", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "cassiquiare", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cassiquiare", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 32, "variants": ["Cassiquiare", "Cassi-quiare", "Cassiqiiiare", "Casslquiare"], "contexts": ["geographic location", "forests mentioned as a starting point"]}, {"entity_id": "cassiquiare", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cassiquiare", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cassiquiare"], "contexts": ["river branch in South America", "dreary banks where natives use terms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cassiquiare", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cassiquiare", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1623, "stable_key": "clu_318aa765ecbffa51", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cassiquiare River", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Most mentions refer to the Cassiquiare River, but 'Cassiopeia' is a constellation, suggesting a mix of entities or OCR errors.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Casiquiare river or canal (Spanish pronunciation: [kasiˈkjaɾe]) is a natural distributary of the upper Orinoco flowing southward into the Rio Negro in Venezuela. As such, it forms a unique natural canal between the Orinoco and Amazon river systems. It is the world's largest river of the kind that links two major river systems, a so-called bifurcation. The area forms a water divide, more dramatically at regional flood stage. The name Casiquiare, first used in that form by Manuel Román, likely derives from the Ye'kuana language name of the river, Kashishiwadi. The first European to describe it was Spanish Jesuit missionary and explorer Cristóbal Diatristán de Acuña in 1639.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casiquiare_canal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Rio Apure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2543, "target_cluster_name": "Rio Apure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apure River is a major tributary of the Orinoco River, and the Cassiquiare River is a natural canal that connects the Orinoco River to the Rio Negro, which flows into the Amazon River. These rivers form a crucial part of the riverine network in northern South America, often discussed together in geographical and exploratory contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Apure River is a major tributary of the Orinoco River, and the Cassiquiare River is a natural canal that connects the Orinoco River to the Rio Negro, which flows into the Amazon River. These rivers form a crucial part of the riverine network in northern South America, often discussed together in geographical and exploratory contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "nicaragua", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 29, "variants": ["Nicaragua", "NICAHAOUA", "Nica-houa"], "contexts": ["country in Central America", "country and region"]}, {"entity_id": "nicaragua", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Nicaragua"], "contexts": ["region where sulphur is brought from", "region where other sulphur is brought"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nicaragua", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Nicaragua", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 1624, "stable_key": "clu_8e99416d817d6db1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nicaragua", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Nicaragua", "wikidata_id": "Q190469", "wikidata_description": "species of fish", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull%20shark", "wikipedia_extract": "The bull shark (Carcharhinus leucas), also known as the Zambezi shark (informally zambi) in Africa and Lake Nicaragua shark in Nicaragua, is a species of requiem shark commonly found worldwide in warm, shallow waters along coasts and in rivers. It is known for its aggressive nature, and presence mainly in warm, shallow brackish and freshwater systems including estuaries and (usually) lower reaches of rivers. Their aggressive nature has led to ongoing shark-culling efforts near beaches to protect beachgoers, which is one of the causes of bull shark populations continuing to decrease. Bull sharks are listed as vulnerable on the IUCN Red List. Bull sharks are euryhaline and can thrive in both salt and fresh water. They are known to travel far up rivers, and have been known to travel up the Mississippi River as far as Alton, Illinois, about 1,100 kilometres (700 mi) from the ocean, but few freshwater interactions with humans have been recorded."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lake Nicaragua", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3423, "target_cluster_name": "lac de Nicaragua", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The bull shark is also known as the Lake Nicaragua shark, indicating a strong geographical association.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The bull shark is also known as the Lake Nicaragua shark, indicating a strong geographical association."}]}, {"canonical_name": "gneiss", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "gneiss", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gneiss", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 23, "variants": ["gneiss"], "contexts": ["type of metamorphic rock", "rock type containing feldspar"]}, {"entity_id": "gneiss", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "gneiss", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["gneiss"], "contexts": ["type of rock mixture", "banded metamorphic rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gneiss", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "gneiss", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1625, "stable_key": "clu_890537e1ade59cce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gneiss", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "granite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1634, "target_cluster_name": "granite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are types of metamorphic rock, often discussed in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are types of metamorphic rock, often discussed in relation to each other