rusive igneous rock, and trachyte is its extrusive equivalent. They share similar mineral compositions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diorite is an intrusive igneous rock, and trachyte is its extrusive equivalent. They share similar mineral compositions."}, {"found_name": "Rock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 383, "target_cluster_name": "rock", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trachyte is a specific type of rock, making 'Rock' a broader category to which it belongs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Trachyte is a specific type of rock, making 'Rock' a broader category to which it belongs."}, {"found_name": "phonolithe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1897, "target_cluster_name": "phonolithe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phonolite is described as a variation of trachyte.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phonolite is described as a variation of trachyte.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "grünstein", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2781, "target_cluster_name": "grünstein", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grünstein is a type of trachyte, a volcanic rock.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Grünstein is a type of trachyte, a volcanic rock.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Geology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 278, "target_cluster_name": "Geology", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "porphyry, and serpentine) and those termed 'volcanic' in the strict sense of the word (as trachyte, basalt, and lava). [footnote] ([Lyell, 'Principales of Geology', vol. i.i., p. 353 and 359.] -- Tr. ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of volcanic rock", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "electric currents", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 726, "target_cluster_name": "electric currents", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "h vocanoes are justy termed in many languages \"fire-emitting mountains,\" mountains of this kind are not formed by the gradual accumulation of ejected currents of lava, but their origin seems rather to", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of rock associated with volcanic formation", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lyell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1710, "target_cluster_name": "Lyell", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "'Plutonic' rocks* (granite, porphyry, and serpentine) and those termed 'volcanic' in the strict sense of the word (as trachyte, basalt, and lava). [footnote] ([Lyell, 'Principales of Geology', vol. i.", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of volcanic rock", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "lava", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1714, "target_cluster_name": "lava", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "are justy termed in many languages \"fire-emitting mountains,\" mountains of this kind are not formed by the gradual accumulation of ejected currents of lava, but their origin seems rather to be a gener", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of ejected material", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "dolerite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "chain. The proxiimity of p 239 active volcanoes is always manifested in the chain of the Andes by the appearance of certain rocks (as dolerite, melaphyre, trachyte, andesite, and dioritic porphyry), w", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rock"}, {"found_name": "melaphyre", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "chain. The proxiimity of p 239 active volcanoes is always manifested in the chain of the Andes by the appearance of certain rocks (as dolerite, melaphyre, trachyte, andesite, and dioritic porphyry), w", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rock"}, {"found_name": "andesite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "chain. The proxiimity of p 239 active volcanoes is always manifested in the chain of the Andes by the appearance of certain rocks (as dolerite, melaphyre, trachyte, andesite, and dioritic porphyry), w", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rock"}, {"found_name": "dioritic porphyry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "chain. The proxiimity of p 239 active volcanoes is always manifested in the chain of the Andes by the appearance of certain rocks (as dolerite, melaphyre, trachyte, andesite, and dioritic porphyry), w", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rock"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bombaim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "bombay", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bombay", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Bombay"], "contexts": ["city with high rainfall", "city in torrid zone"]}, {"entity_id": "bombaim", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Bombaim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 27, "variants": ["Bombaim", "Bombay", "Bombaym"], "contexts": ["location of bazaars", "city name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bombay", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Bombaim", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 1627, "stable_key": "clu_822c08ceb124f125", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bombay", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major port city in India, historically a center of trade.", "note": "Includes historical spellings like 'Bombaim' and a potential confusion with Mombasa.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mumbai ( muum-BY; Marathi: Mumbaī, pronounced [ˈmumbəi] ), also known as Bombay ( bom-BAY; its official name until 1995), is the capital city of the Indian state of Maharashtra. Mumbai is the financial capital and the most populous city proper of India, with an estimated population of 12.5 million (1.25 crore). Mumbai is the centre of the Mumbai Metropolitan Region, which is among the most populous metropolitan areas in the world with a population of over 23 million (2.3 crore). Mumbai lies on the Konkan coast on the west coast of India and has a deep natural harbour. In 2008, Mumbai was named a alpha world city. Mumbai has the highest number of billionaires out of any city in Asia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mombaim", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3699, "target_cluster_name": "Mombaim", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bombaim is the historical Portuguese name for Bombay, indicating a direct continuity of the place name.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bombaim is the historical Portuguese name for Bombay, indicating a direct continuity of the place name.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Jambolana", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "fructo da Eugenia. Jaml>o« lana.^ Lam., uma arvore da femilia das Mjrrtacea, bastante com* mum na India« O nome hindustani do íinicto é ^j^^a. djamán; e em Bombaim chamam- lhe também y^hnM/, parecendo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "djamán", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "fructo da Eugenia. Jaml>o« lana.^ Lam., uma arvore da femilia das Mjrrtacea, bastante com* mum na India« O nome hindustani do íinicto é ^j^^a. djamán; e em Bombaim chamam- lhe também y^hnM/, parecendo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for"}, {"found_name": "átjam", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "fructo da Eugenia. Jaml>o« lana.^ Lam., uma arvore da femilia das Mjrrtacea, bastante com* mum na India« O nome hindustani do íinicto é ^j^^a. djamán; e em Bombaim chamam- lhe também y^hnM/, parecendo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for"}, {"found_name": "jaggam", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jaca descrípção de Orta, a menção dos numerosos espinhos do tronco, o aspecto do fructo, n3o podem deixar duvida sobre a identificação. Ainda hoje em Bombaim chamam ao írMCíojaggam^ o que Dymock consi", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for"}, {"found_name": "jangoma", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jaca descrípção de Orta, a menção dos numerosos espinhos do tronco, o aspecto do fructo, n3o podem deixar duvida sobre a identificação. Ainda hoje em Bombaim chamam ao írMCíojaggam^ o que Dymock consi", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for"}]}, {"canonical_name": "fontes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "fonte", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fonte", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fonte"], "contexts": ["process of melting metal"]}, {"entity_id": "fontes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fontes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 33, "variants": ["Fontes", "fontes"], "contexts": ["temples, area for application", "veins in the legs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fonte", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fontes", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 1628, "stable_key": "clu_789bc3e69e513cc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spring", "confidence": "low", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'process of melting metal' suggests a furnace or forge, while 'temples, area for application' is unclear. The dominant Portuguese term 'fontes' primarily means 'springs' (water), but can also refer to 'sources' or 'fountains'. Given the ambiguity and potential for multiple meanings, confidence is low.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_%28hydrology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "A spring is a natural exit point at which groundwater emerges from an aquifer and flows across the ground surface as surface water. It is a component of the hydrosphere, as well as a part of the water cycle. Springs have long been important for humans as a source of fresh water, especially in arid regions which have relatively little annual rainfall. Springs are driven out onto the surface by various natural forces, such as gravity and hydrostatic pressure. A spring produced by the emergence of geothermally heated groundwater is known as a hot spring. The yield of spring water varies widely from a volumetric flow rate of nearly zero to more than 14,000 litres per second (490 cu ft/s) for the biggest springs."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "April", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 129, "target_cluster_name": "April", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "April is commonly associated with the season of spring in the Northern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: April is commonly associated with the season of spring in the Northern Hemisphere.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Março", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2332, "target_cluster_name": "Março", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "March is the beginning of Spring in the Northern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: March is the beginning of Spring in the Northern Hemisphere.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["temperature scale unit", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 30, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["thermometer inventor", "scale for temperature measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Fahrenheit", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fahrenheit", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1629, "stable_key": "clu_4734fbad25c49604", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fahrenheit", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1686, "death_year": 1736, "description": "German physicist and inventor of the Fahrenheit temperature scale.", "wikidata_id": "Q57073", "wikidata_description": "German-Polish physicist and engineer (1686-1736)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel%20Gabriel%20Fahrenheit", "wikipedia_extract": "Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit FRS (24 May 1686 – 16 September 1736) was a physicist, inventor, and scientific instrument maker. He was born in Poland to a family of German origin, although he spent much of his life in the Dutch Republic. Fahrenheit significantly improved the design and manufacture of thermometers; his were accurate and consistent enough that different observers, each with their own Fahrenheit thermometers, could reliably compare temperature measurements with each other. Fahrenheit is also credited with producing the first successful mercury-in-glass thermometers, which were more accurate than the spirit-filled thermometers of his time and of a generally superior design. The popularity of his thermometers also led to the widespread adoption of his Fahrenheit scale, with which they were provided. Fahrenheit was born in Danzig (Gdańsk), then in the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Franklin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 343, "target_cluster_name": "Franklin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Benjamin Franklin conducted significant experiments with electricity and is known for his contributions to meteorology, including work related to temperature scales. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit was the inventor of the first practical thermometer and the Fahrenheit scale.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Benjamin Franklin conducted significant experiments with electricity and is known for his contributions to meteorology, including work related to temperature scales. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit was the inventor of the first practical thermometer and the Fahrenheit scale.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alessandro Volta", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 966, "target_cluster_name": "Volta", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicists and inventors who made significant contributions to scientific instrumentation and measurement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent physicists and inventors who made significant contributions to scientific instrumentation and measurement."}, {"found_name": "Carl Wilhelm Scheele", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1258, "target_cluster_name": "Scheele", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent chemists and physicists active in the 18th century, known for their work with gases and thermometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent chemists and physicists active in the 18th century, known for their work with gases and thermometry."}, {"found_name": "Galileo Galilei", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 931, "target_cluster_name": "Galileo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential scientists whose work involved precise measurement and the development of scientific instruments, though Galileo predates Fahrenheit by over a century.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential scientists whose work involved precise measurement and the development of scientific instruments, though Galileo predates Fahrenheit by over a century."}]}, {"canonical_name": "feridas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "heridas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Heridas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["heridas", "Heridas"], "contexts": ["wounds", "wounds that the balsam is useful for"]}, {"entity_id": "feridas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "feridas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 31, "variants": ["feridas", "ferida", "ferida grande", "feridas da cabeça"], "contexts": ["healed by antimony", "wounds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Heridas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 1630, "stable_key": "clu_a99fd74f79882061-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wounds", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An injury to living tissue, typically involving a break in the skin.", "note": "Includes various types of wounds like 'green Wounds' and 'inward Wounds'. 'Contusões' (bruises) are also a form of wound.", "wikipedia_extract": "A wound is any disruption of or damage to living tissue, such as skin, mucous membranes, or organs. Wounds can either be the sudden result of direct trauma (mechanical, thermal, chemical), or can develop slowly over time due to underlying disease processes such as diabetes mellitus, venous/arterial insufficiency, or immunologic disease. Wounds can vary greatly in their appearance depending on wound location, injury mechanism, depth of injury, timing of onset (acute vs chronic), and wound sterility, among other factors. Treatment strategies for wounds will vary based on the classification of the wound, therefore it is essential that wounds be thoroughly evaluated by a healthcare professional for proper management. In normal physiology, all wounds will undergo a series of steps collectively known as the wound healing process, which include hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and tissue remodeling. Age, tissue oxygenation, stress, underlying medical conditions, and certain medications are just a few of the many factors known to affect the rate of wound healing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "contusões", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3320, "target_cluster_name": "contusões", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'Wounds' from Portuguese/Spanish.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Direct translation of 'Wounds' from Portuguese/Spanish.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rasis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "rasis", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Rasis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rasis", "Rass"], "contexts": ["author of treatise", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "rasis", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rasis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 32, "variants": ["Rasis"], "contexts": ["Arabian physician, known as Benzacaria", "ancient authority on medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Rasis", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Rasis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1631, "stable_key": "clu_284ca6a09a708a26", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rhazes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Influential Persian physician and alchemist of the Islamic Golden Age.", "note": "Also known as Rasis or Benzacaria.", "wikipedia_extract": "Abū Bakr al-Rāzī, also known as Rhazes (full name: أبو بکر محمد بن زکریاء الرازي, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyāʾ al-Rāzī), 864 or 865 – 925 or 935 CE, was a Persian physician, philosopher and alchemist who lived during the Islamic Golden Age. He is widely regarded as one of the most important figures in the history of medicine, and also wrote on logic, astronomy and grammar. He is also known for his criticism of religion, especially with regard to the concepts of prophethood and revelation. However, the religio-philosophical aspects of his thought, which also included a belief in five \"eternal principles\", are fragmentary and only reported by authors who were often hostile to him. A comprehensive thinker, al-Razi made fundamental and enduring contributions to various fields, which he recorded in over 200 manuscripts, and is particularly remembered for numerous advances in medicine through his observations and discoveries. An early proponent of experimental medicine, he became a successful doctor, and served as chief physician of Baghdad and Ray hospitals."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Serapio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 212, "target_cluster_name": "Serapio", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Serapio e Aviçena*, acho muitos nomes que de- vem ser corrompidos, scilicet, os nomes dos autores ; folga- ria muyto me dixestes disto o que sabeis. • Serapio, 3 19; Avicena, Lib. 2, cap. 3 18 (nota d", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for Rasis", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Avicena", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "está cebar. RUANO Acerca dos indios he usado? ORTA Acerca dos fisicos da Pérsia, Arábia e Turquia se usa desta mezinha, porque sabem elles de cór Avicena, a que chamão elles Abolahi e a seus cinquo li", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: known to Persians, Arabs, and Turks", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Abolahi", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "da Pérsia, Arábia e Turquia se usa desta mezinha, porque sabem elles de cór Avicena, a que chamão elles Abolahi e a seus cinquo livros Canum, e sa- bem Rasis, a quem chamão Benzacaria, e a HaUrodoam e", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Arabic name for Avicenna"}, {"found_name": "Benzacaria", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 638, "target_cluster_name": "Rhafis", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "da Pérsia, Arábia e Turquia se usa desta mezinha, porque sabem elles de cór Avicena, a que chamão elles Abolahi e a seus cinquo livros Canum, e sa- bem Rasis, a quem chamão Benzacaria, e a HaUrodoam e", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Arabic name for Rasis"}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was a foundational figure in Greco-Roman medicine, and Rhazes, like many physicians of his era, built upon and sometimes critiqued Galen's extensive medical theories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was a foundational figure in Greco-Roman medicine, and Rhazes, like many physicians of his era, built upon and sometimes critiqued Galen's extensive medical theories."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his humoral theory and ethical principles were central to medical practice and discourse for centuries, including for physicians like Rhazes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine,' and his humoral theory and ethical principles were central to medical practice and discourse for centuries, including for physicians like Rhazes."}, {"found_name": "HaUrodoam", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "da Pérsia, Arábia e Turquia se usa desta mezinha, porque sabem elles de cór Avicena, a que chamão elles Abolahi e a seus cinquo livros Canum, e sa- bem Rasis, a quem chamão Benzacaria, e a HaUrodoam e", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for Rasis/Rhazes"}]}, {"canonical_name": "arvore", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "arvore", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "arvore", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 25, "variants": ["arvore", "ARVORE", "arvores"], "contexts": ["plant that smells good", "a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "arvores", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "arvores", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["arvores", "arvore", "arvore vitæ"], "contexts": ["trees", "plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "arvore", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "arvores", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 1632, "stable_key": "clu_378a80b6c3634ff8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "A woody perennial plant, typically having a single stem or trunk.", "note": "Plural form 'arvores' also present.", "wikipedia_extract": "In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable as lumber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos. Trees are not a monophyletic taxonomic group but consist of a wide variety of plant species that have independently evolved a trunk and branches as a way to tower above other plants to compete for sunlight. The majority of tree species are angiosperms or hardwoods; of the rest, many are gymnosperms or softwoods. Trees tend to be long-lived, some trees reaching several thousand years old."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ulmeiro", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3691, "target_cluster_name": "Olmo", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "uſou de chapejaras tacs chagas com a aguada infuſaódo ,,entrecaſco do Ulmeiro,ou Choupo,& foycomomilagrofaa brevidadecom ,, quefarou. Advirto que eſta arvore he aquela , que gera humas bolſas , em que", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for the tree"}, {"found_name": "Choupo", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1215, "target_cluster_name": "Poplar", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "uſou de chapejaras tacs chagas com a aguada infuſaódo ,,entrecaſco do Ulmeiro,ou Choupo,& foycomomilagrofaa brevidadecom ,, quefarou. Advirto que eſta arvore he aquela , que gera humas bolſas , em que", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_lab