nt", "quince, a fruit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Marmelos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Marmelo", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 1666, "stable_key": "clu_8cbd1c9208e2b041", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "quince", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cydonia oblonga", "family": "Rosaceae", "description": "A fragrant, yellow fruit used in cooking and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "The quince (; Cydonia oblonga) is the sole member of the genus Cydonia in the Malinae subtribe (which contains apples, pears, and other fruits) of the Rosaceae family. It is a deciduous tree that bears hard, aromatic bright golden-yellow pome fruit, similar in appearance to a pear. Ripe quince fruits are hard, tart, and astringent. They are eaten raw or processed into jam, quince cheese, or alcoholic drinks. The quince tree is sometimes grown as an ornamental plant for its attractive pale pink blossoms and as a miniature bonsai plant. Quinces are shrubs or small trees up to 4 to 6 metres (13 to 20 feet) tall and 3 to 4.5 metres (10 to 15 feet) wide."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 28, "members": [{"entity_id": "maçãas_de_anáfega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "maçãas de anáfega", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["maçãas de anáfega"], "contexts": ["Type of apples discussed"]}, {"entity_id": "maçãs_de_acypreſte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 27, "variants": ["maçãs de acypreſte", "maças de Acypreſte", "maças deaciprefte", "maçasdeatyprette", "maçã", "maçã de acipreste", "maçãs", "maçãs da anafega", "maçãs de Acipreſte", "maçãs de Anafeg", "maçãs de anafega", "maçãs de anaftega", "maçãs de anaſega", "maçãs de cypreste", "maçãsde cypreſte", "maçãsdeacypreſte", "pò de maça de Acypreſte"], "contexts": ["cypress apple used in unguent", "cypress apples"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "maçãas de anáfega", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1667, "stable_key": "clu_e4b9abae689f33dc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cypress apple", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Cupressaceae", "description": "The fruit-like gall produced by cypress trees, used in remedies.", "note": "Also referred to as 'maçãa de anáfega', likely a regional variant.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cypress is a common name for species in several genera in the family Cupressaceae. They grow in temperate climates and subtropical regions of Asia, Europe, and North America. The word cypress is derived from Old French cipres, which was imported from Latin cypressus, the latinisation of the Greek κυπάρισσος (kyparissos). The name derives from Cyparissus, a mythological figure who was turned into a cypress tree after killing a stag. Cypresses are trees or shrubs reaching heights of 1–50 metres (3.3–164.0 ft), exceptionally (in Cupressus austrotibetica) 100 m (330 ft). Many exhibit a conical to columnar form, particularly in their youth, but some are prostrate, irregular, or shrubby."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Juniper Berries", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3704, "target_cluster_name": "Juniperos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cypress trees produce berries that are botanically cones, but are often referred to as berries and used similarly to juniper berries in historical contexts, particularly in flavoring and traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cypress trees produce berries that are botanically cones, but are often referred to as berries and used similarly to juniper berries in historical contexts, particularly in flavoring and traditional medicine."}, {"found_name": "Juniperus communis", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4462, "target_cluster_name": "azimbro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Juniperus communis is a species of juniper, and its berries share culinary and medicinal uses with the 'berries' of cypress trees, suggesting a functional or traditional relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Juniperus communis is a species of juniper, and its berries share culinary and medicinal uses with the 'berries' of cypress trees, suggesting a functional or traditional relationship."}, {"found_name": "ash tree", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2502, "target_cluster_name": "Ash-tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both cypress and ash are types of trees, and in historical texts, they might be grouped together or compared due to their wood properties or common presence in certain landscapes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both cypress and ash are types of trees, and in historical texts, they might be grouped together or compared due to their wood properties or common presence in certain landscapes."}, {"found_name": "camuesas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3690, "target_cluster_name": "camuesas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of cypress apple as 'maçãs de acypreſte' suggests a potential similarity or relation to pome fruits like quince, though the exact nature of this relation requires further textual context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The description of cypress apple as 'maçãs de acypreſte' suggests a potential similarity or relation to pome fruits like quince, though the exact nature of this relation requires further textual context.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ftor", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "alguns a levarão pêra Portugal, e acharamse bem com eila (i). RUANO Pois destoutras maçãas me dizey o nome e o arvore e em que terras as ha e se sam maçãas de anáfega. ORTA O nome em canarym he ftor, ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for maçãas de anáfega"}, {"found_name": "pidards", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "alguns a levarão pêra Portugal, e acharamse bem com eila (i). RUANO Pois destoutras maçãas me dizey o nome e o arvore e em que terras as ha e se sam maçãas de anáfega. ORTA O nome em canarym he ftor, ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for maçãas de anáfega"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cankers", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "cankers", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cankers", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 26, "variants": ["Cankers", "Canker"], "contexts": ["condition, plant does good for it", "condition washed with distilled water"]}, {"entity_id": "cancers", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cancers", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cancers"], "contexts": ["disease that can be cured"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cankers", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cancers", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1668, "stable_key": "clu_28302aa694c96ab9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Canker", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Canker", "note": "While 'cancer' is the modern term for malignant tumors, 'canker' in early modern texts often referred to a broader range of ulcerative conditions, both in humans and plants."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hybrids", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "hybrids", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hybrids", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 20, "variants": ["Hybrids", "hybrids"], "contexts": ["sterile offspring of different species", "offspring of different species or varieties"]}, {"entity_id": "hybrid", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hybrid", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["hybrid"], "contexts": ["offspring of two different species", "offspring of different species"]}, {"entity_id": "hybrids", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hybrids", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["hybrids"], "contexts": ["offspring of different species or breeds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hybrids", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hybrids", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hybrid", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hybrids", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1669, "stable_key": "clu_f5822507773ae11d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hybrid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context refers to the scientific concept of hybridity, particularly as discussed in Darwin's 'Origin of Species'.", "wikidata_id": "Q721808", "wikidata_description": "genetic process of crossbreeding between genetically dissimilar parents to produce a hybrid", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hybrid_(biology)", "wikipedia_extract": "In biology, a hybrid is the offspring resulting from combining the qualities of two organisms of different varieties, subspecies, species or genera through sexual reproduction. Generally, it means that each cell has genetic material from two different organisms, whereas an individual where some cells are derived from a different organism is called a chimera. Hybrids are not always intermediates between their parents such as in blending inheritance (a now discredited theory in modern genetics by particulate inheritance), but can show hybrid vigor, sometimes growing larger or taller than either parent. The concept of a hybrid is interpreted differently in animal and plant breeding, where there is interest in the individual parentage. In genetics, attention is focused on the numbers of chromosomes. In taxonomy, a key question is how closely related the parent species are."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Breed", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2181, "target_cluster_name": "breed", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A breed is a specific group of domestic animals having homogeneous in appearance, behavior, and other characteristics, and in having been developed by human intervention. Hybrids are often discussed in the context of breeding and the creation of new breeds or varieties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A breed is a specific group of domestic animals having homogeneous in appearance, behavior, and other characteristics, and in having been developed by human intervention. Hybrids are often discussed in the context of breeding and the creation of new breeds or varieties."}, {"found_name": "Genus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 263, "target_cluster_name": "genus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hybrids can be formed between different species within the same genus, and the concept of genus is fundamental to understanding the boundaries and relationships between organisms that can produce hybrids.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hybrids can be formed between different species within the same genus, and the concept of genus is fundamental to understanding the boundaries and relationships between organisms that can produce hybrids."}, {"found_name": "Living organisms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 257, "target_cluster_name": "organic beings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The definition of a hybrid is rooted in the sexual reproduction of living organisms. The concept of hybridity is a specific aspect of the broader category of living organisms and their reproduction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The definition of a hybrid is rooted in the sexual reproduction of living organisms. The concept of hybridity is a specific aspect of the broader category of living organisms and their reproduction."}, {"found_name": "Evolution", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 540, "target_cluster_name": "Evolution", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hybridization can play a significant role in evolutionary processes, contributing to genetic diversity and potentially leading to the formation of new species over time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hybridization can play a significant role in evolutionary processes, contributing to genetic diversity and potentially leading to the formation of new species over time."}, {"found_name": "Heredity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1233, "target_cluster_name": "innate", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hybridization is a direct consequence of the principles of heredity, as it involves the transmission of genetic material from two different parents to their offspring.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hybridization is a direct consequence of the principles of heredity, as it involves the transmission of genetic material from two different parents to their offspring."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Paraguay", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "paraguay", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Paraguay", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Paraguay"], "contexts": ["location with specific fly affecting animals", "location where cattle are destroyed"]}, {"entity_id": "paraguay", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Paraguay", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 25, "variants": ["Paraguay"], "contexts": ["province in the first category of census", "territory with boundary commissions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Paraguay", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Paraguay", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1670, "stable_key": "clu_32b4a9316825aff0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paraguay", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in South America, historically a province.", "wikipedia_extract": "Paraguay (Guarani: Paraguái), officially the Republic of Paraguay, is a landlocked country located in the central region of South America. It borders Bolivia to the northwest and north, Brazil to the northeast and east, and Argentina to the southeast, south, and west. Paraguay has access to the Atlantic Ocean via the Paraná–Paraguay Waterway. The country is governed as a unitary presidential republic composed of a capital district and seventeen departments. Its capital and largest city is Asunción. The indigenous Guaraní had been living in eastern Paraguay for at least a millennium before the arrival of Spanish conquistadores in 1524."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brazil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 96, "target_cluster_name": "Brasil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Brazil is a bordering country to Paraguay.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Brazil is a bordering country to Paraguay."}, {"found_name": "South America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 63, "target_cluster_name": "South America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paraguay is located in South America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paraguay is located in South America."}]}, {"canonical_name": "M. Bonpland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__bonpland", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. Bonpland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 26, "variants": ["M. Bonpland", "Bonpland"], "contexts": ["naturalist and explorer", "travel companion"]}, {"entity_id": "bonpland,_m_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bonpland, M.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bonpland, M."], "contexts": ["studied pelagic shells with Humboldt"]}, {"entity_id": "bonpland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bonpland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bonpland"], "contexts": ["found pelagic shells with author", "observer and describer of star fall"]}, {"entity_id": "bonpland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bonpland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bonpland"], "contexts": ["naturalist who studied species diversity", "found identical productions of Old and New World"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Bonpland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bonpland, M.", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1671, "stable_key": "clu_363353acc096f815", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aimé Bonpland", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1773, "death_year": 1858, "description": "French botanist, explorer, and physician associated with Humboldt.", "wikidata_id": "Q405702", "wikidata_description": "French physician, botanist and explorer (1773-1858)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aim%C3%A9%20Bonpland", "wikipedia_extract": "Aimé Jacques Alexandre Bonpland (French: [ɛme bɔ̃plɑ̃]; 22 August 1773 – 11 May 1858) was a French explorer and botanist who traveled with Alexander von Humboldt in Latin America from 1799 to 1804. He co-authored volumes of the scientific results of their expedition. The standard author abbreviation Bonpl. is used to indicate this person as the author when citing a botanical name. Bonpland was born as Aimé Jacques Alexandre Goujaud in La Rochelle, France, on 22, 28, or 29 August 1773. His father was a physician and, around 1790, he joined his brother Michael in Paris, where they both studied medicine."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Boussingault", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 572, "target_cluster_name": "Boussingault", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bonpland was a French naturalist and explorer who collaborated with Alexander von Humboldt on their famous expedition to the Americas. Boussingault also conducted extensive scientific work in South America, making their research areas and geographical focus potentially related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bonpland was a French naturalist and explorer who collaborated with Alexander von Humboldt on their famous expedition to the Americas. Boussingault also conducted extensive scientific work in South America, making their research areas and geographical focus potentially related.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Saussure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 707, "target_cluster_name": "Saussure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked with Alexander von Humboldt, a contemporary and peer of Saussure in the exploration of natural sciences and geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked with Alexander von Humboldt, a contemporary and peer of Saussure in the exploration of natural sciences and geography.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Collaborated extensively with Alexander von Humboldt on scientific expeditions in Latin America and co-authored scientific publications detailing their findings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Collaborated extensively with Alexander von Humboldt on scientific expeditions in Latin America and co-authored scientific publications detailing their findings."}, {"found_name": "Achille Valenciennes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3364, "target_cluster_name": "Valenciennes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Aimé Bonpland on the ichthyology section of the 'Voyage de Humboldt et Bonpland'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Aimé Bonpland on the ichthyology section of the 'Voyage de Humboldt et Bonpland'."}, {"found_name": "Caldas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1895, "target_cluster_name": "Caldas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bonpland was a close collaborator and friend of Alexander von Humboldt, and worked alongside Caldas in South America during the Royal Scientific Expedition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bonpland was a close collaborator and friend of Alexander von Humboldt, and worked alongside Caldas in South America during the Royal Scientific Expedition.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2542, "target_cluster_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aimé Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked extensively in South America, a region also studied by Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and likely shared scientific interests and networks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aimé Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked extensively in South America, a region also studied by Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and likely shared scientific interests and networks.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cadix", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "cadix", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cadix", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 25, "variants": ["Cadix", "Cadiz"], "contexts": ["city in Spain", "city mentioned with merchants"]}, {"entity_id": "cadix", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cadix", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cadix"], "contexts": ["city where Tâmara was professor", "city where Francisco Tâmara was professor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cadix", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cadix", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1672, "stable_key": "clu_9360ea2b0ed84ee9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cádiz", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A port city in Andalusia, Spain.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cádiz ( kə-DIZ, US also  KAY-diz, KA(H)D-iz, Spanish: [ˈkaðiθ]) is a city in Spain and the capital of the province of Cádiz in the autonomous community of Andalusia. It is located in the southwest of the Iberian Peninsula off the Atlantic Ocean separated from neighbouring San Fernando by a narrow isthmus. One of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Western Europe, Cádiz was founded by the Phoenicians as a trading post. In the 18th century, the port in the Bay of Cádiz consolidated itself as the main harbour of mainland Spain, enjoying the virtual monopoly of trade with the Americas until 1778. It is also the site of the University of Cádiz. Situated on a narrow slice of land surrounded by the sea‚ Cádiz is, in most respects, a typical Andalusian city with well-preserved historical landmarks."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Algarve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2147, "target_cluster_name": "Algarve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Algarve is a region in southern Portugal, directly west of Andalusia and historically connected to it through trade and cultural exchange across the border and along the Atlantic coast.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Algarve is a region in southern Portugal, directly west of Andalusia and historically connected to it through trade and cultural exchange across the border and along the Atlantic coast."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Martinique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "martinique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Martinique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 25, "variants": ["Martinique"], "contexts": ["Barbade, Martinique and Saint-Thomas", "island with easier communication"]}, {"entity_id": "martinique", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Martinique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Martinique"], "contexts": ["island where earthquake was felt", "island in the Antilles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Martinique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Martinique", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1673, "stable_key": "clu_c33e7b947df4e195", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Martinique", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q17054", "wikidata_description": "island in the Lesser Antilles, overseas region and department of France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martinique", "wikipedia_extract": "Saint-Pierre (, ; French pronunciation: [sɛ̃ pjɛʁ] ; Martinican Creole: Senpiè) is a town and commune of France's Caribbean overseas department of Martinique, founded in 1635 by Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc. Before the total destruction of Saint-Pierre by a volcanic eruption in 1902, it was the most important city of Martinique culturally and economically, being known as \"the Paris of the Caribbean\". While Fort-de-France was the official administrative capital, Saint-Pierre was the cultural capital of Martinique. After the disaster, Fort-de-France grew in economic importance. Saint-Pierre was founded in 1635 by Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc, a French trader and adventurer, as the first permanent French colony on the island of Martinique. The Great Hurricane of 1780 produced a storm-surge of 8 metres (25 ft) which \"inundated the city, destroying all houses\" and killed 9,000 people."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antilles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1487, "target_cluster_name": "Antilles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Martinique is part of the Antilles archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Martinique is part of the Antilles archipelago."}, {"found_name": "Caribbean Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2786, "target_cluster_name": "Caribbean Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Martinique is located in the Caribbean Sea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Martinique is located in the Caribbean Sea."}, {"found_name": "France", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 46, "target_cluster_name": "France", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Martinique is an overseas department of France.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Martinique is an overseas department of France."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Valencia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "valencia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Valencia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 19, "variants": ["Valencia", "Valence"], "contexts": ["lake mentioned in map description", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "nueva_valencia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nueva Valencia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Nueva Valencia", "Nueva-Valencia"], "contexts": ["city in Caracas province", "city in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "nueva_valencia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nueva Valencia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nueva Valencia"], "contexts": ["city near Trincheras hot springs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Valencia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nueva Valencia", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nueva Valencia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nueva Valencia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1674, "stable_key": "clu_676f40663631234c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Valencia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "The presence of 'Nueva Valencia' and context of Caracas province strongly points to the Venezuelan city.", "wikidata_id": "Q54880", "wikidata_description": "city in Carabobo State, Venezuela", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valencia%2C%20Venezuela", "wikipedia_extract": "Valencia (Spanish pronunciation: [baˈlensja]) is the capital city of Carabobo State and the third-largest city in Venezuela. The city is an economic hub that contains Venezuela's top industries and manufacturing companies. It is also the largest city in the Valencia-Maracay metropolitan region, which with a population of about 4.5 million is the country's second largest after that of Caracas. Caracas lies some 172 kilometres (107 miles) away to the east. The area was already inhabited in the fourth millennium BC. The inhabitants were mainly hunters and gatherers who might have already developed some elementary forms of agriculture."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Venezuela", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1496, "target_cluster_name": "Venezuela", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Valencia is the third-largest city in Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Valencia is the third-largest city in Venezuela."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Religiofo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "religieuses", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "religieuses", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["religieuses", "religieux"], "contexts": ["nuns not included in census", "extinguished subterranean fire with holy water"]}, {"entity_id": "religiofo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Religiofo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 25, "variants": ["Religiofo", "Religiofo Cartuxo", "Religiofo Pauliſta", "Religiofo Trino", "Religiofo do Carmo", "Religiofos", "Religiofos Trinos", "Religiofos de Sao Bernardo", "Religiosa", "Religiosos", "Religiosos Trinos", "Religioſo"], "contexts": ["religious figure who assisted the dying", "religious individual"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "religieuses", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Religiofo", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 1675, "stable_key": "clu_22f57c5e89bada75", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nun", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Religious woman, often in a monastic order, providing care.", "note": "The term 'Religiofo' (and its plural 'religieuses') can refer to any religious person, but in the context of assisting the dying and monastic communities, 'nun' is the most likely specific referent for female religious.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Franciscan monk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4038, "target_cluster_name": "moine franciscain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A nun is a female member of a religious community, often associated with monastic orders like the Franciscans, which also have male members (monks).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A nun is a female member of a religious community, often associated with monastic orders like the Franciscans, which also have male members (monks)."}, {"found_name": "Clergyman", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 450, "target_cluster_name": "Clerigo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nuns are members of a religious order, similar to how clergymen are members of the clergy. Both are distinct roles within religious institutions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nuns are members of a religious order, similar to how clergymen are members of the clergy. Both are distinct roles within religious institutions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Annuaire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "annuaire", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Annuaire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Annuaire"], "contexts": ["publication cited for comet data", "annual publication containing astronomical data"]}, {"entity_id": "annuaire", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Annuaire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Annuaire"], "contexts": ["publication where treatise appeared"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Annuaire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Annuaire", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1676, "stable_key": "clu_0321c6ec4071955d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Almanac", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The French term 'Annuaire' translates to 'annual' or 'almanac'. In the context of scientific publications citing data, 'almanac' is the most fitting referent for a publication containing such information."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chronology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2825, "target_cluster_name": "Chronology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Almanacs are inherently tied to the measurement and organization of time, making chronology a closely related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Almanacs are inherently tied to the measurement and organization of time, making chronology a closely related concept."}, {"found_name": "Astrology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1840, "target_cluster_name": "Astrology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, almanacs often contained astrological predictions and information, establishing a strong link between the two.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, almanacs often contained astrological predictions and information, establishing a strong link between the two."}, {"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Almanacs frequently feature astronomical data, such as celestial positions and events, linking them directly to the field of astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Almanacs frequently feature astronomical data, such as celestial positions and events, linking them directly to the field of astronomy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "organism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 42, "members": [{"entity_id": "organisms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organisms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["organisms"], "contexts": ["living beings in nature", "ancient and recent organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "organism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organism", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 23, "variants": ["organism"], "contexts": ["a living biological entity", "changes fitted to meet environmental changes"]}, {"entity_id": "organisms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "organisms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["organisms"], "contexts": ["living beings", "living beings that change over time"]}, {"entity_id": "animal_organisms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "animal organisms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["animal organisms"], "contexts": ["living creatures on Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "organisms", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organisms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["organisms"], "contexts": ["living beings", "living beings, plants and animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "organisms", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organism", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 1677, "stable_key": "clu_41e57d1260e4bf93", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "organism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q55983715", "wikidata_description": "organisms which have been assigned common names in addition to their taxon name"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dentes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 52, "members": [{"entity_id": "dentes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dentes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["dentes"], "contexts": ["tusks of elephants", "part of elephants, used for ivory"]}, {"entity_id": "dentes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dentes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 23, "variants": ["dentes", "DENTES", "Dentes", "dente"], "contexts": ["teeth", "body part"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth for chewing", "part of deer used to judge age"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Teeth"], "contexts": ["part whose pains are eased by Angelica", "aching teeth treated by Balm"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures for biting and chewing", "correlated with hair in dogs"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth for biting"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dentes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dentes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1678, "stable_key": "clu_727693024e481599", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "teeth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'dentes' can refer to animal tusks, the context of medicinal texts and general biological discussion points to human teeth as the primary referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q1545761", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grevillea", "wikipedia_extract": "Grevillea (), commonly known as spider flowers, is a genus of about 360 species of evergreen flowering plants in the family Proteaceae. Plants in the genus Grevillea are shrubs, rarely trees, with the leaves arranged alternately along the branches, the flowers zygomorphic, arranged in racemes at the ends of branchlets, and the fruit a follicle that splits down one side only, releasing one or two seeds. Plants in the genus Grevillea are shrubs, rarely small trees with simple or compound leaves arranged alternately along the branchlets. The flowers are zygomorphic and typically arranged in pairs along a sometimes branched raceme at the ends of branchlets. The flowers are bisexual, usually with four tepals in a single whorl. There are four stamens and the gynoecium has a single carpel."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4023, "target_cluster_name": "bone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Teeth are a type of bone-like structure, and are often discussed in conjunction with bones in anatomical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Teeth are a type of bone-like structure, and are often discussed in conjunction with bones in anatomical texts."}, {"found_name": "Gums (anatomy)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2243, "target_cluster_name": "gengivas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Teeth are directly embedded in and supported by the gums.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Teeth are directly embedded in and supported by the gums."}, {"found_name": "gengivas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2243, "target_cluster_name": "gengivas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gums are the tissues that surround and support the teeth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gums are the tissues that surround and support the teeth.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arruda", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "arruda", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "arruda", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["arruda", "aruda"], "contexts": ["herb used medicinally and in salads", "herb"]}, {"entity_id": "arruda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arruda", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 24, "variants": ["Arruda", "aarruda", "arruda", "arruda verde", "arrudameya"], "contexts": ["herb used in remedies", "rue, ingredient for ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "arruda", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arruda", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 1679, "stable_key": "clu_45f45d038dd12934", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rue", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ruta graveolens", "family": "Rutaceae", "description": "A pungent herb used medicinally and in culinary preparations.", "note": "Arundo is likely a misspelling or variant of arruda.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ruta graveolens, commonly known as rue, common rue, ruda, arruda or herb-of-grace, is a species of the genus Ruta grown as an ornamental plant and herb. It is native to the Mediterranean. It is grown throughout the world in gardens, especially for its bluish leaves, and sometimes for its tolerance of hot and dry soil conditions. It is also cultivated as a culinary herb, and to a lesser extent as an insect repellent and incense. The specific epithet graveolens refers to the strong-smelling leaves. Rue is a woody, perennial shrub."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Spring", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "spring", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Spring", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 25, "variants": ["Spring", "Spring time"], "contexts": ["season for using herbs", "season when new shoots appear"]}, {"entity_id": "spring", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spring", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spring"], "contexts": ["season of new growth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Spring", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "spring", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 1680, "stable_key": "clu_a04e36c660ec4c63", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "spring", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "April", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 129, "target_cluster_name": "April", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "April is a month that falls within the season of Spring.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "April is a month that falls within the season of Spring."}, {"found_name": "May", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1657, "target_cluster_name": "May", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "May is a month that falls within the season of Spring.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "May is a month that falls within the season of Spring."}, {"found_name": "June", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 69, "target_cluster_name": "June", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "June is a month that falls within the season of Spring.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "June is a month that falls within the season of Spring."}, {"found_name": "Heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 197, "target_cluster_name": "calor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring is a season characterized by increasing heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spring is a season characterized by increasing heat."}, {"found_name": "Cooling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 720, "target_cluster_name": "cooling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring follows winter, a period of cooling, and precedes summer's heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spring follows winter, a period of cooling, and precedes summer's heat."}, {"found_name": "Flow", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3911, "target_cluster_name": "flowing", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring is associated with the flow of water and the growth of plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spring is associated with the flow of water and the growth of plants."}, {"found_name": "Development", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 960, "target_cluster_name": "development", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring is a season of development for plants and animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spring is a season of development for plants and animals."}, {"found_name": "Organic growth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1458, "target_cluster_name": "organic growth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spring is a season of significant organic growth in nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spring is a season of significant organic growth in nature."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Silla de Caracas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "silla_of_caraccas", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Silla of Caraccas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Silla of Caraccas"], "contexts": ["mountain in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "silla_de_caracas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Silla de Caracas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 25, "variants": ["Silla de Caracas", "La Silla de Caracas", "Pic oriental de la Silla de Caracas", "Silla", "Silla de Cayo Romano", "Silla de Ciracas", "Silla de Veragua"], "contexts": ["granitic mountain chain", "mountain in the Cordilleras of the coast"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Silla of Caraccas", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Silla de Caracas", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 1681, "stable_key": "clu_034810350a1cf242", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "El Ávila National Park", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A prominent mountain peak and national park near Caracas, Venezuela.", "note": "The 'Silla de Caracas' refers to the mountain peak which is now part of El Ávila National Park.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ávila National Park, officially known as Waraira Repano National Park for its indigenous Cariban name, protects part of the Cordillera de la Costa Central mountain range, in the coastal region of central-northern Venezuela. The area's highest elevation is Pico Naiguatá, at 2,765 meters (9,072 ft) above sea level. El Ávila is known as \"el pulmón de la ciudad\", the lungs of the city, since it serves many different functions for residents of Caracas, the most basic of these being navigation: the peak of El Ávila marks the north of the city. It is home to the longest cable car ride in the world, as well as activities like running, biking, rock climbing, camping, and zip-lining. El Hotel Humboldt as well as many restaurants, including La Chivera, Casa Pakea, El Jardín de Luna, Le Galipanier, Granja Natalia and Tarahumara, are all nestled in the mountain's hills. Every December, the \"Cruz de Navidad\" is illuminated, a large crucifix high up in the mountain that faces the city and can be seen from very far distances."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1497, "target_cluster_name": "Caracas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "El Ávila National Park is located in the coastal region of central-northern Venezuela, and Caracas is the capital city of Venezuela, implying a close geographical proximity and likely historical connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "El Ávila National Park is located in the coastal region of central-northern Venezuela, and Caracas is the capital city of Venezuela, implying a close geographical proximity and likely historical connection."}]}, {"canonical_name": "géognosie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "géognosie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "géognosie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 21, "variants": ["géognosie", "Géognosie générale", "Géographie", "géognosllques", "géognostique", "géognostiques"], "contexts": ["knowledge related to earth sciences", "science of earth formations"]}, {"entity_id": "geognosie", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Geognosie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Geognosie"], "contexts": ["book title on geology", "study of earth's structure"]}, {"entity_id": "geognosia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Geognosia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geognosia"], "contexts": ["study of earth's inanimate nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "géognosie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Geognosie", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "géognosie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Geognosia", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 1682, "stable_key": "clu_5dcce300d385e125", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "geognosy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This term predates 'geology' and refers to the study of the Earth's structure and composition, particularly its inanimate parts."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Geology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 278, "target_cluster_name": "Geology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geognosy is an older term for geology, focusing on the physical constitution of the Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geognosy is an older term for geology, focusing on the physical constitution of the Earth."}, {"found_name": "Geography", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1068, "target_cluster_name": "Geography", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geognosy is closely related to geography, as it deals with the physical features and structure of the Earth's surface and interior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geognosy is closely related to geography, as it deals with the physical features and structure of the Earth's surface and interior."}, {"found_name": "Mineralogy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3625, "target_cluster_name": "mineralógica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geognosy often involved the study and classification of minerals and rocks, which is the domain of mineralogy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geognosy often involved the study and classification of minerals and rocks, which is the domain of mineralogy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "α Centauri", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "a__du_centaure", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "a. du Centaure", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["a. du Centaure"], "contexts": ["celestial object used for latitude determination"]}, {"entity_id": "α_centauri", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "α Centauri", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 25, "variants": ["α Centauri"], "contexts": ["nearest known star", "bright star in the heavens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "a. du Centaure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "α Centauri", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 1683, "stable_key": "clu_b6360709f80ab7fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alpha Centauri", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While 'Centuria' could refer to a century, in the context of astronomical observation and proximity to 'α Centauri' and 'a. du Centaure', it's highly likely a garbled reference to Alpha Centauri.", "wikidata_id": "Q12176", "wikidata_description": "triple star system in the constellation Centaurus, where one of the three stars, Proxima Centauri, being the closest star to the Earth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha%20Centauri", "wikipedia_extract": "Alpha Centauri (α Centauri, α Cen, or Alpha Cen) is a star system in the southern constellation of Centaurus. It consists of three stars: Rigil Kentaurus (α Centauri A), Toliman (α Centauri B), and Proxima Centauri (α Centauri C). Proxima Centauri is the closest star to the Sun at 4.2465 light-years (ly), which is 1.3020 parsecs (pc), while Alpha Centauri A and B are the nearest stars visible to the naked eye. Rigil Kentaurus and Toliman are Sun-like stars (class G and K, respectively) that together form the binary star system α Centauri AB. To the naked eye, these two main components appear to be a single star with an apparent magnitude of −0.27. It is the brightest star in the constellation and the third-brightest in the night sky, outshone by only Sirius and Canopus. α Centauri AB are the nearest binary stars to the Sun at a distance of 4.344 ly (1.33 pc)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "reasoning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "reasoning", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reasoning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 20, "variants": ["reasoning", "Reasoning"], "contexts": ["Soul's faculty", "mental process of forming conclusions"]}, {"entity_id": "reasonings", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reasonings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["reasonings"], "contexts": ["mental phenomena", "process of logical deduction"]}, {"entity_id": "reasoning", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "reasoning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reasoning"], "contexts": ["substitutes reasoning for assumption"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reasoning", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "reasoning", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reasonings", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "reasoning", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 1684, "stable_key": "clu_0c3e2592e19c301c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "reasoning", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2555318", "wikidata_description": "subfield of computer science and logic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automated%20reasoning", "wikipedia_extract": "In computer science, in particular in knowledge representation and reasoning and metalogic, the area of automated reasoning is dedicated to understanding different aspects of reasoning. The study of automated reasoning helps produce computer programs that allow computers to reason completely, or nearly completely, automatically. Although automated reasoning is considered a sub-field of artificial intelligence, it also has connections with theoretical computer science and philosophy. The most developed subareas of automated reasoning are automated theorem proving (and the less automated but more pragmatic subfield of interactive theorem proving) and automated proof checking (viewed as guaranteed correct reasoning under fixed assumptions). Extensive work has also been done in reasoning by analogy using induction and abduction. Other important topics include reasoning under uncertainty and non-monotonic reasoning."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Deductive reasoning", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3792, "target_cluster_name": "Deduction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Deductive reasoning is a specific form of reasoning, where conclusions are logically derived from premises.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Deductive reasoning is a specific form of reasoning, where conclusions are logically derived from premises."}, {"found_name": "Cognition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 656, "target_cluster_name": "cognition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cognition encompasses the mental processes involved in reasoning, such as perception, attention, memory, and thinking.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cognition encompasses the mental processes involved in reasoning, such as perception, attention, memory, and thinking."}, {"found_name": "Human intellect", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1441, "target_cluster_name": "human intellect", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Human intellect is the faculty that enables reasoning and other cognitive functions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Human intellect is the faculty that enables reasoning and other cognitive functions."}, {"found_name": "Calculation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1380, "target_cluster_name": "calculation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calculation is a form of reasoning that involves numerical operations and logical steps to arrive at a result.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Calculation is a form of reasoning that involves numerical operations and logical steps to arrive at a result."}, {"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The association of ideas is a psychological principle that describes how thoughts and concepts become linked, which is fundamental to many forms of reasoning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The association of ideas is a psychological principle that describes how thoughts and concepts become linked, which is fundamental to many forms of reasoning."}, {"found_name": "Induction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 280, "target_cluster_name": "Induction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Induction is a form of reasoning that involves forming generalizations based on specific observations, contrasting with deductive reasoning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Induction is a form of reasoning that involves forming generalizations based on specific observations, contrasting with deductive reasoning."}]}, {"canonical_name": "impression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "impression", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "impression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 14, "variants": ["impression"], "contexts": ["sensory input", "sensory input received"]}, {"entity_id": "impressions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "impressions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["impressions"], "contexts": ["sensory input or stimuli", "mental representations of stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "impressions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "impressions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["impressions"], "contexts": ["sensory inputs forming consciousness", "sensory inputs to the mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "impression", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "impressions", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "impressions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "impressions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1685, "stable_key": "clu_6ccf6dfc5a79acfc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sensory impression", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'impression' can have other meanings, in these early modern texts concerning psychology and natural philosophy, it most consistently refers to sensory input or its mental representation.", "wikidata_id": "Q74223588", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 July 1956"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Experience", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 105, "target_cluster_name": "experience", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sensory impressions are the raw material of experience.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sensory impressions are the raw material of experience."}, {"found_name": "Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 226, "target_cluster_name": "ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sensory impressions are often the basis for the formation of ideas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sensory impressions are often the basis for the formation of ideas."}]}, {"canonical_name": "suffocação", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "suffocation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "suffocation", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["suffocation"], "contexts": ["lack of air"]}, {"entity_id": "suffocação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "suffocação", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 25, "variants": ["suffocação", "Suffocaçao", "fuffocativa", "fuffocaçao", "fuffocaçaó", "fuffocaçaõ", "suffocar", "suffocatio", "suffocation", "suffocatione", "suffocationes"], "contexts": ["symptom of suffocation", "suffocation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "suffocation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "suffocação", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 1686, "stable_key": "clu_44b88f86f167e2f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Suffocation", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A symptom or condition characterized by a lack of air; can be related to hysteria.", "wikipedia_extract": "Asphyxia or asphyxiation is a condition of deficient supply of oxygen to the body which arises from abnormal breathing. Asphyxia causes generalized hypoxia, which affects all the tissues and organs, some more rapidly than others. There are many circumstances that can induce asphyxia, all of which are characterized by the inability of a person to acquire sufficient oxygen through breathing for an extended period of time. Asphyxia can cause coma or death. In 2015, about 9.8 million cases of unintentional suffocation occurred which resulted in 35,600 deaths. The word asphyxia is from Ancient Greek α- \"without\" and σφύξις sphyxis, \"squeeze\" (throb of heart)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Asphyxia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4150, "target_cluster_name": "asphyxia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Asphyxia is a direct synonym for suffocation, referring to the condition of oxygen deficiency.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Asphyxia is a direct synonym for suffocation, referring to the condition of oxygen deficiency."}, {"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coma can be a consequence of severe suffocation due to lack of oxygen to the brain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coma can be a consequence of severe suffocation due to lack of oxygen to the brain."}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a common symptom and immediate result of severe suffocation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Unconsciousness is a common symptom and immediate result of severe suffocation."}, {"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of reduced consciousness that can result from oxygen deprivation, as seen in suffocation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Stupor is a state of reduced consciousness that can result from oxygen deprivation, as seen in suffocation."}, {"found_name": "Exhaustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3509, "target_cluster_name": "exhaustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The body's struggle against suffocation can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The body's struggle against suffocation can lead to extreme exhaustion."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Northern Asia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "northern_asia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Northern Asia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 25, "variants": ["Northern Asia"], "contexts": ["region with steppes", "greatest dryness of atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "north_asiatic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "North Asiatic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Asiatic"], "contexts": ["relating to northern Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Northern Asia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "North Asiatic", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 1687, "stable_key": "clu_827078f6119fa9d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Northern Asia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia, China, Mongolia, Kazakhstan, etc.", "wikidata_id": "Q27329", "wikidata_description": "subregion of Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North%20Asia", "wikipedia_extract": "North Asia or Northern Asia (Russian: Северная Азия) is the northern region of Asia, which is defined in geographical terms and consists of three federal districts of Russia: Ural, Siberian, and the Far Eastern. North Asia is bordered by the Arctic Ocean to its north; by Eastern Europe to its west; by Central Asia and East Asia to its south; and by the Pacific Ocean and Northern America to its east. It covers an area of 13,100,000 square kilometres (5,100,000 sq mi), or 8.8% of Earth's total land area; and is the largest subregion of Asia by area, occupying approx. 29.4% of Asia's land area, but is also the least populated, with a population of around 37 million, accounting for merely 0.74% of Asia's population. Topographically, the region is dominated by the Eurasian Plate, except for its eastern part, which lies on the North American, Amurian, and Okhotsk Plates. It is divided by three major plains: the West Siberian Plain, Central Siberian Plateau, and Verhoyansk-Chukotka collision zone."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 891, "target_cluster_name": "Polar Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Northern Asia is bordered by the Arctic Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Northern Asia is bordered by the Arctic Ocean."}, {"found_name": "Eastern Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2797, "target_cluster_name": "Eastern Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Northern Asia is bordered by Eastern Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Northern Asia is bordered by Eastern Europe."}, {"found_name": "Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 231, "target_cluster_name": "Asie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Northern Asia is a region of Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Northern Asia is a region of Asia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "gengibre", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "gengibre", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Gengibre", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Gengibre"], "contexts": ["green ginger brought from there", "a plant root"]}, {"entity_id": "gengibre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "gengibre", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 21, "variants": ["gengibre", "gengi", "gengi bre", "gengivre", "gingibre"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a wine remedy", "ginger used in collyrium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Gengibre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "gengibre", "similarity": 0.794}], "id": 1688, "stable_key": "clu_6ad956e058773e1a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ginger", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLANT", "linnaean": "Zingiber officinale", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "description": "A spicy rhizome used in medicine, cooking, and as a spice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ginger (Zingiber officinale) is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems (false stems made of the rolled bases of leaves) about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots. Ginger is in the family Zingiberaceae, which also includes turmeric (Curcuma longa), cardamom (Elettaria cardamomum), and galangal. Ginger originated in Maritime Southeast Asia and was likely domesticated first by the Austronesian peoples. It was transported with them throughout the Indo-Pacific during the Austronesian expansion (c. 5,000 BP), reaching as far as Hawaii."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2918, "target_cluster_name": "Ginger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the Portuguese equivalent of 'ginger'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the Portuguese equivalent of 'ginger'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bengala", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 585, "target_cluster_name": "Bengala", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "porque não vades debalde, diguovos que se chama gengivre acerca de nós, e acerca dos Arábios e Pérsios e Turcos, gim:{ibil, e os Guzarates e Decanins e Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e s", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: local name for ginger", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "galanga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 627, "target_cluster_name": "galanga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is a close relative of ginger, belonging to the same family (Zingiberaceae), and is often used as a spice and in traditional medicine, sometimes interchangeably or in conjunction with ginger.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Galangal is a close relative of ginger, belonging to the same family (Zingiberaceae), and is often used as a spice and in traditional medicine, sometimes interchangeably or in conjunction with ginger.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cardamomo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 636, "target_cluster_name": "Cardamomo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cardamom belongs to the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), and they are often grouped together in historical texts due to their shared family and use as spices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cardamom belongs to the ginger family (Zingiberaceae), and they are often grouped together in historical texts due to their shared family and use as spices.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "croco indiaco", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2464, "target_cluster_name": "croco indiaco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Turmeric belongs to the same family (Zingiberaceae) as ginger and shares a similar rhizomatous growth habit and culinary/medicinal uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Turmeric belongs to the same family (Zingiberaceae) as ginger and shares a similar rhizomatous growth habit and culinary/medicinal uses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Amomum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2029, "target_cluster_name": "Amomum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ginger is a rhizome from plants in the family Zingiberaceae, which is closely related to Amomum (also in Zingiberaceae). Both are known for their aromatic properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ginger is a rhizome from plants in the family Zingiberaceae, which is closely related to Amomum (also in Zingiberaceae). Both are known for their aromatic properties.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Root", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 536, "target_cluster_name": "Root", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "are but the several parts and fruits of the same tree, is the common belief of those which daily use them. Whereof to speak distinctly, Ginger is the root of neither Tree nor Shrub, but of an herbaceo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: is a type of", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "gallico", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "morbo_gallico", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "morbo gallico", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["morbo gallico"], "contexts": ["syphilis, a disease not caused by this remedy"]}, {"entity_id": "gallico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "gallico", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 24, "variants": ["gallico", "Gallico", "Gallicum", "gallicados", "gallicos", "males gallicos", "males gallicos antigos"], "contexts": ["ailment treated by antimony", "syphilis, a chronic disease"]}, {"entity_id": "morbo_gallico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "morbo gallico", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["morbo gallico"], "contexts": ["syphilis, a chronic disease"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "morbo gallico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "gallico", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "morbo gallico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "morbo gallico", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 1689, "stable_key": "clu_6d3d584cfaa56467", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "syphilis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "syphilis", "note": "The term 'morbo gallico' (French disease) was a common historical euphemism for syphilis."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "morbo napolitano", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2860, "target_cluster_name": "morbo napolitano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is listed as a historical name for syphilis in the provided candidate list.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is listed as a historical name for syphilis in the provided candidate list.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "experiencia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "experiencia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "experiencia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["experiencia"], "contexts": ["practical knowledge gained from observation"]}, {"entity_id": "experiencia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "experiencia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 25, "variants": ["experiencia", "experiencia moderna", "experiencianos", "experiencias", "experientia", "experientijs", "experimenta", "experimentamos"], "contexts": ["knowledge gained from practice", "experience as basis for claims"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "experiencia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "experiencia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1690, "stable_key": "clu_95d731136d022eaf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Empirical knowledge", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q83368", "wikidata_description": "theory that states that knowledge comes only or primarily from sensory experience", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empiricism", "wikipedia_extract": "In philosophy, empiricism is an epistemological view which holds that true knowledge or justification comes either only or primarily from sensory experience and empirical evidence. It is one of several competing views within epistemology, along with rationalism and skepticism. Empiricists argue that empiricism is a more reliable method of finding the truth than relying purely on logical reasoning, because humans have cognitive biases and limitations which lead to errors of judgement. Empiricism emphasizes the central role of empirical evidence in the formation of ideas, rather than innate ideas or traditions. Empiricists may argue that traditions (or customs) arise due to relations of previous sensory experiences. Historically, empiricism was associated with the \"blank slate\" concept (tabula rasa), according to which the human mind is \"blank\" at birth and develops its thoughts only through later experience."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "study", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1266, "target_cluster_name": "study", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical knowledge, derived from observation and experimentation, is the foundation upon which many studies are built.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Empirical knowledge, derived from observation and experimentation, is the foundation upon which many studies are built.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "scientific experiment", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 873, "target_cluster_name": "experiment", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Experiencia is the historical term for empirical knowledge, which is directly derived from scientific experiment.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Experiencia is the historical term for empirical knowledge, which is directly derived from scientific experiment."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Murta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "murta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "murta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["murta"], "contexts": ["plant for comparison", "leaves similar to Galanga's"]}, {"entity_id": "murta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Murta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 18, "variants": ["murta", "Murta"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a wine decoction", "leaves used in a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "murta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Murta", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 1691, "stable_key": "clu_38c699f3cbce1484", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Myrtle", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myrtus communis", "family": "Myrtaceae", "description": "A flowering shrub with aromatic leaves and berries.", "wikipedia_extract": "Myrtus communis, the common myrtle or true myrtle, is a species of flowering plant in the myrtle family Myrtaceae. It is an evergreen shrub native to southern Europe, North Africa, Western Asia, Macaronesia, and the Indian subcontinent, and also cultivated. The plant is an evergreen shrub or small tree, growing to 5 metres (16 ft) tall. The leaves are 2–5 centimetres (1–2 in) long, with a fragrant essential oil. The flowers are white or tinged with pink, with five petals and many stamens that protrude from the flower. The fruit is an edible berry, blue-black when ripe."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Laurel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 470, "target_cluster_name": "Laurel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Bay Laurel and Myrtle are evergreen shrubs/trees native to the Mediterranean region, often associated with aromatic foliage and historical significance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Bay Laurel and Myrtle are evergreen shrubs/trees native to the Mediterranean region, often associated with aromatic foliage and historical significance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mastick", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3244, "target_cluster_name": "Mastick", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both mastic and myrtle are Mediterranean shrubs with aromatic properties, often found in similar ecological niches and historically used in perfumery and medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both mastic and myrtle are Mediterranean shrubs with aromatic properties, often found in similar ecological niches and historically used in perfumery and medicine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "avacari", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "herpas contra as cantaras 17 cega. Ha também nesta ilha uma arvore pequena, e porém de maior cantidade que estoutra frutiçe; tem as folhas e a flor como murta, e dá a fruta como murtinhos, e do mesmo ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for a plant with leaves and flowers like myrtle"}]}, {"canonical_name": "chumbo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "chumbo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "chumbo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["chumbo"], "contexts": ["lead, a metal", "lead"]}, {"entity_id": "chumbo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "chumbo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 22, "variants": ["chumbo", "Chumbo", "chumbo queymado"], "contexts": ["mineral substance, lead", "lead used in sugar preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "chumbo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "chumbo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1692, "stable_key": "clu_8c14562204011416", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lead", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "description": "A heavy, soft, bluish-grey metal, known for its toxicity.", "note": "Includes 'chumbo' (Portuguese/Spanish for lead) and alchemical references.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lead ( ) is a chemical element with the symbol Pb (from the Latin plumbum) and atomic number 82. It is a heavy metal, denser than most common materials. Lead is soft, malleable, and has a relatively low melting point. When freshly cut or melted, it appears shiny silvery with a bluish tint, but tarnishes to dull gray on exposure to air. Lead has the highest atomic number of any stable element, and three of its isotopes are endpoints of major nuclear decay chains of heavier elements. Lead is a relatively unreactive post-transition metal."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Litharge", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 775, "target_cluster_name": "Lithargyro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Litharge is a lead oxide, a compound derived from lead.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Litharge is a lead oxide, a compound derived from lead."}, {"found_name": "Quicksilver", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 559, "target_cluster_name": "azougue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quicksilver (mercury) and lead were both considered fundamental metals in alchemy and often discussed together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quicksilver (mercury) and lead were both considered fundamental metals in alchemy and often discussed together."}, {"found_name": "Antimony", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 40, "target_cluster_name": "Antimonio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antimony and lead were both heavy metals with significant alchemical and medicinal uses, often discussed in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antimony and lead were both heavy metals with significant alchemical and medicinal uses, often discussed in relation to each other."}, {"found_name": "Cinnabar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3194, "target_cluster_name": "cinabrio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cinnabar is a mineral form of mercury sulfide, and both mercury and lead were key substances in alchemy and early chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cinnabar is a mineral form of mercury sulfide, and both mercury and lead were key substances in alchemy and early chemistry."}, {"found_name": "Vitriol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 564, "target_cluster_name": "Vitriolo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vitriol (sulfuric acid or sulfates) was often discussed alongside metallic substances like lead in alchemical and medicinal contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vitriol (sulfuric acid or sulfates) was often discussed alongside metallic substances like lead in alchemical and medicinal contexts."}, {"found_name": "Metal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 87, "target_cluster_name": "metal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lead is a type of metal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lead is a type of metal."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Caldas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "calda", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "calda", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["calda", "Caldo", "caldo"], "contexts": ["quality of temperature", "hot quality"]}, {"entity_id": "caldas", "book_id": "polyanthea_med