ption": "The principal part of a country or continent, as distinguished from islands.", "note": "Refers to the continental landmass in the Americas, as opposed to islands. Historical Spanish term for the northern coast of South America.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mainland is defined as \"relating to or forming the main part of a country or continent, not including the islands around it [regardless of status under territorial jurisdiction by an entity].\" The term is often politically, economically and/or demographically more significant than politically associated remote territories, such as exclaves or oceanic islands situated outside the continental shelf. In geography, \"mainland\" can denote the continental part of any polity or the main island within an island nation. In geopolitics, \"mainland\" is sometimes used interchangeably with terms like metropole as an antonym to overseas territories. In the sense of \"heartland\", mainland is the opposite of periphery. In some language a separate concept of \"mainland\" is missing and is replaced with a \"continental portion\". The term is relative: in Tasmania, continental Australia is the mainland, while to residents of Flinders Island, the main island of Tasmania is also \"the mainland\", though the geological Australian continent includes all the former plus the island of New Guinea and all the smaller islands (e.g. the Torres Strait Islands) in between."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Continent", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 25, "target_cluster_name": "continent", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Continent' is a direct synonym for 'Mainland' when referring to the primary landmass of a region, as opposed to islands.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The term 'Continent' is a direct synonym for 'Mainland' when referring to the primary landmass of a region, as opposed to islands."}, {"found_name": "Africa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3, "target_cluster_name": "Africa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Africa, as a continent, is a prime example of a 'Mainland' in geographical and historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Africa, as a continent, is a prime example of a 'Mainland' in geographical and historical contexts."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Europe, as a continent, is a 'Mainland' in contrast to islands like Great Britain or Ireland.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Europe, as a continent, is a 'Mainland' in contrast to islands like Great Britain or Ireland."}]}, {"canonical_name": "blue", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "blue", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "blue", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["blue"], "contexts": ["elementary quality of sensation", "a simple quality of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "blue", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "blue", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 16, "variants": ["blue"], "contexts": ["colour in the spectrum", "color reflected by blue cloth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "blue", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "blue", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1762, "stable_key": "clu_e713e986f7c330ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "blue", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the color blue as a quality of sensation or as part of the spectrum, not a specific chemical substance in this context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue", "wikipedia_extract": "Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model (traditional colour theory). It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Indigo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 733, "target_cluster_name": "indigo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Indigo is a specific shade of blue, often considered a distinct color in historical contexts, particularly in discussions of pigments and dyes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Indigo is a specific shade of blue, often considered a distinct color in historical contexts, particularly in discussions of pigments and dyes."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Iceland spar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "iceland_spar", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Iceland spar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Iceland spar"], "contexts": ["small fragment of Iceland spar"]}, {"entity_id": "iceland_spar", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Iceland spar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 19, "variants": ["Iceland Spar", "Iceland spar"], "contexts": ["double refraction properties", "pure form of calcite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Iceland spar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Iceland spar", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1763, "stable_key": "clu_75a5d7c60dc1ae7e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Iceland spar", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q974568", "wikidata_description": "transparent variety of calcite", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iceland_spar", "wikipedia_extract": "Iceland spar, formerly called Iceland crystal (Icelandic: silfurberg [ˈsɪlvʏrˌpɛrk], lit. 'silver-rock') and also called optical calcite, is a transparent variety of calcite, a crystallized calcium carbonate, originally brought from Iceland and used in demonstrating the polarization of light. Iceland spar is a colourless, transparent variety of calcium carbonate (CaCO3). It crystallizes in the trigonal system, typically forming rhombohedral crystals. It has a Mohs hardness of 3 and exhibits double refraction, splitting a ray of light into two rays that travel at different speeds and directions. Iceland spar forms in sedimentary environments, mainly limestone and dolomite rocks, but also in hydrothermal veins and evaporite deposits."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "quartz", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 323, "target_cluster_name": "quartz", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, which is chemically similar to quartz (calcium carbonate vs. silicon dioxide) and often discussed alongside other minerals in geological and mineralogical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, which is chemically similar to quartz (calcium carbonate vs. silicon dioxide) and often discussed alongside other minerals in geological and mineralogical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Crystal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 696, "target_cluster_name": "Crystal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, a mineral composed of calcium carbonate. Both are crystalline substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, a mineral composed of calcium carbonate. Both are crystalline substances.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lime", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 862, "target_cluster_name": "Lime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, a mineral form of calcium carbonate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, a mineral form of calcium carbonate.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "marmo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1062, "target_cluster_name": "marmo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, the primary mineral in marble.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite, the primary mineral in marble.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "glass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1575, "target_cluster_name": "glass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar (a form of calcite) was a transparent mineral studied for its optical properties, and glass was sometimes compared to it or used in experiments involving similar optical phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iceland spar (a form of calcite) was a transparent mineral studied for its optical properties, and glass was sometimes compared to it or used in experiments involving similar optical phenomena.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "calcite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3140, "target_cluster_name": "calcareous spar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Iceland spar is a transparent variety of calcite."}, {"found_name": "calcareous formations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1162, "target_cluster_name": "formations calcaires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iceland spar is a variety of calcite, which is calcium carbonate, a component of calcareous formations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Iceland spar is a variety of calcite, which is calcium carbonate, a component of calcareous formations."}]}, {"canonical_name": "goma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "goma", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "goma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["goma", "Goma", "gomas"], "contexts": ["gum or resin", "gum from a certain herb"]}, {"entity_id": "goma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "goma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 14, "variants": ["goma", "gomma"], "contexts": ["resin from a plant", "substance extracted from tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "goma", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "goma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1764, "stable_key": "clu_5dd57783707efc5f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "goma", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'goma' (gum/resin) in early modern texts often refers to a class of substances from various plants rather than a single specific species. Further context from the source texts would be needed for a more precise identification."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "resin", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2624, "target_cluster_name": "resina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Goma is a general term that can refer to various sticky, viscous substances, including resins, which are often derived from plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Goma is a general term that can refer to various sticky, viscous substances, including resins, which are often derived from plants."}]}, {"canonical_name": "melancholicos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "melancholico", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Melancholico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Melancholico"], "contexts": ["humoral quality, melancholic"]}, {"entity_id": "melancholicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "melancholicos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 19, "variants": ["melancholicos", "Melancholicos", "melancholico", "melancolico"], "contexts": ["people with melancholy", "afflictions of melancholic nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Melancholico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "melancholicos", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 1765, "stable_key": "clu_937b6b7595d18aa4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "melancholic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Relating to or characterized by melancholy; a humoral imbalance.", "note": "Refers to both a temperament and a humoral state.", "wikipedia_extract": "Melancholia or melancholy (Ancient Greek: μελαγχολία, romanized: melancholía; from μέλαινα χολή, mélaina cholḗ, 'black bile') is a concept found throughout ancient, medieval, and premodern medicine in Europe that describes a condition characterized by markedly depressed mood, bodily complaints, and sometimes hallucinations and delusions. Besides a pathological condition, melancholy could also refer to a mood or temperament and at times it was even used as a description of the human condition in general. Melancholy (or more precisely the 'black bile', from which melancholy derives its name) was regarded as one of the four temperaments matching the four humours. Until the 18th century, doctors and other scholars classified melancholic conditions as such by their perceived common cause – an excess of a notional fluid known as \"black bile\", which was commonly linked to the spleen. Hippocrates and other ancient physicians described melancholia as a distinct disease with mental and physical symptoms, including persistent fears and despondencies, poor appetite, abulia, sleeplessness, irritability, and agitation. Later, fixed delusions were added by Galen and other physicians to the list of symptoms."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cholericas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2858, "target_cluster_name": "cholericas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Choleric is one of the four humors/temperaments, alongside melancholic, sanguine, and phlegmatic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Choleric is one of the four humors/temperaments, alongside melancholic, sanguine, and phlegmatic.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cinza", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "cinza", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cinza", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["cinza"], "contexts": ["remains after burning", "used by jogues, applied to forehead and chest"]}, {"entity_id": "cinza", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cinza", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 17, "variants": ["Cinza", "cinza", "cinza de chumbo", "cinzadavide", "cinzade vides", "cinzados", "cinzas"], "contexts": ["ash used for cleaning", "ash used as remedy"]}, {"entity_id": "ashes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "ashes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ashes"], "contexts": ["residue after burning", "residue from burning"]}, {"entity_id": "ashes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ashes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["ashes", "Ashes"], "contexts": ["of the bark of the Ash", "part of borrage used boiled"]}, {"entity_id": "ashes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ashes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["ashes"], "contexts": ["columns from volcanic eruptions", "volcanic material ejected"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cinza", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cinza", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 1766, "stable_key": "clu_945b483cb581c33d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ash", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The contexts suggest multiple meanings: residue after burning, bark of the Ash tree, and volcanic ash."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Powder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1120, "target_cluster_name": "Powder", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ash is the powdery residue left after the burning of a substance, and is a common example of a