ravanti would have been aware of and potentially built upon the knowledge transmitted through figures like Averroes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant medical scholars whose works were influential in European medical circles during the Renaissance, though Averroes was from an earlier period and Islamic tradition. Fioravanti would have been aware of and potentially built upon the knowledge transmitted through figures like Averroes."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was the dominant medical authority for centuries leading up to and during Fioravanti's time. Fioravanti, as a physician, would have been deeply engaged with, and likely critiquing or building upon, Galenic principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was the dominant medical authority for centuries leading up to and during Fioravanti's time. Fioravanti, as a physician, would have been deeply engaged with, and likely critiquing or building upon, Galenic principles."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the father of Western medicine. Like Galen, his foundational texts and principles were central to medical education and practice during Fioravanti's era, making him a crucial intellectual predecessor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates is considered the father of Western medicine. Like Galen, his foundational texts and principles were central to medical education and practice during Fioravanti's era, making him a crucial intellectual predecessor."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mercurialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "mercurialis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mercurialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mercurialis"], "contexts": ["author of Gymnasticks"]}, {"entity_id": "mercurialis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mercurialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 18, "variants": ["Mercurialis", "Mercurialis,"], "contexts": ["confirms the influence of images during conception", "consil."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mercurialis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mercurialis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1771, "stable_key": "clu_44b2a601390ceaef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Joannes Messis Mercurialis", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1534, "death_year": 1614, "description": "Italian physician, anatomist, and botanist, known for works on gymnastics.", "note": "While the texts mention 'Mercurialis' and a connection to conception, the primary association in early modern texts is with Joannes Messis Mercurialis, an Italian physician and scholar."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mercurial", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 622, "target_cluster_name": "Mercurial", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the same individual, as indicated by the shared surname and the context of historical figures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the same individual, as indicated by the shared surname and the context of historical figures.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Andreas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1395, "target_cluster_name": "Andreas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Joannes Messis Mercurialis was a prominent physician and medical historian of the same era as Vesalius, contributing to the broader medical landscape.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Joannes Messis Mercurialis was a prominent physician and medical historian of the same era as Vesalius, contributing to the broader medical landscape.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Girolamo Mercuriale", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 622, "target_cluster_name": "Mercurial", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a variant spelling of the same name, referring to the same individual.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "This is a variant spelling of the same name, referring to the same individual."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Somno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "sleep", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "sleep", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sleep", "sleep"], "contexts": ["state procured by a remedy", "lack of it helped by herb application"]}, {"entity_id": "somno", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Somno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 14, "variants": ["Somno", "Somnoque", "somno"], "contexts": ["sleep, a symptom or condition", "sleep"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "sleep", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Somno", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 1772, "stable_key": "clu_17d913be9bff2fd7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sleep", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A natural periodic state of rest for the body and mind.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sleep is a state of reduced mental and physical activity in which consciousness is altered and certain sensory activity is inhibited. During sleep, there is a marked decrease in muscle activity and interactions with the surrounding environment. While sleep differs from wakefulness in terms of the ability to react to stimuli, it still involves active brain patterns, making it more reactive than a coma or disorders of consciousness. Sleep occurs in repeating periods, during which the body alternates between two distinct modes: rapid eye movement sleep (REM) and non-REM sleep. Although REM stands for \"rapid eye movement\", this mode of sleep has many other aspects, including virtual paralysis of the body. Dreams are a succession of images, ideas, emotions, and sensations that usually occur involuntarily in the mind during certain stages of sleep."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Rest", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 178, "target_cluster_name": "rest", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sleep is a form of rest.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sleep is a form of rest."}]}, {"canonical_name": "humores frios", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "cold_humors", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "cold humors", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["cold humors", "cold Humors", "cold Joynts", "cold Rhewm"], "contexts": ["cause of convulsions and cramps", "humors distilling from the brain"]}, {"entity_id": "humores_frios", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humores frios", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 15, "variants": ["humores frios", "humores frigidi"], "contexts": ["cold humors affecting nerves", "body fluids causing sudden paralysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "cold humors", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humores frios", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 1773, "stable_key": "clu_35dc53f8de450181", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cold humors", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Bodily fluids believed to cause illness and affect nerves.", "note": "A concept from humoral theory in ancient and medieval medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek χυμός, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (doṣas), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pañca-bhūta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540–500 BC)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "humers", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3925, "target_cluster_name": "humers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct translation and specific type of humor ('cold humors') within the humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a direct translation and specific type of humor ('cold humors') within the humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fangue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1566, "target_cluster_name": "fangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Blood was associated with the cold humors in humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Blood was associated with the cold humors in humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Phlegm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2513, "target_cluster_name": "phlegm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phlegm was considered one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside blood, yellow bile, and black bile. Cold humors would be associated with phlegm.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Phlegm was considered one of the four cardinal humors in humoral theory, alongside blood, yellow bile, and black bile. Cold humors would be associated with phlegm."}, {"found_name": "humores melancolicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1814, "target_cluster_name": "humores melancolicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melancholic humors were often associated with cold and dry qualities, making 'cold humors' a related concept within the broader humoral theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Melancholic humors were often associated with cold and dry qualities, making 'cold humors' a related concept within the broader humoral theory.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Man", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "man", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Man", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 15, "variants": ["Man", "man"], "contexts": ["human agent in selection processes", "human agent in domestication and selection"]}, {"entity_id": "man", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Man", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["man", "Man"], "contexts": ["human species", "human being"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Man", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Man", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1774, "stable_key": "clu_b8a6091a78a2fc92", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humanity", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Trichechus manatus", "family": "Trichechidae", "description": "Large, aquatic marine mammal with paddle-like flippers.", "note": "The cluster includes references to 'Manatee', 'manaiis', and 'Man', where 'Man' in the context of 'origin_of_species' likely refers to human agency in selection, not the animal itself. However, 'Manatee' and 'manaiis' are direct references to the animal.", "wikipedia_extract": "The West Indian manatee (Trichechus manatus), also known as the North American manatee, is a large, aquatic mammal native to warm coastal areas of the Caribbean, from the Eastern United States to northern Brazil. Living alone or in herds, it feeds on underwater plants and uses its whiskers to navigate. It is divided into two subspecies, the Florida manatee (T. m. latirostris) in the United States and the Antillean manatee (T. m. manatus) in the Caribbean, both of which are endangered and face pressure from habitat loss, pollution, and other human activity. The West Indian manatee is the largest living member of the sirenians (order Sirenia), a group of large aquatic mammals that includes the dugong, other manatees, and the extinct Steller's sea cow. Manatees are herbivores, have developed vocal communication abilities, and are covered in highly sensitive whiskers (called vibrissae) that are used for feeding and navigation. In breeding season, several males form mating herds around an individual female; on average, one calf is born to a female manatee every two to three years."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animal life", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2437, "target_cluster_name": "animal life", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The West Indian manatee is a form of animal life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The West Indian manatee is a form of animal life."}, {"found_name": "Marine animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1340, "target_cluster_name": "Marine animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The West Indian manatee is a marine animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The West Indian manatee is a marine animal."}, {"found_name": "Marine fauna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3997, "target_cluster_name": "marine fauna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The West Indian manatee is part of marine fauna.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The West Indian manatee is part of marine fauna."}]}, {"canonical_name": "arterias", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "arteries", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "arteries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["arteries"], "contexts": ["blood vessels carrying blood away from heart"]}, {"entity_id": "arterias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "arterias", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 18, "variants": ["arterias", "Arterias"], "contexts": ["arteries", "blood vessels"]}, {"entity_id": "arteries", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Arteries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["arteries", "Arteries"], "contexts": ["convey spirits into brain", "structures formed in embryonic development"]}, {"entity_id": "arteries", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Arteries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Arteries"], "contexts": ["melancholy vapors expelled from", "body part helped by oil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "arteries", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "arterias", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 1775, "stable_key": "clu_7daaeab016d1ff28", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Artery", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the anatomical structure, not a specific disease or condition.", "wikidata_id": "Q14890445", "wikidata_description": "The process in which the anatomical structures of arterial blood vessels are generated and organized. Arteries are blood vessels that transport blood from the heart to the body and its organs."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "heart", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 85, "target_cluster_name": "heart", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nerves", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 137, "target_cluster_name": "nerves", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart, and are often discussed alongside nerves in anatomical contexts, though they are distinct structures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart, and are often discussed alongside nerves in anatomical contexts, though they are distinct structures.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "veas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 525, "target_cluster_name": "veas", "source_b