rmed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "falling stars", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2314, "target_cluster_name": "falling stars", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "periods of revolution, more correctly termed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling stars). Their planetary velocity, magnitude", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for aerolites", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fire-balls", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "Jupiter's satellites. Comets of short periods of revolution, more correctly termed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling stars", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym of meteoric stones"}, {"found_name": "falling stars", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "Jupiter's satellites. Comets of short periods of revolution, more correctly termed inner comets (Encke, Biela, Faye) -- p. 107 and note. Revolving aerolites (meteoric stones, fire-balls, falling stars", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym of meteoric stones"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "halley", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 11, "variants": ["Halley"], "contexts": ["discovered comet with long period", "pronounced fire-ball of 1686 a cosmical body"]}, {"entity_id": "halley", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Halley"], "contexts": ["computed comet orbit elements", "associated with the comet's name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Halley", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Halley", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1784, "stable_key": "clu_56641df13be70c95", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edmond Halley", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1656, "death_year": 1742, "description": "English astronomer, mathematician, and physicist known for Halley's Comet.", "wikidata_id": "Q19059026", "wikidata_description": "street in Amsterdam, the Netherlands"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Isaac Newton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 71, "target_cluster_name": "Newton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Edmond Halley was a close friend and collaborator of Isaac Newton, famously funding the publication of Newton's Principia Mathematica and providing crucial astronomical data for it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Edmond Halley was a close friend and collaborator of Isaac Newton, famously funding the publication of Newton's Principia Mathematica and providing crucial astronomical data for it."}, {"found_name": "James Bradley", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 974, "target_cluster_name": "Bradley", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "James Bradley was Astronomer Royal after Halley and continued much of the observational work Halley had initiated.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "James Bradley was Astronomer Royal after Halley and continued much of the observational work Halley had initiated."}, {"found_name": "Christopher Columbus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 327, "target_cluster_name": "Columbus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, though in different domains (astronomy/navigation vs. trans-Atlantic voyages).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, though in different domains (astronomy/navigation vs. trans-Atlantic voyages)."}, {"found_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3017, "target_cluster_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, with Vespucci lending his name to the Americas and Halley contributing significantly to navigation and cartography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, with Vespucci lending his name to the Americas and Halley contributing significantly to navigation and cartography."}]}, {"canonical_name": "espicanardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "espiquenardo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "espiquenardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["espiquenardo", "ESPIQUENARDO", "Espiquernardo"], "contexts": ["plant with a strong smell", "plant with a strong scent, likely spikenard"]}, {"entity_id": "espicanardo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "espicanardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["espicanardo", "Eſpicanardo", "efpicanardo", "eſpicanardo"], "contexts": ["spikenard used in decoction", "spice used in a syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "espiquenardo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espicanardo", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 1785, "stable_key": "clu_b6009061b5f708f8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spikenard", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "An aromatic herb or shrub, used in perfumery and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Spikenard, also called nard, nardin, and muskroot, is a class of aromatic amber-colored essential oil derived from Nardostachys jatamansi, a flowering plant in the honeysuckle family which grows in the Himalayas of Nepal, China, and India. The oil has been used over centuries as a perfume, a traditional medicine, or in religious ceremonies across a wide territory from India to Europe. Historically, the name nard has also referred to essential oils derived from other species including the closely related valerian genus, as well as Spanish lavender; these cheaper, more common plants have been used in perfume-making, and sometimes to adulterate true spikenard. The name nard is derived from Latin nardus, from Ancient Greek νάρδος (nárdos), from  Hebrew: נֵרְדְּ (nērd). This word may ultimately derive either from Sanskrit नलद (nálada 'Indian spikenard'), or from Naarda, an ancient Assyrian city (possibly the modern town of Dohuk, Iraq). The \"spike\" in the English name refers to the inflorescence or flowering stem of the plant."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "beldroegas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "beldroega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "beldroega", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["beldroega", "beldroegas"], "contexts": ["plant name, purslane", "plant with leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "beldroegas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "beldroegas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["beldroegas"], "contexts": ["plant used in a syrup", "plant used in decoctions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "beldroega", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "beldroegas", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 1786, "stable_key": "clu_e674fbdd5d53b87b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purslane", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Portulaca oleracea", "family": "Portulacaceae", "description": "Edible plant with succulent leaves, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Portulaca oleracea (common purslane, also known as little hogweed, or pursley) is a succulent plant in the family Portulacaceae. All parts of the plant are edible raw or cooked. Purslane grows from a taproot with fibrous secondary roots. The plant may reach 40 centimetres (16 inches) in height. It has smooth, reddish, mostly prostrate stems, and the leaves, which may be alternate or opposite, are clustered at stem joints and ends. The yellow flowers have five regular parts and are up to 6 millimetres (1⁄4 inch) wide."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "espinhela", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["espinhela", "espinela"], "contexts": ["a type of ruby, reddish", "mineral species"]}, {"entity_id": "espinhela", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Espinhela", "Espinbela", "Espinillam", "espinbela", "espinhela"], "contexts": ["body part discussed", "cartilage or soft bone at the end of the chest"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "espinhela", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Espinhela", "similarity": 0.396}], "id": 1787, "stable_key": "clu_0790f735fa20698a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spinel", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Early modern texts often used 'espinhela' or 'spinellane' to refer to spinel, a gemstone.", "wikidata_id": "Q191310", "wikidata_description": "oxide mineral", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinel", "wikipedia_extract": "Spinel () is the magnesium/aluminium member of the larger spinel group of minerals. It has the formula MgAl2O4 in the cubic crystal system. Its name comes from the Latin word spinella, a diminutive form of spine, in reference to its pointed crystals. Spinel crystallizes in the isometric system; common crystal forms are octahedra, usually twinned. It has no true cleavage, but shows an octahedral parting and a conchoidal fracture. Its hardness is 8, its specific gravity is 3.5–4.1, and it is transparent to opaque with a vitreous to dull luster."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "incenfo macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "encenço_macho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "encenço macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["encenço macho"], "contexts": ["male incense", "a plant called male incense"]}, {"entity_id": "incenfo_macho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "incenfo macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["incenfo macho", "Incenso macho", "Incenſo macho", "incenso macho", "incenſo macho", "pòde incenſo macho"], "contexts": ["male incense", "male incense (likely frankincense)"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "encenço macho", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "incenfo macho", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 1788, "stable_key": "clu_a3b91eeaf52dc87e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Frankincense", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Resinous tree producing aromatic incense, also used medicinally.", "note": "The term 'incenfo macho' or 'encenço macho' likely refers to frankincense, derived from Boswellia species. It could also refer to a plant with similar aromatic properties used as a substitute.", "wikipedia_extract": "Frankincense, also known as olibanum (), is an aromatic resin used in incense and perfume, obtained from trees of the genus Boswellia in the family Burseraceae. The word is from Old French franc encens ('high-quality incense'). There are several species of Boswellia that produce  frankincense, including, but not limited to, Boswellia sacra (syn. B. bhaw-dajiana, syn. B. carteri), B. frereana, B. serrata (B. thurifera), and B. papyrifera. Resin from each is available in various grades which is affected by the time in which it is harvested."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ventofas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "ventosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ventosas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ventosas"], "contexts": ["medical cups for cupping"]}, {"entity_id": "ventofas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ventofas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 18, "variants": ["Ventofas", "Ventofas ſarjadu", "ventofas", "ventofas farjadas"], "contexts": ["cupping glasses used for bleeding", "devices for drawing blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ventosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ventofas", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 1789, "stable_key": "clu_f646926eff6cec25", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cupping glasses", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Medical instruments used for cupping therapy.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cupping therapy is a pseudoscientific treatment method in which a local suction is created on the skin by using heated cups. As an alternative medicine practice, it is primarily used in Asia, but it is also used in Eastern Europe, the Middle East, and Latin America. There is no conclusive evidence supporting the claimed health benefits of cupping, and critics have characterized the practice as quackery. Cupping practitioners attempt to use cupping therapy for a wide array of medical conditions including fevers, chronic low back pain, poor appetite, indigestion, high blood pressure, acne, atopic dermatitis, psoriasis, anemia, stroke rehabilitation, nasal congestion, infertility, and menstrual period cramping. Despite the numerous ailments for which practitioners claim cupping therapy is useful, there is insufficient evidence demonstrating any health benefits. Cupping is generally not harmful for most people."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Glass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 22, "target_cluster_name": "Glass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cupping glasses were often made of glass and used in medical treatments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cupping glasses were often made of glass and used in medical treatments.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cup", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1221, "target_cluster_name": "Cup", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cupping glasses are a specific type of cup used in a medical procedure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cupping glasses are a specific type of cup used in a medical procedure."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Antigos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "antichi", "book_id": "ricet