5, "target_cluster_id": 529, "target_cluster_name": "natural selection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Natural selection can lead to the divergence of character within a population, as different selective pressures favor different traits, potentially leading to speciation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Natural selection can lead to the divergence of character within a population, as different selective pressures favor different traits, potentially leading to speciation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Evolution", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 540, "target_cluster_name": "Evolution", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Divergence of character describes the evolutionary process where populations evolve to be more distinct, often leading to speciation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Divergence of character describes the evolutionary process where populations evolve to be more distinct, often leading to speciation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "character", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 962, "target_cluster_name": "character", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct conceptual subdivision or elaboration of 'Character', indicating variations or differences within the concept itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a direct conceptual subdivision or elaboration of 'Character', indicating variations or differences within the concept itself.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "amygdaloid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 14, "variants": ["amygdaloid", "amygdaloicles", "amygdaloide", "amygdaloides", "amygdaloïde", "amygdaloïdes", "aniygdaloïdes"], "contexts": ["geological formation described as masses", "geological formation, second type"]}, {"entity_id": "amygdaloid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["amygdaloid"], "contexts": ["igneous rock with cavities", "rock type that is shaken by earthquakes"]}, {"entity_id": "amygdaloids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "amygdaloids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["amygdaloids"], "contexts": ["rock texture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "amygdaloid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "amygdaloid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "amygdaloid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "amygdaloids", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 1797, "stable_key": "clu_a51b192066b9c12f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amygdaloid", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Amygdaloid refers to a volcanic rock texture with cavities, often filled with minerals. It's used in geology and mineralogy.", "wikipedia_extract": "The amygdala (; pl.: amygdalae  or amygdalas; also corpus amygdaloideum) is a paired  nuclear complex present in the cerebral hemispheres of vertebrates. It is considered part of the limbic system. In primates, it is located medially within the temporal lobes. It consists of many nuclei, each made up of further subnuclei. The subdivision most commonly made is into the basolateral, central, cortical, and medial nuclei together with the intercalated cell clusters. The amygdala has a primary role in the processing of memory, decision-making, and emotional responses (including fear, anxiety, and aggression).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amygdala"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 49, "target_cluster_name": "brain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The amygdaloid is a nuclear complex within the cerebral hemispheres of the vertebrate brain, specifically located in the temporal lobes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The amygdaloid is a nuclear complex within the cerebral hemispheres of the vertebrate brain, specifically located in the temporal lobes."}, {"found_name": "Conglomérat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2554, "target_cluster_name": "Conglomérat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amygdaloid is a type of igneous rock that is often associated with or found in similar geological contexts as conglomerate rock, particularly in volcanic or extrusive settings. Both are descriptive terms for rock textures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amygdaloid is a type of igneous rock that is often associated with or found in similar geological contexts as conglomerate rock, particularly in volcanic or extrusive settings. Both are descriptive terms for rock textures.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "duperrey", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 13, "variants": ["Duperrey", "M. Duperrey"], "contexts": ["commander of French corvette", "early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "duperrey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Duperrey"], "contexts": ["provided pendulum experiment results", "investigator of magnetic equator changes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Duperrey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Duperrey", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1798, "stable_key": "clu_52ef06e39bf6dbad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Louis Isidore Duperrey", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1786, "death_year": 1865, "description": "French explorer and naval officer, commanded scientific expeditions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 851, "target_cluster_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were French explorers and naturalists who undertook significant scientific expeditions around the same time, often in overlapping regions (e.g., South America).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were French explorers and naturalists who undertook significant scientific expeditions around the same time, often in overlapping regions (e.g., South America)."}, {"found_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3990, "target_cluster_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was a zoologist who, along with his brother Georges Cuvier, was a prominent figure in French natural history during the period of Duperrey's expeditions. They would have been contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Frederick Cuvier was a zoologist who, along with his brother Georges Cuvier, was a prominent figure in French natural history during the period of Duperrey's expeditions. They would have been contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpentine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["Serpentine", "Serpentine de Guanacoa", "serpentine"], "contexts": ["mineral found with weisstein", "serpentine rock, geological material"]}, {"entity_id": "serpentine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["serpentine"], "contexts": ["igneous rock from earth's interior", "rock type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Serpentine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "serpentine", "similarity": 0.744}], "id": 1799, "stable_key": "clu_6d52a1dffed00ac7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Serpentine", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "A succubus (pl. succubi) is a female demon who is described in various European folklore as appearing in the dreams of male humans in order to seduce them. Repeated interactions between a succubus and a man will lead to sexual activity, a bond forming between them, and ultimately sexual intercourse, as she requires semen to survive. The establishment and perpetuation of such a relationship enables the production of a hybrid child known as a cambion, but at the expense of the man, whose mental and physical health will deteriorate rapidly, eventually resulting in his death if the succubus continues courting him for a protracted period. In modern representations, a succubus is often depicted as a beautiful woman seductress or charming, rather than as demonic or frightening, to attract people instead of repulsing them. The male counterpart of the succubus is the incubus.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Succubus"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lyell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1710, "target_cluster_name": "Lyell", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "'Plutonic' rocks* (granite, porphyry, and serpentine) and those termed 'volcanic' in the strict sense of the word (as trachyte, basalt, and lava). [footnote] ([Lyell, 'Principales of Geology', vol. i.", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of 'Plutonic' rock", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "lava", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1714, "target_cluster_name": "lava", "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "manifested between 'Plutonic' rocks* (granite, porphyry, and serpentine) and those termed 'volcanic' in the strict sense of the word (as trachyte, basalt, and lava). [footnote] ([Lyell, 'Principales o", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of 'Plutonic' rock", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "mitscherlich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 15, "variants": ["Mitscherlich"], "contexts": ["researcher on melting points", "author of 'Lehrbuch der Chemie'"]}, {"entity_id": "mitscherlich", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mitscherlich"], "contexts": ["experimenter on crystallisation and water", "scientist who studied crystal expansion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mitscherlich", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mitscherlich", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1800, "stable_key": "clu_66883dfbdef5acc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eilhard Mitscherlich", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1794, "death_year": 1863, "description": "German chemist, discovered isomorphism and laws of chemical proportion.", "wikidata_id": "Q126219257", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Eilhard Mitscherlich was a student and collaborator of Alexander von Humboldt, and his work on isomerism was influenced by Humboldt's ideas on chemical affinity and the unity of nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Eilhard Mitscherlich was a student and collaborator of Alexander von Humboldt, and his work on isomerism was influenced by Humboldt's ideas on chemical affinity and the unity of nature."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "thibet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Thibet"], "contexts": ["influence of elevated plateau", "elevated plateaux"]}, {"entity_id": "thibet", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Thibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 11, "variants": ["Thibet"], "contexts": ["region musk arrived through", "region in Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thibet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Thibet", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1801, "stable_key": "clu_d1485e52c9082060", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tibet", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in Asia, also including an island.", "note": "Includes references to Tibet and Tahiti.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tibet ( ) is a region in the western part of East Asia, covering much of the Tibetan Plateau. It is the homeland of the Tibetans. Other ethnic groups also reside on the plateau, including Mongols, the Lhoba, Monpa, Qiang, Sherpa, and since the 20th century, Han and Hui peoples. Tibet is the highest region on Earth, with an average elevation of 4,380 m (14,000 ft). Lying within the Himalayas, the highest point in Tibet is Mount Everest – the highest peak on Earth, standing 8,848 m (29,000 ft) above sea level. In the 7th century, the Yarlung dynasty expanded into surrounding areas such as Amdo."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "electro-magnetism", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["electro-magnetism"], "contexts": ["branch of physics concerning electric and magnetic forces", "knowledge of electro-magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electro-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electro-magnetic"], "contexts": ["relating electricity and magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetism", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["electro-magnetism", "Electro-Magnetism"], "contexts": ["magnetism produced by electric currents", "theory of electricity and magnetism interaction"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electro-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electro-magnetic"], "contexts": ["relating to electricity and magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity_and_magnetism", "book_id": "