 ["times of equal day and night", "points of equal day and night"]}, {"entity_id": "equinox", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equinox", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["equinox"], "contexts": ["point where ecliptic and celestial equator intersect", "celestial point for measuring time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "equinoxes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "equinoxes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "equinoxes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "equinox", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 1803, "stable_key": "clu_fb7583fb617493eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "equinox", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1347837", "wikidata_description": "implementation of the OSGi framework"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ecliptic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1541, "target_cluster_name": "ecliptic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The equinox is the point where the ecliptic intersects the celestial equator. The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky over the course of a year, and the equinoxes are two points on this path.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The equinox is the point where the ecliptic intersects the celestial equator. The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun across the sky over the course of a year, and the equinoxes are two points on this path."}, {"found_name": "Aries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 787, "target_cluster_name": "Aries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The vernal equinox is traditionally located in the constellation Aries, and the Sun is in Aries at the time of the vernal equinox.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The vernal equinox is traditionally located in the constellation Aries, and the Sun is in Aries at the time of the vernal equinox."}, {"found_name": "Chronology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2825, "target_cluster_name": "Chronology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The equinoxes are important markers in chronological systems, particularly in the establishment of calendars and the measurement of time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The equinoxes are important markers in chronological systems, particularly in the establishment of calendars and the measurement of time."}, {"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The equinox is a fundamental concept in astronomy, relating to the Earth's position and movement in relation to the Sun and the celestial sphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The equinox is a fundamental concept in astronomy, relating to the Earth's position and movement in relation to the Sun and the celestial sphere."}, {"found_name": "Latitude", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 551, "target_cluster_name": "latitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The equinoxes are points where the celestial equator intersects the ecliptic. The celestial equator is defined by the Earth's latitude, and its intersection with the ecliptic is crucial for understanding celestial coordinates and phenomena like the equinox.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The equinoxes are points where the celestial equator intersects the ecliptic. The celestial equator is defined by the Earth's latitude, and its intersection with the ecliptic is crucial for understanding celestial coordinates and phenomena like the equinox."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alepo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "aleppo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aleppo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aleppo"], "contexts": ["major city in Syria", "Pachalik where earthquakes occurred"]}, {"entity_id": "alepo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alepo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Alepo", "Aleppo"], "contexts": ["principal city of Syria", "trading city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Aleppo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alepo", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 1804, "stable_key": "clu_44049b3f558b81e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aleppo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Syria", "wikidata_id": "Q211457", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Aleppo_(2012–2016)", "wikipedia_extract": "The Battle of Aleppo (Arabic: مَعْرَكَةُ حَلَبَ, romanized: Maʿrakat Ḥalab) was a major military confrontation in Aleppo, the largest city in Syria, between the Syrian opposition militias (such as the Free Syrian Army (FSA), the Levant Front and the al-Nusra Front) against the Ba'athist Syrian government, supported by Hezbollah, IRGC-backed Shia militant groups, Iran, and Russia, and against the Kurdish-majority People's Protection Units (YPG). The battle began on 19 July 2012 and was part of the Syrian Civil War. A stalemate that had been in place for four years finally ended in July 2016, when Ba'athist government troops closed the rebels' last supply line into Aleppo with the support of Russian airstrikes. In response, rebel forces launched unsuccessful counteroffensives in September and October that failed to break the siege; in November, government forces embarked on a decisive campaign that resulted in the recapture of all of Aleppo by December 2016. The Syrian government victory was widely seen as a turning point in Syria's civil war. The large-scale devastation of the battle and its importance led combatants to name it the \"mother of battles\" or \"Syria's Stalingrad\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Syria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1702, "target_cluster_name": "Syria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aleppo is described as the largest city in Syria.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aleppo is described as the largest city in Syria."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Forestus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "forster", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Forster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Forster"], "contexts": ["provided pendulum experiment results"]}, {"entity_id": "forestus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Forestus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 17, "variants": ["Forestus", "Forest", "Forest.", "Foresto"], "contexts": ["early modern author cited", "early modern author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Forster", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Forestus", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1805, "stable_key": "clu_83ba2728a9618621", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pieter van Foreest", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1521, "death_year": 1597, "description": "Dutch physician, 'father of Dutch medicine'.", "note": "The spelling 'Forestus' is a Latinization of his name. 'Forster' is likely an OCR error or variant.", "wikidata_id": "Q2838278", "wikidata_description": "Dutch classical scholar"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1620, "target_cluster_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicians in the 16th century, known for their medical writings and practices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent physicians in the 16th century, known for their medical writings and practices."}, {"found_name": "Andreas Vesalius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1395, "target_cluster_name": "Andreas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential physicians and anatomists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to medical knowledge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were influential physicians and anatomists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to medical knowledge."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pieter van Foreest (Forestus) was a physician who built upon and commented on the medical theories of Galen, a foundational figure in ancient medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pieter van Foreest (Forestus) was a physician who built upon and commented on the medical theories of Galen, a foundational figure in ancient medicine."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pieter van Foreest (Forestus) was a physician who, like many of his time, referenced and engaged with the medical tradition established by Hippocrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pieter van Foreest (Forestus) was a physician who, like many of his time, referenced and engaged with the medical tradition established by Hippocrates."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Higado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "higado", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Higado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Higado", "Higa", "hidago", "higado"], "contexts": ["an organ in the body", "liver organ"]}, {"entity_id": "higado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "higado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["higado"], "contexts": ["organ affected by heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Higado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "higado", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 1806, "stable_key": "clu_6c1c2dfad9fb7ad7-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "liver", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A large organ in the abdomen responsible for many metabolic functions.", "wikipedia_extract": "The liver is a major metabolic organ exclusively found in vertebrates, which performs many essential biological functions such as detoxification of the organism, and the synthesis of various proteins and various other biochemicals necessary for digestion and growth. In humans, it is located in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, below the diaphragm and mostly shielded by the lower right rib cage. Its other metabolic roles include carbohydrate metabolism, the production of a number of hormones, conversion and storage of nutrients such as glucose and glycogen, and the decomposition of red blood cells. Anatomical and medical terminology often use the prefix hepat- from ἡπατο-, from the Greek word for liver, such as hepatology, and hepatitis. The liver is also an accessory digestive organ that produces bile, an alkaline fluid containing cholesterol and bile acids,  which emulsifies and aids the breakdown of dietary fat. The gallbladder, a small hollow pouch that sits just under the right lobe of liver, stores and concentrates the bile produced by the liver, which is later excreted to the duodenum to help with digestion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fegato", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 3747, "target_cluster_name": "fegato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Higado is the Spanish word for liver.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Higado is the Spanish word for liver.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "curcuma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Curcuma", "Curcuma Zedoaria", "Curcuma angusUfolia", "Curcuma longa", "curcuma", "curcumani", "curcumanit meimiram"], "contexts": ["plant related to a root", "plant used for medicine and dyeing"]}, {"entity_id": "curcuma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Curcuma"], "contexts": ["plant believed to be a type of Cippero"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Curcuma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Curcuma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1807, "stable_key": "clu_9d76036ffef1942d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turmeric", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Curcuma longa", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "description": "Plant root used for medicine and dyeing, related to Cippero.", "wikipedia_extract": "Turmeric (), or Curcuma longa (), is a flowering plant in the ginger family Zingiberaceae. It is a perennial, rhizomatous, herbaceous plant native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia that requires temperatures between 20 and 30 °C (68 and 86 °F) and high annual rainfall to thrive. Plants are gathered each year for their rhizomes, some for propagation in the following season and some for consumption or dyeing. The rhizomes can be used fresh, but they are often boiled in water and dried, after which they are ground into a deep orange-yellow shelf-stable spice powder commonly used as a coloring and flavoring agent in many Asian cuisines, especially for curries (curry powder). Turmeric powder has a warm, bitter, black pepper-like flavor and earthy, mustard-like aroma. Although long used in Ayurvedic medicine, there is no high-quality clinical evidence that consuming turmeric or the principal turmeric constituent, curcumin, is effective for treating any disease."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "zedoaria", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 767, "target_cluster_name": "zedoaria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Zedoary is a species within the genus Curcuma. Historical texts often use the genus name to refer to specific species within it, especially when the species is well-known or the primary representative of the genus in a particular context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Zedoary is a species within the genus Curcuma. Historical texts often use the genus name to refer to specific species within it, especially when the species is well-known or the primary representative of the genus in a particular context.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "croco indiaco", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2464, "target_cluster_name": "croco indiaco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The historical name 'croco indiaco' is a direct synonym for Turmeric, and 'Curcuma' is the genus name for Turmeric.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The historical name 'croco indiaco' is a direct synonym for Turmeric, and 'Curcuma' is the genus name for Turmeric.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "galanga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 627, "target_cluster_name": "galanga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal, ginger, and turmeric are all rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, commonly used as spices and in traditional medicine, and are often grouped together in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Galangal, ginger, and turmeric are all rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, commonly used as spices and in traditional medicine, and are often grouped together in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cardamomo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 636, "target_cluster_name": "Cardamomo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Turmeric is also a member of the ginger family (Zingiberaceae) and is frequently mentioned in conjunction with cardamom in historical texts concerning spices and medicinal plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Turmeric is also a member of the ginger family (Zingiberaceae) and is frequently mentioned in conjunction with cardamom in historical texts concerning spices and medicinal plants.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mirabolani", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1647, "target_cluster_name": "Mirabolani", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Citrine Myrobalans and Turmeric are plant-derived substances historically used in dyeing and traditional medicine. They were often found in similar trade routes and pharmacopoeias, leading to associations in historical texts, despite being botanically distinct.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Citrine Myrobalans and Turmeric are plant-derived substances historically used in dyeing and traditional medicine. They were often found in similar trade routes and pharmacopoeias, leading to associations in historical texts, despite being botanically distinct.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2918, "target_cluster_name": "Ginger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ginger and turmeric are closely related rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, often used interchangeably or in conjunction as spices and in traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ginger and turmeric are closely related rhizomatous plants from the Zingiberaceae family, often used interchangeably or in conjunction as spices and in traditional medicine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Crocus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2712, "target_cluster_name": "Crocus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Crocus and Turmeric are plants known for their vibrant colors, with saffron (derived from Crocus sativus) and turmeric (Curcuma longa) being historically significant as dyes and spices. The candidate 'turmeric (aka croco indiaco)' explicitly links turmeric to 'croco', which is a cognate of Crocus, suggesting a historical association or confusion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Crocus and Turmeric are plants known for their vibrant colors, with saffron (derived from Crocus sativus) and turmeric (Curcuma longa) being historically significant as dyes and spices. The candidate 'turmeric (aka croco indiaco)' explicitly links turmeric to 'croco', which is a cognate of Crocus, suggesting a historical association or confusion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Scitaminea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2852, "target_cluster_name": "Scitaminea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Turmeric (Curcuma) is a genus within the Zingiberaceae family, which is part of the Zingiberales order. This represents a specific genus within the broader order.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Turmeric (Curcuma) is a genus within the Zingiberaceae family, which is part of the Zingiberales order. This represents a specific genus within the broader order.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Zerumbet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2877, "target_cluster_name": "Zerumbet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Zerumbet ginger and turmeric are both rhizomatous plants in the Zingiberaceae family, often used for culinary and medicinal purposes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Zerumbet ginger and turmeric are both rhizomatous plants in the Zingiberaceae family, often used for culinary and medicinal purposes.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Deckan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "deckan", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Deckan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 16, "variants": ["Deckan", "Deckan interior"], "contexts": ["region name", "region conquered by a king"]}, {"entity_id": "deccan", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Deccan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Deccan"], "contexts": ["region in India with plant exceptions", "region in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Deckan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Deccan", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 1808, "stable_key": "clu_c6dc6b458068e2fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Deccan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q8354327", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deccania", "wikipedia_extract": "Deccania is a monotypic genus of flowering plants in the family Rubiaceae. The genus contains only one species, viz. Deccania pubescens, which is endemic to India."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Goa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 205, "target_cluster_name": "Goa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Goa is geographically separated from the Deccan highlands by the Western Ghats, indicating a close geographical relationship and distinction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Goa is geographically separated from the Deccan highlands by the Western Ghats, indicating a close geographical relationship and distinction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "India", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 17, "target_cluster_name": "India", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of Deccania pubescens states it is endemic to India. The Deccan is a geographical region within India.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The description of Deccania pubescens states it is endemic to India. The Deccan is a geographical region within India."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "ameixas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["ameixas", "ameixa", "ameixas redondas"], "contexts": ["fruit", "fruit that rots and causes fevers"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["ameixas"], "contexts": ["fruit used in diet", "prunes used in a decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas_pafladas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas pafladas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ameixas pafladas"], "contexts": ["dried plums used in remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas_calfacas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas calfacas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ameixas calfacas"], "contexts": ["prunes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas pafladas", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas calfacas", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 1809, "stable_key": "clu_14a337cc8230d21e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plums/Prunes", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Fruit used in diet, remedies, and known to cause fevers when rotting.", "note": "Includes dried plums ('ameixas pafladas', 'ameixas calfacas').", "wikipedia_extract": "A plum is a fruit of some species in Prunus subg. Prunus. Dried plums are usually called prunes. Plums are likely to have been one of the first fruits domesticated by humans, with origins in Eastern Europe, the Caucasus Mountains and China. They were brought to Britain from Asia, and their cultivation has been documented in Andalusia, southern Spain. Plums are a diverse group of species, with trees reaching a height of 5–6 metres (16–20 ft) when pruned."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "animaes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["animaes"], "contexts": ["animals, source of bezoars", "animals that are washed"]}, {"entity_id": "animaes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 16, "variants": ["animaes", "animal", "animalcula", "animali"], "contexts": ["vital spirits", "animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "animaes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "animaes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1810, "stable_key": "clu_b02c1359b8b4a6af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Living creatures, sources of bezoars, subject to washing.", "note": "Can also refer to 'vital spirits' in a more abstract sense.", "wikipedia_extract": "Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia (). With few exceptions, animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have myocytes and are able to move, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 μm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Animals' is the plural form of the entity 'Animal' and refers to the same biological kingdom.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Animals' is the plural form of the entity 'Animal' and refers to the same biological kingdom."}, {"found_name": "Insects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Insects are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Insects are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Mammals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 266, "target_cluster_name": "mammals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mammals are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mammals are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Reptiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 122, "target_cluster_name": "reptiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Reptiles are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Reptiles are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Birds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 5, "target_cluster_name": "birds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Birds are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Birds are a class within the kingdom Animalia, and thus are a specific type of animal."}, {"found_name": "Fish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 94, "target_cluster_name": "Fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fish are a diverse group of aquatic animals, typically considered within the kingdom Animalia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fish are a diverse group of aquatic animals, typically considered within the kingdom Animalia."}, {"found_name": "Crustaceans", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 348, "target_cluster_name": "crustaceans", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crustaceans are a large group of arthropods, belonging to the kingdom Animalia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crustaceans are a large group of arthropods, belonging to the kingdom Animalia."}, {"found_name": "Mollusks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2101, "target_cluster_name": "molluscs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mollusks are a large phylum of invertebrate animals, belonging to the kingdom Animalia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mollusks are a large phylum of invertebrate animals, belonging to the kingdom Animalia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "salva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["salva"], "contexts": ["plant with a similar taste"]}, {"entity_id": "salva", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Salva", "salva", "salva verde"], "contexts": ["ingredient in fomentations", "herb for head wash"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "salva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Salva", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1811, "stable_key": "clu_4f9937b90c9af231", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sage", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Salvia officinalis", "family": "Lamiaceae", "description": "Herb used in fomentations and for head washes, with a distinct taste.", "wikipedia_extract": "Salvia officinalis, common sage or sage, is a perennial, evergreen subshrub, with woody stems, grayish leaves, and blue to purplish flowers. It is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae) and native to the Mediterranean region, though it has been naturalized in many places throughout the world. It has a long history of culinary use, and in modern times it has been used as an ornamental garden plant. The common name \"sage\" is also used for closely related species and cultivars. Cultivars are quite variable in size, leaf and flower color, and foliage pattern, with many variegated leaf types. The Old World type grows to approximately 60 cm (2 ft) tall and wide, with lavender flowers most common, though they can also be white, pink, or purple."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "salvia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4503, "target_cluster_name": "salvia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity itself is listed with its historical name 'salvia'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity itself is listed with its historical name 'salvia'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "ellipse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "ellipse", "book_id": "connexi