d most populated urban area in the world. The GBA—with a total population of approximately 86 million people—includes nine mega cities of Guangdong province: Guangzhou, Shenzhen, Zhuhai, Foshan, Dongguan, Zhongshan, Jiangmen, Huizhou, and Zhaoqing as well as two special administrative regions, Hong Kong, and Macao (Macau). Hong Kong, Guangzhou, and Shenzhen have been described among the world's 50 \"superstar cities\". Surrounding the Pearl River Delta with a total area of 56,000 km2 (comparable in size to Croatia), it is the largest and the richest economic region in South China.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macau"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tyrol", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "tyrol", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tyrol", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 11, "variants": ["Tyrol", "Tyrol méridional"], "contexts": ["region with schists", "region"]}, {"entity_id": "tyrol", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tyrol", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Tyrol"], "contexts": ["region of Cascade of Conzocoli", "region with dolomite masses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tyrol", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tyrol", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1848, "stable_key": "clu_eb4fdd70ec3208f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tyrol", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Austria", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tyrol–South Tyrol–Trentino Euroregion (German: Europaregion Tirol-Südtirol-Trentino; Italian: Euregio Tirolo-Alto Adige-Trentino) is a Euroregion formed by three different regional authorities in Austria and Italy: the Austrian state of Tyrol (i.e. North and East Tyrol) and the Italian autonomous provinces of South Tyrol and Trentino. The boundaries of the association correspond to the former Princely County of Tyrol, a crown land of the Habsburg monarchy (including the former Prince-bishoprics of Trent and Brixen) which for centuries shaped life in the Alpine region. Excluded from the association due to a change of the province by the fascists, are Cortina, Livinallongo, Colle Santa Lucia, Pedemonte, Valvestino and Magasa, but they have voted in 2007/2008 to revert to the provinces of South Tyrol/Trentino, which has not yet been approved by the Italian legislature in Rome. Divided after World War I, the region retained much of its cultural integrity by its traditionally strong attachment to the land and a profound desire for self-government on both sides of the border. The long-standing cultural, social and economic ties, as much as the recognition of convergent interests based on its traditional role as transit country and its largely identical environmental conditions in the Eastern Alps, led to the creation of the Euroregion by the three provinces in 1998.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrol–South_Tyrol–Trentino_Euroregion"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "déclinaison magnétique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 27, "members": [{"entity_id": "déclinaison_magnétique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "déclinaison magnétique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["déclinaison magnétique", "Inclinaison magnétique", "déclinaison", "déclinaison du soleil", "déclinaisons magnétiques"], "contexts": ["magnetic variation", "magnetic declination"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_declination", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Magnetic declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["magnetic declination", "Magnetic declination"], "contexts": ["variation in magnetic compass direction", "angle between magnetic and geographic north"]}, {"entity_id": "declination", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Declination", "declination"], "contexts": ["magnetic declination", "state of decline or weakening"]}, {"entity_id": "declination", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["declination"], "contexts": ["magnetic variation", "magnetic deviation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "déclinaison magnétique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Magnetic declination", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1849, "stable_key": "clu_6278041e095bf553", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magnetic declination", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127448891", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Latitude", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 551, "target_cluster_name": "latitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetic declination varies with latitude.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetic declination varies with latitude."}, {"found_name": "Inclination", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 757, "target_cluster_name": "Inclination", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetic declination and inclination are both components of the Earth's magnetic field vector.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Magnetic declination and inclination are both components of the Earth's magnetic field vector."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cerebellum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_co