685", "wikidata_description": "fishing vessel built in 1997"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Arctic and Antarctic are polar regions, often discussed in contrast or relation to each other in geographical and exploratory contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Arctic and Antarctic are polar regions, often discussed in contrast or relation to each other in geographical and exploratory contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sallafras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "sallafras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Sallafras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Sallafras", "Safras", "Safíafras", "Saissasfras", "Salfafras", "Sassafras"], "contexts": ["wood brought from Florida", "water source for treatment"]}, {"entity_id": "sallafràs", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sallafràs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sallafràs", "Salfafras", "Salſafraz", "pao Salfafras", "sassafras"], "contexts": ["wood used in water preparation", "Sassafras bark used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Sallafras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sallafràs", "similarity": 0.792}], "id": 1912, "stable_key": "clu_4288edc21c34a20e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sassafras", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Sassafras albidum", "family": "Lauraceae", "wikidata_id": "Q131532", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sweet Flag", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 620, "target_cluster_name": "calamo aromático", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Sassafras and Sweet Flag (Acorus calamus) were historically used in traditional medicine and perfumery, often for similar aromatic and medicinal properties. They were sometimes confused or grouped together due to their strong scents and perceived therapeutic benefits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Sassafras and Sweet Flag (Acorus calamus) were historically used in traditional medicine and perfumery, often for similar aromatic and medicinal properties. They were sometimes confused or grouped together due to their strong scents and perceived therapeutic benefits."}]}, {"canonical_name": "emplastro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "emplasto", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "emplasto", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["emplasto", "emplastos"], "contexts": ["preparation applied externally", "form of application for Ambar"]}, {"entity_id": "emplastro", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "emplastro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 11, "variants": ["emplastro", "emplasto", "emplastro confortativo", "emplastro de Diafulphur", "emplastro de Diaquilað", "emplastro de Galba", "emplastros confortativos"], "contexts": ["poultice or plaster, a topical application", "medical application"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "emplasto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "emplastro", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1913, "stable_key": "clu_7d6aeee7b6dd7790", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plaster/Poultice", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A medicinal preparation applied externally to the skin.", "wikipedia_extract": "Fernando Jorge da Silva dos Santos (born 25 April 1971), commonly known by the nickname Emplastro (Portuguese pronunciation: [ẽ'plaʃtɾu]; \"poultice\"), is a Portuguese football fan who became a public figure for videobombing television reporters by staying close behind them. Born in Madalena, Vila Nova de Gaia, into a poor family, Santos, along with his four siblings, became orphans when he was still a child. Due to his mental development difficulties, Santos was accompanied by Gaia's CERCI since he was eight years old, and after leaving the cooperative, he worked at Panificadora Coelho in Vilar do Paraíso, Gaia, for fifteen years. After leaving that job for disability, he began to dedicate himself to his passion for football. His fame in the world of football began in the early 1990s when he started to appear behind television reporters, or on stadium stands covered by TV cameras, whenever there was a big football match or another major media event. He also popularized himself for the saying \"My father is Pinto da Costa and my mother is Vítor Baía\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Liniment", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2249, "target_cluster_name": "linimento", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are topical preparations applied to the body for medicinal purposes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are topical preparations applied to the body for medicinal purposes."}]}, {"canonical_name": "epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "epithimas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Epithimas"], "contexts": ["substance to be prohibited", "cordial preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "epithimas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["epithimas", "epithema", "epithimos", "epitomas"], "contexts": ["cooling applications", "unguent for liver application"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Epithimas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "epithimas", "similarity": 0.839}], "id": 1914, "stable_key": "clu_11ff2cc9bec56c9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Epithyme", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cuscuta epithymum", "family": "Convolvulaceae", "note": "Early modern texts sometimes used plant names for specific preparations or compounds derived from them. 'Epithimas' likely refers to the plant itself, used medicinally.", "wikidata_id": "Q10273352", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Crocus metallorum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 669, "target_cluster_name": "Crocus metallorum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crocus metallorum is a preparation of antimony, and epithyme was often used in conjunction with or as an ingredient in preparations involving antimony, particularly in alchemical and medicinal contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Crocus metallorum is a preparation of antimony, and epithyme was often used in conjunction with or as an ingredient in preparations involving antimony, particularly in alchemical and medicinal contexts."}, {"found_name": "Vitriol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 564, "target_cluster_name": "Vitriolo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vitriol (and its variant caparroſa) was a substance often discussed alongside other mineral and metallic preparations, including those involving antimony, with which epithyme had associations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vitriol (and its variant caparroſa) was a substance often discussed alongside other mineral and metallic preparations, including those involving antimony, with which epithyme had associations."}, {"found_name": "Arsenic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1859, "target_cluster_name": "arsenicum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Arsenic, like antimony, was a substance frequently employed in alchemical and medicinal preparations, and epithyme could be found in similar contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Arsenic, like antimony, was a substance frequently employed in alchemical and medicinal preparations, and epithyme could be found in similar contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "cananor", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 13, "variants": ["Cananor", "Canaoor"], "contexts": ["place with abundance of the anacardo", "city in India"]}, {"entity_id": "cananor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cananor"], "contexts": ["a place, likely a spice source"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Cananor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cananor", "similarity": 0.831}], "id": 1915, "stable_key": "clu_8879b6a7ce13cb40", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kannur", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q4789666", "wikidata_description": "city in Kannur district in Kerala, India"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Persia", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 168, "target_cluster_name": "Persia", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "açajram da ten^a. • ORTA Essa mezinha he pêra fa