ts and later compilations, provided much of the material that Solinus later popularized."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cholera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "choler", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Choler", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["choler", "Choler"], "contexts": ["one of the four humors, associated with anger", "a humor, likely bile"]}, {"entity_id": "cholera", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cholera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 7, "variants": ["cholera"], "contexts": ["humor evacuated by Cañafistola", "bodily fluid purged by Pinones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Choler", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cholera", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 1926, "stable_key": "clu_dd458b9d58fb3644", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Choler", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the ancient concept of one of the four humors, not the modern disease 'cholera'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Anger is an intense emotional state involving a strong, uncomfortable and non-cooperative response to a perceived provocation, hurt, or threat. A person experiencing anger will often experience physical effects, such as increased heart rate, elevated blood pressure, and increased levels of the stress hormones adrenaline and noradrenaline. Some view anger as an emotion that triggers part of the fight or flight response. Anger becomes the predominant feeling behaviorally, cognitively, and physiologically when a person makes the conscious choice to take action to immediately stop the threatening behavior of another outside force. Anger can have many physical and mental consequences. The external expression of anger can be found in facial expressions, body language, physiological responses, and at times public acts of aggression."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Quinsie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "quinsies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Quinsies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Quinsies"], "contexts": ["throat inflammation treated by Jews ear", "medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "quinsie", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Quinsie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 11, "variants": ["Quinsie"], "contexts": ["condition of the throat", "condition cured by the herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Quinsies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Quinsie", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 1927, "stable_key": "clu_187287a14db474f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "quinsy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "quinsy", "wikidata_id": "Q1586014", "wikidata_description": "pus due to an infection behind the tonsil"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "tertiary_formations", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tertiary formations"], "contexts": ["geological strata studied for species disappearance", "geological formations from the tertiary period"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary_formation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary formation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tertiary formation"], "contexts": ["geological period"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["tertiary formations", "Tertiary formations"], "contexts": ["geological period", "geological time period and rock layers"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tertiary"], "contexts": ["geological time period", "geological period"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tertiary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tertiary"], "contexts": ["geological period"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "tertiary formations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tertiary formations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "tertiary formation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tertiary formations", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 1928, "stable_key": "clu_8f925eb7088bec69", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tertiary Period", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q188645", "wikidata_description": "obsolete term for the geological period 66–2.58 million years ago", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tertiary%20period", "note": "Refers to the geological period, specifically the Neogene and Paleogene periods in modern classification.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tertiary ( TUR-shər-ee, US also  TUR-shee-err-ee) is an obsolete geologic period spanning 66 million to 2.6 or 1.8 million years ago. The period began with the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs in the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event, at the start of the Cenozoic Era, and extended to the beginning of the Quaternary glaciation at the end of the Pliocene Epoch. The Tertiary has not been recognised by the International Commission on Stratigraphy (ICS) since the late 1980s, with the timespan of the Tertiary now being split into the earlier Paleogene and the more recent Neogene periods, though the Tertiary continues to be used in some scientific publications. The term Tertiary was first used by Giovanni Arduino during the mid-18th century. He classified geologic time into primitive (or primary), secondary, and tertiary periods based on observations of geology in Northern Italy. Later a fourth period, the Quaternary, was applied."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Geological Period", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 846, "target_cluster_name": "geological period", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Tertiary Period is a specific geological period, and 'Geological Period' is its broader category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Tertiary Period is a specific geological period, and 'Geological Period' is its broader category."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "resemblance", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["resemblance"], "contexts": ["similarity between individuals"]}, {"entity_id": "resemblance", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["RESEMBLANCE", "Resemblance", "resemblance"], "contexts": ["similarity between things", "similarity in a fundamental respect"]}, {"entity_id": "resemblances", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "resemblances", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["resemblances"], "contexts": ["type of relation between ideas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "resemblance", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Resemblance", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "resemblance", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "resemblances", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 1929, "stable_key": "clu_4d3876a9fda938cd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Resemblance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105227892", "wikidata_description": "relation of resemblance between objects"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Resemblance is a key component in how ideas become associated in the mind, forming the basis of associationist psychology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Resemblance is a key component in how ideas become associated in the mind, forming the basis of associationist psychology."}, {"found_name": "Distinction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1236, "target_cluster_name": "distinction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ability to perceive resemblances often relies on the ability to distinguish between entities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The ability to perceive resemblances often relies on the ability to distinguish between entities."}, {"found_name": "Difference", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 698, "target_cluster_name": "difference", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The perception of resemblance is often contrasted with the perception of difference, highlighting their complementary roles in understanding the world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The perception of resemblance is often contrasted with the perception of difference, highlighting their complementary roles in understanding the world."}, {"found_name": "Image", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2026, "target_cluster_name": "image", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mental images often rely on resemblances to other objects or concepts for their recognition and interpretation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mental images often rely on resemblances to other objects or concepts for their recognition and interpretation."}, {"found_name": "Metaphor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1400, "target_cluster_name": "Metaphors", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphors function by drawing resemblances between two seemingly dissimilar things.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Metaphors function by drawing resemblances between two seemingly dissimilar things."}]}, {"canonical_name": "plantes dicotylédones", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "dicotyledonous_plants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dicotyledonous plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dicotyledonous plants"], "contexts": ["type of plants that germinated"]}, {"entity_id": "plantes_dicotylédones", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "plantes dicotylédones", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 12, "variants": ["plantes dicotylédones", "Monocotydélones", "Monocotylédonées", "dicotylédon", "dicotylédones", "monocotyledones", "monocotylédones", "plantes monocolylédones arborescentes", "plantes monocotylédones", "plantes monocotylëdones", "végétaux monocotylédons"], "contexts": ["dicotyledonous plants", "botanical family classification"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dicotyledonous plants", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plantes dicotylédones", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 1930, "stable_key": "clu_fef910e1751165ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dicotyledonous plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Dicotyledonous plants are now classified within the clade Angiosperms, but 'dicotyledonous' remains a valid descriptive term for a large group.", "wikidata_id": "Q8316", "wikidata_description": "historical grouping of flowering plants"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Flowering plants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2985, "target_cluster_name": "flowering plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dicotyledonous plants are a major group within flowering plants (angiosperms).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dicotyledonous plants are a major group within flowering plants (angiosperms)."}, {"found_name": "Plants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 16, "target_cluster_name": "Plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dicotyledonous plants are a major classification of plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dicotyledonous plants are a major classification of plants."}, {"found_name": "Tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 24, "target_cluster_name": "Tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Many dicotyledonous plants are trees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Many dicotyledonous plants are trees."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hist. de las Indias", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "hist__de_las_indias", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hist. de las Indias", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Hist. de las Indias", "Hist. de Ind.", "Hist. de Ind. occid.", "Hist. de las Jndias", "Hist. de ta Revo/.", "Hist. del Nuevo Mnndo", "Hist. des Antilles"], "contexts": ["historical text", "historical work by Gomara"]}, {"entity_id": "the_history_of_índia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "The history of Índia", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["The history of Índia"], "contexts": ["book by Elphinstone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hist. de las Indias", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "The history of Índia", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 1931, "stable_key": "clu_d61de2909276d0a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "History of the Indies", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Historical text detailing the Americas, often attributed to Gomara.", "note": "The primary context refers to Gonzalo Fernández de Oviedo y Valdés's 'Historia general y natural de las Indias'.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Treaty of the Danish West Indies (Danish: Vestindiens traktat), officially the Convention between the United States and Denmark for cession of the Danish West Indies (Danish: Konventionen mellem USA og Danmark), was a 1916 treaty transferring sovereignty of the Danish West Indies from Denmark to the United States in exchange for a sum of US$25,000,000 in gold ($722 million in 2024), and agreement to cede US interest in Greenland. It is one of the most recent permanent expansions of United States territory. Two of the islands had been in Danish possession since the 17th century and St. Croix since 1733. The glory days of the colony had been from around 1750 to 1850, based on transit trade and the production of rum and sugar using African slaves as labour. By the second half of the 19th century, the sugar production was embattled by the cultivation of sugar beets, and although the slaves had been emancipated in 1848, the agricultural land and the trade was still controlled by the white population, and the living conditions of the descendants of the slaves were poor."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Tom. III", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 661, "target_cluster_name": "Tom. III", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a specific title of a publication that would be organized into volumes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a specific title of a publication that would be organized into volumes.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "coro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 12, "variants": ["Coro"], "contexts": ["province of Coro", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "coro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coro"], "contexts": ["location in arid plains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Coro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Coro", "similarity": 0.754}], "id": 1932, "stable_key": "clu_79a780c4fec522e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q1134454", "wikidata_description": "capital of Falcón State and the oldest city in the west of Venezuela"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Venezuela", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_stre