bits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Practices can become habits."}, {"found_name": "Mental act", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3568, "target_cluster_name": "mental act", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Practices can be mental acts or involve them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Practices can be mental acts or involve them."}, {"found_name": "Method", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 981, "target_cluster_name": "method", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Practices are often specific methods or ways of doing things.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Practices are often specific methods or ways of doing things."}]}, {"canonical_name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "recognition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Recognition", "recognition"], "contexts": ["identifying something previously encountered", "identifying something known"]}, {"entity_id": "recognition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["recognition"], "contexts": ["identifying something as familiar", "act of identifying something"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "recognition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "recognition", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1941, "stable_key": "clu_cd9dc6e8bde90da8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Recognition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21142755", "wikidata_description": "mental process; the act of recognizing or the condition of being recognized"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 226, "target_cluster_name": "ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Recognition is the process by which existing ideas are identified and recalled.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Recognition is the process by which existing ideas are identified and recalled."}]}, {"canonical_name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "unconsciousness", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Unconsciousness", "unconsciousness"], "contexts": ["lack of awareness", "symptom in hysterics"]}, {"entity_id": "unconsciousness", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["unconsciousness"], "contexts": ["lack of awareness", "state of being unaware"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "unconsciousness", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "unconsciousness", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1942, "stable_key": "clu_cda12c1be44f751c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Unconsciousness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Unconsciousness", "wikidata_id": "Q12829165", "wikidata_description": "the state, in a normally conscious being, of not being conscious", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unconsciousness", "wikipedia_extract": "Unconsciousness is a state in which a living individual exhibits a complete, or near-complete, inability to maintain an awareness of self and environment or to respond to any human or environmental stimulus. Unconsciousness may occur as the result of traumatic brain injury, brain hypoxia (inadequate oxygen, possibly due to a brain infarction or cardiac arrest), severe intoxication with drugs that depress the activity of the central nervous system (e.g., alcohol and other hypnotic or sedative drugs), severe fatigue, pain, anaesthesia, and other causes. Loss of consciousness should not be confused with the notion of the psychoanalytic unconscious, cognitive processes that take place outside awareness (e.g., implicit cognition), and with altered states of consciousness such as sleep, delirium, hypnosis, and other altered states in which the person responds to stimuli, including trance and psychedelic experiences. Heart failure (HF) (congestive heart failure (CHF)) Myocardial infarction (MI) (heart attack) Intracerebral hemorrhage (hemorrhagic stroke) Traumatic brain injury (TBI) (intracranial injury) Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) In jurisprudence, unconsciousness may entitle the criminal defendant to the defense of automatism, i.e. a state without control of one's own actions, an excusing condition that allows a defendant to argue that they should not be held criminally liable for their actions or omissions. In most countries, courts must consider whether unconsciousness in a situation can be accepted as a defense; it can vary from case to case."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "amentia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1824, "target_cluster_name": "amentia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While not always present, profound states of unresponsiveness or unconsciousness could be described in relation to severe amentia/dementia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: While not always present, profound states of unresponsiveness or unconsciousness could be described in relation to severe amentia/dementia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coma is a state of prolonged unconsciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Coma is a state of prolonged unconsciousness."}, {"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility."}, {"found_name": "sleep", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1316, "target_cluster_name": "sleep", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which is the opposite of the conscious awareness typically present in wakefulness and a profound alteration of the sleep state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which is the opposite of the conscious awareness typically present in wakefulness and a profound alteration of the sleep state.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Syncope", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 300, "target_cluster_name": "Syncope", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Syncope is a state of temporary unconsciousness, often caused by a sudden drop in blood flow to the brain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Syncope is a state of temporary unconsciousness, often caused by a sudden drop in blood flow to the brain.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "modorras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1613, "target_cluster_name": "modorras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which can be a symptom or consequence of extreme lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness is a state of being unaware of one's surroundings, which can be a symptom or consequence of extreme lethargy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Convulsions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 652, "target_cluster_name": "Convulsions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a common feature or consequence of many types of seizures and convulsions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness is a common feature or consequence of many types of seizures and convulsions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Apoplexie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2516, "target_cluster_name": "Apoplexie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A key characteristic and consequence of apoplexy is the loss of consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A key characteristic and consequence of apoplexy is the loss of consciousness.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Vomitos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1493, "target_cluster_name": "Vomitos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "desmayos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1601, "target_cluster_name": "desmayos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting is a temporary state of unconsciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fainting is a temporary state of unconsciousness.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "suffocação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1686, "target_cluster_name": "suffocação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness is a common symptom and immediate result of severe suffocation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness is a common symptom and immediate result of severe suffocation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "falta de memoria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1995, "target_cluster_name": "falta de memoria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Unconsciousness can precede, accompany, or result from conditions causing amnesia, making it a closely related state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Unconsciousness can precede, accompany, or result from conditions causing amnesia, making it a closely related state.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Faintings", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2269, "target_cluster_name": "Faintings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting is a temporary loss of consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fainting is a temporary loss of consciousness.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "surgical shock", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "minute or so after his medulla oblongata is cut, is an inhibition from the seat of injury which quickly passes away. What is known as 'surgical shock '(unconsciousness, pallor, dilatation of splanchni", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for a type of unconsciousness"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "personality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Personality", "personality"], "contexts": ["quality of being a person", "individual character and identity"]}, {"entity_id": "personality", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["personality"], "contexts": ["the enduring self or individual being", "quality of being a person"]}, {"entity_id": "personalities", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "personalities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["personalities"], "contexts": ["distinctive qualities of individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Personality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "personality", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Personality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "personalities", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 1943, "stable_key": "clu_42de2b8f5646ee08", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Personality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q641118", "wikidata_description": "psychological characteristics of an individual (usually referring to only humans)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Character", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_str