the equator, ascend i", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for"}]}, {"canonical_name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "volcanic_action", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Volcanic action", "volcanic action"], "contexts": ["eruption of molten rock from Earth's interior", "geological process"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_action", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["volcanic action", "Volcanic Action"], "contexts": ["processes related to volcanoes", "geological process involving volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_processes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic processes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic processes"], "contexts": ["phenomena related to volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_phenomena", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic phenomena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic phenomena"], "contexts": ["events related to volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_phenomena", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "volcanic phenomena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic phenomena"], "contexts": ["related"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "volcanic action", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "volcanic action", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2000, "stable_key": "clu_9a008bd63a7115aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcanic activity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q505748", "wikidata_description": "phenomena and processes associated with the action of volcanos, geysers and fumaroles", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcanism", "wikipedia_extract": "Volcanism, vulcanism, volcanicity, or volcanic activity is the phenomenon where solids, liquids, gases, and their mixtures erupt to the surface of a solid-surface astronomical body such as a planet or a moon. It is caused by the presence of a heat source, usually internally generated, inside the body; the heat is generated by various processes, such as radioactive decay or tidal heating. This heat partially melts solid material in the body or turns material into gas. The mobilized material rises through the body's interior and may break through the solid surface. For volcanism to occur, the temperature of the mantle must have risen to about half its melting point. At this point, the mantle's viscosity will have dropped to about 1021 Pascal-seconds."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Internal Heat of the Earth", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3620, "target_cluster_name": "Internal Heat of the Earth", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The internal heat of the Earth is the primary source of energy for volcanic activity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The internal heat of the Earth is the primary source of energy for volcanic activity."}, {"found_name": "Internal heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2439, "target_cluster_name": "internal heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Internal heat is the fundamental cause of volcanic activity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Internal heat is the fundamental cause of volcanic activity."}, {"found_name": "Explosion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3650, "target_cluster_name": "explosion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Volcanic eruptions are a form of explosion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Volcanic eruptions are a form of explosion."}, {"found_name": "Combustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 866, "target_cluster_name": "combustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While not the primary mechanism, combustion can be associated with some volcanic phenomena (e.g., gases).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While not the primary mechanism, combustion can be associated with some volcanic phenomena (e.g., gases)."}, {"found_name": "Atmospheric electricity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3166, "target_cluster_name": "atmospheric electricity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Volcanic eruptions can generate significant atmospheric electrical phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Volcanic eruptions can generate significant atmospheric electrical phenomena."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "lapland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Lapland"], "contexts": ["region where observations were made", "location of observations by Bravais and Lottin"]}, {"entity_id": "lapland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Lapland"], "contexts": ["location of arc measurement", "location of Wardhus observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lapland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lapland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2001, "stable_key": "clu_fdcbac450223a15d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lapland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Finland, Norway, Russia, Sweden", "wikidata_id": "Q5700", "wikidata_description": "region of Finland", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapland_%28Finland%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Lapland is the largest and northernmost region of Finland. The 21 municipalities in the region cooperate in a Regional Council. Lapland borders the Finnish region of North Ostrobothnia in the south. It also borders the Gulf of Bothnia and the Norrbotten County in Sweden, Finnmark County and the Troms County in Norway, and Murmansk Oblast and the Republic of Karelia in Russia. The topography of Lapland varies from vast mires and forests in the south to fells in the north. The Arctic Circle crosses Lapland, so polar phenomena such as the midnight sun, the polar night and the northern lights can be viewed in this region."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lapland is a region within the Arctic Circle, and the description mentions its northern location.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lapland is a region within the Arctic Circle, and the description mentions its northern location."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "messier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Messier"], "contexts": ["discovered comet in 1766", "discovered nebulous spot in 1774"]}, {"entity_id": "messier", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Messier"], "contexts": ["determined comet's orbit elements", "astronomer who first saw Encke's comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Messier", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Messier", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2002, "stable_key": "clu_d54f45a3acd90d84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charles Messier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1730, "death_year": 1817, "description": "French astronomer who discovered numerous comets and compiled a famous catalog."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cassini", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1081, "target_cluster_name": "Cassini", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Messier was a famous comet hunter and cataloger of nebulae who worked in Paris during the later part of Cassini's directorship of the Paris Observatory. Cassini's work laid foundational groundwork for Messier's catalog.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Messier was a famous comet hunter and cataloger of nebulae who worked in Paris during the later part of Cassini's directorship of the Paris Observatory. Cassini's work laid foundational groundwork for Messier's catalog.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lacaille", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1260, "target_cluster_name": "Lacaille", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were highly influential astronomers known for their cataloging of deep-sky objects. Lacaille's catalog was one of the earliest comprehensive lists, and Messier built upon and expanded this tradition with his own famous catalog, which is still in use today.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were highly influential astronomers known for their cataloging of deep-sky objects. Lacaille's catalog was one of the earliest comprehensive lists, and Messier built upon and expanded this tradition with his own famous catalog, which is still in use today.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Alexis Bouvard", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3594, "target_cluster_name": "M. Bouvard", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alexis Bouvard was a French astronomer who worked with Charles Messier and was involved in astronomical calculations and observations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alexis Bouvard was a French astronomer who worked with Charles Messier and was involved in astronomical calculations and observations."}, {"found_name": "Camille Flammarion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3596, "target_cluster_name": "Valz", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Camille Flammarion was a prominent French astronomer and popularizer of astronomy, a field in which Messier was a key figure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Camille Flammarion was a prominent French astronomer and popularizer of astronomy, a field in which Messier was a key figure."}, {"found_name": "Friedrich Wilhelm Argelander", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1851, "target_cluster_name": "Argelander", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Friedrich Wilhelm Argelander was a German astronomer known for his star catalog, a field closely related to Messier's work on his own catalog of nebulae and star clusters.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Friedrich Wilhelm Argelander was a German astronomer known for his star catalog, a field closely related to Messier's work on his own catalog of nebulae and star clusters."}, {"found_name": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1081, "target_cluster_name": "Cassini", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini was an Italian-French astronomer whose work at the Paris Observatory, where Messier later worked, laid groundwork for astronomical cataloging.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini was an Italian-French astronomer whose work at the Paris Observatory, where Messier later worked, laid groundwork for astronomical cataloging."}, {"found_name": "Heinrich Wilhelm Olbers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1730, "target_cluster_name": "Olbers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Heinrich Wilhelm Olbers was a German astronomer known for Olbers' paradox and his contributions to the study of nebulae and asteroids, areas related to Messier's catalog.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Heinrich Wilhelm Olbers was a German astronomer known for Olbers' paradox and his contributions to the study of nebulae and asteroids, areas related to Messier's catalog."}, {"found_name": "Hipparchus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 909, "target_cluster_name": "Hipparchus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hipparchus was an ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician who created one of the earliest known comprehensive star catalogs, a precursor to Messier's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hipparchus was an ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician who created one of the earliest known comprehensive star catalogs, a precursor to Messier's work."}, {"found_name": "Johannes Kepler", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 695, "target_cluster_name": "Kepler", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer and mathematician whose laws of planetary motion and work on optics were foundational to astronomy, a field Messier was deeply involved in.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer and mathematician whose laws of planetary motion and work on optics were foundational to astronomy, a field Messier was deeply involved in."}, {"found_name": "Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1260, "target_cluster_name": "Lacaille", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille was a French astronomer who created a catalog of southern hemisphere stars, complementing Messier's northern hemisphere focus and contributing to astronomical cataloging.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille was a French astronomer who created a catalog of southern hemisphere stars, complementing Messier's northern hemisphere focus and contributing to astronomical cataloging."}]}, {"canonical_name": "magnetic storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 26, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetic_storm", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["magnetic storm"], "contexts": ["disturbance in Earth's magnetic field", "sudden disturbance in magnetic needle"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["electric storms"], "contexts": ["storms with noise and light", "storms with electrical phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "storms,_magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Storms, magnetic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Storms, magnetic"], "contexts": ["atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_storms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnetic storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["magnetic storms"], "contexts": ["sudden disturbances in Earth's magnetic field", "disturbances affecting magnetic elements"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_storm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnetic storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic storm"], "contexts": ["disturbance in Earth's magnetic field"]}, {"entity_id": "storm", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["storm"], "contexts": ["violent atmospheric disturbance", "weather phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "tormenta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "tormenta", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tormenta"], "contexts": ["metaphor for a difficult life situation"]}, {"entity_id": "thunderstorm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thunderstorm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["thunderstorm"], "contexts": ["approach causes rapid transitions in electric state", "atmospheric electrical phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "storm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["storm"], "contexts": ["violent atmospheric disturbance"]}, {"entity_id": "storm", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["storm"], "contexts": ["period of severe weather"]}, {"entity_id": "tempêtes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tempêtes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tempêtes"], "contexts": ["severe weather events"]}, {"entity_id": "storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["storms"], "contexts": ["geographical distribution of", "violent atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "thunderstorms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thunderstorms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["thunderstorms"], "contexts": ["frequent near coasts due to electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "magnetic storm", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "magnetic storm", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storm", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric storms", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Storms, magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Storms, magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storm", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 2003, "stable_key": "clu_e31a21fb7de0a559-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "weather events", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'storms' in early modern texts, especially in contexts like Humboldt's 'Kosmos', refers to natural meteorological phenomena rather than diseases. 'Magnetic storms' refers to geomagnetic disturbances.", "wikidata_id": "Q130240049", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather%20events%20during%20wars", "wikipedia_extract": "A storm is any disturbed state of the natural environment or the atmosphere of an astronomical body. It may be marked by significant disruptions to normal conditions such as strong wind, tornadoes, hail, thunder and lightning (a thunderstorm), heavy precipitation (snowstorm, rainstorm), heavy freezing rain (ice storm), strong winds (tropical cyclone, windstorm), wind transporting some substance through the atmosphere such as in a dust storm, among other forms of severe weather. Storms have the potential to harm lives and property via storm surge, heavy rain or snow causing flooding or road impassibility, lightning, wildfires, and vertical and horizontal wind shear. Systems with significant rainfall and duration help alleviate drought in places they move through. Heavy snowfall can allow special recreational activities to take place which would not be possible otherwise, such as skiing and snowmobiling. The English word comes from Proto-Germanic sturmaz meaning \"noise, tumult\"; a synonym is tempest, of French origin."}, "member_count": 13, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Equinoctial gales", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4520, "target_cluster_name": "equinoctial gales", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'equinoctial gales' refer to natural phenomena that can cause disruptions, though 'magnetic storms' are specifically related to solar activity affecting Earth's magnetic field, while 'equinoctial gales' are strong winds associated with the equinoxes. The historical context of 'magnetic storms' as a 'weather event' suggests a connection to atmospheric and geophysical disturbances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'equinoctial gales' refer to natural phenomena that can cause disruptions, though 'magnetic storms' are specifically related to solar activity affecting Earth's magnetic field, while 'equinoctial gales' are strong winds associated with the equinoxes. The historical context of 'magnetic storms' as a 'weather event' suggests a connection to atmospheric and geophysical disturbances."}, {"found_name": "Drought", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3378, "target_cluster_name": "drought", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'drought' are natural phenomena that can have significant impacts on human activities and the environment. While distinct in their physical nature, historical texts might link them as disruptive events.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'drought' are natural phenomena that can have significant impacts on human activities and the environment. While distinct in their physical nature, historical texts might link them as disruptive events."}, {"found_name": "Earthquake", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1720, "target_cluster_name": "earthquakes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'earthquakes' are powerful natural events that can cause widespread disruption. Historically, they might have been grouped as significant, potentially destructive forces of nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'earthquakes' are powerful natural events that can cause widespread disruption. Historically, they might have been grouped as significant, potentially destructive forces of nature."}, {"found_name": "Volcanic eruption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2443, "target_cluster_name": "volcanic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'volcanic eruptions' are natural phenomena that can cause significant environmental and societal impacts. They represent powerful forces of nature that could be discussed in relation to each other in historical texts concerning natural disturbances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'volcanic eruptions' are natural phenomena that can cause significant environmental and societal impacts. They represent powerful forces of nature that could be discussed in relation to each other in historical texts concerning natural disturbances."}]}, {"canonical_name": "movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "movement", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "movement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["movement"], "contexts": ["geological process of land changing position"]}, {"entity_id": "movimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["movimento", "moimentos", "movimento progreſſivo", "movimentoeſquecido", "movimentos arbitrarios", "movimentos desordenados"], "contexts": ["physical motion", "ability to move"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "movement", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "movimento", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 2004, "stable_key": "clu_df7d0080d254bdc1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "movement", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'geological process of land changing position' refers to geological movement, while 'physical motion' refers to physics. Both are encompassed by the broader concept of movement."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Earth's rotation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target