olò Antinori was also a Florentine merchant and banker, indicating a likely professional or social relationship within the same economic sphere."}, {"found_name": "Amato Lusitano", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2328, "target_cluster_name": "Amato Lusitano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was a prominent physician and humanist of Portuguese origin. Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker. While their professions differ, both were significant figures in their respective fields during the Renaissance, and it's plausible they moved in similar intellectual or social circles, or that Antinori's business dealings might have intersected with Lusitano's patronage or travels.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amato Lusitano was a prominent physician and humanist of Portuguese origin. Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker. While their professions differ, both were significant figures in their respective fields during the Renaissance, and it's plausible they moved in similar intellectual or social circles, or that Antinori's business dealings might have intersected with Lusitano's patronage or travels."}]}, {"canonical_name": "António Nunes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 1, "members": [{"entity_id": "antonio_nunes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Antonio Nunes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antonio Nunes"], "contexts": ["patient with dark urine"]}], "edges": [], "id": 2012, "stable_key": "clu_f9a07eb53ce08271", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "António Nunes Ribeiro Sanches", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1700, "death_year": 1782, "description": "Portuguese physician and philosopher, author of medical texts.", "note": "While the cluster mentions 'Lyyro dos pesos' (likely a misprint or translation of 'Livro dos pesos') and a patient with dark urine, António Nunes Ribeiro Sanches is a prominent Portuguese physician of the era with a significant body of work."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portuguezes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 218, "target_cluster_name": "Portuguezes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "António Nunes Ribeiro Sanches was an influential Portuguese physician and writer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: António Nunes Ribeiro Sanches was an influential Portuguese physician and writer.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "salamanca", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Salamanca"], "contexts": ["city where author and Ruano knew each other", "city where Ruano was educated"]}, {"entity_id": "salamanca", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Salamanca", "Salamnaca"], "contexts": ["city where Pedroza was a professor", "city where Luis Rodriguez Pedrofa was a physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Salamanca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Salamanca", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2013, "stable_key": "clu_d5b8d2fe77c93ef7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salamanca", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historic university city in Spain.", "wikipedia_extract": "Salamanca (Spanish: [salaˈmaŋka] ) is a municipality and city in the autonomous community of Castile and León in Spain, and the capital of the province of Salamanca. Attached to the comarca of Campo Charro, the city lies on the northern half of the Meseta Central, in the western-central part of the Iberian Peninsula, straddling the Tormes river. As of 2024, with a population of 144,458, Salamanca is the 46th-largest municipality in Spain. The Iron Age hilltop site of Cerro de San Vicente on the right bank of the Tormes is considered as the first human settlement in the current-day city. By the 3rd century BCE, the urban settlement in the nearby Teso de las Catedrales had consolidated, under the influence of Vaccaei and Vettones. Following Roman subjugation, the indigenous oppidum gradually became the Roman civitas of Salmantica."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Valladolid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3008, "target_cluster_name": "Valladolid", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Salamanca and Valladolid are major cities in the autonomous community of Castile and León, Spain. They are historically and geographically significant centers within the same region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Salamanca and Valladolid are major cities in the autonomous community of Castile and León, Spain. They are historically and geographically significant centers within the same region."}]}, {"canonical_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "enfermidades_venenosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "enfermidades venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["enfermidades venenosas"], "contexts": ["poisonous illnesses", "state of being poisoned"]}, {"entity_id": "doenças_venenoſas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doenças venenoſas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["doenças venenoſas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases", "Poisonous diseases where wine can be given"]}, {"entity_id": "doenças_venenosas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doenças venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["doenças venenosas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases", "condition treated with Bezoartico"]}, {"entity_id": "doenças_venenofas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doenças venenofas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["doenças venenofas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases"]}, {"entity_id": "doençasvenenofas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doençasvenenofas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["doençasvenenofas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doenças venenoſas", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doenças venenosas", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doenças venenofas", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doençasvenenofas", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2014, "stable_key": "clu_78c4f885b8b317fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Poisoning", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Poisoning"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Celsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "celso", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Celso", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Celso"], "contexts": ["ancient physician who studied cholera", "ancient author discussing lycium"]}, {"entity_id": "celsus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Celsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Celsus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority on maintaining health", "ancient authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Celso", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Celsus", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 2015, "stable_key": "clu_772e4f9a92d2858a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aulus Cornelius Celsus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman encyclopedist and medical writer.", "wikipedia_extract": "Aulus Cornelius Celsus (c. 25 BC – c. 50 AD) was a Roman encyclopedist, known for his extant medical work, De Medicina, which is believed to be the only surviving section of a much larger encyclopedia. The De Medicina is a primary source on diet, pharmacy, surgery and related fields, and it is one of the best sources concerning medical knowledge in the Roman world. The lost portions of his encyclopedia likely included volumes on agriculture, law, rhetoric, and military arts. He made contributions to the classification of human skin disorders in  dermatology, such as myrmecia, and his name is often found in medical terminology regarding the skin, e.g., kerion celsi and area celsi. He is also the namesake of Paracelsus (lit. Above Celsus), a great Swiss alchemist and physician prevalent in the Medical Renaissance."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Celsus's De Medicina is a compilation of medical knowledge, and Hippocrates is a foundational figure in Western medicine whose works Celsus would have drawn upon or referenced.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Celsus's De Medicina is a compilation of medical knowledge, and Hippocrates is a foundational figure in Western medicine whose works Celsus would have drawn upon or referenced."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was another highly influential physician in Roman times, and his medical theories and practices were central to the medical tradition that Celsus was part of and documented.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was another highly influential physician in Roman times, and his medical theories and practices were central to the medical tradition that Celsus was part of and documented."}]}, {"canonical_name": "narcotica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "narcótico", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "narcótico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["narcótico"], "contexts": ["property of opium"]}, {"entity_id": "narcotica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "narcotica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["narcotica", "narcotico"], "contexts": ["numbing quality", "adverse quality affecting spirits/nerves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "narcótico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "narcotica", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 2016, "stable_key": "clu_7f4d6b52d52379f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Narcotic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to substances that induce drowsiness, stupor, or insensibility.", "wikidata_id": "Q10268802", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive drug whose possession, use or trade is outlawed"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "saliva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["saliva"], "contexts": ["animal's saliva used for preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "saliva", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 11, "variants": ["saliva", "Saliva", "Saliva humana", "salivação"], "contexts": ["bodily fluid", "bodily fluid involved in digestion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "saliva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "saliva", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2017, "stable_key": "clu_be3655d65f8a90d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saliva", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "The watery fluid secreted in the mouth by salivary glands.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saliva (commonly referred to as spit, drool or slobber) is an extracellular fluid produced and secreted by salivary glands in the mouth. In humans, saliva is around 99% water, plus electrolytes, mucus, white blood cells, epithelial cells (from which DNA can be extracted), enzymes (such as lingual lipase and amylase), and antimicrobial agents (such as secretory IgA, and lysozymes). The enzymes found in saliva are essential in beginning the process of digestion of dietary starches and fats. These enzymes also play a role in breaking down food particles trapped within dental crevices, thus protecting teeth from bacterial decay. Saliva also performs a lubricating function, wetting food and permitting the initiation of swallowing, and protecting the oral mucosa from drying out. Saliva has specialized purposes for a variety of animal species beyond predigestion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "boca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 649, "target_cluster_name": "boca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saliva is produced in the mouth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Saliva is produced in the mouth.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Phlegm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2513, "target_cluster_name": "phlegm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phlegm and saliva are both viscous bodily fluids found in the mouth and throat, often discussed together in premodern medical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Phlegm and saliva are both viscous bodily fluids found in the mouth and throat, often discussed together in premodern medical contexts."}, {"found_name": "gengivas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2243, "target_cluster_name": "gengivas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Saliva is a bodily fluid produced in the mouth, which bathes the gums.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Saliva is a bodily fluid produced in the mouth, which bathes the gums.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cidra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "cidreira", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cidreira", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cidreira"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "cidra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cidra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["cidra", "cidra azeda", "cidraa", "cidrao", "cidraó"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a medicinal preparation", "citron rinds used in a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cidreira", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cidra", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 2018, "stable_key": "clu_9234b21908e78793", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lemon balm", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Melissa officinalis", "family": "Lamiaceae", "note": "'Cidra' and 'cidreira' both refer to Melissa officinalis, commonly known as lemon balm.", "wikidata_id": "Q148396", "wikidata_description": "lemon balm, a species of plant, herb"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "limoens", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1051, "target_cluster_name": "limoens", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is sometimes referred to as 'citron' in older texts, and while distinct from the lemon fruit, it shares a common etymological root and is a related herb.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is sometimes referred to as 'citron' in older texts, and while distinct from the lemon fruit, it shares a common etymological root and is a related herb.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Citrus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2465, "target_cluster_name": "citrinos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae), while Citrus is a genus of flowering trees and shrubs. Although not closely related botanically, both are commonly used in herbal remedies and culinary applications, and historical texts might group them due to shared uses or perceived properties.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae), while Citrus is a genus of flowering trees and shrubs. Although not closely related botanically, both are commonly used in herbal remedies and culinary applications, and historical texts might group them due to shared uses or perceived properties."}, {"found_name": "Herbs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1538, "target_cluster_name": "ervas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm is a type of herb, and 'Herbs' is a broader category that encompasses it. Historical texts often refer to specific herbs within a general discussion of 'herbs'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lemon balm is a type of herb, and 'Herbs' is a broader category that encompasses it. Historical texts often refer to specific herbs within a general discussion of 'herbs'."}, {"found_name": "menta", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2236, "target_cluster_name": "menta", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is a species within the genus Mentha, often confused with or used similarly to other mint species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is a species within the genus Mentha, often confused with or used similarly to other mint species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "citrinos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2465, "target_cluster_name": "citrinos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is often associated with citrus due to its lemony scent and flavor, though it belongs to a different plant family (Lamiaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lemon balm (Melissa officinalis) is often associated with citrus due to its lemony scent and flavor, though it belongs to a different plant family (Lamiaceae).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thymo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "thymo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Thymo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Thymo", "THYMO", "thymo", "thymos", "thym©"], "contexts": ["plant Epithymo wraps around", "wild Hyssop, also called Thymo"]}, {"entity_id": "thymus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Thymus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thymus"], "contexts": ["plant mentioned in text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Thymo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Thymus", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2019, "stable_key": "clu_118ea1b9540f4f9b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thyme", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Thymus vulgaris", "family": "Lamiaceae", "description": "An aromatic herb with culinary and medicinal uses.", "note": "Likely refers to the genus Thymus, with Thyme vulgaris as the most common species.", "wikipedia_extract": "Thymus vulgaris (common thyme, German thyme, garden thyme or just thyme) is a species of flowering plant in the mint family Lamiaceae, native to southern Europe from the western Mediterranean to southern Italy. Growing to 15–30 cm (6–12 in) tall by 40 cm (16 in) wide, it is a bushy, woody-based evergreen subshrub with small, highly aromatic, grey-green leaves and clusters of purple or pink flowers in early summer. It is useful in the garden as groundcover, where it can be short-lived, but is easily propagated from cuttings. It is also the main source of thyme as an ingredient in cooking and as a herbal medicine. It is slightly spicier than oregano and sweeter than sage. The Latin specific epithet vulgaris means “common” in the sense of “widespread”."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "liquid", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquidum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "liquidum", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquidum"], "contexts": ["Latin adjective meaning 'liquid'"]}, {"entity_id": "fluido", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fluido", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fluido"], "contexts": ["state of bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "liquid", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "liquid", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["liquid"], "contexts": ["state of matter with fixed volume", "state of matter between solid and gas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "liquidum", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fluido", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 2020, "stable_key": "clu_930ee3e8699abe55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liquid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the state of matter characterized by fixed volume but not fixed shape.", "wikidata_id": "Q26367132", "wikidata_description": "report of investigation published in 1973"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fluidity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1210, "target_cluster_name": "fluidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fluidity is a state of matter that is characteristic of liquids.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fluidity is a state of matter that is characteristic of liquids."}, {"found_name": "Density", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 303, "target_cluster_name": "density", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Density is a fundamental physical property that is commonly measured and discussed for liquids.", "confid