ralli rofsi", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2033, "stable_key": "clu_8c3b6297f1e602af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red Coral", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Coralli rofsi refers to red coral, a precious mineral harvested from the sea, often used in historical medicine and jewelry."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "ants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 10, "variants": ["ants", "Ants"], "contexts": ["social insects", "insects with notable instincts, slave-making"]}, {"entity_id": "ants", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ants"], "contexts": ["insects studied for reasoning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ants", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "ants", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 2034, "stable_key": "clu_945672d3ea4535e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ants", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Formicidae", "family": "Formicidae", "description": "Social insects known for complex behaviors and colonies.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ants are eusocial insects of the family Formicidae and, along with the related wasps and bees, belong to the order Hymenoptera. Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors in the Cretaceous period. More than 13,800 of an estimated total of 22,000 species have been classified. They are easily identified by their geniculate (elbowed) antennae and the distinctive node-like structure that forms their slender waists. Ants form colonies that range in size from a few dozen individuals often living in small natural cavities to highly organised colonies that may occupy large territories with a sizeable nest (or nests) that consist of millions of individuals, in some cases they reach hundreds of millions of individuals in super colonies. Typical colonies consist of various castes of sterile, wingless females, most of which are workers (ergates), as well as soldiers (dinergates) and other specialised groups."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While ants do not produce honey, they are often discussed in proximity to bees in premodern texts concerning insects and their societal structures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While ants do not produce honey, they are often discussed in proximity to bees in premodern texts concerning insects and their societal structures.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are social insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are social insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Wasps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2271, "target_cluster_name": "Wasps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wasps and ants are both in the order Hymenoptera and share a close evolutionary relationship, often being discussed together in entomological contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wasps and ants are both in the order Hymenoptera and share a close evolutionary relationship, often being discussed together in entomological contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "progenitor", "category": "PERSON", "subcatego