 ["endogenous rock type", "type of rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "euphotide", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "euphotide", "similarity": 0.775}], "id": 2047, "stable_key": "clu_da34a74471308a4d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nepheline", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Euphotide is an archaic term for Nepheline, often encountered in older geological literature, especially in relation to Humboldt's travels. 'Uepheline' is a probable misspelling.", "wikidata_id": "Q407339", "wikidata_description": "tectosilicate mineral", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nepheline", "wikipedia_extract": "Nepheline (from Ancient Greek  νεφέλη (nephélē) 'cloud'), also called nephelite, is a rock-forming mineral in the feldspathoid group – a silica-undersaturated aluminosilicate (Na3KAl4Si4O16) that occurs in intrusive and volcanic rocks with low silica, and in their associated pegmatites. It is used in glass and ceramic manufacturing and other industries, and has been investigated as an ore of aluminium. Nepheline crystals are rare and belong to the hexagonal system, usually having the form of a short, six-sided prism terminated by the basal plane. The crystals appear to have more symmetry than they actually possess, but unsymmetrical etched figures produced artificially on the prism faces indicate that the crystals are hemimorphic and tetartohedral, the only element of symmetry being a polar hexad axis. Nepheline is found in compact, granular aggregates, and can be white, yellow, gray, green, or reddish. Its hardness on the Mohs scale is 5.5 to 6, and its specific gravity 2.60–2.65."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "phonolithe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1897, "target_cluster_name": "phonolithe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Phonolite contains nepheline.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Phonolite contains nepheline.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nepheline syenite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3472, "target_cluster_name": "pyroxenic nepheline", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nepheline is a component of nepheline syenite, as indicated by the candidate 'nepheline syenite (aka pyroxenic nepheline)'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Nepheline is a component of nepheline syenite, as indicated by the candidate 'nepheline syenite (aka pyroxenic nepheline)'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "malabares", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "malapert", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Malapert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Malapert"], "contexts": ["described annular mountain", "annular mountain"]}, {"entity_id": "malabares", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "malabares", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["malabares", "Malabares", "Malavres"], "contexts": ["people who name the coconut tree and fruit", "people who call coquo 'tenga'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Malapert", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "malabares", "similarity": 0.873}], "id": 2048, "stable_key": "clu_e266fc35225ded22", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malabar people", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Indigenous people of the Malabar Coast, known for their naming conventions.", "note": "The term 'malabares' refers to the people of the Malabar region, not a specific individual. The mention of 'Malapert' is likely a misspelling or misinterpretation in the source text."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lombardie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "lombardie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lombardie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Lombardie"], "contexts": ["region with cultivated fields", "region lacking primitive blocks"]}, {"entity_id": "lombardy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lombardy", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Lombardy"], "contexts": ["Italian region", "plain in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lombardie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lombardy", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 2049, "stable_key": "clu_df39d021e9b41ec6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lombardy", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikipedia_extract": "Desiderius, also known as Daufer or Dauferius (born c. 720 – died c. 786), was king of the Lombards in northern Italy, ruling from 756 to 774. The Frankish king of renown, Charlemagne, married Desiderius's daughter and subsequently conquered his realm. Desiderius is remembered for this connection to Charlemagne and for being the last Lombard ruler to exercise regional kingship. Born in Brescia, Desiderius was originally a royal officer, the dux of Tuscia and he became king after the death of Aistulf in 756.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desiderius"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Calabria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2315, "target_cluster_name": "Calabria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calabria is a region in southern Italy, historically associated with the Lombards, who also gave their name to Lombardy. Desiderius was king of the Lombards in northern Italy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Calabria is a region in southern Italy, historically associated with the Lombards, who also gave their name to Lombardy. Desiderius was king of the Lombards in northern Italy."}, {"found_name": "Italy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 88, "target_cluster_name": "Itália", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lombardy is a region in northern Italy. Desiderius was king of the Lombards in northern Italy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lombardy is a region in northern Italy. Desiderius was king of the Lombards in northern Italy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mathieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "mathieu", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mathieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Mathieu", "M. Mathieu"], "contexts": ["member of the Academy of Sciences", "collaborator in magnetic inclination measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "mathieu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mathieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Mathieu"], "contexts": ["author of note in Delambre's work", "author of note on astronomy history"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mathieu", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mathieu", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2050, "stable_key": "clu_d16f9802271fd627", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Claude François Geoffroy", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1751, "death_year": 1831, "description": "French chemist and botanist, member of the Academy of Sciences.", "note": "The context 'author of note in Delambre's work' and 'member of the Academy of Sciences' points strongly to a prominent scientist. Claude François Geoffroy fits this description and is historically associated with botanical and chemical studies which could be referenced in scientific texts. The name 'Mathieu' is a common one and could refer to other individuals, but given the context, this is a strong possibility."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3984, "target_cluster_name": "M. Is. Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the father of Claude François Geoffroy and a prominent naturalist himself. Their shared surname and familial connection indicate a strong relationship within the scientific community of their time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the father of Claude François Geoffroy and a prominent naturalist himself. Their shared surname and familial connection indicate a strong relationship within the scientific community of their time."}, {"found_name": "Lametherie", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2403, "target_cluster_name": "Lametherie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Claude François Geoffroy is listed as 'Mathieu' in the candidate list, which is a surname shared with Jean-Baptiste-Benoît Mathieu de Laumont. This suggests a potential familial or professional connection, possibly indicating that 'Mathieu' in this context refers to Geoffroy, and the shared surname is significant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Claude François Geoffroy is listed as 'Mathieu' in the candidate list, which is a surname shared with Jean-Baptiste-Benoît Mathieu de Laumont. This suggests a potential familial or professional connection, possibly indicating that 'Mathieu' in this context refers to Geoffroy, and the shared surname is significant.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Santa Cruz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "santa_cruz", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santa Cruz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Santa Cruz"], "contexts": ["province in the first category of census", "city in Audiencia de Charcas"]}, {"entity_id": "santa-cruz", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santa-Cruz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Santa-Cruz"], "contexts": ["city in Bolivia", "mountain range in Santa Cruz"]}, {"entity_id": "santa_cruz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Santa Cruz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Santa Cruz", "Santa Cruz de Cochiro"], "contexts": ["original name of Brazil", "island owned by Jorge Vas de Magalhães"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santa Cruz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Santa Cruz", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santa-Cruz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Santa Cruz", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 2051, "stable_key": "clu_e594c13e8bc7e6f8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Santa Cruz", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Bolivia", "note": "While Santa Cruz can refer to multiple places, the context of Humboldt and its mention as a city in Bolivia strongly suggests Santa Cruz de la Sierra.", "wikidata_id": "Q170688", "wikidata_description": "city in eastern Bolivia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa%20Cruz%20de%20la%20Sierra", "wikipedia_extract": "Santa Cruz de la Sierra (Spanish: [ˈsanta ˈkɾus ðe la ˈsjera]; lit. 'Holy Cross of the Mountain Range'), commonly known as Santa Cruz, is the largest city in Bolivia and the capital of the Santa Cruz department. Situated on the Pirai River in the eastern Tropical Lowlands of Bolivia, the Santa Cruz de la Sierra Metropolitan Region is the most populous urban agglomeration in Bolivia with an estimated population of 2.4 million in 2020. It is formed out of a conurbation of seven Santa Cruz municipalities: Santa Cruz de la Sierra, La Guardia, Warnes, Cotoca, El Torno, Porongo, and Montero. The city was first founded in 1561 by Spanish explorer Ñuflo de Chavez about 200 km (124 mi) east of its current location, and was moved several times until it was finally established on the Pirai River in the late 16th century. For much of its history, Santa Cruz was mostly a small outpost town, and even after Bolivia gained its independence in 1825 there was little attention from the authorities or the population in general to settle the region."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Beguino", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "bupin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bupin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bupin"], "contexts": ["author on French and English infrastructure", "author of memoirs on French and English infrastructure"]}, {"entity_id": "beguino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Beguino", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Beguino", "Beguinus", "Biguinus"], "contexts": ["physician and chief smelter", "described as minister of nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bupin", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Beguino", "similarity": 0.861}], "id": 2052, "stable_key": "clu_492d79a9887071e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Beguinus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Physician and alchemist, associated with alchemy and medicine.", "note": "The name 'Beguino' and 'Bupin' likely refer to Beguinus, a known alchemist and physician."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Riobamba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "riobamba", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Riobamba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Riobamba"], "contexts": ["city near Andes", "city with 'Pampa' in name"]}, {"entity_id": "riobamba_nuevo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Riobamba Nuevo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Riobamba Nuevo"], "contexts": ["lieu"]}, {"entity_id": "riobamba", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Riobamba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Riobamba"], "contexts": ["location of earthquake", "town overthrown by earthquake in 1797"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Riobamba", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Riobamba", "similarity": 0.878}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Riobamba Nuevo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Riobamba", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 2053, "stable_key": "clu_76e8f3c533c057f3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Riobamba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q478675", "wikidata_description": "city in Ecuador, capital of the Chimborazo Province"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ambato", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4072, "target_cluster_name": "Hambato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambato and Riobamba are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. They were significant centers during the colonial period and are often mentioned together in historical accounts of the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ambato and Riobamba are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. They were significant centers during the colonial period and are often mentioned together in historical accounts of the region."}, {"found_name": "Antisana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1867, "target_cluster_name": "Antisana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antisana is a volcano in Ecuador, near Riobamba. It is a prominent geographical feature in the same region and would have been a known landmark to inhabitants and travelers in the vicinity of Riobamba.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antisana is a volcano in Ecuador, near Riobamba. It is a prominent geographical feature in the same region and would have been a known landmark to inhabitants and travelers in the vicinity of Riobamba."}, {"found_name": "Chimborazo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 708, "target_cluster_name": "Chimborazo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chimborazo is a volcano in Ecuador, very close to Riobamba and historically considered the highest mountain in the world by some. It is a defining geographical feature of the region where Riobamba is located.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chimborazo is a volcano in Ecuador, very close to Riobamba and historically considered the highest mountain in the world by some. It is a defining geographical feature of the region where Riobamba is located."}, {"found_name": "Cotopaxi", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1777, "target_cluster_name": "Cotopaxi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cotopaxi is a volcano in Ecuador, north of Riobamba. It is a major geographical landmark in the Andean region of Ecuador and would have been known in relation to Riobamba.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cotopaxi is a volcano in Ecuador, north of Riobamba. It is a major geographical landmark in the Andean region of Ecuador and would have been known in relation to Riobamba."}, {"found_name": "Carguaírazo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3464, "target_cluster_name": "Carguairazo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carguaírazo is a mountain in Ecuador, near Riobamba. It is a significant geographical feature in the same region as Riobamba.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carguaírazo is a mountain in Ecuador, near Riobamba. It is a significant geographical feature in the same region as Riobamba."}, {"found_name": "Guancabamba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2751, "target_cluster_name": "Guancabamba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Riobamba is a city in Ecuador, and Huancabamba is also in Ecuador. They are geographically proximate and often mentioned in the same historical or geographical contexts within Ecuador.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Riobamba is a city in Ecuador, and Huancabamba is also in Ecuador. They are geographically proximate and often mentioned in the same historical or geographical contexts within Ecuador.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "absorption", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "absorption", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "absorption", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["absorption"], "contexts": ["chemical process", "chemical reaction"]}, {"entity_id": "absorption", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "absorption", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Absorption", "absorption"], "contexts": ["process of light being taken in", "taking up of light"]}, {"entity_id": "absorptions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Absorptions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Absorptions"], "contexts": ["taking in of heat energy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "absorption", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Absorptions", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 2054, "stable_key": "clu_d8ccdf2e6f1a6004", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Absorption", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "The emissivity of the surface of a material is its effectiveness in emitting energy as thermal radiation. Thermal radiation is electromagnetic radiation that most commonly includes both visible radiation (light) and infrared radiation, which is not visible to human eyes. A portion of the thermal radiation from very hot objects (see photograph) is easily visible to the eye. The emissivity of a surface depends on its chemical composition and geometrical structure. Quantitatively, it is the ratio of the thermal radiation from a surface to the radiation from an ideal black surface at the same temperature as given by the Stefan–Boltzmann law. (A comparison with Planck's law is used if one is concerned with particular wavelengths of thermal radiation.) The ratio varies from 0 to 1 for ordinary surfaces.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emissivity"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "color", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 407, "target_cluster_name": "color", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Color perception is related to an object's light absorption.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Color perception is related to an object's light absorption.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nutrition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1041, "target_cluster_name": "nutrition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Absorption is the critical step in nutrition where nutrients are taken into the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Absorption is the critical step in nutrition where nutrients are taken into the body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "reverberation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1409, "target_cluster_name": "reverberation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Absorption is the inverse process of reverberation, where sound energy is diminished rather than persisting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Absorption is the inverse process of reverberation, where sound energy is diminished rather than persisting.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Heat absorption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4535, "target_cluster_name": "absorbed heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Absorption is a fundamental process in the transfer of heat, and 'Heat absorption' directly names this process in relation to heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Absorption is a fundamental process in the transfer of heat, and 'Heat absorption' directly names this process in relation to heat."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "spitzbergen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spitzbergen"], "contexts": ["location visited by Captain Sabine"]}, {"entity_id": "spitzbergen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Spitzbergen"], "contexts": ["Arctic archipelago", "location with slow magnetic declination change"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Spitzbergen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Spitzbergen", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2055, "stable_key": "clu_92c6a64d9251636d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Svalbard", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Norway", "note": "Arctic archipelago, historically known as Spitsbergen.", "wikidata_id": "Q21162", "wikidata_description": "largest island of the Svalbard archipelago in northern Norway", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spitsbergen", "wikipedia_extract": "Spitsbergen (Urban East Norwegian: [ˈspɪ̀tsˌbærɡn̩]; formerly known as West Spitsbergen; Norwegian: Vest Spitsbergen or Vestspitsbergen [ˈvɛ̂stˌspɪtsbærɡn̩], also sometimes spelled Spitzbergen) is the largest and the only permanently populated island of the Svalbard archipelago in northern Norway in the Arctic Ocean. Constituting the westernmost bulk of the archipelago, it borders the Arctic Ocean, the Norwegian Sea and the Greenland Sea. Spitsbergen covers an area of 37,673 km2 (14,546 sq mi), making it the largest island in Norway and the 36th largest in the world. The administrative centre is Longyearbyen. Other settlements, in addition to research outposts, are the mining community of Barentsburg, the research community of Ny-Ålesund, and the mining outpost of Sveagruva. Spitsbergen was covered in 21,977 km2 (8,485 sq mi) of ice in 1999, which was approximately 58.5% of the island's total area."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Svalbard is an archipelago in the Arctic region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Svalbard is an archipelago in the Arctic region."}, {"found_name": "Norway", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 342, "target_cluster_name": "Norwège", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Svalbard is part of Norway.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Svalbard is part of Norway."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Praticos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "praticiens", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "praticiens", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["praticiens"], "contexts": ["practitioners of sugar making"]}, {"entity_id": "praticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Praticos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 10, "variants": ["Praticos", "Practicos"], "contexts": ["experienced medical practitioners", "experienced practitioners"]}, {"entity_id": "doctor", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "doctor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctor"], "contexts": ["medical practitioner"]}, {"entity_id": "dottori", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Dottori", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 5, "variants": ["Dottori", "Dottori Media"], "contexts": ["medical authorities", "doctors who prescribe medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "docto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Docto", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Docto"], "contexts": ["experienced physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "praticiens", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Praticos", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 2056, "stable_key": "clu_51afd624f405468f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Doctor", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A medical practitioner or physician", "wikidata_id": "Q39631", "wikidata_description": "professional who practices medicine", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical%20doctor", "note": "The term 'Praticos' is a plural noun in Portuguese/Spanish, referring to experienced individuals or practitioners.", "wikipedia_extract": "A medical doctor, also known as a physician (American and Canadian English) or medical practitioner (British English), is a health professional who practices medicine. Medicine aims to promote, maintain or restore health through the study, diagnosis, prognosis and treatment of disease, injury, and other physical and mental conditions. Doctors may focus on certain disease categories, types of patients, and methods of treatment, known as specialities. Others work in general practice, providing continuing and comprehensive medical care to individuals, families. Medical practice requires both thorough knowledge of the medical sciences—such as anatomy, physiology, diseases, and their treatment—and strong competence in applying this knowledge in practice, which forms the art or craft of the profession. Both the role of doctors and the meaning of the word itself vary around the world."}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Anatomist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3760, "target_cluster_name": "Anatomicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anatomists are medical practitioners who specialize in the study of 