ia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cyprus is an island in the Eastern Mediterranean, historically and geographically linked to Anatolia."}, {"found_name": "Izmir", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3440, "target_cluster_name": "Smyrne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Izmir (Smyrna) is a major city located in Anatolia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Izmir (Smyrna) is a major city located in Anatolia."}, {"found_name": "Constantinople", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 778, "target_cluster_name": "Constantinopla", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Constantinople (modern Istanbul) is a major city located in Anatolia, historically significant for its position bridging Europe and Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Constantinople (modern Istanbul) is a major city located in Anatolia, historically significant for its position bridging Europe and Asia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Greenwich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "greenwich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Greenwich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Greenwich"], "contexts": ["location of Observatory"]}, {"entity_id": "greenwich", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Greenwich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Greenwich"], "contexts": ["meridian for time computation", "meridian for astronomical computations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Greenwich", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Greenwich", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 2071, "stable_key": "clu_a956ab02787307a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Greenwich", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q693450", "wikidata_description": "royal borough in the London Region in England", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Borough_of_Greenwich", "wikipedia_extract": "The Royal Borough of Greenwich ( , ,  or ) is a London borough in southeast Greater London, England. The London Borough of Greenwich was formed in 1965 by the London Government Act 1963. The new borough covered the former area of the Metropolitan Borough of Greenwich and most of the Metropolitan Borough of Woolwich to the east. The local council is Greenwich London Borough Council which meets in Woolwich Town Hall. The Royal Borough of Greenwich is a large borough covering almost 50 km² and containing a broad mix of historic, suburban, and rapidly redeveloping districts. It includes the Greenwich Town and Park, the site of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Maritime Greenwich which encompasses the Prime Meridian, the Royal Observatory, Cutty Sark, and the former Royal Naval College."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "corteza", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "corteza", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "corteza", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["corteza", "Corteza de vir arbol", "cor- teza", "correza"], "contexts": ["bark used for medicine", "bark of the tree, aromatic and with good effect"]}, {"entity_id": "corteza", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "corteza", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["corteza", "cortex"], "contexts": ["bark, specifically cinnamon bark", "Latin for bark"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "corteza", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "corteza", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2072, "stable_key": "clu_cffa419f7f6c2b6e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cinnamon bark", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cinnamomum verum", "family": "Lauraceae", "note": "The term 'corteza' in the context of Monardes and Orta, known for their work on New World and Eastern medicinal plants, strongly suggests cinnamon bark, especially given its frequent medicinal use and prominence in early modern pharmacopoeias. 'Cinnamomum verum' is the most commonly recognized species."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cassia", "link_type": "contested_identity", "link_strength": 0.7, "target_cluster_id": 317, "target_cluster_name": "cássia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, cassia and cinnamon were often confused or debated as to whether they were the same species or distinct, though both derived from the genus Cinnamomum.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_contested", "found_relationship": "Historically, cassia and cinnamon were often confused or debated as to whether they were the same species or distinct, though both derived from the genus Cinnamomum."}, {"found_name": "Amomum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2029, "target_cluster_name": "Amomum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amomum is a genus closely related to Cinnamomum, and some species within Amomum have been historically used similarly to or confused with cinnamon.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amomum is a genus closely related to Cinnamomum, and some species within Amomum have been historically used similarly to or confused with cinnamon."}]}, {"canonical_name": "mordedura", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "mordeduras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "mordeduras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["mordeduras", "mordeduras de animales venenosos", "mordeduras de serpientes", "mordeduras venenoſas"], "contexts": ["bites from venomous animals", "poisonous bites"]}, {"entity_id": "mordedura", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mordedura", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 7, "variants": ["mordedura"], "contexts": ["bite, the condition being treated", "injury from biting"]}, {"entity_id": "venemous_beast", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "venemous Beast", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["venemous Beast", "Venem", "Venemous Beast"], "contexts": ["biting treated with ash-tree parts", "bites treated by the herb"]}, {"entity_id": "venenoso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "venenoso", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["venenoso"], "contexts": ["poisonous", "quality of being poisonous"]}, {"entity_id": "bicho_venenoso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bicho venenoso", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bicho venenoso"], "contexts": ["venomous creature bite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "mordeduras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mordedura", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 2073, "stable_key": "clu_b5acc1259767a24a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venomous bite", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Venomous bite", "note": "The context of Monardes, who described New World fauna and their medicinal uses, and the general understanding of 'mordedura' in medical texts of the era, points to bites from venomous animals as the primary referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q39579114", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on April 24, 2012"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Suores", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "sudores", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sudores", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sudores"], "contexts": ["sweating as a healing practice"]}, {"entity_id": "suores", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Suores", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Suores", "suores", "súores"], "contexts": ["sweating", "sweating, a medical practice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "sudores", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Suores", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 2074, "stable_key": "clu_145f2d1b3be2ccd0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Therapeutic sweating", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of medicinal texts and practices strongly indicates that 'Suores' or 'sudores' refers to sweating as a deliberate therapeutic intervention, often induced to treat various ailments."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "evacuação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1559, "target_cluster_name": "evacuação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Therapeutic sweating is a specific type of bodily evacuation aimed at healing.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Therapeutic sweating is a specific type of bodily evacuation aimed at healing.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cauterio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1576, "target_cluster_name": "cauterio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both cauterization and therapeutic sweating were methods used in historical medicine to expel unwanted substances or humors from the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both cauterization and therapeutic sweating were methods used in historical medicine to expel unwanted substances or humors from the body.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arménia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "arménia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Arménia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Arménia"], "contexts": ["region of origin for substances", "country where benjuy is not found"]}, {"entity_id": "armenia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Armenia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Armenia"], "contexts": ["place where borrace is found", "region of origin for a substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Arménia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Armenia", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 2075, "stable_key": "clu_d5747a3821975819", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Armenia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in Western Asia and Eastern Europe.", "note": "Refers to the geographical region and country.", "wikipedia_extract": "Armenia, officially the Republic of Armenia, is a landlocked country in the Armenian highlands of West Asia. It is a part of the Caucasus region and is bordered by Turkey to the west, Georgia to the north, Azerbaijan to the east, and Iran and the Azerbaijani exclave of Nakhchivan to the south. Yerevan is the capital, largest city and financial center. The Armenian highlands have been home to the Hayasa-Azzi, Shupria, and Nairi peoples. By at least 600 BC, an archaic form of Proto-Armenian, an Indo-European language, had diffused into the Armenian highlands. The first Armenian state of Urartu was established in 860 BC, and by the 6th century BC it was replaced by the Satrapy of Armenia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Persia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 168, "target_cluster_name": "Persia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Armenia is geographically adjacent to and historically intertwined with Persia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Armenia is geographically adjacent to and historically intertwined with Persia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cauca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1817, "target_cluster_name": "Cauca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Armenia is listed as a component region of the Caucasus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Armenia is listed as a component region of the Caucasus.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "espadana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "espadana", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "espadana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["espadana"], "contexts": ["plant, possibly iris", "plant with similar leaves to the fruit's leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "espadana", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "espadana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["espadana", "espadanal"], "contexts": ["sword lily root", "roots used in baths"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "espadana", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espadana", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2076, "stable_key": "clu_358a9ac6cf98fa99", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sword lily root", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Iris germanica", "family": "Iridaceae", "description": "Root of the sword lily (Iris), used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Iris × germanica is the accepted name for a species of flowering plants in the family Iridaceae commonly known as the bearded iris or the German bearded iris. It is of hybrid origin. Varieties include I. × g. var. florentina. Iris × germanica grows up to 120 cm (47 in) high and 30 cm (12 in) wide. The roots can go up to 10 cm (3.9 in) deep and it is a rhizomatous perennial that blooms mid to late spring. Hundreds of cultivars exist representing nearly every colour from jet black to sparkling whites and red."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Root", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 536, "target_cluster_name": "Root", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a specific type of root, making it a direct example of the entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a specific type of root, making it a direct example of the entity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lirio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2857, "target_cluster_name": "Lirio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sword lily root (espadana) is a specific part of a plant that shares the common name 'lily' and is likely related botanically or in historical usage.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sword lily root (espadana) is a specific part of a plant that shares the common name 'lily' and is likely related botanically or in historical usage.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "adrac", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai; e a raiz he", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "imgi", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai; e a raiz he", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "o/fá", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Bengallas, quando he verde lhe chamam adrac, e sequo sucte; e o Ma- lavar em verde e em sequo imgi, e em Malaio se chama o/fá. E he huma raiz e erva como espadana, ou como lirío espadanai; e a raiz he", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Algalia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "algalia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "algalia", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["algalia", "algualia"], "contexts": ["used for cold humor pains", "substance with strong smell"]}, {"entity_id": "algalia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Algalia", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["algalia", "Algalia"], "contexts": ["civet used in preparation", "civet, warm paste for abdominal pain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "algalia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Algalia", "similarity": 0.671}], "id": 2077, "stable_key": "clu_289605e9e9afedd5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Civet", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Viverra civetta", "family": "Viverridae", "description": "Substance with strong odor from civet cat, used in perfumery and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "A civet () is a small, lean, mostly nocturnal mammal native to tropical Asia and Africa, especially the tropical forests. The term civet applies to over a dozen different species, mostly from the family Viverridae. Most of the species's diversity is found in southeast Asia. Civets do not form a monophyletic group, as they consist only of certain members of the Viverridae and Eupleridae. The African civet, Civettictis civetta, has historically been the main species from which a musky scent used in perfumery, also referred to as \"civet\", was obtained. The common name is used for a variety of carnivoran mammal species, mostly of the family Viverridae."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ambergris", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1646, "target_cluster_name": "Ambar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both civet and ambergris were historically used as valuable perfumery ingredients and fixatives in medicine and cosmetics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both civet and ambergris were historically used as valuable perfumery ingredients and fixatives in medicine and cosmetics."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rey de Portugal", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "elrey_de_portugal", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "elrey de Portugal", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["elrey de Portugal", "Rey de Portugal"], "contexts": ["king of Portugal", "ruler of Portugal"]}, {"entity_id": "rey_de_portugal", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rey de Portugal", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 8, "variants": ["Rey de Portugal", "Rey da Perſia", "Rey de Polonia"], "contexts": ["King of Portugal", "D. Joao o IV. Rey de Portugal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "elrey de Portugal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rey de Portugal", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2078, "stable_key": "clu_d538e8bd93fb6a66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "King of Portugal", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Monarch of the Portuguese kingdom, patron of exploration and science.", "note": "This refers to the reigning monarch of Portugal during the period of publication of these texts, who often served as patrons for scientific and exploration endeavors.", "wikidata_id": "Q58800860", "wikidata_description": "Head of State of the Kingdom of Portugal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1856, "target_cluster_name": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha held significant positions within the Portuguese monarchy, implying a close relationship to the King of Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha held significant positions within the Portuguese monarchy, implying a close relationship to the King of Portugal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Afonso V of Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3733, "target_cluster_name": "Dom Affonio V", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'."}, {"found_name": "Manuel I of Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3875, "target_cluster_name": "Emanuel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Manuel I was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Manuel I was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'."}, {"found_name": "King", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1604, "target_cluster_name": "elrey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'King' is a generic title that encompasses 'King of Portugal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "'King' is a generic title that encompasses 'King of Portugal'."}, {"found_name": "Catholic Monarchs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4392, "target_cluster_name": "Catholic monarchs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Catholic Monarchs were rulers of Spain, who had significant political and dynastic connections with the Kings of Portugal, particularly during periods of Iberian Union.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Catholic Monarchs were rulers of Spain, who had significant political and dynastic connections with the Kings of Portugal, particularly during periods of Iberian Union."}, {"found_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2083, "target_cluster_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga's description mentions 'The Portuguese state bega', implying a connection to the Portuguese monarchy or state apparatus, which would be headed by a King of Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga's description mentions 'The Portuguese state bega', implying a connection to the Portuguese monarchy or state apparatus, which would be headed by a King of Portugal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "governador", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2773, "target_cluster_name": "governador", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific instance of a King to whom a Governor might be subordinate.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A specific instance of a King to whom a Governor might be subordinate.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cereijas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "cerejas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cerejas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["cerejas", "cereaes"], "contexts": ["fruit that rots and causes fevers", "fruit, likely cherries"]}, {"entity_id": "cereijas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cereijas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["cereijas", "Cereijas", "cereijasnegras", "cerejas"], "contexts": ["fruit used to wrap medicines", "eaten with cold water, caused hiccups"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cerejas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cereijas", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 2079, "stable_key": "clu_24a2723e9ceb2c9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cherries", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Prunus avium", "family": "Rosaceae", "description": "Sweet, red fruit of the cherry tree, used in food and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Prunus avium, commonly called wild cherry, sweet cherry or gean, is a species of cherry, a flowering plant in the rose family, Rosaceae. It is native to Eurasia and naturalized elsewhere. It is an ancestor of P. cerasus (sour cherry). All parts of the plant except for the ripe fruit are slightly toxic, containing cyanogenic glycosides. The species is often cultivated as an ornamental tree. Prunus avium is a deciduous tree growing to 5–25 metres (16–82 feet) tall, with a trunk up to 1.5 m (5 ft) in diameter."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Evora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "évora", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Évora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Évora"], "contexts": ["city in Portugal", "city where book was published"]}, {"entity_id": "evora", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Evora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Evora"], "contexts": ["city where the Archbishop resided", "city where patient resided"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Évora", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Evora", "similarity": 0.733}], "id": 2080, "stable_key": "clu_0050a36d1a68d8c5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Évora", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historic city in Portugal, known for its Roman and medieval heritage.", "wikipedia_extract": "Évora, officially the Very Noble and Ever Loyal City of Évora, is a city and a municipality in Portugal. It has 53,591 inhabitants (2021), in an area of 1,307.08 square kilometers (504.67 mi2). It is considered the historic capital of the Alentejo region and serves as the seat of the Évora District. Due to its well-preserved old town centre, still partially enclosed by medieval walls, and many monuments dating from various historical periods, including a Roman Temple, Évora is a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1986. Due to its inland position, Évora is one of Portugal's hottest cities in the summer, frequently subject to heatwaves. Évora is ranked number two in the Portuguese most livable cities survey of living conditions published yearly by Expresso. It was ranked first in a study concerning competitiveness of the 18 Portuguese district capitals, according to a 2006 study made by University of Minho economics researchers."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alem-Tejo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2336, "target_cluster_name": "Alem-Tejo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Évora is a major city located within the Alentejo region and served as its historical capital.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Évora is a major city located within the Alentejo region and served as its historical capital.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Elvas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2473, "target_cluster_name": "Elvas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Évora is another significant city in the Alentejo region of Portugal, often mentioned in proximity to Elvas in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Évora is another significant city in the Alentejo region of Portugal, often mentioned in proximity to Elvas in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Optalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "obtalmia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "obtalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["obtalmia", "ophtalmias"], "contexts": ["medical condition discussed", "eye disease treated with lycium"]}, {"entity_id": "optalmia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Optalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Optalmia"], "contexts": ["chapter title about eye inflammation", "inflammation of the eye's tunic"]}, {"entity_id": "ophthalmiis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ophthalmiis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ophthalmiis"], "contexts": ["eye disease"]}, {"entity_id": "ophthalmia", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ophthalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ophthalmia"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the eye"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "obtalmia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Optalmia", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 2081, "stable_key": "clu_fd278b9facdc3b83", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ophthalmia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Ophthalmia", "wikidata_id": "Q18558194", "wikidata_description": "Human disease", "note": "Both 'Ophthalmiis' and 'ophthalmia' directly refer to inflammation of the eye."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Florença", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "florença", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Florença", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Florença"], "contexts": ["city of the Bardi house", "city of the Bardi commercial house"]}, {"entity_id": "florença", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Florença", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Florença"], "contexts": ["city associated with a Grand Duke", "city associated with the Grand Duke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Florença", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Florença", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2082, "stable_key": "clu_859bcad1a3aec5e8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Florence", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city in Tuscany, Italy, a center of Renaissance art and commerce.", "note": "Refers to the city and its historical significance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Florence (Italian: Firenze) is the capital and most populous city of the Italian region of Tuscany, with 362,353 inhabitants as of 2025. It is also the capital of the eponymous metropolitan province, which counts 989,460 inhabitants. Florence was a centre of medieval European trade and finance and one of the wealthiest cities of that era. It is considered by many academics to have been the birthplace of the Renaissance, becoming a major artistic, cultural, commercial, political, economic and financial center. During this time, Florence rose to a position of enormous influence in Italy, Europe, and beyond. Its turbulent political history includes periods of rule by the powerful Medici family and numerous religious and republican revolutions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Tuscany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2151, "target_cluster_name": "Toscana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Florence is the capital of the Italian region of Tuscany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Florence is the capital of the Italian region of Tuscany."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "thomaz_rodrigues_da_veiga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Thomaz Rodrigues da Veiga", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomaz Rodrigues da Veiga"], "contexts": ["doctor whose lessons were heard in Coimbra"]}, {"entity_id": "thomas_rodriguez_da_veiga", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "Thomas Rodericus", "Thomas Rodriguez da Veyga"], "contexts": ["author recommending bloodletting for inflammations", "Portuguese scholar on medicine and nation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Thomaz Rodrigues da Veiga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 2083, "stable_key": "clu_6b78a770a7eb5af4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Portuguese scholar and physician, author of medicinal works.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Portuguese people (Portuguese: Portugueses – masculine – or Portuguesas) are a Romance-speaking ethnic group and nation indigenous to Portugal, a country that occupies the west side of the Iberian Peninsula in south-west Europe, who share culture, ancestry and language. The Portuguese state began with the founding of the County of Portugal in 868. Following the Battle of São Mamede (1128), Portugal gained international recognition as a kingdom through the Treaty of Zamora and the papal bull Manifestis Probatum. This Portuguese state paved the way for the Portuguese people to unite as a nation. The Portuguese explored distant lands previously unknown to Europeans—in the Americas, Africa, Asia and Oceania (southwest Pacific Ocean). In 1415, with the conquest of Ceuta, the Portuguese took a significant role in the Age of Discovery, which culminated in a colonial empire."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Afonso V of Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3733, "target_cluster_name": "Dom Affonio V", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are Portuguese figures, suggesting a potential connection within the same national context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are Portuguese figures, suggesting a potential connection within the same national context."}, {"found_name": "King of Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2078, "target_cluster_name": "Rey de Portugal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga's description mentions 'The Portuguese state bega', implying a connection to the Portuguese monarchy or state apparatus, which would be headed by a King of Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga's description mentions 'The Portuguese state bega', implying a connection to the Portuguese monarchy or state apparatus, which would be headed by a King of Portugal."}]}, {"canonical_name": "onça", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "oncia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "oncia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["oncia"], "contexts": ["ounce", "unit of weight"]}, {"entity_id": "onça", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "onça", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["onça"], "contexts": ["ounce", "measure of weight"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "oncia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "onça", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 2084, "stable_key": "clu_9276b17153f3c5e1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ounce", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A unit of weight, commonly 1/12th of a pound.", "wikipedia_extract": "The  ounce () is any of several different units of mass, weight, or volume and is derived almost unchanged from the uncia, an Ancient Roman unit of measurement. The avoirdupois ounce (exactly 28.349523125 g) is 1⁄16 avoirdupois pound; this is the United States customary and British imperial ounce. It is primarily used in the United States. Although the avoirdupois ounce is the mass measure used for most purposes, the 'troy ounce' of exactly 31.1034768 g is used instead for the mass of precious metals such as gold, silver, platinum, palladium, rhodium, etc. The term 'ounce' is also used in other contexts: The ounce-force is a measure of force (see below). Historically, a variety of different ounces measuring mass or volume were used in different jurisdictions by different trades and at different times in history."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "raiz de peracitis", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "remedio, Tomemde raizdeperacitis ,chamada vulgarmente ſombreyra , meya onça, dezedoaria outra meya onça, dealambre preparado outra meya onça,de contra-hierva huma onça, de criſtal preparado,& dealmiſc", "confidence": 0.8, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "an ingredient, identified by a vernacular name and a Latinate term"}, {"found_name": "sombreyra", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "remedio, Tomemde raizdeperacitis ,chamada vulgarmente ſombreyra , meya onça, dezedoaria outra meya onça, dealambre preparado outra meya onça,de contra-hierva huma onça, de criſtal preparado,& dealmiſc", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a vernacular name for raiz de peracitis"}]}, {"canonical_name": "decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "decomposition", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["decomposition", "Decomposition"], "contexts": ["breaking down into constituent parts", "breaking down substances"]}, {"entity_id": "decomposition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["decomposition"], "contexts": ["process of decay in a dead body", "process of rotting or decay"]}], "edg