f8cb8d1455d0b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Duke of Bavaria", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A noble patron, likely supporting scientific or medical endeavors.", "note": "Refers to historical rulers of Bavaria.", "wikipedia_extract": "The following is a list of rulers during the history of Bavaria. Bavaria was ruled by several dukes and kings, partitioned and reunited, under several dynasties. Since 1918, Bavaria has been under a republican form of government, and from 1949, Bavaria has been a democratic state in the Federal Republic of Germany. Ducal Bavaria (also known as the \"Old Stem duchy\") Around 548 the kings of the Franks placed the border region of Bavaria under the administration of a duke—possibly Frankish or possibly chosen from amongst the local leading families—who was supposed to act as a regional governor for the Frankish king. The first duke we know of, and likely the first, was Gariwald, or Garibald I, a member of the powerful Agilolfing family. This was the beginning of a series of Agilolfing dukes that was to last until 788."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dukes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3307, "target_cluster_name": "Dukes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Duke of Bavaria' is a specific instance of the general title 'Dukes'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Duke of Bavaria' is a specific instance of the general title 'Dukes'."}, {"found_name": "Emperor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2523, "target_cluster_name": "Emperadores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bavarian rulers, including Dukes, often had complex relationships with the Holy Roman Emperor, and some later became Kings of Bavaria, a title closely related to Emperor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bavarian rulers, including Dukes, often had complex relationships with the Holy Roman Emperor, and some later became Kings of Bavaria, a title closely related to Emperor."}, {"found_name": "King", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1604, "target_cluster_name": "elrey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bavaria was ruled by Kings in addition to Dukes, and these titles represent sovereign rulers of the same territory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bavaria was ruled by Kings in addition to Dukes, and these titles represent sovereign rulers of the same territory."}, {"found_name": "Princes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2143, "target_cluster_name": "Principes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Duke of Bavaria held princely status within the Holy Roman Empire and later the German Confederation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Duke of Bavaria held princely status within the Holy Roman Empire and later the German Confederation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "molluscs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "molluscs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "molluscs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Molluscs", "molluscs"], "contexts": ["extinct Silurian molluscs that changed greatly", "animal class"]}, {"entity_id": "mollusca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mollusca", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["mollusca"], "contexts": ["main division of muscle shells", "aquatic invertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "mollusca", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mollusca", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mollusca"], "contexts": ["water-breathing classes adapted to land"]}, {"entity_id": "mollusks", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mollusks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mollusks"], "contexts": ["animals of shells", "distinct species of"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "molluscs", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mollusca", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 2101, "stable_key": "clu_883976b5a02f1807", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mollusks", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Invertebrate animals with soft bodies, often protected by a shell.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mollusca is a phylum of protostomic invertebrate animals, whose members are known as molluscs or mollusks (). 86,600 extant species of molluscs are recognized, making it the second-largest animal phylum after Arthropoda. The number of additional fossil species is estimated between 60,000 and 100,000, and the proportion of undescribed species is very high. Many taxa remain poorly studied. Molluscs are the largest marine phylum, comprising about 23% of all the named marine organisms. They are highly diverse, not just in size and anatomical structure, but also in behavior and habitat, as numerous groups are freshwater and even terrestrial species. The phylum is typically divided into 7 or 8 taxonomic classes, of which two are entirely extinct."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ammonites", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 847, "target_cluster_name": "Ammonites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammonites are a type of extinct mollusk.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammonites are a type of extinct mollusk.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "shell-fish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1016, "target_cluster_name": "shell-fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Shellfish is a broad category that historically included mollusks, though modern classification separates them. Texts from the period often grouped them together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Shellfish is a broad category that historically included mollusks, though modern classification separates them. Texts from the period often grouped them together.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Invertebrata", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1434, "target_cluster_name": "Invertebrata", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mollusks are a major phylum within the Invertebrates.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mollusks are a major phylum within the Invertebrates.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "animaes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1810, "target_cluster_name": "animaes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mollusks are a large phylum of invertebrate animals, belonging to the kingdom Animalia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mollusks are a large phylum of invertebrate animals, belonging to the kingdom Animalia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cephalopodes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1187, "target_cluster_name": "Cephalopodes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cephalopodes are a class within the phylum Mollusca.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Cephalopodes are a class within the phylum Mollusca."}, {"found_name": "Trigonia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3361, "target_cluster_name": "Trigonia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trigonia is a genus of bivalve mollusks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Trigonia is a genus of bivalve mollusks."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bermuda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "bermuda", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Bermuda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Bermuda"], "contexts": ["island with peculiar soil and no endemic land birds", "island stocked by birds"]}, {"entity_id": "iles_bermudes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Iles Bermudes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Iles Bermudes", "Bermudes"], "contexts": ["group of islands", "archipelago location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bermuda", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Iles Bermudes", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 2102, "stable_key": "clu_220d490d5daf5847", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bermuda", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An archipelago and British Overseas Territory in the North Atlantic Ocean.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bermuda is a British Overseas Territory in the North Atlantic Ocean. The closest land outside the territory is in the U.S. state of North Carolina, about 1,035 km (643 mi) to the west-northwest. Bermuda is an archipelago consisting of 181 islands, although the most significant islands are connected by bridges and appear to form one landmass. It has a land area of 54 square kilometres (21 sq mi). Bermuda has a tropical climate, with warm winters and hot summers. Its climate also exhibits oceanic features similar to other coastal areas in the Northern Hemisphere with warm, moist air from the ocean ensuring relatively high humidity and stabilising temperatures."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bermuda is an archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bermuda is an archipelago."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "dr__falconer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Dr. Falconer", "Falconer"], "contexts": ["source of information on Indian plants", "informs about tropics' destructive damp and heat"]}, {"entity_id": "dr__falconer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dr. Falconer"], "contexts": ["investigator of Sewalik Hills"]}, {"entity_id": "falconer,_dr_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Falconer, Dr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falconer, Dr."], "contexts": ["researcher of fossil remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Dr. Falconer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dr. Falconer", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Dr. Falconer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Falconer, Dr.", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 2103, "stable_key": "clu_0369536c150c790e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hugh Falconer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1808, "death_year": 1865, "description": "Scottish surgeon, botanist, paleontologist, and geologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q76037335", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=485474"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mr. Blyth", "link_type": "conceptual_ove