es; natural environment in which an organism lives, or the physical environment that surrounds a species population"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Economy of nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1147, "target_cluster_name": "economy of nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of 'habits of life' was often considered within the broader framework of the 'economy of nature.' This relationship explored how the characteristic behaviors and routines of organisms contributed to the overall balance and functioning of ecosystems.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The concept of 'habits of life' was often considered within the broader framework of the 'economy of nature.' This relationship explored how the characteristic behaviors and routines of organisms contributed to the overall balance and functioning of ecosystems."}, {"found_name": "Bodily action", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3565, "target_cluster_name": "bodily action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits are a form of bodily action that has become automatic through repetition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits are a form of bodily action that has become automatic through repetition."}, {"found_name": "Bodily functions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3004, "target_cluster_name": "organic actions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits can be seen as a subset of bodily functions that are performed habitually.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits can be seen as a subset of bodily functions that are performed habitually."}, {"found_name": "Character", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 962, "target_cluster_name": "character", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits contribute significantly to an individual's character.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits contribute significantly to an individual's character."}, {"found_name": "Cognition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 656, "target_cluster_name": "cognition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The formation and maintenance of habits involve cognitive processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The formation and maintenance of habits involve cognitive processes."}, {"found_name": "Cognitive processes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2579, "target_cluster_name": "intellectual operations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits are formed through cognitive processes such as learning and memory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits are formed through cognitive processes such as learning and memory."}, {"found_name": "Consciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 204, "target_cluster_name": "consciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits often operate below the level of conscious awareness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits often operate below the level of conscious awareness."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Desires can lead to the formation of habits, and habits can, in turn, shape desires.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Desires can lead to the formation of habits, and habits can, in turn, shape desires."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits can be influenced by emotions and the exercise of will, and they can also influence emotional states and the capacity for voluntary action.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Habits can be influenced by emotions and the exercise of will, and they can also influence emotional states and the capacity for voluntary action."}, {"found_name": "personal habits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "like plaster, and will never soften again. If the period between twenty and thirty is the critical one in the formation of intellectual and professional habits, the period below twenty is more importa", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "specific type of habits"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "abyssinia", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Abyssinia"], "contexts": ["historical name for Ethiopia", "location with southern vegetable forms"]}, {"entity_id": "abyssinia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Abyssinia", "da Abyssinia"], "contexts": ["region of origin for cardamom", "region of origin for koraríma"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Abyssinia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Abyssinia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2106, "stable_key": "clu_5b7081f87703cc09", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ethiopia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in Eastern Africa, historically known as Abyssinia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ethiopia, officially the Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia, is a landlocked country located in the Horn of Africa region of East Africa. It shares borders with Eritrea to the north, Djibouti to the northeast, Somalia to the east, Kenya to the south, South Sudan to the west, and Sudan to the northwest. Ethiopia covers a land area of 1,104,300 square kilometres (426,400 sq mi). As of 2025, it has around 135 million inhabitants, making it the 14th-most populous country. The national capital and largest city, Addis Ababa, lies several kilometres west of the East African Rift that splits the country into the African and Somali tectonic plates. Anatomically modern humans emerged from modern-day Ethiopia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Etiópia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 657, "target_cluster_name": "Etiópia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Abyssinia is a historical name for Ethiopia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Abyssinia is a historical name for Ethiopia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Africa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3, "target_cluster_name": "Africa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ethiopia is a country located in Africa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ethiopia is a country located in Africa."}, {"found_name": "East Indies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 497, "target_cluster_name": "East Indies", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical geographical term that sometimes encompassed regions of Africa, including Ethiopia, in early European cartography and trade narratives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historical geographical term that sometimes encompassed regions of Africa, including Ethiopia, in early European cartography and trade narratives."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "berkeley", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Berkeley"], "contexts": ["assisted with experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "berkeley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Berkeley"], "contexts": ["philosopher of definite images theory", "philosopher who compared thought to algebra"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Berkeley", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Berkeley", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2107, "stable_key": "clu_8cc2fe6082ab0404", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "George Berkeley", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1685, "death_year": 1753, "description": "Irish philosopher and bishop, known for idealism", "note": "The context 'philosopher of definite images theory' strongly points to George Berkeley. The reference in 'origin_of_species_darwin_1859' is likely a misattribution or a secondary reference to his philosophical ideas.", "wikidata_id": "Q82049", "wikidata_description": "Irish idealist philosopher and Anglican bishop (1685–1753)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George%20Berkeley", "wikipedia_extract": "George Berkeley ( BARK-lee; 12 March 1685 – 14 January 1753), known as Bishop Berkeley (Bishop of Cloyne of the Anglican Church of Ireland), was an Anglo-Irish philosopher, writer, and clergyman who is regarded as the founder of \"immaterialism\", a philosophical theory he developed which later came to be known as subjective idealism. He has also been called \"the father of idealism\" by German philosopher Arthur Schopenhauer. Berkeley played a leading role in the empiricism movement and was one of its pioneers. He was among the most cited philosophers of 18th-century Europe, and his works deeply influenced later thinkers such as Immanuel Kant and David Hume. In 1709, Berkeley published his first major work An Essay Towards a New Theory of Vision, in which he discussed the limitations of human vision and advanced the theory that the proper objects of sight are not material objects, but light and colour. This foreshadowed his most well-known philosophical work A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge, published in 1710, which, after its poor reception, he rewrote in dialogue form and published under the title Three Dialogues Between Hylas and Philonous in 1713."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Philosopher", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 431, "target_cluster_name": "philosopher", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "George Berkeley was a prominent philosopher.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "George Berkeley was a prominent philosopher."}, {"found_name": "Clergyman", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 450, "target_cluster_name": "Clerigo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "George Berkeley was a clergyman.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "George Berkeley was a clergyman."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Toluca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "toluca", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Toluca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Toluca", "Tolu", "Tolù"], "contexts": ["city with road construction", "city in Mexico"]}, {"entity_id": "toluca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Toluca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Toluca"], "contexts": ["volcano with rocky pinnacles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Toluca", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Toluca", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2108, "stable_key": "clu_c6b015c96d24fe8f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Toluca de Lerdo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "References to Humboldt's works in the 19th century clearly indicate the city and volcano in Mexico."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "minas_geraes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 9, "variants": ["Minas Geraes", "Mina^ Geraes", "Minaes Geraes", "Minas-Geraes"], "contexts": ["region in Brazil", "state in Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "minas_geraes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Minas Geraes"], "contexts": ["diamond district in Brazil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Minas Geraes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Minas Geraes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2109, "stable_key": "clu_45eb2ebfe2e41d82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Minas Gerais", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q39109", "wikidata_description": "state in the Southeast Region of Brazil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belo_Horizonte", "wikipedia_extract": "Belo Horizonte is the sixth-largest city in Brazil, with a population of around 2.4 million, and the third largest metropolitan area, containing a population of 6 million. It is the 13th-largest city in South America and the 18th-largest in the Americas. The metropolis is anchor to the Belo Horizonte metropolitan area, ranked as the third most populous metropolitan area in Brazil and the 17th most populous in the Americas. Belo Horizonte is the capital of the state of Minas Gerais, Brazil's second-most populous state. It is the first planned modern city in Brazil. The region was first settled in the early 18th century, but the city as it is known today was planned and constructed in the 1890s to replace Ouro Preto as the capital of Minas Gerais."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "san_carlos_del_rio_negro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["San Carlos del Rio Negro"], "contexts": ["location linked astronomically", "settlement near Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "st__carlos_del_rio_negro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Carlos del Rio Negro"], "contexts": ["location with low magnetic force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Carlos del Rio Negro", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2110, "stable_key": "clu_89f874d38bba65bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "San Carlos de Río Negro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "A settlement and fort in Venezuela on the Rio Negro.", "wikidata_id": "Q1269422", "wikidata_description": "city", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San%20Carlos%20de%20R%C3%ADo%20Negro", "wikipedia_extract": "San Carlos de Río Negro is a city in Venezuela's Amazonas State, serving as the administrative capital of the municipal district of Río Negro. It is inhabited primarily by Amerindian people, in particular the Yanomami and Baniwa (Kurripako) peoples. It sits on the opposite side of the Rio Negro from the Colombian city of San Felipe. The city has approximately 1,200 inhabitants. The city of San Carlos de Río Negro was founded in 1759 as a camp set up by the expedition captained by José Solano y Bote. The expedition moved into the area to explore the extent of the limits of exploration as defined by the Treaty of Madrid between the Crowns of Portugal and Spain."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Equatorial America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1437, "target_cluster_name": "equatorial America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "San Carlos de Río Negro is located in Equatorial America.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "San Carlos de Río Negro is located in Equatorial America."}, {"found_name": "Venezuela", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1496, "target_cluster_name": "Venezuela", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "San Carlos de Río Negro is a city in Venezuela.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "San Carlos de Río Negro is a city in Venezuela."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Quevedo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "quevedo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Quevedo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Quevedo"], "contexts": ["captain of Spanish frigate", "recorded temperature maximum"]}], "edges": [], "id": 2111, "stable_key": "clu_f6926