", "evidence_snippet": "Antigua Guatemala was the former capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and is a significant historical city within the modern nation of Guatemala.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Antigua Guatemala was the former capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and is a significant historical city within the modern nation of Guatemala.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Guatemala", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1592, "target_cluster_name": "Guatimala", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antigua Guatemala was the capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and the name 'Guatemala' itself is often used to refer to the historical region and its capital.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antigua Guatemala was the capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, and the name 'Guatemala' itself is often used to refer to the historical region and its capital."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "honduras", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Honduras"], "contexts": ["gulf", "country in Central America"]}, {"entity_id": "honduras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Honduras"], "contexts": ["region with a type of gargaparilla", "a region where medicinal plants are sourced"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Honduras", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Honduras", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2113, "stable_key": "clu_7721f3f6cb05e33f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Honduras", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Country in Central America, known for its gulf and medicinal plants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Honduras, officially the Republic of Honduras, is a country in Central America. It is bordered to the west by Guatemala, to the southwest by El Salvador, to the southeast by Nicaragua, to the south by the Pacific Ocean at the Gulf of Fonseca, and to the north by the Gulf of Honduras, a large inlet of the Caribbean Sea. Its capital and largest city is Tegucigalpa. Honduras was home to several important Mesoamerican cultures, most notably the Maya, before Spanish colonization in the sixteenth century. The Spanish introduced Catholicism and the now-predominant Spanish language, along with numerous customs that have blended with the indigenous culture. Honduras became independent in 1821 and has since been a republic, although it has consistently endured much social strife and political instability, and remains one of the poorest countries in the Western Hemisphere."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caribbean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2776, "target_cluster_name": "Caribbean Islands", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honduras is bordered to the north by the Gulf of Honduras, a large inlet of the Caribbean Sea. The Caribbean region is geographically proximate and historically interconnected with Honduras.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Honduras is bordered to the north by the Gulf of Honduras, a large inlet of the Caribbean Sea. The Caribbean region is geographically proximate and historically interconnected with Honduras."}, {"found_name": "Central America", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1339, "target_cluster_name": "Central America", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honduras is a country in Central America. The candidate list does not contain 'Central America', but 'Americas' is a broader geographical region that encompasses Central America. However, the prompt asks for SPECIFIC relationships, and 'Americas' is too broad. Therefore, no relationship is listed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Honduras is a country in Central America. The candidate list does not contain 'Central America', but 'Americas' is a broader geographical region that encompasses Central America. However, the prompt asks for SPECIFIC relationships, and 'Americas' is too broad. Therefore, no relationship is listed."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "los_pastos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Los Pastos", "los Pastos"], "contexts": ["mountainous district in Spanish America", "province belonging to Quito"]}, {"entity_id": "los_pa_stos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Los Pa.stos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Los Pa.stos"], "contexts": ["plateau of a province", "province name"]}, {"entity_id": "los_pastos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Los Pastos"], "contexts": ["elevated plateau"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Los Pastos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Los Pastos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Los Pa.stos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Los Pastos", "similarity": 0.584}], "id": 2114, "stable_key": "clu_d5074ca64965aaef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Los Pastos", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "note": "Los Pastos refers to a high-altitude plateau region in southwestern Colombia, known for its mountainous terrain.", "wikidata_id": "Q3897521", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_extract": "Pasto, officially San Juan de Pasto (Spanish pronunciation: [saŋ ˈxwan de ˈpasto]; \"Saint John of Pasto\"), is the capital of the department of Nariño, in southern Colombia. Pasto was founded in 1537 and named after indigenous people of the area. In the 2018 census, the municipality had a population of 392,930. Pasto is located in the Atriz Valley on the Andes cordillera, at the foot of the Galeras volcano. The etymology of the word Pasto can be traced to the indigenous people who inhabited the region at the arrival of the Spanish conquerors, the Pastos. However, the Atriz Valley itself was inhabited by the Quillacingas.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasto,_Colombia"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antioquia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1757, "target_cluster_name": "Antioquia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antioquia is a department in Colombia, adjacent to the department of Nariño where Pasto is located. Both are significant regions within Colombia mentioned in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antioquia is a department in Colombia, adjacent to the department of Nariño where Pasto is located. Both are significant regions within Colombia mentioned in historical texts."}, {"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pasto is located in the Andes mountain range in southern Colombia. The Andes are a major geographical feature that defines the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pasto is located in the Andes mountain range in southern Colombia. The Andes are a major geographical feature that defines the region."}, {"found_name": "Bogotá", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1562, "target_cluster_name": "Bogota", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bogotá is the capital of Colombia, the country in which Pasto is located. Both are major cities within the same nation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bogotá is the capital of Colombia, the country in which Pasto is located. Both are major cities within the same nation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "lamanon", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lamanon"], "contexts": ["made observations", "work known to Cotte"]}, {"entity_id": "lamanon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lamanon"], "contexts": ["made observations on expedition", "author of important letter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lamanon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lamanon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2115, "stable_key": "clu_0970b8f8fa2ec061", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-François de Galaup, comte de Lapérouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1741, "death_year": 1788, "description": "French naval officer and explorer on Pacific expedition", "note": "The text mentions 'Lamanon' in the context of Humboldt's expedition accounts. Lamanon was a naturalist who accompanied Lapérouse on his ill-fated voyage. Humboldt frequently referenced Lapérouse's expedition.", "wikidata_id": "Q3436681", "wikidata_description": "French Navy officer and explorer (1741–1788)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Jean-François de La Rocque de Roqueburne", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4099, "target_cluster_name": "M. de Rossel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were naval officers and explorers, with Roqueburne serving as a hydrographer on Lapérouse's expedition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were naval officers and explorers, with Roqueburne serving as a hydrographer on Lapérouse's expedition."}, {"found_name": "Cosme Churruca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2039, "target_cluster_name": "Churruca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naval officers and cartographers involved in exploration and scientific expeditions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent naval officers and cartographers involved in exploration and scientific expeditions."}, {"found_name": "Felipe Bauzá", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1934, "target_cluster_name": "Bauza", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were naval officers and cartographers involved in significant maritime expeditions and surveys.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were naval officers and cartographers involved in significant maritime expeditions and surveys."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Péninsule", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "péninsule", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Péninsule", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Péninsule"], "contexts": ["Iberian Peninsula", "destination for tobacco"]}, {"entity_id": "península", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Península", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Península", "península"], "contexts": ["where name reached", "Iberian Peninsula"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Péninsule", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Península", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 2116, "stable_key": "clu_907fb6e3df65e146", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peninsula", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A landmass surrounded by water on three sides, such as the Iberian Peninsula.", "note": "Refers to a geographical feature, often the Iberian Peninsula in the context of these texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "A peninsula is a landform that extends from a mainland, is connected to the mainland on only one side, and is mostly surrounded by water. Peninsulas exist on each continent. The largest peninsula in the world is the Arabian Peninsula. The word peninsula derives from Latin  paeninsula, from  paene 'almost' and  insula 'island'. The word entered English in the 16th century. A peninsula is generally defined as a piece of land surrounded on most sides by water."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kamchatka Peninsula", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1028, "target_cluster_name": "Kamtschatka", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kamchatka Peninsula is a specific example of a peninsula, fitting the definition of the entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kamchatka Peninsula is a specific example of a peninsula, fitting the definition of the entity."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "charpentier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Charpentier", "Carpentier"], "contexts": ["author citation", "author on Pyrenean geology"]}, {"entity_id": "charpentier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Charpentier"], "contexts": ["author of 'Constitution Geologique des Pyrenes'", "author of geological work on Pyrenees"]}, {"entity_id": "m__charpentier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "M. Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Charpentier"], "contexts": ["geologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charpentier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Charpentier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charpentier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "M. Charpentier", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 2117, "stable_key": "clu_cf9b925e354b41a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jérôme-Charles-Alexandre Charpentier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1797, "death_year": 1857, "description": "French geologist and author of geological works", "note": "Charpentier is a known geologist and author of works cited by Humboldt, fitting the context."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Victoria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "victoria", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Victoria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Victoria"], "contexts": ["place name in Venezuela", "city in Aragua valleys"]}, {"entity_id": "victoria_land", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Victoria Land", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Victoria Land"], "contexts": ["area near magnetic south pole", "landmass extending from Cape Crozier"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Victoria", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Victoria Land", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2118, "stable_key": "clu_2b86c8eec43fc80b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Victoria", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Contexts suggest two distinct places named Victoria: one in Venezuela, and 'Victoria Land' near the South Pole.", "wikipedia_extract": "Victoria (Alexandrina Victoria; 24 May 1819 – 22 January 1901) was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland from 20 June 1837 until her death in 1901. Her reign of 63 years and 216 days, which was longer than those of any of her predecessors, constituted the Victorian era, a period of industrial, political, scientific, and military change within the United Kingdom marked by a great expansion of the British Empire. In 1876, the British parliament voted to grant her the additional title of Empress of India. Victoria was the daughter of Prince Edward, Duke of Kent and Strathearn (the fourth son of King George III), and Princess Victoria of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld. After the deaths of her father and grandfather in 1820, she was raised under close supervision by her mother and her comptroller, John Conroy. She inherited the throne aged 18 after her father's three elder brothers died without surviving legitimate issue.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Victoria"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Great Britain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 776, "target_cluster_name": "Great Britain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland."}, {"found_name": "England", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 65, "target_cluster_name": "England", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, which includes England.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, which includes England."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "pichincha", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pichincha"], "contexts": ["mountain range", "province in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "rucu-pichincha", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rucu-Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rucu-Pichincha"], "contexts": ["Volcano mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "pichincha", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pichincha"], "contexts": ["volcano near Quito", "mountain mentioned for its elongated form"]}, {"entity_id": "rucu_pichincha", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rucu Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rucu Pichincha"], "contexts": ["active volcano near Quito", "volcano location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pichincha", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rucu Pichincha", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rucu-Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rucu Pichincha", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 2119, "stable_key": "clu_257137a3d3e3dd77", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pichincha", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "note": "Refers to the Pichincha volcano and surrounding region.", "wikidata_id": "Q742216", "wikidata_description": "volcano in Quito Metro, Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "Quito (Spanish pronunciation: [ˈkito] ; Quechua: Kitu), officially San Francisco de Quito, is the capital and second-largest city of Ecuador, with an estimated population of 2.8 million in its metropolitan area. It is also the capital of the province of Pichincha. Quito is in a valley on the eastern slopes of Pichincha, an active stratovolcano in the Andes. Quito's elevation of 2,850 m (9,350 ft) makes it either the highest or the second highest national capital city in the world. This varied standing is because Bolivia is a country with multiple capitals; if La Paz is considered the Bolivian national capital, it tops the list of highest capitals, but if Sucre is specified as the capital, then it is the second highest, behind Quito. Quito is the political and cultural center of Ecuador as the country's major governmental, administrative, and cultural institutions are within the city.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quito"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pichincha is a volcano in the Andes mountain range.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pichincha is a volcano in the Andes mountain range."}, {"found_name": "Antisana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1867, "target_cluster_name": "Antisana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antisana is