ed by syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "corrimientos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "corrimentos", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 2138, "stable_key": "clu_253df3f9e96e437a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Discharges", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Abnormal bodily discharges, often indicating an ailment.", "note": "Refers to abnormal bodily flows or secretions, often treated as a medical condition.", "wikipedia_extract": "A vaginal disease is a pathological condition that affects part or all of the vagina. Sexually transmitted infections that affect the vagina include: Herpes genitalis. The herpes simplex virus (HSV) can infect the vulva, vagina, and cervix, and this may result in small, painful, recurring blisters and ulcers. It is also common for there to be an absence of any noticeable symptoms. Human papillomavirus (HPV), which may cause genital warts. Because of STIs, health authorities and other health outlets recommend safe sex practices when engaging in sexual activity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "encolhimentos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "poucas horasde cozimento viràfubindo a ribada agua boa quantidade doditooleo, cujas virtudes faōadmiraveis paramuytas enfermidades ,principalmente para corrimentos,&encolhimentosdos nervos , & partes ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of illness alongside 'corrimentos'"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "balſamo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Balſamo", "Balíamo"], "contexts": ["precious liquid with marvelous effects", "imitation of true Balsamo"]}, {"entity_id": "balſamo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Balſamo", "balſamo"], "contexts": ["precious medicinal part of the liquor", "balsam"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Balſamo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Balſamo", "similarity": 0.705}], "id": 2139, "stable_key": "clu_c4b1c7a801fc2ca4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A fragrant resinous substance obtained from certain trees, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Balsam is the resinous exudate (or sap) which forms on certain kinds of trees and shrubs. Balsam (from Latin balsamum \"gum of the balsam tree,\" ultimately from a Semitic source such as Hebrew: בֹּשֶׂם, romanized: bośem, lit. 'spice, perfume') owes its name to the biblical Balm of Gilead. Balsams often contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters. The Balsam of Matariyya was a substance famous as a panacea among physicians in the Middle East and Europe during the Antique and Medieval periods. The substance has long been used as a medicine, with early references to the substance recorded as far back as 285 BC. The Balsam of Matariyya was said to be derived from an Egyptian plant and is sometimes also referred to as the balm of Gilead or the balm of Mecca."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "resina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2624, "target_cluster_name": "resina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Balsam is a resinous substance derived from trees, often used medicinally.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Balsam is a resinous substance derived from trees, often used medicinally.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "alcanfor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1614, "target_cluster_name": "alcanfor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both camphor and balsam are aromatic resins used in historical medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both camphor and balsam are aromatic resins used in historical medicine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sapa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2362, "target_cluster_name": "Sapa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grape must reduction, known historically as Sapa, is a concentrated syrup derived from grape must. Balsam, in its various forms (e.g., Balsamis, Balſamo), refers to aromatic resins or oils, often with medicinal or perfumed properties. While distinct, both terms in historical texts can refer to viscous, concentrated, and often aromatic substances, sometimes with overlapping uses or descriptions in medicinal and culinary contexts. The term 'balsamic' in 'balsamic vinegar' itself suggests a connection to balsamic properties, implying a shared characteristic of concentrated, aromatic, and potentially medicinal qualities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Grape must reduction, known historically as Sapa, is a concentrated syrup derived from grape must. Balsam, in its various forms (e.g., Balsamis, Balſamo), refers to aromatic resins or oils, often with medicinal or perfumed properties. While distinct, both terms in historical texts can refer to viscous, concentrated, and often aromatic substances, sometimes with overlapping uses or descriptions in medicinal and culinary contexts. The term 'balsamic' in 'balsamic vinegar' itself suggests a connection to balsamic properties, implying a shared characteristic of concentrated, aromatic, and potentially medicinal qualities.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "roſto", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "roſtro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "roſtro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["roſtro"], "contexts": ["face"]}, {"entity_id": "roſto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "roſto", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["roſto"], "contexts": ["Part of the body affected by paralysis", "body part with color"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "roſtro", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "roſto", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 2140, "stable_key": "clu_76de30624e1d860a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Face", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q355", "wikidata_description": "American online social media and social networking service"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "pez", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pez"], "contexts": ["pitch"]}, {"entity_id": "pez", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["pez"], "contexts": ["substance used for treatment", "pitch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "pez", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pez", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2141, "stable_key": "clu_635a9a90699c1a5e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pitch", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A thick, sticky, dark substance obtained from resin.", "note": "'pezze' context might refer to pieces of cloth or material.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pez (English: , German: [peːts]; stylised as PEZ, officially Pfefferminz, German for peppermint) is the brand name of an Austrian candy and associated manual candy dispensers. The candy is a pressed, dry, straight-edged, curved-corner block 15 mm (5⁄8 inch) long, 8 mm (5⁄16 inch) wide and 5 mm (3⁄16 inch) high, with each Pez dispenser holding 12 candy pieces. Pez was invented in Austria and later exported worldwide. The all-uppercase spelling of the logo echoes the trademark's style on the packaging and the dispensers, with the logo drawn in perspective and giving the appearance that the letters are built out of 44 brick-like Pez mints (14 bricks in the P and 15 in each of the E and Z). Despite the widespread recognition and popularity of the Pez dispenser, the company considers itself to be primarily a candy company, stating that \"[t]oday, billions of PEZ candies are consumed annually in the U.S.A. alone\". Pez dispensers are a part of popular culture in many nations, an example being \"Soul Candy\" in the Japanese manga series Bleach."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "wax", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 716, "target_cluster_name": "wax", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pitch is a viscous, black, sticky substance derived from organic materials, sharing adhesive and viscous properties with waxes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pitch is a viscous, black, sticky substance derived from organic materials, sharing adhesive and viscous properties with waxes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "resina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2624, "target_cluster_name": "resina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pitch is a viscous black liquid obtained from tar, a resinous substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pitch is a viscous black liquid obtained from tar, a resinous substance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pétrole", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2042, "target_cluster_name": "pétrole", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pitch is a viscous, black, tar-like substance derived from petroleum or coal tar. It shares properties and historical uses with petroleum.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pitch is a viscous, black, tar-like substance derived from petroleum or coal tar. It shares properties and historical uses with petroleum.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "carne", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 219, "target_cluster_name": "carne", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "piflàsphalto di Diofcoride, fi può tare artificiale col bitume, 8c con la pece > come fi è detto parlando del bitume- La Mumia , che ci fi porta con carne, ofla, panni, e bitume, non è veramente !a Mu", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: used in artificial preparation of", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cafre", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "he defferente de xá, e o xaismael e o xatamaz, se chamam xá, que quer dizer rey por excellenda e os reis seus sujeitos se chamam paxá, que quer dizer pé de rey II 93 Zangue zmcui quer dizer, em pérsio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "equivalent to 'Zangue zmcui'"}, {"found_name": "Etio- pio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "he defferente de xá, e o xaismael e o xatamaz, se chamam xá, que quer dizer rey por excellenda e os reis seus sujeitos se chamam paxá, que quer dizer pé de rey II 93 Zangue zmcui quer dizer, em pérsio", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "equivalent to 'Zangue zmcui'"}, {"found_name": "lingoa de vacca", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "assi se chamara lingoa de vacca em Avicena, o qual eu duvido ser verdade. ORTA Todos os nomes que temos declarados de Avicena estão treladados** ao pé da letra; por arábio se chama lingoa de vacca e l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for 'pé' (in Avicena)"}, {"found_name": "lingoa de pássaro", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "assi se chamara lingoa de vacca em Avicena, o qual eu duvido ser verdade. ORTA Todos os nomes que temos declarados de Avicena estão treladados** ao pé da letra; por arábio se chama lingoa de vacca e l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for 'pé' (in Avicena)"}, {"found_name": "lingoa de cão", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "assi se chamara lingoa de vacca em Avicena, o qual eu duvido ser verdade. ORTA Todos os nomes que temos declarados de Avicena estão treladados** ao pé da letra; por arábio se chama lingoa de vacca e l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for 'pé' (in Avicena)"}, {"found_name": "capillus peneris", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquio