s."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Principes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "principes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Principes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Principes"], "contexts": ["rulers who can be poisoned"]}, {"entity_id": "principes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Principes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Principes", "Principe", "Principe de Ligne", "principiantes"], "contexts": ["princes", "rulers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Principes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Principes", "similarity": 0.78}], "id": 2143, "stable_key": "clu_d270e55c439c1c90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Princes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Rulers of noble birth, capable of being poisoned.", "note": "While 'Princes' can refer to any primary principle or leader, in the context of poison and rulership, it clearly indicates royal or noble figures.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Duque de Baviera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2100, "target_cluster_name": "Duque de Baviera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Duke of Bavaria held princely status within the Holy Roman Empire and later the German Confederation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Duke of Bavaria held princely status within the Holy Roman Empire and later the German Confederation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Emperor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2523, "target_cluster_name": "Emperadores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Princes' (Principes) in Roman history often referred to the highest-ranking individuals in the Roman Republic and later, the Emperor himself or his designated heir. Therefore, 'Emperor' is a closely related concept, representing the ultimate authority that 'Princes' might serve or aspire to.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'Princes' (Principes) in Roman history often referred to the highest-ranking individuals in the Roman Republic and later, the Emperor himself or his designated heir. Therefore, 'Emperor' is a closely related concept, representing the ultimate authority that 'Princes' might serve or aspire to."}, {"found_name": "Emperadores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2523, "target_cluster_name": "Emperadores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Princes are typically rulers of principalities, often vassals or heirs to an Emperor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Princes are typically rulers of principalities, often vassals or heirs to an Emperor.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "especiarias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["especiarias", "especia", "especiaria"], "contexts": ["aromatic plant products", "these spices"]}, {"entity_id": "especiarias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["especiarias"], "contexts": ["spices, plant-based flavorings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "especiarias", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "especiarias", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2144, "stable_key": "clu_1333b297871c039d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spices", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Aromatic plant products used for flavor and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavoring or coloring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavoring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. Spices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cravo", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1509, "target_cluster_name": "cravo", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "índia e á China ha noti- cias de que foi ali usada muito antes (Cf. Plin., xii, i5; Fliickiger e Han- bury, Pharmac.y 25o; Dymock, Mat. med,, 328). A especiaria, que os portuguezes chamaram e chamam c", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "the name the Portuguese gave to a spice"}, {"found_name": "galanga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 627, "target_cluster_name": "galanga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galangal is explicitly mentioned as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Galangal is explicitly mentioned as a spice in Southeast Asian cookery.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "CuBEBAS", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 765, "target_cluster_name": "CuBEBAS", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cubeb pepper is a spice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cubeb pepper is a spice.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chá", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 855, "target_cluster_name": "chá", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tea, while not a spice itself, was often traded and discussed alongside spices in historical contexts due to its value and exotic origins.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tea, while not a spice itself, was often traded and discussed alongside spices in historical contexts due to its value and exotic origins.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ervas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1538, "target_cluster_name": "ervas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Herbs and spices are often discussed together and distinguished by their culinary use (herbs typically from leaves, spices from other plant parts), indicating a close, albeit distinct, relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Herbs and spices are often discussed together and distinguished by their culinary use (herbs typically from leaves, spices from other plant parts), indicating a close, albeit distinct, relationship.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2918, "target_cluster_name": "Ginger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ginger is a primary example of a spice, and this term represents the broader category to which it belongs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ginger is a primary example of a spice, and this term represents the broader category to which it belongs.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pimenta longa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1750, "target_c