tices were essential knowledge for any physician or alchemist of Croll's era."}, {"found_name": "Paracelsus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 703, "target_cluster_name": "Paracelsus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paracelsus was a revolutionary figure in medicine and alchemy, whose iatrochemical theories and emphasis on practical application profoundly impacted subsequent alchemical and medical thought, including Croll's.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Paracelsus was a revolutionary figure in medicine and alchemy, whose iatrochemical theories and emphasis on practical application profoundly impacted subsequent alchemical and medical thought, including Croll's."}]}, {"canonical_name": "duck", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "ducks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ducks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ducks"], "contexts": ["birds whose eggs hatch under a hen", "birds used in incubation experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "duck", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "duck", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 4, "variants": ["duck"], "contexts": ["domestic vs wild bones comparison", "bird carrying aquatic plants and shells"]}, {"entity_id": "ducks", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ducks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["ducks"], "contexts": ["domesticated bird", "domesticated birds with differing breeds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ducks", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "duck", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ducks", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "ducks", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 2164, "stable_key": "clu_437b92214b75b130", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "duck", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "A waterfowl used in remedies and for cauterization.", "wikipedia_extract": "Duck is the common name for numerous species of waterfowl in the family Anatidae. Ducks are generally smaller and shorter-necked than swans and geese, which are members of the same family. Divided among several subfamilies, they are a form taxon; they do not represent a monophyletic group (the group of all descendants of a single common ancestral species), since swans and geese are not considered ducks. Ducks are mostly aquatic birds, and may be found in both fresh water and sea water. Ducks are sometimes confused with several types of unrelated water birds with similar forms, such as loons or divers, grebes, gallinules and coots. The word duck comes from Old English dūce 'diver', a derivative of the verb *dūcan 'to duck, bend down low as if to get under something, or dive', because of the way many species in the dabbling duck group feed by upending; compare with Dutch duiken and German tauchen 'to dive'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "natural philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "natural_theologie", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "natural Theologie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["natural Theologie"], "contexts": ["demonstrating God's attributes"]}, {"entity_id": "natural_philosophy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natural philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Natural Philosophy", "natural philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of the natural world before modern science", "preceded exact observation in antiquity"]}, {"entity_id": "physical_philosophy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt