e group of minerals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Diallage is part of the pyroxene group of minerals.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "miles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "milles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "milles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["milles"], "contexts": ["unit of length measurement", "unit of measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "miles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "miles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 7, "variants": ["miles"], "contexts": ["unit of distance measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "milles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "miles", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 2202, "stable_key": "clu_0552f5f0eb718d3e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mile", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The context of 'miles', 'milles', and 'millimeter' suggests units of distance measurement. 'Miles' is the most common and broadest term, encompassing the others.", "wikidata_id": "Q253276", "wikidata_description": "unit of length (on ground)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mile", "wikipedia_extract": "The mile, sometimes the international mile or statute mile to distinguish it from other miles, is a British imperial unit and United States customary unit of length; both are based on the older English unit of length equal to 5,280 English feet, or 1,760 yards. The statute mile was standardised as a unit of length between the Commonwealth of Nations and the United States by an international agreement in 1959, when the yard was formally redefined with respect to SI units as 0.9144 metres, making the mile exactly 1609.344 metres (1.609344 kilometres). For everyday use, five miles equates roughly to eight kilometres. Mileage is the term used informally for a distance expressed in miles. With qualifiers, mile is also used to describe or translate a wide range of units derived from or roughly equivalent to the Roman mile (roughly 1.48 km), such as the nautical mile (now 1.852 km exactly), the Italian mile (roughly 1.852 km), and the Chinese mile (now 500 m exactly). The Romans divided their mile into 5,000 pedes (lit. 'feet'), but the greater importance of furlongs in the Elizabethan-era England meant that the statute mile was made equivalent to 8 furlongs or 5,280 feet in 1593."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Gallons", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3321, "target_cluster_name": "gallons", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gallons are a unit of volume, and while the mile is a unit of length, both are historical units of measurement used in commerce and trade.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Gallons are a unit of volume, and while the mile is a unit of length, both are historical units of measurement used in commerce and trade."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Von Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "dechen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dechen"], "contexts": ["mentioned with Oeyhausen and la Roche"]}, {"entity_id": "von_dechen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Von Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Von Dechen", "von Dechen"], "contexts": ["made measurements of mine elevation", "geognosist providing geological observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dechen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Von Dechen", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 2203, "stable_key": "clu_eb5115198e94c0e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heinrich von Dechen", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1800, "death_year": 1889, "description": "German geologist and mining engineer", "wikidata_id": "Q78083", "wikidata_description": "German geologist (1800-1889)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "fresnel", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fresnel"], "contexts": ["mentioned in relation to solar rays and heat"]}, {"entity_id": "fresnel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Fresnel"], "contexts": ["authority on undulatory theory", "scientist who studied light refraction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fresnel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fresnel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2204, "stable_key": "clu_88154df088a68b3f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Augustin-Jean Fresnel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1827, "description": "French physicist, optics pioneer", "wikidata_id": "Q187241", "wikidata_description": "French engineer and physicist (1788-1827)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Laplace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 243, "target_cluster_name": "Laplace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential French scientists working in physics and mathematics during the same period, making significant contributions to optics and mechanics respectively.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential French scientists working in physics and mathematics during the same period, making significant contributions to optics and mechanics respectively.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bessel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 269, "target_cluster_name": "Bessel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Bessel and Fresnel were prominent scientists in the 19th century. Bessel is known for his work in astronomy and geodesy, while Fresnel is known for his contributions to optics and wave theory of light. They represent different but contemporaneous fields of scientific advancement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Bessel and Fresnel were prominent scientists in the 19th century. Bessel is known for his work in astronomy and geodesy, while Fresnel is known for his contributions to optics and wave theory of light. They represent different but contemporaneous fields of scientific advancement.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "physiciens", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 692, "target_cluster_name": "physiciens", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fresnel was a French physicist who made significant contributions to the theory of light, particularly in optics and wave theory.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fresnel was a French physicist who made significant contributions to the theory of light, particularly in optics and wave theory.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fourier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2132, "target_cluster_name": "Fourier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent French mathematicians and physicists who made significant contributions to optics and wave theory. Fresnel's work on diffraction and polarization built upon and extended earlier ideas, and they were contemporaries in the scientific community.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent French mathematicians and physicists who made significant contributions to optics and wave theory. Fresnel's work on diffraction and polarization built upon and extended earlier ideas, and they were contemporaries in the scientific community.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Becquerel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2216, "target_cluster_name": "Becquerel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were French physicists who made significant contributions to optics. Fresnel is known for his wave theory of light, and Becquerel's work on phosphorescence and the photovoltaic effect are related to light phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were French physicists who made significant contributions to optics. Fresnel is known for his wave theory of light, and Becquerel's work on phosphorescence and the photovoltaic effect are related to light phenomena.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Burmá", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "birmans", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Birmans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Birmans"], "contexts": ["land where salses were discovered"]}, {"entity_id": "burmá", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Burmá", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Burmá", "Burma"], "contexts": ["country in Southeast Asia", "region discussed in dr. Mason's book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Birmans", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Burmá", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 2205, "stable_key": "clu_2f4cc83076127832", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Burma", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in Southeast Asia, now officially Myanmar.", "note": "Burma is the historical name for the country now officially known as Myanmar.", "wikipedia_extract": "Myanmar, officially the Republic of the Union of Myanmar and also referred to as Burma (the official English name until 1989), is a country in northwest Southeast Asia. It is the largest country by area in Mainland Southeast Asia and has a population of about 55 million. It is bordered by India and Bangladesh to the northwest, China to the northeast, Laos and Thailand to the east and southeast, and the Andaman Sea and the Bay of Bengal to the south and southwest. The country's capital city is Naypyidaw, while its largest city is Yangon (formerly Rangoon). Early civilisations in the area included the Tibeto-Burman-speaking Pyu city-states in Upper Myanmar and the Mon kingdoms in Lower Myanmar. In the 9th century, the Bamar people entered the upper Irrawaddy valley, and following the establishment of the Pagan Kingdom in the 1050s, the Burmese language and culture and Theravada Buddhism slowly became dominant in the country."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "intuition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "intuition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intuition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["intuition", "Intuition"], "contexts": ["faculty of immediate understanding", "immediate understanding or insight"]}, {"entity_id": "intuition", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "intuition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intuition"], "contexts": ["vague understanding of phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "instinctive_intuitions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "instinctive intuitions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instinctive intuitions"], "contexts": ["innate, unlearned insights"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "intuition", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "intuition", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "intuition", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "instinctive intuitions", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2206, "stable_key": "clu_0ccdac702ec62338", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "intuition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1671627", "wikidata_description": "epistemological theory"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Belief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Intuition is often considered a form of belief that arises without conscious reasoning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Intuition is often considered a form of belief that arises without conscious reasoning."}, {"found_name": "Experience", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 105, "target_cluster_name": "experience", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Intuition can be seen as a rapid synthesis of past experiences and learned patterns.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Intuition can be seen as a rapid synthesis of past experiences and learned patterns."}, {"found_name": "Faculty", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1299, "target_cluster_name": "faculty", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Intuition is often descr