t Greek physician whose works were influential."}, {"found_name": "Aulus Cornelius Celsus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 676, "target_cluster_name": "Cornelius Celfus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Celsus was a Roman encyclopedist whose work De Medicina was a foundational medical text.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Celsus was a Roman encyclopedist whose work De Medicina was a foundational medical text."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work De Materia Medica was highly influential in medicine and botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Dioscorides was a Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work De Materia Medica was highly influential in medicine and botany."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Galen was a highly influential Greek physician and philosopher whose medical theories dominated Western medicine for centuries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Galen was a highly influential Greek physician and philosopher whose medical theories dominated Western medicine for centuries."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Hippocrates is considered the father of Western medicine, whose ethical and clinical approaches were foundational.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician, and Hippocrates is considered the father of Western medicine, whose ethical and clinical approaches were foundational."}, {"found_name": "Paulus of Aegina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 586, "target_cluster_name": "Paulus Ægineta", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician in the Byzantine Empire, and Paulus of Aegina was also a significant Byzantine physician whose medical compendium was widely used.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Johannes Actuarius was a physician in the Byzantine Empire, and Paulus of Aegina was also a significant Byzantine physician whose medical compendium was widely used."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cufcuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "cufcuta", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cufcuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Cufcuta", "Cufcqta", "Cufuta", "Culcuta", "Cuscuta"], "contexts": ["plant similar to Epithymo", "herb used in decoctions"]}, {"entity_id": "cufcuta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cufcuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cufcuta"], "contexts": ["cuscuta used in a syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Cufcuta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cufcuta", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 2248, "stable_key": "clu_ee38b5113b369343", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cuscuta", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cuscuta spp.", "family": "Convolvulaceae", "description": "A genus of parasitic plants, commonly known as dodder or strangle-vine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cuscuta (), commonly known as dodder or amarbel, is a genus of over 201 species of yellow, orange, or red (rarely green) parasitic plants. Formerly treated as the only genus in the family Cuscutaceae, it now is accepted as belonging in the morning glory family, Convolvulaceae, on the basis of the work of the Angiosperm Phylogeny Group. The genus is found throughout the temperate and tropical regions of the world, with the greatest species diversity in subtropical and tropical regions; the genus becomes rare in cool temperate climates, with only four species native to northern Europe. Folk names include strangle tare, strangleweed, scaldweed, beggarweed, lady's laces, fireweed, wizard's net, devil's guts, devil's hair, devil's ringlet, goldthread, hailweed, hairweed, hellbine, love vine, pull-down, angel hair, and witch's hair. Cuscuta can be identified by its thin stems appearing leafless, with the leaves reduced to minute scales. In these respects it closely resembles the similarly parasitic, but unrelated genus, Cassytha."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "epithimo", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 616, "target_cluster_name": "epithimo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cuscuta is the genus name for Cuscuta epithymum, and 'epithimo' is listed as a historical name for this species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cuscuta is the genus name for Cuscuta epithymum, and 'epithimo' is listed as a historical name for this species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Misseltoe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2919, "target_cluster_name": "Misseltoe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Cuscuta and Mistletoe are parasitic plants, though Cuscuta is a holoparasite while Mistletoe is a hemiparasite.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Cuscuta and Mistletoe are parasitic plants, though Cuscuta is a holoparasite while Mistletoe is a hemiparasite.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "prezzemolo di Sparagi", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Cicerbita Fegatella Lattuga Scariola Fumofterno Luppoli Orzo non fcorticatoj Alchechegni Logorizia Capeluenerc Cetracca Adianto nen^ Poht^ice Cufcuta Radici di fuìoc. d'Appio cioè prezzemolo di Sparag", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "an ingredient, possibly a vernacular name or a specific variety of parsley"}]}, {"canonical_name": "linimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "linimenti", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "linimenti", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["linimenti"], "contexts": ["liniments, a type of compound medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "linimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "linimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 8, "variants": ["linimento", "hum linimento", "linimento das almorreymas", "linimento magistral"], "contexts": ["liniment named number 4 for itching", "medicinal ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "linimenti", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "linimento", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 2249, "stable_key": "clu_258f40b47fba7eec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liniment", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A liquid or semi-liquid preparation rubbed on the skin as an anodyne or embrocation.", "wikipedia_extract": "Liniment (from Latin: linere, meaning \"to smear, anoint\"), also called embrocation and heat rub, is a medicated topical preparation for application to the skin. Some liniments have a viscosity similar to that of water; others are lotion or balm; still, others are in transdermal patches, soft solid sticks, and sprays. Liniment usually is rubbed into the skin, which the active ingredients penetrate. Liniments are typically sold to relieve pain and stiffness, such as from muscular aches and strains, and arthritis. These are typically formulated from alcohol, acetone, or similar quickly evaporating solvents and contain counterirritant aromatic chemical compounds, such as methyl salicylate, benzoin resin, menthol, and capsaicin. They produce a feeling of warmth within the muscle of the area they are applied to, typically acting as rubefacients via a counterirritant effect."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "unguento", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 566, "target_cluster_name": "unguento", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Liniments are topical medications, similar in application and purpose to ointments, but typically liquid or semi-liquid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Liniments are topical medications, similar in application and purpose to ointments, but typically liquid or semi-liquid.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "emplastro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1913, "target_cluster_name": "emplastro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are topical preparations applied to the body for medicinal purposes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are topical preparations applied to the body for medicinal purposes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ointment", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 566, "target_cluster_name": "unguento", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ointments are topical preparations applied to the skin for medicinal purposes, similar to liniments, but typically have a thicker, greasy consistency.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ointments are topical preparations applied to the skin for medicinal purposes, similar to liniments, but typically have a thicker, greasy consistency."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cicuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "cicuta", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cicuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cicuta"], "contexts": ["herb used in preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "cicuta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cicuta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Cicuta", "cicuta"], "contexts": ["substance with narcotic qualities", "leaves used in an ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Cicuta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicuta", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2250, "stable_key": "clu_db10a119543641cb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hemlock", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Conium maculatum", "family": "Apiaceae", "note": "Cicuta is the Latin name for hemlock, which can refer to different species, but Conium maculatum is the most common."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "circulação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "circulação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "circulação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["circulação", "circulaçãodo ſangue", "fua circulaçao"], "contexts": ["bodily process", "movement of blood"]}, {"entity_id