b6186553504d37c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polarization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q85900110", "wikidata_description": "process of separation of a relatively homogeneous group into more distinct groups"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Circular polarization", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1952, "target_cluster_name": "Circular polarization", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Circular polarization is a specific type of polarization, indicating a direct and hierarchical relationship within the concept of polarization.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Circular polarization is a specific type of polarization, indicating a direct and hierarchical relationship within the concept of polarization."}, {"found_name": "Diamagnetic Polarity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2841, "target_cluster_name": "diamagnetic polarity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Polarity is a fundamental concept in magnetism, and diamagnetism is a specific magnetic property. The term 'Polarity' in 'Diamagnetic Polarity' directly relates to the concept of polarization.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Polarity is a fundamental concept in magnetism, and diamagnetism is a specific magnetic property. The term 'Polarity' in 'Diamagnetic Polarity' directly relates to the concept of polarization."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__gilbert", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "D. Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["D. Gilbert"], "contexts": ["English physician and natural philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "gilbert", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Gilbert"], "contexts": ["author of 'de Magnete'", "author of 'Physiologia Nova de Magnete'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "D. Gilbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gilbert", "similarity": 0.878}], "id": 2265, "stable_key": "clu_e4738cd2ad84916d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Gilbert", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1544, "death_year": 1603, "description": "English physician and natural philosopher, author of 'De Magnete'.", "wikidata_id": "Q192706", "wikidata_description": "English physician, physicist and natural philosopher (1544-1603)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William%20Gilbert%20%28physicist%29", "wikipedia_extract": "William Gilbert (; 24 May 1544? – 30 November 1603), also known as Gilberd, was an English physician, physicist and natural philosopher. He passionately rejected both the prevailing Aristotelian philosophy and the Scholastic method of university teaching. He is remembered today largely for his book De Magnete (1600). A unit of magnetomotive force, also known as magnetic potential, was named the Gilbert in his honour; it has now been superseded by the Ampere-turn. Gilbert was born in Colchester to Jerome Gilberd, a borough recorder."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "William Gilbert rejected Aristotelian philosophy, indicating a significant intellectual relationship through opposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "William Gilbert rejected Aristotelian philosophy, indicating a significant intellectual relationship through opposition."}, {"found_name": "Galileo Galilei", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 931, "target_cluster_name": "Galileo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent natural philosophers and astronomers of the Renaissance/early modern period, with Gilbert's work on magnetism influencing later scientific thought, including that of Galileo.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were prominent natural philosophers and astronomers of the Renaissance/early modern period, with Gilbert's work on magnetism influencing later scientific thought, including that of Galileo."}, {"found_name": "Isaac Newton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 71, "target_cluster_name": "Newton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "William Gilbert's pioneering work on magnetism, particularly in 'De Magnete', laid foundational concepts that were later built upon and expanded by Isaac Newton in his theories of universal gravitation and mechanics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "William Gilbert's pioneering work on magnetism, particularly in 'De Magnete', laid foundational concepts that were later built upon and expanded by Isaac Newton in his theories of universal gravitation and mechanics."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Doleus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "duretus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Duretus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Duretus"], "contexts": ["physician questioning gold's efficacy"]}, {"entity_id": "doleus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Doleus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Doleus", "Dolens"], "contexts": ["cited author on coughs", "medical author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Duretus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Doleus", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 2266, "stable_key": "clu_3eff428e76698a9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Loys Delaunay", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1543, "death_year": 1627, "description": "French physician and author, known for his pharmacological works.", "note": "The name 'Doleus' is a Latinization of 'Delaunay'. 'Duretus' is likely a misspelling or alternative form."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "lice", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lice"], "contexts": ["animated from corrupt humours in man"]}, {"entity_id": "lice", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lice"], "contexts": ["condition treated by bark in vinegar", "parasitic insects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lice", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Lice", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 2267, "stable_key": "clu_153d1018caff0106", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lice", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q6481228", "wikidata_description": "order of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_lice_infestation", "wikipedia_extract": "Head lice infestation, also known as pediculosis capitis, is the infection of the head hair and scalp by the head louse (Pediculus humanus capitis). Itching from lice bites is common. During a person's first infection, the itch may not develop for up to six weeks. If a person is infected again, symptoms may begin much more quickly. The itch may cause problems with sleeping. Generally, however, it is not a serious condition."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Parasitic worms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3973, "target_cluster_name": "parasitic worms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Head lice are a type of parasitic organism that infests a host, similar to parasitic worms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Head lice are a type of parasitic organism that infests a host, similar to parasitic worms."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "venery", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Venery"], "contexts": ["impotency unto Venery"]}, {"entity_id": "venery", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Venery", "venery"], "contexts": ["provoketh to Venery", "sexual intercourse"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Venery", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Venery", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2268, "stable_key": "clu_aa9b855202dddaab-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Relates to sexual desire, intercourse, and the planet Venus.", "note": "Can refer to the act of sex ('Venery') or the planet 'Venus'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun. It is often called Earth's \"twin\" or \"sister\" among the planets of the Solar System for its orbit being the closest to Earth's, both being terrestrial planets, and having the most similar and nearly equal size, mass, and surface gravity. Venus, though, is significantly different, especially as it has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K (464 °C; 867 °F) and a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, and as an inferior planet it is always relatively close to the Sun, either as the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Star", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1308, "target_cluster_name": "star", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is often referred to as the 'morning star' or 'evening star'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is often referred to as the 'morning star' or 'evening star'."}, {"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a celestial body studied in astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is a celestial body studied in astronomy."}, {"found_name": "Sun", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 196, "target_cluster_name": "sun", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Venus is a planet in the solar system, which includes the Sun.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Venus is a planet in the solar system, which includes the Sun."}, {"found_name": "Mars (astrology)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1551, "target_cluster_name": "Mars", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Venus and Mars are planets with astrological significance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Venus and Mars are planets with astrological significance."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Faintings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "faintings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Faintings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 7, "variants": ["Faintings", "Fainting", "fainting", "faintings"], "contexts": ["condition of the stomach", "after childbirth"]}, {"entity_id": "faintness", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "faintness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["faintness"], "contexts": ["result of eating fungi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Faintings", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "faintness", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2269, "stable_key": "clu_231ec9a0b2e2a72a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fainting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A temporary loss of consciousness due to a sudden decrease in blood flow.", "wikipedia_extract": "Syncope (), commonly known as fainting or passing out, is a loss of consciousness and muscle strength characterized by a fast onset, short duration, and spontaneous recovery. It is caused by a decrease in blood flow to the brain, typically from low blood pressure. There are sometimes symptoms before the loss of consciousness such as lightheadedness, sweating, pale skin, blurred vision, nausea, vomiting, or feeling warm. Syncope may also be associated with a short episode of muscle twitching. Psychiatric causes can also be determined when a patient experiences fear, anxiety, or panic; particularly before a stressful event, usually medical in nature. When consciousness and muscle strength are not completely lost, it is called presyncope."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "syncope", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 300, "target_cluster_name": "Syncope", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Syncope is the medical term for fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Syncope is the medical term for fainting."}, {"found_name": "desmayos", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1601, "target_cluster_name": "desmayos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Spanish for fainting."}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fainting is a temporary loss of consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fainting is a temporary loss of consciousness."}, {"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which can be related to the loss of consciousness in fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which can be related to the loss of consciousness in fainting."}, {"found_name": "Exhaustion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3509, "target_cluster_name": "exhaustion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Extreme physical or mental exhaustion can lead to fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Extreme physical or mental exhaustion can lead to fainting."}, {"found_name": "Nervous shock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 926, "target_cluster_name": "nervous shock", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sudden emotional or physical shock can cause fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sudden emotional or physical shock can cause fainting."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "deafness", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 7, "variants": ["deafness", "Deafness"], "contexts": ["condition helped by Angelica juice/water", "condition helped by oil of seed"]}, {"entity_id": "deaf-mute", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "deaf-mute", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["deaf-mute"], "contexts": ["condition of being deaf and mute"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Deafness", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "deaf-mute", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2270, "stable_key": "clu_836f465504b61f09", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hearing Impairment", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Hearing Impairment"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Wasps", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "wasps", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wasps", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Wasps", "Wasp"], "contexts": ["insect with sting", "insects whose stings are treated"]}, {"entity_id": "wasp", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Wasp", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["wasp", "Wasp"], "contexts": ["insect with a stinging tail", "sting of"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wasps", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Wasp", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 2271, "stable_key": "clu_ee1f2a4b635766eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wasps", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Vespidae", "description": "Flying insects characterized by their narrow waists and stinging ability.", "wikipedia_extract": "A wasp is any insect of the narrow-waisted suborder Apocrita of the order Hymenoptera which is neither a bee nor an ant; this excludes the broad-waisted sawflies (Symphyta), which look somewhat like wasps, but are in a separate suborder. The wasps do not constitute a clade, a complete natural group with a single ancestor, as  bees and ants are deeply nested within the wasps, having evolved from wasp ancestors. Wasps that are members of the clade Aculeata can sting their prey. The most commonly known wasps, such as yellowjackets and hornets, are in the family Vespidae and are eusocial, living together in a nest with an egg-laying queen and non-reproducing workers. Eusociality is favoured by the unusual haplodiploid system of sex determination in Hymenoptera, as it makes sisters exceptionally closely related to each other. However, the majority of wasp species are solitary, with each adult female living and breeding independently."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wasps are hymenopterans, like bees, and are sometimes discussed in relation to bees in premodern texts, though they do not produce honey in the same way.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wasps are hymenopterans, like bees, and are sometimes discussed in relation to bees in premodern texts, though they do not produce honey in the same way.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera, often sharing similar ecological roles and morphology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera, often sharing similar ecological roles and morphology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "formiguas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2511, "target_cluster_name": "formiguas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2034, "target_cluster_name": "Ants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wasps and ants are both in the order Hymenoptera and share a close evolutionary relationship, often being discussed together in entomological contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wasps and ants are both in the order Hymenoptera and share a close evolutionary relationship, often being discussed together in entomological contexts."}, {"found_name": "Ichneumon wasp", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3390, "target_cluster_name": "ichneumon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An ichneumon wasp is a specific type of wasp, making it a direct subtype and thus strongly related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An ichneumon wasp is a specific type of wasp, making it a direct subtype and thus strongly related."}, {"found_name": "Insects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wasps are a type of insect, placing them within this broader biological category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wasps are a type of insect, placing them within this broader biological category."}]}, {"canonical_name": "malignas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "malignant", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "malignant", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["malignant", "Malignant", "malignant Ulcers"], "contexts": ["how foul or Malignant soever they be", "malignant or pestilential quality"]}, {"entity_id": "malignas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "malignas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Malignas", "malignas"], "contexts": ["a type of ailment", "malignant fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "malignant", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "malignas", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 2272, "stable_key": "clu_33dbba4dcddb5f9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malignancy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Malignancy", "wikidata_id": "Q55786988", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cancros", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 271, "target_cluster_name": "cancros", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Malignancy is a direct synonym for cancer in modern medical terminology, and historical texts often used 'malignancy' to describe cancerous growths.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Malignancy is a direct synonym for cancer in modern medical terminology, and historical texts often used 'malignancy' to describe cancerous growths.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Tumor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 556, "target_cluster_name": "tumor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A tumor is a swelling or growth, which can be benign or malignant. Malignancy is the quality of being cancerous or invasive.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A tumor is a swelling or growth, which can be benign or malignant. Malignancy is the quality of being cancerous or invasive."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Dodon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "dodoneus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dodoneus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dodoneus"], "contexts": ["Renaissance botanist"]}, {"entity_id": "dodon", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dodon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dodon", "Dodoneu"], "contexts": ["author of medical observations", "cited for observations on urine color"]}, {"entity_id": "dodoneus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dodoneus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dodoneus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority on blood tears", "cited for fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dodoneus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dodon", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dodoneus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dodoneus", "similarity": 0.647}], "id": 2273, "stable_key": "clu_6b61780d2bf9d4c0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rembert Dodoens", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1517, "death_year": 1585, "description": "Flemish physician and botanist, renowned for his herbal.", "wikidata_id": "Q437466", "wikidata_description": "Flemish physician and botanist (1517–1585)", "wikipedia_extract": "Rembert Dodoens (born Rembert van Joenckema, 29 June 1517 – 10 March 1585) was a Flemish physician and botanist, also known under his Latinized name Rembertus Dodonaeus. He has been called the father of botany. The standard author abbreviation Dodoens is used to indicate this person as the author when citing a botanical name. Dodoens was born Rembert van Joenckema in Mechelen, then the capital of the Spanish Netherlands in 1517. His parents were Denis van Joenckema (d. 1533) and Ursula Roelants.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rembert_Dodoens"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Carolus Clusius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1795, "target_cluster_name": "Clusius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Clusius was a prominent botanist and contemporary of Dodoens, and they corresponded and influenced each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Clusius was a prominent botanist and contemporary of Dodoens, and they corresponded and influenced each other's work."}, {"found_name": "Conrad Gessner", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3258, "target_cluster_name": "Gesner", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gessner was a highly influential naturalist and physician, and a key figure in the development of botany during the same period as Dodoens. They were part of the same intellectual tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Gessner was a highly influential naturalist and physician, and a key figure in the development of botany during the same period as Dodoens. They were part of the same intellectual tradition."}, {"found_name": "Leonhart Fuchs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3738, "target_cluster_name": "Schrod", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fuchs was another leading botanist of the 16th century, whose work, like Dodoens', was foundational for later botanical studies. They are often discussed together as key figures in the history of botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fuchs was another leading botanist of the 16th century, whose work, like Dodoens', was foundational for later botanical studies. They are often discussed together as key figures in the history of botany."}, {"found_name": "Matthiolus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2684, "target_cluster_name": "Brazavolus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mattioli was a prominent physician and botanist whose commentaries on Dioscorides were widely influential, and he was a contemporary and peer of Dodoens in the field of botanical studies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mattioli was a prominent physician and botanist whose commentaries on Dioscorides were widely influential, and he was a contemporary and peer of Dodoens in the field of botanical studies."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dodoens, like many botanists of his era, built upon and commented on the ancient works of Dioscorides, particularly 'De Materia Medica'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dodoens, like many botanists of his era, built upon and commented on the ancient works of Dioscorides, particularly 'De Materia Medica'."}, {"found_name": "Pliny the Elder", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 202, "target_cluster_name": "Plinio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pliny's 'Natural History' was a significant source of botanical knowledge for Renaissance scholars like Dodoens.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Pliny's 'Natural History' was a significant source of botanical knowledge for Renaissance scholars like Dodoens."}]}, {"canonical_name": "confeyção Alchermes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "confectio_alkerimes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Confectio Alkerimes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Confectio Alkerimes"], "contexts": ["compound medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "confeyção_alchermes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "confeyção Alchermes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 7, "variants": ["confeyção Alchermes", "Confeyções de Alchermes", "confectionealchermes", "confeyçao de Alchermes", "confeyçaó de Alchermes", "confeyçoensde Alchermes"], "contexts": ["confectionery medicine", "compound medicine Alchermes confection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Confectio Alkerimes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "confeyção Alchermes", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2274, "stable_key": "clu_afc78c2b6ba8652f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alchermes confection", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A historical medicinal cordial, often red in color, containing many ingredients.", "wikipedia_extract": "Alchermes (, Italian: [alˈkɛrmes]; from the Arabic: القرمز, romanized: al-qirmiz, lit. 'cochineal', from Persian: کرمست, romanized: kermest, lit. 'bloody, red, cochineal, carmine') is a type of Italian liqueur (especially in Tuscany, Emilia-Romagna, and Sicily) prepared by infusing neutral spirits with sugar, cinnamon, cloves, nutmeg, and vanilla, and other herbs and flavoring agents. Its most striking characteristic is its scarlet color, obtained by the addition of Kermes, a small scale insect from which the drink derives its name. Several proprietary variants are commercially available, where the coloring agent is a coal tar-derived dye such as E124 or E126, with alcoholic contents ranging from 21 to 32%. Its chief use is in coloring pastry, although a quick dessert is sometimes made by adding it to custard cream and sugar. In the Italian pudding zuppa inglese, sponge cake or ladyfingers soaked in this liqueur are a major ingredient. Alkermes was according to Ephraim Chamber's Cyclopaedia (1827), a modification of an 8th-century remedy namely Confectio Alkermes in the \"form and consistency of a confection\" (an electuary, which is thick and pasty)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alcohol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 131, "target_cluster_name": "alcohol", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alchermes is a liqueur, which is a type of alcoholic beverage.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alchermes is a liqueur, which is a type of alcoholic beverage."}, {"found_name": "Rosewater", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 292, "target_cluster_name": "Rosewater", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alchermes is a confection that often includes rosewater as an ingredient.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alchermes is a confection that often includes rosewater as an ingredient."}, {"found_name": "Rose sugar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2627, "target_cluster_name": "açucare rosado", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alchermes is a confection that often includes rose sugar as an ingredient.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alchermes is a confection that often includes rose sugar as an ingredient."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Malay Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "malay_archipelago", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Malay Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Malay Archipelago", "Malay archipelago"], "contexts": ["region studied by Wallace", "Malay Archipelago in the south"]}, {"entity_id": "malayalam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "malayalam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["malayalam"], "contexts": ["language in southern India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Malay Archipelago", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "malayalam", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2275, "stable_key": "clu_246569a27a58f5e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malay Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia; Malaysia; Singapore; Brunei; East Timor", "note": "The term 'malayalam' in the context of 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' refers to the Malayalam language, spoken in Kerala, India. However, the overwhelming majority of mentions and the explicit context of 'origin_of_species_darwin_1859' point to the geographical region. The primary identification is the archipelago.", "wikidata_id": "Q128232347", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Great Indian Archipelago", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1471, "target_cluster_name": "Great Indian Archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'Malay Archipelago' is often used interchangeably with or as a synonym for the Indonesian Archipelago, particularly in non-Indonesian contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'Malay Archipelago' is often used interchangeably with or as a synonym for the Indonesian Archipelago, particularly in non-Indonesian contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Banda", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2028, "target_cluster_name": "Banda", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Banda Islands are geographically located within the Malay Archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Banda Islands are geographically located within the Malay Archipelago.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Malay Archipelago is a specific example of an archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Malay Archipelago is a specific example of an archipelago."}, {"found_name": "Borneo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 184, "target_cluster_name": "Borneo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Borneo is a major island within the Malay Archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Borneo is a major island within the Malay Archipelago."}, {"found_name": "Java", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 78, "target_cluster_name": "Java", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Java is a major island within the Malay Archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Java is a major island within the Malay Archipelago."}, {"found_name": "East Indies", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 497, "target_cluster_name": "East Indies", "source_book":