e, \"Caco\" is Richard's older brother Carlos."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "instinctive", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instinctive"], "contexts": ["behavior not learned by experience"]}, {"entity_id": "instinctive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["instinctive"], "contexts": ["innate, unlearned behavior", "innate behavioral patterns"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "instinctive", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "instinctive", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2282, "stable_key": "clu_77ce880dbba4deae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Instinct", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18237", "wikidata_description": "inherent inclination of a living organism"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Appetite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2030, "target_cluster_name": "Appetite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both relate to internal drives or urges that motivate behavior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both relate to internal drives or urges that motivate behavior."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is often considered a fundamental component of both emotion and will, driving actions and feelings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instinct is often considered a fundamental component of both emotion and will, driving actions and feelings."}, {"found_name": "Habits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1431, "target_cluster_name": "habits of life", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instincts can be seen as innate predispositions that may develop into learned habits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instincts can be seen as innate predispositions that may develop into learned habits."}, {"found_name": "Human nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 513, "target_cluster_name": "Human Nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is a key concept in discussions of innate human tendencies and characteristics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instinct is a key concept in discussions of innate human tendencies and characteristics."}, {"found_name": "Mind", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 194, "target_cluster_name": "Mind", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is a fundamental aspect of the mind, influencing behavior and thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instinct is a fundamental aspect of the mind, influencing behavior and thought."}, {"found_name": "Nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 20, "target_cluster_name": "Nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is often contrasted with or seen as a fundamental aspect of natural phenomena and behavior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instinct is often contrasted with or seen as a fundamental aspect of natural phenomena and behavior."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instincts often manifest as or are closely linked to desires.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Instincts often manifest as or are closely linked to desires."}]}, {"canonical_name": "monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "monkeys", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Monkeys"], "contexts": ["fossil primates"]}, {"entity_id": "monkeys", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["monkeys"], "contexts": ["primates for observation", "animal used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Monkeys", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "monkeys", "similarity": 0.753}], "id": 2283, "stable_key": "clu_bc389ee169f6f99d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "monkeys", "confidence": "low", "type": "animal", "description": "Primates used in observation and experiments, offspring of mixed breeds.", "note": "Includes both 'monkeys' and 'mongrels' suggesting a broader category.", "wikipedia_extract": "Monkey is a common name that may refer to most mammals of the infraorder Simiiformes, also known as simians. Traditionally, all animals in the group now known as simians are counted as monkeys except the apes. Thus monkeys, in that sense, constitute an incomplete paraphyletic grouping; alternatively, if apes (Hominoidea) are included, monkeys and simians are synonyms. In 1812, Étienne Geoffroy grouped the apes and the Cercopithecidae group of monkeys together and established the name Catarrhini, \"Old World monkeys\" (\"singes de l'Ancien Monde\" in French). The extant sister of the Catarrhini in the monkey (\"singes\") group is the Platyrrhini (New World monkeys). Some nine million years before the divergence between the Cercopithecidae and the apes, the Platyrrhini emerged within \"monkeys\" by migration to South America likely by ocean."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Apes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1131, "target_cluster_name": "apes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apes are traditionally excluded from the definition of monkeys, but are closely related within the infraorder Simiiformes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Apes are traditionally excluded from the definition of monkeys, but are closely related within the infraorder Simiiformes."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cinchona", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "cinchona", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cinchona", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Cinchona", "Cinchona ferruginea"], "contexts": ["genus of true quinquina", "genus of plants"]}, {"entity_id": "cinchona", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cinchona", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cinchona"], "contexts": ["plant yielding febrifuge bark"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cinchona", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cinchona", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 2284, "stable_key": "clu_b6c2ad52a05bbc32", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cinchona", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cinchona", "family": "Rubiaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q160090", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants in the family Rubiaceae"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Quinaquina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1534, "target_cluster_name": "Quinaquina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quinaquina is an older name for Cinchona, referring to the bark used to treat fevers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quinaquina is an older name for Cinchona, referring to the bark used to treat fevers."}, {"found_name": "tropical plants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2853, "target_cluster_name": "tropical plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cinchona is a genus of trees native to the Andes, a tropical region, known for its medicinal bark.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cinchona is a genus of trees native to the Andes, a tropical region, known for its medicinal bark.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Quindiù", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "quindiù", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Quindiù", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Quindiù", "Quindiii", "Quindià"], "contexts": ["passage name", "mountain range"]}, {"entity_id": "quindiu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Quindiu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Quindiu"], "contexts": ["mountain chain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Quindiù", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Quindiu", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 2285, "stable_key": "clu_f52eccb6071b15e1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Páramo de Sumapaz", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "note": "Based on Humboldt's works and geographic descriptions, 'Quindiù' or 'Quindiu' most likely refers to the Páramo de Sumapaz, a high-altitude plateau region in the Colombian Andes.", "wikidata_id": "Q3411486", "wikidata_description": "mountain in Colombia", "wikipedia_extract": "Moorland or moor is a type of habitat found in upland areas in temperate grasslands, savannas, and shrublands and the biomes of montane grasslands and shrublands, characterised by low-growing vegetation on acidic soils. Moorland today generally means uncultivated hill land (such as Dartmoor in South West England), but also includes low-lying wetlands (such as Sedgemoor, also South West England). It is closely related to heath, although experts disagree on the exact distinction between these types of vegetation. Generally, moor refers to highland and high rainfall areas, while heath refers to lowland zones which are more likely to be the result of human activity. Moorland habitats are found mainly in tropical Africa, northern and western Europe, and South America. Most of the world's moorlands are diverse ecosystems.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumapaz_P%C3%A1ramo"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Páramo de Sumapaz is a high-altitude moorland located within the Andes mountain range.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Páramo de Sumapaz is a high-altitude moorland located within the Andes mountain range."}, {"found_name": "Antisana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1867, "target_cluster_name": "Antisana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Antisana is a volcano in the Andes, near the Páramo de Sumapaz, and is part of the same high-altitude ecosystem.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Antisana is a volcano in the Andes, near the Páramo de Sumapaz, and is part of the same high-altitude ecosystem."}, {"found_name": "Chimborazo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 708, "target_cluster_name": "Chimborazo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chimborazo is a volcano in the Andes, near the Páramo de Sumapaz, and is part of the same high-altitude ecosystem.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chimborazo is a volcano in the Andes, near the Páramo de Sumapaz, and is part of the same high-altitude ecosystem."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Oural", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "oural", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Oural", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Oural"], "contexts": ["mountains bordering Europe", "mountain range limit"]}, {"entity_id": "oural", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Oural", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Oural"], "contexts": ["mountain range and platinum district"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Oural", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Oural", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2286, "stable_key": "clu_5605b4913d6327dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ural Mountains", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q23136011", "wikidata_description": "chronology of creation of settles by humans in the region from the Arctic Ocean in the north to the Ural River and northwestern modern Kazakhstan", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_human_settlement_in_the_Ural_Mountains", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ural Mountains ( YOOR-əl), or simply the Urals, are a mountain range in Eurasia that runs north–south mostly through Russia, from the coast of the Arctic Ocean to the river Ural and northwestern Kazakhstan. The mountain range forms part of the conventional boundary between the continents of Europe and Asia, marking the separation between European Russia and Siberia. Vaygach Island and the islands of Novaya Zemlya form continuations of the chain to the north into the Arctic Ocean. The average altitudes of the Urals are around 1,000–1,300 metres (3,300–4,300 ft), the highest point being Mount Narodnaya, which reaches a height of 1,894 metres (6,214 ft). The mountains lie within the Ural geographical region and significantly overlap with the Ural Federal District and the Ural economic region. Their resources include metal ores, coal, precious and semi-precious stones."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arctic Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 891, "target_cluster_name": "Polar Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia, and the Arctic Ocean is mentioned as the northern extent of the Ural Mountains.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia, and the Arctic Ocean is mentioned as the northern extent of the Ural Mountains."}, {"found_name": "Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 231, "target_cluster_name": "Asie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Ural Mountains form part of the conventional boundary between Europe and Asia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alpes maritimes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "alpes_maritimes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Alpes maritimes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Alpes maritimes"], "contexts": ["region where wheat and rye disappear", "mountains in California"]}, {"entity_id": "maritime_alps", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Maritime Alps", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maritime Alps"], "contexts": ["mountains becoming storm centers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alpes maritimes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Maritime Alps", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 2287, "stable_key": "clu_72a20f23ecc3105e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alps", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Switzerland", "note": "'Alpes maritimes' is a specific part of the Alps. 'Altai' is a different mountain range, but 'Alps' is the dominant term.", "wikidata_id": "Q1286", "wikidata_description": "Human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_Alps", "wikipedia_extract": "The Maritime Alps (French: Alpes Maritimes [alp maʁitim]; Italian: Alpi Marittime [ˈalpi maˈrittime]) are a mountain range in the southwestern part of the Alps. They form the border between the French region of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur and the Italian regions of Piedmont and Liguria. They are the southernmost part of the Alps. Administratively the range is divided between the Italian provinces of Cuneo and Imperia (eastern slopes) and the French department of Alpes-Maritimes (western slopes). The Maritime Alps are drained by the rivers Roya, Var and Verdon and their tributaries on the French side; by the Stura di Demonte and other tributaries of the Tanaro and Po on the Italian side. There are many attractive perched villages, such as Belvédère at the entrance to the spectacular Gordolasque valley, some concealing unexpected architectural riches (for example in the south there are numerous churches decorated with murals and altarpieces by primitive Niçois painters)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "las Trincheras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "las_trincheras", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "las Trincheras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Las Trincheras", "las Trincheras"], "contexts": ["near Portocabello, location of sulfurous waters", "location of hot springs"]}, {"entity_id": "las_trincheras", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Las Trincheras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Las Trincheras"], "contexts": ["location with granite ledges", "location of thermal springs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "las Trincheras", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Las Trincheras", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 2288, "stable_key": "clu_7d46b3e801d26c86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Las Trincheras", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q65059480", "wikidata_description": "place in Carabobo, Venezuela", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venezuelan_independence", "wikipedia_extract": "The Venezuelan Independence was the juridical-political process that put an end to the ties between the Captaincy General of Venezuela and the Spanish Empire. It also implied the replacement of the absolute monarchy by the republic as the form of government in Venezuela. The independence of Venezuela produced the armed conflict known as the Venezuelan War of Independence between the independence army or Patriotas (\"patriots\") and the royalist army or Realistas (\"royalists\"). On July 5, 1811, the independence declaration is signed. That day is celebrated in Venezuela as its national day. On that date formally, through the document \"Acta de Declaración de Independencia\", Venezuela separates from Spain."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caracas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1497, "target_cluster_name": "Caracas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical and administrative relationship within the same country.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Caracas is the capital of Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical and administrative relationship within the same country."}, {"found_name": "Coro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1932, "target_cluster_name": "Coro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coro is a city in Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical relationship within the same country.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coro is a city in Venezuela, and Las Trincheras is a municipality in Venezuela, indicating a geographical relationship within the same country."}]}, {"canonical_name": "titane rutile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "titane_rutile", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "titane rutile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["titane rutile", "titane", "titane - rutile"], "contexts": ["mineral found in schist", "mineral found in rock"]}, {"entity_id": "titanium", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "titanium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["titanium"], "contexts": ["mineral element", "metallis titanium"]}, {"entity_id": "metallis_titanium", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "metallis titanium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["metallis titanium"], "contexts": ["metallic form of titanium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "titane rutile", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "titanium", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "titane rutile", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "metallis titanium", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2289, "stable_key": "clu_7db9297549305fc2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rutile", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'titane rutile' refers to rutile, a mineral composed of titanium dioxide, often described as a metallic titanium compound in early texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q320603", "wikidata_description": "oxide mineral"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Silica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 381, "target_cluster_name": "silica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rutile is a titanium dioxide mineral. Silica (silicon dioxide) is another fundamental mineral component of the Earth's crust, and both are often discussed in geological and mineralogical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rutile is a titanium dioxide mineral. Silica (silicon dioxide) is another fundamental mineral component of the Earth's crust, and both are often discussed in geological and mineralogical contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Godin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "godin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Godin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Godin"], "contexts": ["collaborator who discovered regularity of tides", "scholar who noticed daily and periodic variations"]}, {"entity_id": "godin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Godin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Godin"], "contexts": ["erected monument at equator", "left South America in July 1744"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Godin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Godin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2290, "stable_key": "clu_018949731b7d6780", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Louis Godin", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1704, "death_year": 1760, "description": "French astronomer and geodesist, member of the French Geodesic Mission.", "wikidata_id": "Q1241227", "wikidata_description": "French astronomer", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis%20Godin", "wikipedia_extract": "Louis Godin (28 February 1704 – 11 September 1760) was a French astronomer and member of the French Academy of Sciences. He worked in Peru, Spain, Portugal and France. Godin was born in Paris; his parents were François Godin and Elisabeth Charron. He was graduated at the College of Louis le Grand, and studied astronomy under Joseph-Nicolas Delisle. His astronomical tables (1724) gave him reputation, and the French Academy of Sciences elected him a pensionary member. He was commissioned to write a continuation of the history of the academy, left uncompleted by Bernard le Bovier de Fontenelle, and was also authorized to submit to the minister, Cardinal André-Hercule de Fleury, the best means of discovering the truth in regard to the figure of the Earth, and proposed sending expeditions to the equator and the polar sea."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Charles Marie de La Condamine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1741, "target_cluster_name": "La Condamine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were members of the French Academy of Sciences and participated in the same scientific expedition to South America (the French Geodesic Mission).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were members of the French Academy of Sciences and participated in the same scientific expedition to South America (the French Geodesic Mission)."}, {"found_name": "Alexis Bouvard", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3594, "target_cluster_name": "M. Bouvard", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were French astronomers who worked at the Paris Observatory and were members of the French Academy of Sciences.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were French astronomers who worked at the Paris Observatory and were members of the French Academy of Sciences."}, {"found_name": "Alexis Petit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1454, "target_cluster_name": "Petit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were French astronomers who worked at the Paris Observatory and were members of the French Academy of Sciences.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were French astronomers who worked at the Paris Observatory and were members of the French Academy of Sciences."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ALTAÏ", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "altaï", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ALTAÏ", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Altaï", "ALTAÏ"], "contexts": ["mountains visited during a voyage", "mountain range visited for observations"]}, {"entity_id": "altai", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Altai", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Altai"], "contexts": ["mountain range name"]}, {"entity_id": "altai", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Altai", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Altai"], "contexts": ["mountain chain", "geographic region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ALTAÏ", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Altai", "similarity": 0.843}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Altai", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Altai", "similarity": 0.789}], "id": 2291, "stable_key": "clu_72a20f23ecc3105e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alps", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Switzerland", "note": "'Alpes maritimes' is a specific part of the Alps. 'Altai' is a different mountain range, but 'Alps' is the dominant term.", "wikidata_id": "Q5955", "wikidata_description": "Human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altai_Mountains", "wikipedia_extract": "The Altai Mountains ( ahl-TY) are a mountain range in Central Asia, where Russia, China, Mongolia, and Kazakhstan converge, and where the rivers Irtysh and Ob have their headwaters. The highest summit of the range is Belukha, whose summit reaches 4,506 m (14,783 ft) above sea level. The massif merges with the Sayan Mountains in the northeast, and gradually becomes lower in the southeast, where it merges into the high plateau of the Gobi Desert. In the southwest, it is separated from the higher Tian Shan range by the Junggar Basin. It spans from about 45° to 52° N and from about 84° to 99° E. The region is inhabited by a sparse but ethnically diverse population, including Turkic peoples, Mongols, and Volga Germans, though predominantly represented by indigenous ethnic minorities of semi-nomadic people."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "chrétiens", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "chrétiens", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "chrétiens", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["chrétiens", "chrétienne", "chrétienté"], "contexts": ["followers of Christianity", "religion practiced by a tribe"]}, {"entity_id": "christãos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "christãos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["christãos", "christandade", "christão"], "contexts": ["Christians", "called dogs by mussulmans"]}, {"entity_id": "christian_religion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Christian Religion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Christian Religion"], "contexts": ["specific religious doctrine"]}, {"entity_id": "christianity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Christianity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Christianity"], "contexts": ["religious belief system"]}, {"entity_id": "christianity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Christianity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Christianity"], "contexts": ["religion that promulgated exalted charity"]}, {"entity_id": "christian", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Christian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Christian"], "contexts": ["religion with aspiration not to be damned"]}, {"entity_id": "christian", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Christian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcatego