st of Jewish descent"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portuguezes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 218, "target_cluster_name": "Portuguezes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was a prominent Portuguese physician and humanist of the Renaissance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amato Lusitano was a prominent Portuguese physician and humanist of the Renaissance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mathiolus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 252, "target_cluster_name": "Mathiolus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Amato Lusitano and Mattioli were prominent Italian physicians and botanists of the Renaissance period. They were contemporaries and their contributions to medicine and the study of plants were significant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Amato Lusitano and Mattioli were prominent Italian physicians and botanists of the Renaissance period. They were contemporaries and their contributions to medicine and the study of plants were significant.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Italians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 462, "target_cluster_name": "Italians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was an Italian physician and botanist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amato Lusitano was an Italian physician and botanist.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Monardes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 591, "target_cluster_name": "Monardes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were Spanish physicians and botanists active in the same period, likely aware of each other's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were Spanish physicians and botanists active in the same period, likely aware of each other's work.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Andreas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1395, "target_cluster_name": "Andreas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was a contemporary physician and anatomist whose work, like Vesalius', contributed to the advancement of anatomical knowledge during the Renaissance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amato Lusitano was a contemporary physician and anatomist whose work, like Vesalius', contributed to the advancement of anatomical knowledge during the Renaissance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Niccolao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2011, "target_cluster_name": "Niccolao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was a prominent physician and humanist of Portuguese origin. Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker. While their professions differ, both were significant figures in their respective fields during the Renaissance, and it's plausible they moved in similar intellectual or social circles, or that Antinori's business dealings might have intersected with Lusitano's patronage or travels.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amato Lusitano was a prominent physician and humanist of Portuguese origin. Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker. While their professions differ, both were significant figures in their respective fields during the Renaissance, and it's plausible they moved in similar intellectual or social circles, or that Antinori's business dealings might have intersected with Lusitano's patronage or travels.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano was a physician and botanist who drew heavily on Dioscorides' work, particularly his De Materia Medica, in his own writings on medicinal plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amato Lusitano was a physician and botanist who drew heavily on Dioscorides' work, particularly his De Materia Medica, in his own writings on medicinal plants."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano, like many physicians of his era, was deeply influenced by the medical theories and practices of Galen.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amato Lusitano, like many physicians of his era, was deeply influenced by the medical theories and practices of Galen."}, {"found_name": "Avicenna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amato Lusitano engaged with and commented on the medical works of Avicenna, a foundational figure in Islamic and medieval medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amato Lusitano engaged with and commented on the medical works of Avicenna, a foundational figure in Islamic and medieval medicine."}]}, {"canonical_name": "porcelana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "porcelanas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "porcelanas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["porcelanas"], "contexts": ["porcelain fragments"]}, {"entity_id": "porcelana", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "porcelana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 7, "variants": ["porcelana", "porcellana"], "contexts": ["merchandise from China", "ceramic material traded"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "porcelanas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "porcelana", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2329, "stable_key": "clu_2e16bf65067c0939-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porcelain", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A hard, white, ceramic material, often decorated, originating from China.", "note": "Also refers to a plant with a viscous juice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Porcelain (), also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 °C (2,200 and 2,600 °F). The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware. The manufacturing process used for porcelain is similar to that used for earthenware and stoneware, the two other main types of pottery, although it can be more challenging to produce. It has usually been regarded as the most prestigious type of pottery due to its delicacy, strength, and high degree of whiteness. It is frequently both glazed and decorated."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Minerals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 138, "target_cluster_name": "minerals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Porcelain is made from minerals found in the earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Porcelain is made from minerals found in the earth."}]}, {"canonical_name": "tâmaras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "tâmaras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tâmaras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["tâmaras", "Tâmara", "tâmara"], "contexts": ["cultivated crop", "fruit traded from Arabia to India"]}, {"entity_id": "tamaras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "tamaras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["tamaras"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a remedy", "dates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tâmaras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "tamaras", "similarity": 0.754}], "id": 2330, "stable_key": "clu_b30470427d8290dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dates", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Sweet fruit of the date palm, cultivated and traded.", "wikipedia_extract": "A stem is one of two main structural axes of a vascular plant, the other being the root. It supports leaves, flowers and fruits, transports water and dissolved substances between the roots and the shoots in the xylem and phloem, engages in photosynthesis, stores nutrients, and produces new living tissue. The stem can also be called the culm, halm, haulm, stalk, or thyrsus. The stem is normally divided into nodes and internodes: The nodes are the points of attachment for leaves and can hold one or more leaves. There are sometimes axillary buds between the stem and leaf which can grow into branches (with leaves, conifer cones, or flowers). Adventitious roots (e.g. brace roots) may also be produced from the nodes."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "narcoticos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "drogas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "drogas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["drogas"], "contexts": ["medicinal substances", "items sent to the kingdom"]}, {"entity_id": "narcoticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "narcoticos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 6, "variants": ["narcoticos", "narcotic"], "contexts": ["narcotic remedies", "substances causing torpor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "drogas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "narcoticos", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 2331, "stable_key": "clu_1a4d262d40fbe359", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Narcotics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'drogas' is a broader term for drugs or medicines, but in the context of 'narcoticos', it refers to substances with sedative or stupifying effects.", "wikidata_id": "Q10268802", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive drug whose possession, use or trade is outlawed"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Março", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "marzo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Marzo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marzo"], "contexts": ["month of March"]}, {"entity_id": "março", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Março", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Março"], "contexts": ["month when the patient sought help", "month and day of a medical consultation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Marzo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Março", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2332, "stable_key": "clu_54cb9c5c28114da0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "March", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "April", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 129, "target_cluster_name": "April", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "March is immediately followed by April in the sequence of months.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "March is immediately followed by April in the sequence of months."}, {"found_name": "Aries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 787, "target_cluster_name": "Aries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The astrological sign Aries begins in late March.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The astrological sign Aries begins in late March."}, {"found_name": "Spring", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1628, "target_cluster_name": "fontes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "March is the beginning of Spring in the Northern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "March is the beginning of Spring in the Northern Hemisphere."}, {"found_name": "Summer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 93, "target_cluster_name": "Summer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "March is the end of Summer in the Southern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "March is the end of Summer in the Southern Hemisphere."}]}, {"canonical_name": "caparroſa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "caparosa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "caparosa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["caparosa"], "contexts": ["substancia usada como caustico", "mineral substance mentioned with numbers"]}, {"entity_id": "caparroſa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "caparroſa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["caparroſa", "Caparroſa"], "contexts": ["ingredient in unguent", "herb for bleeding"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "caparosa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "caparroſa", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 2333, "stable_key": "clu_07c6d676222aef12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vitriol", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A sulfate mineral, often used as a caustic or astringent.", "note": "Refers to various sulfate minerals, particularly copper or zinc sulfates.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vitriol is the general chemical name encompassing a class of chemical compounds comprising sulfates of certain metals – originally, iron or copper. Those mineral substances were distinguished by their color, such as green vitriol for hydrated iron(II) sulfate and blue vitriol for hydrated copper(II) sulfate. These materials were found originally as crystals formed by evaporation of groundwater that percolated through sulfide minerals and collected in pools on the floors of old mines. The word vitriol comes from the Latin word vitriolus, meaning \"small glass\", as those crystals resembled small pieces of colored glass. Oil of vitriol was an old name for concentrated sulfuric acid, which was historically obtained through the destructive distillation (pyrolysis) of vitriols (sulfates). The name, abbreviated to vitriol, continued to be used for this viscous liquid long after the minerals came to be termed \"sulfates\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Caparrofa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3693, "target_cluster_name": "caparrofa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caparrofa is listed as a historical name for Vitriol.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Caparrofa is listed as a historical name for Vitriol."}, {"found_name": "Copper", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 274, "target_cluster_name": "copper", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Copper is one of the metals whose sulfates form vitriol (blue vitriol).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Copper is one of the metals whose sulfates form vitriol (blue vitriol)."}, {"found_name": "Iron", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 251, "target_cluster_name": "Iron", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iron is one of the metals whose sulfates form vitriol (green vitriol).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Iron is one of the metals whose sulfates form vitriol (green vitriol)."}, {"found_name": "Acid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1608, "target_cluster_name": "acidos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vitriol is a sulfate, a type of salt derived from sulfuric acid, and thus closely related to the concept of acids in historical chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Vitriol is a sulfate, a type of salt derived from sulfuric acid, and thus closely related to the concept of acids in historical chemistry."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ameyxas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "amexas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "amexas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["amexas"], "contexts": ["fruit described as similar to plums"]}, {"entity_id": "ameyxas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameyxas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["ameyxas", "amcyxas"], "contexts": ["fruit used in remedies", "fresh plums, cooling"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "amexas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameyxas", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 2334, "stable_key": "clu_3065fcd687312eba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "plum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Prunus domestica", "family": "Rosaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q44120", "wikidata_description": "species of plant in the rose family (Rosaceae)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "diureticos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "diurético", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "diurético", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["diurético"], "contexts": ["medicinal property of 'folio indo'"]}, {"entity_id": "diureticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "diureticos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 7, "variants": ["diureticos", "diureticas", "diureticis", "remedios diureticos"], "contexts": ["substances that promote urination", "medicines that promote urination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "diurético", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "diureticos", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 2335, "stable_key": "clu_0aafd6351fc86b84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Diuretic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A substance or property that promotes urination.", "wikipedia_extract": "A diuretic ( ) is any substance that promotes diuresis, the increased production of urine. This includes forced diuresis. A diuretic tablet is sometimes colloquially called a water tablet. There are several categories of diuretics. All diuretics increase the excretion of water from the body, through the kidneys. There exist several classes of diuretic, and each works in a distinct way."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nutrition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1041, "target_cluster_name": "nutrition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diuretics are substances that promote urine production, a bodily function related to waste elimination and fluid balance, which is indirectly linked to the body's overall metabolic and nutritional state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Diuretics are substances that promote urine production, a bodily function related to waste elimination and fluid balance, which is indirectly linked to the body's overall metabolic and nutritional state.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "evacuação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1559, "target_cluster_name": "evacuação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diuretics promote the evacuation of urine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Diuretics promote the evacuation of urine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "tajpát", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Dymock, que aquellas folhas se encontram ainda hoje nas lojas de todos os droguistas da índia; são consideradas um medica- mento estimulante, carminativOi diurético, diaphoreticOy etc; e sSo vulgarmen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vulgar name for Cinnamomum Tamala"}, {"found_name": "tejpãt", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Dymock, que aquellas folhas se encontram ainda hoje nas lojas de todos os droguistas da índia; são consideradas um medica- mento estimulante, carminativOi diurético, diaphoreticOy etc; e sSo vulgarmen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vulgar name for Cinnamomum Tamala"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alem-Tejo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "alemtejo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alemtejo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alemtejo"], "contexts": ["region where expression is current"]}, {"entity_id": "alem-tejo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alem-Tejo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Alem-Tejo"], "contexts": ["Provincia do Alem-Tejo", "region with famous physicians"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alemtejo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alem-Tejo", "similarity": 0.645}], "id": 2336, "stable_key": "clu_9fe8a6bc5b43cbbf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alentejo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "wikidata_id": "Q20963720", "wikidata_description": "geographical, historical and cultural region of Portugal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 37, "target_cluster_name": "Portugal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alentejo is a region in south-central Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alentejo is a region in south-central Portugal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Algarve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2147, "target_cluster_name": "Algarve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Algarve is a neighboring region in Portugal, historically and geographically linked to Alentejo.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Algarve is a neighboring region in Portugal, historically and geographically linked to Alentejo."}, {"found_name": "Évora", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2080, "target_cluster_name": "Evora", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Évora is a major city located within the Alentejo region and served as its historical capital.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Évora is a major city located within the Alentejo region and served as its historical capital."}, {"found_name": "Lisbon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 224, "target_cluster_name": "Lisboa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lisbon is the capital of Portugal and a major port city, historically connected to Alentejo through trade and administration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lisbon is the capital of Portugal and a major port city, historically connected to Alentejo through trade and administration."}]}, {"canonical_name": "ventoſidades", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "ventosidades_grossas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ventosidades grossas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["ventosidades grossas"], "contexts": ["thick flatulence"]}, {"entity_id": "ventoſidades", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ventoſidades", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 7, "variants": ["ventoſidades", "ventosidades"], "contexts": ["flatulence", "Condition caused by drinking wine too soon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ventosidades grossas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ventoſidades", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 2337, "stable_key": "clu_79892e6f67f603a1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flatulence", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Accumulation of gas in the digestive tract; intestinal wind.", "note": "Includes terms for intestinal gas, windiness, and flatulence.", "wikipedia_extract": "Flatulence is the expulsion of gas from the intestines via the anus, commonly referred to as farting. \"Flatus\" is the medical word for gas generated in the stomach or bowels. A proportion of intestinal gas may be swallowed environmental air; hence, flatus is not entirely generated in the stomach or bowels. The scientific study of this area of medicine is termed flatology. Passing gas is a normal bodily process. Flatus is brought to the rectum and pressurized by muscles in the intestines. It is normal to pass flatus (\"to fart\"), though volume and frequency vary greatly among individuals."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Stupor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2697, "target_cluster_name": "stupor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Severe gastrointestinal distress, potentially including extreme flatulence, could lead to stupor in historical medical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Severe gastrointestinal distress, potentially including extreme flatulence, could lead to stupor in historical medical contexts."}, {"found_name": "parreyra brava", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "a quem tiver palpitaçoens , duas atètres onças. 7. Eſe todoseſtes remedios forem baldados,& o fugeyto for velho,defcorado, friorento , ou queyxoſo de ventoſidades ,lhe daremos tres onças de bom vinho ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for raiz de parreyra brava"}]}, {"canonical_name": "rabaças", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "rabaça", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rabaça", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rabaça"], "contexts": ["plant, possibly a synonym for napelo"]}, {"entity_id": "rabaças", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "rabaças", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["rabaças", "rabao", "rabaos", "rabaō"], "contexts": ["radish leaves used in conserve", "leaves used in conserve"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rabaça", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "rabaças", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 2338, "stable_key": "clu_12e8cc9aa3552d5a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Radish", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Raphanus sativus", "family": "Brassicaceae", "description": "Edible root vegetable with a peppery flavor, commonly consumed raw.", "note": "The term 'rabô' in one context appears to refer to a specific type of pepper, but the overwhelming evidence points to 'radish' for the other instances.", "wikipedia_extract": "The radish (Raphanus sativus or Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus) is a flowering plant in the mustard family, Brassicaceae. Its large taproot is commonly used as a root vegetable, although the entire plant is edible and its leaves are sometimes used as a leaf vegetable. Originally domesticated in Asia, radishes are now grown and consumed globally. The radish is sometimes considered to form a species complex with the wild radish and instead treated as the subspecies Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus. Radishes are often used raw as a crunchy salad vegetable with a pungent, slightly spicy flavor, varying in intensity depending on its growing environment. There are numerous varieties varying in size, flavor, color, and length of time they take to mature."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Petrofellino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "petrofellino", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Petrofellino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Petrofellino", "Petrofelino Macedonico", "Petrofilio"], "contexts": ["Parsley, ingredient in syrup", "parsley herb"]}, {"entity_id": "petrofelino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "petrofelino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["petrofelino"], "contexts": ["herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Petrofellino", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "petrofelino", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 2339, "stable_key": "clu_fcb08df250329926", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parsley", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Petroselinum crispum", "family": "Apiaceae", "description": "An aromatic herb used in cooking and traditional medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Parsley, or garden parsley (Petroselinum crispum), is a species of flowering plant in the family Apiaceae that is native to the Balkans. It has been introduced and naturalized in Europe and elsewhere in the world with suitable climates, and is widely cultivated as a herb and a vegetable. It is believed to have been originally grown in Sardinia, and was cultivated in around the 3rd century BC. Linnaeus stated its wild habitat to be Sardinia, whence it was brought to England and apparently first cultivated in Britain in 1548, though literary evidence suggests parsley was used in England in the Middle Ages as early as the Anglo-Saxon period. Parsley is widely used in European, Middle Eastern, and American cuisine. Curly-leaf parsley is often used as a garnish."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Umbellifera", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 882, "target_cluster_name": "Umbellifera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Parsley (Petroselinum crispum) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the parsley family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Parsley (Petroselinum crispum) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the parsley family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ameos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2467, "target_cluster_name": "ameos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammi visnaga is closely related to parsley and shares some botanical characteristics and historical uses within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammi visnaga is closely related to parsley and shares some botanical characteristics and historical uses within the Apiaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "coentro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1767, "target_cluster_name": "coentro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Parsley (Petroselinum) is a closely related herb in the Apiaceae family, often confused or used similarly in culinary and medicinal contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Parsley (Petroselinum) is a closely related herb in the Apiaceae family, often confused or used similarly in culinary and medicinal contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Parsnip", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2356, "target_cluster_name": "Parsnip", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are herbaceous plants in the Apiaceae family, with similar leaf structures and culinary applications, though Parsnip is typically larger and has a different root.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are herbaceous plants in the Apiaceae family, with 