ther's legacy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "leipzig", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Leipzig"], "contexts": ["location of publication", "city where book was published"]}, {"entity_id": "leipzig", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Leipzig"], "contexts": ["city of publication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Leipzig", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Leipzig", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2417, "stable_key": "clu_c271a23979de27f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leipzig", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "City in Germany, significant for publication.", "note": "Refers to the city of Leipzig, Germany, a known center for printing and academic work.", "wikipedia_extract": "Leipzig is the most populous city in the German state of Saxony. The city has a population of 628,718 inhabitants as of 2023. It is the eighth-largest city in Germany and is part of the Central German Metropolitan Region. Leipzig is located about 150 km (90 mi) southwest of Berlin, in the southernmost part of the North German Plain (the Leipzig Bay), at the confluence of the White Elster and its tributaries Pleiße and Parthe. Leipzig has been a trade city since at least the time of the Holy Roman Empire. Via Regia and the Via Imperii, two important medieval trade routes, intersected here, marking the city's economic importance."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Saxony", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2360, "target_cluster_name": "Saxony", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Leipzig is listed as the largest city in Saxony in the description.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Leipzig is listed as the largest city in Saxony in the description.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "synthesis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["synthesis"], "contexts": ["combining elements into a whole", "function of combining elements"]}, {"entity_id": "synthesis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["synthesis"], "contexts": ["combining components into a whole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "synthesis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "synthesis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2418, "stable_key": "clu_a863b3c397d40319", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "synthesis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q851162", "wikidata_description": "multi-step, enzyme-catalyzed process where substrates are converted into more complex products in living organisms. In biosynthesis, simple compounds are modified, converted into other compounds, or joined to form macromolecules", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biosynthesis", "wikipedia_extract": "Biosynthesis, i.e., chemical synthesis occurring in biological contexts, is a term most often referring to multi-step, enzyme-catalyzed processes where chemical substances absorbed as nutrients (or previously converted through biosynthesis) serve as enzyme substrates, with conversion by the living organism either into simpler or more complex products. Examples of biosynthetic pathways include those for the production of amino acids, lipid membrane components, and nucleotides, but also for the production of all classes of biological macromolecules, and of acetyl-coenzyme A, adenosine triphosphate, nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and other key intermediate and transactional molecules needed for metabolism. Thus, in biosynthesis, any of an array of compounds, from simple to complex, are converted into other compounds, and so it includes both the anabolism and catabolism (building up and breaking down) of complex molecules (including macromolecules). Biosynthetic processes are often represented via charts of metabolic pathways. A particular biosynthetic pathway may be located within a single cellular organelle (e.g., mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis pathways), while others involve enzymes that are located across an array of cellular organelles and structures (e.g., the biosynthesis of glycosylated cell surface proteins). Elements of biosynthesis include: precursor compounds, chemical energy (e.g."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Digestion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 338, "target_cluster_name": "digestion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Digestion is a biological process that breaks down nutrients, which can then be used as substrates for biosynthesis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Digestion is a biological process that breaks down nutrients, which can then be used as substrates for biosynthesis."}, {"found_name": "Chemistry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 304, "target_cluster_name": "Chemistry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Biosynthesis is a subfield of chemistry, specifically organic chemistry and biochemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Biosynthesis is a subfield of chemistry, specifically organic chemistry and biochemistry."}, {"found_name": "Development", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 960, "target_cluster_name": "development", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Biosynthesis is a key component of biological development and growth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Biosynthesis is a key component of biological development and growth."}, {"found_name": "Generation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 166, "target_cluster_name": "generation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Biosynthesis is essential for the generation of new biological molecules and structures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Biosynthesis is essential for the generation of new biological molecules and structures."}]}, {"canonical_name": "visual perception", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "visual_perception", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "visual perception", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["visual perception"], "contexts": ["process of interpreting visual information", "process of seeing objects in space"]}, {"entity_id": "visual_perceptions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "visual perceptions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["visual perceptions"], "contexts": ["perceptions gained through sight", "perceptions received through sight"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "visual perception", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "visual perceptions", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 2419, "stable_key": "clu_e5b0598bfeea999a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "visual perception", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Image", "link_type":