ations, stars, and astronomical works firmly points to the celestial constellation.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lyra (Latin for 'lyre', from Ancient Greek: λύρα; pronounced:  LY-rə) is a small constellation. It is one of the 48 listed by the 2nd century astronomer Ptolemy, and is one of the modern 88 constellations recognized by the International Astronomical Union. Lyra was often represented on star maps as a vulture or an eagle carrying a lyre, and hence is sometimes referred to as Vultur Cadens or Aquila Cadens (\"Falling Vulture\" or \"Falling Eagle\"), respectively. Beginning at the north, Lyra is bordered by Draco, Hercules, Vulpecula, and Cygnus. Lyra is nearly overhead in temperate northern latitudes shortly after midnight at the start of summer. From the equator to about the 40th parallel south it is visible low in the northern sky during the same (thus winter) months."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lyra is a constellation, a fundamental object of study in astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lyra is a constellation, a fundamental object of study in astronomy."}, {"found_name": "Celestial body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3227, "target_cluster_name": "celestial body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lyra is a constellation, which is a grouping of celestial bodies (stars).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lyra is a constellation, which is a grouping of celestial bodies (stars)."}, {"found_name": "Aries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 787, "target_cluster_name": "Aries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aries is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aries is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other."}, {"found_name": "Leo (constellation)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2837, "target_cluster_name": "Leo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Leo (constellation) is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Leo (constellation) is another constellation, and constellations are often discussed together or in relation to each other."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Brewster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "brewster", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Brewster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brewster"], "contexts": ["author of 'Life of Sir Isaac Newton'", "author of 'Treaties on Magnetism'"]}, {"entity_id": "brewster", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Brewster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Brewster"], "contexts": ["scholar who concluded refraction without colour", "analyzed solar spectrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brewster", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brewster", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2453, "stable_key": "clu_e9b8a7642a0c29fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "David Brewster", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1781, "death_year": 1868, "description": "Scottish physicist and inventor known for work on optics"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "SIR DAVID BREWSTER", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_str