s of Assisi", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2621, "target_cluster_name": "S. Francisco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are prominent figures in Catholic missionary work and venerated as saints.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are prominent figures in Catholic missionary work and venerated as saints."}, {"found_name": "Jesuits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3023, "target_cluster_name": "Jésuites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Francis Xavier was a co-founder of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Francis Xavier was a co-founder of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits)."}, {"found_name": "Jesus Christ", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 13, "target_cluster_name": "Christ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Francis Xavier was a key figure in spreading Christianity, inspired by Jesus Christ.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Francis Xavier was a key figure in spreading Christianity, inspired by Jesus Christ."}, {"found_name": "S. Francisco", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2621, "target_cluster_name": "S. Francisco", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are prominent figures named Francis, and Francis Xavier is a well-known saint, similar to Francis of Assisi. The concordance likely distinguishes between them by full name or title, but their shared first name and religious significance create a strong association.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are prominent figures named Francis, and Francis Xavier is a well-known saint, similar to Francis of Assisi. The concordance likely distinguishes between them by full name or title, but their shared first name and religious significance create a strong association.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "lentilhas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "lentilhas_de_agoa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "lentilhas de agoa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["lentilhas de agoa", "lentilha de agoa"], "contexts": ["plants that grow on water", "aquatic plant"]}, {"entity_id": "lentilhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lentilhas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["lentilhas", "Lentilhas"], "contexts": ["ingredient for mouthwash", "lentils used in a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "lentilhas de agoa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lentilhas", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 2471, "stable_key": "clu_c8487a7c8e065f2e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lentil", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Lens culinaris", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "A herbaceous annual legume plant grown for its edible seed.", "note": "Includes related terms for aquatic plants and similar-leafed plants, suggesting broader botanical interest.", "wikipedia_extract": "The lentil (Vicia lens or Lens culinaris) is an annual legume grown for its lens-shaped edible seeds or pulses, also called lentils. It is about 40 cm (16 in) tall, and the seeds grow in pods, usually with two seeds in each. Lentil seeds are used around the world for culinary purposes, most commonly in stews or soups. In cuisines of the Indian subcontinent, where lentils are a staple, split lentils (often with their hulls removed) known as dal are often cooked into a thick curry that is usually eaten with rice or roti. The English word \"lentil\" ultimately derives from the Latin lens ('lentil'). The Latin word is of classical Roman or Latin origin and may be the source of the prominent Roman family name Lentulus, just as the family name \"Cicero\" was derived from the chickpea, Cicer arietinum, and \"Fabia\" (as in Quintus Fabius Maximus) from the fava bean (Vicia faba)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "legumes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1901, "target_cluster_name": "legumes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lentils are a specific type of legume, and are often discussed in conjunction with other legumes in historical texts concerning food and agriculture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lentils are a specific type of legume, and are often discussed in conjunction with other legumes in historical texts concerning food and agriculture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Leguminosa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2184, "target_cluster_name": "Leguminosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lentil is a specific type of legume, and Fabaceae is the family that includes legumes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Lentil is a specific type of legume, and Fabaceae is the family that includes legumes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lupini", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2714, "target_cluster_name": "Lupini", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Lupins and Lentils are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Lupins and Lentils are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "escammonea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "escamonea", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "escamonea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["escamonea"], "contexts": ["herb used as purgative", "plant used for purging"]}, {"entity_id": "escammonea", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "escammonea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["escammonea", "Scammonea", "escamonea", "scammonen", "scamonea"], "contexts": ["violent plant substance", "herb used for purging"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "escamonea", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "escammonea", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 2472, "stable_key": "clu_2ef90a29d2c08d6f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scammony", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Convolvulus scammonia", "family": "Convolvulaceae", "description": "Resinous purgative obtained from the root of a bindweed plant.", "wikipedia_extract": "Convolvulus scammonia, commonly known as scammony, is a bindweed native to the countries of the eastern part of the Mediterranean basin; it grows in bushy waste places, from Syria in the south to Crimea in the north, its range extending westward to the Greek islands, but not to northern Africa or Italy. It is a twining perennial, bearing flowers like those of Convolvulus arvensis, and having irregularly arrow-shaped leaves and a thick fleshy root. A cathartic resinous tincture known as scammoniae resina, which is obtained from the dried root by digestion with ethanol has been used as a traditional medicine. Upon consumption, the resin is inert until it has passed from the stomach into the duodenum, where it meets the bile. A chemical reaction occurs between it and taurocholate and glycocholate in the bile, whereby it is converted into a powerful purgative which in high doses becomes a violent gastrointestinal irritant. Scammony kills both roundworm and tapeworm, especially the former, and it was therefore used as an anthelmintic."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Turbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 255, "target_cluster_name": "Turbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Turpeth and Scammony are potent purgative agents derived from plants, and were often discussed together in materia medica for their similar drastic effects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Turpeth and Scammony are potent purgative agents derived from plants, and were often discussed together in materia medica for their similar drastic effects.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Elvas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "elvas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Elvas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Elvas"], "contexts": ["Location of commissioners' meeting", "city where commissioners met"]}, {"entity_id": "elvas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Elvas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Elvas"], "contexts": ["city in Portugal", "city where the bishop is from"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Elvas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Elvas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2473, "stable_key": "clu_096283220888bb7a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elvas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A city in the Alentejo region of Portugal.", "wikipedia_extract": "Elvas (Portuguese pronunciation: [ˈɛlvɐʃ] ), officially the City of Elvas (Portuguese: Cidade de Elvas), is a Portuguese municipality, former episcopal city and frontier fortress of easternmost central Portugal, located in the district of Portalegre in Alentejo. It is situated about 200 kilometres (120 mi) east of Lisbon, and about 8 kilometres (5.0 mi) west of the Spanish fortress of Badajoz, by the Madrid-Badajoz-Lisbon railway. The municipality population as of 2011 was 23,078, in an area of 631.29 square kilometres (243.74 sq mi). The city itself had a population of 16,640 as of 2011. Elvas is among the finest examples of intensive usage of the trace italienne (star fort) in military architecture, and has been a World Heritage Site since 30 June 2012. The inscribed site name is Garrison Border Town of Elvas and its Fortifications."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 37, "target_cluster_name": "Portugal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elvas is a municipality in Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elvas is a municipality in Portugal."}, {"found_name": "Évora", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2080, "target_cluster_name": "Evora", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Évora is another significant city in the Alentejo region of Portugal, often mentioned in proximity to Elvas in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Évora is another significant city in the Alentejo region of Portugal, often mentioned in proximity to Elvas in historical contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bode", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bode", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["bode"], "contexts": ["goat where the stone is found", "animal from which bezoar stone is created"]}, {"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bode", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bode", "bode", "bove"], "contexts": ["goat", "animal used for remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bode", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 2474, "stable_key": "clu_0dc57898d99863d8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Goat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "An animal, specifically a goat, from which the bezoar stone was historically derived.", "wikipedia_extract": "The goat or domestic goat (Capra hircus) is a species of goat-antelope that is mostly kept as livestock. It was domesticated from the wild goat (C. aegagrus) of Southwest Asia and Eastern Europe. The goat is a member of the family Bovidae, meaning it is closely related to the sheep. It was one of the first animals to be domesticated, in Iran around 10,000 years ago. Goats have been used for milk, meat, wool, and skins across much of the world. Milk from goats is often turned into cheese."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "triaga", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 309, "target_cluster_name": "Theriaca", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "na mão, e lhe asopram pêra ver se lhe sai o Da pedra be^ar 233 vento; porque estas tem elles por contrafeitas. Chamase esta pedra pa^ar, do pa^am (bode así chamado), e asi quando ▼os cá pedem alguma m", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for pa^ar/locomium in some contexts"}, {"found_name": "Sheep", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 717, "target_cluster_name": "sheep", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Goats and sheep are both members of the family Bovidae and are often discussed together as closely related livestock.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Goats and sheep are both members of the family Bovidae and are often discussed together as closely related livestock."}, {"found_name": "locomio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "na mão, e lhe asopram pêra ver se lhe sai o Da pedra be^ar 233 vento; porque estas tem elles por contrafeitas. Chamase esta pedra pa^ar, do pa^am (bode así chamado), e asi quando ▼os cá pedem alguma m", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for pa^ar/locomium in some contexts"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "hera", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hera"], "contexts": ["plant with creeping growth", "plant mentioned as an example"]}, {"entity_id": "hera", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Hera", "hera"], "contexts": ["plant used in water for drinking", "leaves used for heating feet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Hera", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hera", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2475, "stable_key": "clu_efba16c6ad2a16ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hera", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "description": "A plant used medicinally, possibly for foot baths and drinking water.", "note": "The context suggests a common herb, but 'Hera' is not a recognized common name for a specific plant in this context. It might be a misspelling or a regional name.", "wikipedia_extract": "In ancient Greek religion, Hera (; Ancient Greek: Ἥρα, romanized: Hḗrā; Ἥρη, Hḗrē in Ionic and Homeric Greek) is the goddess of marriage, women, and family, and the protector of women during childbirth. In Greek mythology, she is queen of the twelve Olympians and Mount Olympus, sister and wife of Zeus, and daughter of the Titans Cronus and Rhea. One of her defining characteristics in myth is her jealous and vengeful nature in dealing with any who offended her, especially Zeus's numerous adulterous lovers and illegitimate offspring. Her iconography usually presents her as a dignified, matronly figure, upright or enthroned, crowned with a polos or diadem, sometimes veiled as a married woman. She is the patron goddess of lawful marriage. She presides over weddings, blesses and legalises marital unions, and protects women from harm during childbirth."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ferula", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "ferula", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ferula", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["ferula"], "contexts": ["plant name, possibly fennel", "plant with similar leaf"]}, {"entity_id": "ferula", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "ferula", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["ferula"], "contexts": ["plant similar to Euphorbium", "plant from which Galbanum is derived"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ferula", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "ferula", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2476, "stable_key": "clu_a8898920d90ca209", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fennel", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Foeniculum vulgare", "family": "Apiaceae", "note": "While 'ferula' can refer to the genus Ferula, in early modern texts, it was often used more broadly and could also refer to plants like fennel, especially when compared to Euphorbium. The context 'plant similar to Euphorbium' suggests a strong, possibly acrid sap, which could apply to Ferula or other plants."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Umbellifera", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 882, "target_cluster_name": "Umbellifera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is a genus within the Apiaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ameos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2467, "target_cluster_name": "ameos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ammi visnaga is botanically related to fennel, both belonging to the Apiaceae family, and they have been historically confused or grouped together due to similar appearances and uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ammi visnaga is botanically related to fennel, both belonging to the Apiaceae family, and they have been historically confused or grouped together due to similar appearances and uses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Apiaceae", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 882, "target_cluster_name": "Umbellifera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fennel belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fennel belongs to the Apiaceae family (also known as Umbelliferae)."}, {"found_name": "Anise", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2499, "target_cluster_name": "anime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are aromatic herbs in the Apiaceae family, often used in similar culinary and medicinal contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are aromatic herbs in the Apiaceae family, often used in similar culinary and medicinal contexts."}, {"found_name": "Coriander", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1767, "target_cluster_name": "coentro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are aromatic herbs in the Apiaceae family, with seeds and leaves used in cooking and traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are aromatic herbs in the Apiaceae family, with seeds and leaves used in cooking and traditional medicine."}, {"found_name": "Sweet Flag", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 620, "target_cluster_name": "calamo aromático", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are aromatic plants with historical medicinal uses, though they belong to different families (Apiaceae for Fennel, Acoraceae for Sweet Flag).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are aromatic plants with historical medicinal uses, though they belong to different families (Apiaceae for Fennel, Acoraceae for Sweet Flag)."}, {"found_name": "coentro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1767, "target_cluster_name": "coentro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is also in the Apiaceae family and shares a similar aromatic quality and historical medicinal applications with coriander.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is also in the Apiaceae family and shares a similar aromatic quality and historical medicinal applications with coriander.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "mantimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "mantimentos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mantimentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mantimentos"], "contexts": ["provisions on the ship"]}, {"entity_id": "mantimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mantimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["mantimento", "mantimentos"], "contexts": ["food", "food, sustenance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mantimentos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mantimento", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2477, "stable_key": "clu_6c26bac126d1a63a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Food", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Sustenance for humans and provisions for voyages.", "wikipedia_extract": "Food is any substance consumed by an organism for nutritional support. Food usually consists of plant, animal, or fungal origin and contains essential nutrients such as carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, or minerals. The substance is ingested by an organism and assimilated by the organism's cells to provide energy, maintain life, or support growth. Different species of animals have different feeding behaviours that satisfy the needs of their metabolisms and have evolved to fill specific ecological niches within specific geographical contexts. Omnivorous humans are highly adaptable and have adapted to obtaining food in many different ecosystems. Humans generally use cooking to prepare food for consumption."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Milk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 637, "target_cluster_name": "Milk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Milk is a nutrient-rich liquid food produced by mammals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Milk is a nutrient-rich liquid food produced by mammals."}, {"found_name": "Honey", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 227, "target_cluster_name": "Honey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Honey is a sweet, viscous food substance produced by honey bees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Honey is a sweet, viscous food substance produced by honey bees."}, {"found_name": "Sugar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 254, "target_cluster_name": "Sugar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sugar is a carbohydrate often used as a food ingredient or sweetener.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sugar is a carbohydrate often used as a food ingredient or sweetener."}, {"found_name": "Salt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 54, "target_cluster_name": "sal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Salt is a mineral substance essential for life and used as a food additive.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Salt is a mineral substance essential for life and used as a food additive."}, {"found_name": "Alcohol", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 131, "target_cluster_name": "alcohol", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alcohol is a substance that can be consumed as a beverage and has nutritional implications.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alcohol is a substance that can be consumed as a beverage and has nutritional implications."}]}, {"canonical_name": "amargofo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "sabor_amarguoz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sabor amarguoz", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sabor amarguoz"], "contexts": ["bitter taste of cate"]}, {"entity_id": "amargofo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "amargofo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["amargofo", "amargofas", "amargofojo"], "contexts": ["bitter taste", "bitter substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sabor amarguoz", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "amargofo", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 2478, "stable_key": "clu_260498a46d6e4176", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bitterness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The quality of having a sharp, pungent taste; a bitter substance.", "note": "Refers to the taste quality or substances possessing it.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bitterness, also known as bitter, is one of the most sensitive of tastes, and many experience it as unpleasant, sharp, or off-putting, but it is sometimes desirable and intentionally added via various bittering agents. Common bitter foods and drinks include coffee, unsweetened cocoa, South American mate, coca tea, Momordica charantia, unripe olives, citrus peel, some varieties of cheese, many plants in the Brassicaceae family, Taraxacum greens, Marrubium vulgare, wild chicory, and escarole. The ethanol in alcoholic beverages tastes bitter, as do the additional bittering ingredients found in some alcoholic beverages, including hops in beer and gentian in bitters. Quinine is also known for its bitter taste and is found in tonic water. Bitterness is of interest to those studying evolution, as well as to various health researchers as a large number of naturally occurring bitter compounds are known to be toxic. The ability to detect bitter-tasting, toxic compounds at low thresholds is thought to serve an important protective function."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "acidez", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2434, "target_cluster_name": "acidez", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, acidity is a related taste quality that can sometimes be confused with or co-occur with bitterness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While distinct, acidity is a related taste quality that can sometimes be confused with or co-occur with bitterness."}, {"found_name": "Taste", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 858, "target_cluster_name": "taste", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitterness is one of the fundamental categories of taste.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bitterness is one of the fundamental categories of taste."}]}, {"canonical_name": "filhos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "fílhos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fílhos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["fílhos"], "contexts": ["children of the king"]}, {"entity_id": "filho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "filho", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["filho"], "contexts": ["male offspring"]}, {"entity_id": "filhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "filhos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["filhos", "filha", "filho"], "contexts": ["offspring, children", "children"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fílhos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "filhos", "similarity": 0.517}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "filho", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "filhos", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 2479, "stable_key": "clu_693438570b9312f3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Children", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q239526", "wikidata_description": "in biology, the product of reproduction of an organism"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "carvaó", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "carvão", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "carvão", "category": "SUBSTANCE