a Greek physician and historian whose work, though distinct from Homer's, engaged with ancient Greek narratives and history.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Arriano", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2263, "target_cluster_name": "Arriano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are ancient Greek writers who wrote about history and exotic lands.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are ancient Greek writers who wrote about history and exotic lands.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Herodotus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 337, "target_cluster_name": "Heródoto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ctesias was a Greek physician and historian, contemporary with Herodotus, and his work 'Persica' was a continuation of Herodotus's 'Histories'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ctesias was a Greek physician and historian, contemporary with Herodotus, and his work 'Persica' was a continuation of Herodotus's 'Histories'."}, {"found_name": "Diodorus Siculus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2667, "target_cluster_name": "Diodorus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diodorus Siculus drew heavily on Ctesias's work, particularly for his accounts of Assyrian and Persian history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diodorus Siculus drew heavily on Ctesias's work, particularly for his accounts of Assyrian and Persian history."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rueus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rueus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rueus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rueus"], "contexts": ["writer on natural history", "author on occult miracles of nature"]}, {"entity_id": "ruelius", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ruelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ruelius"], "contexts": ["mentioned in a note by the author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rueus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ruelius", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 2492, "stable_key": "clu_f0c2c826618178d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean Ruel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1474, "death_year": 1537, "description": "French physician and botanist, wrote on materia medica.", "note": "The spelling 'Rueus'/'Ruelius' is an older Latinization of Jean Ruel.", "wikidata_id": "Q1592626", "wikidata_description": "French botanist (1474-1537)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Euclide", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "euclide", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Euclide", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Euclide"], "contexts": ["ancient mathematician", "mathematician, example of logical impossibility"]}, {"entity_id": "euclid", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Euclid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Euclid"], "contexts": ["ancient mathematician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Euclide", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Euclid", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 2493, "stable_key": "clu_e0312d635207fcd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Euclid", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -300, "death_year": -295, "description": "Ancient Greek mathematician and geometer, author of Elements.", "wikidata_id": "Q1949136", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_geometry", "wikipedia_extract": "Euclidean geometry is a mathematical system attributed to Euclid, an ancient Greek mathematician, which he described in his textbook on geometry, Elements. Euclid's approach consists in assuming a small set of intuitively appealing axioms (postulates) and deducing many other propositions (theorems) from these. One of those is the parallel postulate which relates to parallel lines on a Euclidean plane. Although many of Euclid's results had been stated earlier, Euclid was the first to organize these propositions into a logical system in which each result is proved from axioms and previously proved theorems. The Elements begins with plane geometry, still taught in secondary school (high school) as the first axiomatic system and the first examples of mathematical proofs. It goes on to the solid geometry of three dimensions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Newton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 71, "target_cluster_name": "Newton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newton's mathematical rigor and approach to geometry were deeply influenced by Euclid's Elements, which served as a fundamental text in mathematical education.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Newton's mathematical rigor and approach to geometry were deeply influenced by Euclid's Elements, which served as a fundamental text in mathematical education.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Eratosthenes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1129, "target_cluster_name": "Eratosthenes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent ancient Greek mathematicians, with Euclid being foundational to geometry and Eratosthenes known for his work in geography and mathematics.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent ancient Greek mathematicians, with Euclid being foundational to geometry and Eratosthenes known for his work in geography and mathematics.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mathematicians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1337, "target_cluster_name": "mathematicians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Euclid is considered the 'father of geometry' and his work 'Elements' is a cornerstone of mathematical education and thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Euclid is considered the 'father of geometry' and his work 'Elements' is a cornerstone of mathematical education and thought.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Archimedes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 480, "target_cluster_name": "Archimedes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are foundational figures in ancient Greek mathematics and geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are foundational figures in ancient Greek mathematics and geometry."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are towering figures of ancient Greek intellectual history, with Aristotle's work influencing many fields, including mathematics and logic, which are foundational to Euclid's work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are towering figures of ancient Greek intellectual history, with Aristotle's work influencing many fields, including mathematics and logic, which are foundational to Euclid's work."}, {"found_name": "Plato", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 113, "target_cluster_name": "Plato", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are central figures in ancient Greek philosophy and mathematics. Plato's Academy was a significant intellectual environment, and his philosophical ideas influenced mathematical thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are central figures in ancient Greek philosophy and mathematics. Plato's Academy was a significant intellectual environment, and his philosophical ideas influenced mathematical thought."}, {"found_name": "Ptolemy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 824, "target_cluster_name": "Ptolemy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are highly influential ancient Greek mathematicians and astronomers whose works were foundational for centuries of scientific study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are highly influential ancient Greek mathematicians and astronomers whose works were foundational for centuries of scientific study."}, {"found_name": "Pythagoras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 354, "target_cluster_name": "Pythagoras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are key figures in ancient Greek mathematics, with Pythagoras's theorem being a fundamental concept in Euclidean geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are key figures in ancient Greek mathematics, with Pythagoras's theorem being a fundamental concept in Euclidean geometry."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Centaures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "centaures", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Centaures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Centaures"], "contexts": ["mythical creatures", "mythical creature, origin explained by mistake"]}, {"entity_id": "centaur", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Centaur", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Centaur"], "contexts": ["constellation", "constellation and star for parallax"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Centaures", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Centaur", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 2494, "stable_key": "clu_ce4856822242ead8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alpha Centauri", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "This cluster appears to conflate a star system with mythical creatures. The primary referent from the contexts and common scientific usage is the star system.", "wikidata_id": "Q12176", "wikidata_description": "triple star system in the constellation Centaurus, where one of the three stars, Proxima Centauri, being the closest star to the Earth", "wikipedia_extract": "Alpha Centauri (α Centauri, α Cen, or Alpha Cen) is a star system in the southern constellation of Centaurus. It consists of three stars: Rigil Kentaurus (α Centauri A), Toliman (α Centauri B), and Proxima Centauri (α Centauri C). Proxima Centauri is the closest star to the Sun at 4.2465 light-years (ly), which is 1.3020 parsecs (pc), while Alpha Centauri A and B are the nearest stars visible to the naked eye. Rigil Kentaurus and Toliman are Sun-like stars (class G and K, respectively) that together form the binary star system α Centauri AB. To the naked eye, these two main components appear to be a single star with an apparent magnitude of −0.27. It is the brightest star in the constellation and the third-brightest in the night sky, outshone by only Sirius and Canopus. α Centauri AB are the nearest binary stars to the Sun at a distance of 4.344 ly (1.33 pc).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Stars", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2672, "target_cluster_name": "Stars", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alpha Centauri is a specific star system, an example of the entity 'Stars'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alpha Centauri is a specific star system, an example of the entity 'Stars'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "61 Cygni", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1745, "target_cluster_name": "61 Cygni", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "61 Cygni is another star system that was historically significant in early measurements of stellar parallax and distance, similar to Alpha Centauri.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "61 Cygni is another star system that was historically significant in early measurements of stellar parallax and distance, similar to Alpha Centauri."}, {"found_name": "Aldebaran", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3027, "target_cluster_name": "Aldebaran", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aldebaran is a bright star, and like Alpha Centauri, it would have been a significant object of observation for astronomers studying stars.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aldebaran is a bright star, and like Alpha Centauri, it would have been a significant object of observation for astronomers studying stars."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "ovid", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ovid"], "contexts": ["Roman poet", "ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "ovid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ovid"], "contexts": ["Roman poet who described natural phenomenon", "ancient poet describing eruption"]}, {"entity_id": "ovidio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ovidio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ovidio", "Ovídio"], "contexts": ["classical author of metamorphoses", "classical Greek and Latin mythology comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "ovidio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ovidio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ovidio"], "contexts": ["cited for a quote about monuments and writing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ovid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ovid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2495, "stable_key": "clu_0d6da417cb9a92c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ovid", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman poet, author of 'Metamorphoses' and known for classical mythology.", "wikipedia_extract": "Publius Ovidius Naso (Latin: [ˈpuːbliʊs ɔˈwɪdiʊs ˈnaːsoː]; 20 March 43 BC – AD 17/18), known in English as Ovid ( OV-id), was a Roman poet who lived during the reign of Augustus. He was a younger contemporary of Virgil and Horace, with whom he is often ranked as one of the three canonical poets of Latin literature. The Imperial scholar Quintilian considered him the last of the Latin love elegists. Although Ovid enjoyed enormous popularity during his lifetime, the emperor Augustus exiled him to Tomis, the capital of the newly organised province of Moesia, on the Black Sea, where he remained for the last nine or ten years of his life. Ovid himself attributed his banishment to a carmen et error (\"poem and a mistake\"), but his reluctance to disclose specifics has resulted in much speculation among scholars. Ovid is most famous for the Metamorphoses, a continuous mythological narrative in fifteen books written in dactylic hexameters."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Virgil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 427, "target_cluster_name": "Virgil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent Roman poets of the Augustan age, and their works are often studied together as representative of classical Latin literature. Ovid was a contemporary of Virgil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent Roman poets of the Augustan age, and their works are often studied together as representative of classical Latin literature. Ovid was a contemporary of Virgil.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Augustus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2668, "target_cluster_name": "Augustus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ovid lived during the reign of Augustus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ovid lived during the reign of Augustus."}, {"found_name": "Juvenal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2902, "target_cluster_name": "Juvenal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Juvenal and Ovid were both prominent Roman satirists and poets, often studied together for their literary contributions and social commentary.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Juvenal and Ovid were both prominent Roman satirists and poets, often studied together for their literary contributions and social commentary.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Aarons", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "aarons", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Aarons", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aarons"], "contexts": ["biblical figure associated with breast-plate", "brestplate stones"]}, {"entity