m-to-large trees, most of which are deciduous (dropping their leaves in autumn), although some subtropical species are evergreen. The genus is widespread throughout much of Europe, Asia, and North America. The leaves are usually opposite, and mostly pinnately compound (divided into leaflets in a feather-like arrangement). The seeds, known as \"keys\", are botanically fruits of the type called samara. Some species are dioecious, having male and female flowers on separate plants. Ash wood is strong and elastic, and used for the handles of tools."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1667, "target_cluster_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both cypress and ash are types of trees, and in historical texts, they might be grouped together or compared due to their wood properties or common presence in certain landscapes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both cypress and ash are types of trees, and in historical texts, they might be grouped together or compared due to their wood properties or common presence in certain landscapes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Willow", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1059, "target_cluster_name": "Willow", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ash trees (genus Fraxinus) are deciduous trees that can be found in similar temperate regions and often share ecological niches with willows, though they belong to the Oleaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ash trees (genus Fraxinus) are deciduous trees that can be found in similar temperate regions and often share ecological niches with willows, though they belong to the Oleaceae family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 