dony variety"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ambar", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1646, "target_cluster_name": "Ambar", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Hiacinto Sardine cioè cor- ninole. Granati Smeraldi Zedoaria Doronici Scorze di ccderno Mace Seme di Bafsilico gariofilato Coralli rofsi Ambra gialla ^an an.31 ' an.3 lì. 1 l roppo» Gherofani { Gcn- 1", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for cor- ninole", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "woodpecker", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Woodpecker"], "contexts": ["bird that pecks wood"]}, {"entity_id": "woodpecker", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 5, "variants": ["Woodpecker", "woodpecker"], "contexts": ["bird with specialized beak and tongue", "example of adaptation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Woodpecker", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Woodpecker", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2510, "stable_key": "clu_e7d0d549afa4f330", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Woodpecker", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q11774312", "wikidata_description": "bird that climbs up in trees, probes for insects in tree bark and chisels nest holes in deadwood", "wikipedia_extract": "Woodpeckers are part of the bird family Picidae, which also includes the piculets, wrynecks and sapsuckers. Members of this family are found worldwide, except for Australia, New Guinea, New Zealand, Madagascar and the extreme polar regions. Most species live in forests or woodland habitats, although a few species are known that live in treeless areas, such as rocky hillsides and deserts, and the Gila woodpecker specializes in exploiting cacti. Members of this family are chiefly known for the characteristic behaviour that lent them their common name. Their pecking serves mostly to aid their forage for insect prey in the trunks and branches of trees, and also communication which they achieve by drumming trees with their beaks, producing a reverberatory sound that can be heard at some distance. Some species vary their diet with fruits, birds' eggs, small animals, tree sap, human scraps, and carrion."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Birds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 5, "target_cluster_name": "birds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Woodpeckers are a type of bird.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Woodpeckers are a type of bird."}]}, {"canonical_name": "formiguas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "formiguas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "formiguas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["formiguas", "formiga", "formigas"], "contexts": ["large ants that flew and produced lacre", "insect mistaken for the producer of lacca"]}, {"entity_id": "formigas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "formigas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["formigas", "formiga"], "contexts": ["ants that eat an egg", "insects used in a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "formiguas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "formigas", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2511, "stable_key": "clu_4e7dea688d47b5ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ants", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Insects, including flying ants and those used in preparations.", "note": "Contexts suggest common ants and possibly specific species known for producing lacre or used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ants are eusocial insects of the family Formicidae and, along with the related wasps and bees, belong to the order Hymenoptera. Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors in the Cretaceous period. More than 13,800 of an estimated total of 22,000 species have been classified. They are easily identified by their geniculate (elbowed) antennae and the distinctive node-like structure that forms their slender waists. Ants form colonies that range in size from a few dozen individuals often living in small natural cavities to highly organised colonies that may occupy large territories with a sizeable nest (or nests) that consist of millions of individuals, in some cases they reach hundreds of millions of individuals in super colonies. Typical colonies consist of various castes of sterile, wingless females, most of which are workers (ergates), as well as soldiers (dinergates) and other specialised groups."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "insects", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for 'ants', a type of insect.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Portuguese for 'ants', a type of insect.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ants and bees are both eusocial insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera, with ants evolving from wasp ancestors, closely related to bees.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ants and bees are both eusocial insects belonging to the order Hymenoptera, with ants evolving from wasp ancestors, closely related to bees."}, {"found_name": "Wasps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2271, "target_cluster_name": "Wasps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Duodeno", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "duodenum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "duodenum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["duodenum"], "contexts": ["upper part of the small intestine"]}, {"entity_id": "duodeno", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Duodeno", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Duodeno", "Duodenum", "duodeno"], "contexts": ["duodenum, part of the small intestine", "first part of the small intestine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "duodenum", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Duodeno", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2512, "stable_key": "clu_280acf3a5b00c1d7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Duodenum", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18556187", "wikidata_description": "Human disease"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bucho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3698, "target_cluster_name": "bucho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The duodenum is the first section of the small intestine, immediately following the stomach, and plays a crucial role in digestion initiated by the stomach.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The duodenum is the first section of the small intestine, immediately following the stomach, and plays a cruc