"description": "A medical condition, often referring to a stroke.", "wikipedia_extract": "Apoplexy (from Ancient Greek  ἀποπληξία (apoplexia) 'a striking away') refers to the rupture of an internal organ and the associated symptoms. Informally or metaphorically, the term apoplexy is associated with being furious, especially as \"apoplectic\". Historically, it described what is now known as a hemorrhagic stroke, typically involving a ruptured blood vessel in the brain; modern medicine typically specifies the anatomical location of the bleeding, such as cerebral apoplexy, ovarian apoplexy, or pituitary apoplexy. From the late 14th to the late 19th century, the diagnosis apoplexy referred to any sudden death that began with abrupt loss of consciousness, especially when the victim died within seconds after losing consciousness. The word apoplexy was sometimes used to refer to the symptom of sudden loss of consciousness immediately preceding death. Strokes, ruptured aortic aneurysms, and even heart attacks were referred to as apoplexy in the past, because before the advent of biomedical science, the ability to differentiate abnormal conditions and diseased states was limited."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Coma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2590, "target_cluster_name": "Coma", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coma is a common and severe symptom associated with apoplexy (stroke), representing a state of prolonged unconsciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coma is a common and severe symptom associated with apoplexy (stroke), representing a state of prolonged unconsciousness."}, {"found_name": "Unconsciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A key characteristic and consequence of apoplexy is the loss of consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A key characteristic and consequence of apoplexy is the loss of consciousness."}, {"found_name": "Lignam Sanctae Crucis", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "highly esteemed of the Vertues hereof that they have called it Lignam Sanctae Crucis, Wood of the holy Cross, beleeving it to help the Falling-sickness, Apoplexie, and Palsie,* 8.434 very speedily, no", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for the substance that helps with Falling-sickness, Apoplexie, and Palsie"}]}, {"canonical_name": "paper", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "paper", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "paper", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["paper", "Paper", "Papers"], "contexts": ["material for covering vessel", "used to bind mouth of container"]}, {"entity_id": "sheet_of_paper", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sheet of paper", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sheet of paper"], "contexts": ["physical object with whiteness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "paper", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sheet of paper", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 2517, "stable_key": "clu_e70dbe8afb85a53a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paper", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Livro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 283, "target_cluster_name": "Livro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paper is the