eople drinking herb-infused drink", "fennel good for them"]}, {"entity_id": "nurcino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Nurcino", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nurcino"], "contexts": ["lithotomist surgeon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Nurses", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Nurcino", "similarity": 0.85}], "id": 2518, "stable_key": "clu_1b16c6c3d48d6357", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nurses", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Individuals who care for the sick or infirm.", "note": "Includes both general nurses and potentially surgical practitioners like lithotomists.", "wikipedia_extract": "Nursing is a health care profession that \"integrates the art and science of caring and focuses on the protection, promotion, and optimization of health and human functioning; prevention of illness and injury; facilitation of healing; and alleviation of suffering through compassionate presence\". Nurses practice in many specialties with varying levels of certification and responsibility. Nurses comprise the largest component of most healthcare environments. There are shortages of qualified nurses in many countries. Nurses develop a plan of care, working collaboratively with physicians, therapists, patients, patients' families, and other team members that focuses on treating illness to improve quality of life. In the United Kingdom and the United States, clinical nurse specialists and nurse practitioners diagnose health problems and prescribe medications and other therapies, depending on regulations that vary by state."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Radish", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "radish", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Radish", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Radish"], "contexts": ["seed used as remedy", "root vegetable used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "radish", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "radish", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["radish"], "contexts": ["variety of plant discussed for cross-pollination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Radish", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "radish", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 2519, "stable_key": "clu_21f4b5c060809547", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Radish", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Raphanus sativus L.", "family": "Brassicaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q7224565", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Parsnip", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2356, "target_cluster_name": "Parsnip", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are root vegetables commonly cultivated and consumed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are root vegetables commonly cultivated and consumed."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Maidenhair", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "maidenhair", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Maidenhair", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Maidenhair", "Golden Maidenhair"], "contexts": ["herb used with Liquorice", "plant with black fibers and green leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "maidenhair", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Maidenhair", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Maidenhair"], "contexts": ["herb for coughs and jaundice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Maidenhair", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Maidenhair", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2520, "stable_key": "clu_3f3419a136bca0f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maidenhair Fern", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Adiantum spp.", "family": "Pteridaceae", "description": "A delicate fern with fan-shaped fronds and black stems.", "note": "Refers to plants in the genus Adiantum.", "wikipedia_extract": "Adiantum (), the maidenhair fern (not to be confused with the similar-looking maidenhair spleenwort fern), is a genus of about 250 species of ferns in the subfamily Vittarioideae of the family Pteridaceae, though some researchers place it in its own family, Adiantaceae. The genus name comes from Greek, meaning \"unwetted\", referring to the fronds' ability to shed water without becoming wet. They are distinctive in appearance, with dark, often black stipes and rachises, and bright green, often delicately cut leaf tissue. The sori are borne submarginally, and are covered by reflexed flaps of leaf tissue which resemble indusia. Dimorphism between sterile and fertile fronds is generally subtle. They generally prefer humus-rich, moist, well-drained sites, ranging from bottomland soils to vertical rock walls."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fern", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 994, "target_cluster_name": "Fern", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Maidenhair Fern is a specific type of Fern.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Maidenhair Fern is a specific type of Fern."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Smelling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "smelling", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Smelling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Smelling", "smell"], "contexts": ["sense of smell", "sense of odor"]}, {"entity_id": "smells", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Smells", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["smells", "Smells"], "contexts": ["olfactory perceptions", "sensible qualities of experience"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Smelling", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Smells", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 2521, "stable_key": "clu_428e4f48dcd827c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Smell", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Odor", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 452, "target_cluster_name": "odor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Odor is a direct synonym for Smell, referring to the sensation perceived by the olfactory organ.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Odor is a direct synonym for Smell, referring to the sensation perceived by the olfactory organ."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Major", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "lord_major", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lord Major", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lord Major", "Lord"], "contexts": ["audience for a sermon", "when the Lord shall come"]}, {"entity_id": "major", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Major", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Major"], "contexts": ["editor of a text", "author of India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lord Major", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Major", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 2522, "stable_key": "clu_f8fd0d7405d53eda", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lord Mayor", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Title of a chief magistrate, often an audience for texts."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Governor", "link