enting a different climatic and latitudinal band.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The temperate zone is geographically distinct from Equatorial America, representing a different climatic and latitudinal band."}]}, {"canonical_name": "word-deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "deafness", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["deafness"], "contexts": ["linked to blue eyes in cats"]}, {"entity_id": "word-deafness", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "word-deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["word-deafness"], "contexts": ["inability to understand spoken words", "cases studied by Allen Starr"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "deafness", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "word-deafness", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 2536, "stable_key": "clu_1417880785f873bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pure word deafness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Pure word deafness", "note": "The term 'deafness' is very broad, but 'word-deafness' specifically points to this condition.", "wikidata_id": "Q76005580", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 February 1952"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Rocky Mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rocky_mountains", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rocky Mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rocky Mountains"], "contexts": ["mountain range with erratic boulders"]}, {"entity_id": "rocky_mountains", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rocky Mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Rocky Mountains", "Rocky Mount."], "contexts": ["mountain range", "Rocky Mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "rocky-mountains", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rocky-Mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rocky-Mountains"], "contexts": ["mountain range"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rocky Mountains", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Rocky Mountains", "similarity": 0.817}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rocky Mountains", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Rocky-Mountains", "similarity": 0.945}], "id": 2537, "stable_key": "clu_d41abbb9da1b16f8", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "The Rocky Mountains, also known as the Rockies, are a major mountain range and the largest mountain system in North America. The Rocky Mountains stretch 3,000 mi (4,800 km) in a straight-line distance from the northernmost part of Western Canada, to New Mexico in the Southwestern United States. Depending on differing definitions between Canada and the U.S., its northern terminus is located either in northern British Columbia's Terminal Range south of the Liard River and east of the Trench, or in the northeastern foothills of the Brooks Range/British Mountains that face the Beaufort Sea coasts between the Canning River and the Firth River across the Alaska–Yukon border. Its southernmost point is near the Albuquerque metropolitan area, adjacent to the Rio Grande rift, and north of the Sandia–Manzano Mountain Range, also near Santa Fe, New Mexico. Being the easternmost portion of the North American Cordillera, the Rockies are distinct from the tectonically younger Cascade Range and Sierra Nevada, which both lie farther to its west. The Rockies formed 55 million to 80 million years ago during the Laramide orogeny, in which a number of plates began sliding underneath the North American plate."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "W. Thomson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "thomson", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Thomson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomson"], "contexts": ["author of Chemistry edition"]}, {"entity_id": "w__thomson", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "W. Thomson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["W. Thomson"], "contexts": ["proved experimentally about freezing water", "computed power from solar radiation"]}, {"entity_id": "thomson", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thomson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomson"], "contexts": ["mathematically investigated Faraday's discovery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Thomson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "W. Thomson", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Thomson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thomson", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2538, "stable_key": "clu_ad1de19610fb6e51", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Thomson", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Scientist who investigated water freezing and Faraday's work.", "note": "The initial 'W.' is crucial; assuming 'William' is the most common first name for a Thomson of that era in scientific circles.", "wikidata_id": "Q75961067", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=432095"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Grove", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1923, "target_cluster_name": "Grove", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were leading physicists of the 19th century, contributing significantly to the understanding of thermodynamics and electricity. Grove's 'correlation of forces' laid groundwork for Thomson's later work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were leading physicists of the 19th century, contributing significantly to the understanding of thermodynamics and electricity. Grove's 'correlation of forces' laid groundwork for Thomson's later work.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Joule", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2155, "target_cluster_name": "Joule", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Joule's work on the mechanical equivalent of heat was crucial for the development of thermodynamics, a field in which Thomson (later Lord Kelvin) was a leading figure. They collaborated and corresponded.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Joule's work on the mechanical equivalent of heat was crucial for the development of thermodynamics, a field in which Thomson (later Lord Kelvin) was a leading figure. They collaborated and corresponded.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gulf-stream", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "gulf-stream", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gulf-stream", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Gulf-stream", "GulJ-stream", "Gulf-siream"], "contexts": ["warm waters influencing climate", "ocean current with variable direction"]}, {"entity_id": "gulf-stream", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Gulf-stream", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gulf-stream"], "contexts": ["warm ocean current in the Atlantic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gulf-stream", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gulf-stream", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2539, "stable_key": "clu_cc078f1f8673d5e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gulf Stream", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34771011", "wikidata_description": "river in Somerset County, Maine, United States of America", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gulf Stream is a warm and swift Atlantic ocean current that originates in the Gulf of Mexico and flows through the Straits of Florida and up the eastern coastline of the United States, then veers east near 36°N latitude  (North Carolina) and moves toward Northwest Europe as the North Atlantic Current. The process of western intensification causes the Gulf Stream to be a northward-accelerating current off the east coast of North America. Around 40°0′N 30°0′W, it splits in two, with the northern stream, the North Atlantic Drift, crossing to Northern Europe and the southern stream, the Canary Current, recirculating off West Africa. The Gulf Stream influences the climate of the coastal areas of the East Coast of the United States from Florida to southeast Virginia (near 36°N latitude), and to a greater degree, the climate of Northwest Europe. A consensus exists that the climate of Northwest Europe is warmer than other areas of similar latitude at least partially because of the strong North Atlantic Current. It is part of the North Atlantic Gyre."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Atmospheric currents", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1184, "target_cluster_name": "atmospheric currents", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf Stream is a major oceanic current, and atmospheric currents are its counterpart in the atmosphere, both being large-scale movements of fluids that influence climate and weather.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf Stream is a major oceanic current, and atmospheric currents are its counterpart in the atmosphere, both being large-scale movements of fluids that influence climate and weather."}, {"found_name": "Circulation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2251, "target_cluster_name": "circulação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf Stream is a specific example of oceanic circulation, a broader concept describing the movement of water in the oceans.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf Stream is a specific example of oceanic circulation, a broader concept describing the movement of water in the oceans."}, {"found_name": "Climate", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 613, "target_cluster_name": "climate", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf Stream has a profound impact on the climate of the regions it flows past and influences global climate patterns.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf Stream has a profound impact on the climate of the regions it flows past and influences global climate patterns."}, {"found_name": "Climatology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2199, "target_cluster_name": "Climatologie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The study of the Gulf Stream is integral to the field of climatology, as it is a significant factor in climate formation and variation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The study of the Gulf Stream is integral to the field of climatology, as it is a significant factor in climate formation and variation."}, {"found_name": "Hydrography", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4132, "target_cluster_name": "Hydrographie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf Stream is a key feature studied within hydrography, the branch of applied science which deals with the measurement and description of the physical features of oceans, seas, coastal areas, rivers and other inland waters, and with the prediction of their associated physical processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf Stream is a key feature studied within hydrography, the branch of applied science which deals with the measurement and description of the physical features of oceans, seas, coastal areas, rivers and other inland waters, and with the prediction of their associated physical processes."}, {"found_name": "Marine fauna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3997, "target_cluster_name": "marine fauna", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gulf Stream's temperature and flow significantly influence the distribution and behavior of marine fauna.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gulf Stream's temperature and flow significantly influence the distribution and behavior of marine fauna."}]}, {"canonical_name": "montagnes primitives", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "montagnes_primitives", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "montagnes primitives", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["montagnes primitives", "montagnes calcaires"], "contexts": ["classification of mountains", "ancient mountain ranges"]}, {"entity_id": "primitive_mountains", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "primitive mountains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["primitive mountains"], "contexts": ["ancient mountain ranges"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "montagnes primitives", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "primitive mountains", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 2540, "stable_key": "clu_d31659c91bb6499d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Primitive mountains", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to ancient, possibly foundational, mountain formations in geological history.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Kunlun Mountains constitute one of the longest mountain chains in Asia, extending for more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 mi). In the broadest sense, the chain forms the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau south of the Tarim Basin. Located in Western China, the Kunlun Mountains have been known as the \"Forefather of Mountains\" in China. The exact definition of the Kunlun Mountains varies over time. Older sources used Kunlun to mean the mountain belt that runs across the center of China, that is, Altyn Tagh along with the Qilian and Qin Mountains. Recent sources have the Kunlun range forming most of the south side of the Tarim Basin and then continuing east, south of the Altyn Tagh.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunlun_Mountains"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "diallage métalloïde", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "diallage_métalloïde", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "diallage métalloïde", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["diallage métalloïde", "diallage", "diallage metalloïde"], "contexts": ["mineral found in serpentine", "metallic diallage"]}, {"entity_id": "diallage", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "diallage", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["diallage"], "contexts": ["mineral laminae"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "diallage métalloïde", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "diallage", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 2541, "stable_key": "clu_bd9fbc86c94e175b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Diallage", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "'Diallage métalloïde' refers to a specific variety of diallage.", "wikipedia_extract": "Diallage is an inosilicate, meaning it is a chain silicate, and is a part of the pyroxene group. Diallage is a junction between augite and diopside, just like fassaite. It was named in 1801 by René Just Haüy. Its name derives from the Greek word diallaghé (meaning change, transform, difference), as its composition differs from that of the other minerals in the pyroxene group. It is a fairly common mineral, and is cheap. Diallage is typically an opaque mineral, but translucent specimens can occur."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pyroxene", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2201, "target_cluster_name": "pyroxene", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diallage is part of the pyroxene group of minerals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diallage is part of the pyroxene group of minerals."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ramon_de_la_sagra", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Ramon de la Sagra", "Don Ramon DE LA SaGBA", "M. Ramon de la Sagra", "M. Ramun de la Sagra"], "contexts": ["author of Analts de Cttncias and memoir", "author cited for sugar cane information"]}, {"entity_id": "sagra,_ramon_de_la", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sagra, Ramon de la", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sagra, Ramon de la"], "contexts": ["observations on rain quantity in Havana"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sagra, Ramon de la", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 2542, "stable_key": "clu_c450723ff1b66d6c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ramón de la Sagra", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1798, "death_year": 1871, "description": "Cuban naturalist, economist, and writer.", "wikidata_id": "Q973094", "wikidata_description": "Spanish economist, sociologist, botanist, and political writer (1798–1871)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 42, "target_cluster_name": "Humboldt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ramón de la Sagra was a contemporary naturalist and explorer, similar to Alexander von Humboldt, and they likely interacted within the scientific community of the era.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ramón de la Sagra was a contemporary naturalist and explorer, similar to Alexander von Humboldt, and they likely interacted within the scientific community of the era."}, {"found_name": "Aimé Bonpland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1671, "target_cluster_name": "M. Bonpland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aimé Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked extensively in South America, a region also studied by Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and likely shared scientific interests and networks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aimé Bonpland was a naturalist and explorer who worked extensively in South America, a region also studied by Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and likely shared scientific interests and networks."}, {"found_name": "Alcide d'Orbigny", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3002, "target_cluster_name": "D'Orbigny", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alcide d'Orbigny was a naturalist and explorer who also conducted significant fieldwork in South America, a region of interest to Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Alcide d'Orbigny was a naturalist and explorer who also conducted significant fieldwork in South America, a region of interest to Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rio Apure", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rio_apure", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rio Apure", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rio Apure"], "contexts": ["river bordering the Llanos", "river in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "rio_apure", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rio Apure", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio Apure"], "contexts": ["river falling into the Orinoco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rio Apure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rio Apure", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2543, "stable_key": "clu_c0b8a1e3c2911fe2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apure River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q853315", "wikidata_description": "river in Venezuela"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amazone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1520, "target_cluster_name": "Amazone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apure River is a tributary of the Amazon River.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Apure River is a tributary of the Amazon River.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cassiquiare River", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1623, "target_cluster_name": "Cassiquiare", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Apure River is a major tributary of the Orinoco River, and the Cassiquiare River is a natural canal that connects the Orinoco River to the Rio Negro, which flows into the Amazon River. These rivers form a crucial part of the riverine network in northern South America, often discussed together in geographical and exploratory contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Apure River is a major tributary of the Orinoco River, and the Cassiquiare River is a natural canal that connects the Orinoco River to the Rio Negro, which flows into the Amazon River. These rivers form a crucial part of the riverine network in northern South America, often discussed together in geographical and exploratory contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Harz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "harz", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Harz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Harz"], "contexts": ["location of granite chains", "mountain range"]}, {"entity_id": "harz", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Harz", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Harz"], "contexts": ["mountain range with mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Harz", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Harz", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2544, "stable_key": "clu_33c600981f65c88b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Harz Mountains", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q4186", "wikidata_description": "low mountain range in northern Germany", "wikipedia_extract": "The Hexentanzplatz (literally “Witches′ Dance Floor”) in the Harz mountains is a plateau (454 m above sea level (NN)) that lies high above the Bode Gorge, opposite the Rosstrappe in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany. The Hexentanzplatz is reputedly an Old Saxon cult site, at which pagan celebrations were held in honour of the so-called Hagedisen (forest and mountain goddesses), particularly on the night of 1 May. The place did not become known as the Hexentanzplatz until the cult was banned by the invading Christian Franks. According to tradition the site was guarded by Frankish soldiers in order to enforce the ban and they were chased off by Saxons dressed as witches and riding on broomsticks. Another old Saxon cult site is found on the Brocken. Above the Hexentanzplatz are the remains of the Sachsenwall (“Saxon Dyke”), a wall of granite rocks and possibly part of a larger fortification, more than 1,500 years old.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harz"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Amérique Russe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "amérique_russe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Amérique Russe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Amérique Russe", "Amérique russe"], "contexts": ["Russian territories in America", "Russian territory in North America"]}, {"entity_id": "european_russia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "European Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["European Russia"], "contexts": ["region with elevated plateaux"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Amérique Russe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "European Russia", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 2545, "stable_key": "clu_8b95aca7a2d9ae65", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Russian America", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "note": "The text uses 'Amérique Russe' and 'European Russia', but the context of territories in America points to Russian America (Alaska).", "wikidata_id": "Q106306768", "wikidata_description": "1865-1867 expedition organized by the Smithsonian Institution to Canada, Russian-America (Alaska), and Asia"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bahia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bahia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bahia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bahia"], "contexts": ["port that received slaves", "city and region"]}, {"entity_id": "bahia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bahia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bahia"], "contexts": ["city in Brazil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bahia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bahia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2546, "stable_key": "clu_040efc22572f3dca", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bahia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q2236958", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_extract": "Bahia (English: , bə-(H)EE-ə; Brazilian Portuguese: [baˈi.ɐ] ) is one of the 26 states of Brazil, located in the Northeast Region of the country. It is the fourth-largest Brazilian state by population (after São Paulo, Minas Gerais, and Rio de Janeiro) and the 5th-largest by area. Bahia's capital is the city of Salvador (formerly known as \"Cidade do São Salvador da Bahia de Todos os Santos\", literally \"City of the Holy Savior of the Bay of All the Saints\"), on a spit of land separating the Bay of All Saints from the Atlantic. Once a stronghold of supporters of direct rule of Brazil by the Portuguese monarchy, and dominated by agricultural, slaving, and ranching interests, Bahia is now a predominantly working-class industrial and agricultural state. The state is home to 7% of the Brazilian population and produces 4.2% of the country's GDP. It is divided into 417 municipalities.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahia"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brasilia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2914, "target_cluster_name": "Brasilia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bahia is a state within Brazil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bahia is a state within Brazil.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Corpo Santo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1190, "target_cluster_name": "Corpo Santo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela. This suggests a geographical and cultural connection, as the telenovela likely draws inspiration from or is set within this Brazilian context.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rio Janeiro", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1580, "target_cluster_name": "Rio Janeiro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Rio de Janeiro is the capital of the state of Rio de Janeiro, indicating a regional relationship within Brazil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bahia is a state in Brazil, and Rio de Janeiro is the capital of the state of Rio de Janeiro, indicating a regional relationship within Brazil.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rio de la Madeira", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rio_de_la_madeira", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rio de la Madeira", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rio de la Madeira"], "contexts": ["river forming a boundary", "river mentioned in relation to portage"]}, {"entity_id": "rio_de_madeira", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rio de Madeira", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio de Madeira"], "contexts": ["river in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rio de la Madeira", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rio de Madeira", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 2547, "stable_key": "clu_1eeb729a643c1f8c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Madeira River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q118251", "wikidata_description": "river in Bolivia and Brazil"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amazon River", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1520, "target_cluster_name": "Amazone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Madeira River is a major tributary of the Amazon River.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Madeira River is a major tributary of the Amazon River."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Inca", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "inca", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Inca", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Inca", "Incas"], "contexts": ["language associated with a historical people", "ruler of an empire"]}, {"entity_id": "incas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "incas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["incas", "Incas"], "contexts": ["ancient rulers of South America", "rulers known for despotic acquisitions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Inca", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "incas", "similarity": 0.86}], "id": 2548, "stable_key": "clu_a5aae4fc5af57c5e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inca Empire", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Inca' historically referred to the rulers and the ruling class of the Inca Empire, and by extension, the empire itself. Given the context of exploration and natural history, it refers to the civilization rather than an individual.", "wikidata_id": "Q28573", "wikidata_description": "1438–1533 empire in South America"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Conquistadores", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1981, "target_cluster_name": "Conquistadores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conquistadors were responsible for the conquest of the Inca Empire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conquistadors were responsible for the conquest of the Inca Empire.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Guatire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "guatire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guatire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Guatire"], "contexts": ["location within Caracas", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "guate", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Guate", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guate"], "contexts": ["mountain", "name of a mountain range"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guatire", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Guate", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 2549, "stable_key": "clu_9b91d16b51a98d80", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guatire", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "The mention of 'Guate' in the context of Garcia de Orta's works from 1563, which focused on the East Indies, might suggest an East Asian locality. However, given the overwhelming association of 'Guatire' with Venezuela in Humboldt's works, and the possibility of similar sounding place names or transcription errors, the Venezuelan location is considered the primary referent, especially in a broader early modern geographical context. Further disambiguation would require deeper textual analysis.", "wikidata_id": "Q1026987", "wikidata_description": "city in Venezuela"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tobolsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "tobolsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tobolsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Tobolsk"], "contexts": ["city in Siberia", "location of magnetic force comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "tobolsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tobolsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tobolsk"], "contexts": ["city in Asiatic Continent", "city in Western Siberia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tobolsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tobolsk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2550, "stable_key": "clu_8304c3900aa83872", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tobolsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q168782", "wikidata_description": "town in Tyumen Oblast, Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobolsk", "wikipedia_extract": "Tobolsk (Russian: Тобо́льск, IPA: [tɐˈbolʲsk]) is a town in Tyumen Oblast, Russia, located at the confluence of the Tobol and Irtysh rivers. Founded in 1587, Tobolsk is the second-oldest Russian settlement east of the Ural Mountains in Asian Russia, and was the historic capital of the Siberia region. Population: 100,352 (2021 census); 99,694 (2010 census); 92,880 (2002 census); 94,143 (1989 Soviet census). The town was founded on the site of the Tatar town of Sibir-tura. In 1580, a group of Yermak Timofeyevich's Cossacks initiated the Russian conquest of Siberia, pushing eastwards on behalf of the Tsardom of Russia. After a year of Tatar attacks, Yermak prepared for the conquest of the Khanate of Sibir and a campaign to take the Khanate's capital city, Qashliq."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Irkutsk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2563, "target_cluster_name": "Irkutsk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Irkutsk was a major administrative and trade center in Siberia, historically linked to Tobolsk as part of the Russian expansion into the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Irkutsk was a major administrative and trade center in Siberia, historically linked to Tobolsk as part of the Russian expansion into the region."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cincinnati", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "cincinnati", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cincinnati", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Cincinnati"], "contexts": ["city near Petit Miami", "city where shell was discovered"]}, {"entity_id": "cincinnati", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cincinnati", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cincinnati"], "contexts": ["city in Ohio, USA", "city in the United States of America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cincinnati", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cincinnati", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2551, "stable_key": "clu_f47b86c4f2e14641", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cincinnati", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q43196", "wikidata_description": "city in Ohio, United States; county seat of Hamilton County", "wikipedia_extract": "Cincinnati, colloquially nicknamed Cincy, is the most populous city in Hamilton County, Ohio, United States, and its county seat. Settled in 1788, the city is located on the northern side of the confluence of the Licking and Ohio rivers, the latter of which marks the state line with Kentucky. The third-most populous city in Ohio with a population of 309,317 at the 2020 census, Cincinnati serves as the economic and cultural hub of the tri-state Cincinnati metropolitan area, Ohio's most populous metropolitan area and the nation's 30th-largest at over 2.3 million residents. Throughout much of the 19th century, Cincinnati was among the top 10 U.S. cities by population. The city developed as an inland port for cargo shipping by steamboats, located at the crossroads of the Northern and Southern United States, with fewer immigrants and less influence from Europe than East Coast cities in the same period. However, it received a significant number of German-speaking immigrants, who founded many of the city's cultural institutions."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "plaines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "plaines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "plaines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["plaines", "Plaines"], "contexts": ["flat areas of land", "flat, low-lying land"]}, {"entity_id": "plains", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "plains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["plains"], "contexts": ["large areas of flat land", "flat, open land areas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "plaines", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "plains", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 2552, "stable_key": "clu_644bc5d3ae26215b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "plains", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q160091", "wikidata_description": "flat geographic region"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tunguragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "tunguragua", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tunguragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Tunguragua", "Tonguragua"], "contexts": ["mountain in Ecuador", "volcano in Quito group"]}, {"entity_id": "tunguragua", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tunguragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Tunguragua"], "contexts": ["active volcano", "grand volcano near Penipe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tunguragua", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tunguragua", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2553, "stable_key": "clu_a18ae398932cc101", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tungurahua", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q21228016", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Antisana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1867, "target_cluster_name": "Antisana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "e