unctional units within an organism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organs are functional units within an organism."}, {"found_name": "Tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3073, "target_cluster_name": "tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tissues are collections of cells that form parts of an organism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Tissues are collections of cells that form parts of an organism."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Greek", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "greek", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Greek", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Greek"], "contexts": ["language and culture of ancient Greece", "ancient language and culture"]}, {"entity_id": "greca", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Greca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Greca"], "contexts": ["Greek pitch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Greek", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Greca", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 2570, "stable_key": "clu_91f198a6aa49c9bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Greek", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Relating to the language, culture, or pitch of ancient Greece.", "note": "Refers to the language and culture of ancient Greece, and Greek pitch.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ancient Greek (Ἑλληνική, Hellēnikḗ [hellɛːnikɛ́ː]) includes the forms of the Greek language used in ancient Greece and the ancient world from around 1500 BC to 300 BC. It is often roughly divided into the following periods: Mycenaean Greek (c. 1400 – c. 1200 BC), Dark Ages (c. 1200 – c. 800 BC), the Archaic or Homeric period (c. 800 – c. 500 BC), and the Classical period (c. 500 – c. 300 BC). Ancient Greek was the language of Homer and of fifth-century Athenian historians, playwrights, and philosophers. It has contributed many words to English vocabulary and has been a standard subject of study in educational institutions of the Western world since the Renaissance. This article primarily contains information about the Epic and Classical periods of the language, which are the best-attested periods and considered most typical of Ancient Greek. From the Hellenistic period (c. 300 BC), Ancient Greek was followed by Koine Greek, which is regarded as a separate historical stage, though its earliest form closely resembles Attic Greek, and its latest form approaches Medieval Greek, and Koine may be classified as Ancient Greek in a wider sense – being an ancient rather than medieval form of Greek, though over the centuries increasingly resembling Medieval and Modern Greek."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "past", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "past", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "past", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["past"], "contexts": ["temporal quality of events", "time that has gone by"]}, {"entity_id": "passado", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "passado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["passado"], "contexts": ["period of reputation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "past", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "passado", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 2571, "stable_key": "clu_25f18f3a305e61f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "past", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Age", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "targe