hat encompasses chemical analysis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical composition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 449, "target_cluster_name": "chemical composition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis is the process of identifying the chemical composition of a substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical analysis is the process of identifying the chemical composition of a substance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Analysis", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 724, "target_cluster_name": "Analysis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis is a specific scientific application of the general concept of analysis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical analysis is a specific scientific application of the general concept of analysis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical combination", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 781, "target_cluster_name": "chemical combination", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical combination is the process by which substances are formed, while chemical analysis is the process by which they are broken down or identified.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical combination is the process by which substances are formed, while chemical analysis is the process by which they are broken down or identified.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical properties", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1251, "target_cluster_name": "chemical properties", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis is a process used to determine the chemical properties of a substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical analysis is a process used to determine the chemical properties of a substance.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chemical decomposition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1288, "target_cluster_name": "chemical decomposition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemical analysis often involves inducing chemical decomposition to identify the constituent parts of a substance. Decomposition is a tool used in analysis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chemical analysis often involves inducing chemical decomposition to identify the constituent parts of a substance. Decomposition is a tool used in analysis.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "New Siberia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "new_siberia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Siberia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["New Siberia"], "contexts": ["island with wood-hills", "location of 'Wood Hills'"]}, {"entity_id": "siberia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Siberia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Siberia"], "contexts": ["adjacent continent to Ochotzk Sea", "region with chilly winds affecting Asia and America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New Siberia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Siberia", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 2605, "stable_key": "clu_6c43ab07c899f7ff-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Siberia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A vast geographical region within the Russian Empire.", "wikipedia_extract": "Siberia ( sy-BEER-ee-ə; Russian: Сибирь, romanized: Sibir', IPA: [sʲɪˈbʲirʲ] ), also known as Asian Russia, is an extensive geographical region comprising all of North Asia, from the Ural Mountains in the west (with the Ural River usually forming the southernmost portion of its western boundary) to the Pacific Ocean in the east. It has formed a part of the sovereign territory of Russia and its predecessor states since the lengthy conquest of Siberia, which began with the fall of the Khanate of Sibir in 1582 and concluded with the annexation of Chukotka in 1778. Siberia is vast and sparsely populated, covering an area of over 13.1 million square kilometres (5,100,000 mi2) – about three-quarters of Russia's total area, but home to roughly a quarter of Russia's population. Novosibirsk, Krasnoyarsk, and Omsk are the largest cities in the area. Because Siberia is a geographic and historic concept and not a political entity, there is no single precise definition of its territorial borders. It is further defined as stretching from the territories within the Arctic Circle in the north to the northern borders of Kazakhstan, Mongolia, and China in the south, although the hills of north-central Kazakhstan are also commonly included."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chinese Tartary", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3463, "target_cluster_name": "Chinese Tartary", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chinese Tartary was a historical term used in European geography to refer to a vast region of Central and East Asia, often overlapping with or including parts of Siberia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chinese Tartary was a historical term used in European geography to refer to a vast region of Central and East Asia, often overlapping with or including parts of Siberia."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bonn", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bonn"], "contexts": ["location of tree trunk find", "city in Germany"]}, {"entity_id": "bonn", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bonn"], "contexts": ["city where Professor Helmholtz was", "German city, location of university"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bonn", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bonn", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2606, "stable_key": "clu_0b9096ba5d56ff56", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bonn", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "note": "Directly refers to the city of Bonn in Germany.", "wikipedia_extract": "The University of Bonn, officially the Rhenish Friedrich Wilhelm University of Bonn (German: Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universität Bonn), is a public research university in Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany. It was founded in its present form as the Rhein-Universität (English: Rhine University) on 18 October 1818 by Frederick William III, as the linear successor of the Kurkölnische Akademie Bonn (English: Academy of the Prince-elector of Cologne) which was founded in 1777. The University of Bonn offers many undergraduate and graduate programs in a range of subjects and has 544 professors. The University of Bonn is a member of the German U15 association of major research-intensive universities in Germany and has the title of \"University of Excellence\" under the German Universities Excellence Initiative. Bonn has 8 Clusters of Excellence, the most of any German university; the Hausdorff Center for Mathematics, the Matter and Light for Quantum Computing cluster, Bonn Center for Dependency and Slavery Studies,  PhenoRob: Research for the Future of Crop Production, the Immune Sensory System cluster, ECONtribute: Markets and Public Policy, Color meets Flavor and Our Dynamic Universe. The University and State Library Bonn (ULB Bonn) is the central university and archive library of the Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universität Bonn and North Rhine-Westphalia; it holds more than five million volumes.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Bonn"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "molecular_attraction", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["molecular attraction"], "contexts": ["force between molecules"]}, {"entity_id": "molecular_attraction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["molecular attraction", "Molecular attraction"], "contexts": ["attractive force between molecules", "force within substances affected by temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "molecular attraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Molecular attraction", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 2607, "stable_key": "clu_b0a8c2fc6f41d0ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Molecular attraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q71931701", "wikidata_description": "journal article; published in Nature on 1916-6"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Attractive force", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1111, "target_cluster_name": "attractive force", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This term describes a type of force that is inherently related to the concept of attraction, including molecular attraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "foun