 "Faith", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 498, "target_cluster_name": "faith", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Faith is often based on or informed by Scripture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Faith is often based on or informed by Scripture."}, {"found_name": "Latina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1122, "target_cluster_name": "Latina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latin was the primary language of the Western Christian Church and thus the language of much religious scripture (e.g., the Vulgate Bible) studied in premodern times.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Latin was the primary language of the Western Christian Church and thus the language of much religious scripture (e.g., the Vulgate Bible) studied in premodern times.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "macrobius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Macrobius"], "contexts": ["Roman writer", "Roman writer and commentator"]}, {"entity_id": "macrobius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macrobius"], "contexts": ["Roman writer", "ancient Roman author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Macrobius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Macrobius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2671, "stable_key": "clu_79b3ada4fc4cf8f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Macrobius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman writer and commentator on Cicero and Virgil.", "wikidata_id": "Q313934", "wikidata_description": "ancient Roman author (5th century)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servius_the_Grammarian", "wikipedia_extract": "Servius, distinguished as Servius the Grammarian (Latin: Servius or Seruius Grammaticus), was a late fourth-century and early fifth-century grammarian. He earned a contemporary reputation as the most learned man of his generation in Italy. He authored a set of commentaries on the works of Virgil; these works, In Tria Virgilii Opera Expositio (\"Exposition on Three Works of Virgil\"), Commentarii in Virgilium (\"Commentaries on Virgil\"), Commentarii in Vergilii Opera (\"Commentaries on the Works of Vergil\"), or Vergilii Carmina Commentarii (\"Commentaries on the Poems of Virgil\"), constituted the first incunable to be printed at Florence, by Bernardo Cennini, in 1471. In the Saturnalia of Macrobius, Servius appears as one of the interlocutors; allusions in that work and a letter from Symmachus to Servius indicate that he was not a convert to Christianity. The name Servius also appears as Seruius owing to the unity of the Latin letters V and U from antiquity until as late as the 18th century. Many medieval manuscripts of Servius's commentaries give him the praenomen Marius or Maurus and the cognomen Honoratus."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Servius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1399, "target_cluster_name": "Servius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Servius is described as a grammarian who authored commentaries on Virgil, a classical author whose works Macrobius also commented on. This suggests a shared intellectual tradition and area of study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Servius is described as a grammarian who authored commentaries on Virgil, a classical author whose works Macrobius also commented on. This suggests a shared intellectual tradition and area of study."}]}, {"canonic