PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Moly"], "contexts": ["magical herb from Homer's Odyssey", "magical herb mentioned by Homer"]}, {"entity_id": "moly", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Moly", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moly", "molo"], "contexts": ["identified as a type of wild rue", "herb used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moly", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Moly", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2675, "stable_key": "clu_9c108f8a0e251760", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moly", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "The exact botanical identification of Moly is uncertain, though it's often associated with *Peganum harmala* or certain *Ruta* species.", "wikipedia_extract": "Allium moly, also known as yellow garlic, golden garlic and lily leek, Is a species of flowering plant in the genus Allium, which also includes the flowering and culinary onions and garlic. A bulbous herbaceous perennial from the Mediterranean. It is edible and used as a medicinal and ornamental plant. Allium moly is a bulb type flower, with the size of the bulb affecting the flowering period and the time of the flowering. With lance-shaped grey-green leaves up to 30 cm long, in early summer it produces masses of star-shaped bright yellow flowers in dense umbels. The cultivar 'Jeannine' has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allium_moly"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Garlic", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1734, "target_cluster_name": "Alhos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Moly is described as 'yellow garlic' and belongs to the genus Allium, which includes garlic.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Moly is described as 'yellow garlic' and belongs to the genus Allium, which includes garlic."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "vero ftr uggendole, & colandole, come de* grafsi se detto . DELL^H ARMEL, L'H A R M E L e vna fpezie di ruta faluatics-, Jerta da a!cu- ni (fecondo Diofcoride) Moly, Befaf;ì,& Harmala,)a qua- le da vn", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Harmel", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sweet Flag", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 620, "target_cluster_name": "calamo aromático", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Moly and Sweet Flag are described as aromatic plants, suggesting a potential use in perfumery or medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Moly and Sweet Flag are described as aromatic plants, suggesting a potential use in perfumery or medicine."}]}, {"canonical_name": "mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "mule", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mule"], "contexts": ["Animal whose skin is used in a recipe", "animal with a long lifespan"]}, {"entity_id": "mule", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["mule"], "contexts": ["hybrid of ass and horse", "example of a healthy and long-lived hybrid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mule", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "mule", "similarity": 0.794}], "id": 2676, "stable_key": "clu_a1293101b7e3cafa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mule", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "wikipedia_extract": "The mule is a domestic equine hybrid between a donkey and a horse. It is the offspring of a male donkey (a jack) and a female horse (a mare). The horse and the donkey are different species, with different numbers of chromosomes; of the two possible first-generation hybrids between them, the mule is easier to obtain and more common than the hinny, which is the offspring of a male horse (a stallion) and a female donkey (a jenny). Mules vary widely in size, and may be of any color seen in horses or donkeys. They are more patient, hardier and longer-lived than horses, and are perceived as less obstinate and more intelligent than donkeys. A female mule is called a molly, a Molly mule, or more formally mare mule."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "ass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1968, "target_cluster_name": "ass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mules are the offspring of a male donkey and a female horse, making them a direct product of donkey reproduction and a closely related entity in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mules are the offspring of a male donkey and a female horse, making them a direct product of donkey reproduction and a closely related entity in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "dysenterias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dysenteries", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "dysenteries", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["dysenteries"], "contexts": ["disease treated with Crystal", "intestinal inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "dysenterias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dysenterias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 3, "variants": ["dysenterias", "Dysenterias", "dysenteriam"], "contexts": ["bloody flux", "dysentery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "dysenteries", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dysenterias", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 2677, "stable_key": "clu_35c56e8df4fff90e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dysentery", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An intestinal inflammation causing severe diarrhea with blood and mucus.", "wikipedia_extract": "Dysentery (UK: , US: ), historically known as the bloody flux, is a type of gastroenteritis that results in bloody diarrhea. Other symptoms may include fever, abdominal pain, and a feeling of incomplete defecation. Complications may include dehydration. The cause of dysentery is usually the bacteria from genus Shigella, in which case it is known as shigellosis, or the amoeba Entamoeba histolytica; then it is called amoebiasis. Other causes may include certain chemicals, other bacteria, other protozoa, or parasitic worms. It may spread between people."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "diſinterías", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4446, "target_cluster_name": "diſinterías", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the historical Latinized spelling of dysentery, appearing in premodern texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the historical Latinized spelling of dysentery, appearing in premodern texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fluxos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 193, "target_cluster_name": "fluxos", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Morphew,* 8.404 and al other discolourings, being bathed therewith in the Sun. You may remember that not long since there was a raging Diseas called the Bloody Flux, the Colledg of Physitians not know", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for Bloody Flux", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hematites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hæmatites", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hæmatites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hæmatites"], "contexts": ["iron ore, adheres to lips", "iron ore with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "hematites", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hematites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hematites"], "contexts": ["stone used in a remedy", "hematite, a mineral used in a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hæmatites", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hematites", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 2678, "stable_key": "clu_78023f787c1a4ead", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hematite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "description": "A common iron oxide mineral, used in remedies and as a pigment.", "note": "While 'pegmatite' is a rock type, 'hematites' clearly points to the mineral hematite.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hematite (), also spelled as haematite, is a common iron oxide compound with the formula Fe2O3 and is widely found in rocks and soils. Hematite crystals belong to the rhombohedral lattice system which is designated the alpha polymorph of Fe2O3. It has the same crystal structure as corundum (Al2O3) and ilmenite (FeTiO3). With this crystal structure geometry it forms a complete solid solution at temperatures above 950 °C (1,740 °F). Hematite occurs naturally in black to steel or silver-gray, brown to reddish-brown, or red colors. It is mined as an important ore mineral of iron."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Iron", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 251, "target_cluster_name": "Iron", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hematite is a primary ore of iron.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hematite is a primary ore of iron.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "metal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 87, "target_cluster_name": "metal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hematite is an ore of iron, a metal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hematite is an ore of iron, a metal.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "flies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Flies"], "contexts": ["insects found in amber", "insects that maggots turn into"]}, {"entity_id": "flies", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["flies"], "contexts": ["insects attacking cattle", "insects destroying horses"]}, {"entity_id": "fly", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Fly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fly"], "contexts": ["common winged insect"]}, {"entity_id": "fly", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["fly"], "contexts": ["insect that deposits hundreds of eggs", "insect that lays eggs in animal navels"]}, {"entity_id": "fly", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fly"], "contexts": ["insect studied by entomologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Flies", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "flies", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 2679, "stable_key": "clu_ff27351d5cb1a6cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fly", "confidence": "low", "type": "animal", "note": "The context 'creatures that produce pearls' is highly unusual for flies and suggests a possible misidentification or specific folklore. The 'moUuscos' is likely an OCR error for 'mosquitos' or similar insect, but given the context of pearls, it's highly ambiguous.", "wikidata_id": "Q166111", "wikidata_description": "species of insect in the class \"Muscidae\", sort of fly", "wikipedia_extract": "Flies are insects of the order Diptera, the name being derived from Ancient Greek  δι- (di-) 'two' and  πτερόν (pteron) 'wing'. Insects of this order use only a single pair of wings to fly, the hindwings having evolved into advanced mechanosensory organs known as halteres, which act as high-speed sensors of rotational movement and allow dipterans to perform advanced aerobatics. Diptera is a large order containing more than 150,000 species including horse-flies, crane flies, hoverflies, mosquitoes and others. Flies have a mobile head, with a pair of large compound eyes, and mouthparts designed for piercing and sucking (mosquitoes, black flies and robber flies), or for lapping and sucking in the other groups. Their wing arrangement gives them great manoeuvrability in flight, and claws and pads on their feet enable them to cling to smooth surfaces. Flies undergo complete metamorphosis; the eggs are often laid on the larval food-source and the larvae, which lack true limbs, develop in a protected environment, often inside their food source.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Housefly"}, "member_count": 5, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Insects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Flies are a specific order within the broader class of insects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Flies are a specific order within the broader class of insects."}, {"found_name": "Imago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4025, "target_cluster_name": "imago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Imago refers to the adult, sexually mature stage of an insect, which in the case of flies is the winged form.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Imago refers to the adult, sexually mature stage of an insect, which in the case of flies is the winged form."}, {"found_name": "Worms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3261, "target_cluster_name": "worms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While distinct, some larval forms of flies (maggots) were historically sometimes referred to or grouped with 'worms' due to their appearance and habitat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While distinct, some larval forms of flies (maggots) were historically sometimes referred to or grouped with 'worms' due to their appearance and habitat."}]}, {"canonical_name": "sal Armoniaco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sal_armoniac", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sal Armoniac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sal Armoniac"], "contexts": ["ammonium chloride, added to gunpowder", "chemical substance, debated reaction with Tartar"]}, {"entity_id": "sal_armoniaco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sal Armoniaco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["sal Armoniaco"], "contexts": ["ingred