ionship": "reverse: In Christian theology, the Devil is a primary antagonist of Jesus Christ.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Deos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 555, "target_cluster_name": "Deos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Devil is frequently depicted as an antagonist or fallen angel in relation to God in historical religious and philosophical discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Devil is frequently depicted as an antagonist or fallen angel in relation to God in historical religious and philosophical discourse.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "demon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1104, "target_cluster_name": "demon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Devil is often considered the chief of demons in Christian theology, and the terms are frequently used interchangeably or in close association in historical texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Devil is often considered the chief of demons in Christian theology, and the terms are frequently used interchangeably or in close association in historical texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Satan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2255, "target_cluster_name": "Satan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Satan is a primary figure in Christian theology, often identified with the Devil, the adversary of God and humanity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Satan is a primary figure in Christian theology, often identified with the Devil, the adversary of God and humanity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophy of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "natural_philosophy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Natural Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Natural Philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of the natural world"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophy_of_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philosophy of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Philosophy of Nature", "philosophy of Nature", "philosophy of nature", "Philosophy of nature"], "contexts": ["discipline studying the natural world", "study of the natural world's principles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Natural Philosophy", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philosophy of nature", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 2682, "stable_key": "clu_569936ac8b34be60", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Natural philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astronomy was a core component of natural philosophy, dealing with the study of celestial bodies and phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Astronomy was a core component of natural philosophy, dealing with the study of celestial bodies and phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Physics", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 110, "target_cluster_name": "physics", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Physics, as the study of matter, energy, space, and time, is a direct descendant and a significant part of what was historically encompassed by natural philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Physics, as the study of matter, energy, space, and time, is a direct descendant and a significant part of what was historically encompassed by natural philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Chemistry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 304, "target_cluster_name": "Chemistry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chemistry, particularly in its early forms, was considered a branch of natural philosophy, investigating the composition and properties of substances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Chemistry, particularly in its early forms, was considered a branch of natural philosophy, investigating the composition and properties of substances."}, {"found_name": "Geology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 278, "target_cluster_name": "Geology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geology, the study of the Earth's physical structure and substance, its history, and the processes which act on it, was a part of natural philosophy's broader inquiry into the natural world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geology, the study of the Earth's physical structure and substance, its history, and the processes which act on it, was a part of natural philosophy's broader inquiry into the natural world."}, {"found_name": "Botany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1098, "target_cluster_name": "botany", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Botany, the scientific study of plants, was a significant area within natural philosophy, contributing to the understanding of the natural world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Botany, the scientific study of plants, was a significant area within natural philosophy, contributing to the understanding of the natural world."}, {"found_name": "Anatomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1167, "target_cluster_name": "anatomias", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anatomy, the study of the structure of living things, was a key discipline within natural philosophy, particularly in understanding the human body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Anatomy, the study of the structure of living things, was a key discipline within natural philosophy, particularly in understanding the human body."}, {"found_name": "Logic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 991, "target_cluster_name": "logic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Logic provided the foundational methods of reasoning and argumentation that natural philosophers used to construct their theories about nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Logic provided the foundational methods of reasoning and argumentation that natural philosophers used to construct their theories about nature."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Montano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "montanus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Montanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Montanus"], "contexts": ["both Son and Father"]}, {"entity_id": "montano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Montano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Montano", "Montanus"], "contexts": ["early modern author", "ancient medical authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Montanus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Montano", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 2683, "stable_key": "clu_82b4d44ffad83308", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Benito Montano", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Spanish physician and writer on medicine", "note": "Refers to Benito Montano, author of 'Polyanthea medicinal'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Brazavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "brasavolus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Brasavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Brasavolus"], "contexts": ["ancient authority, affirmed crystal is ice"]}, {"entity_id": "brazavolus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Brazavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Brazavolus", "Brazavol.", "Brazavola", "Braſavolus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority cited", "cited author of medical text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Brasavolus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Brazavolus", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2684, "stable_key": "clu_60633d0149b1b07e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Matthiolus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Italian physician and botanist, influential in medicine and botany.", "note": "The original canonical name 'Brazavolus' appears to be a misspelling or variant of Italian physician Andrea Matthioli (Lodovico Brazzavola was a contemporary physician, but Matthiolus is more likely given the context of classical medical authority).", "wikipedia_extract": "Pietro Andrea Gregorio Mattioli (Italian: [ˈpjɛːtro anˈdrɛːa ɡreˈɡɔːrjo matˈtjɔːli]; 12 March 1501– c. 1578) was a doctor and naturalist born in Siena. His most important work was a commentary on the medicinal plants of Pedanius Dioscorides first published in 1544 which was translated into several languages and went into thirteen editions in his lifetime. Mattioli was born in Siena to physician Francesco and his wife Lucrezia Buoninsegna. Although belonging to the city elite, the family was economically stressed, with thirteen children. His early studies are unknown but he may have studied in Venice, Siena, and Padua before he received his MD at the University of Padua in 1523, and subsequently practiced the profession in Siena, Rome, Trento and Gorizia, becoming personal physician of Ferdinand II, Archduke of Further Austria in Prague and Ambras Castle, and of Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor in Vienna. In 1527 he moved to Trent in the Val di Non."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Capivatius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1906, "target_cluster_name": "Capivatius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent physicians and botanists of the 16th century, known for their contributions to medical and natural history texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent physicians and botanists of the 16th century, known for their contributions to medical and natural history texts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dodon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2273, "target_cluster_name": "Dodon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mattioli was a prominent physician and botanist whose commentaries on Dioscorides were widely influential, and he was a contemporary and peer of Dodoens in the field of botanical studies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mattioli was a prominent physician and botanist whose commentaries on Dioscorides were widely influential, and he was a contemporary and peer of Dodoens in the field of botanical studies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mattioli wrote a significant commentary on Dioscorides' work on medicinal plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mattioli wrote a significant commentary on Dioscorides' work on medicinal plants."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ironsand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ironsand", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ironsand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ironsand", "Iron-sand", "iron sand"], "contexts": ["mineral substance", "iron-bearing sand"]}, {"entity_id": "iron-stone", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Iron-stone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Iron-stone", "iron-stone"], "contexts": ["mineral occurring in ordinary form", "mineral presenting a phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ironsand", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Iron-stone", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 2685, "stable_key": "clu_ff5a6be8051cdffb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Iron sand", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Iron", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 251, "target_cluster_name": "Iron", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iron sand is a naturally occurring form of iron.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Iron sand is a naturally occurring form of iron.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sand", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 14, "target_cluster_name": "sand", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Iron sand is a specific type of sand, characterized by its high iron content.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Iron sand is a specific type of sand, characterized by its high iron content."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ennius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ennius"], "contexts": ["contemporary to Plautus"]}, {"entity_id": "ennius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ennius"], "contexts": ["early Roman poet", "introduced 'mundus' to Latium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ennius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ennius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2686, "stable_key": "clu_29c989e50d0e70b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quintus Ennius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -239, "death_year": -169, "description": "Influential early Roman poet"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Homer", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2160, "target_cluster_name": "Homer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quintus Ennius is often referred to as the 'Father of Roman poetry' and was heavily influenced by Homer, adapting his epic style to Roman themes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quintus Ennius is often referred to as the 'Father of Roman poetry' and was heavily influenced by Homer, adapting his epic style to Roman themes."}, {"found_name": "Cicero", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 308, "target_cluster_name": "Cicero", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet and the national poet of Rome, whose work heavily influenced later Roman writers like Cicero.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet and the national poet of Rome, whose work heavily influenced later Roman writers like Cicero."}, {"found_name": "Virgil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 427, "target_cluster_name": "Virgil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet, and Virgil's Aeneid is considered the national epic of Rome, building upon Ennius's tradition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quintus Ennius was a Roman epic poet, and Virgil's Aeneid is considered the national epic of Rome, building upon Ennius's tradition."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Martialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "martial", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Martial", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martial"], "contexts": ["classical author of epigrams"]}, {"entity_id": "martialis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Martialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Martialis", "Marcial", "Martial"], "contexts": ["cited for medical text", "cited for dulcifying properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Martial", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Martialis", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 2687, "stable_key": "clu_2966e019372b563f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Martial", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 40, "death_year": 104, "description": "Roman poet and epigrammatist, sometimes cited for medical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Juvenal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2902, "target_cluster_name": "Juvenal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Juvenal and Martial were both Roman poets known for their epigrams and satirical observations of Roman society.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Juvenal and Martial were both Roman poets known for their epigrams and satirical observations of Roman society.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nicolaus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nicolaus"], "contexts": ["associated with a composition for kidney stones"]}, {"entity_id": "nicolaus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 4, "variants": ["Nicolaus", "NicolausTreviſanus"], "contexts": ["cited author in the margin", "author of medical text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nicolaus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Nicolaus", "similarity": 0.753}], "id": 2688, "stable_key": "clu_4f788c69b03acff9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nicolaus Salernitanus", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Medieval physician, associated with kidney stone remedies.", "note": "Likely refers to Nicolaus Salernitanus, a prominent physician of the School of Salerno, known for his treatise on urine, and also for works on polypharmacy."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1620, "target_cluster_name": "Amatus Lusitanus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant physicians of the Renaissance period, known for their contributions to medical practice and literature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant physicians of the Renaissance period, known for their contributions to medical practice and literature."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a highly influential ancient Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a cornerstone of herbal medicine for centuries, and would have been a key text for physicians like Nicolaus Salernitanus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was a highly influential ancient Greek physician and pharmacologist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a cornerstone of herbal medicine for centuries, and would have been a key text for physicians like Nicolaus Salernitanus."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen was the most influential physician of antiquity, and his medical theories and practices formed the basis of medical education and practice throughout the Middle Ages and Renaissance, including at the School of Salerno.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen was the most influential physician of antiquity, and his medical theories and practices formed the basis of medical education and practice throughout the Middle Ages and Renaissance, including at the School of Salerno."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine', and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with the Hippocratic Corpus, were fundamental to the medical tradition that Nicolaus Salernitanus would have been part of.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine', and his ethical and diagnostic principles, along with the Hippocratic Corpus, were fundamental to the medical tradition that Nicolaus Salernitanus would have been part of."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lacteas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lacteal_vessels", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lacteal Vessels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lacteal Vessels"], "contexts": ["vessels where gold exits alimentary tract"]}, {"entity_id": "lacteas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lacteas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Lacteas", "lacteas"], "contexts": ["lymphatic vessels", "anatomical structures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lacteal Vessels", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lacteas", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 2689, "stable_key": "clu_794d79701aad0570", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lacteals", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Milk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 637, "target_cluster_name": "Milk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Lacteals are vessels that absorb chyle (a milky fluid) from the intestines during digestion. Historically, the milky appearance of this fluid led to the naming of the lacteals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Lacteals are vessels that absorb chyle (a milky fluid) from the intestines during digestion. Historically, the milky appearance of this fluid led to the naming of the lacteals."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "batavia", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Batavia"], "contexts": ["city from which Dutch Embassadors were sent"]}, {"entity_id": "batavia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Batavia"], "contexts": ["Batavia", "city with observed average temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Batavia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Batavia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2690, "stable_key": "clu_0635958a1f94713a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jakarta", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia", "note": "Batavia was the name of the city (now Jakarta) that served as the capital of the Dutch East Indies. The context of Dutch ambassadors being sent from there confirms this identification.", "wikipedia_extract": "Jakarta, officially the Special Capital Region of Jakarta,  is the de facto capital and largest city of Indonesia and an autonomous region with a status equivalent to that of a province. It is located on the northwest coast of Java, the world's most populous island, bordered by the province of West Java to the south and east and Banten to the west. Its coastline faces the Java Sea to the north, and it shares a maritime border with Lampung to the west. Jakarta's metropolitan area has an estimated population of 41.9 million as of 2025, making it the most populated city in the world and therefore of Southeast Asia and largest urban area in Indonesia. Although Jakarta extends over only 661.23 square kilometres (255.30 square miles) and thus has the smallest area of any Indonesian province, its metropolitan area covers 7,076.31 square kilometres (2,732.18 square miles). Jakarta is the economic, cultural, and political centre of Indonesia, and the ASEAN's second largest economy after Singapore.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakarta"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Java", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 78, "target_cluster_name": "Java", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jakarta is located on the northwest coast of Java.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Jakarta is located on the northwest coast of Java."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "orchis_anthropomorphus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Orchis Anthropomorphus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orchis Anthropomorphus"], "contexts": ["plant with human-like appearance"]}, {"entity_id": "man-orchis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Man-Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Man-Orchis"], "contexts": ["plant with flower representing a man"]}, {"entity_id": "orchis", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["orchis", "Orchis"], "contexts": ["genus with pollen-grain contrivance", "genus of flowering plants"]}, {"entity_id": "orchidaceous_plants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "orchidaceous plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orchidaceous plants"], "contexts": ["plants requiring moth visits for fertilization"]}, {"entity_id": "orchidean", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Orchidean", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orchidean"], "contexts": ["relating to orchids"]}, {"entity_id": "orchidées", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "orchidées", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orchidées"], "contexts": ["flowers found on tree trunks"]}, {"entity_id": "orchideae", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Orchideae", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orchideae"], "contexts": ["orchid family of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Orchis Anthropomorphus", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Orchis", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Man-Orchis", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Orchis", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 2691, "stable_key": "clu_fdbedc0c6f5436f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "orchids", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Orchidaceae", "family": "Orchidaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q25308", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchid", "wikipedia_extract": "Orchids are plants that belong to the family Orchidaceae (), a diverse and widespread group of flowering plants with blooms that are often colourful and fragrant. Orchids are cosmopolitan plants, living in diverse habitats on every continent except Antarctica. The world's richest diversity of orchid genera and species is in the tropics. Many species are epiphytes, living on trees. The flowers and their pollination mechanisms are highly specialized, attracting insect pollinators by colour, pattern, scent, pheromones, and sometimes by mimicking female insects. Orchids have very small seeds, relying on fungal partners for germination."}, "member_count": 7, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Catasetum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4020, "target_cluster_name": "Catasetum", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Catasetum is a genus within the orchid family (Orchidaceae), making it a specific type of orchid.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Catasetum is a genus within the orchid family (Orchidaceae), making it a specific type of orchid."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nutmeg", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nutmeg"], "contexts": ["kernel of a fruit from a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "nutmeg", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Nutmeg"], "contexts": ["spice added to decoctions", "added to poultice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nutmeg", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Nutmeg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2692, "stable_key": "clu_48a45dfb1a5e12fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nutmeg", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myristica fragrans", "family": "Myristicaceae", "description": "A spice obtained from the seed of a tree, used in medicine and cooking.", "note": "'noz noſcada' directly translates to nutmeg, while 'noz' in the other context likely refers to the same commodity.", "wikipedia_extract": "Myristica fragrans, commonly known as the nutmeg tree, is an evergreen species indigenous to the Maluku Islands of Indonesia. This aromatic tree is economically significant as the primary source of two distinct spices: nutmeg, derived from its seed, and mace, obtained from the seed's aril. Valued for centuries in global spice trade, M. fragrans is now widely cultivated throughout tropical regions, including parts of Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Malaysia), South Asia (Kerala in India, Sri Lanka), East Asia (Guangdong and Yunnan in China, Taiwan), the Caribbean (notably Grenada), and South America. Myristica fragrans is an evergreen tree, usually 5–15 m (16–49 ft) tall, but occasionally reaching 20 m (66 ft) or even 30 m (98 ft) on Tidore. The alternately arranged leaves are dark green, 5–15 cm (2.0–5.9 in) long by 2–7 cm (0.8–2.8 in) wide with petioles about 1 cm (0.4 in) long. The species is dioecious, i.e. \"male\" or staminate flowers and \"female\" or carpellate flowers are borne on different plants, although occasional individuals produce both kinds of flower."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 734, "target_cluster_name": "mace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mace is the aril of the nutmeg seed, making it a distinct spice derived from the same plant (Myristica fragrans).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mace is the aril of the nutmeg seed, making it a distinct spice derived from the same plant (Myristica fragrans).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ginger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2918, "target_cluster_name": "Ginger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nutmeg and mace are derived from the same fruit (Myristica fragrans), and ginger is also a spice derived from a rhizome. These are all common spices often found together in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nutmeg and mace are derived from the same fruit (Myristica fragrans), and ginger is also a spice derived from a rhizome. These are all common spices often found together in historical pharmacopoeias and culinary texts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ivy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ivy"], "contexts": ["plant that blossoms twice a year"]}, {"entity_id": "ivy", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ivy"], "contexts": ["plant growing in woods and on walls", "plant used in a cure for wine surfeit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ivy", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Ivy", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2693, "stable_key": "clu_7adfd432fcc142ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ivy", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Hedera helix", "family": "Araliaceae", "note": "The description 'plant that blossoms twice a year' is not typical for common ivy (Hedera helix), which usually flowers once. This might refer to a specific variety, a misobservation, or a different plant with a similar common name.", "wikidata_id": "Q26354", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedera%20helix", "wikipedia_extract": "Hedera helix, simply ivy, or common ivy, English ivy, European ivy, King's choice ivy, is a species of flowering plant in the family Araliaceae. It is native to most of Europe and parts of western Asia. Ivy is a clinging evergreen vine that grows on tree trunks, walls, and fences in gardens, waste spaces, and wild habitats. Ivy is popular as an ornamental plant, but escaped plants have become naturalised outside its native range. Ivy has considerable cultural significance and symbolism. Synonyms include Hedera acuta, Hedera arborea ('tree ivy'), Hedera baccifera, and Hedera grandifolia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Scammony", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2917, "target_cluster_name": "Scammony", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Scammony and Ivy are climbing plants, and their growth habits and potential medicinal uses might lead to them being discussed in similar contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Scammony and Ivy are climbing plants, and their growth habits and potential medicinal uses might lead to them being discussed in similar contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Vine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 455, "target_cluster_name": "Vine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ivy is a type of vine, and 'Vine' is listed as a candidate. The description of Ivy explicitly states it is a 'clinging evergreen vine'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ivy is a type of vine, and 'Vine' is listed as a candidate. The description of Ivy explicitly states it is a 'clinging evergreen vine'."}, {"found_name": "Tree", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 24, "target_cluster_name": "Tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ivy is described as growing on tree trunks, indicating a close association with trees in its natural habitat and growth pattern.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ivy is described as growing on tree trunks, indicating a close association with trees in its natural habitat and growth pattern."}, {"found_name": "herbaceous plant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1217, "target_cluster_name": "herbaceous Plant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While Ivy is a woody vine, it is a plant and 'herbaceous plant' is a broad category that could encompass it in some contexts, though less specific than 'Vine'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "While Ivy is a woody vine, it is a plant and 'herbaceous plant' is a broad category that could encompass it in some contexts, though less specific than 'Vine'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "gnats", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gnats"], "contexts": ["insects indicating common corruption"]}, {"entity_id": "gnats", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Gnats"], "contexts": ["creatures driven away by fern smoke", "insects repelled by smoke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gnats", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Gnats", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 2694, "stable_key": "clu_cd3f87bda268687e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gnat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Culicidae", "description": "Small, biting flying insects, often found near water.", "note": "The singular 'Gnat' is the correct modern identification.", "wikipedia_extract": "A gnat ()  (also knat) is any of many species of tiny flying insects in the dipterid suborder Nematocera, especially those in the families Mycetophilidae, Anisopodidae and Sciaridae. Most often they fly in large numbers, called clouds. \"Gnat\" is a loose descriptive category rather than a phylogenetic or other technical term, so there is no scientific consensus on what constitutes a gnat. Some entomologists consider only non-biting flies to be gnats. Certain universities and institutes also distinguish eye gnats: the Smithsonian Institution describes them as \"non-biting flies, no bigger than a few grains of salt, ... attracted to fluids secreted by your eyes\". As nematoceran flies, adult gnats have antennae with at least six segments that are often long and slender. They are generally slender-bodied with long and narrow wings."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Insects", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gnats are a specific type of insect, and 'Insects' is a broader taxonomic category that includes them.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Gnats are a specific type of insect, and 'Insects' is a broader taxonomic category that includes them."}, {"found_name": "Imago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4025, "target_cluster_name": "imago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Imago refers to the adult, sexually mature stage of an insect, which is the 'gnat' form described.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Imago refers to the adult, sexually mature stage of an insect, which is the 'gnat' form described."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "alhazen", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alhazen"], "contexts": ["medieval scholar on optics"]}, {"entity_id": "alhazen", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Alhazen"], "contexts": ["Saracen astronomer of Spain, 9th century", "Saracen astronomer of Spain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alhazen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Alhazen", "similarity": 0.674}], "id": 2695, "stable_key": "clu_26c60ac46c43dee4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ibn al-Haytham", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 965, "death_year": 1040, "description": "Arab polymath, considered a pioneer of optics and scientific method.", "wikidata_id": "Q11104", "wikidata_description": "Persian physicist, mathematician and astronomer (c. 965 – c. 1040)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were foundational figures in their respective scientific fields, with Galen being the dominant authority in ancient medicine and Ibn al-Haytham being the father of modern optics and a key figure in the scientific method.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were foundational figures in their respective scientific fields, with Galen being the dominant authority in ancient medicine and Ibn al-Haytham being the father of modern optics and a key figure in the scientific method."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were towering intellectual figures whose systematic approaches to knowledge and empirical observations laid groundwork for future scientific inquiry, though Ibn al-Haytham is credited with a more rigorous application of the scientific method.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were towering intellectual figures whose systematic approaches to knowledge and empirical observations laid groundwork for future scientific inquiry, though Ibn al-Haytham is credited with a more rigorous application of the scientific method."}, {"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were significant figures in the history of science, with Dioscorides being a key figure in ancient pharmacology and Ibn al-Haytham a pioneer in optics and scientific methodology. Their works represent important contributions to the understanding of the natural world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were significant figures in the history of science, with Dioscorides being a key figure in ancient pharmacology and Ibn al-Haytham a pioneer in optics and scientific methodology. Their works represent important contributions to the understanding of the natural world."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Arteria magna", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "great_artery", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "great artery", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["great artery"], "contexts": ["major blood vessel"]}, {"entity_id": "arteria_magna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arteria magna", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["arteria magna", "Arteria magna", "Arteria Magna"], "contexts": ["great artery", "major artery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "great artery", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arteria magna", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 2696, "stable_key": "clu_4e54e2a4c290fc70", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aorta", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'Arteria magna' was commonly used in early modern anatomy for the aorta."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "heart", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 85, "target_cluster_name": "heart", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The aorta is the main artery that carries blood from the heart to the rest of the body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The aorta is the main artery that carries blood from the heart to the rest of the body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "coraçao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1519, "target_cluster_name": "coraçao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The aorta is the main artery carrying blood away from the heart.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The aorta is the main artery carrying blood away from the heart.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "stupor", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "stupifies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "stupifies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["stupifies"], "contexts": ["causes numbness or paralysis"]}, {"entity_id": "stupor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "stupor", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["stupor", "Stupor", "stuporis"], "contexts": ["state of stupor", "state of reduced consciousness or sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "stupifies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "stupor", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 2697, "stable_key": "clu_192e8b0d06f22751", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stupor", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Stupor", "wikidata_id": "Q268594", "wikidata_description": "total or nearly total lack of spontaneous movements (disease)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stupor", "wikipedia_extract": "Stupor is the lack of critical mental function and a level of consciousness, in which an affected person is almost entirely unresponsive and responds only to intense stimuli such as pain. The word derives from the Latin stupor (\"numbness, insensibility\"). Stupor is characterized by impaired reaction to external stimuli. Those in a stuporous state are rigid, mute and only appear to be conscious, as the eyes are open and follow surrounding objects. If not stimulated externally, a patient with stupor will appear to be in a sleepy state most of the time. In some extreme cases of severe depressive disorders the patient can become motionless, lose their appetite and become mute."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "amentia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1824, "target_cluster_name": "amentia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor, a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, can be a manifestation or severe symptom of amentia/dementia in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor, a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, can be a manifestation or severe symptom of amentia/dementia in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "unconsciousness", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1942, "target_cluster_name": "unconsciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "sleep", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1316, "target_cluster_name": "sleep", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a significant deviation from normal sleep.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a significant deviation from normal sleep.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "modorras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1613, "target_cluster_name": "modorras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a severe form of lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor is a state of near-unconsciousness or insensibility, which is a severe form of lethargy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Convulsions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 652, "target_cluster_name": "Convulsions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor can be a post-ictal state following a convulsion or seizure, indicating a close temporal and physiological relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor can be a post-ictal state following a convulsion or seizure, indicating a close temporal and physiological relationship.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "tremor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1480, "target_cluster_name": "tremor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tremors can sometimes accompany states of stupor or altered consciousness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tremors can sometimes accompany states of stupor or altered consciousness.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Vomitos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1493, "target_cluster_name": "Vomitos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Stupor can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Stupor can be a symptom of severe illness that also causes vomiting.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "suffocação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target