iferum, and the Canada onion Allium canadense. The name wild onion is applied to a number of Allium species, but A. cepa is exclusively known from cultivation. Its ancestral wild original form is not known, although escapes from cultivation have become established in some regions."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Garlick", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2260, "target_cluster_name": "Garlick", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Garlic and onions are both species of bulbous flowering plants in the genus Allium and are considered close relatives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Garlic and onions are both species of bulbous flowering plants in the genus Allium and are considered close relatives.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Garlic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1734, "target_cluster_name": "Alhos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Garlic (Allium sativum) is a closely related species within the same genus (Allium) as the onion (Allium cepa), often cultivated and used similarly in cuisine and traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Garlic (Allium sativum) is a closely related species within the same genus (Allium) as the onion (Allium cepa), often cultivated and used similarly in cuisine and traditional medicine."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lips", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lips", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lips", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lips", "Lids"], "contexts": ["body part", "stained by Bilberry juice"]}, {"entity_id": "lips", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lips", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "varia