: "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Crocus and Turmeric are plants known for their vibrant colors, with saffron (derived from Crocus sativus) and turmeric (Curcuma longa) being historically significant as dyes and spices. The candidate 'turmeric (aka croco indiaco)' explicitly links turmeric to 'croco', which is a cognate of Crocus, suggesting a historical association or confusion."}]}, {"canonical_name": "fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "fumes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fumes"], "contexts": ["hot fumes for easing piles"]}, {"entity_id": "fumes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["fumes"], "contexts": ["inimical to life", "Latin for 'I have been'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Fumes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fumes", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 2713, "stable_key": "clu_7457b8b2e84e6f9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fumes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to vapors or gases, often with medicinal or harmful connotations."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Wind", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 80, "target_cluster_name": "Wind", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wind can carry and disperse fumes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Wind can carry and disperse fumes."}, {"found_name": "ventoſas ſarjadas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1853, "target_cluster_name": "ventoſas ſarjadas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fumes can be associated with internal bodily processes that produce gas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fumes can be associated with internal bodily processes that produce gas.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lupini", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lupines", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lupines", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lupines"], "contexts": ["plant used in powder"]}, {"entity_id": "lupini", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Lupini", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["lupini", "Lupini"], "contexts": ["lupins for grinding", "lupine seed ingredient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lupines", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Lupini", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 2714, "stable_key": "clu_1bbd0a3bf268f715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lupins", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Lupinus", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "Edible seeds or plants of the genus Lupinus.", "note": "Refers to the plant genus Lupinus or its seeds.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lupinus, commonly known as lupin, lupine, or regionally bluebonnet, is a genus of plants in the legume family Fabaceae. The genus includes over 199 species, with centres of diversity in North and South America. Smaller centres occur in North Africa and the Mediterranean. They are widely cultivated both as a food source and as ornamental plants, but are invasive to some areas. For instance, lupine has become an ecological problem in Iceland since the beginning of the 21st century. The species are mostly herbaceous perennial plants 0.3–1.5 metres (1–5 feet) tall, but some are annual plants and a few are shrubs up to 3 m (10 ft) tall."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kidney Bean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3304, "target_cluster_name": "Kidney Bean", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Lupins and Kidney Beans are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Lupins and Kidney Beans are members of the legume family (Fabaceae)."}, {"found_name": "Lentil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2471, "target_cluster_name": "lentilhas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Lupins and Lentils are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Lupins and Lentils are members of the legume family (Fabaceae)."}, {"found_name": "Pea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2708, "target_cluster_name": "Peas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Lupins and Peas are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Lupins and Peas are members of the legume family (Fabaceae)."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "thames", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thames"], "contexts": ["river where plant grows"]}, {"entity_id": "thames", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Thames"], "contexts": ["river with channels filled", "river whose channels are filled by tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thames", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thames", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2715, "stable_key": "clu_0778cc4264fd119d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "River Thames", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q19686", "wikidata_description": "river in southern England", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River%20Thames", "wikipedia_extract": "The River Thames (  TEMZ), known alternatively in parts as the River Isis, is a river that flows through southern England including London. At 215 miles (346 km), it is the longest river entirely in England and the second-longest in the United Kingdom, after the River Severn. The river rises at Thames Head in Gloucestershire and flows into the North Sea near Tilbury, Essex and Gravesend, Kent, via the Thames Estuary. From the west, it flows through Oxford (where it is sometimes called the Isis), Reading, Henley-on-Thames and Windsor. The Thames also drains the whole of Greater London. The lower reaches of the river are called the Tideway, derived from its long tidal reach up to Teddington Lock."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kent", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1708, "target_cluster_name": "Kent", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kent is bordered by the River Thames to the north.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kent is bordered by the River Thames to the north.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "London", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 6, "target_cluster_name": "London", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The River Thames flows through London.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The River Thames flows through London."}, {"found_name": "River", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1012, "target_cluster_name": "rivers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The River Thames is a type of river.", "co