ext, coupled with 'field of vision,' directly points to the area perceived by sight.", "wikidata_id": "Q2364768", "wikidata_description": "total area or space visible in a person's peripheral vision with the eye looking straight forward"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "apteryx", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Apteryx"], "contexts": ["genus of kiwi birds", "mentioned in text"]}, {"entity_id": "apteryx", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["apteryx"], "contexts": ["living bird species for comparison", "flightless bird native to New Zealand"]}, {"entity_id": "spteryx", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["spteryx"], "contexts": ["bird allied to dinornis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Apteryx", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "apteryx", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Apteryx", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spteryx", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 2731, "stable_key": "clu_45a242e4c2de9dbc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apteryx", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q43642", "wikidata_description": "genus of birds", "wikipedia_extract": "Kiwi are flightless birds endemic to New Zealand of the order Apterygiformes. The five extant species fall into the family Apterygidae and genus Apteryx. Approximately the size of a domestic chicken, kiwi are the smallest ratites (which also include ostriches, emus, rheas, cassowaries and the extinct elephant birds and moa). DNA sequence comparisons have yielded the conclusion that kiwi are much more closely related to the extinct Malagasy elephant birds than to the moa with which they shared New Zealand. There are five recognised species, four of which are currently listed as vulnerable, and one of which is near threatened. All species have been negatively affected by historic deforestation, but their remaining habitat is well protected in large forest reserves and national parks."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "aves", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2148, "target_cluster_name": "aves", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apteryx is a genus of flightless birds native to New Zealand.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Apteryx is a genus of flightless birds native to New Zealand.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ostrich", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 881, "target_cluster_name": "Ostrich", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Apteryx, commonly known as the kiwi, is another large, flightless bird, though distinct from the ostrich.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Apteryx, commonly known as the kiwi, is another large, flightless bird, though distinct from the ostrich.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sediment", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sediment"], "contexts": ["material deposited by water", "material derived from erosion"]}, {"entity_id": "sediment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["sediment"], "contexts": ["mate