hin geological formations, including secondary ones.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The term 'fossiliferous' directly relates to the presence of fossils within geological formations, including secondary ones."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "buckland", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Buckland"], "contexts": ["remarked on fossil classification"]}, {"entity_id": "buckland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Buckland"], "contexts": ["geologist quoted on coprolite abundance", "geologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Buckland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Buckland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2737, "stable_key": "clu_7e43973db398a23e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Buckland", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1784, "death_year": 1856, "description": "Geologist and paleontologist, known for work on fossils and ancient life."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Georges Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 119, "target_cluster_name": "Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Georges Cuvier was a prominent naturalist and paleontologist whose work heavily influenced William Buckland's own paleontological research and publications.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Georges Cuvier was a prominent naturalist and paleontologist whose work heavily influenced William Buckland's own paleontological research and publications."}, {"found_name": "Charles Lyell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1710, "target_cluster_name": "Lyell", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Charles Lyell was a leading geologist of the same era as William Buckland, and they were contemporaries who engaged with similar geological theories and discoveries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Charles Lyell was a leading geologist of the same era as William Buckland, and they were contemporaries who engaged with similar geological theories and discoveries."}, {"found_name": "John Morris", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3446, "target_cluster_name": "Morris", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Morris was a paleontologist and colleague of William Buckland, and they collaborated on geological and paleontological studies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "John Morris was a paleontologist and colleague of William Buckland, and they collaborated on geological and paleontological studies."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Carolines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "caroline_archipelagoes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Caroline Archipelagoes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caroline Archipelagoes"], "contexts": ["archipelagoes with peculiar bats"]}, {"entity_id": "carolines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Carolines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carolines"], "contexts": ["states mentioned", "region in the United States"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Caroline Archipelagoes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Carolines", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 2738, "stable_key": "clu_a571d4b0d6fb2e64", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caroline Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago in the western Pacific Ocean.", "note": "The historical mention in Humboldt likely refers to the Caroline Islands. The reference to 'Carolines' as states in the US is less likely in this context, but the 'Caroline Archipelagoes' strongly confirms the islands.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Caroline Islands (or the Carolines) are a widely scattered archipelago of tiny islands in the western Pacific Ocean, to the north of New Guinea. Politically, they are divided between the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) in the central and eastern parts of the group, and Palau at the extreme western end. Historically, this area was also called Nuevas Filipinas or New Philippines, because they were part of the Spanish East Indies and were governed from Manila in the Philippines. The Carolines are scattered across a distance of approximately 3,540 kilometres (2,200 mi; 1,910 nmi), from the westernmost island, Tobi, in Palau, to the easternmost island, Kosrae, a state of the FSM. The group consists of about 500 small coral islands, east of the Philippines, in the Pacific Ocean. The distance from Yap (one of the larger Caroline islands) to Manila is 1,200 mi (1,900 km)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Caroline Islands are a specific example of an archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Caroline Islands are a specific example of an archipelago."}]}, {"canonical_name": "teſta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "teats", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "teats", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teats"], "contexts": ["developed and rudimentary teats"]}, {"entity_id": "teſta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "teſta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["teſta"], "contexts": ["head, affected by vapors", "part of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "teats", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "teſta", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2739, "stable_key": "clu_522535681d859705", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Testa", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'teſta' in older texts often refers to the seed coat (testa), but the context 'developed and rudimentary teats' suggests a potential ambiguity with 'teats' (mammal anatomy). Given the 'polyanthea_medicinal' source, it's more likely referring to a plant part. The other context is unclear, but 'head, affected by vapors' is too vague. The 'teats' in Darwin refers to mammalian mammary glands."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Shell", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1425, "target_cluster_name": "Shell", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'testa' in botany refers to the protective outer layer of a seed, analogous to a shell.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'testa' in botany refers to the protective outer layer of a seed, analogous to a shell."}]}, {"canonical_name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "purpose", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["purpose"], "contexts": ["organ's purpose"]}, {"entity_id": "purpose", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": 