ention both."}, {"found_name": "Riobamba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2053, "target_cluster_name": "Riobamba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Riobamba is a city in Ecuador, and Huancabamba is also in Ecuador. They are geographically proximate and often mentioned in the same historical or geographical contexts within Ecuador.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Riobamba is a city in Ecuador, and Huancabamba is also in Ecuador. They are geographically proximate and often mentioned in the same historical or geographical contexts within Ecuador."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "maryland", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Maryland"], "contexts": ["state mentioned", "confederated state in the United States"]}, {"entity_id": "maryland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maryland"], "contexts": ["location where aerolite fell"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maryland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Maryland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2752, "stable_key": "clu_2a367561caca95c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maryland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Maryland (US:   MERR-il-ənd) is a state in the Mid-Atlantic and Southern regions of the United States. It borders the states of Virginia to its south, West Virginia to its west, Pennsylvania to its north, and Delaware to its east, as well as with the Atlantic Ocean to its east, and the national capital and federal district of Washington, D.C. to the southwest. With a total area of 12,407 square miles (32,130 km2), Maryland is the ninth-smallest state by land area, and its population of 6,177,224 ranks it the 19th-most populous state and the fifth-most densely populated. Maryland's capital city is Annapolis, and the state's most populous city is Baltimore. Maryland's coastline was first explored by Europeans in the 16th century. Prior to that, it was inhabited by several Native American tribes, mostly the Algonquian peoples."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Astrakhan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "astrakhan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Astrakhan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Astrakhan"], "contexts": ["government in Piussle", "Wologda et Astrakhan 52"]}, {"entity_id": "astrachan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Astrachan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Astrachan"], "contexts": ["city with luxuriant fruit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Astrakhan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Astrachan", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 2753, "stable_key": "clu_4ac1e12b8d94a715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astrakhan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "The spelling 'Astrachan' is an older transliteration of Astrakhan.", "wikidata_id": "Q3927", "wikidata_description": "city in the south of Russia", "wikipedia_extract": "Astrakhan (Russian: Астрахань, IPA: [ˈastrəxənʲ] ) is the largest city and administrative centre of Astrakhan Oblast in southern Russia. The city lies on two banks of the Volga, in the upper part of the Volga Delta, on eleven islands of the Caspian Depression, 100 km (62 mi) from the Caspian Sea, with a population of 475,629 residents at the 2021 Census. At an elevation of 28 meters (92 ft) below sea level, it is the lowest city in Russia. Astrakhan was formerly the capital of the Khanate of Astrakhan (a remnant of the Golden Horde) of the Astrakhan Tatars, and was located on the higher right bank of the Volga, eleven kilometres (6.8 mi) from the present-day city. Situated on caravan and water routes, it developed from a village into a large trading centre, before being conquered by Timur in 1395 and captured by Ivan the Terrible in 1556 and in 1558 it was moved to its present site. The oldest economic and cultural center of the Lower Volga region, it is often called the southernmost outpost of Russia, and the Caspian capital."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kasan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1038, "target_cluster_name": "Kasan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrakhan and Kazan were both important cities in the Golden Horde and later in the Russian Empire, often serving as administrative and trade centers in the Volga region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Astrakhan and Kazan were both important cities in the Golden Horde and later in the Russian Empire, often serving as administrative and trade centers in the Volga region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Volga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1383, "target_cluster_name": "Volga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrakhan is a major city located on the Volga River delta, near its mouth on the Caspian Sea. Historically, it has been a significant port and administrative center closely tied to the river's importance for trade and transportation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Astrakhan is a major city located on the Volga River delta, near its mouth on the Caspian Sea. Historically, it has been a significant port and administrative center closely tied to the river's importance for trade and transportation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mer Caspienne", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1983, "target_cluster_name": "Mer Caspienne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A major port city on the Caspian Sea.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A major port city on the Caspian Sea.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Irkutsk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2563, "target_cluster_name": "Irkutsk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Astrakhan is a city in Russia, located near the Volga River delta on the Caspian Sea. Irkutsk is also a major city in Russia, located in Siberia. Both are significant administrative and economic centers within Russia, and historically, Astrakhan was a key trading post on routes connecting Europe and Asia, similar to Irkutsk's role in Siberian expansion and trade.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Astrakhan is a city in Russia, located near the Volga River delta on the Caspian Sea. Irkutsk is also a major city in Russia, located in Siberia. Both are significant administrative and economic centers within Russia, and historically, Astrakhan was a key trading post on routes connecting Europe and Asia, similar to Irkutsk's role in Siberian expansion and trade.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Barnaul", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2798, "target_cluster_name": "Barnaul", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are major cities in Russia, with Astrakhan being a significant port on the Caspian Sea and Barnaul being a major city in Siberia. Historical texts might discuss them in relation to Russian expansion, trade routes, or administrative centers within the Russian Empire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are major cities in Russia, with Astrakhan being a significant port on the Caspian Sea and Barnaul being a major city in Siberia. Historical texts might discuss them in relation to Russian expansion, trade routes, or administrative centers within the Russian Empire.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Guanacas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "guanacas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guanacas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Guanacas"], "contexts": ["mountain chain", "Andes passage name"]}, {"entity_id": "guancas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Guancas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guancas"], "contexts": ["people of those regions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guanacas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Guancas", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 2754, "stable_key": "clu_cb394adf10013a82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guanacas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "note": "Likely referring to the Guanacas people or the geographical region associated with them in the Andes, consistent with historical accounts of indigenous populations in South America.", "wikidata_id": "Q690026", "wikidata_description": "province of Costa Rica"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cruces", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cruces", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cruces", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Cruces"], "contexts": ["embarcadero", "location on the isthmus"]}, {"entity_id": "cross", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cross", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cross"], "contexts": ["region in the heavens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cruces", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cross", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 2755, "stable_key": "clu_8ea6e41724b91dab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cruces", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Guatemala", "note": "Contexts suggest multiple meanings. 'Cruces' could refer to a place (likely in the Americas, given Humboldt's work) or astronomical references ('a Crucis'). Without more context, pinpointing a single referent is difficult. The primary referent in the text seems to be a place name."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Franconie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "franconie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Franconie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Franconie"], "contexts": ["historical region in Germany", "region containing Ochsenkopf"]}, {"entity_id": "franconia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Franconia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franconia"], "contexts": ["region producing wines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franconie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Franconia", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 2756, "stable_key": "clu_3debe8cce4669f32", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Franconia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region in northern Bavaria, Germany.", "note": "'Franguístan' appears to be a transliteration of the region.", "wikipedia_extract": "Franconia (German: Franken [ˈfʁaŋkn̩] ; East Franconian: Franggn [ˈfrɑŋɡŋ̍]; Bavarian: Frankn) is a geographical region of Germany, characterised by its culture and East Franconian dialect (Ostfränkisch). Franconia is made up of the three Regierungsbezirke (governmental districts) of Lower, Middle and Upper Franconia in Bavaria, the adjacent, Franconian-speaking South Thuringia, south of the Thuringian Forest—which constitutes the language boundary between Franconian and Thuringian—and the eastern parts of Heilbronn-Franconia in Baden-Württemberg. Those parts of the Vogtland lying in Saxony (largest city: Plauen) are sometimes regarded as Franconian as well, because the Vogtlandian dialects are mostly East Franconian. The inhabitants of Saxon Vogtland, however, mostly do not consider themselves Franconian. On the other hand, the inhabitants of the Hessian-speaking parts of Lower Franconia west of the Spessart (largest city: Aschaffenburg) do consider themselves Franconian, although not speaking the dialect. Heilbronn-Franconia's largest city of Heilbronn and its surrounding areas are South Franconian-speaking, and therefore only sometimes regarded as Franconian."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cent_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["cent."], "contexts": ["temperature unit", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "cent_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cent."], "contexts": ["unit of temperature measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cent.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cent.", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 2757, "stable_key": "clu_c0363be539cf734b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Celsius", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a unit of temperature, likely Celsius given the historical context and the mention of temperature measurement.", "wikipedia_extract": "The degree Celsius is the unit of temperature on the Celsius temperature scale (originally known as the centigrade scale in English), one of two temperature scales used in the International System of Units (SI), the other being the closely related Kelvin scale. The degree Celsius (symbol: °C) can refer to a specific point on the Celsius temperature scale or to a difference or range between two temperatures. It is named after the Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius (1701–1744), who proposed the first version of it in 1742. The unit was called centigrade in several languages (from the Latin centum, which means 100, and gradus, which means steps) for many years. In 1948, the International Committee for Weights and Measures renamed it to honor Celsius and also to remove confusion with the term for one hundredth of a gradian in some languages. Most countries use this scale, with the exception of the United States, some island territories, and Liberia, where the Fahrenheit scale is still used."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Sénégal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sénégal", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sénégal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Sénégal"], "contexts": ["location of observations", "location of barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "senegal", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Senegal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senegal"], "contexts": ["river where Bichir is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sénégal", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Senegal", "similarity": 0.749}], "id": 2758, "stable_key": "clu_f91cf3fb452dbdfb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Senegal", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Senegal", "wikidata_id": "Q3775608", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Africa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3, "target_cluster_name": "Africa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Senegal is a country located in Africa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Senegal is a country located in Africa."}]}, {"canonical_name": "îles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "îles_canaries", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["îles Canaries"], "contexts": ["islands", "islands with low barometric variations"]}, {"entity_id": "iles_canaries", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Iles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Iles Canaries"], "contexts": ["islands described in a book"]}, {"entity_id": "canaries", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Canaries"], "contexts": ["location for obtaining medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "canaries", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Canaries"], "contexts": ["islands off the coast of Africa", "islands with plants belonging to African coast"]}, {"entity_id": "canarischen_jnseln", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Canarischen Jnseln", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Canarischen Jnseln"], "contexts": ["location mentioned in a work title"]}, {"entity_id": "canarische_inseln", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Canarische Inseln", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Canarische Inseln"], "contexts": ["book title by Leop. von Buch", "book title about islands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "îles Canaries", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Iles Canaries", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 2759, "stable_key": "clu_09b0039d65674ca7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Canary Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "Direct translation of the German name for the archipelago.", "wikidata_id": "Q5813", "wikidata_description": "autonomous community of Spain, and archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canary%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Canary Islands ( ; Spanish: Canarias [kaˈnaɾjas] ) or Canaries are an archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean and the southernmost autonomous community of Spain, located about 100 kilometres (60 mi) off the northwest coast of Africa. The archipelago has a population of approximately 2.27 million inhabitants, making it the most populous overseas special territory of the European Union. The seven main islands, listed from largest to smallest by area, are Tenerife, Fuerteventura, Gran Canaria, Lanzarote, La Palma, La Gomera, and El Hierro. The only other inhabited island is La Graciosa, which is administratively dependent on Lanzarote. The archipelago also includes numerous smaller islands and islets, such as Alegranza, Isla de Lobos, Montaña Clara, Roque del Oeste, and Roque del Este, as well as various rocks, including Garachico and Anaga. Historically, the island chain was referred to as \"the Fortunate Isles\"."}, "member_count": 6, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Africa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3, "target_cluster_name": "Africa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Canary Islands are located off the northwest coast of Africa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Canary Islands are located off the northwest coast of Africa."}, {"found_name": "Atlantic Ocean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 318, "target_cluster_name": "Atlantic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Canary Islands are an archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Canary Islands are an archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean."}, {"found_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 789, "target_cluster_name": "Galapagos Archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are archipelagos, and historically, such island groups were often discussed in relation to each other due to their unique ecosystems and exploration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are archipelagos, and historically, such island groups were often discussed in relation to each other due to their unique ecosystems and exploration."}, {"found_name": "Azores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 874, "target_cluster_name": "Azores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are Atlantic archipelagos located off the coast of Africa, often discussed together in historical maritime and geographical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are Atlantic archipelagos located off the coast of Africa, often discussed together in historical maritime and geographical contexts."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "toulouse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Toulouse"], "contexts": ["city where observations were made", "city with barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "toulouse", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Toulouse"], "contexts": ["location of observatory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Toulouse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Toulouse", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2760, "stable_key": "clu_37cc03b2fd258453", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Toulouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A city in southern France, historically significant for its printing and scientific observations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Toulouse (, too-LOOZ, French: [tuluz] ; Occitan: Tolosa [tuˈluzɔ] ) is a city in Southern France, the prefecture of the Haute-Garonne department and of the Occitania region. The city is on the banks of the River Garonne, 150 kilometres (93 miles) from the Mediterranean Sea, 230 km (143 mi) from the Atlantic Ocean and 680 km (420 mi) from Paris. It is the fourth-largest city in France after Paris, Marseille, and Lyon, with 511,684 inhabitants within its municipal boundaries (2022); its metropolitan area has a population of 1,513,396 inhabitants (2022). Toulouse is the central city of one of the 22 metropolitan councils of France. Between the 2014 and 2020 censuses, its metropolitan area was the third fastest growing among metropolitan areas larger than 500,000 inhabitants in France. Toulouse is the centre of the European aerospace industry, with the headquarters of Airbus, the SPOT satellite system, ATR and the Aerospace Valley."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "indien", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "indien", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indien", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["indien", "Indien"], "contexts": ["indigenous person", "person from India"]}, {"entity_id": "indian", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Indian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indian"], "contexts": ["foreign personage in mediumship"]}, {"entity_id": "savages", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "savages", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["savages"], "contexts": ["indigenous peoples studied for psychological comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "american_savages", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "American savages", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["American savages"], "contexts": ["indigenous peoples of America, with refined grammar"]}, {"entity_id": "savages", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "savages", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["savages"], "contexts": ["term for uncivilized people", "people with simple chants"]}, {"entity_id": "indigènes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indigènes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["indigènes"], "contexts": ["native inhabitants", "native inhabitants of the land"]}, {"entity_id": "indigenous_people", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "indigenous people", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["indigenous people"], "contexts": ["native inhabitants of a region"]}, {"entity_id": "natives", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natives", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["natives"], "contexts": ["people of a region"]}, {"entity_id": "native_american_nations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "native American nations", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["native American nations"], "contexts": ["indigenous peoples of America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "indien", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Indian", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 2761, "stable_key": "clu_1700bf3ca65396f5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indigenous peoples", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'savages' was historically used to refer to indigenous peoples, often in a derogatory manner, but in scientific and philosophical texts, it frequently referred to groups studied for comparative anthropology or psychology.", "wikidata_id": "Q1363781", "wikidata_description": "descendants of the population that lived in the territory of modern Brazil before the Portuguese colonization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous%20peoples%20in%20Brazil", "description": "Original inhabitants of a region or continent", "wikipedia_extract": "Indigenous peoples in Brazil or Native Brazilians (Portuguese: Brasileiros nativos) are the peoples whose ancestors lived in Brazil before European contact around 1500 and those pre-Columbian forebears. Indigenous peoples once comprised an estimated 2,000 distinct tribes and nations inhabiting what is now Brazil. The 2010 Brazil census recorded 305 Indigenous ethnic groups. Historically, many Indigenous peoples of Brazil were semi-nomadic and combined hunting, fishing, and gathering with migratory agriculture. Many tribes were massacred by European settlers, and others assimilated into the growing Brazilian population. The Indigenous population was decimated by European diseases, declining from a pre-Columbian high of 2 million to 3 million to approximately 300,000 by 1997, distributed among 200 tribes."}, "member_count": 9, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3017, "target_cluster_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amerigo Vespucci's voyages and naming of the Americas are intrinsically linked to the history and understanding of Indigenous peoples in the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amerigo Vespucci's voyages and naming of the Americas are intrinsically linked to the history and understanding of Indigenous peoples in the region."}]}, {"canonical_name": "HOMMES", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hommes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "HOMMES", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["hommes", "HOMMES"], "contexts": ["male enslaved people", "category of people counted"]}, {"entity_id": "humans", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "humans", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["humans"], "contexts": ["human beings, people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "HOMMES", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "humans", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 2762, "stable_key": "clu_5dc20acef445f831", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humans", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'HOMMES' can refer to men, in the context of Humboldt's scientific writings and James's psychology, it refers to people or humankind generally."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Adam", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 326, "target_cluster_name": "Adam", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Biblical progenitor of humanity, a foundational figure in the concept of 'Humans'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Biblical progenitor of humanity, a foundational figure in the concept of 'Humans'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Wales", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "wales", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Wales", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Wales"], "contexts": ["early modern scholar", "source of published observations"]}, {"entity_id": "welsh", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Welsh", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Welsh"], "contexts": ["made observations during balloon ascents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Wales", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Welsh", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 2763, "stable_key": "clu_2d25acfbe960e4c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Wales", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Early modern scholar who made balloon ascent observations.", "note": "The context points to an individual scholar named Wales, likely English, associated with scientific observations, possibly related to balloon ascents as a historical practice of scientific inquiry."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "essai_polit,_sur_la_nouvelle-espagne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "Essai polilique sur la Nouvelle-Espagne"], "contexts": ["title of a work", "political essay title"]}, {"entity_id": "essai_polit__sur_la_nouv__espagne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne"], "contexts": ["political essay on New Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "essai_politique_sur_la_nouvelle_espagne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne"], "contexts": ["work by Humboldt on New Spain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2764, "stable_key": "clu_35e4728bd214c33c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Political Essay on the Kingdom of New Spain", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This is a direct translation and title of a major work by Alexander von Humboldt."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Geography", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1068, "target_cluster_name": "Geography", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Political Essay on the Kingdom of New Spain' is a foundational work in the field of geography, specifically focusing on the political and economic geography of a region. The candidate 'Geography' represents the broader academic discipline to which this essay belongs.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Political Essay on the Kingdom of New Spain' is a foundational work in the field of geography, specifically focusing on the political and economic geography of a region. The candidate 'Geography' represents the broader academic discipline to which this essay belongs."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Castille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "castille", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Castille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Castille", "Castilla del Oro"], "contexts": ["kingdom in Spain", "unit of weight for gold"]}, {"entity_id": "castalia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Castalia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Castalia"], "contexts": ["spring at Delphi", "spring at the foot of Phaedriadae"]}, {"entity_id": "caftilla", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Caftilla", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Caftilla", "Caſtilla"], "contexts": ["region with similar trees and plants", "Turpentine made in Castile"]}, {"entity_id": "caftilla", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Caftilla", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caftilla"], "contexts": ["region where many people were cured"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Castille", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Castalia", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 2765, "stable_key": "clu_323cbe4b7a2c4e67", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Castile", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "The context 'spring at Delphi' might refer to Castalia, a spring at Delphi, but the dominant references point to the historical region in Spain, especially given the context of 'kingdom in Spain'. Humboldt's 'Relation historique' and 'Kosmos' would primarily use 'Castille' for the Spanish region.", "wikidata_id": "Q217196", "wikidata_description": "former country", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_of_Castile", "wikipedia_extract": "On July 6, 2016, Philando Castile, a 32-year-old African American man, was fatally shot during a traffic stop by police officer Jeronimo Yanez of the St. Anthony police department in the Minneapolis–Saint Paul metropolitan area. About 9 p.m., Castile was driving with his girlfriend, Diamond Reynolds, and her four-year-old daughter when he was pulled over by Yanez and another officer in Falcon Heights, a suburb of Saint Paul, Minnesota. Castile, who was licensed to carry a firearm, told Yanez that he had a firearm. Yanez replied, \"Don't reach for it then\". Castile responded, \"I'm, I, I was reaching for...\", to which Yanez replied, \"Don't pull it out\"."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Spaniards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3322, "target_cluster_name": "Spaniards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Castile was a major kingdom and historical region within Spain, central to its formation and identity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Castile was a major kingdom and historical region within Spain, central to its formation and identity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "leonhard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Leonhard", "Leonliard"], "contexts": ["author of rock characteristics", "author of Characteristik der Fctsarten"]}, {"entity_id": "leonhard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Leonhard"], "contexts": ["editor of publication", "author of geological works"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Leonhard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Leonhard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2766, "stable_key": "clu_29cbf3653b32f537", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karl Leonhard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1775, "death_year": 1833, "description": "Geologist and mineralogist, author of 'Rock Characteristics'.", "note": "This refers to Karl Leonhard, a prominent German geologist and mineralogist, known for his classifications and publications.", "wikidata_id": "Q112258", "wikidata_description": "Austrian philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl%20Leonhard%20Reinhold", "wikipedia_extract": "Karl Leonhard Reinhold (; Austrian German: [ˈraɪnhɔld]; 26 October 1757 – 10 April 1823) was an Austrian philosopher who helped to popularise the work of Immanuel Kant in the late 18th century. His \"elementary philosophy\" (Elementarphilosophie) also influenced German idealism, notably Johann Gottlieb Fichte, as a critical system grounded in a fundamental first principle. He was the father of Ernst Christian Gottlieb Reinhold (1793–1855), also a philosopher. Reinhold was born in Vienna. In late 1772, at the age of fourteen, he entered the Jesuit (Roman Catholic) seminary St. Anne's Church, Vienna (Jesuitenkollegium St."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Immanuel Kant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 643, "target_cluster_name": "Kant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Karl Leonhard Reinhold was a philosopher who helped to popularise the work of Immanuel Kant and whose philosophy influenced German idealism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "