 Europe is a major geographical and geopolitical region within the continent of Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Eastern Europe is a major geographical and geopolitical region within the continent of Europe."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Barnaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "barnaul", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Barnaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Barnaul"], "contexts": ["city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "barnaul", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Barnaul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Barnaul"], "contexts": ["location of magnetic station", "city on magnetic stations line"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Barnaul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Barnaul", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2798, "stable_key": "clu_897790439691117d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Barnaul", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q6014", "wikidata_description": "city in eastern Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnaul", "wikipedia_extract": "Barnaul (Russian: Барнау́л, IPA: [bərnɐˈul] ) is the largest city and administrative centre of Altai Krai, Russia, located at the confluence of the Barnaulka and Ob rivers in the West Siberian Plain. As of the 2021 census, its population was 630,877, making it the 20th-largest city in Russia and the fourth-largest in the Siberian Federal District. Located in the south of western Siberia on the left bank of the Ob River, Barnaul is a major transport, industrial, cultural, medical and educational hub of Siberia. Barnaul was founded by the wealthy Demidov family, who intended to develop the production of copper and silver, which continued after the factories were taken over by the Crown. Barnaul became a major centre of silver production in Russia. Barnaul was granted city status in 1771."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kasan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1038, "target_cluster_name": "Kasan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Barnaul and Kazan are both major cities located on rivers in Russia, serving as regional centers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Barnaul and Kazan are both major cities located on rivers in Russia, serving as regional centers.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Irkutsk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2563, "target_cluster_name": "Irkutsk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Barnaul is a city in Siberia, Russia, located on the Ob River. Irkutsk is also a major city in Siberia, located on the Angara River. Both cities are important industrial and cultural centers in the Siberian region of Russia and share geographical proximity and historical development within the context of Russian expansion into Siberia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Barnaul is a city in Siberia, Russia, located on the Ob River. Irkutsk is also a major city in Siberia, located on the Angara River. Both cities are important industrial and cultural centers in the Siberian region of Russia and share geographical proximity and historical development within the context of Russian expansion into Siberia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Astrakhan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2753, "target_cluster_name": "Astrakhan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are major cities in Russia, with Astrakhan being a significant port on the Caspian Sea and Barnaul being a major city in Siberia. Historical texts might discuss them in relation to Russian expansion, trade routes, or administrative centers within the Russian Empire.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both are major cities in Russia, with Astrakhan being a significant port on the Caspian Sea and Barnaul being a major city in Siberia. Historical texts might discuss them in relation to Russian expansion, trade routes, or administrative centers within the Russian Empire."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cord", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cord", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "cord", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["cord"], "contexts": ["spinal cord", "part of the central nervous system"]}, {"entity_id": "corde", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Corde", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Corde"], "contexts": ["the heart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cord", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corde", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 2799, "stable_key": "clu_716173577b1ce63d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "spinal cord", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'cord' can refer to other things like heart strings, in the context of James's 'Principles of Psychology' and medical texts, 'spinal cord' is the most likely anatomical referent, especially when considering 'heart' as a separate mention. 'Corde' in a medicinal context could also refer to heart strings, but 'spinal cord' is a more prominent anatomical structure discussed."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Brain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 49, "target_cluster_name": "brain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spinal cord is a direct extension of the brain and forms the central nervous system along with it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The spinal cord is a direct extension of the brain and forms the central nervous system along with it."}, {"found_name": "Nerve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 214, "target_cluster_name": "nervos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spinal cord is composed of nervous tissue and is a major component of the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The spinal cord is composed of nervous tissue and is a major component of the nervous system."}, {"found_name": "Nervous system", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1588, "target_cluster_name": "nervous system", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spinal cord is a fundamental part of the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The spinal cord is a fundamental part of the nervous system."}, {"found_name": "Motor neuron", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2581, "target_cluster_name": "motor nerve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Motor neurons are a type of nerve cell found within the spinal cord that transmit signals from the central nervous system to muscles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Motor neurons are a type of nerve cell found within the spinal cord that transmit signals from the central nervous system to muscles."}, {"found_name": "Nervous tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The spinal cord is primarily composed of nervous tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The spinal cord is primarily composed of nervous tissue."}]}, {"canonical_name": "distinguish", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "distinguish", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "distinguish", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["distinguish"], "contexts": ["recognize differences between objects", "mental act of differentiating"]}, {"entity_id": "distinçam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "distinçam", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["distinçam"], "contexts": ["difference in naming substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "distinguish", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "distinçam", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 2800, "stable_key": "clu_3a000fc08f1d0f7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "distinguish", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Distinction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1236, "target_cluster_name": "distinction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'distinguish' is the verb form of the noun 'distinction', referring to the act of making or perceiving a difference.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'distinguish' is the verb form of the noun 'distinction', referring to the act of making or perceiving a difference."}]}, {"canonical_name": "world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "world", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["world", "World"], "contexts": ["the earth and its inhabitants", "philosophical concept of reality"]}, {"entity_id": "world", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["world"], "contexts": ["external reality interacting with mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "world", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "world", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2801, "stable_key": "clu_77ef716fcd90ab0a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "world", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Humanity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 422, "target_cluster_name": "humanity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'world' is the domain of human existence and experience, making 'Humanity' a closely related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'world' is the domain of human existence and experience, making 'Humanity' a closely related concept."}, {"found_name": "External world", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 978, "target_cluster_name": "external world", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'world' is often used to refer to the external, observable reality, which is contrasted with internal or ideal realms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'world' is often used to refer to the external, observable reality, which is contrasted with internal or ideal realms."}, {"found_name": "Ideal world", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3566, "target_cluster_name": "ideal world", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'world' can also refer to the totality of existence, encompassing both the material and the ideal or conceptual realms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'world' can also refer to the totality of existence, encompassing both the material and the ideal or conceptual realms."}]}, {"canonical_name": "curiosity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "curiosity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "curiosity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["curiosity"], "contexts": ["desire to know or learn", "a strong desire to know or learn something"]}, {"entity_id": "curiosity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "curiosity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["curiosity"], "contexts": ["desire to know or learn"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "curiosity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "curiosity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2802, "stable_key": "clu_b16e82e1597f5ede", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "curiosity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q366791", "wikidata_description": "desire to learn, explore, or investigate", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curiosity", "wikipedia_extract": "Curiosity (from Latin cūriōsitās, from cūriōsus \"careful, diligent, curious\", akin to cura \"care\") is a quality related to inquisitive thinking, such as exploration, investigation, and learning, evident in humans and other animals. Curiosity helps human development, from which derives the process of learning and desire to acquire knowledge and skill. The term curiosity can also denote the behavior, characteristic, or emotion of being curious, in regard to the desire to gain knowledge or information. Curiosity as a behavior and emotion is the driving force behind human development, such as progress in science, language, and industry. Curiosity can be considered to be an evolutionary adaptation based on an organism's ability to learn. Certain curious animals (namely, corvids, octopuses, dolphins, elephants, rats, etc.) will pursue information in order to adapt to their surrounding and learn how things work."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ambition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4281, "target_cluster_name": "Ambition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambition is a strong desire for achievement, which is often fueled by curiosity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ambition is a strong desire for achievement, which is often fueled by curiosity."}, {"found_name": "Appetite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2030, "target_cluster_name": "Appetite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity can be seen as an intellectual appetite for knowledge.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curiosity can be seen as an intellectual appetite for knowledge."}, {"found_name": "Desire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 925, "target_cluster_name": "desire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity is a form of desire, specifically a desire to know or learn.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curiosity is a form of desire, specifically a desire to know or learn."}, {"found_name": "Emotion and Will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3531, "target_cluster_name": "Emotion and Will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity is an emotion that can drive the will towards exploration and learning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curiosity is an emotion that can drive the will towards exploration and learning."}, {"found_name": "Human intellect", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1441, "target_cluster_name": "human intellect", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity is a fundamental aspect of human intellect and its development.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curiosity is a fundamental aspect of human intellect and its development."}, {"found_name": "Ignorance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 189, "target_cluster_name": "Ignorance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity often arises from a state of ignorance, prompting a desire to overcome it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Curiosity often arises from a state of ignorance, prompting a desire to overcome it."}]}, {"canonical_name": "stream", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "stream", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "stream", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["stream"], "contexts": ["continuous flow", "continuous flow of thought"]}, {"entity_id": "stream", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "stream", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["stream"], "contexts": ["smooth stream on whose surface undulations die away"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "stream", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stream", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2803, "stable_key": "clu_09cf89c95ff8cc7e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stream", "confidence": "high", "type": "place"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "waterfall", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4282, "target_cluster_name": "waterfall", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A waterfall is a feature where a stream or river flows over a vertical drop.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A waterfall is a feature where a stream or river flows over a vertical drop."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Wolfe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "wolfe", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Wolfe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Wolfe"], "contexts": ["experimenter on recognition", "conducted experiments on recognition"]}, {"entity_id": "wolphio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Wolphio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wolphio"], "contexts": ["early modern medical author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Wolfe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Wolphio", "similarity": 0.878}], "id": 2804, "stable_key": "clu_51539c529b948444", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christoph Wirsung", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1595, "death_year": 1667, "description": "German physician and alchemist, noted for pancreatic duct discovery.", "note": "The mention of 'Wolphio' is likely a misspelling or variant of Wirsung.  James might be referencing a psychologist discussing historical concepts, potentially linking to earlier medical authors like Wirsung.", "wikidata_id": "Q1086119", "wikidata_description": "German pharmacist and translator"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "positivistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "positivistic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "positivistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["positivistic"], "contexts": ["point of view adopted", "psychology will remain positivistic"]}, {"entity_id": "positive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "positive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["positive"], "contexts": ["type of error in time estimation"]}, {"entity_id": "positive", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "positive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["positive"], "contexts": ["one electric state"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "positivistic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "positive", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "positive", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "positive", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2805, "stable_key": "clu_298170393256b99e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "positivism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'positivistic' and 'positive' in the context of James's 'Principles of Psychology' and Somerville's 'Connexion of the Physical Sciences' refers to the philosophical stance of positivism, especially its emphasis on empirical evidence and scientific observation.", "wikidata_id": "Q131015", "wikidata_description": "philosophy of science based on the view that information derived from scientific observation is the exclusive source of all authoritative knowledge"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Empirical law", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4194, "target_cluster_name": "empirical law", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Positivism emphasizes empirical observation and verification as the basis for knowledge, making empirical laws a core concept within its framework.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Positivism emphasizes empirical observation and verification as the basis for knowledge, making empirical laws a core concept within its framework."}, {"found_name": "Experience", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 105, "target_cluster_name": "experience", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Positivism is fundamentally an empiricist philosophy, grounding knowledge in sensory experience and observation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Positivism is fundamentally an empiricist philosophy, grounding knowledge in sensory experience and observation."}, {"found_name": "General laws", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3235, "target_cluster_name": "Laws in general", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A key aim of positivism is to discover general laws that govern phenomena, based on empirical evidence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A key aim of positivism is to discover general laws that govern phenomena, based on empirical evidence."}, {"found_name": "Causality", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 977, "target_cluster_name": "cause", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Positivism seeks to understand the causal relationships between phenomena, often through the identification of laws.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Positivism seeks to understand the causal relationships between phenomena, often through the identification of laws."}]}, {"canonical_name": "violin", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "violin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "violin", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 3, "variants": ["violin"], "contexts": ["musical instrument", "musical instrument held in the hand"]}, {"entity_id": "violin", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "violin", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["violin"], "contexts": ["instrument used to excite vibrations", "stringed instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "violin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "violin", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 2806, "stable_key": "clu_1e4fde42cafa92b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "violin", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Le Violon d'Ingres (French for Ingres' Violin) is a black-and-white photograph created by American visual artist Man Ray in 1924. It is one of his best-known photographs and of surrealist photography. The picture was first published in the Surrealist magazine Littérature in June 1924. It shows model Kiki de Montparnasse from the back, nude to below her waist, with two f-holes painted on to draw similarities between the shape of her body and a violin. The photograph takes its name from a popular French expression, le violon d'Ingres, which means a hobby, in reference to the fact that the French neoclassical painter Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres used to play the violin as a pastime when he was not painting. Man Ray admired Ingres's work and he drew inspiration from his painting The Valpinçon Bather (1808) for this photograph.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Violon_d'Ingres"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "piano-forte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2591, "target_cluster_name": "piano-forte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are musical instruments, and historical texts might discuss them in relation to each other within the context of music or instrument making.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are musical instruments, and historical texts might discuss them in relation to each other within the context of music or instrument making.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Musical instruments", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2894, "target_cluster_name": "musical instruments", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The violin is a type of musical instrument.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The violin is a type of musical instrument."}, {"found_name": "vibrating string", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1465, "target_cluster_name": "vibrating string", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The sound of a violin is produced by vibrating strings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The sound of a violin is produced by vibrating strings."}]}, {"canonical_name": "animal kingdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "animal_kingdom", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "animal kingdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["animal kingdom"], "contexts": ["all animal life", "shared with man, general animal life"]}, {"entity_id": "animal_kingdom", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "animal kingdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["animal kingdom"], "contexts": ["all animal life forms", "realm of animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "animal kingdom", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "animal kingdom", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2807, "stable_key": "clu_71bc24e4b616fed5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "animal kingdom", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the collec