kers for chronological systems and calendars.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "past", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2571, "target_cluster_name": "past", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, directly relating to the concept of the past.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, directly relating to the concept of the past.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Time and Space", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2573, "target_cluster_name": "Time and Space", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, directly relating to the concept of time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chronology is the science of arranging events in the order of their occurrence in time, directly relating to the concept of time.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "janvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2772, "target_cluster_name": "janvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "January is a unit of time within the system of chronology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: January is a unit of time within the system of chronology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Instant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3560, "target_cluster_name": "instant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An instant is a specific point in time, a basic element of chronology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "An instant is a specific point in time, a basic element of chronology."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Carlini", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "carlini", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carlini", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carlini"], "contexts": ["measured from Mount Columbier", "measured Mount Columbier in 1821"]}], "edges": [], "id": 2826, "stable_key": "clu_44ceaf1e45956fec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Francesco Carlini", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1783, "death_year": 1862, "description": "Italian astronomer and geodeticist, measured Mount Columbier.", "note": "The reference to 'Carletti' and 'mordicin' is likely a separate, but possibly related, historical figure or term in the context of early exploration and botany. However, 'Carlini' is strongly associated with scientific measurement and Mount Columbier."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Serpentarius", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpentarius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Serpentarius", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Serpentarius"], "contexts": ["constellation with a star", "constellation with a starless region"]}, {"entity_id": "π_serpentarii", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "π Serpentarii", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["π Serpentarii"], "contexts": ["double star system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Serpentarius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "π Serpentarii", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 2827, "stable_key": "clu_71d3c061273f65a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Serpens", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The mention of 'π Serpentarii' confirms the astronomical context.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ophiuchus () is a large constellation straddling the celestial equator. Its name comes from the Ancient Greek ὀφιοῦχος (ophioûkhos), meaning \"serpent-bearer\", and it is commonly represented as a man grasping a snake. The serpent is represented by the constellation Serpens. Ophiuchus was one of the 48 constellations listed by the 2nd-century astronomer Ptolemy, and it remains one of the 88 modern constellations. An old alternative name for the constellation was Serpentarius. Ophiuchus lies between Aquila, Serpens, Scorpius, Sagittarius, and Hercules, northwest of the center of the Milky Way.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophiuchus"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_tension", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["electric tension"], "contexts": ["electrical potential", "electrical potential in atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_tension", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric tension"], "contexts": ["electrical potential difference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric tension", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric tension", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2828, "stable_key": "clu_0a705d4c218b986c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electric potential difference", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Atmospheric electricity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3166, "target_cluster_name": "atmospheric electricity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atmospheric electricity is a phenomenon directly related to electric potential difference, as it involves the buildup and discharge of electrical charge in the atmosphere, which is a manifestation of potential difference.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Atmospheric electricity is a phenomenon directly related to electric potential difference, as it involves the buildup and discharge of electrical charge in the atmosphere, which is a manifestation of potential difference."}, {"found_name": "Electric current", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 726, "target_cluster_name": "electric currents", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric current is the flow of electric charge, which is driven by electric potential difference. A potential difference is necessary for a current to flow.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electric current is the flow of electric charge, which is driven by electric potential difference. A potential difference is necessary for a current to flow."}, {"found_name": "Electrical phenomena", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3624, "target_cluster_name": "electrical phenomena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electric potential difference is a fundamental concept underlying many electrical phenomena, such as discharges, currents, and fields.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electric potential difference is a fundamental concept underlying many electrical phenomena, such as discharges, currents, and fields."}, {"found_name": "Galvanism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3585, "target_cluster_name": "galvanism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galvanism, the study of electricity produced by chemical reactions, inherently involves electric potential difference as the driving force for the observed electrical effects.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galvanism, the study of electricity produced by chemical reactions, inherently involves electric potential difference as the driving force for the observed electrical effects."}, {"found_name": "Induction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 280, "target_cluster_name": "Induction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Electromagnetic induction, the process of generating an electromotive force (which is a form of electric potential difference) in a conductor by changing the magnetic field around it, is a key related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Electromagnetic induction, the process of generating an electromotive force (which is a form of electric potential difference) in a conductor by changing the magnetic field around it, is a key related concept."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "scythia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Scythia"], "contexts": ["ancient region described as earthquake-free", "ancient geographical region"]}, {"entity_id": "scythia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scythia"], "contexts": ["region where jacinthos were found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Scythia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Scythia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2829, "stable_key": "clu_4ba8bf6fe87c1fe8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scythia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ukraine", "note": "Ancient region, modern country is an approximation.", "wikidata_id": "Q10664835", "wikidata_description": "genus", "wikipedia_extract": "Scythia (UK: , also US: ) or Scythica (UK: , also US: ) was a geographic region defined in the ancient Graeco-Roman world that encompassed the Pontic steppe. It was inhabited by Scythians, an ancient Eastern Iranian equestrian nomadic people. The names Scythia and Scythica are themselves Latinisations of the Ancient Greek names Skuthia (Σκυθία) and Skuthikē (Σκυθική), which were themselves derived from the ancient Greek names for the Scythians, Skuthēs (Σκύθης) and Skuthoi (Σκύθοι), derived from the Scythian endonym *Skuδa. The territory of the Scythian kingdom of the Pontic steppe extended from the Don river in the east to the Danube river in the west, and covered the territory of the treeless steppe immediately north of the Black Sea's coastline, which was inhabited by nomadic pastoralists, as well as the fertile black-earth forest-steppe area to the north of the treeless steppe, which was inhabited by an agricultural population. The northern border of this Scythian kingdom were the deciduous woodlands, while several rivers, including Don and Dnipro, flowed southwards across this region and emptied into the Black Sea. Between the 9th and 5th centuries BC, the climate in the steppes was cool and dry, which was a catalyst for the emergence of equestrian nomadic pastoralism in the northern Pontic region.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scythia"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 231, "target_cluster_name": "Asie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Scythia was a region within the broader geographical context of Asia, particularly in ancient Graeco-Roman understanding.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Scythia was a region within the broader geographical context of Asia, particularly in ancient Graeco-Roman understanding."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Scythia encompassed the Pontic steppe, which borders and is often considered part of Eastern Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Scythia encompassed the Pontic steppe, which borders and is often considered part of Eastern Europe."}]}, {"canonical_name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "corals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["corals"], "contexts": ["marine invertebrate animals", "marine invertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "corals", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["corals"], "contexts": ["marine invertebrates forming reefs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "corals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "corals", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2830, "stable_key": "clu_aec819706fa4c6aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coral", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Corals are marine invertebrates, and the context of reef formation is characteristic.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Crinoidea", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4381, "target_cluster_name": "Crinoidea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Crinoidea (sea lilies and feather stars) are echinoderms, a phylum distinct from corals (Cnidaria), but both are marine invertebrates and were sometimes grouped t