bits and periods", "law relating planetary orbital periods and distances"]}, {"entity_id": "kepler’s_laws", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Kepler’s laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kepler’s laws"], "contexts": ["laws describing planetary motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Kepler’s law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Kepler’s laws", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 2895, "stable_key": "clu_f140219e8f3d1ced", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kepler's laws", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q83219", "wikidata_description": "सूर्य के चारों ओर ग्रहों की गति का वर्णन करने वाले वैज्ञानिक नियम", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kepler%27s%20laws%20of%20planetary%20motion", "wikipedia_extract": "In astronomy, Kepler's laws of planetary motion give good approximations for the orbits of planets around the Sun. They were published by Johannes Kepler from 1608-1621 in three works Astronomia nova, Harmonice Mundi and Epitome Astronomiae Copernicanae. The laws were based on Kepler's concept of solar fibrils adapted to the accurate astronomical data of Tycho Brahe. These laws replaced the circular orbits and epicycles of Copernicus's heliostatic model of the planets with a heliocentric model that described elliptical orbits with planetary velocities that vary accordingly. The three laws state that: The orbit of a planet is an ellipse with the Sun at one of the two foci. A line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kepler's laws are fundamental to the field of astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kepler's laws are fundamental to the field of astronomy."}, {"found_name": "Elliptical orbit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2341, "target_cluster_name": "elliptical orbit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kepler's first law states that planets move in elliptical orbits.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kepler's first law states that planets move in elliptical orbits."}, {"found_name": "Heliocentric model", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3647, "target_cluster_name": "revolution round the sun", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kepler's laws describe planetary motion within a heliocentric framework.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kepler's laws describe planetary motion within a heliocentric framework."}, {"found_name": "Angular velocity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3064, "target_cluster_name": "angular motion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kepler's second law relates to the rate of change of the angle swept out by the radius vector.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Kepler's second law relates to the rate of change of the angle swept out by the radius vector."}, {"found_name": "Gravity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 77, "target_cluster_name": "gravity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Newton's law of universal gravitation explains the physical basis for Kepler's laws.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Newton's law of universal gravitation explains the physical basis for Kepler's laws."}]}, {"canonical_name": "planetary motions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count":