ecific, often negative, type of opinion or judgment about oneself or others.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Conceit can be seen as a specific, often negative, type of opinion or judgment about oneself or others."}, {"found_name": "Conception", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 282, "target_cluster_name": "conception", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conception is the initial formation of an idea or opinion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Conception is the initial formation of an idea or opinion."}, {"found_name": "Association of Ideas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1992, "target_cluster_name": "association of ideas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Doctrine of Association, and the concept of associated ideas, directly relates to how opinions are formed and linked in the mind.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Doctrine of Association, and the concept of associated ideas, directly relates to how opinions are formed and linked in the mind."}, {"found_name": "Basilisk", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "conformation: before the birth can bear the name of the Parent, or be so much as properly called an _Embryon_. CHAPTER VII Of the Basilisk Many Opinions are passant concerning the Basilisk or little K", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "serpent, also called Cockatrice"}]}, {"canonical_name": "delusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "delusions", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "delusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["delusions"], "contexts": ["false beliefs or perceptions"]}, {"entity_id": "delusions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "delusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["delusions"], "contexts": ["false beliefs held despite evidence", "fixed false beliefs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "delusions", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "delusions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2925, "stable_key": "clu_41aa7fbda4f61f69", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Delusion", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Delusion"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Mania", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1323, "target_cluster_name": "Madness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mania, as understood historically, often involved disordered thinking and perception, which could include or be characterized by delusions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Mania, as understood historically, often involved disordered thinking and perception, which could include or be characterized by delusions."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Herod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "herod", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Herod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herod"], "contexts": ["Jewish king"]}, {"entity_id": "herod", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Herod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Herod"], "contexts": ["ancient historian", "ancient historian, Herodotus"]}, {"entity_id": "herod_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Herod.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herod."], "contexts": ["ancient Greek historian"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Herod", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herod", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Herod", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herod.", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 2926, "stable_key": "clu_e7ef52620ade1db4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Herod the Great", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -73, "death_year": -4, "description": "King of Judea, client king of Rome.", "note": "The context of 'ancient Greek historian' for Herod is likely a misattribution or confusion with other historical figures.", "wikidata_id": "Q51672", "wikidata_description": "king of Judea from 37/6 BCE to 4/1 BCE"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Augustus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2668, "target_cluster_name": "Augustus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Herod the Great was a client king of Rome, appointed by Julius Caesar and later confirmed by Augustus. His reign and interactions with Rome were heavily influenced by the political landscape under Augustus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Herod the Great was a client king of Rome, appointed by Julius Caesar and later confirmed by Augustus. His reign and interactions with Rome were heavily influenced by the political landscape under Augustus."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Justinian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "justinian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Justinian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Justinian"], "contexts": ["Emperor, took in with Hippocrates"]}, {"entity_id": "justinian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Justinian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Justinian"], "contexts": ["ruled during comet appearance in 539", "ancient source on Aetna"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Justinian", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Justinian", "similarity": 0.695}], "id": 2927, "stable_key": "clu_dc0738dec6ff854b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Justinian I", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 482, "death_year": 565, "description": "Byzantine emperor known for legal reforms and building projects.", "wikidata_id": "Q41866", "wikidata_description": "Eastern Roman Emperor who ruled from 527 to 565 (482-565)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Constantine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3293, "target_cluster_name": "Constantino", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Constantine I (the Great) was a significant Roman Emperor who preceded Justinian I and established Constantinople as the new capital, influencing the Eastern Roman Empire that Justinian would later rule.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Constantine I (the Great) was a significant Roman Emperor who preceded Justinian I and established Constantinople as the new capital, influencing the Eastern Roman Empire that Justinian would later rule."}, {"found_name": "Augustus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2668, "target_cluster_name": "Augustus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Augustus was the first Roman Emperor, establishing the Principate. Justinian I, ruling centuries later, sought to restore the Roman Empire to its former glory, echoing Augustus's foundational role.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Augustus was the first Roman Emperor, establishing the Principate. Justinian I, ruling centuries later, sought to restore the Roman Empire to its former glory, echoing Augustus's foundational role."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Polybius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "polybius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Polybius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Polybius"], "contexts": ["Greek historian"]}, {"entity_id": "polybius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Polybius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Polybius"], "contexts": ["ancient historian", "ancient writer comparing peninsulas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Polybius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Polybius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2928, "stable_key": "clu_157ad3f6a9447d84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polybius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek historian and writer on Roman history.", "wikidata_id": "Q1990508", "wikidata_description": "genus of crabs", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polybius%20%28crab%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Polybius (; Ancient Greek: Πολύβιος, Polýbios; c. 200 – c. 118 BC) was an ancient Greek historian of the middle Hellenistic period. He is noted for his Histories, a universal history documenting the rise of Rome in the Mediterranean in the third and second centuries BC. It covers the period 264–146, recording in detail events in Italy, Iberia, Greece, Macedonia, Syria, Egypt and Africa, and documents the Punic Wars and Macedonian Wars among many others. Polybius's Histories is important not only for being the only Hellenistic historical work to survive in any substantial form, but also for its analysis of constitutional change and the mixed constitution. Polybius's discussion of the separation of powers in government, of checks and balances to limit power, and his introduction of \"the people\", all influenced Montesquieu's The Spirit of the Laws, John Locke's Two Treatises of Government, and the framers of the United States Constitution. Polybius was a close friend and mentor to the Roman politician and general Scipio Aemilianus (also called Scipio Africanus the Younger), and had a lasting influence on his decision-making and life."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Diodorus Siculus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2667, "target_cluster_name": "Diodorus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were ancient Greek historians who wrote universal histories covering significant periods of the ancient world, with Diodorus Siculus also documenting the rise of Rome.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both were ancient Greek historians who wrote universal histories covering significant periods of the ancient world, with Diodorus Siculus also documenting the rise of Rome."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Crato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "cratevas", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cratevas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cratevas"], "contexts": ["predecessor to Dioscorides"]}, {"entity_id": "crato", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Crato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Crato", "Crat."], "contexts": ["early modern author", "cited for medical consultations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cratevas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Crato", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 2929, "stable_key": "clu_4a60a37647efd39f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cratevas", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Greek physician and botanist, author of a herbal.", "note": "The text mentions 'predecessor to Dioscorides' and 'early modern author'. Cratevas was an ancient Greek physician and botanist, contemporary with or slightly before Dioscorides. The 'early modern author' context might refer to the re-publication or discussion of his work, or a confusion with another author named Crato. However, Cratevas is the most likely historical figure given the other clues.", "wikidata_id": "Q743993", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek physician and rhizotomist"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dioscorides was a Greek physician and botanist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a foundational text for pharmacology and botany for centuries. Cratevas was also a Greek physician and botanist known for his work on plants, likely influencing or being influenced by contemporaries like Dioscorides.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Dioscorides was a Greek physician and botanist whose work 'De Materia Medica' was a foundational text for pharmacology and botany for centuries. Cratevas was also a Greek physician and botanist known for his work on plants, likely influencing or being influenced by contemporaries like Dioscorides."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine' and his humoral theory and ethical principles were highly influential. Cratevas was a physician in ancient Greece, and as such, would have been part of the medical tradition that Hippocrates established and shaped.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates is considered the 'Father of Medicine' and his humoral theory and ethical principles were highly influential. Cratevas was a physician in ancient Greece, and as such, would have been part of the medical tradition that Hippocrates established and shaped."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Vitex", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "vitex", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Vitex", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vitex"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "vitex", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Vitex", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Vitex"], "contexts": ["plant genus, subject of correction", "genus of plants used in Hindu medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Vitex", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Vitex", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2930, "stable_key": "clu_692fa6ca5c353797", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chaste tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Vitex agnus-castus Linnaeus", "family": "Verbenaceae", "note": "The genus Vitex encompasses many species, but in early modern medicinal contexts, Vitex agnus-castus was the most prominent, known for its contraceptive properties and association with chastity.", "wikidata_id": "Q29533", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "agnus castus", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3274, "target_cluster_name": "agnus castus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A common historical synonym for Chaste tree, often used in botanical and medicinal texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "A common historical synonym for Chaste tree, often used in botanical and medicinal texts."}, {"found_name": "Agnocasto", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2879, "target_cluster_name": "Agnocasto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A direct translation/cognate of 'Chaste tree' in Portuguese, indicating the same plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "A direct translation/cognate of 'Chaste tree' in Portuguese, indicating the same plant."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thomas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "thomas", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Thomas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomas"], "contexts": ["biblical figure"]}, {"entity_id": "thomas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Thomas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Thomas"], "contexts": ["author on Tortura oris", "apothecary"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thomas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Thomas", "similarity": 0.791}], "id": 2931, "stable_key": "clu_2c9d7f0f50073f08", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thomas Aquinas", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1225, "death_year": 1274, "description": "Dominican friar, philosopher, theologian, and Doctor of the Church.", "note": "The context of 'author on Tortura oris' strongly suggests Thomas Aquinas, whose Summa Theologica was a foundational text, though direct reference to this specific procedure is less common.", "wikidata_id": "Q9438", "wikidata_description": "Italian Dominican friar, philosopher, Catholic priest, and Doctor of the Church (1225–1274)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Alberto Magno", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2461, "target_cluster_name": "Alberto Magno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Albertus Magnus was the teacher of Thomas Aquinas, another highly influential philosopher and theologian. Their intellectual relationship is central to the history of Scholasticism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Albertus Magnus was the teacher of Thomas Aquinas, another highly influential philosopher and theologian. Their intellectual relationship is central to the history of Scholasticism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Albertus Magnus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2461, "target_cluster_name": "Alberto Magno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Albertus Magnus was a prominent medieval philosopher and theologian who was a major influence on Thomas Aquinas's thought. Aquinas was a student of Albertus Magnus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Albertus Magnus was a prominent medieval philosopher and theologian who was a major influence on Thomas Aquinas's thought. Aquinas was a student of Albertus Magnus."}, {"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 62, "target_cluster_name": "Aristotle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aristotle's philosophy was a foundational element of Thomas Aquinas's theological and philosophical system. Aquinas extensively studied and commented on Aristotle's works.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Aristotle's philosophy was a foundational element of Thomas Aquinas's theological and philosophical system. Aquinas extensively studied and commented on Aristotle's works."}, {"found_name": "Augustine of Hippo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1967, "target_cluster_name": "Austin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Augustine of Hippo was a highly influential Church Father whose theological writings were a primary source and inspiration for Thomas Aquinas's own theological work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Augustine of Hippo was a highly influential Church Father whose theological writings were a primary source and inspiration for Thomas Aquinas's own theological work."}, {"found_name": "Avicenna", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1492, "target_cluster_name": "Avicena", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Avicenna's philosophical and medical works were known and engaged with by Thomas Aquinas, particularly in the areas of logic and natural philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Avicenna's philosophical and medical works were known and engaged with by Thomas Aquinas, particularly in the areas of logic and natural philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Galen's medical theories, though predating Aquinas, were part of the classical medical tradition that Aquinas, like other medieval scholars, would have been aware of and sometimes commented upon in relation to natural philosophy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Galen's medical theories, though predating Aquinas, were part of the classical medical tradition that Aquinas, like other medieval scholars, would have been aware of and sometimes commented upon in relation to natural philosophy."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hippocrates's medical ethics and theories, as part of the classical medical tradition, were known to scholars like Aquinas, who engaged with ancient authorities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hippocrates's medical ethics and theories, as part of the classical medical tradition, were known to scholars like Aquinas, who engaged with ancient authorities."}, {"found_name": "Moses", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 673, "target_cluster_name": "Musa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Moses, as the lawgiver in the Old Testament, is a significant figure in the theological framework that Thomas Aquinas developed, particularly concerning divine law and natural law.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Moses, as the lawgiver in the Old Testament, is a significant figure in the theological framework that Thomas Aquinas developed, particularly concerning divine law and natural law."}]}, {"canonical_name": "pétrifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "petrifications", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "petrifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["petrifications"], "contexts": ["organic matter turned to stone"]}, {"entity_id": "pétrifications", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pétrifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["pétrifications"], "contexts": ["fossilized remains", "fossils"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "petrifications", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "pétrifications", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2932, "stable_key": "clu_b958a2be38fa2553", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fossils", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern texts often used 'pétrifications' to refer to organic matter that had been mineralized or fossilized, including things like fossilized bones and shells.", "wikidata_id": "Q126956840", "wikidata_description": "fictional disease from Lies of P"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Amber", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 820, "target_cluster_name": "amber", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amber is a fossilized tree resin, and thus a type of fossilized organic matter.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Amber is a fossilized tree resin, and thus a type of fossilized organic matter."}, {"found_name": "Bitumen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 160, "target_cluster_name": "bitume", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bitumen is a naturally occurring viscous deposit of crude oil, often found in fossiliferous rock formations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bitumen is a naturally occurring viscous deposit of crude oil, often found in fossiliferous rock formations."}, {"found_name": "Coal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 857, "target_cluster_name": "Charcoal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Coal is a combustible black or brownish-black sedimentary rock, formed as rock strata called coal seams. Coal is a fossil fuel.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Coal is a combustible black or brownish-black sedimentary rock, formed as rock strata called coal seams. Coal is a fossil fuel."}, {"found_name": "Fossiliferous strata", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 913, "target_cluster_name": "fossiliferous strata", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fossiliferous strata are rock layers that contain fossils, directly relating to the study and origin of fossils.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fossiliferous strata are rock layers that contain fossils, directly relating to the study and origin of fossils."}, {"found_name": "Organic debris", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4147, "target_cluster_name": "débris organiques", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Organic debris is the precursor material from which many fossils are formed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Organic debris is the precursor material from which many fossils are formed."}, {"found_name": "Sediment", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2732, "target_cluster_name": "sediment", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sediments are materials that form fossils through the process of fossilization, often found in sedimentary rocks where fossils are preserved.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sediments are materials that form fossils through the process of fossilization, often found in sedimentary rocks where fossils are preserved."}, {"found_name": "Bones", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 347, "target_cluster_name": "Bones", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bones are a common component of fossils.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bones are a common component of fossils."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cork", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "globe_of_cork", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Globe of Cork", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Globe of Cork"], "contexts": ["floating device for needle experiment"]}, {"entity_id": "cork", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cork", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Cork"], "contexts": ["material placed in vessel to prevent drowning", "stopper for vessels"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Globe of Cork", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Cork", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2933, "stable_key": "clu_9e4142ee786f87e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cork", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Quercus suber Linnaeus", "family": "Fagaceae", "note": "While 'cork' can refer to the material, in the context of experiments involving needles and preventing drowning, it specifically refers to the buoyant material derived from the cork oak tree.", "wikidata_id": "Q36647", "wikidata_description": "city in County Cork, Munster Province, Ireland"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bellows", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3909, "target_cluster_name": "Bellows", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bellows are often used in conjunction with furnaces and hearths to provide air for combustion, and cork could be used in the construction or sealing of such devices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bellows are often used in conjunction with furnaces and hearths to provide air for combustion, and cork could be used in the construction or sealing of such devices."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Elba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "elba", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Elba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elba"], "contexts": ["modern name for Ilua"]}, {"entity_id": "elba", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Elba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Elba"], "contexts": ["island where white earth is found", "place associated with refined bolo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elba", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Elba", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2934, "stable_key": "clu_dadc57bc4494980f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Island in Italy and a river in Europe.", "note": "Contexts differentiate between the island and the river.", "wikipedia_extract": "Elba (Italian: isola d'Elba, pronounced [ˈiːzola ˈdelba]; Latin: Ilva) is a Mediterranean island in Tuscany, Italy, 10 km (6.2 mi) from the coastal town of Piombino on the Italian mainland, and the largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago. It is also part of the Arcipelago Toscano National Park, and the third largest island in Italy, after Sicily and Sardinia. It is located in the Tyrrhenian Sea about 50 km (30 mi) east of the French island of Corsica. The island is part of the province of Livorno and is divided into seven municipalities, with a total population of about 30,000 inhabitants which increases considerably during the summer. The municipalities are Portoferraio (which is also the island's principal town), Campo nell'Elba, Capoliveri, Marciana, Marciana Marina, Porto Azzurro, and Rio. It is famous for being the site of Napoleon's first exile, from 1814 to 1815."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dicaearchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "nearchus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nearchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nearchus"], "contexts": ["Nearchus the Admiral of Alexander"]}, {"entity_id": "dicaearchus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dicaearchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dicaearchus"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek geographer", "mentioned 'parallel of the diaphragm'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nearchus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dicaearchus", "similarity": 0.87}], "id": 2935, "stable_key": "clu_7c0d6699e816e44f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nearchus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek commander and geographer", "note": "The Portuguese 'Nearcho' is a direct transliteration of the ancient Greek commander Nearchus, mentioned in historical contexts. 'Neighbors' is a clear mistranscription or misinterpretation within the English text of the same entity.", "wikidata_id": "Q1216034", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek philosopher", "wikipedia_extract": "Nearchus or Nearchos (Greek: Νέαρχος; c. 360 – 300 BC) was one of the Greek officers, a navarch, in the army of Alexander the Great. He is known for his celebrated expeditionary voyage starting from the Indus River, through the Persian Gulf and ending at the mouth of the Tigris River following the Indian campaign of Alexander the Great, in 326–324 BC. A native of Lato in Crete and son of Androtimus, his family settled at Amphipolis in Macedonia at some point during Philip II's reign (we must assume after Philip took the city in 357 BC), at which point Nearchus was probably a young boy. He was almost certainly older than Alexander, as were Ptolemy, Erigyius, and the others of the ‘boyhood friends’; so depending on when Androtimus came to Macedonia Nearchus was quite possibly born in Crete. Nearchus, along with Ptolemy, Erigyius and Laomedon, and Harpalus, was one of Alexander's ‘mentors’ – and he was exiled by Philip as a result of the Pixodarus affair (A 3.6.5; P 10.3). It is not known where the exiles went, but they were recalled only after Philip's death, on Alexander's accession."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Balsamis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "balsam", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Balsam", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Balsam"], "contexts": ["healing substance for wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "balsamis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Balsamis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Balsamis", "Balsamis Chymicisingenere", "Balsamo"], "contexts": ["balsams, medicinal preparations", "chemical preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Balsam", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Balsamis", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 2936, "stable_key": "clu_812b024e82ac4dea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q428750", "wikidata_description": "species of plant in the family Cucurbitaceae"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "aromas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1044, "target_cluster_name": "aromi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Balsams are often used for their aromatic properties and are derived from plants known for their scents.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Balsams are often used for their aromatic properties and are derived from plants known for their scents."}, {"found_name": "oils", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 203, "target_cluster_name": "olio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Oils can be extracted from balsams, and some balsams are oily in nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Oils can be extracted from balsams, and some balsams are oily in nature."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rush", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "rushes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rushes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rushes"], "contexts": ["plant used for charcoal in gunpowder"]}, {"entity_id": "rush", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Rush", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rush", "Rushes"], "contexts": ["plant used for size comparison", "plant used in proverb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rushes", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Rush", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 2937, "stable_key": "clu_cf7e86a664f0d6cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rush", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Juncus", "family": "Juncaceae", "note": "Refers to plants of the genus Juncus, commonly known as rushes. The contexts provided align with their use as materials and components.", "wikipedia_extract": "Juncus is a genus of monocotyledonous flowering plants, commonly known as rushes. It is the largest genus in the family Juncaceae, containing around 340 species. Rushes of the genus Juncus are herbaceous plants that superficially resemble grasses or sedges. They have historically received little attention from botanists; in his 1819 monograph, James Ebenezer Bicheno described the genus as \"obscure and uninviting\". The form of the flower differentiates rushes from grasses or sedges. The flowers of Juncus comprise five whorls of floral parts: three sepals, three petals (or, taken together, six tepals), two to six stamens (in two whorls) and a stigma with three lobes."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Docks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2358, "target_cluster_name": "Docks", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Dock and Rush are both common wetland plants, often found in similar habitats and sometimes confused or grouped together in historical texts discussing marsh or waterside vegetation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Dock and Rush are both common wetland plants, often found in similar habitats and sometimes confused or grouped together in historical texts discussing marsh or waterside vegetation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Grass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 465, "target_cluster_name": "Grass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rushes superficially resemble grasses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rushes superficially resemble grasses."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Corallines", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "coralline", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Coralline", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coralline"], "contexts": ["plant that can indurate underwater"]}, {"entity_id": "corallines", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Corallines", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Corallines"], "contexts": ["marine organisms", "marine organisms, often plant-like"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Coralline", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Corallines", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 2938, "stable_key": "clu_1bccabb9923f528b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coralline", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Refers to a group of marine algae, not a single species. The term 'coralline' in early modern texts could encompass various red algae that form calcareous deposits.", "wikidata_id": "Q1547337", "wikidata_description": "collective term for those islands of the Seychelles that are not on the shallow Seychelles Bank"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Algae", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 957, "target_cluster_name": "algæ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Corallines are a type of red algae, specifically belonging to the order Corallinales. Historical texts often grouped them together or discussed them in close proximity due to their marine habitat and plant-like appearance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Corallines are a type of red algae, specifically belonging to the order Corallinales. Historical texts often grouped them together or discussed them in close proximity due to their marine habitat and plant-like appearance."}, {"found_name": "Marine flora", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1084, "target_cluster_name": "Marine plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Corallines are marine organisms, and 'Marine flora' is a broad category encompassing all plant and plant-like life in the sea. Corallines would be a specific component of marine flora.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Corallines are marine organisms, and 'Marine flora' is a broad category encompassing all plant and plant-like life in the sea. Corallines would be a specific component of marine flora."}, {"found_name": "Seaweeds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2319, "target_cluster_name": "Seaweeds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Corallines are a type of marine algae, and 'seaweeds' is a common term for macroscopic marine algae. While not all seaweeds are corallines, they share the same general habitat and biological classification.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Corallines are a type of marine algae, and 'seaweeds' is a common term for macroscopic marine algae. While not all seaweeds are corallines, they share the same general habitat and biological classification."}, {"found_name": "Fossil plants", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3671, "target_cluster_name": "Fossil plants", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fossilized corallines are frequently found and studied, making them relevant to the category of 'Fossil plants'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Fossilized corallines are frequently found and studied, making them relevant to the category of 'Fossil plants'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Milius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "milius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Milius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Milius"], "contexts": ["accounted carbuncle flame a vulgar error"]}, {"entity_id": "milius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Milius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Milius"], "contexts": ["cited for case in Bafilic. Chym.", "author of Basilic. Chymic."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Milius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Milius", "similarity": 0.603}], "id": 2939, "stable_key": "clu_6132299a28d5499a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lorenzo Bellini", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1643, "death_year": 1704, "description": "Italian physician and anatomist, discussed carbuncle as a vulgar error.", "note": "The identification of 'Milius' as Lorenzo Bellini is based on the context of discussing carbuncles and the period. Further confirmation from the specific cited works is needed.", "wikidata_id": "Q128715075", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aristotle", "link_type": "conceptual_