arrot (Daucus carota) is a well-known species within the Apiaceae family, and the family is sometimes referred to as the carrot family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Parsnip", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2356, "target_cluster_name": "Parsnip", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are root vegetables belonging to the Apiaceae family, sharing some morphological similarities and culinary uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are root vegetables belonging to the Apiaceae family, sharing some morphological similarities and culinary uses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Vegetables", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3853, "target_cluster_name": "Vegetables", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carrots are a type of vegetable.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carrots are a type of vegetable."}, {"found_name": "Root", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 536, "target_cluster_name": "Root", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carrots are a root vegetable.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carrots are a root vegetable."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Suffolk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "suffolk", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Suffolk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Suffolk"], "contexts": ["county in England"]}, {"entity_id": "suffolk", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Suffolk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Suffolk", "Susfolk"], "contexts": ["location where Wall-Rue grows", "county in England"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Suffolk", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Suffolk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2941, "stable_key": "clu_f6a10cc378303179-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sussex", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A county in England, mentioned for local flora and fauna observations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sussex (/ˈsʌsɪks/;  from the Old English Sūþseaxe; lit. 'South Saxons'; 'Sussex') is an area of South East England that was historically a kingdom and, later, a county. The current ceremonial counties of East Sussex and West Sussex cover approximately the same area. The two ceremonial counties border Surrey to the north, Kent to the north-east, the English Channel to the south, and Hampshire to the west. Sussex contains the city of Brighton and Hove and its wider city region, part of the South Downs National Park and the national landscape of the High Weald, and Chichester Harbour. Its coastline is 137 miles (220 km) long. The Kingdom of Sussex emerged in the fifth century in the area that had previously been inhabited by the Regni tribe in the Romano-British period."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "England", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 65, "target_cluster_name": "England", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sussex is a historical county within England.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sussex is a historical county within England."}, {"found_name": "East", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 458, "target_cluster_name": "East", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sussex is located in the East of England.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sussex is located in the East of England."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Carthage", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "carthage", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Carthage", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carthage"], "contexts": ["city whose gates were battered"]}, {"entity_id": "carthage", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carthage", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Carthage"], "contexts": ["location of hot springs phenomenon", "location of hot springs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Carthage", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Carthage", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2942, "stable_key": "clu_3f73f7eef3e0b314", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carthage", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Tunisia", "note": "The spelling 'Carthagène' is an archaic French rendering of Carthage.", "wikidata_id": "Q976381", "wikidata_description": "city in and county seat of Panola County, Texas, United States", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carthage%2C%20Texas", "wikipedia_extract": "Carthage (Arabic: قرطاج, ) was an ancient city in Northern Africa, on the eastern side of the Lake of Tunis in what is now Tunisia. Carthage was one of the most important trading hubs of the Ancient Mediterranean and one of the most affluent cities of the classical world. It became the capital city of the civilization of Ancient Carthage and later Roman Carthage. The city developed from a Phoenician colony into the capital of a Punic empire which dominated large parts of the Southwest Mediterranean during the first millennium BC. The legendary Queen Elissa, Alyssa or Dido, originally from Tyre, is regarded as the founder of the city, though her historicity has been questioned. In the myth, Dido asked for land from a local tribe, which told her that she could get as much land as an oxhide could cover."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Méditerranée", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 256, "target_cluster_name": "Méditerranée", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carthage was a major ancient city on the Mediterranean coast of North Africa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Carthage was a major ancient city on the Mediterranean coast of North Africa.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Africa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3, "target_cluster_name": "Africa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carthage was a major city in Northern Africa.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carthage was a major city in Northern Africa."}, {"found_name": "Mediterranean", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 256, "target_cluster_name": "Méditerranée", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carthage was a major trading hub in the Ancient Mediterranean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Carthage was a major trading hub in the Ancient Mediterranean."}]}, {"canonical_name": "wrist", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "wrists", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "wrists", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["wrists"], "contexts": ["part of the arm"]}, {"entity_id": "wrist", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "wrist", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["wrist"], "contexts": ["joint between hand and forearm", "deeper organs, central segments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wrists", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wrist", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 2943, "stable_key