Su∣gar of Roses; Syrup of dryed Roses, and Ho∣ney of Roses; The Cordial Pouder called Diarhodon Abhatis', and Aromatica Rosa∣rum; The D", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "preparation of roses"}, {"found_name": "Ho∣ney of Roses", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Roses; Conserve both moist and dry, which is more usually called Su∣gar of Roses; Syrup of dryed Roses, and Ho∣ney of Roses; The Cordial Pouder called Diarhodon Abhatis', and Aromatica Rosa∣rum; The D", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "preparation of roses"}, {"found_name": "Vine∣gar of Roses", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Roses; Conserve both moist and dry, which is more usually called Su∣gar of Roses; Syrup of dryed Roses, and Ho∣ney of Roses; The Cordial Pouder called Diarhodon Abhatis', and Aromatica Rosa∣rum; The D", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "preparation of roses"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hops", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "hops", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hops", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hops"], "contexts": ["herb used in preparation", "well-known cultivated plant"]}, {"entity_id": "hops", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hops", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hops"], "contexts": ["rural inhabitants producing hops"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hops", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hops", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 2951, "stable_key": "clu_c0921489ff1e6f98", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hops", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Humulus lupulus Linnaeus", "family": "Cannabaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q58393", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dodonaea%20viscosa", "wikipedia_extract": "Hops are the flowers (also called seed cones or strobiles) of the hop plant Humulus lupulus, a member of the Cannabaceae family of flowering plants. They are used primarily as a bittering, flavouring, and stability agent in beer, to which, in addition to bitterness, they impart floral, fruity, or citrus flavours and aromas. Hops are also used for various purposes in other beverages and herbal medicine. The hops plants have separate female and male plants, and only female plants are used for commercial production. The hop plant is a vigorous climbing herbaceous perennial, usually trained to grow up strings in a field called a hopfield, hop garden (in the South of England), or hop yard (in the West Country and United States) when grown commercially. Many different varieties of hops are grown by farmers around the world, with different types used for particular styles of beer."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Wild", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "wild", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wild", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Wild", "wild"], "contexts": ["contrasted with garden asparagus", "type of carrot"]}, {"entity_id": "wild_plants", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "wild plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wild plants"], "contexts": ["grouped into miscellaneous aggregate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wild", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wild plants", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 2952, "stable_key": "clu_8641be1ad5963270", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wild plants", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'wild' is used adjectivally here, referring to plants not cultivated. It's not a specific plant name."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dutch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "dutch", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dutch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dutch"], "contexts": ["fashion of cooking", "people who eat the herb"]}, {"entity_id": "dutch", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Dutch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dutch"], "contexts": ["eager about pigeons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dutch", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Dutch", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2953, "stable_key": "clu_a99bce3a969653c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dutch people", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "People from the Netherlands.", "wikidata_id": "Q200569", "wikidata_description": "citizens or residents of The Netherlands"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Scab", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "scab", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Scab", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Scab"], "contexts": ["skin condition", "skin condition cured by Beech water"]}, {"entity_id": "scab", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Scab", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scab"], "contexts": ["condition treated with goat's milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scab", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Scab", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2954, "stable_key": "clu_0187204b7c0442bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scabies", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Scabies", "wikipedia_extract": "Scabies (, SKAY-beez; also sometimes known as the seven-year itch) is a contagious human skin infestation by the tiny (0.2–0.45 mm) mite Sarcoptes scabiei, variety hominis. The word is from Latin: scabere, lit. 'to scratch'. It is a particular public health problem in crowded settings such as care homes, schools, refugee camps, prisons, and hospitals. The most common symptoms are severe itchiness and a pimple-like rash. Occasionally, tiny burrows may appear on the skin from eggs that are about to hatch.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scabies"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Itch", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 610, "target_cluster_name": "Itch", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The provided description explicitly states that 'Itch' is a historical name for Scabies, and 'Scab' is listed as an alias for Scabies in the candidate list.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The provided description explicitly states that 'Itch' is a historical name for Scabies, and 'Scab' is listed as an alias for Scabies in the candidate list.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "malady", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "malady", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "malady", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Malady", "malady"], "contexts": ["offending condition", "general term for illness"]}, {"entity_id": "malady", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "malady", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["malady"], "contexts": ["illness or sickness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "malady", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "malady", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2955, "stable_key": "clu_738db04083f8e791", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Illness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Illness", "wikidata_id": "Q3052614", "wikidata_description": "specific phobia that involves an irrational fear of contracting a disease"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "males", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3175, "target_cluster_name": "males", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'illness' is given the synonym 'malady', indicating a close semantic relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'illness' is given the synonym 'malady', indicating a close semantic relationship.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "fibres", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fibres", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "fibres", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["fibres", "Fibres"], "contexts": ["part of Crosswort root", "part of the plant's root"]}, {"entity_id": "vegetable_fibers", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vegetable fibers", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vegetable fibers"], "contexts": ["component of coal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "fibres", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vegetable fibers", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 2956, "stable_key": "clu_75915e00471d05b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plant fibers", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "coton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 705, "target_cluster_name": "coton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cotton is a primary example of a plant fiber, and historical texts often discuss them together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cotton is a primary example of a plant fiber, and historical texts often discuss them together.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Poppy Seed", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "poppy_seed", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Poppy Seed", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Poppy Seed"], "contexts": ["seed used for comparison", "added to Melilot preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "poppi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Poppi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "SEED", "count": 1, "variants": ["Poppi"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a recipe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Poppy Seed", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Poppi", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2957, "stable_key": "clu_a7456c60739c8ccd", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Poppy seed is an oilseed obtained from the poppy plant (Papaver somniferum). The tiny, kidney-shaped seeds have been harvested from dried seed pods by various civilizations for thousands of years. It is still widely used in many countries, especially in Central Europe and South Asia, where it is legally grown, used in food products and sold in shops. The seeds are used whole or ground into meal as an ingredient in many foods – especially in pastry and bread – and they are pressed to yield poppyseed oil. The poppy seed is mentioned in ancient medical texts from many civilizations. For instance, the Egyptian Ebers Papyrus, written c. 1550 BCE, lists the poppy seed as a sedative."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Seed", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 211, "target_cluster_name": "Seed", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Poppy Seed is a specific type of seed, derived from the poppy plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Poppy Seed is a specific type of seed, derived from the poppy plant.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "opio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 665, "target_cluster_name": "opio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Poppy seeds are derived from the same plant species (Papaver somniferum) as opium.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Poppy seeds are derived from the same plant species (Papaver somniferum) as opium.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Papauero", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 837, "target_cluster_name": "Papauero", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Poppy Seed is a direct product of the Poppy plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Poppy Seed is a direct product of the Poppy plant.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Genesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "genesis", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Genesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Genesis"], "contexts": ["natal chart or birth", "astrological concept of birth chart"]}, {"entity_id": "genesis", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Genesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Genesis"], "contexts": ["biblical text mentioning animal colour"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Genesis", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Genesis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2958, "stable_key": "clu_f618e96667cde715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genesis", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "Refers to the biblical book or the concept of origin/creation.", "wikidata_id": "Q9184", "wikidata_description": "the first book of the Bible"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Scripture", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2670, "target_cluster_name": "Scripture", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Genesis is the first book of the Hebrew Bible and Christian Old Testament, thus a part of Scripture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Genesis is the first book of the Hebrew Bible and Christian Old Testament, thus a part of Scripture.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Evangelho", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1304, "target_cluster_name": "Evangelho", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gospel of Thomas presents a collection of sayings attributed to Jesus, which can be seen as a distinct, though non-canonical, narrative of early Christian thought, sometimes contrasted with the canonical Gospels which begin with the Nativity narrative (often linked to Genesis in a broader sense of origins).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Gospel of Thomas presents a collection of sayings attributed to Jesus, which can be seen as a distinct, though non-canonical, narrative of early Christian thought, sometimes contrasted with the canonical Gospels which begin with the Nativity narrative (often linked to Genesis in a broader sense of origins).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "dissolve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1327, "target_cluster_name": "dissolve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pralaya, the dissolution of the universe, is a concept that follows creation and precedes a new genesis in Hindu cosmology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pralaya, the dissolution of the universe, is a concept that follows creation and precedes a new genesis in Hindu cosmology.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "thirst", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["Thirst"], "contexts": ["immoderate desire for drink", "sensation of needing to drink"]}, {"entity_id": "thirst", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["thirst"], "contexts": ["physical sensation of needing drink"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thirst", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "thirst", "similarity": 0.998}], "id": 2959, "stable_key": "clu_35e4755904d6233a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thirst", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An uncomfortable sensation indicating a need for fluids.", "wikipedia_extract": "Thirst is the craving for potable fluids, resulting in the basic instinct of animals to drink. It is an essential mechanism involved in fluid balance. It arises from a lack of fluids or an increase in the concentration of certain osmolites, such as sodium. If the water volume of the body falls below a certain threshold or the osmolite concentration becomes too high, structures in the brain detect changes in blood constituents and signal thirst. Continuous dehydration can cause acute and chronic diseases, but is most often associated with renal and neurological disorders. Excessive thirst, called polydipsia, along with excessive urination, known as polyuria, may be an indication of diabetes mellitus or diabetes insipidus."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dimness of Sight", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "dimness_of_sight", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dimness of Sight", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dimness of Sight", "dimness of Sight"], "contexts": ["reduced visual acuity", "helped by willow water"]}, {"entity_id": "dimness_of_sight", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "dimness of sight", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dimness of sight"], "contexts": ["reduced visual acuity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dimness of Sight", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "dimness of sight", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 2960, "stable_key": "clu_f67bd5f40a13b4e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Blurred vision", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Blurred vision", "wikidata_id": "Q4930803", "wikidata_description": "symptom, lack of sharpness of vision resulting in the inability to see fine detail"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bavaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "bavaria", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Bavaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bavaria"], "contexts": ["region of Dukes", "Dukes of Bavaria"]}, {"entity_id": "baviera", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Baviera", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Baviera"], "contexts": ["regi