"Loeb", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Matthias Jakob Schleiden was a botanist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Matthias Jakob Schleiden was a botanist.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "palæontologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2529, "target_cluster_name": "palæontologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology also includes the study of fossil plants, making it related to botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Paleontology also includes the study of fossil plants, making it related to botany.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "entomologists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "entomologists", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "entomologists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["entomologists"], "contexts": ["experts in insect classification", "scientists who study insects"]}, {"entity_id": "entomologist", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "entomologist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["entomologist"], "contexts": ["one who studies insects", "scientist studying insects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "entomologists", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "entomologist", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 2987, "stable_key": "clu_0874db1fa3d21be4", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Entomology, from Ancient Greek ἔντομον (éntomon), meaning \"insect\", and λόγος (lógos), meaning \"study\", is the branch of zoology that focuses on insects. Those who study entomology are known as entomologists. In the past, the term insect was less specific, and historically the definition of entomology would also include the study of animals in other arthropod groups, such as arachnids, myriapods, and crustaceans. The field is also referred to as insectology in American English, while in British English insectology implies the study of the relationships between insects and humans. Over 1.3 million insect species have been described by entomology. Entomology is rooted in nearly all human cultures from prehistoric times, primarily in the context of agriculture (especially biological control and beekeeping)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Wallace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2104, "target_cluster_name": "Wallace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wallace made significant contributions to entomology, particularly in the study of butterflies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wallace made significant contributions to entomology, particularly in the study of butterflies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Westwood", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2372, "target_cluster_name": "Westwood", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Obadiah Westwood was a prominent entomologist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Obadiah Westwood was a prominent entomologist.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alligators", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "alligators", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Alligators", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 2, "variants": ["alligators", "Alligators"], "contexts": ["example of male fighting behavior", "large semi-aquatic reptiles"]}, {"entity_id": "alligators", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "alligators", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["alligators", "alligator"], "contexts": ["alligators similar to those of Florida", "also called cayman"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Alligators", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "alligators", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 2988, "stable_key": "clu_3c6dd71aeb4334f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "alligators", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Alligatoridae", "description": "Large, semi-aquatic reptiles with blunt snouts and smooth legs.", "wikipedia_extract": "An alligator, or colloquially gator, is a large reptile in the genus Alligator of the family Alligatoridae in the order Crocodilia. The two extant species are the American alligator (A. mississippiensis) and the Chinese alligator (A. sinensis). Additionally, several extinct species of alligator are known from fossil remains. Alligators first appeared during the late Eocene epoch about 37 million years ago. The term \"alligator\" is likely an anglicized form of el lagarto, Spanish for \"the lizard\", which early Spanish explorers and settlers in Florida called the alligator. Early English spellings of the name included allagarta and alagarto."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "crocodiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 108, "target_cluster_name": "crocodiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description explicitly states that 'crocodile' is sometimes used loosely to include all extant members of the order Crocodilia, which includes alligators.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The description explicitly states that 'crocodile' is sometimes used loosely to include all extant members of the order Crocodilia, which includes alligators.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "reptiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 122, "target_cluster_name": "reptiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alligators are a specific type of crocodilian, which is an order within the traditional classification of reptiles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alligators are a specific type of crocodilian, which is an order within the traditional classification of reptiles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Saurians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2218, "target_cluster_name": "Saurians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alligators are a type of saurian.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alligators are a type of saurian.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Schiödte", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "schiödte", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Schiödte", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Schiödte"], "contexts": ["naturalist who studied cave insects", "researcher on blind insects"]}, {"entity_id": "schleiden", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Schleiden", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Schleiden"], "contexts": ["scholar regarding mucilaginous deposits", "author on plant cell development"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Schiödte", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Schleiden", "similarity": 0.86}], "id": 2989, "stable_key": "clu_720a6befb0a14adc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hermann Burmeister", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1807, "death_year": 1892, "description": "German entomologist and zoologist, studied cave insects.", "note": "Schiödte is likely a typo or misattribution within the text for Hermann Burmeister, who is known for his work on insects, including those from caves. Schleiden refers to Matthias Schleiden, a botanist, but the context 'mucilaginous deposits' fits Burmeister's broader zoological work more closely, or potentially a different scholar entirely. Given the strong association with 'cave insects' for Schiödte and the presence of Burmeister in similar scientific circles, he is the most probable referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q100703296", "wikidata_description": "street in Halle (Saale), Saxony-Anhalt, Germany"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Mozart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "mozart", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mozart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mozart"], "contexts": ["musician who played pianoforte at three years old", "musical powers of"]}, {"entity_id": "mozart", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Mozart", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mozart"], "contexts": ["example of departed spirit", "composer describing his creative process"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mozart", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Mozart", "similarity": 0.806}], "id": 2990, "stable_key": "clu_f01de8ba92152cf0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1756, "death_year": 1791, "description": "Austrian composer, child prodigy.", "wikidata_id": "Q254", "wikidata_description": "Austrian composer of the Classical period (1756–1791)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Philippi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "philippi", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Philippi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Philippi"], "contexts": ["observations of marine inhabitants in Sicily", "scientist discussing tertiary species"]}, {"entity_id": "philippi", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philippi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Philippi"], "contexts": ["published geography of AEtna plants", "author of geography of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Philippi", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philippi", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2991, "stable_key": "clu_c76e03d34b41476c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rudolf Albert von Koelliker", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1817, "death_year": 1905, "description": "Swiss anatomist and biologist, pioneering cell theory and histology", "note": "The name 'Philippi' appears in contexts mentioning Darwin and Humboldt. While there was a botanist named Philippi who studied plants in South America, the specific texts cited (Darwin's *Origin of Species* and Humboldt's *Kosmos*) do not prominently feature him. Rudolf Albert von Koelliker, a prominent Swiss anatomist and biologist whose work on cells and tissues was influential, is a more likely referent given the broad scientific context, though the direct link to 'Philippi' is tenuous. Further investigation of these specific editions might clarify."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "metamorphic schists", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "metamorphic_schists", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "metamorphic schists", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["metamorphic schists"], "contexts": ["transformed rock type", "rock type forming mountain-summits"]}, {"entity_id": "metamorphic_rocks", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1