"concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "structural changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "modification_of_structure", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "modification of structure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["modification of structure"], "contexts": ["change in physical form"]}, {"entity_id": "structural_changes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "structural changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["structural changes"], "contexts": ["alterations in physical form"]}, {"entity_id": "modification_of_structure", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "modification of structure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["modification of structure"], "contexts": ["change in physical form"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "modification of structure", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "structural changes", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "modification of structure", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "modification of structure", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3001, "stable_key": "clu_177751b4ef2b8f00", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Structural change", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105493634", "wikidata_description": "clinical trial"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "D'Orbigny", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "d’orbigny", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "D’Orbigny", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["D’Orbigny"], "contexts": ["conchologist who defined many species"]}, {"entity_id": "d'orbigny", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "D'Orbigny", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["D'Orbigny"], "contexts": ["described fossils", "described Ammonites and Gryphites"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "D’Orbigny", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "D'Orbigny", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 3002, "stable_key": "clu_ed90aecfadb34181", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alcide d'Orbigny", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1802, "death_year": 1857, "description": "French naturalist, paleontologist, and conchologist, known for extensive fieldwork.", "wikipedia_extract": "Alcide Charles Victor Marie Dessalines d'Orbigny (French pronunciation: [alsid ʃaʁl viktɔʁ maʁi dɛsalin dɔʁbiɲi]; 6 September 1802 – 30 June 1857) was a French naturalist who made major contributions in many areas, including zoology (including malacology), palaeontology, geology, archaeology and anthropology. D'Orbigny was born in Couëron (Loire-Atlantique), the son of a ship's physician and amateur naturalist. The family moved to La Rochelle in 1820, where his interest in natural history was developed while studying the marine fauna and especially the microscopic creatures that he named \"foraminiferans\". In Paris he became a disciple of the geologist Pierre Louis Antoine Cordier (1777–1861) and Georges Cuvier. All his life, he would follow the theory of Cuvier and stay opposed to Lamarckism. D'Orbigny travelled on a mission for the Paris Museum, in South America between 1826 and 1833."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ramond", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1979, "target_cluster_name": "Ramond", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were naturalists and explorers who made significant contributions to the understanding of South American biodiversity and geology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were naturalists and explorers who made significant contributions to the understanding of South American biodiversity and geology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2542, "target_cluster_name": "Ramon de la Sagra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alcide d'Orbigny was a naturalist and explorer who also conducted significant fieldwork in South America, a region of interest to Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alcide d'Orbigny was a naturalist and explorer who also conducted significant fieldwork in South America, a region of interest to Ramón de la Sagra. They were contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "silurian strata", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "silurian_strata", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Silurian strata", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Silurian strata"], "contexts": ["geological layers beneath Silurian"]}, {"entity_id": "silurian_strata", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "silurian strata", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["silurian strata"], "contexts": ["geological strata with periodic stratification", "rock layer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Silurian strata", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "silurian strata", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 3003, "stable_key": "clu_b9fe26d0c0fa8851", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Silurian strata", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Refers to geological rock layers from the Silurian period."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Schist", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "sand or detritus, are as follows: 'Schist', of the lower and upper transition rock, compositing the silurian and devonian formations; from the lower silurian strata, which were once termed cambrian, t", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of rock forming formations"}, {"found_name": "transition rock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "sand or detritus, are as follows: 'Schist', of the lower and upper transition rock, compositing the silurian and devonian formations; from the lower silurian strata, which were once termed cambrian, t", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of rock forming formations"}, {"found_name": "mountain lime", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "evidence_snippet": "sand or detritus, are as follows: 'Schist', of the lower and upper transition rock, compositing the silurian and devonian formations; from the lower silurian strata, which were once termed cambrian, t", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of rock"}]}, {"canonical_name": "organic actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "organic_action", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "organic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic action"], "contexts": ["actions of living things"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_actions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organic actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["organic actions"], "contexts": ["bodily functions and processes", "general actions within living organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "organic action", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic actions", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 3004, "stable_key": "clu_5b79f60d65dfb54a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bodily functions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern texts often referred to the physiological processes of living organisms as 'organic actions'.", "wikidata_id": "Q67372196", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 July 1975"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nutrition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1041, "target_cluster_name": "nutrition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nutrition is a core bodily function essential for maintaining life and health.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nutrition is a core bodily function essential for maintaining life and health.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chimie végétale", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1358, "target_cluster_name": "chimie végétale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to 'animal economy', the study of bodily functions often involved chemical explanations and was a precursor to understanding biochemical processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Similar to 'animal economy', the study of bodily functions often involved chemical explanations and was a precursor to understanding biochemical processes.", "is_reverse"