istorical classifications of human populations, often based on perceived physical and cultural differences.", "wikidata_id": "Q55878123", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article in American Anthropologist, vol. 100 no. 3, September 1998"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "population", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1899, "target_cluster_name": "population", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Human races are constituent components of the broader concept of population.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Human races are constituent components of the broader concept of population.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sexes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2515, "target_cluster_name": "Sexes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The historical concept of 'Sexes' often encompassed discussions of human biological differences, which were frequently intertwined with contemporary understandings of 'Human races'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The historical concept of 'Sexes' often encompassed discussions of human biological differences, which were frequently intertwined with contemporary understandings of 'Human races'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "embryo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "embryos", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "embryos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["embryos"], "contexts": ["developing organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "embryo", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "embryo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["embryo"], "contexts": ["general structure marked out with precision", "developing organism before birth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "embryos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "embryo", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 3006, "stable_key": "clu_42aca3f5da9440c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Embryo", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'embryo' has remained consistent in its biological meaning.", "wikidata_id": "Q192154", "wikidata_description": "superdivision of plants"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "madre", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 47, "target_cluster_name": "madre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The womb is the organ that accommodates embryonic development.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The womb is the organ that accommodates embryonic development.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "ovo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 558, "target_cluster_name": "ovo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An embryo develops from a fertilized egg.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An embryo develops from a fertilized egg.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "organism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2569, "target_cluster_name": "organism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An embryo is an early developmental stage of an organism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An embryo is an early developmental stage of an organism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "uter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2646, "target_cluster_name": "uter", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The uterus is the organ where an embryo develops.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The uterus is the organ where an embryo develops.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "ossemens", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "ossemens", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ossemens", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["ossemens", "ossemens fossiles"], "contexts": ["bones found in caves", "bones of mastodontes"]}, {"entity_id": "osseous_ones", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "osseous ones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["osseous ones"], "contexts": ["connections turned into bone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ossemens", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "osseous ones", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3007, "stable_key": "clu_349383225953fca3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bones", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context indicates the anatomical meaning of bones, not their mineral composition as a substance."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Valladolid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "valladolid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Valladolid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Valladolid", "Valladolid de Mechoacan"], "contexts": ["intendancy in Nouvelle-Espagne", "city in New Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "valladolid", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Valladolid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valladolid"], "contexts": ["location of professorship"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Valladolid", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Valladolid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3008, "stable_key": "clu_cf1af73a0a5822d0", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Valladolid ( VAL-ə-də-LID; Spanish: [baʎaðoˈlið] ) is a city in Spain and the largest city as well as primary seat of government and de facto capital of the autonomous community of Castile and León. It is also the capital of the province of Valladolid. With a population of 299,816, it is the 13th largest municipality in Spain. The city is located roughly in the centre of the northern half of the Iberian Peninsula's Meseta Central, at the confluence of the Pisuerga and Esgueva rivers 15 km (9.3 mi) before they join the Duero, surrounded by winegrowing areas. The area was settled in pre-Roman times by the Celtic Vaccaei people, and then by Romans themselves. The settlement was purportedly founded after 1072, growing in prominence within the context of the Crown of Castile, being endowed with fairs and different institutions such as a collegiate church, University (1241), Royal Court and Chancellery and a royal mint."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Salamanca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2013, "target_cluster_name": "Salamanca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Salamanca and Valladolid are major cities in the autonomous community of Castile and León, Spain. They are historically and geographically significant centers within the same region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Salamanca and Valladolid are major cities in the autonomous community of Castile and León, Spain. They are historically and geographically significant centers within the same region.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "produits", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "produits", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "produits", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["produits", "produit net"], "contexts": ["goods for trade", "agricultural yields or goods"]}, {"entity_id": "material_products", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "material products", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["material products"], "contexts": ["substances formed by geological processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "produits", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "material products", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 3009, "stable_key": "clu_4f4cf55296b9f018", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "products", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This is a very general term. In the geological context, it refers to natural substances formed. In the trade context, it refers to manufactured or gathered goods. The most general referent is 'products'.", "wikidata_id": "Q542929", "wikidata_description": "substance (species) formed from a chemical reaction"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Malte-Brun", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "malte-brun", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Malte-Brun", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Malte-Brun", "Malte- Brun"], "contexts": ["compared idol to celestial spirits", "author of Géogr."]}, {"entity_id": "malte-brun", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Malte-Brun", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Malte-Brun"], "contexts": ["editor of travel annals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Malte-Brun", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Malte-Brun", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3010, "stable_key": "clu_202d4e4829ce4de3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conrad Malte-Brun", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1775, "death_year": 1826, "description": "Danish-French geographer and writer, editor of 'Annales des voyages'.", "wikidata_id": "Q980235", "wikidata_description": "Dano-French geographer", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conrad%20Malte-Brun", "wikipedia_extract": "Conrad Malte-Brun (French: [kɔ̃ʁad malt bʁœ̃]; born Malthe Conrad Bruun; 12 August 1775 – 14 December 1826), sometimes referred to simply as Malte-Brun, was a Dano-French geographer and journalist. His second son, Victor Adolphe Malte-Brun, was also a geographer. Today he is perhaps best remembered for coining the name for both the geographic regions of Oceania (French Océanie) around 1812 and Indo-China in 1804. Born in Thisted to an administrator of Danish crown lands, Malte-Brun was originally destined for a career as a pastor, but chose instead to attend classes at the University of Copenhagen, and became a supporter of the French Revolution and an activist in favor of freedom of the press. Following the harsh censorship laws instituted by the Danish ruler crown prince Frederick in September 1799, he was indicted because of his many pamphlets which contained outright criticism of the government, which the new censorship laws forbade. A particular cause for offence was a pamphlet he published in 1795 entitled \"Catechism of the Aristocrats.\" The case of Peter Andreas Heiberg, who for similar crimes had been sentenced to exile at Christmas of 1799, did not make Malte-Brun optimistic about his prospects."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Laet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2748, "target_cluster_name": "Laet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Johannes de Laet and Conrad Malte-Brun were prominent geographers and writers on exploration and geography. Laet's work on the New World predates Malte-Brun's, but they belong to the same intellectual tradition of geographical scholarship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Johannes de Laet and Conrad Malte-Brun were prominent geographers and writers on exploration and geography. Laet's work on the New World predates Malte-Brun's, but they belong to the same intellectual tradition of geographical scholarship.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alexandre VI", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "alexandre_vi", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Alexandre VI", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Alexandre VI", "pape Alexandre VI"], "contexts": ["pope who issued a bull", "Pope who issued a decree in favor of Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "alexandre_vi", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alexandre VI", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alexandre VI"], "contexts": ["Pope who issued a papal bull"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alexandre VI", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alexandre VI", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3011, "stable_key": "clu_77ae396331b7dccb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pope Alexander VI", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1431, "death_year": 1503, "description": "Papal authority involved in dividing hemispheres", "wikidata_id": "Q108316", "wikidata_description": "pope of the Catholic Church 1492–1503 from the Borgia family (1430/32-1503)", "wikipedia_extract": "Giovanni Borgia, 2nd Duke of Gandía (Spanish: Juan de Borja; Valencian: Joan Borja; c. 1476 – 14 June 1497) was the second child of Pope Alexander VI and Vannozza dei Cattanei and a member of the House of Borgia. He was the brother of Cesare, Gioffre, and Lucrezia Borgia. Giovanni Borgia was the pope's favourite son, and Alexander VI granted him important positions and honours. He was murdered in Rome on 14 June 1497. The case remained unsolved and is still considered one of the most notorious scandals of the Borgia era.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Alexander_VI"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Huaura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "huaura", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Huaura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Huaura", "Huauta"], "contexts": ["city in Peru", "geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "huaura", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Huaura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Huaura"], "contexts": ["location in Peru"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Huaura", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Huaura", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3012, "stable_key": "clu_956786a2d775131a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Huaura", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Buch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "buch", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Buch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Buch", "Buchanan"], "contexts": ["author of geographical letter", "scholar who published observations on Tyrol"]}, {"entity_id": "von_buch", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Von Buch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Von Buch"], "contexts": ["with Gay-Lussac and Von Buch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Buch", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Von Buch", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3013, "stable_key": "clu_d58784ceb14241e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leopold von Buch", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1774, "death_year": 1853, "description": "German geologist and paleontologist, known for volcanology"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "nummulites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "nummulites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "nummulites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["nummulites", "nuœmulites"], "contexts": ["fossil foraminifera", "fossil found in coarse limestone"]}, {"entity_id": "nummulites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nummulites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nummulites"], "contexts": ["fossil foraminifera"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "nummulites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nummulites", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 3014, "stable_key": "clu_9ea4e6d929c802b5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nummulites", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Nummulites are extinct marine protozoa, commonly found as fossils, which fits the description of fossil foraminifera.", "wikidata_id": "Q1604065", "wikidata_description": "genus of foraminifera", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nummulite", "wikipedia_extract": "A nummulite is a large lenticular fossil, characterised by its numerous coils, subdivided by septa into chambers. They are the shells of the fossil and present-day marine protozoan Nummulites, a type of foraminiferan. Nummulites commonly vary in diameter from 13 to 50 mm (0.51 to 1.97 in)  and are common in Eocene to Miocene marine rocks, particularly around southwest Asia and the Mediterranean in the area that once constituted the Tethys Ocean, such as Eocene limestones from Egypt or from Pakistan. Fossils up to six inches wide are found in the Middle Eocene rocks of Turkey. They are valuable as index fossils. The ancient Egyptians used nummulite shells as coins and the pyramids were constructed using limestone that contained nummulites."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Trilobites", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 959, "target_cluster_name": "Trilobites", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nummulites are extinct marine protozoa, often found in fossil deposits alongside trilobites, representing other ancient marine life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nummulites are extinct marine protozoa, often found in fossil deposits alongside trilobites, representing other ancient marine life.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "législation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "législation", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "législation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["législation"], "contexts": ["system of laws", "abstract concept"]}, {"entity_id": "legislation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "legislation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["legislation"], "contexts": ["process of making laws"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "législation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "legislation", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 3015, "stable_key": "clu_f8b0da67dc97e7b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "legislation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q49371", "wikidata_description": "product of enrolling, enacting, or promulgating law by a legislature, parliament, or analogous governing body; publication type"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "villa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "villa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "villa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["villa", "Vilela"], "contexts": ["town or small city", "settlement type"]}, {"entity_id": "villas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Villas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Villas"], "contexts": ["towns within the kingdom"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "villa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Villas", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 3016, "stable_key": "clu_8ced08911cc5f9a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "villa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A town or a small city, often with surrounding lands.", "wikipedia_extract": "A villa is a type of house that was originally an ancient Roman upper class country house that provided an escape from urban life. Since its origins in the Roman villa, the idea and function of a villa have evolved considerably. After the fall of the Roman Republic, villas became small farming compounds, which were increasingly fortified in Late Antiquity, sometimes transferred to the Church for reuse as a monastery. They gradually re-evolved through the Middle Ages into elegant upper-class country homes. In the early modern period, any comfortable detached house with a garden near a city or town was likely to be described as a villa; most surviving villas have now been engulfed by suburbia. In modern parlance, \"villa\" can refer to various types and sizes of residences, ranging from the suburban semi-detached double villa to, in some countries, especially around the Mediterranean, residences of above average size in the countryside. the villa urbana, a suburban or country seat that could easily be reached from Rome or another city for a night or two."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "casa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1272, "target_cluster_name": "casa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A villa is a type of house, often a large or luxurious one, and the terms are sometimes used interchangeably or with overlapping meanings in historical contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A villa is a type of house, often a large or luxurious one, and the terms are sometimes used interchangeably or with overlapping meanings in historical contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "amerigo_vespucci", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Amerigo Vespucci"], "contexts": ["explorer and cartographer", "Discoverer of Cape San Augustin"]}, {"entity_id": "amerigo_vespucci", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amerigo Vespucci"], "contexts": ["figure in naming of America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "similarity": 0.736}], "id": 3017, "stable_key": "clu_027d24aa8e602280", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amerigo Vespucci", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1454, "death_year": 1512, "description": "Explorer and cartographer, for whom America was named."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Columbus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 327, "target_cluster_name": "Columbus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amerigo Vespucci was a contemporary explorer whose voyages to the Americas occurred shortly after Columbus's, and the continent was named after Vespucci, creating a significant historical connection and often a point of comparison.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amerigo Vespucci was a contemporary explorer whose voyages to the Americas occurred shortly after Columbus's, and the continent was named after Vespucci, creating a significant historical connection and often a point of comparison.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Drake", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 461, "target_cluster_name": "Drake", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery, undertaking voyages that expanded European geographical knowledge and contributed to the mapping of the Americas and the circumnavigation of the globe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery, undertaking voyages that expanded European geographical knowledge and contributed to the mapping of the Americas and the circumnavigation of the globe.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Italians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 462, "target_cluster_name": "Italians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Amerigo Vespucci was an Italian explorer, financier, and navigator.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Amerigo Vespucci was an Italian explorer, financier, and navigator.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Marco Polo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 596, "target_cluster_name": "Marco Polo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are famous explorers whose voyages and discoveries were documented and widely disseminated, influencing European understanding of the world.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cook", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1072, "target_cluster_name": "Cook", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery, whose voyages and writings contributed to the European understanding of the Americas and the subsequent naming of the continent.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery, whose voyages and writings contributed to the European understanding of the Americas and the subsequent naming of the continent.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Halley", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1784, "target_cluster_name": "Halley", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, with Vespucci lending his name to the Americas and Halley contributing significantly to navigation and cartography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were key figures in the Age of Discovery and exploration, with Vespucci lending his name to the Americas and Halley contributing significantly to navigation and cartography.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Freycinet", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1898, "target_cluster_name": "Freycinet", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Vespucci was an early explorer whose voyages and cartographic contributions laid groundwork for later explorers like Freycinet.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Vespucci was an early explorer whose voyages and cartographic contributions laid groundwork for later explorers like Freycinet.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bauza", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1934, "target_cluster_name": "Bauza", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Felipe Bauzá and Amerigo Vespucci were Italian explorers who made significant contributions to the mapping and understanding of the Americas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Felipe Bauzá and Amerigo Vespucci were Italian explorers who made significant contributions to the mapping and understanding of the Americas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Anghiera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2195, "target_cluster_name": "Anghiera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures in the Age of Discovery, with Vespucci being a famous explorer and cartographer, and Peter Martyr d'Anghiera being a chronicler of these voyages and an early historian of the New World.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures in the Age of Discovery, with Vespucci being a famous explorer and cartographer, and Peter Martyr d'Anghiera being a chronicler of these voyages and an early historian of the New World.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rodriguez", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2198, "target_cluster_name": "Rodriguez", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were explorers of the Americas.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were explorers of the Americas.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_n