nd 109,106 at the 2002 census. It was previously known as Pyaty Pochtovy (until 1949) and Tomsk-7 (until 1992). The city is located 15 kilometers (9.3 miles) northwest of Tomsk on the right bank of the Tom River. Seversk has a humid continental climate (Köppen climate classification Dfb) with warm summer. The annual average temperature is 0.6 °C (33.1 °F).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomsk"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Jésuites", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "jésuites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jésuites", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Jésuites"], "contexts": ["religious order", "religious order at convent"]}, {"entity_id": "jesuítas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "jesuítas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["jesuítas"], "contexts": ["religious order"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jésuites", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "jesuítas", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 3023, "stable_key": "clu_4a5efb62d0595df4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jesuits", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Members of the Society of Jesus, a Catholic religious order", "wikidata_id": "Q10351458", "wikidata_description": "historic bridge in the city of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "S. Francisco Xavier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2470, "target_cluster_name": "S. Francisco Xavier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Francis Xavier was a co-founder of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Francis Xavier was a co-founder of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Campo de Gigantes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "campo_de_gigantes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Campo de Gigantes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Campo de Gigantes"], "contexts": ["Campo de Gigantes", "field near Suacha with Mastodon bones"]}, {"entity_id": "campo_de_gigantes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Campo de Gigantes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Campo de Gigantes"], "contexts": ["field name with giant bones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Campo de Gigantes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Campo de Gigantes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3024, "stable_key": "clu_fa9bb51b9bd2d34f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Field of Giants", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Unknown", "note": "The exact geographical location referred to as 'Campo de Gigantes' is unclear from the provided context. It is described as a place with 'giant bones', suggesting it might be a paleontological site, but its modern identifier is not readily available.", "wikipedia_extract": "Giants Stadium (sometimes referred to as Giants Stadium at the Meadowlands) was a stadium located in East Rutherford, New Jersey, in the Meadowlands Sports Complex. The venue was open from 1976 to 2010, and primarily hosted sporting events and concerts. It was the home field of the New York metropolitan area's two National Football League teams, the New York Giants (from 1976 to 2009) and the New York Jets (from 1984 to 2009). It also was the home field of two New York metro area professional soccer teams, the NASL's New York Cosmos (from 1977 to 1984) and Major League Soccer's New York Red Bulls (from 1996 to 2009). The stadium's maximum seating capacity was 80,242. The structure was 756 feet (230 m) long, 592 feet (180 m) wide and 144 feet (44 m) high from service level to the top of the seating bowl and 178 feet (54 m) high to the top of the south tower.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giants_Stadium"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "orthocératites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "orthocératites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "orthocératites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["orthocératites"], "contexts": ["extinct marine cephalopod genus", "fossil organism, first traces of organization"]}, {"entity_id": "orthoceraitites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Orthoceraitites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orthoceraitites"], "contexts": ["fossil cephalopod genus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "orthocératites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Orthoceraitites", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 3025, "stable_key": "clu_dca80e26c3d05e5f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orthoceras", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Orthocerida is an extinct suborder of nautiloid cephalopods, and 'Orthocerates' refers to fossils of these creatures. The context of 'fossil cephalopod genus' confirms this.", "wikidata_id": "Q3906531", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthoceras%20%28plant%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Orthoceras, from Ancient Greek ὀρθός (orthós), meaning \"straight\", and κέρας (kéras), meaning \"horn\", is a genus of extinct nautiloid cephalopod restricted to Middle Ordovician-aged marine limestones of the Baltic States and Sweden. This genus is sometimes called Orthoceratites and misspelled as Orthocera, Orthocerus, or Orthoceros. Orthoceras was formerly thought to have had a worldwide distribution due to the genus' use as a wastebasket taxon for numerous species of conical-shelled nautiloids throughout the Paleozoic and Triassic. Since this work was carried out and re-cataloging of the genus, Orthoceras sensu stricto refers to Orthoceras regulare, of Ordovician-aged Baltic Sea limestones of Sweden and neighboring areas. These are slender, elongate shells with the middle of the body chamber transversely constricted, and a subcentral orthochoanitic siphuncle. The surface is ornamented by a network of fine lirae (Sweet 1964:K224)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Harris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "harris", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Harris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Harris"], "contexts": ["gave magnetic declination value", "mentioned in relation to astronomical observations"]}, {"entity_id": "harris", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Harris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Harris"], "contexts": ["experiments in electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Harris", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Harris", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3026, "stable_key": "clu_7327beecdcde0699", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sir William Rowan Hamilton", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1805, "death_year": 1865, "description": "Irish mathematician and physicist, known for quaternions", "note": "The context mentions 'experiments in electricity' and 'magnetic declination value'. While Harris is a plausible surname for a scientist of the period, the provided context snippets do not strongly point to a specific prominent scientist named Harris. Harris (17th century) is a possibility but is not strongly supported by the context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "Irish mathematician and astronomer (1805-1865)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aldebaran", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "aldebaran", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Aldebaran", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Aldebaran"], "contexts": ["star occultation observed", "star used for occultation calculations"]}, {"entity_id": "aldebaran", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aldebaran", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aldebaran"], "contexts": ["star in Taurus constellation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Aldebaran", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Aldebaran", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3027, "stable_key": "clu_ab4d135c4d0f3605", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aldebaran", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Aldebaran is a star, not an animal. The context of 'star occultation observed' and 'star in Taurus constellation' clearly identifies it as an astronomical object.", "wikidata_id": "Q2623410", "wikidata_description": "French robotics company", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldebaran", "wikipedia_extract": "Aldebaran (Arabic: الدَّبَران, lit. 'The Follower') is a star in the zodiac constellation of Taurus. It has the Bayer designation α Tauri, which is Latinized to Alpha Tauri and abbreviated Alpha Tau or α Tau. Aldebaran varies in brightness from an apparent visual magnitude of 0.75 down to 0.95, making it the brightest star in the constellation, as well as (typically) the fourteenth-brightest star in the night sky. It is at a distance of approximately 67 light-years. The star lies along the line of sight to the nearby Hyades cluster, but is unrelated and much older than the young cluster."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Orion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1046, "target_cluster_name": "Orion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aldebaran is a star located in the constellation Orion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aldebaran is a star located in the constellation Orion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Centaures", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2494, "target_cluster_name": "Centaures", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aldebaran is a bright star, and like Alpha Centauri, it would have been a significant object of observation for astronomers studying stars.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aldebaran is a bright star, and like Alpha Centauri, it would have been a significant object of observation for astronomers studying stars.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Sirius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1700, "target_cluster_name": "Sirius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Aldebaran is another prominent star in the night sky, often discussed in astronomical contexts alongside Sirius.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Aldebaran is another prominent star in the night sky, often discussed in astronomical contexts alongside Sirius.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "61 Cygni", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1745, "target_cluster_name": "61 Cygni", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are stars, with Aldebaran being a prominent star often discussed in astronomical contexts alongside other celestial bodies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are stars, with Aldebaran being a prominent star often discussed in astronomical contexts alongside other celestial bodies.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "pinus_occidentalis", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pinus occidentalis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pinus occidentalis"], "contexts": ["pine tree found in Cuba", "species of pine tree"]}, {"entity_id": "pinus_occidentalis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pinus occidentalis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pinus occidentalis"], "contexts": ["pine tree in Mexico"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3028, "stable_key": "clu_ffb3656bdd3bfe86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pinus occidentalis", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pinus occidentalis Sw.", "family": "Pinaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q3388586", "wikidata_description": "species of plant, Hispaniolan Pine", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinus%20occidentalis", "wikipedia_extract": "Pinus occidentalis, also known as the Hispaniolan pine, Hispaniola pine or pino criollo, is a pine tree endemic to the island of Hispaniola (split between the Dominican Republic and Haiti). It is the eponymous species of the Hispaniolan pine forests ecosystem, in which it constitutes a majority of the biomass present. Another endemic species, the Hispaniolan crossbill (Loxia megaplaga), feeds almost exclusively on the cones of P. occidentalis."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pines", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "