blished in Astronomy and Astrophysics"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dolor del espaldas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "dolor_del_espaldas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "dolor del espaldas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["dolor del espaldas", "dolon de costado", "dolores de costado"], "contexts": ["pain in the back", "side pains"]}, {"entity_id": "pains_in_the_back", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pains in the back", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["pains in the back"], "contexts": ["discomfort in the dorsal region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dolor del espaldas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pains in the back", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 3172, "stable_key": "clu_68a715158290ad54", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Back pain", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Back pain", "wikidata_id": "Q5781808", "wikidata_description": "type of pain felt in the back"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ciatica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1501, "target_cluster_name": "Ciatica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sciatica is a specific type of pain that originates in the lower back and radiates down the leg, making 'back pain' a broader but directly related category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sciatica is a specific type of pain that originates in the lower back and radiates down the leg, making 'back pain' a broader but directly related category.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "opilaviodes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "opilaviodes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "opilaviodes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["opilaviodes", "opilacion"], "contexts": ["obstructions treated by the water", "obstruction or hardness in the body"]}, {"entity_id": "opilaçam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "opilaçam", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["opilaçam"], "contexts": ["condition of obstruction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "opilaviodes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "opilaçam", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3173, "stable_key": "clu_13de6c8a5642f0a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obstruction", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A blockage or impeded flow within the body.", "note": "Medical term referring to blockages in bodily humors or vessels.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bowel obstruction, also known as intestinal obstruction, is a mechanical or functional obstruction of the intestines that prevents the normal movement of the products of digestion. Either the small bowel or large bowel may be affected. Signs and symptoms include abdominal pain, vomiting, bloating and not passing gas. Mechanical obstruction is the cause of about 5 to 15% of cases of severe abdominal pain of sudden onset requiring admission to hospital. Causes of bowel obstruction include adhesions, hernias, volvulus, endometriosis, inflammatory bowel disease, appendicitis, tumors, diverticulitis, ischemic bowel, tuberculosis and intussusception. Small bowel obstructions are most often due to adhesions and hernias, while large bowel obstructions are most often due to tumors and volvulus."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sudor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "sudor", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sudor", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["sudor"], "contexts": ["sweat, bodily fluid", "bodily fluid"]}, {"entity_id": "sweat", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sweat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sweat"], "contexts": ["manner of blood expulsion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "sudor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sweat", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3174, "stable_key": "clu_fa8d961cbf792e48", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sudorific", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A substance or remedy that induces sweating.", "wikipedia_extract": "Perspiration, also known as sweat, is the fluid secreted by sweat glands in the skin of mammals. Two types of sweat glands can be found in humans: eccrine glands and apocrine glands. The eccrine sweat glands are distributed over much of the body and are responsible for secreting the watery, brackish sweat most often triggered by excessive body temperature. Apocrine sweat glands are restricted to the armpits and a few other areas of the body and produce an odorless, oily, opaque secretion which then gains its characteristic odor from bacterial decomposition. In humans, sweating is primarily a means of thermoregulation, which is achieved by the water-rich secretion of the eccrine glands. Maximum sweat rates of an adult can be up to 2–4 litres (0.5–1 US gal) per hour or 10–14 litres (2.5–3.5 US gal) per day, but is less in children prior to puberty."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "males", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "males", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "males", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["males"], "contexts": ["illnesses", "serious illnesses"]}, {"entity_id": "males", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "males", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["males"], "contexts": ["human ailments discussed in the book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "males", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "males", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3175, "stable_key": "clu_856d63cb7b859723", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Illness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Illness", "note": "This cluster refers to general ailments or sickness rather than a specific disease."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "enfermidade", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 694, "target_cluster_name": "enfermidade", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'illness' is given the historical name 'enfermidade', which is the Portuguese word for illness or sickness.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "The entity 'illness' is given the historical name 'enfermidade', which is the Portuguese word for illness or sickness."}, {"found_name": "disease", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 883, "target_cluster_name": "disease", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'illness' is a general term that encompasses specific instances of 'disease'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'illness' is a general term that encompasses specific instances of 'disease'."}, {"found_name": "malady", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2955, "target_cluster_name": "malady", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'illness' is given the synonym 'malady', indicating a close semantic relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The entity 'illness' is given the synonym 'malady', indicating a close semantic relationship."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cliſteres", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "cliſteres", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cliſteres", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["cliſteres", "clyíteres"], "contexts": ["enema for bowel evacuation", "medical instruments for enemas"]}, {"entity_id": "clyſteis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "clyſteis", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["clyſteis"], "contexts": ["type of medicinal administration discussed", "medical preparation for internal administration"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cliſteres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "clyſteis", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3176, "stable_key": "clu_4e35ae8a18bbfc91", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Enema", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q4038065", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_extract": "An enema, also known as a clyster, is the rectal administration of a fluid by injection into the lower bowel via the anus. The word enema can also refer to the liquid injected, as well as to a device for administering such an injection. In standard medicine, the most frequent uses of enemas are to relieve constipation and for bowel cleansing before a medical examination or procedure; also, they are employed as a lower gastrointestinal series (also called a barium enema), to treat traveler's diarrhea, as a vehicle for the administration of food, water or medicine, as a stimulant to the general system, as a local application and, more rarely, as a means of reducing body temperature, as treatment for encopresis, and as a form of rehydration therapy (proctoclysis) in patients for whom intravenous therapy is not applicable. The principal medical usages of enemas are: As bowel stimulants, enemas are employed for the same purposes as orally administered laxatives: to relieve constipation; to treat fecal impaction; to empty the colon before a medical procedure such as a colonoscopy. When oral laxatives are not indicated or sufficiently effective, enemas may be a sensible and necessary measure. A large volume enema can be given to cleanse as much of the colon as possible of feces."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "D.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "d_", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "D.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["D.", "B."], "contexts": ["initial for a person's name"]}, {"entity_id": "d__c_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "D. C.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["D. C."], "contexts": ["author of scientific name", "initials associated with a plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "D.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "D. C.", "similarity": 0.87}], "id": 3177, "stable_key": "clu_0e51311034619a6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "D. C.", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Author of scientific name, likely abbreviated.", "note": "The abbreviation 'D.' or 'D. C.' is too ambiguous to identify a specific individual without further context from the texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q61", "wikidata_description": "capital city of the United States of America"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "algodao", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "algodon", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Algodon", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Algodon"], "contexts": ["plant product brought from the Indies"]}, {"entity_id": "algodao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "algodao", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["algodao"], "contexts": ["used for application", "plant fiber used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Algodon", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "algodao", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3178, "stable_key": "clu_e3c9b651b171a9d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cotton", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Gossypium spp.", "family": "Malvaceae"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Berbéria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "berbería", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Berbería", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Berbería"], "contexts": ["place of origin for rhubarb"]}, {"entity_id": "berbéria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Berbéria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Berbéria"], "contexts": ["coast mentioned as potential origin", "region where rhubarb is traded"]}, {"entity_id": "barbaric", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Barbaric", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Barbaric"], "contexts": ["coast where coral grows"]}, {"entity_id": "barbaria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Barbaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Barbaria"], "contexts": ["region from which gengivre comes", "region in Northeast Africa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Berbería", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Berbéria", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 3179, "stable_key": "clu_24cc16b219f60f87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Barbary Coast", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Algeria, Libya, Morocco, Tunisia", "wikidata_id": "Q501768", "wikidata_description": "name used to designate the territory of the Barbary and going from Libya to Morocco", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbary%20Coast", "wikipedia_extract": "The Barbary Coast (also Barbary, Berbery, or Berber Coast) were the coastal areas of central and western North Africa, more specifically, the Maghreb and the Ottoman borderlands consisting of the regencies in Algiers, Tunis, and Tripoli, as well as the Sultanate of Morocco from the 16th to 19th centuries. The term originates from an exonym for the Berbers. Barbary was not always a unified political entity. From the 16th century onward, it was divided into four political entities—from west to east—the Alawi Sultanate, the Regency of Algiers, the Regency of Tunis, and the Regency of Tripoli. Major rulers and petty monarchs during the times of the Barbary states' plundering parties included the sultan of Morocco, the dey of Algiers, bey of Tunis, and pasha of Tripoli, respectively. The slave trade was not just an economic lifeline to the Barbary States, but was often justified as a form of jihad against Christian states.", "note": "Refers to the historical coastal region of North Africa."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arabia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 169, "target_cluster_name": "Arabia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Barbary Coast is a region in North Africa, historically linked to Arab influence and trade routes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Barbary Coast is a region in North Africa, historically linked to Arab influence and trade routes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "raham", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "os Mouros muytos nesta terra lhe chamam somente ravam^ mas todos confessam não aver outro, senão o da China; que he asi, que nam ha ruibarbo trazido de Berbéria nem rapam indico; senão o que se traz á", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for rhubarb"}, {"found_name": "rapam indico", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "os Mouros muytos nesta terra lhe chamam somente ravam^ mas todos confessam não aver outro, senão o da China; que he asi, que nam ha ruibarbo trazido de Berbéria nem rapam indico; senão o que se traz á", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of rhubarb"}]}, {"canonical_name": "perfume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fragancia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "fragancia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fragancia"], "contexts": ["quality of the Saissasfras bark"]}, {"entity_id": "perfume", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "perfume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["perfume"], "contexts": ["used from flowers", "fragrance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "fragancia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "perfume", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 3180, "stable_key": "clu_ea4305b10a6dcca1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fragrance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Pictor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "pintores", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "pintores", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["pintores"], "contexts": ["wondrous color for painters"]}, {"entity_id": "pictor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pictor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pictor", "Piator", "Pintor"], "contexts": ["mentioned in relation to an imagined physician", "occupation of Manoelda Paz"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "pintores", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pictor", "similarity": 0.867}], "id": 3181, "stable_key": "clu_66050b1b850f6934", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Painter", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "An individual skilled in the art of painting.", "wikidata_id": "Q1630100", "wikidata_description": "person who poses for any visual artist as part of the creative process"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "abeto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "abeto", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Abeto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Abeto"], "contexts": ["tree from which turpentine is collected"]}, {"entity_id": "abeto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "abeto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["abeto", "abete"], "contexts": ["material for good water vessels", "tree yielding resin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Abeto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "abeto", "similarity": 0.804}], "id": 3182, "stable_key": "clu_d0fced123dca4cab", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Until the end of the Ethiopian monarchy in 1975, there were two categories of nobility in Ethiopia and Eritrea. The Mesafint (Ge'ez: መሳፍንት masāfint, modern transcription mesāfint, singular መስፍን masfin, modern mesfin, \"prince\"), the hereditary royal nobility, formed the upper echelon of the ruling class. The Mekwanint (Ge'ez: መኳንንት makʷanint, modern mekʷanint, singular መኰንን makʷanin, modern mekʷanin or Amharic: መኮንን mekonnen, \"officer\") were the appointed nobles, often of humble birth, who formed the bulk of the aristocracy. Until the 20th century, the most powerful people at court were generally members of the Mekwanint appointed by the monarch, while regionally, the Mesafint enjoyed greater influence and power. Emperor Haile Selassie greatly curtailed the power of the Mesafint to the benefit of the Mekwanint, who by then were essentially coterminous with the Ethiopian government. The Mekwanint were officials who had been granted specific offices in the Abyssinian government or court."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cedro", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2508, "target_cluster_name": "cedro", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for 'fir tree', a type of conifer closely related to cedar.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spanish for 'fir tree', a type of conifer closely related to cedar.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pino", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2460, "target_cluster_name": "Pino", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are coniferous trees, often grouped together in botanical and economic contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are coniferous trees, often grouped together in botanical and economic contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "trementina", "link_type": "derivation", "link_strength": 0.4, "target_cluster_id": 1737, "target_cluster_name": "Trementina", "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": ". Pi- glianli da gli animali come se detto di fopra . DELLA RAGIA. LaragiaciI liquore,che naturalmente difti Ila dal Pi* no, dalla picea , dall'abete, e fimili ; e ritrouafene di due ma- niere ; vna l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "derived from various trees"}, {"found_name": "riccialaricma", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": ". Pi- glianli da gli animali come se detto di fopra . DELLA RAGIA. LaragiaciI liquore,che naturalmente difti Ila dal Pi* no, dalla picea , dall'abete, e fimili ; e ritrouafene di due ma- niere ; vna l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of trementina"}]}, {"canonical_name": "pedra be^ar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "piedra_bera", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "piedra Bera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["piedra Bera"], "contexts": ["stone with demonstrated virtues"]}, {"entity_id": "pedra_be^ar", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pedra be^ar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["pedra be^ar"], "contexts": ["a specific type of stone", "stone used as medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "bola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bola"], "contexts": ["Arabic name for mocebar"]}, {"entity_id": "bolas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bolas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bolas"], "contexts": ["balls found in stomachs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "piedra Bera", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pedra be^ar", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3183, "stable_key": "clu_de56214e1b51133f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bezoar", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "'Bola' is the Arabic word for bezoar, a medicinal concretion found in the stomach of some animals. 'Bolas' in the second source likely refers to the same.", "wikidata_id": "Q897514", "wikidata_description": "subspecies of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bezoar%20ibex", "wikipedia_extract": "The bezoar ibex (Capra aegagrus aegagrus) is a wild goat subspecies that is native to the montane forested areas in the Anatolia, Caucasus and the Zagros Mountains. The bezoar ibex, which weighs around 60 kg (130 lb) is known particularly for the size of its horns; it possesses the world's longest horns in relation to body weight, and can exceed 1.4 m (4 ft 7 in) high, those in the south and east desert ranges are typically smaller by 30 percent. Females are slightly smaller and their horns tend to grow to 0.2 m (7.9 in). Males have a dark brown summer coat, while the females have a more reddish-gold, and both sexes shift to a gray-colored coat in winter. Both sexes also have a tuft of hair extending from the chin. The ibexes have a black stripe from the spine that extends over the shoulder, limbs, and neck."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bola", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "também quando quierem encarnar algumas chagas, e tem pêra bto nas suas boticas huma mezinha chamada mocebar, feita de a\\eínre e mirra, á qual elles chamam bola, e desta usam muito para curar cavalos, ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for mocebar"}, {"found_name": "carambola", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Lembranças da Imdea, 39, nos Subsídios; Fernão Mendes Pinto, Peregrin. cap. xum). Nota (4) Orta refere-se a uma de duas espécies vizinhas: Averrhoa Coram- bola, Linn., chamada vulgarmente carambola, e", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vulgar name for Averrhoa Coram-bola"}, {"found_name": "Camariz", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Lembranças da Imdea, 39, nos Subsídios; Fernão Mendes Pinto, Peregrin. cap. xum). Nota (4) Orta refere-se a uma de duas espécies vizinhas: Averrhoa Coram- bola, Linn., chamada vulgarmente carambola, e", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "contested_identity", "found_relationship": "possibly related to kamctranga"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bartholomeus Urbevetanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "bartholomeus_anglicus", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Bartholomeus Anglicus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bartholomeus Anglicus"], "contexts": ["author of 'De Proprietatibus Rerum'"]}, {"entity_id": "bartholomeus_urbevetanus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Bartholomeus Urbevetanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bartholomeus Urbevetanus", "Bartholomeu de Glanvilla"], "contexts": ["author of commentaries", "author of a book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Bartholomeus Anglicus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Bartholomeus Urbevetanus", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 3184, "stable_key": "clu_4e32e768bb985309", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bartholomew Anglicus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "English Franciscan friar and scholastic", "note": "The text uses both 'Bartholomeus Urbevetanus' and 'Bartholomeus Anglicus'. Bartholomew Anglicus is the known author of 'De Proprietatibus Rerum'.", "wikidata_id": "Q809312", "wikidata_description": "13th-century scholastic scholar, author of the compendium De proprietatibus rerum"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Anglois", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1727, "target_cluster_name": "Anglois", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bartholomew Anglicus was an English Franciscan friar and encyclopedist, making him a direct historical and national antecedent to the concept of 'English people'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bartholomew Anglicus was an English Franciscan friar and encyclopedist, making him a direct historical and national antecedent to the concept of 'English people'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "dano", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "daño", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "daño", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["daño"], "contexts": ["harmful effect of remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "dano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dano", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["dano", "damnos", "danos"], "contexts": ["harm or damage", "damage caused by acids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "daño", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dano", "similarity": 0.541}], "id": 3185, "stable_key": "clu_27d4a1839c49a682", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "harm", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Dano' and 'daño' in this context refer to harm or damage, not a specific disease."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "adelgaçao", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "adelgazar", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "adelgazar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["adelgazar"], "contexts": ["to thin or make slender"]}, {"entity_id": "adelgaçao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "adelgaçao", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["adelgaçao"], "contexts": ["thinning, a quality", "thinning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "adelgazar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "adelgaçao", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 3186, "stable_key": "clu_daf60814aa005c4d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thinning", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the concept of becoming or making something thinner or slender.", "wikidata_id": "Q500094", "wikidata_description": "selective removal of parts of a plant"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "leite azedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "leite_azedo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "leite azedo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["leite azedo"], "contexts": ["preparation for asthma", "ingredient mixed with powder"]}, {"entity_id": "leite_asinino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "leite asinino", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["leite asinino"], "contexts": ["donkey milk for eye ailments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "leite azedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "leite asinino", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3187, "stable_key": "clu_b122cecdd734d8b5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Donkey milk", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q129528842", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Donkey milk (or ass milk, or jenny milk) is the milk from the domesticated donkey (Equus asinus). It has been used since antiquity for cosmetic purposes as well as infant nutrition. Donkey milk has been used by humans for alimentary and cosmetic purposes since Egyptian antiquity. Doctors recommended it to treat several afflictions because of its supposed healing and cosmetic virtues. Hippocrates (460–370 BC) was the first to write of the medicinal use of donkey milk and prescribed it for numerous conditions, including poisoning, fevers, infectious diseases, edema, wounds, nose bleeds, and liver trouble. In the Roman era, donkey milk was a recognized remedy; Pliny the Elder (23–79 AD) in his encyclopedic work, Naturalis Historia, wrote extensively about its health benefits, i.e. to fight fever, fatigue, eye strain, weakened teeth, face wrinkles, poisonings, ulcerations, asthma, and certain gynecological troubles."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "aceto", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1565, "target_cluster_name": "aceto", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are described as 'leite azedo' (sour milk) in Portuguese, suggesting a shared characteristic of sourness or fermentation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are described as 'leite azedo' (sour milk) in Portuguese, suggesting a shared characteristic of sourness or fermentation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "canje", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "esta aguoa duas vezes a beber no dia, scilicet, huma polia ma- nhãa ás seis horas, e outra ás duas depois do meo dia. Nestes dias damos a comer ao emfermo leite azedo mistu- rado com arroz, e franguos", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for aguoa deste arroz"}]}, {"canonical_name": "amarello", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "amarello", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "amarello", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["amarello", "Amarello"], "contexts": ["color used for dyeing", "river name"]}, {"entity_id": "amarella", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "amarella", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["amarella"], "contexts": ["yellow color"]}, {"entity_id": "yellow", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "yellow", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["yellow"], "contexts": ["comparison to another plant", "color descriptor"]}, {"entity_id": "yellowness", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "yellowness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["yellowness"], "contexts": ["quality of being yellow"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "amarello", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "amarella", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 3188, "stable_key": "clu_c20a5f08b6979ec9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yellow", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1148004", "wikidata_description": "mosquito species, a vector for diseases including yellow fever and Zika fever", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aedes%20aegypti", "note": "Refers to the color yellow, used metaphorically and literally for dyeing.", "wikipedia_extract": "Aedes aegypti (; US: ; from Ancient Greek  αηδής (aēdḗs) 'unpleasant' and Latin  aegyptī 'Egyptian') – sometimes called the Egyptian mosquito, dengue mosquito, or yellow fever mosquito – is a mosquito that spreads diseases such as dengue fever, yellow fever, and chikungunya. The mosquito can be recognized by black and white markings on its legs and a marking in the form of a lyre on the upper surface of its thorax. The mosquito is native to north Africa, but is now a common invasive species that has spread to tropical, subtropical, and temperate regions throughout the world. Aedes aegypti is a 4-to-7-millimetre-long (5⁄32 to 35⁄128 in), dark mosquito which can be recognized by white markings on its legs and a marking in the form of a lyre on the upper surface of its thorax. Females are larger than males. Microscopically females possess small palps tipped with silver or white scales, and their antennae have sparse short hairs, whereas those of males are feathery."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "D. João de Castro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__joão_de_castro", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "D. João de Castro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["D. João de Castro"], "contexts": ["second viceroy who died in India", "author of travelogue"]}, {"entity_id": "d__joaode_castro", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "D. Joaode Castro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["D. Joaode Castro"], "contexts": ["person who experienced throat issues"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "D. João de Castro", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "D. Joaode Castro", "similarity": 0.606}], "id": 3189, "stable_key": "clu_808e2f8b878e400d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "João de Castro", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1500, "death_year": 1548, "description": "Viceroy of Portuguese India, known for scientific and medical interests.", "wikidata_id": "Q1352529", "wikidata_description": "Portuguese explorer"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "conde de Redondo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "conde_de_redondo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "conde de Redondo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["conde de Redondo"], "contexts": ["recipient of ode", "Viceroy of India"]}, {"entity_id": "senhor_conde_do_redondo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Senhor Conde do Redondo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senhor Conde do Redondo"], "contexts": ["patron of remedy making"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "conde de Redondo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Senhor Conde do Redondo", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3190, "stable_key": "clu_51ed3d27aec7a9db", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Count of Redondo", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Noble patron associated with remedy making", "note": "Specific individual not readily identifiable without more context.", "wikidata_id": "Q9843838", "wikidata_description": "noble title in the Kingdom of Portugal", "wikipedia_extract": "Count of Redondo (Portuguese: Conde de Redondo) was a Portuguese title of nobility created by a royal decree, in 1481, by King Manuel I of Portugal, and granted to D. Vasco Coutinho, the son of Fernando Coutinho, Marshall of Portugal. Duarte de Castelo Branco, 7th Count of Redondo Francisco de Castelo Branco, 8th Count of Redondo Fernão de Sousa de Castelo Branco Coutinho e Meneses, 10th Count of Redondo Tomé de Sousa Coutinho Castelo Branco e Meneses, 11th Count of Redondo Fernando de Sousa Coutinho, 12th Count of Redondo Tomé Xavier de Sousa Coutinho de Castelo Branco e Meneses, 13th Count of Redondo, 1st Marquis of Borba Fernando Maria de Sousa Coutinho, 14th Count of Redondo, 2nd Marquis of Borba José Luís Gonzaga de Sousa Coutinho Castelo Branco e Meneses, 15th Count of Redondo Fernando Luís de Sousa Coutinho Castelo Branco e Meneses, 16th Count of Redondo, 3rd Marquis of Borba Fernando José Luís Burnay de Sousa Coutinho, 17th Count of Redondo (1883–1945); António Luís Carvalho de Sousa Coutinho, 18th Count of Redondo (1925-2007); 7th Marquis of Valença, 4th Marquis of Borba, 15th Count of Vimioso, 8th Count of Soure; Fernando Patrício de Portugal de Sousa Coutinho, 19th Count of Redondo (1956), 8th Marquis of Valença, 6th Marquis of Borba, 16th Count of Vimioso, 9th Count of Soure? The family owned a number of properties throughout Portugal. Amongst the more well-known properties are the Palace of the Counts of Redondo in Lisbon, and the Quinta do Relógio in Sintra."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fidalgo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 748, "target_cluster_name": "Fidalgo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Count of Redondo is a noble title, and 'fidalgo' refers to a member of the nobility in Portugal and Spain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Count of Redondo is a noble title, and 'fidalgo' refers to a member of the nobility in Portugal and Spain.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Major", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2522, "target_cluster_name": "Major", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are titles of nobility or high office, indicating a position of authority and social standing within a hierarchical system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are titles of nobility or high office, indicating a position of authority and social standing within a hierarchical system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "pao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "pao", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["pao"], "contexts": ["wood from a tree", "wood mentioned by Galen"]}, {"entity_id": "paopo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "paopo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["paopo"], "contexts": ["substance for mixing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pao", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "paopo", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 3191, "stable_key": "clu_195fe1ca6e8f9454", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wood", "confidence": "medium", "type": "substance", "description": "Organic material from trees, used for various purposes including medicine.", "note": "The term 'pao' in Portuguese means wood. 'Paoalvo' likely refers to white bread, possibly as a contrast or a specific preparation.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wood is a structural tissue/material found as xylem in the stems and roots of trees and other woody plants. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression. Wood is sometimes defined as only the secondary xylem in the stems of trees, or more broadly to include the same type of tissue elsewhere, such as in the roots of trees or shrubs. In a living tree, it performs a mechanical-support function, enabling woody plants to grow large or to stand up by themselves. It also conveys water and nutrients among the leaves, other growing tissues, and the roots. Wood may also refer to other plant materials with comparable properties, and to material engineered from wood, woodchips, or fibers."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "almagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "almagre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "almagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["almagre", "alma"], "contexts": ["red earth used for comparison", "Arabic name for diamond"]}, {"entity_id": "almagra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "almagra", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["almagra"], "contexts": ["mixed with aypo juice to dry milk", "red ochre"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "almagre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "almagra", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 3192, "stable_key": "clu_924ed609c374e399", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "red ochre", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "A natural clay earth pigment, rich in iron oxide.", "wikidata_id": "Q16963274", "wikidata_description": "award for lifetime achievement by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander artist"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ungría", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "ungría", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ungría", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ungría", "Hungria"], "contexts": ["European country"]}, {"entity_id": "ungria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ungria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ungria"], "contexts": ["used in water for application", "origin of Queen's water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ungría", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ungria", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 3193, "stable_key": "clu_b63000866b1d1d2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hungary", "confidence": "high", "type": "