Antidotary", "confidence": "high", "type": "text"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "manjar branco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "manjar_branco", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "manjar branco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["manjar branco"], "contexts": ["food used for comparison of texture and taste"]}, {"entity_id": "manjar_branco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "manjar branco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["manjar branco"], "contexts": ["white dish, a food preparation", "food for the sick"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "manjar branco", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "manjar branco", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3206, "stable_key": "clu_eddb65856ea303de", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Manjar branco is a pure white Brazilian coconut pudding similar to blancmange. It is identical to the Puerto Rican tembleque. In Brazil manjar branco is made in a ring (Savarin) mold and is served with a sauce made of pitted prunes poached in port wine. Commercial mixes for manjar branco and tembleque are available in Brazil, the U.S., and in other Latin American countries. In order to improve the texture and taste, unsweetened shredded coconut may be added to the mix. Manjar branco in Portugal usually refers to blancmange, but the manjar branco of Coimbra is an unusual dessert made from chicken breast, milk, sugar, rice flour, orange zest, and salt."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "braccio", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "braça", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "braça", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["braça"], "contexts": ["unit of measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "braccio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "braccio", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["braccio", "braccia"], "contexts": ["unit of measurement for height", "unit of measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "braça", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "braccio", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 3207, "stable_key": "clu_0e6f69ea91eb1818", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Braccio (unit of length)", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "An Italian unit of length, approximately 0.63 meters."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Soria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "soria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Soria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Soria"], "contexts": ["country where Muse are from"]}, {"entity_id": "soria", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Soria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Soria"], "contexts": ["country of origin for oriental balm", "country where Cippero root originates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Soria", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Soria", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3208, "stable_key": "clu_af5d5692906229a6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Soria", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Mulerus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "muuer", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "MuUer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["MuUer"], "contexts": ["editor of Geogr. Gr. Minores"]}, {"entity_id": "mulerus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mulerus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mulerus", "Mulero"], "contexts": ["author of Miracula Chymica", "author of chemical miracles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "MuUer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mulerus", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3209, "stable_key": "clu_15a9389bd4dc2ff9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mulerus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Editor and author in medicine and science."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Libra", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "libra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "libra", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["libra"], "contexts": ["unit of weight"]}, {"entity_id": "libra", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Libra", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Libra"], "contexts": ["constellation where conjunction took place", "constellation with planetary conjunction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "libra", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Libra", "similarity": 0.71}], "id": 3210, "stable_key": "clu_a05771fd2662a747", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Libra", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to both a unit of weight (pound) and a constellation."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "scale", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 747, "target_cluster_name": "scale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Libra is a Latin word for 'balance' or 'scale', and was historically used to refer to weighing instruments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Libra is a Latin word for 'balance' or 'scale', and was historically used to refer to weighing instruments.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Universidade de Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "universidade_de_coimbra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Universidade de Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Universidade de Coimbra"], "contexts": ["university in Portugal"]}, {"entity_id": "universidade_de_coimbra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Universidade de Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Universidade de Coimbra"], "contexts": ["university where Antonio Mendes taught", "institution where Padre Luis taught"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Universidade de Coimbra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Universidade de Coimbra", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3211, "stable_key": "clu_0f5cc4eb8ef2ff31", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "The University of Coimbra (UC) is a public research university in Coimbra, Portugal. Officially chartered by King Denis on March 1, 1290, and recognised by Pope Nicholas IV on August 9, 1290, it is the oldest university in Portugal and one of the world's oldest in continuous operations, having begun operations in Lisbon before moving permanently to Coimbra in 1537. It had an influential role in the development of higher education in the Portuguese-speaking countries, and in their history. Due to its historic influence, architecture, unique culture and traditions, it was declared in 2013 a World Heritage Site by UNESCO. The university is organized into eight faculties, granting bachelor's (licenciatura), master's (mestrado) and doctorate (doutoramento) degrees in nearly all major fields. It lends its name to the Coimbra Group of European research universities founded in 1985, of which it was a founding member."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Coimbra", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1964, "target_cluster_name": "Coimbra", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The University of Coimbra is a major institution located in the city of Coimbra, indicating a strong geographical and institutional connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The University of Coimbra is a major institution located in the city of Coimbra, indicating a strong geographical and institutional connection.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "abelha", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "abelha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "abelha", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["abelha"], "contexts": ["bee that makes honey"]}, {"entity_id": "abelha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "abelha", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["abelha", "abelhas"], "contexts": ["bee, source of venom", "bee sting treatment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "abelha", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "abelha", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3212, "stable_key": "clu_d4fc73cfc12a6626", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "João Batista Lopes Abelha (born 3 January 1958), simply known as Abelha, is a Brazilian former professional footballer and manager who played as a goalkeeper. Abelha began his career at Ferroviária, a club in the city where he was born. In 1984 he transferred to Flamengo where he won the Guanabara Cup. He arrived at São Paulo to compete for a position with Barbirotto, but was unsuccessful, and then played for São Bento, Inter de Limeira, Botafogo de Ribeirão Preto, ABC, Honda Hamamatsu and São Bento, his last club in 1992. Living in Japan, he began his career as a goalkeeper coach in 1994, assisting Paulo Roberto Falcão on the Japan national football team. He managed São Bento for the first time in 1997, and in 1999 he was Nelsinho Baptista's assistant at Colo-Colo."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the Portuguese word for 'bee', the producer of honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is the Portuguese word for 'bee', the producer of honey.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "insects", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 4, "target_cluster_name": "insects", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for 'bee', a type of insect.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Portuguese for 'bee', a type of insect.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Portuguese for 'bee'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Portuguese for 'bee'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Caſtelhanos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "ckistelhanos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ckistelhanos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ckistelhanos"], "contexts": ["people from Castile"]}, {"entity_id": "caſtelhanos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Caſtelhanos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Caſtelhanos", "Caftelhanos"], "contexts": ["Spaniards", "Castilians, referring to Spanish people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ckistelhanos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Caſtelhanos", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3213, "stable_key": "clu_377e775f1501d94e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Castilians", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to people from the historical region of Castile.", "wikidata_id": "Q217196", "wikidata_description": "former country"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portuguezes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 218, "target_cluster_name": "Portuguezes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Castilians are a related Iberian ethnic group, and historical interactions between Portuguese and Castilians (Spaniards) are significant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Castilians are a related Iberian ethnic group, and historical interactions between Portuguese and Castilians (Spaniards) are significant.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rodriguez", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2198, "target_cluster_name": "Rodriguez", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cabeza de Vaca was a Castilian explorer.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cabeza de Vaca was a Castilian explorer.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "bolotas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "bolota", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bolota", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bolota"], "contexts": ["acorn-like fruit"]}, {"entity_id": "bolotas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bolotas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["bolotas", "bolotas fec.cas"], "contexts": ["acorns used in broth", "acorn shells used in preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bolota", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bolotas", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3214, "stable_key": "clu_ba539190e69b30f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Acorns", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "family": "Fagaceae", "description": "Fruits of oak trees, used in preparations and as food.", "note": "The mention of 'bolla' (myrrh) and 'bolandas' (items from Africa) suggests potential semantic drift or confusion in the historical texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "This glossary of botanical terms is a list of definitions of terms and concepts relevant to botany and plants in general. Terms of plant morphology are included here as well as at the more specific Glossary of plant morphology and Glossary of leaf morphology. For other related terms, see Glossary of phytopathology, Glossary of lichen terms, and List of Latin and Greek words commonly used in systematic names. Surface of an organ facing away from the organ's axis, e.g. the lower surface of a lateral organ such as a leaf or petal. Contrast adaxial. To abandon development of a structure or organ."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "leycenços", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "leicenços", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "leicenços", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["leicenços"], "contexts": ["skin eruptions, possibly erysipelas"]}, {"entity_id": "leycenços", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "leycenços", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["leycenços"], "contexts": ["skin eruption", "ulcers, a consequence of thick milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "leicenços", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "leycenços", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 3215, "stable_key": "clu_16b103b83744807a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "erysipelas", "confidence": "medium", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "erysipelas", "note": "The term 'leicenços' is not a standard medical term. Erysipelas is a plausible interpretation based on the description of skin eruptions.", "wikidata_id": "Q207092", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Infection", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1792, "target_cluster_name": "Infection", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Erysipelas is a bacterial infection, making 'Infection' a direct and relevant category.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Erysipelas is a bacterial infection, making 'Infection' a direct and relevant category."}]}, {"canonical_name": "cumo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "çumo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "çumo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["çumo"], "contexts": ["meaning of tabaxir"]}, {"entity_id": "cumo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cumo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["cumo", "cumbos", "cumos"], "contexts": ["incense for rubbing", "plant part, mixed with milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "çumo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cumo", "similarity": 0.806}], "id": 3216, "stable_key": "clu_29ad4367dd9df421", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bamboo sap", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The context 'tabaxir' strongly suggests tabasheer, a siliceous deposit from bamboo, which is botanically a plant product.", "wikidata_id": "Q1642659", "wikidata_description": "substance obtained from babmoo"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "bambu", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2449, "target_cluster_name": "bambu", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Directly derived from bamboo.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Directly derived from bamboo.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "vinho verde", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ou vinho verde, aque chamão de enforcado , ſerá muyto melhor ,& defte vinho coado ,& efpremido daráő ao doente de cinco em cinco horas quatro onças. O çumo de tres duzias debichos , chamados Millepede", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "a type of wine, also called 'de enforcado'"}, {"found_name": "de enforcado", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ou vinho verde, aque chamão de enforcado , ſerá muyto melhor ,& defte vinho coado ,& efpremido daráő ao doente de cinco em cinco horas quatro onças. O çumo de tres duzias debichos , chamados Millepede", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "a vernacular name for 'vinho verde'"}]}, {"canonical_name": "parteyras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "parteiras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "parteiras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["parteiras"], "contexts": ["midwives"]}, {"entity_id": "parteyras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "parteyras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 3, "variants": ["parteyras", "Parteiras", "Parteyras"], "contexts": ["midwives, causing head issues in newborns", "medical practitioner"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "parteiras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "parteyras", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 3217, "stable_key": "clu_07f25d0376594a87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "midwife", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Practitioner assisting in childbirth, sometimes causing newborn head issues.", "wikidata_id": "Q185196", "wikidata_description": "medical professional who practices obstetrics as a health science", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midwife", "wikipedia_extract": "A midwife (pl.: midwives) is a health professional who cares for mothers and newborns around childbirth, a specialisation known as midwifery. The education and training for a midwife concentrates extensively on the care of women throughout their lifespan; concentrating on being experts in what is normal and identifying conditions that need further evaluation. In most countries, midwives are recognised as skilled healthcare providers. Midwives are trained to recognise variations from the normal progress of labour and understand how to deal with deviations from normal. They may intervene in high risk situations such as breech births, twin births, using non-invasive techniques. For complications related to pregnancy and birth that are beyond the midwife's scope of practice, including surgical and instrumental deliveries, they refer their patients to physicians or surgeons."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fale", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fale", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "fale", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["fale"], "contexts": ["fossil substance from metal veins", "mineral prepared by art"]}, {"entity_id": "fale", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fale", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["fale"], "contexts": ["salt"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "fale", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fale", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3218, "stable_key": "clu_3b76b970cb7a27ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "calamine", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The description 'fossil substance from metal veins' and the association with 'salt' in early modern texts often refers to calamine, a zinc ore.", "wikidata_id": "Q408008", "wikidata_description": "sorosilicate mineral"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "acqua rola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "acqua_rola", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "acqua rola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Acqua Rola", "acqua rola"], "contexts": ["rose water", "rose water, a common solvent and ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "aguarolada", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "aguaRolada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["aguaRolada", "agua rolada"], "contexts": ["rolled water used in a preparation", "rolled water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "acqua rola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aguaRolada", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3219, "stable_key": "clu_92b7459405e05123", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rose water", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'rolled water' is mentioned, the primary and more common referent in early modern texts is rose water.", "wikidata_id": "Q900450", "wikidata_description": "flavoured water made by steeping rose petals in water", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_water", "wikipedia_extract": "A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa (), in the family Rosaceae (), or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. They form a group of plants that can be erect shrubs, climbing, or trailing, with stems that are often armed with sharp prickles. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows. Most species are native to Asia, with smaller numbers native to Europe, North America, and Northwest Africa. Species, cultivars and hybrids are all widely grown for their beauty and often are fragrant."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Vermiculare", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "vermiculare", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Vermiculare", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Vermiculare"], "contexts": ["ingredient in an ointment", "plant mentioned in a list"]}, {"entity_id": "vermicular", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Vermicular", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Vermicular"], "contexts": ["herb superior to others for scorbutic affections", "herb, medicinal plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Vermiculare", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vermicular", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 3220, "stable_key": "clu_dfadf14de73a4173", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vervain", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Verbena officinalis", "family": "Verbenaceae", "note": "The term 'vermiculare' likely refers to plants in the genus Verbena, commonly known as vervain. While the specific species isn't definitively stated, Verbena officinalis is the most common medicinal vervain."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "figa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "ficine", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "ficine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ficine"], "contexts": ["a plant's pulp"]}, {"entity_id": "figa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "figa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["figa"], "contexts": ["liver", "liver ailments cured by well water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "ficine", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "figa", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 3221, "stable_key": "clu_71a9475b5dd92989", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fig", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ficus carica", "family": "Moraceae", "note": "'Figa' and 'ficine' clearly refer to the fig fruit and tree, Ficus carica.", "wikidata_id": "Q1759071", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Pastilhas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "pastilli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "pastilli", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["pastilli"], "contexts": ["Latin term for trochisci"]}, {"entity_id": "pastilhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pastilhas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pastilhas", "Pastilhas contra camaras", "pastilhas"], "contexts": ["form of medicine", "medicinal preparations discussed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "pastilli", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pastilhas", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 3222, "stable_key": "clu_51989c7660a26c4d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pills", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Dosage forms of medicine, often compounded.", "wikipedia_extract": "The combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP), often referred to as the birth control pill or colloquially as \"the pill\", is a type of birth control that is designed to be taken orally by women. It is the oral form of combined hormonal contraception. The pill contains two important hormones: a progestin (a synthetic form of the hormone progestogen/progesterone) and estrogen (usually ethinylestradiol or 17β estradiol). When taken correctly, it alters the menstrual cycle to eliminate ovulation and prevent pregnancy. Combined oral contraceptive pills were first approved for contraceptive use in the United States in 1960, and remain a very popular form of birth control. They are used by more than 100 million women worldwide  including about 9 million women in the United States."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Catapotia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3951, "target_cluster_name": "Catapotia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'Pills' and 'Pastilhas' refer to solid dosage forms of medicine intended for oral administration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both 'Pills' and 'Pastilhas' refer to solid dosage forms of medicine intended for oral administration.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "feffo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fefta", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Fefta", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fefta"], "contexts": ["medical preparation name"]}, {"entity_id": "feffo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "feffo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["feffo"], "contexts": ["rectum", "affected by water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Fefta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feffo", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3223, "stable_key": "clu_b297925a42c8f144", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scrofula", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Scrofula", "note": "'Fefta' and 'feffo' are likely archaic or variant spellings/pronunciations of 'scrofula', a disease that historically had various names and manifestations. Contexts referencing preparations and body parts align with this interpretation.", "wikidata_id": "Q4678337", "wikidata_description": "human medical condition"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ciatica", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1501, "target_cluster_name": "Ciatica", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins, the Juyce being used as a Bath un∣to them, or made into an Oyntment: It is no less effectual to give eas of pains to the hot Gout,, the Sciatica, and the Inflamations and Swelling", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Kernels or Knots in the Neck or Throat", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cobras", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1535, "target_cluster_name": "cobras", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "applied outwardly, helpeth the Kernels of the Throat, commonly called the Kings evil; or taken inwardly and applied to the place stung or bitten by any Serpent, healeth it speedily. If the Roots be br", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Kernels of the Throat", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "veronica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "veronica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "veronica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Veronica", "veronica"], "contexts": ["common name for Abrotanum femina", "alternative name for abrotea"]}, {"entity_id": "veronica", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "veronica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["veronica"], "contexts": ["flower found in Himalaya", "flower not found in Himalaya"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "veronica", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "veronica", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3224, "stable_key": "clu_ddbbb5d231e8daa1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "germander", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Teucrium chamaedrys", "family": "Lamiaceae", "note": "In early modern European texts, 'veronica' often referred to Speedwell (Veronica officinalis). However, in the context of 'Abrotanum femina', it is more likely referring to Germander, specifically Teucrium species, which were sometimes confused or grouped with 'Abrotanum'.", "wikidata_id": "Q157714", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teucrium", "wikipedia_extract": "Teucrium is a cosmopolitan genus of flowering plants in the mint family Lamiaceae, commonly known as germanders. Plants in this genus are perennial herbs or shrubs, with branches that are more or less square in cross-section, leaves arranged in opposite pairs, and flowers arranged in thyrses, the corolla with mostly white to cream-coloured, lobed petals. Plants in the genus Teucrium are perennial herbs or shrubs with four-cornered stems, often with simple hairs and sessile glands. The leaves are arranged in opposite pairs, simple or with three leaflets sometimes with lobed or serrated edges. The flowers are arranged in a thyrse, sometimes in a cyme in leaf axils. The flowers have five more or less similar sepals fused at the base, and the corolla is white or cream-coloured with five lobes forming two lips."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "folhas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 195, "target_cluster_name": "folhas", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "deaguabebão oximel muyto brando, em quedeytemde infuſaohuma mãocheadeAbro- tanumfemina,aqueosHerbolarios chamão Veronica ,que tem (alem dehú cheyromuyto agradavel )as folhas dofeytiodas do Acyprefte ,", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: a plant also called Abrotanum semen", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Oximel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 671, "target_cluster_name": "Oximel", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "detodos oslegumes;aconſelho-lhes porèm ,que comão todos os dias ( ſe puderem ) miolos depardacs,&devitela juntamenteguizados ,&que emlugar deaguabebão oximel muyto brando, em quedeytemde infuſaohuma m", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for Abrotanumsemina", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "conceit", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2946, "target_cluster_name": "conceit", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "Nominal apprehension of Plants; to instance but in few. An herb there is commonly called _Betonica Pauli_, or _Pauls Betony_; hereof the People have some conceit in reference to St. _Paul_; whereas in", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "rectificar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "rectificar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "rectificar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["rectificar", "Rectificar"], "contexts": ["purifying by redistillation", "chemical process of preparing medicines"]}, {"entity_id": "correction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "correction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["correction"], "contexts": ["adjustment to a value", "adjustment to data"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "rectificar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "correction", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3225, "stable_key": "clu_1bf2a0033b3b99b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rectification", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'rectificar' in the context of scientific texts, especially with 'purifying by redistillation' and 'correction' related to values, points to the chemical process of rectification."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lerius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lelius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lelius"], "contexts": ["author of conf. med."]}, {"entity_id": "lerius", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lerius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lerius"], "contexts": ["early navigator", "early navigator comparing sea to meadows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Lelius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lerius", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3226, "stable_key": "clu_e53b535868cedaf0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gerardus Mercator", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1512, "death_year": 1594, "description": "Flemish cartographer and geographer, influential in mapmaking.", "note": "The name 'Lerius' or 'Lelius' is likely a misspelling or archaic variant of 'Mercator'. The context of 'early navigator' and 'author of conf. med.' (likely referring to 'confessio' or 'configurationes', relevant to cartography) supports this identification, though direct textual evidence is missing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "algæ", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 957, "target_cluster_name": "algæ", "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "parent plant grew. The quantity of algæ in that accumulation known as the sargassa or grassy sea is so great, that the early navigators, Columbus and Lerius, compared it to extensively inundated meado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of plant found in the sargassa sea"}, {"found_name": "sargassa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "parent plant grew. The quantity of algæ in that accumulation known as the sargassa or grassy sea is so great, that the early navigators, Columbus and Lerius, compared it to extensively inundated meado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "accumulation of algae, also called the grassy sea"}, {"found_name": "grassy sea", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "parent plant grew. The quantity of algæ in that accumulation known as the sargassa or grassy sea is so great, that the early navigators, Columbus and Lerius, compared it to extensively inundated meado", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for the sargassa sea"}]}, {"canonical_name": "celestial body", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "celestial_body", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "celestial body", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["celestial body"], "contexts": ["object in outer space", "object in space"]}, {"entity_id": "heavenly_bodies", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "heavenly bodies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["heavenly bodies"], "contexts": ["celestial objects like planets and stars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "celestial body", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "heavenly bodies", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 3227, "stable_key": "clu_ab4d7470088509bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Celestial body", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6999", "wikidata_description": "physical body of astronomically-significant size, mass, or role, naturally occurring in a universe", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astrological_sign", "wikipedia_extract": "In Western astrology, astrological signs are the zodiac, twelve 30-degree sectors that are crossed by the Sun's 360-degree orbital path as viewed from Earth in its sky. The signs enumerate from the first day of spring, known as the First Point of Aries, which is the vernal equinox. The astrological signs are Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarius, and Pisces. The Western zodiac originated in Babylonian astrology, and was later influenced by the Hellenistic culture. Each sign was named after a constellation the sun annually moved through while crossing the sky. This observation is emphasized in the simplified and popular sun sign astrology."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "astronomy", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 276, "target_cluster_name": "astronomy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Celestial bodies are the primary objects of study in astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Celestial bodies are the primary objects of study in astronomy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "latitude", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 551, "target_cluster_name": "latitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Latitude is a coordinate used to specify position on a celestial body, including the Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Latitude is a coordinate used to specify position on a celestial body, including the Earth.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "solar system", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1528, "target_cluster_name": "solar system", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Sun is the central celestial body of our solar system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Sun is the central celestial body of our solar system.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Longitude", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 310, "target_cluster_name": "Longitude", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Longitude is a coordinate used to specify a position on the surface of a celestial body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Longitude is a coordinate used to specify a position on the surface of a celestial body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Jupiter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 553, "target_cluster_name": "Jupiter", "source_book": "llm_gene