, which are forms of gaseous emissions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The description mentions sulfur dioxide is released naturally by volcanic activity, which are forms of gaseous emissions.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "silkworm", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "silk-worms", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Silk-worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Silk-worms"], "contexts": ["insects whose seed hatched on women"]}, {"entity_id": "silkworm", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "silkworm", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["silkworm"], "contexts": ["larva of a moth producing silk", "larva producing silk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Silk-worms", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "silkworm", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 3296, "stable_key": "clu_b3d214ab40381691", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Silkworm", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q134747", "wikidata_description": "insect from the moth family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombyx_mori", "wikipedia_extract": "Bombyx mori, commonly known as the domestic silk moth, is a domesticated moth species belonging to the family Bombycidae. It is the closest relative of Bombyx mandarina, the wild silk moth. Silkworms are the larvae of silk moths. The silkworm is of particular economic value, being a primary producer of silk. The silkworm's preferred food are the leaves of white mulberry, though they may eat other species of mulberry, and even leaves of other plants. Domestic silk moths are entirely dependent on humans for reproduction, as a result of millennia of selective breeding."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "caterpillar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 908, "target_cluster_name": "caterpillar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A silkworm is a specific type of caterpillar, known for producing silk.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A silkworm is a specific type of caterpillar, known for producing silk.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Silkworms are insects that produce a valuable substance (silk), and are sometimes discussed alongside other insects that produce valuable substances like honey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Silkworms are insects that produce a valuable substance (silk), and are sometimes discussed alongside other insects that produce valuable substances like honey.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Birdlime", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3843, "target_cluster_name": "Birdlime", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Birdlime was historically made from the berries of the mistletoe plant, which is a host plant for the silkworm's wild relative, Bombyx mandarina. Both are associated with specific plant-insect interactions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Birdlime was historically made from the berries of the mistletoe plant, which is a host plant for the silkworm's wild relative, Bombyx mandarina. Both are associated with specific plant-insect interactions."}, {"found_name": "larvæ", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2720, "target_cluster_name": "larvæ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Silkworms are a specific type of larva (of the domestic silk moth, Bombyx mori) that were of significant economic and scientific interest in premodern times, often discussed in relation to their larval stage and development.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Silkworms are a specific type of larva (of the domestic silk moth, Bombyx mori) that were of significant economic and scientific interest in premodern times, often discussed in relation to their larval stage and development.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Swan", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "swan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Swan", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Swan"], "contexts": ["form Jupiter took to beget Leda"]}, {"entity_id": "swan", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Swan", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Swan"], "contexts": ["constellation where a new star was discovered", "vanishing star in the swan"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Swan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Swan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3297, "stable_key": "clu_c4bce4e24b99a403", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Swan", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q55829744", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_extract": "The mute swan (Cygnus olor) is a species of swan and a member of the waterfowl family Anatidae. It is native to much of Europe and parts of Asia and (as a rare winter visitor) the far north of Africa. It is an introduced species in North America, home to the largest populations outside of its native range, with additional smaller introductions in Australasia and Southern Africa. The name \"mute\" derives from it being less vocal than other swan species. Measuring 125 to 160 cm (49 to 63 in) in length, this large swan is wholly white in plumage, with an orange beak bordered with black. It is recognisable by its pronounced knob atop the beak, which is larger in males.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mute_swan"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Eternity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "eternity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Eternity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Eternity"], "contexts": ["concept of endless time"]}, {"entity_id": "eternity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "eternity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["eternity"], "contexts": ["infinite or unending time"]}, {"entity_id": "eternal", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "eternal", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["eternal"], "contexts": ["lasting forever"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Eternity", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "eternity", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Eternity", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "eternal", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 3298, "stable_key": "clu_518ff5b57a5d1453", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eternity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q138045", "wikidata_description": "endless time, an infinite duration"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Space", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 240, "target_cluster_name": "Space", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Eternity is often discussed in relation to the vastness and timelessness of space and the universe.", "c