ty is a concept directly related to the philosophical and theological understanding of time, often as its absence or infinite extension.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Eternity is a concept directly related to the philosophical and theological understanding of time, often as its absence or infinite extension.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "vivacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vivacity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "vivacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vivacity"], "contexts": ["quality of being lively or energetic"]}, {"entity_id": "vivacity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vivacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vivacity"], "contexts": ["lively and animated state", "liveliness and energy"]}, {"entity_id": "vitality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "vitality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vitality"], "contexts": ["state of being strong and active"]}, {"entity_id": "vitality", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vitality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vitality"], "contexts": ["quality of being alive"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "vivacity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vivacity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3299, "stable_key": "clu_091abf74ea76c997", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vitality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q24702794", "wikidata_description": "French esports organisation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Team%20Vitality", "wikipedia_extract": "Team Vitality, or simply Vitality, is a French esport club founded in 2013 by Fabien \"Neo\" Devide, Nicolas Maurer, Corentin \"Gotaga\" Houssein, and Kevin \"BrokyBrawks\" Georges. Team Vitality has teams in eleven PC/console esports titles (League of Legends, Valorant, Counter-Strike 2, Rocket League, EA FC, StarCraft II, Tekken, Rennsport, Street Fighter 6, Call of Duty Warzone and Teamfight Tactics), with several professional teams and content creators from across Europe and South Korea. Vitality also has teams in four mobile esports titles (Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, Honor of Kings, Free Fire and PUBG Mobile) under the name \"Bigetron by Vitality\" after they acquired the Indonesian organization Bigetron Esports in 2025. The organization has previously fielded teams in numerous other titles, including Call of Duty, Call of Duty Mobile, Fortnite, Rainbow Six: Siege, PUBG, H1Z1, Clash Royale, Street Fighter, Formula 1, Hearthstone, Gears of War and Arena of Valor, as well as previously operating in India. Since 2019, the organization has made the Stade de France its training center, becoming the first resident club of the stadium. Team Vitality entered the professional League of Legends on 9 December 2015, after it acquired Gambit Gaming's spot in the 2016 EU LCS Spring Split."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hemorrhoi∣dal_veins", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins", "Hemorrhoidal Veins"], "contexts": ["veins around the anus", "veins affected by bleeding"]}, {"entity_id": "arteria_venoſa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "arteria venoſa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["arteria venoſa"], "contexts": ["venous artery, a blood vessel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hemorrhoi∣dal Veins", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "arteria venoſa", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3300, "stable_key": "clu_16843c64abed0509", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hemorrhoidal veins", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "note": "The term 'arteria venosa' in this context likely refers to a vessel that carries venous blood but might have arterial-like characteristics or be part of the arterial system feeding the hemorrhoids. However, 'hemorrhoidal veins' is the most direct interpretation of the primary term."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "living forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "living_form", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "living form", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["living form"], "contexts": ["essential nature of a living being"]}, {"entity_id": "living_forms", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "living forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["living forms"], "contexts": ["organisms with organizational capacity", "all biological organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "living form", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "living forms", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 3301, "stable_key": "clu_f66a10452d7d688f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "living organisms", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This term refers to entities possessing life and organizational capacity, contrasting with inorganic matter."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Retentive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "retentive", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Retentive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Retentive", "Retentive Faculty"], "contexts": ["bodily virtue", "bodily function of retention"]}, {"entity_id": "retentiva", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Retentiva", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Retentiva"], "contexts": ["retentive virtue"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Retentive", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Retentiva", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 3302, "stable_key": "clu_424f58d4142689e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Retentive", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A bodily virtue or function related to the power of retention.", "wikipedia_extract": "Anal retentiveness is a personality trait that is characterized by excessive concern with details. The concept originated in Freudian psychoanalytic theory, where one aspect of the anal stage of psychosexual development is pleasure in the retention of feces. Fixation in this stage can potentially result in a personality marked by frugality, obstinacy and orderliness. Despite its psychoanalytic roots and the literal meaning of the words, in common usage the term generally refers merely to certain kinds of obsessive behaviour. In Freudian psychology, the anal stage is said to follow the oral stage of infant or early-childhood development, and occurs from around the age of eighteen months to three years old. The infant's attention moves from oral stimulation to anal stimulation, usually synchronous with learning to control its excretory functions.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anal_retentiveness"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bacchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bacchus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Bacchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bacchus", "Bacchinus"], "contexts": ["god to whom vine was dedicated", "commends Chamomel syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "bacchus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bacchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bacchus"], "contexts": ["god of wine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Bacchus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bacchus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3303, "stable_key": "clu_c3ef53cb5a789b01", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bacchus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman god of wine, fertility, and revelry.", "note": "This refers to the mythological deity, not a historical person.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacchus", "wikipedia_extract": ""}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hercules", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 495, "target_cluster_name": "Hercules", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are figures from Roman mythology. Bacchus is a god, while Hercules is a demigod/hero, but both are prominent figures in ancient Roman narratives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are figures from Roman mythology. Bacchus is a god, while Hercules is a demigod/hero, but both are prominent figures in ancient Roman narratives.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Apollo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 841, "target_cluster_name": "Apollo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bacchus is another major deity in Greek and Roman mythology, like Apollo, representing revelry and ecstasy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Bacchus is another major deity in Greek and Roman mythology, like Apollo, representing revelry and ecstasy.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Kidney Bean", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "kidney_bean", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Kidney Bean", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kidney Bean", "Kidneywort"], "contexts": ["alternative name for French Bean", "plant with thick, flat, round leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "kidney-bean", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "kidney-bean", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["kidney-bean"], "contexts": ["plant used as an example in acclimatisation debate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Kidney Bean", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "kidney-bean", "similarity": 0.832}], "id": 3304, "stable_key": "clu_86a4d5776b8ba0e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kidney Bean", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Phaseolus vulgaris", "family": "Fabaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q1077410", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lablab", "wikipedia_extract": "Lablab purpureus is a species of bean in the family Fabaceae. It is native to sub-Saharan Africa and it is cultivated throughout the tropics for food. English language common names include hyacinth bean, lablab-bean bonavist bean/pea, dolichos bean, seim or sem bean, lablab bean, Egyptian kidney bean, Indian bean, bataw and Australian pea. Lablab is a monotypic genus. The name lablab which is also capitalized as its genus name is given by Robert Sweet from the previous name of Dolichos lablab by Carl Linnaeus, its epithet comes from Arabic: لَبْلَاب, romanized: lablāb. According to the British biologist and taxonomist Bernard Verdcourt, there are two cultivated subspecies of Lablab purpureus (L.) Sweet: Lablab purpureus subsp."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Leguminosa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2184, "target_cluster_name": "Leguminosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kidney Bean is a specific type of legume, and Fabaceae is the family that includes legumes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kidney Bean is a specific type of legume, and Fabaceae is the family that includes legumes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Peas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2708, "target_cluster_name": "Peas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Peas and Kidney Beans are both legumes, belonging to the Fabaceae family, and are commonly cultivated for their edible seeds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Peas and Kidney Beans are both legumes, belonging to the Fabaceae family, and are commonly cultivated for their edible seeds.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lupini", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2714, "target_cluster_name": "Lupini", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Lupins and Kidney Beans are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Lupins and Kidney Beans are members of the legume family (Fabaceae).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Noli me Tangere", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "noli_me_tangere", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Noli me Tangere", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Noli me Tangere", "Nolime tangere"], "contexts": ["aggressive spreading ulcer", "disease name with reference"]}, {"entity_id": "noli_me_tangere", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Noli me tangere", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noli me tangere"], "contexts": ["a type of cancerous sore"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Noli me Tangere", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Noli me tangere", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3305, "stable_key": "clu_d19290315bcacdea", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Noli me tangere ('touch me not') is the Latin version of a phrase spoken, according to John 20:17, by Jesus to Mary Magdalene when she recognized him after His resurrection. The original Koine Greek phrase is Μή μου ἅπτου (mḗ mou háptou). The biblical scene has been portrayed in numerous works of Christian art from Late Antiquity to the present. The phrase has also been used in literature, and later in a variation by military units since the late 18th century. According to Maurice Zundel (1897–1975), in asking Mary Magdalene not to touch him, Jesus indicates that once the resurrection is accomplished, the link between human beings and his person must no longer be physical, but must be a bond of heart to heart. \"He must establish this gap, she must understand that the only possible way is faith, that the hands can not reach the person and that it is from within, from within only, that the we can approach Him.\" Likewise, later, when Thomas reached out to touch the wounds of Jesus, Christ declares: \"blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed\" [John 20:29] because \"He knows it is useless.\" Christians of Western Catholic tradition, namely Catholics, Lutherans, and Anglicans, would say this statement is to be received in relation to the Ascension of Jesus. That is, because he had not yet ascended to the right hand of God, it was more of a \"not yet\" statement rather than a \"never\" cling to me."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "leprolos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1291, "target_cluster_name": "leprolos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Noli me Tangere is a historical term often used synonymously with or as a specific type of severe skin ulceration, which could include manifestations of leprosy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Noli me Tangere is a historical term often used synonymously with or as a specific type of severe skin ulceration, which could include manifestations of leprosy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Swellings", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 55, "target_cluster_name": "Swellings", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "and applied to the Forehead; It also helpeth the Spots or Blemishes that happen in or about the Eyes, or in any other parts: The said wa∣ter fomented on Swellings and hot Inflamati∣ons of Womens sore ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: a type of spreading ulcer", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Religion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "religion", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Religion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Religion", "Reli∣gion"], "contexts": ["system of faith and worship", "matters of faith and belief"]}, {"entity_id": "religion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "religion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["religion"], "contexts": ["belief in a higher power or divine order"]}, {"entity_id": "religious", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "religious", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["religious"], "contexts": ["rel