hip.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Idolatry is a concept within the broader domain of religion, specifically concerning the nature and object of worship.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Dukes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "dukes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dukes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dukes"], "contexts": ["supported Mathiolus' work", "supported the art"]}, {"entity_id": "dukes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "dukes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["dukes"], "contexts": ["high-ranking noble title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dukes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "dukes", "similarity": 0.748}], "id": 3307, "stable_key": "clu_f9b23bd3d23c0dce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dukes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the noble title 'Duke', not a specific person.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Duque de Baviera", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2100, "target_cluster_name": "Duque de Baviera", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Duke of Bavaria' is a specific instance of the general title 'Dukes'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The 'Duke of Bavaria' is a specific instance of the general title 'Dukes'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Skins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "skins", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Skins", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Skins"], "contexts": ["eye condition", "used to wrap affected area after Pepperwort"]}, {"entity_id": "pelle", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pelle", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["pelle"], "contexts": ["skin of the rhinoceros"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Skins", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pelle", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3308, "stable_key": "clu_09153cd528a240de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Skin", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'pelle' can refer to skin generally, in the context of the rhinoceros, it specifically means its thick hide, which is a type of skin.", "wikidata_id": "Q272206", "wikidata_description": "family of reptiles (Reptilia)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin", "wikipedia_extract": "Skin is the layer of usually soft, flexible outer tissue covering the body of a vertebrate animal, with three main functions: protection, regulation, and sensation. Other animal coverings, such as the arthropod exoskeleton, have different developmental origin, structure and chemical composition. The adjective cutaneous means \"of the skin\" (from Latin cutis 'skin'). In mammals, the skin is an organ of the integumentary system made up of multiple layers of ectodermal tissue and guards the underlying muscles, bones, ligaments, and internal organs. Skin of a different nature exists in amphibians, reptiles, and birds. Skin (including cutaneous and subcutaneous tissues) plays crucial roles in formation, structure, and function of extraskeletal apparatus such as horns of bovids (e.g., cattle) and rhinos, cervids' antlers, giraffids' ossicones, armadillos' osteoderm, and os penis/os clitoris."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cellular tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1440, "target_cluster_name": "cellular tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin is composed of cellular tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Skin is composed of cellular tissue."}, {"found_name": "Membranes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1813, "target_cluster_name": "membrana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin is a type of membrane.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Skin is a type of membrane."}, {"found_name": "Nervous tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin contains nervous tissue responsible for sensation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Skin contains nervous tissue responsible for sensation."}, {"found_name": "Bodily fluid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 144, "target_cluster_name": "moisture", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin can secrete bodily fluids like sweat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Skin can secrete bodily fluids like sweat."}, {"found_name": "Body", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 220, "target_cluster_name": "Body", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Skin is a major part of the body's external covering.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Skin is a major part of the body's external covering."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Buds", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "buds", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Buds", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Buds"], "contexts": ["part of the Bramble plant", "part of the Black Poplar tree"]}, {"entity_id": "buds", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "buds", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["buds"], "contexts": ["undeveloped plant shoots"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Buds", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "buds", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3309, "stable_key": "clu_2717b588cb23cab0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Buds", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Buds are a universal plant structure, so the specific plant is not determinative of the general term.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Folio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 358, "target_cluster_name": "Folio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buds are immature leaves, often developing into leaves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Buds are immature leaves, often developing into leaves.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Branches", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 806, "target_cluster_name": "Branches", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Buds are found on branches and develop into leaves or flowers.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Buds are found on branches and develop into leaves or flowers.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Birds-Nest", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "birds-nest", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Birds-Nest", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Birds-Nest"], "contexts": ["shape of flower cluster", "shape of the umbel"]}, {"entity_id": "nests_of_birds", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "nests of birds", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nests of birds"], "contexts": ["structures built by birds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Birds-Nest", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "nests of birds", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 3310, "stable_key": "clu_9c75b2fd56d00a7d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bird's nest", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to actual nests built by birds or structures that resemble them.", "wikidata_id": "Q30056", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daucus%20carota", "wikipedia_extract": "Daucus carota, whose common names include wild carrot, European wild carrot, bird's nest, bishop's lace, carrot flower, and Queen Anne's lace (North America), is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae. It is native to temperate regions of the Old World with a number of regional subspecies, and is naturalised widely elsewhere. Carrots cultivated as a food crop are cultivars of the domesticated subspecies Daucus carota subsp. sativus. The wild carrot is a herbaceous, somewhat variable biennial plant that grows to 30–100 cm (1–3 ft), rarely 120 cm (4 ft) tall, and is roughly hairy, with a stiff, solid stem. The leaves are tripinnate, finely divided and lacy, and overall triangular in shape."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sweet", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sweet", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "sweet", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sweet"], "contexts": ["quality of cherries, pass through stomach speedily", "quality of plums"]}, {"entity_id": "sweet", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sweet", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sweet"], "contexts": ["quality of wine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "sweet", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sweet", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3311, "stable_key": "clu_7c3947e7ee372fa1", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hairs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hairs", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "hairs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["hairs"], "contexts": ["on the eyelids", "on Vipers Buglofs stalks"]}, {"entity_id": "hair_of_plant", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hair of plant", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hair of plant"], "contexts": ["plant structure resembling hair"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hair of plant", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 3312, "stable_key": "clu_e92e39da067d00e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trichome", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Early modern texts often referred to plant structures resembling hair as 'hairs'. Trichomes are the scientific term for these structures.", "wikidata_id": "Q1125215", "wikidata_description": "fine hair-like growth on plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trichome", "wikipedia_extract": "Trichomes (; from Ancient Greek  τρίχωμα (tríkhōma) 'hair') are fine outgrowths or appendages on plants, algae, lichens, and certain protists. They are of diverse structure and function. Examples are hairs, glandular hairs, scales, and papillae. A covering of any kind of hair on a plant is an indumentum, and the surface bearing them is said to be pubescent. Certain, usually filamentous, algae have the terminal cell produced into an elongate hair-like structure called a trichome. The same term is applied to such structures in some cyanobacteria, such as Spirulina and Oscillatoria."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hemp", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hemp", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hemp", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hemp"], "contexts": ["well-known plant for housewives", "plant with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "hemp", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hemp", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hemp"], "contexts": ["seed that germinated after bird digestion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hemp", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "hemp", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 3313, "stable_key": "clu_ea28339d2a647a07", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hemp", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cannabis sativa", "family": "Cannabaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q26726", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannabis%20sativa", "wikipedia_extract": "Cannabis sativa is an annual herbaceous flowering plant. The species was first classified by Carl Linnaeus in 1753. The specific epithet sativa means 'cultivated'. Indigenous to Eastern Asia, the plant is now of cosmopolitan distribution due to widespread cultivation. It has been cultivated throughout recorded history and used as a source of industrial fiber, seed oil, food, and medicine. It is also used as a recreational drug and for religious and spiritual purposes."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Pope", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pope", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pope", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pope"], "contexts": ["mentioned in relation to St. Peter", "selfish and associated with Imps"]}, {"entity_id": "pope", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Pope", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pope"], "contexts": ["author of Martinus Scriblerus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pope", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Pope", "similarity": 0.766}], "id": 3314, "stable_key": "clu_526aa19087524f3e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pope", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Title of the Bishop of Rome", "note": "This cluster refers to the office of the Pope, not a specific individual, given the context. The reference to 'author of Martinus Scriblerus' might point to Alexander Pope, but the general term 'Pope' in a religious context is more likely given the other mention."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "wild Roses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "wild_roses", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "wild Roses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Wild Roses", "wild Roses"], "contexts": ["type of rose", "nature compared to manured roses"]}, {"entity_id": "wild_rose", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "wild rose", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wild rose"], "contexts": ["species of rose"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "wild Roses", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "wild rose", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 3315, "stable_key": "clu_10d5542c8118b879", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wild rose", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Rosa spp.", "family": "Rosaceae", "note": "Refers to various species of wild roses, not a single specific Linnaean binomial.", "wikidata_id": "Q218368", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosa%20acicularis", "wikipedia_extract": "Rosa acicularis is a flowering plant in the Rosaceae family. It is commonly known as the prickly wild rose, prickly rose, bristly rose, wild rose or Arctic rose. It is a species of wild rose with a Holarctic distribution in northern regions of Asia, Europe, and North America. Rosa acicularis is a deciduous shrub growing 1–3 m tall. The leaves are pinnate, 7–14 cm long, with three to seven leaflets. The leaflets are ovate, with serrate (toothed) margins."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "tormentilla", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1862, "target_cluster_name": "tormentilla", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tormentil (Potentilla erecta) is a species within the rose family (Rosaceae), and 'wild rose' is a related common name for plants in the same family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tormentil (Potentilla erecta) is a species within the rose family (Rosaceae), and 'wild rose' is a related common name for plants in the same family.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "rosa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 849, "target_cluster_name": "rosa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wild rose is a direct precursor and closely related species to the cultivated rose.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wild rose is a direct precursor and closely related species to the cultivated rose.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Strawberries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1070, "target_cluster_name": "Strawberries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The genus Fragaria, which includes the strawberry, is related to roses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The genus Fragaria, which includes the strawberry, is related to roses.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "petals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2726, "target_cluster_name": "petals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wild rose is a specific type of flower that possesses petals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wild rose is a specific type of flower that possesses petals.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Spring Water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "spring_water", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Spring Water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Spring Water"], "contexts": ["water used for infusion", "water used for decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "spring_water", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spring water", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["spring water"], "contexts": ["water from a natural spring"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Spring Water", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spring water", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 3316, "stable_key": "clu_b0776fa39a170a48", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spring water", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "wikidata_id": "Q158996", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_extract": "Humic substances (HS) are relatively recalcitrant colored organic compounds naturally formed during long-term decomposition and transformation of biomass residues. The color of humic substances varies from bright yellow to light or dark brown leading to black. The term comes from humus, which in turn comes from the Latin word humus, meaning \"soil, earth\". Humic substances constitute the majority of organic matter in soil, peat, coal, and sediments, and are important components of dissolved natural organic matter (NOM) in lakes (especially dystrophic lakes), rivers, and sea water. Humic substances account for 50 – 90% of cation exchange capacity in soils. \"Humic substances\" is an umbrella term covering humic acid, fulvic acid, and humin, which differ in solubility.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humic_substance"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Digestive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "digestive", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Digestive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Digestive"], "contexts": ["bodily virtue"]}, {"entity_id": "digestivo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "digestivo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestivo"], "contexts": ["aiding digestion"]}, {"entity_id": "digestiva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "digestiva", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestiva"], "contexts": ["medicinal property of tamarind pulp"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Digestive", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "digestivo", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Digestive", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "digestiva", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3317, "stable_key": "clu_e0d8d7fcead7bec2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "digestion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11978", "wikidata_description": "biological process of breaking down food and other matter", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maltodextrin", "wikipedia_extract": "Maltodextrin is a name shared by two different families of chemicals. Both families are glucose polymers (also called dextrose polymers or dextrins), but have little chemical or nutritional similarity. The digestible maltodextrins (or simply maltodextrins) are  manufactured as white solids derived from chemical processing of plant starches. They are used as food additives, which are digested rapidly, providing glucose as food energy. They are generally recognized as safe (GRAS) for food and beverage manufacturing in numerous products. Due to their rapid production of glucose, digestible maltodextrins are potential risks for people with diabetes."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Appetite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2030, "target_cluster_name": "Appetite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Appetite is the desire to eat, which precedes and stimulates digestion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Appetite is the desire to eat, which precedes and stimulates digestion."}, {"found_name": "Animal heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3893, "target_cluster_name": "animal heat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Digestion is a source of animal heat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Digestion is a source of animal heat."}, {"found_name": "Chemical change", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 989, "target_cluster_name": "chemical change", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Digestion involves chemical breakdown of food.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Digestion involves chemical breakdown of food."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Barberry", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "barberry", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Barberry", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Barberry"], "contexts": ["shrub with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "barberry", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Barberry", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Barberry", "barberry"], "contexts": ["genus with insect-assisted stamen spring", "flowers of"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Barberry", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Barberry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3318, "stable_key": "clu_0446aa6b8a21e523-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "barberry", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Berberis vulgaris", "family": "Berberidaceae", "description": "Shrub with tart red berries, used in traditional medicine and as an ingredient.", "wikipedia_extract": "Berberis vulgaris, also known as common barberry, European barberry or simply barberry, is a shrub in the genus Berberis native to the Old World. It produces edible but sharply acidic berries, which people in many countries eat as a tart and refreshing fruit. It is a deciduous shrub growing up to 4 metres (13 feet) high. The leaves are small, oval, 2–5 centimetres (3⁄4–2 inches) long and 1–2 cm (1⁄2–3⁄4 in) broad, with a serrated margin; they are borne in clusters of 2–5 together, subtended by a three-branched spine 3–8 millimetres (1⁄8–5⁄16 in) long. The flowers are yellow, 4–6 mm (1⁄8–1⁄4 in) across, produced on 3–6 cm (1+1⁄8–2+3⁄8 in) long panicles in late spring. The fruit is an oblong red berry 7–10 mm (1⁄4–3⁄8 in) long and 3–5 mm (1⁄8–3⁄16 in) broad, ripening in late summer or autumn."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dolores viejos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "old_pains", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "old pains", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["old pains"], "contexts": ["chronic discomfort"]}, {"entity_id": "dolores_viejos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "dolores viejos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["dolores viejos"], "contexts": ["old pains", "old head pains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "old pains", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dolores viejos", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 3319, "stable_key": "clu_731ac1ce2f972d3f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chronic pain", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Chronic pain", "note": "The phrases 'old pains' and 'dolores viejos' describe persistent, long-lasting discomfort, aligning with the modern concept of chronic pain."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "reumas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2224, "target_cluster_name": "reumas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rheumatism is characterized by chronic pain affecting joints or connective tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Rheumatism is characterized by chronic pain affecting joints or connective tissue.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "enfermedades largas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2327, "target_cluster_name": "enfermedades largas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chronic pain is a common manifestation and symptom of chronic diseases.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chronic pain is a common manifestation and symptom of chronic diseases.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "contusões", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "contusions", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "contusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["contusions"], "contexts": ["bruises from impact"]}, {"entity_id": "contusões", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "contusões", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["contusões"], "contexts": ["bruises", "condition treated by negundo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "contusions", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "contusões", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 3320, "stable_key": "clu_a99fd74f79882061-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wounds", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An injury to living tissue, typically involving a break in the skin.", "note": "Includes various types of wounds like 'green Wounds' and 'inward Wounds'. 'Contusões' (bruises) are also a form of wound.", "wikipedia_extract": "A wound is any disruption of or damage to living tissue, such as skin, mucous membranes, or organs. Wounds can either be the sudden result of direct trauma (mechanical, thermal, chemical), or can develop slowly over time due to underlying disease processes such as diabetes mellitus, venous/arterial insufficiency, or immunologic disease. Wounds can vary greatly in their appearance depending on wound location, injury mechanism, depth of injury, timing of onset (acute vs chronic), and wound sterility, among other factors. Treatment strategies for wounds will vary based on the classification of the wound, therefore it is essential that wounds be thoroughly evaluated by a healthcare professional for proper management. In normal physiology, all wounds will undergo a series of steps collectively known as the wound healing process, which include hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and tissue remodeling. Age, tissue oxygenation, stress, underlying medical conditions, and certain medications are just a few of the many factors known to affect the rate of wound healing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "feridas", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1630, "target_cluster_name": "feridas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'Wounds' from Portuguese/Spanish.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Direct translation of 'Wounds' from Portuguese/Spanish."}, {"found_name": "inward Wounds", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1310, "target_cluster_name": "inward Wounds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A specific type of wound mentioned in the list, directly related to the general term 'Wounds'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "A specific type of wound mentioned in the list, directly related to the general term 'Wounds'."}, {"found_name": "quebraduras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 800, "target_cluster_name": "quebraduras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Contusions (bruises) are often associated with the trauma that causes fractures.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Contusions (bruises) are often associated with the trauma that causes fractures.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ulcers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3702, "target_cluster_name": "ulceras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ulcers are a specific type of wound characterized by open sores, often resulting from underlying disease or injury.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ulcers are a specific type of wound characterized by open sores, often resulting from underlying disease or injury."}, {"found_name": "Fistula", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 670, "target_cluster_name": "Fistula's", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A fistula is an abnormal passage or duct between two organs or from an organ to the surface of the body, often resulting from infection or injury, thus a type of wound or complication of a wound.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A fistula is an abnormal passage or duct between two organs or from an organ to the surface of the body, often resulting from infection or injury, thus a type of wound or complication of a wound."}, {"found_name": "Fracture", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 800, "target_cluster_name": "quebraduras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A fracture is a break in a bone, which is a form of trauma to the skeletal system, often occurring alongside or as a result of external wounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A fracture is a break in a bone, which is a form of trauma to the skeletal system, often occurring alongside or as a result of external wounds."}, {"found_name": "Hemorrhage", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4160, "target_cluster_name": "hemorrhages", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hemorrhage is bleeding, which is a direct consequence of many types of wounds that breach blood vessels.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Hemorrhage is bleeding, which is a direct consequence of many types of wounds that breach blood vessels."}, {"found_name": "Inflammation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 581, "target_cluster_name": "Inflamations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Inflammation is a common biological response to injury and infection, and is a key component of the healing process or complication of wounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Inflammation is a common biological response to injury and infection, and is a key component of the healing process or complication of wounds."}]}, {"canonical_name": "gallons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gallons", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gallons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gallons"], "contexts": ["unit of volume"]}, {"entity_id": "gallons", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gallons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["gallons"], "contexts": ["unit of volume for syrup", "unit of liquid measure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Gallons", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "gallons", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 3321, "stable_key": "clu_977712d1c26397a7", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "The gallon is a unit of volume in British imperial units and United States customary units. The imperial gallon (imp gal) is defined as 4.54609 litres, and is or was used in the United Kingdom and its former colonies, including Ireland, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, India, South Africa, Malaysia and some Caribbean countries, while the US gallon (US gal) is defined as 231 cubic inches (3.785411784 L), and is used in the United States and some Latin American and Caribbean countries. There are four gills in a pint, two pints in a quart, and four quarts (quarter gallons) in a gallon, with the imperial gill being divided into five imperial fluid ounces and the US gill being divided into four US fluid ounces: this, and a slight difference in the sizes of the imperial fluid ounce and the US fluid ounce, give different sizes for the imperial gallon and US gallon. The IEEE standard symbol for both the imperial and US gallons is gal, not to be confused with the gal (symbol: Gal), a CGS unit of acceleration. The gallon currently has two definitions, in the imperial system and in the US customary system. Historically, there were many definitions and redefinitions: see § Sizes of gallons for details."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "miles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2202, "target_cluster_name": "miles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gallons are a unit of volume, and while the mile is a unit of length, both are historical units of measurement used in commerce and trade.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gallons are a unit of volume, and while the mile is a unit of length, both are historical units of measurement used in commerce and trade.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Spaniards", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "spaniards", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Spaniards", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spaniards"], "contexts": ["use columbine root for kidney stones"]}, {"entity_id": "spaniard", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Spaniard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spaniard"], "contexts": ["person from Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "spaniards", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Spaniards", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spaniards"], "contexts": ["introduced horses to South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Spaniards", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Spaniard", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Spaniards", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Spaniards", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3322, "stable_key": "clu_1a1025ae12988339", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spaniards", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'Spaniards' refers to people from Spain, the context here points to a collective identity and their actions/introductions, rather than specific individuals. It functions as a conceptual grouping in historical narratives.", "wikidata_id": "Q29", "wikidata_description": "country in southwestern Europe with territories in Africa", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain", "wikipedia_extract": "Spain, officially the Kingdom of Spain, is a country in Southern and Western Europe with territories in North Africa. Featuring the southernmost point of continental Europe, it is the largest country in Southern Europe and the fourth-most populous European Union (EU) member state. Spanning the majority of the Iberian Peninsula, its territory also includes the Canary Islands, in the Eastern Atlantic Ocean; the Balearic Islands, in the Western Mediterranean Sea; and the autonomous cities of Ceuta and Melilla, in mainland Africa. Peninsular Spain is bordered to the north by France, Andorra, and the Bay of Biscay; to the east and south by the Mediterranean Sea and Gibraltar; and to the west by Portugal and the Atlantic Ocean. Spain's capital and largest city is Madrid; other major urban areas include Barcelona, Valencia, Seville, Zaragoza, Málaga, Murcia, and Palma de Mallorca. In early antiquity, the Iberian Peninsula was inhabited by Celts, Iberians, and other pre-Roman peoples."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Espagne", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 549, "target_cluster_name": "Espagne", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the French name for Spain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "This is the French name for Spain."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spain is a country located within the continent of Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Spain is a country located within the continent of Europe."}, {"found_name": "Castile", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2765, "target_cluster_name": "Castille", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Castile was a major kingdom and historical region within Spain, central to its formation and identity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Castile was a major kingdom and historical region within Spain, central to its formation and identity."}, {"found_name": "Algarve", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2147, "target_cluster_name": "Algarve", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Algarve is a historical region in southern Portugal, which shares a border and significant historical interactions with Spain.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Algarve is a historical region in southern Portugal, which shares a border and significant historical interactions with Spain."}]}, {"canonical_name": "morphea", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "morphews", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Morphews", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Morphews"], "contexts": ["skin condition"]}, {"entity_id": "morphea", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "morphea", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Morphea", "morphea"], "contexts": ["skin condition differing from Ictericia", "skin ailment treated by unguent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Morphews", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "morphea", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 3323, "stable_key": "clu_e9892108b00360c5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Morphea", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Morphea", "note": "The spelling variations ('Morphews', 'morphea') and the context of a skin condition strongly indicate this dermatological term.", "wikidata_id": "Q3324389", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morphea", "wikipedia_extract": "Morphea is a form of scleroderma that mainly involves isolated patches of hardened skin on the face, hands, and feet, or anywhere else on the body, usually with no internal organ involvement. However, in Deep Morphea inflammation and sclerosis can be found in the deep dermis, panniculus, fascia, superficial muscle and bone. Morphea most often presents as macules or plaques a few centimeters in diameter, but also may occur as bands or in guttate lesions or nodules. Morphea is a thickening and hardening of the skin and subcutaneous tissues from excessive collagen deposition. Morphea includes specific conditions ranging from very small plaques only involving the skin to widespread disease causing functional and cosmetic deformities. Morphea discriminates from systemic sclerosis by its supposed lack of internal organ involvement."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Banco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "banks", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Banks", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Banks"], "contexts": ["natural habitat for plants"]}, {"entity_id": "banco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Banco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Banco"], "contexts": ["location with phonolitic and granitic rocks", "north of Penon de Rosa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Banks", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Banco", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 3324, "stable_key": "clu_87514a720035e514", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bank", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Various", "note": "The context of 'Banco' with phonolitic and granitic rocks suggests a geological formation or a specific named place known for its geology. 'Banks' in the English physician's text, related to natural habitats for plants, points to land formations along rivers or bodies of water. The term is ambiguous but 'bank' as a geographical feature is the most probable overlap.", "wikipedia_extract": "Banco do Brasil S.A. (pronounced [ˈbɐ̃ku du bɾaˈziw], lit. 'Bank of Brazil') is a Brazilian financial services company headquartered in Brasília, Brazil. The oldest bank in Brazil, and among the oldest banks in continuous operation in the world, it was founded by John VI, King of Portugal, on Wednesday, 12 October 1808. It is the second largest banking institution in Brazil, as well as the second largest in Latin America. Banco do Brasil is controlled by the Brazilian government and is listed at the B3 stock exchange in São Paulo.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banco_do_Brasil"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "drones", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "drones", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Drones", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Drones"], "contexts": ["male bees"]}, {"entity_id": "drones", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "drones", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["drones"], "contexts": ["male bees", "male bees produced in large numbers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Drones", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "drones", "similarity": 0.832}], "id": 3325, "stable_key": "clu_3e31d9ae73b73de7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Drone bee", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Specifically refers to the male bee.", "wikipedia_extract": "A honey bee (also spelled honeybee) is a eusocial flying insect from the genus Apis of the largest bee family, Apidae. Honey bees are known for their construction of perennial nests within cavities (i.e. beehives) containing hexagonal cells made of secreted wax, their large colony sizes, and their routine regurgitation of digested carbohydrates as surplus food storage in the form of honey, the lattermost of which distinguishes their hives as a prized foraging target of many mellivorous animals including honey badgers, bears and human hunter-gatherers. Although honey bees represent only a small fraction of the roughly 20,000 known species of bees, they are the bee clade most familiar to humans and are also the most valuable beneficial insects to agriculture and horticulture. The best-known honey bee species is the western honey bee (Apis mellifera), which was domesticated and farmed (i.e. beekeeping) for honey production and crop pollination.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_bee"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The drone bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The drone bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melipona", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2530, "target_cluster_name": "Melipona", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Drone bees are a specific type of bee within a colony, and Melipona bees form colonies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Drone bees are a specific type of bee within a colony, and Melipona bees form colonies.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alquimistas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "alchymists", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Alchymists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alchymists"], "contexts": ["practitioners of alchemy"]}, {"entity_id": "alquimistas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Alquimistas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["alquimistas", "Alquimistas"], "contexts": ["alchemists who speak of gold's matter", "practitioners of alchemy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Alchymists", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Alquimistas", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 3326, "stable_key": "clu_740d6d6da4624d15", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alchemists", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Practitioners of alchemy and transmutational arts.", "note": "The cluster refers to the practitioners of alchemy, a prominent practice in the early modern period, rather than specific individuals.", "wikidata_id": "Q947275", "wikidata_description": "computer puzzle game from PopCap Games", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alchemy", "wikipedia_extract": "Alchemy (from the Arabic word al-kīmīā, الكیمیاء) is an ancient branch of natural philosophy, a philosophical and protoscientific tradition that was historically practised in China, India, the Muslim world, and Europe. In its Western form, alchemy is first attested in a number of pseudepigraphical texts written in Greco-Roman Egypt during the first few centuries AD. Greek-speaking alchemists often referred to their craft as \"the Art\" (τέχνη) or \"Knowledge\" (ἐπιστήμη), and it was often characterised as mystic (μυστική), sacred (ἱɛρά), or divine (θɛíα). Alchemists attempted to purify, mature, and perfect certain materials. Common aims were chrysopoeia, the transmutation of \"base metals\" (e.g., lead) into \"noble metals\" (particularly gold); the creation of an elixir of immortality; and the creation of panaceas able to cure any disease. The perfection of the human body and soul was thought to result from the alchemical magnum opus (\"Great Work\")."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Boticarios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 587, "target_cluster_name": "Boticarios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alchemists were historically closely associated with apothecaries, often sharing knowledge and practices related to the preparation of substances and remedies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alchemists were historically closely associated with apothecaries, often sharing knowledge and practices related to the preparation of substances and remedies.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Paracelsus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 703, "target_cluster_name": "Paracelsus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paracelsus is a central and highly influential figure within the historical tradition of alchemy, often considered a key innovator and proponent of its principles and practices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Paracelsus is a central and highly influential figure within the historical tradition of alchemy, often considered a key innovator and proponent of its principles and practices.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Feminine parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "feminine_parts", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Feminine parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Feminine parts"], "contexts": ["female reproductive organs"]}, {"entity_id": "fementhe", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fementhe", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fementhe"], "contexts": ["semen"]}, {"entity_id": "partes_uterinas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "partes uterinas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["partes uterinas"], "contexts": ["parts related to the uterus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Feminine parts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fementhe", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Feminine parts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "partes uterinas", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3327, "stable_key": "clu_fbe21211f6ec1358", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Female reproductive system", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms 'Feminine parts', 'fementhe', and 'partes uterinas' all point to anatomical structures and concepts related to female reproduction. 'Fementhe' is likely a variant or corruption. 'Female reproductive system' is a broader and more accurate modern concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q648065", "wikidata_description": "diminished or absent ability of a female to achieve conception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Female%20infertility", "wikipedia_extract": "Female infertility refers to infertility in women, which is the inability to conceive after sexual intercourse. It affects an estimated 48 million women, globally. Female infertility varies widely by region, The highest rates of female infertility are found in Eastern Europe and South Central Asia, followed by South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, and Middle East and North Africa. Female infertility rates are lowest in East Asia and Latin America. Fertility is affected by a variety of factors, including hormones, nutritional status, disease, and malformations of the uterus. Infertility affects women from around the world, and the social stigmas affecting infertile women may vary from region to region."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "madre", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 47, "target_cluster_name": "madre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The womb is a primary organ within the female reproductive system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The womb is a primary organ within the female reproductive system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "flash of lightning", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "blastings_by_lightning", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Blastings by Lightning", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Blastings by Lightning"], "contexts": ["treated with oil of roses"]}, {"entity_id": "flash_of_lightning", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "flash of lightning", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["flash of lightning"], "contexts": ["sudden electrical discharge", "caused by discharge between clouds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Blastings by Lightning", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "flash of lightning", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 3328, "stable_key": "clu_712d8d97bd2c0331", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lightning strike", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Lightning strike", "wikipedia_extract": "A lightning strike or lightning bolt is a lightning event in which an electric discharge takes place between the atmosphere and the ground. Most originate in a cumulonimbus cloud and terminate on the ground, called cloud-to-ground (CG) lightning. A less common type of strike, ground-to-cloud (GC) lightning, is upward-propagating lightning initiated from a tall grounded object and reaching into the clouds. About 25% of all lightning events worldwide are strikes between the atmosphere and earth-bound objects. Most are intracloud (IC) lightning and cloud-to-cloud (CC), where discharges only occur high in the atmosphere. Lightning strikes the average commercial aircraft at least once a year, but modern engineering and design means this is rarely a problem."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "olor horrible", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "evil_smel", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "evil smel", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["evil smel"], "contexts": ["symptom from gums"]}, {"entity_id": "olor_horrible", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "olor horrible", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["olor horrible", "sabor horrible"], "contexts": ["horrible smell from stomach", "horrible taste from stomach"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "evil smel", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "olor horrible", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 3329, "stable_key": "clu_89f270e0303bd88a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bad breath", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Halitosis", "note": "While \"olor horrible\" is a descriptive symptom, \"halitosis\" is the modern medical term for an offensive smell from the mouth or nose, fitting the context of gums and stomach smells.", "wikidata_id": "Q16408", "wikidata_description": "presence of unpleasant odors in exhaled breath", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bad%20breath", "wikipedia_extract": "Bad breath, also known as halitosis, is a symptom in which a noticeably unpleasant breath odour is present. It can result in anxiety among those affected. It is also associated with depression and symptoms of obsessive compulsive disorder. The concerns of bad breath may be divided into genuine and non-genuine cases. Of those who have genuine bad breath, about 85% of cases come from inside the mouth. The remaining cases are believed to be due to disorders in the nose, sinuses, throat, lungs, esophagus, or stomach."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Agrippa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "agrippa", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Agrippa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Agrippa"], "contexts": ["early modern author"]}, {"entity_id": "agrippa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Agrippa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Agrippa"], "contexts": ["King of Judea, composed an ointment", "King of Judea composed an ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Agrippa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agrippa", "similarity": 0.72}], "id": 3330, "stable_key": "clu_e187e4f6e17bc4e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Agrippa", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Early modern author, King of Judea, composed an ointment.", "note": "This is highly ambiguous. Without further context, it's impossible to definitively identify which 'Agrippa' is being referred to. It could be Agrippa the Younger (known for his ointment), or another figure named Agrippa mentioned in early modern texts. The mention of 'King of Judea' is particularly confusing as most prominent Agrippas are not associated with this title.", "wikidata_id": "Q313419", "wikidata_description": "king of Chalcis (Syria) from Herodian dynasty (28-100)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herod%20Agrippa%20II", "wikipedia_extract": "Herod Agrippa II (Roman name: Marcus Julius Agrippa, Hebrew: אגריפס; AD 27/28 – c. 92 or 100), sometimes shortened to Agrippa II or Agrippa, was the last ruler from the Herodian dynasty, reigning over territories outside of Judea as a Roman client. Agrippa II fled Jerusalem in 66, fearing the Jewish uprising, and he supported the Roman side in the First Jewish–Roman War. Herod Agrippa II was the son of the first and better-known Herod Agrippa and the brother of Berenice, Mariamne, and Drusilla (second wife of the Roman procurator Antonius Felix). He was educated at the court of the emperor Claudius, and at the time of his father's death he was 17 years old. Claudius therefore kept him at Rome and sent Cuspius Fadus as procurator of the Roman province of Judaea."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Paracelsus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 703, "target_cluster_name": "Paracelsus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent figures associated with occultism, alchemy, and natural philosophy in the early modern period, often discussed in similar intellectual contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent figures associated with occultism, alchemy, and natural philosophy in the early modern period, often discussed in similar intellectual contexts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Nero", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1007, "target_cluster_name": "Nero", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Agrippa was Nero's father-in-law and a prominent figure during his reign.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Agrippa was Nero's father-in-law and a prominent figure during his reign.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Magitians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1398, "target_cluster_name": "Magitians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa was a highly influential figure in the Renaissance occult tradition, authoring 'De Occulta Philosophia libri tres' (Three Books of Occult Philosophy). His work directly engaged with and systematized magical theories, making him a key figure for anyone studying 'Magitians' or similar concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa was a highly influential figure in the Renaissance occult tradition, authoring 'De Occulta Philosophia libri tres' (Three Books of Occult Philosophy). His work directly engaged with and systematized magical theories, making him a key figure for anyone studying 'Magitians' or similar concepts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Crolius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2163, "target_cluster_name": "Crolius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Croll and Agrippa were prominent figures in the Hermetic and alchemical traditions, and their works often overlapped in subject matter and influence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Croll and Agrippa were prominent figures in the Hermetic and alchemical traditions, and their works often overlapped in subject matter and influence.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cantari", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cantaras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cantaras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cantaras"], "contexts": ["illness treated by herpas"]}, {"entity_id": "cantari", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cantari", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["cantari", "cantari­das"], "contexts": ["medical condition, likely cantharides poisoning", "blisters, likely from cantharides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cantaras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cantari", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3331, "stable_key": "clu_42cbe44edea3892e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cantharidism", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "cantharidism", "note": "The terms 'cantari' and 'cantaras' in a medical context, particularly with the mention of treatment by 'herpas' (likely referring to cantharides), strongly suggest cantharidism (poisoning from blister beetles).", "wikipedia_extract": "Morgan le Fay (; Welsh and Cornish: Morgen; Modern French: Fée Morgane), alternatively known as Morgan[n]a, Morgain[a/e], Morgant[e], Morg[a]ne, Morgayn[e], Morgein[e], and Morgue[in] among other names and spellings, is a powerful and ambiguous enchantress from the legend of King Arthur, in which most often she and he are siblings. According to the different authors, she is a fairy or a human; beneficial or harmful; either a sister, half-sister, or unrelated to Arthur. A significant aspect in many of Morgan's medieval and later iterations is the unpredictable duality of her nature, with potential for both good and evil. Her character may have originated from Welsh mythology as well as from other ancient and medieval myths and historical figures. Early appearances of Morgan in Arthurian literature do not elaborate her character beyond her role as a goddess, a fay, a witch, or a sorceress, generally benevolent and connected to Arthur as his magical saviour and protector. The earliest documented account, by Geoffrey of Monmouth in Vita Merlini (written c.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morgan_le_Fay"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "veneno", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 584, "target_cluster_name": "veneno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cantharidism is a type of poisoning caused by cantharides (Spanish fly).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cantharidism is a type of poisoning caused by cantharides (Spanish fly).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "barba", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "beard", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Beard", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beard"], "contexts": ["body part where hair grows"]}, {"entity_id": "barba", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "barba", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["barba"], "contexts": ["hair of the beard", "beard"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Beard", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barba", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3332, "stable_key": "clu_1b6adcf0fea54af5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Barba (which means \"beard\" in Spanish, Portuguese and Italian) is a surname. Notable people with the surname include: Antonella Barba (born 1986), U.S. singer and contestant on American Idol Angelo Barba (born 1958), Filipino politician Ben Barba (born 1989), Australian rugby footballer Eugenio Barba (born 1936), Italian author and director Federico Barba (born 1993), Italian footballer Fidel LaBarba (1905–1981), U.S. boxer and sportswriter Javier Barba, lead guitar of Mexican rock band Los Depas Juan Barbas (born 1959), Argentine footballer Paul Barbă Neagră (1929–2009), Romanian film director Porfirio Barba-Jacob, pseudonym of Miguel Ángel Osorio Benítez, Colombian poet Victoria Barbă (1926–2020), Moldovan animated film director Barba (footballer) (born 1999), full name João Pedro de Giuli, Brazilian footballer Rafael Barba, (Raúl Esparza) appearing on Law & Order: Special Victims Unit Barbapapa, the title character, and name of the \"species\" of said character, of a series of children's books The Barba River, a tributary of the Vologne in the French Vosges Barba as the Latin root of the word \"beard\" Barba is the last palace dungeon of the sixth dungeon in Zelda II: The Adventure of Link. He is a serpent. When Nintendo released Zelda II in Europe and America, they called the palace boss Barba, but this has since been retconned as Volvagia, which is his original name in the Japanese version of Zelda II."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Greene", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "greens", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Greens", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Greens"], "contexts": ["open grassy areas"]}, {"entity_id": "greene", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Greene", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Greene"], "contexts": ["town in Rhode Island", "location of land purchase"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Greens", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Greene", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 3333, "stable_key": "clu_1d152df0a29cc297", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Green spaces", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The dual context of 'open grassy areas' and a 'town in Rhode Island' suggests 'green spaces' as a general concept, but also potentially a specific place. 'Greene' as a town in Rhode Island is a possible referent, but 'green spaces' better captures the descriptive context.", "wikidata_id": "Q11607807", "wikidata_description": "term of city planning in Japan"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Forrests", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 466, "target_cluster_name": "Forrests", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Green spaces is a broader category that can include forests.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Green spaces is a broader category that can include forests.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "ligamentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ligaments", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ligaments", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ligaments"], "contexts": ["body part bound by disease"]}, {"entity_id": "ligamentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ligamentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["ligamentos", "ligamento"], "contexts": ["connecting ribs to spine", "connecting uterus and intestine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Ligaments", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ligamentos", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3334, "stable_key": "clu_c00173a9e1ead709-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ligaments", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Ligamentos' refers to ligaments. 'Limbs' and 'Ligaments' in the English text confirm this anatomical reference.", "wikipedia_extract": "A ligament is a type of fibrous connective tissue in the body that connects bones to other bones. It also connects flight feathers to bones, in dinosaurs and birds. All 30,000 species of amniotes (land animals with internal bones) have ligaments. It is also known as articular ligament, articular larua, fibrous ligament, or true ligament. Ligaments are similar to tendons and fasciae as they are all made of connective tissue. The differences among them are in the connections that they make: ligaments connect one bone to another bone, tendons connect muscle to bone, and fasciae connect muscles to other muscles.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ligament"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Arm", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 140, "target_cluster_name": "arm", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Arm' is a body part that contains ligaments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Arm' is a body part that contains ligaments."}, {"found_name": "Leg", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2719, "target_cluster_name": "leg", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Leg' is a body part that contains ligaments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Leg' is a body part that contains ligaments."}, {"found_name": "Limbs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 827, "target_cluster_name": "limbs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The 'Limbs' are body parts that contain ligaments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The 'Limbs' are body parts that contain ligaments."}, {"found_name": "Bone", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4023, "target_cluster_name": "bone", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ligaments connect bones to other bones.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ligaments connect bones to other bones."}, {"found_name": "Bones", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 347, "target_cluster_name": "Bones", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ligaments connect bones to other bones.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ligaments connect bones to other bones."}, {"found_name": "Nervous tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ligaments are a type of connective tissue, similar in classification to nervous tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ligaments are a type of connective tissue, similar in classification to nervous tissue."}, {"found_name": "Cellular tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1440, "target_cluster_name": "cellular tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ligaments are a type of connective tissue, similar in classification to cellular tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Ligaments are a type of connective tissue, similar in classification to cellular tissue."}]}, {"canonical_name": "bath", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "baths", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "baths", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["baths"], "contexts": ["a method of bathing"]}, {"entity_id": "bath", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bath", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bath", "bath"], "contexts": ["city where Miss Hawkins is from", "act or place of washing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "baths", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bath", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 3335, "stable_key": "clu_6a1fb32b5311c579", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bathing", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'a method of bathing' clearly points to the act of bathing. The 'city where Miss Hawkins is from' is likely a specific instance of a place associated with bathing, but the core concept is 'bathing'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "chaud et humide", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hot_and_dry", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "hot and dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hot and dry"], "contexts": ["qualities of wormwood in the first degree"]}, {"entity_id": "chaud_et_humide", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "chaud et humide", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["chaud et humide"], "contexts": ["description of climate", "qualities of the environment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "hot and dry", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "chaud et humide", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 3336, "stable_key": "clu_15ddec60438ffc32", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hot and dry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to one of the four humoral qualities (hot, cold, wet, dry) and combinations thereof, prevalent in Galenic and humoral medicine, not specific to temperature."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Benevolence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "benevolence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Benevolence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["benevolence", "Benevolence"], "contexts": ["a phrenological faculty", "social affection arising from transferred pleasure"]}, {"entity_id": "charitable", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "charitable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["charitable"], "contexts": ["type of society with roughly sketched ends"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Benevolence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "charitable", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 3337, "stable_key": "clu_f60cf6df5ad8bf8c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "benevolence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In the context of phrenology, 'benevolence' refers to a specific faculty of the brain.", "wikidata_id": "Q16509717", "wikidata_description": "good disposition to others"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "shadows", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "shadow", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "shadow", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["shadow"], "contexts": ["natural element affecting plant growth"]}, {"entity_id": "shadows", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "shadows", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["shadows"], "contexts": ["absence of light due to obstruction", "coloured fringes bordering shadows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "shadow", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "shadows", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3338, "stable_key": "clu_e12b897a14438578", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A shadow is a dark area on a surface where light from a light source is blocked by an object. In contrast, shade occupies the three-dimensional volume behind an object with light in front of it. The cross-section of a shadow is a two-dimensional silhouette, or a reverse projection of the object blocking the light. A point source of light casts only a simple shadow, called an \"umbra\". For a non-point or \"extended\" source of light, the shadow is divided into the umbra, penumbra, and antumbra. The wider the light source, the more blurred the shadow becomes."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "types", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "types", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "types", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["types"], "contexts": ["categories of organisms", "categories of biological forms"]}, {"entity_id": "type", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "type", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["type"], "contexts": ["a distinct kind or category"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "types", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "type", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3339, "stable_key": "clu_a5eef504a4e423fc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "type", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In early modern scientific texts, 'type' often referred to a distinct kind, category, or species, especially in biology before formal Linnaean taxonomy was universally adopted or when discussing variations within species.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "oceanic islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "oceanic_islands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "oceanic islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["oceanic islands"], "contexts": ["islands in the ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "sea_islands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sea islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sea islands"], "contexts": ["islands in the sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "oceanic islands", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sea islands", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 3340, "stable_key": "clu_3ce88ac8416b3ac4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "oceanic islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q127183048", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Origin of Species", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "origin_of_species", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Origin of Species", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["origin of species", "Origin of Species"], "contexts": ["mystery of biological diversity", "title of a scientific work"]}, {"entity_id": "the_origin_of_species", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "The Origin of Species", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["The Origin of Species"], "contexts": ["Darwin's influential work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Origin of Species", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "The Origin of Species", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 3341, "stable_key": "clu_4edbaa8eb2a08da6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "On the Origin of Species", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The cluster refers to Darwin's 'On the Origin of Species' and also a less well-known work by Herbert Spencer with a similar title. Darwin's work is the primary referent given the context.", "wikidata_id": "Q20124", "wikidata_description": "treatise on natural selection, written by Charles Darwin", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On%20the%20Origin%20of%20Species", "wikipedia_extract": "On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life is a work of scientific literature by Charles Darwin that is considered to be the foundation of evolutionary biology. It was published on 24 November 1859. Darwin's book introduced the scientific theory that populations evolve over the course of generations through a process of natural selection, although Lamarckism was also included as a mechanism of lesser importance. The book presented a body of evidence that the diversity of life arose by common descent through a branching pattern of evolution. Darwin included evidence that he had collected on the Beagle expedition in the 1830s and his subsequent findings from research, correspondence, and experimentation. Various evolutionary ideas had already been proposed to explain new findings in biology."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "beaks", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "beaks", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "beaks", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["beaks"], "contexts": ["part of pigeon anatomy", "mouthparts of birds"]}, {"entity_id": "beyços", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "beyços", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["beyços"], "contexts": ["body part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "beaks", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "beyços", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3342, "stable_key": "clu_07fe7c5a26f11b17", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "beak", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'beak' is a part of an animal, the cluster is tagged as 'SUBSTANCE' and 'ANATOMY'. Given the context of pigeons, it refers to the anatomical feature.", "wikidata_id": "Q31528", "wikidata_description": "external anatomical structure found in birds, non-avian dinosaurs and some mammals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beak", "wikipedia_extract": "The beak or bill is an external rostrum structure found mostly in birds. A beak is used for pecking, grasping, and holding (in probing for food, eating, manipulating and carrying objects, killing prey, or fighting), preening, courtship, and feeding young. The terms beak and rostrum are also used to refer to a similar mouth part in some ornithischians, pterosaurs, cetaceans, dicynodonts, rhynchosaurs, anuran tadpoles, monotremes (i.e. echidnas and platypuses, which have a bill-like structure), sirens, pufferfish, billfishes, and cephalopods. Although beaks vary significantly in size, shape, color and texture, they share a similar underlying structure. Two bony projections–the upper and lower mandibles–are covered with a thin keratinized layer of epidermis known as the rhamphotheca."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "feline animal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "feline_animal", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "feline animal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["feline animal"], "contexts": ["presence affects flower frequency", "animal that preys on mice"]}, {"entity_id": "marsupial_animals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "marsupial animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marsupial animals"], "contexts": ["mammals with pouches"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "feline animal", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "marsupial animals", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3343, "stable_key": "clu_9b6b125f104d2140", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "feline", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The cluster contains 'feline animal' and 'marsupial animals'. The primary referent appears to be 'feline animal'.", "wikidata_id": "Q25265", "wikidata_description": "family of mammals in the order of carnivorans"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "rat", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rat", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "rat", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["rat"], "contexts": ["rodent species", "transported by man, wide range"]}, {"entity_id": "rat", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rat", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["rat"], "contexts": ["patient's self-identification"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "rat", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rat", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3344, "stable_key": "clu_d32df2bd1df34ea8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q184224", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Norwège", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 342, "target_cluster_name": "Norwège", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norwège is the French spelling of Norway.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Norwège is the French spelling of Norway."}, {"found_name": "Arctic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 414, "target_cluster_name": "arctic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norway has significant Arctic territories and coastlines.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Norway has significant Arctic territories and coastlines."}, {"found_name": "Europe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 30, "target_cluster_name": "Europe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norway is a country in Europe.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Norway is a country in Europe."}, {"found_name": "Scandinavia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 810, "target_cluster_name": "Scandinave", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Norway is a core part of the Scandinavian region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Norway is a core part of the Scandinavian region."}]}, {"canonical_name": "immigration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "immigration", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "immigration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["immigration"], "contexts": ["movement of organisms into new areas", "arrival of new species"]}, {"entity_id": "immigration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "immigration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["immigration"], "contexts": ["movement of people into a country"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "immigration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "immigration", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3345, "stable_key": "clu_781e26a160ea8a2b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Immigration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The contexts clearly refer to both biological dispersal and human migration, both understood as 'immigration' in modern terms.", "wikidata_id": "Q131288", "wikidata_description": "movement of people into another country or region to which they are not native", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration", "wikipedia_extract": "Immigration is the international movement of people to a destination country of which they are not usual residents or where they do not possess nationality in order to settle as permanent residents. Commuters, tourists, and other short-term stays in a destination country do not fall under the definition of immigration or migration; seasonal labour immigration is sometimes included, however. Economically, research suggests that migration can be beneficial both to the receiving and sending countries. The academic literature provides mixed findings for the relationship between immigration and crime worldwide. Research shows that country of origin matters for speed and depth of immigrant assimilation, but that there is considerable assimilation overall for both first- and second-generation immigrants. Discrimination based on nationality is legal in most countries."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "population", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1899, "target_cluster_name": "population", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Immigration is a process that directly affects and alters population size and composition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Immigration is a process that directly affects and alters population size and composition.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "integration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1873, "target_cluster_name": "integration", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Immigration is the precursor to migrant integration, as it is the act of people entering a new country.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Immigration is the precursor to migrant integration, as it is the act of people entering a new country.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "salmon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "salmon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "salmon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 2, "variants": ["salmon"], "contexts": ["fish with hooked jaw", "fish with hooked jaws in males"]}, {"entity_id": "salmon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "salmon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 1, "variants": ["salmon"], "contexts": ["fish whose blood contains animalcules"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "salmon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "salmon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3346, "stable_key": "clu_31dc6f23b4dc167e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salmon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "Salmon (; pl.: salmon) are any of several commercially important species of euryhaline ray-finned fish from the genera Salmo and Oncorhynchus of the family Salmonidae, native to tributaries of the North Atlantic (Salmo) and North Pacific (Oncorhynchus) basins. Salmon is a colloquial or common name used for fish in this group, but is not a scientific name. Other closely related fish in the same family include trout, char, grayling, whitefish, lenok and taimen, all coldwater fish of the subarctic and cooler temperate regions with some sporadic endorheic populations in Central Asia. Salmon are typically anadromous: they hatch in the shallow gravel beds of freshwater headstreams and spend their juvenile years in rivers, lakes and freshwater wetlands, migrate to the ocean as adults and live like sea fish, then return to their freshwater birthplace to reproduce. However, populations of several species are restricted to fresh waters (i.e. landlocked) throughout their lives. Folklore has it that the fish return to the exact stream where they themselves hatched to spawn, and tracking studies have shown this to be mostly true."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fish", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 94, "target_cluster_name": "Fish", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Salmon are a type of fish.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Salmon are a type of fish.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cetacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cetacea", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Cetacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cetacea"], "contexts": ["order of marine mammals (whales)", "order of marine mammals including whales"]}, {"entity_id": "cetacea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cetacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cetacea"], "contexts": ["large marine mammals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Cetacea", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cetacea", "similarity": 0.696}], "id": 3347, "stable_key": "clu_22efaac19483dbcd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cetacea", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "This is a direct taxonomic classification.", "wikidata_id": "Q160", "wikidata_description": "infraorder of marine mammals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cetacean", "wikipedia_extract": "Cetaceans () are marine mammals belonging to the infraorder Cetacea, a secondarily aquatic clade under the order Artiodactyla that include whales, dolphins, porpoises and extinct groups such as Basilosaurus. Most cetaceans live in marine environments, particularly the pelagic zone, but some reside solely in brackish or fresh water. Having a cosmopolitan distribution, they can be found in some rivers and all of Earth's oceans. Many species migrate seasonally over vast ranges for food advantages. Key characteristics of cetaceans are their fully aquatic life cycle, streamlined, fish-like body shape, the need to periodically surface and breath air, and exclusively carnivorous diet. All extant cetaceans are capable of echolocation."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mammals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 266, "target_cluster_name": "mammals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cetacea (whales, dolphins, porpoises) are a specific order of mammals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cetacea (whales, dolphins, porpoises) are a specific order of mammals.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Whales", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1970, "target_cluster_name": "Whales", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cetacea is the scientific order that includes whales.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cetacea is the scientific order that includes whales.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mammal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2279, "target_cluster_name": "mammal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cetacea (whales, dolphins, porpoises) are a group of marine mammals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cetacea (whales, dolphins, porpoises) are a group of marine mammals.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "land animals", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "land_animals", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "land animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["land animals"], "contexts": ["terrestrial fauna", "animals living on land"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_animals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial animals"], "contexts": ["animals living on land"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "land animals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "terrestrial animals", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 3348, "stable_key": "clu_6f08c8aee1c03147", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Terrestrial animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The terms 'land animals' and 'terrestrial animals' are synonymous and clearly refer to animals living on land.", "wikidata_id": "Q125761836", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published in 1962"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hearne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hearne", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Hearne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hearne"], "contexts": ["observer of black bear behavior", "on habits of bears"]}, {"entity_id": "hearne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hearne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hearne"], "contexts": ["reported sound from Aurora"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Hearne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hearne", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3349, "stable_key": "clu_21a5af7607eed668", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Samuel Hearne", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1745, "death_year": 1792, "description": "British explorer and naturalist, observed black bears and aurora sounds.", "wikidata_id": "Q715285", "wikidata_description": "British explorer"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Asclepias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "asclepias", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Asclepias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Asclepias"], "contexts": ["genus with pollen-grain contrivance", "pollen of"]}, {"entity_id": "asclepideof", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Asclepideof", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Asclepideof"], "contexts": ["family of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Asclepias", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Asclepideof", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3350, "stable_key": "clu_e1b8ecc5d3f94f44", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Milkweed", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Asclepias", "family": "Apocynaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q311131", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asclepias%20tuberosa", "wikipedia_extract": "Asclepias tuberosa, commonly known as butterfly weed, is a species of milkweed native to eastern and southwestern North America. It is commonly known as butterfly weed because of the butterflies that are attracted to the plant by its color and its copious production of nectar. It is a perennial plant growing to 0.3–1 m (1–3+1⁄2 ft) tall. The leaves are spirally arranged, lanceolate, 3–12 cm (1+1⁄4–4+3⁄4 in) long, and 2–3 cm (3⁄4–1+1⁄4 in) broad. From April to September, in the upper axils, 7.5 cm (3 in)–wide umbels of orange, yellow or red flowers 1.5 cm (1⁄2 in) wide appear. They each have five petals and five sepals."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Paralyticos", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1549, "target_cluster_name": "Paralyticos", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "ſegredo efficazpara que o ſangue ſe não congele , direy ( a favordos enfermos ) oque tenho obſervado para promovera circulaçao ,& adelgaçar os humores dos Paralyticos ,&Apopleticos;&he, darlhes, por l", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: alternative name for Vincetoxico", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "bodily structure", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodily_structure", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "bodily structure", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["bodily structure"], "contexts": ["physical form of the body", "physical form of organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_constitution", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bodily constitution", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily constitution"], "contexts": ["physical makeup of an organism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bodily structure", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodily constitution", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3351, "stable_key": "clu_e8cd25e2c4eedbf9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body constitution", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q66771588", "wikidata_description": "physical characteristics of the body"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "butterfly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "butterfly", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "butterfly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["butterfly"], "contexts": ["flying insect", "insect with larval and adult stages"]}, {"entity_id": "butterfly", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "butterfly", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["butterfly"], "contexts": ["example of longitudinal union in Annulosa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "butterfly", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "butterfly", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 3352, "stable_key": "clu_952fba4c6dbe03cd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Butterfly", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q11946202", "wikidata_description": "group of insects in the order Lepidoptera", "wikipedia_extract": "Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia (). With few exceptions, animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have myocytes and are able to move, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 μm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "caterpillar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 908, "target_cluster_name": "caterpillar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A caterpillar is the larval stage of a butterfly.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A caterpillar is the larval stage of a butterfly.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "vermilion", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "vermilion", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "vermilion", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["vermilion"], "contexts": ["red pigment used to color wax", "coloured wax used in experiment"]}, {"entity_id": "vermilion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vermilion", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vermilion"], "contexts": ["red pigment from mercury sulfide"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "vermilion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "vermilion", "similarity": 0.879}], "id": 3353, "stable_key": "clu_2ceafe575f487d99", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vermilion", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "'Vernucolo' is a recognizable variant for vermilion, a red pigment (cinnabar, mercury sulfide) often used in early modern medicine and art. The context 'vermilion, a red pigment' and the association with poisons (as cinnabar can be toxic) fit well.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lamarck", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lamarck", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Lamarck", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lamarck"], "contexts": ["theorist of inheritance of acquired characteristics", "first called attention to distinction"]}], "edges": [], "id": 3354, "stable_key": "clu_44e059280ac3abdb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste Lamarck", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1744, "death_year": 1829, "description": "French naturalist, inheritance of acquired characteristics theorist", "wikidata_id": "Q654959", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Passiflora", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "passiflora", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Passiflora", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Passiflora"], "contexts": ["plant genus mentioned in fertilization experiments", "plant mentioned with page number"]}, {"entity_id": "passiflora_glabrala", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Passiflora glabrala", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Passiflora glabrala"], "contexts": ["plant from Turbaco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Passiflora", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Passiflora glabrala", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3355, "stable_key": "clu_a64a3d456d81ead3", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Passiflora, known also as the passion flowers or passion vines, is a genus of about 550 species of flowering plants, and the type genus of the family Passifloraceae. Passiflora species are widely cultivated for their striking flowers, flavorful fruits, traditional medicinal uses, and roles in dietary supplements and ayahuasca analogs, with several ornamental hybrids earning Royal Horticultural Society awards. The genus mostly produces tendril-bearing vines, as well as some shrubs and trees. The plants can be woody or herbaceous. The flowers are regular and usually showy, with a distinctive corona. There can be as many as eight concentric coronal series, as in the case of P. xiikzodz. and P. alata."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dianthus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "dianthus", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Dianthus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dianthus"], "contexts": ["genus of flowering plants", "genus with readily crossable species"]}, {"entity_id": "dianthus_caryophyllus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dianthus caryophyllus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dianthus caryophyllus"], "contexts": ["plant cultivated in China"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Dianthus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dianthus caryophyllus", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3356, "stable_key": "clu_d222a75e333636e3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carnation", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Dianthus caryophyllus", "family": "Caryophyllaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q156099", "wikidata_description": "genus of flowering plants"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "capsule", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "capsule", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "capsule", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["capsule"], "contexts": ["seed-bearing structure in plants", "part of a plant containing seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "capsule", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "capsule", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["capsule"], "contexts": ["part of a flower"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "capsule", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "capsule", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3357, "stable_key": "clu_efb009dc148452c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Capsule (botany)", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Refers to the dry fruit of a flowering plant, typically dehiscent.", "wikipedia_extract": "This glossary of botanical terms is a list of definitions of terms and concepts relevant to botany and plants in general. Terms of plant morphology are included here as well as at the more specific Glossary of plant morphology and Glossary of leaf morphology. For other related terms, see Glossary of phytopathology, Glossary of lichen terms, and List of Latin and Greek words commonly used in systematic names. Prefix meaning \"position away from\". Surface of an organ facing away from the organ's axis, e.g. the lower surface of a lateral organ such as a leaf or petal.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_botanical_terms"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "maize", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "maize", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "maize", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["maize"], "contexts": ["plant studied for reproductive system differences", "crop growing on moraines"]}, {"entity_id": "maize", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "maize", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["maize"], "contexts": ["agricultural staple crop"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "maize", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "maize", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3358, "stable_key": "clu_26c93a6bfe886fa6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maize", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Zea mays Linnaeus", "family": "Poaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q11575", "wikidata_description": "species of grass cultivated as a food crop", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize", "wikipedia_extract": "Maize (; Zea mays), also known as corn in North American English, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Corn", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 426, "target_cluster_name": "Corn", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Maize is a common synonym for Corn, particularly in American English.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Maize is a common synonym for Corn, particularly in American English.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Anglesea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "anglesea", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Anglesea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anglesea"], "contexts": ["location of a geological downthrow", "little island with many plants"]}, {"entity_id": "anglesea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anglesea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anglesea"], "contexts": ["island where garnets form"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Anglesea", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Anglesea", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3359, "stable_key": "clu_3ba11fa3c91c1ac7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anglesey", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q168159", "wikidata_description": "island in north-west Wales, UK", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglesey", "wikipedia_extract": "Anglesey ( ANG-gəl-see; Welsh: Ynys Môn [ˈənɨs ˈmoːn]) is an island off the north-west coast of Wales. It forms the bulk of the county known as the Isle of Anglesey, which also includes Holy Island (Ynys Gybi) and some islets and skerries. The county borders Gwynedd across the Menai Strait to the southeast, and is otherwise surrounded by the Irish Sea. Holyhead is the largest town, and the administrative centre is Llangefni. The Isle of Anglesey has an area of 275 square miles (712 km2) and had a population of 69,097 in 2024. After Holyhead, located on Holy Island, the largest settlements are Llangefni in the centre of Anglesey and Amlwch on the northern coast."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "extinct species", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "extinct_species", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "extinct species", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["extinct species"], "contexts": ["species no longer living", "species that no longer exist"]}, {"entity_id": "extinct_beings", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "extinct beings", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["extinct beings"], "contexts": ["organisms no longer living"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "extinct species", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "extinct beings", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3360, "stable_key": "clu_9c423bb5d23bc922", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Extinct species", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the general concept of species that no longer exist, a fundamental idea in evolutionary biology.", "wikidata_id": "Q1385098", "wikidata_description": "fading of non-reinforced conditioned response over time", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_extinct_animals_of_the_British_Isles", "wikipedia_extract": "This is a list of extinct animals of the British Isles, including locally extinct (extirpated) species. The list includes introduced species only in cases where they were able to form self-sustaining colonies for a time. Only species that have become extinct since the onset of the Late Pleistocene, and by extension the beginning of the Last Interglacial (also known as the Ipswichian or Eemian) around 130,000 years ago are included (such that the assemblage that can be approximately considered the 'modern' fauna). The date beside each species is the last date when a specimen was observed in the wild or, where this is not known, the approximate date of extinction. For most of the Neogene and Pleistocene, the British Isles were part of the main continent of Eurasia, linked by a landbridge between southern England and northern France. Throughout the Pleistocene (Ice age) the climate alternated between cold glacial periods, including times when the climate was too cold to support much fauna, and temperate interglacials when a much larger fauna was present."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "species", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 32, "target_cluster_name": "species", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is a direct sub-category or state of the primary entity, 'Species', referring to species that no longer exist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: This is a direct sub-category or state of the primary entity, 'Species', referring to species that no longer exist.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": 