of Winterswijk in the Netherlands. Vanbreenia was named after Dr. P.J.H. van Bree from the Zoological Museum Amsterdam. Its grammatical gender (in Latin) is feminine."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "molluscs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2101, "target_cluster_name": "molluscs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trigonia is a genus of bivalve mollusks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Trigonia is a genus of bivalve mollusks.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Martens", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "martens", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Martens", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Martens"], "contexts": ["conducted similar experiments", "experiment on seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "marty", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Marty", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marty"], "contexts": ["critic of Wundt's apperception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Martens", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Marty", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3362, "stable_key": "clu_f8cbe9fe8ad8cdf8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Friedrich Martens", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1842, "death_year": 1911, "description": "German physicist and meteorologist, conducted atmospheric experiments.", "note": "The mention of 'Marty' alongside 'Martens' might suggest a misreading or a different individual, but given the context of 'experiments' and scientific discourse, Friedrich Martens is a plausible, though not definitively confirmed, referent. The context 'critic of Wundt's apperception' is not directly linked to Martens' known work and might refer to a different individual if the name 'Marty' is accurate."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gmelin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gmelin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Gmelin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gmelin"], "contexts": ["concluded independent creation of species", "on distribution"]}, {"entity_id": "gley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Gley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gley"], "contexts": ["researcher on physiological changes during work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Gmelin", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Gley", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 3363, "stable_key": "clu_965640a9cd6074ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leopold Gmelin", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1853, "description": "German chemist, known for color index and chemical research.", "note": "The context of 'independent creation of species' points to a different Gmelin (Johann Georg Gmelin) or a misattribution. Leopold Gmelin is primarily a chemist. The 'physiological changes during work' is too general. Given the ambiguity and potential for multiple Gmelins, confidence is medium.", "wikidata_id": "Q23776845", "wikidata_description": "Architect, Art Historian"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Valenciennes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "valenciennes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Valenciennes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Valenciennes"], "contexts": ["cited for fish adaptation to fresh water", "on fresh-water fish"]}, {"entity_id": "valenciennes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Valenciennes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valenciennes"], "contexts": ["naturalist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Valenciennes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Valenciennes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3364, "stable_key": "clu_9605a3a1c9f4902e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Achille Valenciennes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1794, "death_year": 1863, "description": "French zoologist, specialized in ichthyology and carcinology.", "wikidata_id": "Q340257", "wikidata_description": "French zoologist, ichthyologist, and malacologist (1794–1865)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achille%20Valenciennes", "wikipedia_extract": "Achille Valenciennes (9 August 1794 – 13 April 1865) was a French zoologist. Valenciennes was born in Paris, and studied under Georges Cuvier. His study of parasitic worms in humans made an important contribution to the study of parasitology. He also carried out diverse systematic classifications, linking fossil and current species. He worked with Cuvier on the 22-volume \"Histoire Naturelle des Poissons\" (Natural History of Fish) (1828–1848), carrying on alone after Cuvier died in 1832. In 1832, he succeeded Henri Marie Ducrotay de Blainville (1777–1850) as chair of Histoire naturelle des mollusques, des vers et des zoophytes at the Muséum national d'histoire naturelle."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Buffon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 473, "target_cluster_name": "Buffon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Georges Cuvier, Buffon's successor as director of the Jardin du Roi.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Georges Cuvier, Buffon's successor as director of the Jardin du Roi.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "M. Bonpland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1671, "target_cluster_name": "M. Bonpland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Aimé Bonpland on the ichthyology section of the 'Voyage de Humboldt et Bonpland'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Aimé Bonpland on the ichthyology section of the 'Voyage de Humboldt et Bonpland'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Milne Edwards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2528, "target_cluster_name": "Milne Edwards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Henri Milne Edwards on several important works, particularly on ichthyology (the study of fish). Their joint publications indicate a close working relationship and shared scientific focus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Achille Valenciennes was a zoologist who collaborated with Henri Milne Edwards on several important works, particularly on ichthyology (the study of fish). Their joint publications indicate a close working relationship and shared scientific focus.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "water-birds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "water-birds", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "water-birds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["water-birds"], "contexts": ["transport seeds of fresh-water plants", "birds adapted for water"]}, {"entity_id": "aquatic_birds", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "aquatic birds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["aquatic birds"], "contexts": ["birds frequenting river spots"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "water-birds", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "aquatic birds", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 3365, "stable_key": "clu_eea68cdec2772b1d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Waterfowl", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'water-birds' and 'aquatic birds' are synonymous and refer to birds that live on or near water.", "wikidata_id": "Q21651", "wikidata_description": "order of birds", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anseriformes", "wikipedia_extract": "Anseriformes is an order of birds   also known as waterfowl that comprises about 180 living species of birds in three families: Anhimidae (three species of screamers), Anseranatidae (the magpie goose), and Anatidae, the largest family, which includes over 170 species of waterfowl, among them the ducks, geese, and swans. Most modern species in the order are highly adapted for an aquatic existence at the water surface. With the exception of screamers, males have penises, a trait that has been lost in the Neoaves, the clade consisting of all other modern birds except the galliformes and paleognaths. Due to their aquatic nature, most species are web-footed. Anseriformes are one of only two types of modern bird to be confirmed present during the Mesozoic alongside the other dinosaurs, and in fact were among the very few birds to survive their extinction, along with their cousins, the Galliformes. These two groups only occupied two ecological niches during the Mesozoic, living in water and on the ground, while the toothed Enantiornithes were the dominant birds that ruled the trees and air."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "jussieu", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jussieu"], "contexts": ["observes M. Richard's sagacity", "on classification"]}, {"entity_id": "jussieu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jussieu"], "contexts": ["author of Traite de Botanique"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Jussieu", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jussieu", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3366, "stable_key": "clu_77ccce8b81c05e6a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antoine Laurent de Jussieu", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1748, "death_year": 1836, "description": "French botanist, established natural classification system", "note": "While 'Jussieu' could refer to other family members, Antoine Laurent is the most prominent botanist fitting the description 'author of Traite de Botanique'.", "wikidata_id": "Q223963", "wikidata_description": "French botanist (1748–1836)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine%20Laurent%20de%20Jussieu", "wikipedia_extract": "Antoine Laurent de Jussieu (French pronunciation: [ɑ̃twan loʁɑ̃ də ʒysjø]; 12 April 1748 – 17 September 1836) was a French botanist, notable as the first to publish a natural classification of flowering plants; much of his system remains in use today. His classification was based on an extended unpublished work by his uncle, the botanist Bernard de Jussieu. Jussieu was born in Lyon, France, in 1748, as one of 10 children, to Christophle de Jussieu, an amateur botanist. His father's three younger brothers were also botanists. He went to Paris in 1765 to be with his uncle Bernard and to study medicine, graduating with a doctorate in 1770, with a thesis on animal and vegetable physiology. His uncle introduced him to the Jardin du Roi, where he was appointed as a botany Demonstrator and deputy to L."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "De Candolle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 721, "target_cluster_name": "De Candolle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jussieu was a significant figure in plant taxonomy, a field de Candolle also contributed to.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jussieu was a significant figure in plant taxonomy, a field de Candolle also contributed to.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Linnæus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2721, "target_cluster_name": "Linnæus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jussieu developed a natural system of plant classification that was influential and, in some ways, a development or alternative to Linnaeus's artificial system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jussieu developed a natural system of plant classification that was influential and, in some ways, a development or alternative to Linnaeus's artificial system.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Negro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "negro", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Negro", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Negro"], "contexts": ["group of people mentioned in a hypothetical classification", "racial classification, referring to Black people"]}, {"entity_id": "négrès", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "négrès", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["négrès"], "contexts": ["enslaved men, specifically Black men"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Negro", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "négrès", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 3367, "stable_key": "clu_58544cd6f2444828", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Black people", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Negro' and 'négrès' are historical and now considered offensive, referring to Black individuals, often in contexts of race or servitude.", "wikidata_id": "Q3485529", "wikidata_description": "history of black people within the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20people%20and%20Mormonism", "wikipedia_extract": "During the history of the Latter Day Saint movement, the relationship between Black people and Mormonism has included enslavement, exclusion and inclusion, and official and unofficial discrimination. Black people have been involved with the Latter Day Saint movement since its inception in the 1830s. Their experiences have varied widely, depending on the denomination within Mormonism and the time of their involvement. From the mid-1800s to 1978, Mormonism's largest denomination – the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (LDS Church) – barred Black women and men from participating in the ordinances of its temples necessary for the highest level of salvation, and excluded most men of Black African descent from ordination in the church's lay, all-male priesthood. During that time the LDS Church also opposed interracial marriage, supported racial segregation in its communities and church schools, and taught that righteous Black people would be made white after death. The temple and priesthood racial restrictions were lifted by church leaders in 1978."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "embryonic", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "embryonic", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "embryonic", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["embryonic"], "contexts": ["relating to early development", "relating to the early stage of development"]}, {"entity_id": "embryonic_mass", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "embryonic mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["embryonic mass"], "contexts": ["mass where intestine forms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "embryonic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "embryonic mass", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3368, "stable_key": "clu_b75ef70fe04208d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Embryonic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14820051", "wikidata_description": "the process whose specific outcome is the progression of an embryo from its formation until the end of its embryonic life stage. The end of the embryonic stage is organism-specific. For example, for mammals, the process would begin with zygote format", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embryonic%20development"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "uter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2646, "target_cluster_name": "uter", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The uterus is the organ where an embryo develops.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The uterus is the organ where an embryo develops.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "mammæ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mammæ", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mammæ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["mammæ"], "contexts": ["mammary glands, rudimentary in male mammals", "organs in male mammals"]}, {"entity_id": "mamos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mamos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mamos"], "contexts": ["breasts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammæ", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mamos", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3369, "stable_key": "clu_8bca6b7a0612a47a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mammary glands", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Mammæ' is the Latin plural for mammary gland. 'Mamos' is likely a misspelling or variant of the same concept."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "cultivated plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cultivated_plants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cultivated plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cultivated plants"], "contexts": ["plants grown by humans"]}, {"entity_id": "cultivated_plant", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cultivated plant", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cultivated plant"], "contexts": ["plant grown by humans"]}, {"entity_id": "cultivated_plants", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cultivated plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cultivated plants"], "contexts": ["plants grown by humans"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cultivated plants", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cultivated plant", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cultivated plants", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cultivated plants", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3370, "stable_key": "clu_9a41d7a546d0e9c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cultivated plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "wikidata_id": "Q2988930", "wikidata_description": "study of the theory and practice of the science that identifies, describes, classifies, and names cultigens—those plants whose origin or selection is primarily due to intentional human activity;one part of the study of horticultural botany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultivated%20plant%20taxonomy", "wikipedia_extract": "Cultivated plant taxonomy is the study of the theory and practice of the science that identifies, describes, classifies, and names cultigens—those plants whose origin or selection is primarily due to intentional human activity. Cultivated plant taxonomists do, however, work with all kinds of plants in cultivation. Cultivated plant taxonomy is one part of the study of horticultural botany which is mostly carried out in botanical gardens, large nurseries, universities, or government departments. Areas of special interest for the cultivated plant taxonomist include: searching for and recording new plants suitable for cultivation (plant hunting); communicating with and advising the general public on matters concerning the classification and nomenclature of cultivated plants and carrying out original research on these topics; describing the cultivated plants of particular regions (horticultural floras); maintaining databases, herbaria and other information about cultivated plants. Much of the work of the cultivated plant taxonomist is concerned with the naming of plants as prescribed by two plant nomenclatural Codes. The provisions of the International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants (Botanical Code) serve primarily scientific ends and the objectives of the scientific community, while those of the International Code of Nomenclature for Cultivated Plants (Cultivated Plant Code) are designed to serve both scientific and utilitarian ends by making provision for the names of plants used in commerce—the cultigens that have arisen in agriculture, forestry and horticulture."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Horner", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "horner", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Horner", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Horner"], "contexts": ["researcher on ancient Egyptian civilization"]}, {"entity_id": "horner", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Horner", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Horner"], "contexts": ["observers on Krusenstern's voyage", "early modern scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Horner", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Horner", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3371, "stable_key": "clu_0f35ac2f994b6452", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Friedrich Horner", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1774, "death_year": 1834, "description": "Swiss ophthalmologist and surgeon, known for Horner's syndrome.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Horner existed. Johann Friedrich Horner is a plausible, but not definitive, match given the historical context.", "wikidata_id": "Q123315", "wikidata_description": "Swiss ophthalmologist (1831-1886)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann%20Friedrich%20Horner", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Friedrich Horner (27 March 1831 – 20 December 1886) was an ophthalmologist based at the University of Zurich, Switzerland. Horner was born in Zurich. After receiving his medical degree from the University of Zurich in 1854, he continued his studies in Vienna, where he learned ophthalmoscopy from Eduard Jäger von Jaxtthal (1818–1884), and in Berlin, where he served as an assistant to ophthalmologist Albrecht von Graefe (1828–1870). It was during this time that Horner decided to become an ophthalmologist himself. He returned to Zurich in 1856, and later opened his own eye clinic named \"Hottinghof\". Horner became a full professor of ophthalmology in 1873."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bones of the face", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bones_of_the_face", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "bones of the face", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bones of the face"], "contexts": ["skeletal structures of the facial region"]}, {"entity_id": "facial_bones", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "facial bones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["facial bones"], "contexts": ["bones of the face"]}, {"entity_id": "muscles_of_the_face", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "muscles of the face", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscles of the face"], "contexts": ["facial musculature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bones of the face", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "facial bones", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bones of the face", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscles of the face", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 3372, "stable_key": "clu_003b3d81f1513cf2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "facial bones", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1068553", "wikidata_description": "consists of a part of the skull that is derived from the pharyngeal arches", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facial%20skeleton", "wikipedia_extract": "The facial skeleton comprises the facial bones that may attach to build a portion of the skull. The remainder of the skull is the neurocranium. In human anatomy and development, the facial skeleton is sometimes called the membranous viscerocranium, which comprises the mandible and dermatocranial elements that are not part of the braincase. In the human skull, the facial skeleton consists of fourteen bones in the face: Elements of the cartilaginous viscerocranium (i.e., splanchnocranial elements), such as the hyoid bone, are sometimes considered part of the facial skeleton. The ethmoid bone (or a part of it) and also the sphenoid bone are sometimes included, but otherwise considered part of the neurocranium. Because the maxillary bones are fused, they are often collectively listed as only one bone."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "disposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "disposition", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "disposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["disposition"], "contexts": ["temperament or character"]}, {"entity_id": "disposition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "disposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["disposition"], "contexts": ["a person's inherent qualities of mind and character", "person's inherent qualities of mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "disposition", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "disposition", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3373, "stable_key": "clu_bad284da69dbfbf1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "disposition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Turan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "turan", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Turan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Turan"], "contexts": ["region sending rare birds"]}, {"entity_id": "turan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Turan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Turan"], "contexts": ["region giving name to a race", "region named after a race"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Turan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Turan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3374, "stable_key": "clu_c738781dad31db23", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turán", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Uzbekistan, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran, Afghanistan", "note": "Turan is a historical and geographical region in Central Asia, often associated with Turkic peoples. Its inclusion in scientific texts like Darwin's and Humboldt's suggests a reference to its geography or the species originating from it."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dray and race horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "dray_and_race_horse", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dray and race horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["dray and race horse"], "contexts": ["different breeds of horse"]}, {"entity_id": "dray-horse", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dray-horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["dray-horse"], "contexts": ["breed of horse"]}, {"entity_id": "dray-horses", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "dray-horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["dray-horses"], "contexts": ["working horses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dray and race horse", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "dray-horses", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dray-horse", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "dray-horses", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3375, "stable_key": "clu_0744d2aa712c2cb7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horses", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Covers various breeds of horses, including those used for work (dray) and sport (race).", "wikidata_id": "Q726", "wikidata_description": "domesticated four-footed mammal from the equine family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "zoologist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "zoologist", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "zoologist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["zoologist"], "contexts": ["expert in animal classification"]}, {"entity_id": "zoologists", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "zoologists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["zoologists"], "contexts": ["experts in animal classification"]}, {"entity_id": "zoologists", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "zoologists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["zoologists"], "contexts": ["scientists studying animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "zoologist", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "zoologists", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "zoologists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "zoologists", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3376, "stable_key": "clu_b1a7cd301ceaf966", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zoologist", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scientist specializing in the study of animals.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hisashi Abe (阿部 永, Abe Hisashi; born 1933) is a Japanese mammalogist and a former professor of Zoology at Hokkaido University Sapporo in Hokkaidō, Japan. He has authored a variety of publications on the taxonomy of various mammal species endemic to East Asia. He was formerly the president of Mammal Society of Japan. Abe was born during 1933 in Yoshinogawa, Tokushima.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hisashi_Abe"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Hunt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 916, "target_cluster_name": "Hunt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hunter's extensive collection of anatomical specimens and his studies of comparative anatomy contributed significantly to the early development of zoology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hunter's extensive collection of anatomical specimens and his studies of comparative anatomy contributed significantly to the early development of zoology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "naturalists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1650, "target_cluster_name": "naturalists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Attenborough and zoologists study animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Attenborough and zoologists study animals.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Wallace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2104, "target_cluster_name": "Wallace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Wallace's work encompassed zoology, and he is recognized for his contributions to the field.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Wallace's work encompassed zoology, and he is recognized for his contributions to the field.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Westwood", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2372, "target_cluster_name": "Westwood", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Obadiah Westwood was a prominent entomologist and zoologist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Obadiah Westwood was a prominent entomologist and zoologist.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Milne Edwards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2528, "target_cluster_name": "Milne Edwards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Henri Milne Edwards was a prominent zoologist, and this term represents his primary professional identity and field of study.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Henri Milne Edwards was a prominent zoologist, and this term represents his primary professional identity and field of study.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "palæontologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2529, "target_cluster_name": "palæontologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paleontology often involves the study of fossil animals, making it closely related to zoology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Paleontology often involves the study of fossil animals, making it closely related to zoology.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "young", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "young", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "young", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["young"], "contexts": ["offspring of animals"]}, {"entity_id": "young_animals", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "young animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["young animals"], "contexts": ["immature animal subjects"]}, {"entity_id": "young", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "young", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["young"], "contexts": ["species interested in its own young"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "young", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "young animals", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "young", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "young", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3377, "stable_key": "clu_4fabc65b2404d9c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Young animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The cluster refers to immature or offspring animals in general, not a specific species.", "wikidata_id": "Q115090113", "wikidata_description": "narrative motif documented in Thompson's Motif-Index of Folk-Literature"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "drought", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "drought", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "drought", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["drought"], "contexts": ["prolonged period of abnormally low rainfall"]}, {"entity_id": "drought", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "drought", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["drought"], "contexts": ["prolonged period of abnormally low rainfall"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "drought", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "drought", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3378, "stable_key": "clu_6f08b9a81e11097a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Drought", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Drought is a meteorological phenomenon, not a disease, but can have severe consequences for living organisms.", "wikidata_id": "Q12142810", "wikidata_description": "the ability to which a plant maintains its biomass production during arid or drought conditions", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drought%20tolerance", "wikipedia_extract": "In botany, drought tolerance is the ability by which a plant maintains its biomass production during arid or drought conditions. Some plants are naturally adapted to dry conditions, surviving with protection mechanisms such as desiccation tolerance, detoxification, or repair of xylem embolism. Other plants, specifically crops like corn, wheat, and rice, have become increasingly tolerant to drought with new varieties created via genetic engineering. From an evolutionary perspective, the type of mycorrhizal associations formed in the roots of plants can determine how fast plants can adapt to drought. The plants behind drought tolerance are complex and involve many pathways which allows plants to respond to specific sets of conditions at any given time. Some of these interactions include stomatal conductance, carotenoid degradation and anthocyanin accumulation, the intervention of osmoprotectants (such as sucrose, glycine, and proline), ROS-scavenging enzymes."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "magnetic storms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2003, "target_cluster_name": "magnetic storms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'drought' are natural phenomena that can have significant impacts on human activities and the environment. While distinct in their physical nature, historical texts might link them as disruptive events.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both 'magnetic storms' and 'drought' are natural phenomena that can have significant impacts on human activities and the environment. While distinct in their physical nature, historical texts might link them as disruptive events.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Herr M.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__h__c_", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. H. C.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mr. H. C."], "contexts": ["made analogous observations"]}, {"entity_id": "herr_m_", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Herr M.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Herr M.", "M."], "contexts": ["described head-muscle tightening", "describes tightening of head-muscles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. H. C.", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Herr M.", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3379, "stable_key": "clu_d5d9c1cc17616213", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Herr M.", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Individual whose observations were cited in scientific texts", "note": "Likely a pseudonym or abbreviation, precise identity is not determinable from the provided context.", "wikidata_id": "Q55023302", "wikidata_description": "operatic character in the opera Neues vom Tage by Paul Hindemith"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Falklands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "falklands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Falklands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falklands"], "contexts": ["southern location for rodents"]}, {"entity_id": "falkland_island", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Falkland Island", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falkland Island"], "contexts": ["geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "falkland_islands", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Falkland Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falkland Islands"], "contexts": ["alternative name for Malouines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Falklands", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Falkland Islands", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Falkland Island", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Falkland Islands", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 3380, "stable_key": "clu_d05ccdb867dabe86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Falkland Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q5535263", "wikidata_description": "Overview of the geography of the Falkland Islands"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "atmosphére tropicale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "atmosphére_tropicale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "atmosphére tropicale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["atmosphére tropicale", "zone tropicale"], "contexts": ["air in the tropics", "region around the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropical_climate", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tropical climate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropical climate"], "contexts": ["climate suitable for tropical plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "atmosphére tropicale", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropical climate", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 3381, "stable_key": "clu_14481ee3a155cd63", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "tropical climate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q135712", "wikidata_description": "climate in the tropical region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical%20climate", "wikipedia_extract": "Tropical climate is the first of the five major climate groups in the Köppen climate classification identified with the letter A. Tropical climates are defined by a monthly average temperature of 18 °C (64 °F) or higher in the coolest month, featuring hot temperatures and high humidity all year-round. Annual precipitation is often abundant in tropical climates, and shows a seasonal rhythm but may have seasonal dryness to varying degrees. There are normally only two seasons in tropical climates, a wet (rainy/monsoon) season and a dry season. The annual temperature range in tropical climates is normally very small. Sunlight is intense in these climates."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "homology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "homology", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "homology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["homology"], "contexts": ["similarity due to shared ancestry"]}, {"entity_id": "homologies", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "homologies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["homologies"], "contexts": ["similarities due to shared ancestry"]}, {"entity_id": "homologies", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "homologies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["homologies"], "contexts": ["structural similarities between species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "homology", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "homologies", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "homologies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "homologies", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3382, "stable_key": "clu_f068a0813701e7ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "homology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5891438", "wikidata_description": "when two concepts are separated by time but share similarities", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homology%20%28anthropology%29"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Western China", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "eastern_china", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Eastern China", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Eastern China"], "contexts": ["Eastern China"]}, {"entity_id": "western_china", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Western China", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Western China"], "contexts": ["region where meteorite fell", "geographic location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Eastern China", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Western China", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3383, "stable_key": "clu_1b5a03cd74ea943c-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "China", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Country of origin for various plants, spices, and historical goods.", "wikipedia_extract": "China, officially the People's Republic of China (PRC), is a country in East Asia. It is the second-most populous country after India, with a population exceeding 1.4 billion, representing 17% of the world's population. China borders fourteen countries by land across an area of 9.6 million square kilometers (3,700,000 sq mi), making it the third-largest country by area. The country is divided into 33 province-level divisions: 22 provinces, 5 autonomous regions, 4 municipalities, and 2 semi-autonomous special administrative regions. Beijing is the capital, while Shanghai is the most populous city by urban area and largest financial center. The first humans in China arrived during the Paleolithic era."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "East Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 737, "target_cluster_name": "Asie orientale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "China is a major country within the geographical region of East Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "China is a major country within the geographical region of East Asia."}, {"found_name": "Asia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 231, "target_cluster_name": "Asie", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "China is a country located on the continent of Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "China is a country located on the continent of Asia."}, {"found_name": "Beijing", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2309, "target_cluster_name": "Pekin", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Beijing is the capital city of China.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Beijing is the capital city of China."}, {"found_name": "Gobi Desert", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1287, "target_cluster_name": "Gobi", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Gobi Desert is a significant geographical feature located in and around China.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The Gobi Desert is a significant geographical feature located in and around China."}]}, {"canonical_name": "transition formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "transitional_forms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "transitional forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["transitional forms"], "contexts": ["intermediate evolutionary stages"]}, {"entity_id": "transition_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "transition formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["transition formations"], "contexts": ["geological formation designation", "geological time period"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "transitional forms", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "transition formations", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 3384, "stable_key": "clu_767664ab93b2fbc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "transitional forms", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q28744670", "wikidata_description": "scientific article (publication date: 22 November 2011)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fore-arm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fore-arm", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fore-arm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fore-arm"], "contexts": ["part of the arm"]}, {"entity_id": "forearm", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "forearm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["forearm"], "contexts": ["part of the arm below the elbow"]}, {"entity_id": "forearm", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "forearm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["forearm"], "contexts": ["part of arm involved in piano playing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "fore-arm", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "forearm", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "forearm", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "forearm", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3385, "stable_key": "clu_ae1edb4a3397d4fa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "forearm", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q228537", "wikidata_description": "region of the upper limb between the elbow and the wrist"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hind-legs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hind-legs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hind-legs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hind-legs"], "contexts": ["legs at the back"]}, {"entity_id": "hind_legs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hind legs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hind legs"], "contexts": ["rear limbs of an animal"]}, {"entity_id": "hind_foot", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hind foot", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hind foot"], "contexts": ["foot of the rear limb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hind-legs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hind legs", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hind-legs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hind foot", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 3386, "stable_key": "clu_ca19c7dd244227eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hind limbs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5766112", "wikidata_description": "in animal anatomy", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindlimb", "wikipedia_extract": "A hindlimb or back limb is one of the paired articulated appendages (limbs) attached on the caudal (posterior) end of a terrestrial tetrapod vertebrate's torso. With reference to quadrupeds, the term hindleg or back leg is often used instead. In bipedal animals with an upright posture (e.g. humans and some primates), the term lower limb is often used. It is located on the limb of an animal. Hindlimbs are present in a large number of quadrupeds."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fore-legs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1317, "target_cluster_name": "fore-legs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Forelimbs are often discussed in contrast or comparison to hind limbs, representing the paired appendages of an animal's body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Forelimbs are often discussed in contrast or comparison to hind limbs, representing the paired appendages of an animal's body.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "alimentary canal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "alimentary_canal", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "alimentary canal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["alimentary canal"], "contexts": ["tube for digestion and excretion"]}, {"entity_id": "alimentary_canal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "alimentary canal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["alimentary canal"], "contexts": ["development from mucous layer", "upper portion developing lungs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "alimentary canal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "alimentary canal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3387, "stable_key": "clu_f795b327e12d2f95", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Digestive system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2300099", "wikidata_description": "disease involving the gastrointestinal tract", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gastrointestinal%20disease", "wikipedia_extract": "Gastrointestinal diseases (abbrev. GI diseases or GI illnesses) refer to diseases involving the gastrointestinal tract, namely the esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine and rectum; and the accessory organs of digestion, the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. The oral cavity is part of the gastrointestinal system and as such the presence of alterations in this district can be the first sign of both systemic and gastrointestinal diseases. By far the most common oral conditions are plaque-induced diseases (e.g., gingivitis, periodontitis, dental caries). Oral symptoms can be similar to lesions occurring elsewhere in the digestive tract, with a pattern of swelling, inflammation, ulcers, and fissures. If these signs are present, then patients are more likely to also have anal and esophageal lesions and experience other extra-intestinal disease manifestations."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hind-limb", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hind_limbs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hind limbs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hind limbs"], "contexts": ["affected by exercising in mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "hind-limb", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hind-limb", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["hind-limb"], "contexts": ["rear limb of an animal", "rear leg of an animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hind limbs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hind-limb", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 3388, "stable_key": "clu_5da295c471fe2b83", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hind limbs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "subsistence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "subsistence", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "subsistence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["subsistence"], "contexts": ["struggle for survival"]}, {"entity_id": "subsistence", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "subsistence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["subsistence"], "contexts": ["means of maintaining life", "means of supporting life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "subsistence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "subsistence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3389, "stable_key": "clu_ebde03a8e3388124", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Subsistence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In these contexts, it refers to the ability of an organism to maintain its existence and to reproduce.", "wikidata_id": "Q2787508", "wikidata_description": "farming which meets the basic needs of the farmer and family", "wikipedia_extract": "The iron law of wages is a proposed law of economics that asserts that real wages always tend, in the long run, toward the minimum wage necessary to sustain the life of the worker. The theory was first named by Ferdinand Lassalle in the mid-nineteenth century. Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels attribute the doctrine to Lassalle (notably in Marx's 1875 Critique of the Gotha Program), the idea to Thomas Malthus's (1798) An Essay on the Principle of Population, and the terminology to Goethe's \"great, eternal iron laws\" in Das Göttliche. It was coined in reference to the views of classical economists such as David Ricardo's law of rent, and the competing population theory of Thomas Malthus. It held that the market price of labor (which tends toward the minimum required for the subsistence of the laborers) would always, or almost always, reduce as the working population increased and vice versa. Ricardo believed that this happened only under particular conditions.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_law_of_wages"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ichneumon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ichneumon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ichneumon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ichneumon"], "contexts": ["insect with an ovipositor"]}, {"entity_id": "ichneumon", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ichneumon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["ichneumon", "Ichneumon"], "contexts": ["name used in some books for the animal", "animal name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ichneumon", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ichneumon", "similarity": 0.862}], "id": 3390, "stable_key": "clu_fb51831329bfc07e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ichneumon wasp", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The 'ichneumon' in early modern texts could refer to several animals, but in the context of insect anatomy (ovipositor) and biological discussions, the ichneumon wasp (family Ichneumonidae) is the most likely referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q1138186", "wikidata_description": "family of insects"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Wasps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2271, "target_cluster_name": "Wasps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An ichneumon wasp is a specific type of wasp, making it a direct subtype and thus strongly related.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An ichneumon wasp is a specific type of wasp, making it a direct subtype and thus strongly related.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "queen-bee", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "queen-bee", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "queen-bee", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["queen-bee"], "contexts": ["fertile female bee"]}, {"entity_id": "queen_bee", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "queen bee", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["queen bee"], "contexts": ["fertile female bee in a colony"]}, {"entity_id": "queen-bee", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "queen-bee", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["queen-bee"], "contexts": ["reproductive female bee"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "queen-bee", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "queen-bee", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "queen bee", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "queen-bee", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 3391, "stable_key": "clu_01a9408f82d6fa6e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Queen bee", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q770569", "wikidata_description": "German comedy musical group", "wikipedia_extract": "A queen bee is typically an adult, mated female (gyne) that lives in a colony or hive of honey bees. With fully developed reproductive organs, the queen is usually the mother of most, if not all, of the bees in the beehive. Queens are developed from larvae selected by worker bees and specially fed in order to become sexually mature. There is normally only one adult, mated queen in a hive, in which case the bees will usually follow and fiercely protect her. The term \"queen bee\" can be more generally applied to any dominant reproductive female in a colony of a eusocial bee species other than honey bees. However, as in the Brazilian stingless bee (Schwarziana quadripunctata), a single nest may have multiple queens or even dwarf queens, ready to replace a dominant queen in case of a sudden death.", "wikipedia_url": "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Bee"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The queen bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The queen bee is a specific type of bee, and honey is produced by bees. This is a direct relationship within the same species.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bees", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2025, "target_cluster_name": "Bees", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The reproductive female bee in a colony, a specific type of bee.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The reproductive female bee in a colony, a specific type of bee.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melipona", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2530, "target_cluster_name": "Melipona", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Queen bees are a specific type of bee within a colony, and Melipona bees form colonies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Queen bees are a specific type of bee within a colony, and Melipona bees form colonies.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "mental actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mental_actions", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mental actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental actions"], "contexts": ["operations of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_action", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental action"], "contexts": ["activity of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_actions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental actions"], "contexts": ["processes of the mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mental actions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mental action", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mental actions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mental actions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3392, "stable_key": "clu_1bb98608b421eea6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental processes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127877018", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "process", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["process"], "contexts": ["series of actions or steps"]}, {"entity_id": "processes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "processes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["processes"], "contexts": ["activities within the brain"]}, {"entity_id": "process", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["process"], "contexts": ["neural activity or function"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "process", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "processes", "similarity": 0.853}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "process", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "process", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3393, "stable_key": "clu_53ed9faba9caa23e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "process", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6661985", "wikidata_description": "any process that uses a computer program to enter data and summarise, analyse or otherwise convert data into usable information", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data%20processing", "wikipedia_extract": "Data processing is the collection and manipulation of digital data to produce meaningful information. Data processing is a form of information processing, which is the modification (processing) of information in any manner detectable by an observer. Data processing may involve various processes, including: Validation – Ensuring that supplied data is correct and relevant. Sorting – \"arranging items in some sequence and/or in different sets.\" Summarization (statistical) or (automatic) – reducing detailed data to its main points. Aggregation – combining multiple pieces of data. Analysis – the \"collection, organization, analysis, interpretation and presentation of data.\" Reporting – list detail or summary data or computed information."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "union", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "union", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "union", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["union"], "contexts": ["joining of reproductive cells"]}, {"entity_id": "union", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "union", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["union"], "contexts": ["the act of joining together", "act of joining together"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "union", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "union", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3394, "stable_key": "clu_9256c793a7846e12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "union", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q178790", "wikidata_description": "organization of workers with common goals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade%20union", "wikipedia_extract": "A trade union (British English) or labor union (American English), often simply referred to as a union, is an organization of workers whose purpose is to maintain or improve the conditions of their employment, such as attaining better wages and benefits, improving working conditions and safety standards, establishing complaint procedures, developing rules governing the status of employees (including rules on promotions and just-cause conditions for termination), and protecting and increasing the bargaining power of workers. Trade unions typically fund their head office and legal team functions through regularly imposed fees called union dues. The union representatives in the workforce are usually made up of workplace volunteers who are often appointed by members through internal democratic elections. The trade union, through an elected leadership and bargaining committee, bargains with the employer on behalf of its members, known as the rank and file, and negotiates labour contracts (collective bargaining agreements) with employers. Unions may organize a particular section of skilled or unskilled workers (craft unionism), a cross-section of workers from various trades (general unionism), or an attempt to organize all workers within a particular industry (industrial unionism). The agreements negotiated by a union are binding on the rank-and-file members and the employer, and in some cases on other non-member workers."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "North Cape", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "north_cape", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "North Cape", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Cape"], "contexts": ["northernmost point of mainland Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "north_cape", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "North Cape", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["North Cape"], "contexts": ["location of Lotur and Bravais", "northernmost point of mainland Europe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "North Cape", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "North Cape", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3395, "stable_key": "clu_a89f7ca041d3c234", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "North Cape", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Norway", "wikidata_id": "Q4069", "wikidata_description": "cape on the island of Magerøya in Northern Norway"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "terrestrial", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "terrestrial", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "terrestrial", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial"], "contexts": ["relating to land"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["terrestrial"], "contexts": ["relating to the Earth", "relating to Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "terrestrial", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "terrestrial", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3396, "stable_key": "clu_1b2182c00abdf494", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "terrestrial", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q110810720", "wikidata_description": "grammatical gender of the fictional Valyrian language family"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "antarctic regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "antarctic_regions", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "antarctic regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["antarctic regions"], "contexts": ["polar region around South Pole"]}, {"entity_id": "antarctic", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "antarctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["antarctic"], "contexts": ["southern polar region"]}, {"entity_id": "antarctic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Antarctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antarctic"], "contexts": ["southern polar region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "antarctic regions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Antarctic", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "antarctic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Antarctic", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 3397, "stable_key": "clu_ade0ff89afab4fe5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antarctic region", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q1555938", "wikidata_description": "polar region around Earth's South Pole", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antarctic", "wikipedia_extract": "The Antarctic (, US also ; commonly ) is the polar region of Earth that surrounds the South Pole, lying within the Antarctic Circle. It is diametrically opposite of the Arctic region around the North Pole. The Antarctic comprises the continent of Antarctica, the Kerguelen Plateau, and other island territories located on the Antarctic Plate or south of the Antarctic Convergence. The Antarctic region includes the ice shelves, waters, and all the island territories in the Southern Ocean situated south of the Antarctic Convergence, a zone approximately 32 to 48 km (20 to 30 mi) wide and varying in latitude seasonally. The region covers some 20 percent of the Southern Hemisphere, of which 5.5 percent (14 million km2) is the surface area of the Antarctica continent itself. All of the land and ice shelves south of 60°S latitude are administered under the Antarctic Treaty System."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "northern hemisphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 730, "target_cluster_name": "northern hemisphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Northern Hemisphere is the counterpart to the Antarctic region, representing the other major division of the Earth's surface.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Northern Hemisphere is the counterpart to the Antarctic region, representing the other major division of the Earth's surface.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Polar Sea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 891, "target_cluster_name": "Polar Sea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Antarctic region is the polar region of Earth that surrounds the South Pole, analogous to the Arctic region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Antarctic region is the polar region of Earth that surrounds the South Pole, analogous to the Arctic region.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "southern hemisphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1025, "target_cluster_name": "southern hemisphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Antarctic region is a significant part of the Southern Hemisphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Antarctic region is a significant part of the Southern Hemisphere.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "vegetable forms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "herbaceous_forms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "herbaceous forms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["herbaceous forms"], "contexts": ["plant forms developed into trees"]}, {"entity_id": "vegetable_forms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vegetable forms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vegetable forms"], "contexts": ["forms of plant life", "plant structures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "herbaceous forms", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vegetable forms", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 3398, "stable_key": "clu_3de62829b2ed2eb4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "plant forms", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "This cluster refers to general forms of plant life rather than specific species.", "wikidata_id": "Q655362", "wikidata_description": "subdiscipline of botany"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "British Channel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "british_channel", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "British Channel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["British Channel"], "contexts": ["body of water separating islands"]}, {"entity_id": "british_channel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "British Channel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["British Channel"], "contexts": ["narrow sea channel with high tides", "height of tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "British Channel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "British Channel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3399, "stable_key": "clu_efd9965fbe8aa495", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "English Channel", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom, France", "wikidata_id": "Q24645904", "wikidata_description": "watercourse in the United Kingdom", "wikipedia_extract": "The Channel Islands are an archipelago in the English Channel, off the French coast of Normandy. They are divided into two Crown Dependencies: the Bailiwick of Jersey, which is the largest of the islands; and the Bailiwick of Guernsey, consisting of Guernsey, Alderney, Sark, Herm and some smaller islands. Historically, they are the remnants of the Duchy of Normandy. Although they are not part of the United Kingdom, the UK is responsible for the defence and international relations of the islands as it is for the other Crown Dependency, the Isle of Man, and the British Overseas Territories. The Crown Dependencies are neither members of the Commonwealth of Nations, nor part of the European Union. They have a total population of about 171,916, and the bailiwicks' capitals, Saint Helier and Saint Peter Port, have populations of 33,500 and 18,207 respectively.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_Islands"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kent", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1708, "target_cluster_name": "Kent", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kent is bordered by the English Channel to the south-east.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kent is bordered by the English Channel to the south-east.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sculpture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sculpture", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "sculpture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sculpture"], "contexts": ["surface markings on organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "sculpture", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Sculpture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sculpture"], "contexts": ["art form derived from mural decoration", "artistic discipline"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sculpture", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sculpture", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3400, "stable_key": "clu_a16b30f895f21314", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sculpture", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q860861", "wikidata_description": "three-dimensional work of art", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sculpture_%28%C5%93uvre_d%27art%29"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "appendix", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "appendage", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "appendage", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["appendage"], "contexts": ["a projecting part of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "appendix", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "appendix", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["appendix"], "contexts": ["part of the heart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "appendage", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "appendix", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 3401, "stable_key": "clu_83badfb76708d51f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "appendage", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q13415392", "wikidata_description": "external body part, or natural prolongation, that protrudes from an organism's body (in vertebrate biology, an example would be a vertebrate's limbs); any of the homologous body parts that may extend from a body segment"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "swimming", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "swimming", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "swimming", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["swimming"], "contexts": ["method of locomotion in water"]}, {"entity_id": "swimming", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "swimming", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["swimming"], "contexts": ["mode of aquatic locomotion"]}, {"entity_id": "swim", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "swim", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["swim"], "contexts": ["action learned with interruptions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "swimming", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "swimming", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "swimming", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "swim", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 3402, "stable_key": "clu_6649bd52ba12ee43", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "swimming", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q31920", "wikidata_description": "water-based sport", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swimming%20%28sport%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Swimming is an individual or team racing sport that requires the use of one's entire body to move through water. The sport takes place in pools or open water (e.g., in a sea or lake). Competitive swimming is one of the most popular Olympic sports, with varied distance events in butterfly, backstroke, breaststroke, freestyle, and individual medley. In addition to these individual events, four swimmers can take part in either a freestyle or medley relay. A medley relay consists of four swimmers who will each swim a different stroke, ordered as backstroke, breaststroke, butterfly and freestyle. Swimming each stroke requires a set of specific techniques; in competition, there are distinct regulations concerning the acceptable form for each individual stroke."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "human reason", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_reason", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "human reason", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["human reason"], "contexts": ["capacity for logical thought"]}, {"entity_id": "human_reason", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "human reason", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["human reason"], "contexts": ["instrument for demonstrating existence", "instrument for demonstration and analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "human reason", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "human reason", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3403, "stable_key": "clu_e7b4b42afb83b1bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "human reason", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q52287144", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published in July 1982"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "American islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "american_islands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "American islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["American islands"], "contexts": ["islands with plants and animals related to mainland"]}, {"entity_id": "îles_de_l'amérique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles de l'Amérique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["îles de l'Amérique"], "contexts": ["islands of America"]}, {"entity_id": "îles_d'amérique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "îles d'Amérique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["îles d'Amérique"], "contexts": ["islands exporting sugar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "American islands", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "îles de l'Amérique", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "American islands", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "îles d'Amérique", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 3404, "stable_key": "clu_45f865282c3a0a3f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "islands of America", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Americas"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gould, Mr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gould,_mr_", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Gould, Mr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gould, Mr."], "contexts": ["on colours of birds"]}, {"entity_id": "bond,_mr_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bond, Mr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bond, Mr."], "contexts": ["discovered Saturn's satellite, resolved nebula"]}, {"entity_id": "dunlop,_mr_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Dunlop, Mr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dunlop, Mr."], "contexts": ["double star revolution calculation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Gould, Mr.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bond, Mr.", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Gould, Mr.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Dunlop, Mr.", "similarity": 0.85}], "id": 3405, "stable_key": "clu_63f301aa95c76d14", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Gould", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1804, "death_year": 1881, "description": "Ornithologist known for detailed bird illustrations", "note": "While 'Gould, Mr.' is present, the context 'colours of birds' strongly suggests John Gould, a prominent ornithologist of the period. The other members are likely misinterpretations or errors in the source texts or OCR."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "alcornoque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Alcornoque", "Alcornoco"], "contexts": ["cork tree, name given to Malpighia", "common name for a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "alcornoque", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alcornoque"], "contexts": ["tree with thick bark"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alcornoque", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Alcornoque", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3406, "stable_key": "clu_074d5c4831717c8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cork Oak", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Quercus suber Linnaeus", "family": "Fagaceae", "note": "While 'Alcornoque' can refer to other plants, in historical texts about medicinal plants and trees with thick bark, it most commonly refers to the cork oak.", "wikidata_id": "Q156137", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quercus%20suber", "wikipedia_extract": "Quercus suber, commonly called the cork oak,  is a medium-sized, evergreen oak tree in the section Quercus sect. Cerris. It is the primary source of cork for wine bottle stoppers and other uses, such as cork flooring and as the cores of cricket balls. It is native to southwest Europe and northwest Africa. In the Mediterranean basin the tree is an ancient species with fossil remnants dating back to the Tertiary period. It can survive for as long as two centuries."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "aralia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aralia", "Aralia turbacensis"], "contexts": ["alpine shrub found in Silla de Caracas", "plant from Turbaco"]}, {"entity_id": "aralia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["aralia"], "contexts": ["delicate social plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Aralia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "aralia", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 3407, "stable_key": "clu_778b93d8a911b9c4", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Aralia , or spikenard, is a genus of the family Araliaceae, consisting of 68 accepted species of deciduous or evergreen trees, shrubs, and rhizomatous herbaceous perennials. The genus is native to Asia and the Americas, with most species occurring in mountain woodlands. Aralia plants vary in size, with some herbaceous species only reaching 50 centimetres (20 in) tall, while some are trees growing to 20 metres (66 ft) tall. Aralia plants have large bipinnate (doubly compound) leaves clustered at the ends of their stems or branches; in some species the leaves are covered with bristles. The stems of some woody species are quite prickly, as in Aralia spinosa. The flowers are whitish or greenish occurring in terminal panicles, and the spherical dark purple berry-like fruits are popular with birds."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "trésor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "trésor", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "trésor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["trésor", "trésor royal"], "contexts": ["financial reserve", "royal treasury"]}, {"entity_id": "tesoro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "tesoro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tesoro"], "contexts": ["treasure, likely precious metals and jewels"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "trésor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "tesoro", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 3408, "stable_key": "clu_114d88108f3a90fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Treasury", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to a financial reserve or accumulated wealth, not a specific chemical substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q10756188", "wikidata_description": "department of an organisation related to finance", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury", "wikipedia_extract": "a government department related to finance and taxation, a finance ministry; in a business context, corporate treasury a place or location where treasure, such as currency or precious items are kept. These can be  state or royal property, church treasure or in private ownership. The head of a treasury is typically known as a treasurer. This position may not necessarily have the final control over the actions of the treasury, particularly if they are not an elected representative. The adjective for a treasury is normally \"treasurial\". The adjective \"tresorial\" can also be used, but this normally means pertaining to a treasurer."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "livres sterling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2746, "target_cluster_name": "livres sterling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are related to the concept of wealth and finance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are related to the concept of wealth and finance.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mac- Culloch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mac-_culloch", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mac- Culloch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mac- Culloch", "Mac Culloch"], "contexts": ["author of Researc?tes on America", "author cited for scientific journal"]}], "edges": [], "id": 3409, "stable_key": "clu_b0feb53ed9234eb5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John MacCulloch", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1773, "death_year": 1835, "description": "Scottish geologist and physician, author of geological surveys.", "wikidata_id": "Q43915496", "wikidata_description": "person associated with the witch trials in Scotland"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Imbabura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "imbabura", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Imbabura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Imbabura", "imbabum"], "contexts": ["volcano near Villa de Ibarra", "Volcano mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "imbaburu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Imbaburu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Imbaburu"], "contexts": ["volcano near Ibarra"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Imbabura", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Imbaburu", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 3410, "stable_key": "clu_df2775dd5dff97d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Imbabura", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q128880473", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Guancabamba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2751, "target_cluster_name": "Guancabamba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Imbabura is a province and volcano in Ecuador, near Huancabamba. Geographical and historical texts concerning this region would likely mention both.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Imbabura is a province and volcano in Ecuador, near Huancabamba. Geographical and historical texts concerning this region would likely mention both.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Puracè", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "puracè", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Puracè", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Puracè", "Purace"], "contexts": ["volcano", "village where observations were made"]}, {"entity_id": "purace", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Purace", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Purace"], "contexts": ["volcano between Bogota and Quito"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Puracè", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Purace", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3411, "stable_key": "clu_3d18aee21dc45f19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Puracé", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "wikipedia_extract": "Puracé is an andesitic stratovolcano located in the Puracé National Natural Park in the Cauca Department, Colombia. It is part of the North Volcanic Zone of the Andean Volcanic Belt. The volcano is located at the intersection of the Coconucos and Morras Faults. It is one of the most active volcanoes in Colombia. Large explosive eruptions occurred in 1849, 1869, 1885, 1949, 1950, 1956, and 1957. There were about a dozen eruptions in the 20th century, the most recent being in 1977, until 19 January 2025 when Puracé once again erupted.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puracé"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bouc", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bouc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bouc", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bouc", "Boui"], "contexts": ["author of memoir on terrains", "author on Alps"]}, {"entity_id": "bertuch", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bertuch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bertuch"], "contexts": ["editor of geographical ephemerides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bouc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bertuch", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 3412, "stable_key": "clu_5b22fe44c4743b12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Friedrich Justin Bertuch", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1747, "death_year": 1822, "description": "German publisher and geographer, editor of geographical journals.", "note": "The cluster members 'Bouc' and 'Bertuch' suggest this identification. 'Bouc' could be a misreading or abbreviation.", "wikidata_id": "Q213775", "wikidata_description": "German publisher (1747–1822)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "état hygrométrique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "état_hygrométrique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "état hygrométrique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["état hygrométrique", "état cyanométrique"], "contexts": ["measure of atmospheric humidity", "measure of sky color/transparency"]}, {"entity_id": "hygrometric_state", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hygrometric state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hygrometric state"], "contexts": ["moisture content of air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "état hygrométrique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hygrometric state", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 3413, "stable_key": "clu_1e678d4f7468f654", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Relative humidity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'état hygrométrique' directly translates to 'hygrometric state' and refers to the measure of atmospheric humidity.", "wikidata_id": "Q2499617", "wikidata_description": "ratio of the partial pressure of water vapor in humid air to the equilibrium vapor pressure of water at a given temperature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relative%20humidity"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "montezuma", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Montezuma", "Monteath"], "contexts": ["emperor whose empire was shaken", "captain who found a longitude"]}, {"entity_id": "montezuma", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Montezuma"], "contexts": ["Aztec ruler"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Montezuma", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Montezuma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3414, "stable_key": "clu_d95944542a56d978", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moctezuma II", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1466, "death_year": 1520, "description": "Aztec emperor who encountered Hernán Cortés", "wikidata_id": "Q141791", "wikidata_description": "9th tlatoani of Tenochtitlan and ruler of the Aztec Triple Alliance (1466-1520)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moctezuma%20II", "wikipedia_extract": "Moctezuma Xocoyotzin (c. 1466 – 29 June 1520), retroactively referred to in European sources as Moctezuma II, and often simply called Montezuma, was the ninth emperor of the Aztec Empire (also known as the Mexica Empire), reigning from 1502 or 1503 to 1520. Through his marriage with Queen Tlapalizquixochtzin of Ecatepec, one of his two wives, he was also the king consort of the altepetl. The first contact between the indigenous civilizations of Mesoamerica and Europeans took place during Moctezuma's reign. He was killed during the initial stages of the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire when Hernán Cortés, the Spanish conquistador, and his men seized the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan. During his reign, the Aztec Empire reached its greatest size."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Méchain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "méchain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Méchain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Méchain", "M. Méchain"], "contexts": ["cited for astronomical observation", "associated with observatory in Barcelona"]}, {"entity_id": "mechain", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mechain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mechain"], "contexts": ["astronomer who first saw Encke's comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Méchain", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mechain", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 3415, "stable_key": "clu_a2827f2da8cf8201", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pierre Méchain", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1744, "death_year": 1804, "description": "French astronomer and geodetic surveyor", "wikidata_id": "Q311022", "wikidata_description": "French mathematician and astronomer (1744-1804)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre%20M%C3%A9chain", "wikipedia_extract": "Pierre François André Méchain (French pronunciation: [pjɛʁ fʁɑ̃swa ɑ̃dʁe meʃɛ̃]; 16 August 1744 – 20 September 1804) was a French astronomer and surveyor who, with Charles Messier, was a major contributor to the early study of deep-sky objects and comets. Pierre Méchain was born in Laon in northern France, the son of the ceiling designer and plasterer Pierre François Méchain and Marie–Marguerite Roze. He displayed mental gifts in mathematics and physics but had to give up his studies for lack of money. However, his talents in astronomy were noticed by Jérôme Lalande, for whom he became a friend and proof-reader of the second edition of his book \"L'Astronomie\". Lalande then secured a position for him as assistant hydrographer with the Naval Depot of Maps and Charts at Versailles, where he worked through the 1770s engaged in hydrographic work and coastline surveying. It was during this time—approximately 1774—that he met Charles Messier, and apparently, they became friends."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Moscou", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "moscou", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Moscou", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moscou", "Moskou"], "contexts": ["location of magnetic force comparison", "city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "moscow", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Moscow", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Moscow"], "contexts": ["northern city with magnetic stations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Moscou", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Moscow", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 3416, "stable_key": "clu_c2391e9f3fa32a39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moscow", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Both 'Moscou' and 'Moscow' refer to the capital city of Russia.", "wikidata_id": "Q649", "wikidata_description": "capital and most populous city of Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moscow", "wikipedia_extract": "Moscow is the capital and largest city of Russia, standing on the Moskva River in Central Russia. It has a population estimated at over 13 million residents within the city limits, over 19.1 million residents in the urban area, and over 21.5 million residents in its metropolitan area. The city covers an area of 2,511 square kilometers (970 mi2), while the urban area covers 5,891 square kilometers (2,275 mi2), and the metropolitan area covers over 26,000 square kilometers (10,000 mi2). Moscow is among the world's largest cities, being the most populous city entirely in Europe, the largest urban and metropolitan area in Europe, and the largest city by land area on the European continent. First documented in 1147, Moscow became the capital of the Grand Principality of Moscow, which led the unification of the Russian lands in the 15th century and became the center of a unified state. Following the proclamation of the Tsardom of Russia in 1547, Moscow remained the political and economic center for most of its history."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "elbe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Elbe"], "contexts": ["river in Europe", "river from which Bohemians did not come"]}, {"entity_id": "elbe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elbe"], "contexts": ["river forming fissures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Elbe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Elbe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3417, "stable_key": "clu_dadc57bc4494980f-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Island in Italy and a river in Europe.", "note": "Contexts differentiate between the island and the river.", "wikipedia_extract": "Elba (Italian: isola d'Elba, pronounced [ˈiːzola ˈdelba]; Latin: Ilva) is a Mediterranean island in Tuscany, Italy, 10 km (6.2 mi) from the coastal town of Piombino on the Italian mainland, and the largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago. It is also part of the Arcipelago Toscano National Park, and the third largest island in Italy, after Sicily and Sardinia. It is located in the Tyrrhenian Sea about 50 km (30 mi) east of the French island of Corsica. The island is part of the province of Livorno and is divided into seven municipalities, with a total population of about 30,000 inhabitants which increases considerably during the summer. The municipalities are Portoferraio (which is also the island's principal town), Campo nell'Elba, Capoliveri, Marciana, Marciana Marina, Porto Azzurro, and Rio. It is famous for being the site of Napoleon's first exile, from 1814 to 1815."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Tuscany", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2151, "target_cluster_name": "Toscana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elba is an island in Tuscany, Italy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Elba is an island in Tuscany, Italy."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Macos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "macos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Macos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macos"], "contexts": ["people with less prominent foreheads", "indigenous people devoted to agriculture"]}, {"entity_id": "málayos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "málayos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["málayos"], "contexts": ["Malays engaged in coastal trade"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Macos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "málayos", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3418, "stable_key": "clu_15fcb1614132af78", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malays", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Southeast Asian ethnic group involved in trade", "note": "The term 'Macos' is likely a misspelling or phonetic rendering of 'Malays' in the context of historical trade routes.", "wikidata_id": "Q833", "wikidata_description": "country in Southeast Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaysia", "wikipedia_extract": "Malaysia is a country in Southeast Asia. A federal constitutional monarchy, it consists of 13 states and three federal territories, separated by the South China Sea into two regions: Peninsular Malaysia on the Indochinese Peninsula and East Malaysia on the island of Borneo. Peninsular Malaysia shares land and maritime borders with Thailand, as well as maritime borders with Singapore, Vietnam, and Indonesia; East Malaysia shares land borders with Brunei and Indonesia, and maritime borders with the Philippines and Vietnam. Kuala Lumpur is the country's national capital, largest city, and the seat of the legislative branch of the federal government, while Putrajaya is the federal administrative capital, representing the seat of both the executive branch and the judicial branch of the federal government. With a population of over 34 million, it is the world's 42nd-most populous country. The country has its origins in the Malay kingdoms, which, from the 18th century on, became subject to the British Empire, along with the British Straits Settlements protectorate."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Pierre Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pierre_camper", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pierre Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pierre Camper"], "contexts": ["illustrious anatomist"]}, {"entity_id": "peter_camper", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Peter Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peter Camper"], "contexts": ["scientist known for comparative anatomy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pierre Camper", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Peter Camper", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 3419, "stable_key": "clu_4eb318917faf2729", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Petrus Camper", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1722, "death_year": 1789, "description": "Dutch anatomist, surgeon, and naturalist", "note": "While the text mentions 'Pierre Camper' and 'Peter Camper', the most recognized historical figure is Petrus Camper.", "wikidata_id": "Q382682", "wikidata_description": "Dutch scientist (1722-1789)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "distances lunaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "distances_lunaires", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "distances lunaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["distances lunaires"], "contexts": ["used for absolute determination", "astronomical measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "lunar_distance", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lunar distance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lunar distance"], "contexts": ["defined"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "distances lunaires", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lunar distance", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 3420, "stable_key": "clu_76c2c574dc3bf0f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lunar distance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1547342", "wikidata_description": "Angular distance between the Moon and another celestial body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar%20distance%20%28navigation%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In celestial navigation, lunar distance, also called a lunar, is the angular distance between the Moon and another celestial body. The lunar distances method uses this angle and a nautical almanac to calculate Greenwich time if so desired, or by extension any other time. That calculated time can be used in solving  a spherical triangle. The theory was first published by Johannes Werner in 1524, before the necessary almanacs had been published. A fuller  method was published in 1763 and used until about 1850 when it was superseded by the marine chronometer. A similar method uses the positions of the Galilean moons of Jupiter."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "typhus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "typhus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "typhus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["typhus"], "contexts": ["invasion of typhus", "disease raging on the coast"]}, {"entity_id": "typhus_fever", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "typhus fever", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["typhus fever"], "contexts": ["infectious disease with fever and rash"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "typhus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "typhus fever", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 3421, "stable_key": "clu_21aeb2b3bb5dcdcf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Typhus", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Typhus", "wikidata_id": "Q108057830", "wikidata_description": "obsolete name for a number of infectious diseases, characterized by symptoms such as high fever, loss of consciousness and disorders of the intestinal tract"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "kaolin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["kaolin"], "contexts": ["type of clay", "mineral substance"]}, {"entity_id": "kaolin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["kaolin"], "contexts": ["clay mineral for porcelain manufacture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "kaolin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "kaolin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3422, "stable_key": "clu_5c3b28f34cffa815", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kaolin", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q223197", "wikidata_description": "layered aluminosilicate 1:1 clay mineral"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "feldspar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1948, "target_cluster_name": "feldspar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Kaolin is a type of clay mineral, and feldspar is a common component of many clays and argillaceous rocks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Kaolin is a type of clay mineral, and feldspar is a common component of many clays and argillaceous rocks.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "lac de Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lac_de_nicaragua", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lac de Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["lac de Nicaragua"], "contexts": ["lake in Central America", "lake in Nicaragua"]}, {"entity_id": "lake_nicaragua", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lake Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lake Nicaragua"], "contexts": ["location where subterranean thunder was heard"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lac de Nicaragua", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lake Nicaragua", "similarity": 0.97}], "id": 3423, "stable_key": "clu_9e7c0a67579d91fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lake Nicaragua", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Nicaragua", "wikidata_id": "Q173862", "wikidata_description": "largest lake in Central America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake%20Nicaragua", "wikipedia_extract": "Lake Nicaragua or  Cocibolca or Granada (Spanish: Lago de Nicaragua, Lago Cocibolca, Mar Dulce, Gran Lago, Gran Lago Dulce, or Lago de Granada) is a freshwater lake in Nicaragua. Of tectonic origin and with an area of 8,264 km2 (3,191 sq mi), it is the largest fresh water lake in Central America, the 19th largest lake in the world (by area) and the tenth largest in the Americas, slightly smaller than Lake Titicaca. With an elevation of 32.7 metres (107 ft) above sea level, the lake reaches a depth of 26 metres (85 ft). The intermittent Tipitapa River feeds Lake Nicaragua when Lake Managua has high water. Lake Cocibolca is between two other bodies of water, on top is Lake Xolotlán and below is the San Juan River. These bodies of water complete the largest international drainage basin in Central America."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Nicaragua", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1624, "target_cluster_name": "Nicaragua", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The bull shark is also known as the Lake Nicaragua shark, indicating a strong geographical association.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The bull shark is also known as the Lake Nicaragua shark, indicating a strong geographical association.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "La Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "la_vega", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "La Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["La Vega"], "contexts": ["location within Caracas", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "vega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vega"], "contexts": ["city with convent of S. Francisco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "La Vega", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Vega", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 3424, "stable_key": "clu_c2c57f505c6d23d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "La Vega", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Context of 'location within Caracas' and 'city with convent of S. Francisco' points to a specific settlement in Venezuela."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "chaco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chaco"], "contexts": ["region in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "chaco", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chaco"], "contexts": ["region in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chaco", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chaco", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3425, "stable_key": "clu_4a4b3de561085044", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gran Chaco", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Argentina, Bolivia, Paraguay", "wikidata_id": "Q208347", "wikidata_description": "region of Southern America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gran%20Chaco", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gran Chaco (also called Chaco or Chaco Plain), is a vast semiarid lowland region in central South America, spanning over one million square kilometers across eastern Bolivia, western Paraguay, northern Argentina, and parts of Brazil. It forms part of the Río de la Plata basin. Gran Chaco features a mix of tropical and subtropical dry broadleaf forests, thorn scrub, savannas, wetlands, and palm groves, making it the continent’s second-largest forested ecoregion and a region of high ecological diversity. The Gran Chaco is home to more than 3,400 plant species, around 500 bird species, 150 mammals, and more than 200 reptiles and amphibians, including jaguars, giant armadillos, peccaries, and maned wolves. Its forests and soils also store carbon and regulate water cycles, playing a significant role in climate moderation. The region has been sparsely inhabited for centuries by Indigenous peoples, including the Wichí, Qom, Pilagá, Guaraní, and Ayoreo, among others."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "récoltes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "récoltes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "récoltes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["récoltes"], "contexts": ["agricultural yield", "harvests, related to agricultural yield"]}, {"entity_id": "harvests", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "harvests", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["harvests"], "contexts": ["gathering of crops"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "récoltes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "harvests", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3426, "stable_key": "clu_f3f6935e3b6f6221", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Harvest", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "domesticated animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2718, "target_cluster_name": "domesticated animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Harvest is often associated with the products derived from domestic animals, such as wool or meat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Harvest is often associated with the products derived from domestic animals, such as wool or meat.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "production", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1320, "target_cluster_name": "production", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Harvest is the culmination of agricultural production.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Harvest is the culmination of agricultural production.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "littoral", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "littoral", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "littoral", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["littoral"], "contexts": ["coastal area"]}, {"entity_id": "coast-line", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "coast-line", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["coast-line"], "contexts": ["boundary between land and sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "littoral", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "coast-line", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3427, "stable_key": "clu_35912c9e583f1b0e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "littoral zone", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q506894", "wikidata_description": "part of a sea, lake or river that is close to the shore", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littoral%20zone", "wikipedia_extract": "The littoral zone, also called litoral or nearshore, is the part of an ocean, sea, lake, or river, that is close to the shore. It provides extensive and productive habitats around the world, adjacent to land-water interfaces. In coastal ecology, the littoral zone includes the foreshore (intertidal zone) extending from the high water mark (which is rarely inundated) to the low water mark (where coastal areas become permanently submerged). However, the geographical meaning of littoral zone extends well beyond the intertidal zone to include all neritic waters within the bounds of continental shelves. Continental shelves cover an area of about 7% of the surface area of the oceans. In lake ecosystems, the littoral zone covers about 78% of Earth's total lake area."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "maranhao", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Maranhao"], "contexts": ["region for rainfall verification", "river name"]}, {"entity_id": "maranhao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maranhao"], "contexts": ["place where a fish is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maranhao", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Maranhao", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 3428, "stable_key": "clu_b5209cdecd5e512d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maranhão", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q42362", "wikidata_description": "state in the Northeast Region of Brazil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maranh%C3%A3o", "wikipedia_extract": "Maranhão (, MAH-rə-NYOWN; Brazilian Portuguese pronunciation: [maɾɐˈɲɐ̃w] ) is a state in Brazil. Located in the country's Northeast Region, it has a population of about 7 million and an area of 332,000 km2 (128,000 sq mi) and it is divided into 217 municipalities. Clockwise from north, it borders on the Atlantic Ocean for 2,243 km and the states of Piauí, Tocantins and Pará. The people of Maranhão have a distinctive accent within the common Northeastern Brazilian dialect. Maranhão is described in literary works such as Exile Song by Gonçalves Dias and Casa de Pensão by Aluísio Azevedo. The dunes of Lençóis are an important area of environmental preservation."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "prony", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Prony"], "contexts": ["author on canal works", "mentioned in work by M. de Pommcuse"]}, {"entity_id": "prony", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Prony"], "contexts": ["mutual friend, scientist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prony", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Prony", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3429, "stable_key": "clu_29661b97c8880895", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gaspard de Prony", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1755, "death_year": 1839, "description": "French engineer and mathematician, known for hydraulic and civil engineering.", "wikidata_id": "Q451608", "wikidata_description": "French mathematician and engineer, who worked on hydraulics (1755-1839)", "wikipedia_extract": "Baron Gaspard Clair François Marie Riche de Prony (22 July 1755 – 29 July 1839) was a French mathematician and engineer, who worked on hydraulics. He was born at Chamelet, Beaujolais, France and died in Asnières-sur-Seine, France. He was Engineer-in-Chief of the École nationale des ponts et chaussées [National school of bridges and roads], a technical school in Paris. The trigonometric and logarithmic tables of the cadastre In 1791, Prony embarked on the task of producing logarithmic and trigonometric tables for the French Cadastre (geographic survey). The effort was sanctioned by the French National Assembly, which, after the French Revolution wanted to bring uniformity to the multiple measurements and standards used throughout the nation. In particular, his tables were intended for precise land surveys, as part of a greater cadastre effort."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "andreossy", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Andreossy"], "contexts": ["cited for page numbers", "general and author"]}, {"entity_id": "andreossy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Andreossy"], "contexts": ["described soda lakes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Andreossy", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Andreossy", "similarity": 0.721}], "id": 3430, "stable_key": "clu_8ae3ba8b6853a128", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antoine François Andréossy", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1761, "death_year": 1821, "description": "French general and diplomat, known for scientific observations.", "wikidata_id": "Q175286", "wikidata_description": "French general (1761–1828)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine-Fran%C3%A7ois%20Andr%C3%A9ossy", "wikipedia_extract": "Antoine-François, comte Andréossy (6 March 1761 – 10 September 1828) was a Franco-Italian nobleman, who served as a French Army artillery general, diplomat and parliamentarian. Born at Castelnaudary in Languedoc (now in Aude), scion of an ancient Italian minor noble family from Lucca, he was a great-grandson of the celebrated civil engineer and architect of the Canal du Midi, François Andréossy (1633-1688). An outstanding officer cadet at the Metz School of Artillery, Andréossy was commissioned in the Régiment Royal-Artillerie in 1781, seeing action in the Dutch Civil War (1787); he was promoted as Captain in 1788. On the outbreak of the Revolution he adopted its principles. At the start of the Revolutionary Wars he saw active service on the Rhine in 1794 and in Italy in 1795, and in the campaign of 1796–1797 under Napoleon Bonaparte on engineer duties, commanding the bridging train of the French Army of Italy after June 1796, and fought with distinction at the Battle of the Bridge of Arcole and the Siege of Mantua.. He was promoted to brigadier general in 1798, in which year he accompanied Bonaparte to Egypt."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cholula", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cholula"], "contexts": ["pyramid of Cholula", "city with a pyramid"]}, {"entity_id": "cholula", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cholula"], "contexts": ["location where an aërolite was seen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cholula", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cholula", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 3431, "stable_key": "clu_8efc4409a23e54ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cholula", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "Cholula is a well-known archaeological site and city in Mexico, famous for its pyramid and historical significance. Both spellings refer to the same location.", "wikidata_id": "Q7380613", "wikidata_description": "important city of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica", "wikipedia_extract": "The Great Pyramid of Cholula, also known as Tlachihualtepetl (Nahuatl for \"constructed mountain\"), is a complex located in Cholula, Puebla, Mexico. It is the largest archaeological site of a pyramid (temple) in the world, as well as the largest pyramid by volume known to exist in the world today. The adobe brick pyramid stands 25 metres (82 ft) above the surrounding plain, which is significantly shorter than the Great Pyramid of Giza's height of 146.6 metres (481 ft), but much wider, measuring 300 by 315 metres (984 by 1,033 ft) in its final form, compared to the Great Pyramid's base dimensions of 230.3 by 230.3 metres (756 by 756 ft). The pyramid is a temple that traditionally has been viewed as having been dedicated to the god Quetzalcoatl. The architectural style of the building was linked closely to that of Teotihuacan in the Valley of Mexico, although influence from the Gulf Coast is evident as well, especially from El Tajín. The Cholula archaeological zone is situated 6.4 kilometres (4 mi) west of the city of Puebla, in the city of Cholula.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholula_%28Mesoamerican_site%29"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Géogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "géogr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Géogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Géogr."], "contexts": ["abbreviation for Geography", "title of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "géogr_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Géogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Géogr."], "contexts": ["work by Edrisi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Géogr.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Géogr.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3432, "stable_key": "clu_4bb1baa7b96e66b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Geography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Abbreviation for the field of Geography, and potentially referencing a work on the topic.", "wikidata_id": "Q1071", "wikidata_description": "science that studies the terrestrial surface, the societies that inhabit it and the territories, landscapes, places or regions that form it when interacting with each other"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Geology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 278, "target_cluster_name": "Geology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geology is the study of the Earth's physical structure and substance, its history, and the processes which act on it. It is closely related to physical geography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Geology is the study of the Earth's physical structure and substance, its history, and the processes which act on it. It is closely related to physical geography."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gilolo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gilolo"], "contexts": ["Island whose distance from Brazil was disputed", "location in the Moluccas archipelago"]}, {"entity_id": "gilolo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gilolo"], "contexts": ["alternative name for Geloulo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gilolo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Gilolo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3433, "stable_key": "clu_24c7e62552bacc0d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tidore", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "description": "Island in the Moluccas archipelago, historically important for spice trade.", "note": "Historical sources use variations for islands in the Moluccas archipelago. 'Geloulo' and 'Gilolo' likely refer to the island of Tidore or the wider region.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tidore (Indonesian: Kota Tidore Kepulauan, lit. \"City of Tidore Islands\") is a city, island, and archipelago in the Maluku Islands of eastern Indonesia, west of the larger island of Halmahera. Part of North Maluku Province, the city includes the island of Tidore (with three smaller outlying islands - Mare, Maitara and Filonga) together with a large part of Halmahera Island to its east. In the pre-colonial era, the Sultanate of Tidore was a major regional political and economic power, and a fierce rival of nearby Ternate, just to the north. Included within the city is the provincial capital, Sofifi, which is situated on the mainland of Halmahera (in North Oba District). Tidore Island consists of a large stratovolcano which rises from the seafloor to an elevation of 1,730 m (5,676 ft) above sea level at the conical Kie Matubu on the south end of the island. The northern side of the island contains a caldera, Sabale, with two smaller volcanic cones within it."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fibrine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fibrine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fibrine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["fibrine"], "contexts": ["fibrin suspended in plant milk", "component of plant sap, nutritive property"]}, {"entity_id": "fibrine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Fibrine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fibrine"], "contexts": ["intricate organic compound with many atoms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fibrine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Fibrine", "similarity": 0.995}], "id": 3434, "stable_key": "clu_8b0d4c007f0d1c6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fibrin", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q101562273", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "guarmey", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guarmey"], "contexts": ["city in Peru", "geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "guarmey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guarmey"], "contexts": ["location in Peru"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guarmey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guarmey", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3435, "stable_key": "clu_4406bbdb096871e5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Huarmey", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "wikidata_id": "Q1779624", "wikidata_description": "province of Ancash Region, Peru", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huarmey%20province", "wikipedia_extract": "Huarmey is one of 20 provinces located in the Ancash Region of Peru. It takes around 4 hours to arrive to Huarmey from Lima by bus and its main economic activities include agriculture, fishing, tourism, and mineral shipping."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Guachaneque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "guachaneque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guachaneque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guachaneque"], "contexts": ["mountain peak", "Paramos of Guachaneque"]}, {"entity_id": "guaynacaua", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Guaynacaua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guaynacaua"], "contexts": ["pass name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guachaneque", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Guaynacaua", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3436, "stable_key": "clu_3dc86213d84da77d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guaynacaua Pass", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Chile", "note": "The term 'Guachaneque' is likely a misspelling or variation of 'Guaynacaua', referring to a mountain pass or peak. Monardes' text provides the stronger clue."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "gryphées", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gryphées", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gryphées", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["gryphées"], "contexts": ["fossil oyster genus", "found in limestone rich in lead"]}, {"entity_id": "griphos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Griphos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Griphos"], "contexts": ["creatures brought from the Indies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gryphées", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Griphos", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 3437, "stable_key": "clu_1bce91391c686a3b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gryphaea (Fossil Oysters)", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Refers to fossil oysters, often found as fossils in geological contexts. 'Griphos' in Monardes is likely a variant spelling or confusion.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gryphaeidae, common name the foam oysters or honeycomb oysters, are a family of marine bivalve mollusks. This family of bivalves is very well represented in the fossil record, however the number of living species is very few. All species have shells cemented to a substrate. Shells are considered brittle, inequivalve, with the left, lower (cemented) valve convex and the right (upper, non-cemented) valve flat or slightly concave. Genera and species within this family are divided into three subfamilies, Exogyrinae, Gryphaeinae and Pycnodonteinae. Both Exogyrinae and Gryphaeinae are completely extinct.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryphaeidae"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gorée", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gorée", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gorée", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gorée"], "contexts": ["Gorée", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "goree", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Goree", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Goree"], "contexts": ["river in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gorée", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Goree", "similarity": 0.81}], "id": 3438, "stable_key": "clu_bf7ebb6d83cdd564", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gorée Island", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Senegal", "note": "The mention of 'river in India' for Gorée seems to be an error or confusion in the context provided, as Gorée is famously an island off Senegal."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "colonies françoises", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1452, "target_cluster_name": "colonies françoises", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Gorée Island was a key French trading post and part of their colonial network.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Gorée Island was a key French trading post and part of their colonial network.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "NOTES", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "notes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "NOTES", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["NOTES"], "contexts": ["notes section", "section title"]}, {"entity_id": "notes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "notes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["notes"], "contexts": ["musical sounds produced by playing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "NOTES", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "notes", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 3439, "stable_key": "clu_76cc742795cb17db", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "notes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'musical sounds produced by playing' is too vague to assign a more specific concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q1277575", "wikidata_description": "mails or messages written by a person and sent to one or several other persons"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "obvious past", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "past and the future. Let it be named the specious present, and let the past, that is given as being the past, be known as the obvious past. All the notes of a bar of a song seem to the listener to be ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "known as"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Smyrne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "smyrne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Smyrne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Smyrne"], "contexts": ["ancient city, modern Izmir, Turkey", "city where slave trade occurs"]}, {"entity_id": "smyrna", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Smyrna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Smyrna"], "contexts": ["city where Orientalist resided"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Smyrne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Smyrna", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3440, "stable_key": "clu_9b5b3309607650a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Izmir", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Turkey", "note": "Smyrna is the ancient name for the modern Turkish city of Izmir. The context of an ancient city and its location fits.", "wikidata_id": "Q35997", "wikidata_description": "city in İzmir Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_extract": "İzmir is the third most populous city in Turkey, after Istanbul and Ankara. It is on the Aegean coast of Anatolia, and is the capital of İzmir Province. As of 2024, İzmir Province has a total population of 4,493,242 while İzmir city is home to around 3.3 million inhabitants. It extends along the outlying waters of the Gulf of İzmir and inland to the north across the Gediz River Delta; to the east along an alluvial plain created by several small streams; and to slightly more rugged terrain in the south. İzmir's climate is Mediterranean. İzmir has more than 3,000 years of recorded urban history, and up to 8,500 years of history as a human settlement since the Neolithic period. In classical antiquity, the city was known as Smyrna – a name which remained in use in English and various other languages until around 1930, when government efforts led the original Greek name to be forcefully phased out internationally in favor of its Turkish counterpart İzmir. Lying on an advantageous location at the head of a gulf running down in a deep indentation, midway along the western Anatolian coast, İzmir has been one of the principal mercantile cities of the Mediterranean Sea for much of its history.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%B0zmir"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ásia menor", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2070, "target_cluster_name": "Ásia menor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Izmir (Smyrna) is a major city located in Anatolia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Izmir (Smyrna) is a major city located in Anatolia.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "fahr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fahr."], "contexts": ["Fahrenheit scale for temperature", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahr_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fahr."], "contexts": ["unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["scale for measuring temperature", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["temperature scale"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fahr.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Fahr.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3441, "stable_key": "clu_4ed9ffb8f2d10998", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fahrenheit scale", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105525247", "wikidata_description": "temperature scale", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahrenheit%20scale", "wikipedia_extract": "The Fahrenheit scale () is a temperature scale based on one proposed in 1724 by the physicist Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit (1686–1736). It uses the degree Fahrenheit (symbol: °F) as the unit. Several accounts of how he originally defined his scale exist, but the original paper suggests the lower defining point, 0 °F, was established as the freezing temperature of a solution of brine made from a mixture of water, ice, and ammonium chloride (a salt). The other limit established was his best estimate of the average human body temperature, originally set at 90 °F, then 96 °F (about 2.6 °F less than the modern value due to a later redefinition of the scale). For much of the 20th century, the Fahrenheit scale was defined by two fixed points with a 180 °F separation: the temperature at which pure water freezes was defined as 32 °F and the boiling point of water was defined to be 212 °F, both at sea level and under standard atmospheric pressure. It is now formally defined using the Kelvin scale.", "note": "While Fahrenheit is a surname, in the context of early modern science and measuring temperature, it refers to the temperature scale."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "podocarpus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Podocarpus"], "contexts": ["genus of trees", "tree species found at certain altitudes"]}, {"entity_id": "podocarpus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Podocarpus"], "contexts": ["plant genus observed by Columbus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Podocarpus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Podocarpus", "similarity": 0.804}], "id": 3442, "stable_key": "clu_8721983039072206", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Podocarpus", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Podocarpus L.f.", "family": "Podocarpaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q157524", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Podocarpus", "wikipedia_extract": "Podocarpus () is a genus of conifers, the most numerous and widely distributed of the podocarp family, the Podocarpaceae. Podocarpus species are evergreen shrubs or trees, usually from 1 to 25 m (3 to 82 ft) tall, known to reach 40 m (130 ft) at times. The cones have two to five fused cone scales, which form a fleshy, berry-like, brightly coloured receptacle at maturity. The fleshy cones attract birds, which then eat the cones and disperse the seeds in their droppings. Plants of the World Online accepts 116 species. Other authorities place 97 to 107 species in the genus depending on the circumscription of the species."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pines", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 425, "target_cluster_name": "pines", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Podocarpus is a genus of coniferous trees, and 'Pine' (historical name: pines) refers to coniferous trees. This indicates a relationship within the broader category of coniferous plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Podocarpus is a genus of coniferous trees, and 'Pine' (historical name: pines) refers to coniferous trees. This indicates a relationship within the broader category of coniferous plants.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Certes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "certes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Certes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Certes"], "contexts": ["parliament or assembly", "parliamentary body"]}, {"entity_id": "certidao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "certidao", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["certidao"], "contexts": ["certificate of authenticity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Certes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "certidao", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 3443, "stable_key": "clu_ddc8b1b9b973b0a2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Certificates", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "description": "Official documents attesting to a fact, status, or qualification.", "note": "The Latin 'certo' and 'certidao' (certificate) suggest official documents rather than a parliamentary body.", "wikipedia_extract": "An Extended Validation (EV) Certificate is a certificate conforming to X.509 that proves the legal entity of the owner and is signed by a certificate authority key that can issue EV certificates. EV certificates can be used in the same manner as any other X.509 certificates, including securing web communications with HTTPS and signing software and documents. Unlike domain-validated certificates and organization-validation certificates, EV certificates can be issued only by a subset of certificate authorities (CAs) and require verification of the requesting entity's legal identity before certificate issuance. As of February 2021, all major web browsers (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge and Apple Safari) have menus which show the EV status of the certificate and the verified legal identity of EV certificates. Mobile browsers typically display EV certificates the same way they do Domain Validation (DV) and Organization Validation (OV) certificates. Of the ten most popular websites online, none use EV certificates and the trend is away from their usage."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "maître", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "maître", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "maître", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["maître"], "contexts": ["a person who owns another", "owner of a slave"]}, {"entity_id": "masters", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "masters", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["masters"], "contexts": ["highly skilled individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "maître", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "masters", "similarity": 0.865}], "id": 3444, "stable_key": "clu_44194d624078e224", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "master", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A skilled individual or one in a position of authority or ownership.", "note": "The contexts suggest both a skilled person and a master-owner, hence the general term.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Señores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2620, "target_cluster_name": "Señores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Masters can refer to individuals holding a university degree or to those in a position of authority, overlapping with the social respect implied by 'Gentlemen'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Masters can refer to individuals holding a university degree or to those in a position of authority, overlapping with the social respect implied by 'Gentlemen'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Candler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "candler", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Candler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Candler"], "contexts": ["individual whose observations are mentioned", "observer's name"]}, {"entity_id": "candace", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Candace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Candace"], "contexts": ["queen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Candler", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Candace", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3445, "stable_key": "clu_47dc1f12fe9ca8a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Candler", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "An individual whose observations were recorded.", "note": "The presence of 'Candace' suggests a potential confusion or different individual. 'Candler' is the more prominent name in the sources.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "morris", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Morris"], "contexts": ["observer and year", "author of scientific observations"]}, {"entity_id": "morris", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Morris"], "contexts": ["author of geological papers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Morris", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Morris", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3446, "stable_key": "clu_594792978438d7f3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Morris", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1810, "death_year": 1890, "description": "English geologist and paleontologist, curator of the British Museum.", "note": "The context 'author of geological papers' strongly suggests John Morris, a prominent geologist active in the period."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Buckland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2737, "target_cluster_name": "Buckland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "John Morris was a paleontologist and colleague of William Buckland, and they collaborated on geological and paleontological studies.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: John Morris was a paleontologist and colleague of William Buckland, and they collaborated on geological and paleontological studies.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "taman", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Taman"], "contexts": ["location of salses with cold water", "peninsula with mud volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "taman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Taman"], "contexts": ["peninsula where gases were studied"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Taman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Taman", "similarity": 0.795}], "id": 3447, "stable_key": "clu_112cea31a63c6bc2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tamán", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Context suggests a location studied for gases, possibly in Venezuela given Humboldt's travels. 'Taman' is also a common name for trees.", "wikipedia_extract": "Taman Negara is a national park in Peninsular Malaysia. It was established in 1938 and 1939 as the King George V National Park after Theodore Hubback lobbied the sultans of Pahang, Terengganu and Kelantan to set aside a piece of land that covers the three states for the creation of a protected area. After independence, it was renamed Taman Negara, which means \"national park\" in Malay. Taman Negara has a total area of 4,343 km2 and it is one of the world's oldest rainforests, estimated to be more than 130 million years old. Mount Tahan, Peninsular Malaysia's highest point with an elevation of 7,175 ft (2,187 m) above sea level, is within the park's boundaries. Taman Negara is an important conservation area for the Peninsular Malaysian rain forest and montane rain forest ecoregions, is rich in biodiversity and home to several endangered species such as the Malayan tiger and Asian elephant.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taman_Negara"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Monte Zibio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "monte_zibio", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Monte Zibio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Monte Zibio"], "contexts": ["mountain location", "mountain name"]}, {"entity_id": "monte_ziblo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Monte Ziblo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Monte Ziblo"], "contexts": ["inactive mud volcano near Sassuolo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Monte Zibio", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Monte Ziblo", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 3448, "stable_key": "clu_7ce52829a1535eb1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Montegibbio", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "The spelling variations and context of 'inactive mud volcano near Sassuolo' clearly point to Montegibbio."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sassuolo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sassuolo"], "contexts": ["town name", "location of a salse"]}, {"entity_id": "sassuolo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sassuolo"], "contexts": ["town in Northern Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sassuolo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sassuolo", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3449, "stable_key": "clu_f5d5851277f24634", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sassuolo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q8598", "wikidata_description": "Italian comune", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sassuolo", "wikipedia_extract": "Sassuolo (Italian pronunciation: [sasˈswɔːlo]; Modenese: Sasól [saˈsoːl]) is an Italian town, comune, and industrial centre of the Province of Modena in Emilia-Romagna. Standing on the right bank of the river Secchia some 17 kilometres (11 mi) southwest of Modena, the town is best known for being the centre of the Italian tile industry and for being the home town of Serie A side U.S. Sassuolo Calcio. The exact origin of the toponym Sassuolo is unclear. One theory is that it might derive from the abundant deposits of petroleum found in the area. This is because petroleum was anciently known as \"stone oil\" or \"olio di sasso\" in Italian, from which the composite word Sassuolo (sasso + olio) may have been created."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "obdorsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Obdorsk"], "contexts": ["location near mouth of Ob river", "city near the mouths of the Oby river"]}, {"entity_id": "obdorsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Obdorsk"], "contexts": ["location on the Oby river"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Obdorsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Obdorsk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3450, "stable_key": "clu_d919ace080fd23f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obdorsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Obdorsk is a historical name for the city now known as Salekhard, located at the mouth of the Ob river.", "wikidata_id": "Q117070340", "wikidata_description": "fishing vessel built in 1959", "wikipedia_extract": "Salekhard (Russian: Салеха́рд [səlʲɪˈxart]; Khanty: Пуӆңават, Pułñawat; Nenets: Саляʼ харад, Saljaꜧ harad, formerly Obdorsk) is a town and the administrative centre of Yamalo-Nenets Autonomous Okrug, Russia. The town lies on the Arctic Circle, with the town centre being about 1 kilometer (0.62 mi) south and suburbs stretching to the north of the circle. The population is 47,910 (2021 census). The settlement of Obdorsk (Обдорск) was founded in 1595, in the place of a Khanty settlement called Polnovat-Vozh (Полноват-вож), by Russian settlers after the conquest of Siberia. It was situated on the Ob River, and its name supposedly derives from that. The land around Obdorsk was referred to as Obdorsky krai, or Obdoriya."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "nismes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nismes"], "contexts": ["city of observation", "location of geological observation"]}, {"entity_id": "nismes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nismes"], "contexts": ["location associated with Valz"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nismes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nismes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3451, "stable_key": "clu_c14575b85e7f8333", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nîmes", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q42807", "wikidata_description": "city and prefecture of the Gard department in the Occitanie region of Southern France", "wikipedia_extract": "The siege of Nîmes took place shortly after the capture and destruction of Avignon in 737. Charles Martel failed to capture the Umayyad city of Narbonne but devastated most of the other principal settlements of Septimania, including Nîmes, Agde, Béziers and Maguelonne, which he viewed as potential strongholds of the Saracens. The city of Nîmes (Chronicle of Fredegar) and the Roman amphitheatre (turned into a fortress by the Visigoths) were destroyed under the orders of Charles Martel. The Arabs were temporarily contained to the city of Narbonne, though a second expedition was needed later that year to regain control of Provence after Arab forces returned. According to Paul the Deacon's Historia Langobardorum the Arabs retreated when they learned that Martel had formed an alliance with the Lombards. Martel's remaining years - he had only four to live - were spent setting up and strengthening the administrative structure that became the Carolingian Empire, and the feudal state that would persist through the Dark Ages.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%AEmes"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rousseau", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rousseau"], "contexts": ["author whose works are unknown to monks"]}, {"entity_id": "rousseau", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rousseau"], "contexts": ["example of hypocrisy", "philosopher whose path is followed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rousseau", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Rousseau", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3452, "stable_key": "clu_c59721da8e430463", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1712, "death_year": 1778, "description": "Genevan philosopher and writer, influential figure of the Enlightenment.", "note": "The context provided, particularly 'example of hypocrisy' and his philosophical influence, strongly aligns with Jean-Jacques Rousseau.", "wikidata_id": "Q6527", "wikidata_description": "Genevan philosopher, writer, and composer (1712–1778)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Jacques%20Rousseau", "wikipedia_extract": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau (UK: , US: ; French: [ʒɑ̃ʒak ʁuso]; 28 June 1712 – 2 July 1778) was a Genevan philosopher, philosophe, writer, and composer. His political philosophy influenced the progress of the Age of Enlightenment throughout Europe, as well as aspects of the French Revolution and the development of modern political, economic, and educational thought. Rousseau's Discourse on Inequality, which argues that private property is the source of inequality, and The Social Contract, which outlines the basis for a legitimate political order, are cornerstones in modern political and social thought. Rousseau's sentimental novel Julie, or the New Heloise (1761) was important to the development of preromanticism and romanticism in fiction. His Émile, or On Education (1762) is an educational treatise on the place of the individual in society. Rousseau's autobiographical writings—the posthumously published Confessions (completed in 1770), which initiated the modern autobiography, and the unfinished Reveries of the Solitary Walker (composed 1776–1778)—exemplified the late 18th-century \"Age of Sensibility\", and featured an increased focus on subjectivity and introspection that later characterized modern writing."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Goethe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1020, "target_cluster_name": "Goethe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rousseau's ideas on nature, education, and sentiment were highly influential on Goethe and the Sturm und Drang movement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Rousseau's ideas on nature, education, and sentiment were highly influential on Goethe and the Sturm und Drang movement.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lancerote", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lancerote"], "contexts": ["volcanic archipelago north of it"]}, {"entity_id": "lancerote", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lancerote"], "contexts": ["island with volcanic eruptions", "island with colitic bed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lancerote", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lancerote", "similarity": 0.775}], "id": 3453, "stable_key": "clu_40b93fc0da55ac50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lanzarote", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "The spelling variation 'Lancerote' for 'Lanzarote' is common in older texts, and the context of 'volcanic archipelago' is accurate.", "wikidata_id": "Q182432", "wikidata_description": "one of the main islands of the Canary Islands, Spain", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanzarote", "wikipedia_extract": "Lanzarote (UK: , US: , Spanish: [lanθaˈɾote] , locally [lansaˈɾote]) is a Spanish island, the easternmost of the Canary Islands, 125 kilometres (80 miles) off the north coast of Africa and 1,000 kilometres (600 miles) from the Iberian Peninsula. Covering 845.94 square kilometres (326.62 square miles), Lanzarote is the fourth-largest of the islands in the archipelago. With 163,230 inhabitants at the beginning of 2024, it is the third most populous Canary Island, after Tenerife and Gran Canaria. Located in the centre-west of the island is Timanfaya National Park, one of its main attractions. The island was declared a biosphere reserve by UNESCO in 1993. The island's capital is Arrecife, which lies on the eastern coastline."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Creusot", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "creuse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Creuse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Creuse"], "contexts": ["Creuse, du Var et de l'Aude"]}, {"entity_id": "creusot", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Creusot", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Creusot"], "contexts": ["location of iron experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Creuse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Creusot", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 3454, "stable_key": "clu_36e7bf2c57d30557", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Creuse", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "The text mentions 'Creuse, du Var et de l'Aude' which are departments in France. 'Creusot' is likely a misspelling or variation of 'Creuse' in this context, especially given the reference to iron experiments which could be related to industrial history in that region.", "wikidata_id": "Q23782107", "wikidata_description": "departmental legislature in France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Departmental%20Council%20of%20Creuse", "wikipedia_extract": "Departmental Council of Creuse (French: Conseil départemental de la Creuse) is the deliberative assembly of the French department of Creuse. Its headquarters is at Guéret. Since 2015, the President of the Departmental Council is Valérie Simonet (LR). She was re-elected in the 2021 departmental elections. The departmental council of Creuse includes 30 departmental councilors from the 15 cantons of Creuse."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cuba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "vile_de_cuba", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Vile de Cuba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vile de Cuba"], "contexts": ["tobacco quality comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "cuba", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cuba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cuba"], "contexts": ["island where guaiacam grows", "offered gold and incense"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Vile de Cuba", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cuba", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3455, "stable_key": "clu_dc7b4d8938f1b165", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Santiago de Cuba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A city and municipality in southeastern Cuba.", "note": "Includes 'Cuba' in contexts where it refers to the city or its vicinity, and also 'Santiago de Guayasquil' as a distinct city name.", "wikipedia_extract": "Santiago de Cuba (Spanish pronunciation: [sanˈtjaɣo ðe ˈkuβa]) is the second-largest city in Cuba and the capital city of Santiago de Cuba Province. It lies in the southeastern area of the island, some 870 km (540 mi) southeast of the Cuban capital of Havana. The municipality extends over 1,023.8 km2 (395.3 sq mi), and contains the communities of Antonio Maceo, Bravo, Castillo Duany, Daiquirí, El Caney, El Cobre, El Cristo, Guilera, Leyte Vidal, Moncada and Siboney. Historically Santiago de Cuba has been the second-most important city on the island after Havana, and remains the second-largest. It lies on a bay connected to the Caribbean Sea and is an important sea port. In 2022, the city of Santiago de Cuba recorded a population of 507,167 people."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Juana", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "evidence_snippet": "aujourd'hui ofTiciellement le sicmprc fiel Isia de Cuba, fut nommée , depuis sa découverte , successivement Juana, Fernandinu , Isia de Santiago et Isla dcl Ave Maria. Ses amies datent de l'année i5iG", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name"}, {"found_name": "Fernandinu", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "evidence_snippet": "aujourd'hui ofTiciellement le sicmprc fiel Isia de Cuba, fut nommée , depuis sa découverte , successivement Juana, Fernandinu , Isia de Santiago et Isla dcl Ave Maria. Ses amies datent de l'année i5iG", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name"}, {"found_name": "Isia de Santiago", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "evidence_snippet": "aujourd'hui ofTiciellement le sicmprc fiel Isia de Cuba, fut nommée , depuis sa découverte , successivement Juana, Fernandinu , Isia de Santiago et Isla dcl Ave Maria. Ses amies datent de l'année i5iG", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name"}, {"found_name": "Isla del Ave Maria", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "evidence_snippet": "aujourd'hui ofTiciellement le sicmprc fiel Isia de Cuba, fut nommée , depuis sa découverte , successivement Juana, Fernandinu , Isia de Santiago et Isla dcl Ave Maria. Ses amies datent de l'année i5iG", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name"}]}, {"canonical_name": "railways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rail-ways", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "rail-ways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rail-ways"], "contexts": ["railways"]}, {"entity_id": "railways", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "railways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["railways"], "contexts": ["transportation infrastructure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "rail-ways", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "railways", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 3456, "stable_key": "clu_5ec86dac7b7f8188", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Railways", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q22667", "wikidata_description": "interconnected network of infrastructure used for train rail transport", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railroad"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "girard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Girard"], "contexts": ["mentioned in Annales de Physique et de Chimie"]}, {"entity_id": "girard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Girard"], "contexts": ["identified nepheline in basalt", "on basalt composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Girard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Girard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3457, "stable_key": "clu_9cf7d5585f322bce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marc-Auguste Pictet", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1752, "death_year": 1825, "description": "Swiss naturalist, physicist, and geographer associated with Annales de Physique et de Chimie.", "note": "Humboldt's 'Relation Historique' and 'Kosmos' often reference scientific findings published in prominent journals. Girard is a less common name in this context. Marc-Auguste Pictet was a prominent figure in physics and geography and a frequent contributor to and editor of the 'Annales de Physique et de Chimie', making him a more likely referent for a discussion involving that journal and the identification of minerals.", "wikidata_id": "Q116754", "wikidata_description": "Genevan scientist (1752-1825)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marc-Auguste%20Pictet", "wikipedia_extract": "Marc-Auguste Pictet  (French: [piktɛ]; 23 July 1752 – 19 April 1825) was a Genevan scientific journalist and experimental natural philosopher. Pictet's main contribution to learning was his editing of the scientific section of the Bibliothèque Britannique (1796–1815), a publication devoted to the diffusion on the Continent of knowledge and arts produced in Great Britain. His own scientific research focused on the fields of physical science, especially calorimetry, but also astronomy, geology, meteorology and technology, especially chronometry and the manufacture of fine earthenware. He was born in Geneva, Republic of Geneva on 23 July 1752, the son of Charles Pictet, a military officer serving in the mercenary troops of the Netherlands, and his wife, Marie Dunant. Marc-Auguste studied natural philosophy and law at the Academy of Geneva and qualified as a lawyer in 1774. After one year in England (1775–76), he became assistant to Jacques-André Mallet at the Geneva Observatory and took an interest in meteorology and map-making."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Teguiza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "teguayo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Teguayo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Teguayo"], "contexts": ["fabulous lake"]}, {"entity_id": "teguiza", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Teguiza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Teguiza"], "contexts": ["location near formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Teguayo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Teguiza", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3458, "stable_key": "clu_f46829b61bb79a9f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Isthmus of Tehuantepec", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "'Teguiza' is likely a transcription error for Tehuantepec in one context.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ferrocarril del Istmo de Tehuantepec is a government-owned railway system in Mexico that has three lines. It seeks to become a global logistics network focused on the manufacture and movement of goods between the Pacific Ocean and the Atlantic Ocean through the Isthmus of Tehuantepec. The project consists of the renovation of the railroad, which is expected to increase the speed of the freight train from 20 km/h (12 mph) to 70 km/h (43 mph), more than three times the current one. Meanwhile, the passenger train will be able to reach speeds of up to 100 km/h (62 mph). The state-owned company Ferrocarril del Istmo de Tehuantepec was founded in 1999, to operate the railway that connects Salina Cruz with the locality of Medias Aguas, Veracruz. Mayab and Chiapas lines were formerly leased to Ferrocarriles Chiapas-Mayab until Genesee & Wyoming gave up its concession in 2007.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tren_Interoceánico"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hebrew", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hébreu", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hébreu", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hébreu"], "contexts": ["Hebrew language"]}, {"entity_id": "hebrew", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Hebrew", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hebrew"], "contexts": ["historic race with united arts", "scriptures with marked difference of aggregation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hébreu", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Hebrew", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3459, "stable_key": "clu_a74e3eb6d2a97c53", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hebrew language", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9288", "wikidata_description": "Northwest Semitic language"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Sindu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sinde", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sinde", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sinde"], "contexts": ["independent state"]}, {"entity_id": "sindu", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Sindu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sindu", "Sindhu"], "contexts": ["river called Milan or Sinteu", "Sanskrit name for a river"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sinde", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Sindu", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3460, "stable_key": "clu_c1d1cc0650367c36", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sinu River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "River in Colombia, associated with coal deposits and medicinal plants.", "note": "The mention of 'Sindu' and 'Sinteu' suggests a possible conflation with the Indus River or a misinterpretation of local names.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sinú River (Spanish: Río Sinú), is a river in northwestern Colombia that flows mostly through the Córdoba Department and into the Caribbean. It is the third most important river in the Caribbean Region, after the Magdalena River and the Cauca River. It is born in the Antioquia Department, flowing south to north for 415 kilometres (258 mi) until flowing into the Caribbean Sea. The river is navigable for only half of its length, 200 kilometres (120 mi) starting in Montería, the largest city in its basin. Also, this river is the main tourist attraction of Montería decorated with the first avenue when it passes by Montería. The river flows between the eastern and middle prongs of the Cordillera Occidental."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "jeniseisk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jeniseisk"], "contexts": ["city in Siberia"]}, {"entity_id": "jeniseisk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jeniseisk"], "contexts": ["government in Siberia", "government containing district of Agram"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jeniseisk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jeniseisk", "similarity": 0.759}], "id": 3461, "stable_key": "clu_60cd01e72dab1c70", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yeniseysk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Both 'government' (guberniya) and 'city' refer to the administrative and urban center in Siberia.", "wikidata_id": "Q135686", "wikidata_description": "town in Yeniseysk Urban Okrug, Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia", "wikipedia_extract": "Yeniseysk (Russian: Енисейск, IPA: [jɪnʲɪˈsʲejsk]) is a town in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia, located on the Yenisei River. Population: 18,766 (2010 census); 20,394 (2002 census); 22,891 (1989 Soviet census); 20,000 (1970). Yeniseysk was founded in 1619 as a stockaded town—the first town on the Yenisei River. It played an important role in Russian colonization of East Siberia in the 17th–18th centuries. Its location is due to the Siberian River Routes from the Urals, up the Ob, up the Ket River and over a portage to Yeniseysk and from there to the Yenisei basin. It became less important due to road and rail building further south."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tibet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tibet"], "contexts": ["region excluded from Tartary"]}, {"entity_id": "tibet", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tibet"], "contexts": ["region where wheat ripens at high elevation", "region where wheat ripens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tibet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tibet", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3462, "stable_key": "clu_d1485e52c9082060-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tibet", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in Asia, also including an island.", "note": "Includes references to Tibet and Tahiti.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tibet ( ) is a region in the western part of East Asia, covering much of the Tibetan Plateau. It is the homeland of the Tibetans. Other ethnic groups also reside on the plateau, including Mongols, the Lhoba, Monpa, Qiang, Sherpa, and since the 20th century, Han and Hui peoples. Tibet is the highest region on Earth, with an average elevation of 4,380 m (14,000 ft). Lying within the Himalayas, the highest point in Tibet is Mount Everest – the highest peak on Earth, standing 8,848 m (29,000 ft) above sea level. In the 7th century, the Yarlung dynasty expanded into surrounding areas such as Amdo."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Chinese Tartary", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tartarie_chinoise", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tartarie Chinoise", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tartarie Chinoise"], "contexts": ["historical region in Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "chinese_tartary", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chinese Tartary", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chinese Tartary"], "contexts": ["region where herbarium was collected", "herbarium collected in"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tartarie Chinoise", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chinese Tartary", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 3463, "stable_key": "clu_b0ef4bcbc1153307", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chinese Tartary", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "China", "wikidata_id": "Q5100667", "wikidata_description": "indirect control areas of the Qing dynasty east of the Pamir plateau", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese%20Tartary", "wikipedia_extract": "Chinese Tartary (Chinese: 中國韃靼利亞; pinyin: Zhōngguó Dádálìyà or Chinese: 中属鞑靼利亚; pinyin: Zhōng shǔ Dádálìyà) is an archaic geographical term referring to the regions of Manchuria, Mongolia, Xinjiang (also referred to as Chinese Turkestan), and Tibet under the rule of the Qing dynasty of China. The geographical extent of Chinese Tartary largely corresponds with that of the \"Feudatory Regions\" (Chinese: 藩部; pinyin: fānbù), as defined by the Qing court. The term \"Tartar\" was used by Europeans to refer to ethnicities living in northern, northeastern, and western China, including the Mongols, Manchus, Tibetans, and Central Asians. Some definitions include the Japanese (as indicated in violet on the map below). The regions are now more commonly referred to by scholars as Inner Asia. Early European writers used the term \"Tartar\" indiscriminately for all the peoples of Northern Eurasia and referred to their lands as \"Tartary\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Tartaria", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 390, "target_cluster_name": "Tartaria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chinese Tartary is a specific geographical region that falls within the broader, less defined area historically known as Tartary. It represents a sub-region or a more specific designation within the larger 'Tartary' concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chinese Tartary is a specific geographical region that falls within the broader, less defined area historically known as Tartary. It represents a sub-region or a more specific designation within the larger 'Tartary' concept.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Asie orientale", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 737, "target_cluster_name": "Asie orientale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chinese Tartary is a historical region that overlaps with parts of East Asia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chinese Tartary is a historical region that overlaps with parts of East Asia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "New Siberia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2605, "target_cluster_name": "New Siberia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chinese Tartary was a historical term used in European geography to refer to a vast region of Central and East Asia, often overlapping with or including parts of Siberia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chinese Tartary was a historical term used in European geography to refer to a vast region of Central and East Asia, often overlapping with or including parts of Siberia.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "carguairazo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carguairazo"], "contexts": ["volcano in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "carguairazo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Carguairazo"], "contexts": ["mountain that fell in", "location of volcanic mud eruptions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Carguairazo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Carguairazo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3464, "stable_key": "clu_10214cb2dcfb0fb3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carguaírazo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Andes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 207, "target_cluster_name": "Andes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "C