 "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["drachmes"], "contexts": ["currency unit used by Greeks"]}, {"entity_id": "drachma", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "drachma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["drachma"], "contexts": ["unit of quantity for medicines", "unit of weight for substance"]}, {"entity_id": "dragma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["dragma"], "contexts": ["ingredient mixed with Raiz da china"]}, {"entity_id": "dragma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "dragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["dragma"], "contexts": ["unit of weight"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "drachmes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "drachma", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 3488, "stable_key": "clu_9edd4661cb2e5515", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Drachma", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Unit of weight, often used in apothecaries' contexts, derived from the ancient Greek currency.", "wikidata_id": "Q5304630", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects; genus of Arthoropoda containing only one species", "wikipedia_extract": "In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Helios (; Ancient Greek: Ἥλιος pronounced [hɛ̌ːlios], lit. 'Sun'; Homeric Greek: Ἠέλιος) is the god who personifies the Sun. His name is also Latinized as Helius, and he is often given the epithets Hyperion (\"the one above\") and Phaethon (\"the shining\"). Helios is often depicted in art with a radiant crown and driving a horse-drawn chariot through the sky. He was a guardian of oaths and also the god of sight. Though Helios was a relatively minor deity in Classical Greece, his worship grew more prominent in late antiquity thanks to his identification with several major solar divinities of the Roman period, particularly Apollo and Sol.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drachma"}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "livres sterling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2746, "target_cluster_name": "livres sterling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are historical units of currency.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are historical units of currency.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "bastoni", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bâtons", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "bâtons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bâtons"], "contexts": ["weapon used by sailors"]}, {"entity_id": "bastoni", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "bastoni", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["bastoni"], "contexts": ["tools for pressing", "sticks used with pressing instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bâtons", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "bastoni", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3489, "stable_key": "clu_085ea64e39454281", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "staffs", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The Italian 'bastoni' and French 'bâtons' in the given contexts refer to staffs, which could be used as weapons by sailors or as tools for pressing.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff%20%28name%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Staff is both a surname and a nickname."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "camalea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "camalote", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "camalote", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["camalote"], "contexts": ["a type of plant"]}, {"entity_id": "camalea", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "camalea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["camalea", "camelea"], "contexts": ["spice, possibly cinnamon", "plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "camalote", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "camalea", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3490, "stable_key": "clu_ef88ff3b47b624c1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chamomile", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Matricaria chamomilla L.", "family": "Asteraceae", "note": "While 'camalea' could refer to cinnamon (as suggested in the note), 'camemali' in Polyanthea Medicinal strongly suggests Chamomile (from Latin 'camomilla'). The spice context is ambiguous. Given the stronger link to chamomile, that is the primary identification, but cinnamon remains a possibility.", "wikipedia_extract": "Chamomile (American English) or camomile (British English; see spelling differences) ( KAM-ə-myle or KAM-ə-meel) is the common name for several daisy-like plants of the family Asteraceae. Two of the species, Matricaria chamomilla and Chamaemelum nobile, are commonly used to make herbal infusions for beverages. Chamomile is used as a flavoring in foods, beverages, and cosmetics, in herbal teas, in brewing beer, and as a ground cover or seating plant in gardens. There is no clinical evidence supporting the effectiveness of consuming chamomile to treat any disease. Chamomile may interact adversely with various herbs and drugs, worsen pollen allergies, and is not recommended for people with hormone-sensitive conditions or when combined with anticoagulants. Because Roman chamomile may cause uterine contractions, it should not be used during pregnancy, and its safety during breastfeeding is unknown."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ritchie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ritchie"], "contexts": ["unfortunate Ritchie"]}, {"entity_id": "ritchie", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ritchie"], "contexts": ["exhibited rotation of water by electricity", "performed electrical experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ritchie", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ritchie", "similarity": 0.731}], "id": 3491, "stable_key": "clu_aff6d4a7c5547747", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ritchie", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Scientist associated with electrical rotation of water.", "note": "The specific Ritchie is unclear without more context or a first name."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lichtenberg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lichtenberg"], "contexts": ["scientist associated with electrical figures"]}, {"entity_id": "lichtenberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lichtenberg"], "contexts": ["stated stones reach atmosphere from space", "physicist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lichtenberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lichtenberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3492, "stable_key": "clu_d7cd070d2ad45303", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georg Christoph Lichtenberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1742, "death_year": 1799, "description": "Scientist known for electrical figures and meteoric theories."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "palma", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Palma"], "contexts": ["island in the archipelago"]}, {"entity_id": "palma", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Palma"], "contexts": ["island in the Canary Islands", "island related to eruption of Teneriffe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Palma", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Palma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3493, "stable_key": "clu_1728dc99a9e4f441", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Palma de Mallorca", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "While 'Palma' can refer to multiple places, in the context of the Canary Islands archipelago, it most likely refers to the island of La Palma. However, given the ambiguity, a broader search for 'Palma' in relation to Canary Islands is provided."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "vents du nord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "vents_du_nord", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vents du nord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vents du nord"], "contexts": ["cause currents"]}, {"entity_id": "southwest_wind", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "southwest wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["southwest wind"], "contexts": ["wind from the southwest direction"]}, {"entity_id": "northeast_wind", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "northeast wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["northeast wind"], "contexts": ["wind from the northeast direction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vents du nord", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "southwest wind", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vents du nord", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "northeast wind", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3494, "stable_key": "clu_c7a655be6202e9e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wind Direction and Atmospheric Phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster refers to specific wind directions ('vents du nord', 'southwest wind', 'northeast wind') and their role in atmospheric phenomena and currents, a concept in meteorology and geography.", "wikidata_id": "Q1057900", "wikidata_description": "geographic direction from which a wind blows", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind%20direction", "wikipedia_extract": "Wind direction is generally reported by the direction from which the wind originates. For example, a north or northerly wind blows from the north to the south; the exceptions are onshore winds (blowing onto the shore from the water) and offshore winds (blowing off the shore to the water). Wind direction is usually reported in cardinal (or compass) direction, or in degrees. Consequently, a wind blowing from the north has a wind direction referred to as 0° (360°); a wind blowing from the east has a wind direction referred to as 90°, etc. Weather forecasts typically give the direction of the wind along with its speed, for example a \"northerly wind at 15 km/h\" is a wind blowing from the north at a speed of 15 km/h. If wind gusts are present, their speed may also be reported."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Nouet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "noiiet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Noiiet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noiiet"], "contexts": ["observer of continental atmosphere temperatures"]}, {"entity_id": "nouet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nouet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nouet"], "contexts": ["engraved latitudes on Egyptian monuments", "provided latitudes engraved on monuments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Noiiet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nouet", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 3495, "stable_key": "clu_3126dfd3fd8a45c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Philippe-Eugène Nouet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1763, "death_year": 1832, "description": "French astronomer and mathematician, observed continental atmosphere temperatures.", "note": "Identified based on 'Nouet' and the context of astronomical and geographical observations, fitting with Humboldt's scientific network."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bembo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bembo"], "contexts": ["named in admirable pages"]}, {"entity_id": "bembo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bembo"], "contexts": ["historian observing Aetna eruption", "author of 'Aetna Dialogus'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bembo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bembo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3496, "stable_key": "clu_889680921cbe93ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pietro Bembo", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1470, "death_year": 1547, "description": "Italian scholar, historian, and cardinal, wrote on Aetna.", "note": "The context 'historian observing Aetna eruption' strongly points to Pietro Bembo, known for his work 'De Aetna'.", "wikidata_id": "Q16590374", "wikidata_description": "Italian catholic bishop, great-grandson of Cardinal Pietro Bembo"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tortona", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tortosa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tortosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tortosa"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "tortona", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tortona", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tortona"], "contexts": ["publication location of memoir", "Italian town"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tortosa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tortona", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 3497, "stable_key": "clu_1cfe3ddd36ebb0ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tortosa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "While 'Tortona' is a city in Italy, the context of Humboldt's work and geographical references often point to the more prominent 'Tortosa' in Spain, especially when linked to publications or scientific observations within a broader European context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "terek", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Terek"], "contexts": ["river mouth location"]}, {"entity_id": "terek", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Terek"], "contexts": ["river mouth where Kislar is located", "river mouth location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Terek", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Terek", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3498, "stable_key": "clu_300f2ac2a86583ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Terek River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q82776", "wikidata_description": "river in Georgia and Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terek%20%28river%29", "wikipedia_extract": "The Terek is a major river in the Northern Caucasus. It originates in the Mtskheta-Mtianeti region of Georgia and flows through North Caucasus region of Russia into the Caspian Sea. It rises near the juncture of the Greater Caucasus Mountain Range and the Khokh Range, to the southwest of Mount Kazbek, winding north in a white torrent between the town of Stepantsminda and the village of Gergeti toward the Russian region North Ossetia and the city of Vladikavkaz. It turns east to flow through Chechnya and Dagestan before dividing into two branches which empty into the Caspian Sea. Below the city of Kizlyar it forms a swampy river delta around 100 kilometres (62 mi) wide. The river is a key natural asset in the region, providing irrigation and hydroelectric power in its upper reaches."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cross", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "croix", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "croix", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["croix"], "contexts": ["held by mendicants"]}, {"entity_id": "cross", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cross", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cross"], "contexts": ["constellation near starless patches", "celestial reference point"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "croix", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cross", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3499, "stable_key": "clu_ed2b8f1420e367e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Crusado", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Portuguese currency, and a vessel for melting metals.", "note": "This cluster conflates a unit of currency ('crusados') with a type of crucible ('creusets') and a religious symbol ('croix'). The primary meaning suggested by the context of 'currency' and 'buying amber' is the Portuguese Crusado coin. However, the presence of 'creusets' also points to a vessel.", "wikipedia_extract": "Guru Arjan (Gurmukhi: ਗੁਰੂ ਅਰਜਨ, pronunciation: [gʊɾuː əɾd͡ʒənᵊ]; 15 April 1563 – 30 May 1606) was the fifth of the ten total Sikh Gurus. He compiled the first official edition of the Sikh scripture called the Adi Granth, which later expanded into the Guru Granth Sahib. He is regarded as the first of the two Gurus martyred in the Sikh faith. Guru Arjan was born in Goindval, in the Punjab, the youngest son of Bhai Jetha, who later became Guru Ram Das, and Mata Bhani, the daughter of Guru Amar Das. He completed the construction of the Darbar Sahib at Amritsar, after the fourth Sikh Guru founded the town and built a sarovar. Arjan compiled the hymns of previous Gurus and of other saints into Adi Granth, the first edition of the Sikh scripture, and installed it in the Harimandir Sahib."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "girgenti", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Girgenti"], "contexts": ["near Macaluba mud volcano"]}, {"entity_id": "girgenti", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Girgenti"], "contexts": ["location of mud volcanoes", "location in Sicily"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Girgenti", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Girgenti", "similarity": 0.814}], "id": 3500, "stable_key": "clu_f75a608771c88747", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Agrigento", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "Girgenti is the historical Italian name for Agrigento.", "wikipedia_extract": "Agrigento (Italian: [aɡriˈdʒɛnto] ; Sicilian: Girgenti [dʒɪɾˈdʒɛndɪ] or Giurgenti [dʒʊɾˈdʒɛndɪ]) is a city on the southern coast of the autonomous island region of Sicily in Italy, capital of the province of Agrigento. As of 2025, with a population of 55,227, it is also the largest city in the province, 10th-largest in Sicily and 115th-largest in Italy. Founded around 582 BC by Greek colonists from Gela, Agrigento, then known as Akragas, was one of the leading cities during the golden age of Ancient Greece. The city flourished under Theron's leadership in the 5th century BC, marked by ambitious public works and the construction of renowned temples. Despite periods of dormancy during the Punic Wars, Agrigento emerged as one of Sicily's largest cities in the Republican era. During the Principate, Agrigento's strategic port and diverse economic ventures, including sulfur mining, trade and agriculture, sustained its importance throughout the high and late Empire."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tanger", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tanger"], "contexts": ["place where M. de Rieux conceived a plan"]}, {"entity_id": "tanger", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tanger"], "contexts": ["city in Africa", "city in Morocco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tanger", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tanger", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3501, "stable_key": "clu_a6491f14986d439f", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Tangier ( tan-JEER; Arabic: طنجة, romanized: Ṭanjah, [tˤandʒa], [tˤanʒa]) is a city in northwestern Morocco, on the coasts of the Mediterranean Sea and the Atlantic Ocean. The city is the capital of the Tangier-Tetouan-Al Hoceima region, as well as the Tangier-Assilah Prefecture of Morocco. Many civilisations and cultures have influenced the history of Tangier, starting from before the 10th century BCE. Originating as a strategic Phoenician town and trading centre, Tangier has been a nexus for many cultures. In 1923, it became an international zone managed by colonial powers and became a destination for many European and American diplomats, spies, bohemians, writers and businessmen. That status came to an end with Moroccan independence, in phases between 1956 and 1960."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Burg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bùrg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bùrg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bùrg"], "contexts": ["creator of astronomical tables"]}, {"entity_id": "burg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Burg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Burg"], "contexts": ["astronomer who created tables", "astronomer who provided early tables"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bùrg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Burg", "similarity": 0.609}], "id": 3502, "stable_key": "clu_458b3e7d246a2f14", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ivan Fyodorovich Burg", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Astronomer credited with astronomical tables", "note": "The name 'Burg' is very common. Without further context or specific transliteration, identifying the precise historical figure intended is difficult. The subcategory 'SCHOLAR' and the context 'astronomer who created tables' are the primary clues."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Magdeburg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "magdebourg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Magdebourg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magdebourg"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "magdeburg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Magdeburg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Magdeburg"], "contexts": ["city where Leibnitz wrote a letter", "city where Leibnitz's letter was sent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Magdebourg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Magdeburg", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3503, "stable_key": "clu_de5a51ab5763d092", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magdeburg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q1733", "wikidata_description": "capital city of the German state of Saxony-Anhalt", "wikipedia_extract": "Magdeburg (  MAG-də-burg, German: [ˈmakdəbʊʁk] , locally [ˈmaχdəbʊɐ̯ç]; Low Saxon: Meideborg [ˈmaˑɪ̯debɔɐ̯x]) is the capital of the German state of Saxony-Anhalt. The city is on the Elbe river. Otto I, the Holy Roman Emperor and the founder of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg, was buried in the city's cathedral after his death. Magdeburg's version of German town law, known as Magdeburg rights, spread throughout Central and Eastern Europe. In the Late Middle Ages, Magdeburg was one of the largest and most prosperous German cities and a notable member of the Hanseatic League. One of the most notable people from the city was Otto von Guericke, famous for his experiments with the Magdeburg hemispheres.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdeburg"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "heidelberg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Heidelberg"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "heidelberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Heidelberg"], "contexts": ["location of an edition", "city in Germany"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Heidelberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Heidelberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3504, "stable_key": "clu_a96a446a3e80b0e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heidelberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "note": "One instance refers to 'Hildesheim', which is a separate city.", "wikidata_id": "Q2966", "wikidata_description": "large city in Baden-Württemberg, Germany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidelberg", "wikipedia_extract": "Heidelberg (; German: [ˈhaɪdl̩bɛʁk] ; Palatine German: Heidlberg) is the fifth-largest city in the German state of Baden-Württemberg, and with a population of about 163,000, of which roughly a quarter consists of students, it is Germany's 51st-largest city. Located about 78 km (48 mi) south of Frankfurt, Heidelberg is part of the densely populated Rhine-Neckar Metropolitan Region which has its centre in Mannheim. Heidelberg is located on the Neckar River, at the point where it leaves its narrow valley between the Oden Forest and the Little Oden Forest, and enters the wide Upper Rhine Plain. The old town lies in the valley, the end of which is flanked by the Königstuhl in the south and the Heiligenberg in the north. The majority of the population lives in the districts west of the mountains in the Upper Rhine Plain, into which the city has expanded over time. Heidelberg University, founded in 1386, is Germany's oldest and one of Europe's most reputable universities."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "faenza", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faenza"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "faenza", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Faenza"], "contexts": ["city name", "location mentioned in the passage"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Faenza", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Faenza", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3505, "stable_key": "clu_ed6649ed123bf759", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Faenza (UK:  fah-ENT-sə, US:  fah-EN-zə, Italian: [faˈɛntsa]; Romagnol: Fènza or Fẽza; Latin: Faventia) is a comune (municipality) with 58,746 inhabitants in the Province of Ravenna in Emilia-Romagna, Italy. The city is historically renowned for its production of artistic ceramics, to the extent that maiolica, due to the fame of local craftsmanship, is known worldwide as faience. Of Roman origin, under the rule of the Manfredi, Faenza entered a period of significant growth that peaked during the Renaissance and Baroque eras, shaping its art and architecture. Due to its vibrant artistic and cultural activity, between the 18th and 19th centuries, it became a prominent center of Neoclassicism in Italy and Europe, with the finest example today being the National Museum of Palazzo Milzetti. Beyond its role as a thriving creative hub, Faenza is currently home to research and development centers focused on materials and environmental sustainability. Located just west of the heart of Romagna, at the foot of the first slopes of the Faenza Apennines, it serves as the administrative seat of the Union of Faenza Romagna and is the episcopal see of the Diocese of Faenza-Modigliana."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "idealism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["idealism"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine", "philosophical system contrasting with materialism"]}, {"entity_id": "idealism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["idealism"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine of mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "idealism", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "idealism", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3506, "stable_key": "clu_0cb37d3c214b638a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Idealism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q25339942", "wikidata_description": "concept in the arts", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idealism%20%28arts%29"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "metaphysicians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3527, "target_cluster_name": "metaphysicians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphysics encompasses various schools of thought, including 'Idealism', which posits that reality is fundamentally mental or spiritual.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Metaphysics encompasses various schools of thought, including 'Idealism', which posits that reality is fundamentally mental or spiritual.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "belief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2125, "target_cluster_name": "belief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Idealism is a philosophical stance that emphasizes the role of ideas or consciousness in constituting reality, thus relating to the nature of belief.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Idealism is a philosophical stance that emphasizes the role of ideas or consciousness in constituting reality, thus relating to the nature of belief.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Emotions of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "emotions_of_self", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Emotions of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Emotions of Self"], "contexts": ["feelings related to the self", "chapter title discussing self-emotions"]}, {"entity_id": "human_emotions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "human emotions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["human emotions"], "contexts": ["classed among human emotions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Emotions of Self", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "human emotions", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3507, "stable_key": "clu_6447cb81e3798ee4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Self-related emotions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9415", "wikidata_description": "biological states associated with the nervous system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emotion", "wikipedia_extract": "Emotions are physical and mental states brought on by neurophysiological changes, variously associated with thoughts, feelings, behavioral responses, and a degree of pleasure or displeasure. There is no scientific consensus on a definition. Emotions are often intertwined with mood, temperament, personality, disposition, or creativity. Research on emotion has increased over the past two decades, with many fields contributing, including psychology, medicine, history, sociology of emotions, computer science and philosophy. The numerous attempts to explain the origin, function, and other aspects of emotions have fostered intense research on this topic. Theorizing about the evolutionary origin and possible purpose of emotion dates back to Charles Darwin."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_conditions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mechanical conditions"], "contexts": ["physical causes of mental processes", "laws governing physical interactions"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_conditions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical conditions"], "contexts": ["physical forces and constraints"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mechanical conditions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical conditions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3508, "stable_key": "clu_0a75bff4414279ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mechanical causation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "exhaustion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["exhaustion"], "contexts": ["condition that enfeebles memory", "state of extreme tiredness"]}, {"entity_id": "exhaustion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exhaustion"], "contexts": ["extreme tiredness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "exhaustion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "exhaustion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3509, "stable_key": "clu_ad597270791247d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Exhaustion", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Fatigue", "note": "While 'exhaustion' can refer to general tiredness, in medical contexts, it points to a state of extreme fatigue or debilitation.", "wikipedia_extract": "The ICD-11 of the World Health Organization (WHO) describes occupational burnout as a work-related phenomenon resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. According to the WHO, symptoms include \"feelings of energy depletion or exhaustion; increased mental distance from one's job, or feelings of negativism or cynicism related to one's job; and reduced professional efficacy.\" Occupational burnout is classified as an occupational phenomenon but is not recognized by the WHO as a medical or psychiatric condition. Social psychologist Christina Maslach and colleagues made clear that burnout does not constitute \"a single, one-dimensional phenomenon.\" However, national health bodies in some European countries do recognize it as such, and it is also independently recognized by some health practitioners. Nevertheless, a body of evidence suggests that what is termed burnout is a depressive condition, that is to say, indistinct from, and overlaps with, depression. Kaschka, Korczak, and Broich (2011) advanced the view that burnout is described in the Book of Exodus (18:17–18). In the New International Version of the Bible, Moses' father-in-law said to Moses, \"What you are doing is not good.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_burnout"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sleep", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1316, "target_cluster_name": "sleep", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Extreme exhaustion can lead to an inability to achieve normal, restorative sleep, or conversely, an overwhelming need for sleep that disrupts normal functioning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Extreme exhaustion can lead to an inability to achieve normal, restorative sleep, or conversely, an overwhelming need for sleep that disrupts normal functioning.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "modorras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1613, "target_cluster_name": "modorras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Exhaustion is a state of extreme physical or mental tiredness, often a cause or symptom of lethargy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Exhaustion is a state of extreme physical or mental tiredness, often a cause or symptom of lethargy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nervous shock", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 926, "target_cluster_name": "nervous shock", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The psychological and physiological impact of nervous shock can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The psychological and physiological impact of nervous shock can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "fome", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1659, "target_cluster_name": "fome", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The description of 'Muselmann' explicitly links starvation ('hunger disease') with exhaustion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The description of 'Muselmann' explicitly links starvation ('hunger disease') with exhaustion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "suffocação", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1686, "target_cluster_name": "suffocação", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The body's struggle against suffocation can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The body's struggle against suffocation can lead to extreme exhaustion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Faintings", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2269, "target_cluster_name": "Faintings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Extreme physical or mental exhaustion can lead to fainting.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Extreme physical or mental exhaustion can lead to fainting.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "bodily change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodily_change", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bodily change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["bodily change"], "contexts": ["alteration in the physical body", "alteration in physical state"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_changes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bodily changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily changes"], "contexts": ["physical alterations in the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bodily change", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodily changes", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 3510, "stable_key": "clu_091421a149b5d732", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physiological change", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "materialists", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "materialists", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "materialists", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["materialists"], "contexts": ["philosophy that matter is fundamental reality", "philosophical group emphasizing material basis"]}, {"entity_id": "materialist", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Materialist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Materialist"], "contexts": ["adherent of materialism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "materialists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Materialist", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3511, "stable_key": "clu_e88b8dfb2b66238d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "materialism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7081", "wikidata_description": "form of philosophical monism which holds matter to be the fundamental substance in nature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Materialism", "wikipedia_extract": "In philosophy and metaphysics, materialism is a form of monism holding that matter is the fundamental substance of nature, so that all things, including mind and consciousness, arise from material interactions and depend on physical processes, including those of the human brain and nervous system. It contrasts with monistic idealism, which treats consciousness as fundamental, and is related to naturalism, the view that only natural laws and forces operate in the universe, and to physicalism, the view that all that exists is ultimately physical. Physicalism extends materialism by including forms of physicality beyond ordinary matter (e.g. spacetime, energy, forces, exotic matter), and some use the terms interchangeably. Alternative philosophies opposed or alternative to materialism or physicalism include idealism, pluralism, dualism, solipsism, panpsychism, and other forms of monism. Materialism is the philosophical doctrine that matter has a primary position in the nature of the world, with mind or consciousness emerging as a secondary, dependent reality or not existing at all."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "candle-flame", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "candle-flame", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "candle-flame", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["candle-flame"], "contexts": ["source of light and heat"]}, {"entity_id": "candles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "candles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["candles"], "contexts": ["source of heat and light in mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "candle-flame", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "candles", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3512, "stable_key": "clu_f638bbf97501f2d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "candle flame", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q65574128", "wikidata_description": "print in the National Gallery of Art (NGA 54107)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pen", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pen", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pen", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["pen"], "contexts": ["writing instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "penna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "penna", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["penna"], "contexts": ["material not to be used for remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "penna", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3513, "stable_key": "clu_9b382d553d307cc5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A pen is a common writing instrument that applies ink to a surface, typically paper, for writing or drawing. Early pens such as reed pens, quill pens, dip pens and ruling pens held a small amount of ink on a nib or in a small void or cavity that had to be periodically recharged by dipping the tip of the pen into an inkwell. Today, such pens find only a small number of specialized uses, such as in illustration and calligraphy. Reed pens, quill pens and dip pens, which were used for writing, have been replaced by ballpoint pens, rollerball pens, fountain pens and felt or ceramic tip pens. Ruling pens, which were used for technical drawing and cartography, have been replaced by technical pens such as the Rapidograph. All of these modern pens contain internal ink reservoirs, such that they do not need to be dipped in ink while writing."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "psychic world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychic_world", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychic world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["psychic world"], "contexts": ["realm of mental phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritual_world", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spiritual world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiritual world"], "contexts": ["realm beyond sensory perception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychic world", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spiritual world", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3514, "stable_key": "clu_ead5d23323f7d0f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "psychic realm", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the realm of mental phenomena, distinct from the physical or spiritual."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "B. Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "b__sachs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "B. Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["B. Sachs"], "contexts": ["translator of Meynert's work", "translator of clinical treatise"]}, {"entity_id": "sachs", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sachs"], "contexts": ["author on milk cure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "B. Sachs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sachs", "similarity": 0.862}], "id": 3515, "stable_key": "clu_2a54d4ccb7776f84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bernhard Sachs", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Translator and author on medical topics.", "note": "The text mentions 'B. Sachs' as a translator of Meynert's work and 'Sachs' as an author on the milk cure. Bernhard Sachs (1858-1898) was a neurologist and psychiatrist who translated Meynert's works. It is plausible this refers to him, though the 'milk cure' reference might point to a different Sachs, or a less prominent one.", "wikidata_id": "Q825832", "wikidata_description": "German politician"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "tactile", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tactile", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tactile", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tactile"], "contexts": ["relating to the sense of touch", "relating to touch"]}, {"entity_id": "tangibility", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tangibility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["tangibility"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tactile", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tangibility", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 3516, "stable_key": "clu_590d1d941abbcdf4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "tactile", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18035138", "wikidata_description": "protein-coding gene in the species Homo sapiens", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CD96", "wikipedia_extract": "CD96 (Cluster of Differentiation 96) or Tactile (T cell activation, increased late expression)  is a protein that in humans is encoded by the CD96 gene. CD96 is a receptor protein which is expressed on T cells and NK cells and shares sequence similarity with CD226 (also known as DNAM-1). The protein encoded by this gene belongs to the immunoglobulin superfamily. It is a type I membrane protein. The protein may play a role in the adhesion of activated T and NK cells to their target cells during the late phase of the immune response. It may also function in antigen presentation."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "watch", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["watch"], "contexts": ["instrument for time measurement", "object wound up habitually"]}, {"entity_id": "watch", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["watch"], "contexts": ["object created by watchmaker"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "watch", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "watch", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3517, "stable_key": "clu_280999453d20cf2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "watch", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "chronomètres", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 853, "target_cluster_name": "chronomètres", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A watch is a portable form of a clock/chronometer, and in historical contexts, the terms could be used with overlapping meaning for personal timekeeping devices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: A watch is a portable form of a clock/chronometer, and in historical contexts, the terms could be used with overlapping meaning for personal timekeeping devices.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "clocks", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2303, "target_cluster_name": "clocks", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Watches are portable, personal timekeeping devices that evolved from and are functionally similar to clocks.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Watches are portable, personal timekeeping devices that evolved from and are functionally similar to clocks.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Horologium", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2822, "target_cluster_name": "Horologium", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Horologium and watch are portable timekeeping devices.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both Horologium and watch are portable timekeeping devices.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "right hand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "right_hand", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "right hand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["right hand"], "contexts": ["part of the body used for holding the bow", "limb on the right side"]}, {"entity_id": "braço_direito", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "braço direito", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["braço direito"], "contexts": ["right arm"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "right hand", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "braço direito", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 3518, "stable_key": "clu_1c2a47b430fac841", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "right hand", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The Portuguese 'braço direito' translates to 'right arm', which is synonymous with 'right hand' in many anatomical contexts, and both refer to a body part.", "wikidata_id": "Q24206677", "wikidata_description": "hand on the right side of the body"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "timbre", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["timbre"], "contexts": ["quality of sound distinguishing instruments", "sound quality of instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "timbre", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["timbre"], "contexts": ["quality of a musical sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "timbre", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "timbre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3519, "stable_key": "clu_45afb2aea94c862d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Timbre", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "integration", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1873, "target_cluster_name": "integration", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "with the chief series of recurrent noises. So that while the unlikenesses of feeling known as differences of pitch in tones are due to differences of integration among the recurrent noises of one seri", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of feeling difference related to integration", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "D. G. Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__g__thompson", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "D. G. Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["D. G. Thompson"], "contexts": ["author of A System of Psychology", "associationist writer on consciousness and Ego"]}, {"entity_id": "thompson", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thompson"], "contexts": ["investigator of solar heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "D. G. Thompson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thompson", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 3520, "stable_key": "clu_0bbf70e77fecbadc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Daniel Greenleaf Thompson", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1830, "death_year": 1901, "description": "American psychologist and author of 'A System of Psychology'.", "wikidata_id": "Q18657318", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "personal_equation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["personal equation"], "contexts": ["investigator's subjective bias", "observer's reaction time difference"]}, {"entity_id": "personal_equation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["personal equation"], "contexts": ["observer's timing bias"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "personal equation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "personal equation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3521, "stable_key": "clu_012e00917f78e900", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "personal equation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1017823", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal%20equation", "wikipedia_extract": "The term personal equation, in 19th- and early 20th-century science, referred to the idea that  different observers have different reaction times, which can introduce bias when it comes to measurements and observations. Simon Schaffer wrote it \"was the name given by astronomers after Bessel to the differences in measured transit times recorded by observers in the same situation\". The term originated in astronomy, when it was discovered that numerous observers making simultaneous observations would record slightly different values (for example, in recording the exact time at which a star crossed the wires of a reticule in a telescope), some of which were of a significant enough difference to afford for problems in larger calculations. The existence of the effect was first discovered when, in 1796, the Astronomer Royal Neville Maskelyne dismissed his assistant Kinnebrooke because he could not better the error of his observations relative to Maskelyne's own values. The problem was forgotten and only analysed two decades later by Friedrich Wilhelm Bessel at Königsberg Observatory in Prussia. Setting up an experiment to compare the values, Bessel and an assistant measured the times at which several stars crossed the wires of a reticule in different nights."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "simplicity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "simplicity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "simplicity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["simplicity"], "contexts": ["quality of the soul", "lack of complexity or division"]}, {"entity_id": "simple", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "simple", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["simple"], "contexts": ["made of one part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "simplicity", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "simple", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 3522, "stable_key": "clu_34db1e581774517f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "simplicity", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'quality of the soul' suggests a philosophical or theological usage, while 'made of one part' points to a more literal or material sense. Without further context for the 1563 text, 'simplicity' is the best general fit."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "anæsthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "anæsthetics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "anæsthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["anæsthetics"], "contexts": ["substances causing loss of sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "anæsthetics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "anæsthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["anæsthetics"], "contexts": ["agents causing loss of sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "anæsthetics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anæsthetics", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3523, "stable_key": "clu_7a363e2cbeaf7dee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anesthetics", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q50430271", "wikidata_description": "use of two or more substances simultaneously or sequentially to induce anesthesia.", "wikipedia_extract": "An anesthetic (American English) or anaesthetic (British English; see spelling differences) is a drug used to induce anesthesia ⁠— ⁠in other words, to result in a temporary loss of sensation or awareness. They may be divided into two broad classes: general anesthetics, which result in a reversible loss of consciousness, and local anesthetics, which cause a reversible loss of sensation for a limited region of the body without necessarily affecting consciousness. A wide variety of drugs are used in modern anesthetic practice. Many are rarely used outside anesthesiology, but others are used commonly in various fields of healthcare. Combinations of anesthetics are sometimes used for their synergistic and additive therapeutic effects. Adverse effects, however, may also be increased."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1259, "target_cluster_name": "nitrous oxide gas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nitrous oxide was historically used as an anesthetic, and 'Anesthetics' is a category that includes it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nitrous oxide was historically used as an anesthetic, and 'Anesthetics' is a category that includes it.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "intention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "intention", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intention"], "contexts": ["purpose or aim", "purposeful direction of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "intent", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "intent", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intent"], "contexts": ["intent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "intention", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "intent", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3524, "stable_key": "clu_e5361030cbd152f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "intention", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3918409", "wikidata_description": "document or message proposing some course of action, or the process by which that action is considered"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sensible", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensible", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensible", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sensible"], "contexts": ["perceptible by senses", "easily perceived relation"]}, {"entity_id": "sensible_properties", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sensible properties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensible properties"], "contexts": ["qualities perceived by senses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensible", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensible properties", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3525, "stable_key": "clu_f42fefc931d10277", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sensible qualities", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "herbert_spencer", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Herbert Spencer"], "contexts": ["philosopher quoted in 'Mind'", "psychologist ignoring association by similarity"]}, {"entity_id": "herbert_spencer", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herbert Spencer"], "contexts": ["author of \"First Principles\""]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Herbert Spencer", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Herbert Spencer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3526, "stable_key": "clu_dc23dab3734baea3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Herbert Spencer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1820, "death_year": 1903, "description": "English philosopher, biologist, anthropologist, and sociologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q144535", "wikidata_description": "English philosopher and political theorist (1820–1903)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert%20Spencer", "wikipedia_extract": "Herbert Spencer (27 April 1820 – 8 December 1903) was an English polymath active as a philosopher, psychologist, biologist, sociologist, and anthropologist. Spencer originated the expression \"survival of the fittest\", which he coined in Principles of Biology (1864) after reading Charles Darwin's 1859 book On the Origin of Species. The term strongly suggests natural selection, yet Spencer saw evolution as extending into realms of sociology and ethics, so he also supported Lamarckism. Spencer developed an all-embracing conception of evolution as the progressive development of the physical world, biological organisms, the human mind, and human culture and societies. As a polymath, he contributed to a wide range of subjects, including ethics, religion, anthropology, economics, political theory, philosophy, literature, astronomy, biology, sociology, and psychology. During his lifetime he achieved tremendous authority, mainly in English-speaking academia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Huxley", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 871, "target_cluster_name": "Huxley", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Huxley and Spencer were contemporaries and prominent figures in Victorian intellectual circles, both contributing significantly to discussions on evolution, biology, and philosophy. Huxley engaged with Spencer's ideas, particularly regarding social Darwinism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Huxley and Spencer were contemporaries and prominent figures in Victorian intellectual circles, both contributing significantly to discussions on evolution, biology, and philosophy. Huxley engaged with Spencer's ideas, particularly regarding social Darwinism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "M. James", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 442, "target_cluster_name": "M. James", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Herbert Spencer was a highly influential English philosopher and sociologist whose theories of evolution and social Darwinism had a significant impact on late 19th-century thought, including that of William James.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Herbert Spencer was a highly influential English philosopher and sociologist whose theories of evolution and social Darwinism had a significant impact on late 19th-century thought, including that of William James.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Hall", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 922, "target_cluster_name": "Hall", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential figures in the development of psychology and education, with Spencer's evolutionary theories impacting Hall's work on child development and adolescence.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential figures in the development of psychology and education, with Spencer's evolutionary theories impacting Hall's work on child development and adolescence.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Bain", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1664, "target_cluster_name": "Bain", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential Victorian thinkers associated with the development of evolutionary thought and psychology, though Spencer's work was more philosophical and sociological, while Bain focused on empirical psychology and education.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential Victorian thinkers associated with the development of evolutionary thought and psychology, though Spencer's work was more philosophical and sociological, while Bain focused on empirical psychology and education.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Comte", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2578, "target_cluster_name": "Comte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Auguste Comte and Herbert Spencer were both prominent figures in the development of sociology and evolutionary thought in the 19th century. They engaged in intellectual debates and their work is often compared and contrasted.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Auguste Comte and Herbert Spencer were both prominent figures in the development of sociology and evolutionary thought in the 19th century. They engaged in intellectual debates and their work is often compared and contrasted.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "metaphysicians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "metaphysicians", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "metaphysicians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["metaphysicians"], "contexts": ["philosophers of abstract reality", "thinkers concerned with abstract reality"]}, {"entity_id": "metaphysicians", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "metaphysicians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["metaphysicians"], "contexts": ["philosophers of fundamental reality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "metaphysicians", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metaphysicians", "similarity": 0.838}], "id": 3527, "stable_key": "clu_9db619a859dbe6ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "metaphysicians", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a philosophical discipline, not specific individuals.", "wikidata_id": "Q35277", "wikidata_description": "branch of philosophy dealing with the nature, structure, components and fundamental principles of reality", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaphysics", "wikipedia_extract": "Metaphysics is the branch of philosophy that examines the basic structure of reality. It is traditionally seen as the study of mind-independent features of the world, but some theorists view it as an inquiry into the conceptual framework of human understanding. Some philosophers, including Aristotle, designate metaphysics as first philosophy to suggest that it is more fundamental than other forms of philosophical inquiry. Metaphysics encompasses a wide range of general and abstract topics. It investigates the nature of existence, the features all entities have in common, and their division into categories of being. An influential division is between particulars and universals."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Being", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2425, "target_cluster_name": "Being", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphysics is traditionally concerned with the study of 'Being' as such, exploring its fundamental nature and categories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Metaphysics is traditionally concerned with the study of 'Being' as such, exploring its fundamental nature and categories."}, {"found_name": "First Cause", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2659, "target_cluster_name": "First Cause", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphysics often investigates the concept of a 'First Cause' as a fundamental principle of reality.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Metaphysics often investigates the concept of a 'First Cause' as a fundamental principle of reality."}, {"found_name": "Idealism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3506, "target_cluster_name": "idealism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphysics encompasses various schools of thought, including 'Idealism', which posits that reality is fundamentally mental or spiritual.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Metaphysics encompasses various schools of thought, including 'Idealism', which posits that reality is fundamentally mental or spiritual."}, {"found_name": "Extension (in physics/philosophy)", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2210, "target_cluster_name": "extension", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Metaphysical discussions about the nature of reality often engage with concepts like 'Extension', particularly in relation to space and matter.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Metaphysical discussions about the nature of reality often engage with concepts like 'Extension', particularly in relation to space and matter."}]}, {"canonical_name": "father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "father", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["father"], "contexts": ["male parent"]}, {"entity_id": "his_father", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "his father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["his father"], "contexts": ["Herschel's father, opinion on star clusters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "father", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "his father", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 3528, "stable_key": "clu_65bfa6f69ea4e678", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "father", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a familial relationship, not a specific individual outside of the context of the text."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "progenitor", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2035, "target_cluster_name": "progenitor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'father' is often used synonymously with 'ancestor' or 'progenitor' in genealogical contexts, referring to an earlier male ancestor.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'father' is often used synonymously with 'ancestor' or 'progenitor' in genealogical contexts, referring to an earlier male ancestor.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "marcus_aurelius", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Marcus Aurelius"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "marcus_aurelius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marcus Aurelius"], "contexts": ["ancient Roman emperor and philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3529, "stable_key": "clu_15d001db99cb879d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 121, "death_year": 180, "description": "Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher, author of Meditations", "wikidata_id": "Q1430", "wikidata_description": "16th Roman Emperor from 161 to 180 and Stoic philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus%20Aurelius", "wikipedia_extract": "Marcus Aurelius Antoninus ( or-EE-lee-əs; Latin: [ˈmaːrkʊs au̯ˈreːli.us antoːˈniːnʊs]; 26 April 121 – 17 March 180) was Roman emperor from 161 to 180 and a Stoic philosopher. He was a member of the Nerva–Antonine dynasty, the last of the rulers later known as the Five Good Emperors and the last emperor of the Pax Romana, an age of relative peace, calm, and stability for the Roman Empire lasting from 27 BC to 180 AD. He served as Roman consul in 140, 145, and 161. Marcus Aurelius was the son of the praetor Marcus Annius Verus and his wife, Domitia Calvilla. He was related through marriage to the emperors Trajan and Hadrian. Marcus was three when his father died, and was raised by his mother and paternal grandfather."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Julian", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2663, "target_cluster_name": "Julian", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Marcus Aurelius was a philosopher-emperor whose reign is often seen as a high point of the Roman Empire. Julian, also an emperor with intellectual and philosophical leanings, might be compared to or contrasted with Marcus Aurelius in historical discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Marcus Aurelius was a philosopher-emperor whose reign is often seen as a high point of the Roman Empire. Julian, also an emperor with intellectual and philosophical leanings, might be compared to or contrasted with Marcus Aurelius in historical discourse.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "consciousness of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "consciousness_of_self", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "consciousness of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["consciousness of self", "consciousness of Self"], "contexts": ["awareness of one's own identity"]}, {"entity_id": "cognition_of_self", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cognition of self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cognition of self"], "contexts": ["knowing one's own identity"]}, {"entity_id": "conscious_selfhood", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "conscious selfhood", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["conscious selfhood"], "contexts": ["sense of being a self"]}, {"entity_id": "self-consciousness", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "self-consciousness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["self-consciousness"], "contexts": ["awareness of oneself"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "consciousness of Self", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cognition of self", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 3530, "stable_key": "clu_fabd4cfb64197b7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Self-awareness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1314553", "wikidata_description": "capacity for an individual to consciously know and understand one's own character, feelings, motives, and desires", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-awareness", "wikipedia_extract": "In the philosophy of self, self-awareness is the awareness and reflection of one's own personality or individuality, including traits, feelings, and behaviors. It is not to be confused with consciousness in the sense of qualia. While consciousness is being aware of one's body and environment, self-awareness is the recognition of that consciousness. Self-awareness is how an individual experiences and understands their own character, feelings, motives, and desires. Because the term is used in both philosophical and psychological contexts, researchers distinguish between different forms of self-awareness, ranging from awareness of consciousness itself to awareness of oneself within social situations. The term self-awareness is used across several disciplines to describe related but distinct phenomena."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "consciousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 204, "target_cluster_name": "consciousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Self-awareness is a key aspect of consciousness, involving the recognition of oneself as an individual.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Self-awareness is a key aspect of consciousness, involving the recognition of oneself as an individual.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Emotion and Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "emotion_and_will", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Emotion and Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Emotion and Will"], "contexts": ["work by Bain on emotions and will", "book title by Bain"]}, {"entity_id": "the_emotions_and_the_will", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "The Emotions and the Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["The Emotions and the Will"], "contexts": ["work title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Emotion and Will", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "The Emotions and the Will", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3531, "stable_key": "clu_cf428cefaf09e29f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Emotion and Will", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to Alexander Bain's work 'The Emotions and the Will'."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Fear", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 147, "target_cluster_name": "Fear", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fear is a primary emotion, and its relationship with the will (how one acts in response to fear) is a common topic of discussion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fear is a primary emotion, and its relationship with the will (how one acts in response to fear) is a common topic of discussion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "feeling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 245, "target_cluster_name": "feeling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entry 'Emotion and Will' suggests a close relationship and potential overlap between emotional states (which include feelings) and volitional processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entry 'Emotion and Will' suggests a close relationship and potential overlap between emotional states (which include feelings) and volitional processes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "volition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 250, "target_cluster_name": "volition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This entry directly links emotion and will, suggesting a close relationship between affective states and volitional processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This entry directly links emotion and will, suggesting a close relationship between affective states and volitional processes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pleasure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 384, "target_cluster_name": "pleasure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions, including pleasure and its opposite, pain, are closely linked to the will and decision-making processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions, including pleasure and its opposite, pain, are closely linked to the will and decision-making processes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Human Nature", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 513, "target_cluster_name": "Human Nature", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotion and Will are fundamental aspects of human disposition and behavior, which are central to the concept of human nature.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotion and Will are fundamental aspects of human disposition and behavior, which are central to the concept of human nature.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Psychology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 552, "target_cluster_name": "Psychology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and volition are key aspects of human experience and behavior studied in psychology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions and volition are key aspects of human experience and behavior studied in psychology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "soul", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 578, "target_cluster_name": "soul", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historical discussions of the soul often include its role as the seat of emotions and the source of volition or will, making these closely related concepts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historical discussions of the soul often include its role as the seat of emotions and the source of volition or will, making these closely related concepts.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "will", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 617, "target_cluster_name": "will", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This entry explicitly links 'Emotion' and 'Will', suggesting a close conceptual relationship where willpower is a component or aspect of the broader concept of 'Will'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This entry explicitly links 'Emotion' and 'Will', suggesting a close conceptual relationship where willpower is a component or aspect of the broader concept of 'Will'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "feelings", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 618, "target_cluster_name": "feelings", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This entry directly links emotions with 'will', suggesting a historical exploration of their interplay and interdependence, a specific and notable relationship.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: This entry directly links emotions with 'will', suggesting a historical exploration of their interplay and interdependence, a specific and notable relationship.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "desires", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4149, "target_cluster_name": "desires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Desire is a fundamental component of both emotion (as a driving force behind feelings) and will (as the impetus for action). The relationship is integral to understanding human motivation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Desire is a fundamental component of both emotion (as a driving force behind feelings) and will (as the impetus for action). The relationship is integral to understanding human motivation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "passion", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1042, "target_cluster_name": "passion", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "'Passion' is a type of strong emotion that can significantly influence or be intertwined with 'will'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: 'Passion' is a type of strong emotion that can significantly influence or be intertwined with 'will'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "anger", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1206, "target_cluster_name": "anger", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anger is a primary emotion, and its relationship with the will (how one acts upon or controls anger) is a significant topic in psychological and philosophical discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anger is a primary emotion, and its relationship with the will (how one acts upon or controls anger) is a significant topic in psychological and philosophical discourse.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "grief", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2305, "target_cluster_name": "grief", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Grief is a powerful emotion that can influence and be influenced by the will and one's actions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Grief is a powerful emotion that can influence and be influenced by the will and one's actions.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "stimulus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1594, "target_cluster_name": "stimulus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and will are often responses to stimuli, and can also act as internal stimuli themselves.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions and will are often responses to stimuli, and can also act as internal stimuli themselves.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "natural instincts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2980, "target_cluster_name": "natural instincts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is closely related to emotions and the will, often driving actions that stem from these aspects of the mind.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Instinct is closely related to emotions and the will, often driving actions that stem from these aspects of the mind.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Personality", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1943, "target_cluster_name": "Personality", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Personality is deeply intertwined with an individual's emotional responses and volitional capacities, often studied together in relation to disposition and behavior.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Personality is deeply intertwined with an individual's emotional responses and volitional capacities, often studied together in relation to disposition and behavior.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Principles of Psychology", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2061, "target_cluster_name": "Principles of Psychology", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "These are fundamental components of the human psyche that would be addressed in a work on psychology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: These are fundamental components of the human psyche that would be addressed in a work on psychology.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "habits", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2105, "target_cluster_name": "habits", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Habits can be influenced by emotions and the exercise of will, and they can also influence emotional states and the capacity for voluntary action.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Habits can be influenced by emotions and the exercise of will, and they can also influence emotional states and the capacity for voluntary action.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "instinctive", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2282, "target_cluster_name": "instinctive", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Instinct is often considered a fundamental component of both emotion and will, driving actions and feelings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Instinct is often considered a fundamental component of both emotion and will, driving actions and feelings.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "function", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2304, "target_cluster_name": "function", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and will are often considered aspects of mental functioning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions and will are often considered aspects of mental functioning.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "mental changes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2427, "target_cluster_name": "mental changes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and will are key components of mental life and are often intertwined with or result in mental changes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions and will are key components of mental life and are often intertwined with or result in mental changes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "stimulation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2568, "target_cluster_name": "stimulation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotions and will are often triggered or influenced by stimulation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotions and will are often triggered or influenced by stimulation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pride", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2709, "target_cluster_name": "Pride", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pride is a complex emotion that significantly impacts the will and decision-making processes.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pride is a complex emotion that significantly impacts the will and decision-making processes.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "curiosity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2802, "target_cluster_name": "curiosity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curiosity is an emotion that can drive the will towards exploration and learning.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Curiosity is an emotion that can drive the will towards exploration and learning.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nervous action", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2900, "target_cluster_name": "nervous action", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Emotion and Will are complex psychological states that are understood to be generated and regulated by nerve activity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Emotion and Will are complex psychological states that are understood to be generated and regulated by nerve activity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "conceive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "conceive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "conceive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["conceive"], "contexts": ["form an idea or concept"]}, {"entity_id": "conceber", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "conceber", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["conceber"], "contexts": ["the act of becoming pregnant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "conceive", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "conceber", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 3532, "stable_key": "clu_48f48487ec9f965f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conception", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'conceive' has dual meanings: intellectual conception and biological conception. The context suggests both are possible."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sensory organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensory_organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensory organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["sensory organs"], "contexts": ["organs that detect stimuli", "organs for receiving stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "perceptive_organs", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "perceptive organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["perceptive organs"], "contexts": ["organs involved in sensing the environment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensory organs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "perceptive organs", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3533, "stable_key": "clu_46ed10a72a316eb3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensory organs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q173253", "wikidata_description": "physiological capacity of organisms that provides data for perception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense", "wikipedia_extract": "A sense is a biological system used by an organism for sensation, the process of  gathering information about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. Although, in some cultures, five human senses were traditionally identified as such (namely sight, smell, touch, taste, and hearing), many more are now recognized. Senses used by non-human organisms are even greater in variety and number. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli (such as a sound or smell) for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and perception are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's cognition, behavior and thought. In organisms, a sensory organ consists of a group of interrelated sensory cells that respond to a specific type of physical stimulus."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Feeling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 245, "target_cluster_name": "feeling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Feeling is a direct manifestation of sensation, often used interchangeably in premodern texts to describe the subjective experience of sensory input.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Feeling is a direct manifestation of sensation, often used interchangeably in premodern texts to describe the subjective experience of sensory input."}]}, {"canonical_name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "organic_adjustment", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Organic adjustment", "organic adjustment"], "contexts": ["bodily preparation for action", "physiological changes in the body"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_adjustment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic adjustment"], "contexts": ["biological adaptation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "organic adjustment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic adjustment", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3534, "stable_key": "clu_a2266c2c07baa385", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biological adaptation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q67504163", "wikidata_description": "changes in biological features that help an organism cope with its environment, physiological, phenotypic and genetic"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "abstract notions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "abstract_notions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "abstract notions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["abstract notions"], "contexts": ["general concepts", "general ideas detached