", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mechanical motion"], "contexts": ["movement caused by physical forces", "motion governed by mechanical laws"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "material motion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical motion", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 3556, "stable_key": "clu_d300d9c00e879f13", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mechanical motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "nervous centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "central_nervous_system", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "central nervous system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["central nervous system"], "contexts": ["brain and spinal cord"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_centres", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["nervous centres"], "contexts": ["central parts of the nervous system", "central control for bodily functions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "central nervous system", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous centres", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3557, "stable_key": "clu_c4155fb4747a1be2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "central nervous system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q47273", "wikidata_description": "main information-processing organs of the nervous system, consisting of the brain, spinal cord, and meninges"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "r__h__hutton", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "R. H. Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["R. H. Hutton"], "contexts": ["author in Contemporary Review"]}, {"entity_id": "hutton", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hutton"], "contexts": ["scientist in Shehallien experiment", "author of 'Fossil Flora'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "R. H. Hutton", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hutton", "similarity": 0.855}], "id": 3558, "stable_key": "clu_1af47f943a81ec96", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Hutton", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1726, "death_year": 1797, "description": "Scottish geologist, farmer, naturalist, and chemical manufacturer", "note": "The reference to R. H. Hutton is likely a misattribution or a different person. The context of 'Shehallien experiment' strongly points to James Hutton, the geologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q192927", "wikidata_description": "Scottish geologist, physician, chemical manufacturer, naturalist, and experimental agriculturalist; (1726-1797)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James%20Hutton", "wikipedia_extract": "James Hutton  ( ; 3 June O.S. 1726 – 26 March 1797) was a Scottish geologist, agriculturalist, chemical manufacturer, naturalist and physician. Often referred to as the \"Father of Modern Geology,\" he played a key role in establishing geology as a modern science. Hutton advanced the idea that the physical world's remote history can be inferred from evidence in present-day rocks. Through his study of features in the landscape and coastlines of his native Scottish Lowlands, such as Salisbury Crags or Siccar Point, he developed the theory that geological features could not be static but underwent continuing transformation over indefinitely long periods of time. From this he argued, in agreement with many other early geologists, that the Earth could not be young."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "geologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 956, "target_cluster_name": "geologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "James Hutton is considered the father of modern geology, proposing key principles like uniformitarianism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: James Hutton is considered the father of modern geology, proposing key principles like uniformitarianism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Captain Hutton", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2377, "target_cluster_name": "Captain Hutton", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Appears to be the same individual, with 'Captain Hutton' being a historical epithet or title.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Appears to be the same individual, with 'Captain Hutton' being a historical epithet or title.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "affection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "affection_of_the_soul", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "affection of the soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["affection of the soul"], "contexts": ["clumsy term for mental states"]}, {"entity_id": "affection", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "affection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["affection"], "contexts": ["a feeling or sensation", "fondness or liking"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affection of the soul", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affection", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 3559, "stable_key": "clu_e87c75305959fd7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Affection", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q316", "wikidata_description": "strong, positive emotion based on affection", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love", "wikipedia_extract": "Love is an emotion involving strong attraction, affection, emotional attachment or concern for a person, animal, or thing. It is expressed in many forms, encompassing a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue, good habit, deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love of food. Love is considered to be both positive and negative, with its virtue representing kindness, compassion, and affection—\"the unselfish, loyal, and benevolent concern for the good of another\"—and its vice representing a moral flaw akin to vanity, selfishness, amour-propre, and egotism. It may also describe compassionate and affectionate actions towards other humans, oneself, or animals. In its various forms, love acts as a major facilitator of interpersonal relationships, and owing to its central psychological importance, is one of the most common themes in the creative arts."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sympathia", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 104, "target_cluster_name": "sympathia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sympathy and affection are closely related emotional states, often discussed together in premodern texts concerning human sentiment and interaction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sympathy and affection are closely related emotional states, often discussed together in premodern texts concerning human sentiment and interaction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fear", "link_type": "conce