e identification of 'Rabier' with a specific historical figure is uncertain given the varied spellings ('Rabis', 'Rabi mofes') and limited context. 'Rabier' could also refer to a more general concept or a lesser-known individual.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "compound", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "compound", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "compound", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["compound"], "contexts": ["substance formed from elements"]}, {"entity_id": "compounds", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "compounds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["compounds"], "contexts": ["mixtures of elements"]}, {"entity_id": "compounds", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "compounds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["compounds"], "contexts": ["substances formed from elements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "compound", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "compounds", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "compounds", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "compounds", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3574, "stable_key": "clu_68db0f52ad8746cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q11173", "wikidata_description": "pure chemical substance consisting of two or more different chemical elements", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical%20compound", "wikipedia_extract": "A chemical compound is a chemical substance composed of many identical molecules (or molecular entities) containing atoms from more than one chemical element held together by chemical bonds. A molecule consisting of atoms of only one element is therefore not a compound. A compound can be transformed into a different substance by a chemical reaction, which may involve interactions with other substances. In this process, bonds between atoms may be broken or new bonds formed or both. There are four major types of compounds, distinguished by how the constituent atoms are bonded together. Molecular compounds are held together by covalent bonds, ionic compounds are held together by ionic bonds, intermetallic compounds are held together by metallic bonds, and coordination complexes are held together by coordinate covalent bonds."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "contraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "contraction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "contraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["contraction"], "contexts": ["action of a muscle"]}, {"entity_id": "contraction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "contraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["contraction"], "contexts": ["process of becoming smaller", "process of decreasing in size"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "contraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "contraction", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3575, "stable_key": "clu_df06e698fb3cee85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127006", "wikidata_description": "process in which force is generated within muscle tissue, resulting in a change in muscle geometry. Force generation involves a chemo-mechanical energy conversion step that is carried out by the actin/myosin complex activity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscle%20contraction", "wikipedia_extract": "Muscle contraction is the activation of tension-generating sites within muscle cells. In physiology, muscle contraction does not necessarily mean muscle shortening because muscle tension can be produced without changes in muscle length (isometric contraction), such as when holding something heavy in the same position. The termination of muscle contraction is followed by muscle relaxation, which is a return of the muscle fibers to their low tension-generating state. For the contractions to happen, the muscle cells must rely on the change in action of two types of filament: thin and thick filaments. The major constituent of thin filaments is a chain formed by helical coiling of two strands of actin, and thick filaments dominantly consist of chains of the motor-protein myosin. Together, these two filaments form myofibrils - the basic functional organelles in the skeletal muscle system."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "coexistence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "coexistence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "coexistence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["coexistence"], "contexts": ["relationship of things existing together"]}, {"entity_id": "co-existence", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "co-existence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["co-existence"], "contexts": ["state of existing together"]}, {"entity_id": "co-existences", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "co-existences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["co-existences"], "contexts": ["relations of existing together"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "coexistence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "co-existence", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "coexistence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "co-existences", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 3576, "stable_key": "clu_af282271de5c950b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "design", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "design", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "design", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["design"], "contexts": ["purposeful planning or intention"]}, {"entity_id": "design", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "design", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["design"], "contexts": ["purposeful arrangement of parts", "purposeful arrangement in creation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "design", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "design", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3577, "stable_key": "clu_96b82c0a2f896c05", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "design", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q82604", "wikidata_description": "creation of a plan or convention for the construction of an object or a system; process of creation; act of creativity and innovation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design", "wikipedia_extract": "A design is the concept or proposal for an object, process, or system. The word design refers to something that is or has been intentionally created by a thinking agent, and is sometimes used to refer to the inherent nature of something – its design. The verb to design expresses the process of developing a design. In some cases, the direct construction of an object without an explicit prior plan may also be considered to be a design, such as in arts and crafts. A design is expected to have a purpose within a specific context, typically aiming to satisfy certain goals and constraints while taking into account aesthetic, functional and experiential considerations. Traditional examples of designs are architectural and engineering drawings, circuit diagrams, sewing patterns, and less tangible artefacts such as business process models."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "seconds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "seconds", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "seconds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["seconds"], "contexts": ["unit of time"]}, {"entity_id": "seconds", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "seconds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["seconds"], "contexts": ["subdivision of a minute", "unit of time/angle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "seconds", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "seconds", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3578, "stable_key": "clu_3ab2a4e502385c0c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "seconds", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lame", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lalanne", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Lalanne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lalanne"], "contexts": ["researcher on summation of finger contacts"]}, {"entity_id": "lame", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lame", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lame"], "contexts": ["mathematician, physicist", "discordant views on electricity distribution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Lalanne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lame", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 3579, "stable_key": "clu_7b9f3767e077eb10", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Auguste Lame", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1795, "death_year": 1870, "description": "French mathematician and physicist, contributor to elasticity theory.", "wikidata_id": "Q2871265", "wikidata_description": "Belgian entomologist", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auguste%20Lameere", "wikipedia_extract": "Auguste Alfred Lucien Gaston Lameere (12 June 1864 – 6 May 1942) was a Belgian entomologist. He made several collecting expeditions to the Algerian Sahara region. He was born in Ixelles. He was a professor and dean (1906–1907) of the faculty of sciences at the Université libre de Bruxelles. An active member of the Royal Belgian Entomological Society, he was the author of numerous articles, notably on Coleoptera and the famous Manuel de la Faune de Belgique which had a great influence on the entomologists of his country. Frog Arthroleptis lameerei, also known as Lameere's squeaker, is named after him."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "phases", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "phase", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "phase", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["phase"], "contexts": ["stage or aspect of a process"]}, {"entity_id": "phases", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "phases", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["phases"], "contexts": ["state of oscillation in a wave", "in comets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "phase", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phases", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3580, "stable_key": "clu_dfe2dcae90094617", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "phase", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Chapter X.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "chapter_x", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Chapter X", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chapter X"], "contexts": ["reference to a specific chapter"]}, {"entity_id": "chapter_x_", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Chapter X.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chapter X.", "CHAPTER X."], "contexts": ["chapter title", "reference to a chapter in a work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Chapter X", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Chapter X.", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3581, "stable_key": "clu_9376b79e1f1d73b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chapter", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a section of a book, not a specific concept itself.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "body of clergy in Roman Catholic, Anglican, and Nordic Lutheran churches", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "conflict", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "conflict", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "conflict", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["conflict"], "contexts": ["opposition or struggle"]}, {"entity_id": "conflict", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "conflict", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["conflict"], "contexts": ["a state of opposition or disagreement", "disagreement or struggle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "conflict", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "conflict", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3582, "stable_key": "clu_a1d0cc070143b7a4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conflict", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Represents opposition or struggle, a general concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q211067", "wikidata_description": "situation occurring when an individual or organization is involved in multiple interests, one of which could possibly corrupt the motivation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict%20of%20interest", "wikipedia_extract": "A conflict of interest (COI) is a situation in which a person or organization is involved in multiple interests, financial or otherwise, and serving one interest could involve working against another. Typically, this relates to situations in which the personal interest of an individual or organization might adversely affect a duty owed to make decisions for the benefit of a third party. An \"interest\" is a commitment, obligation, duty or goal associated with a specific social role or practice. By definition, a \"conflict of interest\" occurs if, within a particular decision-making context, an individual is subject to two coexisting interests that are in direct conflict with each other (\"competing interests\"). This is important because under these circumstances, the decision-making process can be disrupted or compromised, affecting the integrity or reliability of the outcomes. Typically, a conflict of interest arises when an individual occupies two social roles simultaneously, generating opposing benefits or loyalties."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "resistance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 123, "target_cluster_name": "resistance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conflict inherently involves opposing forces or wills, where resistance is a key component.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conflict inherently involves opposing forces or wills, where resistance is a key component.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "problem", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 438, "target_cluster_name": "problem", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conflicts often present as problems that require resolution.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conflicts often present as problems that require resolution.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "discord", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1209, "target_cluster_name": "discord", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Discord is a state of conflict or disagreement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Discord is a state of conflict or disagreement.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Demonstrations", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1397, "target_cluster_name": "Demonstrations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Demonstrations often arise from or lead to conflict.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Demonstrations often arise from or lead to conflict.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "movements", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1872, "target_cluster_name": "movements", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conflict often involves opposing movements.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conflict often involves opposing movements.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Tuileries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tuileries", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Tuileries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tuileries"], "contexts": ["Parisian palace and gardens"]}, {"entity_id": "tuileries", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tuileries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tuileries"], "contexts": ["gardens with decimal time clock", "location with a clock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Tuileries", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tuileries", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3583, "stable_key": "clu_780cbc727bb03822", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tuileries Garden", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "Refers to the Tuileries Garden in Paris, known for its historical significance and public spaces.", "wikidata_id": "Q818517", "wikidata_description": "public garden in Paris, France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuileries_Garden", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tuileries Garden (French: Jardin des Tuileries, IPA: [ʒaʁdɛ̃ de tɥilʁi]) is a public garden between the Louvre and the Place de la Concorde in the 1st arrondissement of Paris, France. Created by Catherine de' Medici as the garden of the Tuileries Palace in 1564, it was opened to the public in 1667 and became a public park after the French Revolution. Since the 19th century, it has been a place for Parisians to celebrate, meet, stroll and relax. Covering an area of 25.5 hectares (63 acres), it is one of the most iconic parks in Paris. During the 2024 Summer Olympics and Paralympics, it was the site of the Olympic and Paralympic cauldron. The Italian Garden of Catherine de' Medici (16th century) In July 1559, after the accidental death of her husband, Henry II, Queen Catherine de' Medici decided to leave her residence of the Hôtel des Tournelles, at the eastern part of Paris, near the Bastille."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "nervous impressions", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_impressions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous impressions", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous impressions"], "contexts": ["signals transmitted by nerves"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_impressions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous impressions", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nervous impressions"], "contexts": ["signals transmitted by nerves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous impressions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous impressions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3584, "stable_key": "clu_680efa82c7f6c6b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous impulse", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern term for signals transmitted by nerves.", "wikidata_id": "Q3150791", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nervous system", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1588, "target_cluster_name": "nervous system", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Nervous impulses are the signals transmitted through the nervous system.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Nervous impulses are the signals transmitted through the nervous system.", "is_reve