 "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["electrical phenomena"], "contexts": ["observable effects of electricity", "phenomena related to electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric phenomena", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electrical phenomena", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 3624, "stable_key": "clu_a27d0dfa3eef68e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrical phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3405599", "wikidata_description": "physical phenomenon involving electricity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20electrical%20phenomena", "wikipedia_extract": "This is a list of electrical phenomena. Electrical phenomena are a somewhat arbitrary division of electromagnetic phenomena. Biefeld–Brown effect — Thought by the person who coined the name, Thomas Townsend Brown, to be an anti-gravity effect, it is generally attributed to electrohydrodynamics (EHD) or sometimes electro-fluid-dynamics, a counterpart to the well-known magneto-hydrodynamics. Bioelectrogenesis — The generation of electricity by living organisms. Capacitive coupling — Transfer of energy within an electrical network or between distant networks by means of displacement current. Contact electrification — The phenomenon of electrification by contact."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "magnetism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 241, "target_cluster_name": "magnetism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnetism and electricity are closely related phenomena, often studied together under the umbrella of electromagnetism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnetism and electricity are closely related phenomena, often studied together under the umbrella of electromagnetism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "color", "link_