s an intrinsic property of a body. In modern physics, it is generally defined as the strength of an object's gravitational attraction to other bodies - as measured by an observer moving along at the same speed. It was traditionally believed to be related to the quantity of matter in a body, until the discovery of the atom and particle physics. It was found that different atoms and different elementary particles, theoretically with the same amount of matter, have nonetheless different masses. Mass in modern physics has multiple definitions which are conceptually distinct, but physically equivalent. Mass can be experimentally defined as a measure of the body's inertia, meaning the resistance to acceleration (change of velocity) when a net force is applied."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "basalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1665, "target_cluster_name": "basalt", "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "the Earth’s crust, a portion of the molten nucleus is extruded; and when this is cooled with comparative rapidity, through free radiation and contact with cold masses; it forms a substance known as tr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of substance"}, {"found_name": "trap", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "evidence_snippet": "the Earth’s crust, a portion of the molten nucleus is extruded; and when this is cooled with comparative rapidity, through free radiation and contact with cold masses; it forms a substance known as tr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of substance"}]}, {"canonical_name": "astronomical science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mathematical_astronomy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mathematical astronomy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mathematical astronomy"], "contexts": ["astronomy based on mathematical principles"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical_science", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "astronomical science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomical science"], "contexts": ["study of celestial objects", "antiquity of"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mathematical astronomy", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "astronomical science", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 3627, "stable_key": "clu_b28d0e49aadce90f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astronomy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "humani corporis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_anatomy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "human anatomy", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["human anatomy"], "contexts": ["study of the human body"]}, {"entity_id": "humani_corporis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humani corporis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["humani corporis"], "contexts": ["of the human body", "human body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "human anatomy", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humani corporis", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 3628, "stable_key": "clu_5fc06ac9a3981f79", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Human Anatomy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3999", "wikidata_description": "scientific study of the morphology of the human body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human%20anatomy", "wikipedia_extract": "Human anatomy (gr. ἀνατομία, \"dissection\", from ἀνά, \"up\", and τέμνειν, \"cut\") is primarily the scientific study of the morphology of the human body. Anatomy is subdivided into gross anatomy and microscopic anatomy. Gross anatomy (also called macroscopic anatomy, topographical anatomy, regional anatomy, or anthropotomy) is the study of anatomical structures that can be seen by the naked eye. Microscopic anatomy is the study of minute anatomical structures assisted with microscopes, which includes histology (the study of the organization of tissues), and cytology (the study of cells). Anatomy, human physiology (the study of function), and biochemistry (the study of the chemistry of living structures) are complementary basic medical sciences that are generally together (or in tandem) to students studying medical sciences."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ouvidos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 136, "target_cluster_name": "Ouvidos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The ear is a part of human anatomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The ear is a part of human anatomy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "bodily parts", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3109, "target_cluster_name": "bodily parts", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Human Anatomy is the study of the structure of the human body.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Human Anatomy is the study of the structure of the human body.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "muscles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 265, "target_cluster_name": "muscles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Muscles are a fundamental component of human anatomy, and texts discussing anatomy would frequently mention musc