ON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "conrado_geſnero", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Conrado Geſnero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Conrado Geſnero", "Conrado Geſnero Tigurino"], "contexts": ["author of a book on animals"]}, {"entity_id": "conrado_gefnero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Conrado Gefnero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Conrado Gefnero"], "contexts": ["author cited for praising eggs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Conrado Geſnero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Conrado Gefnero", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 3676, "stable_key": "clu_dbf9da735508032b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conrad Gessner", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1516, "death_year": 1565, "description": "Swiss physician, botanist, zoologist, and bibliographer.", "wikidata_id": "Q60116", "wikidata_description": "Swiss physician, bibliographer and naturalist (1516–1565)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conrad_Gessner", "wikipedia_extract": "The salamander is an amphibian of the order Urodela which once, like many real creatures, often was suppositiously ascribed fantastic and sometimes occult qualities by pre-modern authors, as in the allegorical descriptions of animals in medieval bestiaries. The legendary salamander is often depicted as a typical salamander in shape, with a lizard-like form, but is usually ascribed an affinity with fire, sometimes specifically elemental fire. Ancient and medieval commentators ascribed many fantastical abilities to the natural salamander. Many of these qualities are rooted in verifiable traits of the natural creature but often exaggerated. A large body of legend, mythology, and symbolism has developed around this creature over the centuries. Carl Linnaeus in the 10th edition of Systema Naturae of 1758 established the scientific description of the salamander and noted the chief characteristics described by the ancients: the reported ability to live in fire, and the oily exudates."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Genoua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "genoua", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Genoua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Genoua"], "contexts": ["natural place of don Christoual Coló", "city where Cañafistola was brought to"]}, {"entity_id": "genoa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Genoa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Genoa"], "contexts": ["city where a stone is located"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Genoua", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Genoa", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 3677, "stable_key": "clu_79a40e5c269f501a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genoa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major port city in Italy, historically significant for trade and exploration.", "note": "Refers to the city of Genoa, also known historically as Gênes or Geneve in some texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Genoa ( JEN-oh-ə; Italian: Genova [ˈdʒɛːnova] ; Ligurian: Zêna [ˈzeːna]) is the sixth-largest city in Italy and the capital of the Italian region of Liguria. As of 2025, 565,301 people live within the city's administrative limits. While its metropolitan city has 818,629 inhabitants, more than 1.5 million people live in the wider metropolitan area stretching along the Italian Riviera. On the Gulf of Genoa in the Ligurian Sea, Genoa has historically been one of the most important ports on the Mediterranean: it is the busiest port in Italy and in the Mediterranean Sea and twelfth-busiest in the European Union. Genoa was the capital of one of the most powerful maritime republics for over seven centuries, from the 11th century to 1797. Particularly from the 12th century to the 15th century, the city played a leading role in the history of commerce and trade in Europe, becoming one of the largest naval powers of the continent and considered among the wealthiest cities in the world."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Genève", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 419, "target_cluster_name": "Genève", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Genève is the historical name for Genoa, indicating a direct linguistic translation or alternative name for the same place.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Genève is the historical name for Genoa, indicating a direct linguistic translation or alternative name for the same place."}]}, {"canonical_name": "mestruos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mestruos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "mestruos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mestruos"], "contexts": ["menstrual fluxes", "menstrual flux"]}, {"entity_id": "menstruo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "menstruo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["menstruo"], "contexts": ["menstrual cycle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "mestruos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "menstruo", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 3678, "stable_key": "clu_84243031ebf4854b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "menstruation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Mestruos', 'menstruo', and 'menstruepurgationis' all refer to menstrual cycles or fluxes.", "wikidata_id": "Q50430082", "wikidata_description": "chemical compounds that induce menstruation, through direct action or by relieving a condition causing amenorrhea", "wikipedia_extract": "Menstruation in Islam relates to various purity related restrictions in Islamic jurisprudence. The ḥayḍ (Arabic: حيض) is the religious state of menstruation in Islam. The Qur'an makes specific mention of menstruation in al-Baqara 2:222:And they ask you about menstruation. Say, \"It is harm, so keep away from wives during menstruation. And do not approach them until they are pure. And when they have purified themselves, then come to them from where Allāh has ordained for you.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menstruation_in_Islam"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "cenar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cenar", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cenar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["cenar"], "contexts": ["evening meal", "dining"]}, {"entity_id": "jantar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "jantar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["jantar"], "contexts": ["eating lunch, a daily activity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cenar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "jantar", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3679, "stable_key": "clu_a4e32c1da4f8a6c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "supper", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'jantar' can mean lunch, in the context of early modern medical texts and the association with 'cenar', 'supper' or 'evening meal' is the more appropriate interpretation.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fuerte", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fuerte", "book_id": "historia_m