 requires further textual context."}, {"found_name": "fruit", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 72, "target_cluster_name": "frutas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Quince is a type of fruit, and 'fruit' is a broader category that encompasses it.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Quince is a type of fruit, and 'fruit' is a broader category that encompasses it."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Olmo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "olmo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Olmo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Olmo"], "contexts": ["tree compared in size and leaf"]}, {"entity_id": "olmo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Olmo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Olmo"], "contexts": ["elm", "elm tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Olmo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Olmo", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3691, "stable_key": "clu_3bbb46fc159e1fc2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elm", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ulmus L.", "family": "Ulmaceae", "note": "Linnaean binomial is for the genus, specific species not identifiable from context.", "wikidata_id": "Q131113", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elm", "wikipedia_extract": "Elms are deciduous and semi-deciduous trees comprising the genus Ulmus in the family Ulmaceae. They are distributed over most of the Northern Hemisphere, inhabiting the temperate and tropical-montane regions of North America and Eurasia, ranging southward in Western Asia to Iran, in Africa to Libya, and in Southeast Asia into Indonesia. Elms are components of many kinds of natural forests. Moreover, during the 19th and early 20th centuries, many species and cultivars were also planted as ornamental street, garden, and park trees in Europe, North America, and parts of the Southern Hemisphere, notably Australasia. Some individual elms reached great size and age. However, in recent decades, most mature elms of European or North American origin have died from Dutch elm disease, caused by a microfungus dispersed by bark beetles."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cozimento", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1654, "target_cluster_name": "cozimento", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "nafcem as moſcas ;& tem differentes nomes , porque huns the chamão Ulmeiro , outros Choupo , outros Salgueiro. A entrecaſca do pao do Ulmeiro cura o feu cozimento todas aschagas interiores,& exteriore", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cura", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1748, "target_cluster_name": "cura", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "em que nafcem as moſcas ;& tem differentes nomes , porque huns the chamão Ulmeiro , outros Choupo , outros Salgueiro. A entrecaſca do pao do Ulmeiro cura o feu cozimento todas aschagas interiores,& ex", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for the tree with medicinal bark", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "arvore", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1632, "target_cluster_name": "arvore", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Elm is a tree.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Elm is a tree.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "legno santo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "palo_santto", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Palo santto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Palo santto"], "contexts": ["holy wood, likely a medicinal tree"]}, {"entity_id": "legno_santo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "legno santo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["legno santo"], "contexts": ["common name for guaiacan wood", "source of oil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Palo santto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "legno santo", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 3692, "stable_key": "clu_429878b7b9e72f30", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guaiacum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Guaiacum officinale", "family": "Zygophyllaceae", "note": "Both 'legno santo' and 'Palo santto' are direct translations of 'holy wood' or 'sacred wood', which strongly corresponds to the common names for guaiacum, especially its medicinal use.", "wikidata_id": "Q1429385", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guaiacum", "wikipedia_extract": "Guaiacum (), sometimes spelled Guajacum, is a genus of flowering plants in the caltrop family Zygophyllaceae. It contains five species of slow-growing shrubs and trees, reaching a height of approximately 20 m (66 ft) but usually less than half of that. All are native to subtropical and tropical regions of the Americas and are commonly known as lignum-vitae, guayacán (Spanish), or gaïac (French). The genus name originated in Taíno, the language spoken by the native Taínos of the Bahamas; it was adopted into English in 1533, the first word in that language of American origin. Members of the genus have a variety of uses, including as lumber, for medicinal purposes, and as ornamentals. The trade of all species of Guaiacum is controlled under CITES Appendix II."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "caparrofa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "caparroía", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "caparroía", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["caparroía"], "contexts": ["oil for expelling poison"]}, {"entity_id": "caparrofa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "caparrofa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["caparrofa"], "contexts": ["substance with emetic properties", "mucilaginous substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "caparroía", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "caparrofa", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 3693, "stable_key": "clu_4f71542e221baf2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caparrofa", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'caparrofa' appears to refer to a specific plant or substance used in historical medicine, but its precise modern identification is not readily available without further textual context. It is described as having emetic properties and being used in oils for expelling poison."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "caparroſa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2333, "target_cluster_name": "caparroſa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caparrofa is listed as a historical name for Vitriol.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caparrofa is listed as a historical name for Vitriol.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hermes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hermes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Hermes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hermes"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher on metal duality"]}, {"entity_id": "hermes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hermes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hermes"], "contexts": ["Ancient authority, author of 'de radicibus'", "King of Damascus, grandfather of Mesues"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Hermes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hermes", "similarity": 0.719}], "id": 3694, "stable_key": "clu_9c378cc4a306b5fb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hermes Trismegistus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Legendary ancient philosopher and alchemical authority.", "note": "The mention of 'ancient philosopher on metal duality' and 'Ancient authority, author of 'de radicibus'' points to Hermes Trismegistus, a key figure in Hermeticism and alchemy."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aconito", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "áconito", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Áconito", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Áconito"], "contexts": ["poisonous plant"]}, {"entity_id": "aconito", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aconito", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aconito"], "contexts": ["species of Aconito", "herb that kills dogs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Áconito", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aconito", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 3695, "stable_key": "clu_bd5ab27e3a2ed770", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aconite", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Aconitum", "family": "Ranunculaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q155904", "wikidata_description": "genus of flowering plants in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aconitum", "wikipedia_extract": "Aconitum (), also known as aconite, monkshood, wolfsbane, leopard's bane, devil's helmet, or blue rocket, is a genus of over 250 species of flowering plants belonging to the family Ranunculaceae. These herbaceous, frequently toxic perennial plants are chiefly native to the mountainous parts of the Northern Hemisphere in North America, Europe, and Asia, growing in the moisture-retentive but well-draining soils of mountain meadows. Most Aconitum species are extremely poisonous and must be handled very carefully. Several Aconitum hybrids, such as the Arendsii form of Aconitum carmichaelii, have won gardening awards—such as the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit. The name aconitum comes from the Greek word ἀκόνιτον, which may derive from the Greek akon for dart or javelin, the tips of which were poisoned with the substance, or from akonae, because of the rocky ground on which the plant was thought to grow. The Greek name lycoctonum, which translates literally to \"wolf's bane\", is thought to indicate the use of its juice to poison arrows or baits used to kill wolves."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "solutivas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "solutivas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "solutivas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["solutivas", "solutivos"], "contexts": ["dissolving quality"]}, {"entity_id": "solutive", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "solutive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["solutive"], "contexts": ["dissolving medicines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "solutivas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "solutive", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 3696, "stable_key": "clu_356e2b149486f9e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solutive", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "justicia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "justicia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "justicia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["justicia", "justiça"], "contexts": ["Latin for justice", "what is not found against Moorish kings"]}, {"entity_id": "justice", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Justice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Justice"], "contexts": ["mutual limitations in society"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "justicia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Justice", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 3697, "stable_key": "clu_668f18554b26ef66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Justice", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'justicia' in Portuguese can refer to both the legal/ethical concept of justice and specific plants (e.g., Justicia sect. Jacobinia). Given the context 'mutual limitations in society', the concept of justice is the most likely referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q16533", "wikidata_description": "official who presides over court proceedings"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "guerras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 483, "target_cluster_name": "guerras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of justice is often invoked in discussions and justifications of war (e.g., 'just war' theory).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The concept of justice is often invoked in discussions and justifications of war (e.g., 'just war' theory).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "vertue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2161, "target_cluster_name": "vertue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Justice is a cardinal virtue, and the two concepts are deeply intertwined in ethical and philosophical discourse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Justice is a cardinal virtue, and the two concepts are deeply intertwined in ethical and philosophical discourse.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "bucho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bucho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bucho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["bucho", "buchos"], "contexts": ["shrunken stomach, cause of death", "stomachs of goats where the stone forms"]}, {"entity_id": "bucho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bucho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bucho"], "contexts": ["stomach of an animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bucho", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bucho", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3698, "stable_key": "clu_50bc0d2e03245208", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stomach", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'bucho' in Portuguese can refer to the stomach, particularly in an animal or as a shrunken/diseased organ. The context 'shrunken stomach, cause of death' and 'stomach of an animal' points to the anatomical organ.", "wikidata_id": "Q156103", "wikidata_description": "medical condition characterized by inflammation of the stomach and/or small intestine"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Intestine", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2647, "target_cluster_name": "intestine", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The stomach is a primary organ of the digestive system, directly preceding the intestines in function and anatomical location.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The stomach is a primary organ of the digestive system, directly preceding the intestines in function and anatomical location."}, {"found_name": "Esophagus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 641, "target_cluster_name": "Ofofago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The esophagus is the tube that connects the pharynx to the stomach, making it anatomically and functionally linked.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The esophagus is the tube that connects the pharynx to the stomach, making it anatomically and functionally linked."}, {"found_name": "Duodenum", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2512, "target_cluster_name": "Duodeno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The duodenum is the first section of the small intestine, immediately following the stomach, and plays a crucial role in digestion initiated by the stomach.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The duodenum is the first section of the small intestine, immediately following the stomach, and plays a crucial role in digestion initiated by the stomach."}, {"found_name": "Pharynx", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2644, "target_cluster_name": "fauces", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The pharynx is the part of the throat behind the mouth and nasal cavity, and above the esophagus and larynx – it is the passage for food from the mouth to the esophagus and thus to the stomach.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The pharynx is the part of the throat behind the mouth and nasal cavity, and above the esophagus and larynx – it is the passage for food from the mouth to the esophagus and thus to the stomach."}, {"found_name": "Food", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 615, "target_cluster_name": "alimentos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The stomach's primary function is to receive and digest food.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The stomach's primary function is to receive and digest food."}, {"found_name": "Bodily fluid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 144, "target_cluster_name": "moisture", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The stomach produces and contains various bodily fluids, such as gastric juices, essential for digestion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The stomach produces and contains various bodily fluids, such as gastric juices, essential for digestion."}, {"found_name": "Acid", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1608, "target_cluster_name": "acidos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The stomach secretes hydrochloric acid, a key component of gastric juice for digestion.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The stomach secretes hydrochloric acid, a key component of gastric juice for digestion."}, {"found_name": "Humors", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 28, "target_cluster_name": "humores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In premodern medicine, the stomach was considered a site for the generation and interaction of bodily humors, particularly related to digestion and temperament.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "In premodern medicine, the stomach was considered a site for the generation and interaction of bodily humors, particularly related to digestion and temperament."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mombaim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mombaim", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mombaim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mombaim", "Mombaça"], "contexts": ["city and island with better areca", "city on African coast with Baneanes"]}, {"entity_id": "mombaça", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mombaça", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mombaça"], "contexts": ["origin of snake stone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mombaim", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mombaça", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3699, "stable_key": "clu_822c08ceb124f125-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bombay", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major port city in India, historically a center of trade.", "note": "Includes historical spellings like 'Bombaim' and a potential confusion with Mombasa.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mumbai ( muum-BY; Marathi: Mumbaī, pronounced [ˈmumbəi] ), also known as Bombay ( bom-BAY; its official name until 1995), is the capital city of the Indian state of Maharashtra. Mumbai is the financial capital and the most populous city proper of India, with an estimated population of 12.5 million (1.25 crore). Mumbai is the centre of the Mumbai Metropolitan Region, which is among the most populous metropolitan areas in the world with a population of over 23 million (2.3 crore). Mumbai lies on the Konkan coast on the west coast of India and has a deep natural harbour. In 2008, Mumbai was named a alpha world city. Mumbai has the highest number of billionaires out of any city in Asia."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Bombaim", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1627, "target_cluster_name": "Bombaim", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bombaim is the historical Portuguese name for Bombay, indicating a direct continuity of the place name.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bombaim is the historical Portuguese name for Bombay, indicating a direct continuity of the place name."}, {"found_name": "çupari", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "chamam çupari; e estes tem muyto pouca, somente na fralda do mar, e he muyto boa essa que ha em Chaul, porque he mercadoria pêra Ormuz ; e milhor he a de Mombaim, terra e ylha de que elrei nosso senho", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "vernacular name for"}]}, {"canonical_name": "kely", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "kely", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "kely", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["kely", "kelay"], "contexts": ["Indian name for banana"]}, {"entity_id": "little", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "little", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["little"], "contexts": ["quantity of blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "kely", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "little", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 3700, "stable_key": "clu_d4560cd416568a39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Banana", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "family": "Musaceae", "note": "The context states 'Indian name for banana'. While 'kely' could be related to a diminutive in some languages, in the context of the text by Orta (known for describing Eastern plants), it's likely referring to an indigenous name for a plant, and banana is a strong candidate given the commonality of its cultivation and description in early modern texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q503", "wikidata_description": "elongated, edible fruit produced by several kinds of large herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aldrovando", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "aldrovando", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Aldrovando", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aldrovando", "Âldrovando"], "contexts": ["author of a book", "author of a book where 'poma paradisea' is applied"]}, {"entity_id": "aldrovando", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Aldrovando", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aldrovando"], "contexts": ["author cited for properties of clay"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Aldrovando", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aldrovando", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3701, "stable_key": "clu_b2abe09ab6652e30", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ulysses Aldrovandi", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1522, "death_year": 1605, "description": "Italian naturalist, botanist, and entomologist, founder of the Bologna museum.", "wikidata_id": "Q321358", "wikidata_description": "Italian naturalist (1522-1605)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulisse%20Aldrovandi", "wikipedia_extract": "Ulisse Aldrovandi (11 September 1522 – 4 May 1605) was an Italian naturalist, the moving force behind Bologna's botanical garden, one of the first in Europe. Carl Linnaeus and the comte de Buffon reckoned him the father of natural history studies. He is usually referred to, especially in older scientific literature in Latin, as Aldrovandus; his name in Italian is equally given as Aldroandi. Aldrovandi was born in Bologna to Teseo Aldrovandi and his wife, a noble but poor family. His father was a lawyer, and Secretary to the Senate of Bologna, but died when Ulisse was seven years old. His widowed mother wanted him to become a jurist."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ulceras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ulceras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ulceras", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ulceras", "ulceras da bexiga"], "contexts": ["ulcers treated with cataplasm", "bladder ulcers"]}, {"entity_id": "ulcus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ulcus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ulcus"], "contexts": ["wound mentioned in Hippocrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ulceras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ulcus", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 3702, "stable_key": "clu_0b4d74ea7e150586", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ulcers", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Ulcers", "wikidata_id": "Q1477", "wikidata_description": "inflammatory bowel disease that causes ulcers in the colon", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulcerative%20colitis", "wikipedia_extract": "Ulcerative colitis (UC) is one of the two types of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), with the other type being Crohn's disease. It is a long-term condition that results in inflammation and ulcers of the colon and rectum. The primary symptoms of active disease are abdominal pain and diarrhea mixed with blood (hematochezia). Weight loss, fever, and anemia may also occur. Often, symptoms come on slowly and can range from mild to severe. Symptoms typically occur intermittently with periods of no symptoms between flares."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "consumption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 73, "target_cluster_name": "consumption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In the context of phthisis, particularly tuberculosis, the disease process can lead to the formation of ulcers in the lungs and other tissues.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In the context of phthisis, particularly tuberculosis, the disease process can lead to the formation of ulcers in the lungs and other tissues.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "contusões", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3320, "target_cluster_name": "contusões", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ulcers are a specific type of wound characterized by open sores, often resulting from underlying disease or injury.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ulcers are a specific type of wound characterized by open sores, often resulting from underlying disease or injury.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "morbo napolitano", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2860, "target_cluster_name": "morbo napolitano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Syphilis, particularly in its primary and secondary stages, is characterized by the formation of ulcers (chancre, condylomata lata). Therefore, 'ulcers' is a directly related pathological manifestation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Syphilis, particularly in its primary and secondary stages, is characterized by the formation of ulcers (chancre, condylomata lata). Therefore, 'ulcers' is a directly related pathological manifestation.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "carbunculos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1918, "target_cluster_name": "carbunculos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While Lyme disease is not primarily characterized by ulcers, skin manifestations can sometimes lead to ulceration, and 'ulcers' are a related pathological skin condition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While Lyme disease is not primarily characterized by ulcers, skin manifestations can sometimes lead to ulceration, and 'ulcers' are a related pathological skin condition.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "falsificaçam", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "falsificaçam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "falsificaçam", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["falsificaçam", "falsificadas"], "contexts": ["falsification of the stone", "state of being counterfeit"]}, {"entity_id": "falsificado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "falsificado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["falsificado"], "contexts": ["falsified, referring to remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "fraude", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fraude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fraude"], "contexts": ["illegal activity related to customs"]}, {"entity_id": "fraudentamente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fraudentamente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fraudentamente"], "contexts": ["deceptive practice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "falsificaçam", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "falsificado", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 3703, "stable_key": "clu_39bf9387d9fd4111", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Falsification", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the act of making something counterfeit or imitation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1332286", "wikidata_description": "process of making, adapting, or imitating objects, statistics, or documents with the intent to deceive", "wikipedia_extract": "Preference falsification is the act of misrepresenting a preference under perceived public pressure. It involves publicly expressing a preference that differs from one's underlying privately held preference (or simply, a public preference at odds with one’s private preference). People frequently convey to each other preferences that differ from what they would communicate privately under credible cover of anonymity (such as in opinion surveys to researchers or pollsters). Techniques such as list experiments can be used to uncover preference falsification. The term was coined by economist and political scientist Timur Kuran in a 1987 article, \"Chameleon voters and public choice.\" On controversial matters that induce preference falsification, he showed there, widely disliked policies may appear popular. The distribution of public preferences, which Kuran defines as public opinion, may differ greatly from private opinion, which is the distribution of private preferences known only to individuals themselves.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgery"}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Juniperos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "juniperos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Juniperos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Juniperos", "junipero"], "contexts": ["trees compared to pepper trees", "plant compared to pimenta"]}, {"entity_id": "junipero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "junipero", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["junipero"], "contexts": ["ingredient for anti-paralytic confection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Juniperos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "junipero", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3704, "stable_key": "clu_fe76284369516053", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Juniper Berries", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Juniperus communis", "family": "Cupressaceae", "description": "The small, bluish-black, aromatic cones of the juniper tree, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Juniperus communis, the common juniper, is a species of small tree or shrub in the cypress family Cupressaceae. An evergreen conifer, it has the largest geographical range of any woody plant, with a Holarctic distribution throughout the cool temperate Northern Hemisphere. Juniperus communis is highly variable in form, ranging from 10 metres (33 feet)—rarely 16 m (52 ft)—tall to a low, often prostrate spreading shrub in exposed locations. It has needle-like leaves in whorls of three; the leaves are green, with a single white stomatal band on the inner surface. It never attains the scale-like adult foliage of other members of the genus. It is dioecious, with male and female cones on separate plants so requiring wind pollination to transfer pollen from male to female cones."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1667, "target_cluster_name": "maçãs de acypreſte", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cypress trees produce berries that are botanically cones, but are often referred to as berries and used similarly to juniper berries in historical contexts, particularly in flavoring and traditional medicine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cypress trees produce berries that are botanically cones, but are often referred to as berries and used similarly to juniper berries in historical contexts, particularly in flavoring and traditional medicine.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Rufo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rufo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rufo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rufo"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on medicine", "physician whose pills were changed"]}, {"entity_id": "rufo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rufo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rufo"], "contexts": ["made pestilential pyrolas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Rufo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rufo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3705, "stable_key": "clu_60dd105945060693", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rufus of Ephesus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 70, "death_year": 130, "description": "Greek physician and medical writer, known for anatomical and pathological studies."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fel", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fel", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["fel"], "contexts": ["animal bile", "bile, a bodily fluid"]}, {"entity_id": "fel_della", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fel della", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["fel della"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a powder"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fel della", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 3706, "stable_key": "clu_9bd2ad1bbb89d2ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rooster's bile", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "The bile produced by a rooster, used in historical remedies.", "note": "The term 'fel' can refer to animal bile in general, but 'fel de gallo' specifically points to rooster's bile.", "wikipedia_extract": "A list of Equinox episodes shows the full set of editions of the defunct (July 1986 - December 2006) Channel 4 science documentary series Equinox. 31 July Turbo: Once Around the Block, about British motor racing; the Ferrari F1/86 and the Imola Circuit in Emilia-Romagna in northern Italy, and Tifosi spectators; the 1984 British Grand Prix at Brands Hatch; the Motronic engine control units of Robert Bosch GmbH; Keith Duckworth, known for the Cosworth DFV, and Michael Kranefuss, head of Ford Motor Sport; Walter Hayes of Ford of Europe; Renault began turbocharged motor racing vehicles at the 1977 British Grand Prix on 16 July 1977, with the Renault RS01; Cosworth in Northampton; Beatrice Foods of the US, and Ford form an agreement in 1985, with Australian Alan Jones, with designer John Baldwin; the 1981 Italian Grand Prix and crash of John Watson; the assembled Cosworth GBA V6 engine is tested at Northampton. Produced by Patrick Uden, made by Uden Associates."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pimenta longa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1750, "target_cluster_name": "pimenta longa", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jilfil, e asi a chamam os Arábios físicos e o vulgar. Se Avicena (se- gundo tralada o Belunense) a chama, Jul/ul, e darfulful á pimenta longa % tf ai fel, e Serapio • % que eram Arábios, to- davia me ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for"}, {"found_name": "Jilfil", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jilfil, e asi a chamam os Arábios físicos e o vulgar. Se Avicena (se- gundo tralada o Belunense) a chama, Jul/ul, e darfulful á pimenta longa % tf ai fel, e Serapio • % que eram Arábios, to- davia me ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for"}, {"found_name": "Jul/ul", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jilfil, e asi a chamam os Arábios físicos e o vulgar. Se Avicena (se- gundo tralada o Belunense) a chama, Jul/ul, e darfulful á pimenta longa % tf ai fel, e Serapio • % que eram Arábios, to- davia me ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for"}, {"found_name": "falfel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Jilfil, e asi a chamam os Arábios físicos e o vulgar. Se Avicena (se- gundo tralada o Belunense) a chama, Jul/ul, e darfulful á pimenta longa % tf ai fel, e Serapio • % que eram Arábios, to- davia me ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "alternative name for"}]}, {"canonical_name": "gesso", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gesso", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "gesso", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["gesso"], "contexts": ["used to sophisticate substances", "plaster used as adulterant"]}, {"entity_id": "gesso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "gesso", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["gesso"], "contexts": ["gypsum used in preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "gesso", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "gesso", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3707, "stable_key": "clu_3ff9a9f3463a6624", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gypsum", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'gesso' in this context refers to gypsum, a mineral commonly used in medicinal preparations and sometimes for adulteration."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lagrimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lagrimas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Lagrimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lagrimas"], "contexts": ["tears", "coagulated and dry liquid from trees"]}, {"entity_id": "in_lagrima", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "in lagrima", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["in lagrima"], "contexts": ["form of storax"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Lagrimas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "in lagrima", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3708, "stable_key": "clu_dc3e547629bd718c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tears", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Bodily fluid secreted by the eyes, or substances resembling it.", "note": "Can refer to literal tears or plant exudates described as such.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tears (tear film) are a transparent fluid secreted primarily by the lacrimal glands (tear gland) found in the eyes of all land mammals. According to the mode of production, tears are classified into four types: basal, closed eye, emotional, and reflex. The basal rate of tear secretion is ~0.5–2.2 µL/min, and irritation can increase secretion by up to ~100-fold, reaching ~300 µL/min. Tears are made up of water, electrolytes, proteins, lipids, and mucins that form layers on the surface of eyes. The four types of tears differ significantly in their composition. Some of the functions of tears include lubricating the eyes (basal tears), removing irritants (reflex tears), and also aiding the immune system."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Pacem", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pacem", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pacem", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pacem"], "contexts": ["port and kingdom in Sumatra", "place or concept"]}, {"entity_id": "peace", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "peace", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["peace"], "contexts": ["absence of war"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Pacem", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "peace", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 3709, "stable_key": "clu_bc94c16be269f03c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peace", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'absence of war' strongly suggests the concept of peace, not a geographical place.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace", "wikipedia_extract": "Peace is a state of harmony in the absence of hostility and violence. In a societal sense, peace is commonly used to mean a lack of conflict (such as war) and freedom from fear of violence between individuals or groups. Promotion of peace is a core tenet of many philosophies, religions, and ideologies, many of which consider it a core tenet of their philosophy. Some examples are: religions such as Buddhism and Christianity, important figures like Gandhi, and throughout literature like \"Perpetual Peace: A Philosophical Sketch\" by Immanuel Kant, \"The Art of Peace\" by Morihei Ueshiba, or ideologies that strictly adhere to it such as Pacifism within a sociopolitical scope. It is a frequent subject of symbolism and features prominently in art and other cultural traditions. The representation of peace has taken many shapes, with a variety of symbols pertaining to it based on culture, context, and history; each with their respective symbolism whose nature can be very complex."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "D. Pedro Mascarenhas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__pedro_mascarenhas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "D. Pedro Mascarenhas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["D. Pedro Mascarenhas"], "contexts": ["historical figure, ambassador in Rome", "vice-reí who tried to curb luxury"]}, {"entity_id": "dom_francisco_mascarenhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dom Francisco Mascarenhas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dom Francisco Mascarenhas"], "contexts": ["patient who recovered from illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "D. Pedro Mascarenhas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dom Francisco Mascarenhas", "similarity": 0.879}], "id": 3710, "stable_key": "clu_e792a977b8bf8039", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pedro Mascarenhas", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Historical figure, ambassador, and patient who recovered from illness.", "note": "While 'Dom Francisco Mascarenhas' is also mentioned, 'D. Pedro Mascarenhas' is more frequent and likely the primary referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q527755", "wikidata_description": "Portuguese explorer and colonial administrator"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "crísteis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "crísteis", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "crísteis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["crísteis"], "contexts": ["medicinal suppositories", "medicinal preparation from fruit peels"]}, {"entity_id": "cristaes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cristaes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["cristaes"], "contexts": ["substance derived from wine residue"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "crísteis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cristaes", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3711, "stable_key": "clu_a1d2d040c1c3f84d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Suppositories", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The context 'medicinal suppositories' is a direct match. 'Cristaes' could refer to crystals, but in a medicinal context related to wine residue, it could also point to effervescence or crystalline precipitates used in preparations like suppositories.", "wikidata_id": "Q180799", "wikidata_description": "dosage form used to deliver medications by insertion into a body orifice where it dissolves or melts to exert local or systemic effects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suppository", "wikipedia_extract": "Intravaginal administration is a route of administration where the substance is applied inside the vagina. Pharmacologically, it has the potential advantage to result in effects primarily in the vagina or nearby structures (such as the vaginal portion of cervix) with limited systemic adverse effects compared to other routes of administration. Formulation methods include vaginal tablets, vaginal cream, vaginal gel, vaginal suppository and vaginal ring. It is used as an administration method for issues related to women's health, such as contraception. Medicines primarily delivered by intravaginal administration include vaginally administered estrogens and progestogens (a group of hormones including progesterone), and antibacterials and antifungals to treat bacterial vaginosis and yeast infections respectively. Medicines may also be administered intravaginally as an alternative to oral route in the case of nausea or other digestive problems."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "falconer", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Falconer"], "contexts": ["identified plant", "observed the costus plant and its drug"]}, {"entity_id": "falconet", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Falconet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falconet"], "contexts": ["doctor cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Falconer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Falconet", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3712, "stable_key": "clu_f8823d2f42813a21", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Falconer", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Doctor and scholar cited for plant identification and medical practice.", "note": "The name 'Falconet' is a plausible misspelling or variant, but 'Falconer' is the more common form for historical scholars/doctors. Without more context, certainty is reduced.", "wikidata_id": "Q28001428", "wikidata_description": "person who breeds, trains, or hunts with hawks"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "adstringente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "adstringente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "adstringente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["adstringente"], "contexts": ["property of areca", "medicinal property of sandalwood"]}, {"entity_id": "adstringunt", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "adstringunt", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["adstringunt"], "contexts": ["to astringe, a remedy for tremors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "adstringente", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "adstringunt", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 3713, "stable_key": "clu_dde23a0fad96dda4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astringent", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of properties of areca and remedies for tremors clearly indicates an astringent quality or action.", "wikidata_id": "Q378705", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound that tends to shrink or constrict body tissues"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "letrados", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "letrados", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "letrados", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["letrados"], "contexts": ["Legal experts appointed by sovereigns", "learned individuals"]}, {"entity_id": "letrados", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Letrados", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Letrados"], "contexts": ["learned individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "letrados", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Letrados", "similarity": 0.665}], "id": 3714, "stable_key": "clu_0589033f26cd34f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Legal scholar", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Learned individuals, often with legal or academic expertise.", "wikidata_id": "Q185351", "wikidata_description": "legal scholar or academic, a professional who studies, teaches, and develops law", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurist", "wikipedia_extract": "A jurist is a person with expert knowledge of law; someone who analyzes and comments on law. This person is usually a specialist legal scholar, mostly (but not always) with a formal education in law (a law degree) and often a legal practitioner. In the United Kingdom the term \"jurist\" is mostly used for legal academics, while in the United States the term may also be applied to a judge. With reference to Roman law, a \"jurist\" (in English) is a jurisconsult (iurisconsultus). The English term jurist is to be distinguished from similar terms in other European languages, where it may be synonymous with legal professional, meaning anyone with a professional law degree that qualifies for admission to the legal profession, including such positions as judge or attorney. In Germany, Scandinavia and a number of other countries jurist denotes someone with a professional law degree, and it may be a protected title, such as in Norway."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "baxáj", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "baxáj", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "baxáj", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["baxáj"], "contexts": ["title for governor", "title for governor in Alepo"]}, {"entity_id": "baгаб", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Baгаб", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Baгаб"], "contexts": ["title of nobility"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "baxáj", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Baгаб", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3715, "stable_key": "clu_f4225c31982f6d39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pasha", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "High-ranking official or nobleman, often in the Ottoman Empire.", "note": "The spelling 'Baгаб' in polyanthea_medicinal appears to be a transcription error or archaic spelling.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Savonarola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "savonarola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Savonarola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Savonarola"], "contexts": ["alternative spelling of Savanerola", "physician who wrote medical books"]}, {"entity_id": "savonarola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Savonarola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Savonarola"], "contexts": ["physician of the Duke's household"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Savonarola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Savonarola", "similarity": 0.71}], "id": 3716, "stable_key": "clu_53a30ee2f7643114", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Girolamo Savonarola", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1452, "death_year": 1498, "description": "Italian Dominican friar, preacher, and executed ruler of Florence.", "note": "The context 'physician of the Duke's household' might refer to a different Savonarola or a misinterpretation within the text, as Girolamo Savonarola was primarily a religious and political figure.", "wikidata_id": "Q164823", "wikidata_description": "Italian Dominican friar and preacher (1452-1498)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "preço", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "preço", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "preço", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["preço"], "contexts": ["price", "monetary value"]}, {"entity_id": "price", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "price", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["price"], "contexts": ["monetary value of goods"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "preço", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "price", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3717, "stable_key": "clu_d94bb832b4cba896", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Price", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "panditos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "panditos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "panditos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["panditos"], "contexts": ["Hindu priests or scholars", "religious scholars"]}, {"entity_id": "panditos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Panditos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Panditos"], "contexts": ["natural healers in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "panditos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Panditos", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 3718, "stable_key": "clu_1b2dc985237fba3d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pandits", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Hindu priests, scholars, or healers in India.", "wikidata_id": "Q2336535", "wikidata_description": "native explorers who assisted the British in surveying Tibet and Central Asia; used as their pseudonym in accounts of their reports", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pundit%20%28explorer%29", "wikipedia_extract": "The term pundit was used in the second half of the 19th century to denote native Indian surveyors used by the British to secretly explore regions north of British India. The Pundit was the code-name for one of the first native explorers, Nain Singh, who was originally a schoolteacher (or pundit). His accomplishments were so remarkable that the whole group of around twenty native explorers became known as the Pundits. Two of the most famous pundits included the cousins Nain Singh and Kishen Singh (code-named A.K.) One of the greatest projects of 19th century geography was the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India. The British also wanted geographical information on the lands further north. This was not just out of scientific curiosity: the Russians were attempting to expand their empire into Central Asia, and the British feared that they might have set their eyes on gaining the riches of India, which was at that time a British colony."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Papa Paulo IV", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "papa_paulo_iv", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Papa Paulo IV", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["papa Paulo IV", "Papa Paulo IV"], "contexts": ["sent Rontecalli to India", "Pope Paul IV"]}, {"entity_id": "paulo_iv_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Paulo IV.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paulo IV."], "contexts": ["Pope who suffered from erysipelas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Papa Paulo IV", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Paulo IV.", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 3719, "stable_key": "clu_f3d6f5723c7cea7d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pope Paul IV", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1476, "death_year": 1559, "description": "Pope during the Counter-Reformation, known for his policies.", "note": "Both 'Papa Paulo IV' and 'Paulo IV.' clearly refer to Pope Paul IV, a significant figure in the 16th century, aligning with the early modern period.", "wikidata_id": "Q101430", "wikidata_description": "pope of catholic church 1555-1559", "wikipedia_extract": "This page is a list of popes by country of origin and nationality. There have been 265 popes, from the continents of Asia, Europe, Africa, South America, and North America. Since the office of pope has existed for almost two millennia, many of the countries of origin of popes no longer exist, and so they are grouped under three periods: the Roman Empire period, the Middle Ages to modernity, and since the creation of Vatican City with the 1929 Lateran Treaty. Countries are listed in chronological order within each section. As of 2025, 265 men have been pope, with at least one pope hailing (in chronological order) from Asia (9), Europe (251), Africa (3), or the Americas (2). Every pope since Pope Pius XI has been a citizen of Vatican City (established in the 1929 Lateran Treaty).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Paul_IV"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bonifá", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bonifá", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Bonifá", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bonifá"], "contexts": ["cited authority", "cited by Serapio"]}, {"entity_id": "bonifucci", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bonifucci", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bonifucci"], "contexts": ["cited author on nourishing foods"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Bonifá", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bonifucci", "similarity": 0.849}], "id": 3720, "stable_key": "clu_74f94c549b5d15f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boniface", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Cited authority on nutrition and health in early modern texts.", "note": "The name 'Bonifá' and 'Bonifucci' likely refer to an author or scholar cited in these texts. The common element is 'Boniface'. It's difficult to pinpoint a specific historical figure with certainty without more context, but it refers to a cited authority.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "The Université de Saint-Boniface (USB) is a French-language public university located in the St. Boniface neighbourhood of Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada. An affiliated institution of the University of Manitoba, the university offers general and specialized university degree programs as well as technical and professional training. In 2014, 1,368 regular students were enrolled. Its Continuing Education Division, which includes a language school, has also counted over 4,200 enrolments. Université de Saint-Boniface was established by Father Norbert Provencher (1787–1853) in 1818, making it western Canada's oldest post-secondary educational institution.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boniface_%28name%29"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Santos", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2401, "target_cluster_name": "Santos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Boniface is a name associated with several saints, most notably Saint Boniface.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Boniface is a name associated with several saints, most notably Saint Boniface.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sorveira", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sorveira", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sorveira", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sorveira"], "contexts": ["tree where lacre does not grow", "tree name"]}, {"entity_id": "sorveyra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sorveyra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sorveyra"], "contexts": ["tree name"]}, {"entity_id": "sorva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sorva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sorva"], "contexts": ["fruit compared to Jaca"]}, {"entity_id": "sorvas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sorvas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sorvas"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sorveira", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sorveyra", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 3721, "stable_key": "clu_657d354ceba7f17f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strawberry Tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Arbutus unedo Linnaeus", "family": "Ericaceae", "note": "The 'sorveira' or 'sorveira' is a Portuguese name for Arbutus unedo, commonly known as the Strawberry Tree. 'Sorvas' could be a variation or related term.", "wikidata_id": "Q329046", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbutus_unedo", "wikipedia_extract": "Arbutus unedo, commonly known as strawberry tree, also called madrone, is an evergreen shrub or small tree in the family Ericaceae, native to the Mediterranean Basin and Western Europe. The tree is well known for its fruits, the arbutus berry, which bear some resemblance to the strawberry, hence the common name strawberry tree. However, it is not closely related to true strawberries of the genus Fragaria. Its presence in Ireland also lends it the name Irish strawberry tree, or cain, or cane apple (from the Irish name for the tree, caithne), or sometimes Killarney strawberry tree. The strawberry tree is the national tree of Italy because of its green leaves, its white flowers and its red berries, colors that recall the Italian flag. The flower of the strawberry tree is the national flower of Italy."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "murtinhos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1917, "target_cluster_name": "murtinhos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Strawberry Tree (Arbutus unedo) is another common evergreen shrub native to the Mediterranean region, often found alongside or in similar ecological niches as the common myrtle. They share a similar botanical family (Ericaceae vs. Myrtaceae) and are frequently mentioned together in descriptions of Mediterranean flora.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Strawberry Tree (Arbutus unedo) is another common evergreen shrub native to the Mediterranean region, often found alongside or in similar ecological niches as the common myrtle. They share a similar botanical family (Ericaceae vs. Myrtaceae) and are frequently mentioned together in descriptions of Mediterranean flora.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "voto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "voto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "voto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["voto"], "contexts": ["Opinion or judgment"]}, {"entity_id": "voto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "voto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["voto", "voto contrario"], "contexts": ["opinion", "opposition to a medical decision"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "voto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "voto", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3722, "stable_key": "clu_808ed0a8aafe59a1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Opinion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "evaporaçao", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "evapora", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "evapora", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["evapora"], "contexts": ["turns into vapor"]}, {"entity_id": "evaporaçao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "evaporaçao", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["evaporaçao"], "contexts": ["evaporation, a process of vaporization", "evaporation causing syncope"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "evapora", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "evaporaçao", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 3723, "stable_key": "clu_3c7e88d4f786ce1b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Evaporation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q132814", "wikidata_description": "type of vaporization of a liquid that occurs from its surface; surface phenomenon"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "évaporation", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2390, "target_cluster_name": "évaporation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Evaporation' is presented with its historical French spelling 'évaporation', indicating a direct linguistic equivalent.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "The entity 'Evaporation' is presented with its historical French spelling 'évaporation', indicating a direct linguistic equivalent."}, {"found_name": "Condensation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1255, "target_cluster_name": "condensations", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evaporation and condensation are inverse processes in the water cycle and phase transitions of matter, often discussed together.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evaporation and condensation are inverse processes in the water cycle and phase transitions of matter, often discussed together."}, {"found_name": "Heat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 197, "target_cluster_name": "calor", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evaporation is a process driven by heat, involving the absorption of thermal energy to change state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evaporation is a process driven by heat, involving the absorption of thermal energy to change state."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous state", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1478, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evaporation is the process by which a substance in a liquid or solid state changes to a gaseous state.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evaporation is the process by which a substance in a liquid or solid state changes to a gaseous state."}, {"found_name": "Humidity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 27, "target_cluster_name": "Humidity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evaporation directly contributes to atmospheric humidity by releasing water vapor into the air.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Evaporation directly contributes to atmospheric humidity by releasing water vapor into the air."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Bosphoro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bósforo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Bósforo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bósforo"], "contexts": ["location associated with cardamomo origin"]}, {"entity_id": "bosphoro", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Bosphoro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bosphoro", "Bofphoro"], "contexts": ["place where Cardamomo is said to come from", "place of origin for a substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Bósforo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Bosphoro", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 3724, "stable_key": "clu_14bdb57479db3ea5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strait of Istanbul", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Turkey", "note": "Bosphorus is a well-known strait and a historical trade route, making it a plausible origin for spices like Cardamomo.", "wikidata_id": "Q35958", "wikidata_description": "narrow strait in northwestern Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dardanelles", "wikipedia_extract": "The Dardanelles ( DAR-də-NELZ; Turkish: Çanakkale Boğazı, lit. 'Strait of Çanakkale'; Greek: Δαρδανέλλια, romanized: Dardanéllia), also known as the Strait of Gallipoli (after the Gallipoli peninsula), is a narrow, natural strait and internationally significant waterway in northwestern Turkey that forms part of the continental boundary between Asia and Europe and separates Asian Turkey from European Turkey. Together with the Bosporus, the Dardanelles forms the Turkish Straits. One of the world's narrowest straits used for international navigation, the Dardanelles connects the Sea of Marmara with the Aegean and Mediterranean seas while also allowing passage to the Black Sea by extension via the Bosporus. The Dardanelles is 61 kilometres (38 mi) long and 1.2 to 6 kilometres (0.75 to 3.73 mi) wide. It has an average depth of 55 metres (180 ft) with a maximum depth of 103 metres (338 ft) at its narrowest point abreast the city of Çanakkale."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "gergelim", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gergelim", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "gergelim", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["gergelim"], "contexts": ["sesame, used for oil"]}, {"entity_id": "gergelim", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "gergelim", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["gergelim"], "contexts": ["oil used in a liniment", "sesame used in fomentations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "gergelim", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "gergelim", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3725, "stable_key": "clu_807d59637c2019e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sesame", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Sesamum indicum L.", "family": "Pedaliaceae", "note": "The term 'gergelim' is the Portuguese word for sesame, a plant well-known for its oil used in medicinal preparations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sesame (; Sesamum indicum) is a plant in the genus Sesamum, also called benne. Numerous wild relatives occur in Africa and a smaller number in India. It is widely naturalized in tropical regions around the world and is cultivated for its edible seeds, which grow in pods. World production in 2018 was 6 million tonnes (5.9 million long tons), with Sudan, Myanmar, and India as the largest producers. Sesame seed is one of the oldest oilseed crops known, domesticated well over 3,000 years ago. Sesamum has many other species, most being wild and native to sub-Saharan Africa.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "S.W.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "s_w_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "S.W.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["S.W."], "contexts": ["direction of monsoon"]}, {"entity_id": "s_w_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "S.W.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["S.W."], "contexts": ["direction: South-West", "direction of wind bias"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "S.W.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "S.W.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3726, "stable_key": "clu_62281c2199e69d46", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "South-West", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ArcUipo da Torre do Tombo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "arcuipo_da_torre_do_tombo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ArcUipo da Torre do Tombo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ArcUipo da Torre do Tombo"], "contexts": ["archive where a copy is kept"]}, {"entity_id": "torre_do_tombo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Torre do Tombo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Torre do Tombo"], "contexts": ["archive in Lisbon"]}, {"entity_id": "torre_do_tombo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Torre do Tombo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Torre do Tombo"], "contexts": ["place of office"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ArcUipo da Torre do Tombo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Torre do Tombo", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Torre do Tombo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Torre do Tombo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3727, "stable_key": "clu_db23ec85a9feabcc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Torre do Tombo National Archive", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "wikidata_id": "Q1769248", "wikidata_description": "national archives of Portugal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torre%20do%20Tombo%20National%20Archive", "wikipedia_extract": "The Torre do Tombo National Archive (Portuguese: Arquivo Nacional da Torre do Tombo), commonly known simply as the Torre do Tombo ([ˈtoʁɨ ðu ˈtõbu]; literally \"Tower of the Tome\") is the national archive of Portugal, located in Lisbon. Established in 1378, it is one of the oldest archival institutions in the world. The archive is one of the oldest institutions in Portugal, since its installation in one of the towers of the castle in Lisbon, occurring during the reign of Ferdinand I, and likely in 1378 (the date where the first testimony originated). The archive served as the King's and nobilities' reference, with documents supporting the administration of the kingdom and overseas territories, and documenting the relationships between the State and foreign kingdoms. This institution was maintained by the Royal Keeper, an office sometimes paired with the post of Chief Chronicler of the Kingdom. The first known Royal Keeper was João Annes de Almada, called \"the Great\", appointed by king John I, Ferdinand's successor, who separated the office from the court chancellor's."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sementes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sementes_de_leguminosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sementes de leguminosas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sementes de leguminosas"], "contexts": ["food eaten by the crew"]}, {"entity_id": "sementes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sementes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sementes"], "contexts": ["seeds of plants", "seeds of plants used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sementes de leguminosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sementes", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 3728, "stable_key": "clu_528ed944952d2042", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Seeds", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Mestres", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mestres", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mestres", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["mestres"], "contexts": ["masters who examined the body"]}, {"entity_id": "mestres", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mestres", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mestres"], "contexts": ["masters", "masters, referring to ancient authorities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mestres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mestres", "similarity": 0.834}], "id": 3729, "stable_key": "clu_76ae9ae7ef3ce15b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Masters", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Professors or experts in a field, often medical.", "note": "This refers to a title or role rather than specific individuals."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "autopsia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "autopsia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "autopsia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["autopsia"], "contexts": ["examination of a dead body"]}, {"entity_id": "autopsia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "autopsia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["autopsia"], "contexts": ["autopsy", "examination of bodies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "autopsia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "autopsia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3730, "stable_key": "clu_0617dd5d660b4fef", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Autopsia is a European art project focused on music and visual production, gathering authors of different professions in the realization of multimedia projects. Its art practice began in London in the late 1970s and continued during the 1980s in the former Yugoslavia. Since 1990, Autopsia has been based in Prague, Czech Republic. At the beginning of its activity, Autopsia issued dozens of mini-recordings. In the period after 1989, twenty CDs were issued, at first for Staalplaat, then for German label Hypnobeat and London's Gymnastic Records. One of its compositions is a part of the soundtrack for Peter Greenaway's The Pillow Book (1996)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mortandades", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mortindade", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mortindade", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mortindade"], "contexts": ["great mortality"]}, {"entity_id": "mortandades", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mortandades", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["mortandades"], "contexts": ["deaths preceding plague", "mortalities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mortindade", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mortandades", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 3731, "stable_key": "clu_edfe20c0835b21f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mortality", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Mortality (high death rate)", "note": "While 'mortality' can refer to the state of being mortal, in this medical context it signifies a high death rate, often due to disease outbreaks."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "golfaó", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "golfam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "golfam", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["golfam"], "contexts": ["plant with similar appearance"]}, {"entity_id": "golfaó", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "golfaó", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["golfaó"], "contexts": ["plant used with ointment", "plant for fluxes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "golfam", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "golfaó", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 3732, "stable_key": "clu_01388f68f59758ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "gulfam", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The terms 'golfaó' and 'golfam' appear in contexts of medicinal use and visual similarity. Without more specific botanical descriptions, precise identification is difficult. 'Gulfam' can refer to various plants in different regions."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dom Affonio V", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__affonso_v", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "D. Affonso V", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["D. Affonso V"], "contexts": ["king whose cruzado value is noted"]}, {"entity_id": "dom_affonio_v", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dom Affonio V", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dom Affonio V", "Dom Affonfo Sexto"], "contexts": ["King whose chapel scribe suffered", "King"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "D. Affonso V", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dom Affonio V", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 3733, "stable_key": "clu_3dcb9a4922236cf8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Afonso V of Portugal", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1432, "death_year": 1481, "description": "King of Portugal, mentioned in historical contexts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Afonso V (European Portuguese: [ɐˈfõsu]; 15 January 1432 – 28 August 1481), also known as the African (Portuguese: o Africano), was King of Portugal from 1438 until he died in 1481, with a brief interruption in 1477. The son of Edward, King of Portugal, and Eleanor of Aragon, Afonso acceded to the throne when he was only six years old. His early reign was marked by a struggle over the regency between his mother, Eleanor, and his uncle, Pedro, Duke of Coimbra. Pedro was appointed sole regent in 1439, but the Braganza faction at court continued to challenge his authority. Influenced by his other uncle, Afonso I, Duke of Braganza, the King dismissed Pedro in 1448 and defeated him in the Battle of Alfarrobeira in 1449. Between 1458 and 1471, Afonso pursued military campaigns in Africa, concentrating efforts predominantly on Morocco."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Portuguezes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 218, "target_cluster_name": "Portuguezes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Afonso V was a significant historical figure and monarch of Portugal, directly associated with the Portuguese people.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Afonso V was a significant historical figure and monarch of Portugal, directly associated with the Portuguese people.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Fidalgo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 748, "target_cluster_name": "Fidalgo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fidalgo is a Portuguese noble title, and Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a prominent figure associated with this social class.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fidalgo is a Portuguese noble title, and Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a prominent figure associated with this social class.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Carlos V.", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 771, "target_cluster_name": "Carlos V.", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent European monarchs of the 15th century, with significant political and military roles in their respective kingdoms and in relation to each other.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent European monarchs of the 15th century, with significant political and military roles in their respective kingdoms and in relation to each other.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1856, "target_cluster_name": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha was a Portuguese nobleman and official who served under Afonso V of Portugal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: D. Pedro Antonio de Noronha was a Portuguese nobleman and official who served under Afonso V of Portugal.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rey de Portugal", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2078, "target_cluster_name": "Rey de Portugal", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Afonso V was a King of Portugal, a specific instance of the entity 'King of Portugal'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2083, "target_cluster_name": "Thomas Rodriguez da Veiga", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are Portuguese figures, suggesting a potential connection within the same national context.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are Portuguese figures, suggesting a potential connection within the same national context.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Quastionibus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "questões", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "questões", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["questões"], "contexts": ["economic issues studied in Eastern regions"]}, {"entity_id": "quastionibus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Quastionibus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Quastionibus", "Quastion."], "contexts": ["section of a book", "abbreviation for question"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "questões", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Quastionibus", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 3734, "stable_key": "clu_4947d1804b91cc97", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "questions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context suggests 'questions' as in a series of inquiries or a section of a book dedicated to posing and answering questions.", "wikidata_id": "Q1505023", "wikidata_description": "formal request of a parliament to the respective government", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpellation%20%28politics%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Interpellation is a formal parliamentary procedure through which members of parliament request that the government explain, clarify, or justify its policies, actions, or decisions. Typically presented in written or oral form, this process mandates that the government respond within a specified timeframe and may lead to subsequent debates or even votes of no confidence. The primary objectives of interpellation are to ensure governmental accountability—upholding the principles of responsible government—as well as to monitor the exercise of executive power and safeguard against its abuse while promoting transparency and public participation in policy deliberations. Interpellation in parliamentary politics can be traced back to Latin and French. The term comes from the Latin verb interpellāre, meaning “to interrupt by speaking” or “to call out.” The word was subsequently absorbed into medieval French as “interpellation,” retaining the meaning of “to call out” or “to interrupt.” Over time, the term came to be used to describe the formal mechanism by which legislators in parliament question or challenge government ministers on their policies or actions. In English, the concept of “interpellation” in this political sense emerged in the early to mid-19th century, influenced by French constitutional practice, and became established in parliamentary politics as a tool for ensuring government accountability and transparency."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Alfama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "alfama", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alfama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alfama"], "contexts": ["neighborhood in Lisbon"]}, {"entity_id": "alfama", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alfama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Alfama"], "contexts": ["location of a woman", "location in Lisbon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alfama", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alfama", "similarity": 0.817}], "id": 3735, "stable_key": "clu_576eafe2f27e202e", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "The Alfama (Portuguese pronunciation: [alˈfɐmɐ]) is the oldest neighborhood of Lisbon, spreading on the slope between the São Jorge Castle and the Tagus river. The district includes the freguesias (parishes) of São Miguel, Santo Estêvão, São Vicente de Fora and part of the two streets, \"Freguesia da Sé: Rua do Barão\" and \"Rua São João da Praça\". It contains many important historical attractions, as well as an abundance of Fado bars and restaurants. The name Alfama originates from the Arabic al-ḥamma (الحَمّة), meaning \"hot springs\". The Arabic word comes from the root meaning \"to heat\" or \"to become hot\", from which the word hammam (Arabic: حَمَّام, lit. 'bath, bathhouse') also derives. The Rua do Barão is one of the streets of the Freguesia da Sé, which begins at the Rua São João da Praça (where once stood the Door of the Alfama) and ends at Rua Augusto Rosa (at the Cathedral's walls)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lisboa", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 224, "target_cluster_name": "Lisboa", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Alfama is a historic district within Lisbon, known for its narrow streets and Fado music.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Alfama is a historic district within Lisbon, known for its narrow streets and Fado music.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "semente de linhaça", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "semente_de_emdivia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "semente de emdivia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["semente de emdivia"], "contexts": ["seed used in fevers"]}, {"entity_id": "semente_de_linhaça", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "semente de linhaça", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["semente de linhaça"], "contexts": ["emollient remedy ingredient", "seed used in an emplastrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "semente de emdivia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "semente de linhaça", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 3736, "stable_key": "clu_8a1b2095d26a826f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "flaxseed", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Linum usitatissimum L.", "family": "Linaceae", "note": "The context 'emollient remedy ingredient' strongly points to flaxseed. 'Semente de linhaça' is the direct Portuguese translation.", "wikidata_id": "Q911332", "wikidata_description": "seed of the flax plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "lino", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3688, "target_cluster_name": "lino", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Flaxseed is the seed of the flax plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Flaxseed is the seed of the flax plant.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "borbulhas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "burbulhas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "burbulhas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["burbulhas"], "contexts": ["small blisters"]}, {"entity_id": "borbulhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "borbulhas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["borbulhas"], "contexts": ["skin eruption", "blisters that can form on the skin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "burbulhas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "borbulhas", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 3737, "stable_key": "clu_848b0af3e9ee3408", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Bolhás is a village in Somogy county, Hungary. Its name derives from the Hungarian word bolha (English: flea). According to the legends the village belonged to a knight who was called Bolha. The Hungarian folk songs Megismerni a kanászt and Volt nekem egy kecském were collected in 1922 in Bolhás by Zoltán Kodály."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Schrod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "schrad_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Schrad.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Schrad."], "contexts": ["botanist"]}, {"entity_id": "schrod", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Schrod", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Schrod", "Schrodé"], "contexts": ["cited for virtues of turpentine", "cited for virtues of oil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Schrad.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Schrod", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 3738, "stable_key": "clu_f813e38547037c30", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leonhart Fuchs", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1501, "death_year": 1566, "description": "German botanist and physician, author of 'De Historia Stirpium'", "note": "The abbreviation 'Schrad.' is unusual for Fuchs. However, given the context of botany and citations for plant virtues, Fuchs is a strong candidate for prominent botanists of the era. If it refers to a different 'Schrod', further context would be needed."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dodon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2273, "target_cluster_name": "Dodon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Fuchs was another leading botanist of the 16th century, whose work, like Dodoens', was foundational for later botanical studies. They are often discussed together as key figures in the history of botany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Fuchs was another leading botanist of the 16th century, whose work, like Dodoens', was foundational for later botanical studies. They are often discussed together as key figures in the history of botany.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mizaldus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2710, "target_cluster_name": "Mizaldus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were influential German physicians and botanists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to the study of medicinal plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were influential German physicians and botanists of the Renaissance, contributing significantly to the study of medicinal plants.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Terçaas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "terciárias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "terciárias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["terciárias"], "contexts": ["fever type"]}, {"entity_id": "terçaas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Terçaas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Terçaas"], "contexts": ["fevers treated with Quinaquina", "tertian fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "terciárias", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Terçaas", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3739, "stable_key": "clu_adcdc6ba575fc3d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sadness/Tertian Fever", "confidence": "low", "type": "disease", "description": "Emotional distress or intermittent fever type.", "note": "This cluster conflates two distinct concepts: 'tristeza' (sadness) and 'terciárias' (tertian fever, a type of malaria)."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "febre latica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 4497, "target_cluster_name": "febre latica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, sadness was sometimes associated with or considered a symptom of certain types of fever, specifically tertian fever.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historically, sadness was sometimes associated with or considered a symptom of certain types of fever, specifically tertian fever.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "compostos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "composições", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "composições", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["composições"], "contexts": ["medicinal preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "compostos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "compostos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["compostos", "compoſtos"], "contexts": ["compound remedies", "compounds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "composições", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "compostos", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3740, "stable_key": "clu_38e72b4abc442593", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Compound remedies", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Both 'compostos' and 'composições' in Portuguese directly translate to 'compounds' or 'compositions' in the context of medicinal preparations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Oswald Croll or Crollius (c. 1563 – December 1609) was an alchemist, and professor of medicine at the University of Marburg in Hesse, Germany. A strong proponent of alchemy and using chemistry in medicine, he was heavily involved in writing books and influencing thinkers of his day towards viewing chemistry and alchemy as two separate fields. Croll received his doctorate in medicine in 1582 at Marburg, then continued studies at Heidelberg, Strasburg, and Geneva. After working as a tutor, he arrived in Prague in 1597. He remained there for two years, and again from 1602 until his death.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswald_Croll"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "remedios", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1584, "target_cluster_name": "remedios", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Compound remedies are a specific category of medicines made from multiple ingredients.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Compound remedies are a specific category of medicines made from multiple ingredients.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "medicamente", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2482, "target_cluster_name": "medicamente", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Compound remedies are mixtures of multiple ingredients used as medicaments.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Compound remedies are mixtures of multiple ingredients used as medicaments.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "pungente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pungente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pungente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["pungente"], "contexts": ["quality of pepper"]}, {"entity_id": "pungente", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pungente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["pungente"], "contexts": ["quality, pungent", "quality of blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pungente", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pungente", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3741, "stable_key": "clu_e9f86b0e4baf893d", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Pungency (  PUN-jən-see), commonly referred to as spiciness, hotness or heat,  is a sensation that contributes to the flavor of certain foods such as chili peppers. Highly pungent foods may be experienced as unpleasant. The term piquancy ( PEEK-ən-see) is sometimes applied to foods with a lower degree of pungency that are \"agreeably stimulating to the palate\". In addition to chili peppers, piquant ingredients include wasabi, horseradish and mustard. The primary substances responsible for pungency are capsaicin (in chilis), piperine (in peppercorns) and allyl isothiocyanate (in radishes, mustard and wasabi). In colloquial speech, the term \"pungency\" can refer to any strong, sharp smell or flavor."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dor no estomaguo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "dor_no_estomaguo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dor no estomaguo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dor no estomaguo"], "contexts": ["stomach pain"]}, {"entity_id": "de_stomachi_dolore", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "de Stomachi dolore", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["de Stomachi dolore"], "contexts": ["on stomach pain"]}, {"entity_id": "asdoresdeestomago", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "asdoresdeestomago", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["asdoresdeestomago"], "contexts": ["excellent for stomach pains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dor no estomaguo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "de Stomachi dolore", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dor no estomaguo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "asdoresdeestomago", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3742, "stable_key": "clu_77ad428f2c2beeb1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dyspepsia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Dyspepsia", "note": "The Portuguese term 'de Stomachi dolore' directly translates to stomach pain, a symptom of dyspepsia.", "wikidata_id": "Q653971", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigestion", "wikipedia_extract": "Postcholecystectomy syndrome (PCS) describes the presence of abdominal symptoms after a cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal). Symptoms occur in about 5 to 40 percent of patients who undergo cholecystectomy, and can be transient, persistent or lifelong. The chronic condition is diagnosed in approximately 10% of postcholecystectomy cases. The pain associated with postcholecystectomy syndrome is usually ascribed to either sphincter of Oddi dysfunction or to post-surgical adhesions. A recent 2008 study shows that postcholecystectomy syndrome can be caused by biliary microlithiasis. Approximately 50% of cases are due to biliary causes such as remaining stone, biliary injury, dysmotility and choledococyst."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ovelha", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ovelhas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ovelhas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["ovelhas"], "contexts": ["animals that eat the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "ovelha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ovelha", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["ovelha", "ovelhas"], "contexts": ["sheep's milk", "animals that move when male is killed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ovelhas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ovelha", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 3743, "stable_key": "clu_575ab3aea731bbfa-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sheep", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Reproductive body laid by female birds, reptiles, amphibians, fish, and insects.", "note": "The context for 'ovelhas' (sheep) is anomalous; it appears to refer to animals that eat a plant, not the egg itself.", "wikipedia_extract": "An egg is an organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly fertilized egg cell – a zygote. Within the vessel, an embryo is incubated until it has become an animal fetus that can survive on its own, at which point the animal hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other kingdoms are termed \"spores\", or in spermatophytes \"seeds\", or in gametophytes \"egg cells\". Most arthropods, vertebrates (excluding live-bearing mammals), and mollusks lay eggs, although some, such as scorpions, do not. Reptile eggs, bird eggs, and monotreme eggs are laid out of water and are surrounded by a protective shell, either flexible or inflexible. Eggs laid on land or in nests are usually kept within a warm and favorable temperature range while the embryo grows."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Faroe", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3983, "target_cluster_name": "Faroe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'sheep' in Portuguese.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Direct translation of 'sheep' in Portuguese.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Innamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "onamomo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "onamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["onamomo"], "contexts": ["planta com nomes em diversas linguoas"]}, {"entity_id": "innamomo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Innamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Innamomo", "Innamoniol"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a preparation", "cinnamon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "onamomo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Innamomo", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3744, "stable_key": "clu_da8349cf6cb8762c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indian Almond", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Terminalia catappa L.", "family": "Combretaceae", "note": "The phonetic similarity between 'onamomo'/'Innamomo' and botanical names of plants with diverse linguistic appellations suggests a well-known plant. Indian Almond is known for its various medicinal uses and is described with multiple names. Further research into the specific text 'ricettario_fiorentino_1597' might clarify this."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "herva dos paſſarinhos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "herva_de_besteiros", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "herva de besteiros", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["herva de besteiros"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "herva_dos_paſſarinhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "herva dos paſſarinhos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["herva dos paſſarinhos"], "contexts": ["bird's herb, medicinal plant", "plant used in a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "herva de besteiros", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "herva dos paſſarinhos", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3745, "stable_key": "clu_c2ce169af13f36da", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ribes uva-crispa", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ribes uva-crispa L.", "family": "Grossulariaceae", "note": "The Portuguese names 'herva dos passarinho' and 'herva de besteiros' are historically associated with gooseberry (Ribes uva-crispa), often cited for its medicinal properties.", "wikidata_id": "Q41503", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribes%20uva-crispa", "wikipedia_extract": "Ribes uva-crispa, commonly known as the gooseberry or European gooseberry, is a species of flowering shrub native to Europe, northwestern Africa, and southwestern Asia. It belongs to the family Grossulariaceae, which includes currants and gooseberries. The plant is cultivated for its edible fruit, which is used in culinary applications ranging from desserts to preserves. The goose in gooseberry has been seen as a corruption of either the Dutch word kruisbes or the allied German Krausbeere, or of the earlier forms of the French groseille. Alternatively, the word has been connected to the Middle High German krus ('curl, crisped'), in Latin as grossularia (also the name of its subgenus within Ribes). However, the Oxford English Dictionary takes the more literal derivation from goose and berry as probable because \"the grounds on which plants and fruits have received names associating them with animals are so often inexplicable that the inappropriateness in the meaning does not necessarily give good grounds for believing that the word is an etymological corruption\"."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Uvas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1605, "target_cluster_name": "Uvas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ribes uva-crispa is the scientific name for the gooseberry, a closely related fruit often discussed alongside grapes in historical texts concerning fruit cultivation and consumption.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ribes uva-crispa is the scientific name for the gooseberry, a closely related fruit often discussed alongside grapes in historical texts concerning fruit cultivation and consumption.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hyoscyamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hyoscyamus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hyoscyamus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hyoscyamus"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "hyoscyamo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Hyoscyamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hyoscyamo"], "contexts": ["henbane herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Hyoscyamus", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Hyoscyamo", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 3746, "stable_key": "clu_0ad1fd4854d71403", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Water-hemlock", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLANT", "description": "A genus of poisonous herbaceous plants, often found near water.", "note": "Helosciadium is an older name for some species now classified under the genus Berula, but it also refers to plants sometimes identified with Henbane (Hyoscyamus).", "wikipedia_extract": "Cicuta, commonly known as water hemlock, is a genus of four species of highly poisonous plants in the family Apiaceae. They are perennial herbaceous plants which grow up to 2.5 meters (8 ft) tall, having distinctive small green or white flowers arranged in an umbrella shape (umbel). Plants in this genus may also be referred to as cowbane or poison parsnip. Cicuta is native to temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere, mainly North America and Europe, typically growing in wet meadows, along streambanks and other wet and marshy areas. In the United Kingdom it is frequently found along canals. These plants bear a close resemblance to other members in the family Apiaceae and may be confused with a number of edible or poisonous plants."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fegato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fegato", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "fegato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["fegato"], "contexts": ["liver, a proper part of animals", "liver color of good Sinopia"]}, {"entity_id": "feſſo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "feſſo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["feſſo"], "contexts": ["anus, body part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "fegato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feſſo", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3747, "stable_key": "clu_b43d009954621979", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "liver", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'fegato' clearly refers to the liver. 'Fesso' is less certain in this context but could be a variant spelling or refer to the anus. Given the prevalence of 'fegato', the primary referent is liver."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Higado", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1806, "target_cluster_name": "Higado", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Higado is the Spanish word for liver.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Higado is the Spanish word for liver."}, {"found_name": "Bile", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 454, "target_cluster_name": "bilis", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Bile is produced by the liver.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Bile is produced by the liver."}, {"found_name": "Blood", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1488, "target_cluster_name": "sangue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The liver is a major organ involved in blood processing and detoxification.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The liver is a major organ involved in blood processing and detoxification."}, {"found_name": "Internal organs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1428, "target_cluster_name": "internal organ", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The liver is a key internal organ.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The liver is a key internal organ."}, {"found_name": "Organs", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 141, "target_cluster_name": "organs", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The liver is an organ.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The liver is an organ."}]}, {"canonical_name": "astringente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "astringente", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "astringente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["astringente"], "contexts": ["property of alum", "strong property of round alum"]}, {"entity_id": "astringente", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "astringente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["astringente"], "contexts": ["quality of food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "astringente", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "astringente", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3748, "stable_key": "clu_3add99b2e75344b1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astringent", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A substance that causes tissues to contract or tighten.", "wikipedia_extract": "An astringent (sometimes called adstringent) is a chemical that shrinks or constricts body tissues. The word derives from the Latin adstringere, which means \"to bind fast\". Astringency, the dry, puckering or numbing mouthfeel caused by the tannins in unripe fruits, lets the fruit mature by deterring eating. Tannins, being a kind of polyphenol, bind salivary proteins and make them precipitate and aggregate, producing a rough, \"sandpapery\", or dry sensation in the mouth. Smoking tobacco is also reported to have an astringent effect. In a scientific study, astringency was still detectable by subjects who had local anesthesia applied to their taste nerves, but not when both these and the trigeminal nerves were disabled."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cartamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cartamo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cartamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cartamo", "cartamo"], "contexts": ["a plant with seeds difficult to separate", "safflower herb"]}, {"entity_id": "cartamo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cartamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cartamo"], "contexts": ["safflower, seed used in preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Cartamo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cartamo", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 3749, "stable_key": "clu_3e8b365021f27e4e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "safflower", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Carthamus tinctorius L.", "family": "Asteraceae", "wikidata_id": "Q156625", "wikidata_description": "species of plant, safflower"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mel", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1544, "target_cluster_name": "mel", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "em quatro, ou cinco partes , fem tocar nas chagas , & logo fetoquemcom eftaagua , que he admiravel. Tomem de vinho branco fem geflo hum quartilho ,de mel duas onças &meya , em panela vidrada , ferva a", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for verdete", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Tito", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "tito", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Tito", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tito"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "tito", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tito", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tito"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor", "an Emperor who made remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "titius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Titius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Titius"], "contexts": ["mythological figure punished in Hades"]}, {"entity_id": "titus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Titus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Titus"], "contexts": ["person associated with a house"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Tito", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tito", "similarity": 0.879}], "id": 3750, "stable_key": "clu_565faa93e563e53c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Titus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 39, "death_year": 81, "description": "Roman Emperor, known for the Flavian dynasty.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The identification of 'Titius' and 'Titus' is ambiguous. Titus is a known historical figure and a common Roman name. Titius could refer to a lesser-known figure or be an error."}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "balfamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "balfamo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "balfamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["balfamo"], "contexts": ["ingredient in Mumia"]}, {"entity_id": "balfamo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "balfamo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["balfamo"], "contexts": ["prepared substance", "plant-based substance used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "balfamo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "balfamo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3751, "stable_key": "clu_2c6163bb32df58d2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'balfamo' (or 'balsamo') in early modern texts could refer to various aromatic resins or plants known for their fragrant properties. Without more specific context, it's difficult to pinpoint a single species. It might refer to plants like *Commiphora* species (myrrh/frankincense) or even specific preparations derived from them.", "wikidata_id": "Q428750", "wikidata_description": "species of plant in the family Cucurbitaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Momordica%20charantia", "wikipedia_extract": "Momordica charantia (commonly called bitter melon, gouya,  cerassee, goya, bitter apple, bitter gourd, bitter squash, balsam-pear, karela, karavila and many more names listed below) is a tropical and subtropical vine of the family Cucurbitaceae, widely grown in Asia, Africa, and the Caribbean for its edible fruit. Its many varieties differ substantially in the shape and bitterness of the fruit. Bitter melon originated in Africa, where it was a dry-season staple food of ǃKung hunter-gatherers. Wild or semi-domesticated variants spread across Asia in prehistory, and it was likely fully domesticated in Southeast Asia. It is widely used in the cuisines of East Asia, South Asia, and Southeast Asia. This herbaceous, tendril-bearing vine grows up to 5 m (16 ft) in length."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "estoraque", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2873, "target_cluster_name": "estoraque", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Storax is a natural fragrant resin, and 'Balsam' is a related category of fragrant resins.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Storax is a natural fragrant resin, and 'Balsam' is a related category of fragrant resins.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Rosewater", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 292, "target_cluster_name": "Rosewater", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Rosewater is often prepared using balsam and rose petals, and historically, balsam was a common ingredient in perfumery and medicinal preparations involving rose derivatives.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Rosewater is often prepared using balsam and rose petals, and historically, balsam was a common ingredient in perfumery and medicinal preparations involving rose derivatives."}]}, {"canonical_name": "opoponaco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "opopo_naco", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "opopo naco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["opopo naco"], "contexts": ["plant infused in wine"]}, {"entity_id": "opoponaco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "opoponaco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["opoponaco"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a fomentation", "ingredient in electuary"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "opopo naco", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "opoponaco", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3752, "stable_key": "clu_ad12d45ba6969ae5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Opoponax", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Opoponax chironium", "family": "Apiaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q13560912", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opopanax%20chironium", "wikipedia_extract": "Opopanax chironium, common name Hercules' all-heal, is a herb of the family Apiaceae. Opopanax chironium grows 1–3 metres (3 feet 3 inches – 9 feet 10 inches) high. This perennial herb has a branching stem, thick and rough close to the base. Leaves are serrate, pinnate, with long petioles. It produces a large, flat, yellow inflorescence at the top of the branches. The plant thrives in warm climates like Iran, Italy, Greece and Turkey, but also grows in cooler climates."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Elixir vitæ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "elixir_vita", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Elixir vita", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elixir vita"], "contexts": ["medicinal preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "elixir_vitæ", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Elixir vitæ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Elixir vitæ", "Elixir vita"], "contexts": ["medicinal elixir, likely elixir of life", "elixir of life, a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Elixir vita", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Elixir vitæ", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3753, "stable_key": "clu_6852e514079bf48a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elixir Vitae", "confidence": "high", "type": "preparation"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ipericaó", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "iperico", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Iperico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Iperico"], "contexts": ["herb oil"]}, {"entity_id": "ipericaó", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ipericaó", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["ipericaó", "Ipericão"], "contexts": ["herb used in preparation", "St. John's wort"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Iperico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ipericaó", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 3754, "stable_key": "clu_d963abf0802052d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "St. John's Wort", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Hypericum perforatum", "family": "Hypericaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q158289", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypericum%20perforatum", "wikipedia_extract": "Hypericum perforatum, commonly known as St. John's wort (sometimes perforate St. John's wort or common St. John's wort), is a flowering plant in the family Hypericaceae. It is a hairless, perennial herb with woody roots, yellow flowers marked by black glands, and leaves that appear perforated due to translucent glands, producing thousands of seeds per plant. H. perforatum is the type species of its genus, known for its historical use in folklore and traditional medicine."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Sulphur", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 313, "target_cluster_name": "Sulphur", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "together do signifie nothing, not only in the dictionary of man, but the subtiler vocabulary of Satan. That there is any power in _Bitumen_, Pitch, or Brimstone, to purifie the air from his uncleannes", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: an alternative name for Hipericon", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Root", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 536, "target_cluster_name": "Root", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "there is in _Hipericon_ [SN: St. Johns _Wort, so called by Magicians_.] to make good the name of _fuga Dæmonis_, any such Magick as is ascribed unto the Root _Baaras_ by _Josephus_, or _Cynospastus_ b", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: vernacular name for Hipericon", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "epithimo", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 616, "target_cluster_name": "epithimo", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cuscuta epithymum (epithimo) is known to parasitize St. John's Wort (Hypericum perforatum), creating a direct ecological and historical relationship between the two plants.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cuscuta epithymum (epithimo) is known to parasitize St. John's Wort (Hypericum perforatum), creating a direct ecological and historical relationship between the two plants.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "pneumonia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pneumonia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pneumonia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["pneumonia", "Pulmonia"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the lungs", "pneumonia, an acute lung inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "pneumonia", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pneumonia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pneumonia"], "contexts": ["lung inflammation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "pneumonia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pneumonia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3755, "stable_key": "clu_3c148e2d06fdf97e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pneumonia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Pneumonia", "wikidata_id": "Q32540", "wikidata_description": "disease in which an eosinophil, a type of white blood cell, accumulates in the lung", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eosinophilic%20pneumonia", "wikipedia_extract": "Pneumonia is an inflammatory condition of the lung primarily affecting the small air sacs known as alveoli. Symptoms typically include some combination of productive or dry cough, chest pain, fever, and difficulty breathing. The severity of the condition is variable. Pneumonia is usually caused by infection with viruses or bacteria, and less commonly by other microorganisms. Identifying the responsible pathogen can be difficult. Diagnosis is often based on symptoms and physical examination."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "consumption", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 73, "target_cluster_name": "consumption", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs. While distinct, it shares symptoms with and can be a complication of phthisis.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs. While distinct, it shares symptoms with and can be a complication of phthisis.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Pleurisy", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 188, "target_cluster_name": "Pleurisy", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, and can lead to pleurisy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura, the membranes surrounding the lungs. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, and can lead to pleurisy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cough", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1561, "target_cluster_name": "Cough", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, frequently causing a cough, often with phlegm or pus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs, frequently causing a cough, often with phlegm or pus.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "causas exteriores", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "causas_exteriores", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "causas exteriores", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["causas exteriores", "causas interiores"], "contexts": ["external causes", "internal causes"]}, {"entity_id": "foreign_cause", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "foreign cause", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["foreign cause"], "contexts": ["external influence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "causas exteriores", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "foreign cause", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3756, "stable_key": "clu_2d45c7837c96a4c1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "External causes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5422022", "wikidata_description": "reason for the existence of a medical condition which can be associated with a specific object or acute process that was caused by something outside the body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/External%20cause", "wikipedia_extract": "In medicine, an external cause is a reason for the existence of a medical condition which can be associated with a specific object or acute process that was caused by something outside the body. Such causes are classified as \"E codes\" in ICD 9. External Cause of Injury Codes (E codes) are ICD-9-CM codes or ICD-10 codes that are used to define the mechanism of death or injury, along with the place of occurrence of the event. [1] E codes are assigned on death certificates based on the manner of death. ICD-10 codes in the range V01–X59 refer to unintentional injuries. Codes in the range X60–X84 refer to intentional self-harm."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "inflammations", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "inflammations", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "inflammations", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["inflammations"], "contexts": ["inflammation of lungs", "inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "œdematous_inflammations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "œdematous inflammations", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["œdematous inflammations"], "contexts": ["swelling due to fluid accumulation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "inflammations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "œdematous inflammations", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 3757, "stable_key": "clu_8b7e43e7212a4a90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inflammation", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Inflammation"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Catholicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "catholicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Catholicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Catholicos"], "contexts": ["Catholics who confess", "religious group"]}, {"entity_id": "catholic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Catholic", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Catholic"], "contexts": ["ancestors who believed in church building for sins"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Catholicos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Catholic", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3758, "stable_key": "clu_0091e74358cc07bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Catholicism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Catholicos' and 'Catholic' in these contexts refer to adherents and the broader religious system of Catholicism, rather than a specific individual.", "wikidata_id": "Q9592", "wikidata_description": "largest Christian Church, made up of 24 autonomous Churches in communion with the Pope, part of the Latin Church aka Latin Christianity aka western Christianity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic%20Church", "wikipedia_extract": "The Catholic Church (Latin: Ecclesia Catholica), commonly known as the Roman Catholic Church, is the largest Christian denomination, with an estimated 1.27 to 1.41 billion baptized members worldwide as of 2025. One of the world's oldest continuously functioning institutions, it has played a formative role in the development of Western civilization. The Church consists of 24 sui iuris (autonomous) churches—the Latin Church and 23 Eastern Catholic Churches—which together encompass nearly 3,500 dioceses and eparchies governed by bishops. The pope, as bishop of Rome, serves as the Church's chief pastor. Catholic doctrine is rooted in the Nicene Creed. The Church teaches that it is the \"one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church\" founded by Jesus Christ in the Great Commission; that its bishops are the successors of the apostles; and that the pope is the successor of Saint Peter, to whom Christ entrusted a unique pastoral role."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Catholico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "catholico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Catholico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Catholico"], "contexts": ["religious affiliation"]}, {"entity_id": "roman_catholic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Roman Catholic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Roman Catholic"], "contexts": ["religious denomination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Catholico", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Roman Catholic", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3759, "stable_key": "clu_82efbd2c34240f28", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Catholicism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Similar to cluster 4238, 'Catholico' and 'Roman Catholic' refer to the religious affiliation and denomination of Catholicism.", "wikidata_id": "Q9592", "wikidata_description": "largest Christian Church, made up of 24 autonomous Churches in communion with the Pope, part of the Latin Church aka Latin Christianity aka western Christianity"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Anatomicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "anatomicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Anatomicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anatomicos"], "contexts": ["scholars who study anatomy", "physicians who perform anatomy"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomist", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "anatomist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["anatomist"], "contexts": ["expert in anatomy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Anatomicos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anatomist", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 3760, "stable_key": "clu_b351794f8e374e6d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anatomist", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A scholar or expert who studies and practices anatomy.", "wikidata_id": "Q10872101", "wikidata_description": "scientist with a specialty in anatomy", "wikipedia_extract": "Anatomy (from Ancient Greek  ἀνατομή (anatomḗ) 'dissection') is the branch of morphology concerned with the study of the internal and external structure of organisms and their parts. Anatomy is a branch of natural science that deals with the structural organization of living things. It is an old science, having its beginnings in prehistoric times. Anatomy is inherently tied to developmental biology, embryology, comparative anatomy, evolutionary biology, and phylogeny, as these are the processes by which anatomy is generated, both over immediate and long-term timescales. Anatomy and physiology, which study the structure and function of organisms and their parts respectively, make a natural pair of related disciplines, and are often studied together. Human anatomy is one of the essential basic sciences that are applied in medicine, and is often studied alongside physiology.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatomiste"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Astronomers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 901, "target_cluster_name": "Astronomers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anatomists are scientists who study the structure of living things, a field that can overlap with the observational and analytical aspects of astronomy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anatomists are scientists who study the structure of living things, a field that can overlap with the observational and analytical aspects of astronomy.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Andreas", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1395, "target_cluster_name": "Andreas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Andreas Vesalius is one of the most famous anatomists in history, and this term broadly categorizes individuals in his field.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Andreas Vesalius is one of the most famous anatomists in history, and this term broadly categorizes individuals in his field.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Praticos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2056, "target_cluster_name": "Praticos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Anatomists are medical practitioners who specialize in the study of anatomy, a core component of medical practice.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Anatomists are medical practitioners who specialize in the study of anatomy, a core component of medical practice.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hemisphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hemisphere", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hemisphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["hemisphere", "Hemisphere"], "contexts": ["half of a sphere", "cause of excess cold in southern"]}, {"entity_id": "southern_hemisphere", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "southern hemisphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["southern hemisphere"], "contexts": ["lower half of a planet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Hemisphere", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "southern hemisphere", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 3761, "stable_key": "clu_c130d45672bfc40a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hemisphere", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context describes geographical hemispheres and anatomical hemispheres. 'Hemisphere' is a general term applicable to both.", "wikidata_id": "Q399984", "wikidata_description": "bipartite divisions of Earth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemispheres%20of%20Earth", "wikipedia_extract": "In geography and cartography, hemispheres of Earth are any division of the globe into two equal halves (hemispheres), typically divided into northern and southern halves by the Equator and into western and eastern halves by the Prime meridian. Hemispheres can be divided geographically or culturally, or based on religion or prominent geographic features. Use of these divisions is applied when studying Earth's geographic distribution, cultural differences, and other geographic, demographic and socioeconomic features. Geographical hemispheres are primarily split by latitudinal (north-south) and longitudinal (east-west) markers: Alternative Earth hemispheres can divide the globe along cultural or religious lines, or be used to maximize the prominence of geographic features. For example: Geographical feature-based hemispheres Alternative hemisphere schemes can divide the planet in a way that maximizes the prominence of one geographic feature or another in each division, such as the land-water division: Land Hemisphere: Centered near 47°N, 1°E, near the city of Nantes, France, this hemisphere contains the largest possible area of land, including most of the world's continents and major landmasses. Water Hemisphere: Centered near 47°S, 179°W, in the South Pacific Ocean, this hemisphere contains the largest possible area of water, including most of the Indian, Pacific, and Southern Oceans."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "rays of light", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rays_of_light", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "rays of light", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["rays of light"], "contexts": ["electromagnetic radiation", "components of light"]}, {"entity_id": "rays_of_light", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rays of light", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rays of light"], "contexts": ["de-oxidized carbonic acid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "rays of light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rays of light", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3762, "stable_key": "clu_e9b64662602134eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Light rays", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context clearly refers to the propagation of light, which in early modern science was often conceptualized as rays. The mention of 'de-oxidized carbonic acid' might hint at chemical reactions involving light, but the core concept is light propagation."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "definite proportions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "definite_proportions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "definite proportions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["definite proportions", "Definite Proportions"], "contexts": ["law of chemical combination", "law of fixed ratios in chemical compounds"]}, {"entity_id": "definite_proportions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "definite proportions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["definite proportions"], "contexts": ["law of chemical combination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "definite proportions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "definite proportions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3763, "stable_key": "clu_e71bb0ea9838a69c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Law of Definite Proportions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q327158", "wikidata_description": "statement that a given chemical compound always contains its elements in fixed ratio (by mass) and does not depend on its source and method of preparation", "wikipedia_extract": "In chemistry, the whole number rule states that the masses of the isotopes are whole number multiples of the mass of the hydrogen atom. The rule is a modified version of Prout's hypothesis proposed in 1815, to the effect that atomic weights are multiples of the weight of the hydrogen atom. It is also known as the Aston whole number rule after Francis W. Aston who was awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1922 \"for his discovery, by means of his mass spectrograph, of isotopes, in a large number of non-radioactive elements, and for his enunciation of the whole-number rule\". The law of definite proportions was formulated by Joseph Proust around 1800 and states that all samples of a chemical compound will have the same elemental composition by mass. The atomic theory of John Dalton expanded this concept and explained matter as consisting of discrete atoms with one kind of atom for each element combined in fixed proportions to form compounds.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law_of_definite_proportions"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Atomic constitution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "atomic_constitution", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Atomic constitution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["atomic constitution", "Atomic constitution"], "contexts": ["internal arrangement of atoms", "arrangement of atoms in a substance"]}, {"entity_id": "atomic_arrangement", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Atomic arrangement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Atomic arrangement"], "contexts": ["changed by electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Atomic constitution", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Atomic arrangement", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 3764, "stable_key": "clu_a8358596ca27a8d2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atomic structure", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms 'Atomic constitution' and 'Atomic arrangement' clearly refer to the internal organization of atoms, which is the modern concept of atomic structure.", "wikidata_id": "Q12355387", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "chemical composition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 449, "target_cluster_name": "chemical composition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Atomic structure directly influences and defines the chemical composition of elements and compounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Atomic structure directly influences and defines the chemical composition of elements and compounds.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cornwall", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "cornwall", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cornwall", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cornwall"], "contexts": ["county of Dalcoath mine", "location of hot-springs in mines"]}, {"entity_id": "cornwall", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Cornwall", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cornwall"], "contexts": ["Cornishmen take to mining because Cornwall is metalliferous"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Cornwall", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cornwall", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3765, "stable_key": "clu_0c23a995d0195f60", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cornwall", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornwall", "wikipedia_extract": "Cornwall (; Cornish: Kernow [ˈkɛrnɔʊ] or [ˈkɛrnɔ]) is a ceremonial county in South West England. It is one of the Celtic nations and the homeland of the Cornish people. The county is bordered by the Atlantic Ocean to the north and west, Devon to the east, and the English Channel to the south. The largest urban area is the Redruth and Camborne conurbation. The county is predominantly rural, with an area of 1,375 square miles (3,562 km2) and an estimated population of 585,655 in 2024. The county's major settlements include the city of Truro and St Austell in the centre, Redruth and Cambourne adjacent to each other in the south, and Penzance and Falmouth on the southern coast."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dynamic theory of heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "dynamic_theory_of_heat", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "dynamic theory of heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dynamic Theory of Heat", "dynamic theory of heat"], "contexts": ["heat as a form of energy", "heat as molecular motion"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_theory_of_heat", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical theory of heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical theory of heat"], "contexts": ["theory relating heat and mechanical work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "dynamic theory of heat", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical theory of heat", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 3766, "stable_key": "clu_f485994685581302", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kinetic theory of heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms 'dynamic theory of heat' and 'mechanical theory of heat' are early descriptions of the modern kinetic theory of heat, which posits heat as a form of energy related to the motion of particles."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bar magnets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bar_magnets", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "bar magnets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["bar magnets"], "contexts": ["magnets used in experiment", "magnets of same length, size, intensity"]}, {"entity_id": "magnets", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "magnets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnets"], "contexts": ["objects with magnetic properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "bar magnets", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "magnets", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 3767, "stable_key": "clu_ece06f0950005099", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bar magnet", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q29645971", "wikidata_description": "a rod of uniformely magnetized material with two opposite magnetic poles at its ends"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pole of the earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pole_of_the_earth", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "pole of the earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["pole of the earth"], "contexts": ["axis of Earth's rotation", "rotational axis of the earth"]}, {"entity_id": "pole", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pole", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["pole"], "contexts": ["transfer of matter in battery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "pole of the earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pole", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3768, "stable_key": "clu_e0a72685fabf93ca", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's magnetic poles", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'axis of Earth's rotation' suggests the geographic poles, while 'transfer of matter in battery' points towards magnetic poles. Given the physical science context, magnetic poles are more likely."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "pole", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2352, "target_cluster_name": "pole", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity 'Pole' can refer to the magnetic poles of the Earth, which are also referred to as 'Earth's magnetic poles'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The entity 'Pole' can refer to the magnetic poles of the Earth, which are also referred to as 'Earth's magnetic poles'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "magnets", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 822, "target_cluster_name": "magnets", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Magnets possess poles, and the Earth's magnetic field is characterized by magnetic poles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Magnets possess poles, and the Earth's magnetic field is characterized by magnetic poles.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "north pole", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2136, "target_cluster_name": "north pole", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The North Pole is intrinsically linked to the Earth's magnetic field and its magnetic poles, which were a subject of significant scientific inquiry and debate during the historical period.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The North Pole is intrinsically linked to the Earth's magnetic field and its magnetic poles, which were a subject of significant scientific inquiry and debate during the historical period.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "double star", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "double_star", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "double star", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["double star"], "contexts": ["eclipse observed by Admiral Smyth", "astronomical object"]}, {"entity_id": "double_stars", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Double stars", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Double stars"], "contexts": ["two stars orbiting each other"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "double star", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Double stars", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 3769, "stable_key": "clu_5f5fe05732f37589", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Binary star", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q50053", "wikidata_description": "star system consisting of two stars rotating around a common center of mass"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "etherial medium", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "etherial_medium", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "etherial medium", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["etherial medium"], "contexts": ["hypothesized medium for light propagation"]}, {"entity_id": "etherial_medium", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "etherial medium", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["etherial medium"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium filling space", "medium for undulations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "etherial medium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "etherial medium", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3770, "stable_key": "clu_4e9dbfca97b0ac74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Luminiferous aether", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q208702", "wikidata_description": "postulated medium for the propagation of light", "wikipedia_extract": "Luminiferous aether or ether (luminiferous meaning 'light-bearing') is the formerly postulated medium for the propagation of light. It was invoked to explain the ability of the apparently wave-based light to propagate through empty space (a vacuum), something that waves should not be able to do. The assumption of a spatial plenum (space completely filled with matter) of luminiferous aether, rather than a spatial vacuum, provided the theoretical medium that was required by wave theories of light. The aether hypothesis was the topic of considerable debate throughout its history, as it required the existence of an invisible and infinite material with no interaction with physical objects. As the nature of light was explored, especially in the 19th century, the physical qualities required of an aether became increasingly contradictory. By the late 19th century, the existence of the aether was being questioned, although there was no physical theory to replace it.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luminiferous_aether"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Air", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 109, "target_cluster_name": "air", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was conceived as a medium filling all space, including the atmosphere, and was often discussed in relation to the properties of air and other gases.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The luminiferous aether was conceived as a medium filling all space, including the atmosphere, and was often discussed in relation to the properties of air and other gases."}, {"found_name": "Atmosphere", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 213, "target_cluster_name": "atmosphere", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was thought to permeate the Earth's atmosphere and was sometimes conflated with or discussed in relation to atmospheric phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The luminiferous aether was thought to permeate the Earth's atmosphere and was sometimes conflated with or discussed in relation to atmospheric phenomena."}, {"found_name": "Fluids", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 393, "target_cluster_name": "fluids", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was often described as a fluid, albeit a very subtle and pervasive one, and its properties were sometimes compared to those of other fluids.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The luminiferous aether was often described as a fluid, albeit a very subtle and pervasive one, and its properties were sometimes compared to those of other fluids."}, {"found_name": "Gaseous substances", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2321, "target_cluster_name": "gaseous", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was often conceptualized as a highly rarefied, gas-like substance, and its properties were sometimes discussed in analogy to gases.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The luminiferous aether was often conceptualized as a highly rarefied, gas-like substance, and its properties were sometimes discussed in analogy to gases."}, {"found_name": "Medium", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1133, "target_cluster_name": "medium", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The luminiferous aether was fundamentally understood as a medium through which light propagated, making 'medium' a core conceptual relation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The luminiferous aether was fundamentally understood as a medium through which light propagated, making 'medium' a core conceptual relation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "physical geographers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "physical_geographers", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "physical geographers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["physical geographers"], "contexts": ["divided globe into botanical districts"]}, {"entity_id": "geographers", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Geographers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geographers"], "contexts": ["lunar motions important to them"]}, {"entity_id": "geographers", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "geographers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["geographers"], "contexts": ["disciplines studying Earth's surface"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "physical geographers", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "geographers", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Geographers", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "geographers", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 3771, "stable_key": "clu_a6cd4545dc305ca6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Geographers", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scholars studying Earth's surface and its features.", "wikidata_id": "Q901402", "wikidata_description": "scholar whose area of study is geography", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geographer", "wikipedia_extract": "A geographer is a physical scientist, social scientist or humanist whose area of study is geography, the study of Earth's natural environment and human society, including how society and nature interact. The Greek prefix \"geo\" means \"earth\" and the Greek suffix, \"graphy\", meaning \"description\", so a geographer is someone who studies the earth. The word \"geography\" is a Middle French word that is believed to have been first used in 1540. Although geographers are historically known as people who make maps, map making is actually the field of study of cartography, a subset of geography. Geographers do not study only the details of the natural environment or human society, but they also study the reciprocal relationship between these two. For example, they study how the natural environment contributes to human society and how human society affects the natural environment."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "physiciens", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 692, "target_cluster_name": "physiciens", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Many early physicists were also involved in geography, particularly in areas like geodesy, cartography, and the study of natural phenomena that informed geographical understanding.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Many early physicists were also involved in geography, particularly in areas like geodesy, cartography, and the study of natural phenomena that informed geographical understanding.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Astronomers", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 901, "target_cluster_name": "Astronomers", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geographers study the Earth's surface, including its physical features and human populations. Early astronomers often had strong ties to geography, particularly in terms of navigation and mapping.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Geographers study the Earth's surface, including its physical features and human populations. Early astronomers often had strong ties to geography, particularly in terms of navigation and mapping.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "geologists", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 956, "target_cluster_name": "geologists", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geographers and geologists study aspects of the Earth, with significant overlap in their methodologies and areas of inquiry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Geographers and geologists study aspects of the Earth, with significant overlap in their methodologies and areas of inquiry.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "observer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2793, "target_cluster_name": "observer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geographers observe and describe the Earth's features and phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Geographers observe and describe the Earth's features and phenomena.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "locality", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "localities", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "localities", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["localities"], "contexts": ["where venomous snakes are specifically different"]}, {"entity_id": "locality", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "locality", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["locality"], "contexts": ["specific area", 