PT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["indestructibility of Matter"], "contexts": ["principle that matter cannot be created or destroyed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "indestructibility of matter", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "indestructibility of Matter", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 3778, "stable_key": "clu_8d046a04c883fc41", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conservation of Mass", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "In physics and chemistry, the law of conservation of mass or principle of mass conservation states that for any system which is closed to all incoming and outgoing transfers of matter, the mass of the system must remain constant over time. The law implies that mass can neither be created nor destroyed, although it may be rearranged in space, or the entities associated with it may be changed in form. For example, in chemical reactions, the mass of the chemical components before the reaction is equal to the mass of the components after the reaction. Thus, during any chemical reaction and low-energy thermodynamic processes in an isolated system, the total mass of the reactants, or starting materials, must be equal to the mass of the products. The concept of mass conservation is widely used in many fields such as chemistry, mechanics, and fluid dynamics. Historically, mass conservation in chemical reactions was primarily demonstrated in the 17th century and finally confirmed by Antoine Lavoisier in the late 18th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Chemistry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 304, "target_cluster_name": "Chemistry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The conservation of mass is a fundamental principle in chemistry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The conservation of mass is a 