], "contexts": ["events causing rivers to flow into each other"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Flood", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "floods", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3783, "stable_key": "clu_f7ea798e65ca42ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flood", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flood", "wikipedia_extract": "A flood is an overflow of water (or rarely other fluids) that submerges land that is usually dry. In the sense of \"flowing water\", the word may also be applied to the inflow of the tide. Floods are of significant concern in agriculture, civil engineering and public health. Human changes to the environment often increase the intensity and frequency of flooding. Examples for human changes are land use changes such as deforestation and removal of wetlands, changes in waterway course or flood controls such as with levees. Global environmental issues also influence causes of floods, namely climate change which causes an intensification of the water cycle and sea level rise."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sound", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 258, "target_cluster_name": "sound", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A flood is a large amount of water, often overflowing its usual boundaries. A sound is a body of water, and floods can occur within or affect sounds.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A flood is a large amount of water, often overflowing its usual boundaries. A sound is a body of water, and floods can occur within or affect sounds.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "destruction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2527, "target_cluster_name": "destruction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A flood is a natural disaster involving widespread destruction of property and life.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A flood is a natural disaster involving widespread destruction of property and life.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "verities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "verities", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "verities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["verities"], "contexts": ["causes which determine their verities"]}, {"entity_id": "truths", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Truths", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Truths"], "contexts": ["held forth by the Gospel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "verities", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Truths", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3784, "stable_key": "clu_93f1243e6d74848d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Truths", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "utility", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Utility"], "contexts": ["usefulness or practical value"]}, {"entity_id": "utility", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Utility"], "contexts": ["importance in construction of parts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Utility", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Utility", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3785, "stable_key": "clu_4b782479b578dc27", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Utility", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1951366", "wikidata_description": "organization that maintains the infrastructure for a public service"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "visibilities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "visibilities", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "visibilities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["visibilities"], "contexts": ["things that can be seen"]}, {"entity_id": "visibility", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "visibility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["visibility"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "visibilities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "visibility", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 3786, "stable_key": "clu_5cd285e852a88fa7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Visibility", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q30301666", "wikidata_description": "condition of being visible; state of an object that can be seen"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "wisdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wisdom", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "wisdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wisdom"], "contexts": ["knowledge and good judgment"]}, {"entity_id": "sagesse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sagesse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sagesse"], "contexts": ["quality of being wise"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wisdom", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sagesse", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 3787, "stable_key": "clu_07823ff636a9930b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "wisdom", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisdom", "wikipedia_extract": "Wisdom, also known as sapience, is the ability to apply knowledge, experience, and good judgment to navigate life's complexities. It is often associated with insight, discernment, and ethics in decision-making. Throughout history, wisdom has been regarded as a key virtue in philosophy, religion, and psychology, representing the ability to understand and respond to reality in a balanced and thoughtful manner. Unlike intelligence, which primarily concerns problem-solving and reasoning, wisdom involves a deeper comprehension of human nature, moral principles, and the long-term consequences of actions. Philosophically, wisdom has been explored by thinkers from Ancient Greece to modern times. Socrates famously equated wisdom with recognizing one's own ignorance, while Aristotle saw it as practical reasoning (phronesis) and deep contemplation (sophia)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Doctrine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "doctrine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Doctrine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Doctrine"], "contexts": ["a set of beliefs held by a group"]}, {"entity_id": "doctrine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "doctrine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctrine"], "contexts": ["a belief or set of beliefs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Doctrine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "doctrine", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 3788, "stable_key": "clu_fbef3c8c0a1ea165", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "doctrine", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Syndrome", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "disposition conforming unto contiguity, an union of one Magnetical Body with another, and no violent haling of the weak unto the stronger. And this is also the doctrine of _Gilbertus_, by whom this mo", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for concourse"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "calf", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Calf"], "contexts": ["idol made from ear-rings"]}, {"entity_id": "calf", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["calf"], "contexts": ["young bovine animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Calf", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "calf", "similarity": 0.757}], "id": 3789, "stable_key": "clu_e22a42bea87eb478", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "calf", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cattle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 101, "target_cluster_name": "cattle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Calf is a young bovine, a type of cattle.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": 