, "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychological misapprehension"], "contexts": ["error in understanding psychological phenomena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Misapprehension", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "psychological misapprehension", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 3791, "stable_key": "clu_66abc61cb82c7412", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Misconception", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q25345174", "wikidata_description": "statement popularly believed but which is known to be false in reliable sources"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Deduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "deduction", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Deduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Deduction"], "contexts": ["process of reasoning from general to specific"]}, {"entity_id": "deduction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "deduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["deduction"], "contexts": ["reasoning from general to specific"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deduction", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "deduction", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 3792, "stable_key": "clu_bf45e550ed7ba74c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Deductive reasoning", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mathematics", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 161, "target_cluster_name": "mathematics", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Deductive reasoning is a primary method of proof and logical inference in mathematics, where conclusions are derived from established premises.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Deductive reasoning is a primary method of proof and logical inference in mathematics, where conclusions are derived from established premises.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "reasoning", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1684, "target_cluster_name": "reasoning", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Deductive reasoning is a specific form of reasoning, where conclusions are logically derived from premises.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Deductive reasoning is a specific form of reasoning, where conclusions are logically derived from premises.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "evil", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["evil"], "contexts": ["morally wrong and undesirable"]}, {"entity_id": "evil", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Evil"], "contexts": ["moral or spiritual badness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "evil", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Evil", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 3793, "stable_key": "clu_d2f79626aa033e54", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Evil", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q15292", "wikidata_description": "profound immorality"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "spirit", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 476, "target_cluster_name": "spirit", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In many theological and philosophical frameworks, 'Evil' is conceptualized as a force or principle that stands in opposition to 'Spirit' or the divine.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In many theological and philosophical frameworks, 'Evil' is conceptualized as a force or principle that stands in opposition to 'Spirit' or the divine.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "pleasure", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 384, "target_cluster_name": "pleasure", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evil is often understood as the opposite of good, and by extension, the opposite of pleasure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Evil is often understood as the opposite of good, and by extension, the opposite of pleasure.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "demonio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 477, "target_cluster_name": "demonio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A demon is a type of evil supernatural entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A demon is a type of evil supernatural entity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "guerras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 483, "target_cluster_name": "guerras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "War is often considered an manifestation of evil or a cause of great suffering and destruction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: War is often considered an manifestation of evil or a cause of great suffering and destruction.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "soul", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 578, "target_cluster_name": "soul", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The soul was often seen as the battleground between good and evil, or as capable of succumbing to or resisting evil influences, making 'Evil' a conceptually related entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The soul was often seen as the battleground between good and evil, or as capable of succumbing to or resisting evil influences, making 'Evil' a conceptually related entity.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Atheism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 993, "target_cluster_name": "Atheism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In some theological frameworks, atheism is associated with or seen as a source of evil.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In some theological frameworks, atheism is associated with or seen as a source of evil.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Idolatry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2662, "target_cluster_name": "Idolatry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In monotheistic traditions, idolatry is often considered a form of evil or sin.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In monotheistic traditions, idolatry is often considered a form of evil or sin.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pilate", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pilate"], "contexts": ["received accusation against the Saviour"]}, {"entity_id": "pilate", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pilate"], "contexts": ["character in procession"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pilate", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Pilate", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3794, "stable_key": "clu_e070709737b17f4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pontius Pilate", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman governor of Judea, historical and religious figure.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pontius Pilate (Latin: Pontius Pilatus; Greek: Πόντιος Πιλᾶτος, romanized: Póntios Pilátos) was the fifth governor of the Roman province of Judaea, serving under Emperor Tiberius from 26/27 to 36/37 AD. He is best known for being the official who presided over the trial of Jesus and ultimately ordered his crucifixion. Pilate's importance in Christianity is underscored by his prominent place in both the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds. Because the gospels portray Pilate as reluctant to execute Jesus, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church believes that Pilate became a Christian and venerates him as both a martyr and a saint, a belief which was also historically held by the Coptic Church. Pontius Pilate is the best-attested figure to hold the position of Roman governor, though few sources about his rule have survived. Virtually nothing is known about his life prior to becoming governor or the circumstances of his appointment.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontius_Pilate"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "St. John", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2497, "target_cluster_name": "St. John", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Pontius Pilate was the Roman governor who ordered the execution of John the Baptist.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Pontius Pilate was the Roman governor who ordered the execution of John the Baptist.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "belus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Belus"], "contexts": ["ancient deity"]}, {"entity_id": "belus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Belus"], "contexts": ["pyramid of Belus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Belus", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Belus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3795, "stable_key": "clu_dd32f7a62ecd8dbd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Belus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient deity associated with Babylonian pyramids", "note": "Belus is a complex figure, often conflated with Babylonian gods like Marduk or with legendary kings.", "wikidata_id": "Q14950162", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_extract": "Belus or Belos (Ancient Greek: Βῆλος, Bêlos) in classical Greek or classical Latin texts (and later material based on them) in a Babylonian context refers to the Babylonian god Bel Marduk. Though often identified with Greek Zeus and Latin Jupiter as Zeus Belos or Jupiter Belus, in other cases Belus is euhemerized as an ancient king who founded Babylon and built the ziggurat. He is recognized and worshipped as the god of war. Eusebius of Caesarea (Praeparatio Evangelica 9.18) cites Artabanus as stating in his Jewish History that Artabanus found in anonymous works that giants who had been dwelling in Babylonia were destroyed by the gods for impiety, but one of them named Belus escaped and settled in Babylon and lived in the tower which he built and named the Tower of Belus. A little later Eusebius (9.41) cites Abydenus' Concerning the Assyrians for the information that the site of Babylon:... was originally water, and called a sea.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belus_(Babylonian)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Statue", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "statue", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Statue", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Statue"], "contexts": ["sculpted representation"]}, {"entity_id": "sculpture", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sculpture", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sculpture"], "contexts": ["art form of carved material"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Statue", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sculpture", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3796, "stable_key": "clu_0fef6a0db83bcafc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sculpture", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q860861", "wikidata_description": "three-dimensional work of art"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "zeno", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zeno"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher known for paradoxes of motion"]}, {"entity_id": "zeno", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zeno"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher known for paradoxes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Zeno", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Zeno", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3797, "stable_key": "clu_e1b963c456efad35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zeno of Citium", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -334, "death_year": -262, "description": "Greek Stoic philosopher known for paradoxes", "wikidata_id": "Q171303", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher, founder of Stoicism", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno%20of%20Citium", "wikipedia_extract": "Zeno of Citium (; Koine Greek: Ζήνων ὁ Κιτιεύς, Zēnōn ho Kitieus; c. 334 – c. 262 BC) was a Hellenistic philosopher   from Citium (Κίτιον, Kition), Cyprus. He was the founder of the Stoic school of philosophy, which he taught in Athens from about 300 BC. Based on the moral ideas of the Cynics, Stoicism laid great emphasis on goodness and peace of mind gained from living a life of virtue in accordance with nature. It proved very popular, and flourished as one of the major schools of philosophy from the Hellenistic period through to the Roman era, and enjoyed revivals in the Renaissance as Neostoicism and in the current era as Modern Stoicism."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Commonwealths", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "commonwealths", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Commonwealths", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commonwealths"], "contexts": ["political states or communities"]}, {"entity_id": "commonwealth", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Commonwealth", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commonwealth"], "contexts": ["the general public or state"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Commonwealths", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Commonwealth", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 3798, "stable_key": "clu_f757f5c8f011f648", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Commonwealth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q408", "wikidata_description": "country in Oceania"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "New Holland", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1011, "target_cluster_name": "New Holland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "New Holland is an historical name for Australia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "New Holland is an historical name for Australia."}, {"found_name": "unity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 409, "target_cluster_name": "unity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A commonwealth implies a unified political entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A commonwealth implies a unified political entity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "juments", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Juments"], "contexts": ["work animals"]}, {"entity_id": "juments", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["juments"], "contexts": ["female horses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Juments", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "juments", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 3799, "stable_key": "clu_b5b1a81d4408c652", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mares", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The French term 'juments' specifically refers to female horses (mares).", "wikidata_id": "Q726", "wikidata_description": "domesticated four-footed mammal from the equine family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Elian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "elian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Elian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elian"], "contexts": ["Roman writer on animals"]}, {"entity_id": "eliano", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Eliano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Eliano"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek writer"]}, {"entity_id": "ælianus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ælianus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ælianus"], "contexts": ["ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "ælian_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ælian.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ælian."], "contexts": ["ancient author cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elian", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Eliano", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 3800, "stable_key": "clu_a16d623e45319028", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aelian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 170, "death_year": 230, "description": "Roman writer and teacher, known for 'On the Nature of Animals'", "note": "The Spanish 'Eliano' is a direct transliteration/cognate of the Latin 'Aelianus', referring to Claudius Aelianus.", "wikidata_id": "Q313782", "wikidata_description": "Roman author and teacher (c.175–c.235)"}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Dioscorides", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 33, "target_cluster_name": "Dioscorides", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Dioscorides and Aelian were ancient Greek writers whose works on natural history and medicine were highly influential and frequently cited by later scholars.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Dioscorides and Aelian were ancient Greek writers whose works on natural history and medicine were highly influential and frequently cited by later scholars."}, {"found_name": "Galen", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 29, "target_cluster_name": "Galen", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Galen and Aelian were influential figures in ancient Greek medicine and natural history, and their works were foundational for subsequent medical and scientific thought.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Galen and Aelian were influential figures in ancient Greek medicine and natural history, and their works were foundational for subsequent medical and scientific thought."}, {"found_name": "Hippocrates", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 31, "target_cluster_name": "Hippocrates", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both Hippocrates and Aelian were foundational figures in ancient Greek medicine and natural history, whose works were extensively studied and referenced throughout antiquity and beyond.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Both Hippocrates and Aelian were foundational figures in ancient Greek medicine and natural history, whose works were extensively studied and referenced throughout antiquity and beyond."}]}, {"canonical_name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "testimony", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["testimony"], "contexts": ["evidence or statement from a witness"]}, {"entity_id": "testimony", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["testimony"], "contexts": ["statements made by witnesses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testimony", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "testimony", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3801, "stable_key": "clu_42b64c75b1fea448", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "testimony", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1196258", "wikidata_description": "solemn attestation as to the truth of a matter", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testimony", "wikipedia_extract": "Testimony is a solemn attestation as to the truth of a matter. The words \"testimony\" and \"testify\" both derive from the Latin word testis, referring to the notion of a disinterested third-party witness. In the law, testimony is a form of evidence in which a witness makes a \"solemn declaration or affirmation ... for the purpose of establishing or proving some fact\". According to Bryan A. Garner, the editor of Black's Law Dictionary, the word \"testimony\" is properly used as a mass noun (that is, always uninflected regardless of number), and not a count noun."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "CHAPTER VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chapter_viii", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "CHAPTER VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["CHAPTER VIII"], "contexts": ["Section heading"]}, {"entity_id": "section_viii", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "SECTION VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["SECTION VIII"], "contexts": ["heading of a text section"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "CHAPTER VIII", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "SECTION VIII", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 3802, "stable_key": "clu_52a6116950f96832", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "chapter heading", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to section or chapter titles within texts."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Rhodomanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhodomanus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rhodomanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodomanus"], "contexts": ["editor of ancient texts"]}, {"entity_id": "rhode", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rhode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhode"], "contexts": ["author on Lemnian matters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rhodomanus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rhode", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 3803, "stable_key": "clu_54ed351091f8fbfe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christoph Rhode", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Editor of ancient texts, author on Lemnian matters.", "note": "The spelling 'Rhodomanus' is a Latinization. 'Rhode' is likely the same individual, possibly a misreading or variant spelling.", "wikidata_id": "Q94878982", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "trajan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trajan"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "trajan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trajan"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trajan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trajan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3804, "stable_key": "clu_d3e776ec04358621", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trajan", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 53, "death_year": 117, "description": "Roman emperor known for expansion and public works.", "wikidata_id": "Q1425", "wikidata_description": "13th Roman Emperor (98–117)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trajan", "wikipedia_extract": "Trajan ( TRAY-jən; born Marcus Ulpius Traianus, 18 September 53 – c. 9 August 117) was a Roman emperor from AD 98 to 117, remembered as the second of the Five Good Emperors of the Nerva–Antonine dynasty. He was a philanthropic ruler and a successful soldier-emperor who presided over one of the greatest military expansions in Roman history, during which, by the time of his death, the Roman Empire reached its maximum territorial extent. He was given the title of Optimus ('the best') by the Roman Senate. Trajan was born in the municipium of Italica in the present-day Andalusian province of Seville in southern Spain, an Italic settlement in Hispania Baetica; his gens Ulpia came from the town of Tuder in the Umbria region of central Italy. His namesake father, Marcus Ulpius Traianus, was a general and distinguished senator."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Laurentius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "laurentius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Laurentius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laurentius"], "contexts": ["host of a feast"]}, {"entity_id": "laurent", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Laurent", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laurent"], "contexts": ["person associated with a project"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Laurentius", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Laurent", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3805, "stable_key": "clu_53a65fc0943c6d59", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laurentius", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Unidentified individual, possibly a host or collaborator.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Laurentius or Laurent exist. Without more context, identification is uncertain.", "wikidata_id": "Q17590", "wikidata_description": "Christian saint, martyr and a deacon of Rome (225-258)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Philes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "philes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philes"], "contexts": ["author who delivered proprieties of animals in Greek Iambicks"]}, {"entity_id": "philo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Philo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philo"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on imagination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Philo", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3806, "stable_key": "clu_0f07c0834c44b76a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Greek poet and writer, author of 'On the Properties of Animals'.", "note": "The entry mentions both 'Philes' and 'Philo'. Ioannes Philes is the author of 'De Animalium Proprietatibus'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Anglicus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "anglicus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Anglicus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anglicus"], "contexts": ["nickname for Bartholomeus Glanvil"]}, {"entity_id": "angli", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Angli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Angli"], "contexts": ["English people who consume many sweets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anglicus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Angli", "similarity": 0.841}], "id": 3807, "stable_key": "clu_8f122aec72b3a423", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bartholomew Anglicus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Medieval scholar, author of 'De Proprietatibus Rerum'.", "note": "The cluster refers to 'Anglicus' and 'Angli', both pointing to Bartholomew Anglicus and his work, as well as English people."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "salamander", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Salamander"], "contexts": ["creature associated with fire in fables"]}, {"entity_id": "salamander", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["salamander"], "contexts": ["fossil, conjectured antediluvian man"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salamander", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "salamander", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 3808, "stable_key": "clu_601936b891fe14cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salamander", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Early modern texts sometimes conflated salamanders with fossil remains, leading to a 'fossil salamander' concept, but the primary referent remains the amphibian.", "wikidata_id": "Q3469592", "wikidata_description": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "Salamanders are a group of amphibians typically characterized by their lizard-like appearance, with slender bodies, blunt snouts, short limbs projecting at right angles to the body, and the presence of a tail in both larvae and adults. All ten extant salamander families are grouped together under the order Urodela, the sole surviving order from the group Caudata. Urodela is a scientific Latin term based on the Ancient Greek οὐρά δήλη: ourà dēlē \"conspicuous tail\". Caudata is the Latin for \"tailed ones\", from cauda: \"tail\". Salamander diversity is highest in eastern North America, especially in the Appalachian Mountains; most species are found in the Holarctic realm, with some species present in the Neotropical realm. Salamanders never have more than four toes on their front legs and five on their rear legs, but some species have fewer digits and others lack hind limbs.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salamander"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "reptiles", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 122, "target_cluster_name": "reptiles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While modern classification separates amphibians from reptiles, historical texts sometimes grouped salamanders with reptiles due to superficial similarities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While modern classification separates amphibians from reptiles, historical texts sometimes grouped salamanders with reptiles due to superficial similarities.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "frog", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 699, "target_cluster_name": "frog", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both frogs and salamanders are amphibians, often grouped together in premodern texts due to their similar life cycles and habitats.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both frogs and salamanders are amphibians, often grouped together in premodern texts due to their similar life cycles and habitats.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Saurians", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2218, "target_cluster_name": "Saurians", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Salamanders are often grouped with or considered similar to saurian creatures in pre-modern texts, despite modern biological classification.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Salamanders are often grouped with or considered similar to saurian creatures in pre-modern texts, despite modern biological classification.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Ethicks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ethicks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ethicks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ethicks"], "contexts": ["Aristotle's work on moral philosophy"]