: "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Box", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3841, "stable_key": "clu_fffef1e4c245231c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boxwood", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Buxus sempervirens Linnaeus", "family": "Buxaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q27344", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_extract": "Buxus sempervirens, the common box, European box, or boxwood, is a species of flowering plant in the genus Buxus, native to western and southern Europe, northwest Africa, and southwest Asia, from southern England south to northern Morocco, and east through the northern Mediterranean region to Turkey. Buxus colchica of western Caucasus and B. hyrcana of northern Iran and eastern Caucasus are commonly treated as synonyms of B. sempervirens. Buxus sempervirens is an evergreen shrub or small tree growing 1 to 9 m (3 to 30 ft) tall, with a trunk up to 20 centimetres (8 in) in diameter (exceptionally to 10 m tall and 45 cm diameter). Arranged in opposite pairs along the stems, the leaves are green to yellow-green, oval, 1.5–3 cm long, and 0.5–1.3 cm broad.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buxus_sempervirens"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lemnius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lemnius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lemnius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemnius"], "contexts": ["author on minerals and scripture"]}, {"entity_id": "lemnio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lemnio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemnio"], "contexts": ["author cited for plague observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lemnius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lemnio", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3842, "stable_key"