Geometric figures are defined by their similarities and differences in shape and proportion.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "appearance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2433, "target_cluster_name": "appearance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Figure is a component of appearance, particularly in a visual or geometric sense.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Figure is a component of appearance, particularly in a visual or geometric sense.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "triangle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2811, "target_cluster_name": "triangle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A triangle is a specific type of geometric figure.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A triangle is a specific type of geometric figure.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Geometry", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2912, "target_cluster_name": "Geometry", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Geometric figures are the objects of study in geometry.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Geometric figures are the objects of study in geometry.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "infusion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["infusion"], "contexts": ["process of steeping a substance in liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "infusion", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Infusion"], "contexts": ["method of making syrups"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "infusion", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Infusion", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 3850, "stable_key": "clu_4c2a3c9cdd27bb13", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Infusion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A method of preparing liquid medicines by steeping ingredients.", "note": "Includes Italian and English terms for the same medicinal practice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Infusion is the process of extracting chemical compounds or flavors from plant material in a solvent such as water, oil, or alcohol, by allowing the material to remain suspended in the solvent over time (a process often called steeping). An infusion is also the name for the resultant liquid. The process of infusion is distinct from both decoction—a method of extraction involving boiling the plant material—and percolation, in which water is passed through the material (as in a coffeemaker). The first recorded use of essential oils was in the 10th or 11th century by the Persian polymath Avicenna, possibly in The Canon of Medicine. Tea is far older than this, dating back to the 10th century BC as the earliest recorded reference. Infusion is a chemical process that uses botanicals (typically dried herbs, flowers or berries) that are volatile and release their active ingredients readily in water, oil, or alcohol."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fomentaçao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 501, "target_cluster_name": "fomentaçao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Infusions are often used as the liquid base for fomentations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Infusions are often used as the liquid base for fomentations.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alphonsus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alphonsus"], "contexts": ["Duke of Ferrara"]}, {"entity_id": "alphonsus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alphonsus"], "contexts": ["King of Naples"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alphonsus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alphonsus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3851, "stable_key": "clu_3c7ab0ae13532344", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alfonso V of Naples", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1400, "death_year": 1458, "description": "King of Naples, patron of arts and sciences.", "note": "The cluster refers to an 'Alphonsus' who was either Duke of Ferrara or King of Naples. Alfonso V of Naples was a significant figure and patron, fitting the context of historical texts. Alfonso II d'Este, Duke of Ferrara, also existed, but Alfonso V of Naples is more commonly referenced in general historical and scientific discourse of the period."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Egg-shells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "egg-shells", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Egg-shells", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Egg-shells"], "contexts": ["ingredient for dishes"]}, {"entity_id": "egg-shells", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "egg-shells", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["egg-shells"], "contexts": ["fragments preserved with bones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Egg-shells", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "egg-shells", "simi