region along the Nile river, which is located in northern Sudan and southern Egypt", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nubia", "wikipedia_extract": "Nubia ( NEW-bee-uh; Nobiin: Nobīn; Arabic: النُوبَة, romanized: an-Nūba) is a region along the Nile river encompassing the area between the confluence of the Blue and White Niles (near Khartoum in central Sudan) and the First Cataract (south of Aswan in southern Egypt). It was the seat of one of the earliest civilizations of ancient Africa, the Kerma culture, which lasted from around 2500 BC until its conquest by the New Kingdom of Egypt under Pharaoh Thutmose I around 1500 BC. Egyptian heirs subsequently ruled much of Nubia for the next four centuries. Nubia was home to several empires, most prominently the Kingdom of Kush. This kingdom conquered Egypt in the eighth century BC under Piye, ruling as its 25th Dynasty. From the 3rd century BC to the 3rd century AD, northern Nubia was invaded and annexed by Egypt, then under the rule of the Greeks and later the Romans; this territory was known in the Greco-Roman world as the Dodekaschoinos."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thrushes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thrushes"], "contexts": ["type of bird"]}, {"entity_id": "thrushes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["thrushes"], "contexts": ["diving birds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thrushes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "thrushes", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 3857, "stable_key": "clu_129d1a39388964e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thrush", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "The song thrush (Turdus philomelos) is a thrush that breeds across the West Palearctic. It has brown upperparts and black-spotted cream or buff underparts and has four recognised subspecies. Its distinctive song, which features repeated musical phrases, has frequently been referenced in poetry. The song thrush breeds in forests, gardens and parks. It is partially migratory, with many birds wintering in southern Europe, North Africa and the Middle East. The species has also been introduced into New Zealand and Australia.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_thrush"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "boughs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["boughs"], "contexts": ["branches of a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "boughs", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["boughs"], "contexts": ["large branches of a tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "boughs", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "boughs", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3858, "stable_key": "clu_a615b942e7de4aee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boughs", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "While 'boughs' are parts of plants, they are too general to specify a Linnaean binomial or family without further context. The contexts suggest general tree branches.", "wikidata_id": "Q2923673", "wikidata_description": "part of a tree; a shoot axis that develops from an axillary bud meristem or from equal divisions of a meristematic apical cell"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "parasitical Plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "parasitical_plants", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "parasitical Plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["parasitical Plants"], "contexts": ["plants that live on others"]}, {"entity_id": "parasitical_plants", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "parasitical plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["parasitical plants"], "contexts": ["plants that live on other plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "parasitical Plants", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "parasitical plants", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 3859, "stable_key": "clu_ca25a9d702e8a9a9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parasitic plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "wikidata_id": "Q127498", "wikidata_description": "type of plant that derives some or all of its nutritional requirements from another living plant", "wikipedia_extract": "Plant diseases are diseases in plants caused by pathogens (infectious organisms) and environmental conditions (physiological factors). Organisms that cause infectious disease include fungi, oomycetes, bacteria, viruses, viroids, virus-like organisms, phytoplasmas, protozoa, nematodes and parasitic plants. Not included are ectoparasites like insects, mites, vertebrates, or other pests that affect plant health by eating plant tissues and causing injury that may admit plant pathogens. The study of plant disease is called plant pathology. Most phytopathogenic fungi are Ascomycetes or Basidiomycetes. They reproduce both sexually and asexually via the production of spores and other structures.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasitic_plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Misseltoe", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2919, "target_cluster_name": "Misseltoe", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mistletoe is a hemiparasitic plant, a specific type of parasitic plant.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mistletoe is a hemiparasitic plant, a specific type of parasitic plant.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "_India_", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 17, "target_cluster_name": "India", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "groweth, it is of constant shape, and maintains a regular figure; like other supercrescences, and such as living upon the stock of others, are termed parasitical Plants, as Polypody, Moss, the smaller", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "region producing a type of Misseltoe"}, {"found_name": "_America_", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1, "target_cluster_name": "America", "source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "evidence_snippet": "groweth, it is of constant shape, and maintains a regular figure; like other supercrescences, and such as living upon the stock of others, are termed parasitical Plants, as Polypody, Moss, the smaller", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "region producing a type of Misseltoe"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Capillaries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "capillaries", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Capillaries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Capillaries"], "contexts": ["tiny blood vessels"]}, {"entity_id": "capillaries", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "capillaries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["c