a centaur and a famous teacher of heroes in Greek mythology, including Achilles. Hercules, as a hero, would have been part of the same mythological tradition and potentially interacted with or been taught by figures like Chiron.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chiron was a centaur and a famous teacher of heroes in Greek mythology, including Achilles. Hercules, as a hero, would have been part of the same mythological tradition and potentially interacted with or been taught by figures like Chiron.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Orpheus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2666, "target_cluster_name": "Orpheus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chiron, the centaur, was a renowned teacher and healer in Greek mythology, and is sometimes depicted as a mentor or associate of Orpheus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chiron, the centaur, was a renowned teacher and healer in Greek mythology, and is sometimes depicted as a mentor or associate of Orpheus.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Æsculapius", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2903, "target_cluster_name": "Æsculapius", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chiron was a centaur known for his knowledge of medicine and healing, and was a teacher of many heroes, including Asclepius.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chiron was a centaur known for his knowledge of medicine and healing, and was a teacher of many heroes, including Asclepius.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Catapotia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "catapotia", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Catapotia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Catapotia"], "contexts": ["Greek name for pills"]}, {"entity_id": "catapotijs", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Catapotijs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Catapotijs"], "contexts": ["pills, medicinal preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Catapotia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Catapotijs", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3951, "stable_key": "clu_da9c3d3b843ded03", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pills", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Both texts clearly refer to medicinal pills.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "1Fe-LSD, also known as 1-(ferrocenecarbonyl)-LSD or as SYN-L-234, is a psychedelic drug of the lysergamide family related to lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD). It is thought to be a prodrug of LSD. The drug was patented by Lizard Labs in 2024. Subsequently, it was encountered online as a novel designer drug being sold in Germany in November 2025. 1Fe-LSD as the hemi-L-tartrate salt has been sold in the form of blotter containing 200 μg per tab and micropills containing 300 μg per pill. 1Fe-LSD contains ferrocene, an iron compound, which is an orange organometallic compound and is assumed to result in the distinctive orange color of 1Fe-LSD blotter and pills.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1Fe-LSD"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pastilhas", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3222, "target_cluster_name": "Pastilhas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'Pills' and 'Pastilhas' refer to solid dosage forms of medicine intended for oral administration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Both 'Pills' and 'Pastilhas' refer to solid dosage forms of medicine intended for oral administration."}, {"found_name": "Pills", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 2099, "target_cluster_name": "Pills", "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "Past, and that Past into little Cakes called Troches. 5. Having made them, dry them well in the shadow and keep them in a Pot for your use. Chap. 14. Of Pills. 1. THey are called Pilule because they r", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: Greek name for pills", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Catapotia", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "english_physician_1652", "evidence_snippet": "the shadow and keep them in a Pot for your use. Chap. 14. Of Pills. 1. THey are called Pilule because they re∣semble little Balls, the Greeks call them Catapotia. 2. It is the Opinion of Modern Physit", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Greek name for pills"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Jelly", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "jelly", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Jelly", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jelly"], "contexts": ["thick medicinal substance"]}, {"entity_id": "jelly", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "jelly", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["jelly"], "contexts": ["gelatinous substance that vibrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Jelly", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "jelly", "similarity": 0.748}], "id": 3952, "stable_key": "clu_811d37e5b651f0f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jelly", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Ferdinand Joseph LaMothe (né Lemott, later Morton; c. September 20, 1890 – July 10, 1941), known professionally as Jelly Roll Morton, was an American blues and jazz pianist, bandleader, and composer of Louisiana Creole descent. Morton was jazz's first arranger, proving that a genre rooted in improvisation could retain its essential characteristics when notated. His composition \"Jelly Roll Blues\", published in 1915, was one of the first published jazz compositions. He also claimed to have invented the genre. Morton also wrote \"King Porter Stomp\", \"Wolverine Blues\", \"Black Bottom Stomp\", and \"I Thought I Heard Buddy Bolden Say\", the last being a tribute to New Orleans musicians from the turn of the 20th century.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jelly_Roll_Morton"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Gums", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1838, "target_cluster_name": "Gums", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Jelly is a semi-solid substance that often has a gelatinous consistency similar to gums.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Jelly is a semi-solid substance that often has a gelatinous consistency similar to gums.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Infortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "infortune", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Infortune", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Infortune"], "contexts": ["astrological influence"]}, {"entity_id": "infortunio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "infortunio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["infortunio"], "contexts": ["misfortune, adverse event"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Infortune", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "infortunio", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 3953, "stable_key": "clu_c5dd5e67d0050791", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astrological Influence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'infortune' in relation to astrological influence points towards this concept."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "fortune", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1404, "target_cluster_name": "fortune", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In pre-modern contexts, fortune was frequently understood as being directly influenced or determined by astrological forces and celestial configurations. 'Infortune' is explicitly listed as a related term, highlighting this connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In pre-modern contexts, fortune was frequently understood as being directly influenced or determined by astrological forces and celestial configurations. 'Infortune' is explicitly listed as a related term, highlighting this connection.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melancolicos", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1826, "target_cluster_name": "Melancolicos", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "In premodern thought, astrological influences were often considered significant factors in the causation of diseases and temperaments, including melancholy.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: In premodern thought, astrological influences were often considered significant factors in the causation of diseases and temperaments, including melancholy.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Sr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sr_", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Sr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sr."], "contexts": ["salutation to a correspondent"]}, {"entity_id": "sr__hou^", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sr. Hou^", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["sr. Hou^"], "contexts": ["owner of a famous mango tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Sr.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sr. Hou^", "similarity": 0.843}], "id": 3954, "stable_key": "clu_a06539b2bc097c50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sir", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The presence of 'Sr.' and 'sr. Hou^' suggests an honorific title, though the 'Hou^' might refer to a specific person or entity. 'Sir' is the most general modern equivalent."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Major", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2522, "target_cluster_name": "Major", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The title 'Sir' denotes knighthood or a certain social standing, and a Lord Mayor, especially in historical English contexts, would often be a knighted individual of considerable status.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The title 'Sir' denotes knighthood or a certain social standing, and a Lord Mayor, especially in historical English contexts, would often be a knighted individual of considerable status.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Beauty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "beauty", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Beauty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beauty"], "contexts": ["aesthetic quality"]}, {"entity_id": "beauty", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "beauty", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["beauty"], "contexts": ["abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Beauty", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "beauty", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 3955, "stable_key": "clu_2184734473dd953d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Beauty", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7242", "wikidata_description": "aesthetic concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "harmony", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 895, "target_cluster_name": "harmony", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Harmony is frequently considered a component or a characteristic of beauty, especially in aesthetics and art.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Harmony is frequently considered a component or a characteristic of beauty, especially in aesthetics and art.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "appearance", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2433, "target_cluster_name": "appearance", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Beauty is a specific aspect of appearance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Beauty is a specific aspect of appearance.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Curdled Milk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "curdled_milk", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Curdled Milk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Curdled Milk"], "contexts": ["milk that has coagulated"]}, {"entity_id": "curdled_milk", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "curdled milk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["curdled milk"], "contexts": ["preparation of milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Curdled Milk", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "curdled milk", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 3956, "stable_key": "clu_750316407ffbf7b6", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Nicole Ann Bell (born September 8, 1991), known professionally as Nicole Dollanganger, is a Canadian-American singer-songwriter. Dollanganger's music is characterized by a feminine, high-pitched vocal style, minimalist instrumentation, and lyrical themes frequently pertaining to true crime, violence, sexuality, and romance. Her music has been described as \"lo-fi\", \"atmospheric\", and \"folk\", and is associated with the folk, folk pop, bedroom pop, slowcore and dream pop genres. In 2015, Rolling Stone named Dollanganger in an article titled \"10 New Artists You Need to Know,\" stating that her \"gothic folk songs,\" described as \"macabre millennial nightmares, softened by dream-pop tranquilizers, reverberating from a casket-shaped music box,\" are \"as beautiful as they are brutal.\" Most of her releases were released as digital-only, and a majority of them were initially published by Dollanganger herself through the music website Bandcamp. Her stage name comes from the Dollanganger Series of novels by V. C."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Butter", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 399, "target_cluster_name": "Butter", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Butter is a dairy product derived from churned cream, which is itself a product of milk. Curdled milk is a related dairy product.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Butter is a dairy product derived from churned cream, which is itself a product of milk. Curdled milk is a related dairy product.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Milk", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 637, "target_cluster_name": "Milk", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curdled Milk is a processed form of Milk.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Curdled Milk is a processed form of Milk.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "leite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 879, "target_cluster_name": "leite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Curdled Milk is a direct transformation of Milk, often discussed in relation to its properties and uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Curdled Milk is a direct transformation of Milk, often discussed in relation to its properties and uses.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Forgetfulness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "forgetfulness", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Forgetfulness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["Forgetfulness"], "contexts": ["inability to recall"]}, {"entity_id": "esquecimentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "esquecimentos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["esquecimentos"], "contexts": ["forgetfulness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Forgetfulness", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "esquecimentos", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 3957, "stable_key": "clu_3c63f24104dfb3f2", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Forgetting or disremembering is the apparent loss or modification of information already encoded and stored in an individual's short or long-term memory. It is a spontaneous or gradual process in which old memories are unable to be recalled from memory storage. Problems with remembering, learning and retaining new information are a few of the most common complaints of older adults. Studies show that retention improves with increased rehearsal. This improvement occurs because rehearsal helps to transfer information into long-term memory. Forgetting curves (amount remembered as a function of time since an event was first experienced) have been extensively analyzed."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "amentia", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1824, "target_cluster_name": "amentia", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Forgetfulness is a primary symptom and often a defining characteristic of historical descriptions of amentia/dementia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Forgetfulness is a primary symptom and often a defining characteristic of historical descriptions of amentia/dementia.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "falta de memoria", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1995, "target_cluster_name": "falta de memoria", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Forgetfulness is a core symptom and often a defining characteristic of amnesia.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: Forgetfulness is a core symptom and often a defining characteristic of amnesia.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "M.A.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "m_a_", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "M.A.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["M.A."], "contexts": ["academic degree"]}, {"entity_id": "m_a_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "M.A.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["M.A."], "contexts": ["academic degree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "M.A.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "M.A.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3958, "stable_key": "clu_fe1d84a27b96bedb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Master of Arts", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2091008", "wikidata_description": "type of master's degree in the fields of humanities and social sciences"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ALBEMARLE STREET", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "albemarle_street", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ALBEMARLE STREET", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ALBEMARLE STREET"], "contexts": ["street in London"]}, {"entity_id": "albemarle_street", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ALBEMARLE STREET", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ALBEMARLE STREET"], "contexts": ["street address of publisher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ALBEMARLE STREET", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ALBEMARLE STREET", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3959, "stable_key": "clu_8e2445624e32e5fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Albemarle Street", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q134545274", "wikidata_description": "street in Baltimore, Maryland, the United States"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "London", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 6, "target_cluster_name": "London", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Albemarle Street is a street in London, a significant location within the city.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Albemarle Street is a street in London, a significant location within the city.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "horticulture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "horticulture", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "horticulture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["horticulture"], "contexts": ["practice of cultivating gardens"]}, {"entity_id": "horticulture", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "horticulture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["horticulture"], "contexts": ["practice of garden cultivation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "horticulture", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "horticulture", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3960, "stable_key": "clu_0f61f612dd2140f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horticulture", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "May", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1657, "target_cluster_name": "May", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Similar to agriculture, May is a key month for horticultural practices such as planting and tending gardens.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Similar to agriculture, May is a key month for horticultural practices such as planting and tending gardens.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "cultivation", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1429, "target_cluster_name": "cultivation", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Horticulture is the art or practice of garden cultivation and management. Cultivation is central to horticulture.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Horticulture is the art or practice of garden cultivation and management. Cultivation is central to horticulture.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "hyacinth", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hyacinth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hyacinth", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hyacinth"], "contexts": ["cultivated plant studied for variation"]}, {"entity_id": "hyacinthina", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hyacinthina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hyacinthina"], "contexts": ["hyacinth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hyacinth", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hyacinthina", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 3961, "stable_key": "clu_ab848335c4652d00", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Hyacinthus  is a genus of bulbous herbs and spring-blooming perennials. They are fragrant flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Scilloideae and are commonly called hyacinths (). The genus is native predominantly to the Eastern Mediterranean region from the south of Turkey to the Palestine region, although naturalized more widely. The name comes from Greek mythology: Hyacinth was killed by Zephyrus, the god of the west wind, jealous of his love for Apollo. He then transformed the drops of Hyacinth's blood into flowers. Several species of Brodiaea, Scilla, and other plants that have flower clusters borne along the stalk that were formerly classified in the Liliaceae family also have common names with the word \"hyacinth\" in them."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "flores", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 663, "target_cluster_name": "flores", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hyacinth is a specific type of flower.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hyacinth is a specific type of flower.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Lirio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2857, "target_cluster_name": "Lirio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are bulbous flowering plants often mentioned in similar contexts regarding gardens and medicinal uses.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are bulbous flowering plants often mentioned in similar contexts regarding gardens and medicinal uses.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "wild species", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wild_species", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "wild species", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["wild species"], "contexts": ["natural, non-domesticated animal forms"]}, {"entity_id": "wild_animals", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "wild animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["wild animals"], "contexts": ["untamed creatures for food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "wild species", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wild animals", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 3962, "stable_key": "clu_f5052b728ffea19a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wild Species", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'wild species' can refer to animals, the broader context here refers to the concept of naturally occurring, non-domesticated forms in general, encompassing both animals and plants."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "caudal vertebræ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "caudal_vertebræ", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "caudal vertebræ", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["caudal vertebræ"], "contexts": ["vertebrae in the tail region"]}, {"entity_id": "caudal_vertebræ",