Dodo and its Kindred'.", "wikidata_id": "Q1387751", "wikidata_description": "British geologist and zoologist (1811-1853)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Forbes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 912, "target_cluster_name": "Forbes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naturalists and ornithologists of the mid-19th century, collaborating and corresponding on various scientific matters, including taxonomy and biogeography.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent naturalists and ornithologists of the mid-19th century, collaborating and corresponding on various scientific matters, including taxonomy and biogeography.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Dr. Falconer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2103, "target_cluster_name": "Dr. Falconer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were prominent naturalists and paleontologists who collaborated and corresponded.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were prominent naturalists and paleontologists who collaborated and corresponded.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mr. Buckley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__buckley", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. Buckley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mr. Buckley"], "contexts": ["owner of Leicester sheep flock"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__knightley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Mr. Knightley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mr. Knightley"], "contexts": ["character in Miss Austen's 'Emma'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Buckley", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Mr. Knightley", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 3967, "stable_key": "clu_041d7b88e9d6dfa8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mr. Buckley", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Owner of a Leicester sheep flock; a literary character.", "note": "Ambiguous referent due to presence of both a real person associated with sheep and a fictional character."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "wants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "wants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wants"], "contexts": ["human desires and needs"]}, {"entity_id": "wants", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "wants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wants"], "contexts": ["needs or desires"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "wants", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "wants", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3968, "stable_key": "clu_7238bb69d0a7c6a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Desire", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "desires", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 4149, "target_cluster_name": "desires", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'wants' is a direct synonym for 'desires' in many contexts, referring to a feeling of lack or a longing for something.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'wants' is a direct synonym for 'desires' in many contexts, referring to a feeling of lack or a longing for something.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hieracium", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hieracium", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Hieracium", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hieracium"], "contexts": ["genus with polymorphic species"]}, {"entity_id": "hieracium", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Hieracium", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hieracium"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Hieracium", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Hieracium", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3969, "stable_key": "clu_85450d724d593df8", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "known by the common name hawkweed and classically as hierakion (from ancient Greek ἱέραξ, hierax 'hawk'), is a genus of flowering plant in the family Asteraceae, and closely related to dandelion (Taraxacum), chicory (Cichorium), prickly lettuce (Lactuca) and sow thistle (Sonchus), which are part of the tribe Cichorieae. Hawkweeds, with their 10,000+ recorded species and subspecies, do their part to make Asteraceae the second largest family of flowering plants. Some botanists group all these species or subspecies into approximately 800 accepted species, while others prefer to accept several thousand species. Since most hawkweeds reproduce exclusively asexually by means of seeds that are genetically identical to their mother plant (apomixis or agamospermy), clones or populations that consist of genetically identical plants are formed and some botanists (especially in UK, Scandinavia and Russia) prefer to accept these clones as good species (arguing that it is impossible to know how these clones are interrelated) whereas others (mainly in Central Europe and USA) try to group them into a few hundred more broadly defined species. What is here treated as the single genus Hieracium is now treated by most European experts as two different genera, Hieracium and Pilosella, with species such as Hieracium pilosella, Hieracium floribundum and Hieracium aurantiacum referred to the latter genus. Many members of the genus Pilosella reproduce both by stolons (runners like those of strawberries) and by seeds, whereas true Hieracium species reproduce only by seeds."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "elatior", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "elatior", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "elatior", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["elatior"], "contexts": ["scientific name for primrose"]}, {"entity_id": "i__elatior", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "I. elatior", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["I. elatior"], "contexts": ["plant from Turbaco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "elatior", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "I. elatior", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 3970, "stable_key": "clu_3ba9c3486625f7e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Primula elatior", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Primula elatior", "family": "Primulaceae", "note": "The context 'plant from Turbaco' might refer to a different species or a misidentification, but 'elatior' in scientific names typically refers to this primrose.", "wikidata_id": "Q157842", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primula_elatior", "wikipedia_extract": "Primula elatior, the oxlip (or true oxlip), is a species of flowering plant in the family Primulaceae, native to  nutrient-poor and calcium-rich damp woods and meadows throughout Europe, with northern borders in Denmark and southern parts of Sweden, eastwards to the Altai Mountains and on the Kola Peninsula in Russia, and westwards in the British Isles. The oxlip is a herbaceous or semi-evergreen  perennial plant growing to 30 cm (12 in) tall by 25 cm (10 in) broad, with a rosette of leaves 5–15 cm long and 2–6 cm broad. It produces light yellow flowers in spring, in clusters of 10-30 together on a single stem 10–30 cm (4–12 in) tall, each flower 9–15 mm broad. It may be confused with the closely related cowslip (Primula veris), which has a similar general appearance, although P. veris has smaller, bell-shaped, bright yellow flowers (and red dots inside the flower), and a corolla tube without folds. The leaves of P."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "cowslip", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2361, "target_cluster_name": "cowslip", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Primula elatior is a closely related species within the same genus, often confused or grouped with cowslip.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Primula elatior is a closely related species within the same genus, often confused or grouped with cowslip.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "condor", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "condor", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "condor", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["condor"], "contexts": ["bird that lays a couple of eggs"]}, {"entity_id": "condor", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "condor", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["condor"], "contexts": ["confined to the Andes and declivities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "condor", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "condor", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3971, "stable_key": "clu_3968eba28aa25537", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Andean condor", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The context 'confined to the Andes' strongly suggests the Andean condor.", "wikidata_id": "Q170598", "wikidata_description": "species of bird", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andean_condor", "wikipedia_extract": "The Andean flamingo (Phoenicoparrus andinus) is a species of flamingo native to the Andes mountains of South America. Until 2014, it was classified in genus Phoenicopterus. It is closely related to James's flamingo, and the two make up the genus Phoenicoparrus. The Chilean flamingo, Andean flamingo, and James' flamingo are all sympatric, and all live in colonies (including shared nesting areas). Distribution, habitat, and movements This Andean flamingo is native to the wetlands of the high Andes mountain range from southern Peru to northwestern Argentina and northern Chile. Andean flamingos are migratory, with the ability to travel up to 700 miles in one day."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "limit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "limit", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "limit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["limit"], "contexts": ["point or boundary"]}, {"entity_id": "limitation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "limitation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["limitation"], "contexts": ["state of being restricted or confined"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "limit", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "limitation", "similarity": 0.833}], "id": 3972, "stable_key": "clu_7fdb1988b9246ba5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Limit", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q17376040", "wikidata_description": "type of company used in many jurisdictions; use specific QIDs like Q149789 for the US or others for your country"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "parasitic worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "parasitic_worms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "parasitic worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["parasitic worms"], "contexts": ["worms that live on or in other organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "worms", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["worms"], "contexts": ["invertebrate animals with simple consciousness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "parasitic worms", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "worms", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 3973, "stable_key": "clu_ed92f833abd9a38f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parasitic worms", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The second mention of 'worms' in James's 'Principles of Psychology' is likely metaphorical or philosophical, but 'parasitic worms' is the primary specific referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q51516710", "wikidata_description": "London :Printed by order of the Trustees of the British Museum,1915. | London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine Library & Archives Service (archiv"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Lice", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2267, "target_cluster_name": "Lice", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Head lice are a type of parasitic organism that infests a host, similar to parasitic worms.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Head lice are a type of parasitic organism that infests a host, similar to parasitic worms.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 19, "target_cluster_name": "animals", "source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "evidence_snippet": "so-called epidemics appear to be due to parasitic worms, which have from some cause, possibly in part through facility of diffusion amongst the crowded animals, been disproportionably favoured: and he", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "reverse: type of animal", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "desert", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "desert", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "desert", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["desert"], "contexts": ["arid geographical area"]}, {"entity_id": "désert", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Désert", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Désert"], "contexts": ["geographic region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "desert", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Désert", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 3974, "stable_key": "clu_133e88649b587bda", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A desert is a landscape where little precipitation occurs and, consequently, living conditions create unique biomes and ecosystems. The lack of vegetation exposes the unprotected surface of the ground to denudation. About one-third of the land surface of the Earth is arid or semi-arid. This includes much of the polar regions, where little precipitation occurs, and which are sometimes called polar deserts or \"cold deserts\". Deserts can be classified by the amount of precipitation that falls, by the temperature that prevails, by the causes of desertification or by their geographical location. Deserts are formed by weathering processes as large variations in temperature between day and night strain the rocks, which consequently break into pieces."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "cotton-trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cotton-trees", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cotton-trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cotton-trees"], "contexts": ["plants yielding cotton fiber"]}, {"entity_id": "cotton-tree", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cotton-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cotton-tree"], "contexts": ["tree producing cotton"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cotton-trees", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cotton-tree", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 3975, "stable_key": "clu_87cebfad9afcb992", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cotton", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Gossypium herbaceum", "family": "Malvaceae", "note": "The specific species of cotton can vary, but the genus Gossypium is consistent.", "wikidata_id": "Q719312", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "stag", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "stag", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "stag", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["stag"], "contexts": ["example of male weapon importance"]}, {"entity_id": "stag", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "stag", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["stag"], "contexts": ["extant animal found as fossil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "stag", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "stag", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3976, "stable_key": "clu_5aa77a74674f3e22", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red Deer", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'stag' specifically refers to a male deer, and 'red deer' is a common species associated with this term in British contexts, the early modern texts might have used 'stag' more generally. However, given the context of Darwin and Humboldt, 'red deer' is a strong possibility.", "wikidata_id": "Q79794", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_deer", "wikipedia_extract": "Red Deer is a city in Alberta, Canada, located midway on the Calgary–Edmonton Corridor. Red Deer serves central Alberta, and its key industries include health care, retail trade, construction, oil and gas, hospitality, manufacturing and education. It is surrounded by Red Deer County and borders on Lacombe County. The city is in aspen parkland, a region of rolling hills, alongside the Red Deer River. The area was inhabited by First Nations including the Blackfoot, Plains Cree and Stoney before the arrival of European fur traders in the late eighteenth century. A First Nations trail ran from the Montana Territory across the Bow River near present-day Calgary and on to Fort Edmonton, later known as the Calgary and Edmonton Trail."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Deer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 802, "target_cluster_name": "Deer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Red Deer is a specific species within the Cervidae family, which is the family that defines 'deer'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Red Deer is a specific species within the Cervidae family, which is the family that defines 'deer'.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "divergence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "divergence", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "divergence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["divergence"], "contexts": ["process of becoming different"]}, {"entity_id": "divergences", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "divergences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["divergences"], "contexts": ["movements away from a central point"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "divergence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "divergences", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 3977, "stable_key": "clu_7d242d54696f6467", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Divergence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "opposite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1457, "target_cluster_name": "opposite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Divergence implies moving apart or becoming different, which relates to the idea of opposition or contrast.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Divergence implies moving apart or becoming different, which relates to the idea of opposition or contrast.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sub-classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sub-classes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "sub-classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sub-classes"], "contexts": ["taxonomic rank below class"]}, {"entity_id": "sub-classes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sub-classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sub-classes"], "contexts": ["subdivisions within social classes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sub-classes", "target_bo