 "contexts": ["examined aerolite from Maryland"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Professor Silliman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Professor Silliman", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3980, "stable_key": "clu_de4dc8d4068a1d12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Professor Silliman", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1779, "death_year": 1865, "description": "American chemist, geologist, and museum curator", "wikidata_id": "Q817692", "wikidata_description": "early American chemist and science educator (1779–1864)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "warmer countries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "warmer_countries", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "warmer countries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["warmer countries"], "contexts": ["nations with higher temperatures"]}, {"entity_id": "warm_countries", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "warm countries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["warm countries"], "contexts": ["abound in venomous snakes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "warmer countries", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "warm countries", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 3981, "stable_key": "clu_a29d1ab0488e6f87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "warmer regions", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "N/A"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "European species of plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "european_species_of_plants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "European species of plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["European species of plants"], "contexts": ["plants from Azores to England"]}, {"entity_id": "european_species", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "European species", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["European species"], "contexts": ["plant species found in Europe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "European species of plants", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "European species", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3982, "stable_key": "clu_cfbef7429126d5e1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "European flora", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Refers to plant species found within geographical Europe.", "wikidata_id": "Q31180018", "wikidata_description": "the collective plants of Europe", "wikipedia_extract": "Xanthium strumarium (rough cocklebur, Noogoora burr, clotbur, common cocklebur, large cocklebur, woolgarie bur, Siberian cocklebur) is a species of annual plants in the flowering plant family Asteraceae. Some sources claim it originates in southern Europe and Asia, but has been extensively naturalized elsewhere. Others, such as the Flora of China and Flora of North America, state it originates in the Americas but was an early introduction to Eurasia. The species is monoecious, with the flowers borne in separate unisexual heads: staminate (male) heads situated above the pistillate (female) heads in the inflorescence. The pistillate heads consist of two pistillate flowers surrounded by a spiny involucre. Upon fruiting, these two flowers ripen into two brown to black achenes and they are completely enveloped by the involucre, which becomes a bur.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xanthium_strumarium"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Floras", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2595, "target_cluster_name": "Floras", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "European flora is a specific type of flora, defined by its geographical location (Europe).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: European flora is a specific type of flora, defined by its geographical location (Europe).", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Faroe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "faroe", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Faroe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faroe"], "contexts": ["cold climate location for rodents"]}, {"entity_id": "faroe_islands", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Faroe Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faroe Islands"], "contexts": ["islands where waters do not freeze"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Faroe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Faroe Islands", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 3983, "stable_key": "clu_1111d905a7825b64", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Faroe Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Denmark", "wikidata_id": "Q4628", "wikidata_description": "North Atlantic archipelago organized as an autonomous constituent country of the Kingdom of Denmark", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faroe%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Faroe Islands ( FAIR-oh), also known as the Faroes, are an archipelago in the North Atlantic Ocean and a rigsdel ('autonomous territory') of the Kingdom of Denmark. Located between Iceland, Norway, and the Hebrides and Shetland isles of Scotland, the islands have a population of 54,870 as of November 2025 and a land area of 1,393 km2 (538 sq mi). The official language is Faroese, which is partially mutually intelligible with Icelandic. The terrain is rugged, dominated by fjords and cliffs with sparse vegetation and few trees. As a result of their proximity to the Arctic Circle, the islands experience perpetual civil twilight during summer nights and very short winter days; nevertheless, they experience a subpolar oceanic climate and mild temperatures year-round due to the Gulf Stream. The capital, Tórshavn, receives the fewest recorded hours of sunshine of any city in the world at only 840 per year."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "ovelha", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 3743, "target_cluster_name": "ovelha", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Direct translation of 'sheep' in Portuguese.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "Direct translation of 'sheep' in Portuguese."}, {"found_name": "Greenland", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 316, "target_cluster_name": "Greenland", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Greenland is one of the three constituent parts of the Kingdom of Denmark, alongside Denmark proper and the Faroe Islands.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Greenland is one of the three constituent parts of the Kingdom of Denmark, alongside Denmark proper and the Faroe Islands.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Goat", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2022, "target_cluster_name": "Goat", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sheep and goats are commonly grouped together as livestock and share many agricultural and biological similarities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sheep and goats are commonly grouped together as livestock and share many agricultural and biological similarities."}, {"found_name": "Cattle", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 101, "target_cluster_name": "cattle", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sheep and cattle are both common types of livestock, often raised in similar agricultural contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Sheep and cattle are both common types of livestock, often raised in similar agricultural contexts."}, {"found_name": "Danemark", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1030, "target_cluster_name": "Danemark", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Faroe Islands are a constituent country within the Kingdom of Denmark.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Faroe Islands are a constituent country within the Kingdom of Denmark.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "M. Is. Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__is__geoffroy_st__hilaire", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "M. Is. Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Is. Geoffroy St. Hilaire"], "contexts": ["remarked on malconformations"]}, {"entity_id": "m__geoffroy_de_saint-hilnire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. Geoffroy de Saint-Hilnire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Geoffroy de Saint-Hilnire"], "contexts": ["scholar who recognized an analogy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "M. Is. Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "M. Geoffroy de Saint-Hilnire", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 3984, "stable_key": "clu_5ceba728ace928cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1805, "death_year": 1861, "description": "French zoologist known for teratology and evolutionary ideas", "wikidata_id": "Q521190", "wikidata_description": "French zoologist (1805-1861)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 851, "target_cluster_name": "Auguste de Saint-Hilaire", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the son of Auguste de Saint-Hilaire and also a prominent naturalist and zoologist. They shared a surname and were part of the same intellectual and familial lineage in French science.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the son of Auguste de Saint-Hilaire and also a prominent naturalist and zoologist. They shared a surname and were part of the same intellectual and familial lineage in French science.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Mathieu", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2050, "target_cluster_name": "Mathieu", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the father of Claude François Geoffroy and a prominent naturalist himself. Their shared surname and familial connection indicate a strong relationship within the scientific community of their time.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was the father of Claude François Geoffroy and a prominent naturalist himself. Their shared surname and familial connection indicate a strong relationship within the scientific community of their time.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Milne Edwards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2528, "target_cluster_name": "Milne Edwards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was a contemporary zoologist and a significant figure in the development of evolutionary thought, known for his work on teratology and classification. He was a colleague and sometimes collaborator with Milne Edwards, and they shared a similar scientific milieu and interests in comparative anatomy and zoology.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Isidore Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was a contemporary zoologist and a significant figure in the development of evolutionary thought, known for his work on teratology and classification. He was a colleague and sometimes collaborator with Milne Edwards, and they shared a similar scientific milieu and interests in comparative anatomy and zoology.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Compositous", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "compositous", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Compositous", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Compositous"], "contexts": ["family of flowering plants (Asteraceae)"]}, {"entity_id": "compositof", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Compositof", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Compositof"], "contexts": ["family of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Compositous", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Compositof", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 3985, "stable_key": "clu_375a00b18b434796", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Asteraceae", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Asteraceae", "note": "The term 'Compositous' or 'Compositof' refers to the family Asteraceae, also known as Compositae.", "wikidata_id": "Q25400", "wikidata_description": "large family of flowering plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asteraceae", "wikipedia_extract": "Asteraceae ( ) is a large family of flowering plants that consists of over 32,000 known species in over 1,900 genera within the order Asterales. The number of species in Asteraceae is rivaled only by the Orchidaceae, and which is the larger family is unclear as the quantity of extant species in each family is unknown. The Asteraceae were first described in the year 1740 and given the original name Compositae. The family is commonly known as the aster, daisy, composite, or sunflower family. Most species of Asteraceae are herbaceous plants, and may be annual, biennial, or perennial, but there are also shrubs, vines, and trees. The family has a widespread distribution, from subpolar to tropical regions, in a wide variety of habitats."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Eupatorio", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2241, "target_cluster_name": "Eupatorio", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Eupatorium is a genus within the Asteraceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Eupatorium is a genus within the Asteraceae family.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Geoffroy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 1, "members": [{"entity_id": "geoffroy", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Geoffroy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geoffroy"], "contexts": ["proposed law of compensation of growth"]}], "edges": [], "id": 3986, "stable_key": "clu_6cdfb597cb725ef1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1772, "death_year": 1844, "description": "French naturalist and comparative anatomist", "note": "The context 'ancient pharmacologist' is potentially misleading, as Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire was a prominent 19th-century naturalist. The text might be referring to an earlier figure or an incorrect attribution.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "tertiary periods", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tertiary_periods", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary periods", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tertiary periods"], "contexts": ["geological time divisions"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary_period", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tertiary period", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tertiary period"], "contexts": ["geological era"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "tertiary periods", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tertiary period", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 3987, "stable_key": "clu_c8c1dce26c257100", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Neogene Period", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'tertiary periods' was a common term in the 19th century, it has been superseded by the Neogene and Paleogene periods within the Cenozoic Era. The context of geological time divisions points to this."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "penguin", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "penguin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "penguin", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["penguin"], "contexts": ["flightless aquatic bird"]}, {"entity_id": "penguins", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "penguins", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["penguins"], "contexts": ["animal whose excrements were found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "penguin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "penguins", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 3988, "stable_key": "clu_09dd678a01bf8694", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "penguin", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q9147", "wikidata_description": "family of birds in the order Sphenisciformes"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "transparent tissue", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "transparent_tissue", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "transparent tissue", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["transparent tissue"], "contexts": ["body tissue that allows light to pass"]}, {"entity_id": "transparent_and_opaque_masses", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "transparent and opaque masses", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["transparent and opaque masses"], "contexts": ["materials with different light properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "transparent tissue", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "transparent and opaque masses", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3989, "stable_key": "clu_75ad136aa0af5810", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "transparent and opaque materials", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to materials based on their light transmission properties, not a specific substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q2643571", "wikidata_description": "property of light and its interaction with substances or objects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_properties", "wikipedia_extract": "The optical properties of a material define how it interacts with light. The optical properties of matter are studied in optical physics (a subfield of optics) and applied in materials science. The optical properties of matter include: Transmittance and Transmission coefficient Reflectance and Reflectivity (reflection coefficient) A basic distinction is between isotropic materials, which exhibit the same properties regardless of the direction of the light, and anisotropic ones, which exhibit different properties when light passes through them in different directions. The optical properties of matter can lead to a variety of interesting optical phenomena. Optical properties of carbon nanotubes Fox, Mark (2010). Optical properties of solids."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "frederick_cuvier", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Frederick Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Frederick Cuvier"], "contexts": ["naturalist who compared instinct and habit"]}, {"entity_id": "george_cuvier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "George Cuvier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["George Cuvier"], "contexts": ["scientist known for fossil remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "George Cuvier", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3990, "stable_key": "clu_da9e099825972d7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Frederick Cuvier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1773, "death_year": 1838, "description": "French zoologist and paleontologist, brother of Georges Cuvier.", "note": "Although 'George Cuvier' is also mentioned, the primary referent seems to be Frederick Cuvier based on the Darwin reference. George Cuvier is also a valid, famous naturalist."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Cuvier", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 119, "target_cluster_name": "Cuvier", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was Georges Cuvier's younger brother and a fellow naturalist who collaborated with him on scientific work.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Frederick Cuvier was Georges Cuvier's younger brother and a fellow naturalist who collaborated with him on scientific work.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Buffon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 473, "target_cluster_name": "Buffon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was Georges-Louis Leclerc, Comte de Buffon's son and collaborator on the Histoire Naturelle.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Frederick Cuvier was Georges-Louis Leclerc, Comte de Buffon's son and collaborator on the Histoire Naturelle.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Duperrey", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1798, "target_cluster_name": "Duperrey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was a zoologist who, along with his brother Georges Cuvier, was a prominent figure in French natural history during the period of Duperrey's expeditions. They would have been contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Frederick Cuvier was a zoologist who, along with his brother Georges Cuvier, was a prominent figure in French natural history during the period of Duperrey's expeditions. They would have been contemporaries and part of the same scientific milieu.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Milne Edwards", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2528, "target_cluster_name": "Milne Edwards", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Frederick Cuvier was the brother of Georges Cuvier and also a zoologist. He worked on mammals and comparative anatomy, areas that would have been of direct interest and relevance to Milne Edwards's own research.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Frederick Cuvier was the brother of Georges Cuvier and also a zoologist. He worked on mammals and comparative anatomy, areas that would have been of direct interest and relevance to Milne Edwards's own research.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "song", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "song", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "song", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["song"], "contexts": ["sequence of musical notes"]}, {"entity_id": "song", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "song", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["song"], "contexts": ["short musical composition with lyrics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "song", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "song", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3991, "stable_key": "clu_0dbc00f992c44ef3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "song", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bodily organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodily_organs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "bodily organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily organs"], "contexts": ["physiological structures of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bodily organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily organs"], "contexts": ["physical structures of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bodily organs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bodily organs", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3992, "stable_key": "clu_6a8d306e62bc34e1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bodily organs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "ovaries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ovaries", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ovaries", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ovaries"], "contexts": ["female reproductive organs in plants"]}, {"entity_id": "ovario", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ovario", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ovario"], "contexts": ["egg-producing organ"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ovaries", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ovario", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 3993, "stable_key": "clu_a42b4eb8ff14e0d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ovary", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'ovaries' in this context refers to the plant reproductive organ that contains ovules, not mammalian ovaries. 'Ovario' from Polyanthea Medicinal strongly supports this interpretation."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "canary-bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "canary-bird", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "canary-bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["canary-bird"], "contexts": ["bird used in hybridization experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "canards", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "canards", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["canards"], "contexts": ["ducks"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "canary-bird", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "canards", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 3994, "stable_key": "clu_36d4834e004e6077", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Canary", "confidence": "medium", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'canary-bird' clearly refers to the bird, 'canards' in the Humboldt text is likely a misspelling or a different word referring to ducks, leading to a low confidence for the cluster as a whole encompassing both. However, the strong presence of 'canary-bird' makes the bird identification high confidence.", "wikidata_id": "Q2936174", "wikidata_description": "rose cultivar"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Matthiola annua", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "matthiola_annua", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Matthiola annua", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Matthiola annua"], "contexts": ["closely related plant form"]}, {"entity_id": "mathiola_annua", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mathiola annua", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mathiola annua"], "contexts": ["plant used for paper preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Matthiola annua", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mathiola annua", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 3995, "stable_key": "clu_481d1d6fb357e40d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stock", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Matthiola incana", "family": "Brassicaceae", "note": "The specific epithet 'annua' is sometimes used, but 'incana' is the more commonly accepted species for ornamental stock. Given the context of its use in scientific texts, it refers to the ornamental plant.", "wikidata_id": "Q27961", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthiola", "wikipedia_extract": "Matthiola () is a genus of flowering plant in the mustard family Brassicaceae. It is named after Italian naturalist Pietro Andrea Mattioli (1501–1577). The genus contains about 50 species of annual, biennial and perennial herbaceous plants and subshrubs. Many are cultivated for their heavily scented, colorful flowers. The common name stock may be applied to the whole genus, more specifically to varieties and cultivars of Matthiola incana. The common names evening stock and night-scented stock are applied to varieties of Matthiola longipetala (syn."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "vegetative systems", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vegetative_systems", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "vegetative systems", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vegetative systems"], "contexts": ["plant structures for growth"]}, {"entity_id": "system", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["system"], "contexts": ["interconnected parts of an organism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "vegetative systems", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "system", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 3996, "stable_key": "clu_79fd6f65a6154968", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vegetative system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In the context of Darwin's 'Origin of Species,' 'vegetative systems' refers to the non-reproductive parts of plants responsible for growth and sustenance.", "wikidata_id": "Q69906033", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on January 31, 1970"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "marine fauna", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "marine_fauna", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name":