, "name": "Nova Scotia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nova Scotia"], "contexts": ["Canadian province"]}, {"entity_id": "nova_scotia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nova Scotia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nova Scotia"], "contexts": ["location with high tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Nova Scotia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nova Scotia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3998, "stable_key": "clu_4de54a15a778862c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nova Scotia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "wikidata_id": "Q1952", "wikidata_description": "province of Canada", "wikipedia_extract": "Sydney is a former city and urban community on the east coast of Cape Breton Island in Nova Scotia, Canada within the Cape Breton Regional Municipality. Sydney was founded in 1785 by the British, incorporated as a city in 1904, and dissolved on 1 August 1995, when it was amalgamated into the regional municipality. Sydney served as Cape Breton Island's colonial capital until 1820, when the colony was annexed to  Nova Scotia. A rapid population expansion occurred just after the turn of the 20th century, when Sydney became home to one of North America's main steel mills. During both the First and Second World Wars, it was a major staging area for England-bound convoys. The post-war period witnessed a major decline in the number of people employed at the Dominion Steel and Coal Corporation steel mill, and the Nova Scotia and Canadian governments had to nationalize it in 1967 to save the region's biggest employer, forming the new crown corporation called the Sydney Steel Corporation.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sydney,_Nova_Scotia"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "marine inhabitants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "marine_inhabitants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "marine inhabitants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marine inhabitants"], "contexts": ["creatures living in the sea"]}, {"entity_id": "marine_animalcules", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "marine animalcules", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marine animalcules"], "contexts": ["tiny marine organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "marine inhabitants", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "marine animalcules", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 3999, "stable_key": "clu_4ba7c941e6b31b5d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marine organisms", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Includes both general 'marine inhabitants' and specific 'marine animalcules' which are small organisms.", "wikidata_id": "Q124321800", "wikidata_description": "organism living in a marine environment"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "chapter", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chapter", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "chapter", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chapter"], "contexts": ["section of a book"]}, {"entity_id": "capitulo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "capitulo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["capitulo"], "contexts": ["section of a book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "chapter", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "capitulo", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 4000, "stable_key": "clu_54140c91c139b63f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chapter", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Both English and Portuguese terms refer to a division within a book.", "wikidata_id": "Q88392887", "wikidata_description": "part that a work consists of (use a more specific object if possible)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "xilaloes", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "e outro deradeiro capitulo he de outra a que elles chamam H**: por onde pôde ser que quando soube milhor a natureza deste simples que quis escrever o capitulo mais copioso. E no que rcprende a Savaner", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "a substance synonym for"}, {"found_name": "linaloes", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "e outro deradeiro capitulo he de outra a que elles chamam H**: por onde pôde ser que quando soube milhor a natureza deste simples que quis escrever o capitulo mais copioso. E no que rcprende a Savaner", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "a substance synonym for"}, {"found_name": "alma", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "se he usada na física. ORTA Em arábio, ao qual emitam os Mouros todos onde quer que estam, se chama alma\\, posto que Serapio o chama por outro nome, capitulo 391, e o gentio todo, onde se acha a nacen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "iraa", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "se he usada na física. ORTA Em arábio, ao qual emitam os Mouros todos onde quer que estam, se chama alma\\, posto que Serapio o chama por outro nome, capitulo 391, e o gentio todo, onde se acha a nacen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}, {"found_name": "itam", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "se he usada na física. ORTA Em arábio, ao qual emitam os Mouros todos onde quer que estam, se chama alma\\, posto que Serapio o chama por outro nome, capitulo 391, e o gentio todo, onde se acha a nacen", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Mylodon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mylodon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mylodon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mylodon"], "contexts": ["extinct giant ground sloth"]}, {"entity_id": "mylodon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mylodon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mylodon"], "contexts": ["extinct sloth-like mammal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mylodon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mylodon", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 4001, "stable_key": "clu_4a0d652f2eee741e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mylodon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q1749282", "wikidata_description": "family of mammals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mylodontidae", "wikipedia_extract": "Mylodon is an extinct genus of ground sloth belonging to the family Mylodontidae, known from southern South America. With a total length of 3 to 4 m and a body mass of 1-2 tonnes, it is one of the largest mylodontids (though it was considerably exceeded in size by the mylodontid Lestodon). The oldest finds probably date to the Lower Pleistocene; however, most of the fossil remains date to the Late Pleistocene. Its distribution ranged from southern Bolivia and the Pampas in the north southwards to the southernmost part of Patagonia at around 52-53 degrees south, the furthest south of any Pleistocene ground sloth, with some of the best known remains of the species being from the eponymous Cueva del Milodon (cave of Mylodon) in southern Chile. In addition to skeletal remains, Mylodon is also known from preserved skin and hair. Preserved dung (coprolites) indicates that Mylodon was a primarily a grazer, feeding on grasses and sedges."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mastodontes", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 914, "target_cluster_name": "mastodontes", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Mylodon is another extinct megafauna, often discussed alongside mastodons in paleontological contexts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Mylodon is another extinct megafauna, often discussed alongside mastodons in paleontological contexts.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "waters", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "waters", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "waters", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["waters"], "contexts": ["those in the waters of the sea"]}, {"entity_id": "water-courses", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "water-courses", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["water-courses"], "contexts": ["channels through which water flows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "waters", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "water-courses", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 4002, "stable_key": "clu_f2f93b6180c495b7", "ground