ontext of Darwin's work on hybridization and 'Saliceto' in Monardes' medicinal history. Assuming 'Sageret' refers to Auguste Sageret, a French botanist known for his work on plant hybridization. 'Saliceto' might refer to a different figure or a misreading, but given the context of grafts, Sageret is the more likely intended referent for the Darwin citation.", "wikidata_id": "Q2871651", "wikidata_description": "French botanist (1763-1851)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pureté", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pureté", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pureté", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pureté"], "contexts": ["state of being pure"]}, {"entity_id": "purity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "purity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["purity"], "contexts": ["state of being unadulterated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "pureté", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "purity", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 4036, "stable_key": "clu_b4d2ad8de9a83f4c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1401905", "wikidata_description": "mass fraction of fine metal in a precious metal object", "wikipedia_extract": "The fineness of a precious metal object (coin, bar, jewelry, etc.) represents the weight of fine metal therein, in proportion to the total weight which includes alloying base metals and any impurities. Alloy metals are added to increase hardness and durability of coins and jewelry, alter colors, decrease the cost per weight, or avoid the cost of high-purity refinement. For example, copper is added to the precious metal silver to make a more durable alloy for use in coins, housewares and jewelry. Coin silver, which was used for making silver coins in the past, contains 90% silver and 10% copper, by mass. Sterling silver contains 92.5% silver and 7.5% of other metals, usually copper, by mass. Various ways of expressing fineness have been used and two remain in common use: millesimal fineness expressed in units of parts per 1,000 and karats or carats used only for gold.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fineness"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "valckenaer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valckenaer"], "contexts": ["author"]}, {"entity_id": "valckenaer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valckenaer"], "contexts": ["scholar who wrote a diatribe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Valckenaer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Valckenaer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4037, "stable_key": "clu_9a3245125ce3e60e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Valckenaer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scholar and author of a diatribe, likely Lodewijk Caspar Valckenaer.", "note": "Lodewijk Caspar Valckenaer was a Dutch classical scholar and philologist. The context suggests a learned individual whose work is being referenced.", "wikidata_id": "Q2155572", "wikidata_description": "Dutch classical scholar (1715–1785)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "moine franciscain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "moine_franciscain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "moine franciscain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategor