ay of Bengal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bay of Bengal"], "contexts": ["northern part of the Indian Ocean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mer du Bengal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bay of Bengal", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 4061, "stable_key": "clu_359c466363f82ffa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bay of Bengal", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q107540080", "wikidata_description": "bio-geographic province in MEOW scheme", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tampa Bay Buccaneers (colloquially known as the Bucs) are a professional American football team based in Tampa, Florida. The Buccaneers compete in the National Football League (NFL) as a member of the National Football Conference (NFC) South division. They joined the NFL in 1974 as an expansion team, along with the Seattle Seahawks, and played their first season in 1976 as a member of the American Football Conference (AFC) West division. Before the 1977 season, Tampa Bay switched conferences and divisions with Seattle, becoming a member of the NFC Central division. The Seahawks eventually rejoined the NFC in 2002, meaning the Buccaneers joined the Cleveland Browns, Pittsburgh Steelers, and Baltimore/Indianapolis Colts as the only NFL teams not playing in their original conference. As a result of the league's realignment before the 2002 season, the Buccaneers joined three former NFC West teams to form the NFC South.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tampa_Bay_Buccaneers"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "mer", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 921, "target_cluster_name": "mer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Bay of Bengal is a specific sea, a part of the larger interconnected body of seawaters referred to as 'the sea'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Bay of Bengal is a specific sea, a part of the larger interconnected body of seawaters referred to as 'the sea'.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Zeilan", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3252, "target_cluster_name": "Zeilan", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Sri Lanka is located southwest of the Bay of Bengal.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Sri Lanka is located southwest of the Bay of Bengal.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lahore", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lahore"], "contexts": ["independent state"]}, {"entity_id": "lahore", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lahore"], "contexts": ["location of earthquake propagation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lahore", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lahore", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4062, "stable_key": "clu_b6548ccc9c746bd4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lahore", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Pakistan", "wikidata_id": "Q11739", "wikidata_description": "capital of the Punjab province, Pakistan", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lahore", "wikipedia_extract": "Lahore is the capital and largest city of the Pakistani province of Punjab. It is the second-largest city in Pakistan, after Karachi, and 27th largest in the world, with a population of over 14 million. Lahore is one of Pakistan's major industrial, educational and economic hubs. It has been the historic capital and cultural centre of the wider Punjab region, and is one of Pakistan's most socially liberal, progressive, and cosmopolitan cities. Lahore's origin dates back to antiquity. The city has been inhabited for around two millennia, although it rose to prominence in the late 10th century with the establishment of the Walled City, its fortified interior."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "jakutsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jakutsk"], "contexts": ["city in Siberia"]}, {"entity_id": "jakutsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jakutsk"], "contexts": ["city in northern Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jakutsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jakutsk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4063, "stable_key": "clu_9737188088ba1c7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yakutsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q6610", "wikidata_description": "city in eastern Russia, capital of the Republic of Sakha (Yakutia)", "wikipedia_extract": "Yakutsk is the capital and largest city of Sakha, Russia, located about 450 km (280 mi) south of the Arctic Circle. Fueled by the mining industry, Yakutsk has become one of Russia's most rapidly growing regional cities, with a population of 355,443 at the 2021 census. Yakutsk has an average annual temperature of −8.0 °C (17.6 °F), winter high temperatures consistently well below −20 °C (−4 °F), and a record low of −64.4 °C (−83.9 °F) has been recorded. As a result, Yakutsk is the coldest major city in the world (although a number of smaller towns in that region are slightly colder). Yakutsk is also the largest city located in continuous permafrost; the only other large city is Norilsk, also in Siberia. Yakutsk is in the Central Yakutian Lowland and is a major port on the Lena River.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yakutsk"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Corée", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "corée", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Corée", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Corée"], "contexts": ["country excluded from Tartary"]}, {"entity_id": "korea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Korea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Korea"], "contexts": ["northern part of the country"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Corée", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Korea", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 4064, "stable_key": "clu_d4b204e7ad8b1208", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Korea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "South Korea, North Korea", "wikipedia_extract": "The history of North Korea began with the end of World War II in 1945. The surrender of Japan led to the division of Korea at the 38th parallel, with the Soviet Union occupying the north, and the United States occupying the south. The Soviet Union and the United States failed to agree on a way to unify the country, and in 1948, they established two separate governments — the Soviet-aligned Democratic People's Republic of Korea and the American-aligned Republic of Korea — each claiming to be the legitimate government of all of Korea. In 1950, the Korean War broke out. After much destruction, the war ended with a stalemate. The division at the 38th parallel was replaced by the Korean Demilitarized Zone.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_North_Korea"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "indo-germanique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "indo-germanique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indo-germanique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["indo-germanique"], "contexts": ["language family"]}, {"entity_id": "indo-germanic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Indo-Germanic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indo-Germanic"], "contexts": ["tribes inhabiting Turan"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "indo-germanique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Indo-Germanic", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 4065, "stable_key": "clu_ded657c450f20333", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indo-European languages", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Indo-Germanic' is an older term for the Indo-European language family."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hongrois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hongrois", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hongrois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["hongrois"], "contexts": ["language spoken in Hungary"]}, {"entity_id": "hungarus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hungarus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hungarus"], "contexts": ["husband of phthisical patient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hongrois", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hungarus", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 4066, "stable_key": "clu_acc0fcbc0eecef0c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hungarian", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to nationality or people from Hungary, not a specific person.", "wikidata_id": "Q28", "wikidata_description": "country in Central Europe"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Saragosse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saragosse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saragosse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saragosse"], "contexts": ["city where treaty was concluded"]}, {"entity_id": "saragoça", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Saragoça", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saragoça"], "contexts": ["city where contract was celebrated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saragosse", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Saragoça", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 4067, "stable_key": "clu_b9c71cf457a429c4", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Zaragoza (Spanish: [θaɾaˈɣoθa] ), traditionally known in English as Saragossa ( SARR-ə-GOSS-ə), is the capital city of the province of Zaragoza and of the autonomous community of Aragon, Spain. It lies by the Ebro river and its tributaries, the Huerva and the Gállego, roughly in the centre of both Aragon and the Ebro basin. In the 1st century BCE, Romans founded the colony of Caesaraugusta over the Sedetani settlement of Salduie. The city was the capital of the Upper March of Al-Andalus under Umayyad rule, later thriving as a capital of a rump state ruled by the Banu Hud, the taifa of Zaragoza. It swiftly became the political and spiritual centre of the Kingdom of Aragon after the 1118 Christian conquest, and its inhabitants managed to preserve and consolidate initially granted privileges over the course of the Late Middle Ages. As of 2025, Zaragoza had a population of 699,007 inhabitants, making it the fourth most populous in Spain, on a land area of 973.78 square kilometres (375.98 square miles) and is the centre of a metropolitan area of around 0.76 million."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "économie animale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "économie_animale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "économie animale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["économie animale"], "contexts": ["animal body system"]}, {"entity_id": "animal_economy", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "animal economy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["animal economy"], "contexts": ["study of animal functions and characteristics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "économie animale", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "animal economy", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 4068, "stable_key": "clu_cc4b51dbce925d8f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Animal economy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nutrition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1041, "target_cluster_name": "nutrition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Animal economy refers to the physiological processes within an organism, of which nutrition is a fundamental part.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Animal economy refers to the physiological processes within an organism, of which nutrition is a fundamental part.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "chimie végétale", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1358, "target_cluster_name": "chimie végétale", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, the study of living organisms and their processes (animal economy) was closely linked to what would become organic chemistry, particularly in understanding biological functions through chemical principles.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Historically, the study of living organisms and their processes (animal economy) was closely linked to what would become organic chemistry, particularly in understanding biological functions through chemical principles.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "BIake", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "biake", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "BIake", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["BIake"], "contexts": ["confirmed Dbanalagiri measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "biese", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Biese", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Biese"], "contexts": ["author on Aristotle's philosophy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "BIake", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Biese", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 4069, "stable_key": "clu_0f02d49ac4c88044", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Richard Blake", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Author on Aristotle, confirmed astronomical measurements.", "note": "The spelling is inconsistent ('BIake', 'Biese'). Richard Blake is a likely candidate given the context of Aristotelian philosophy."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "archipel des Chonos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "archipel_des_chonos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "archipel des Chonos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["archipel des Chonos"], "contexts": ["archipelago of sunken mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago_'de_los_chonos'", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Archipelago 'de los Chonos'", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Archipelago 'de los Chonos'"], "contexts": ["South American archipelago"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "archipel des Chonos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Archipelago 'de los Chonos'", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 4070, "stable_key": "clu_4a443fa12d5750ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chonos Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Chile", "wikidata_id": "Q1076210", "wikidata_description": "island group in Chile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chonos%20Archipelago", "wikipedia_extract": "The Chonos Archipelago is a series of low, mountainous, elongated islands with deep bays, traces of a submerged Chilean Coast Range. Most of the islands are forested with little or no human settlement. The deep Moraleda Channel separates the islands of the Chonos Archipelago from the mainland of Chile and from Magdalena Island. The largest islands are Melchor Island, Benjamin Island, Traiguén Island, Riveros Island, Cuptana Island, James Island, Victoria Island, Simpson Island, Level Island, Luz Island. Far out in the Pacific is Guamblin Island with the Isla Guamblin National Park. The National park comprises about 106 km2."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "archipelago", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 175, "target_cluster_name": "archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Chonos Archipelago is an example of an archipelago, and the term 'archipelago' itself originates from the Aegean Islands, which are also an archipelago.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Chonos Archipelago is an example of an archipelago, and the term 'archipelago' itself originates from the Aegean Islands, which are also an archipelago.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "collao", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Collao"], "contexts": ["high plains in Peru"]}, {"entity_id": "collao", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Collao"], "contexts": ["land in Peru with bituminose mountains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Collao", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Collao", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4071, "stable_key": "clu_16622b1c114d70c7", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Collao is a parish (administrative division) in Siero, a municipality within the province and autonomous community of Asturias, in northern Spain. The parish is located on Highway AS-113. It is 0.27 km2 (0.10 sq mi) in size, and is situated at an elevation of 230 m (750 ft) above sea level. The population is 488 Collao and other nearby parishes, such as Lieres and Carbayín, are historically associated with Andalusian mining. Collao's patron saint festivities honor San Cristóbal, and are held every year on the first Sunday of July. The Gazetteer of SADEI identifies five notable sites within the parish: The Cirigüeyu, Cordón, Fuentemil, Llorián and La Quintana."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hambato", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hambato"], "contexts": ["river basin"]}, {"entity_id": "hambato", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hambato"], "contexts": ["city nearer to earthquake center"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hambato", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hambato", "similarity": 0.866}], "id": 4072, "stable_key": "clu_d0f5520e735de1f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ambato", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q210161", "wikidata_description": "city in Ecuador, capital of the Tungurahua Province", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambato%2C_Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "Ambato (Spanish pronunciation: [amˈbato]; full form, San Juan de Ambato; Quechua: Ampatu Llaqta) is a city located in the central Andean valley of Ecuador. Lying on the banks of the Ambato River, the city also sits beneath several tall mountains. It is the capital city of the Tungurahua Province, situated at an elevation of 2,577 meters above sea level. It is variously nicknamed \"City of Flowers and Fruits\", \"Land of the Three Juans\", and \"Garden of Ecuador.\" Ambato's inhabitants are called Ambateños or Guaytambos (after a type of native peach that the valley is famous for producing). The current mayor of Ambato is Diana Caiza. The city has been fully or partially destroyed by earthquakes several times in its history, most recently on August 5, 1949, when the city and its cathedral were almost completely devastated."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Quito", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 210, "target_cluster_name": "Quito", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambato and Quito are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. Historical texts often discuss them in proximity due to their geographical and administrative connections within the colonial period.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ambato and Quito are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. Historical texts often discuss them in proximity due to their geographical and administrative connections within the colonial period.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Guayaquil", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 631, "target_cluster_name": "Guayaquil", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are cities in Ecuador.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are cities in Ecuador.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Riobamba", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2053, "target_cluster_name": "Riobamba", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Ambato and Riobamba are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. They were significant centers during the colonial period and are often mentioned together in historical accounts of the region.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Ambato and Riobamba are both cities in Ecuador, located relatively close to each other in the Andean highlands. They were significant centers during the colonial period and are often mentioned together in historical accounts of the region.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Call", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "call", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Call", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Call"], "contexts": ["high plain name"]}, {"entity_id": "dial", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dial", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dial"], "contexts": ["name of a river's mouth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Call", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dial", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 4073, "stable_key": "clu_8a4bee1245e267f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kall", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "country": "Switzerland", "note": "The association with 'Dial' (a river's mouth) and 'Call' (a high plain name) makes the exact referent ambiguous. 'Kall' is a Swiss municipality, but its connection to a river mouth or high plain is not immediately clear without more context.", "wikipedia_extract": "Kristina Lee \"Kris\" Halliwell Collins (born July 1, 1996), better known as Kallmekris, is a Canadian social media personality, actress, comedian, and hairdresser from British Columbia. She is known for her skits that range from thirty seconds to a minute long. Collins began posting on TikTok in April 2020. As of February 2026, she has 50.5 million followers, making her the most-followed TikToker from Canada and the 38th overall. Her YouTube channel, which she began uploading to in 2020, has 12.8 million subscribers as of February 2026. In July 2025, Collins made her feature directorial debut with the found footage horror film, House on Eden.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KallMeKris"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chillo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chillo"], "contexts": ["valley location"]}, {"entity_id": "chillo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chillo"], "contexts": ["near Quito"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chillo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chillo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4074, "stable_key": "clu_de4c3bf7679749b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chillo Valley", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "AEK Basketball Club (Greek: ΚΑΕ ΑΕΚ Greek pronunciation: [ˈaek]; Αθλητική Ένωσις Κωνσταντινουπόλεως Athlitikí Énosis Konstantinoupóleos, \"Athletic Union of Constantinople\"), and also known as AEK B.C. or simply AEK, and more commonly known in European competitions as AEK Athens, is a Greek professional basketball club based in Athens, Attica, Greece, part of the major multi-sport club AEK. The club was established in Athens in 1924 by Greek refugees from Constantinople in the wake of the Greco-Turkish War (1919–1922). AEK B.C. is considered by as one of the three best Greek clubs in basketball and Greek sports club, as it maintains more than 30 sports' departments. AEK was the first-ever Greek basketball team, not only to reach a European Cup Final, but also to win a European title.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AEK_B.C."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "vallées", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 417, "target_cluster_name": "vallées", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Chillo Valley is a specific instance of a valley, making it a related entity.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Chillo Valley is a specific instance of a valley, making it a related entity.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Cumacatar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cumacatar", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cumacatar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cumacatar"], "contexts": ["location of hot springs"]}, {"entity_id": "cumaca", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cumaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cumaca"], "contexts": ["province where Cinnamon grows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cumacatar", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cumaca", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 4075, "stable_key": "clu_02e8370c484f568e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cumacatar", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "Likely refers to a location in the Amazon basin where medicinal plants were found."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "orographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "orographie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "orographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orographie"], "contexts": ["study of mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "orography", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "orography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orography"], "contexts": ["study of mountain ranges"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "orographie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "orography", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 4076, "stable_key": "clu_90d046f5ab8b068f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q129292457", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Jabal Hafeet (Arabic: جَبَل حَفِيْت, romanized: Jabal Ḥafīt, \"Mount Hafeet\"; variously transcribed Jabel or Jebal and Hafit – literally \"empty mountain\") is a mountain in the region of Tawam, on the border of the United Arab Emirates and Oman. It is often considered an outlier of the Hajar Mountains in Eastern Arabia due to its proximity to the main range. To the north is the UAE city of Al Ain, in the Eastern Region of the Emirate of Abu Dhabi, and the adjacent Omani town of Al-Buraimi. The sole mountain in the Emirate of Abu Dhabi, and one of the highest mountains in the country, it has given its name to a period in UAE history, the Hafit Period of the Bronze Age (3200 to 2600 BCE), because of the discovery of a cluster of important beehive tombs at its foothills. As of 2017, the mountain is recognised as being part of a national park, and was incorporated into the Sheikh Zayed Network of Protected Areas in 2018. Jebel Hafeet has a hot desert climate (BWh) according to the Köppen climate classification.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jebel_Hafeet"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dolomit", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dolomit"], "contexts": ["mineral discussed in a geological work"]}, {"entity_id": "dolomit", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dolomit"], "contexts": ["type of rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dolomit", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dolomit", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4077, "stable_key": "clu_018bb2ddf3c151be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "dolomite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "Limestone is a type of carbonate sedimentary rock which is the main source of the material lime. It is composed mostly of the minerals calcite and aragonite, which are different crystal forms of calcium carbonate CaCO3. Limestone forms when these minerals precipitate out of water containing dissolved calcium. This can take place through both biological and nonbiological processes, though biological processes, such as the accumulation of corals and shells in the sea, have likely been more important for the last 540 million years. Limestone often contains fossils which provide scientists with information on ancient environments and on the evolution of life. About 20% to 25% of sedimentary rock is carbonate rock, and most of this is limestone.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limestone"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "porto_seguro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porto Seguro"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "porto_seguro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porto Seguro"], "contexts": ["location north of Os Ilheos"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Porto Seguro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Porto Seguro", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 4078, "stable_key": "clu_6a3c563ea4eb60e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porto Seguro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q2596732", "wikidata_description": "microregion in Bahia, Brazil"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Saint- Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saint-_paul", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saint- Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saint- Paul"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "st__paul", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Paul"], "contexts": ["city in diamond district"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saint- Paul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Paul", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4079, "stable_key": "clu_ac6d67d9fcc6db87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint-Paul", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "Given the context of diamonds, this likely refers to Saint-Paul, a commune in the Nord department of France, known for its historical industrial importance, rather than other places named Saint-Paul.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saint Paul (often abbreviated to St. Paul) is the capital city of the U.S. state of Minnesota and the county seat of Ramsey County. As of the 2020 census, the city had a population of 311,527, making it Minnesota's second-most populous city and the 63rd-most populous in the United States. Saint Paul and neighboring Minneapolis form the core of the Twin Cities metropolitan area, the third most populous in the Midwest with around 3.7 million residents. The Minnesota State Capitol and the state government offices sit on a hill next to downtown Saint Paul overlooking a bend in the Mississippi River.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paul,_Minnesota"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Fertentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fertentes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fertentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fertentes"], "contexts": ["mountain range connected to Serra do Espinhaço"]}, {"entity_id": "pregny", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pregny", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pregny"], "contexts": ["location near Geneva"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fertentes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pregny", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 4080, "stable_key": "clu_04fe636bdc03adc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fermentes", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "The text provides two conflicting references: 'Fertentes' in the context of Brazil and 'Pregny' near Geneva. 'Fertentes' is more likely the intended referent given the other Brazilian locations, and may refer to a specific part of the Serra do Espinhaço."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhodes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodes"], "contexts": ["island"]}, {"entity_id": "rhodes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodes"], "contexts": ["island city, source of spice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rhodes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Rhodes", "similarity": 0.846}], "id": 4081, "stable_key": "clu_7a70035c1af64d48", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Rhodes ( ; Greek: Ρόδος, romanized: Ródos [ˈroðos]) is the largest of Greece's Dodecanese islands and their historical capital; it is the ninth largest island in the  Mediterranean Sea. Administratively, Rhodes constitutes a separate municipality within the Rhodes regional unit, which is part of the South Aegean administrative region. The principal town of the island and seat of the municipality is the city of Rhodes, home to 50,636 inhabitants according to the 2011 census. By 2022, the island’s population had grown to 125,113 people. Located northeast of Crete and southeast of Athens, Rhodes is often referred to by several nicknames: the \"Island of the Sun\" after its patron sun god Helios; \"The Pearl Island\"; and \"The Island of the Knights\", a reference to the Knights of Saint John of Jerusalem, who ruled the island from 1310 to 1522. Historically, Rhodes was famous for the Colossus of Rhodes, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vasiva", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vasiva"], "contexts": ["city name"]}, {"entity_id": "vasiva", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vasiva"], "contexts": ["Mission where natives use inelegant terms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Vasiva", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Vasiva", "similarity": 0.666}], "id": 4082, "stable_key": "clu_a3ffd644c747c80b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vasiva", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "The primary context suggests a Brazilian location, possibly a mission settlement. 'Vasiva' is likely a place name, though its modern equivalent is uncertain without further textual context."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "itacolumite", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Itacolumite"], "contexts": ["type of rock"]}, {"entity_id": "quartzose_itacolumite", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quartzose itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["quartzose itacolumite"], "contexts": ["rock containing quartz and itacolumite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Itacolumite", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quartzose itacolumite", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 4083, "stable_key": "clu_668299d64a0e7d8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Itacolumite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q128241969", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "granite", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1634, "target_cluster_name": "granite", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are types of rock, potentially discussed in relation to geological formations.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are types of rock, potentially discussed in relation to geological formations.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "basalt", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1665, "target_cluster_name": "basalt", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Itacolumite is a type of metamorphic rock that can be formed from the alteration of basalt.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Itacolumite is a type of metamorphic rock that can be formed from the alteration of basalt.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "feldspar", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1948, "target_cluster_name": "feldspar", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Itacolumite is a type of sandstone that is often flexible and contains feldspar grains.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Itacolumite is a type of sandstone that is often flexible and contains feldspar grains.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "formations trapéennes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "formations_trapéennes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "formations trapéennes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["formations trapéennes"], "contexts": ["rock formations bordering plains"]}, {"entity_id": "mineral_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mineral formations", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mineral formations"], "contexts": ["rocks and minerals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "formations trapéennes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mineral formations", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 4084, "stable_key": "clu_a809fce32eb0c2e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Travertine formations", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The French 'trapéennes' likely refers to travertine, a form of limestone deposited by mineral springs."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "allemagne_septentrionale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Allemagne septentrionale"], "contexts": ["northern Germany"]}, {"entity_id": "southern_germany", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Southern Germany", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Southern Germany"], "contexts": ["geographic location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Southern Germany", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 4085, "stable_key": "clu_b7e5d0113531790c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Northern Germany", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q46613", "wikidata_description": "geographic region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Germany", "wikipedia_extract": "Northern Germany (German: Norddeutschland, [ˈnɔʁtdɔɪ̯tʃlant] ) is a linguistic, geographic, socio-cultural and historic region in the northern part of Germany which includes the coastal states of Schleswig-Holstein, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern and Lower Saxony and the two city-states Hamburg and Bremen. It contrasts with Southern Germany, Western Germany, and Eastern Germany. Northern Germany generally refers to the Sprachraum area north of the Uerdingen and Benrath line isoglosses, where Low German dialects are spoken. These comprise the Low Saxon dialects in the west (including the Westphalian language area up to the Rhineland), the East Low German region along the Baltic coast with Western Pomerania, the Altmark and northern Brandenburg, as well as the North Low German dialects. Although from the 19th century onwards, the use of Standard German was strongly promoted especially by the Prussian administration, Low German dialects are still present in rural areas, with an estimated number of five to eight million active speakers. However, since World War II and the immigration of expellees from the former eastern territories of Germany, its prevalence has steadily reduced."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "topazes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["topazes"], "contexts": ["stones found on the coast"]}, {"entity_id": "topazes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["topazes"], "contexts": ["magnificent crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "topazes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "topazes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4086, "stable_key": "clu_c75b23090a14d63b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Topaz", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q134985", "wikidata_description": "nesosilicate mineral"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Thèbes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thèbes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Thèbes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thèbes"], "contexts": ["ancient city in Egypt"]}, {"entity_id": "thebes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thebes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thebes"], "contexts": ["ancient Egyptian city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Thèbes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Thebes", "similarity": 0.819}], "id": 4087, "stable_key": "clu_2d8d4009dd3b3c2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thebes", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Egypt", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Thebes ( THEEBZ; Greek: Θήβα, Thíva [ˈθiva]; Ancient Greek: Θῆβαι, Thêbai [tʰɛ̂ːbai̯]) is a city in Boeotia, Central Greece, and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world. It is the largest city in Boeotia and a major center for the region, along with Livadeia and Tanagra. It played an important role in Greek mythology, as the site of the stories of Cadmus, Oedipus, Dionysus, Heracles and others. One myth had the city founded by Agenor, which gave rise to the (now somewhat obscure) name \"Agenorids\" to denote Thebans. Archaeological excavations in and around Thebes have revealed a Mycenaean settlement and clay tablets written in the Linear B script, indicating the importance of the site in the Bronze Age. Thebes was the largest city of the ancient region of Boeotia and was the leader of the Boeotian confederacy.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thebes,_Greece"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gaillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gaillard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gaillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gaillard"], "contexts": ["author in Annales de Chimie"]}, {"entity_id": "tillard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tillard"], "contexts": ["on the sudden appearance of Sabrina"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gaillard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tillard", "similarity": 0.866}], "id": 4088, "stable_key": "clu_a403b07f7d99630d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste Gaillard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1759, "death_year": 1834, "description": "French chemist and editor of Annales de Chimie", "note": "The name 'Tillard' in the second mention is likely a typo or misreading, given the context and the consistent mention of Gaillard in relation to Annales de Chimie.", "wikidata_id": "Q22209598", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "brèche", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "brèche", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "brè