nits of muscle tissue' and 'fibers within muscles' directly correspond to the biological term 'muscle fibers'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical resultants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_resultants", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mechanical resultants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical resultants"], "contexts": ["outcome of combined mechanical forces"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_result", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical result", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical result"], "contexts": ["change of position or motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mechanical resultants", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical result", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 4191, "stable_key": "clu_ccc3631fdb312057", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Resultant force", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "spirit of the age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "spirit_of_the_age", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spirit of the age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spirit of the age"], "contexts": ["symbolic speech for collective sentiment"]}, {"entity_id": "spirit_of_the_age", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spirit of the age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spirit of the age"], "contexts": ["prevailing intellectual and cultural climate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spirit of the age", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spirit of the age", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4192, "stable_key": "clu_ac243df98f71d3a1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zeitgeist", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q211679", "wikidata_description": "intellectual fashion or dominant school of thought that typifies and influences the culture of a particular period in time"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mental affections", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mental_affections", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mental affections", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental affections"], "contexts": ["subjective mental states"]}, {"entity_id": "affections", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "affections", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["affections"], "contexts": ["emotional states or desires"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mental affections", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affections", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 4193, "stable_key": "clu_410fcd79eefa7aaa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental disorders", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Mental disorders"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "empirical law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "empirical_law", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "empirical law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["empirical law"], "contexts": ["law based on observation and experience"]}, {"entity_id": "empirical_laws", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "empirical laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["empirical laws"], "contexts": ["laws derived from observation and experiment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "empirical law", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "empirical laws", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 4194, "stable_key": "clu_c64472f0b1f07754", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Empirical law", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3258494", "wikidata_description": "law or formula deduced from experimental facts, or validated by experience", "wikipedia_extract": "Zipf's law () is an empirical law stating that when a set of measured values is sorted in decreasing order, the value of the n-th entry is often approximately inversely proportional to n. The best-known instance of Zipf’s law applies to the frequency distribution of words in a text or corpus of natural language: {\\displaystyle \\ {\\mathsf {word\\ frequency}}\\ \\propto \\ {\\frac {1}{\\ {\\mathsf {word\\ rank}}\\ }}~.} It is usually found that the most common word occurs approximately twice as often as the next common one, three times as often as the third most common, and so on. For example, in the Brown Corpus of American English text, the word \"the\" is the most frequently occurring word, and by itself accounts for nearly 7% of all word occurrences (69,971 out of slightly over 1 million). True to Zipf's law, the second-place word \"of\" accounts for slightly over 3.5% of words (36,411 occurrences), followed by \"and\" (28,852).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zipf's_law"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "tendency", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1462, "target_cluster_name": "tendency", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Tendencies, especially seasonal ones, can be observed and formulated as empirical laws.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Tendencies, especially seasonal ones, can be observed and formulated as empirical laws.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "theory", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 68, "target_cluster_name": "theory", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical laws can be explained by or incorporated into theories.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Empirical laws can be explained by or incorporated into theories.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "law", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 377, "target_cluster_name": "law", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The term 'law' in 'law of nature' can refer to an empirical generalization derived from observation, similar to how 'empirical law' is used. Both terms describe observed regularities.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The term 'law' in 'law of nature' can refer to an empirical generalization derived from observation, similar to how 'empirical law' is used. Both terms describe observed regularities.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "laws", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 489, "target_cluster_name": "laws", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical laws are a specific type of scientific law derived from observation and experiment, fitting within the broader concept of scientific law.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Empirical laws are a specific type of scientific law derived from observation and experiment, fitting within the broader concept of scientific law.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "discoveries", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 505, "target_cluster_name": "discoveries", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical laws are often the result of discoveries.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Empirical laws are often the result of discoveries.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "phenomenon", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2306, "target_cluster_name": "phenomenon", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Empirical laws are generalizations derived from observing phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Empirical laws are generalizations derived from observing phenomena.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "positivistic", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2805, "target_cluster_name": "positivistic", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Positivism emphasizes empirical observation and verification as the basis for knowledge, making empirical laws a core concept within its framework.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Positivism emphasizes empirical observation and verification as the basis for knowledge, making empirical laws a core concept within its framework.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "feeling centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "feeling_centres", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "feeling centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["feeling centres"], "contexts": ["brain areas for tactile sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "percipient_centres", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "percipient centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["percipient centres"], "contexts": ["brain areas for processing perception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "feeling centres", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "percipient centres", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 4195, "stable_key": "clu_eb90ece8dd6a135f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cortical areas for sensation and perception", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms are somewhat vague and could refer to general sensory or perceptual processing areas in the brain, not specific localized 'centers'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "atom", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "atom", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "atom", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["atom"], "contexts": ["basic unit of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "atom", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "atom", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["atom"], "contexts": ["basic unit of matter with nucleus and atmosphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "atom", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "atom", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4196, "stable_key": "clu_413db76145d6abf0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "atom", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "spiritualistic opinion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "spiritualistic_opinion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spiritualistic opinion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiritualistic opinion"], "contexts": ["view of the soul as metaphysical entity"]}, {"entity_id": "theological_opinion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "theological opinion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["theological opinion"], "contexts": ["beliefs about God and religion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spiritualistic opinion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "theological opinion", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 4197, "stable_key": "clu_dcfcd5956935d950", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "religious belief", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'spiritualistic opinion' in the context of James's 'Principles of Psychology' refers to beliefs that attributed mental phenomena to spiritual forces or entities, which falls under the broader category of religious or theological belief systems.", "wikidata_id": "Q1482612", "wikidata_description": "religion in the country"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "internal activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "internal_activity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "internal activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["internal activity"], "contexts": ["scholastic principle of mind"]}, {"entity_id": "internal_action", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "internal action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["internal action"], "contexts": ["mental or psychological processes"]}, {"entity_id": "psychic_process", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychic process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychic process"], "contexts": ["mental processes underlying behavior"]}, {"entity_id": "psychological_process", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "psychological process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychological process"], "contexts": ["mental processes and their determination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "internal activity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "internal action", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 4198, "stable_key": "clu_cf0ff8f38d0ab484", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mental process", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Internal activity' and 'internal action' in these philosophical texts refer to the workings of the mind or consciousness.", "wikidata_id": "Q12047513", "wikidata_description": "concept in psychology that refers to the sequence of thoughts, emotions, and behaviors that reflect the mind’s function and interaction with the environment"}, "member_count": 4, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "criminals", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "criminals", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "criminals", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["criminals"], "contexts": ["individuals who commit crimes"]}, {"entity_id": "criminale", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Criminale", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Criminale"], "contexts": ["criminal magistrate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "criminals", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Criminale", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 4199, "stable_key": "clu_68b1ddfd05a876bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Criminals", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Individuals who have committed offenses against the law.", "wikidata_id": "Q2159907", "wikidata_description": "person who has committed a crime"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "moral feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "moral_feeling", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "moral feeling", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["moral feeling"], "contexts": ["sense of right and wrong"]}, {"entity_id": "moral_sentiments", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "moral sentiments", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["moral sentiments"], "contexts": ["feelings related to right and wrong"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "moral feeling", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "moral sentiments", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 4200, "stable_key": "clu_39b199efc568917b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moral feeling", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Conscientiousness", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2716, "target_cluster_name": "Conscientiousness", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Conscience is a specific type of moral feeling, concerned with the internal sense of right and wrong.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Conscience is a specific type of moral feeling, concerned with the internal sense of right and wrong.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "contractions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "contractions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "contractions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["contractions"], "contexts": ["involuntary muscle shortening"]}, {"entity_id": "contracções", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Contracções", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["Contracções"], "contexts": ["symptom of excess acidity of pancreatic juice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "contractions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Contracções", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 4201, "stable_key": "clu_c4d8eeb16ddabbeb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "contractions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "'Contractions' and 'Contracções' refer to the involuntary shortening of muscles, a common medical symptom.", "wikidata_id": "Q127006", "wikidata_description": "process in which force is generated within muscle tissue, resulting in a change in muscle geometry. Force generation involves a chemo-mechanical energy conversion step that is carried out by the actin/myosin complex activity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscle_contraction", "wikipedia_extract": "Muscle contraction is the activation of tension-generating sites within muscle cells. In physiology, muscle contraction does not necessarily mean muscle shortening because muscle tension can be produced without changes in muscle length (isometric contraction), such as when holding something heavy in the same position. The termination of muscle contraction is followed by muscle relaxation, which is a return of the muscle fibers to their low tension-generating state. For the contractions to happen, the muscle cells must rely on the change in action of two types of filament: thin and thick filaments. The major constituent of thin filaments is a chain formed by helical coiling of two strands of actin, and thick filaments dominantly consist of chains of the motor-protein myosin. Together, these two filaments form myofibrils - the basic functional organelles in the skeletal muscle system."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Muscles", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 265, "target_cluster_name": "muscles", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Muscles are the biological structures that perform muscular contraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Muscles are the biological structures that perform muscular contraction."}, {"found_name": "colica", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 547, "target_cluster_name": "colica", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Colic involves muscular contractions (peristalsis) to force a blockage.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Colic involves muscular contractions (peristalsis) to force a blockage.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Cramps", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1602, "target_cluster_name": "Cramps", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Cramps are a form of involuntary muscle contraction.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Cramps are a form of involuntary muscle contraction.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "sinapisme", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sinapisme", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sinapisme", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["sinapisme"], "contexts": ["medical application for skin irritation"]}, {"entity_id": "senapismi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Senapismi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senapismi"], "contexts": ["preparation for mustard plasters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sinapisme", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Senapismi", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 4202, "stable_key": "clu_b86113fc7afd1967", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sinapism", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "note": "'Sinapisme' and 'Senapismi' refer to a medicinal plaster made with mustard, a common historical remedy.", "wikidata_id": "Q1862320", "wikidata_description": "medicinal poultice"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Algiers", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "algiers", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Algiers", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Algiers"], "contexts": ["location mentioned in a subject's delusion"]}, {"entity_id": "algiers", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Algiers", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Algiers"], "contexts": ["French officers observed light phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Algiers", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Algiers", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4203, "stable_key": "clu_92720f60a6b6aa80", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Algiers", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Algeria", "wikidata_id": "Q3561", "wikidata_description": "capital city of Algeria", "wikipedia_extract": "Algiers is the capital city of Algeria, located on the Mediterranean Sea in the north-central portion of the country. In 2025, an estimated 4.325 million people resided within the urban area. Algiers is the largest city in Algeria, the third-largest city on the Mediterranean, the sixth-largest city in the Arab world, and the 29th-largest city in Africa by population. Algiers is the capital of the Algiers Province; it extends over many communes without having its own separate governing body. It extends along the Bay of Algiers surrounded by the Mitidja Plain and major mountain ranges. Its favorable location made it the center of Ottoman and French influences for the region, shaping it to be a diverse metropolis.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algiers"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "dynamically", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dynamically", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "dynamically", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dynamically"], "contexts": ["related to action and effect"]}, {"entity_id": "dynamic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "dynamic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dynamic"], "contexts": ["surrounding the terrestrial globe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "dynamically", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dynamic", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 4204, "stable_key": "clu_9c174c4363d6b051", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dynamically", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1268978", "wikidata_description": "type system paradigm in which objects' types are enforced at runtime"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "article", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "article", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "article", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["article"], "contexts": ["grammatical word like 'the'"]}, {"entity_id": "articles", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "articles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["articles"], "contexts": ["determiners like 'a', 'an', 'the'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "article", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "articles", "similarity": 0.836}], "id": 4205, "stable_key": "clu_3948af3424d6529a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "determiner", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to grammatical articles like 'a', 'an', 'the'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Amer. Jour. of Science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "amer__jour__of_science", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Amer. Jour. of Science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amer. Jour. of Science"], "contexts": ["scientific journal"]}, {"entity_id": "amer__journ__of_science", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amer. Journ. of Science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amer. Journ. of Science"], "contexts": ["published description of comet nucleus and tail"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Amer. Jour. of Science", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Amer. Journ. of Science", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 4206, "stable_key": "clu_cbae61e9be0bfb1f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "American Journal of Science", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "A historical scientific journal."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Revue Philosophique", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1823, "target_cluster_name": "Revue Philosophique", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The American Journal of Science is a prominent scientific journal. The Revue Philosophique, while focused on philosophy, would likely engage with or reference scientific developments and journals like the American Journal of Science, especially when philosophical implications of scientific discoveries are discussed.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The American Journal of Science is a prominent scientific journal. The Revue Philosophique, while focused on philosophy, would likely engage with or reference scientific developments and journals like the American Journal of Science, especially when philosophical implications of scientific discoveries are discussed.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Comptes Rendus de l'Acad. des Sciences", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2585, "target_cluster_name": "Comptes Rendus de l'Acad. des Sciences", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are prominent scientific journals from the 19th century that published research across various scientific disciplines, including those covered by the Comptes Rendus.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are prominent scientific journals from the 19th century that published research across various scientific disciplines, including those covered by the Comptes Rendus.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "C. Wolf", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "c__wolf", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "C. Wolf", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["C. Wolf"], "contexts": ["philosopher cited for rational psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "wolf", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Wolf", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wolf"], "contexts": ["Professor, computed solar heat variation periods"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "C. Wolf", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wolf", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 4207, "stable_key": "clu_83fcefd150194754", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christian Wolff", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1679, "death_year": 1754, "description": "German philosopher, philosopher of rational psychology", "note": "The context of 'rational psychology' strongly points to Christian Wolff.", "wikidata_id": "Q76510", "wikidata_description": "German philosopher (1679–1754)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Wolf", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1463, "target_cluster_name": "Wolf", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Caspar Friedrich Wolff and Christian Wolff are often confused or conflated in historical texts due to the similarity in their names and the fact that Christian Wolff was a prominent philosopher and jurist of the same era. While Caspar Friedrich Wolff is known for his work in embryology and biology, Christian Wolff's philosophical contributions were also highly influential.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Caspar Friedrich Wolff and Christian Wolff are often confused or conflated in historical texts due to the similarity in their names and the fact that Christian Wolff was a prominent philosopher and jurist of the same era. While Caspar Friedrich Wolff is known for his work in embryology and biology, Christian Wolff's philosophical contributions were also highly influential.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "excluded middle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "excluded_middle", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "excluded middle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["excluded middle"], "contexts": ["logical principle"]}, {"entity_id": "excluded_middle", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "excluded middle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["excluded middle"], "contexts": ["logical principle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "excluded middle", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "excluded middle", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4208, "stable_key": "clu_5277c3239dce8b4b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Law of Excluded Middle", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q468422", "wikidata_description": "logical principle stating that for every proposition, either it or its negation is true", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law_of_excluded_middle", "wikipedia_extract": "In logic, the semantic principle (or law) of bivalence states that every declarative sentence expressing a proposition (of a theory under inspection) has exactly one truth value, either true or false. A logic satisfying this principle is called a two-valued logic or bivalent logic. In formal logic, the principle of bivalence becomes a property that a semantics may or may not possess. It is not the same as the law of excluded middle, however, and a semantics may satisfy that law without being bivalent. The principle of bivalence is studied in philosophical logic to address the question of which natural-language statements have a well-defined truth value. Sentences that predict events in the future, and sentences that seem open to interpretation, are particularly difficult for philosophers who hold that the principle of bivalence applies to all declarative natural-language statements."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "object of the thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "object_of_the_thought", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "object of the thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["object of the thought"], "contexts": ["content or focus of a mental idea"]}, {"entity_id": "object_of_thought", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "object of thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["object of thought"], "contexts": ["antithesis of subject"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "object of the thought", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "object of thought", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 4209, "stable_key": "clu_f51b446ba027009a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "object of thought", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "vocabulary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vocabulary", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "vocabulary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vocabulary"], "contexts": ["set of words used in a language"]}, {"entity_id": "vocabulary", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vocabulary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vocabulary"], "contexts": ["limited for peasant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vocabulary", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vocabulary", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4210, "stable_key": "clu_c3907d859810940f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vocabulary", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "intercourse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "intercourse", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intercourse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intercourse"], "contexts": ["communication and social interaction"]}, {"entity_id": "intercourse", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "intercourse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intercourse"], "contexts": ["social interaction and communication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "intercourse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "intercourse", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4211, "stable_key": "clu_f3d59a9a958825bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "social interaction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q609298", "wikidata_description": "interactions between two or more individuals (more narrowly humans)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "inanimate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "inanimate", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inanimate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inanimate"], "contexts": ["not alive"]}, {"entity_id": "inanimate_things", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "inanimate things", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inanimate things"], "contexts": ["non-living objects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inanimate", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "inanimate things", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 4212, "stable_key": "clu_d61ade84ccdeecae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "inanimate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q51927539", "wikidata_description": "grammatical and/or semantic category of nouns"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "annihilation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "annihilation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "annihilation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["annihilation"], "contexts": ["complete destruction"]}, {"entity_id": "annihilation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "annihilation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["annihilation"], "contexts": ["idea of matter ceasing to exist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "annihilation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "annihilation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4213, "stable_key": "clu_a94c4aecd666840e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "annihilation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "symbols", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "symbols", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "symbols", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["symbols"], "contexts": ["representations of ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "symbols", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "symbols", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["symbols"], "contexts": ["signs representing ideas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "symbols", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "symbols", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4214, "stable_key": "clu_8c2d13804001ae94", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "symbols", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3241972", "wikidata_description": "symbol or sign (concept unrelated to computing)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "draught", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "draught", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "draught", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["draught"], "contexts": ["pulling heavy loads"]}, {"entity_id": "draught", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "draught", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["draught"], "contexts": ["pulling of a load"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "draught", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "draught", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4215, "stable_key": "clu_6f7e52ec95df7a38", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Draft", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'draught' can refer to a gulp of liquid, in the context of 'pulling heavy loads' and 'pulling of a load', it refers to a force or movement, often in the context of mechanics or physical exertion.", "wikidata_id": "Q31603", "wikidata_description": "process used to allocate certain players to sports teams", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draft_%28boating%29", "wikipedia_extract": "The NBA draft is an annual National Basketball Association's (NBA) event, dating back to 1947, in which the teams in the league draft players to join their organizations who are eligible and who declare for the draft. The current NBA consists of 30 teams, and all thirty teams have at least one draft pick throughout the two draft rounds. Historically, the vast majority of players drafted into the NBA are college basketball players. It is now common for players to also be drafted from international professional leagues, the G League Ignite team, and youth professional basketball leagues. College players who have finished their four-year college eligibility are automatically eligible for selection, while the underclassmen have to declare their eligibility and give up their remaining college eligibility. International players who are at least 22 years old are automatically eligible for selection, while the players younger than 22 have to declare their eligibility."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "psychic constitution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychic_constitution", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychic constitution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychic constitution"], "contexts": ["mental makeup of a section"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_constitution", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental constitution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental constitution"], "contexts": ["innate structure of the mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychic constitution", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mental constitution", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 4216, "stable_key": "clu_8cc7f4fd28e177a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mental constitution", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "attitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "attitude", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "attitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attitude"], "contexts": ["typical posture of an object"]}, {"entity_id": "attitude_of_mind", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "attitude of mind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attitude of mind"], "contexts": ["mental disposition during discussion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attitude", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attitude of mind", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 4217, "stable_key": "clu_75ab9c5c1a8830f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "attitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Max Müller", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "max_müller", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Max Müller", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Max Müller"], "contexts": ["scholar on science of thought"]}, {"entity_id": "max_müller", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Max Müller", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Max Müller"], "contexts": ["linguist, proposed language origin theory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Max Müller", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Max Müller", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4218, "stable_key": "clu_6b0ecf03a7b0587f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Max Müller", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1823, "death_year": 1900, "description": "German-British scholar, linguist, and orientalist"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "concrete", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "concrete", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "concrete", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["concrete"], "contexts": ["view of spiritual self as a whole"]}, {"entity_id": "concrete", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "concrete", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["concrete"], "contexts": ["physical, tangible things"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "concrete", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "concrete", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4219, "stable_key": "clu_f661403bd5fb1618", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "concrete", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the tangible, physical aspects contrasted with the abstract or spiritual.", "wikidata_id": "Q223557", "wikidata_description": "singular aggregation of tangible substance(s) such as matter or radiation, with overall properties"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fictions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fictions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fictions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fictions"], "contexts": ["imaginary or invented things"]}, {"entity_id": "fictions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fictions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fictions"], "contexts": ["unverifiable and illusory mental representations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fictions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fictions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4220, "stable_key": "clu_c2823fda251155a3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fiction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to imaginary or invented things, or unverifiable mental representations."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "negating", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "negating", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "negating", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["negating"], "contexts": ["act of denying or disagreeing"]}, {"entity_id": "negation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "negation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["negation"], "contexts": ["denial or opposite of something"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "negating", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "negation", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 4221, "stable_key": "clu_3712a0e0f2de11ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "negation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the act of denying or disagreeing, or the state of being the opposite."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "shame", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "shame", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "shame", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["shame"], "contexts": ["feeling of humiliation or distress"]}, {"entity_id": "shame", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "shame", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["shame"], "contexts": ["feeling of humiliation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shame", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "shame", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4222, "stable_key": "clu_28e7cd6e53526abe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Shame", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the emotion of shame and related expressions.", "wikidata_id": "Q152282", "wikidata_description": "a self-conscious emotion associated with feelings of worthlessness or humiliation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "wikipedia_extract": "Shame is an unpleasant self-conscious and moral emotion often associated with negative self-evaluation; motivation to quit; and feelings of pain, exposure, distrust, powerlessness, and worthlessness. Shame is a discrete, basic emotion, described as a moral or social emotion that drives people to hide or deny their wrongdoings. Moral emotions are emotions that have an influence on a person's decision-making skills and monitors different social behaviors. The focus of shame is on the self or the individual with respect to a perceived audience. It can bring about profound feelings of deficiency, defeat, inferiority, unworthiness, or self-loathing. Our attention turns inward; we isolate from our surroundings and withdraw into closed-off self-absorption."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mortification", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mortification", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mortification", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mortification"], "contexts": ["extreme embarrassment or shame"]}, {"entity_id": "mortificação", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mortificação", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mortificação"], "contexts": ["ascetic practice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mortification", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mortificação", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 4223, "stable_key": "clu_c22da4613695c7c7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mortification", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context in James's 'Principles of Psychology' points towards extreme embarrassment or shame, a psychological concept. The Portuguese 'mortificação' in a medicinal context could refer to gangrene or necrosis (literal death of tissue), but the given context leans more towards the psychological. Given the mixed contexts, the primary psychological meaning is chosen."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "self-satisfaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "self-satisfaction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "self-satisfaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["self-satisfaction"], "contexts": ["feeling of contentment with oneself"]}, {"entity_id": "self-satisfaction", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "self-satisfaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["self-satisfaction"], "contexts": ["fulfillment of one's own desires"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "self-satisfaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "self-satisfaction", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4224, "stable_key": "clu_b245e834eb9b7abb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "self-satisfaction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q17992551", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "hunting", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hunting", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hunting", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hunting"], "contexts": ["instinct to pursue and capture prey"]}, {"entity_id": "hunter", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hunter", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hunter"], "contexts": ["role in early society"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hunting", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hunter", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 4225, "stable_key": "clu_36807f690ec361af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hunting", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the activity of pursuing and capturing prey, both as an instinct and a social activity."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "domesticated animals", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2718, "target_cluster_name": "domesticated animals", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hunting is often contrasted with or seen as a precursor to the domestication of animals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hunting is often contrasted with or seen as a precursor to the domestication of animals.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "prey", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1117, "target_cluster_name": "prey", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hunting is the activity directly associated with the concept of prey, as it describes the act of pursuing and capturing prey.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hunting is the activity directly associated with the concept of prey, as it describes the act of pursuing and capturing prey.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "admiration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "admiration", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "admiration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["admiration"], "contexts": ["respect and approval"]}, {"entity_id": "admiration", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "admiration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["admiration"], "contexts": ["feeling of wonder"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "admiration", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "admiration", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4226, "stable_key": "clu_6b26f3a8a9ffddb2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "admiration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2824657", "wikidata_description": "social emotion"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "African explorer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "african_explorer", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "African explorer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["African explorer"], "contexts": ["one who explores the continent of Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "african_travellers", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "African travellers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["African travellers"], "contexts": ["people who travel in Africa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "African explorer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "African travellers", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 4227, "stable_key": "clu_48523e8598ccc38b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "African explorer", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Individual who undertakes expeditions to explore Africa.", "note": "This is a descriptive category rather than a specific individual. The term 'African explorers' or 'African travellers' in early modern texts often referred to a group of individuals known for their journeys into the interior of Africa. Without specific names, identifying individual modern referents is impossible. The description reflects the general role.", "wikidata_id": "Q135794063", "wikidata_description": "Commemorative medals › Personality medals - Milled - German East Africa"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Columbus", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 327, "target_cluster_name": "Columbus", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "While Columbus was European, his voyages were part of a broader era of European exploration that included significant exploration of Africa by various individuals and groups. The term 'African explorer' could refer to contemporaries or predecessors involved in exploring the African continent, placing Columbus's activities within a larger context of global exploration.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: While Columbus was European, his voyages were part of a broader era of European exploration that included significant exploration of Africa by various individuals and groups. The term 'African explorer' could refer to contemporaries or predecessors involved in exploring the African continent, placing Columbus's activities within a larger context of global exploration.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Africains", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1245, "target_cluster_name": "Africains", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An African explorer is a specific type of person from Africa, making it a closely related concept.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An African explorer is a specific type of person from Africa, making it a closely related concept.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "observer", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2793, "target_cluster_name": "observer", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "An explorer is a type of observer, and an African explorer is a specific instance of this role.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: An explorer is a type of observer, and an African explorer is a specific instance of this role.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "despotism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "despotism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "despotism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["despotism"], "contexts": ["exercise of absolute power"]}, {"entity_id": "despotism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "despotism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["despotism"], "contexts": ["rule by a single absolute authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "despotism", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "despotism", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4228, "stable_key": "clu_a206bdadbbccdc9b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "dictation", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'dictation' in the sense of speaking for writing is a valid interpretation, the juxtaposed 'despotism' from Spencer's text suggests a possible metaphorical use of 'dictation' referring to absolute rule or an imposed authority. Given the ambiguity and the time frame, 'dictation' as a concept related to communication and authority is chosen.", "wikidata_id": "Q1087138", "wikidata_description": "transcription of spoken text", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictation%20%28exercise%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Dictation is the transcription of spoken text: one person who is \"dictating\" speaks and another who is \"taking dictation\" writes down the words as they are spoken. Among speakers of several languages, dictation is used as a test of language skill, similar to spelling bees in the English-speaking world. Secondary to teaching language skills, the exercise of dictation has also been used to introduce students to literary works, and to instill morals. Dictation has also been used in an attempt to capture endangered or dying languages, as in the case of Victoria Howard, a Chinook speaker who dictated songs and stories to Melville Jacobs. The word was first used in English in 1624, in the sense \"written material for dictating or transcribing\" (for example, an important manuscript or book passage). It originated from the Latin word dictātiō (in its accusative form dictātiōnem), the act of the verb dictāre (\"to say repeatedly\", \"to say words for transcription\", \"to command\"), which is the frequentative of dīcere (\"to utter\", \"to talk\") and the root of other Latinate English-words such as diction, dictionary, dictator and dictate."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "good fame", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "good_fame", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "good fame", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["good fame"], "contexts": ["reputation for good deeds"]}, {"entity_id": "fama_commum", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fama commum", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fama commum"], "contexts": ["common opinion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "good fame", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fama commum", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 4229, "stable_key": "clu_e50ddd3e16ec7d85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reputation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster encompasses concepts of public recognition, good deeds, and common opinion, all falling under the umbrella of reputation.", "wikipedia_extract": "Reputation management, refers to the influencing, controlling, enhancing, or concealing of an individual's or group's reputation. It is a marketing technique used to modify a person's or a company's reputation in a positive way. The growth of the internet and social media led to growth of reputation management companies, with search results as a core part of a client's reputation. Online reputation management (ORM) involves overseeing and influencing the search engine results related to products and services. Ethical grey areas include mug shot removal sites, astroturfing customer review sites, censoring complaints, and using search engine optimization tactics to influence results. In other cases, the ethical lines are clear; some reputation management companies are closely connected to websites that publish unverified and libelous statements about people.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reputation_management"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "vertue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2161, "target_cluster_name": "vertue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A person's reputation is often built upon their perceived virtues.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A person's reputation is often built upon their perceived virtues.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Psychologische Analysen", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychologische_analysen", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Psychologische Analysen", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Psychologische Analysen"], "contexts": ["work discussing the 'pure Ego'"]}, {"entity_id": "psychological_analysis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "psychological analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychological analysis"], "contexts": ["analysis of mental experiences"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Psychologische Analysen", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "psychological analysis", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 4230, "stable_key": "clu_1428c95245ca9bf7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Psychological Analysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the study and analysis of mental processes and experiences.", "wikidata_id": "Q7256412", "wikidata_description": "American magazine and media organization", "wikipedia_extract": "Psychoanalysis is a set of theories and techniques to discover unconscious processes and their influence on conscious thought, emotion and behavior. Based on dream interpretation, psychoanalysis is also a talk therapy method for treating mental disorders. Established in the early 1890s by Sigmund Freud, it takes into account Darwin's theory of evolution, neurology findings, ethnology reports, and, in some respects, the clinical research of his mentor Josef Breuer. Freud developed and refined the theory and practice of psychoanalysis until his death in 1939. In an encyclopedic article, he identified four foundational beliefs: \"the assumption that there are unconscious mental processes, the recognition of the theory of repression and resistance, the appreciation of the importance of sexuality and of the Oedipus complex.\" Two of Freud's early colleagues, Alfred Adler and Carl Jung, soon developed their own methods, individual and analytical psychology, respectively, which he said were not forms of psychoanalysis. After the author's death, neo-Freudian thinkers like Erich Fromm, Karen Horney and Harry Stack Sullivan created some subfields.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychoanalysis"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bodily warmth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodily_warmth", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bodily warmth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily warmth"], "contexts": ["sensation of heat from a body"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_heat", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bodily heat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily heat"], "contexts": ["internal temperature of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bodily warmth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodily heat", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 4231, "stable_key": "clu_e585de4f998189ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body temperature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q190259", "wikidata_description": "ability of an organism to keep its body temperature within certain boundaries"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Kantian Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "kantian_philosophy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Kantian Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kantian Philosophy"], "contexts": ["philosophy of Immanuel Kant"]}, {"entity_id": "kantian_doctrine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Kantian doctrine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kantian doctrine"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine about space and time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Kantian Philosophy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Kantian doctrine", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 4232, "stable_key": "clu_9cfe0971f73a9eef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kantian Philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q128820042", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Noumenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "noumenon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Noumenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noumenon"], "contexts": ["unknowable reality beyond phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "noumenon", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Noumenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noumenon"], "contexts": ["thing-in-itself, reality behind appearances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Noumenon", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Noumenon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4233, "stable_key": "clu_b1996283094c0091", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Noumenon", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Philosophical concept from Kantian philosophy.", "wikidata_id": "Q239581", "wikidata_description": "in philosophy, a posited object or event that exists independently of human sense and/or concept, unlike a phenomenon"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Reality", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 832, "target_cluster_name": "Reality", "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "still stands inside the mind. In the function of knowing there is a multiplicity to be connected, and Kant brings this multiplicity inside the mind. The Reality becomes a mere empty _locus_, or unknow", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for Reality", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "locus", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "a multiplicity to be connected, and Kant brings this multiplicity inside the mind. The Reality becomes a mere empty _locus_, or unknowable, the so-called Noumenon; the manifold phenomenon is in the mi", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for unknowable"}]}, {"canonical_name": "perceived object", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "perceived_object", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "perceived object", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["perceived object"], "contexts": ["thing that is sensed"]}, {"entity_id": "perceived_object", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "perceived object", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["perceived object"], "contexts": ["the entity that is perceived"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "perceived object", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "perceived object", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4234, "stable_key": "clu_854cb61aeceb2f1a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Perceived object", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q51984184", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published in February 2007"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "foreign object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "foreign_object", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "foreign object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["foreign object"], "contexts": ["item not belonging to the self"]}, {"entity_id": "external_object", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["external object"], "contexts": ["thing perceived as outside oneself"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "foreign object", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external object", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 4235, "stable_key": "clu_a9881c3377c5fdb7", "ground