e another in sequence or coexistence. A feeling proper is ei", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of feeling"}]}, {"canonical_name": "physiognomy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "physiognomy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "physiognomy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["physiognomy"], "contexts": ["characteristic appearance or nature"]}, {"entity_id": "vegetable_physiognomy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vegetable physiognomy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vegetable physiognomy"], "contexts": ["appearance of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "physiognomy", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vegetable physiognomy", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 4290, "stable_key": "clu_db07a373986a5d55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physiognomy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Includes both the study of human character from facial features and the appearance of plants."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sensorium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensorium", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensorium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensorium"], "contexts": ["part of brain receiving sensory input"]}, {"entity_id": "organasensuum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "organasensuum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["organasensuum"], "contexts": ["organs of sense"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensorium", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "organasensuum", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 4291, "stable_key": "clu_27ab7783e50149df", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensorium", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the sensory apparatus or the part of the brain receiving sensory input."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "nerve-tissue", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2577, "target_cluster_name": "nerve-tissue", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The part of the brain or nervous system that receives and interprets sensory information; directly involves nervous tissue.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The part of the brain or nervous system that receives and interprets sensory information; directly involves nervous tissue.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "nervous actions", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2584, "target_cluster_name": "nervous actions", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The sensorium is the part of the nervous system responsible for receiving and processing sensory information, which underlies many nervous actions.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The sensorium is the part of the nervous system responsible for receiving and processing sensory information, which underlies many nervous actions.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "handkerchief", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "handkerchief", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "handkerchief", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["handkerchief"], "contexts": ["object used for remembering"]}, {"entity_id": "handkerchief", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "handkerchief", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["handkerchief"], "contexts": ["piece of cloth for wiping"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "handkerchief", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "handkerchief", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4292, "stable_key": "clu_cdea7cf5cd9b70fa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Handkerchief", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q192411", "wikidata_description": "piece of cloth for personal use", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeon_drop", "wikipedia_extract": "The pigeon drop or Spanish handkerchief or Chilean handkerchief is a confidence trick in which a victim (the mark or pigeon) is persuaded to pay to secure the right to a supposed large sum of money or valuable object. One of the con artists will typically claim to have found the money or valuable abandoned on the ground just before talking to the mark, or will place it on the ground beforehand and pretend to happen upon it at the same time as the mark, hence the term \"drop\". Typically the scam involves two or three scammers (con artists) performing together to defraud the mark. One will arrange to \"find\" a valuable item along with the mark in a public place, such as by leaving it on the ground for the mark to find or approaching them with it in hand. A second scammer will pass by and enter the conversation, seeming to provide an independent opinion. One of the two scammers will pose as someone senior and knowledgeable, such as a lawyer, banker, or businessperson, or the senior might be a third scammer posing as a boss or authority called by one of the others."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Panno", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 654, "target_cluster_name": "Panno", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A handkerchief is a specific type of clothing item.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A handkerchief is a specific type of clothing item.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "hygiene", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hygiene", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hygiene", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hygiene"], "contexts": ["practices for maintaining health"]}, {"entity_id": "hygiene", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hygiene", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hygiene"], "contexts": ["practice influenced by anatomy and physiology"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hygiene", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hygiene", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4293, "stable_key": "clu_2b5b2a14e677c0f5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hygiene", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "salud", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 152, "target_cluster_name": "salud", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Hygiene is the practice or science of maintaining health and preventing disease, directly related to the concept of health itself.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Hygiene is the practice or science of maintaining health and preventing disease, directly related to the concept of health itself.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "tenacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tenacity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tenacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tenacity"], "contexts": ["persistence in remembering"]}, {"entity_id": "tenacity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tenacity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tenacity"], "contexts": ["strength of material to resist breaking"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tenacity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tenacity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4294, "stable_key": "clu_54726e94a097471a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tenacity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Covers both psychological persistence and material strength.", "wikidata_id": "Q7699426", "wikidata_description": "mineral's behavior when deformed or broken"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "desultory memory", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "evidence_snippet": "knowledge of them is in no way analogous to a memory properly so called. Cf. _supra_, p. 646. [584] On Intelligence, i, 258-9. [585] Not that _mere_ native tenacity will make a man great. It must be c", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "a type of memory possessed by imbeciles"}]}, {"canonical_name": "immunity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "immunity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "immunity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["immunity"], "contexts": ["resistance to harm"]}, {"entity_id": "immunidade", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "immunidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["immunidade"], "contexts": ["protection from snake bites"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "immunity", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "immunidade", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 4295, "stable_key": "clu_a163a5067f8269e3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "immunity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Both 'immunity' and 'immunidade' directly translate to the modern concept of immunity in a biological or medical context.", "wikidata_id": "Q182581", "wikidata_description": "state of being insusceptible or resistant to a noxious agent or process", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immunity%20%28medicine%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In biology, immunity is the state of being insusceptible or resistant to a noxious agent or process, especially a pathogen or infectious disease. Immunity may occur naturally or be produced by prior exposure or immunization. The immune system has innate and adaptive components. Innate immunity is present in all metazoans, immune responses: inflammatory responses and phagocytosis. The adaptive component, on the other hand, involves more advanced lymphatic cells that can distinguish between specific \"non-self\" substances in the presence of \"self\". The reaction to foreign substances is etymologically described as inflammation while the non-reaction to self substances is described as immunity."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "decay", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "decay", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "decay", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["decay"], "contexts": ["gradual decline"]}, {"entity_id": "decay", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "decay", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["decay"], "contexts": ["process of decomposition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "decay", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "decay", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4296, "stable_key": "clu_35febdc666cb6f2e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Decay", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Encompasses both biological decomposition and gradual decline.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "chemical decomposition", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1288, "target_cluster_name": "chemical decomposition", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Decay, in a chemical context, refers to the slow breakdown of a substance, often due to oxidation or other chemical reactions. This is a form of chemical decomposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Decay, in a chemical context, refers to the slow breakdown of a substance, often due to oxidation or other chemical reactions. This is a form of chemical decomposition.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "change", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1334, "target_cluster_name": "change", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Decay is a process of gradual deterioration or change, often involving decomposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Decay is a process of gradual deterioration or change, often involving decomposition.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "destruction", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2527, "target_cluster_name": "destruction", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Decay is a process of destruction or decomposition.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Decay is a process of destruction or decomposition.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Victor Hugo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 1, "total_mentions": 1, "members": [{"entity_id": "victor_hugo", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Victor Hugo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Victor Hugo"], "contexts": ["author of 'Satyr' poem"]}], "edges": [], "id": 4297, "stable_key": "clu_08924e68b8a055fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Victor Hugo", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1802, "death_year": 1885, "description": "French poet, novelist, and playwright", "note": "The reference in 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' is likely a different individual or an anachronism, as Victor Hugo lived much later. The mention in 'principles_of_psychology_james_1890' aligns with his literary output."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Æneid", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "æneid", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Æneid", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Æneid"], "contexts": ["epic poem by Virgil"]}, {"entity_id": "æneid_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Æneid.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Æneid."], "contexts": ["title of Virgil's epic poem"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Æneid", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Æneid.", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 4298, "stable_key": "clu_dd9da8eac045bb9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aeneid", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The mention in 'polyanthea_medicinal' might be a broader reference to classical texts or a specific medicinal preparation derived from classical knowledge.", "wikidata_id": "Q19537658", "wikidata_description": "1854 Portuguese translation by Mendes"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "engraving", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "engraving", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "engraving", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["engraving"], "contexts": ["print made from an engraved plate"]}, {"entity_id": "engravings", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "engravings", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["engravings"], "contexts": ["can be exactly copied by process"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "engraving", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "engravings", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 4299, "stable_key": "clu_76397b8e539dbbd2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "engraving", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The terms 'engraving' and 'engravings' refer to the artistic technique and the resulting printed image.", "wikidata_id": "Q1640824", "wikidata_description": "words, texts, lettering, or symbols marked on a work, building, or object, including texts, legends, documentation notes, or commemoration", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_engraving", "wikipedia_extract": "Wood engraving is a printmaking technique, in which an artist works an image into a block of wood. Functionally a variety of woodcut, it uses relief printing, where the artist applies ink to the face of the block and prints using relatively low pressure. The areas of the design that show ink when printed are those left uncut on the surface of the wood block. By contrast, ordinary engraving, like etching, uses a metal plate for the matrix and is printed by the intaglio method, where the ink fills the valleys, the removed areas. As a result of the relatively lower pressure used to print, the blocks for wood engravings deteriorate less quickly than the copper plates of engravings, and may have a distinctive white-on-black character. Thomas Bewick developed the wood engraving technique in Great Britain at the end of the 18th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "paper", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2517, "target_cluster_name": "paper", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Paper is a common substrate for engravings.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Paper is a common substrate for engravings.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "walls", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "walls", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "walls", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["walls"], "contexts": ["vertical structures enclosing a space"]}, {"entity_id": "walls", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "walls", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["walls"], "contexts": ["outer transformed part of heart cells"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "walls", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "walls", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4300, "stable_key": "clu_187691a79ec83eae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cell walls", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'vertical structures enclosing a space' suggests architectural walls, while 'outer transformed part of heart cells' refers to biological cell walls. The term 'walls' alone is ambiguous without further context, but in a scientific text, biological structures are more likely.", "wikidata_id": "Q21181366", "wikidata_description": "process that results in the assembly, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of the cell wall, the rigid or semi-rigid envelope lying outside the cell membrane of plant, fungal and most prokaryotic cells, maintaining their shape and prote"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Bunsen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bunsen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bunsen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bunsen"], "contexts": ["learned scholar who applied expression to Cosmos"]}, {"entity_id": "bunsen", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Bunsen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bunsen"], "contexts": ["proposed language origin theory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bunsen", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Bunsen", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4301, "stable_key": "clu_5a9beab61c5eded2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Robert Bunsen", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1811, "death_year": 1899, "description": "German chemist known for spectroscopy and the Bunsen burner", "note": "Both mentions likely refer to the influential chemist Robert Bunsen, given his prominence in scientific discourse during the period.", "wikidata_id": "Q94824670", "wikidata_description": "German physician (1808-1882)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "quantitative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "quantitative", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quantitative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quantitative"], "contexts": ["abstraction based on number and magnitude"]}, {"entity_id": "quantitative", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "quantitative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quantitative"], "contexts": ["relating to quantity or amount"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "sour