ilar Mediterranean or warm temperate climates. Historically, they might have been grouped or compared due to their shrubby habit and flowering characteristics.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Ladano", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2640, "target_cluster_name": "Ladano", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Labdanum is a resin obtained from species of Cistus (rockrose).", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Labdanum is a resin obtained from species of Cistus (rockrose).", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Carlina", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2887, "target_cluster_name": "Carlina", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Carlina is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asteraceae, commonly known as carline thistles. Cistus is a genus of flowering plants in the rockrose family, Cistaceae. Both are Mediterranean shrubs and often found in similar habitats, and historically, their medicinal uses might have been confused or grouped together due to superficial similarities in appearance or habitat.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Carlina is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asteraceae, commonly known as carline thistles. Cistus is a genus of flowering plants in the rockrose family, Cistaceae. Both are Mediterranean shrubs and often found in similar habitats, and historically, their medicinal uses might have been confused or grouped together due to superficial similarities in appearance or habitat.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "inspiration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "inspiration", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "inspiration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inspiration"], "contexts": ["mysterious revelation of laws"]}, {"entity_id": "inspiração", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "inspiração", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inspiração"], "contexts": ["act of breathing in"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "inspiration", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "inspiração", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 4306, "stable_key": "clu_5b6bdc39f6f2a83c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "inspiration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "natureza", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1530, "target_cluster_name": "natureza", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The concept of vital force can be related to the idea of inspiration, both in a physiological and metaphorical sense.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The concept of vital force can be related to the idea of inspiration, both in a physiological and metaphorical sense.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Roger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "roger", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Roger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Roger"], "contexts": ["considered measurements of Mount Blanc"]}, {"entity_id": "rogerio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rogerio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rogerio"], "contexts": ["learned author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Roger", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rogerio", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 4307, "stable_key": "clu_5355a3e05337da3c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Roger Bacon", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1214, "death_year": 1294, "description": "English philosopher, scientist, and Franciscan friar", "note": "The text mentions 'Roger' and 'Rogerio' in contexts of measurement and authorship, strongly suggesting Roger Bacon, a scholar known for scientific observations.", "wikidata_id": "Q171677", "wikidata_description": "English polymath, philosopher and friar (c.1219/20–c.1292)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "double-refracting prism", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "double-refracting_prism", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "double-refracting prism", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["double-refracting prism"], "contexts": ["optical instrument for light separation"]}, {"entity_id": "doubly-refracting_prism", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "doubly-refracting prism", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["doubly-refracting prism"], "contexts": ["optical instrument for splitting light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "double-refracting prism", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "doubly-refracting prism", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 4308, "stable_key": "clu_d5243fe5d404fd72", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Doubly-refracting prism", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikipedia_extract": "Polarimetry is the measurement and interpretation of the polarization of transverse waves, most notably electromagnetic waves, such as radio or light waves. Typically polarimetry is done on electromagnetic waves that have traveled through or have been reflected, refracted or diffracted by some material in order to characterize that object. Polarimetry is used in biomedical imaging as well as remote sensing applications, such as planetary science, astronomy, and weather radar. Polarimetry of thin films and surfaces is commonly known as ellipsometry. Polarimetry can also be included in computational analysis of waves. For example, radars often consider wave polarization in post-processing to improve the characterization of the targets.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polarimetry"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Glass", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 22, "target_cluster_name": "Glass", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Doubly-refracting prisms are a specific type of prism, typically made of glass.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Doubly-refracting prisms are a specific type of prism, typically made of glass.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "prismatic micrometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "prismatic_micrometer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "prismatic micrometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["prismatic micrometer"], "contexts": ["instrument using prisms for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "micrometer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "micrometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["micrometer"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring small distances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "prismatic micrometer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "micrometer", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 4309, "stable_key": "clu_70f953c33303dca1-3", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A microscope (from Ancient Greek  μικρός (mikrós) 'small' and  σκοπέω (skopéō) 'to look (at); examine, inspect') is a laboratory instrument used to examine objects that are too small to be seen by the naked eye. Microscopy is the science of investigating small objects and structures using a microscope. Microscopic means being invisible to the eye unless aided by a microscope. There are many types of microscopes, and they may be grouped in different ways. One way is to describe the method an instrument uses to interact with a sample and produce images, either by sending a beam of light or electrons through a sample in its optical path, by detecting photon emissions from a sample, or by scanning across and a short distance from the surface of a sample using a probe. The most common microscope (and the first to be invented) is the optical microscope, which uses lenses to refract visible light that passed through a thinly sectioned sample to produce an observable image."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "prism", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1874, "target_cluster_name": "prism", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A prismatic micrometer is an instrument that utilizes a prism for measurement.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A prismatic micrometer is an instrument that utilizes a prism for measurement.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "capsule", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "capsule", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "capsule", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["capsule"], "contexts": ["part of a moss sporophyte"]}, {"entity_id": "cápsulas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cápsulas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cápsulas"], "contexts": ["part of the poppy plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "capsule", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cápsulas", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 4310, "stable_key": "clu_ea532e1d082b11ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "capsule", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'capsule' in early modern texts can refer to a botanical structure (like in mosses) or a medicinal preparation, making it a concept with multiple applications.", "wikidata_id": "Q519410", "wikidata_description": "relatively stable shell containing medicine"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "equatorial axis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "equatorial_axis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "equatorial axis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["equatorial axis"], "contexts": ["axis of the globe at the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "equatorial_axes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equatorial axes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["equatorial axes"], "contexts": ["diameter of planet at equator"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "equatorial axis", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "equatorial axes", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 4311, "stable_key": "clu_5b6bbaeab6e3f9b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Equatorial diameter", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The 'equatorial axis' in early modern texts refers to the diameter of a celestial body or planet that lies along the equator. This is synonymous with the equatorial diameter."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "fine arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fine_arts", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fine arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fine arts"], "contexts": ["art forms valued for beauty"]}, {"entity_id": "fine_arts", "book_id": "first_princip