ype of celestial object."}]}, {"canonical_name": "condition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "condition", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "condition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["condition"], "contexts": ["state or requirement"]}, {"entity_id": "condição", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "condição", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["condição"], "contexts": ["quality or state"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "condition", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "condição", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 4318, "stable_key": "clu_0665c32d118b0081", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "condition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q12136", "wikidata_description": "abnormal condition negatively affecting organisms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disease", "wikipedia_extract": "A disease is a particular abnormal condition that adversely affects the structure or function of all or part of an organism and is not immediately due to any external injury. Diseases are often known to be medical conditions that are associated with specific signs and symptoms. A disease may be caused by external factors such as pathogens or by internal dysfunctions. For example, internal dysfunctions of the immune system can produce a variety of different diseases, including various forms of immunodeficiency, hypersensitivity, allergies, and autoimmune disorders. In humans, disease is often used more broadly to refer to any condition that causes pain, dysfunction, distress, social problems, or death to the person affected, or similar problems for those in contact with the person. In this broader sense, it sometimes includes injuries, disabilities, disorders, syndromes, infections, isolated symptoms, deviant behaviors, and atypical variations of structure and function, while in other contexts and for other purposes these may be considered distinguishable categories."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "vegetable and animal matter", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vegetable_and_animal_matter", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vegetable and animal matter", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vegetable and animal matter"], "contexts": ["substances from plants and animals"]}, {"entity_id": "animal_and_vegetable_matter", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "animal and vegetable matter", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["animal and vegetable matter"], "contexts": ["diamagnetic substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vegetable and animal matter", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "animal and vegetable matter", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 4319, "stable_key": "clu_f71e71a670c7203e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "organic matter", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to matter derived from living organisms, encompassing both plant and animal origins.", "wikidata_id": "Q1783121", "wikidata_description": "matter composed of organic compounds"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Giordano Bruno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "giordano_bruno", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Giordano Bruno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Giordano Bruno"], "contexts": ["author of philosophical work"]}, {"entity_id": "giordano_bruno", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Giordano Bruno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Giordano Bruno"], "contexts": ["Renaissance philosopher and cosmologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Giordano Bruno", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Giordano Bruno", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4320, "stable_key": "clu_fdd1dcb9603c65e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Giordano Bruno", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1548, "death_year": 1600, "description": "Renaissance philosopher, astronomer, and mathematician.", "wikidata_id": "Q36330", "wikidata_description": "Italian Dominican friar, philosopher and mathematician (1548–1600)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giordano_Bruno", "wikipedia_extract": "The unicursal hexagram is a hexagram or six-pointed star that can be traced or drawn unicursally, in one continuous line rather than by two overlaid triangles. The hexagram can also be depicted inside a circle with the points touching it. It is often depicted in an interlaced form with the lines of the hexagram passing over and under one another to form a knot. It is a specific instance of the far more general shape discussed in Blaise Pascal's 1639 Hexagrammum Mysticum. In his work titled Essays upon the Mathematics of Mordente: One Hundred and Sixty Articles against the Mathematicians and Philosophers of this Age (Prague: 1588), Italian philosopher, cosmological theorist, and Hermetic occultist Giordano Bruno used a symbol called Figura Amoris (\"figure of love\") part of the Hermetic trinity in his mathesis. This figure has a square outline; within it are the six lines of a unicursal hexagram."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Anaximenes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "anaximenes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anaximenes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "