arly in astronomy and related fields.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Royal Society is a learned society that historically supported and engaged with scientific institutions like observatories, particularly in astronomy and related fields.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fizeau", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fizeau"], "contexts": ["compared flame to silver and carbon point"]}, {"entity_id": "fizeau", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fizeau"], "contexts": ["accomplished experiment on light theory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Fizeau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fizeau", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4360, "stable_key": "clu_347bc8d9ebe22520", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hippolyte Fizeau", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1819, "death_year": 1896, "description": "French physicist known for measuring the speed of light.", "wikidata_id": "Q216814", "wikidata_description": "French physicist (1819–1896)"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Becquerel", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2216, "target_cluster_name": "Becquerel", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both were French physicists who worked on light and its properties. Fizeau is known for measuring the speed of light, and Becquerel's work on phosphorescence and the photovoltaic effect are related to light phenomena.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both were French physicists who worked on light and its properties. Fizeau is known for measuring the speed of light, and Becquerel's work on phosphorescence and the photovoltaic effect are related to light phenomena.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Melloni", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2444, "target_cluster_name": "Melloni", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Melloni was a physicist who studied heat and optics, and Fizeau was a contemporary physicist who also made important contributions to optics, particularly in measuring the speed of light.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Melloni was a physicist who studied heat and optics, and Fizeau was a contemporary physicist who also made important contributions to optics, particularly in measuring the speed of light.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "terrestrial_physics", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial physics"], "contexts": ["physics of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_physics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial physics"], "contexts": ["reasonings of terrestrial physics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "terrestrial physics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "terrestrial physics", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4361, "stable_key": "clu_f1f279d6697593b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Terrestrial physics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7703538", "wikidata_description": ""}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "meteorological phenomenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "meteorological_phenomenon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteorological phenomenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meteorological phenomenon"], "contexts": ["atmospheric event"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_phenomena", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "atmospheric phenomena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric phenomena"], "contexts": ["observable atmospheric events"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteorological phenomenon", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "atmospheric phenomena", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 4362, "stable_key": "clu_33d58efc7d6ecf6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meteorological phenomenon", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q16332653", "wikidata_description": "phenomenon that takes place in the atmosphere"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Piedmontese", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piedmontese", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Piedmontese", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piedmontese"], "contexts": ["region with valleys"]}, {"entity_id": "piedmont", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Piedmont", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piedmont"], "contexts": ["region connected to Savoy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Piedmontese", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Piedmont", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 4363, "stable_key": "clu_a4b6776312dae1bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Piedmont", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q1216", "wikidata_description": "region in North-West Italy", "wikipedia_extract": "Piedmont ( PEED-mont; Italian: Piemonte [pjeˈmonte]; Piedmontese: Piemont [pjeˈmʊŋt]) is one of the 20 regions of Italy, located in the Northwest of the country. It borders the Liguria region to the south, the Lombardy and Emilia-Romagna regions to the east, and the Aosta Valley region to the northwest. Piedmont also borders Switzerland to the north and France to the west. Piedmont has an area of 25,387.07 km2 (9,802.00 mi2), making it the 2nd-largest region of Italy in area after Sicily. With a population of 4,251,868, it is also the 7th-most populous region as of 2025. The capital of Piedmont is Turin, which was the first capital of the Kingdom of Italy, from 1861 to 1865.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piedmont"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "waves_of_sound", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["waves of sound"], "contexts": ["penetrate to us from earth's interior"]}, {"entity_id": "waves_of_sound", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["waves of sound"], "contexts": ["propagation of sound through a medium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "waves of sound", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "waves of sound", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4364, "stable_key": "clu_3238ab434f9b1ce7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sound waves", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3629144", "wikidata_description": "measure of the energy loss of sound propagation in media; phenomenon when hearing protectors help reduce acoustic flux from flowing into the ears, is measured in decibels (dBs)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Alpine chain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alpine_chain", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Alpine chain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alpine chain"], "contexts": ["mountain range in Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "alpine_chains", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Alpine chains", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alpine chains"], "contexts": ["mountain range"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Alpine chain", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Alpine chains", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 4365, "stable_key": "clu_5189105edc204b81-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alps", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major European mountain range, notable for its arctic fauna and flora.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Alps () are some of the highest and most extensive mountain ranges in Europe, stretching approximately 1,200 km (750 mi) across eight Alpine countries (from west to east): Monaco, France, Switzerland, Italy, Liechtenstein, Germany, Austria, Slovenia, and Hungary. The Alpine arch extends from Nice on the western Mediterranean to Trieste on the Adriatic and Vienna at the beginning of the Pannonian Basin. The mountains were formed over tens of millions of years as the African and Eurasian tectonic plates collided. Extreme shortening  caused by the event resulted in marine sedimentary rocks rising by thrusting and folding into high mountain peaks such as Mont Blanc and the Matterhorn. Mont Blanc spans the French-Italian border, and at 4,809 m (15,778 ft) is the highest mountain in the Alps. The Alpine region area contains 82 peaks higher than 4,000 m (13,000 ft)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cyclades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cyclades", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cyclades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cyclades"], "contexts": ["islands experiencing earthquakes"]}, {"entity_id": "cycladi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cycladi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cycladi"], "contexts": ["islands where Cippero root originates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cyclades", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cycladi", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 4366, "stable_key": "clu_a91e0f54aac7db19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cyclades", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "wikidata_id": "Q171132", "wikidata_description": "Greek island group in the Aegean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclades", "wikipedia_extract": "The Cyclades (; Greek: Κυκλάδες, romanized: Kykládes, IPA: [ciˈkla.ðes]) are an island group in the Aegean Sea, southeast of mainland Greece and a former administrative prefecture of Greece. They are one of the island groups which constitute the Aegean archipelago. The name (Κυκλάδες νήσοι, Kykládes nísoi, 'encircling islands') refers to the archipelago forming a circle around the sacred island of Delos. The largest island of the Cyclades is Naxos; the most populated, however, is Syros. The significant Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age Cycladic culture is best known for its schematic, flat sculptures carved out of the islands' pure white marble centuries before the great Middle Bronze Age Minoan civilization arose in Crete to the south. These figures have been looted from burials to satisfy a thriving Cycladic antiquities market since the early 20th century."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "grego", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 773, "target_cluster_name": "grego", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Cyclades are an island group in the Aegean Sea, part of modern-day Greece.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Cyclades are an island group in the Aegean Sea, part of modern-day Greece.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "electric process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_process", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric process"], "contexts": ["electrical phenomena in storms"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_processes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric processes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric processes"], "contexts": ["energy generation on the sun's surface"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric process", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric processes", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 4367, "stable_key": "clu_eccbfd338995af8a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrical phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q56552099", "wikidata_description": "article"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mean temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mean_temperature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mean temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mean tem