ples governing wind direction and patterns, potentially including phenomena like the Coriolis effect.", "wikidata_id": "Q56664297", "wikidata_description": "architectural structure in Angus, Scotland, UK"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Trade winds", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2069, "target_cluster_name": "Trade winds", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Trade winds are governed by fundamental laws of atmospheric motion and planetary rotation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Trade winds are governed by fundamental laws of atmospheric motion and planetary rotation.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "western coast", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "western_coast", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "western coast", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["western coast"], "contexts": ["coastline on the west"]}, {"entity_id": "eastern_coasts", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Eastern coasts", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Eastern coasts"], "contexts": ["location of colder climates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "western coast", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Eastern coasts", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 4395, "stable_key": "clu_2444471a592bc340", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Western coast", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "various", "note": "The term 'western coast' is too general to pinpoint a specific modern country without further context. However, the concept itself is clear."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "swamps", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "swamps", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "swamps", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["swamps"], "contexts": ["wet, marshy land"]}, {"entity_id": "swamps", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "swamps", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["swamps"], "contexts": ["wet, marshy land"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "swamps", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "swamps", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4396, "stable_key": "clu_b6cb1651894ff683", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "swamp", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Various", "wikidata_id": "Q19000458", "wikidata_description": "horse disease caused by a retrovirus and transmitted by bloodsucking insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equine_infectious_anemia", "wikipedia_extract": "Equine infectious anemia or equine infectious anaemia (EIA), also known by horsemen as swamp fever, is a horse disease caused by a retrovirus (Equine infectious anemia virus) and transmitted by bloodsucking insects. The virus (EIAV) is endemic in the Americas, parts of Europe, the Middle and Far East, Russia, and South Africa. The virus is a lentivirus, like human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). Like HIV, EIA can be transmitted through blood, milk, and body secretions. Transmission is primarily through biting flies, such as the horse-fly and deer-fly. The virus survives up to 4 hours in the vector."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Gay-Lussacii", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gay-lussacii", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gay-Lussacii", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gay-Lussacii"], "contexts": ["scientist, friend of Humboldt"]}, {"entity_id": "m__gay-lussac", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "M. Gay-Lussac", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Gay-Lussac"], "contexts": ["ascended in balloon, suffered from air rarity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Gay-Lussacii", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "M. Gay-Lussac", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 4397, "stable_key": "clu_6c369b05409a67e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1778, "death_year": 1850, "description": "French chemist and physicist, known for gas laws and balloon ascents."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "sugar-cane", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sugar-cane", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sugar-cane", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sugar-cane"], "contexts": ["plant requiring hot climate"]}, {"entity_id": "sugar-cane", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sugar-cane", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sugar-cane"], "contexts": ["plant source of sugar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sugar-cane", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sugar-cane", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4398, "stable_key": "clu_8956eb73e3d0abe4-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sugar", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Saccharum officinarum", "family": "Poaceae", "description": "Sweet crystalline substance obtained from various plants, especially sugarcane.", "note": "Refers to the plant source and the substance derived from it.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saccharum officinarum is a large, strong-growing species of grass in the sugarcane genus. Its stout stalks are rich in sucrose, a disaccharide sugar which accumulates in the stalk internodes. It originated in New Guinea, and is now cultivated in tropical and subtropical countries worldwide for the production of sugar, ethanol and other products. It is one of the most productive and most intensively cultivated kinds of sugarcane. Saccharum officinarum, a perennial plant, grows in clumps consisting of a number of strong unbranched stems. A network of rhizomes forms under the soil which sends up secondary shoots near the parent plant."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Ormuz", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1542, "target_cluster_name": "Ormuz", "source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "evidence_snippet": "Guzarates e os Decanins a chamam çupari; e estes tem muyto pouca, somente na fralda do mar, e he muyto boa essa que ha em Chaul, porque he mercadoria pêra Ormuz ; e milhor he a de Mombaim, terra e ylh", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "reverse: local name", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "theory of terrestrial refraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "theory_of_terrestrial_refraction", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "theory of terrestrial refraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["theory of terrestrial refraction"], "contexts": ["theory about light bending in atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_refraction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Terrestrial Refraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Terrestrial Refraction"], "contexts": ["bending of light by atmosphere near earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "theory of terrestrial refraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Terrestrial Refraction", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 4399, "stable_key": "clu_555501f911422cec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "terrestrial refraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q254634", "wikidata_description": "deviation of light or other electromagnetic wave from a straight line as it passes through the atmosphere", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmospheric_refraction", "wikipedia_extract": "The sunrise equation or sunset equation can be used to derive the time of sunrise or sunset for any solar declination and latitude in terms of local solar time when sunrise and sunset actually occur. {\\displaystyle \\cos \\omega _{\\circ }=-\\tan \\phi \\times \\tan \\delta } is the solar hour angle at either sunrise (when negative value is taken) or sunset (when positive value is taken); is the latitude of the observer on the Earth; The Earth rotates at an angular velocity of 15°/hour. Therefore, the expression {\\displaystyle \\omega _{\\circ }/\\mathrm {15} ^{\\circ }} is in degree, gives the interval of time in hours from sunrise to local solar noon or from local solar noon to sunset. The sign convention is typically that the observer latitude is 0 at the equator, positive for the Northern Hemisphere and negative for the Southern Hemisphere, and the solar declination is 0 at the vernal and autumnal equinoxes when the sun is exactly above the equator, positive during the Northern Hemisphere summer and negative during the Northern Hemisphere winter. The expression above is always applicable for latitudes between the Arctic Circle and Antarctic Circle. North of the Arctic Circle or south of the Antarctic Circle, there is at least one day of the year with no sunrise or sunset."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Neetee", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "neetee", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Neetee", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Neetee"], "contexts": ["village and pass in Uttarakhand, India"]}, {"entity_id": "neetee", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Neetee", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Neetee"], "contexts": ["location on Moorcroft's route"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Neetee", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Neetee", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4400, "stable_key": "clu_097bbea6316b7f9d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Niti Pass", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q2982089", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_extract": "The 1954 Sino-Indian Agreement, also called the Panchsheel Agreement, officially the Agreement on Trade and Intercourse Between Tibet Region and India, was signed by China and India in Peking on 29 April 1954. The preamble of the agreement stated the panchsheel, or  the five principles of peaceful coexistence, that China proposed and India favoured. The agreement reflected the adjustment of the previously existing trade relations between Tibet and India to the changed context of India's decolonisation and China's assertion of suzerainty over Tibet. Bertil Lintner writes that in the agreement, \"Tibet was referred to, for the first time in history, as 'the Tibet Region of China'\". The agreement expired on 6 June 1962, as per the original term limit, in the midst of the Sino-Indian border tensions. It was not renewed.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Col_de_Niti"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Ross", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ross", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ross", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ross"], "contexts": ["navigator, provided testimony on storms"]}, {"entity_id": "ross", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Ross", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ross"], "contexts": ["observed barometer depression in Antarctic Ocean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ross", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ross", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4401, "stable_key": "clu_adcb53f7e4d3e45b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Clark Ross", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1800, "death_year": 1862, "description": "British naval officer and explorer, studied storms and Antarctic meteorology.", "wikidata_id": "Q200564", "wikidata_description": "British explorer and naval officer (1800–1862)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Von Baer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "von_baer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Von Baer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Von Baer"], "contexts": ["academician, studied meteorology of Northern Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "von_baer", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Von Baer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Von Baer"], "contexts": ["established truth of organismal development"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Von Baer", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Von Baer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4402, "stable_key": "clu_b1fdddcf0c9eabf2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karl Ernst von Baer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1792, "death_year": 1876, "description": "Baltic German scientist, founder of embryology, studied organismal development.", "wikidata_id": "Q57190", "wikidata_description": "Baltic German scientist (1792-1876)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "history of organisms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "history_of_organisms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "history of organisms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["history of organisms"], "contexts": ["study of the development of organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "biological_history", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "biological history", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["biological history"], "contexts": ["development of life on Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "history of organisms", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "biological history", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 4403, "stable_key": "clu_1db7b7e3aa904497", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Evolutionary History", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'study of the development of organisms' and 'development of life on Earth' strongly points to the history of life and its evolutionary processes.", "wikidata_id": "Q1098275", "wikidata_description": "processes by which organisms evolved on Earth"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Natural history", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1755, "target_cluster_name": "Natural history", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Evolutionary History, defined as the history of organisms, is a core aspect of natural history.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Evolutionary History, defined as the history of organisms, is a core aspect of natural history.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "palæozoic period", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2534, "target_cluster_name": "palæozoic period", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Paleozoic Era represents a significant period in the evolutionary history of life on Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Paleozoic Era represents a significant period in the evolutionary history of life on Earth.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "arborescent ferns", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "arborescent_ferns", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "arborescent ferns", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arborescent ferns"], "contexts": ["plants found in equatorial regions"]}, {"entity_id": "arborescent_ferns", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "arborescent ferns", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arborescent ferns"], "contexts": ["tree-like fern species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "arborescent ferns", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "arborescent ferns", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4404, "stable_key": "clu_28ebbaa29b3dbaa1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tree ferns", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Cyatheaceae, Dicksoniaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q623232", "wikidata_description": "order of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyatheales", "wikipedia_extract": "The order Cyatheales, which includes most tree ferns, is a taxonomic order of the fern class, Polypodiopsida. No clear morphological features characterize all of the Cyatheales, but DNA sequence data indicate the order is monophyletic. Some species in the Cyatheales have tree-like growth forms from a vertical rhizome, others have shorter or horizontal expanding rhizomes. Some species have scales on the stems and leaves, while others have hairs. However, most plants in the Cyatheales are tree ferns and have trunk-like stems up to 10 metres (33 ft) tall. It is unclear how many times the tree form has evolved and been lost in the order."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "South-Indian archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "south-indian_archipelago", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "South-Indian archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["South-Indian archipelago"], "contexts": ["islands inhabited by races"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago_indiano", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "archipelago Indiano", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["archipelago Indiano"], "contexts": ["geographic region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "South-Indian archipelago", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "archipelago Indiano", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 4405, "stable_key": "clu_52e0ea07484884cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indian Ocean islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The term 'South-Indian archipelago' is vague and could encompass various island groups in the Indian Ocean. 'archipelago Indiano' is more direct but still requires interpretation within the historical context."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Indian Archipelago", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2789, "target_cluster_name": "Indian Archipelago", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Indian Archipelago is a major group of islands located within the Indian Ocean.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Indian Archipelago is a major group of islands located within the Indian Ocean.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "New Zealand", "link_type": "co