comet, calculated star distances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peters, Dr.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peters, Mr.", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 4416, "stable_key": "clu_94e265b4399748f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christian August Peters", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1752, "death_year": 1828, "description": "Astronomer who observed comets and calculated star distances.", "note": "There were multiple individuals named Peters. This identification is based on the described astronomical contributions."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "star dust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "star_dust", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "star dust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["star dust"], "contexts": ["interstellar material"]}, {"entity_id": "star_dust", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "star dust", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["star dust"], "contexts": ["material in Milky Way"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "star dust", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "star dust", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4417, "stable_key": "clu_54e53dd1eac27691", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stardust", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The mention of 'star' alone in 'first_principles_spencer_1862' might be ambiguous, but the context of 'stardust' in other sources strongly suggests this referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q93717770", "wikidata_description": "vocal track by Willie Nelson; 1978 studio recording; cover version"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "New Bedford", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "new_bedford", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Bedford", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New Bedford"], "contexts": ["location where comet was observed"]}, {"entity_id": "bedford", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bedford", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bedford"], "contexts": ["location of Admiral Smyth's observatory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New Bedford", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bedford", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 4418, "stable_key": "clu_3b70a65c9c265212", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "New Bedford", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q49147", "wikidata_description": "city in Bristol County, Massachusetts, United States", "wikipedia_extract": "New Bedford is a city in Bristol County, Massachusetts, United States. It is located on the Acushnet River in what is known as the South Coast region, abutting Buzzards Bay. At the 2020 census, New Bedford had a population of 101,079, making it the state's ninth-largest city and the largest of the South Coast region. It is the second-largest city in the Providence metropolitan area and included in the greater Boston combined statistical area. Up through the 17th century, the area was the territory of the Wampanoag Indians. English colonists bought the land on which New Bedford would later be built from the Wampanoag in 1652, and the original colonial settlement that would later become the city was founded by English Quakers in the late 17th century.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Bedford,_Massachusetts"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Lana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lana", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Lana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lana"], "contexts": ["material brought from the Indies"]}, {"entity_id": "lani", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "lani", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lani"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Lana", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "lani", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 4419, "stable_key": "clu_b222fef89ac3716b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wool", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'material brought from the Indies' suggests a specific type of wool, possibly from South America (like alpaca or vicuña), but the term 'Lana' itself broadly refers to wool.", "wikidata_id": "Q42329", "wikidata_description": "natural fibre from the soft hair of sheep or other mammals"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "piedra de Sangre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piedra_de_sangre", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "piedra de Sangre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["piedra de Sangre"], "contexts": ["stone with red spots like blood"]}, {"entity_id": "pedra_sanguinha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pedra sanguinha", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["pedra sanguinha"], "contexts": ["ingredient in falcon plaster"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "piedra de Sangre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pedra sanguinha", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 4420, "stable_key": "clu_003967f1a80a3638", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Haematite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The description 'stone with red spots like blood' strongly points to haematite, a mineral known for its reddish-brown streak and often having blood-like inclusions."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "bracelete", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bracelete", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "bracelete", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bracelete"], "contexts": ["amulet worn on the arm"]}, {"entity_id": "braceletes_de_alambres", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "braceletes de alambres", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["braceletes de alambres"], "contexts": ["bracelets made of wire"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "bracelete", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "braceletes de alambres", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 4421, "stable_key": "clu_18b68e08b22320ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bracelet", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The terms refer to adornments worn on the arm, fitting the definition of a bracelet. The specific materials (wire) provide further detail."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "reales", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "reales", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "reales", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reales"], "contexts": ["unit of weight"]}, {"entity_id": "reales", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "reales", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reales"], "contexts": ["currency unit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "reales", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "reales", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4422, "stable_key": "clu_fd4f1ee5c5b48d1b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Real (currency)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'currency unit' definitively identifies 'reales' as a form of money. The mention of a 'unit of weight' is likely a secondary or archaic usage, or a misinterpretation in translation, as 'real' as a unit of weight is not common."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Solatro", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "solatro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Solatro", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Solatro"], "contexts": ["plant root used to induce dreams"]}, {"entity_id": "solatro", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "solatro", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["solatro"], "contexts": ["herb with high moisture content"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Solatro", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "solatro", "similarity": 0.792}], "id": 4423, "stable_key": "clu_d2970c50a974ac8a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solanum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Solanum", "family": "Solanaceae", "note": "Refers to plants in the Solanum genus, which includes species like nightshade. Context suggests medicinal uses, potentially narcotic or dream-inducing.", "wikidata_id": "Q146555", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Opi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "opi", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Opi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Opi"], "contexts": ["substance requested by an Indian"]}, {"entity_id": "opioi", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "opioi", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["opioi"], "contexts": ["term for opium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Opi", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "opioi", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 4424, "stable_key": "clu_9cdc8def07631257", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Opium", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Narcotic substance obtained from the opium poppy.", "note": "'Oppio' and its variations refer to opium.", "wikipedia_extract": "Opium (also known as poppy tears, or Lachryma papaveris) is the dried latex obtained from the seed capsules of the opium poppy Papaver somniferum. Approximately 12 percent of opium is made up of the analgesic alkaloid morphine, which is processed chemically to produce heroin and other synthetic opioids for medicinal use and for the illegal drug trade. Opium's main psychoactive alkaloids, primarily morphine, act on μ-opioid receptors, causing analgesia, with long-term use leading to tolerance and dependence. The latex also contains the closely related opiates codeine and thebaine, and non-analgesic alkaloids such as papaverine and noscapine. The traditional, labor-intensive method of obtaining the latex is to scratch (\"score\") the immature seed pods (fruits) by hand; the latex leaks out and dries to a sticky yellowish residue that is later scraped off and dehydrated. The English word for opium is borrowed from Latin, which in turn comes from Ancient Greek: ὄπιον (ópion), a diminutive of ὀπός (opós, 'juice of a plant')."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "partes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "partes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "partes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["partes"], "contexts": ["regions from which people come"]}, {"entity_id": "outras_muytas_partes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "outras muytas partes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["outras muytas partes"], "contexts": ["many other places"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "partes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "outras muytas partes", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 4425, "stable_key": "clu_3f64b411cabaa88a", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Parteš (Serbian Cyrillic: Партеш) or Partesh (Albanian definite form: Parteshi), is a town and municipality located in the Gjilan District of Kosovo. The municipality was established on 19 August 2010. It is inhabited by Serbs, and as of 2013, it has an estimated population of 5,300 inhabitants. The municipality consists of town of Parteš and two villages: Pasjane and Donja Budriga. The settlements of Parteš, Donja Budriga and Pasjane are inhabited by ethnic Serbs. There are four Serbian Orthodox churches within the municipality."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "region caliente", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "region_caliente", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "region caliente", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["region caliente"], "contexts": ["climate affecting medicine intake"]}, {"entity_id": "regiões_quentes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "regiões quentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regiões quentes"], "contexts": ["hot geographical areas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "region caliente", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "regiões quentes", "similarity": 0.945}], "id": 4426, "stable_key": "clu_b2f0307463b73fb2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hot region", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Various", "wikipedia_extract": "\"Hot\" is a song recorded by South Korean girl group Le Sserafim for their fifth extended play of the same name. It was released as the EP's lead single by Source Music on March 14, 2025. The music video was released on the same day. A remix version of the track featuring English singer Jade, was released on April 14, 2025. On February 16, 2025, Source Music announced that Le Sserafim would release Hot, their fifth extended play, on March 14. A trailer, titled \"Born Fire,\" was released on February 21.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_(Le_Sserafim_song)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "marido", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "marido", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "marido", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["marido"], "contexts": ["husband"]}, {"entity_id": "marido", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "marido", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["marido"], "contexts": ["husband"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "marido", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "marido", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4427, "stable_key": "clu_54625d893ed6ccbb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "husband", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the role of husband, not a specific individual.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Limó", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "limó", "book_id": "historia_medicina