"polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "diſenterias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["diſenterias"], "contexts": ["condition treated with remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "diſinterías", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "diſenterias", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 4446, "stable_key": "clu_35c56e8df4fff90e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dysentery", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An intestinal inflammation causing severe diarrhea with blood and mucus.", "wikipedia_extract": "Dysentery (UK: , US: ), historically known as the bloody flux, is a type of gastroenteritis that results in bloody diarrhea. Other symptoms may include fever, abdominal pain, and a feeling of incomplete defecation. Complications may include dehydration. The cause of dysentery is usually the bacteria from genus Shigella, in which case it is known as shigellosis, or the amoeba Entamoeba histolytica; then it is called amoebiasis. Other causes may include certain chemicals, other bacteria, other protozoa, or parasitic worms. It may spread between people."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "dysenterias", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2677, "target_cluster_name": "dysenterias", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "This is the historical Latinized spelling of dysentery, appearing in premodern texts.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "This is the historical Latinized spelling of dysentery, appearing in premodern texts."}, {"found_name": "diarrhea", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 1313, "target_cluster_name": "Dyarrhea", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Diarrhea is a primary symptom of dysentery, and historical texts often describe them in close relation.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Diarrhea is a primary symptom of dysentery, and historical texts often describe them in close relation."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Expulfiva", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "expulfiva", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Expulfiva", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Expulfiva"], "contexts": ["expulsive virtue"]}, {"entity_id": "expulfiva", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "expulfiva", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["expulfiva"], "contexts": ["expulsive faculty of kidneys"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Expulfiva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "expulfiva", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 4447, "stable_key": "clu_6fdedf165324a036", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "expulsive faculty", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pan", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pan", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "pan", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pan"], "contexts": ["food made from grain"]}, {"entity_id": "pan", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pan", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pan"], "contexts": ["another name for betle leaf"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "pan", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4448, "stable_key": "clu_99ad2ab9551fd3b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Betel leaf", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'pan' is ambiguous. In the context of the Monardes text, it likely refers to bread or food from grain. However, one mention in the 'Coloquios da Orta' suggests 'pan' might be a misspelling or colloquialism for 'betel' or a similar plant leaf used for chewing, especially given the geographical context of the text (Asia/Americas). Further context from the original texts is needed for certainty.", "wikidata_id": "Q130149531", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published on 25 January 2018", "wikipedia_extract": "The areca nut ( or ) or betel nut () is the fruit of the areca palm (Areca catechu). The palm is originally native to the Philippines, but was carried widely through the tropics by the Austronesian migrations and trade since at least 1500 BCE due to its use in betel nut chewing. It is widespread in cultivation and is considered naturalized in much of the tropical Pacific (Melanesia and Micronesia), South Asia, Southeast Asia, and parts of east Africa. It is not to be confused with betel (Piper betle) leaves that are often used to wrap it. The practice of betel nut chewing, often together with other herbs as a stimulant drug, dates back thousands of years, and continues to the present day in many countries. Betel nut chewing is addictive due to the presence of the stimulant arecoline and causes adverse health effects—mainly oral and esophageal cancers, as well as cardiovascular disease.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areca_nut"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Coca", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2856, "target_cluster_name": "Coca", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both are leaves used in traditional medicine and rituals.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both are leaves used in traditional medicine and rituals.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cubilece", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cubilece", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cubilece", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cubilece"], "contexts": ["container where snow floats for cooling"]}, {"entity_id": "cube", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "cube", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cube"], "contexts": ["solid contained by six plane square surfaces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cubilece", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cube", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 4449, "stable_key": "clu_2cc384aeecd554d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cube", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "socotorino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "socotorino", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "socotorino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["socotorino"], "contexts": ["aloes from Socotora island"]}, {"entity_id": "socotorino", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Socotorino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Socotorino"], "contexts": ["type of aloe juice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "socotorino", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Socotorino", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 4450, "stable_key": "clu_44bc0e29d59e0a78", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Socotrine Aloe", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Aloe perryi Baker", "family": "Asphodelaceae", "note": "The specific species can vary, but 'Socotrine' refers to aloe from the island of Socotra.", "wikidata_id": "Q140942", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Aloe", "link_typ