dern_term": "Cardiac condition", "wikidata_id": "Q49130403", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on December 21, 1981"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alfandega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alfandega"], "contexts": ["near the specimen"]}, {"entity_id": "alfandega", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alfandega"], "contexts": ["customs house where Joſeph Correa was judge"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alfandega", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alfandega", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4487, "stable_key": "clu_a4ac02faa21a31d3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Customs house", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "wikidata_id": "Q218653", "wikidata_description": "government office building for import and export of goods"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Aristolochia indica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aristolochia_indica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Aristolochia indica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aristolochia indica"], "contexts": ["plant cited as possible antidote"]}, {"entity_id": "aristolochia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aristolochia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aristolochia"], "contexts": ["plant with three species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Aristolochia indica", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aristolochia", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 4488, "stable_key": "clu_86b1a1dddd67de25", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aristolochia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Aristolochia Linnaeus", "family": "Aristolochiaceae", "note": "While 'Aristolochia tinbacencis' is mentioned, it's likely a specific species or variety within the genus Aristolochia. The broader genus is the most fitting modern referent given the context of multiple species.", "wikidata_id": "Q156212", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aristolochia", "wikipedia_extract": "Aristolochia californica, the California pipevine, California Dutchman's-pipe, or California snakeroot is a perennial woody vine of western North America. The vine is endemic to northern California. It is native to the Sacramento Valley, northern Sierra Nevada foothills, San Francisco Bay Area, Northern Inner California Coast Ranges, southeastern Klamath Mountains. The plant grows along riparian streambank areas, in chaparral, oak woodland, and mixed evergreen forest habitats. It is found below 700 metres (2,300 ft) in elevation. Aristolochia californica is a deciduous vine."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "martellos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "martellos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "martellos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["martellos"], "contexts": ["hammers"]}, {"entity_id": "martello", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "martello", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["martello"], "contexts": ["hammer used for crushing nuts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "martellos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "martello", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 4489, "stable_key": "clu_e14f32ece37761b1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hammer", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "terra arménia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "terra_arménia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "terra arménia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["terra arménia"], "contexts": ["argillaceous earth used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "terra_armenia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "terra Armenia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["terra Armenia"], "contexts": ["type of medicinal earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "terra arménia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "terra Armenia", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 4490, "stable_key": "clu_8d1e2bb780282914", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q891670", "wikidata_description": "natural mixture of hydrated silicate clays colored with red iron oxide", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian%20bole", "wikipedia_extract": "Armenian bole, also known as bolus armenus or bole armoniac, is an earthy clay, usually red, native to Armenia but also found in other places. The term Armenian was later referred to a specific quality of the clay. Originally used in medication, it has also been used as a pigment, as a poliment or base for gilding, and for other uses. It is red due to the presence of iron oxide; the clay also contains hydrous silicates of aluminum and possibly magnesium. Historically, the term bolu or bolus was used only for medicinal earths and Armenian bole was used as an astringent, prescribed against diarrhea, dysentery, and bleeding. References to Armenian bole were made by Theophrastus, Dioscorides (c."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Reforma", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "reforma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Reforma", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reforma"], "contexts": ["religious movement"]}, {"entity_id": "reform", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Reform", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reform"], "contexts": ["political ideology of individual liberty"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Reforma", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Reform", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 4491, "stable_key": "clu_536fd36fa2a92715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reformation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Reforma' in Portuguese and 'Reform' in English, in this context, refer to the major religious and subsequent political movements.", "wikidata_id": "Q29640250", "wikidata_description": "episode of Holby City (S11 E45)"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "tintoreiros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tintoreiros", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tintoreiros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["tintoreiros"], "contexts": ["dyers who rejected sândalo"]}, {"entity_id": "tintori", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "tintori", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["tintori"], "contexts": ["dyers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tintoreiros", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "tintori", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 4492, "stable_key": "clu_84f734d435d0c6ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dyers", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Artisans involved in dyeing textiles and other materials.", "wikidata_id": "Q6147194", "wikidata_description": "occupation where a person is involved in dyeing", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyer%20%28occupation%29"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "abundância", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "abundância", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "abundância", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["abundância"], "contexts": ["state of having plenty"]}, {"entity_id": "abundance", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "abundance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["abundance"], "contexts": ["large quantity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "abundância", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "abundance", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 4493, "stable_key": "clu_2220e5c797f23980", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "abundance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q336019", "wikidata_description": "ecological quantity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_%28ecology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In ecology, local abundance is  the relative representation of a species in a particular ecosystem. It is usually measured as the number of individuals found per sample. The ratio of abundance of one species to one or multiple other species living in an ecosystem is referred to as relative species abundances. Both indicators are relevant for computing biodiversity. A variety of sampling methods are used to measure abundance. For larger animals, these may include spotlight counts, track counts and roadkill counts, as well as presence at monitoring stations."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "mulas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mulas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mulas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mulas"], "contexts": ["animals that eat the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "mula", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mula", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mula"], "contexts": ["animal involved in illness transfer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mulas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mula", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 4494, "stable_key": "clu_a1293101b7e3cafa-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mule", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "wikipedia_extract": "The mule is a domestic equine hybrid between a donkey and a horse. It is the offspring of a male donkey (a jack) and a female horse (a mare). The horse and the donkey are different species, with different numbers of chromosomes; of the two possible first-generation hybrids between them, the mule is easier to obtain and more common than the hinny, which is the offspring of a male horse (a stallion) and a female donkey (a jenny). Mules vary widely in size, and may be of any color seen in horses or donkeys. They are more patient, hardier and longer-lived than horses, and are perceived as less obstinate and more intelligent than donkeys. A female mule is called a molly, a Molly mule, or more formally mare mule."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "horse", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 115, "target_cluster_name": "horse", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The mule is a hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "The mule is a hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse."}]}, {"canonical_name": "Lyro", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lyro", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Lyro", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lyro"], "contexts": ["cited reference"]}, {"entity_id": "lyre", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "lyre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lyre"], "contexts": ["stringed musical instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Lyro", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lyre", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 4495, "stable_key": "clu_4a26c49cd7eb2341", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lyre", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'Lyro' in the 1563 text is likely an archaic or variant spelling of 'lyre', a stringed musical instrument.", "wikidata_id": "Q25366088", "wikidata_description": "genus of algae"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "musical instruments", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2894, "target_cluster_name": "musical instruments", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Lyre is a specific type of musical instrument.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Lyre is a specific type of musical instrument.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "latão", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "latão", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "latão", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["latão"], "contexts": ["trabalho onde se obtinha espodio"]}, {"entity_id": "lataó", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lataó", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lataó"], "contexts": ["brass"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "latão", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lataó", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 4496, "stable_key": "clu_b13baea0cc14a897", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Brass", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The spellings 'latão' and 'lataó' are early modern Portuguese/Latin variants for brass, an alloy of copper and zinc."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "febre latica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "febre_latica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "febre latica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["febre latica"], "contexts": ["fever treated with cooked Raiz da china"]}, {"entity_id": "malignæ_febris", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Malignæ febris", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Malignæ febris"], "contexts": ["a dangerous fever"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "febre latica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Malignæ febris", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 4497, "stable_key": "clu_39013ff5aca96875-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fever", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An acute illness characterized by elevated body temperature and potential delirium.", "wikipedia_extract": "Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C (99.0 and 100.9 °F) in humans. The increase in set point triggers increased muscle contractions and causes a feeling of cold or chills. This results in greater heat production and efforts to conserve heat. When the set point temperature returns to normal, a person feels hot, becomes flushed, and may begin to sweat. Rarely a fever may trigger a febrile seizure, with this being more common in young children."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "febre", "link_type": "cross_linguistic", "link_strength": 0.9, "target_cluster_id": 1486, "target_cluster_name": "febre", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spanish for fever.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_cross_linguistic", "found_relationship": "Spanish for fever."}, {"found_name": "Quartan Fever", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 645, "target_cluster_name": "quartas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A type of fever characterized by occurring every fourth day.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A type of fever characterized by occurring every fourth day."}, {"found_name": "Quotidian fever", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2230, "target_cluster_name": "quotidianas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A type of fever characterized by occurring daily.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A type of fever characterized by occurring daily."}, {"found_name": "Tertian Fever", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2326, "target_cluster_name": "Tercianas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A type of fever characterized by occurring every third day.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "A type of fever characterized by occurring every third day."}, {"found_name": "Sadness/Tertian Fever", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 3739, "target_cluster_name": "Terçaas", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Historically, sadness was sometimes associated with or considered a symptom of certain types of fever, specifically tertian fever.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Historically, sadness was sometimes associated with or considered a symptom of certain types of fever, specifically tertian fever."}]}, {"canonical_name": "pecegos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pecegos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pecegos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecegos"], "contexts": ["peaches"]}, {"entity_id": "pecego", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pecego", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecego"], "contexts": ["type of fruit to avoid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pecegos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pecego", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 4498, "stable_key": "clu_a911a8066ec2cf02", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peach", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Prunus persica", "family": "Rosaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q13189", "wikidata_description": "species of fruit tree (for the fruit use Q13202263)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach", "wikipedia_extract": "The peach (Prunus persica) is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits. The tree is regarded as handsome and is planted in gardens for its springtime blooms in addition to fruit production. It is relatively short lived, usually not exceeding twenty years of age. Peaches were first domesticated and cultivated in China during the Neolithic period."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "franccfco_roflclli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franccfco Roflclli"], "contexts": ["Name mentioned in title"]}, {"entity_id": "francifco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Francifco", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Francifco"], "contexts": ["mentioned in comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Francifco", "similarity": 0.865}], "id": 4499, "stable_key": "clu_0c8897dff9e2bcab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Francesco Roselli", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Italian physician and author of medical texts.", "note": "The OCR noise in 'Franccfco Roflclli' and the shortened 'Francifco' clearly point to this individual.", "wikidata_id": "Q60627968", "wikidata_description": "researcher ORCID ID = 0000-0001-9935-6899"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Niccolao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2011, "target_cluster_name": "Niccolao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Francesco Roselli was a Florentine merchant and banker, and Niccolò Antinori was also a Florentine merchant and banker, indicating a likely professional or social relationship within the same economic sphere.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Francesco Roselli was a Florentine merchant and banker, and Niccolò Antinori was also a Florentine merchant and banker, indicating a likely professional or social relationship within the same economic sphere.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "liguria", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liguria"], "contexts": ["region in Italy with noble herbs"]}, {"entity_id": "liguria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liguria"], "contexts": ["region in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Liguria", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Liguria", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4500, "stable_key": "clu_5efef44a79ce5fad", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Liguria ( lig-YOOR-ee-ə, Italian: [liˈɡuːrja]; Ligurian: Ligûria [liˈɡyːɾja]) is a region of north-western Italy; its capital is Genoa. Its territory is crossed by the Alps and the Apennines mountain range and is roughly coextensive with the former territory of the Republic of Genoa. Liguria is bordered by France (Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur) to the west, Piedmont to the north, and Emilia-Romagna and Tuscany to the east. It rests on the Ligurian Sea, and has a population of 1,509,908 as of 2025. The region is part of the Alps–Mediterranean Euroregion. The name Liguria predates Latin and is of obscure origin."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Toscana", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2151, "target_cluster_name": "Toscana", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Liguria is a neighboring region to Tuscany.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Liguria is a neighboring region to Tuscany.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "cammino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cammino", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "cammino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cammino"], "contexts": ["hearth"]}, {"entity_id": "camino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "camino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["camino"], "contexts": ["chimney"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "cammino", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "camino", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 4501, "stable_key": "clu_6341eff35dd1aebe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hearth", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The terms 'cammino' and 'camino' refer to a hearth or fireplace, a common feature in early modern homes and laboratories.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "furnace", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 100, "target_cluster_name": "furnace", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "A hearth is a place where a fire is built, often within a building, and a furnace is a device used to generate heat, often by burning fuel, making them functionally related in the context of heating.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: A hearth is a place where a fire is built, often within a building, and a furnace is a device used to generate heat, often by burning fuel, making them functionally related in the context of heating.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "nefpoie", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nefpoie", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "nefpoie", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nefpoie"], "contexts": ["medlar fruits"]}, {"entity_id": "nefperas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "nefperas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nefperas"], "contexts": ["fruit used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "nefpoie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "nefperas", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 4502, "stable_key": "clu_6e136c64c1d9eee2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medlar", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Mespilus germanica Linnaeus", "family": "Rosaceae", "note": "'Nefpoie' and 'nefperas' are consistent with historical variations of the word for medlar, a fruit used medicinally. The context 'medlar fruits' solidifies this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q2088434", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimusops%20elengi", "wikipedia_extract": "Mimusops elengi is a medium-sized evergreen tree found in tropical forests in South Asia, Southeast Asia and northern Australia. English common names include Spanish cherry, medlar, and bullet wood. Its timber is valuable and its fruit is edible. As the trees give thick shade and flowers emit fragrance, it is a prized collection for gardens. It is used as an ornamental tree in many places. The flowers may also be used in natural perfume."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Pear", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 826, "target_cluster_name": "Pear-tree", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Medlar is a fruit that is often compared to pears in terms of its texture and culinary uses, and both are pome fruits within the Rosaceae family.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "Medlar is a fruit that is often compared to pears in terms of its texture and culinary uses, and both are pome fruits within the Rosaceae family."}]}, {"canonical_name": "salvia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "salvia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "salvia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["salvia"], "contexts": ["sage, herb used in conserves"]}, {"entity_id": "salvia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "salvia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["salvia"], "contexts": ["herb used in decoctions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "salvia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "salvia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4503, "stable_key": "clu_fd208aff7ea17b85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sage", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Salvia officinalis Linnaeus", "family": "Lamiaceae"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "sage", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1811, "target_cluster_name": "Salva", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The entity itself is listed with its historical name 'salvia'.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_synonyms", "found_relationship": "The entity itself is listed with its historical name 'salvia'."}]}, {"canonical_name": "montano", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "montano", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "montano", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["montano"], "contexts": ["herb, likely mountain calamint"]}, {"entity_id": "monte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "monte", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["monte"], "contexts": ["plant used in a powder for pleurisy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "montano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "monte", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 4504, "stable_key": "clu_378a7cdb3e1d770e", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Montano in Italian, both Montano and Montaño in Spanish, is a surname. Aldo Montano (fencer born 1910), Italian fencer Aldo Montano (fencer born 1978), Italian fencer and grandson of the above fencer Alicia Gómez Montano (1955–2020), Spanish journalist Mario Aldo Montano, Italian fencer and son of the fencer born in 1910 Cesar Montano, multi-awarded Filipino actor and film director Justiniano Montano (1905–2005), Filipino politician Linda Montano, American contemporary feminist performance artist Machel Montano (born 1974), Trinidad and Tobago soca singer, record producer and songwriter Mark Montano, American interior designer, artist, writer and TV personality Severino Montano (1915–1980), Filipino playwright, writer, director and actor Benito Arias Montano (1527–1598) (not Montaño), Spanish orientalist Also a governor and a soldier of the same name and same period es:Montano (desambiguación) rarer Spanish surnames as Montano, no ñ."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Calamentof", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Pcfta fotti Imen te , & con Mtlc ftiumato quanto bafta. Fa lutouaro m buona forma» DJ AC A LAMENTO ck G aleno fecondo Mefuc. Alamcntol \\^ montano { Calamentof uuiabj Sermontano ^ an j vi- Seme di Petr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for montano"}, {"found_name": "Sermontano", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Pcfta fotti Imen te , & con Mtlc ftiumato quanto bafta. Fa lutouaro m buona forma» DJ AC A LAMENTO ck G aleno fecondo Mefuc. Alamcntol \\^ montano { Calamentof uuiabj Sermontano ^ an j vi- Seme di Petr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for montano"}, {"found_name": "Appio", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "Pcfta fotti Imen te , & con Mtlc ftiumato quanto bafta. Fa lutouaro m buona forma» DJ AC A LAMENTO ck G aleno fecondo Mefuc. Alamcntol \\^ montano { Calamentof uuiabj Sermontano ^ an j vi- Seme di Petr", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for parsley"}, {"found_name": "calamento", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "evidence_snippet": "bagno a cófuraa-r zionc del vino, cioc per hore fó, come s'c detto deii'oI;o nar dino p OLIO D( STORACE DiMefue. Pyrechro ^ugo di calamento montano |;j.nTorace onc.iii. i^^^- ^ohofcfamino liba. j.x. V", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "synonym for montano"}]}, {"canonical_name": "Hypociflide", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hypociflide", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Hypociflide", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hypociflide"], "contexts": ["likely a medicinal herb"]}, {"entity_id": "hypociftidis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hypociftidis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hypociftidis"], "contexts": ["ingredient in an emplaster"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Hypociflide", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hypociftidis", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 4505, "stable_key": "clu_d1068a1b9b187cfa-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hypocistis", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cistanche phelypaea", "family": "Orobanchaceae", "description": "Parasitic plant extract used for binding and in medicinal preparations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cistanche phelypaea, also spelled Cistanche phelipaea, is a species of plant in the family Orobanchaceae. It has a wide range of distribution from the Arabian Peninsula and the Syrian Desert in the east, through the Sahara, Cyprus, Crete and the southwest of the Iberian Peninsula, to Macaronesia in the west. Cistanche phelypaea is a chlorophyll-free, obligate parasitic plant with stout fleshy flowering stems bearing bright yellow flowers and rising to 20–50 cm (7.9–19.7 in) tall from a generally swollen base. The stem is glabrous and yellow to purple gray. Leaves are ovate-lanceolate, obtuse, glabrous and normally brown in color. Inflorescence is dense, more or less cylindrical."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cauda_equina", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cauda equina"], "contexts": ["herb ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "cauda_equina", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cauda equina"], "contexts": ["plant used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Cauda equina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cauda equina", "similarity": 0.836}], "id": 4506, "stable_key": "clu_319e153191f1b485", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horsetail", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Equisetum arvense Linnaeus", "family": "Equisetaceae", "note": "Literally 'horse's tail', this refers to the appearance of the plant, not a body part.", "wikidata_id": "Q81492131", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 February 2005"}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "tormentilla", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 1862, "target_cluster_name": "tormentilla", "source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "evidence_snippet": "vezes. Secom afebre houver fluxo de almorreymas , ou de mezes, ou houver ſangue pelaboca ,poderemos ajuntar ao cozimento das Tizanas huma oytavade naiz de tormentilla , huma pouca Tratado II. Cap. СХ.", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "true_synonym", "found_relationship": "reverse: synonym for polygonum", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aureola", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aureola"], "contexts": ["substance ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "aureola", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["aureola"], "contexts": ["ring of light around the sun"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Aureola", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "aureola", "similarity": 0.575}], "id": 4507, "stable_key": "clu_b298a47e2a071662", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Halo", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The dual context suggests both a substance (likely medicinal, perhaps related to gold or a pigment) and a visual phenomenon (optical halo)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "pena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pena", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "pena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pena"], "contexts": ["penalty or punishment"]}, {"entity_id": "penas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "penas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["penas"], "contexts": ["punishments suffered by the condemned"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "pena", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "penas", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 4508, "stable_key": "clu_16aa67893135ec82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Penalty", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to punishment or legal consequence."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Donzelli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "donzelli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Donzelli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Donzelli"], "contexts": ["officials of the art"]}, {"entity_id": "donzellas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "donzellas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["donzellas"], "contexts": ["young unmarried women"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Donzelli", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "donzellas", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 4509, "stable_key": "clu_5ab796c6a40e6898", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Donzelli", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Likely an individual or family name referenced in early modern texts.", "note": "The context 'officials of the art' suggests an individual or group associated with a guild or profession. 'Young unmarried women' refers to a different, though homophonous, term. Without further context, the specific identity is uncertain.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donzelli", "wikipedia_extract": "Donzelli is an Italian surname."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Niccolao", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2011, "target_cluster_name": "Niccolao", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker, and the Donzelli family were also prominent Florentine merchants and bankers, suggesting a potential business or social connection.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Niccolò Antinori was a Florentine merchant and banker, and the Donzelli family were also prominent Florentine merchants and bankers, suggesting a potential business or social connection.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "part.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "part_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "part.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["part."], "contexts": ["part of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "part", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Part", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Part"], "contexts": ["concept opposed to Whole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "part.", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Part", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 4510, "stable_key": "clu_176916bfb91246d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Part", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the conceptual division of a whole into constituent elements.", "wikidata_id": "Q114980050", "wikidata_description": "relation among parts and wholes"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Spinola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "spinola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Spinola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spinola"], "contexts": ["cited for Decuria & Liçam 6"]}, {"entity_id": "spinoza", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Spinoza", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spinoza"], "contexts": ["17th-century philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Spinola", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Spinoza", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 4511, "stable_key": "clu_baedbf2ef1952d49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Baruch Spinoza", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1632, "death_year": 1677, "description": "17th-century Dutch philosopher of Portuguese-Jewish origin.", "note": "The spelling 'Spinola' in the first text is likely a variant or error for Spinoza.", "wikidata_id": "Q35802", "wikidata_description": "Dutch philosopher (1632–1677)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch%20Spinoza", "wikipedia_extract": "Baruch (de) Spinoza (24 November 1632 – 21 February 1677), also known under his Latinized pen name Benedictus de Spinoza, was a philosopher of Portuguese-Jewish origin, who was born and lived in the Dutch Republic. A forerunner of the Age of Enlightenment, Spinoza significantly influenced modern biblical criticism, 17th-century rationalism, and Dutch intellectual culture, establishing himself as one of the most important and radical philosophers of the early modern period. Influenced by Stoicism, Thomas Hobbes, René Descartes, Ibn Tufayl, and heterodox Christians, Spinoza was a leading philosopher of the Dutch Golden Age. Spinoza was born in Amsterdam to a Marrano family that fled Portugal for the more tolerant Dutch Republic. He received a traditional Jewish education, learning Hebrew and studying sacred texts within the Portuguese Jewish community, where his father was a prominent merchant. As a young man, Spinoza challenged rabbinic authority and questioned Jewish doctrines, leading to his permanent expulsion from his Jewish community in 1656."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Kant", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 643, "target_cluster_name": "Kant", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Spinoza was a rationalist philosopher whose metaphysics and epistemology were significant points of reference and critique for Kant's own philosophical system, particularly in Kant's development of transcendental idealism.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Spinoza was a rationalist philosopher whose metaphysics and epistemology were significant points of reference and critique for Kant's own philosophical system, particularly in Kant's development of transcendental idealism.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "Schelling", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2600, "target_cluster_name": "Schelling", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Schelling's philosophy, particularly his later work, engaged with and was influenced by Spinoza's pantheistic system and concept of substance.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Schelling's philosophy, particularly his later work, engaged with and was influenced by Spinoza's pantheistic system and concept of substance.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "continuo movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "continuo_movimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "continuo movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["continuo movimento"], "contexts": ["ongoing movement"]}, {"entity_id": "motion_is_continuous", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Motion is continuous", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Motion is continuous"], "contexts": ["principle of perpetual motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "continuo movimento", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Motion is continuous", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 4512, "stable_key": "clu_f8d4701976fa4843", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Perpetual Motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the theoretical concept of a machine that can do work indefinitely without an external energy source, often discussed in early modern physics."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Templo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "templo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Templo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Templo"], "contexts": ["place of worship"]}, {"entity_id": "the_temple", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "the Temple", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["the Temple"], "contexts": ["area with lawyers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Templo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "the Temple", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 4513, "stable_key": "clu_a3e7ee9889b9113c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "temple", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "note": "The dual context of 'place of worship' and 'area with lawyers' strongly suggests the Inns of Court in London, commonly known as 'The Temple'."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "exulceratione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "exulceratione", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "exulceratione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exulceratione"], "contexts": ["ulceration"]}, {"entity_id": "exhilaration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "exhilaration", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exhilaration"], "contexts": ["feeling of great happiness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "exulceratione", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "exhilaration", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 4514, "stable_key": "clu_2243fe0f0604fc35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ulceration", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "ulceration", "note": "The term 'exulceratione' clearly refers to ulceration, while 'exhilaration' appears to be a mistranscription or a separate, unrelated concept within the context of early modern medical texts. The primary meaning in medical dictionaries points to ulceration.", "wikidata_id": "Q18554343", "wikidata_description": "Human disease"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "desesperança", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "desesperança", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "desesperança", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["desesperança"], "contexts": ["despair"]}, {"entity_id": "despair", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "despair", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["despair"], "contexts": ["loss of hope"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "desesperança", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "despair", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 4515, "stable_key": "clu_dfb7e84b0e0cc6a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "despair", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "despair", "note": "In early modern medicine, emotional states like despair were often considered to have physical manifestations or could be indicative of illness. 'desesperança' is the Portuguese word for despair.", "wikidata_id": "Q1747066", "wikidata_description": "feeling that there is no hope and that you can do nothing to improve a difficult or worrying situation"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "menstruo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "menstruo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "menstruo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["menstruo"], "contexts": ["solvent or menstruum for extraction"]}, {"entity_id": "menstruum", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "menstruum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["menstruum"], "contexts": ["dissolving liquid, e.g., water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "menstruo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "menstruum", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 4516, "stable_key": "clu_73b7756d50873b74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solvent", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q146505", "wikidata_description": "substance that dissolves a solute (a chemically different liquid, solid or gas), resulting in a solution", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solvent", "wikipedia_extract": "A solvent (from the Latin solvō, \"loosen, untie, solve\") is a substance that dissolves a solute, resulting in a solution. A solvent is usually a liquid but can also be a solid, a gas, or a supercritical fluid. Water is a solvent for polar molecules, and the most common solvent used by living things; all the ions and proteins in a cell are dissolved in water within the cell. Major uses of solvents are in paints, paint removers, inks, and dry cleaning. Specific uses for organic solvents are in dry cleaning (e.g. tetrachloroethylene); as paint thinners (toluene, turpentine); as nail polish removers and solvents of glue (acetone, methyl acetate, ethyl acetate); in spot removers (hexane, petrol ether); in detergents (citrus terpenes); and in perfumes (ethanol)."}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Igreja", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "igreja", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Igreja", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Igreja"], "contexts": ["Church"]}, {"entity_id": "church", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Church", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Church"], "contexts": ["religious part of government"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Igreja", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Church", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 4517, "stable_key": "clu_3257d9a3afb5367e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Church", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Igreja' is Portuguese for 'Church'. In the context of early modern texts, it refers to the institution of the Church, often with political or social implications, rather than a specific theological doctrine or building in isolation."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Divinity", "link_type": "same_referent", "link_strength": 1.0, "target_cluster_id": 2345, "target_cluster_name": "Divinity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Church is an institution often seen as representing or serving Divinity on Earth.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: The Church is an institution often seen as representing or serving Divinity on Earth.", "is_reverse": true}, {"found_name": "unity", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 409, "target_cluster_name": "unity", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "The Church is often conceived as a unified body of believers.", "confide