": "Inequality", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 4519, "stable_key": "clu_0ddeebb6ab1c5fae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orbital Inequalities", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to deviations from perfectly regular or predictable astronomical orbits, a concept studied in celestial mechanics."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "periodic inequalities", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": null, "target_cluster_name": null, "source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "evidence_snippet": "body. All such changes, being accomplished in short periods, some in a few months, others in years, or in hundreds of years, are denominated periodic inequalities. The inequalities of the other kind, ", "confidence": 0.65, "auto_label": "subtype_relation", "found_relationship": "type of inequality"}]}, {"canonical_name": "equinoctial gales", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "equinoctial_gales", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equinoctial gales", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["equinoctial gales"], "contexts": ["strong winds during equinoxes"]}, {"entity_id": "equinoxial_gale", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "equinoxial gale", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["equinoxial gale"], "contexts": ["weather event causing leaf detachment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "equinoctial gales", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "equinoxial gale", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 4520, "stable_key": "clu_87e2a4a110b7e7c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Equinoctial gales", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While not a disease, these are significant weather events discussed in scientific contexts."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "magnetic storms", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 2003, "target_cluster_name": "magnetic storms", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Both 'magnetic storms' and 'equinoctial gales' refer to natural phenomena that can cause disruptions, though 'magnetic storms' are specifically related to solar activity affecting Earth's magnetic field, while 'equinoctial gales' are strong winds associated with the equinoxes. The historical context of 'magnetic storms' as a 'weather event' suggests a connection to atmospheric and geophysical disturbances.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Both 'magnetic storms' and 'equinoctial gales' refer to natural phenomena that can cause disruptions, though 'magnetic storms' are specifically related to solar activity affecting Earth's magnetic field, while 'equinoctial gales' are strong winds associated with the equinoxes. The historical context of 'magnetic storms' as a 'weather event' suggests a connection to atmospheric and geophysical disturbances.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "ethereal electric medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ethereal_electric_medium", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ethereal electric medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ethereal electric medium"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium filling space"]}, {"entity_id": "ethereal_medium", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ethereal medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ethereal medium"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium for light and motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name"