rations in the air", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vibrations_in_the_air", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "vibrations in the air", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vibrations in the air"], "contexts": ["passing over Pan's pipes"]}, {"entity_id": "aerial_vibrations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "aerial vibrations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["aerial vibrations"], "contexts": ["waves in the air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "vibrations in the air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "aerial vibrations", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 4542, "stable_key": "clu_b7a8a9d35ba69b8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sound waves", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'vibrations in the air' and 'aerial vibrations' passing over 'Pan's pipes' strongly suggests the concept of sound waves, which are mechanical waves traveling through a medium like air.", "wikidata_id": "Q3882459", "wikidata_description": "longitudinal wave that propagates by means of adiabatic compression and decompression;travels with the speed of sound which depends on the medium they're passing through"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "Protoxides of metals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "protoxides_of_metals", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Protoxides of metals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Protoxides of metals"], "contexts": ["metal compounds with crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "protoxides", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "protoxides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["protoxides"], "contexts": ["oxides with one atom of oxygen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Protoxides of metals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "protoxides", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 4543, "stable_key": "clu_df03be0aa61dc3ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metal oxides", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Protoxides are a specific type of metal oxide, with 'metal oxides' being the broader modern category.", "wikidata_id": "Q1795231", "wikidata_description": "binary chemical compound of a metallic element and oxygen", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxyde_m%C3%A9tallique", "wikipedia_extract": "Un oxyde métallique est un composé chimique de l'oxygène et d'un métal. En raison de la différence d'électronégativité entre les métaux (électropositifs) et l'oxygène (fortement électronégatif), ces composés sont pour la plupart ioniques (constitués d'anions oxyde et de cations métalliques) ou du moins fortement polaires. Un grand nombre d'oxydes métalliques, à l'exception notable de ceux des métaux alcalins, sont connus sous la forme de minéraux. La corrosion des métaux exposés à l'air les transforme aussi généralement en oxydes (mais aussi en hydroxydes, parfois en sulfures ou en carbonates). Les oxydes métalliques, présents dans les minerais, sont la matière première de la métallurgie : c'est à partir d'oxydes que sont élaborés les métaux. Les oxydes métalliques donnent de la couleur aux poteries, le verre, aux fusées de feu d'artifice ou aux émaux."}, "cross_references": [{"found_name": "Zinc", "link_type": "conceptual_overlap", "link_strength": 0.5, "target_cluster_id": 288, "target_cluster_name": "Zinc", "source_book": "llm_generated", "evidence_snippet": "Zinc forms various oxides, such as zinc oxide, which are compounds of zinc and oxygen.", "confidence": 0.85, "auto_label": "llm_related", "found_relationship": "reverse: Zinc forms various oxides, such as zinc oxide, which are compounds of zinc and oxygen.", "is_reverse": true}]}, {"canonical_name": "Table-lands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "table-lands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Table-lands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Table-lands"], "contexts": ["high table-lands influence on atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "table-lands", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "table-lands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["table-lands"], "contexts": ["elevated flat areas of land"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Table-lands", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "table-lands", "similarity": 0.97}], "id": 4544, "stable_key": "clu_d9271896ee0ca885", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "plateau", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}, "cross_references": []}, {"canonical_name": "transits", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "transits", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "transits", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["transits"], "contexts": ["celestial event"]}, {"entity_id": "transit", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "transit", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["transit"], "contexts": ["act of passing through or across"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "transits", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "transit", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 4545, "stable_key": "clu_f406c23b6d14b912", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "transit (astronomy)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of celestial bodies and passing around a central point strongly suggests astronomical transits, such as the transit of a planet across the face of the sun."}, "cross_references": []}]}